DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"paliperidone-palmitate": {
  "origin": "Paliperidone grew out of risperidone, the atypical antipsychotic that Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium brought to the US market in 1993. Researchers knew that the liver turns risperidone into 9-hydroxyrisperidone, an active metabolite that does much of the work. Janssen developed that metabolite as its own drug, paliperidone, approved as oral Invega in 2006. To solve the problem of missed pills, the company attached a long palmitic acid (a fatty acid) tail and milled the ester into nanocrystals that dissolve slowly in muscle. Invega Sustenna, the monthly shot, was approved in 2009. Longer versions followed: Invega Trinza every three months in 2015 and Invega Hafyera every six months in 2021. A newer product, Erzofri, uses the same molecule. These products mattered because relapse in schizophrenia is often driven by stopping medication.",
  "timeline": [["1993", "Risperidone, the parent drug, is approved in the US."], ["2006", "Oral paliperidone (Invega) is approved."], ["2009", "FDA approves monthly Invega Sustenna."], ["2015", "Three-month Invega Trinza is approved."], ["2021", "Six-month Invega Hafyera is approved."]],
  "cell": "Once the palmitate ester is cleaved, free paliperidone blocks dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic pathway, D2 blockade reduces the overactive dopamine signaling linked to hallucinations and delusions. D2 receptors are Gi-coupled, so blocking them removes dopamine's brake on adenylyl cyclase in those neurons. Paliperidone also blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors with high affinity. 5-HT2A blockade on cortical and nigrostriatal circuits allows some dopamine release in the striatum, which helps explain why movement side effects are less common than with older drugs at modest exposure. Blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway removes dopamine's inhibition of pituitary lactotrophs, so prolactin rises. Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vessels and H1 blockade causes sedation and weight gain. It has almost no muscarinic activity, so dry mouth and constipation are uncommon.",
  "body": [["Brain (mood and thinking)", "Calmer dopamine signaling in limbic areas reduces psychotic symptoms over weeks of steady exposure."], ["Brain (movement)", "D2 blockade in the striatum can cause restlessness (akathisia), stiffness and tremor, more so at higher exposure."], ["Pituitary", "Loss of dopamine's brake raises prolactin, which can cause breast enlargement, milk discharge and menstrual changes. Paliperidone raises prolactin more than many atypicals."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade widens vessels and can drop blood pressure on standing, with dizziness or fainting."], ["Metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C effects raise appetite, and weight, glucose and lipids can climb over time."], ["Muscle (injection site)", "The deltoid or gluteal muscle serves as a slow-release reservoir, and local pain or swelling can occur."]],
  "journey": "After deep IM injection, the nanocrystals sit in muscle and dissolve slowly because the palmitate ester is poorly water soluble. Dissolved ester is hydrolyzed to paliperidone, which enters the blood. The first two injections go into the deltoid, where blood flow is higher, to reach useful levels within days. Peak levels come about two weeks after a dose. Because release is the slow step, the apparent half-life is about four to seven weeks. Paliperidone itself is barely metabolized, so CYP2D6 differences matter little. About 59% leaves unchanged in urine, which is why kidney impairment raises levels and moderate to severe impairment is not recommended.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects trace to receptor blockade. Striatal D2 blockade produces akathisia and parkinsonism, and long exposure can lead to tardive dyskinesia from adaptive changes in dopamine receptors. Pituitary D2 blockade raises prolactin. Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostasis and, rarely, priapism. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare reaction to sudden central dopamine blockade with rigidity, fever and autonomic instability. The QT interval can lengthen from potassium channel effects. Elderly people with dementia have higher death rates, mostly from heart and infection causes, which is a class boxed warning. Because the depot cannot be removed, side effects can last for weeks. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Paliperidone is literally what risperidone becomes in your liver, so taking paliperidone skips the CYP2D6 step that makes risperidone levels vary between people.",
  "src": ["Invega Sustenna (paliperidone palmitate) FDA label", "Invega Trinza and Invega Hafyera FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"olanzapine-pamoate": {
  "origin": "Olanzapine was designed by chemists at Eli Lilly in the 1980s, working to build a drug with the benefits of clozapine but without its risk of agranulocytosis. They kept clozapine's general three-ring shape and changed parts of it, creating a thienobenzodiazepine. Oral olanzapine (Zyprexa) was approved in 1996 and became one of the most used antipsychotics. To help people who stop pills, Lilly paired olanzapine with pamoic acid, forming a salt that dissolves very slowly in muscle. Zyprexa Relprevv was approved by the FDA in 2009. During trials, a small number of patients became very sleepy or confused within hours of an injection, which led to strict observation rules under a REMS program.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Lilly chemists develop olanzapine as a safer relative of clozapine."], ["1996", "FDA approves oral olanzapine (Zyprexa)."], ["2009", "FDA approves Zyprexa Relprevv, the long-acting pamoate injection."]],
  "cell": "Olanzapine blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, damping the excess dopamine signal linked to positive symptoms of schizophrenia. It also blocks 5-HT2A receptors strongly, which favors dopamine release in the striatum and prefrontal cortex and lowers the risk of movement side effects compared with older drugs. Its strong blockade of histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2C receptors drives sedation and appetite. It also blocks muscarinic receptors and alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. The pamoate salt itself has no activity; it only controls how fast olanzapine reaches the blood. If a large amount reaches the blood quickly, the combined H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 blockade produces the sedation and delirium seen in post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome.",
  "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "Lower limbic dopamine signaling eases hallucinations and delusions with steady release over weeks."], ["Brain (alertness)", "H1 blockade causes sleepiness, and a sudden high level can cause deep sedation, confusion or coma."], ["Appetite and metabolism", "H1 and 5-HT2C blockade increase hunger. Weight gain, higher glucose, diabetic ketoacidosis in rare cases and higher lipids can follow."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade relaxes vessels and can cause dizziness on standing."], ["Autonomic system", "Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention, and can add to confusion."], ["Buttock muscle", "The gluteal muscle holds the depot. Injection into a small vessel may let drug escape quickly, which is the leading theory for post-injection syndrome."]],
  "journey": "The suspension is injected deep into the gluteal muscle. The pamoate salt slowly dissolves and separates into olanzapine and pamoic acid, so release, not elimination, sets the pace. Levels peak a few days after a shot and the apparent half-life is about 30 days. In the liver, olanzapine is mainly glucuronidated and oxidized by CYP1A2, with a minor CYP2D6 role. Smoking induces CYP1A2 and lowers levels. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. It is not for IV or subcutaneous use, and it is not interchangeable with the short-acting olanzapine IM shot.",
  "sidefx": "Post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome is the hallmark risk: if drug enters the blood too fast, levels spike and the strong H1, muscarinic and alpha-1 blockade causes sleepiness, confusion, slurred speech, unsteady walking or coma. That is why each injection is followed by at least three hours of observation in a registered facility. Weight gain and high glucose come from appetite effects and changes in insulin sensitivity. Movement effects are less common than with typical drugs but can occur from D2 blockade. NMS and DRESS are rare but serious. There is no antidote; care is supportive, and symptoms fade as levels fall.",
  "fun": "Pamoic acid, the partner in this salt, is also used to make long-acting forms of other drugs, such as hydroxyzine pamoate and the deworming drug pyrantel pamoate.",
  "src": ["Zyprexa Relprevv (olanzapine pamoate) FDA label and REMS", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"haloperidol-decanoate": {
  "origin": "Haloperidol was made in 1958 at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen. His team was modifying pethidine (meperidine)-like molecules in search of better painkillers and noticed that one butyrophenone compound calmed animals in a way that resembled chlorpromazine. That compound was haloperidol. It spread quickly in Europe and was approved in the US in 1967. Because many people with schizophrenia relapse after stopping pills, chemists attached a ten-carbon decanoic acid chain to haloperidol's hydroxyl group and dissolved it in sesame oil. This oily depot releases drug over about a month. Haloperidol decanoate became available in the US in the 1980s and remains a common long-acting injection.",
  "timeline": [["1958", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes haloperidol in Belgium."], ["1967", "Haloperidol is approved in the US."], ["1980s", "Haloperidol decanoate becomes available in the US."]],
  "cell": "After esterases cut off the decanoate chain, haloperidol binds dopamine D2 receptors with high affinity and blocks them. D2 receptors are Gi-coupled, so dopamine normally lowers cAMP in the receiving neuron. In the mesolimbic pathway, blocking this signal reduces hallucinations and delusions. The same blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway upsets the dopamine-acetylcholine balance that controls movement, causing extrapyramidal symptoms. In the tuberoinfundibular pathway, dopamine normally suppresses prolactin release, so blockade raises prolactin. Haloperidol has only weak alpha-1 activity and very little muscarinic or histamine blockade, so it causes less sedation, low blood pressure and dry mouth than low-potency drugs, but more movement problems.",
  "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "Strong D2 blockade in limbic areas reduces positive symptoms such as hallucinations and paranoia."], ["Brain (movement)", "Striatal D2 blockade causes dystonia, parkinsonism and akathisia. With years of use, tardive dyskinesia can develop."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin rises, which can cause breast changes, milk discharge, menstrual changes and sexual dysfunction."], ["Heart", "Haloperidol can lengthen the QT interval by blocking cardiac potassium (hERG) channels, raising the risk of torsades de pointes."], ["Temperature control", "Dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus contributes to the fever and instability of NMS."]],
  "journey": "Haloperidol decanoate in sesame oil is given by deep IM injection, never IV. The oily depot slowly releases the ester into tissue fluid, where esterases free active haloperidol. Levels peak about six days after an injection, and the apparent half-life is about three weeks, which allows monthly dosing. Steady state takes several months. In the liver, haloperidol is processed by CYP3A4, CYP2D6, glucuronidation and reduction to reduced haloperidol. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. About 90% is bound to plasma proteins.",
  "sidefx": "Movement side effects are the main issue and come from nigrostriatal D2 blockade. Acute dystonia, including dangerous laryngeal spasm, can occur early. Akathisia and parkinsonism follow, and tardive dyskinesia develops after long exposure as receptors become hypersensitive. Anticholinergic drugs such as benztropine or diphenhydramine treat acute dystonia, but there is no reversal for the drug itself. QT prolongation reflects hERG potassium channel blockade. NMS is a rare, severe reaction with rigidity, fever and rising CK. Since the depot keeps releasing drug, effects can last for weeks, which is why oral tolerance is confirmed first.",
  "fun": "Haloperidol was discovered while Janssen's team was hunting for painkillers, a reminder that some of medicine's key drugs were found by watching animals closely for unexpected effects.",
  "src": ["Haloperidol decanoate injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"fluphenazine-decanoate": {
  "origin": "Fluphenazine is a phenothiazine, a family that began with chlorpromazine, made in France in 1950 by Rhone-Poulenc and found in 1952 to calm patients with psychosis. Chemists then tuned the phenothiazine ring with different side chains. Adding a trifluoromethyl group and a piperazine side chain produced fluphenazine, a very potent D2 blocker developed by Squibb in the late 1950s. In the 1960s Squibb attached fatty acid esters to its free hydroxyl group, first enanthate and then decanoate, and dissolved them in sesame oil. Fluphenazine decanoate was among the first long-acting injectable antipsychotics, a major step for keeping people out of the hospital. The Prolixin Decanoate brand is discontinued, but generic fluphenazine decanoate remains available.",
  "timeline": [["1950", "Chlorpromazine, the first phenothiazine antipsychotic, is synthesized in France."], ["1950s", "Squibb develops fluphenazine, a high-potency phenothiazine."], ["1960s", "Long-acting fluphenazine esters (enanthate, then decanoate) are introduced."]],
  "cell": "The depot releases fluphenazine decanoate, and esterases cut off the decanoate chain to free fluphenazine. Fluphenazine competitively blocks postsynaptic dopamine D2 receptors. In the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways, this reduces the excess dopamine signaling linked to hallucinations and delusions. In the nigrostriatal pathway, the same blockade reduces dopamine's control of movement and allows relative cholinergic excess, causing extrapyramidal symptoms. In the tuberoinfundibular pathway it removes dopamine's inhibition of prolactin release. As a high-potency piperazine phenothiazine, it has only weak muscarinic, H1 and alpha-1 blockade compared with chlorpromazine, so it causes less sedation and low blood pressure but more movement effects.",
  "body": [["Brain (psychosis)", "One injection can keep D2 receptors blocked for weeks, reducing positive symptoms and relapse."], ["Brain (movement)", "Akathisia, parkinsonism and dystonia are common and can persist for weeks because the depot keeps releasing drug."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin rises, with possible breast changes, menstrual changes and sexual dysfunction."], ["Blood", "Phenothiazines can rarely cause leukopenia or agranulocytosis, raising infection risk."], ["Liver", "Cholestatic jaundice is a known, uncommon phenothiazine reaction."], ["Temperature and autonomic system", "Dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus can impair heat control and plays a role in NMS."]],
  "journey": "Fluphenazine decanoate is dissolved in sesame oil and injected IM or subcutaneously. The ester leaks out of the oil depot slowly, and esterases release active fluphenazine. Antipsychotic effect can begin within one to three days, with a peak level about a day or two after injection. The half-life is about one to two weeks, so effects last three to four weeks or longer and it takes months to reach steady state. The liver oxidizes fluphenazine, largely by CYP2D6, and conjugates it, with elimination in urine and bile.",
  "sidefx": "Extrapyramidal symptoms are the signature problem and come from strong nigrostriatal D2 blockade. Acute dystonia can involve the neck, eyes or larynx. Akathisia and parkinsonism are common, and tardive dyskinesia can appear after long use as dopamine receptors become supersensitive. Anticholinergic drugs treat acute dystonia and parkinsonism, but the depot itself cannot be reversed, so symptoms can persist. NMS is a rare, life-threatening reaction. Low blood counts, cholestatic jaundice and seizures are rare phenothiazine effects. Elderly people with dementia have increased mortality, a class boxed warning.",
  "fun": "Fluphenazine is roughly 50 times more potent per milligram at D2 receptors than chlorpromazine, which is why it is called a high-potency antipsychotic.",
  "src": ["Fluphenazine decanoate injection FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"dextromethorphan-quinidine": {
  "origin": "This combination joins two old drugs for a new use. Dextromethorphan was developed in the 1950s as a non-addictive cough suppressant related to the opioid levorphanol, and was approved as a cough medicine in 1958. Quinidine comes from the bark of the cinchona tree, the same South American source as quinine, and was long used for heart rhythm problems. In the 1990s, researchers working with neurologist Richard Smith in San Diego noticed that dextromethorphan seemed to help pseudobulbar affect, a condition of sudden, uncontrollable laughing or crying after brain injury, ALS or multiple sclerosis. The problem was that the liver broke it down too quickly. Adding a small amount of quinidine, which blocks CYP2D6, kept dextromethorphan in the blood. Avanir Pharmaceuticals developed the product as Nuedexta, approved in 2010 as the first FDA-approved treatment for pseudobulbar affect.",
  "timeline": [["1958", "Dextromethorphan is approved as a cough suppressant."], ["1990s", "Researchers explore dextromethorphan with quinidine for pseudobulbar affect."], ["2010", "FDA approves Nuedexta, the first treatment for pseudobulbar affect."]],
  "cell": "Dextromethorphan blocks the NMDA glutamate receptor in an uncompetitive way, meaning it binds inside the open channel and reduces excess excitatory signaling. It is also a sigma-1 receptor agonist; sigma-1 is a chaperone protein in neuron membranes that modulates calcium signaling and glutamate release. It weakly inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake. How these actions calm pseudobulbar affect is not fully known, but the circuits linking the cortex, brainstem and cerebellum that control emotional expression are thought to be involved. Quinidine's role is pharmacokinetic. It competitively inhibits CYP2D6, the liver enzyme that O-demethylates dextromethorphan into dextrorphan. With CYP2D6 blocked, dextromethorphan levels rise many times over and stay high.",
  "body": [["Brain (emotional expression)", "Fewer and milder bursts of crying or laughing that do not match how the person feels."], ["Brain (balance)", "Dizziness and falls can occur, partly from central effects on balance."], ["Liver enzymes", "Quinidine blocks CYP2D6, which can raise levels of other drugs that rely on it, such as some antidepressants and beta-blockers."], ["Heart", "Quinidine can lengthen the QT interval by blocking potassium channels, so torsades de pointes is a risk with QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Blood", "Quinidine can trigger immune-mediated thrombocytopenia, with bruising or bleeding."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common."]],
  "journey": "Both parts are taken by mouth as one capsule. Quinidine peaks in about two hours and dextromethorphan in about three to four hours. Without quinidine, dextromethorphan is cleared so fast by CYP2D6 that little stays in the blood. With quinidine, its half-life stretches to about 13 hours. Dextromethorphan is then handled more by CYP3A4. Quinidine itself is cleared by CYP3A4 with a half-life of about seven hours. The amount of quinidine is far lower than what was once used for arrhythmias. Metabolites leave in urine. Benefit builds over days to weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Dizziness and falls come from dextromethorphan's central effects. QT prolongation and torsades come from quinidine's potassium channel blockade and grow with CYP3A4 inhibitors or other QT drugs. Quinidine can bind platelets and trigger antibodies that destroy them, causing sudden thrombocytopenia; the drug is stopped if this happens. Blocking CYP2D6 raises other drugs' levels, and with serotonergic drugs, the combination can cause serotonin syndrome. Liver injury and hypersensitivity can occur. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "The quinidine in this capsule is not there to treat anything; it is a 'booster' that disables a liver enzyme, the same idea used when ritonavir boosts other HIV drugs.",
  "src": ["Nuedexta (dextromethorphan and quinidine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"ciclesonide": {
  "origin": "Ciclesonide was developed in Germany by Altana Pharma (formerly Byk Gulden) with a clear goal: an inhaled steroid that stays inactive until it reaches the lungs. Chemists built it as a prodrug ester. Enzymes in airway cells cut it into the active form, des-ciclesonide, so less active steroid lands in the mouth and throat. In the US, the nasal spray Omnaris was approved in 2006 and the asthma inhaler Alvesco in 2008, followed by the Zetonna nasal aerosol. Ciclesonide also drew attention in 2020, when lab studies suggested it could block coronavirus replication, though clinical trials did not show a clear benefit.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Altana (Byk Gulden) develops ciclesonide as an on-site activated inhaled steroid."], ["2006", "Omnaris nasal spray is approved in the US."], ["2008", "FDA approves Alvesco inhalation aerosol for asthma."]],
  "cell": "Ciclesonide itself barely binds the glucocorticoid receptor. Esterases in the airway lining remove its isobutyryl group to form des-ciclesonide, which binds the receptor with about 100 times higher affinity. Inside the cell, the bound receptor moves to the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes and, more importantly, blocks pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kB and AP-1. Fewer cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13 are made, so eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the airway decline. Airway swelling and twitchiness fall over days to weeks. Des-ciclesonide also forms fatty acid conjugates inside lung cells that act as a slow-release store.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Less inflammation and swelling make airways less reactive, with fewer asthma attacks and better lung function over weeks."], ["Mouth and throat", "Less active steroid lands in the mouth than with some inhaled steroids, but thrush and hoarseness can still occur."], ["Nose (nasal forms)", "Reduced congestion, sneezing and runny nose in allergic rhinitis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Steroid that reaches the blood can suppress the body's own cortisol production, especially at high exposure."], ["Bones and eyes", "Long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density and raise the risk of cataracts and glaucoma."], ["Growth", "Children on inhaled steroids may have a small slowing of growth."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled as a fine aerosol, much of the dose reaches the lungs. Lung esterases convert it to des-ciclesonide, which peaks in blood about an hour after inhalation. The swallowed portion has almost no oral bioavailability because both parent drug and active metabolite are destroyed on first pass through the liver by CYP3A4. Des-ciclesonide is about 99% bound to plasma proteins, which leaves little free drug to act elsewhere. Its half-life is about six to seven hours. Metabolites leave mainly in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect glucocorticoid action where the drug lands or reaches. Steroid on the throat lining lowers local immunity, so thrush can occur; rinsing the mouth helps. Steroid that reaches the blood can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, so switching from oral steroids too fast can unmask adrenal insufficiency. Immune suppression can make chickenpox and measles more severe. Long-term exposure can thin bones, slow growth and affect the eyes. It does not open airways quickly and is not for acute attacks. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Because ciclesonide is switched on by enzymes inside airway cells, it has been described as an 'on-site activated' steroid.",
  "src": ["Alvesco (ciclesonide) FDA label", "Omnaris (ciclesonide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"flunisolide": {
  "origin": "Flunisolide is a synthetic corticosteroid from the era when companies raced to make steroids that worked strongly in the nose and lungs but were broken down quickly once swallowed. It was developed by Syntex, the company in Palo Alto known for its steroid chemistry, and was introduced in the US in the early 1980s as Nasalide nasal spray. An inhaler form for asthma followed. Over time the brands Nasalide, Nasarel and the Aerospan HFA inhaler were discontinued, but generic flunisolide nasal solution remains available.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Synthetic corticosteroids with fluorine substitutions are developed for stronger effect."], ["1980s", "Flunisolide nasal spray (Nasalide) is introduced in the US."], ["2006", "The Aerospan HFA inhaler form is approved."]],
  "cell": "Flunisolide is a fluorinated corticosteroid that enters cells of the nasal lining and binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. The receptor-drug complex moves into the nucleus. There it turns up anti-inflammatory genes and blocks pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kB and AP-1. The result is fewer cytokines and chemokines, so mast cells, eosinophils and T cells gather less in the nasal tissue. Vessels leak less fluid, so swelling, mucus and sneezing decrease. Because these are gene-level effects, benefit takes several days to build.",
  "body": [["Nose", "Less swelling, runny nose, itching and sneezing in allergic and non-allergic rhinitis, building over days."], ["Nasal lining", "Local steroid can thin the lining, causing nosebleeds and, rarely, septal perforation."], ["Throat and sinuses", "Less post-nasal drip as inflammation calms; sore throat and altered taste or smell can occur."], ["Adrenal glands", "With excessive use, absorbed steroid can suppress the body's cortisol production."], ["Eyes", "Long-term corticosteroid exposure is linked to cataracts and glaucoma."]],
  "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, flunisolide acts mostly on the nasal lining. Some is absorbed through the lining, and much of the spray is swallowed. The swallowed drug is heavily removed on its first pass through the liver, which converts it to a 6-beta-hydroxy metabolite with little activity. The half-life in blood is short, about one to two hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Benefit starts within days, with full effect over one to three weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Nasal burning and stinging are partly from the solution itself. Nosebleeds and rare septal perforation reflect steroid thinning of the nasal mucosa. Lower local immunity can allow nasal Candida infection. At high exposure, absorbed steroid can suppress the adrenal axis, and switching from oral steroids can unmask adrenal insufficiency. Immune suppression can worsen chickenpox or measles. Children may show slowed growth with long use. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Flunisolide nasal solution FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"arformoterol": {
  "origin": "Arformoterol is one mirror-image half of formoterol, a long-acting beta-2 agonist developed in Japan by Yamanouchi in the 1970s. Formoterol is a racemic mix of two enantiomers. The (R,R) form does the bronchodilating work, while the (S,S) form binds the receptor poorly. Sepracor, a US company known for developing single-isomer versions of existing drugs, purified the active (R,R) form and turned it into a nebulizer solution for COPD. Brovana was approved by the FDA in 2006 as the first long-acting beta-2 agonist given by nebulizer, which helped patients who struggle to use handheld inhalers.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Formoterol is developed by Yamanouchi in Japan."], ["2006", "FDA approves Brovana (arformoterol) nebulizer solution for COPD."]],
  "cell": "Arformoterol binds beta-2 adrenergic receptors on bronchial smooth muscle. These are Gs-coupled receptors, so binding activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cyclic AMP. Cyclic AMP turns on protein kinase A, which lowers calcium in the cell and inhibits myosin light chain kinase. The muscle relaxes and the airway opens. Formoterol-type molecules are moderately lipophilic, so they enter the cell membrane and leak out to the receptor over time, giving fast onset with a 12-hour effect. Beta-2 activation on mast cells also reduces mediator release. At higher exposure, beta-2 receptors in the heart, skeletal muscle and liver cause tachycardia, tremor, low potassium and high glucose.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Bronchial smooth muscle relaxes within about 20 minutes and stays open for about 12 hours."], ["Heart", "Beta stimulation can raise heart rate and, in some people, cause arrhythmias."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Beta-2 activation causes tremor and leg cramps."], ["Potassium balance", "Beta-2 activation pushes potassium into cells via the Na-K ATPase, lowering blood potassium."], ["Liver and glucose", "Increased glycogen breakdown raises blood glucose."]],
  "journey": "Arformoterol is breathed in as a mist from a nebulizer. Most of its action happens in the airway, and a small part is absorbed into the blood, peaking in about one to three hours. It is mainly broken down by direct glucuronidation, with some O-demethylation by CYP2D6 and CYP2C19. It leaves mostly in urine. The half-life is about 26 hours in COPD patients, though the dosing is twice daily because bronchodilation lasts about 12 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from beta-2 stimulation outside the lungs. Tachycardia and arrhythmias arise from cardiac beta receptors. Tremor and cramps come from skeletal muscle beta-2 receptors. Low potassium follows from potassium shifting into cells, and high glucose from liver glycogen breakdown. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare and calls for stopping the drug. It is not a rescue drug and is not indicated for asthma, where LABA use without a steroid was linked to serious events. Beta-blockers can reverse effects but may trigger bronchospasm.",
  "fun": "Arformoterol is an example of a 'chiral switch': taking an existing racemic drug and developing only its active mirror image.",
  "src": ["Brovana (arformoterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"olodaterol": {
  "origin": "Olodaterol was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany as a beta-2 agonist that lasts a full day. Earlier long-acting agonists such as salmeterol and formoterol needed twice-daily dosing. Boehringer designed olodaterol to bind tightly and stay near the receptor for 24 hours, and paired it with its Respimat soft-mist inhaler, which releases a slow-moving cloud. Striverdi Respimat was approved by the FDA in 2014 for COPD. In 2015 olodaterol was combined with tiotropium as Stiolto Respimat.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops olodaterol as a once-daily beta-2 agonist."], ["2014", "FDA approves Striverdi Respimat for COPD."], ["2015", "Stiolto Respimat (tiotropium and olodaterol) is approved."]],
  "cell": "Olodaterol is a selective beta-2 adrenergic agonist with high intrinsic activity, meaning it nearly fully activates the receptor. Binding to beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle activates Gs, which turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cyclic AMP. Protein kinase A then lowers intracellular calcium and reduces myosin light chain phosphorylation, so the muscle relaxes. Olodaterol stays associated with the receptor and lung tissue for a long time, which gives a 24-hour effect. Its onset is fast, within about five minutes.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Bronchodilation starts within minutes and lasts about 24 hours, easing breathlessness in COPD."], ["Heart", "Beta stimulation can raise heart rate and cause palpitations or arrhythmias."], ["Muscles", "Beta-2 activation in skeletal muscle can cause tremor."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Potassium shifts into cells and glucose can rise at higher exposure."]],
  "journey": "The Respimat inhaler produces a slow soft mist that is breathed into the lungs. Plasma levels peak within about 10 to 20 minutes. About 60% is bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by glucuronidation and O-demethylation by CYP2C9 and CYP2C8. It leaves in both feces and urine. The terminal half-life is about 45 hours, while the effective half-life is about 7.5 hours, matching once-daily dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from beta-2 activation outside the lung: fast heart rate and arrhythmias, tremor, low potassium and raised glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. Olodaterol is not a rescue inhaler and is not approved for asthma because long-acting beta agonists without an inhaled steroid were linked to serious asthma events. Using it with another LABA raises the risk of overdose effects. There is no specific antidote; a cardioselective beta-blocker can be used with caution in severe cases.",
  "src": ["Striverdi Respimat (olodaterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"aclidinium": {
  "origin": "Aclidinium is a modern descendant of atropine, the alkaloid from plants such as belladonna and jimsonweed. For centuries people smoked Datura leaves to ease asthma, which worked because of their antimuscarinic action. Chemists later made quaternary ammonium versions that stay in the lungs and do not cross into the brain. Aclidinium was developed by Almirall in Spain to be broken down very quickly in the blood, so it acts in the airway with few effects elsewhere. Partnered with Forest Laboratories in the US, it was approved by the FDA in 2012 as Tudorza Pressair, a dry powder inhaler for COPD.",
  "timeline": [["1800s", "Smoking Datura (stramonium) for asthma is common in Western medicine."], ["2012", "FDA approves Tudorza Pressair (aclidinium) for COPD."], ["2019", "Aclidinium combined with formoterol (Duaklir Pressair) is approved."]],
  "cell": "In the airway, acetylcholine released from vagal nerves binds M3 muscarinic receptors on smooth muscle and glands. M3 is Gq-coupled, raising IP3 and calcium to contract the muscle and drive mucus secretion. Aclidinium competitively blocks these receptors. It binds all five muscarinic subtypes but dissociates much more slowly from M3 than from M2. M2 receptors sit on nerve endings and limit acetylcholine release, so letting M2 recover sooner is useful. Blocking M3 relaxes the airway and reduces mucus. Aclidinium is an ester that plasma butyrylcholinesterase breaks down quickly, which is the key to its low systemic anticholinergic effect.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Airways relax and stay open for about 12 hours, with less mucus."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth is less common than with some antimuscarinics because little active drug circulates."], ["Eyes", "If the powder reaches the eyes, or in susceptible people, pupil dilation can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Antimuscarinic action can make it harder to urinate, especially with an enlarged prostate."]],
  "journey": "Aclidinium is inhaled as a dry powder with a lactose carrier. It reaches the airways and is absorbed into the blood, peaking within about ten minutes. In plasma it is quickly hydrolyzed, mostly by butyrylcholinesterase, into an inactive alcohol and an inactive acid. So the amount of active drug in blood is very small. Its effective half-life is about five to eight hours, with dosing twice daily. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect muscarinic blockade wherever the drug reaches. Eye exposure can widen the pupil and trigger narrow-angle glaucoma. Bladder muscarinic blockade can cause urinary retention. Rapid breakdown in plasma limits classic effects such as fast heart rate and constipation. Paradoxical bronchospasm can occur. Hypersensitivity is possible, including in people with severe milk protein allergy because of the lactose carrier. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "The quaternary ammonium group in drugs like aclidinium carries a permanent positive charge, which keeps them from crossing into the brain the way atropine does.",
  "src": ["Tudorza Pressair (aclidinium) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"revefenacin": {
  "origin": "Revefenacin was discovered by Theravance (later Theravance Biopharma), a California company focused on respiratory drugs, and developed with Mylan. Most long-acting muscarinic antagonists came as handheld inhalers, which can be hard for frail COPD patients to use. Revefenacin was designed to last 24 hours and to be given by nebulizer. Yupelri was approved by the FDA in 2018 as the first once-daily nebulized long-acting antimuscarinic for COPD.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Theravance discovers revefenacin as a long-acting antimuscarinic."], ["2018", "FDA approves Yupelri, a once-daily nebulized LAMA for COPD."]],
  "cell": "Revefenacin is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors, with special effect at the M3 receptor on airway smooth muscle. Normally vagal acetylcholine activates Gq-coupled M3 receptors, increasing IP3 and calcium and causing contraction and mucus secretion. Revefenacin binds the receptor and leaves it very slowly, so bronchoconstriction is blocked for about 24 hours. It binds all five subtypes but stays longest at M3. In the liver it is hydrolyzed to an active metabolite that is less potent.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Bronchodilation over hours that lasts a full day, with less mucus."], ["Eyes", "Antimuscarinic action can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma if pressure rises."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."], ["Mouth and throat", "Cough and upper airway irritation are among the common effects."]],
  "journey": "Revefenacin is inhaled as a nebulized solution. A small part of the dose reaches the blood, peaking shortly after nebulization. It is about 71% bound to plasma proteins. The liver hydrolyzes it to an active metabolite. The metabolite is taken up by the liver transporters OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, so drugs such as rifampin or cyclosporine that block them raise its exposure. It is not recommended in liver impairment. Elimination is mainly in feces. The terminal half-life ranges widely, about 22 to 70 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from muscarinic blockade. Eye pain, blurred vision or halos can signal narrow-angle glaucoma. Trouble urinating reflects bladder muscarinic blockade. Paradoxical bronchospasm and hypersensitivity can occur. Because it acts mostly in the lungs, dry mouth and systemic effects are relatively mild. It is not a rescue drug. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Yupelri (revefenacin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"fluticasone-umeclidinium-vilanterol": {
  "origin": "Trelegy Ellipta combines three drugs from GSK's respiratory program into one inhaler. Fluticasone furoate is a newer member of the fluticasone family, made to stay in lung tissue for a full day. Umeclidinium is a long-acting antimuscarinic, and vilanterol is a 24-hour beta-2 agonist that GSK developed with Theravance. Before Trelegy, people with severe COPD often juggled two or three devices. In 2017 the FDA approved Trelegy as the first once-daily single-inhaler triple therapy for COPD, and an asthma indication followed in 2020. Trials showed fewer COPD flares with triple therapy than with dual combinations.",
  "timeline": [["2013", "Breo Ellipta (fluticasone furoate and vilanterol) and Anoro Ellipta (umeclidinium and vilanterol) are approved."], ["2017", "FDA approves Trelegy Ellipta, the first once-daily single-inhaler triple therapy for COPD."], ["2020", "Trelegy is approved for asthma."]],
  "cell": "Each part targets a different pathway. Fluticasone furoate binds the glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity; the complex moves to the nucleus and blocks NF-kB and AP-1, cutting cytokine production and inflammatory cell influx. Umeclidinium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors with slow release from M3 on airway smooth muscle, blocking acetylcholine's Gq-driven calcium rise and contraction. Vilanterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and protein kinase A activity, which relaxes airway smooth muscle. The two bronchodilators work on opposite sides of airway tone, so their effects add, while the steroid lowers inflammation that drives flares.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Two bronchodilators keep airways open for 24 hours while the steroid calms inflammation, reducing COPD flares."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid deposited in the mouth can cause thrush and voice changes."], ["Lungs (infection)", "Inhaled steroids raise the risk of pneumonia in people with COPD."], ["Heart", "Vilanterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Eyes and bladder", "Umeclidinium can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention; steroids add cataract and glaucoma risk."], ["Bones and adrenal glands", "Long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density and suppress cortisol production."]],
  "journey": "All three drugs are inhaled as a dry powder from one Ellipta device. Vilanterol and umeclidinium peak in blood within minutes, and fluticasone furoate within about an hour. Swallowed drug is poorly available because of first-pass metabolism. Fluticasone furoate and vilanterol are broken down by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise their levels. Umeclidinium is mainly metabolized by CYP2D6. All three are mostly eliminated in feces. Half-lives are about 24 hours for fluticasone furoate and about 11 hours for the other two, supporting once-daily use.",
  "sidefx": "Each part brings its own side effects. The steroid can cause thrush, pneumonia in COPD, adrenal suppression, bone loss and eye effects. Umeclidinium's muscarinic blockade can worsen glaucoma and urinary retention. Vilanterol's beta-2 action can cause fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not a rescue inhaler and is not combined with other LABA or LAMA products. There is no single antidote.",
  "src": ["Trelegy Ellipta FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"reslizumab": {
  "origin": "Reslizumab came from research on interleukin-5, the cytokine that tells the bone marrow to make eosinophils and keeps them alive in tissue. In the 1990s researchers at Schering-Plough developed a humanized antibody against IL-5. Early asthma trials in unselected patients were disappointing. Later work showed these antibodies help mainly people whose asthma is driven by eosinophils. The drug passed through several companies and was finally developed by Teva, which ran trials in patients with high blood eosinophil counts. Cinqair was approved by the FDA in 2016 for severe eosinophilic asthma. It is the only anti-IL-5 antibody given by IV infusion.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering-Plough develops a humanized anti-IL-5 antibody."], ["2015", "Mepolizumab, the first anti-IL-5 antibody, is approved."], ["2016", "FDA approves reslizumab (Cinqair) for severe eosinophilic asthma."]],
  "cell": "IL-5 is made by Th2 cells and group 2 innate lymphoid cells. It binds the IL-5 receptor alpha chain on eosinophils and their precursors, which then pairs with the common beta chain and activates JAK-STAT signaling. That signal drives eosinophil production, maturation, activation and survival. Reslizumab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds circulating IL-5 and prevents it from attaching to IL-5 receptor alpha. Without IL-5, fewer eosinophils are made and existing eosinophils die by apoptosis. Fewer eosinophils mean less release of toxic granule proteins and leukotrienes that damage the airway and drive asthma attacks.",
  "body": [["Blood", "Blood eosinophil counts drop within days."], ["Airways", "Fewer eosinophils in the airway lining reduce inflammation, mucus and flares, with modest improvement in lung function."], ["Immune system", "Eosinophils help fight parasitic worms, so helminth infections are treated before starting."], ["Muscles", "Muscle aches and a rise in CPK have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Anaphylaxis can occur during or soon after infusion."]],
  "journey": "Reslizumab is given as an IV infusion in a healthcare setting, so it enters the blood completely and peaks at the end of the infusion. As a large protein, it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down slowly by protein-digesting enzymes throughout the body, not by the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is about 24 days, so it is given every four weeks.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning is anaphylaxis, an immune reaction to the foreign protein, so patients are watched after each infusion. Lower eosinophils weaken defense against parasitic worms. A small imbalance in cancers was seen in trials, although a cause was not shown. Muscle aches and CPK rise are reported. It does not treat acute attacks, and steroids are not stopped suddenly. There is no antidote; effects fade as the antibody is cleared over weeks.",
  "src": ["Cinqair (reslizumab) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tezepelumab": {
  "origin": "Tezepelumab targets thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP), a cytokine first found in the 1990s in the supernatant of a mouse thymic stromal cell line. Researchers later learned that airway lining cells release TSLP when they meet allergens, viruses or pollution, and that it sits at the top of the inflammatory cascade. Amgen and AstraZeneca developed a human antibody against it. Because TSLP acts upstream of many pathways, the drug reduced asthma attacks in people with and without high eosinophil counts. Tezspire was approved by the FDA in 2021 for severe asthma with no restriction to a specific phenotype or biomarker, a first among asthma biologics. An approval for chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps followed.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "TSLP is identified from a mouse thymic stromal cell line."], ["2021", "FDA approves Tezspire (tezepelumab) for severe asthma."]],
  "cell": "When the airway epithelium is stressed by allergens, viruses or smoke, its cells release alarmins: TSLP, IL-33 and IL-25. TSLP binds a receptor made of the TSLP receptor chain plus IL-7 receptor alpha on dendritic cells, mast cells, innate lymphoid cells and T cells. This drives Th2 responses with IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, which in turn raise IgE, recruit eosinophils and increase nitric oxide production. TSLP also supports some non-type 2 inflammation. Tezepelumab is a human IgG2 lambda antibody that binds TSLP and prevents it from reaching its receptor. Blocking this signal at the top of the cascade lowers eosinophils, IgE and FeNO together.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Fewer asthma attacks and better lung function, regardless of baseline eosinophil level."], ["Blood", "Blood eosinophils and IgE levels fall over weeks."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Reduced inflammation can shrink nasal polyps."], ["Immune system", "Defense against parasitic worms may be weaker, and live vaccines are avoided."], ["Joints and throat", "Joint pain, back pain and sore throat are among reported effects."]],
  "journey": "Tezepelumab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system and peaks about three to ten days later. It is spread mainly in the blood and tissue fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein digestion throughout the body, not by liver enzymes or kidneys. The half-life is about 26 days, which supports dosing every four weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Because TSLP helps drive immune responses, blocking it may weaken defense against parasitic worms, so these are treated first. Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash or allergic conjunctivitis can occur, and anaphylaxis is rare. Sore throat, joint pain and back pain are common. It does not stop acute bronchospasm, and steroids are not stopped abruptly. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tezepelumab acts on the airway's 'alarm signal' itself, which is why it can help asthma that is not driven by eosinophils.",
  "src": ["Tezspire (tezepelumab) FDA label", "Menzies-Gow A, et al. NAVIGATOR trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]
},
"tobramycin-inhaled": {
  "origin": "Tobramycin was isolated from the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius in the 1960s and developed by Eli Lilly, which introduced injectable tobramycin in the 1970s. It was strong against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, the bacterium that chronically infects the lungs of people with cystic fibrosis. IV aminoglycosides reach low levels in thick sputum and can damage the ears and kidneys. Researchers, including Bonnie Ramsey and colleagues in Seattle, showed that breathing tobramycin directly into the lungs gives very high sputum levels with little in the blood. PathoGenesis Corporation developed a preservative-free solution, TOBI, approved by the FDA in 1997. A dry powder form, TOBI Podhaler, followed in 2013.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Tobramycin is isolated from Streptomyces tenebrarius."], ["1970s", "Injectable tobramycin is introduced."], ["1997", "FDA approves TOBI inhalation solution for cystic fibrosis."], ["1999", "A key NEJM trial confirms benefit of inhaled tobramycin in CF."], ["2013", "TOBI Podhaler dry powder is approved."]],
  "cell": "Tobramycin is a positively charged aminoglycoside. It first binds negatively charged lipopolysaccharide on the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria, then crosses the inner membrane by an oxygen-dependent transport process, which is why it does not work against anaerobes. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit. This blocks formation of the initiation complex and causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made. Some of these proteins insert into the membrane and make it leakier, letting in more drug. Killing is concentration-dependent with a post-antibiotic effect, so the very high sputum levels from inhalation kill Pseudomonas well.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Less Pseudomonas in sputum, better lung function and fewer hospital stays in cystic fibrosis."], ["Airways", "Cough, voice changes and bronchospasm can occur from local irritation."], ["Ears", "Even small amounts in blood can damage hair cells in the inner ear, causing tinnitus or hearing loss."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed drug is taken up by proximal tubule cells and can injure them, though this is uncommon with inhalation."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Aminoglycosides can worsen neuromuscular weakness, as in myasthenia gravis."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled tobramycin lands on airway surfaces and sputum, where levels peak within about ten minutes and stay far above the level needed to kill Pseudomonas. Only a small amount crosses into the blood, peaking in about an hour. Tobramycin is not metabolized. The absorbed fraction leaves unchanged through the kidneys with a serum half-life of about two hours. Much of the unabsorbed drug is coughed up in sputum. Treatment is usually given in alternating on and off months to reduce resistance.",
  "sidefx": "Local airway irritation explains cough, voice changes and bronchospasm. Ototoxicity happens because aminoglycosides build up in inner ear fluid and damage hair cells through free radical injury; the damage can be permanent. Nephrotoxicity reflects uptake into proximal tubule cells. Aminoglycosides also block presynaptic calcium entry at the neuromuscular junction, worsening weakness. They can cross the placenta and cause congenital deafness. Combining with loop diuretics or other nephrotoxic drugs increases risk. There is no antidote; calcium can partly reverse neuromuscular blockade.",
  "src": ["TOBI (tobramycin inhalation solution) FDA label", "Ramsey BW, et al. N Engl J Med 1999", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"amikacin-liposome-inhaled": {
  "origin": "Amikacin was made in 1972 in Japan by Hiroshi Kawaguchi and colleagues at Bristol-Banyu, who chemically added a side chain to kanamycin, a Streptomyces antibiotic. The side chain shields the molecule from many bacterial enzymes that inactivate aminoglycosides. Decades later, Insmed packaged amikacin inside tiny lipid spheres called liposomes so it could be inhaled and taken up by the lung's macrophages, where Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) bacteria hide. Arikayce was approved by the FDA in 2018 under accelerated approval for refractory MAC lung disease, the first drug approved through the Limited Population Pathway for Antibacterial and Antifungal Drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1972", "Amikacin is synthesized from kanamycin in Japan."], ["2018", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Arikayce for refractory MAC lung disease."]],
  "cell": "Amikacin enters bacteria by binding the cell surface and then crossing the inner membrane through an energy-dependent process. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit. This causes mRNA misreading and stops normal protein synthesis, which kills the bacteria. Its extra side chain blocks access for most aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, so it often works when other aminoglycosides fail. MAC bacteria live inside alveolar macrophages and in biofilms. The liposome form is taken up by macrophages, which carry the drug to where the bacteria live. Liposomes also help the drug move through mucus and biofilm.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Over months of therapy, more patients' sputum cultures turn negative for MAC."], ["Airways", "Voice change, cough, bronchospasm and coughing up blood can occur from local effects."], ["Lung tissue", "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis, an inflammatory reaction in the lung, can occur."], ["Ears", "Absorbed amikacin can damage inner ear hair cells, causing hearing loss, tinnitus or dizziness."], ["Kidneys", "Uptake into proximal tubule cells can cause kidney injury."]],
  "journey": "The liposome suspension is inhaled using a specific nebulizer system once daily. Liposomes deposit in the airways and alveoli, where macrophages take them up. Amikacin is slowly released inside the lung, so lung levels are high while blood levels stay modest, peaking about one to four hours after inhalation. Amikacin is not metabolized. The absorbed portion leaves unchanged in urine, with a serum half-life of about six hours. Much of the drug stays in the lung or is coughed up.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning covers respiratory reactions such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis, bleeding, bronchospasm and worsening of underlying lung disease. These come from direct delivery of drug and lipid to diseased airways. Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity reflect aminoglycoside build-up in inner ear and kidney cells. Neuromuscular blockade can worsen weakness in myasthenia gravis. Aminoglycosides can harm the fetus's hearing. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Arikayce (amikacin liposome inhalation suspension) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"zileuton": {
  "origin": "Zileuton grew out of the discovery of leukotrienes. In the 1930s researchers described a 'slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis' that caused long-lasting airway contraction. In 1979 Bengt Samuelsson's group in Sweden showed this substance was a mix of leukotrienes, made from arachidonic acid by the enzyme 5-lipoxygenase. Samuelsson shared the 1982 Nobel Prize for work on these lipid signals. Abbott Laboratories developed zileuton to block 5-lipoxygenase directly. Zyflo was approved by the FDA in 1996 as the first drug to stop leukotriene synthesis. An extended-release form, Zyflo CR, followed in 2007.",
  "timeline": [["1938", "Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis is described."], ["1979", "Samuelsson's group identifies leukotrienes."], ["1982", "Samuelsson shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for this work."], ["1996", "FDA approves Zyflo (zileuton) for asthma."], ["2007", "Extended-release Zyflo CR is approved."]],
  "cell": "When inflammatory cells such as mast cells, eosinophils and neutrophils are activated, phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid from membranes. The enzyme 5-lipoxygenase, helped by 5-lipoxygenase-activating protein (FLAP), converts arachidonic acid into 5-HPETE and then leukotriene A4. LTA4 becomes LTB4, which attracts neutrophils, or the cysteinyl leukotrienes LTC4, LTD4 and LTE4. Cysteinyl leukotrienes act on CysLT1 receptors to contract airway smooth muscle, increase mucus and make vessels leak. Zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase by chelating its iron, so none of these leukotrienes are made. This differs from montelukast, which only blocks the CysLT1 receptor.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Less bronchoconstriction, swelling and mucus, with modest improvement in lung function and symptoms."], ["Immune cells", "Less LTB4 means fewer neutrophils drawn in; eosinophil recruitment also falls."], ["Liver", "ALT can rise, usually in the first months, and rare serious liver injury occurs."], ["Brain", "Sleep disturbance and changes in mood or behavior have been reported."], ["Gut", "Dyspepsia, nausea and abdominal pain are common."]],
  "journey": "Zileuton is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1.7 hours. It is about 93% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it through CYP1A2, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 and by glucuronidation. Metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is short, about 2.5 hours, which is why the immediate-release form needs dosing four times daily. It inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise theophylline and warfarin levels.",
  "sidefx": "Liver injury is the main concern. It is thought to relate to how the liver processes the drug, and ALT is monitored for the first year. Neuropsychiatric events such as sleep problems and behavior changes are reported with leukotriene modifiers. Because zileuton inhibits CYP1A2, levels of theophylline can rise and cause toxicity. It is not a rescue drug for acute attacks. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Zyflo and Zyflo CR (zileuton) FDA label", "Samuelsson B. Science 1983", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mometasone-formoterol": {
  "origin": "Dulera pairs two drugs with separate histories. Mometasone furoate is a potent synthetic corticosteroid developed by Schering-Plough, first sold as a skin cream (Elocon) in the 1980s and later as a nasal spray and asthma inhaler. Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist discovered by Yamanouchi in Japan in the 1970s, notable for working within minutes yet lasting about 12 hours. Combining a steroid with a long-acting bronchodilator in one inhaler had proven useful with earlier products. Dulera was approved by the FDA in 2010 for asthma and is now marketed by Merck, which merged with Schering-Plough in 2009.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Formoterol is developed by Yamanouchi in Japan."], ["1980s", "Mometasone furoate is introduced as a topical steroid."], ["2010", "FDA approves Dulera (mometasone and formoterol) for asthma."]],
  "cell": "Mometasone furoate binds the glucocorticoid receptor in airway cells with very high affinity. The receptor complex moves to the nucleus, where it blocks the pro-inflammatory transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1 and switches on anti-inflammatory genes. This reduces Th2 cytokines, eosinophils, mast cells and airway swelling. Formoterol binds beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle, activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase, and raises cAMP. Protein kinase A lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle quickly. Steroids also increase beta-2 receptor production, and beta agonists may help the glucocorticoid receptor move to the nucleus, so the two parts support each other.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Less inflammation over one to two weeks plus fast, 12-hour bronchodilation, giving fewer asthma attacks."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid deposited in the mouth can cause thrush and hoarseness."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Muscles and electrolytes", "Beta-2 activation can cause tremor, low potassium and higher glucose."], ["Adrenal glands and bones", "Absorbed steroid at high exposure can suppress cortisol production and lower bone density."], ["Eyes", "Long-term steroid exposure is linked to cataracts and glaucoma."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled from a pressurized metered-dose inhaler. Formoterol is absorbed and peaks in blood within about an hour; it is broken down by glucuronidation and O-demethylation, with a half-life of about ten hours. Mometasone is absorbed slowly from the lung; swallowed drug is almost all destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass through the liver. Mometasone has a long half-life of about 25 hours, is highly protein bound, and leaves mainly in feces.",
  "sidefx": "The steroid part can cause thrush by lowering local immunity, along with adrenal suppression, bone loss, slowed growth in children and eye changes. The formoterol part causes beta-2 effects outside the lung: fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not used to relieve acute attacks. The old boxed warning on LABA asthma deaths was removed from ICS/LABA combinations in 2017 after safety trials. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Dulera (mometasone furoate and formoterol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"fluticasone-furoate-vilanterol": {
  "origin": "Breo Ellipta was developed by GSK with Theravance as a once-daily successor to Advair, which combines fluticasone propionate and salmeterol twice daily. Fluticasone furoate is a different ester of fluticasone that binds the receptor very strongly and stays in lung tissue for about a day. Vilanterol is a beta-2 agonist designed for 24-hour action. Both were put in the Ellipta dry powder inhaler. Breo was approved by the FDA in 2013 for COPD and in 2015 for asthma. A large trial in Salford, England, tested it in everyday practice and found better asthma control than usual care.",
  "timeline": [["2000", "Advair, the twice-daily fluticasone propionate and salmeterol inhaler, is approved in the US."], ["2013", "FDA approves Breo Ellipta for COPD."], ["2015", "Breo Ellipta is approved for asthma."]],
  "cell": "Fluticasone furoate binds the glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity and is retained in lung tissue. The receptor complex enters the nucleus, blocks NF-kB and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes and turns on anti-inflammatory genes. Fewer cytokines are made, so eosinophils, T cells and mast cells decline and swelling falls. Vilanterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase on airway smooth muscle. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle. Vilanterol has a fast onset and lasts 24 hours.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Once-daily bronchodilation plus reduced inflammation give fewer flares in asthma and COPD."], ["Lungs (infection)", "In COPD, inhaled steroids raise pneumonia risk."], ["Mouth and throat", "Thrush and hoarseness can occur from steroid deposited in the mouth."], ["Heart", "Vilanterol can increase heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Bones and adrenal glands", "Long-term steroid exposure can lower bone density, raise fracture risk and suppress cortisol production."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled as dry powder with a lactose carrier. Vilanterol peaks in blood within about ten minutes and fluticasone furoate within about an hour. Swallowed drug has very low availability because CYP3A4 in the gut and liver breaks it down. Both are metabolized by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ketoconazole raise their levels. Fluticasone furoate has a half-life of about 24 hours and leaves mainly in feces; vilanterol leaves in urine and feces as metabolites.",
  "sidefx": "Steroid effects include thrush, pneumonia in COPD, adrenal suppression, bone loss and eye changes such as cataracts and glaucoma. Beta-2 effects from vilanterol include fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. The lactose carrier contains milk proteins, which can cause reactions in severe milk allergy. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Breo Ellipta (fluticasone furoate and vilanterol) FDA label", "Woodcock A, et al. Salford Lung Study, Lancet 2017"]
},
"budesonide-glycopyrrolate-formoterol": {
  "origin": "Breztri Aerosphere puts three established drugs into one pressurized inhaler. Budesonide is a corticosteroid developed by Astra (later AstraZeneca) in Sweden in the 1970s. Glycopyrrolate is a quaternary antimuscarinic first marketed in 1961 for peptic ulcers and later used in anesthesia. Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist from Japan. The Aerosphere technology, developed by Pearl Therapeutics (acquired by AstraZeneca in 2013), uses porous phospholipid particles that keep the drugs evenly suspended. Breztri was approved by the FDA in 2020 for COPD.",
  "timeline": [["1961", "Glycopyrrolate is first approved, for peptic ulcer disease."], ["1970s", "Budesonide is developed by Astra in Sweden."], ["2016", "Bevespi Aerosphere (glycopyrrolate and formoterol) is approved."], ["2020", "FDA approves Breztri Aerosphere for COPD."]],
  "cell": "Budesonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor; in the nucleus it blocks NF-kB and AP-1 and lowers inflammatory cytokines, reducing airway inflammation. Glycopyrrolate competitively blocks muscarinic receptors, mainly M3 on airway smooth muscle and glands. M3 is Gq-coupled, so blocking it prevents the IP3 and calcium rise that causes contraction and mucus secretion. Formoterol activates Gs-coupled beta-2 receptors, raising cAMP and protein kinase A activity, which relaxes smooth muscle within minutes. The two bronchodilators work through opposite controls of airway tone, so they add together, while budesonide lowers the inflammation that causes flares.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Dual bronchodilation plus anti-inflammatory action reduces COPD flares and breathlessness."], ["Lungs (infection)", "Inhaled steroids raise pneumonia risk in COPD."], ["Mouth and throat", "Thrush and voice changes can occur from steroid in the mouth."], ["Eyes and bladder", "Glycopyrrolate can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate; glycopyrrolate can also add to it."], ["Bones and adrenal glands", "Long-term steroid exposure can thin bones and suppress cortisol production."]],
  "journey": "All three are inhaled from one metered-dose inhaler. They peak in blood within about an hour. Budesonide that is swallowed is mostly destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass. Budesonide has a half-life of about five hours. Glycopyrrolate is barely metabolized and leaves mainly unchanged in urine, with a half-life of about 12 to 15 hours. Formoterol is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation with a half-life of about ten hours. Dosing is twice daily.",
  "sidefx": "Each part adds its own risks. Budesonide can cause thrush, pneumonia, adrenal suppression and bone loss. Glycopyrrolate's muscarinic blockade can worsen glaucoma and urinary retention and cause dry mouth. Formoterol can cause fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not a rescue inhaler. There is no single antidote.",
  "src": ["Breztri Aerosphere FDA label", "Rabe KF, et al. ETHOS trial, N Engl J Med 2020"]
},
"glycopyrrolate-formoterol": {
  "origin": "Glycopyrrolate was first marketed in 1961 by A. H. Robins as Robinul for peptic ulcers, because blocking muscarinic receptors reduces stomach acid. It later became a staple in anesthesia to dry secretions. Formoterol is a long-acting beta-2 agonist from Japan. Pearl Therapeutics, a California company later bought by AstraZeneca, developed a co-suspension inhaler that holds both drugs on light, porous phospholipid particles. Bevespi Aerosphere was approved by the FDA in 2016 for COPD as a dual bronchodilator in a pressurized inhaler.",
  "timeline": [["1961", "Glycopyrrolate (Robinul) is first approved."], ["1970s", "Formoterol is developed in Japan."], ["2016", "FDA approves Bevespi Aerosphere for COPD."]],
  "cell": "Glycopyrrolate is a quaternary ammonium antagonist at muscarinic receptors. In the airway it blocks M3 receptors on smooth muscle and glands, so acetylcholine from vagal nerves can no longer trigger the Gq, IP3 and calcium signal that causes contraction and mucus secretion. Formoterol activates beta-2 receptors on the same muscle, turning on Gs, adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and relaxes the muscle. One drug removes the contracting signal and the other adds a relaxing signal, so bronchodilation is greater than with either alone.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Faster and greater bronchodilation in COPD, improving FEV1 and breathlessness."], ["Mouth", "Muscarinic blockade can cause dry mouth."], ["Bladder", "Antimuscarinic action can worsen urinary retention."], ["Eyes", "Glycopyrrolate can raise eye pressure in narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Beta-2 activation can lower potassium and raise glucose."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled from a metered-dose inhaler and reach the blood within minutes. Glycopyrrolate is permanently charged, so it is poorly absorbed from the gut and does not cross into the brain; it is barely metabolized and leaves mainly unchanged in urine with a half-life of about 12 hours. Formoterol is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation with a half-life of about ten hours. Dosing is twice daily.",
  "sidefx": "Glycopyrrolate's muscarinic blockade explains dry mouth, urinary retention and glaucoma risk. Formoterol's beta-2 stimulation explains fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. It is not approved for asthma or for rescue use. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Bevespi Aerosphere FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"albuterol-budesonide": {
  "origin": "For decades the standard asthma rescue inhaler was albuterol alone. Albuterol (salbutamol) was developed in 1966 by David Jack's team at Allen & Hanburys in England. Budesonide is an inhaled steroid developed by Astra in Sweden in the 1970s. Researchers learned that many asthma attacks happen because inflammation goes untreated, and that people often reach only for their rescue inhaler. Adding a steroid to every rescue puff was studied in the MANDALA trial, which showed fewer severe attacks than albuterol alone. AstraZeneca and Avillion developed Airsupra, approved by the FDA in 2023 as the first albuterol and inhaled steroid rescue inhaler in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Albuterol (salbutamol) is developed in England."], ["1970s", "Budesonide is developed by Astra in Sweden."], ["2022", "The MANDALA trial reports fewer severe attacks with albuterol plus budesonide."], ["2023", "FDA approves Airsupra as an as-needed rescue inhaler."]],
  "cell": "Albuterol is a short-acting selective beta-2 agonist. It binds beta-2 receptors on airway smooth muscle, activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. Protein kinase A then lowers calcium and reduces myosin light chain kinase activity, relaxing the muscle within minutes. Budesonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor; the complex moves to the nucleus, blocks NF-kB and AP-1 and switches on anti-inflammatory genes, lowering cytokines and inflammatory cells in the airway. Delivering both at once means that each time a person needs relief, inflammation is also being treated.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Fast relief of bronchospasm with an added anti-inflammatory dose, reducing severe attacks."], ["Heart", "Albuterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Muscles", "Tremor is a common effect of beta-2 activation in skeletal muscle."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Potassium shifts into cells and glucose can rise."], ["Mouth and throat", "Steroid in the mouth can cause thrush and hoarseness."], ["Adrenal glands", "Frequent use adds up steroid exposure, which can suppress cortisol production."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled from a metered-dose inhaler. Albuterol works within about five minutes and lasts four to six hours. Swallowed albuterol is sulfated in the gut wall and liver, and it leaves in urine with a half-life of about four to five hours. Budesonide is absorbed from the lung; swallowed budesonide is largely destroyed by CYP3A4 on first pass. Budesonide's half-life is about two to four hours, with metabolites leaving in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Albuterol's beta-2 effects cause tremor, fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Budesonide can cause thrush, voice change and, with heavy use, adrenal suppression, bone loss and eye effects. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. Needing it more often is a sign asthma is worsening. It is not a daily controller. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Airsupra (albuterol and budesonide) FDA label", "Papi A, et al. MANDALA trial, N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"tiotropium-olodaterol": {
  "origin": "Stiolto Respimat combines two once-daily bronchodilators from Boehringer Ingelheim. Tiotropium, approved in the US in 2004 as Spiriva, became the best-known long-acting antimuscarinic for COPD. Olodaterol, a 24-hour beta-2 agonist, was approved alone in 2014. Both are delivered by the Respimat soft-mist inhaler, which produces a slow cloud that is easier to inhale than a fast spray. Stiolto was approved by the FDA in 2015 for COPD, giving patients two bronchodilators in one daily inhalation.",
  "timeline": [["2004", "Tiotropium (Spiriva HandiHaler) is approved in the US."], ["2014", "Olodaterol (Striverdi Respimat) is approved."], ["2015", "FDA approves Stiolto Respimat for COPD."]],
  "cell": "Tiotropium is a quaternary ammonium muscarinic antagonist. It binds all muscarinic subtypes but dissociates very slowly from M3 and faster from M2, so M3 on airway smooth muscle stays blocked for over 24 hours. This prevents acetylcholine from triggering the Gq, IP3 and calcium signal that contracts the airway and drives mucus secretion. Olodaterol is a selective beta-2 agonist that activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and protein kinase A to relax the muscle. Blocking contraction and promoting relaxation together gives more bronchodilation than either drug alone.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Sustained 24-hour bronchodilation with improved FEV1 and breathlessness."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth is a common antimuscarinic effect."], ["Eyes", "Mist sprayed into the eyes can dilate the pupil and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention."], ["Heart", "Beta-2 stimulation can raise heart rate and cause arrhythmias."]],
  "journey": "Each daily dose is two puffs of soft mist. Both drugs reach peak blood levels within minutes. Tiotropium is minimally metabolized and is excreted mainly unchanged in urine, so kidney impairment raises levels; its terminal half-life is about five to six days, with an effective half-life near 25 hours. Olodaterol is cleared by glucuronidation and O-demethylation by CYP2C9 and CYP2C8 and leaves in urine and feces, with a terminal half-life of about 45 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Tiotropium's muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth and can worsen glaucoma and urinary retention. Olodaterol's beta-2 stimulation can cause fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm and angioedema are rare. It is not for acute attacks or for asthma. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Stiolto Respimat FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"umeclidinium-vilanterol": {
  "origin": "Anoro Ellipta was developed by GSK with Theravance and approved by the FDA in 2013 as the first once-daily inhaler in the US combining a long-acting antimuscarinic and a long-acting beta-2 agonist for COPD. Umeclidinium was designed to bind muscarinic receptors for a full day. Vilanterol was designed as a 24-hour beta-2 agonist and is also used in Breo and Trelegy. The Ellipta dry powder device holds each drug in a separate blister strip that opens at the same time.",
  "timeline": [["2013", "FDA approves Anoro Ellipta, the first once-daily LAMA/LABA inhaler in the US."], ["2014", "Umeclidinium alone (Incruse Ellipta) is approved."]],
  "cell": "Umeclidinium competitively blocks muscarinic receptors and leaves M3 on airway smooth muscle slowly. This stops acetylcholine from activating the Gq, IP3 and calcium pathway that contracts the airway. Vilanterol binds beta-2 receptors on the same muscle, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase and raising cAMP. Protein kinase A then lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle. Removing the contracting signal and adding a relaxing one gives more bronchodilation than either drug alone, and both last about 24 hours.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Once-daily bronchodilation improves FEV1 and breathlessness in COPD."], ["Throat and sinuses", "Sore throat, sinusitis and upper respiratory infections are common."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention."], ["Eyes", "Antimuscarinic action can raise eye pressure in narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Heart", "Vilanterol can cause fast heart rate and, rarely, atrial fibrillation."], ["Gut", "Constipation and diarrhea can occur, partly from muscarinic effects on gut motility."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are inhaled as a dry powder with a lactose carrier and reach peak blood levels within about 5 to 15 minutes. Umeclidinium is metabolized mainly by CYP2D6 and by glucuronidation and is excreted mostly in feces. Vilanterol is metabolized by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise its level, and leaves in urine and feces. Both have half-lives of about 11 hours, and the effect lasts 24 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Umeclidinium's antimuscarinic action explains urinary retention and glaucoma risk. Vilanterol's beta-2 action explains fast heart rate, palpitations, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare. The lactose carrier contains milk proteins. It is not approved for asthma or rescue use. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Anoro Ellipta (umeclidinium and vilanterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"aclidinium-formoterol": {
  "origin": "Duaklir Pressair combines two bronchodilators in a breath-actuated dry powder inhaler. Aclidinium was developed by Almirall in Spain as an antimuscarinic that is rapidly broken down in blood. Formoterol is a fast-acting long-acting beta-2 agonist developed in Japan in the 1970s. The combination was first approved in Europe and was approved by the FDA in 2019 for COPD. The Pressair device gives a click and a color change to show that a full dose was inhaled.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Formoterol is developed in Japan."], ["2012", "Aclidinium (Tudorza Pressair) is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves Duaklir Pressair for COPD."]],
  "cell": "Aclidinium competitively blocks muscarinic receptors, staying longer at M3 than at M2. Blocking M3 on airway smooth muscle prevents acetylcholine's Gq, IP3 and calcium signal, so the airway does not contract and glands secrete less mucus. Formoterol activates beta-2 receptors on the same muscle, turning on Gs, adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and relaxes the muscle within minutes. The two actions add together. Aclidinium is quickly hydrolyzed in plasma, which keeps its effects mostly in the lungs.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Twice-daily dual bronchodilation with fast onset, improving FEV1 in COPD."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can worsen urinary retention."], ["Eyes", "Antimuscarinic action can worsen narrow-angle glaucoma."], ["Heart", "Formoterol can raise heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Beta-2 activation can lower potassium and raise glucose."]],
  "journey": "The powder is inhaled and both drugs reach blood within about five to ten minutes. Aclidinium is rapidly hydrolyzed by plasma esterases into inactive products, so little active drug circulates; its effective half-life is about five to eight hours. Formoterol is broken down by glucuronidation and O-demethylation and has a half-life of about ten hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Dosing is twice daily.",
  "sidefx": "Aclidinium's antimuscarinic action explains urinary retention and glaucoma risk, though fast breakdown in plasma limits systemic effects. Formoterol's beta-2 effects cause fast heart rate, tremor, low potassium and high glucose. Paradoxical bronchospasm and hypersensitivity are rare; the lactose carrier contains milk proteins. It is not for asthma or rescue use. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Duaklir Pressair (aclidinium and formoterol) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"albuterol-ipratropium": {
  "origin": "This combination pairs two lines of bronchodilator history. Ipratropium descends from atropine, the alkaloid in belladonna and Datura, plants once smoked to relieve asthma. In the 1970s Boehringer Ingelheim made ipratropium, a quaternary form of atropine that stays in the airways and does not enter the brain. Albuterol (salbutamol) was developed in 1966 by David Jack's group at Allen & Hanburys in England as a selective beta-2 agonist. Studies showed the two together opened airways more than either alone in COPD. Combivent metered-dose inhaler was approved in the US in 1996, the nebulized form (DuoNeb) in 2001 and Combivent Respimat in 2011. The DuoNeb brand is discontinued but generic nebulizer solution remains widely used in EMS and hospitals.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Albuterol is developed at Allen & Hanburys."], ["1970s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops ipratropium from atropine."], ["1996", "Combivent inhaler is approved in the US."], ["2001", "The nebulized combination (DuoNeb) is approved."], ["2011", "Combivent Respimat is approved."]],
  "cell": "Albuterol binds beta-2 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle and activates Gs and adenylyl cyclase. Higher cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, relaxing the muscle within minutes. Ipratropium is a quaternary muscarinic antagonist that blocks M1, M2 and M3 receptors. Blocking M3 prevents acetylcholine from triggering the Gq, IP3 and calcium signal that contracts the airway and drives mucus secretion. Vagal tone is a large part of airway narrowing in COPD, so this helps. The two drugs act through different pathways and give more bronchodilation together than alone.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Fast, additive bronchodilation with less mucus, useful in COPD and acute bronchospasm."], ["Heart", "Albuterol raises heart rate and can cause palpitations and ECG changes."], ["Muscles", "Tremor and nervousness come from beta-2 activation."], ["Electrolytes and glucose", "Potassium shifts into cells and glucose rises."], ["Mouth", "Ipratropium blocks muscarinic receptors on salivary glands and causes dry mouth."], ["Eyes", "Mist escaping a mask into the eyes can dilate the pupil and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled as a mist or spray, both drugs act directly on the airways. Albuterol works within about 5 to 15 minutes and lasts four to five hours. The swallowed portion is sulfated in the gut wall and liver, and albuterol leaves in urine with a half-life of about four to six hours. Ipratropium is a charged molecule that is barely absorbed from the lung or gut. Most of it is swallowed and leaves in feces. Its effect starts within about 15 minutes and lasts up to four to six hours.",
  "sidefx": "Albuterol's beta-2 effects explain tremor, fast heart rate, low potassium and high glucose. Ipratropium's antimuscarinic action explains dry mouth and, if it reaches the eyes, pupil dilation and angle-closure glaucoma; it can also worsen urinary retention. Paradoxical bronchospasm is rare but serious. Excessive use of inhaled sympathomimetics has been linked to deaths. There is no specific antidote; a beta-blocker can reverse severe albuterol effects but may worsen bronchospasm.",
  "fun": "Asthma cigarettes made with stramonium (Datura) leaves were sold in pharmacies into the 20th century, an early form of inhaled antimuscarinic therapy.",
  "src": ["Combivent Respimat FDA label", "Ipratropium bromide and albuterol sulfate inhalation solution FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"brensocatib": {
  "origin": "Brensocatib came from a link between a rare genetic disease and common lung damage. People with Papillon-Lefevre syndrome lack the enzyme dipeptidyl peptidase 1 (DPP1, also called cathepsin C). Their neutrophils cannot activate their protein-cutting enzymes, yet they do not suffer the severe infections that might be expected; they mainly have thick skin on the palms and soles and gum disease. That suggested that blocking DPP1 could calm neutrophil damage without wrecking immunity. AstraZeneca discovered the molecule and licensed it to Insmed in 2016. In the ASPEN trial, it reduced flares of non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis. The FDA approved Brinsupri in 2025 as the first DPP1 inhibitor and the first approved treatment for non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis.",
  "timeline": [["1999", "Loss of cathepsin C is identified as the cause of Papillon-Lefevre syndrome."], ["2016", "Insmed licenses the DPP1 inhibitor from AstraZeneca."], ["2025", "FDA approves Brinsupri (brensocatib) for non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis."]],
  "cell": "In the bone marrow, developing neutrophils make serine proteases such as neutrophil elastase, proteinase 3 and cathepsin G as inactive forms. DPP1 cuts two amino acids off each one to turn them on before they are packed into granules. Brensocatib reversibly inhibits DPP1 in these precursor cells. Neutrophils that mature during treatment carry less active protease. In bronchiectasis, neutrophils swarm the airways and release elastase, which damages airway walls, slows mucus clearance and keeps a cycle of infection and inflammation going. Less active elastase breaks that cycle. Because the effect is set during neutrophil development, it builds over weeks.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Lower sputum elastase activity and fewer bronchiectasis flares."], ["Skin", "Rash, dry skin and thickened skin (hyperkeratosis), similar to features of Papillon-Lefevre syndrome."], ["Gums and teeth", "Gum and periodontal problems can occur, so dental care is part of monitoring."], ["Immune system", "Neutrophil function is altered, so live vaccines are evaluated before and during treatment."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension has been reported among the common adverse reactions in trials."]],
  "journey": "Brensocatib is taken by mouth once daily. It is about 87% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A, with minor CYP2C8 and CYP2D6 roles; thiocyanate is a major circulating metabolite. About half leaves in urine and about a quarter in feces. The half-life is about 25 to 39 hours. Its effect on lung neutrophils lags because only newly made neutrophils carry the change.",
  "sidefx": "Skin and gum effects mirror what happens in people born without DPP1, because the enzyme also activates proteases in skin and gum tissue. Hyperkeratosis, rash, dry skin and periodontal disease are the main label warnings. Changes in neutrophil protease activity are the reason live vaccines are considered carefully. Headache and upper respiratory infection are common. There is no antidote; effects wane as new neutrophils form after stopping.",
  "fun": "Brensocatib is an example of 'human knockout' drug design: a rare genetic disease showed what blocking an enzyme would do before any drug existed.",
  "src": ["Brinsupri (brensocatib) FDA label", "Chalmers JD, et al. ASPEN trial, N Engl J Med 2025"]
},
"treprostinil-inhaled": {
  "origin": "Treprostinil traces back to prostacyclin, a lipid made by blood vessel walls that was discovered in 1976 by Salvador Moncada, John Vane and colleagues. Prostacyclin widens vessels and stops platelets, but natural prostacyclin (epoprostenol) breaks down in minutes and needs a constant IV line. Treprostinil is a more stable synthetic analog developed by United Therapeutics, first approved as subcutaneous Remodulin in 2002. United Therapeutics then developed an inhaled solution, Tyvaso, approved in 2009 for pulmonary arterial hypertension. Inhalation delivers the drug to ventilated parts of the lung. A dry powder form, Tyvaso DPI, followed in 2022, and another dry powder product, Yutrepia, was later approved. Tyvaso is also approved for pulmonary hypertension from interstitial lung disease.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Moncada, Vane and colleagues discover prostacyclin."], ["2002", "Subcutaneous treprostinil (Remodulin) is approved."], ["2009", "FDA approves Tyvaso inhalation solution for PAH."], ["2021", "Tyvaso is approved for pulmonary hypertension with interstitial lung disease."], ["2022", "Tyvaso DPI dry powder is approved."]],
  "cell": "Treprostinil activates the prostacyclin (IP) receptor on pulmonary artery smooth muscle. IP is Gs-coupled, so binding raises adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP. Protein kinase A lowers calcium and relaxes the muscle, widening pulmonary arteries and lowering pulmonary vascular resistance. Treprostinil also binds EP2 and DP1 receptors, which add to vasodilation. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks activation and aggregation. Prostacyclin signaling also slows smooth muscle growth in vessel walls. Because inhaled drug lands in alveoli that are breathing, it widens vessels next to ventilated lung, which helps match blood flow to air.",
  "body": [["Lungs (blood vessels)", "Pulmonary arteries widen, lowering resistance and easing the load on the right heart, with better walking distance."], ["Airways", "Cough and throat irritation are common, and bronchospasm can occur in reactive airways."], ["Systemic vessels", "Absorbed drug can widen vessels elsewhere, causing flushing, headache, dizziness and low blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "Platelet inhibition can increase bleeding risk."], ["Gut", "Nausea and diarrhea reflect prostaglandin effects on the gut."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled treprostinil deposits in the lungs and is absorbed into blood, peaking in about 10 to 15 minutes. It is about 91% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C8, so gemfibrozil raises levels and rifampin lowers them. Metabolites leave mostly in urine. The half-life is about four hours, and the effect lasts about three to four hours, which is why it is taken in several sessions a day.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from prostacyclin receptor activation outside the lung: flushing, headache and low blood pressure from systemic vasodilation, and bleeding from platelet inhibition. Cough and throat irritation come from direct airway contact. Bronchospasm can occur in people with reactive airways. Stopping prostacyclin therapy suddenly can cause rebound worsening of PAH. There is no antidote; effects fade within hours.",
  "src": ["Tyvaso and Tyvaso DPI (treprostinil) FDA labels", "Moncada S, et al. Nature 1976"]
},
"vanzacaftor-tezacaftor-deutivacaftor": {
  "origin": "Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, found in 1989 by teams led by Lap-Chee Tsui, John Riordan and Francis Collins. CFTR is a chloride and bicarbonate channel. In the most common mutation, F508del, the protein misfolds and rarely reaches the cell surface. Vertex Pharmaceuticals developed drugs that fix the protein: ivacaftor (2012) opens the channel, and correctors help it fold. Trikafta (elexacaftor, tezacaftor and ivacaftor) in 2019 transformed care. Alyftrek is Vertex's next triple combination, approved by the FDA in December 2024. It swaps elexacaftor for a new corrector, vanzacaftor, and uses deutivacaftor, a version of ivacaftor in which some hydrogen atoms are replaced with deuterium so it lasts longer and can be taken once daily. Vertex obtained deutivacaftor when it acquired the molecule from Concert Pharmaceuticals.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "The CFTR gene is identified."], ["2012", "Ivacaftor, the first CFTR potentiator, is approved."], ["2019", "Trikafta (elexacaftor, tezacaftor, ivacaftor) is approved."], ["2024", "FDA approves Alyftrek (vanzacaftor, tezacaftor, deutivacaftor)."]],
  "cell": "In F508del and other processing mutations, CFTR misfolds in the endoplasmic reticulum and is destroyed before reaching the cell surface. Vanzacaftor and tezacaftor are correctors that bind different sites on CFTR and help it fold, process and travel to the apical membrane of epithelial cells. Once there, the channel may still open poorly. Deutivacaftor is a potentiator that binds CFTR and increases the chance the channel is open (gating). Together, more channels reach the surface and each passes more chloride and bicarbonate. Better ion flow draws water onto airway surfaces, thinning mucus and improving clearance. The same effect in sweat glands lowers sweat chloride.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Thinner mucus, better FEV1 and fewer pulmonary flares."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride falls, a direct marker that CFTR is working better."], ["Pancreas and gut", "Better bicarbonate and fluid secretion may improve digestion and nutrition."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and serious liver injury is a boxed warning."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been seen in children taking CFTR modulators."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, and is more common in some groups taking CFTR modulators."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth once daily with fat-containing food, which improves absorption. All three drugs are over 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes them mainly by CYP3A, so strong CYP3A inducers lower levels and inhibitors raise them, and grapefruit is avoided. Deuterium makes carbon-deuterium bonds harder for enzymes to break, slowing deutivacaftor metabolism and allowing once-daily dosing. Elimination is mainly in feces. Sweat chloride falls within days to weeks.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning is drug-induced liver injury and liver failure, seen with this class, so liver tests are checked regularly. The exact cause is not fully known. Rash and hypersensitivity can occur. Cataracts in children are a class concern. Because all three rely on CYP3A, interactions with inducers such as rifampin can make the drug ineffective. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Deutivacaftor is one of a small group of 'deuterated' drugs; swapping hydrogen for its heavier twin deuterium can slow metabolism without changing how the drug fits its target.",
  "src": ["Alyftrek FDA label", "Riordan JR, et al. Science 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mannitol-inhaled": {
  "origin": "Mannitol is a sugar alcohol named after manna, the sweet dried sap of the flowering ash tree (Fraxinus ornus) from which it was first obtained in the early 1800s. It has long been used IV to reduce brain swelling. The Australian company Pharmaxis developed a dry powder of inhaled mannitol: first as Aridol, a bronchial challenge test that provokes airway narrowing in asthma, and then as Bronchitol, a treatment to hydrate mucus in cystic fibrosis. Bronchitol was approved by the FDA in 2020 as add-on maintenance therapy for adults with cystic fibrosis and is marketed in the US by Chiesi.",
  "timeline": [["1800s", "Mannitol is isolated from manna, the dried sap of the flowering ash."], ["2010", "Aridol inhaled mannitol challenge test is approved in the US."], ["2020", "FDA approves Bronchitol for cystic fibrosis in adults."]],
  "cell": "Inhaled mannitol does not act on a receptor. It dissolves in the thin layer of fluid on the airway surface and makes that fluid hyperosmolar. Water then moves from airway cells into the surface liquid by osmosis. In cystic fibrosis, faulty CFTR channels leave the airway surface dehydrated and mucus thick and sticky. Rehydration lowers mucus viscosity and helps cilia sweep it out. Mannitol also triggers cough, which helps clear mucus. The same hyperosmolar stress can trigger mast cells to release mediators such as histamine and leukotrienes, which is why it can cause bronchospasm and why it works as a challenge test for asthma.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Wetter, thinner mucus is cleared more easily, with modest lung function benefit in CF."], ["Airway smooth muscle", "Airway narrowing can occur from mast cell mediator release, so a bronchodilator is used before each dose."], ["Throat", "Cough and throat irritation are common from direct contact with the powder."], ["Lungs (bleeding)", "Coughing up blood can occur, especially in damaged CF airways."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed mannitol is filtered and leaves in urine largely unchanged."]],
  "journey": "Mannitol is inhaled from capsules as a dry powder. It acts on the airway surface within minutes. Some is absorbed into the blood. A small fraction is metabolized by the liver, and most is filtered by the kidneys and excreted unchanged. The half-life is about 4.7 hours. Protein binding is negligible. Before first use, a supervised test dose checks for airway hyperreactivity.",
  "sidefx": "Bronchospasm is the main risk. The hyperosmolar airway surface triggers mast cells and airway nerves, causing narrowing; pre-treatment with a short-acting bronchodilator reduces this. Coughing up blood (hemoptysis) can occur because cough and osmotic stress affect fragile CF airways. Cough, sore throat and vomiting are common. There is no antidote; a short-acting bronchodilator treats bronchospasm.",
  "fun": "The same inhaled mannitol powder is used in a different product to deliberately provoke airway narrowing as a test for asthma.",
  "src": ["Bronchitol (mannitol) FDA label", "Aridol (mannitol) FDA label"]
},
"sarilumab": {
  "origin": "Sarilumab targets interleukin-6, a cytokine cloned in 1986 by Tadamitsu Kishimoto's group in Osaka, Japan, and others. IL-6 turned out to drive fever, the acute-phase response, anemia of inflammation and joint damage in rheumatoid arthritis. The first IL-6 receptor blocker, tocilizumab, came from Kishimoto's work with Chugai. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, which make fully human antibodies, to create sarilumab, and developed it with Sanofi. Kevzara was approved by the FDA in 2017 for rheumatoid arthritis. Approvals followed for polymyalgia rheumatica in 2023 and for polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis.",
  "timeline": [["1986", "The IL-6 gene is cloned."], ["2010", "Tocilizumab, the first IL-6 receptor blocker, is approved in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Kevzara (sarilumab) for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2023", "Sarilumab is approved for polymyalgia rheumatica."]],
  "cell": "IL-6 signals in two ways. In classic signaling it binds membrane IL-6 receptor alpha on liver cells and some immune cells. In trans-signaling, IL-6 binds soluble IL-6 receptor, and that complex can act on almost any cell. Either way, the complex recruits gp130, which activates JAK1 and JAK2 and then STAT3. STAT3 turns on genes for acute-phase proteins such as CRP and hepcidin, drives Th17 differentiation and B-cell antibody production, and activates synovial fibroblasts and osteoclasts. Sarilumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that binds both soluble and membrane IL-6 receptor alpha and blocks IL-6 from binding. This lowers synovial inflammation, CRP and joint damage.",
  "body": [["Joints", "Less synovitis means less pain, swelling and stiffness, and slower joint damage."], ["Liver", "CRP, fibrinogen and hepcidin production fall because the liver's IL-6 signal is blocked. Liver enzymes can rise."], ["Blood", "Neutrophil and platelet counts can fall. Anemia of inflammation may improve as hepcidin drops."], ["Immune system", "Serious infections, including TB reactivation and fungal infections, are more likely."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides rise, a known class effect of IL-6 blockade."], ["Gut", "IL-6 blockade is linked to GI perforation, especially in people with diverticulitis."]],
  "journey": "Sarilumab is injected under the skin every two weeks. It is absorbed slowly through lymphatics and peaks about two to four days later. Clearance has two parts: normal antibody breakdown by protein digestion, and target-mediated clearance when it binds receptors. At low levels target binding dominates, so the half-life depends on concentration and reaches up to about ten days at steady state. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes, but blocking IL-6 can raise CYP enzyme activity that inflammation had suppressed, changing levels of drugs such as warfarin.",
  "sidefx": "Serious infections are the boxed warning, because IL-6 helps coordinate defense against bacteria and fungi. CRP can stay low during infection because the liver's IL-6 signal is blocked, which can hide fever and lab clues. Neutropenia reflects IL-6's role in neutrophil production and movement. Lipid rises and liver enzyme rises are class effects. GI perforation is linked to impaired gut wall healing. There is no antidote; the effect fades over weeks.",
  "src": ["Kevzara (sarilumab) FDA label", "Hirano T, et al. Nature 1986", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"canakinumab": {
  "origin": "Canakinumab came from research into rare autoinflammatory diseases. In 2001 Hal Hoffman and colleagues found that cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes (CAPS) are caused by mutations in the NLRP3 gene. NLRP3 forms the inflammasome, a protein machine that activates IL-1 beta. People with these mutations make too much IL-1 beta and suffer fevers, rashes and deafness. Novartis developed a fully human antibody against IL-1 beta, and Ilaris was approved by the FDA in 2009 for CAPS. Later approvals include periodic fever syndromes, Still's disease and gout flares. The large CANTOS trial in 2017 showed that blocking IL-1 beta lowered repeat heart attacks, supporting the idea that inflammation drives atherosclerosis.",
  "timeline": [["2001", "NLRP3 mutations are linked to cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes."], ["2009", "FDA approves Ilaris (canakinumab) for CAPS."], ["2017", "The CANTOS trial links IL-1 beta blockade to fewer cardiovascular events."], ["2023", "Canakinumab is approved for gout flares."]],
  "cell": "Inside macrophages and other innate immune cells, danger signals such as urate crystals, damaged cell contents or microbial products activate the NLRP3 inflammasome. This activates caspase-1, which cuts pro-IL-1 beta into active IL-1 beta. Active IL-1 beta binds the IL-1 receptor type I and recruits the accessory protein, activating MyD88, IRAK kinases and NF-kB. This drives fever, IL-6 production, the acute-phase response and neutrophil recruitment. Canakinumab binds IL-1 beta with high affinity and prevents it from engaging the receptor. It does not bind IL-1 alpha or the natural IL-1 receptor antagonist, so it is more targeted than rilonacept or anakinra.",
  "body": [["Whole body (fever)", "Fever and fatigue resolve within days because IL-1 beta is a key fever signal in the hypothalamus."], ["Skin", "The hive-like rashes of CAPS clear as IL-1 beta signaling in the skin falls."], ["Joints", "Gout flares and arthritis calm because urate crystals drive IL-1 beta release."], ["Liver", "CRP and serum amyloid A fall, reducing the risk of AA amyloidosis over time."], ["Immune system", "Infections, especially respiratory infections, are more common, and serious infections can occur."]],
  "journey": "Canakinumab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly and peaks about seven days later. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein digestion throughout the body and recycled by FcRn, giving a long half-life of about 26 days. This allows dosing every four to eight weeks for many conditions. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Blocking IL-1 beta weakens part of the innate immune response, so infections are more likely and TB screening comes first. Neutropenia can occur. In systemic juvenile arthritis and Still's disease, macrophage activation syndrome can occur, related to the underlying disease. Live vaccines are avoided. In CANTOS, fatal infections were slightly more common. There is no antidote; effects fade over weeks.",
  "fun": "In CANTOS, people on canakinumab also had fewer lung cancer diagnoses and deaths, an unexpected finding that sparked new research into inflammation and cancer.",
  "src": ["Ilaris (canakinumab) FDA label", "Ridker PM, et al. CANTOS, N Engl J Med 2017", "Hoffman HM, et al. Nat Genet 2001"]
},
"rilonacept": {
  "origin": "Rilonacept was built by Regeneron using its 'trap' technology, which fuses parts of natural receptors to the Fc tail of an antibody to make a soluble decoy. The same approach later produced aflibercept for eye disease. Rilonacept joins the outer parts of the IL-1 receptor type I and the IL-1 receptor accessory protein, so it grabs IL-1 the way the cell surface would. Arcalyst was approved by the FDA in 2008 for cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes. In 2021 it became the first FDA-approved treatment for recurrent pericarditis, developed for that use with Kiniksa Pharmaceuticals.",
  "timeline": [["2008", "FDA approves Arcalyst (rilonacept) for CAPS."], ["2021", "Rilonacept is approved for recurrent pericarditis."]],
  "cell": "IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta both signal by binding IL-1 receptor type I, which then recruits the IL-1 receptor accessory protein. This complex activates MyD88, IRAK kinases and NF-kB, turning on genes for fever, IL-6, acute-phase proteins and neutrophil recruitment. Rilonacept is a dimeric fusion protein that contains both receptor pieces linked to the Fc part of human IgG1. It acts as a soluble decoy: it binds IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta tightly in the fluid around cells so they cannot reach cell-surface receptors. In recurrent pericarditis, IL-1 released from damaged pericardial cells and inflammasome activation keep the inflammation returning, so trapping IL-1 prevents recurrences.",
  "body": [["Pericardium", "Recurrent pericarditis flares are prevented, with less chest pain and lower CRP."], ["Whole body (fever)", "Fevers and fatigue of CAPS ease as IL-1 signaling falls."], ["Skin", "The hive-like rashes of CAPS clear as trapped IL-1 can no longer drive skin inflammation."], ["Liver", "CRP and serum amyloid A fall as the liver's inflammatory signal drops."], ["Immune system", "Infections are more likely, and combination with TNF blockers is not recommended."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol and triglycerides can rise, so lipids are checked periodically."]],
  "journey": "Rilonacept is injected under the skin once weekly. It is absorbed slowly and distributed mainly in blood and tissue fluid. As a protein with an IgG Fc tail, it is broken down by protein digestion and partly recycled by FcRn. Its half-life is about seven days in recurrent pericarditis. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Because it traps both IL-1 alpha and beta, innate immune defense is weakened, so serious infections can occur. Combining with TNF blockers raised serious infection rates with other IL-1 blockers. Injection site reactions are common. Lipid rises are reported. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Rilonacept's 'trap' design, a receptor fused to an antibody tail, is the same idea behind aflibercept for macular degeneration.",
  "src": ["Arcalyst (rilonacept) FDA label", "Klein AL, et al. RHAPSODY trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]
},
"ixekizumab": {
  "origin": "Ixekizumab came from the discovery of Th17 cells in the mid-2000s, a T cell subset that makes IL-17 and drives psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases. IL-17A was found to be a major signal in psoriatic plaques. Eli Lilly developed ixekizumab, a humanized antibody that binds IL-17A with high affinity. Taltz was approved by the FDA in 2016 for plaque psoriasis, then for psoriatic arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis and non-radiographic axial spondyloarthritis. Secukinumab, another IL-17A antibody, had been approved a year earlier.",
  "timeline": [["2005", "Th17 cells are described as a distinct T helper lineage."], ["2015", "Secukinumab, the first IL-17A antibody, is approved."], ["2016", "FDA approves Taltz (ixekizumab) for plaque psoriasis."]],
  "cell": "In psoriasis, dendritic cells in the skin release IL-23, which keeps Th17 cells and other immune cells producing IL-17A. IL-17A binds a receptor made of IL-17RA and IL-17RC on keratinocytes. This activates Act1 and TRAF6, leading to NF-kB and other pathways. Keratinocytes then make chemokines that recruit neutrophils, antimicrobial peptides and more cytokines, and they multiply quickly. Ixekizumab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds IL-17A and prevents it from engaging its receptor. Without this signal, the inflammatory loop in the skin breaks, keratinocyte growth slows and plaques clear. IL-17A also drives inflammation at entheses and joints, explaining benefit in spondyloarthritis.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Psoriatic plaques thin and clear, often by week 12."], ["Joints and entheses", "Less joint swelling and enthesitis in psoriatic arthritis and spondyloarthritis."], ["Mouth and skin (infection)", "IL-17 is key for defense against Candida, so oral thrush and skin fungal infections are more common."], ["Gut", "IL-17 helps protect the gut lining, and blocking it can trigger or worsen inflammatory bowel disease."], ["Injection site", "Redness and pain at the injection site are common."]],
  "journey": "Ixekizumab is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly, peaking about four days later. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by protein digestion and recycled by FcRn. Its half-life is about 13 days, which allows dosing every four weeks after starting doses. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Blocking IL-17A weakens mucosal and skin defense against fungi, so Candida infections are more common. Bacterial and serious infections can occur, and TB is screened first. Inflammatory bowel disease can start or worsen because IL-17 helps maintain the gut barrier. Eczema-like rashes and hypersensitivity can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Taltz (ixekizumab) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"bimekizumab": {
  "origin": "Bimekizumab was developed by UCB in Belgium. After IL-17A antibodies proved effective, researchers showed that IL-17F, a closely related cytokine, also drives inflammation in psoriasis and arthritis and is often more abundant in skin. UCB designed a single antibody that binds both IL-17A and IL-17F. In head-to-head trials it cleared psoriasis more completely than some other biologics. Bimzelx was approved by the FDA in 2023 for plaque psoriasis, followed in 2024 by psoriatic arthritis, axial spondyloarthritis and hidradenitis suppurativa.",
  "timeline": [["2016", "IL-17A antibodies are established for psoriasis."], ["2023", "FDA approves Bimzelx (bimekizumab) for plaque psoriasis."], ["2024", "Bimekizumab is approved for psoriatic arthritis, axial spondyloarthritis and hidradenitis suppurativa."]],
  "cell": "IL-17A and IL-17F are made by Th17 cells, gamma-delta T cells and innate lymphoid cells under IL-23 stimulation. They exist as IL-17A dimers, IL-17F dimers and IL-17A/F heterodimers, all of which signal through the IL-17RA and IL-17RC receptor complex. Signaling activates Act1 and NF-kB in keratinocytes, fibroblasts and synovial cells, driving chemokines, neutrophil recruitment and tissue inflammation. Bimekizumab is a humanized IgG1 antibody with two binding sites that each recognize both IL-17A and IL-17F. Blocking both forms gives a deeper reduction in IL-17 signaling than blocking IL-17A alone.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Rapid and often complete clearing of psoriatic plaques."], ["Joints and entheses", "Less joint swelling and enthesitis in psoriatic arthritis and axial spondyloarthritis."], ["Skin folds (HS)", "Fewer painful nodules and draining tunnels in hidradenitis suppurativa."], ["Mouth", "Oral candidiasis is common because IL-17A and IL-17F both defend mucosa against fungi."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin can rise, so they are checked periodically."], ["Mood", "Suicidal ideation and behavior are listed in the label warnings."]],
  "journey": "Bimekizumab is injected under the skin and peaks in about three to four days. It is broken down like other IgG antibodies by protein digestion and recycled by FcRn. Its half-life is about 23 days, allowing dosing every four or eight weeks after starting doses. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Dual blockade of IL-17A and IL-17F strongly weakens antifungal defense in the mouth and skin, so Candida infections are more common than with IL-17A blockers. Other infections can occur and TB is screened first. Inflammatory bowel disease can start or worsen because IL-17 supports the gut barrier. Liver enzyme rises are monitored. Suicidal thoughts were reported in trials. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Bimzelx (bimekizumab) FDA label", "Reich K, et al. BE RADIANT trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]
},
"brodalumab": {
  "origin": "Brodalumab was discovered by Amgen and developed with AstraZeneca. Unlike other IL-17 drugs, it blocks the receptor, IL-17RA, rather than the cytokine. That shuts down several IL-17 family members at once. Trials showed strong clearing of psoriasis, but a small number of suicides were reported in the program. Amgen withdrew in 2015 and Valeant (now Bausch Health) took over. A causal link was not established, but when the FDA approved Siliq in 2017, it carried a boxed warning about suicidal ideation and was limited to a REMS program.",
  "timeline": [["2015", "Amgen ends its involvement after reports of suicidal behavior in trials."], ["2017", "FDA approves Siliq (brodalumab) with a boxed warning and REMS."]],
  "cell": "IL-17RA is a shared receptor subunit used by several IL-17 family cytokines. Paired with IL-17RC it carries IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-17A/F signals; paired with IL-17RE it carries IL-17C; and paired with IL-17RB it carries IL-17E (IL-25). Signaling through Act1 and TRAF6 activates NF-kB and drives keratinocyte proliferation, chemokine release and neutrophil recruitment. Brodalumab is a human IgG2 antibody that binds IL-17RA and blocks all of these signals. This broad blockade quiets the IL-23 and Th17 loop that sustains psoriatic plaques.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Rapid thinning and clearing of psoriatic plaques."], ["Mood", "Suicidal ideation and behavior occurred in trials, which is why a REMS program and mood counseling are required."], ["Mouth and skin (infection)", "Candida and other fungal infections are more common because IL-17 signaling defends mucosa."], ["Gut", "Brodalumab is contraindicated in Crohn's disease, which IL-17 blockade can worsen."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia can occur, as IL-17 helps drive neutrophil production."]],
  "journey": "Brodalumab is injected under the skin every two weeks after starting doses. It peaks in about three days. Its clearance is nonlinear: at low levels, binding to IL-17RA on cells pulls the antibody out of circulation faster (target-mediated clearance). Otherwise it is broken down by protein digestion like other antibodies. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning covers suicidal ideation and behavior; a direct causal role has not been proven, but monitoring under the REMS is required. Blocking IL-17RA weakens antifungal defense, so Candida infections are common. Serious infections and TB reactivation can occur. Crohn's disease can be triggered or worsened, reflecting IL-17's protective role in the gut. Neutropenia reflects reduced neutrophil signaling. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Siliq (brodalumab) FDA label and REMS", "Lebwohl M, et al. AMAGINE trials, N Engl J Med 2015"]
},
"tildrakizumab": {
  "origin": "Tildrakizumab grew out of the discovery that IL-23, not IL-12, is the main driver of Th17 inflammation. IL-23 was described in 2000 as a cytokine sharing the p40 subunit with IL-12 but having its own p19 subunit. Ustekinumab blocks p40 and so blocks both IL-12 and IL-23. Researchers then made antibodies to p19 alone, which spare IL-12. Tildrakizumab was developed at Merck and licensed to Sun Pharma, and Ilumya was approved by the FDA in 2018 for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis. It is given every 12 weeks after starting doses.",
  "timeline": [["2000", "IL-23 is described as a cytokine with a unique p19 subunit."], ["2009", "Ustekinumab, an IL-12 and IL-23 p40 blocker, is approved."], ["2018", "FDA approves Ilumya (tildrakizumab) for plaque psoriasis."]],
  "cell": "IL-23 is made by dendritic cells and macrophages. It is a two-part cytokine: p19 plus p40. It binds the IL-23 receptor and IL-12 receptor beta-1 on Th17 cells, gamma-delta T cells and innate lymphoid cells, activating JAK2, TYK2 and STAT3. This keeps Th17 cells alive and making IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-22, which drive keratinocyte growth and skin inflammation. Tildrakizumab is a humanized IgG1 kappa antibody that binds the p19 subunit, so IL-23 cannot reach its receptor. IL-12, which shares p40 but not p19, is not blocked, so Th1 immunity that controls intracellular infections is relatively preserved.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Psoriatic plaques thin and clear over weeks, with long-lasting effect between doses."], ["Immune system", "Upper respiratory infections are more common, and serious infections and TB can occur."], ["Injection site", "Redness or pain at the injection site may occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea has been reported in clinical trials."], ["Whole body", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema and hives can occur."]],
  "journey": "Tildrakizumab is injected under the skin by a healthcare provider. It is absorbed slowly and peaks about six days later. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by protein digestion and recycled by FcRn, giving a half-life of about 23 days. Because IL-23 sits upstream, a single dose has effects that last longer than the drug's level suggests, allowing dosing every 12 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Blocking IL-23 lowers Th17 activity, which weakens some defenses against bacteria and fungi, so infections can increase and TB is screened first. Because IL-12 is spared, the effect on intracellular pathogen defense is smaller than with p40 blockers. Hypersensitivity can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Ilumya (tildrakizumab) FDA label", "Oppmann B, et al. Immunity 2000"]
},
"anifrolumab": {
  "origin": "Anifrolumab is based on the 'interferon signature' in lupus. Interferon was discovered in 1957 by Alick Isaacs and Jean Lindenmann as a substance that interferes with virus growth. Decades later, researchers found that many people with systemic lupus erythematosus have high activity of genes switched on by type I interferons. Instead of blocking one interferon, MedImmune (part of AstraZeneca) made an antibody against the shared receptor, IFNAR1, to block them all. After the TULIP trials, Saphnelo was approved by the FDA in 2021 for moderate to severe SLE, the first new lupus drug in about a decade after belimumab.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Isaacs and Lindenmann discover interferon."], ["2000s", "The type I interferon gene signature is described in lupus."], ["2011", "Belimumab is approved for lupus."], ["2021", "FDA approves Saphnelo (anifrolumab) for SLE."]],
  "cell": "In lupus, immune complexes containing self DNA and RNA are taken up by plasmacytoid dendritic cells and trigger Toll-like receptors 7 and 9. These cells release large amounts of type I interferons, mainly IFN-alpha. Type I interferons bind IFNAR1 and IFNAR2, activating JAK1 and TYK2 and then STAT1 and STAT2. This switches on hundreds of interferon-stimulated genes, activates B cells and dendritic cells and promotes autoantibody production, feeding a loop. Anifrolumab is a human IgG1 kappa antibody that binds IFNAR1 and blocks signaling by all type I interferons, including IFN-alpha, IFN-beta and IFN-omega. The interferon signature falls and disease activity improves.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Lupus rashes improve as interferon-driven skin inflammation calms."], ["Joints", "Joint pain and swelling decrease as interferon-driven inflammation calms."], ["Immune system", "Interferon-stimulated gene activity drops, with less B cell and dendritic cell activation."], ["Nerves (zoster)", "Type I interferons control herpes viruses, so shingles is more common, including disseminated cases."], ["Airways", "Upper respiratory infections and bronchitis are more common."], ["Steroid needs", "Many patients can lower their oral steroid use, reducing steroid side effects."]],
  "journey": "Anifrolumab is given as an IV infusion every four weeks, so it reaches full levels at the end of the infusion. Its clearance is nonlinear: binding to IFNAR1 on cells, which then internalize it, removes it faster at low concentrations. Otherwise it is broken down by protein digestion like other IgG antibodies. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Type I interferons are front-line antiviral defense, so blocking their receptor raises the risk of viral infections, especially herpes zoster and respiratory infections. Serious infections can occur. Infusion reactions and hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, are possible. Live vaccines are avoided. It has not been studied in severe active lupus nephritis or severe CNS lupus. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Saphnelo (anifrolumab) FDA label", "Morand EF, et al. TULIP-2, N Engl J Med 2020", "Isaacs A, Lindenmann J. Proc R Soc Lond B 1957"]
},
"voclosporin": {
  "origin": "Voclosporin is a close cousin of cyclosporine, which was found in the early 1970s at Sandoz in Switzerland from the soil fungus Tolypocladium inflatum. Jean-Francois Borel showed cyclosporine's immune-suppressing power, and it changed organ transplantation in the 1980s. Cyclosporine levels vary widely, though, which makes dosing hard. Chemists at the Canadian company Isotechnika modified one amino acid of cyclosporine to make voclosporin, which binds calcineurin more strongly and has more predictable levels. Aurinia Pharmaceuticals developed it for lupus nephritis, and Lupkynis was approved by the FDA in 2021 as the first oral drug for lupus nephritis, used with background immunosuppression.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Cyclosporine is discovered at Sandoz from Tolypocladium inflatum."], ["1983", "Cyclosporine is approved in the US for transplant rejection."], ["2021", "FDA approves Lupkynis (voclosporin) for lupus nephritis."]],
  "cell": "In T cells, antigen recognition raises calcium, which activates calcineurin, a calcium and calmodulin-dependent phosphatase. Calcineurin removes phosphate groups from NFAT, letting it move into the nucleus and switch on IL-2 and other cytokine genes. Voclosporin binds cyclophilin A inside the cell, and this complex blocks calcineurin. NFAT stays in the cytoplasm, IL-2 is not made and T cell activation stalls. In the kidney, calcineurin also controls the podocyte protein synaptopodin. Inhibiting calcineurin protects synaptopodin from breakdown, stabilizing the podocyte actin skeleton and reducing protein leak. This is why proteinuria drops quickly in lupus nephritis.",
  "body": [["Kidneys (filter)", "Protein in the urine falls as podocytes stabilize and immune attack calms."], ["Kidneys (blood flow)", "Calcineurin inhibitors constrict the afferent arteriole, which can drop GFR, so eGFR is checked regularly."], ["Blood pressure", "Hypertension is common, related to vasoconstriction and sodium retention."], ["Immune system", "Suppressed T cells raise the risk of serious infections and of lymphoma and skin cancer."], ["Nervous system", "Tremor, headache and rare PRES can occur."], ["Electrolytes", "Potassium can rise because calcineurin inhibitors reduce potassium excretion."]],
  "journey": "Voclosporin is taken by mouth twice daily. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in about 1.5 hours. It is about 97% bound to plasma proteins and spreads into red blood cells. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided and inducers lower its levels. It is also a P-gp substrate. It leaves mainly in bile and feces. The half-life is about 30 hours.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning covers serious infections and cancers such as lymphoma and skin cancer, because suppressing T cells weakens immune surveillance. Kidney function can fall because calcineurin inhibition constricts the afferent arteriole. Hypertension and hyperkalemia reflect effects on vessel tone and kidney tubules. Neurotoxicity, including PRES, can occur. QT prolongation is listed. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Cyclosporine, the parent of voclosporin, was found in a soil sample collected in Norway, which is part of why it changed transplantation history.",
  "src": ["Lupkynis (voclosporin) FDA label", "Rovin BH, et al. AURORA 1, Lancet 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"auranofin": {
  "origin": "Gold has a long medical story. In 1890 Robert Koch found that gold cyanide inhibited the tuberculosis bacillus in the lab. In 1929 the French physician Jacques Forestier began using injectable gold salts for rheumatoid arthritis, on the idea that RA might be infectious. Gold injections became a standard RA treatment for decades. Smith Kline & French developed auranofin as an oral gold compound, approved by the FDA in 1985 as Ridaura. It was easier to take than weekly injections, but its effect was modest. Use fell after methotrexate and biologics took over. Researchers continue to study auranofin in the lab for cancer and infections, but those uses are not approved.",
  "timeline": [["1890", "Robert Koch reports gold cyanide inhibits tuberculosis bacteria."], ["1929", "Jacques Forestier begins treating rheumatoid arthritis with gold salts."], ["1985", "FDA approves oral auranofin (Ridaura)."]],
  "cell": "Auranofin contains gold(I) bound to a thioglucose and a triethylphosphine ligand. In the body, gold is released from these ligands and binds sulfhydryl groups and selenocysteine on proteins. A key target is thioredoxin reductase, an enzyme that keeps cells in a reduced state. Inhibiting it raises oxidative stress inside cells. Gold also builds up in macrophage lysosomes and reduces phagocytosis, lysosomal enzyme release and production of cytokines and prostaglandins. It can suppress NF-kB activity. How these effects add up to slow rheumatoid arthritis is not fully known, but the net result is less synovial inflammation over months.",
  "body": [["Joints", "Slow reduction in joint swelling and pain over three to six months, with lower rheumatoid factor levels."], ["Gut", "Loose stools or diarrhea are very common, and rare severe enterocolitis can occur."], ["Skin and mouth", "Itchy rashes and mouth sores are common gold reactions."], ["Kidneys", "Gold can cause proteinuria and membranous nephropathy, an immune-complex kidney injury."], ["Blood", "Gold can suppress the bone marrow, causing low platelets, low white cells or aplastic anemia."], ["Lungs", "Rare interstitial pneumonitis can occur."]],
  "journey": "Auranofin is taken by mouth, and about a quarter of the gold is absorbed. Blood gold peaks in one to two hours. About 60% of blood gold is protein bound, much of it to albumin and red cells. Gold builds up in tissues such as the synovium, kidneys and liver over time. The blood half-life of gold is about 26 days, and total body gold has a half-life of about 80 days. Absorbed gold leaves about 60% in urine and the rest in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Gold toxicity is the boxed warning. Bone marrow suppression, causing low platelets, low white cells and rarely aplastic anemia, is thought to be immune mediated, so blood counts and urinalysis are checked monthly. Proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome come from immune complexes in the kidney. Rash, itching and mouth sores reflect gold deposits and immune reactions in skin and mucosa. Diarrhea is the most common effect. Dimercaprol has been used historically as a chelator for severe gold toxicity.",
  "fun": "Gold therapy for arthritis began because doctors thought rheumatoid arthritis might be a form of tuberculosis.",
  "src": ["Ridaura (auranofin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"subcutaneous-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "Immune globulin therapy began with blood plasma. In the 1940s Edwin Cohn at Harvard developed a method to separate plasma into fractions, including gamma globulin, which carries antibodies. In 1952 Ogden Bruton, an army pediatrician, described a boy with no gamma globulin who kept getting infections, the first known case of agammaglobulinemia. Bruton treated him with gamma globulin injected under the skin, and the infections fell. IV forms later became standard, but in the 2000s concentrated subcutaneous products returned to allow home infusion. Vivaglobin was the first US subcutaneous product in 2006, followed by Hizentra in 2010 and others, including HyQvia, which adds hyaluronidase. Hizentra was later approved for CIDP.",
  "timeline": [["1940s", "Edwin Cohn develops plasma fractionation to isolate gamma globulin."], ["1952", "Ogden Bruton describes agammaglobulinemia and treats it with subcutaneous gamma globulin."], ["2006", "The first US subcutaneous immune globulin (Vivaglobin) is approved."], ["2010", "Hizentra 20% subcutaneous immune globulin is approved."], ["2018", "Hizentra is approved for CIDP maintenance."]],
  "cell": "Immune globulin is pooled IgG from thousands of healthy donors, so it contains antibodies against a broad range of bacteria and viruses. In primary immunodeficiency it replaces missing antibodies. These IgG molecules bind pathogens, coat them for phagocytes through Fc gamma receptors (opsonization), neutralize toxins and viruses and activate complement. In autoimmune nerve disease such as CIDP, extra effects matter. Large amounts of IgG saturate FcRn, the receptor that normally recycles IgG, so the patient's own harmful autoantibodies are broken down faster. IgG also blocks Fc receptors on macrophages, neutralizes autoantibodies with anti-idiotype antibodies and dampens complement activation.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Steady IgG levels lower infection rates in people with primary immunodeficiency."], ["Nerves", "In CIDP, immune attack on myelin is damped, helping maintain strength and function."], ["Skin (infusion sites)", "Swelling, redness and itching where fluid is infused under the skin are very common and fade within days."], ["Blood vessels", "IgG raises blood viscosity, and thrombosis can occur, especially with risk factors."], ["Brain coverings", "Aseptic meningitis with headache and neck stiffness can occur, though it is less common than with IV forms."], ["Red blood cells", "Donor antibodies against blood group antigens can cause hemolysis."]],
  "journey": "The concentrated solution is infused under the skin, often at home, through one or more sites. IgG is absorbed slowly through lymphatics into the blood, peaking about two to three days later. Because absorption is gradual and dosing is often weekly, IgG levels stay steadier than with monthly IV infusions. IgG is broken down by cells of the reticuloendothelial system, while FcRn recycling protects it and gives a half-life of about three to four weeks. With HyQvia, hyaluronidase temporarily loosens tissue so larger volumes can be infused.",
  "sidefx": "Local reactions come from a volume of protein solution in the tissue under the skin. Thrombosis, the boxed warning, is linked to higher blood viscosity and procoagulant factors, so hydration is part of care. Aseptic meningitis likely reflects immune effects in the meninges. Hemolysis comes from donor anti-A and anti-B antibodies. Hypersensitivity is more likely in IgA-deficient patients with anti-IgA antibodies. As a human plasma product, it carries a theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents despite screening and viral inactivation. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Ogden Bruton's first treatment for agammaglobulinemia in 1952 was given under the skin, so modern home subcutaneous therapy is in a sense a return to where the field started.",
  "src": ["Hizentra (immune globulin subcutaneous, human) FDA label", "Bruton OC. Pediatrics 1952", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"oxaprozin": {
  "origin": "Oxaprozin is a propionic acid NSAID, a family that includes ibuprofen and naproxen. It was developed by Wyeth Laboratories as part of the search for anti-inflammatory drugs with long action. Its standout feature is a very long half-life, which allows once-daily dosing. It was approved in the US in 1992 as Daypro. It remains available as generic tablets for osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and juvenile arthritis.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Propionic acid NSAIDs such as ibuprofen are introduced."], ["1992", "Oxaprozin (Daypro) is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Oxaprozin reversibly inhibits both COX-1 and COX-2. These enzymes convert arachidonic acid, released from membranes by phospholipase A2, into prostaglandin H2, the parent of PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. Less PGE2 at sites of injury means less vasodilation, swelling and sensitization of pain nerve endings. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever. COX-1 blockade in the stomach lining reduces protective prostaglandins. In platelets, reversible COX-1 blockade lowers thromboxane while the drug is present. COX-2 blockade in vessel walls lowers prostacyclin, tipping balance toward clotting.",
  "body": [["Joints", "Less swelling and pain in arthritis, with steady effect from once-daily dosing."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep kidney blood flow up when it is stressed; blocking them can cause kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher thrombotic risk and fluid retention can raise blood pressure and worsen heart failure."], ["Skin", "Oxaprozin can cause photosensitivity and rash in sun-exposed skin, a reaction noted in its label."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare serious liver injury can occur."]],
  "journey": "Oxaprozin is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking in three to five hours. It is about 99% bound to albumin, and binding saturates at higher levels, so free drug rises more than expected. The liver breaks it down by oxidation and glucuronidation, with metabolites leaving in urine and feces. Its half-life is very long, about 41 to 55 hours, so it takes several days to reach steady levels and effects last more than a day.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from losing prostaglandins. In the stomach, less mucus and bicarbonate lead to ulcers and bleeding. In the kidney, reduced vasodilating prostaglandins cause kidney injury, salt retention and high potassium. Unbalanced COX-2 blockade raises heart attack and stroke risk, the boxed warning. The long half-life means side effects can linger after stopping. It can cause a false-positive urine benzodiazepine screen. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Oxaprozin can cause false-positive results on urine screening tests for benzodiazepines for several days after the last dose.",
  "src": ["Daypro (oxaprozin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"flurbiprofen": {
  "origin": "Flurbiprofen was developed by the Boots Company in Nottingham, England, the same research group that discovered ibuprofen in the early 1960s under Stewart Adams. Boots chemists kept exploring propionic acid derivatives and found that adding a fluorine atom and a second ring produced a more potent molecule. Flurbiprofen was introduced in the UK in the 1970s. In the US, ophthalmic flurbiprofen (Ocufen) was approved in the 1980s to prevent pupil constriction during eye surgery, and oral tablets (Ansaid) followed in the late 1980s. The oral brand is discontinued, but generic tablets remain. Flurbiprofen lozenges for sore throat are sold in some other countries.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Boots scientists discover ibuprofen and related propionic acids."], ["1970s", "Flurbiprofen is introduced in the UK."], ["1980s", "Ophthalmic and oral flurbiprofen are approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Flurbiprofen is a racemic mix, and the S-enantiomer does most of the COX inhibition. It reversibly blocks COX-1 and COX-2, so arachidonic acid is not converted to prostaglandin H2 and downstream PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane. Less PGE2 reduces inflammation, pain nerve sensitization and fever. In the eye, prostaglandins released during surgery constrict the pupil through the iris sphincter, so blocking them helps keep the pupil wide. In the stomach, loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens mucosal protection. In platelets, reversible COX-1 blockade lowers thromboxane while drug is present.",
  "body": [["Joints", "Less pain, stiffness and swelling in osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis."], ["Eye (ophthalmic form)", "Prevents pupil constriction during eye surgery by blocking iris prostaglandins."], ["Stomach", "Reduced protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce blood flow, causing kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher risk of heart attack and stroke, fluid retention and raised blood pressure."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth, flurbiprofen is well absorbed and peaks in about 1.5 hours. It is about 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C9 hydroxylation, then glucuronidation, so people who are CYP2C9 poor metabolizers have higher levels. Metabolites leave in urine. The half-life is about five hours. Eye drops act locally with very little reaching the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Like other NSAIDs, its side effects come from loss of prostaglandins: stomach ulcers and bleeding from reduced mucosal protection, kidney injury and fluid retention from reduced renal blood flow, and increased heart attack and stroke risk from unbalanced COX-2 blockade, which is the boxed warning. Liver injury and serious skin reactions are rare. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Flurbiprofen tablets FDA label", "Ocufen (flurbiprofen ophthalmic) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mefenamic-acid": {
  "origin": "Mefenamic acid belongs to the fenamates, a group of NSAIDs based on anthranilic acid. Scientists at Parke-Davis in the US discovered the fenamates in the early 1960s while screening compounds for anti-inflammatory effect. Mefenamic acid was marketed in several countries in the 1960s and approved in the US as Ponstel in 1967. It became especially known for treating painful periods, because prostaglandins drive uterine cramping. Ponstel remains available along with generic capsules.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Parke-Davis scientists discover the fenamate NSAIDs."], ["1967", "Mefenamic acid (Ponstel) is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Mefenamic acid reversibly inhibits COX-1 and COX-2, the enzymes that turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2. This lowers production of PGE2 and PGF2-alpha. In the uterus, PGF2-alpha and PGE2 cause strong contractions and constrict blood vessels during menstruation, which produces cramping pain. Lowering them reduces cramping and menstrual pain. Fenamates are also described as blocking prostaglandin receptors, an extra action beyond reducing synthesis. Elsewhere, reduced PGE2 lowers pain nerve sensitivity and fever, while loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens stomach protection and reversibly reduces platelet thromboxane.",
  "body": [["Uterus", "Fewer prostaglandin-driven contractions ease menstrual cramps."], ["Nerves", "Pain nerve endings become less sensitive as PGE2 falls."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and can be severe, a classic reason to stop the drug."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce filtration and raise potassium."], ["Blood", "Rare autoimmune hemolytic anemia has been reported."]],
  "journey": "Mefenamic acid is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in two to four hours. It is over 90% bound to plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C9 to 3-hydroxymethyl mefenamic acid, which is then glucuronidated. About half leaves in urine and the rest in feces. The half-life is about two hours. Use is typically short-term, matching its label for no more than about a week.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from prostaglandin loss: ulcers and bleeding in the stomach, reduced kidney blood flow and potassium rise, and increased thrombotic risk from unbalanced COX-2 blockade, which is the boxed warning. Diarrhea is more common than with many NSAIDs. Rash and rare hemolytic anemia have immune causes. In late pregnancy, NSAIDs can close the fetal ductus arteriosus early and reduce amniotic fluid. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Ponstel (mefenamic acid) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"diflunisal": {
  "origin": "Diflunisal was developed by Merck Sharp & Dohme in the 1970s as an improved salicylate. Chemists added a difluorophenyl ring to salicylic acid, making a more potent and longer-acting drug that is not converted to salicylic acid in the body. It was approved in the US in 1982 as Dolobid. Decades later, Jeffery Kelly's lab at Scripps Research showed that diflunisal binds and stabilizes transthyretin, a blood protein that can misfold into amyloid. A clinical trial found that diflunisal slowed nerve damage in hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis, an off-label use that helped pave the way for drugs like tafamidis.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Merck develops diflunisal from salicylic acid."], ["1982", "Diflunisal (Dolobid) is approved in the US."], ["2013", "A randomized trial shows diflunisal slows transthyretin amyloid polyneuropathy."]],
  "cell": "Diflunisal reversibly inhibits COX-1 and COX-2, lowering prostaglandin production from arachidonic acid. This reduces inflammation and pain nerve sensitization. Unlike aspirin, it does not acetylate COX, so its antiplatelet effect is reversible and depends on dose. It has little fever-lowering effect at usual doses, possibly because it does not enter the brain well. Separately, transthyretin is a four-part protein that carries thyroxine. When the tetramer falls apart, the single units can misfold into amyloid fibrils. Diflunisal binds the thyroxine-binding pockets and holds the tetramer together, slowing amyloid formation.",
  "body": [["Joints and muscles", "Pain relief and less inflammation in osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis and mild to moderate pain."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce kidney blood flow and cause kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "Higher thrombotic risk, fluid retention and raised blood pressure are NSAID class effects."], ["Nerves (TTR amyloid)", "Stabilizing transthyretin slows amyloid nerve damage, an off-label use."], ["Ears", "Tinnitus can occur, as with other salicylate derivatives."]],
  "journey": "Diflunisal is taken by mouth and absorbed well, peaking in two to three hours. It is over 99% bound to plasma proteins. The liver conjugates it mainly with glucuronic acid, and about 90% leaves in urine as glucuronides. It is not turned into salicylic acid. The half-life is about 8 to 12 hours and gets longer at higher doses because elimination saturates, so twice-daily dosing works. It can raise acetaminophen levels.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect prostaglandin loss: GI ulcers and bleeding, kidney injury and fluid retention, and higher cardiovascular risk, the class boxed warning. As a salicylic acid derivative, labeling notes a possible link to Reye syndrome in children with viral illness. Tinnitus and rash can occur. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "An arthritis pill from 1982 turned out to stabilize a misfolding blood protein, an example of an old drug finding a new purpose.",
  "src": ["Diflunisal tablets FDA label", "Berk JL, et al. JAMA 2013", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"salsalate": {
  "origin": "Salsalate is part of the salicylate story that began with willow bark, used for pain and fever since ancient times. In 1763 Edward Stone reported willow bark for fevers to the Royal Society. Salicylic acid was later made in the lab, and in 1897 Felix Hoffmann at Bayer made acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin). Salsalate is salicylsalicylic acid: two salicylic acid molecules joined by an ester bond. It was sold as Disalcid and in generic form, but it is marketed in the US without FDA approval, as an older drug that never went through the modern approval process. It drew new interest when trials led by Steven Shoelson at Joslin Diabetes Center showed it can modestly lower blood glucose in type 2 diabetes, though that use is not approved.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Willow bark is used for pain and fever."], ["1763", "Edward Stone reports willow bark for fever to the Royal Society."], ["1897", "Felix Hoffmann at Bayer synthesizes aspirin."], ["2010s", "Trials show salsalate modestly lowers glucose in type 2 diabetes."]],
  "cell": "Salsalate is a prodrug. It is hydrolyzed to two molecules of salicylic acid in the small intestine and after absorption. Salicylate is a weak direct inhibitor of COX-1 and COX-2 enzyme activity and also lowers COX-2 gene expression. It also inhibits IKK-beta, a kinase that activates NF-kB, the master switch for many inflammatory genes. This NF-kB effect is thought to underlie both its anti-inflammatory action and its effect on insulin sensitivity. Because it is nonacetylated, salsalate does not permanently acetylate platelet COX-1 the way aspirin does, so its antiplatelet effect is small.",
  "body": [["Joints", "Reduced pain and inflammation in arthritis, with full benefit after a few days of steady dosing."], ["Stomach", "Less stomach injury than aspirin, because salsalate is absorbed largely intact and is nonacetylated, though ulcers can still occur."], ["Platelets", "Minimal antiplatelet effect, which may suit people at bleeding risk compared with aspirin."], ["Ears", "High salicylate levels cause tinnitus and hearing loss, an early sign of toxicity."], ["Metabolism", "In studies, salsalate lowered blood glucose modestly through NF-kB inhibition, an unapproved use."], ["Airways", "People with aspirin-sensitive asthma can still react."]],
  "journey": "Salsalate is taken by mouth. It is insoluble in stomach acid and dissolves in the small intestine, where it is absorbed and hydrolyzed to salicylic acid. Salicylic acid peaks in about 1.5 to 4 hours and is about 90% bound to albumin, less at high levels. The liver conjugates salicylic acid with glycine to form salicyluric acid and with glucuronic acid. These pathways saturate, so the half-life rises sharply as levels go up, from a few hours to over half a day. Products leave in urine, and alkaline urine speeds salicylate excretion.",
  "sidefx": "Salicylism, with tinnitus, hearing loss, nausea and confusion, comes from high salicylate levels, which rise unpredictably because metabolism saturates. Severe salicylate toxicity causes respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis from uncoupling of cell metabolism. GI ulcers and kidney effects follow from prostaglandin loss, though they are less marked than with aspirin. Reye syndrome is a risk in children with viral illness. There is no specific antidote; severe toxicity is treated with urine alkalinization and, if needed, hemodialysis.",
  "src": ["Salsalate tablets product labeling", "Goldfine AB, et al. TINSAL-T2D, Ann Intern Med 2010", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"tretinoin-topical": {
  "origin": "Tretinoin is all-trans retinoic acid, the active acid form of vitamin A. Vitamin A was identified in the early 20th century, and its role in skin was clear from the rough skin seen with deficiency. In 1969 dermatologists Albert Kligman and James Fulton at the University of Pennsylvania reported that topical tretinoin cleared acne by loosening plugged follicles. Johnson and Johnson's Ortho division brought it to market as Retin-A, approved in 1971. In 1988 a JAMA study by John Voorhees's group at Michigan showed it improved sun-damaged skin, fueling a public boom, and Renova was later approved for photodamage. Kligman's career also includes controversial skin experiments on prisoners at Holmesburg Prison, now a well-known case in research ethics.",
  "timeline": [["1913", "Vitamin A (fat-soluble factor A) is identified."], ["1969", "Kligman and Fulton report topical tretinoin for acne."], ["1971", "Retin-A is approved in the US."], ["1988", "A JAMA study shows tretinoin improves photoaged skin."], ["1995", "Renova is approved for photodamage."]],
  "cell": "Tretinoin enters skin cells and binds cellular retinoic acid binding proteins, which carry it to the nucleus. There it binds retinoic acid receptors RAR-alpha, RAR-beta and RAR-gamma; RAR-gamma is the main form in the epidermis. RARs pair with RXRs and bind retinoic acid response elements in DNA, changing gene transcription. In the hair follicle, this normalizes how keratinocytes mature and lowers their stickiness, so plugs loosen and fall out (comedolysis) and new microcomedones form less. Epidermal turnover speeds up and the stratum corneum becomes thinner. In sun-damaged skin, it boosts collagen production and reduces collagen breakdown.",
  "body": [["Hair follicles", "Comedones loosen and are expelled, and new ones form less, clearing acne over weeks."], ["Epidermis", "Faster cell turnover and a thinner outer layer cause dryness, peeling and redness early on."], ["Dermis", "With long use, increased collagen improves fine wrinkles and texture in sun-damaged skin."], ["Pigment cells", "Uneven pigment and dark spots fade; irritation can sometimes cause light or dark patches."], ["Sun sensitivity", "A thinner outer layer lets more UV through, raising sunburn risk."]],
  "journey": "Applied to the skin, tretinoin stays mostly in the epidermis and follicles. Only a very small amount is absorbed into the blood, and blood retinoid levels stay close to the body's natural levels. Absorbed tretinoin is metabolized by liver CYP enzymes and leaves in urine and feces. Improvement in acne takes two to three weeks or more, and full benefit often needs over six weeks. The early 'purge' reflects existing microcomedones being pushed to the surface.",
  "sidefx": "Redness, peeling, burning and dryness come from faster turnover and thinning of the outer skin layer and from retinoid effects on skin barrier lipids. Increased sensitivity to sun, wind and cold follows from the thinner stratum corneum. Harsh soaps and other irritants make this worse. Systemic effects are not expected with topical use, but because oral retinoids cause birth defects, topical tretinoin is often avoided in pregnancy. The gel forms are flammable. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The same molecule taken by mouth, all-trans retinoic acid, is a cancer drug that forces leukemia cells in acute promyelocytic leukemia to mature.",
  "src": ["Retin-A (tretinoin) FDA label", "Kligman AM, et al. Arch Dermatol 1969", "Weiss JS, et al. JAMA 1988"]
},
"tazarotene": {
  "origin": "Tazarotene was designed at Allergan in the 1990s as a receptor-selective retinoid. Natural retinoic acid binds all retinoic acid receptors, and the goal was a molecule that would hit only the ones that matter most in skin. Allergan's chemists built an acetylenic retinoid prodrug that, once activated, binds RAR-beta and RAR-gamma. Tazorac gel was approved by the FDA in 1997 for plaque psoriasis and acne, making it the first topical retinoid approved for psoriasis. Cream, foam and lotion forms followed, and in 2019 it was combined with halobetasol as Duobrii.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Allergan develops tazarotene as an RAR-beta and RAR-gamma selective retinoid."], ["1997", "FDA approves Tazorac gel for psoriasis and acne."], ["2019", "Duobrii (halobetasol and tazarotene) lotion is approved."]],
  "cell": "Tazarotene is a prodrug. Esterases in the skin remove its ethyl ester to form tazarotenic acid. Tazarotenic acid binds RAR-beta and RAR-gamma, but not RXR receptors. The bound receptor pairs with RXR in the nucleus and changes gene expression. In psoriasis it normalizes keratinocyte differentiation, slows the overactive cell division and lowers inflammatory markers. It also switches on tazarotene-induced genes, which are thought to restrain cell growth. In acne it loosens plugged follicles, like other retinoids.",
  "body": [["Skin (psoriasis)", "Plaques become thinner, less scaly and less raised over weeks."], ["Hair follicles", "Comedones loosen and acne lesions decrease."], ["Epidermis", "Burning, redness, peeling and itching are common, especially on healthy skin around lesions."], ["Sun sensitivity", "Changes in the outer skin layer raise sunburn risk."], ["Fetus", "Retinoids can cause birth defects, so pregnancy is avoided during treatment."]],
  "journey": "Tazarotene is applied to the skin, where it is quickly converted to tazarotenic acid. Only a small amount reaches the blood, more when large areas are treated. In blood, tazarotenic acid is over 99% bound to proteins and has a half-life of about 18 hours. It is further oxidized to sulfoxide and sulfone metabolites and leaves in urine and feces. Psoriasis improves over two to four weeks, with maximum response after two to three months.",
  "sidefx": "Irritation, burning and peeling come from retinoid effects on keratinocyte turnover and barrier function, and worsen when the product touches healthy skin. Photosensitivity follows from a thinner outer skin layer. The key safety issue is embryofetal toxicity: retinoic acid signaling directs fetal development, so a negative pregnancy test and effective contraception are part of the label. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Tazorac (tazarotene) FDA label", "Duobrii FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"retapamulin": {
  "origin": "Retapamulin is a pleuromutilin, a class that began with a mushroom. In 1951 researchers in New York, Frederick Kavanagh and colleagues, isolated pleuromutilin from the fungus then called Pleurotus mutilus (now Clitopilus). For decades its derivatives, such as tiamulin, were used only in animals. GlaxoSmithKline developed retapamulin as a semisynthetic ointment for skin infections, and Altabax was approved by the FDA in 2007 for impetigo, the first pleuromutilin approved for human use. A systemic pleuromutilin, lefamulin, followed in 2019. US availability of Altabax is now limited.",
  "timeline": [["1951", "Pleuromutilin is isolated from a mushroom fungus."], ["1970s", "Pleuromutilin derivatives such as tiamulin enter veterinary use."], ["2007", "FDA approves Altabax (retapamulin) ointment for impetigo."], ["2019", "Lefamulin becomes the first systemic pleuromutilin approved for humans."]],
  "cell": "Retapamulin binds the 50S subunit of the bacterial ribosome at the peptidyl transferase center, in domain V of 23S rRNA near ribosomal protein L3. This is the site where amino acids are joined into a growing protein chain. Binding blocks peptidyl transfer, interferes with tRNA binding in the P site and prevents normal formation of active 50S subunits. Protein synthesis stops, which is mainly bacteriostatic. Because its binding site differs from those of macrolides, lincosamides and other 50S drugs, cross-resistance with other antibiotic classes is uncommon. It is active against Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible) and Streptococcus pyogenes, the main causes of impetigo.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Impetigo lesions heal as staph and strep stop growing."], ["Application site", "Mild irritation or itching where the ointment is applied."], ["Immune system", "Contact sensitization can cause allergic dermatitis."], ["Nose", "Nosebleeds were reported with nasal use, which is not an approved route."]],
  "journey": "Retapamulin ointment is applied to the skin lesion, where it acts locally. Very little reaches the blood, even on damaged skin, so systemic levels are low and usually not measurable. The small amount absorbed is about 94% bound to plasma proteins and is metabolized by CYP3A4. Systemic half-life is not clinically relevant. Treatment courses are short, and no response after a few days is a reason to reassess.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly local because so little enters the blood. Irritation and itching reflect contact with inflamed skin. Contact dermatitis is a delayed immune reaction to the drug. Superinfection with nonsusceptible organisms can occur if used too long. It is not used in the nose, eyes, mouth or vagina. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Retapamulin's ancestor, pleuromutilin, was isolated from a gilled mushroom, an unusual source for an antibiotic used in people.",
  "src": ["Altabax (retapamulin) FDA label", "Kavanagh F, et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1951"]
},
"silver-sulfadiazine": {
  "origin": "Silver has been used against infection for centuries, and silver nitrate solution was used on burns in the 1960s. Sulfonamides date back to 1932, when Gerhard Domagk at Bayer showed that the dye Prontosil cured streptococcal infections. In 1968 Charles Fox Jr. at Columbia University combined silver with the sulfonamide sulfadiazine into a cream that was easier to use than silver nitrate and active against Pseudomonas, a deadly burn pathogen. Silver sulfadiazine (Silvadene) was approved in the US in the 1970s and became the standard topical agent for burns for decades. Newer silver dressings now compete with it, but it remains widely used.",
  "timeline": [["1932", "Gerhard Domagk discovers the antibacterial effect of Prontosil."], ["1960s", "Silver nitrate solution is used to prevent burn infection."], ["1968", "Charles Fox Jr. introduces silver sulfadiazine for burns."], ["1970s", "Silver sulfadiazine cream (Silvadene) is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "The silver sulfadiazine complex slowly releases silver ions and sulfadiazine where it touches the wound. Silver ions bind proteins and enzymes in bacterial cell membranes and cell walls, particularly their sulfhydryl groups, and they bind DNA. This disrupts membrane function, respiration and replication, killing bacteria. Sulfadiazine is a PABA analog that competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, blocking bacterial folate synthesis and DNA building blocks. Together these give broad activity against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and some yeasts such as Candida. The cream works on the surface and does not penetrate deep eschar well.",
  "body": [["Burn wound", "Fewer bacteria colonize the burn surface, lowering the risk of wound infection and sepsis."], ["Skin around wound", "Burning, itching or rash where the cream is applied."], ["Blood", "Transient leukopenia often appears within a few days, thought by many to reflect white cells moving out of circulation rather than marrow damage, and typically recovers on its own."], ["Red blood cells", "Absorbed sulfadiazine can cause hemolysis in people with G6PD deficiency."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed sulfonamide can rarely cause interstitial nephritis."], ["Skin color", "Silver deposits can rarely cause gray discoloration (argyria-like)."]],
  "journey": "Silver sulfadiazine cream is spread over the burn. Very little silver is absorbed. Some sulfadiazine is absorbed, more so when burns are large, and blood levels can approach those from oral sulfa drugs in extensive burns. Absorbed sulfadiazine is partly acetylated in the liver and leaves in urine, with a variable half-life of about ten hours. In very large burns, the propylene glycol in the cream can also be absorbed and cause hyperosmolality.",
  "sidefx": "Transient leukopenia is common early and is usually mild and self-limited. Sulfonamide hypersensitivity, from rash to Stevens-Johnson syndrome, comes from immune reactions to absorbed sulfadiazine and its metabolites. Hemolysis occurs in G6PD deficiency because sulfonamides cause oxidative stress in red cells. Sulfonamides can displace bilirubin from albumin, so they are avoided in late pregnancy, premature infants and newborns because of kernicterus risk. Fungal overgrowth can follow suppression of bacteria. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Silver sulfadiazine changed burn care so much that for decades it was the default cream on nearly every major burn unit's shelf.",
  "src": ["Silvadene (silver sulfadiazine) FDA label", "Fox CL Jr. Arch Surg 1968", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"ketoconazole-topical": {
  "origin": "Ketoconazole was designed by chemists at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, as part of a long program building azole antifungals in the 1960s and 1970s. Earlier imidazoles like miconazole worked well on skin but were poorly absorbed. Ketoconazole, reported in the late 1970s, was the first azole that could be swallowed to treat deep fungal infections, and the FDA approved oral Nizoral in 1981. Over time oral ketoconazole proved hard on the liver and the adrenal glands, and in 2013 the FDA restricted the tablets to serious infections with no other option. The creams, foams and shampoos never carried those problems because almost none of the drug reaches the blood. Today topical ketoconazole is a mainstay for tinea versicolor and seborrheic dermatitis, where its activity against Malassezia yeast is the key.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica chemists develop ketoconazole as an orally active azole antifungal."], ["1981", "FDA approves oral ketoconazole (Nizoral) tablets."], ["2013", "FDA restricts oral ketoconazole because of liver injury and adrenal effects; topical forms are unaffected."]],
  "cell": "Fungi build their cell membranes with ergosterol instead of cholesterol. Ketoconazole is an imidazole that binds the heme iron of the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51, encoded by ERG11). With that enzyme blocked, lanosterol cannot be converted on the path to ergosterol. Two things then go wrong for the fungus: ergosterol runs short, and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols pile up in the membrane. The membrane becomes leaky and disordered, membrane-bound enzymes such as chitin synthase work poorly, and growth stops (a mainly fungistatic effect). Dermatophytes, Candida and Malassezia are all sensitive. Less Malassezia on the scalp and face means fewer irritating fatty acids from yeast lipases, which is why seborrheic dermatitis and dandruff improve.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Dermatophyte and yeast growth in the outer skin layer slows, so scaling, redness and itching of ringworm, jock itch and tinea versicolor fade over one to two weeks."], ["Scalp", "As a shampoo it lowers Malassezia counts on the scalp, reducing flaking and the itchy, greasy scale of seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Rest of the body", "Because measurable blood levels are not seen with topical use, the liver toxicity and hormone effects of oral ketoconazole do not occur."]],
  "journey": "Ketoconazole cream, foam or shampoo is spread on the skin, where the drug dissolves into the oily stratum corneum and stays mostly in the outer layers and hair follicles. Very little crosses into the blood, and plasma levels after topical use are usually not detectable. Shampoo is left on briefly and rinsed away, but some drug binds to the scalp and hair. Any small amount absorbed would be broken down by the liver. The local effect builds over days with regular use.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are local. The drug and its vehicle can irritate skin, causing burning or itching, and some people develop allergic contact dermatitis. Some cream products contain sulfites, which can trigger allergic reactions in sensitive people. Shampoo in the eyes stings. Because so little is absorbed, the oral drug's problems, which come from blocking human P450 enzymes in the liver and adrenal steroid pathways, are not expected. There is no antidote; the product is washed off.",
  "fun": "Oral ketoconazole blocks human steroid-making enzymes so well that it has been used off-label to lower cortisol in Cushing syndrome, and a related single isomer, levoketoconazole, was later approved for that purpose.",
  "src": ["Nizoral and ketoconazole cream FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on oral ketoconazole, 2013"]
},
"ciclopirox": {
  "origin": "Ciclopirox is a synthetic hydroxypyridone antifungal developed at the German company Hoechst AG in the early 1970s. It does not belong to the azole or allylamine families, and it was valued because it attacks fungi in a different way, by grabbing metal ions they need. Creams and lotions came first for skin infections. In 1999 the FDA approved ciclopirox nail lacquer (Penlac), the first topical prescription treatment approved in the US for fungal nail infection. It is painted on like clear nail polish, and the labeled program pairs it with regular trimming and professional removal of diseased nail.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Hoechst AG in Germany develops ciclopirox as a new class of topical antifungal."], ["1999", "FDA approves ciclopirox nail lacquer (Penlac), the first topical prescription treatment for onychomycosis in the US."]],
  "cell": "Ciclopirox is a chelator. It binds trivalent metal ions, especially iron (Fe3+) and aluminum (Al3+), with high affinity. Many fungal enzymes need iron to work, including cytochromes in the mitochondrial electron transport chain and the enzymes catalase and peroxidase that destroy toxic hydrogen peroxide. By pulling iron away from these enzymes, ciclopirox cripples energy production and leaves the cell exposed to oxidative damage. It also disrupts the uptake of nutrients across the fungal cell membrane. The result is fungicidal activity against dermatophytes, Candida and some bacteria. Because the target is a basic need rather than one enzyme, resistance is rare. It also has some anti-inflammatory activity, which helps red, irritated skin.",
  "body": [["Nails", "The lacquer slowly penetrates the nail plate and kills fungus in the nail bed. Clear nail grows in from the base over many months as infected nail is trimmed away."], ["Skin", "Creams and gels clear tinea and Candida skin infections and calm inflammation in the treated area."], ["Scalp", "The shampoo form reduces Malassezia yeast, easing seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Rest of the body", "Only a small fraction is absorbed, so no systemic effects are expected."]],
  "journey": "Ciclopirox is applied directly to the nail or skin. The lacquer dries into a film that acts as a drug reservoir, letting drug diffuse into and through the hard keratin of the nail. From the skin, only a small fraction is absorbed. The absorbed part has a short half-life of under two hours, is conjugated with glucuronic acid, and leaves in the urine. Nail clearing takes many months because the nail itself must grow out.",
  "sidefx": "Most problems are local. Redness and irritation around the nail come from the drug and its solvents touching the surrounding skin. Nail shape or color changes and ingrown nails can occur. The lacquer contains flammable solvents, so it is kept away from flame. Cure rates are modest because the drug must cross thick nail to reach the fungus. There is no antidote; the lacquer can be removed with alcohol.",
  "fun": "Researchers have studied ciclopirox's iron-grabbing ability in cancer and viral research, because human cells also depend on iron-containing enzymes.",
  "src": ["Penlac (ciclopirox) nail lacquer FDA label", "Loprox FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"efinaconazole": {
  "origin": "Efinaconazole was discovered by the Japanese company Kaken Pharmaceutical, which set out to build a triazole that could work through a toenail when painted on. The challenge with nail fungus is that most antifungals bind tightly to keratin and get stuck before reaching the nail bed. Kaken's chemists found a triazole with low keratin binding, and the product was formulated as a low-surface-tension solution that wicks under and through the nail. The FDA approved it as Jublia in June 2014, marketed in the US by Valeant (later Bausch Health). It was the first new topical prescription treatment for toenail fungus in the US in many years.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Kaken Pharmaceutical in Japan develops efinaconazole as a topical triazole for nails."], ["2014", "FDA approves efinaconazole topical solution (Jublia) for toenail onychomycosis."]],
  "cell": "Efinaconazole is a triazole that blocks fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), a cytochrome P450 enzyme. Its triazole nitrogen binds the heme iron in the enzyme's active site, stopping the removal of a methyl group from lanosterol. Ergosterol, the main sterol of fungal membranes, runs short while methylated sterol precursors build up. The membrane loses its normal packing and permeability, and membrane enzymes falter. Against Trichophyton rubrum and Trichophyton mentagrophytes, the main causes of toenail fungus, this leads to fungistatic and, at the high local levels reached in nail, fungicidal effects. A key property is weak binding to keratin, so more drug stays free to act on the fungus.",
  "body": [["Nails", "The solution travels through and under the nail to the nail bed, where Trichophyton lives. As fungus dies, new clear nail grows out over about a year."], ["Skin around nails", "Some solution touches the nail folds, which can cause redness, blistering or pain at the site."], ["Rest of the body", "Systemic exposure is very low, so effects on the liver and other organs are not expected."]],
  "journey": "The solution is brushed onto the nail with a flow-through applicator. Its low surface tension lets it spread into the space under the nail plate as well as through it. Very little reaches the blood. Plasma levels are low, and the small absorbed amount is metabolized in the liver and leaves in urine and feces. With daily use over months, the drug builds up in the nail. Results take close to a year because the nail grows slowly.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are mostly where the liquid touches skin: dermatitis, small blisters and pain around the nail, and ingrown toenails. These come from the drug and the solvent drying and irritating the thin skin of the nail folds. The solution is flammable. Complete cure rates in trials were modest because nail fungus is deep and slow to clear. There is no antidote; the product is simply stopped.",
  "fun": "Efinaconazole's design focused on keratin binding: drugs that cling to nail protein are trapped, so a weaker grip on keratin let more drug reach the fungus underneath.",
  "src": ["Jublia (efinaconazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tavaborole": {
  "origin": "Tavaborole came from Anacor Pharmaceuticals, a California company that built drugs around boron, an element rarely used in medicine. Anacor's scientists found that small boron-containing rings called oxaboroles could lock onto RNA inside an enzyme. Because the molecule is small and water-loving, it passes through the nail plate better than many antifungals. The FDA approved tavaborole as Kerydin in July 2014 for toenail fungus, a few weeks after efinaconazole. Anacor later also produced crisaborole, a boron drug for eczema, and was acquired by Pfizer in 2016.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Anacor Pharmaceuticals develops oxaborole compounds that use boron to target fungal enzymes."], ["2014", "FDA approves tavaborole (Kerydin) for toenail onychomycosis."], ["2016", "Pfizer acquires Anacor."]],
  "cell": "Tavaborole targets fungal leucyl-tRNA synthetase (LeuRS), the enzyme that loads the amino acid leucine onto its matching transfer RNA. This enzyme has an editing site that checks for mistakes. Tavaborole's boron atom forms a covalent adduct with the two hydroxyl groups on the end adenosine of tRNA-Leu right inside that editing site. This traps the tRNA in the enzyme, so the enzyme cannot charge any more tRNA with leucine. Without charged tRNA-Leu, the ribosome stalls and the fungus cannot make new proteins, so growth stops. This mechanism is different from the azoles, which act on cell membranes.",
  "body": [["Nails", "Tavaborole moves through the nail plate to reach Trichophyton fungus in the nail and nail bed. Over months, clear nail replaces infected nail."], ["Skin around nails", "Peeling, redness and dermatitis can develop where the liquid touches the surrounding skin."], ["Rest of the body", "Systemic exposure is low, so the drug acts locally."]],
  "journey": "The solution is dropped onto the nail and under its tip, where it spreads and soaks in. Its small size and solubility help it cross the dense keratin of the nail. Some drug is absorbed into the blood at low levels, where it is extensively metabolized and the products are excreted mainly by the kidneys. Because the nail grows slowly, treatment runs close to a year.",
  "sidefx": "The main side effects are local: peeling, redness and dermatitis at the application site and ingrown toenails. These come from the solution irritating skin around the nail. The solution is flammable. Cure rates in trials were low because killing fungus deep in a thick nail is difficult. No systemic toxicity is expected, and there is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Boron is uncommon in drugs; the cancer drug bortezomib and Anacor's tavaborole and crisaborole are among the few approved medicines that contain it.",
  "src": ["Kerydin (tavaborole) FDA label", "Rock FL et al. Science 2007 (oxaborole tRNA trapping mechanism)"]
},
"nystatin-topical": {
  "origin": "Nystatin was the first effective, safe antifungal antibiotic. It was discovered around 1950 by two women at the New York State Department of Health: microbiologist Elizabeth Lee Hazen and chemist Rachel Fuller Brown. Hazen collected soil samples and found that a bacterium in soil from a farm in Virginia, later named Streptomyces noursei, made a substance that killed fungi. Brown purified it in Albany and mailed samples back and forth with Hazen in New York City. They named it nystatin after New York State. Squibb brought it to market as Mycostatin in the mid-1950s. Hazen and Brown gave their patent royalties to a nonprofit research fund that supported science and women in science.",
  "timeline": [["1950", "Hazen and Brown report nystatin from Streptomyces noursei."], ["1950s", "Squibb markets nystatin as Mycostatin, the first widely used antifungal antibiotic."], ["1994", "Hazen and Brown are inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame."]],
  "cell": "Nystatin is a polyene macrolide, a large ring with a string of conjugated double bonds on one side and hydroxyl groups on the other. It binds ergosterol, the sterol in fungal cell membranes, much more strongly than cholesterol in human membranes. Several nystatin and ergosterol molecules gather to form ring-shaped pores through the membrane. Potassium, other ions and small molecules leak out through these pores, the cell loses its internal balance and dies. Nystatin is active against Candida yeasts but not against dermatophytes or bacteria, which is why it is used for yeast rashes and not ringworm.",
  "body": [["Skin", "In diaper rash, skin folds and other moist areas infected with Candida, the yeast dies and redness, satellite spots and itching settle within days."], ["Rest of the body", "Nystatin is not absorbed through intact skin or mucous membranes, so it has no effects beyond where it is applied."]],
  "journey": "Nystatin stays on the surface. The cream, ointment or powder puts drug in direct contact with yeast on the skin, and the molecule is too large and poorly soluble to cross intact skin. Nothing meaningful enters the blood, so there is no liver metabolism or kidney clearance to consider. The powder is preferred for very moist areas because it also keeps the skin drier. It works only while it is on the skin.",
  "sidefx": "Nystatin is very well tolerated. Because human cells use cholesterol instead of ergosterol and the drug is not absorbed, there is no systemic toxicity. Irritation or allergy at the site can occur, sometimes from the vehicle. A rash that does not respond may be caused by a dermatophyte or bacteria, which nystatin does not cover. There is no antidote; the product is washed off.",
  "fun": "Hazen and Brown never met in a lab every day; they worked about 150 miles apart and mailed cultures and samples between New York City and Albany.",
  "src": ["Nystatin cream and ointment FDA labels", "Hazen EL, Brown R. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1950", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"econazole": {
  "origin": "Econazole is an imidazole antifungal from Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, which created a whole family of azole antifungals in the 1960s and 1970s, including miconazole and later ketoconazole. Econazole is a close relative of miconazole, with a chlorine atom swapped on one ring. It was developed for skin use and became a broad-spectrum cream for athlete's foot, jock itch, ringworm, tinea versicolor and skin yeast. In the US it was sold as Spectazole cream, a brand now discontinued, and a foam form (Ecoza) was approved later. Generic cream remains common.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica develops econazole as a topical imidazole antifungal."], ["1980s", "Econazole nitrate cream is marketed in the US as Spectazole."]],
  "cell": "Econazole blocks fungal lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51). Its imidazole ring binds the heme iron of the enzyme so it can no longer remove the 14-alpha methyl group from lanosterol. Ergosterol production falls and toxic methylated sterols build up in the fungal membrane, which becomes leaky and loses normal function. At the high concentrations reached in skin, the membrane damage can be directly fungicidal. Econazole is active against dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton), Candida and Malassezia, and also has some activity against gram-positive bacteria, which can help when a rash is secondarily infected.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Fungal growth in the outer skin slows and stops, so scaling, itching and redness of athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm ease, often within days."], ["Skin folds", "Activity against both Candida and dermatophytes, plus some antibacterial action, makes it useful in warm, moist folds where mixed infections occur."], ["Rest of the body", "Absorption is minimal, so systemic effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "Econazole is spread on the rash and a margin of normal skin. It dissolves into the stratum corneum and builds up there, with the highest levels in the outer layers. The label reports that less than one percent of an applied dose is recovered in urine and feces, showing how little enters the body. The small absorbed amount is metabolized in the liver.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are local: burning, itching, stinging and redness where it is applied, from irritation by the drug or the cream base. Allergic contact sensitization can develop. Because blood levels are negligible, the drug interactions and liver effects that come with oral azoles are not expected. There is no antidote; the product is stopped and washed off.",
  "fun": "Miconazole, econazole and ketoconazole all came from the same Janssen azole program, which turned one chemical idea into a long line of antifungal drugs.",
  "src": ["Econazole nitrate cream FDA label", "Ecoza (econazole) foam FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"selenium-sulfide": {
  "origin": "Selenium sulfide is a simple inorganic compound of selenium and sulfur. Its use against dandruff dates to the mid-20th century, when it was introduced in medicated suspensions and shampoos sold under the Selsun name in the 1950s. Dermatologists then found it also clears tinea versicolor, a common rash caused by Malassezia yeast. It remains one of the classic over-the-counter dandruff ingredients, and a stronger lotion is available by prescription.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Selenium sulfide suspension is introduced for dandruff under the Selsun name."], ["1950s", "Selenium sulfide becomes a standard treatment for tinea versicolor."]],
  "cell": "Selenium sulfide works in two ways. First, it is cytostatic to the epidermis: it slows the turnover of keratinocytes in the scalp and hair follicles, so fewer skin cells are shed as visible flakes. The exact molecular target is not fully defined. Second, it is antifungal against Malassezia (Pityrosporum) yeasts. Malassezia lives on oily skin and breaks down sebum into irritating fatty acids that drive the inflammation and scaling of seborrheic dermatitis and the patches of tinea versicolor. Reducing yeast numbers removes that trigger, so scaling and discoloration improve.",
  "body": [["Scalp", "Less rapid cell turnover and fewer Malassezia yeast lead to less flaking and itching in dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Skin of the trunk", "In tinea versicolor the yeast is suppressed, so the scaly light or dark patches stop spreading. Skin color can take weeks to months to even out."], ["Hair", "Hair can become oily or dry, change color, or shed more, which reflects effects on the hair shaft and scalp."]],
  "journey": "Selenium sulfide is applied as a lotion or shampoo, left on for a short time and rinsed off. It acts at the skin surface and in hair follicles. Absorption through intact skin is minimal, and the drug is removed by rinsing. Use on broken or inflamed skin is avoided because absorption could be higher there. Recurrence is common, so repeated applications are often used.",
  "sidefx": "The main side effects are irritation and burning, especially if it reaches the eyes or genital skin, because the compound is irritating to thin, sensitive tissue. It can tarnish or damage metal jewelry, which reacts with selenium and sulfur. Changes in hair oiliness, color or shedding can occur. Swallowing is toxic, so it is kept from children. There is no specific antidote; the product is rinsed off.",
  "fun": "Selenium sulfide can discolor gray, blond or chemically treated hair, which is why careful rinsing after use is part of the instructions.",
  "src": ["Selenium sulfide lotion 2.5% labeling", "OTC dandruff and seborrheic dermatitis monograph (21 CFR 358)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
,
"betamethasone-dipropionate-topical": {
  "origin": "The story of strong skin steroids starts in 1952, when dermatologists Marion Sulzberger and Victor Witten showed that hydrocortisone rubbed on the skin could clear eczema. Chemists then raced to make steroids stronger. In 1954 Josef Fried and Emily Sabo at Squibb found that adding a fluorine atom at the 9-alpha position greatly boosted glucocorticoid power. Building on that, Schering Corporation developed betamethasone around 1960, a fluorinated, 16-beta-methyl steroid with strong anti-inflammatory action and little salt-retaining effect. Adding two propionate esters made betamethasone dipropionate much more fat-soluble, so it soaks into skin well. Schering sold it as Diprosone, and an augmented vehicle (Diprolene) later pushed the same molecule into the super-high potency class.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Sulzberger and Witten report topical hydrocortisone for skin disease."], ["1954", "Fried and Sabo show 9-alpha fluorination boosts steroid potency."], ["c. 1960", "Schering develops betamethasone, a fluorinated 16-beta-methyl glucocorticoid."], ["1970s", "Betamethasone dipropionate is marketed for skin as Diprosone."]],
  "cell": "Betamethasone dipropionate is a lipophilic ester that crosses the cell membrane and binds the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm. Binding releases heat shock proteins, and the receptor-steroid complex moves into the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 so less arachidonic acid is freed to make prostaglandins and leukotrienes. The complex also ties up the transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, which lowers production of cytokines like IL-1, IL-2, TNF-alpha and chemokines. Fewer immune cells are drawn into the skin, local blood vessels constrict, and keratinocyte and fibroblast division slows. The fluorine and methyl groups make it bind the receptor tightly and resist breakdown in skin, which is why it is high potency.",
  "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, swelling and itch in psoriasis and eczema fall quickly as cytokine and prostaglandin production drops."], ["Skin cells", "Slower keratinocyte turnover thins psoriatic plaques, but long use also thins normal skin and weakens collagen, causing atrophy and stretch marks."], ["Blood vessels", "Constriction of small dermal vessels causes visible blanching; with long use the vessel walls weaken, leading to telangiectasia."], ["Adrenal axis", "Enough absorbed steroid can suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, lowering the body's own cortisol output."], ["Metabolism", "Large absorbed amounts can raise blood sugar and, rarely, cause Cushing syndrome features."]],
  "journey": "Applied as a thin film, betamethasone dipropionate dissolves into the oily stratum corneum, which acts as a reservoir that keeps releasing drug for hours. Absorption depends on the vehicle (ointment more than cream or lotion), skin thickness, inflammation and area treated. Thin skin, broken skin, large areas, children and occlusion all raise absorption. What reaches the blood is metabolized in the liver and excreted by the kidneys and bile. The local effect starts within days.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow directly from strong glucocorticoid action. Suppressed fibroblasts and keratinocytes produce skin atrophy, striae and easy tearing. Weakened vessel walls cause telangiectasia. Local immune suppression lets untreated fungal or bacterial infections spread. When enough is absorbed, the HPA axis is suppressed, which can cause adrenal insufficiency after stopping, along with hyperglycemia and Cushing features. There is no antidote; the drug is reduced or stopped, and suppressed adrenal function recovers with time.",
  "fun": "Topical steroid potency is ranked by a skin blanching test developed by McKenzie and Stoughton in 1962: the more a steroid whitens the skin by constricting vessels, the stronger it is.",
  "src": ["Betamethasone dipropionate cream FDA label", "Sulzberger MB, Witten VH. J Invest Dermatol 1952", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"fluocinonide": {
  "origin": "Fluocinonide was developed at Syntex, the company that grew up in Mexico City making steroid hormones from wild yams and later moved to Palo Alto, California. In the late 1950s and 1960s Syntex chemists built fluocinolone acetonide, a steroid with two fluorine atoms and an acetonide ring. They then added an acetate ester at carbon 21, which made the molecule even more fat-soluble and potent in skin. This was fluocinonide, sold as Lidex. It became one of the best known high-potency skin steroids in the US. A stronger 0.1% cream (Vanos) came later, and many generic forms remain in use.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Syntex develops fluocinolone acetonide, a doubly fluorinated topical steroid."], ["1960s", "Syntex creates fluocinonide, the 21-acetate ester of fluocinolone acetonide, sold as Lidex."]],
  "cell": "Fluocinonide crosses into skin cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The activated receptor enters the nucleus, where it increases transcription of anti-inflammatory genes, including annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2. Less arachidonic acid means fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes. The receptor also blocks NF-kB and AP-1, cutting cytokine and adhesion molecule production, so fewer white cells enter the skin. The two fluorine atoms (6-alpha and 9-alpha), the acetonide and the acetate ester all raise receptor affinity and skin penetration, putting fluocinonide in the high-potency group.",
  "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Itching, redness and swelling of psoriasis and eczema settle within days as inflammatory mediators fall."], ["Skin structure", "Reduced fibroblast and keratinocyte activity can thin the skin, causing atrophy, striae and fragile skin with long use."], ["Blood vessels", "Local vasoconstriction lightens redness; long-term use can cause small visible vessels."], ["Adrenal axis", "Systemic absorption from large areas or occlusion can suppress cortisol production, and children are especially susceptible."]],
  "journey": "Fluocinonide is applied in a thin film and enters the stratum corneum, where it forms a depot that keeps feeding drug into deeper layers. Occlusion greatly increases penetration and is used only in specific cases. Absorption is higher in thin, inflamed or broken skin. The absorbed fraction is metabolized in the liver and leaves through the kidneys and bile.",
  "sidefx": "Because fluocinonide is potent, the classic topical steroid effects appear faster with misuse: thin skin, stretch marks, visible vessels and acne-like or rosacea-like eruptions on the face. Immune suppression in the skin can mask or worsen infection. Absorbed drug can suppress the HPA axis, with Cushing syndrome and hyperglycemia in severe cases, and children have a larger skin area for their weight. There is no antidote; stopping or tapering lets the skin and adrenal axis recover.",
  "fun": "Syntex made early fortunes turning diosgenin from Mexican wild yams into steroid hormones, the same chemistry that later produced the first oral contraceptive norethindrone.",
  "src": ["Fluocinonide cream FDA label", "Vanos (fluocinonide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"triamcinolone-topical": {
  "origin": "Triamcinolone was created at Lederle Laboratories in the mid-1950s, during the rush to build steroids more powerful than cortisone. Lederle chemists added a 9-alpha fluorine (for potency) and a 16-alpha hydroxyl group (to cut salt retention) to the prednisolone skeleton. Joining the 16 and 17 hydroxyls with acetone formed triamcinolone acetonide, which was much more potent on skin. Squibb later sold it widely as Kenalog. Today triamcinolone acetonide comes as creams, ointments, lotions and sprays for skin, a dental paste for mouth sores, and injectable forms used in joints and skin lesions. It is a workhorse medium-potency steroid.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Lederle Laboratories develops triamcinolone, a fluorinated prednisolone derivative."], ["1950s", "Triamcinolone acetonide, formed with acetone, is found to be far more potent on skin."], ["1960s", "Kenalog brand topical triamcinolone acetonide becomes widely used."]],
  "cell": "Triamcinolone acetonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor inside skin and immune cells. The ligand-bound receptor sheds its chaperone proteins and moves to the nucleus. There it turns on genes like annexin A1, which blocks phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis, and it represses NF-kB and AP-1 so cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha fall. Mast cells, T cells and eosinophils become less active in the treated skin, and small vessels constrict. The acetonide ring makes the drug fat-soluble so it penetrates skin, and the 9-alpha fluorine increases receptor binding.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Inflamed, itchy eczema and dermatitis calm within days as immune cell recruitment and mediator release decline."], ["Mouth", "As a dental paste, it sticks to mouth ulcers and reduces local inflammation and pain."], ["Skin structure", "With long use, reduced collagen production thins skin and can cause striae, especially in folds."], ["Adrenal axis", "Large areas, occlusion or long courses can allow enough absorption to suppress the body's cortisol production."]],
  "journey": "Topical triamcinolone soaks into the stratum corneum, which holds a reservoir of drug. Potency rises with strength and vehicle, and with occlusion, which the label permits for some conditions. Thin or inflamed skin and children absorb more. The absorbed drug is metabolized in the liver to inactive forms that leave in urine. Relief begins within days.",
  "sidefx": "Topical triamcinolone causes the usual steroid effects when overused: skin atrophy and stretch marks from reduced collagen, visible vessels, and steroid acne or perioral dermatitis on the face. Local immune suppression can let infections spread, so occlusion is stopped if infection appears. Absorbed steroid can suppress the HPA axis and cause Cushing features, more likely in children. There is no antidote; recovery follows stopping or tapering.",
  "fun": "The same molecule is injected directly into keloids and alopecia areata patches, where it can cause a dent in the skin from local fat atrophy.",
  "src": ["Triamcinolone acetonide cream FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"desonide": {
  "origin": "After topical hydrocortisone was shown to work in 1952, most steroid research aimed for more power, often by adding fluorine. That made strong drugs, but they could thin delicate skin. Desonide went the other way. It is a non-fluorinated steroid, a 16,17-acetonide of 16-alpha-hydroxyprednisolone, built to calm mild inflammation with a lower risk of thinning skin. It found its place on the face, eyelids, skin folds and in children, where potent steroids cause the most harm. Over the years it was sold as creams, ointments and lotions, and newer gel and foam versions were approved in the 2000s.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Sulzberger and Witten show hydrocortisone works on the skin, starting topical steroid therapy."], ["2000s", "Newer desonide gel and foam vehicles are approved for atopic dermatitis."]],
  "cell": "Desonide binds the glucocorticoid receptor in skin cells. The activated receptor moves to the nucleus and increases transcription of anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin A1, which blocks phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also represses NF-kB and AP-1, which reduces cytokines that recruit T cells and eosinophils into eczema. Because it has no fluorine, it binds the receptor less strongly and is broken down more easily than fluorinated steroids. That gives a milder anti-inflammatory and vasoconstrictive effect with less suppression of collagen-making fibroblasts.",
  "body": [["Facial and fold skin", "Mild eczema and dermatitis on the face, eyelids, groin and armpits settle with less risk of atrophy than with stronger steroids."], ["Children's skin", "It is a common choice for children with eczema because low potency means less HPA axis risk, though diapers act like occlusion and raise absorption."], ["Skin structure", "Thinning, stretch marks and visible vessels are less common than with potent steroids but can still occur with long use."], ["Adrenal axis", "HPA axis suppression is uncommon but possible with large areas or prolonged use."]],
  "journey": "Desonide is spread in a thin layer and absorbed into the stratum corneum. Thin skin on the face and folds absorbs more than thick skin on palms. Diapers and skin folds trap the drug and act like an occlusive dressing. The small absorbed fraction is metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine. Relief usually begins within days, and the label calls for reassessment if there is no improvement within two weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are the same type as other steroids but less frequent: mild burning, dryness, atrophy, striae and perioral dermatitis, all from glucocorticoid effects on skin cells and vessels. Local immune suppression can worsen untreated infections. Absorption from large areas can still suppress the HPA axis, particularly in small children. There is no antidote; stopping usually reverses the effects.",
  "fun": "Topical steroids are ranked into seven US potency classes, and desonide sits near the mildest end, class VI, while halobetasol sits at class I.",
  "src": ["Desonide cream FDA label", "Desonate and Verdeso FDA labels", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mometasone-topical": {
  "origin": "Mometasone furoate was developed by Schering-Plough in the 1980s with a clear goal: a strong topical steroid that worked once daily with less systemic effect. Its chemists put a furoate ester at the 17 position and chlorine atoms at 9 and 21, creating a very lipophilic molecule that binds the glucocorticoid receptor tightly. The FDA approved it as Elocon cream in 1987. The same molecule later became a widely used nasal spray (Nasonex) and inhaler (Asmanex), because it is potent where applied but is largely broken down by the liver after any absorption.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Schering-Plough designs mometasone furoate as a potent once-daily steroid."], ["1987", "FDA approves Elocon (mometasone furoate) for skin."], ["1997", "Mometasone nasal spray (Nasonex) is approved for allergic rhinitis."]],
  "cell": "Mometasone furoate enters skin cells and binds the cytosolic glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity. The receptor-drug complex moves to the nucleus, where it induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and arachidonic acid release. It also represses NF-kB and AP-1 driven genes, lowering IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha and other cytokines. The result is less recruitment of inflammatory cells, less vascular leak and vasoconstriction, plus antiproliferative effects on keratinocytes. The furoate ester helps the drug stay in skin and supports once-daily dosing.",
  "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, itch, scaling and swelling of eczema and psoriasis decrease with once-daily use."], ["Skin structure", "Prolonged use can thin the skin through reduced collagen synthesis."], ["Eyes", "Use near the eyes can raise eye pressure or contribute to cataracts, like other corticosteroids."], ["Adrenal axis", "Large-area, prolonged or occlusive use can suppress cortisol production, with a higher risk in children."]],
  "journey": "Mometasone furoate partitions into the lipid-rich stratum corneum and builds a local reservoir. Its high lipophilicity and strong receptor binding give a long local effect, allowing once-daily application. Only a small fraction crosses into the blood, where it is highly protein bound and extensively metabolized by the liver, then excreted in urine and bile.",
  "sidefx": "Strong receptor activation in skin explains local side effects: atrophy, striae, telangiectasia and steroid acne. Suppressed local immunity can allow infections to spread. Absorbed drug can suppress the HPA axis and cause hyperglycemia or Cushing syndrome, especially in children, who have more skin area for their weight. Near the eyes, steroid effects on the trabecular meshwork and lens can cause glaucoma and cataract. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped or tapered.",
  "fun": "Mometasone is a rare drug that reached patients as a skin cream, a nasal spray and an asthma inhaler, all built on the same molecule.",
  "src": ["Elocon (mometasone furoate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"halobetasol": {
  "origin": "Halobetasol propionate is one of the strongest topical steroids ever made. It was developed by Ciba-Geigy in Switzerland and introduced in the US as Ultravate around 1990. Its structure stacks every potency-boosting feature known at the time: fluorine atoms at the 6-alpha and 9-alpha positions, a chlorine at carbon 21, a 16-beta methyl group and a 17-propionate ester. This puts it in class I, the super-high potency group, used for short courses on thick psoriasis plaques and severe dermatoses. Later products added a lower-strength lotion and a combination with the retinoid tazarotene (Duobrii, approved in 2019), designed to allow longer use with less steroid exposure.",
  "timeline": [["c. 1990", "Halobetasol propionate is introduced in the US as Ultravate, a super-high potency steroid."], ["2019", "FDA approves Duobrii, combining halobetasol with tazarotene for plaque psoriasis."]],
  "cell": "Halobetasol propionate binds the glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity. The complex moves to the nucleus, where it switches on annexin A1 (which blocks phospholipase A2 and the arachidonic acid cascade) and switches off NF-kB and AP-1 driven cytokines and adhesion molecules. In psoriasis this reduces T-cell activation and the IL-17 and IL-23 driven inflammation, and slows the rapid division of keratinocytes so plaques flatten. Its halogens and ester make it resist breakdown in skin, which keeps the receptor activated for a long time.",
  "body": [["Psoriatic plaques", "Thick, scaly plaques flatten and lose redness quickly because inflammation and keratinocyte overgrowth are strongly suppressed."], ["Skin structure", "The same strong antiproliferative effect causes atrophy, striae and telangiectasia if used too long or on thin skin."], ["Adrenal axis", "Even short courses over large areas can suppress the HPA axis, which is why treatment length and weekly amount are limited."], ["Eyes", "Steroid reaching the eye area can raise eye pressure and contribute to cataracts."]],
  "journey": "Halobetasol is applied as a thin layer and penetrates the stratum corneum, forming a reservoir. Its high potency means even modest absorption can matter. Absorption rises sharply with occlusion, large areas and thin or broken skin. The absorbed fraction is metabolized by the liver and excreted by the kidneys. Effects appear within days.",
  "sidefx": "Because it is super-potent, halobetasol's side effects are the extreme end of topical steroid effects. Strong suppression of fibroblasts and keratinocytes causes skin atrophy and stretch marks. HPA axis suppression and Cushing syndrome can occur, and hyperglycemia may appear with heavy use. Local immune suppression can let infection spread. Use is limited to short courses and avoided on the face and folds for some products. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and the adrenal axis recovers over time.",
  "fun": "Pairing halobetasol with tazarotene in one lotion helps offset each drug's weak point: the retinoid helps counter steroid-induced skin thinning, and the steroid calms retinoid irritation.",
  "src": ["Ultravate (halobetasol propionate) FDA label", "Duobrii FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"desoximetasone": {
  "origin": "Desoximetasone was developed by the German company Hoechst and introduced as Topicort in the 1970s. It is unusual among potent topical steroids because it lacks the hydroxyl or ester group at carbon 17 that most others carry. The name reflects this: it is a 17-desoxy relative of dexamethasone. Despite missing that group, it is fluorinated and methylated and turned out to be a high-potency steroid with good penetration. It became available in creams, ointments, a gel useful for hairy areas and later a spray.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Hoechst develops desoximetasone and introduces it as Topicort."], ["2010s", "A desoximetasone spray formulation is approved for plaque psoriasis."]],
  "cell": "Desoximetasone binds the glucocorticoid receptor in skin cells. The receptor complex enters the nucleus and induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and lowers prostaglandin and leukotriene production. It also suppresses NF-kB and AP-1 dependent genes for cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules, so fewer immune cells move into inflamed skin. Local vasoconstriction reduces redness. Its 9-alpha fluorine and 16-alpha methyl groups raise receptor binding and resistance to breakdown.",
  "body": [["Skin inflammation", "Redness, itch and scaling in eczema and psoriasis improve within days as inflammatory signaling falls."], ["Hairy areas", "The gel form spreads into the scalp and hairy skin without a greasy base, reaching inflamed skin under the hair."], ["Skin structure", "Long use can thin skin and cause stretch marks from reduced collagen and cell turnover."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorption can suppress cortisol production; children are more susceptible, including to intracranial hypertension from steroid exposure."]],
  "journey": "Desoximetasone dissolves into the stratum corneum, which stores and slowly releases it. Penetration depends on the base, with ointment strongest, and rises with occlusion, inflamed skin and large areas. The absorbed fraction is metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine. Clinical effects appear within hours to days.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect strong glucocorticoid action: thinning skin, stretch marks, visible vessels and folliculitis locally. Infections can worsen because skin immunity is suppressed. Enough absorption suppresses the HPA axis, which can cause Cushing syndrome, hyperglycemia and, in children, slowed growth or raised intracranial pressure. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped or tapered.",
  "fun": "Desoximetasone shows that the 17-position group is not essential for potency: the molecule reaches high potency even without the ester most strong topical steroids rely on.",
  "src": ["Topicort (desoximetasone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"fluocinolone-topical": {
  "origin": "Fluocinolone acetonide was developed at Syntex in the late 1950s and introduced as Synalar. Syntex chemists added two fluorine atoms (at the 6-alpha and 9-alpha positions) and a 16,17 acetonide ring to the steroid core, which made a potent, skin-penetrating anti-inflammatory steroid. It later became the parent of fluocinonide. Fluocinolone itself found many niches: a scalp oil (Derma-Smoothe/FS) that can be used in children with eczema, a shampoo for seborrheic dermatitis, ear drops, and part of Tri-Luma, a melasma cream combining it with hydroquinone and tretinoin that the FDA approved in 2002. It is even used in tiny implants placed in the eye for chronic uveitis and macular edema.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Syntex develops fluocinolone acetonide, sold as Synalar."], ["2002", "FDA approves Tri-Luma, combining fluocinolone with hydroquinone and tretinoin for melasma."], ["2005", "A fluocinolone intravitreal implant (Retisert) is approved for chronic uveitis."]],
  "cell": "Fluocinolone acetonide binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor-drug complex moves to the nucleus, where it increases transcription of annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2, and decreases transcription of cytokine and adhesion molecule genes by blocking NF-kB and AP-1. Less arachidonic acid release means fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes. Inflammatory cells such as T cells and eosinophils stay out of the skin, mast cells release less, and small vessels constrict. At low strengths in oil or shampoo, it delivers a milder, medium to low potency effect suited to the scalp and to children.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Eczema, dermatitis and psoriasis become less red and itchy as inflammatory signaling is suppressed."], ["Scalp", "The oil and shampoo reach skin under hair, easing scale and itch of seborrheic dermatitis and scalp eczema."], ["Pigment (in Tri-Luma)", "Combined with hydroquinone and tretinoin, the steroid reduces irritation from the other two and helps fade melasma."], ["Skin structure", "Long use can thin skin and cause folliculitis or stretch marks."]],
  "journey": "Fluocinolone in cream, oil or shampoo enters the stratum corneum and hair follicles, where it forms a local reservoir. Absorption is greater with occlusion, thin or inflamed skin and large areas. The absorbed fraction is broken down in the liver and excreted in urine. Shampoo is rinsed off, so exposure is brief. Effects begin within hours to days.",
  "sidefx": "Like other topical steroids, it can cause skin thinning, stretch marks, folliculitis and perioral dermatitis, from suppression of skin cells and immunity. Infections may grow under occlusion, so occlusion is stopped if infection develops. Extensive use can suppress the HPA axis and cause hyperglycemia or Cushing features. The oil contains peanut oil, which matters for people with peanut allergy. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "The same steroid used in a scalp oil is also built into rice-grain-sized eye implants that release drug for years inside the eye.",
  "src": ["Synalar (fluocinolone acetonide) FDA label", "Derma-Smoothe/FS FDA label", "Tri-Luma FDA label"]
},
"betamethasone-valerate-topical": {
  "origin": "Betamethasone valerate is the 17-valerate ester of betamethasone, a fluorinated steroid developed around 1960. In the early 1960s the British company Glaxo introduced it as Betnovate, and it quickly became one of the most used skin steroids in the world. Pairing the five-carbon valerate ester with the 17 position made the molecule very fat-soluble, so it penetrated skin far better than hydrocortisone. In the US it was sold as Valisone, and later as a foam (Luxiq) for the scalp. It sits in the medium-potency range and is a common comparison point in steroid potency tables.",
  "timeline": [["c. 1960", "Betamethasone, a fluorinated 16-beta-methyl glucocorticoid, is developed."], ["1960s", "Glaxo introduces betamethasone valerate as Betnovate in the UK."], ["1962", "McKenzie and Stoughton describe the skin blanching assay used to rank topical steroid potency."]],
  "cell": "Betamethasone valerate binds the glucocorticoid receptor, a nuclear receptor that waits in the cytoplasm bound to heat shock proteins. Ligand binding frees the receptor, which enters the nucleus and acts on glucocorticoid response elements. It induces annexin A1, which blocks phospholipase A2 and the formation of prostaglandins and leukotrienes, and it represses NF-kB and AP-1, lowering cytokines and adhesion molecules. The result is vasoconstriction, less edema and itch, and reduced proliferation of keratinocytes and fibroblasts. The 17-valerate ester can shift to the 21 position over time, which lowers potency, so formulation matters.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Redness, swelling and itch of eczema, psoriasis and contact dermatitis lessen as inflammatory mediators fall."], ["Scalp", "The lotion and foam reach the scalp skin through hair, easing scalp psoriasis and dermatitis."], ["Blood vessels", "Dermal vessels constrict, producing visible blanching that is the basis of potency testing."], ["Skin structure", "Reduced keratinocyte and fibroblast growth explains atrophy, striae and visible vessels with long use."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorbed drug can suppress cortisol production, especially in children, with large areas or with occlusion."]],
  "journey": "Applied as cream, ointment, lotion or foam, betamethasone valerate enters the stratum corneum, which acts as a reservoir. Ointment gives more penetration than cream or lotion of the same strength. Occlusion, thin or inflamed skin and large areas increase absorption. Absorbed betamethasone has a plasma half-life of several hours but a longer biologic effect, is metabolized by the liver and excreted mainly in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from glucocorticoid receptor activation: skin atrophy and striae from reduced collagen, telangiectasia, steroid acne and perioral dermatitis. Local immune suppression can let infection spread. Allergic contact dermatitis to the steroid itself can occur. Systemic absorption may suppress the HPA axis and raise blood sugar. Near the eyes, cataract and glaucoma are possible. There is no antidote; the product is stopped or tapered.",
  "fun": "Betamethasone valerate is so widely studied that it is often used as the benchmark comparator in clinical trials of new topical steroids.",
  "src": ["Betamethasone valerate cream FDA label", "Luxiq (betamethasone valerate) foam FDA label", "McKenzie AW, Stoughton RB. Arch Dermatol 1962"]
},
"calcipotriene-betamethasone": {
  "origin": "This combination joins two separate ideas. The first came in the 1980s, when researchers noticed that vitamin D compounds slowed skin cell growth and that psoriasis improved in some patients taking vitamin D for bone disease. Natural active vitamin D raises blood calcium too much, so LEO Pharma in Denmark designed calcipotriol (called calcipotriene in the US), an analog that acts on skin but is broken down quickly once absorbed. It was approved in the US as Dovonex in the 1990s. The second idea is the potent steroid betamethasone dipropionate. LEO combined them in one ointment, Taclonex, approved by the FDA in 2006. The pair works better than either alone, and the steroid reduces the vitamin D analog's skin irritation. A foam (Enstilar), scalp suspension and cream (Wynzora) followed.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "LEO Pharma develops calcipotriol, a vitamin D analog with low calcium effects."], ["1990s", "Calcipotriene (Dovonex) is approved in the US for plaque psoriasis."], ["2006", "FDA approves Taclonex ointment, combining calcipotriene and betamethasone dipropionate."], ["2015", "Enstilar foam version is approved."]],
  "cell": "Calcipotriene binds the vitamin D receptor (VDR), a nuclear receptor in keratinocytes and immune cells. VDR partners with the retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin D response elements on DNA. In psoriatic skin this slows keratinocyte proliferation, pushes cells toward normal differentiation and shifts T cells away from the IL-17 and IL-23 driven inflammation of psoriasis. Betamethasone dipropionate binds the glucocorticoid receptor, which induces annexin A1 to block phospholipase A2 and represses NF-kB driven cytokines. The two pathways attack psoriasis from different sides: one normalizes skin cell growth, the other shuts down inflammation.",
  "body": [["Psoriatic plaques", "Plaques flatten and lose scale and redness, often visibly within one to two weeks, as cell overgrowth and inflammation both decline."], ["Skin tolerance", "The steroid calms the burning and irritation that calcipotriene alone often causes."], ["Calcium balance", "Absorbed calcipotriene can raise blood and urine calcium, especially when large amounts are used."], ["Adrenal axis", "Absorbed betamethasone can suppress cortisol production with large areas or heavy use."], ["Skin structure", "Long-term steroid exposure risks atrophy, though calcipotriene does not itself thin skin."]],
  "journey": "The ointment, foam, cream or suspension is rubbed into plaques once daily. Both drugs enter the skin, and only small amounts reach the blood. Absorbed calcipotriene is rapidly broken down in the liver to inactive metabolites, which is why its effect on calcium is limited. Absorbed betamethasone is metabolized by the liver. Both leave in bile and urine. The weekly amount and body area treated are limited to keep absorption low.",
  "sidefx": "Calcipotriene can irritate skin and, if too much is absorbed, increase intestinal calcium absorption and bone resorption, causing hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria; the product is stopped until calcium normalizes. Betamethasone can cause skin thinning, striae, HPA axis suppression, hyperglycemia and Cushing features with heavy use. Allergic contact dermatitis is possible. There is no antidote for either; levels fall after stopping.",
  "fun": "Calcipotriol is cleared from the blood so quickly that its effect on blood calcium is roughly one-hundredth or less that of natural calcitriol, which is what made it practical for skin use.",
  "src": ["Taclonex FDA label", "Enstilar FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
,
"crisaborole": {
  "origin": "Crisaborole came from Anacor Pharmaceuticals in Palo Alto, California, a company built around boron chemistry. Anacor's scientists found that benzoxaboroles, small rings containing a boron atom, could fit into enzyme active sites in unusual ways. One of these, crisaborole, inhibited phosphodiesterase 4 and was small enough to penetrate skin. The FDA approved it as Eucrisa ointment in December 2016 for mild to moderate eczema, the first new nonsteroidal prescription eczema treatment in the US in more than a decade. Pfizer bought Anacor that same year, and the approval was later extended to infants as young as 3 months.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Anacor Pharmaceuticals develops boron-based benzoxaborole drugs."], ["2016", "Pfizer acquires Anacor; FDA approves crisaborole (Eucrisa) for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis."], ["2020", "Approval is expanded to children 3 months and older."]],
  "cell": "Crisaborole inhibits phosphodiesterase 4 (PDE4), the main enzyme that breaks down cyclic AMP inside immune cells such as T cells, monocytes and dendritic cells, and in keratinocytes. Its boron atom binds the bimetal (zinc and magnesium) center in the PDE4 catalytic pocket. With PDE4 blocked, cAMP levels rise and activate protein kinase A. PKA phosphorylates CREB and interferes with NF-kB signaling, which reduces production of inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-4, IL-13, IL-23 and others involved in eczema. Less cytokine release means less inflammation and itch. Crisaborole does not act on the glucocorticoid receptor, so it does not cause skin atrophy.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Red, itchy eczema patches calm over a few weeks as cytokine production drops."], ["Sensitive areas", "Because it does not thin skin, it can be used on the face, eyelids and folds where steroids cause more harm."], ["Rest of the body", "Absorbed crisaborole is quickly broken down to inactive products, so systemic PDE4 effects like nausea are not prominent."]],
  "journey": "Crisaborole ointment is spread thinly on eczema. Its low molecular weight lets it penetrate the skin barrier. Some drug reaches the blood, where it is highly protein bound and rapidly hydrolyzed to inactive metabolites. These are further oxidized and conjugated, then excreted mainly by the kidneys. Because the circulating parent drug is short-lived and inactive metabolites dominate, effects stay local. Improvement develops over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "The most common side effect is burning or stinging where it is applied, which is likely irritation of inflamed skin and usually does not mean allergy. True hypersensitivity, including contact urticaria, can occur and leads to stopping the drug. Skin atrophy does not occur because there is no steroid receptor activity. There is no antidote; the ointment is washed off.",
  "fun": "Crisaborole and tavaborole, from the same company, both use boron, but one blocks a human immune enzyme and the other traps fungal tRNA.",
  "src": ["Eucrisa (crisaborole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"roflumilast-topical": {
  "origin": "Roflumilast was first developed by the German company Altana (formerly Byk Gulden) as an oral PDE4 inhibitor for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, approved in the US as Daliresp in 2011. The pill caused nausea, diarrhea and weight loss, which limited its use. Arcutis Biotherapeutics in California reasoned that applying roflumilast to the skin could deliver its anti-inflammatory power with far less body exposure. They developed a cream and foam that the FDA approved as Zoryve: cream for plaque psoriasis in 2022, foam for seborrheic dermatitis in 2023, and a lower strength cream for atopic dermatitis in 2024.",
  "timeline": [["2011", "Oral roflumilast (Daliresp) is approved for severe COPD."], ["2022", "FDA approves roflumilast cream (Zoryve) for plaque psoriasis."], ["2023", "Roflumilast foam is approved for seborrheic dermatitis."], ["2024", "A lower strength cream is approved for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis."]],
  "cell": "Roflumilast is a highly selective, potent inhibitor of phosphodiesterase 4 (PDE4), the main enzyme that degrades cyclic AMP in keratinocytes and immune cells. Blocking PDE4 raises intracellular cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and the transcription factor CREB and dampens NF-kB signaling. This reduces production of inflammatory cytokines, including TNF-alpha, IL-17, IL-23 and interferon-gamma, central to psoriasis, and type 2 cytokines involved in eczema. Its main metabolite, roflumilast N-oxide, also inhibits PDE4. Roflumilast is much more potent at PDE4 than crisaborole, which helps it work once daily.",
  "body": [["Skin plaques", "Psoriasis plaques become less red, thick and scaly, including in skin folds, where steroids are riskier."], ["Scalp and face", "The foam calms the redness and greasy scale of seborrheic dermatitis."], ["Itch", "Itch drops as inflammatory cytokine signaling in the skin falls."], ["Gut and appetite", "At topical exposure, nausea and diarrhea are much less common than with the oral COPD pill, but they can occur."]],
  "journey": "Roflumilast cream or foam is rubbed into the skin once daily. A small amount is absorbed and reaches a steady blood level. In the liver, CYP3A4 and CYP1A2 convert it to the active N-oxide metabolite. Both are highly protein bound, and the parent drug has a long effective half-life after topical use of several days. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. Strong CYP3A4 or CYP1A2 inhibitors can raise exposure.",
  "sidefx": "Topical roflumilast is generally well tolerated. Diarrhea, headache, insomnia and nausea are reported because some drug reaches the blood and PDE4 inhibition affects the gut and brain, as with the oral form. Local burning is less common than with crisaborole. Using it with oral roflumilast would double PDE4 inhibition. There is no antidote; it is stopped.",
  "fun": "Roflumilast cream is a rare case of a pill drug becoming more successful in dermatology than in its original use, by moving it from the gut to the skin.",
  "src": ["Zoryve (roflumilast) cream and foam FDA labels", "Daliresp (roflumilast) FDA label"]
},
"tapinarof": {
  "origin": "Tapinarof has one of the strangest origins in dermatology. It is a small molecule first found as a natural product of Photorhabdus luminescens, a glowing bacterium that lives inside soil nematodes that invade insects. The bacterium makes the compound to help the worm and itself survive inside the insect. Researchers noticed it had anti-inflammatory activity in skin. The Canadian company Welichem Biotech developed it as benvitimod, approved in China for psoriasis in 2019. Dermavant Sciences developed it in the US, where the FDA approved it as Vtama cream for plaque psoriasis in May 2022 and for atopic dermatitis in December 2024. It is the first approved drug that works by turning on the aryl hydrocarbon receptor.",
  "timeline": [["2019", "Tapinarof (benvitimod) is approved in China for psoriasis."], ["2022", "FDA approves tapinarof (Vtama) cream for plaque psoriasis in adults."], ["2024", "FDA approves tapinarof for atopic dermatitis in adults and children 2 years and older."]],
  "cell": "Tapinarof binds and activates the aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR), a transcription factor that sits in the cytoplasm of keratinocytes and immune cells. When tapinarof binds, AhR moves into the nucleus and pairs with ARNT. The pair binds xenobiotic response elements on DNA and changes which genes are made. Three effects follow. Th17 cytokines IL-17A and IL-17F decrease, which drives psoriasis plaques. Skin barrier proteins such as filaggrin and loricrin increase, which helps the barrier defect in eczema. And the NRF2 antioxidant pathway turns on, reducing oxidative stress. Tapinarof does not act on the glucocorticoid receptor.",
  "body": [["Psoriatic skin", "Lower IL-17 signaling reduces redness, thickness and scale of plaques over weeks."], ["Eczema skin", "More barrier proteins and less inflammation reduce dryness, itch and redness in atopic dermatitis."], ["Hair follicles", "Folliculitis is the most typical side effect, likely because AhR activation affects follicle cells."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are mostly low or undetectable, so systemic immune suppression is not described."]],
  "journey": "Tapinarof cream is applied once daily. Most drug stays in the skin, and blood levels after use are usually low or undetectable. The small absorbed amount is highly protein bound and metabolized by the liver through several CYP pathways, sulfation and glucuronidation. Effects build over weeks, and in trials some patients stayed clear for a time after stopping, consistent with gene-level changes in skin.",
  "sidefx": "Folliculitis, small red bumps around hair follicles, is the hallmark side effect, thought to reflect AhR activation in follicles, and is more common under occlusion or on hairy areas. Contact dermatitis and headache are also reported. Skin atrophy is not expected because it is not a steroid. There is no antidote; the cream is stopped.",
  "fun": "The nematode-bacteria partners that make tapinarof can make dead insects glow, because Photorhabdus luminescens is bioluminescent.",
  "src": ["Vtama (tapinarof) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"delgocitinib": {
  "origin": "Delgocitinib was discovered by Japan Tobacco, which runs a pharmaceutical research arm. It is a pan-JAK inhibitor, blocking all four Janus kinases, designed for use on the skin. In Japan it was approved in 2020 as an ointment (Corectim) for atopic dermatitis, making it the first topical JAK inhibitor approved anywhere. LEO Pharma, the Danish dermatology company, licensed it outside Japan and developed a cream for chronic hand eczema, a hard-to-treat condition that can damage people's ability to work. The cream was approved in the European Union in 2024 and by the FDA as Anzupgo in July 2025, the first FDA-approved drug specifically for chronic hand eczema.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "Japan Tobacco discovers delgocitinib, a pan-JAK inhibitor."], ["2020", "Delgocitinib ointment is approved in Japan for atopic dermatitis."], ["2024", "Delgocitinib cream is approved in the European Union for chronic hand eczema."], ["2025", "FDA approves delgocitinib cream (Anzupgo) for moderate to severe chronic hand eczema in adults."]],
  "cell": "Delgocitinib reversibly blocks the ATP binding site of all four Janus kinases: JAK1, JAK2, JAK3 and TYK2. These enzymes sit on the inner tails of cytokine receptors. When a cytokine such as IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-22 or interferon binds its receptor, paired JAKs phosphorylate each other and then recruit and phosphorylate STAT proteins. Phosphorylated STATs travel to the nucleus and turn on inflammatory genes. By blocking the JAKs, delgocitinib stops this relay in keratinocytes and immune cells of the hand skin. Itch signaling (IL-31), type 2 inflammation (IL-4, IL-13) and barrier disruption (IL-22) are all reduced.",
  "body": [["Hand skin", "Itch, redness, scaling and painful cracks on the hands and wrists decrease over weeks as cytokine signaling is blocked."], ["Itch nerves", "Blocking IL-31 and related signals quiets itch quickly, often within days."], ["Immune system", "Systemic exposure is low but not zero, so the label lists class concerns such as serious infections."], ["Skin cancer risk", "Non-melanoma skin cancers are listed in the label as a potential risk."]],
  "journey": "Delgocitinib cream is applied to the hands and wrists. Very little reaches the blood; plasma levels are low after repeated use. The absorbed drug is mostly unchanged in circulation, has limited metabolism mainly by CYP3A4/5, and leaves mostly unchanged in urine and feces. The half-life after repeated use is about a day. Itch relief begins within days and skin clearing over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Local application site reactions can occur. The label lists serious infections, non-melanoma skin cancers and the JAK inhibitor class risks seen with oral JAK drugs, because JAK enzymes support normal immune defense. With such low blood levels these risks are thought to be much smaller than with pills, but vaccinations such as zoster are usually completed beforehand. Combining it with other JAK inhibitors or strong immunosuppressants is not recommended. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Delgocitinib was the first topical JAK inhibitor approved anywhere in the world, in Japan in 2020.",
  "src": ["Anzupgo (delgocitinib) cream FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"clascoterone": {
  "origin": "Clascoterone is cortexolone 17-alpha-propionate, a steroid ester developed by the Italian company Cassiopea, part of Cosmo Pharmaceuticals. Androgens such as dihydrotestosterone drive oil glands and are a root cause of acne, but drugs that block androgens throughout the body, like spironolactone, cause hormonal side effects and are unsuitable for many males. Cassiopea's idea was an antiandrogen that works in the skin and then breaks down quickly into an inactive form. The FDA approved it as Winlevi cream in August 2020 for acne in people 12 and older. It was the first topical acne drug with a new mechanism of action in roughly four decades, and the first topical androgen receptor inhibitor for acne.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Cassiopea develops clascoterone (cortexolone 17-alpha-propionate) as a topical antiandrogen."], ["2020", "FDA approves clascoterone cream (Winlevi) for acne vulgaris."]],
  "cell": "In sebaceous glands, testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). DHT binds androgen receptors in sebocytes, the receptor moves to the nucleus and turns on genes that make sebocytes grow and produce more oil. Clascoterone competitively binds the androgen receptor in sebocytes and follicle cells, blocking DHT from activating it. Less receptor activation means less sebum production and less release of inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and IL-8 from sebocytes. Lower oil also starves Cutibacterium acnes, which feeds on sebum. In skin and blood, clascoterone is hydrolyzed to cortexolone, which has much weaker activity, limiting effects elsewhere.",
  "body": [["Oil glands", "Sebum output falls because androgen signals to the glands are blocked."], ["Acne lesions", "Both inflammatory pimples and comedones decrease over about 12 weeks."], ["Adrenal axis", "In trials, some users showed HPA axis suppression, likely because cortexolone is related to cortisol precursors; it reversed after stopping."], ["Electrolytes", "Mild rises in potassium were seen in some trial participants."]],
  "journey": "Clascoterone cream is applied to the skin twice daily. It penetrates to the sebaceous glands and some is absorbed, reaching steady blood levels within about five days. In the skin and blood it is hydrolyzed to cortexolone, an inactive or weak metabolite. Protein binding is moderate to high. Because it is quickly converted, systemic antiandrogen effects are minimal.",
  "sidefx": "Local redness, scaling, dryness and itching are the most common side effects, from irritation of treated skin. HPA axis suppression was seen in some trial participants, more likely with large areas, long use or occlusion; it is linked to the steroid structure and its metabolite. Hyperkalemia appeared in a few participants. Because systemic antiandrogen effects are minimal, the feminizing effects of oral antiandrogens are not expected. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Clascoterone gives a hormonal acne treatment option that can be used in males, since its antiandrogen action stays mostly in the skin.",
  "src": ["Winlevi (clascoterone) FDA label", "Hebert A et al. JAMA Dermatology 2020 (phase 3 trials)"]
},
"trifarotene": {
  "origin": "Trifarotene was developed by Galderma, a Swiss-based dermatology company with a long history in retinoids that also created adapalene. Earlier retinoids like tretinoin bind all three retinoic acid receptor types (alpha, beta and gamma). Galderma's researchers aimed for a molecule that hit only RAR-gamma, the form most common in the epidermis, hoping for effective acne treatment with less body exposure. The FDA approved it as Aklief cream in October 2019, the first new retinoid molecule approved for acne in the US in about 20 years. It was also the first to be studied specifically for acne on the chest, shoulders and back as well as the face.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "Galderma's earlier retinoid adapalene is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves trifarotene (Aklief) for acne of the face and trunk."]],
  "cell": "Trifarotene is a selective agonist of retinoic acid receptor gamma (RAR-gamma), the main RAR in the epidermis. It enters keratinocytes and binds RAR-gamma in the nucleus. The RAR-gamma/RXR heterodimer then binds retinoic acid response elements on DNA and changes gene expression. This normalizes how follicle-lining cells divide and shed, so they stop sticking together and plugging the follicle, which prevents microcomedones (the first step of acne). It also reduces inflammatory signaling. Because it is selective and broken down quickly in the liver, effects stay mostly in the skin.",
  "body": [["Hair follicles", "Follicle linings shed normally, so fewer clogged pores and blackheads form."], ["Acne lesions", "Inflammatory and noninflammatory acne lesions on the face, chest and back decrease over weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Redness, scaling, dryness and stinging are common in the first weeks as cell turnover speeds up and the barrier thins."], ["Sun sensitivity", "The thinner outer layer raises the risk of sunburn."]],
  "journey": "Trifarotene cream is applied in the evening to clean skin. Very little reaches the blood, and plasma levels are low and short-lived. What is absorbed is very highly protein bound and quickly metabolized in the liver by CYP2C9, CYP3A4, CYP2C8 and CYP2B6, then excreted mainly in the feces. This rapid breakdown is part of why selective topical retinoids have low systemic exposure.",
  "sidefx": "Retinoid irritation is the main side effect: redness, scaling, dryness, stinging and burning, worst in the first month. Faster turnover thins the stratum corneum and weakens the barrier, which explains the dryness and higher sensitivity to sun, wind and cold. Waxing treated skin can tear it. Moisturizer from the start is part of the labeled regimen. There is no antidote; reducing frequency or stopping allows the skin to recover.",
  "fun": "Trifarotene is so selective for RAR-gamma that its effect on the other two RAR types is many times weaker, making it the first fourth-generation topical retinoid.",
  "src": ["Aklief (trifarotene) FDA label", "Tan J et al. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019 (phase 3 trials)"]
},
"adapalene-benzoyl-peroxide": {
  "origin": "This gel combines two drugs with long histories. Benzoyl peroxide is an old organic peroxide used for acne since the mid-20th century because it kills acne bacteria and does not breed resistance. Adapalene was designed by Galderma researchers as a synthetic, chemically stable retinoid; it was approved in the US as Differin in 1996 and became available over the counter in 2016. Most retinoids such as tretinoin break down when mixed with benzoyl peroxide, but adapalene stays stable. That allowed Galderma to put both in one gel, approved by the FDA as Epiduo in 2008. A stronger version, Epiduo Forte, followed in 2015.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves adapalene (Differin) for acne."], ["2008", "FDA approves Epiduo, the adapalene and benzoyl peroxide combination gel."], ["2015", "A higher-strength version, Epiduo Forte, is approved."], ["2016", "Adapalene alone becomes available over the counter."]],
  "cell": "Adapalene, a naphthoic acid derivative, binds the nuclear retinoic acid receptors beta and gamma. Activated receptors pair with RXR and change gene expression, normalizing the differentiation of keratinocytes lining hair follicles so they shed instead of sticking together. This breaks up existing comedones and prevents new microcomedones. Adapalene also reduces inflammatory signals by inhibiting AP-1 and lowering toll-like receptor 2 expression on immune cells. Benzoyl peroxide penetrates the follicle, breaks down into benzoic acid and releases reactive oxygen species that oxidize bacterial proteins, killing Cutibacterium acnes. Because it acts by oxidation rather than a single target, bacteria do not develop resistance to it.",
  "body": [["Follicles", "Retinoid action clears blackheads and whiteheads and prevents new plugs."], ["Bacteria", "Benzoyl peroxide lowers C. acnes counts, reducing inflamed pimples."], ["Skin surface", "Both drugs dry and irritate, causing redness, scaling and stinging, especially in the first weeks."], ["Hair and fabric", "Benzoyl peroxide is a bleaching agent and can lighten hair and colored fabric that it touches."]],
  "journey": "The gel is applied once daily to the face or trunk. Adapalene stays mostly in the follicles and outer skin, and plasma levels are very low. What is absorbed is minimally metabolized and eliminated mainly in bile. Benzoyl peroxide is converted to benzoic acid within the skin; any absorbed benzoic acid is conjugated with glycine to hippuric acid and excreted by the kidneys. Improvement can appear within one to four weeks, with fuller effect by about 12 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Irritation is the main problem: redness, dryness, scaling, stinging and burning from faster skin turnover (adapalene) and oxidative damage to the skin surface (benzoyl peroxide). The thinned outer layer makes skin more sensitive to sun and waxing. Rare serious allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, have been linked to benzoyl peroxide. Bleaching of hair and fabrics is common. There is no antidote; the product is reduced or stopped.",
  "fun": "Mixing tretinoin with benzoyl peroxide can destroy the tretinoin by oxidation, and adapalene's resistance to this was key to making a one-tube combination.",
  "src": ["Epiduo and Epiduo Forte FDA labels", "Differin (adapalene) FDA label", "American Academy of Dermatology acne guidelines"]
},
"clindamycin-benzoyl-peroxide": {
  "origin": "Clindamycin is a semisynthetic relative of lincomycin, an antibiotic made by Streptomyces lincolnensis, a soil bacterium found in a sample from near Lincoln, Nebraska. Chemists at Upjohn modified lincomycin in the 1960s by replacing a hydroxyl group with chlorine, creating clindamycin, which the FDA approved in 1970. Dermatologists soon used it on the skin for acne. Over time C. acnes became resistant to topical antibiotics used alone. Benzoyl peroxide, an old oxidizing agent that bacteria cannot resist, was added to protect clindamycin. Combination gels such as BenzaClin and Duac reached the US market in the early 2000s, and lower benzoyl peroxide versions (Acanya, Onexton) followed.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Upjohn chemists create clindamycin from lincomycin, made by Streptomyces lincolnensis."], ["1970", "FDA approves clindamycin."], ["2000s", "Clindamycin and benzoyl peroxide combination gels (BenzaClin, Duac) are approved for acne."]],
  "cell": "Clindamycin binds the 23S ribosomal RNA of the 50S subunit of bacterial ribosomes, near the peptidyl transferase center. This blocks peptide chain elongation, so C. acnes cannot make proteins and stops growing. With fewer bacteria, less sebum is broken down into irritating free fatty acids, and less bacterial material triggers innate immune signaling, so inflamed papules and pustules settle. Benzoyl peroxide penetrates the follicle and releases reactive oxygen species that oxidize bacterial proteins and membranes, killing C. acnes directly. It is also mildly comedolytic. Because benzoyl peroxide kills bacteria in a nonspecific way, it lowers the number of clindamycin-resistant C. acnes that would emerge with clindamycin alone.",
  "body": [["Follicles", "Bacteria counts in follicles fall, reducing inflamed pimples and pustules within weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Benzoyl peroxide can cause dryness, peeling and redness through oxidative irritation."], ["Gut", "Small amounts of absorbed clindamycin can rarely alter gut flora, allowing C. difficile overgrowth and colitis."], ["Hair and fabric", "Benzoyl peroxide bleaches hair, towels and colored clothing."]],
  "journey": "The gel is applied to clean skin once or twice daily depending on the product. Some kits are mixed by the pharmacist and have a limited shelf life. Benzoyl peroxide is converted to benzoic acid in the skin, and any absorbed benzoic acid is excreted by the kidneys as hippuric acid. A small fraction of clindamycin is absorbed, metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and excreted in urine and bile. Blood levels are minimal.",
  "sidefx": "Skin dryness, peeling and irritation come from benzoyl peroxide's oxidative action. Clindamycin's main serious risk, even when applied to skin, is C. difficile colitis, because absorbed drug can kill normal gut bacteria and let toxin-producing C. difficile grow. Erythromycin products compete for the same ribosome site and antagonize clindamycin. Rare serious allergic reactions to benzoyl peroxide have been described by the FDA. There is no antidote; the product is stopped, and C. difficile colitis is treated separately.",
  "fun": "Benzoyl peroxide is also used as a flour bleaching agent and in plastics manufacturing, the same oxidizing power that makes it useful against acne bacteria.",
  "src": ["BenzaClin and Duac FDA labels", "Cleocin (clindamycin) FDA label", "American Academy of Dermatology acne guidelines"]
},
"clindamycin-tretinoin": {
  "origin": "This gel combines two well established acne drugs. Clindamycin, created at Upjohn in the 1960s from the Streptomyces product lincomycin and approved in 1970, reduces acne bacteria. Tretinoin, all-trans retinoic acid (the acid form of vitamin A), was shown to treat acne by dermatologist Albert Kligman and his trainee James Fulton at the University of Pennsylvania in the late 1960s, and Retin-A was approved by the FDA in 1971. Combining them in one stable gel took formulation work, because tretinoin breaks down easily. The combination was approved by the FDA in 2006 (Ziana), with Veltin and generics following.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "Kligman and Fulton report topical tretinoin for acne."], ["1970", "FDA approves clindamycin."], ["1971", "FDA approves tretinoin (Retin-A) for acne."], ["2006", "FDA approves a clindamycin and tretinoin combination gel (Ziana)."]],
  "cell": "Clindamycin binds the 23S rRNA of the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks peptide chain elongation, stopping protein synthesis in Cutibacterium acnes. Fewer bacteria means less sebum breakdown into irritating fatty acids and less activation of innate immune receptors. Tretinoin enters keratinocytes and binds the nuclear retinoic acid receptors alpha, beta and gamma. The RAR/RXR dimer changes gene expression to speed turnover of the follicle lining and reduce the stickiness of cells, so existing comedones are pushed out and new microcomedones do not form. The two drugs work on different steps of acne: the plug and the bacteria.",
  "body": [["Follicles", "Faster cell turnover clears blackheads and whiteheads and prevents new plugs."], ["Bacteria", "Clindamycin lowers C. acnes numbers, reducing inflammatory papules and pustules."], ["Skin surface", "Tretinoin causes dryness, peeling and redness, and thins the outer layer, raising sunburn risk."], ["Gut", "Absorbed clindamycin can, rarely, disrupt gut flora and allow C. difficile colitis."]],
  "journey": "The gel is applied once at bedtime, partly because tretinoin breaks down in sunlight. Only small amounts of either drug reach the blood. Absorbed clindamycin is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and excreted in urine and bile. Absorbed tretinoin is quickly isomerized and oxidized in the liver and binds tightly to albumin. Improvement appears over four to eight weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Tretinoin's increase in cell turnover thins the protective stratum corneum, causing dryness, peeling, redness and sun sensitivity. Clindamycin can cause C. difficile colitis even from skin use because small absorbed amounts reach the gut. Erythromycin competes for the same ribosome site and antagonizes clindamycin. Benzoyl peroxide applied at the same time can oxidize tretinoin. There is no antidote; the gel is stopped, and colitis is treated separately.",
  "fun": "Kligman, who helped introduce tretinoin for acne, later studied it for sun-damaged skin, which led to tretinoin being marketed for fine wrinkles.",
  "src": ["Ziana (clindamycin and tretinoin) FDA label", "Kligman AM et al. Arch Dermatol 1969", "Retin-A FDA label"]
},
"minocycline-topical-foam": {
  "origin": "Minocycline is a semisynthetic tetracycline developed at Lederle Laboratories in the 1960s and approved as an oral antibiotic in the early 1970s. Oral minocycline became a common acne treatment, but it can cause dizziness, skin discoloration and rare serious reactions. Putting minocycline on the skin was hard because it breaks down in water and light. The Israeli company Foamix Pharmaceuticals developed a hydrophobic, oil-based foam that keeps minocycline stable and delivers it into hair follicles. The FDA approved it as Amzeeq for acne in October 2019, the first topical minocycline. A lower-strength foam, Zilxi, was approved in 2020 for rosacea.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Lederle Laboratories develops minocycline, a semisynthetic tetracycline."], ["1971", "Oral minocycline is approved in the US."], ["2019", "FDA approves minocycline foam (Amzeeq), the first topical minocycline, for acne."], ["2020", "Minocycline foam (Zilxi) is approved for rosacea."]],
  "cell": "Minocycline binds the 16S rRNA of the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking at the A site, so new amino acids cannot be added and protein synthesis stops. This is bacteriostatic against Cutibacterium acnes. Minocycline also has anti-inflammatory actions separate from killing bacteria: it inhibits neutrophil chemotaxis, matrix metalloproteinases and some cytokine production. The hydrophobic foam carries the lipophilic drug into the oily pilosebaceous unit, where acne begins.",
  "body": [["Hair follicles", "Drug concentrates in the oily follicle, lowering C. acnes and inflammation, so inflammatory papules and pustules decrease over weeks."], ["Skin surface", "Local irritation is uncommon, since the vehicle is not drying like many acne products."], ["Rest of the body", "Blood levels are far below those from oral minocycline, so systemic tetracycline effects are much less likely, though the label carries class warnings."]],
  "journey": "The foam is applied once daily and melts into the skin, carrying minocycline into hair follicles. Only a small amount reaches the blood, far less than with oral dosing. Absorbed minocycline is lipophilic, partly metabolized in the liver, has a long half-life in oral data, and is excreted in urine and feces. Bathing or swimming is avoided for an hour after application so drug is not washed off.",
  "sidefx": "The propellant is flammable until dry, so fire and smoking are avoided after application. Because some minocycline is absorbed, the label includes tetracycline class warnings: tooth discoloration and slowed bone growth in children and in pregnancy (tetracyclines bind calcium in teeth and bone), photosensitivity, C. difficile colitis, liver injury, intracranial hypertension, autoimmune syndromes and hyperpigmentation. These are much less likely at topical exposure. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Minocycline can stain skin, nails and even bone blue-gray with long oral use, a pigmentation rarely seen with the foam because so little reaches the blood.",
  "src": ["Amzeeq (minocycline) topical foam FDA label", "Zilxi FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"doxycycline-low-dose-rosacea": {
  "origin": "Doxycycline was developed by Pfizer in the 1960s as a semisynthetic tetracycline and approved in 1967. In the 1980s and 1990s Lorne Golub and colleagues at Stony Brook University found that tetracyclines can inhibit collagen-destroying enzymes called matrix metalloproteinases, even at doses too low to kill bacteria. This led CollaGenex Pharmaceuticals to develop low-dose doxycycline (Periostat, approved in 1998) for gum disease. CollaGenex then made a capsule with immediate and delayed-release beads for rosacea, which was approved as Oracea in 2006. It was the first oral drug approved in the US for rosacea and was designed to be anti-inflammatory without selecting for resistant bacteria.",
  "timeline": [["1967", "FDA approves doxycycline, a Pfizer semisynthetic tetracycline."], ["1980s", "Golub and colleagues show tetracyclines inhibit matrix metalloproteinases."], ["1998", "Low-dose doxycycline (Periostat) is approved for periodontitis."], ["2006", "FDA approves Oracea for inflammatory lesions of rosacea."]],
  "cell": "At antibacterial doses, doxycycline binds the 30S ribosomal subunit and blocks aminoacyl-tRNA binding, stopping bacterial protein synthesis. At the low, subantimicrobial levels used for rosacea, its main effects are anti-inflammatory. It inhibits matrix metalloproteinases (such as MMP-8 and MMP-9), partly by binding the zinc and calcium these enzymes need. Lower MMP activity reduces the activation of kallikrein-5, which normally cuts cathelicidin into the inflammatory peptide LL-37 that drives rosacea redness and pustules. Doxycycline also reduces neutrophil chemotaxis and reactive oxygen species. Blood levels stay below those needed to kill most bacteria.",
  "body": [["Facial skin", "Inflammatory papules and pustules of rosacea decrease over 4 to 12 weeks as MMP and cathelicidin activity fall."], ["Bacteria", "At this dose it does not significantly kill bacteria or select for resistance in studies, so it is not used for infections."], ["Esophagus", "Capsules lodged in the esophagus can cause irritation and ulcers, so it is taken upright with fluid."], ["Skin and sun", "Tetracyclines can cause photosensitivity and exaggerated sunburn."]],
  "journey": "The capsule contains immediate-release and delayed-release beads, so blood levels stay low and steady over the day. It is taken on an empty stomach, because calcium, iron, magnesium and antacids bind doxycycline in the gut and lower absorption. It reaches peak levels in a few hours, is highly protein bound, is only minimally metabolized, and leaves largely unchanged in feces and urine. Its half-life is long, close to a day.",
  "sidefx": "Doxycycline chelates calcium, which explains tooth discoloration and enamel defects in young children and fetal exposure late in pregnancy. Photosensitivity comes from the drug absorbing UV light in skin. Esophageal ulcers occur when a capsule dissolves against the esophageal wall. Like other tetracyclines it can rarely cause intracranial hypertension, C. difficile colitis and drug-induced lupus. Using it with isotretinoin raises the risk of intracranial hypertension. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Low-dose doxycycline was first approved for gum disease, where its ability to block collagen-eating enzymes helped protect the tissue around teeth.",
  "src": ["Oracea (doxycycline) FDA label", "Golub LM and colleagues, tetracycline and matrix metalloproteinase research, 1980s", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
,
"podofilox": {
  "origin": "Podofilox is purified podophyllotoxin, a lignan from the roots of the mayapple (Podophyllum peltatum) of eastern North America and its Himalayan relative. Native American healers used mayapple root as a strong purgative, and its crude resin, podophyllin, entered Western medicine in the 1800s. Podophyllotoxin was isolated from the resin in 1880. In 1942 the New Orleans physician I. W. Kaplan reported that podophyllin resin destroyed genital warts. The crude resin varied in strength and had to be applied by clinicians, so a purified, standardized form of the active compound was developed that patients could apply themselves at home. Semisynthetic podophyllotoxin derivatives also became the cancer drugs etoposide and teniposide.",
  "timeline": [["1880", "Podophyllotoxin is isolated from podophyllin resin."], ["1942", "Kaplan reports podophyllin resin for genital warts."], ["1983", "Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin derivative, is approved for cancer."]],
  "cell": "Podofilox binds tubulin, the protein building block of microtubules, at a site near the colchicine binding site. This stops tubulin from polymerizing into microtubules. Without microtubules, a dividing cell cannot build its mitotic spindle, so it arrests in metaphase (M phase). Wart tissue caused by human papillomavirus is full of rapidly dividing keratinocytes, so these cells are especially sensitive. Arrested cells die, and the wart tissue becomes necrotic and sloughs off over several days. The local inflammation that follows peaks a few days after application. The drug does not kill the virus itself, which is why warts can come back. Etoposide works differently, by inhibiting topoisomerase II.",
  "body": [["Wart tissue", "Visible genital and perianal warts become inflamed, turn white or dark, erode and fall off over days as the dividing cells die."], ["Surrounding skin", "Healthy skin touched by the drug can become red, sore and eroded because skin cells there also divide."], ["Rest of the body", "Systemic absorption is limited when applied to small areas as labeled, but large amounts could cause toxicity, which is why the treated area is limited."]],
  "journey": "Podofilox gel or solution is applied only to visible wart tissue and allowed to dry. Most drug stays in the wart. Some is absorbed, more so from large areas or broken skin. Data on absorbed podofilox are limited, with a short half-life of a few hours. Treatment runs in weekly cycles of a few days on and a few days off, giving inflamed tissue time to slough and heal.",
  "sidefx": "Because podofilox kills dividing cells, it injures nearby healthy skin, causing burning, pain, inflammation and erosion. These effects peak in the first week. Too much drug or treating too large an area increases absorption, and systemic podophyllin poisoning (seen with the crude resin) can cause nausea, nerve damage and bone marrow suppression, the same pathway behind microtubule toxicity. The diagnosis is confirmed before use because cancerous lesions can look like warts. There is no antidote; it is washed off and care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Mayapple was used by Native American peoples as a purgative and also as a poison, and its toxin turned out to be the parent of a widely used chemotherapy drug.",
  "src": ["Condylox (podofilox) FDA label", "Kaplan IW. New Orleans Med Surg J 1942", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"ivermectin-topical": {
  "origin": "Ivermectin traces back to a soil sample collected in the 1970s near a golf course in Shizuoka Prefecture, Japan, by Satoshi Omura of the Kitasato Institute. Omura cultured a new bacterium, Streptomyces avermitilis, and sent it to William C. Campbell's team at Merck in New Jersey. They found the broth killed parasitic worms in mice and isolated the avermectins. A slightly modified form, ivermectin, was launched for animals in 1981 and approved for people in 1987, when Merck began donating it to fight river blindness. Omura and Campbell shared the 2015 Nobel Prize. Ivermectin skin products came later: a lotion for head lice (Sklice, 2012) and a cream for rosacea (Soolantra, 2014), which targets Demodex mites on the face.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Satoshi Omura isolates Streptomyces avermitilis from Japanese soil; Merck scientists find antiparasitic avermectins."], ["1981", "Ivermectin is introduced for veterinary use."], ["1987", "Merck begins donating ivermectin (Mectizan) for river blindness."], ["2014", "FDA approves ivermectin cream (Soolantra) for inflammatory lesions of rosacea."], ["2015", "Omura and Campbell share the Nobel Prize for avermectin discovery."]],
  "cell": "Ivermectin binds glutamate-gated chloride channels found only in invertebrate nerve and muscle cells. Binding locks the channels open, so chloride flows in and the cells hyperpolarize. The mite or worm becomes paralyzed and dies. Mammals do not have glutamate-gated chloride channels, and ivermectin is kept out of the human brain by P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier, which protects human GABA-A channels from it. In rosacea, Demodex folliculorum mites are more numerous in affected skin, and their bodies and associated bacteria trigger inflammation. Killing the mites lowers this trigger. Ivermectin also has anti-inflammatory effects of its own, reducing cytokine release and neutrophil activity.",
  "body": [["Facial skin", "Inflammatory bumps and pimples of rosacea decrease by about four weeks and more by twelve as Demodex counts and inflammation fall."], ["Mites", "Demodex mites in hair follicles are paralyzed and die."], ["Redness", "Background redness may improve less, since the cream targets lesions rather than blood vessels."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are very low, so systemic effects are not expected with labeled use."]],
  "journey": "Ivermectin cream is applied once daily to the face. A small amount is absorbed, reaching low steady blood levels after about two weeks. The absorbed drug is very lipophilic and highly protein bound, is metabolized by CYP3A4 in the liver, and leaves mostly in the feces. Its plasma half-life after topical use is about six days. The lotion for lice is applied once to dry hair and rinsed off.",
  "sidefx": "The main side effects are skin burning and irritation where applied. A temporary flare of rosacea can occur early, possibly as mites die and release inflammatory material. Systemic toxicity, which with large oral overdoses involves the nervous system, is not expected because so little is absorbed and P-glycoprotein limits brain entry. There is no antidote; the cream is stopped.",
  "fun": "Demodex mites live in the hair follicles of most adults, and people with rosacea tend to carry more of them.",
  "src": ["Soolantra (ivermectin) cream FDA label", "Sklice (ivermectin) lotion FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2015 announcement"]
},
"brimonidine-topical": {
  "origin": "Brimonidine is an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist developed by Allergan for glaucoma. As eye drops (Alphagan), approved in 1996, it lowers eye pressure by reducing fluid production. Its ability to constrict small blood vessels made it a candidate for the persistent redness of rosacea, a problem that had no approved treatment. Galderma developed a facial gel, which the FDA approved as Mirvaso in 2013, the first drug approved specifically for the facial redness (erythema) of rosacea. It reduces redness for several hours but does not cure rosacea.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "Brimonidine eye drops (Alphagan) are approved for glaucoma."], ["2013", "FDA approves brimonidine gel (Mirvaso) for persistent facial erythema of rosacea."]],
  "cell": "Brimonidine is a highly selective agonist of alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. In the face, alpha-2 receptors are found on the smooth muscle of small superficial dermal arterioles and veins. Brimonidine binding activates the inhibitory G protein (Gi), which lowers cyclic AMP and favors smooth muscle contraction. The small vessels narrow, and less blood fills the skin, so redness fades within about 30 minutes. Because the drug acts directly on vessels, the effect lasts only as long as it stays on the receptors, about up to 12 hours. In the brain, alpha-2 stimulation lowers sympathetic outflow, which is why absorbed drug can lower blood pressure.",
  "body": [["Facial vessels", "Small vessels constrict, reducing persistent redness for several hours after application."], ["Redness rebound", "As the effect wears off, some people have redness worse than before, or flushing, from vessels re-dilating."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug can lower blood pressure and heart rate by reducing sympathetic outflow from the brainstem."], ["Brain", "Dizziness and sleepiness can occur, more so with accidental swallowing."]],
  "journey": "The gel is spread in a thin, even layer over the face once daily. Redness begins to fade within about 30 minutes and is lowest at about 3 to 6 hours. A small amount is absorbed into the blood, where it has a half-life of about three hours. The liver extensively metabolizes it, and metabolites are excreted by the kidneys. Accidental ingestion by young children can produce high blood levels.",
  "sidefx": "Rebound or worsened redness and flushing can happen as vasoconstriction fades and vessels re-open. Absorbed drug can lower blood pressure and heart rate, and the label warns about potentiating vascular insufficiency such as Raynaud phenomenon. Young children who swallowed the gel have had serious slowing of breathing, low heart rate, low blood pressure and coma, because alpha-2 stimulation in the brain is strong. Allergic contact dermatitis is possible. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "The same molecule that whitens rosacea redness also lightens bloodshot eyes as a low-strength eye drop sold over the counter.",
  "src": ["Mirvaso (brimonidine) gel FDA label", "Alphagan P FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"oxymetazoline-topical": {
  "origin": "Oxymetazoline is an imidazoline vasoconstrictor developed at the German company E. Merck in the early 1960s and best known as a nasal decongestant spray, sold in the US as Afrin. Like brimonidine, its ability to constrict small blood vessels made it a candidate for the persistent redness of rosacea. Allergan developed a facial cream, approved by the FDA as Rhofade in January 2017. It became the second approved drug for rosacea redness. A related eye drop (Upneeq), approved in 2020, uses oxymetazoline to lift a drooping eyelid by tightening a small eyelid muscle.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Oxymetazoline is developed in Germany as a vasoconstrictor."], ["2017", "FDA approves oxymetazoline cream (Rhofade) for persistent facial erythema of rosacea."], ["2020", "Oxymetazoline eye drops (Upneeq) are approved for acquired eyelid ptosis."]],
  "cell": "Oxymetazoline is an agonist at alpha-1A adrenergic receptors, with partial alpha-2 activity. Alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle signal through the Gq protein, which activates phospholipase C, makes IP3 and raises intracellular calcium. Calcium drives smooth muscle contraction, so small dermal arterioles in the face narrow and less blood fills the skin. Redness fades within about an hour and stays reduced for much of the day. Its alpha-2 action adds to vasoconstriction. The same mechanism shrinks swollen nasal blood vessels when used as a spray.",
  "body": [["Facial vessels", "Constriction of superficial dermal vessels reduces persistent redness, starting within about an hour and lasting up to about 12 hours."], ["Facial skin", "Application site dermatitis and worsening of inflammatory bumps can occur in some people."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug could raise blood pressure in people with heart disease and can worsen vascular insufficiency."], ["Eyes", "If it reaches the eye in a person with narrow angles, it can dilate the pupil and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."]],
  "journey": "The cream is applied once daily in a thin layer over the face, avoiding eyes and lips, and hands are washed immediately afterward. A small amount is absorbed into the blood. Oxymetazoline undergoes little metabolism in the liver and is excreted by the kidneys and feces. The effect begins within about an hour, peaks in 3 to 6 hours and lasts up to about 12 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from alpha-adrenergic stimulation. Absorbed drug can raise blood pressure and narrow vessels elsewhere, which matters in heart disease and Raynaud phenomenon. Alpha stimulation can dilate the pupil and precipitate angle-closure glaucoma. Local dermatitis and worsening papules can occur. Using it with oxymetazoline nasal sprays or eye drops adds exposure. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Oxymetazoline nasal spray is the classic cause of rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa) when used for more than a few days, a reminder of how blood vessels can adapt to repeated vasoconstriction.",
  "src": ["Rhofade (oxymetazoline) cream FDA label", "Upneeq FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"hydroquinone": {
  "origin": "Hydroquinone is a simple phenol long used in photography as a film developer. Its ability to lighten skin was noticed in the mid-20th century, and in 1965 dermatologists Kenneth Arndt and Thomas Fitzpatrick published a study of topical hydroquinone as a depigmenting agent. In 1975 Albert Kligman and Willis described a combination of hydroquinone, tretinoin and a steroid for melasma, which inspired Tri-Luma, an FDA-approved triple combination cream from 2002. Single-ingredient prescription hydroquinone creams, however, have been marketed in the US without FDA approval. Over-the-counter hydroquinone products were removed from legal sale in the US after the 2020 OTC monograph reform under the CARES Act.",
  "timeline": [["1965", "Arndt and Fitzpatrick report topical hydroquinone as a depigmenting agent."], ["1975", "Kligman and Willis describe a hydroquinone, tretinoin and steroid formula for melasma."], ["2002", "FDA approves Tri-Luma, a hydroquinone, tretinoin and fluocinolone combination."], ["2020", "OTC hydroquinone products lose legal US marketing status under OTC monograph reform."]],
  "cell": "Melanin is made in melanocytes inside organelles called melanosomes. The key enzyme is tyrosinase, a copper-containing enzyme that oxidizes tyrosine to DOPA and DOPA to dopaquinone, the starting point for melanin. Hydroquinone is structurally similar to tyrosine and acts as an alternative substrate and inhibitor of tyrosinase, so less dopaquinone and less melanin are made. Hydroquinone is also oxidized into reactive quinones and oxygen radicals that damage melanosome membranes and melanocytes, adding to its effect. Existing pigment is shed slowly as skin cells turn over, which is why lightening takes weeks to months.",
  "body": [["Pigmented skin", "Dark patches of melasma, age spots and post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation gradually lighten as new melanin production falls."], ["Sun-exposed skin", "UV light strongly stimulates melanocytes, so without sunscreen the pigment returns or worsens."], ["Skin (long term)", "Prolonged use, especially at high strength, can cause exogenous ochronosis, a blue-black darkening from pigment deposits in the dermis."], ["Skin surface", "Redness, stinging and contact dermatitis can occur."]],
  "journey": "Hydroquinone cream is applied only to the dark areas. Some drug is absorbed through the skin and conjugated in the liver, then excreted by the kidneys as sulfate and glucuronide conjugates. Systemic exposure at topical use is not considered clinically relevant. Its effect depends on continued use and sun protection; lightening is usually seen over two to three months.",
  "sidefx": "Irritation and contact dermatitis come from the reactive, oxidizing nature of hydroquinone and its quinones. Exogenous ochronosis, a paradoxical darkening, is thought to arise when a related enzyme, homogentisic acid oxidase, is inhibited in the skin, leading to deposits of pigment-like polymers in the dermis. Peroxide products on the same area can cause temporary dark staining. Some products contain sulfites. There is no antidote; it is stopped, and ochronosis can be slow to fade.",
  "fun": "Hydroquinone was a standard ingredient in black-and-white photo developers because it reduces silver salts to metallic silver, the same oxidizing and reducing chemistry it shows in skin.",
  "src": ["Tri-Luma FDA label", "Arndt KA, Fitzpatrick TB. JAMA 1965", "FDA notice on OTC hydroquinone after the CARES Act"]
},
"capsaicin-topical": {
  "origin": "Capsaicin is the compound that makes chili peppers hot. Peppers of the genus Capsicum were domesticated in the Americas thousands of years ago and spread worldwide after the 1500s. An impure extract was obtained in 1816 by the German pharmacist Christian Friedrich Bucholz, and in 1876 the English chemist John Clough Thresh isolated it in purer form and named it capsaicin. Low-strength creams were used for pain for decades. The molecule's target, the TRPV1 receptor, was cloned in 1997 by David Julius's lab at UCSF, work that shared the 2021 Nobel Prize. A high-strength patch, Qutenza, was approved by the FDA in 2009 for postherpetic neuralgia and in 2020 for painful diabetic neuropathy of the feet.",
  "timeline": [["1816", "Bucholz obtains an impure pungent extract from peppers."], ["1876", "Thresh isolates and names capsaicin."], ["1997", "Julius's lab clones TRPV1, the capsaicin receptor."], ["2009", "FDA approves the high-strength capsaicin patch (Qutenza) for postherpetic neuralgia."], ["2021", "Julius shares the Nobel Prize for discovering temperature and touch receptors."]],
  "cell": "Capsaicin binds TRPV1 (transient receptor potential vanilloid 1), a ligand-gated cation channel on the endings of pain-sensing C fibers and some A-delta fibers. TRPV1 normally opens with heat above about 43 C and with acid. Capsaicin opens it, letting sodium and calcium flood in, so the nerve fires and releases substance P and CGRP; this is the burning feeling. With high or repeated exposure, the large calcium influx overwhelms the nerve ending. Mitochondria fail, calcium-dependent enzymes break down the cytoskeleton, and the fine nerve terminals in the epidermis retract. This process, called defunctionalization, leaves the area less able to send pain signals for weeks to months until fibers regrow.",
  "body": [["Skin nerves", "Pain fiber endings in the treated area first fire intensely, then retract, reducing neuropathic pain for up to about three months after one patch."], ["Skin", "Redness and burning appear during application from substance P and CGRP release, which dilate vessels."], ["Heart and vessels", "Pain during the procedure can transiently raise blood pressure."], ["Airway and eyes", "Airborne capsaicin irritates eyes, nose and throat, causing coughing and sneezing."]],
  "journey": "The high-strength patch is applied in a clinic for 30 to 60 minutes, then removed and the area cleansed with a special gel. Capsaicin penetrates skin to the nerve endings. Systemic levels are low and short-lived, with a half-life of under two hours, and absorbed drug is metabolized by liver CYP enzymes. Low-strength creams are rubbed in several times daily and work gradually as nerve endings are repeatedly desensitized.",
  "sidefx": "Burning pain during and after application comes directly from TRPV1 activation. Redness and swelling follow neuropeptide release. Pain can transiently raise blood pressure. Reduced sensation in the treated area follows nerve terminal retraction. Contact with eyes and mucous membranes causes severe burning, and inhaled particles cause coughing. Cooling and the cleansing gel reduce the burn. There is no antidote; capsaicin is lipophilic, so oil-based cleansers remove it better than water.",
  "fun": "Birds lack a capsaicin-sensitive TRPV1, so they eat chili peppers without feeling the burn and spread the seeds.",
  "src": ["Qutenza (capsaicin) FDA label", "Caterina MJ, Julius D et al. Nature 1997", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2021 announcement"]
},
"pramoxine": {
  "origin": "Pramoxine (also called pramocaine) is a synthetic local anesthetic introduced in the 1950s. Most local anesthetics belong to two chemical families, esters such as benzocaine and amides such as lidocaine. Pramoxine is a morpholine ether and belongs to neither, which turned out to be useful: people allergic to ester or amide anesthetics usually do not react to it. It is too irritating for injection and is used only on skin and mucous membranes. Today it is a common over-the-counter ingredient in anti-itch lotions, hemorrhoid creams and combinations with hydrocortisone or calamine.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Pramoxine is introduced as a topical anesthetic distinct from ester and amide types."]],
  "cell": "Pramoxine reversibly blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the membranes of sensory nerve endings in the skin. When an itch or pain stimulus arrives, these channels normally open and let sodium rush in to fire an action potential. Pramoxine binds inside the channel and stabilizes it in an inactive state, so the nerve cannot depolarize enough to fire and the signal never reaches the spinal cord. Both itch fibers and pain fibers are small and lightly myelinated or unmyelinated, so they are blocked easily. Its ether link and morpholine ring make it chemically different from ester and amide anesthetics, which explains low cross-allergy.",
  "body": [["Skin nerves", "Itch and pain from bites, minor burns, rashes and poison ivy are dulled within minutes."], ["Anorectal area", "In hemorrhoid products it numbs the sensitive anal skin, easing itching and burning."], ["Rest of the body", "Absorption is negligible, so systemic effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "Pramoxine is applied as a lotion, cream, gel or foam directly on the itchy or painful area. It acts within minutes on nerve endings just under the surface and lasts several hours. Very little is absorbed, and systemic metabolism is not well characterized. Its effect wears off as it diffuses away from the nerve endings and is washed off.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are uncommon and local: stinging, dryness or allergic contact dermatitis. Because it does not reach meaningful blood levels, the CNS and heart toxicity seen with large absorbed amounts of lidocaine or benzocaine methemoglobinemia are not expected. Eye contact is avoided because it irritates. There is no antidote; the product is washed off.",
  "fun": "Pramoxine is often chosen as a numbing agent for people who report allergy to the 'caine' anesthetics, because its chemical family is different.",
  "src": ["OTC external analgesic monograph (21 CFR 348)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"zinc-oxide": {
  "origin": "Zinc oxide is one of the oldest skin remedies. Ancient Greek, Roman and Indian medical writers described zinc-containing ores and ash used in ointments for wounds and eye problems. It became a standard ingredient in European pharmacies, appearing in pastes such as Lassar's paste in the 1800s. In the 20th century it became the main ingredient of diaper rash ointments and a mineral sunscreen. In 2019 the FDA proposed that only two sunscreen active ingredients, zinc oxide and titanium dioxide, had enough data to be considered generally recognized as safe and effective.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Zinc-containing preparations are used in ointments for wounds and skin by Greek, Roman and Indian healers."], ["1800s", "Zinc oxide pastes become standard in European dermatology."], ["2019", "FDA proposes zinc oxide and titanium dioxide as the sunscreen actives generally recognized as safe and effective."]],
  "cell": "Zinc oxide works mostly physically rather than through a receptor. It is an insoluble white powder that, in a paste or ointment, forms a coating over the skin. This barrier keeps urine, stool, enzymes and moisture away from irritated skin, so the outer layer can repair itself. Zinc oxide is mildly astringent, tightening surface proteins and drying weeping areas, and has mild antiseptic action. Zinc ions released in small amounts may support wound healing because many skin enzymes need zinc. As a sunscreen, zinc oxide particles scatter and absorb ultraviolet light across both UVB and UVA ranges, protecting skin cells from DNA damage.",
  "body": [["Diaper area", "A thick layer shields irritated skin from urine and stool, letting diaper rash heal."], ["Chafed skin", "The barrier reduces friction and moisture, helping raw skin recover."], ["Sun protection", "Particles reflect, scatter and absorb UVA and UVB, giving broad-spectrum protection."], ["Rest of the body", "Zinc oxide is not meaningfully absorbed through intact skin."]],
  "journey": "Zinc oxide sits on top of the skin. It is not absorbed in meaningful amounts through intact skin, so there is no metabolism or excretion to speak of. It is removed by washing, which is why it is reapplied with diaper changes or after swimming. Its effect begins immediately once the barrier forms.",
  "sidefx": "Topical zinc oxide is very safe. Side effects are rare, such as irritation or allergy to other ingredients in the base. A thick paste can trap bacteria or yeast if infected skin is not cleaned first. Large amounts swallowed can upset the stomach. Inhaling zinc oxide fumes, as in welding galvanized metal, causes metal fume fever, which is unrelated to skin use. There is no antidote needed.",
  "fun": "Calamine lotion is mostly zinc oxide with a little iron oxide added, which gives it its pink color.",
  "src": ["OTC skin protectant monograph (21 CFR 347)", "FDA proposed rule on sunscreen active ingredients, 2019"]
},
"lidocaine-topical": {
  "origin": "Lidocaine was synthesized in 1943 by Swedish chemists Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist at Stockholm University. They were studying compounds related to gramine, a plant alkaloid that numbed the tongue. Lundqvist tested the new compound on himself. It proved more reliable and less allergenic than older ester anesthetics like procaine. The Swedish company Astra marketed it as Xylocaine in 1948, and it became the most widely used local anesthetic in the world. Topical forms such as ointment, jelly and viscous solution numb skin and mucous membranes, and lidocaine is also given by injection and IV as an antiarrhythmic.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine in Stockholm."], ["1948", "Astra launches lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["1960s", "Lidocaine becomes standard IV treatment for ventricular arrhythmias."]],
  "cell": "Lidocaine is an amide local anesthetic. In tissue it exists as both an uncharged base and a charged form. The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane. Inside, it picks up a proton and the charged form binds a site inside the pore of voltage-gated sodium channels (such as Nav1.7 and Nav1.8 on pain fibers). It binds best to open and inactivated channels, so nerves that fire often are blocked first (use-dependent block). With sodium channels blocked, the nerve cannot reach threshold and action potentials stop, so pain signals do not travel. Small, unmyelinated pain and temperature fibers block before larger touch and motor fibers. Inflamed, acidic tissue holds more drug in the charged form outside the nerve, which slows onset.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Minor burns, scrapes and insect bites become numb within minutes."], ["Mouth and throat", "Mucosa numbs in 2 to 5 minutes, easing pain from sores or procedures; swallowing reflexes are also blunted, raising aspiration risk."], ["Airway devices", "Lidocaine jelly lubricates and numbs during tube or catheter placement."], ["Brain", "Too much absorbed lidocaine causes numb lips, ringing ears, agitation and seizures, then CNS depression."], ["Heart", "High levels slow conduction and can cause low blood pressure, slow heart rate and arrest."]],
  "journey": "Topical lidocaine is absorbed little through intact skin but readily through mucous membranes and broken skin. Absorbed drug binds alpha-1 acid glycoprotein and is metabolized in the liver by CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 to the active metabolites MEGX and GX, which are excreted by the kidneys. The systemic half-life is about one and a half to two hours. Numbness lasts about 30 to 60 minutes on mucosa and longer on skin.",
  "sidefx": "Local anesthetic systemic toxicity occurs when too much is absorbed, such as with large mucosal areas or several lidocaine products together. Sodium channel block in the brain first removes inhibitory neurons (causing agitation and seizures), then depresses the whole CNS. In the heart, sodium channel block slows conduction and depresses contraction. Intravenous lipid emulsion is used as a rescue therapy for severe toxicity. Methemoglobinemia can occur. Numbing the throat can cause aspiration and bite injuries.",
  "fun": "Bengt Lundqvist tested lidocaine on himself, including nerve block injections, before it was studied in patients.",
  "src": ["Xylocaine (lidocaine) ointment FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"diphenhydramine-topical": {
  "origin": "Diphenhydramine was discovered in 1943 by George Rieveschl, a chemistry professor at the University of Cincinnati, while he was searching for drugs to relax muscle spasms. A student working with him helped make the compound, and it proved to be a strong antihistamine. Parke-Davis developed it as Benadryl, and in 1946 it became the first prescription antihistamine approved by the FDA. Its sedating effect later made it a sleep aid as well. Topical diphenhydramine creams and sprays, often with zinc acetate, are sold over the counter for insect bites and poison ivy itch.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "George Rieveschl's team at the University of Cincinnati discovers diphenhydramine."], ["1946", "FDA approves Benadryl, the first prescription antihistamine in the US."]],
  "cell": "Histamine released from mast cells in skin binds H1 receptors on sensory nerve endings and small blood vessels. On nerves this causes itch; on vessels it causes dilation and leak, making wheals. Diphenhydramine is an inverse agonist at H1 receptors: it binds and stabilizes the receptor in its inactive state, so histamine signaling through the Gq pathway is blocked. It also blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, giving a local anesthetic effect that dulls itch and pain directly. In combination products, zinc acetate is an astringent that dries oozing rashes. As a first-generation antihistamine it also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, which matters if much is absorbed.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Itch and pain from insect bites, minor burns and plant rashes decrease within minutes as H1 signaling and nerve firing are blocked."], ["Weeping rashes", "Zinc acetate in some products dries oozing blisters."], ["Brain", "If absorbed in large amounts it crosses into the brain, causing drowsiness or, in children, confusion and agitation."], ["Autonomic system", "Absorbed drug blocks muscarinic receptors, causing dry mouth, flushing, fast heart rate and urinary retention."]],
  "journey": "Applied to intact skin, only a small amount is absorbed. Absorption rises sharply when it is used on large areas, broken skin, or blistering rashes like chickenpox. Absorbed diphenhydramine is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP2D6 and excreted in urine, with a half-life of several hours. Topical effects start within minutes and last several hours.",
  "sidefx": "Repeated use can cause allergic contact dermatitis, because the drug can sensitize the skin. When large amounts are absorbed, especially in children or when combined with oral diphenhydramine, anticholinergic toxicity can occur: hot dry skin, flushing, dilated pupils, fast heart rate, urinary retention, confusion and hallucinations. This follows from muscarinic receptor blockade in the brain and body. In selected severe cases physostigmine can reverse anticholinergic delirium; otherwise care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Rieveschl was originally looking for a muscle relaxant, and diphenhydramine's reliable drowsiness later made it the active ingredient in many over-the-counter sleep aids.",
  "src": ["Benadryl Itch Stopping Cream Drug Facts label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"abametapir": {
  "origin": "Abametapir was developed by the Australian company Hatchtech, which focused on a weak point of head lice treatment: most older products kill live lice but not all their eggs, so treatment often had to be repeated and nits combed out. Hatchtech studied metalloproteinase enzymes that louse eggs need to develop and hatch, and found that abametapir, a simple bipyridine metal chelator, blocks them. The FDA approved it as Xeglyze lotion in July 2020 for head lice in people 6 months and older, given as a single application.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Hatchtech in Australia develops abametapir as a louse egg metalloproteinase inhibitor."], ["2020", "FDA approves abametapir lotion (Xeglyze) for head lice."]],
  "cell": "Abametapir (5,5'-dimethyl-2,2'-bipyridine) is a metal chelator. Louse eggs depend on metalloproteinases, enzymes that need metal ions such as zinc to work, for development of the embryo, hatching and early survival. Abametapir binds these metal ions and blocks the enzymes, so eggs fail to develop or hatch. It is also toxic to live lice through the same enzyme inhibition. Because it acts on both lice and eggs (lousicidal and ovicidal), a single treatment is usually enough, and nit combing or a second application is not required in most cases.",
  "body": [["Scalp and hair", "Lice and unhatched eggs die after a single application of about ten minutes, then the lotion is rinsed out."], ["Scalp skin", "Redness, irritation and a burning feeling can occur."], ["Liver enzymes", "Absorbed abametapir inhibits CYP3A4, CYP2B6 and CYP1A2 for about two weeks, raising levels of some other drugs."], ["Infants", "The lotion contains benzyl alcohol, which can be dangerous for infants under 6 months."]],
  "journey": "The lotion is applied to dry hair and scalp, left on for a short time and rinsed out. Some drug is absorbed, peaking in the blood within hours. The liver metabolizes it by CYP1A2 to a carboxyl metabolite, which lasts longer in the blood than the parent drug. Abametapir has a half-life of under a day, and its metabolites are excreted mostly in urine. The enzyme inhibition explains why the label advises avoiding certain other drugs for two weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Local effects include scalp redness, irritation, rash and eye irritation, because the lotion contacts skin and can drip into eyes. The benzyl alcohol vehicle can cause gasping syndrome in very young infants, where the immature liver cannot clear benzyl alcohol and acid builds up. Swallowing the lotion is dangerous, so children are supervised during use. Drug interactions follow CYP inhibition. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Abametapir was chosen because louse eggs, unlike many lice treatments' targets, rely on metal-dependent enzymes to break out of their shells.",
  "src": ["Xeglyze (abametapir) lotion FDA label", "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention head lice treatment guidance"]
},
"tirbanibulin": {
  "origin": "Tirbanibulin was discovered by Kinex Pharmaceuticals (later Athenex) in Buffalo, New York, and was first studied as an oral cancer drug. It disrupts microtubules and interferes with Src kinase signaling, two features that slow dividing cells. Researchers later tested it as an ointment for actinic keratoses, the rough precancerous patches caused by sun damage. The Spanish company Almirall licensed it for dermatology. The FDA approved it as Klisyri in December 2020, notable for a short five-day treatment course compared with weeks for older field treatments such as 5-fluorouracil or imiquimod.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Kinex Pharmaceuticals develops tirbanibulin as an anticancer microtubule and Src signaling inhibitor."], ["2020", "FDA approves tirbanibulin ointment (Klisyri) for actinic keratoses of the face and scalp."]],
  "cell": "Tirbanibulin binds tubulin at the colchicine site and blocks tubulin polymerization, so microtubules break down. Without microtubules, dividing cells cannot form a mitotic spindle and arrest in the G2/M phase of the cell cycle. Arrested cells undergo apoptosis. Atypical keratinocytes in actinic keratoses divide much faster than normal skin cells, so they are hit hardest. Tirbanibulin also interferes with Src tyrosine kinase signaling, which supports cell growth and migration. The result is selective loss of abnormal cells in the treated field, followed by healing.",
  "body": [["Sun-damaged skin", "Actinic keratoses in the treated field are cleared as abnormal dividing cells die."], ["Skin surface", "Redness, flaking, crusting and swelling appear, peaking around day 8, and usually fade within weeks."], ["Eyes", "Ointment transferred near the eyes can cause irritation."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are low after use on a limited area."]],
  "journey": "Tirbanibulin ointment is applied once daily for five days to a defined area on the face or balding scalp. The area is not washed or touched for about eight hours after application. Only low levels reach the blood. The absorbed drug is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and, to a lesser degree, CYP2C8, with a short half-life. Clearance of lesions is assessed about eight weeks after the course.",
  "sidefx": "The local skin reactions, including redness, scaling, crusting, swelling, erosion and blisters, are directly linked to the drug killing dividing cells in the treated area and the inflammation that follows. They are typically milder and shorter than with 5-fluorouracil because the course is so brief. Eye irritation happens if ointment is spread to the eyelids. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tirbanibulin began life as an oral cancer drug and found its approval instead as a five-day ointment for sun damage.",
  "src": ["Klisyri (tirbanibulin) ointment FDA label", "Blauvelt A et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (phase 3 trials)"]
},
"berdazimer": {
  "origin": "Berdazimer sodium was developed by Novan, a North Carolina company built on nitric oxide chemistry research at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. Nitric oxide is a gas the body makes to fight microbes and control blood vessels; its role as a signal molecule earned the 1998 Nobel Prize. The challenge was delivering a short-lived gas into skin. Novan attached nitric oxide-releasing groups to a large silica-based polymer that releases the gas only when mixed with a hydrogel. The FDA approved it as Zelsuvmi in January 2024 for molluscum contagiosum, a contagious poxvirus skin infection common in children, as the first prescription treatment that can be applied at home.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Nobel Prize awarded for the discovery of nitric oxide as a signaling molecule."], ["2000s", "Novan develops nitric oxide-releasing polymers based on university research in North Carolina."], ["2024", "FDA approves berdazimer gel (Zelsuvmi) for molluscum contagiosum."]],
  "cell": "Berdazimer sodium is a large polysiloxane molecule carrying N-diazeniumdiolate groups. On its own it is stable. When it is mixed with the acidic hydrogel from the second tube, the diazeniumdiolate groups are protonated and release nitric oxide gas directly onto molluscum bumps. Nitric oxide is thought to work against the molluscum contagiosum poxvirus by damaging viral proteins and DNA through reactive nitrogen species, and by stimulating local immune responses that help clear infected keratinocytes. The FDA label states the exact mechanism is not known. Nitric oxide lasts only seconds, so its action stays local.",
  "body": [["Molluscum bumps", "Lesions become inflamed and then clear over weeks as infected cells are damaged and the immune system responds."], ["Skin surface", "Pain, redness, itching, peeling and swelling at the application site are common."], ["Rest of the body", "Nitric oxide is quickly oxidized to nitrite and nitrate, so systemic exposure is negligible."]],
  "journey": "Just before each use, equal amounts from two tubes are mixed and applied to each bump, then allowed to dry. Nitric oxide is released at the skin surface and lasts only seconds before being converted to nitrite and nitrate. These are naturally present in the body and are excreted by the kidneys. Washing, bathing and swimming are avoided for an hour so the gel stays in place. Clearance is usually assessed at about 12 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are local: pain, redness, itching, peeling, dermatitis, swelling and erosion at the treated bumps. They reflect nitric oxide's reactive, inflammatory effects on skin and the immune response against infected cells. Allergic contact dermatitis has been reported. Because nitric oxide is so short-lived, systemic effects like low blood pressure are not expected. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Nitric oxide is the same molecule released by nitroglycerin to relax blood vessels, but in berdazimer it is used as a local antiviral weapon.",
  "src": ["Zelsuvmi (berdazimer) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1998 announcement"]
},
"methoxsalen": {
  "origin": "Methoxsalen (8-methoxypsoralen) comes from plants. Healers in Egypt and India used extracts of plants such as Ammi majus (bishop's weed) with sunlight to repigment vitiligo for centuries. In the late 1940s chemists in Egypt isolated 8-methoxypsoralen from Ammi majus, and the Cairo dermatologist Abdel Monem El Mofty used it with sunlight to treat vitiligo. In 1974 John Parrish, Thomas Fitzpatrick and colleagues at Harvard reported in the New England Journal of Medicine that oral methoxsalen plus artificial UVA light (PUVA) cleared severe psoriasis. In 1987 Richard Edelson and colleagues reported photopheresis, in which a patient's blood cells are treated with methoxsalen and UVA outside the body, for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Plant extracts containing psoralens are used with sunlight for vitiligo in Egypt and India."], ["1940s", "8-methoxypsoralen is isolated from Ammi majus in Egypt and used by El Mofty for vitiligo."], ["1974", "Parrish, Fitzpatrick and colleagues report oral methoxsalen plus UVA (PUVA) for psoriasis."], ["1987", "Edelson and colleagues report extracorporeal photopheresis for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Methoxsalen is a flat furocoumarin that slips between DNA base pairs (intercalation). On its own it does little. When UVA light (320 to 400 nm) hits it, the drug becomes reactive and forms covalent bonds with pyrimidine bases, mainly thymine. A first photon makes a monoadduct with one strand; a second photon can link it to the other strand, forming interstrand cross-links. Cross-linked DNA cannot be copied, so DNA synthesis and division of rapidly growing keratinocytes stop. PUVA also triggers apoptosis of skin T lymphocytes, which drive psoriasis and cutaneous lymphoma, and stimulates melanocytes to make more melanin.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Psoriasis plaques clear as hyperproliferating keratinocytes stop dividing and skin T cells die."], ["Pigment", "Melanocytes make more melanin, causing tanning and repigmentation in vitiligo."], ["Eyes", "The drug binds to the lens when exposed to UV light, which can cause cataracts; UVA-blocking glasses are worn for a day after each dose."], ["Long-term skin", "Repeated PUVA ages skin and raises the risk of skin cancers, including squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma."], ["Gut and liver", "Nausea is common, and liver injury is reported rarely."]],
  "journey": "Methoxsalen is taken by mouth with low-fat food or milk before a UVA session. Absorption varies between products; the soft gelatin capsule is absorbed faster and more completely, so timing and UVA dosimetry are product-specific. Peak photosensitivity comes about one and a half to two hours after the soft capsule. It is highly bound to albumin, extensively metabolized by the liver and excreted in urine. Skin sensitivity lasts about eight hours, but lens binding can last up to a day. For photopheresis, methoxsalen is added to blood outside the body.",
  "sidefx": "Severe burns occur when the drug is activated by too much UVA or sunlight, because DNA damage and inflammation spread to normal skin. Lens binding and UV activation can cause cataracts. Cumulative DNA damage explains premature aging and skin cancers, which is why the label carries a boxed warning and restricts use to experienced physicians. Other photosensitizing drugs and foods such as limes, figs and celery add to phototoxicity. There is no antidote; care is supportive with sun avoidance.",
  "fun": "Lime juice on the skin in the sun can cause blistering burns called phytophotodermatitis, from psoralens similar to methoxsalen.",
  "src": ["Oxsoralen-Ultra (methoxsalen) FDA label", "Parrish JA et al. N Engl J Med 1974", "Edelson R et al. N Engl J Med 1987"]
},
"afamelanotide": {
  "origin": "Afamelanotide began in the 1980s at the University of Arizona, where researchers Victor Hruby and Mac Hadley modified alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH), the body's natural tanning signal. Swapping two amino acids produced a peptide that was far more potent and longer lasting. Their idea was a tan without sun that might protect fair skin from skin cancer, and the compound was first called Melanotan-1. The Australian company Clinuvel Pharmaceuticals developed it for erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP), a rare genetic disease in which light causes severe burning pain. It was approved in Europe in 2014 and by the FDA in 2019 as Scenesse, a small implant under the skin. A different peptide, melanotan II, is sold illegally online as a tanning product and is not FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Hruby and Hadley at the University of Arizona develop a potent alpha-MSH analog (Melanotan-1)."], ["2014", "Afamelanotide (Scenesse) is approved in the European Union for EPP."], ["2019", "FDA approves afamelanotide implant for adults with EPP."]],
  "cell": "Afamelanotide is a 13-amino-acid analog of alpha-MSH ([Nle4, D-Phe7]-alpha-MSH). The changes make it resistant to enzyme breakdown and bind the melanocortin 1 receptor (MC1R) more strongly than the natural hormone. MC1R on melanocytes is a Gs-coupled receptor; activation raises cyclic AMP, activates protein kinase A and the transcription factor MITF, and increases tyrosinase and other melanin-making enzymes. Melanocytes shift toward eumelanin, the brown-black pigment. Eumelanin absorbs and scatters visible light and quenches reactive oxygen species. In EPP, protoporphyrin IX builds up in skin and is excited by visible light, creating damaging oxygen radicals; more eumelanin reduces that phototoxic reaction.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Skin darkens over days to weeks as melanocytes make more eumelanin, giving light protection without sun exposure."], ["Pain in light", "People with EPP can spend more time in light before the burning pain begins."], ["Moles and freckles", "Existing nevi and freckles darken, so full-body skin exams are done twice yearly."], ["Gut and head", "Nausea and headache are common, likely from melanocortin receptor effects outside the skin."]],
  "journey": "The rice-grain-sized implant is placed under the skin, usually above the hip, by a trained clinician. It dissolves and releases afamelanotide over several days, with most drug released early. The peptide has a short plasma half-life and is broken down by peptide hydrolysis like other small proteins. Skin pigmentation and protection last about two months per implant, so implants are repeated.",
  "sidefx": "Darkening of skin, moles and freckles follows directly from MC1R activation in melanocytes, which is why skin checks are part of the label. Nausea, headache and flushing may reflect activation of other melanocortin receptors. Implant site reactions occur. Hypersensitivity reactions are possible, so patients are observed after insertion. Unapproved melanotan products carry unknown risks. There is no antidote; the effect fades as the implant is used up.",
  "fun": "In EPP the pain is triggered mainly by visible light, not just UV, so ordinary sunscreens help little; eumelanin helps because it absorbs visible light as well.",
  "src": ["Scenesse (afamelanotide) implant FDA label", "Langendonk JG et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (EPP trials)"]
}
,
"tralokinumab": {
  "origin": "Tralokinumab is a fully human antibody first developed by Cambridge Antibody Technology in the UK, a company later absorbed into AstraZeneca's MedImmune unit. It was first tested in asthma, where interleukin-13 seemed a promising target, but large asthma trials did not show enough benefit. Meanwhile, research showed IL-13 is the main type 2 cytokine expressed in the skin of people with atopic dermatitis. LEO Pharma licensed the antibody in 2016 and developed it for eczema. It was approved in Europe in 2021 (Adtralza) and by the FDA in December 2021 as Adbry, the first approved biologic that targets IL-13 alone. Use was extended to adolescents in 2023.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Cambridge Antibody Technology and MedImmune develop tralokinumab, first studied for asthma."], ["2016", "LEO Pharma licenses tralokinumab for skin disease."], ["2021", "FDA approves tralokinumab (Adbry) for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults."], ["2023", "Approval is extended to patients 12 years and older."]],
  "cell": "Atopic dermatitis is driven by type 2 inflammation, in which Th2 cells, ILC2 cells and others release IL-4 and IL-13. In skin, IL-13 is especially prominent. IL-13 normally binds IL-13 receptor alpha-1, which then pairs with IL-4 receptor alpha to form a signaling complex that activates JAK1 and TYK2 and the transcription factor STAT6. STAT6 switches on genes that weaken the skin barrier (lowering filaggrin and loricrin), attract eosinophils and other immune cells via chemokines, increase IgE class switching and sensitize itch nerves. Tralokinumab is an IgG4 antibody that binds IL-13 at the site that contacts IL-13 receptor alpha-1, so IL-13 cannot bind either IL-13 receptor alpha-1 or the decoy receptor IL-13 receptor alpha-2. The STAT6 signal does not start.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Inflammation, redness and thickening decrease, and the barrier recovers over weeks to months."], ["Itch", "Less IL-13 signaling to sensory nerves and immune cells reduces itch."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis and keratitis can occur, possibly because IL-13 helps maintain goblet cells that make the tear film's mucus layer."], ["Immune defense", "IL-13 helps fight intestinal worms, so existing helminth infections are treated before starting."]],
  "journey": "Tralokinumab is injected under the skin. As a large antibody, it is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system, reaching peak levels about five to eight days later. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Like other antibodies, it is not broken down by the liver's CYP enzymes but by the body's general protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about three weeks, which allows dosing every two weeks or less often.",
  "sidefx": "Conjunctivitis is the most distinctive side effect of IL-13 blockade, thought to relate to reduced goblet cell mucus in the eye surface. Injection site reactions occur. Hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, is possible with any antibody. Because IL-13 helps expel parasitic worms, helminth infections can worsen. Live vaccines are avoided during treatment. Cytokine changes can alter CYP enzyme levels, affecting narrow-margin drugs. There is no antidote; the antibody is cleared over weeks.",
  "fun": "Tralokinumab failed in asthma before succeeding in eczema, a reminder that the same cytokine can play different roles in different organs.",
  "src": ["Adbry (tralokinumab) FDA label", "Wollenberg A et al. Br J Dermatol 2021 (ECZTRA trials)"]
},
"lebrikizumab": {
  "origin": "Lebrikizumab was created at Genentech as a humanized antibody against interleukin-13 and was first studied in asthma, where results were mixed and development stopped. Dermatologists then focused on IL-13 as the dominant cytokine in eczema skin. Dermira licensed the antibody for skin disease in 2017, and Eli Lilly acquired Dermira in 2020 to complete development, with Almirall holding European rights. It was approved in Europe in 2023 and by the FDA in September 2024 as Ebglyss for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults and children 12 and older. After an initial period of every-two-week dosing, many people can be maintained on monthly injections.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Genentech develops lebrikizumab, first studied for asthma."], ["2017", "Dermira licenses lebrikizumab for atopic dermatitis."], ["2020", "Eli Lilly acquires Dermira."], ["2024", "FDA approves lebrikizumab (Ebglyss) for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis."]],
  "cell": "Lebrikizumab binds soluble IL-13 with high affinity. It grabs IL-13 at a spot that overlaps the docking site for IL-4 receptor alpha. IL-13 can still attach to IL-13 receptor alpha-1, but the second chain, IL-4 receptor alpha, cannot join to form the active receptor pair. Without that pair, JAK1, TYK2 and STAT6 are not activated, and the type 2 gene program is not switched on. This differs from tralokinumab, which blocks IL-13 from binding IL-13 receptor alpha-1 at all. Lebrikizumab leaves IL-13 receptor alpha-2 binding intact, so this decoy receptor can still capture and clear IL-13.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Lesions become less red and thickened, and the barrier improves as type 2 signaling is suppressed."], ["Itch", "Itch decreases within weeks because IL-13 no longer drives itch nerve sensitization and inflammation."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis and keratitis can occur, likely from loss of IL-13 support for tear-film goblet cells."], ["Immune defense", "IL-13 helps fight helminth worms, so worm infections are treated beforehand."]],
  "journey": "Lebrikizumab is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly through lymphatics, reaching peak blood levels in about a week. It distributes mostly in blood and extracellular fluid. It is cleared by general protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or kidneys. Its half-life is about 24 days, which supports less frequent injections once the skin is controlled.",
  "sidefx": "Conjunctivitis and keratitis are the hallmark side effects of IL-13 blockade, along with injection site reactions. Hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis and angioedema, can occur. Parasitic infections may worsen because IL-13 helps the gut expel worms. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; the antibody washes out over weeks to months.",
  "fun": "Lebrikizumab and tralokinumab both block IL-13 but grab it at different spots, so they shut down the same signal by different molecular routes.",
  "src": ["Ebglyss (lebrikizumab) FDA label", "Silverberg JI et al. N Engl J Med 2023 (ADvocate trials)"]
},
"nemolizumab": {
  "origin": "Interleukin-31 was described in 2004 as a cytokine made mainly by Th2 cells that strongly triggers itch. Researchers at Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan, part of the Roche group, created nemolizumab, a humanized antibody that blocks the IL-31 receptor. It was first approved in Japan in 2022 for itch in atopic dermatitis. Galderma licensed it for the rest of the world and studied it in prurigo nodularis, a disease of intensely itchy hard nodules. The FDA approved it as Nemluvio in August 2024 for prurigo nodularis and in December 2024 for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in patients 12 and older, used with topical therapy.",
  "timeline": [["2004", "Interleukin-31 is identified as an itch-inducing cytokine."], ["2022", "Nemolizumab is approved in Japan for itch in atopic dermatitis."], ["2024", "FDA approves nemolizumab (Nemluvio) for prurigo nodularis and later for atopic dermatitis."]],
  "cell": "IL-31 is released by Th2 cells and other immune cells in itchy skin. It signals through a receptor made of two chains, IL-31 receptor alpha (IL-31RA) and oncostatin M receptor beta, found on sensory nerve fibers in the skin and dorsal root ganglia, keratinocytes and some immune cells. When IL-31 binds, the receptor activates JAK1 and JAK2, then STAT3 and other pathways, which excite itch neurons and promote inflammation, nerve sprouting and skin thickening. Nemolizumab is an IgG2 antibody that binds IL-31RA itself, so IL-31 cannot engage the receptor. The itch signal is cut at its source, and with less scratching, the itch-scratch cycle that builds nodules and eczema slowly breaks.",
  "body": [["Itch nerves", "Itch drops quickly, often within the first weeks, because IL-31 can no longer activate sensory neurons."], ["Skin nodules", "In prurigo nodularis, hard nodules flatten and heal over months as scratching stops and IL-31 driven fibrosis is reduced."], ["Eczema skin", "Lesions improve as itch-driven scratching and inflammation decrease."], ["Airways and skin", "New or worsening eczema-like rashes and asthma symptoms were reported in prurigo nodularis trials."]],
  "journey": "Nemolizumab is given by injection under the skin from a pen that mixes powder and liquid. It is absorbed slowly through lymphatics, peaking about four to six days later, and remains in the blood and tissue fluid. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by general protein catabolism into amino acids. The half-life is about 18 days, supporting dosing every four weeks and, for some people with eczema, every eight weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, injection site reactions and eczema-like rashes are reported. Some people in prurigo nodularis trials developed new eczema or asthma symptoms, possibly because blocking IL-31 shifts the balance of type 2 signals. Hypersensitivity, including angioedema, can occur. Live vaccines are avoided. Cytokine changes can alter liver CYP enzymes. There is no antidote; the antibody is cleared over weeks.",
  "fun": "IL-31 is sometimes called the itch cytokine, and dogs with allergic itch are treated with a veterinary antibody against IL-31 itself.",
  "src": ["Nemluvio (nemolizumab) FDA label", "Dillon SR et al. Nat Immunol 2004", "Kwatra SG et al. N Engl J Med 2023 (prurigo nodularis trial)"]
},
"abrocitinib": {
  "origin": "Janus kinases were discovered around 1990 and nicknamed 'just another kinase' before their central role in cytokine signaling was understood. The first JAK inhibitors approved were ruxolitinib for myelofibrosis in 2011 and tofacitinib for rheumatoid arthritis in 2012. Pfizer then designed abrocitinib to be more selective for JAK1, the kinase used by many eczema and itch cytokines, while sparing JAK2, which supports blood cell production. The FDA approved it as Cibinqo in January 2022 for adults with refractory moderate to severe atopic dermatitis, and in 2023 for adolescents 12 and older. Like other JAK inhibitors it carries a boxed warning added after a large tofacitinib safety study.",
  "timeline": [["c. 1990", "Janus kinases are discovered as a new family of tyrosine kinases."], ["2012", "Tofacitinib becomes the first JAK inhibitor approved for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2021", "FDA strengthens JAK inhibitor boxed warnings after the ORAL Surveillance study."], ["2022", "FDA approves abrocitinib (Cibinqo) for refractory moderate to severe atopic dermatitis."], ["2023", "Approval is extended to adolescents 12 and older."]],
  "cell": "Many cytokines signal through receptors that have no enzyme activity of their own and rely on Janus kinases attached to their inner tails. When a cytokine binds, the paired JAKs phosphorylate each other and the receptor, creating docking sites for STAT proteins, which are then phosphorylated, pair up and move to the nucleus to switch on inflammatory genes. Abrocitinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor selective for JAK1. JAK1 is needed for signaling by IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, IL-22 and TSLP, key drivers of eczema inflammation, barrier disruption and itch. Blocking JAK1 shuts down all of these at once, inside the cell, which is why itch can improve within days.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Eczema lesions clear and the barrier improves as type 2 and IL-22 signaling are blocked."], ["Itch nerves", "Blocking JAK1 downstream of IL-31 and other itch cytokines relieves itch within days."], ["Blood", "Dose-related drops in platelets and lymphocytes occur, so blood counts are checked."], ["Immune defense", "Weaker cytokine signaling raises the risk of serious infections and herpes zoster."], ["Lipids and muscle", "LDL cholesterol and creatine kinase can rise."], ["Heart and vessels", "JAK inhibitors as a class carry boxed warnings for heart attack, stroke and blood clots."]],
  "journey": "Abrocitinib is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It is moderately protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C19, with CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 contributing, into active metabolites M1 and M2 that add to the effect. The parent half-life is about three to five hours, and metabolites leave mainly in urine. Strong CYP2C19 inhibitors raise levels, and strong inducers lower them.",
  "sidefx": "JAK1 supports immune defense, so serious infections, herpes zoster and reactivated tuberculosis can occur. The boxed warning lists serious infections, death, cancer, major cardiovascular events and blood clots, based on class data. Drops in platelets come from effects on blood cell signaling and explain why antiplatelet drugs are contraindicated early in treatment at higher doses. Nausea, headache and acne are common. Lipid and CK rises reflect cytokine effects on metabolism. There is no antidote; with a short half-life, effects fade within days of stopping.",
  "fun": "The name Janus comes from the two-faced Roman god, because JAK enzymes have two kinase-like domains, one active and one regulatory.",
  "src": ["Cibinqo (abrocitinib) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on JAK inhibitors, 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ritlecitinib": {
  "origin": "Alopecia areata is an autoimmune disease in which CD8 T cells and natural killer cells attack growing hair follicles. Research in the 2010s showed that interferon-gamma and gamma-chain cytokines like IL-15 sustain this attack, and that JAK inhibitors could regrow hair in mice and people. Pfizer designed ritlecitinib as an irreversible inhibitor of JAK3 and the TEC kinase family, to block lymphocyte signals while sparing JAK1 and JAK2. The FDA approved it as Litfulo in June 2023 for severe alopecia areata in people 12 and older, the first treatment for this condition approved for adolescents. Baricitinib had been approved for adults with alopecia areata a year earlier.",
  "timeline": [["2014", "Researchers report that JAK inhibition reverses alopecia areata in mice and people."], ["2022", "Baricitinib becomes the first FDA-approved systemic treatment for severe alopecia areata in adults."], ["2023", "FDA approves ritlecitinib (Litfulo) for severe alopecia areata in patients 12 and older."]],
  "cell": "Ritlecitinib forms a covalent bond with cysteine 909 in the ATP pocket of JAK3. JAK1, JAK2 and TYK2 lack this cysteine, which gives ritlecitinib selectivity for JAK3. JAK3 pairs with JAK1 on receptors for common gamma-chain cytokines (IL-2, IL-4, IL-7, IL-15 and IL-21), which control the growth and activity of T cells and NK cells. Ritlecitinib also irreversibly binds a matching cysteine in TEC family kinases (BTK, ITK, TXK, BMX and TEC), which relay signals from T-cell and NK-cell receptors. Blocking both pathways reduces the cytotoxic CD8 T-cell and NK-cell attack on hair follicles in the anagen (growth) phase. Follicles recover their immune privilege and resume growing hair.",
  "body": [["Hair follicles", "Hair regrows on the scalp, and sometimes eyebrows and eyelashes, over months as the immune attack subsides."], ["Blood", "Lymphocyte and platelet counts can fall, reflecting reduced JAK3 and TEC signaling in blood cells."], ["Immune defense", "Serious infections, herpes zoster and tuberculosis reactivation can occur."], ["Skin", "Acne, rash and urticaria are reported, and nonmelanoma skin cancer risk is listed."], ["Liver and muscle", "Liver enzymes and creatine kinase can rise."]],
  "journey": "Ritlecitinib is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It has low protein binding. Because it binds JAK3 covalently, its effect outlasts its short plasma half-life of about two hours; the enzyme stays blocked until the cell makes new JAK3. It is metabolized by several pathways, including glutathione S-transferase and CYP3A, CYP2C8 and CYP1A2, and leaves mainly in urine. It inhibits CYP3A and CYP1A2, raising levels of some other drugs.",
  "sidefx": "Weakening T-cell and NK-cell signaling explains the main risks: serious infections, herpes zoster and tuberculosis reactivation, and possibly cancer. The JAK class boxed warning covers infections, death, cancer, cardiovascular events and blood clots. Reduced lymphocyte and platelet counts reflect cytokine blockade in blood cells. Headache, diarrhea, acne and rash are common. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; new enzyme must be made after stopping.",
  "fun": "Ritlecitinib's selectivity hinges on a single amino acid: JAK3 has a cysteine in its active site that its JAK cousins lack.",
  "src": ["Litfulo (ritlecitinib) FDA label", "Xing L et al. Nat Med 2014", "King B et al. Lancet 2023 (ALLEGRO trial)"]
},
"deuruxolitinib": {
  "origin": "Deuruxolitinib is a modified version of ruxolitinib, a JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor approved in 2011 for myelofibrosis. Concert Pharmaceuticals, a Massachusetts company, specialized in replacing certain hydrogen atoms in known drugs with deuterium, a heavier, stable form of hydrogen. Carbon-deuterium bonds are harder for liver enzymes to break, which can slow metabolism and change how a drug behaves. Concert applied this to ruxolitinib and studied it in severe alopecia areata. Sun Pharma acquired Concert in 2023, and the FDA approved deuruxolitinib as Leqselvi in July 2024 for adults with severe alopecia areata.",
  "timeline": [["2011", "Ruxolitinib becomes the first JAK inhibitor approved in the US."], ["2017", "Deutetrabenazine becomes the first deuterated drug approved by the FDA."], ["2023", "Sun Pharma acquires Concert Pharmaceuticals."], ["2024", "FDA approves deuruxolitinib (Leqselvi) for severe alopecia areata in adults."]],
  "cell": "Deuruxolitinib is a reversible, ATP-competitive inhibitor of JAK1 and JAK2. In alopecia areata, CD8 T cells that carry the NKG2D receptor attack hair follicles. Interferon-gamma (which signals through JAK1 and JAK2) and IL-15 (which signals through JAK1 and JAK3) keep this attack going in a feed-forward loop. Deuruxolitinib blocks STAT phosphorylation downstream of these cytokines, so the follicle stops being flagged as a target and T-cell activity falls. Deuterium at specific positions slows its breakdown by CYP enzymes compared with ruxolitinib, changing the drug's exposure without changing how it binds the kinases.",
  "body": [["Hair follicles", "Scalp hair regrows over months as the immune attack on follicles is suppressed."], ["Blood", "Blocking JAK2, which supports red cell and platelet production, can cause anemia, and neutrophil and lymphocyte counts can fall."], ["Immune defense", "Serious infections, herpes zoster and tuberculosis reactivation can occur."], ["Lipids", "Cholesterol levels can rise, a known effect of JAK inhibition on lipid metabolism."], ["Skin", "Acne is common, possibly from changes in cytokine signaling around hair follicles."]],
  "journey": "Deuruxolitinib is taken by mouth twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking in about one and a half hours. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP2C9 with a smaller CYP3A4 contribution, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. The half-life is about four hours. People whose CYP2C9 works poorly, and those taking moderate or strong CYP2C9 inhibitors such as fluconazole, would have much higher levels, which is why genotype and interactions matter.",
  "sidefx": "JAK1 and JAK2 blockade dampens immune defense and blood cell production. That explains serious infections, herpes zoster, anemia, neutropenia and lymphopenia. The class boxed warning covers serious infections, death, cancer, major cardiovascular events and blood clots. Rises in cholesterol and CK are related to cytokine effects on metabolism. Headache and acne are common. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; effects fade over days after stopping.",
  "fun": "Deuterium is chemically almost the same as hydrogen but about twice as heavy, enough to make the bonds it forms tougher for enzymes to break.",
  "src": ["Leqselvi (deuruxolitinib) FDA label", "Jakafi (ruxolitinib) FDA label", "THRIVE-AA1 and THRIVE-AA2 phase 3 trials"]
},
"spesolimab": {
  "origin": "In 2011 researchers led by Slaheddine Marrakchi reported in the New England Journal of Medicine that some families with generalized pustular psoriasis (GPP) had mutations in IL36RN, the gene for the IL-36 receptor antagonist. Without this natural brake, IL-36 signaling ran out of control, causing sudden flares of widespread sterile pustules, fever and illness. The condition was called DITRA. That discovery made the IL-36 receptor an obvious target. Boehringer Ingelheim developed spesolimab, a humanized antibody against the receptor. The FDA approved it as Spevigo in September 2022 as an IV infusion for GPP flares, the first approved treatment for GPP in the US. An under-the-skin form for preventing flares followed in 2024.",
  "timeline": [["2011", "IL36RN mutations are linked to generalized pustular psoriasis (DITRA)."], ["2022", "FDA approves IV spesolimab (Spevigo) for GPP flares in adults."], ["2024", "Subcutaneous spesolimab is approved for GPP when not flaring, including patients 12 and older."]],
  "cell": "IL-36 alpha, beta and gamma are members of the IL-1 family made by keratinocytes. They bind the IL-36 receptor (IL1RL2), which pairs with IL-1 receptor accessory protein and signals through MyD88, IRAK kinases, NF-kB and MAP kinases. This makes keratinocytes release chemokines such as CXCL1 and CXCL8 (IL-8), which pull neutrophils into the epidermis, forming sterile pustules. It also drives the IL-23 and IL-17 axis, feeding the loop. Normally the IL-36 receptor antagonist (IL-36Ra) limits this. Spesolimab is an IgG1 antibody that binds IL-36R and blocks all three IL-36 agonists, shutting down the neutrophil recruitment that defines GPP.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Widespread pustules often clear within about a week of an infusion as neutrophil recruitment stops."], ["Whole body", "Fever, raised white blood cell count and CRP fall as the inflammatory flare settles."], ["Skin pain", "Burning skin pain lessens as inflammation resolves."], ["Immune defense", "Infections, including urinary and respiratory infections, can occur, and tuberculosis is screened for before starting."]],
  "journey": "For a flare, spesolimab is given as an IV infusion, so peak blood levels are reached at the end of the infusion. For prevention, it is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly through lymphatics. As an antibody, it remains in blood and tissue fluid and is broken down by general protein catabolism. Its half-life is about 25 days, so one infusion keeps working for weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Blocking IL-36 signaling dampens one arm of innate defense, so infections can occur, and latent tuberculosis can reactivate. Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions are possible, and DRESS (a severe drug reaction with rash, fever and organ involvement) has been reported. Fatigue, nausea, vomiting, headache and itch occurred in trials. Live vaccines are avoided. There is no antidote; the antibody clears over weeks.",
  "fun": "GPP flares can be triggered by withdrawing systemic steroids, pregnancy or infection, and before spesolimab there was no FDA-approved therapy for it.",
  "src": ["Spevigo (spesolimab) FDA label", "Marrakchi S et al. N Engl J Med 2011", "Bachelez H et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (Effisayil 1 trial)"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"eflornithine-cream": {
  "origin": "Eflornithine, also called DFMO (difluoromethylornithine), was designed in the late 1970s by chemists at the Merrell research center in Strasbourg, France, as an enzyme-activated, irreversible inhibitor of ornithine decarboxylase. The goal was cancer treatment, because fast-growing tumor cells need polyamines to divide. It did not succeed as a broad cancer drug, but it proved able to kill the parasite that causes West African sleeping sickness, and the injectable form was approved by the FDA in 1990. Hair follicles in their growth phase also depend on ornithine decarboxylase, so the idea of a skin cream followed. Vaniqa cream, developed with interest from the shaving company Gillette, was approved in 2000 as the first prescription cream to slow unwanted facial hair in women. Brand availability has varied since, and some similar products are sold as unapproved drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1978", "Merrell researchers report DFMO as an irreversible ornithine decarboxylase inhibitor."], ["1990", "FDA approves injectable eflornithine for West African sleeping sickness."], ["2000", "FDA approves Vaniqa cream for unwanted facial hair in women."], ["2023", "An oral eflornithine product is approved to lower relapse risk in high-risk neuroblastoma."]],
  "cell": "Ornithine decarboxylase (ODC) is the rate-limiting enzyme of polyamine synthesis. It removes a carboxyl group from ornithine to make putrescine, which is then built into spermidine and spermine. Polyamines bind DNA and RNA and are needed for rapid cell division. Eflornithine looks like ornithine, so ODC accepts it into the active site. During the reaction the drug is turned into a reactive form that bonds covalently to the enzyme, so it is a suicide (mechanism-based) inhibitor and the enzyme is destroyed. In the hair bulb during anagen (the growth phase), matrix cells divide quickly and ODC activity is high. With polyamines depleted, these cells divide more slowly, so the hair shaft grows more slowly and may look finer. It does not destroy the follicle, so hair returns after the cream is stopped.",
  "body": [["Hair follicles", "Matrix cells in growing follicles divide more slowly, so facial hair grows back more slowly between hair removal sessions. Visible change takes weeks."], ["Skin", "The cream can sting, burn or tingle, and it can cause redness, rash and acne-like bumps, especially on freshly shaved or irritated skin."], ["Hair after stopping", "Because the follicle is not destroyed, hair growth returns to its pretreatment rate about eight weeks after the cream is stopped."], ["Whole body", "Very little drug is absorbed through intact facial skin, so effects outside the skin are not expected."]],
  "journey": "The cream is rubbed into the skin of the face and under the chin. Only a small fraction, typically under one percent, crosses intact skin into the blood. The absorbed drug is not significantly metabolized and is cleared unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about eight hours. Most of the effect stays in the follicles near where it is applied. The effect builds slowly over four to eight weeks because hair growth itself is slow.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local. Stinging, burning and redness come from irritation of the skin barrier, which is worse right after hair removal when the skin is broken. Acne and pseudofolliculitis barbae (ingrown hair bumps) relate to changes in how hairs grow and exit the follicle. Contact allergy can occur. Systemic toxicity seen with the injectable form, such as bone marrow suppression and hearing loss, is not expected because so little cream is absorbed. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Injectable eflornithine earned the nickname \"the resurrection drug\" because it could revive people who were near coma from late-stage sleeping sickness.",
  "src": ["Vaniqa (eflornithine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Metcalf BW et al. J Am Chem Soc 1978"]
},
"bimatoprost-eyelash": {
  "origin": "Bimatoprost was developed by the eye care company Allergan as a glaucoma drop. Allergan scientists, including David Woodward, described it as a prostamide, a synthetic relative of prostaglandin F2-alpha. It was approved as Lumigan for high eye pressure in 2001. Glaucoma patients and their doctors soon noticed a striking side effect: eyelashes grew longer, thicker and darker. Allergan studied that effect on purpose, and in 2008 the FDA approved Latisse, a version applied to the base of the upper eyelashes with a sterile brush, for hypotrichosis (thin or sparse lashes). It is a rare case of a side effect becoming its own approved use.",
  "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves bimatoprost (Lumigan) eye drops for glaucoma and ocular hypertension."], ["2000s", "Eyelash growth is recognized as a consistent side effect of prostaglandin glaucoma drops."], ["2008", "FDA approves Latisse for hypotrichosis of the eyelashes."]],
  "cell": "Bimatoprost binds prostaglandin F (FP) receptors and proposed prostamide receptors, which are G-protein coupled receptors. In the hair follicle of the eyelash, this signaling lengthens anagen, the active growth phase, and pushes more resting follicles into anagen. More follicles growing for longer means more lashes and longer lashes. The bulb also gets larger, which gives thicker hairs. Prostaglandin signaling increases melanin production by follicle melanocytes, so lashes turn darker. The same receptors in the ciliary body and trabecular meshwork increase aqueous humor outflow, which is why the molecule also lowers eye pressure.",
  "body": [["Eyelashes", "Lashes become longer, fuller and darker over about two to four months as more follicles stay in the growth phase."], ["Eyelid skin", "Melanocytes in the eyelid skin can make more pigment, causing darkening of the lid margin that usually fades after stopping."], ["Iris", "Iris pigmentation can increase, mainly if drug gets into the eye. This change may be permanent, which matters most in light-colored eyes."], ["Conjunctiva", "Prostaglandin-induced vasodilation can make the eye look red and itchy."], ["Eye pressure", "Drug that reaches the eye can lower intraocular pressure, which matters for people already using pressure-lowering drops."], ["Nearby skin", "Hair can grow on skin that the solution touches repeatedly, so the solution is kept to the lid margin."]],
  "journey": "The solution is brushed along the skin of the upper lid margin at the base of the lashes. A small amount is absorbed into the follicles and lid skin, and some may reach the tear film. Systemic exposure is very low. Any bimatoprost that reaches the blood is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about 45 minutes, through oxidation, N-deethylation and glucuronidation, then leaves in urine and feces. Lashes respond slowly, with full effect at about three to four months, and return to their earlier state over weeks to months after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from prostaglandin signaling where the drug lands. Redness and itching reflect vasodilation of conjunctival vessels. Darker eyelid skin and iris come from stimulated melanin production. Unwanted hair growth happens where the solution touches other skin. Rarely, drug inside the eye can contribute to inflammation or macular edema in at-risk eyes. There is no antidote; stopping use lets most cosmetic effects fade, though iris color change may not.",
  "fun": "Latisse is often cited as one of the best-known examples of a drug side effect, longer eyelashes in glaucoma patients, being turned into a separate FDA-approved product.",
  "src": ["Latisse (bimatoprost) FDA label", "Lumigan (bimatoprost) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"travoprost": {
  "origin": "The idea of treating glaucoma with prostaglandins came from Laszlo Bito at Columbia University in the 1970s and 1980s. He found that small amounts of prostaglandin F2-alpha lowered eye pressure in animals instead of raising it as expected. The natural molecule caused too much redness and irritation, so chemists modified it. Latanoprost, from Pharmacia, came first in 1996. Alcon developed travoprost, a more selective FP receptor agonist made as an isopropyl ester prodrug, and the FDA approved it as Travatan in 2001. In 2006 Travatan Z was approved without the common preservative benzalkonium chloride, using a different preservative system that is gentler on the ocular surface. A slow-release travoprost implant was approved separately in 2023.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Laszlo Bito begins research showing prostaglandins can lower eye pressure."], ["1996", "Latanoprost becomes the first prostaglandin analog approved for glaucoma in the US."], ["2001", "FDA approves travoprost (Travatan) for open-angle glaucoma and ocular hypertension."], ["2006", "Travatan Z is approved without benzalkonium chloride preservative."], ["2023", "FDA approves iDose TR, a travoprost intracameral implant."]],
  "cell": "Travoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. Esterases in the cornea split off the ester to make travoprost free acid, the active drug. The free acid is a selective full agonist at the prostaglandin F (FP) receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on ciliary muscle cells. FP activation turns on signaling that increases production of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs). These enzymes break down collagen and other extracellular matrix between ciliary muscle bundles. Wider spaces between the muscle bundles let more aqueous humor drain out through the uveoscleral (unconventional) pathway. Production of aqueous humor is not reduced. More outflow with the same production lowers intraocular pressure.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls over hours and the effect lasts more than a day, which protects the optic nerve in glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "Eye redness (hyperemia) is common because FP stimulation dilates conjunctival vessels."], ["Iris", "Iris melanocytes make more melanin, so hazel or mixed-color irises can slowly turn browner, and this change can be permanent."], ["Eyelashes and lids", "Lashes grow longer and darker and eyelid skin can darken, the same effect used for cosmetic lash products."], ["Orbit fat", "Long-term use can shrink fat around the eye (prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy), giving a sunken look."], ["Retina", "In eyes with a torn lens capsule or other risk factors, prostaglandins can contribute to cystoid macular edema or uveitis."]],
  "journey": "A drop placed on the eye soaks into the cornea, where esterases convert the prodrug into the active free acid as it passes through. The free acid reaches the aqueous humor and ciliary body within hours, with peak pressure lowering at about 12 hours. Only a small amount enters the blood through the conjunctiva and nasal lining. In plasma the free acid is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about 45 minutes, by beta-oxidation like natural fatty acids. Inactive metabolites leave in urine.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects are FP receptor effects in nearby tissues. Vasodilation causes red eyes and itching. Stimulated melanogenesis darkens the iris and lid skin. Effects on hair follicles cause lash growth. Changes in orbital fat cells cause periorbital fat atrophy. Prostaglandin signaling can increase inflammation and vessel leakage, which is why macular edema and uveitis are rare warnings. Systemic effects are minimal because so little reaches the blood. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Travatan Z replaced benzalkonium chloride with a preservative system based on borate, zinc and sorbitol, aimed at being kinder to the eye surface for people using drops for decades.",
  "src": ["Travatan Z (travoprost) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tafluprost": {
  "origin": "Tafluprost was discovered in Japan through work by the eye care company Santen and the chemical company Asahi Glass, which had expertise in fluorine chemistry. The team replaced the hydroxyl group at carbon 15 of the prostaglandin chain with two fluorine atoms, which increased affinity for the FP receptor. Tafluprost was first marketed in Europe and Japan in the late 2000s. In the United States it was approved in 2012 as Zioptan, the first preservative-free prostaglandin analog, supplied in single-dose containers for people whose eyes react to preservatives.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "Latanoprost opens the era of prostaglandin analogs for glaucoma in the US."], ["2000s", "Santen and Asahi Glass develop tafluprost, and it is launched in Europe and Japan."], ["2012", "FDA approves Zioptan, the first preservative-free prostaglandin analog in the US."]],
  "cell": "Tafluprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. Corneal esterases convert it to tafluprost acid, the active form. Tafluprost acid binds the prostaglandin F (FP) receptor with high affinity, helped by the 15,15-difluoro substitution. The FP receptor is a Gq-coupled receptor; activation in the ciliary muscle increases matrix metalloproteinase expression, which remodels the extracellular matrix between muscle bundles. This opens the uveoscleral outflow pathway, so aqueous humor drains more easily. Aqueous production is unchanged, but higher outflow lowers intraocular pressure.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure drops within a few hours, peaks around 12 hours and stays lower for at least a day."], ["Ocular surface", "Without preservative, the drops are less likely to damage surface cells, which helps people with dry eye or preservative sensitivity."], ["Conjunctiva", "Prostaglandin-induced vasodilation can make the eyes red and itchy."], ["Iris and lids", "Increased melanin production can darken the iris and eyelid skin over months, and the iris change may be permanent."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes may become longer, thicker and darker from prolonged hair follicle growth phase."], ["Head", "Headache is reported in some people, likely from vascular effects of absorbed drug."]],
  "journey": "The drop is absorbed through the cornea, where esterases unmask the active acid. Effect begins in two to four hours and lasts about a day. A small amount reaches the blood through the conjunctiva and nasal mucosa, but plasma tafluprost acid falls below measurable levels within about 30 minutes. The acid is broken down by fatty acid beta-oxidation and glucuronidation, and metabolites leave in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect FP receptor activity in and around the eye. Redness comes from conjunctival vasodilation. Darkening of iris and lids comes from more melanin made by melanocytes. Eyelash changes come from longer anagen in lash follicles. Rarely, prostaglandin-driven inflammation and vascular leakage can cause uveitis or macular edema in eyes at risk. Systemic effects are minimal. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tafluprost was the first prostaglandin glaucoma drop in the US sold completely free of preservatives, in small single-use vials.",
  "src": ["Zioptan (tafluprost) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"latanoprostene-bunod": {
  "origin": "Latanoprostene bunod was designed by the French company Nicox, which specializes in drugs that release nitric oxide. The chemists attached a nitric oxide-donating group (butanediol mononitrate) to latanoprost acid, the active form of the well-known glaucoma drug latanoprost. The goal was a single molecule that opens two drainage routes in the eye. Bausch + Lomb licensed and developed the drug, and the FDA approved it as Vyzulta in 2017. It was the first nitric oxide-donating drug approved to lower eye pressure.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "Latanoprost is approved in the US, establishing FP receptor agonists for glaucoma."], ["2000s", "Nicox designs a nitric oxide-donating version of latanoprost acid."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vyzulta (latanoprostene bunod)."]],
  "cell": "After the drop enters the cornea, esterases split latanoprostene bunod into two active parts. The first, latanoprost acid, activates prostaglandin F (FP) receptors in the ciliary muscle. That induces matrix metalloproteinases, remodels the matrix and increases uveoscleral outflow. The second part, butanediol mononitrate, releases nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase in trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal cells, raising cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which relaxes the contractile cells of the meshwork and widens the conventional (trabecular) drainage route. With both pathways open, aqueous humor leaves the eye faster and intraocular pressure falls.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Pressure falls through both the uveoscleral and trabecular outflow routes, with peak effect around 11 to 13 hours after a drop."], ["Trabecular meshwork", "Nitric oxide relaxes the meshwork, the drainage tissue that is often stiff in open-angle glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "Both prostaglandins and nitric oxide dilate vessels, so redness and irritation are common."], ["Iris and lids", "As with other prostaglandin drops, iris and eyelid pigmentation can increase over time."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes can grow longer and darker from the prostaglandin component."]],
  "journey": "The drop is absorbed through the cornea, and esterases split it into latanoprost acid and butanediol mononitrate in the eye tissues. Pressure lowering starts in one to three hours and lasts at least a day. A small amount of latanoprost acid reaches the blood, where it is cleared quickly, with a plasma half-life of about 20 minutes, by beta-oxidation in the liver. Metabolites are excreted mainly by the kidneys. The nitric oxide part is short lived and is metabolized locally.",
  "sidefx": "Conjunctival hyperemia and eye irritation come from vasodilation by both the prostaglandin and the nitric oxide parts. Iris and eyelid darkening and lash growth are FP receptor effects on melanocytes and hair follicles. Prostaglandin signaling can promote inflammation and leakage, so macular edema and uveitis are rare warnings in at-risk eyes. Systemic effects are minimal. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "The scientists behind nitric oxide signaling won the 1998 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, and this drop puts that same signal to work in the eye's drain.",
  "src": ["Vyzulta (latanoprostene bunod) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"netarsudil": {
  "origin": "Rho kinase (ROCK) inhibitors grew out of research on how cells control their internal skeleton. In Japan, fasudil was approved in 1995 for brain vessel spasm, and ripasudil was approved in Japan as a glaucoma drop in 2014. In the United States, Aerie Pharmaceuticals designed netarsudil, a ROCK inhibitor that also blocks the norepinephrine transporter. The FDA approved it as Rhopressa in 2017, the first US drug that works directly on the trabecular meshwork, the tissue that is diseased in open-angle glaucoma. A combination with latanoprost, Rocklatan, followed in 2019.",
  "timeline": [["1995", "Fasudil, a ROCK inhibitor, is approved in Japan for cerebral vasospasm."], ["2014", "Ripasudil is approved in Japan as the first ROCK inhibitor glaucoma drop."], ["2017", "FDA approves netarsudil (Rhopressa)."], ["2019", "FDA approves Rocklatan, netarsudil combined with latanoprost."]],
  "cell": "Trabecular meshwork cells behave like smooth muscle. Rho GTPase activates Rho-associated coiled-coil kinase (ROCK1 and ROCK2), which phosphorylates and inhibits myosin light chain phosphatase. That keeps myosin light chains phosphorylated, building actin stress fibers and making the cells contract and stiffen, which resists outflow. Netarsudil inhibits ROCK, so myosin light chains are dephosphorylated, stress fibers break down and the cells relax. The meshwork becomes more porous, and aqueous humor drains more easily through the conventional pathway into Schlemm canal. Netarsudil also lowers episcleral venous pressure, the back pressure the drain empties against. It inhibits the norepinephrine transporter too, which may reduce aqueous production. Its corneal metabolite AR-13503 is also active.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls mainly through better trabecular outflow, the route that is impaired in open-angle glaucoma."], ["Conjunctiva", "ROCK inhibition relaxes vascular smooth muscle, so eye redness is the most common side effect."], ["Cornea", "Drug can deposit in the corneal epithelium in a whorl pattern (corneal verticillata). It usually does not affect vision."], ["Eye surface vessels", "Small bleeds under the conjunctiva can appear, likely linked to vessel dilation."], ["Whole body", "Blood levels are mostly too low to measure, so systemic effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "A drop soaks into the cornea, where esterases convert netarsudil into its active metabolite AR-13503. Pressure lowering develops over hours and lasts about a day. Plasma levels of netarsudil and its metabolite are mostly below what tests can detect, so little is known about systemic metabolism and excretion. Nearly all of its action stays inside the eye.",
  "sidefx": "The main effects come from relaxing smooth muscle in vessel walls, which dilates conjunctival vessels and causes redness and sometimes small hemorrhages. Corneal verticillata are fine drug-related deposits in the corneal epithelium, similar to those seen with amiodarone, and usually fade after stopping. Stinging at instillation is common. Blurred vision and tearing can occur. There is no specific antidote, and systemic toxicity is not expected.",
  "fun": "Netarsudil was the first new class of glaucoma drop approved in the US in more than 20 years when it arrived in 2017.",
  "src": ["Rhopressa (netarsudil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"brinzolamide": {
  "origin": "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors started with sulfa drugs. In 1940 researchers noticed that sulfanilamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase. That led Lederle chemists to make acetazolamide in the early 1950s, and in 1954 ophthalmologist Bernard Becker showed that it lowered eye pressure in glaucoma. Pills caused tingling, fatigue and acidosis, so for decades researchers tried to make an eye drop that could reach the ciliary body. Merck succeeded first with dorzolamide in 1994. Alcon developed brinzolamide, a related sulfonamide made as a suspension, and the FDA approved it as Azopt in 1998. A fixed combination with brimonidine, Simbrinza, was approved in 2013.",
  "timeline": [["1940", "Sulfanilamide is found to inhibit carbonic anhydrase."], ["1954", "Bernard Becker reports that acetazolamide lowers intraocular pressure."], ["1994", "Dorzolamide becomes the first topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor approved in the US."], ["1998", "FDA approves brinzolamide (Azopt)."], ["2013", "FDA approves Simbrinza (brinzolamide with brimonidine)."]],
  "cell": "Aqueous humor is made by the ciliary process epithelium. Inside these cells, carbonic anhydrase II rapidly converts carbon dioxide and water into carbonic acid, which splits into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate secretion into the posterior chamber pulls sodium along, and water follows by osmosis. Brinzolamide is a sulfonamide that binds the zinc in the active site of carbonic anhydrase II and blocks it. Less bicarbonate formed means less sodium and water moving into the eye, so aqueous humor production falls and intraocular pressure goes down. Drug absorbed into the blood binds carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, but at the low levels reached it has little effect on body acid-base balance.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous production slows, so intraocular pressure falls, with peak effect around two hours after a drop."], ["Vision", "The drop is a white suspension, so vision is briefly blurred until it spreads and clears."], ["Taste", "Drug that drains down the tear duct reaches the mouth and can cause a bitter or sour taste."], ["Ocular surface", "Eye discomfort, foreign body sensation and lid inflammation can occur from local irritation."], ["Blood", "Absorbed drug accumulates in red blood cells by binding carbonic anhydrase, but systemic acidosis is uncommon."], ["Cornea", "Corneal endothelial cells also use carbonic anhydrase to pump fluid, so eyes with weak endothelium can swell."]],
  "journey": "The suspension is placed on the eye and enters through the cornea and sclera to reach the ciliary body. Some drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed into the blood. There it binds tightly to carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, so whole-blood levels build up slowly and the whole-blood half-life is very long, about 111 days. It is partly metabolized to N-desethyl brinzolamide, which is also active, and is excreted mainly unchanged in urine along with metabolites.",
  "sidefx": "Blurred vision after dosing happens because the drop is a particulate suspension. Bitter taste comes from drug draining into the throat, where carbonic anhydrase in taste cells is affected. Because brinzolamide is a sulfonamide, rare sulfonamide-type hypersensitivity reactions, including severe skin reactions, can occur. Systemic effects of carbonic anhydrase inhibition, such as tingling, fatigue and acidosis, are much less common than with pills. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Brinzolamide can stay in red blood cells for months, because it latches onto the carbonic anhydrase enzyme those cells carry in large amounts.",
  "src": ["Azopt (brinzolamide) FDA label", "Becker B. Am J Ophthalmol 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"apraclonidine": {
  "origin": "Apraclonidine is a chemical cousin of clonidine, the alpha-2 agonist blood pressure drug developed in Germany in the 1960s. Clonidine drops lowered eye pressure but also crossed into the brain and dropped blood pressure. Chemists added a polar para-amino group to the clonidine molecule, which made it much less able to cross the blood-brain barrier. Alcon developed this para-aminoclonidine as Iopidine. It was approved by the FDA in the late 1980s to prevent the sudden pressure spikes that can follow laser eye surgery, and a lower strength was later approved for short-term add-on glaucoma treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Clonidine is developed in Germany as an alpha-2 agonist."], ["1980s", "Alcon develops apraclonidine (para-aminoclonidine) eye drops for use around laser eye surgery."], ["1990s", "A lower-strength apraclonidine drop is approved for short-term glaucoma treatment."]],
  "cell": "Apraclonidine is a relatively selective agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors in the ciliary body. Alpha-2 receptors couple to Gi proteins, which inhibit adenylyl cyclase and lower cAMP in the ciliary epithelium. Less cAMP means less aqueous humor secretion, so intraocular pressure falls. Unlike brimonidine, apraclonidine keeps meaningful alpha-1 agonist activity. Alpha-1 stimulation contracts the Mueller muscle of the upper lid (lid lift), constricts conjunctival vessels (whiter eyes) and contracts the iris dilator (mild pupil widening). The polar amino group keeps most drug out of the brain, so central sedation and hypotension are less common than with clonidine.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous humor production falls within about an hour, which blunts pressure spikes after laser procedures."], ["Conjunctiva", "Alpha-1 vasoconstriction blanches the white of the eye, which can also reduce bleeding during laser surgery."], ["Eyelid", "Contraction of the Mueller muscle raises the upper lid, so the eye can look more open."], ["Pupil", "Alpha-1 stimulation of the iris dilator can make the pupil slightly larger."], ["Mouth and nose", "Drug draining down the tear duct can cause dry mouth and dry nose from alpha-2 effects on glands."], ["Heart and brain", "A small amount reaches the circulation and can cause slow heart rate, low blood pressure or tiredness in sensitive people."]],
  "journey": "The drop is absorbed through the cornea and conjunctiva and reaches the ciliary body quickly, with onset in about an hour and peak effect in three to five hours. Some drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed into the blood, where its half-life is about eight hours. Because of its polar structure, little crosses the blood-brain barrier. It is cleared mainly by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Lid retraction, conjunctival blanching and mild mydriasis are alpha-1 effects. Dry mouth and fatigue reflect alpha-2 effects from absorbed drug. A common problem with longer use is an allergic-like follicular conjunctivitis with red, itchy eyes and lid swelling, which is one reason the drug is used short term. Slow heart rate and low blood pressure can occur from systemic alpha-2 stimulation. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Apraclonidine drops are used as a diagnostic test for Horner syndrome. The denervated iris is supersensitive to its alpha-1 effect, so the small pupil dilates and the droopy lid lifts.",
  "src": ["Iopidine (apraclonidine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"pilocarpine-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Pilocarpine comes from the leaves of jaborandi shrubs (Pilocarpus species) native to Brazil, where people used the leaves to cause sweating and drooling. In 1873 the Brazilian physician Symphronio Coutinho brought jaborandi leaves to Paris. In 1875 the active alkaloid, pilocarpine, was isolated independently by chemists in France and England. Within a few years European ophthalmologists, including Adolf Weber in Germany, found that it shrank the pupil and lowered eye pressure in glaucoma. Pilocarpine became a mainstay of glaucoma care for about a century. Newer drops have largely replaced it for glaucoma, but in 2021 the FDA approved a pilocarpine drop (Vuity) for presbyopia, the age-related loss of near focus, and another (Qlosi) followed in 2023.",
  "timeline": [["1873", "Symphronio Coutinho brings jaborandi leaves from Brazil to Paris."], ["1875", "Pilocarpine is isolated from jaborandi leaves."], ["1870s", "Pilocarpine is first used as a miotic to treat glaucoma."], ["2021", "FDA approves a pilocarpine drop (Vuity) for presbyopia."]],
  "cell": "Pilocarpine is a direct-acting agonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, mainly M3 receptors on the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle. M3 receptors couple to Gq, which activates phospholipase C, raises IP3 and releases calcium, causing smooth muscle contraction. Contraction of the iris sphincter makes the pupil small (miosis) and pulls the peripheral iris away from the drainage angle. Contraction of the longitudinal ciliary muscle pulls on the scleral spur, which opens the spaces of the trabecular meshwork and increases conventional aqueous outflow, so intraocular pressure falls. Ciliary muscle contraction also relaxes the zonules and lets the lens become rounder, shifting focus to near (accommodation). A small pupil adds a pinhole effect that improves near vision, which is the basis for its presbyopia use.",
  "body": [["Pupil", "The iris sphincter contracts and the pupil becomes small within 10 to 30 minutes, which makes dim light look darker."], ["Drainage angle", "Ciliary muscle pull opens the trabecular meshwork, so aqueous humor drains faster and eye pressure falls."], ["Focus", "Accommodative spasm shifts focus toward near, causing blurred distance vision, especially in younger people."], ["Brow and head", "Strong ciliary muscle contraction causes a brow ache or headache, worst in the first days of use."], ["Retina", "Ciliary muscle pull can tug on the peripheral retina, and rare retinal tears or detachment have been reported."], ["Whole body", "Absorbed drug can stimulate muscarinic receptors elsewhere, causing sweating, drooling, nausea, slow heart rate or wheezing with excess use."]],
  "journey": "The drop crosses the cornea and acts on the iris and ciliary muscle within minutes. Miosis lasts about four to eight hours, which is why glaucoma dosing used to be frequent. Esterases in eye tissue and plasma break pilocarpine down. Some drug drains through the tear duct and is absorbed, but its plasma half-life is short, about an hour, and it is cleared by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly all effects are muscarinic. Brow ache and blurred vision come from ciliary muscle spasm. Poor night vision comes from the small pupil letting in less light. Traction on the retina explains rare retinal detachment, so a retinal exam is part of standard precautions. Absorbed drug can cause systemic cholinergic effects such as bradycardia, bronchospasm, sweating and salivation. Atropine, a muscarinic antagonist, reverses systemic cholinergic toxicity.",
  "fun": "Pilocarpine is also used in the sweat test for cystic fibrosis. It is pushed into the skin with a small electric current to make the skin sweat so the sweat chloride can be measured.",
  "src": ["Vuity (pilocarpine) FDA label", "Isopto Carpine (pilocarpine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"dexamethasone-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Corticosteroids entered eye care soon after cortisone was first used in patients in 1948. By the early 1950s cortisone drops were calming eye inflammation such as iritis. Chemists then tried to make stronger steroids. Dexamethasone, a fluorinated glucocorticoid about 25 times stronger than cortisol as an anti-inflammatory, was made in the late 1950s by chemists at Merck and was approved in 1958. Eye drop suspensions such as Maxidex became standard for treating inflammation after surgery and for steroid-responsive eye disease. Newer delivery forms include an intraocular injection (Dexycu) and a dissolving insert placed in the tear duct (Dextenza), both approved in 2018, and an intravitreal implant (Ozurdex).",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Cortisone is first given to a patient, opening the steroid era."], ["1950s", "Steroid eye drops come into use for ocular inflammation."], ["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after being developed at Merck."], ["2018", "FDA approves Dextenza (canalicular insert) and Dexycu (intraocular suspension)."]],
  "cell": "Dexamethasone is lipophilic and passes through cell membranes. In the cytoplasm it binds the glucocorticoid receptor, which releases heat shock proteins and moves into the nucleus. There it binds glucocorticoid response elements and switches on anti-inflammatory genes, including annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and so cuts the supply of arachidonic acid for prostaglandins and leukotrienes. The receptor also binds and blocks the transcription factors NF-kB and AP-1, turning down cytokines (IL-1, TNF-alpha), adhesion molecules and COX-2. The result is less capillary dilation and leakage, less fibrin, fewer white cells entering eye tissue and less scarring. The same gene effects in the trabecular meshwork can reduce aqueous outflow and raise eye pressure in steroid responders.",
  "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Fewer inflammatory cells and less protein leakage in the front of the eye after surgery or in iritis, which eases pain and light sensitivity."], ["Conjunctiva and cornea", "Redness, swelling and allergic-type inflammation settle, but wound healing of the cornea is slowed."], ["Trabecular meshwork", "Changes in meshwork cells reduce outflow, so some people, called steroid responders, develop raised eye pressure that can damage the optic nerve."], ["Lens", "Long-term use can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Immune defense", "Suppressed local immunity makes it easier for herpes simplex, fungi and bacteria to infect the eye."]],
  "journey": "Shaken before use, the suspension releases drug that soaks into the cornea and conjunctiva and reaches the aqueous humor within hours. Effects last several hours per drop. A small amount drains through the tear duct and is absorbed. In the body dexamethasone is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours, and is excreted by the kidneys. Systemic steroid effects are rare with eye drops.",
  "sidefx": "Raised eye pressure happens because glucocorticoid signaling changes trabecular meshwork cells, adding extracellular material and stiffening the drain. Cataract relates to steroid effects on lens fiber cells. Slower healing and infection risk come from suppressed fibroblast activity and immune responses, which is why steroids can worsen herpes simplex keratitis and fungal infections. Thinning of the cornea or sclera and perforation can occur with prolonged use in eyes that are already thin. There is no antidote; pressure usually falls after the steroid is tapered and stopped.",
  "fun": "The Dextenza insert placed in the tear drainage canal contains fluorescein, so a clinician can see it glow under blue light and confirm it is still in place.",
  "src": ["Maxidex (dexamethasone) FDA label", "Dextenza (dexamethasone insert) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"loteprednol": {
  "origin": "Loteprednol came from the \"soft drug\" idea of chemist Nicholas Bodor at the University of Florida, who worked on what he called retrometabolic drug design. The goal was a drug that does its job at the target and is then quickly broken down into inactive pieces before it can cause harm. Bodor started from cortienic acid, an inactive metabolite of prednisolone, and built an ester steroid that tissue esterases would quickly turn back into inactive acid. The result, loteprednol etabonate, was approved by the FDA in 1998 as Lotemax and Alrex. The design was meant to keep strong anti-inflammatory activity while lowering the chance of raising eye pressure.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Nicholas Bodor designs loteprednol etabonate using the soft drug approach."], ["1998", "FDA approves Lotemax and Alrex (loteprednol) eye drops."], ["2000s", "Combination with tobramycin (Zylet) and gel forms are introduced."], ["2020", "Eysuvis is approved for short-term treatment of dry eye flares."]],
  "cell": "Loteprednol binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity. The receptor complex moves to the nucleus, where it switches on annexin A1 (lipocortin), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and limits prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis. It also blocks NF-kB and AP-1 driven transcription of cytokines, adhesion molecules and COX-2, reducing redness, swelling and cell infiltration. Structurally it lacks the 20-ketone of classic steroids and instead has a 17-beta chloromethyl ester. Esterases in ocular tissue quickly hydrolyze it to the inactive metabolites delta-1 cortienic acid etabonate and delta-1 cortienic acid. This fast local inactivation is thought to explain why it raises intraocular pressure less often than prednisolone acetate.",
  "body": [["Eye inflammation", "Postoperative and allergic inflammation settles as fewer inflammatory cells and mediators reach the eye tissues."], ["Conjunctiva", "In allergic conjunctivitis, itching and redness improve because late-phase inflammation is suppressed."], ["Eye pressure", "Pressure rises are less common than with stronger steroids, but they can still happen, so pressure is monitored with longer use."], ["Lens", "Cataract can occur with prolonged steroid use."], ["Cornea and defense", "Healing slows and the risk of herpes, fungal and bacterial infections rises because local immunity is suppressed."]],
  "journey": "Applied as a suspension, gel or ointment, loteprednol enters the cornea and conjunctiva. As it moves through tissues, esterases convert it into inactive carboxylic acids. Plasma levels after eye use are below the limit of detection, so systemic effects are not expected. Effects last several hours per dose.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are glucocorticoid effects in the eye. Higher eye pressure comes from steroid changes in the trabecular meshwork, though the rapid breakdown makes this less frequent than with other steroids. Cataract, delayed wound healing and secondary infection reflect suppressed cell growth and immunity. Blurred vision and burning come from the drop itself. There is no antidote; effects resolve after stopping under supervision.",
  "fun": "Loteprednol is a textbook example of a \"soft drug\", a medicine designed from the start to self-destruct into harmless pieces after it acts.",
  "src": ["Lotemax (loteprednol) FDA label", "Bodor N. retrometabolic drug design papers", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"difluprednate": {
  "origin": "Difluprednate is a difluorinated derivative of prednisolone that was first used in Japan as a strong steroid cream for skin disease. Researchers later reformulated it as an eye drop emulsion, a fine oil-in-water mixture that keeps the drug evenly dispersed without the need to shake. The FDA approved it as Durezol in 2008 for inflammation and pain after eye surgery, and its labeling was later extended to anterior uveitis. It is one of the most potent topical steroids used in the eye.",
  "timeline": [["2008", "FDA approves Durezol emulsion for postoperative eye inflammation and pain."], ["2010s", "Durezol labeling adds treatment of endogenous anterior uveitis."]],
  "cell": "Difluprednate is a prodrug. In the eye it is deacetylated to difluoroprednisolone butyrate (DFB), the active form. DFB binds the glucocorticoid receptor with high affinity; fluorine atoms at the 6 and 9 positions increase receptor potency. The activated receptor moves into the nucleus, induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and the arachidonic acid cascade, and represses NF-kB and AP-1 targets such as IL-1, TNF-alpha, adhesion molecules and COX-2. The anterior chamber gets fewer white cells and less protein flare, and pain and light sensitivity improve. Its strength also makes trabecular meshwork changes, and therefore pressure rises, more frequent.",
  "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Marked reduction in cells and flare after surgery and in uveitis, often faster than with older steroid drops."], ["Pain", "Less prostaglandin and cytokine production reduces ocular pain and photophobia after surgery."], ["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure rises are relatively common, especially in children and steroid responders, because of steroid effects on the drain."], ["Cornea", "Corneal edema and slowed epithelial healing can occur."], ["Lens", "Cataract and posterior capsule opacity are reported with steroid exposure."], ["Infection risk", "Suppressed immunity raises the risk of herpes, fungal and bacterial infection of the eye."]],
  "journey": "The emulsion spreads over the eye and the drug enters the cornea, where it is converted to the active DFB. Effects last several hours per dose. Systemic levels after eye use are very low and are not well characterized. Any absorbed steroid would be handled by the liver like other glucocorticoids.",
  "sidefx": "Because it is very potent, difluprednate shows typical steroid eye effects more often. Pressure rises come from glucocorticoid-induced changes in trabecular meshwork cells that reduce outflow. Cataract, delayed healing and infection risk reflect suppressed cell growth and immunity. Corneal edema and redness can occur. There is no antidote; pressure usually returns toward baseline after supervised tapering.",
  "fun": "Durezol is an emulsion rather than a suspension, so the drug stays evenly mixed and the bottle does not need shaking for a consistent dose.",
  "src": ["Durezol (difluprednate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ketorolac-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Ketorolac was developed at the drug company Syntex in California in the 1980s, in a program led by chemist Joseph Muchowski. It was a strong NSAID with pain relief close to that of opioids in some settings, and the injectable form (Toradol) was approved in 1989. Because prostaglandins drive much of the pain and swelling after eye surgery and much of the itch in allergic eye disease, an eye drop was developed and approved as Acular in the early 1990s. Lower-strength and preservative-free versions followed, and in 2014 ketorolac was combined with phenylephrine in an irrigation solution (Omidria) used during cataract surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ketorolac is developed at Syntex."], ["1989", "Injectable ketorolac (Toradol) is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Acular ketorolac eye drops are approved."], ["2014", "FDA approves Omidria, ketorolac with phenylephrine for use during lens surgery."]],
  "cell": "Ketorolac reversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2 in ocular tissues. These enzymes turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the precursor of PGE2, PGF2-alpha and other prostanoids. In the eye these prostaglandins break down the blood-aqueous barrier, dilate vessels, sensitize corneal pain nerves and make the iris sphincter contract during surgery. They also promote fluid leakage from perifoveal retinal capillaries, which drives cystoid macular edema after cataract surgery. With COX blocked, less prostaglandin is made, so there is less pain, less inflammation, less pupil constriction during surgery and less itch in allergic conjunctivitis.",
  "body": [["Cornea and nerves", "Pain after eye surgery and after corneal procedures eases because prostaglandins no longer sensitize corneal nerve endings."], ["Anterior chamber", "Inflammation and protein leakage drop because the blood-aqueous barrier stays more intact."], ["Pupil", "During surgery, blocking prostaglandin release helps keep the pupil from constricting."], ["Conjunctiva", "In seasonal allergic conjunctivitis, itching is reduced."], ["Retina", "Less prostaglandin lowers the chance of fluid buildup in the macula after cataract surgery."], ["Corneal surface", "Prolonged use can slow epithelial healing and, rarely, cause corneal melting."]],
  "journey": "The drop penetrates the cornea and reaches the aqueous humor within an hour. Effects last about six to eight hours. Only a tiny amount reaches the blood, where ketorolac is highly protein bound, metabolized by the liver and cleared by the kidneys. Systemic NSAID effects are not expected with eye use.",
  "sidefx": "Stinging and burning on instillation are common. With prolonged or frequent use, loss of prostaglandins that support the corneal epithelium can cause punctate keratitis, epithelial breakdown and, rarely, corneal melting or perforation, especially after complicated surgery or with diabetes or dry eye. NSAIDs can slow healing and, in theory, increase bleeding of eye tissues during surgery. Cross-sensitivity with aspirin and other NSAIDs is possible. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Omidria is added to the irrigation fluid used during cataract surgery so the pupil stays wide and postoperative pain is lower, combining an alpha-1 agonist with an NSAID.",
  "src": ["Acular (ketorolac) FDA label", "Omidria FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"bromfenac": {
  "origin": "Bromfenac has an unusual history. It was first approved in the United States in 1997 as an oral painkiller, Duract, by Wyeth-Ayerst. Within a year reports of severe liver failure, some fatal, appeared in people who took it for longer than the recommended short course, and it was withdrawn in 1998. In Japan, Senju Pharmaceutical developed bromfenac as an eye drop, where the tiny amounts used do not cause the same liver risk. The eye drop was approved in the US as Xibrom in 2005, followed by once-daily versions such as Prolensa (2013) and BromSite (2016) for inflammation and pain after cataract surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1997", "Oral bromfenac (Duract) is approved in the US as a short-term pain reliever."], ["1998", "Oral bromfenac is withdrawn after reports of severe liver injury."], ["2005", "FDA approves bromfenac eye drops (Xibrom)."], ["2013", "Prolensa, a once-daily formulation, is approved."]],
  "cell": "Bromfenac inhibits cyclooxygenase, with relatively more potency against COX-2 than COX-1. Blocking COX stops arachidonic acid from being converted to prostaglandin H2, so less PGE2 and related prostanoids are made in the cornea, iris, ciliary body and retina. These prostaglandins normally open the blood-aqueous barrier, dilate vessels, sensitize corneal nerves and cause macular capillary leakage. Bromfenac's bromine atom makes the molecule more lipophilic, so it penetrates the cornea well and builds up useful tissue levels, which supports once-daily dosing.",
  "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Less postoperative inflammation, with fewer cells and less flare in the front of the eye."], ["Pain", "Eye pain and light sensitivity after cataract surgery are reduced because corneal nerves are less sensitized."], ["Retina", "Lower prostaglandin levels help protect against cystoid macular edema after cataract surgery."], ["Corneal surface", "Long use can delay epithelial healing and rarely cause keratitis or corneal melting."], ["Whole body", "Plasma levels after eye use are below detection, so the liver toxicity of the old oral form is not expected."]],
  "journey": "Bromfenac drops are absorbed through the cornea, reaching aqueous humor levels that persist long enough for one dose a day. Systemic exposure is so low that plasma levels are below the limit of detection. Any absorbed drug would be highly protein bound and metabolized by the liver.",
  "sidefx": "Ocular side effects come from reduced prostaglandins and the drop itself. Corneal epithelial prostaglandins help healing, so blocking them can lead to keratitis, epithelial breakdown and rarely corneal melting or perforation, especially with extended use or diabetes. Some formulations contain sulfite, which can trigger allergic reactions in sensitive people. NSAIDs may slow healing and can increase tissue bleeding. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Bromfenac went from a failed pill, withdrawn for liver failure, to a successful eye drop, because the eye needs only a tiny fraction of the drug exposure that pills deliver.",
  "src": ["Prolensa (bromfenac) FDA label", "FDA withdrawal of Duract, 1998", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"nepafenac": {
  "origin": "Nepafenac was developed by the eye care company Alcon as a prodrug of amfenac, an older NSAID. Amfenac itself crosses the cornea poorly. Converting it into an amide (nepafenac) made a neutral molecule that soaks through the cornea far more easily and is then turned back into amfenac inside the eye. The FDA approved Nevanac in 2005 for pain and inflammation after cataract surgery. A stronger once-daily form, Ilevro, followed in 2012, and labeling later added reduction of the risk of macular edema after cataract surgery in people with diabetes.",
  "timeline": [["2005", "FDA approves Nevanac (nepafenac) for pain and inflammation after cataract surgery."], ["2012", "FDA approves Ilevro, a once-daily nepafenac formulation."]],
  "cell": "Nepafenac is an amide prodrug with no strong COX activity of its own. It penetrates the cornea and is hydrolyzed by intraocular hydrolases in the iris, ciliary body, retina and choroid to amfenac. Amfenac inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 and cyclooxygenase-2, blocking conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2. Lower prostaglandin levels mean the blood-aqueous and blood-retinal barriers leak less, corneal nerves are less sensitized and macular capillaries leak less fluid. Because conversion happens in tissues that have the hydrolase, including the retina, drug activity reaches the back of the eye.",
  "body": [["Anterior chamber", "Inflammation after cataract surgery decreases, with fewer cells and less flare."], ["Pain", "Postoperative pain and photophobia ease as prostaglandin-driven nerve sensitization drops."], ["Retina", "Amfenac formed in the retina lowers prostaglandins that cause cystoid macular edema, including in people with diabetes."], ["Cornea", "Epithelial healing can slow and, rarely, keratitis or corneal melting occurs."], ["Lens capsule", "Capsular opacity and a sticky sensation are reported after surgery, partly reflecting the surgery itself."]],
  "journey": "Drops placed on the eye are absorbed through the cornea, and nepafenac spreads through the eye before hydrolases convert it to amfenac. Amfenac levels in aqueous humor peak in about one to two hours. Very little reaches the blood. Absorbed amfenac is highly bound to albumin, then hydroxylated and glucuronidated and excreted mainly in urine.",
  "sidefx": "The main risks come from losing prostaglandins in the corneal epithelium, which support healing. This can cause punctate keratitis, epithelial breakdown and, rarely, corneal melting or perforation, especially with use beyond the labeled course or in eyes with diabetes, dry eye or complicated surgery. Increased bleeding of eye tissue is possible, and raised eye pressure and headache are reported. Cross-sensitivity with other NSAIDs is possible. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Nepafenac is one of few eye drops designed so its active drug is made inside the eye, which helps it reach the retina from a surface drop.",
  "src": ["Ilevro (nepafenac) FDA label", "Nevanac (nepafenac) FDA label"]
},
"gatifloxacin-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "The quinolone antibiotics began in 1962 when George Lesher and colleagues at Sterling-Winthrop found nalidixic acid as a by-product of making the antimalarial chloroquine. Adding a fluorine atom in the 1970s and 1980s produced the far stronger fluoroquinolones. Gatifloxacin, an 8-methoxy fluoroquinolone, was discovered by the Japanese company Kyorin. An oral form (Tequin) was approved in the US in 1999 but was withdrawn in 2006 because it caused dangerous swings in blood sugar. The eye drop, made by Allergan, never had that problem because so little reaches the blood. Zymar was approved in 2003 for bacterial conjunctivitis, and a stronger version, Zymaxid, replaced it in 2010.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered at Sterling-Winthrop."], ["1999", "Oral gatifloxacin (Tequin) is approved in the US."], ["2003", "FDA approves gatifloxacin eye drops (Zymar)."], ["2006", "Oral gatifloxacin is withdrawn in the US because of blood sugar disturbances."], ["2010", "Zymaxid, a stronger gatifloxacin eye drop, is approved."]],
  "cell": "Bacteria must constantly cut and reseal their DNA to manage supercoiling as they copy it. DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II, subunits GyrA and GyrB) adds negative supercoils ahead of the replication fork, and topoisomerase IV separates the linked daughter chromosomes after copying. Gatifloxacin binds the enzyme-DNA complex after the DNA has been cut and traps it, so the breaks cannot be resealed. Stalled replication forks and double-strand breaks trigger bacterial death, so the drug is bactericidal and kills faster at higher concentrations. Gyrase is the main target in gram-negative bacteria and topoisomerase IV in gram-positive bacteria. The 8-methoxy group improves activity against gram-positive organisms and means two mutations are usually needed for resistance.",
  "body": [["Conjunctiva", "Susceptible bacteria such as Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae are killed, so redness and discharge improve in a few days."], ["Tear film", "High drug levels are reached in tears right after each drop, which favors concentration-dependent killing."], ["Taste", "Drug draining down the tear duct can cause an altered or bitter taste."], ["Ocular surface", "Irritation, eye pain and temporary blurring can occur from the drop itself."], ["Normal eye flora", "Use can select resistant bacteria or allow fungal overgrowth."]],
  "journey": "The drop mixes with the tear film and enters the conjunctiva and cornea, giving high local levels shortly after instillation. Most is washed away by tears through the nasolacrimal duct. The amount absorbed into the blood is very small. Absorbed gatifloxacin has a half-life of roughly 7 to 14 hours, is little metabolized and is excreted mainly unchanged in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Eye irritation, worsening redness and eye pain come from the drop on an already inflamed surface. Altered taste comes from drainage into the throat. Killing susceptible bacteria can let resistant bacteria or fungi overgrow. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur as with any fluoroquinolone. The blood sugar problems and other systemic warnings of oral fluoroquinolones are not expected with eye use because blood levels are so low. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Gatifloxacin is a drug that disappeared from US pharmacies as a pill but stayed available as an eye drop.",
  "src": ["Zymaxid (gatifloxacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ofloxacin-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Ofloxacin was developed by the Japanese company Daiichi Seiyaku in the early 1980s as one of the first broad-spectrum fluoroquinolones. It built on nalidixic acid, discovered in 1962, and on the discovery that a fluorine atom greatly boosted potency. Oral ofloxacin was approved in the US around 1990. Allergan then developed the eye drop, Ocuflox, approved in the early 1990s for bacterial conjunctivitis and corneal ulcers. Ofloxacin is a mixture of two mirror-image forms, and Daiichi later developed the active form alone as levofloxacin.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered."], ["1980s", "Daiichi Seiyaku develops ofloxacin in Japan."], ["1990s", "Ofloxacin eye drops (Ocuflox) are approved in the US."], ["1996", "Levofloxacin, the active isomer of ofloxacin, is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Ofloxacin inhibits two bacterial type II topoisomerases. DNA gyrase introduces negative supercoils to relieve strain as DNA unwinds for copying, and topoisomerase IV separates interlinked daughter chromosomes. The drug binds the enzyme while it is attached to cut DNA and stabilizes this cleavage complex, so the strand cannot be resealed. Replication stalls and double-strand breaks accumulate, which kills the bacterium. Killing is concentration dependent. Ofloxacin has strong gram-negative activity, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which matters for contact lens related corneal ulcers.",
  "body": [["Conjunctiva", "Bacterial conjunctivitis improves as susceptible organisms are killed and discharge decreases."], ["Cornea", "In bacterial keratitis, including Pseudomonas ulcers, frequent drops keep corneal drug levels high enough to stop the infection."], ["Ocular surface", "Burning, stinging, redness and light sensitivity can follow each drop."], ["Normal flora", "Resistant bacteria or fungi can overgrow with prolonged use."], ["Immune system", "Rare hypersensitivity reactions can occur, including severe skin reactions."]],
  "journey": "The drop enters the tear film and penetrates the cornea, reaching useful levels in corneal tissue and some in the aqueous humor. Much is drained through the tear duct. Systemic absorption is very low. Absorbed ofloxacin has a half-life of about four to eight hours, is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Local burning and discomfort are effects of the solution on the ocular surface. Overgrowth of resistant or fungal organisms follows suppression of normal flora. Corneal white precipitates are uncommon with ofloxacin, unlike ciprofloxacin, because ofloxacin stays more soluble at tear pH. Hypersensitivity, including rare anaphylaxis or Stevens-Johnson syndrome, is an immune reaction to the quinolone structure. Tendon and nerve problems of oral quinolones are not expected with eye use. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Ofloxacin is a 50:50 mix of two mirror-image molecules, and nearly all of its antibacterial power comes from one of them, which became the separate drug levofloxacin.",
  "src": ["Ocuflox (ofloxacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"polymyxin-b-trimethoprim": {
  "origin": "This eye drop combines two antibiotics with very different backstories. Polymyxins were discovered in 1947 by several research groups working independently, from the soil bacterium Bacillus polymyxa (now Paenibacillus polymyxa); one of these groups was at Burroughs Wellcome. Trimethoprim came from the rational drug design program of George Hitchings and Gertrude Elion at Burroughs Wellcome, who searched for molecules that block folate enzymes more in microbes than in people. Hitchings and Elion shared the 1988 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Combining the two in one drop (Polytrim) gave coverage of both gram-positive and gram-negative eye pathogens without using an aminoglycoside.",
  "timeline": [["1947", "Polymyxins are discovered from Bacillus polymyxa."], ["1950s", "Hitchings and colleagues at Burroughs Wellcome develop trimethoprim."], ["1988", "Hitchings and Elion share the Nobel Prize for principles of drug design."]],
  "cell": "Polymyxin B is a cationic, ring-shaped polypeptide with a fatty acid tail. Its positive charges bind the negatively charged lipid A of lipopolysaccharide on gram-negative bacteria, pushing away the magnesium and calcium ions that hold the outer membrane together. The fatty tail then inserts into the membrane like a detergent, disrupting both the outer and inner membranes so cell contents leak out and the bacterium dies. Trimethoprim binds bacterial dihydrofolate reductase thousands of times more tightly than the human enzyme. Blocking it stops conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate, which is needed to make thymidine, purines and some amino acids, so bacterial DNA synthesis halts. Trimethoprim covers many gram-positive organisms including Staphylococcus and Streptococcus pneumoniae, while polymyxin B covers gram-negatives including Pseudomonas aeruginosa.",
  "body": [["Conjunctiva", "Bacterial conjunctivitis and blepharoconjunctivitis improve as both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms are suppressed."], ["Eyelids", "Infections along the lid margin can be treated by the same drop as it bathes the lid area."], ["Ocular surface", "Burning, stinging and redness can occur after each drop."], ["Immune system", "Allergy can cause lid swelling, itching and rash around the eye."]],
  "journey": "The drops stay on the ocular surface and in the tear film, giving high local concentrations. Polymyxin B is a large charged molecule that is poorly absorbed through tissues, and trimethoprim reaches only tiny amounts in the blood. Most drug drains through the tear duct or is washed away. Systemic effects are not expected.",
  "sidefx": "Local irritation is the main side effect, from the drop on inflamed tissue. Hypersensitivity can cause lid swelling, itching and redness. The kidney and nerve toxicity of injected polymyxin B, and the folate-related blood effects of oral trimethoprim, are not expected with eye drops because almost no drug reaches the blood. Prolonged use can allow resistant organisms or fungi to overgrow. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Trimethoprim was one of the early triumphs of rational drug design: it was built to fit the bacterial version of an enzyme better than the human one.",
  "src": ["Polytrim (polymyxin B and trimethoprim) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize 1988 Physiology or Medicine"]
},
"lifitegrast": {
  "origin": "Lifitegrast was designed as a small molecule that mimics the part of ICAM-1 that binds the immune cell integrin LFA-1. Research in the 1990s and 2000s showed that LFA-1 and ICAM-1 binding is a key step in T-cell driven inflammation, including in dry eye disease. The compound was developed by SARcode Bioscience, a small California company, and SARcode was bought by Shire in 2013. The FDA approved Xiidra in 2016 as the first drug approved to treat both the signs and symptoms of dry eye disease. Ownership later moved to Novartis and then to Bausch + Lomb.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "SARcode Bioscience develops lifitegrast as a small-molecule LFA-1 antagonist."], ["2013", "Shire acquires SARcode and the lifitegrast program."], ["2016", "FDA approves Xiidra (lifitegrast) for dry eye disease."]],
  "cell": "In dry eye disease, stress on the ocular surface raises ICAM-1 on corneal and conjunctival cells and on blood vessels. T cells carry the integrin LFA-1 (CD11a/CD18). When LFA-1 binds ICAM-1, it helps T cells stick to vessel walls, migrate into the tissue and form the immune synapse with antigen-presenting cells. That leads to T-cell activation and release of cytokines such as interferon-gamma and IL-1, which damage goblet cells and the epithelium and worsen tear film instability. Lifitegrast binds LFA-1 and blocks its interaction with ICAM-1. Fewer T cells enter and activate, cytokine release falls and the inflammation cycle eases over weeks.",
  "body": [["Ocular surface", "Corneal staining and surface inflammation decrease as T-cell activity is reduced."], ["Dryness symptoms", "Eye dryness and discomfort can begin to improve within about two weeks of regular use."], ["Taste", "Drug draining to the throat commonly causes a bitter or altered taste."], ["Eye on dosing", "Burning or irritation at instillation and brief blurred vision are common."], ["Nose and head", "Headache and sinusitis are reported, probably reflecting drainage and local irritation."]],
  "journey": "Each single-use vial is placed in the eye and the drug acts in the conjunctiva and cornea. A small amount reaches the blood, with low plasma levels that peak within minutes to hours and fall with a half-life of hours. It is not extensively metabolized and is eliminated mainly in feces. Effects build over weeks of twice-daily use rather than after a single drop.",
  "sidefx": "Dysgeusia, an unpleasant taste, occurs because drug drains through the nasolacrimal duct to the mouth. Instillation-site irritation and blurred vision come from the solution on a sensitive surface. Because it acts locally on immune cell adhesion, broad immune suppression is not expected. Rare hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "An older antibody that blocked LFA-1 throughout the body, efalizumab for psoriasis, was withdrawn in 2009. Lifitegrast uses the same target but stays almost entirely in the eye.",
  "src": ["Xiidra (lifitegrast) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"perfluorohexyloctane": {
  "origin": "Perfluorohexyloctane belongs to a family of chemicals called semifluorinated alkanes, which have one part made of carbon and fluorine and one part made of carbon and hydrogen. They are clear, very slippery and do not mix with water. The German company Novaliq built an eye drop technology around these liquids and developed perfluorohexyloctane for dry eye. Bausch + Lomb licensed it, and the FDA approved it as Miebo in 2023. It was the first FDA-approved drug for dry eye disease that directly targets tear evaporation, a central problem in meibomian gland dysfunction.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Novaliq develops eye drop technology based on semifluorinated alkanes."], ["2023", "FDA approves Miebo (perfluorohexyloctane) for the signs and symptoms of dry eye disease."]],
  "cell": "Perfluorohexyloctane does not bind a receptor or enzyme. Its action is physical. The tear film has an outer oily layer made by the meibomian glands that slows evaporation. In meibomian gland dysfunction this layer is thin or poor, so tears evaporate fast, the film becomes salty (hyperosmolar) and surface cells become inflamed. Perfluorohexyloctane has very low surface tension, so a single small drop spreads rapidly across the eye. It forms a thin layer at the air-tear interface that acts as a barrier against evaporation. Tear evaporation falls, tear osmolarity improves and surface cells are less stressed, which shows as less corneal staining and less dryness over time.",
  "body": [["Tear film", "Evaporation slows because the drug forms a barrier at the air-liquid interface, keeping the watery layer in place."], ["Cornea", "Corneal staining decreases over weeks as surface cells are less stressed by dryness."], ["Dryness symptoms", "Discomfort and dryness improve within days to weeks, especially in people with meibomian gland dysfunction."], ["Vision", "Blurred vision right after a drop is common as the liquid spreads."], ["Conjunctiva", "Some people notice mild redness of the white of the eye after using the drops."]],
  "journey": "The drop spreads over the eye surface and acts there. It contains no water and no preservative. A small amount is absorbed. Absorbed drug is not metabolized; instead it is thought to be carried to the lungs and breathed out. Systemic exposure is low.",
  "sidefx": "Blurred vision occurs because the liquid has a different refractive behavior than tears until it spreads evenly. Mild redness may reflect surface irritation. Because it has no receptor target and no preservative, few other side effects are expected. Hypersensitivity is possible. Contact lenses are removed before use per labeling. There is no antidote, and none is needed.",
  "fun": "Miebo contains no water at all, so there is nothing in the bottle for bacteria to grow in, which is why it needs no preservative.",
  "src": ["Miebo (perfluorohexyloctane) FDA label"]
},
"varenicline-nasal": {
  "origin": "Varenicline traces back to cytisine, a plant alkaloid from the laburnum tree that was used in Eastern Europe to help people quit smoking. At Pfizer in the 1990s, a team led by chemist Jotham Coe used cytisine as a starting point to design varenicline, a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors. It was approved as Chantix in 2006 for smoking cessation. Later, the company Oyster Point Pharma found a new use: when sprayed in the nose at a tiny fraction of the smoking dose, varenicline triggers the nerve reflex that makes tears. The FDA approved this nasal spray, Tyrvaya, in 2021 as the first nasal spray for dry eye disease.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Pfizer chemists design varenicline using the plant alkaloid cytisine as a model."], ["2006", "FDA approves oral varenicline (Chantix) for smoking cessation."], ["2021", "FDA approves Tyrvaya varenicline nasal spray for dry eye disease."]],
  "cell": "The nasal lining is densely supplied by branches of the trigeminal nerve, which carry nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Varenicline binds these receptors, including the alpha4beta2 subtype, and opens their cation channels, so sodium and calcium flow in and the nerve endings fire. The signal travels to the brainstem and activates the trigeminal parasympathetic pathway, the nasolacrimal reflex. Parasympathetic fibers then release acetylcholine onto the lacrimal glands, conjunctival goblet cells and meibomian glands. More natural tears are produced, including water, mucin and lipid. This is the same reflex that makes the eyes water when something irritates the nose. It works through the nose, not through drops in the eye.",
  "body": [["Lacrimal glands", "Basal tear production increases within minutes as the nasolacrimal reflex is triggered."], ["Ocular surface", "With more natural tears, the eye surface is better covered and dryness signs can improve."], ["Nose", "Sneezing occurs in most people right after spraying, along with nasal irritation and sometimes nosebleeds."], ["Throat and airway", "Cough and throat irritation are common as spray reaches the back of the nose."], ["Brain", "Systemic levels are far lower than with smoking-cessation tablets, so central effects such as nausea or vivid dreams are less expected."]],
  "journey": "The spray is absorbed partly through the nasal lining. It acts locally on trigeminal nerve endings within minutes. Some reaches the blood, peaking within about two hours, but exposure is much lower than with oral varenicline. In the body it is barely metabolized and is excreted mostly unchanged in the urine with a half-life of about 19 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Sneezing, cough and throat and nose irritation come directly from activating nicotinic receptors on sensory nerves in the nose, the very effect that drives the tear reflex. Nasal discomfort and minor nosebleeds come from local irritation. Because blood levels are low, the nausea and sleep effects of oral varenicline are not expected at labeled use. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "In clinical trials, about four out of five people sneezed after using the spray, a side effect that shows the nasal nerve reflex is working.",
  "src": ["Tyrvaya (varenicline nasal spray) FDA label", "Chantix (varenicline) FDA label", "Coe JW et al. J Med Chem 2005"]
},
"faricimab": {
  "origin": "Faricimab grew out of decades of research on how abnormal blood vessels form in the eye. In 1989 Napoleone Ferrara and colleagues at Genentech isolated vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), and anti-VEGF injections transformed care of wet macular degeneration in the 2000s. Researchers then found that a second signal, angiopoietin-2 (Ang-2), makes vessels unstable and leaky. Roche and Genentech used their CrossMAb antibody engineering to build one antibody with two different arms, one binding VEGF-A and one binding Ang-2. The FDA approved it as Vabysmo in 2022 for wet age-related macular degeneration and diabetic macular edema, making it the first bispecific antibody approved for the eye. Approval for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion followed in 2023.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Napoleone Ferrara's team at Genentech isolates VEGF."], ["2006", "Ranibizumab, an anti-VEGF antibody fragment, is approved for wet AMD."], ["2022", "FDA approves faricimab (Vabysmo) for wet AMD and diabetic macular edema."], ["2023", "Faricimab is approved for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion."]],
  "cell": "In wet AMD, diabetic macular edema and vein occlusion, low oxygen and inflammation drive retinal cells to release VEGF-A. VEGF-A binds VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells, triggering tyrosine kinase signaling that makes cells divide, form new vessels and loosen their junctions, so fluid leaks. Ang-2 competes with angiopoietin-1 at the Tie2 receptor and blocks its stabilizing signal, which leaves vessels leaky and inflamed and makes them more sensitive to VEGF. Faricimab is a humanized bispecific IgG1 antibody. One arm neutralizes VEGF-A so it cannot reach VEGFR-1 or VEGFR-2. The other arm neutralizes Ang-2, so Tie2 signaling from angiopoietin-1 can restore stable vessel walls. The Fc region is engineered to remove immune effector functions and FcRn binding, which helps the antibody clear quickly once it leaves the eye.",
  "body": [["Retina", "Fluid in and under the retina dries as vessel leakage falls, which can stabilize or improve vision."], ["Choroidal vessels", "Abnormal new vessels in wet AMD stop growing and leaking."], ["Retinal vessels", "Blocking Ang-2 helps vessels stay stable, which may allow longer gaps between injections."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, eye pain, floaters and a brief rise in eye pressure are related to the needle injection."], ["Inside the eye", "Rare inflammation, retinal vasculitis or vessel blockage can occur and may threaten vision."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the circulation, but arterial clot events are a class warning for anti-VEGF drugs."]],
  "journey": "Faricimab is injected directly into the vitreous gel by an eye specialist. From there it spreads to the retina and choroid and slowly leaves the eye. Small amounts enter the blood, peaking within about two days, with a systemic half-life of about seven and a half days. As a protein, it is broken down by normal protein catabolism into amino acids. The effect in the eye lasts long enough to space injections weeks to months apart.",
  "sidefx": "Most serious risks come from the injection itself: endophthalmitis (infection inside the eye) from introducing organisms, retinal tear or detachment from needle and vitreous traction, and pressure spikes from adding volume to the eye. Intraocular inflammation and rare retinal vasculitis are immune reactions to the antibody inside the eye. Arterial thromboembolic events are a theoretical class risk because VEGF helps maintain healthy vessels. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Faricimab's name hides its trick: one antibody that grabs two different targets, a design called bispecific.",
  "src": ["Vabysmo (faricimab-svoa) FDA label", "Ferrara N, Henzel WJ. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1989"]
},
"brolucizumab": {
  "origin": "Brolucizumab started as a single-chain antibody fragment developed by ESBATech, a Swiss biotech company spun out of the University of Zurich. Alcon acquired ESBATech in 2009, and the program continued under Novartis. The team stripped an antibody down to its smallest binding part so that much more drug could fit in one tiny injection, with the hope of longer intervals between treatments for wet age-related macular degeneration. The FDA approved it as Beovu in 2019. In 2020 reports of retinal vasculitis and retinal vessel blockage, sometimes with severe vision loss, led to updated warnings, and its use became more cautious. Approval for diabetic macular edema followed in 2022.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "VEGF is isolated, opening the path to anti-VEGF eye therapy."], ["2009", "Alcon acquires ESBATech, developer of the brolucizumab fragment."], ["2019", "FDA approves brolucizumab (Beovu) for wet AMD."], ["2020", "Labeling is updated with warnings for retinal vasculitis and retinal vascular occlusion."], ["2022", "Brolucizumab is approved for diabetic macular edema."]],
  "cell": "VEGF-A released by stressed retinal cells binds VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2 on endothelial cells, activating tyrosine kinase signaling that makes vessel cells divide, sprout and leak. Brolucizumab is a humanized single-chain variable fragment (scFv), the antigen-binding part of an antibody with no constant regions, at about 26 kilodaltons. It binds all major isoforms of VEGF-A and blocks their binding to VEGFR-1 and VEGFR-2. Without VEGF signaling, endothelial proliferation and permeability fall, choroidal neovascular membranes regress and fluid in the retina resorbs. Its small size lets a high molar amount fit into one injection and may help it penetrate retinal tissue, aiming for longer-lasting effect.",
  "body": [["Retina", "Retinal fluid and swelling decrease, which can stabilize or improve central vision."], ["Choroid", "Abnormal new vessels beneath the retina in wet AMD regress and stop leaking."], ["Retinal vessels", "Rarely, immune-mediated inflammation of retinal vessels (vasculitis) can block them and cause severe vision loss."], ["Eye after injection", "Floaters, conjunctival bleeding, eye pain and pressure rise relate to the injection procedure."], ["Lens", "Cataract is reported in trials, partly linked to repeated injections."], ["Whole body", "Small amounts reach the blood, and arterial clot events are a class warning for anti-VEGF agents."]],
  "journey": "Brolucizumab is injected into the vitreous by an eye specialist. It diffuses into the retina and choroid and then slowly exits the eye. Small amounts reach the blood, peaking within about a day, with a systemic half-life of about four and a half days. Being a small protein, it is broken down by proteolysis, and some may be filtered by the kidneys. Maintenance injections can be spaced weeks to months apart.",
  "sidefx": "Intraocular inflammation is more common than with some other anti-VEGF drugs, and in some people it involves the retinal vessels, leading to occlusive vasculitis. This is thought to be an immune response, and anti-drug antibodies are associated with higher risk. Endophthalmitis, retinal detachment and pressure spikes come from the injection. Arterial clot events are a theoretical class effect of VEGF blockade. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Brolucizumab is roughly one-sixth the size of a full antibody, which is why so many more molecules fit into each injection.",
  "src": ["Beovu (brolucizumab-dbll) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"pegcetacoplan": {
  "origin": "Pegcetacoplan descends from compstatin, a small ring-shaped peptide found in 1996 by John Lambris and colleagues at the University of Pennsylvania. They screened huge libraries of random peptides displayed on bacteriophages and found one that binds complement component C3, the central hub of the complement system. Apellis Pharmaceuticals built on this work, pairing two compstatin-like peptides with polyethylene glycol (PEG) to make the drug last longer. Under the name Empaveli it was approved in 2021 as an under-the-skin treatment for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. In 2023 the FDA approved an eye injection, Syfovre, as the first treatment for geographic atrophy, the advanced dry form of age-related macular degeneration.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "The Lambris lab at the University of Pennsylvania discovers compstatin, a C3-binding peptide."], ["2021", "FDA approves pegcetacoplan (Empaveli) for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria."], ["2023", "FDA approves Syfovre as the first treatment for geographic atrophy."]],
  "cell": "The complement system is a cascade of blood and tissue proteins. All three pathways (classical, lectin and alternative) meet at C3. C3 convertases split C3 into C3a, an inflammatory signal, and C3b, which coats cells (opsonization), amplifies the alternative pathway and builds the C5 convertase. C5 is then split into C5a and C5b, and C5b starts the membrane attack complex (C5b-9) that punches holes in cells. Genetic and pathology studies link overactive complement to retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptor loss in geographic atrophy. Pegcetacoplan binds C3 and C3b and blocks C3 cleavage and the amplification loop. Less C3b deposition, less C3a and less downstream C5 activation and membrane attack complex means less complement-driven injury, so atrophy lesions grow more slowly. It does not restore cells that are already lost.",
  "body": [["Retina", "Geographic atrophy lesions enlarge more slowly over months, but vision already lost does not come back."], ["Choroid", "New wet macular degeneration (choroidal neovascularization) develops in some treated eyes, so eyes are monitored for it."], ["Inside the eye", "Inflammation, including rare retinal vasculitis that can block vessels and cause severe vision loss, has been reported."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, eye discomfort, floaters and a rise in eye pressure are linked to the injection procedure."], ["Whole body", "Systemic levels after eye injection are low, so body-wide complement suppression is not expected as it is with the subcutaneous product."]],
  "journey": "Syfovre is injected into the vitreous by an eye specialist, where the PEG chains slow its clearance so each dose lasts weeks. The drug diffuses toward the retina and slowly leaves the eye. Systemic concentrations are low. It is expected to be broken down into peptides and amino acids, while the PEG portion is cleared slowly by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Endophthalmitis, retinal detachment and pressure rises come from the injection itself. Intraocular inflammation and occlusive retinal vasculitis are thought to be immune reactions, possibly related to the drug or its PEG component. The higher rate of new wet AMD may relate to how complement inhibition changes the local balance of signals in the choroid. The subcutaneous form carries a boxed warning for serious infections with encapsulated bacteria, but that risk relates to body-wide complement blockade. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Compstatin was found by phage display, a method that lets viruses that infect bacteria carry billions of random peptides so scientists can fish out the few that stick to a target.",
  "src": ["Syfovre (pegcetacoplan) FDA label", "Empaveli (pegcetacoplan) FDA label", "Sahu A, Kay BK, Lambris JD. J Immunol 1996"]
},
"avacincaptad-pegol": {
  "origin": "Avacincaptad pegol is an aptamer, a short strand of synthetic RNA that folds into a shape that grabs a protein target, a little like an antibody. Aptamers became possible in 1990 when two groups, Craig Tuerk and Larry Gold, and Andrew Ellington and Jack Szostak, described methods to evolve RNA strands in a test tube to bind targets (the method called SELEX). The first aptamer drug, pegaptanib, was approved for wet macular degeneration in 2004. Avacincaptad pegol, which binds complement C5, was first developed as ARC1905 by Archemix, then by Ophthotech, later renamed IVERIC bio, which Astellas acquired in 2023. The FDA approved it as Izervay in 2023 for geographic atrophy.",
  "timeline": [["1990", "Tuerk and Gold, and Ellington and Szostak, describe in vitro selection of RNA aptamers."], ["2004", "Pegaptanib becomes the first FDA-approved aptamer drug."], ["2023", "FDA approves avacincaptad pegol (Izervay) for geographic atrophy."]],
  "cell": "In the complement cascade, C5 convertase splits C5 into C5a and C5b. C5a is a strong anaphylatoxin that attracts and activates inflammatory cells. C5b joins C6, C7, C8 and several C9 molecules to form the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), a pore that can damage retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptor cells. Avacincaptad pegol is a pegylated RNA aptamer that binds C5 and blocks its cleavage. Neither C5a nor C5b is formed, so the membrane attack complex does not assemble and C5a-driven inflammation is reduced. Unlike C3 inhibition, upstream C3 activity, including opsonization by C3b, is preserved. Less terminal complement injury slows the growth of geographic atrophy lesions.",
  "body": [["Retina", "Geographic atrophy lesions grow more slowly over months; lost vision is not restored."], ["Choroid", "Conversion to wet macular degeneration (choroidal neovascularization) can occur, so eyes are watched for new bleeding or fluid."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, eye pain, blurred vision and a rise in eye pressure follow the injection procedure."], ["Inside the eye", "Intraocular inflammation and rare endophthalmitis can occur."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood, so body-wide complement effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "The drug is injected into the vitreous by an eye specialist. The PEG chain slows its exit from the eye, giving long residence in the vitreous. It is expected to be broken down by endonucleases and exonucleases, enzymes that cut RNA, and very little reaches the circulation. Effects show only over months of repeated injections.",
  "sidefx": "Most serious events relate to the injection: endophthalmitis from bacteria introduced by the needle, retinal detachment from traction and pressure spikes from added volume. Conversion to wet AMD may reflect shifts in local signaling when complement is blocked. Inflammation inside the eye can occur. Because the RNA is chemically modified and pegylated, it resists breakdown and does not act like a gene medicine. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Aptamers are sometimes called chemical antibodies because they are built from RNA or DNA instead of protein, yet bind their targets with antibody-like precision.",
  "src": ["Izervay (avacincaptad pegol) FDA label", "Tuerk C, Gold L. Science 1990", "Ellington AD, Szostak JW. Nature 1990"]
},
"verteporfin": {
  "origin": "Verteporfin is a benzoporphyrin derivative developed in Vancouver, Canada. Chemist David Dolphin at the University of British Columbia and immunologist Julia Levy co-founded Quadra Logic Technologies (later QLT) to develop light-activated drugs. Porphyrins are pigment molecules related to the heme in blood, and they can absorb light and pass the energy to oxygen. QLT and Ciba Vision (Novartis) developed verteporfin for photodynamic therapy of abnormal blood vessels under the retina. The FDA approved it as Visudyne in 2000. It was one of the first treatments that could slow vision loss in wet macular degeneration, before anti-VEGF injections largely replaced it.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Quadra Logic Technologies is founded in Vancouver to develop photodynamic drugs."], ["2000", "FDA approves verteporfin (Visudyne) for photodynamic therapy of wet macular degeneration."], ["2006", "Anti-VEGF injections become first-line therapy and largely replace photodynamic therapy for wet AMD."]],
  "cell": "Verteporfin is a light-activated drug with no receptor target. In the blood it rides mainly on low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and other lipoproteins. Fast-growing new vessel cells in choroidal neovascularization express many LDL receptors, so they take up more of the drug. About 15 minutes after the infusion starts, a nonthermal laser at 689 nanometers is shone only on the abnormal vessels. Verteporfin absorbs this red light and moves into an excited state, then passes the energy to oxygen to make singlet oxygen and other reactive oxygen species. These damage the endothelial cell membranes, activate platelets and cause local clotting, so the leaking vessels close. Tissue that is not lit is largely spared because the drug only becomes toxic where the light hits.",
  "body": [["Choroidal vessels", "Abnormal leaking vessels are sealed by clotting, which slows vision loss in certain forms of wet macular degeneration."], ["Retina", "Some people notice temporary blurred vision, visual field changes or, rarely, severe loss of vision within a week."], ["Skin", "Drug circulating in the skin makes it sensitive to sunlight and bright indoor light for about five days, so burns can occur."], ["Eyes", "The eyes are also sensitive to bright light during the precaution period."], ["Infusion site", "Pain, swelling and inflammation can occur, and leakage out of the vein can damage tissue that is then exposed to light."], ["Back", "Back pain during the infusion is a known reaction of uncertain cause."]],
  "journey": "Verteporfin is given by intravenous infusion and is carried by lipoproteins and albumin. It reaches the neovascular tissue within minutes, which is why the laser is applied at a fixed time after the infusion starts. Its plasma half-life is about five to six hours. Liver and plasma esterases form a minor diacid metabolite, and the drug leaves mainly in the bile and feces, with almost none in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Photosensitivity happens because the drug is in skin and eye tissues and will make reactive oxygen anywhere strong light falls, so direct sunlight and bright lights are avoided for five days. Extravasation is dangerous for the same reason: drug in the tissues plus light causes burns. Severe decreased vision within a week can reflect injury to normal choroidal vessels or retinal pigment epithelium. Infusion back pain and rare anaphylaxis can occur. There is no specific antidote; protection from light is the key precaution.",
  "fun": "Researchers later found that verteporfin also blocks the YAP-TEAD signaling complex in cells, without any light, and it is widely used as a laboratory tool in cancer biology.",
  "src": ["Visudyne (verteporfin) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"lotilaner-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Lotilaner began as a veterinary medicine. It belongs to the isoxazoline class of parasite killers, and it came out of animal health research and was marketed by Elanco as Credelio, a chewable flea and tick treatment for dogs approved in 2018. Researchers at Tarsus Pharmaceuticals realized that the same chemistry could kill Demodex mites, tiny relatives of spiders that live in human eyelash follicles and can cause Demodex blepharitis, with crusty collarettes at the base of the lashes. They developed a lotilaner eye drop, and in 2023 the FDA approved Xdemvy as the first treatment specifically for Demodex blepharitis.",
  "timeline": [["2018", "Lotilaner is approved for dogs as the flea and tick product Credelio."], ["2023", "FDA approves Xdemvy lotilaner eye drops for Demodex blepharitis."]],
  "cell": "Demodex folliculorum and Demodex brevis are mites with nerve and muscle cells controlled by gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)-gated chloride channels. In mites, GABA opening these channels lets chloride in, which calms nerve and muscle activity. Lotilaner is an isoxazoline that blocks these mite GABA-gated chloride channels. Without this inhibitory signal, mite nerves fire uncontrollably, causing spastic paralysis and death. The drug is lipophilic, so it concentrates in the oily lid margin and follicles where the mites live. It has much lower affinity for mammalian GABA-A receptors, which gives a wide safety margin in people.",
  "body": [["Eyelash follicles", "Mites in the follicles are killed, so mite counts fall over weeks."], ["Lash base", "Collarettes, the waxy cylindrical crusts around lash bases, clear as mite activity stops."], ["Eyelid margin", "Redness and inflammation of the lid margin improve as the mite load drops."], ["Eye on dosing", "Stinging and burning at instillation occur in about one in ten people."], ["Eyelid glands", "Chalazion or stye is reported uncommonly."]],
  "journey": "The drops are placed on the eye and spread to the lid margin, where the lipophilic drug concentrates in the oily secretions and follicles. A full course lasts six weeks so that mites hatching from eggs are also killed. Systemic exposure is low, and metabolism and excretion after eye use are not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Stinging and burning come from the solution on the ocular surface. Chalazion, stye and punctate keratitis are uncommon. Because lotilaner binds mammalian GABA-A channels weakly, nervous system effects are not expected at the tiny exposures from eye drops. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The drug in a human eye drop for eyelash mites is the same molecule sold as a chewable that kills fleas and ticks on dogs.",
  "src": ["Xdemvy (lotilaner ophthalmic solution) FDA label", "Credelio (lotilaner) FDA veterinary label"]
},
"ciprofloxacin-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Ciprofloxacin was discovered in 1981 by chemist Klaus Grohe and colleagues at Bayer in Germany. Adding a cyclopropyl group to the fluoroquinolone core gave a drug with far stronger activity, especially against gram-negative bacteria such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Oral ciprofloxacin was approved in the US in 1987 and became one of the most used antibiotics in the world. Alcon developed eye forms, Ciloxan drops and later an ointment, which became standard for bacterial conjunctivitis and corneal ulcers, including Pseudomonas ulcers in contact lens wearers.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Nalidixic acid, the first quinolone, is discovered."], ["1981", "Klaus Grohe's team at Bayer synthesizes ciprofloxacin."], ["1987", "Oral ciprofloxacin is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Ciprofloxacin eye drops and ointment (Ciloxan) become available in the US."]],
  "cell": "Ciprofloxacin enters bacteria through porin channels and binds DNA gyrase (GyrA and GyrB subunits) and topoisomerase IV. These enzymes cut both strands of DNA, pass another segment through and reseal the cut, which relieves supercoiling and separates daughter chromosomes. Ciprofloxacin traps the enzyme on cut DNA, forming a stable cleavage complex. Replication forks crash into these complexes and the DNA breaks, which triggers cell death. Killing is concentration dependent. Gyrase is the main target in gram-negative bacteria and topoisomerase IV in gram-positive bacteria. Human topoisomerases are much less affected.",
  "body": [["Cornea", "In bacterial corneal ulcers, high drug levels stop bacterial growth, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and allow healing."], ["Conjunctiva", "Bacterial conjunctivitis resolves as Haemophilus, Staphylococcus and other organisms are killed."], ["Corneal deposits", "A white crystalline precipitate can form in a corneal ulcer as drug crystallizes at the pH of the eye surface."], ["Ocular surface", "Burning, crusting and foreign body sensation can occur after drops."], ["Taste", "Drug draining down the tear duct can leave a bad taste."]],
  "journey": "Drops or ointment provide high concentrations in tears and corneal tissue. Most of the dose is washed away by tears and drains through the nasolacrimal duct. Only a small amount reaches the blood, where ciprofloxacin has a half-life of about four hours and is cleared mainly by the kidneys. Systemic effects are not expected.",
  "sidefx": "The white corneal precipitate happens because ciprofloxacin is less soluble at the near-neutral pH of the eye surface than in the acidic solution in the bottle. It usually clears and does not stop treatment. Burning and discomfort come from the drop. Prolonged use can allow resistant bacteria or fungi to overgrow. Hypersensitivity, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur. The tendon, nerve and heart rhythm warnings of oral fluoroquinolones are not expected with eye use. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Ciprofloxacin became a household name in 2001 when it was widely used to protect people exposed to anthrax spores in the US.",
  "src": ["Ciloxan (ciprofloxacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"tobramycin-dexamethasone-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "This combination joins two older drugs. Tobramycin is an aminoglycoside from the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius, part of a group of compounds found by Eli Lilly researchers in the late 1960s and approved in the 1970s. Dexamethasone is a fluorinated corticosteroid made at Merck in the late 1950s. Alcon combined them in TobraDex, a suspension and ointment for eye inflammation where bacterial infection is present or likely, such as after surgery or with blepharitis. A version with a lower steroid strength in a thicker vehicle, TobraDex ST, was approved later.",
  "timeline": [["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after development at Merck."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly researchers find tobramycin in Streptomyces tenebrarius cultures."], ["1970s", "Tobramycin is approved for clinical use in the US."], ["1980s", "TobraDex ophthalmic suspension becomes available."]],
  "cell": "Tobramycin crosses bacterial membranes by an oxygen-dependent transport step, so it works on aerobic bacteria. Inside, it binds the 30S ribosomal subunit, blocks formation of the initiation complex and makes the ribosome misread mRNA. Faulty proteins insert into the membrane, letting in more drug, and the cell dies (bactericidal). It covers many gram-negative organisms, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Staphylococcus. Dexamethasone enters cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor, which moves to the nucleus. It induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and prostaglandin and leukotriene production, and represses NF-kB and AP-1 driven cytokines and adhesion molecules. The steroid calms the inflammation, while the antibiotic deals with the bacteria that steroid suppression might otherwise let grow.",
  "body": [["Eyelids and conjunctiva", "Inflammation such as blepharitis or postoperative swelling improves while susceptible bacteria are controlled."], ["Anterior segment", "Less redness, swelling, fibrin and cellular infiltration as the steroid blocks inflammatory signaling."], ["Eye pressure", "The steroid can raise intraocular pressure in some people by changing the trabecular meshwork, so pressure is monitored with use beyond about ten days."], ["Lens", "Prolonged steroid use can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Cornea", "Healing can slow and herpes simplex or fungal keratitis can flare because local immunity is suppressed."], ["Immune system", "Tobramycin can cause local allergy with itching and lid swelling."]],
  "journey": "The suspension is shaken and placed on the eye. Tobramycin acts mainly on the surface and in superficial tissues, while dexamethasone penetrates the cornea into the anterior chamber. Effects last several hours per dose. Systemic absorption is minimal. Any absorbed dexamethasone is metabolized by liver CYP3A4, and any absorbed tobramycin is cleared unchanged by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "The steroid part drives most risks: raised eye pressure from trabecular meshwork changes, cataract, delayed healing, thinning of the cornea or sclera and secondary infection, including fungal and herpes keratitis, because immune defenses are suppressed. Tobramycin can cause local allergy and may let resistant organisms or fungi overgrow. The kidney and ear toxicity of injected aminoglycosides is not expected with eye use. There is no antidote; effects are managed by stopping under supervision.",
  "src": ["TobraDex (tobramycin and dexamethasone) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"neomycin-polymyxin-dexamethasone": {
  "origin": "This three-part eye product brings together antibiotics and a steroid from the golden age of antibiotic discovery. Neomycin was isolated in 1949 by Selman Waksman and Hubert Lechevalier at Rutgers University from the soil bacterium Streptomyces fradiae. Polymyxins were found in 1947 in Bacillus polymyxa. Dexamethasone was made at Merck in the late 1950s. Alcon combined the three in Maxitrol suspension and ointment, giving broad antibacterial coverage plus strong anti-inflammatory action for steroid-responsive eye inflammation with bacterial infection or risk of it.",
  "timeline": [["1947", "Polymyxins are discovered from Bacillus polymyxa."], ["1949", "Waksman and Lechevalier isolate neomycin from Streptomyces fradiae."], ["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after development at Merck."]],
  "cell": "Neomycin is an aminoglycoside that binds the 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of mRNA and faulty protein synthesis, which kills many gram-negative bacteria and Staphylococcus aureus. It is weak against Pseudomonas and streptococci. Polymyxin B fills the Pseudomonas gap: it is a cationic peptide that binds lipid A of lipopolysaccharide, displaces stabilizing calcium and magnesium and disrupts gram-negative outer and inner membranes like a detergent. Dexamethasone binds the glucocorticoid receptor, which moves into the nucleus, induces annexin A1 to inhibit phospholipase A2, and represses NF-kB and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes. Together, the antibiotics cover a broad spectrum while the steroid calms inflammation.",
  "body": [["Eyelids and conjunctiva", "Steroid-responsive inflammation such as blepharitis or conjunctivitis improves while bacteria are controlled."], ["Anterior segment", "Postoperative redness, swelling and cell infiltration decrease."], ["Skin around the eye", "Neomycin is a common contact allergen, so some people develop itchy, red, swollen lids that can look like worsening infection."], ["Eye pressure", "The steroid can raise intraocular pressure in steroid responders."], ["Lens", "Long courses of steroid can cause posterior subcapsular cataract."], ["Cornea", "Herpes simplex and fungal keratitis can worsen because local immunity is suppressed, and healing slows."]],
  "journey": "The suspension or ointment acts on the ocular surface and lids. Dexamethasone penetrates the cornea into the eye, while the antibiotics stay mostly on the surface. Systemic absorption is minimal. Any absorbed dexamethasone is metabolized by liver CYP3A4, and absorbed neomycin and polymyxin B are cleared by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Neomycin sensitization is a classic cause of allergic contact dermatitis, with lid itching and swelling caused by a delayed T-cell reaction to the drug. Steroid effects explain raised eye pressure, cataract, slowed healing, thinning of the cornea or sclera and secondary fungal or herpes infection. Antibiotic use can allow resistant organisms to overgrow. Ear and kidney toxicity of systemic aminoglycosides and polymyxins is not expected with eye use. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Neomycin was named Allergen of the Year in 2010 by the American Contact Dermatitis Society because it so often causes skin allergy.",
  "src": ["Maxitrol (neomycin, polymyxin B and dexamethasone) FDA label", "Waksman SA, Lechevalier HA. Science 1949"]
},
"methazolamide": {
  "origin": "Methazolamide comes from the sulfonamide family. In 1940 researchers found that sulfanilamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase. Chemists at Lederle Laboratories then made acetazolamide in the early 1950s, and in 1954 ophthalmologist Bernard Becker showed that it lowers eye pressure. Lederle chemists modified the molecule further, adding a methyl group to the thiadiazole ring, to make methazolamide. It was introduced in the late 1950s as Neptazane. It is more lipid soluble than acetazolamide, enters the eye better and depends less on the kidneys for clearance. The brand has been discontinued, but generic tablets are available.",
  "timeline": [["1940", "Sulfanilamide is found to inhibit carbonic anhydrase."], ["1954", "Bernard Becker reports acetazolamide lowers eye pressure in glaucoma."], ["1950s", "Lederle introduces methazolamide (Neptazane)."]],
  "cell": "Methazolamide noncompetitively inhibits carbonic anhydrase, mainly isoenzyme II, in the ciliary process epithelium. Carbonic anhydrase speeds the reaction of carbon dioxide and water into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate secretion into the posterior chamber carries sodium and water with it to form aqueous humor. With the enzyme blocked, less bicarbonate, sodium and water move into the eye, so aqueous production and intraocular pressure fall. In the proximal tubule of the kidney, carbonic anhydrase inhibition reduces bicarbonate reabsorption, causing a mild bicarbonate diuresis and metabolic acidosis. This renal effect is weaker than with acetazolamide.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous humor production falls and intraocular pressure drops within a few hours, with peak effect at six to eight hours."], ["Kidneys", "Bicarbonate is lost in the urine, causing mild metabolic acidosis and more urine; potassium can be lost too."], ["Nerves", "Tingling of fingers, toes and around the mouth is common, likely related to acidosis and changes in nerve ion balance."], ["Taste", "Carbonated drinks taste flat because carbonic anhydrase on sour-sensing taste cells helps detect carbonation."], ["Brain and mood", "Fatigue, malaise, depression and confusion can occur, especially in older adults."], ["Urinary tract", "Kidney stones can form because alkaline urine and less citrate favor calcium phosphate stones."]],
  "journey": "Methazolamide is taken by mouth and absorbed well from the gut. It is moderately protein bound and lipid soluble, so it enters the eye easily. Effects begin in two to four hours. It is partly metabolized in the liver, with roughly a quarter excreted unchanged in urine. Its half-life is about 14 hours, and pressure lowering lasts 10 to 18 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from carbonic anhydrase inhibition throughout the body. Kidney effects cause metabolic acidosis and low potassium. Acidosis and electrolyte shifts lead to tingling, fatigue and loss of appetite. Taste changes come from blocked carbonic anhydrase in taste cells. Kidney stones relate to altered urine chemistry. Because it is a sulfonamide, rare but serious reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, aplastic anemia, agranulocytosis and liver necrosis can occur. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Research in mice showed that carbonic anhydrase on sour-sensing taste cells is what lets us sense the fizz of carbonation, which explains why these drugs make soda taste flat.",
  "src": ["Methazolamide tablets FDA label", "Becker B. Am J Ophthalmol 1954", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"ganciclovir-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Ganciclovir is a guanosine analog made in the early 1980s, and researchers at Syntex in California were among those who developed it. It was approved in 1989 as an intravenous drug (Cytovene) for cytomegalovirus retinitis, a blinding infection in people with AIDS. Ganciclovir is also active against herpes simplex virus. A gel for herpes simplex eye infection was first used in Europe, and in 2009 the FDA approved Zirgan, ganciclovir eye gel, for acute herpetic keratitis with dendritic ulcers.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ganciclovir is synthesized and developed, including work at Syntex."], ["1989", "Intravenous ganciclovir is approved for CMV retinitis."], ["2009", "FDA approves ganciclovir ophthalmic gel (Zirgan) for herpetic keratitis."]],
  "cell": "Ganciclovir is an inactive prodrug until it gets into a virus-infected cell. In cells infected with herpes simplex virus, the viral thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate to make ganciclovir monophosphate. Normal cells do this poorly, which concentrates activation in infected cells. Cellular kinases then add two more phosphates to form ganciclovir triphosphate. This molecule competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate at the viral DNA polymerase, and when incorporated it slows and eventually stops chain elongation. Viral replication in the corneal epithelium falls, so the branching dendritic ulcer heals.",
  "body": [["Cornea", "Viral replication in the corneal epithelium falls, and dendritic ulcers heal over days."], ["Vision", "The gel causes brief blurred vision after each application."], ["Ocular surface", "Irritation, redness and punctate keratitis can occur from the gel and from the drug's effect on surface cells."], ["Whole body", "Very little drug reaches the blood, so the bone marrow toxicity of IV ganciclovir is not expected."]],
  "journey": "The gel is applied into the lower conjunctival sac, where it spreads over the cornea and stays longer than a drop. The drug enters corneal epithelial cells, and in infected cells it is phosphorylated to the active triphosphate. Systemic absorption is minimal. By comparison, systemic ganciclovir is cleared by the kidneys with a half-life of about two and a half to three and a half hours.",
  "sidefx": "Blurred vision comes from the gel coating the eye. Irritation and punctate keratitis reflect contact of the gel with surface cells, and some uptake by healthy cells. Systemic ganciclovir causes neutropenia, anemia and thrombocytopenia because it also affects dividing marrow cells, but these effects are not expected with eye gel because blood levels are tiny. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The same molecule used as a gel for a cold sore virus in the cornea was first approved as an IV drug to save the sight of people with AIDS-related CMV retinitis.",
  "src": ["Zirgan (ganciclovir ophthalmic gel) FDA label", "Cytovene (ganciclovir) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"trifluridine-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Trifluridine, also called trifluorothymidine, was first made in the early 1960s by Charles Heidelberger and colleagues at the University of Wisconsin, the same researcher behind the cancer drug 5-fluorouracil. It was studied first as an anticancer agent. Around the same time, Herbert Kaufman showed in 1962 that idoxuridine, a related nucleoside, could treat herpes infection of the cornea, the first proven antiviral therapy. Trifluridine proved more potent and better tolerated for herpes keratitis, and the eye drop Viroptic was approved by the FDA in 1980. Decades later, trifluridine returned to cancer care when it was combined with tipiracil in an oral tablet approved in 2015 for colorectal cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Herbert Kaufman reports idoxuridine for herpes keratitis, the first clinical antiviral therapy."], ["1960s", "Charles Heidelberger's group synthesizes trifluridine."], ["1980", "FDA approves trifluridine eye drops (Viroptic)."], ["2015", "Trifluridine with tipiracil is approved as an oral cancer treatment."]],
  "cell": "Trifluridine is a thymidine analog with three fluorine atoms on its methyl group. Cellular thymidine kinase phosphorylates it to the monophosphate, which inhibits thymidylate synthase and reduces the supply of normal thymidine. Further phosphorylation produces trifluridine triphosphate, which DNA polymerases incorporate into DNA in place of thymidine. The resulting DNA is defective, and herpes simplex virus cannot produce working copies of its genome, so viral replication in the corneal epithelium falls. Unlike acyclovir and ganciclovir, trifluridine does not need viral thymidine kinase for activation, so it also enters DNA of uninfected cells. That lack of selectivity explains its toxicity to the corneal surface with prolonged use.",
  "body": [["Cornea", "Herpes simplex replication in the epithelium falls, so dendritic and geographic ulcers heal over days to about two weeks."], ["Corneal epithelium", "Because healthy cells also take up the drug, prolonged use can cause punctate keratopathy and slow healing."], ["Eyelids", "Lid swelling and mild burning or stinging can occur after drops."], ["Whole body", "Systemic absorption is negligible, so the bone marrow effects seen with oral trifluridine are not expected."]],
  "journey": "Drops are placed on the eye frequently because the drug is cleared quickly. It penetrates the corneal epithelium and is rapidly metabolized in the eye and blood. Systemic absorption is minimal, and trifluridine that does reach the blood has a half-life of only about 12 minutes. The short presence on the eye is why dosing is frequent.",
  "sidefx": "Burning and stinging come from the solution. Superficial punctate keratopathy and epithelial toxicity happen because trifluridine is incorporated into the DNA of normal dividing corneal cells, not just virus-infected ones. This is why the course is limited in length. Hypersensitivity and stromal edema can occur. There is no antidote; toxicity resolves after stopping.",
  "fun": "The same molecule that heals herpes ulcers on the eye is also part of an oral pill for advanced colorectal cancer.",
  "src": ["Viroptic (trifluridine) FDA label", "Kaufman HE. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1962", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"natamycin": {
  "origin": "Natamycin was isolated in 1955 by scientists at the Dutch company Gist-Brocades from a strain of the soil bacterium Streptomyces natalensis, collected in soil near Pietermaritzburg in the Natal province of South Africa, which gave the drug its name. It was first called pimaricin. Natamycin is a polyene like amphotericin B and nystatin, but it binds fungal sterol without making large pores, and it is poorly absorbed, so it is well suited to surface infections. It became the only antifungal eye drop approved by the FDA, marketed as Natacyn, and is a key treatment for fungal keratitis caused by filamentous fungi such as Fusarium. It is also widely used as a food preservative.",
  "timeline": [["1955", "Natamycin is isolated from Streptomyces natalensis at Gist-Brocades in the Netherlands."]],
  "cell": "Natamycin binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes, which human cells replace with cholesterol. Unlike amphotericin B and nystatin, it does not form large ion-leaking pores. Instead, binding ergosterol blocks ergosterol-dependent processes in the membrane, such as amino acid and glucose transport proteins and the fusion of vacuoles. Without these, fungal growth stops, and at higher concentrations the cells die. It is active against filamentous fungi such as Fusarium and Aspergillus and against Candida.",
  "body": [["Cornea", "Fungal growth in the corneal stroma is halted, which allows corneal ulcers to heal over weeks."], ["Conjunctiva", "Irritation, redness and swelling of the conjunctiva can occur."], ["Vision", "The thick suspension coats the eye and blurs vision temporarily."], ["Whole body", "Natamycin is barely absorbed, so systemic effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "The suspension is placed on the eye frequently at first. It adheres to the ulcer and the cornea, and drug penetrates the corneal stroma, best when the epithelium is missing over the ulcer. Penetration into deep stroma and the inside of the eye is limited. Systemic absorption is negligible, and it is not significantly metabolized.",
  "sidefx": "Eye irritation, chemosis and redness come from the suspension on inflamed tissue. Blurred vision is due to the particles in the suspension. Because natamycin acts on ergosterol rather than cholesterol and is not absorbed, the kidney toxicity of amphotericin B is not seen. Its limited penetration means deep infections may not respond. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Natamycin is approved as a food additive (E235 in Europe) and is used on the surface of many cheeses to stop mold growth.",
  "src": ["Natacyn (natamycin) FDA label", "Struyk AP et al. Antibiotics Annual 1957-1958"]
},
"dexamethasone-intravitreal-implant": {
  "origin": "Treating swelling at the back of the eye with steroid drops does not work well, because drops cannot deliver enough drug to the retina. The solution was to place a tiny slow-release steroid implant directly inside the eye. Oculex Pharmaceuticals developed an early version, and Allergan acquired the company in 2003. Allergan used a biodegradable polymer made of lactic and glycolic acid (PLGA), the same type of material used in dissolving stitches, and created an injector that places the rod-shaped implant into the vitreous through a fine needle. The FDA approved Ozurdex in 2009 for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion, then for noninfectious posterior uveitis in 2010 and diabetic macular edema in 2014.",
  "timeline": [["1958", "Dexamethasone is approved after development at Merck."], ["2003", "Allergan acquires Oculex Pharmaceuticals, developer of the implant technology."], ["2009", "FDA approves Ozurdex for macular edema after retinal vein occlusion."], ["2010", "Ozurdex is approved for noninfectious posterior uveitis."], ["2014", "Ozurdex is approved for diabetic macular edema."]],
  "cell": "Dexamethasone released from the implant diffuses into retinal cells and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor complex moves into the nucleus and changes gene transcription. It induces annexin A1, which inhibits phospholipase A2 and the prostaglandin and leukotriene cascade. It also blocks NF-kB and AP-1, turning down inflammatory cytokines, adhesion molecules and VEGF. In retinal vessels, this tightens endothelial junctions and stabilizes the blood-retinal barrier, so less fluid leaks into the macula. In uveitis, fewer inflammatory cells enter the vitreous. The PLGA polymer slowly breaks down by hydrolysis into lactic acid and glycolic acid, which the body handles normally.",
  "body": [["Macula", "Swelling in the central retina decreases over weeks, which can improve vision in vein occlusion and diabetic macular edema."], ["Vitreous", "In uveitis, inflammation and haze in the vitreous decrease."], ["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure rises in a sizeable share of eyes because steroids reduce outflow through the trabecular meshwork; some need drops or surgery."], ["Lens", "Cataract develops or worsens in many eyes over time with repeated implants."], ["Eye after injection", "Conjunctival bleeding, pain and floaters relate to the injection procedure."], ["Whole body", "Plasma levels are mostly undetectable, so systemic steroid effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "The implant is injected into the vitreous and releases dexamethasone gradually as the polymer erodes. Peak effect comes at about two months in vein occlusion studies, and benefit lasts about three to six months. The polymer degrades completely to lactic and glycolic acid. Plasma dexamethasone is mostly undetectable; any absorbed drug would be metabolized by liver CYP3A4.",
  "sidefx": "Steroid effects inside the eye are the main issue. Raised eye pressure comes from glucocorticoid-induced changes in trabecular meshwork cells that block outflow. Cataract reflects steroid effects on lens fiber cells. Endophthalmitis and retinal detachment are risks of any injection into the eye. The implant can migrate into the front chamber, especially in eyes without an intact lens capsule, where it can damage the cornea. There is no antidote; pressure is managed with drops or surgery.",
  "fun": "Ozurdex dissolves completely inside the eye, breaking down into the same lactic and glycolic acids that dissolving stitches leave behind.",
  "src": ["Ozurdex (dexamethasone intravitreal implant) FDA label"]
},
"fluocinolone-intravitreal-implant": {
  "origin": "Fluocinolone acetonide is a potent fluorinated corticosteroid first made around 1960 by chemists at Syntex and used widely in skin creams. For chronic eye inflammation, the idea was to place a small device inside the eye that releases steroid steadily for years. Control Delivery Systems, working with pharmacologist Paul Ashton, developed the first such device, Retisert, which is sutured into the eye during surgery and was approved in 2005 for chronic noninfectious posterior uveitis. A tiny injectable version followed: Iluvien, developed by Alimera Sciences with pSivida, was approved in 2014 for diabetic macular edema, and Yutiq was approved in 2018 for posterior uveitis.",
  "timeline": [["1960", "Fluocinolone acetonide is developed at Syntex around this time."], ["2005", "Retisert, a surgically placed fluocinolone implant, is approved for posterior uveitis."], ["2014", "FDA approves Iluvien injectable implant for diabetic macular edema."], ["2018", "Yutiq injectable implant is approved for chronic noninfectious posterior uveitis."]],
  "cell": "Fluocinolone acetonide released from the implant enters retinal and choroidal cells and binds the glucocorticoid receptor. The receptor complex moves into the nucleus, where it induces anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin A1 (which inhibits phospholipase A2) and represses NF-kB and AP-1 targets including cytokines, adhesion molecules and VEGF. This reduces leukocyte stasis in retinal vessels and stabilizes tight junctions of the blood-retinal barrier, so less fluid leaks into the macula. The implant is a tiny nonbioerodible polyimide tube with a membrane that lets drug out at a low, steady rate, giving continuous steroid effect for about three years.",
  "body": [["Macula", "Chronic diabetic macular edema decreases with steady low-level steroid exposure, which can improve or stabilize vision."], ["Posterior segment", "In uveitis, recurrences of inflammation in the back of the eye become less frequent."], ["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure rises in many eyes because steroids reduce trabecular outflow; some need drops or glaucoma surgery."], ["Lens", "Cataract is very common over the three-year release period in eyes that still have their natural lens."], ["Eye after injection", "Bleeding under the conjunctiva, eye pain and floaters relate to the procedure, and the implant itself may be seen as a floater."], ["Whole body", "Plasma levels are below quantification, so systemic steroid effects are not expected."]],
  "journey": "The injectable implant is placed into the vitreous through a fine needle. It releases fluocinolone at a low continuous rate for about 36 months. Effects build over weeks to months. Plasma levels stay below the limit of quantification. The implant does not dissolve and remains in the eye after the drug is used up.",
  "sidefx": "Long, continuous steroid exposure inside the eye explains the high rates of cataract and raised intraocular pressure. Pressure rises come from glucocorticoid effects on trabecular meshwork cells, and some eyes need glaucoma surgery. Endophthalmitis and retinal detachment are risks of injection. The implant can migrate to the front of the eye if the lens capsule is not intact. There is no antidote; the effect cannot be quickly stopped once the implant is in place.",
  "fun": "The Iluvien implant is smaller than a grain of rice and keeps releasing medicine for about three years, long after people forget it is there.",
  "src": ["Iluvien (fluocinolone acetonide intravitreal implant) FDA label", "Retisert FDA label", "Yutiq FDA label"]
},
"cenegermin": {
  "origin": "Cenegermin is a recombinant form of nerve growth factor (NGF), the first growth factor ever discovered. In the early 1950s Rita Levi-Montalcini, working with Viktor Hamburger at Washington University in St. Louis, found that a mouse tumor released a substance that made nerve fibers sprout. Biochemist Stanley Cohen helped purify the factor, and Levi-Montalcini and Cohen shared the 1986 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. In 1998 Italian researchers led by Alessandro Lambiase reported that NGF drops helped heal neurotrophic corneal ulcers, which occur when corneal nerves are damaged. The Italian company Dompe produced human NGF in E. coli, and the FDA approved Oxervate in 2018 as the first treatment for neurotrophic keratitis.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Rita Levi-Montalcini and colleagues discover nerve growth factor."], ["1986", "Levi-Montalcini and Stanley Cohen share the Nobel Prize for growth factor discoveries."], ["1998", "Lambiase and colleagues report NGF eye drops heal neurotrophic corneal ulcers."], ["2017", "Cenegermin is approved in Europe for neurotrophic keratitis."], ["2018", "FDA approves Oxervate (cenegermin) for neurotrophic keratitis."]],
  "cell": "Neurotrophic keratitis happens when the trigeminal nerve supply to the cornea is damaged, for example by herpes infection, surgery or diabetes. Without nerve signals, the corneal epithelium loses trophic support, tear secretion falls and wounds do not heal. Cenegermin is recombinant human NGF made in E. coli and is identical in sequence to the mature human protein. It binds the high-affinity receptor TrkA, a tyrosine kinase, which activates the PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways to promote cell survival, proliferation and differentiation. It also binds the low-affinity p75 neurotrophin receptor. On corneal epithelial cells this drives healing of persistent defects. On corneal nerve endings it supports regrowth and sensitivity. It may also support tear secretion and the conjunctival goblet cells.",
  "body": [["Corneal epithelium", "Persistent epithelial defects and ulcers close over weeks as epithelial cells grow and differentiate."], ["Corneal nerves", "Nerve fibers may regrow, and corneal sensitivity can improve in some people."], ["Tear film", "Tear production and surface quality may improve, helping maintain healing."], ["Eye discomfort", "As nerves recover, eye pain can appear, which is the most common side effect."], ["Ocular surface", "Redness, foreign body sensation, increased tearing and corneal deposits can occur."]],
  "journey": "Cenegermin is placed on the eye as drops many times a day over an eight-week course. It acts locally on the cornea and conjunctiva and is degraded by proteases like other proteins. Systemic absorption is minimal and not clinically meaningful. Bottles are kept cold and used within a day to protect the protein.",
  "sidefx": "Eye pain is common and is thought to reflect returning corneal nerve function, since NGF also sensitizes pain-sensing nerves. Inflammation, redness and foreign body sensation come from local effects on an already damaged surface. Corneal deposits have been reported. Because it acts locally and is broken down quickly, systemic effects are not expected. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Rita Levi-Montalcini did some of her early nerve research in a home laboratory in her bedroom in Italy during World War II.",
  "src": ["Oxervate (cenegermin-bkbj) FDA label", "Lambiase A et al. N Engl J Med 1998", "Nobel Prize 1986 Physiology or Medicine"]
},
"oxymetazoline-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Oxymetazoline is an imidazoline alpha agonist developed in Germany in the early 1960s by Wolfgang Fruhstorfer at E. Merck. It became famous as a long-acting nasal decongestant spray. Researchers later reasoned that its alpha-1A activity could lift a drooping eyelid by contracting the Mueller muscle, a small smooth muscle in the upper lid that is controlled by sympathetic nerves. The company Osmotica, later RVL Pharmaceuticals, developed an eye drop, and the FDA approved Upneeq in 2020 as the first drug for acquired blepharoptosis (drooping upper eyelid) in adults. A cream form had been approved in 2017 for facial redness from rosacea.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Wolfgang Fruhstorfer at E. Merck develops oxymetazoline."], ["2017", "Oxymetazoline cream is approved for persistent facial redness of rosacea."], ["2020", "FDA approves Upneeq oxymetazoline eye drops for acquired blepharoptosis."]],
  "cell": "The superior tarsal (Mueller) muscle is a thin smooth muscle that helps hold the upper lid up. It is controlled by sympathetic nerves through alpha-adrenergic receptors. Oxymetazoline is an agonist at alpha-1A receptors, which couple to Gq. Activation increases phospholipase C activity, raises IP3 and releases calcium, causing smooth muscle contraction. Contraction of Mueller muscle raises the upper lid by about one to two millimeters, widening the eye opening. Oxymetazoline also stimulates alpha-2 receptors, and alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction of conjunctival vessels makes the eyes look whiter. It does not strengthen the main lid lifting muscle, the levator, so it helps mild drooping rather than severe ptosis.",
  "body": [["Upper eyelid", "The lid rises a small amount within minutes as Mueller muscle contracts, with effect lasting several hours."], ["Visual field", "Lifting the lid can improve the upper field of vision in people whose drooping lid blocks sight."], ["Conjunctiva", "Vasoconstriction makes the eyes look whiter, though some people get redness or irritation."], ["Cornea", "Punctate keratitis and dry eye can occur."], ["Drainage angle", "Alpha stimulation can widen the pupil slightly, which could trigger angle-closure glaucoma in eyes with narrow angles."], ["Heart and vessels", "Absorbed drug can affect blood pressure, which matters in people with heart disease or uncontrolled blood pressure."]],
  "journey": "The drop is absorbed into the eyelid tissues and conjunctiva. Lid lift begins within about 5 to 15 minutes, peaks around one to two hours and lasts about six to eight hours. A small amount reaches the blood, where oxymetazoline has a half-life of several hours based on nasal data. Little is metabolized, and it is excreted in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from alpha-adrenergic stimulation. Vasoconstriction and surface irritation lead to keratitis, redness and dryness. Pupil changes can narrow the drainage angle in predisposed eyes. Absorbed drug can raise or lower blood pressure through alpha-1 and alpha-2 effects. Headache is reported. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "The drug in a common nasal decongestant spray is the same one used in an eye drop that lifts a droopy eyelid.",
  "src": ["Upneeq (oxymetazoline ophthalmic) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"acoltremon": {
  "origin": "Acoltremon works on TRPM8, the cold and menthol sensor on nerve endings. TRPM8 was identified in 2002 by the groups of David Julius at the University of California, San Francisco, and Ardem Patapoutian at Scripps Research, work that contributed to their shared 2021 Nobel Prize. Researchers noticed that cool air and cold sensations trigger reflex tearing through corneal nerves. Aerie Pharmaceuticals developed a TRPM8 agonist eye drop, AR-15512, and Alcon acquired the program in 2022. The FDA approved acoltremon as Tryptyr in 2025 for the signs and symptoms of dry eye disease, the first approved drug that targets TRPM8.",
  "timeline": [["2002", "The TRPM8 cold and menthol receptor is identified."], ["2021", "David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian share the Nobel Prize for discovering temperature and touch receptors."], ["2022", "Alcon acquires Aerie Pharmaceuticals and the AR-15512 program."], ["2025", "FDA approves acoltremon (Tryptyr) for dry eye disease."]],
  "cell": "The cornea is the most densely innervated surface in the body, supplied by the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. Some of its sensory nerve endings carry TRPM8, a cation channel that opens with cooling or menthol. As tears evaporate and the corneal surface cools, TRPM8 channels open and the nerves fire, which reflexly drives basal tear secretion through the lacrimal functional unit (brainstem and parasympathetic nerves to the lacrimal gland). Acoltremon is a TRPM8 agonist. It opens these channels, increasing the firing of cold-sensing nerve endings. This signal travels to the brainstem, and parasympathetic output increases tear production by the lacrimal glands.",
  "body": [["Tear glands", "Tear production increases within the first days of treatment as the corneal cold-sensing reflex is activated."], ["Ocular surface", "Better tear coverage can ease dryness signs over time."], ["Cornea on dosing", "A cooling or stinging sensation at instillation is very common because the drug activates cold-sensing nerves."], ["Eye", "Redness, irritation and blurred vision can occur."], ["Whole body", "Systemic absorption is expected to be minimal, so effects elsewhere are not expected."]],
  "journey": "Drops are placed on the eye and act on corneal nerve endings at the surface. Tear production rises soon after use. Pharmacokinetic details in people are not well established, and systemic absorption is expected to be minimal.",
  "sidefx": "Instillation site pain and a cold, stinging feeling occur in about half of users and come straight from activating TRPM8, the same channel that makes menthol feel cold. Irritation, redness and blurred vision can occur from the drop. Because the action is local on nerve endings, systemic effects are not expected. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The receptor this drop targets is the same one that makes peppermint and menthol feel cool on the skin and in the mouth.",
  "src": ["Tryptyr (acoltremon) FDA label", "McKemy DD, Neuhausser WM, Julius D. Nature 2002", "Peier AM et al. Cell 2002"]
},
"timolol-dorzolamide": {
  "origin": "This combination joins two Merck glaucoma drugs. Timolol, a nonselective beta blocker, became in 1978 the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US (Timoptic) and quickly became a mainstay of glaucoma treatment. Dorzolamide was designed at Merck in the 1980s through a long effort to make a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that could work as an eye drop instead of a pill. Using knowledge of the enzyme's structure, Merck chemists made a water-soluble drug able to cross the cornea. Dorzolamide was approved as Trusopt in 1994, and Merck combined it with timolol as Cosopt, approved in 1998. A preservative-free version, Cosopt PF, followed later.",
  "timeline": [["1978", "Timolol (Timoptic) becomes the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US."], ["1994", "FDA approves dorzolamide (Trusopt), the first topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor in the US."], ["1998", "FDA approves Cosopt, dorzolamide combined with timolol."]],
  "cell": "Both drugs reduce aqueous humor production by the ciliary epithelium, but in different ways. Dorzolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase II, which normally converts carbon dioxide and water into bicarbonate and a hydrogen ion. Bicarbonate secretion drags sodium and water into the posterior chamber, so blocking the enzyme cuts fluid formation. Timolol blocks beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors on the ciliary epithelium. Beta-2 receptors normally couple to Gs, raising cAMP and supporting aqueous secretion, so blocking them lowers cAMP and further cuts production. With less aqueous made and outflow unchanged, intraocular pressure falls more than with either drug alone.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Aqueous production falls from two mechanisms at once, lowering intraocular pressure within one to two hours."], ["Eye on dosing", "Burning, stinging and redness are common after drops."], ["Taste", "Dorzolamide draining to the throat causes a bitter taste."], ["Heart", "Absorbed timolol can slow the heart and worsen heart block or heart failure because it blocks beta-1 receptors."], ["Lungs", "Timolol blocks beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle, which can trigger bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."], ["Cornea", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibition can impair the corneal endothelial pump, so corneas with weak endothelium may swell."]],
  "journey": "The drops pass through the cornea to the ciliary body. A meaningful amount drains down the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed through the nasal mucosa directly into the circulation, bypassing liver first-pass metabolism. Timolol is metabolized by liver CYP2D6 and has a systemic half-life of about four hours. Dorzolamide binds carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells and has a very long elimination half-life of over four months, being slowly converted to N-desethyldorzolamide. Both are excreted by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Timolol's systemic beta blockade explains slow heart rate, heart block, low blood pressure, fatigue and bronchospasm, which can be severe in people with asthma. Because nasal absorption skips first-pass metabolism, even eye drops can produce these effects. Dorzolamide is a sulfonamide, so rare sulfonamide hypersensitivity reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, can occur. Bitter taste and stinging are local effects. For serious beta blocker toxicity, treatment may include atropine and glucagon.",
  "fun": "Dorzolamide is often cited as one of the earliest marketed drugs created with the help of structure-based drug design.",
  "src": ["Cosopt (dorzolamide and timolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"brimonidine-timolol": {
  "origin": "This combination joins brimonidine and timolol. Timolol, from Merck, became the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US in 1978. Brimonidine is a highly selective alpha-2 adrenergic agonist developed by Allergan and approved in 1996 as Alphagan. Compared with the earlier apraclonidine, it has much less alpha-1 activity and causes fewer allergic reactions. Allergan combined the two into one bottle as Combigan, approved by the FDA in 2007, to lower eye pressure with two mechanisms while reducing the number of drops a person needs.",
  "timeline": [["1978", "Timolol (Timoptic) becomes the first beta blocker eye drop approved in the US."], ["1996", "FDA approves brimonidine (Alphagan) for glaucoma."], ["2007", "FDA approves Combigan, brimonidine combined with timolol."]],
  "cell": "Brimonidine binds alpha-2 adrenergic receptors in the ciliary body. These couple to Gi, which inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, reducing aqueous humor secretion. Brimonidine also increases uveoscleral outflow, probably through prostaglandin-related effects in the ciliary muscle. Timolol blocks beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors on the ciliary epithelium. Normally beta-2 activation raises cAMP and supports aqueous production, so blocking it lowers production further. The two drugs together lower cAMP from two directions and add an outflow effect, giving a larger drop in intraocular pressure than either alone.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Lower aqueous production plus more uveoscleral outflow reduces intraocular pressure, starting within about an hour."], ["Conjunctiva", "Allergic conjunctivitis with redness and itching can develop with brimonidine over weeks to months."], ["Brain", "Brimonidine crosses the blood-brain barrier, so drowsiness and fatigue can occur; in infants it can cause severe sedation and breathing pauses."], ["Mouth", "Dry mouth comes from alpha-2 effects of absorbed brimonidine on salivary glands."], ["Heart and vessels", "Timolol slows the heart and both drugs can lower blood pressure."], ["Lungs", "Timolol's beta-2 blockade can cause bronchospasm in asthma or COPD."]],
  "journey": "Drops enter the eye through the cornea and conjunctiva. Some drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed through the nasal mucosa straight into the circulation. Brimonidine has a systemic half-life of about three hours and is metabolized in the liver. Timolol has a half-life of about four hours and is metabolized by liver CYP2D6. Metabolites are excreted in the urine. Each dose lasts about 12 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Brimonidine's alpha-2 action in the brain causes sleepiness and, in infants and small children, can cause apnea, low heart rate and coma, so the drops are not used in young children. Allergic follicular conjunctivitis is a delayed immune reaction to brimonidine. Timolol's beta blockade can cause bradycardia, heart block, fatigue and bronchospasm, because nasal absorption bypasses first-pass metabolism. For serious beta blocker toxicity, atropine and glucagon may be used; care is otherwise supportive.",
  "fun": "Brimonidine is also used as a skin gel to reduce facial redness in rosacea, because the same alpha-2 effect narrows small skin blood vessels.",
  "src": ["Combigan (brimonidine and timolol) FDA label", "Alphagan P (brimonidine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"brinzolamide-brimonidine": {
  "origin": "Simbrinza combines two glaucoma drugs that reduce eye pressure without a beta blocker. Brinzolamide, a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor from Alcon, was approved as Azopt in 1998, building on the discovery in the 1950s that carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower eye pressure. Brimonidine, a selective alpha-2 agonist from Allergan, was approved as Alphagan in 1996. Many people cannot use timolol-containing combinations because of asthma, COPD, slow heart rate or heart block. Alcon developed this fixed combination for them, and the FDA approved Simbrinza in 2013. It was the first fixed-dose glaucoma combination in the US without a beta blocker.",
  "timeline": [["1954", "Bernard Becker shows that carbonic anhydrase inhibition lowers eye pressure."], ["1996", "FDA approves brimonidine (Alphagan)."], ["1998", "FDA approves brinzolamide (Azopt)."], ["2013", "FDA approves Simbrinza, the first US fixed glaucoma combination without a beta blocker."]],
  "cell": "Brinzolamide is a sulfonamide that binds the zinc ion in the active site of carbonic anhydrase II in the ciliary process epithelium. With the enzyme blocked, less bicarbonate is made from carbon dioxide and water, so less sodium and water follow into the posterior chamber and aqueous humor production falls. Brimonidine is a highly selective alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. Alpha-2 receptors couple to Gi proteins, which inhibit adenylyl cyclase and lower cAMP in the ciliary epithelium, further reducing aqueous secretion. Brimonidine also increases uveoscleral outflow. Two separate ways of reducing production plus an outflow effect give a larger pressure drop than either drug alone.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls within one to two hours, and the drops are used three times daily to keep it down."], ["Vision", "Brinzolamide is a suspension, so vision is briefly blurred after each drop."], ["Taste and mouth", "Bitter taste comes from brinzolamide draining to the throat, and dry mouth from brimonidine's alpha-2 effect on salivary glands."], ["Brain", "Brimonidine crosses into the brain and can cause drowsiness and fatigue; infants are at risk of severe sedation and breathing problems."], ["Heart and vessels", "Brimonidine can modestly lower blood pressure, but there is no beta blocker effect on heart rate or airways."], ["Conjunctiva", "Allergic reactions with red, itchy eyes can develop over time, mainly from brimonidine."]],
  "journey": "The suspension is shaken and placed on the eye. Both drugs cross the cornea to the ciliary body. Some drains through the nasolacrimal duct and is absorbed. Brinzolamide binds carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells, giving a very long whole-blood half-life of about 111 days, and is metabolized to N-desethylbrinzolamide, partly by CYP3A4. Brimonidine has a plasma half-life of about three hours and is metabolized in the liver. Both are excreted mainly by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Blurred vision and bitter taste come from brinzolamide's suspension and drainage to the mouth. As a sulfonamide, brinzolamide can rarely cause serious hypersensitivity reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Brimonidine's central alpha-2 action explains sleepiness and fatigue, and in infants can cause apnea and coma, so it is not used in very young children. Allergic conjunctivitis is a delayed immune response. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Simbrinza was built for people whose lungs or hearts cannot tolerate timolol, giving them a two-drug combination without any beta blocker.",
  "src": ["Simbrinza (brinzolamide and brimonidine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"netarsudil-latanoprost": {
  "origin": "Rocklatan pairs a new and an established glaucoma drug. Latanoprost, developed by Johan Stjernschantz and colleagues at Pharmacia in Sweden from Laszlo Bito's discovery that prostaglandins lower eye pressure, was approved as Xalatan in 1996 and became the most widely prescribed glaucoma drop. Netarsudil, a Rho kinase inhibitor designed by Aerie Pharmaceuticals, was approved as Rhopressa in 2017. Because one drug improves drainage through the uveoscleral route and the other through the trabecular meshwork, Aerie combined them in a single once-daily drop. The FDA approved Rocklatan in 2019.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Laszlo Bito shows that prostaglandins can lower intraocular pressure."], ["1996", "FDA approves latanoprost (Xalatan)."], ["2017", "FDA approves netarsudil (Rhopressa)."], ["2019", "FDA approves Rocklatan, netarsudil combined with latanoprost."]],
  "cell": "Aqueous humor leaves the eye through two routes. The conventional route passes through the trabecular meshwork into Schlemm canal and episcleral veins. Netarsudil inhibits Rho-associated protein kinase (ROCK), which normally keeps myosin light chains phosphorylated and the meshwork cells contracted and stiff. Blocking ROCK relaxes these cells and loosens the meshwork, increasing conventional outflow. It also lowers episcleral venous pressure and inhibits the norepinephrine transporter. The second, uveoscleral route passes through the ciliary muscle. Latanoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug converted by corneal esterases to latanoprost acid, an agonist at the prostaglandin FP receptor. FP activation induces matrix metalloproteinases that remodel the ciliary muscle matrix and widen this route. Opening both routes gives more pressure lowering than either drug alone.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls through both drainage routes, with peak effect about 8 to 12 hours after a drop."], ["Conjunctiva", "Eye redness is very common, from vasodilation by both components, and small conjunctival hemorrhages can occur."], ["Cornea", "Netarsudil can form whorl-shaped deposits in the corneal epithelium (verticillata), usually without affecting vision."], ["Iris and lids", "Latanoprost can slowly darken the iris and eyelid skin; iris change may be permanent."], ["Eyelashes", "Lashes may become longer, thicker and darker."], ["Retina", "Prostaglandin signaling can contribute to macular edema in eyes at risk."]],
  "journey": "The single drop is absorbed through the cornea, where esterases convert netarsudil to its active metabolite AR-13503 and latanoprost to latanoprost acid. Pressure lowering begins in a few hours and lasts about a day. Very little reaches the blood. Latanoprost acid in plasma is cleared in minutes, with a half-life of about 17 minutes, by liver beta-oxidation, and metabolites leave in the urine. Netarsudil and its metabolite are mostly undetectable in plasma.",
  "sidefx": "Redness and hemorrhage reflect vascular smooth muscle relaxation by netarsudil and vasodilation by the prostaglandin. Corneal verticillata are drug deposits in the epithelium. Iris and lid darkening and lash growth come from FP receptor stimulation of melanocytes and hair follicles. Prostaglandin-driven inflammation can contribute to macular edema, uveitis and reactivation of herpes keratitis in susceptible eyes. Instillation pain is common. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Rocklatan is a two-pathway drop: one ingredient works on the eye's main drain and the other on its backup drain.",
  "src": ["Rocklatan (netarsudil and latanoprost) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"bimatoprost-implant": {
  "origin": "Glaucoma drops only work if they are used every day, and many people find that hard. Allergan, which had developed bimatoprost drops (Lumigan, approved 2001), set out to deliver the same drug from a tiny biodegradable implant placed inside the front chamber of the eye. The implant is made of a dissolving polymer that releases bimatoprost slowly over months. The FDA approved Durysta in 2020 as the first intracameral (inside the anterior chamber) implant for lowering eye pressure in open-angle glaucoma or ocular hypertension. Allergan is now part of AbbVie.",
  "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves bimatoprost (Lumigan) eye drops."], ["2020", "FDA approves Durysta, the first intracameral implant for glaucoma."]],
  "cell": "Bimatoprost is a synthetic prostamide related to prostaglandin F2-alpha. It acts on prostaglandin FP receptors and proposed prostamide receptors in the ciliary body and trabecular meshwork. FP receptor activation induces matrix metalloproteinases that remodel the extracellular matrix of the ciliary muscle, opening the uveoscleral (pressure-insensitive) outflow route. It also increases outflow through the trabecular pathway. Because the implant sits in the anterior chamber angle, drug is released right next to the tissues it acts on. That allows a very small total amount of drug, which reduces exposure of the conjunctiva and is linked to less redness than with drops.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls within days and stays lower for months, with duration varying between people."], ["Corneal endothelium", "The implant can contact or affect the inner layer of cells on the cornea, causing endothelial cell loss and corneal swelling, especially in narrow angles."], ["Conjunctiva", "Redness still occurs in some people but is generally milder than with daily drops."], ["Iris", "Iritis (inflammation in the front of the eye) can occur after placement."], ["Eye after injection", "Foreign body sensation, eye pain, light sensitivity and bleeding are related to the procedure."]],
  "journey": "An eye specialist injects the implant into the anterior chamber, where it settles in the lower drainage angle. The polymer slowly erodes and releases bimatoprost into the aqueous humor over months. Systemic exposure is minimal. Bimatoprost that does reach the blood has a half-life of about 45 minutes and is hydrolyzed and then oxidized, N-deethylated and glucuronidated before excretion in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Corneal endothelial injury happens because the implant sits close to the inner corneal surface, and endothelial cells do not regenerate well, so loss can cause lasting corneal swelling. Risk is higher in eyes with narrow angles or prior corneal problems. Iritis and pressure spikes can follow placement. Endophthalmitis is a risk of any injection into the eye. Prostaglandin effects such as redness can occur. There is no antidote; an implant can be removed surgically if needed.",
  "fun": "Durysta delivers in one tiny implant what would otherwise take months of daily drops, aimed at people who struggle to use drops every day.",
  "src": ["Durysta (bimatoprost implant) FDA label", "Lumigan (bimatoprost) FDA label"]
},
"travoprost-implant": {
  "origin": "Glaukos, a company known for microscopic stents placed in the eye's drainage system during glaucoma surgery, developed the iDose TR implant. It is a tiny titanium device filled with travoprost that is anchored through the trabecular meshwork into the eye wall. A membrane controls how fast the drug leaves the reservoir, so the eye receives a steady supply of travoprost for many months. The FDA approved iDose TR in December 2023 for lowering eye pressure in open-angle glaucoma and ocular hypertension. Travoprost itself had been approved as Travatan drops in 2001.",
  "timeline": [["2001", "FDA approves travoprost (Travatan) eye drops."], ["2023", "FDA approves iDose TR, a travoprost intracameral implant."]],
  "cell": "Travoprost is an isopropyl ester prodrug. As it elutes from the implant into the aqueous humor, esterases in ocular tissue hydrolyze it to travoprost free acid, a selective full agonist at the prostaglandin FP receptor. FP activation in the ciliary muscle increases matrix metalloproteinase activity, which remodels the extracellular matrix and widens the uveoscleral outflow pathway. It also increases trabecular outflow. The titanium implant releases drug continuously at low levels directly in the anterior chamber, so the target tissues see a steady signal while the ocular surface sees much less drug than with drops.",
  "body": [["Eye pressure", "Intraocular pressure falls within days and stays lower for many months as the reservoir releases drug."], ["Drainage angle", "The implant is anchored through the trabecular meshwork into the sclera and stays in place."], ["Corneal endothelium", "Endothelial cell loss can occur, a concern with devices placed in the anterior chamber."], ["Front of the eye", "Iritis, eye pain, redness and dry eye are reported after placement."], ["Vision", "Reduced visual acuity and visual field changes are listed among reactions in the label."]],
  "journey": "An eye surgeon places the implant through a small incision. Travoprost diffuses out through a membrane into the aqueous humor, where it is converted to the active free acid and acts on nearby ciliary tissue. Systemic exposure is minimal. Any travoprost acid that reaches the blood has a plasma half-life of about 45 minutes and is cleared by beta-oxidation, with metabolites in the urine. When the drug is used up, the titanium implant can be removed and replaced.",
  "sidefx": "Corneal endothelial cell loss and device dislocation relate to having a device in the anterior chamber. Iritis and pressure spikes can follow the procedure. Endophthalmitis is a risk of any surgery that opens the eye. Prostaglandin effects such as redness and iris changes are possible but less prominent than with drops because surface exposure is lower. There is no antidote; the implant can be removed surgically.",
  "fun": "The iDose TR is made of titanium and is smaller than a grain of rice, yet it holds months of glaucoma medicine.",
  "src": ["iDose TR (travoprost intracameral implant) FDA label", "Travatan Z (travoprost) FDA label"]
},
"darifenacin": {
  "origin": "Darifenacin was discovered by scientists at Pfizer's research center in Sandwich, England, during the 1990s, as part of efforts to make muscarinic blockers that favor the M3 receptor, the subtype that drives bladder contraction. The idea was that sparing M1 receptors in the brain would cause fewer memory and thinking problems. Novartis took over development, and the FDA approved darifenacin extended-release tablets as Enablex in 2004 for overactive bladder. It joined a crowded field of antimuscarinics that started with the much older drug oxybutynin.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Darifenacin is discovered at Pfizer in Sandwich, England."], ["2004", "FDA approves darifenacin extended-release tablets (Enablex) for overactive bladder."]],
  "cell": "The detrusor muscle of the bladder wall contracts when parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine onto muscarinic receptors. M3 receptors couple to Gq, activating phospholipase C and raising IP3 and calcium, which triggers contraction. M2 receptors are more numerous and help by reducing cAMP-mediated relaxation. In overactive bladder, unwanted contractions cause urgency and leakage. Darifenacin is a competitive antagonist with relative selectivity for M3 receptors. It blocks acetylcholine at the detrusor, so involuntary contractions are fewer and weaker and the bladder holds more urine. Its lower affinity for M1 receptors, which are important in the brain, is linked to fewer cognitive effects, although M3 blockade in glands and the gut still causes typical anticholinergic effects.",
  "body": [["Bladder", "Fewer involuntary detrusor contractions mean less urgency, fewer leaks and a larger bladder capacity, building over about two weeks."], ["Mouth", "M3 receptors drive salivary secretion, so dry mouth is the most common side effect."], ["Gut", "Reduced muscarinic tone slows gut movement, causing constipation and indigestion; severe constipation can occur."], ["Eyes", "Blurred vision can occur from blocked muscarinic receptors in the ciliary muscle and iris."], ["Urinary outflow", "In people with bladder outlet obstruction, urinary retention can occur."], ["Skin and temperature", "Reduced sweating can cause heat intolerance."]],
  "journey": "Darifenacin is taken as an extended-release tablet and absorbed in the gut, with substantial first-pass metabolism by the liver. Peak levels come about seven hours after a dose. It is about 98 percent protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels. The half-life is about 13 to 19 hours. Metabolites leave about 60 percent in urine and 40 percent in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect muscarinic blockade outside the bladder. M3 blockade in salivary glands causes dry mouth, in gut smooth muscle constipation, and in the eye blurred vision. Reduced detrusor contraction can lead to retention when the outlet is narrowed. Sweat glands use muscarinic receptors, so heat illness is possible. Angioedema is a rare allergic reaction. Physostigmine can reverse severe anticholinergic toxicity, and otherwise care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Darifenacin was one of the first bladder drugs designed specifically to favor the M3 muscarinic receptor subtype rather than block all five.",
  "src": ["Enablex (darifenacin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"trospium": {
  "origin": "Trospium is a quaternary ammonium compound related to atropine-like alkaloids. It was developed in Germany and was used in Europe for years to relax smooth muscle of the bladder and gut before reaching the US. Its permanent positive charge keeps it from crossing into the brain easily, which made it attractive for older adults worried about confusion. The FDA approved trospium tablets as Sanctura in 2004 for overactive bladder, followed by an extended-release capsule. The Sanctura brand has been discontinued, but generics remain available.",
  "timeline": [["2004", "FDA approves trospium tablets (Sanctura) for overactive bladder."], ["2007", "An extended-release trospium capsule is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Trospium is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors M1 through M5, without strong subtype selectivity. In the bladder, blocking M3 receptors stops Gq-mediated calcium release that drives detrusor contraction, and blocking M2 receptors removes their inhibition of cAMP-mediated relaxation. Involuntary contractions are fewer, urgency decreases and the bladder holds more. Trospium has a permanent positive charge from its quaternary nitrogen. Charged molecules cross lipid membranes poorly, and trospium is also a substrate of P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier, so very little enters the brain. This is why it causes fewer central effects such as confusion than tertiary amines like oxybutynin.",
  "body": [["Bladder", "Detrusor overactivity decreases, with fewer urge episodes and leaks, often within about a week."], ["Mouth", "Saliva production drops because salivary muscarinic receptors are blocked, causing dry mouth."], ["Gut", "Constipation, indigestion and abdominal pain come from reduced gut smooth muscle activity."], ["Brain", "Because it barely crosses the blood-brain barrier, confusion and memory effects are uncommon compared with other antimuscarinics."], ["Heart", "Blocking cardiac M2 receptors can speed the heart rate."], ["Eyes and skin", "Blurred vision and reduced sweating with heat intolerance can occur."]],
  "journey": "Trospium is poorly absorbed from the gut, with bioavailability under ten percent, and food lowers absorption further, so it is taken on an empty stomach. Peak levels come in about five to six hours. Little is metabolized by CYP enzymes; some is hydrolyzed and glucuronidated. It is cleared mainly unchanged by the kidneys through active tubular secretion, so kidney impairment raises levels. The half-life is about 20 hours. Unabsorbed drug leaves in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention all come from muscarinic blockade outside the bladder. Tachycardia reflects cardiac M2 blockade. Heat intolerance follows reduced sweating. Because it depends on renal tubular secretion, it can interact with other drugs secreted by the same transporters and builds up in kidney disease. Angioedema is a rare serious reaction. Severe anticholinergic toxicity is managed supportively, and physostigmine may be used for peripheral and central effects.",
  "fun": "Trospium's built-in positive charge works like a passport the brain will not stamp, keeping most of the drug out of the central nervous system.",
  "src": ["Trospium chloride FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"pentosan-polysulfate": {
  "origin": "Pentosan polysulfate is made from xylan, a sugar chain extracted from beechwood, which is chemically sulfated to resemble heparin and the natural glycosaminoglycans that coat the bladder lining. It was first developed in Germany by Bene-Arzneimittel and used in Europe as an anticoagulant and anti-inflammatory agent. In the 1980s, urologist C. Lowell Parsons at the University of California, San Diego proposed that interstitial cystitis is linked to a leaky glycosaminoglycan layer on the bladder surface and studied pentosan to repair it. The FDA approved Elmiron in 1996 as the only oral drug for bladder pain and discomfort of interstitial cystitis. In 2018 researchers at Emory University described a distinctive pigmentary maculopathy in long-term users, and the label was updated with this warning in 2020.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "C. Lowell Parsons studies pentosan for interstitial cystitis."], ["1996", "FDA approves Elmiron for bladder pain of interstitial cystitis."], ["2018", "Emory researchers describe pigmentary maculopathy with long-term pentosan use."], ["2020", "Elmiron labeling adds a warning about retinal pigmentary changes."]],
  "cell": "The bladder urothelium is covered by a glycosaminoglycan (GAG) layer that keeps urine components such as potassium from leaking into the bladder wall. In interstitial cystitis this layer is thought to be defective, so solutes irritate nerves and mast cells and cause pain. Pentosan polysulfate is a low-molecular-weight sulfated polysaccharide that resembles GAGs. A small fraction of each oral dose is absorbed and excreted in the urine, where it is thought to adhere to the urothelium and patch the deficient GAG layer, limiting permeation of irritating solutes. It may also reduce mast cell activation. Because it is heparin-like, it has weak anticoagulant activity, about one-fifteenth that of heparin, and some fibrinolytic activity.",
  "body": [["Bladder", "Bladder pain and discomfort may lessen, but relief usually takes three to six months to appear."], ["Blood", "Weak anticoagulant activity can cause easy bruising or rectal and gum bleeding, especially with other blood thinners."], ["Retina", "Long-term use can cause pigmentary maculopathy, with trouble reading, adapting to dim light or seeing details, which may not fully reverse."], ["Hair", "Hair thinning or loss (alopecia) is a common reported side effect."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea, indigestion and abdominal pain occur in some people."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and the drug is partly processed by the liver."]],
  "journey": "Pentosan is taken by mouth, but only a few percent of each dose is absorbed. Absorbed drug peaks in about two hours and is bound in tissues including the urothelium, liver and spleen. It is desulfated in the liver and spleen and broken into smaller pieces in the kidney. A small fraction appears in urine, which is the portion thought to act on the bladder. Most of the dose passes through and leaves unabsorbed in the feces.",
  "sidefx": "Bleeding risk comes from its heparin-like anticoagulant and fibrinolytic effects, which add to warfarin, heparin, aspirin and NSAIDs. The cause of pigmentary maculopathy is not fully understood but is thought to involve direct toxicity to the retinal pigment epithelium, with risk rising with cumulative exposure; baseline and periodic retinal exams are recommended in the label. Hair loss and gut upset are common. There is no specific antidote, and bleeding is managed by stopping the drug and supportive care.",
  "fun": "This bladder drug starts out as a sugar chain from beech trees, which chemists then sulfate to make it look like the bladder's own protective coating.",
  "src": ["Elmiron (pentosan polysulfate sodium) FDA label", "Pearce WA et al. Ophthalmology 2018", "Parsons CL, Mulholland SG. J Urol 1987"]
},
"vardenafil": {
  "origin": "Vardenafil followed the surprising story of sildenafil. In the early 1990s Pfizer tested sildenafil for angina, and volunteers reported erections as a side effect, which led to Viagra in 1998. The science behind it, nitric oxide signaling through cGMP, earned Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad the 1998 Nobel Prize. Bayer chemists in Germany then designed vardenafil, a PDE5 inhibitor with a structure close to sildenafil but with a different ring system that increased its potency. Bayer and GlaxoSmithKline brought it to market, and the FDA approved it as Levitra in 2003. An orally disintegrating version, Staxyn, followed in 2010. The Levitra brand has since been discontinued in the US, while generics remain.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Sildenafil (Viagra) becomes the first approved PDE5 inhibitor; nitric oxide signaling wins the Nobel Prize."], ["2003", "FDA approves vardenafil (Levitra) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2010", "Vardenafil orally disintegrating tablet (Staxyn) is approved."]],
  "cell": "During sexual stimulation, nerves and endothelial cells in the corpus cavernosum release nitric oxide (NO). NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase in smooth muscle cells, raising cyclic GMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes the smooth muscle of the cavernosal arteries and sinusoids. Blood flows in and the erection forms as outflow veins are compressed. Phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5) breaks down cGMP and ends the signal. Vardenafil is a potent selective PDE5 inhibitor, so cGMP stays higher for longer, but only when NO is being released, which is why stimulation is still needed. Vardenafil also weakly inhibits PDE6 in retinal rods and cones, which explains color vision changes.",
  "body": [["Penis", "Cavernosal smooth muscle relaxes more fully with sexual stimulation, allowing a firmer and longer erection."], ["Blood vessels", "Mild vasodilation elsewhere causes small drops in blood pressure, flushing and headache."], ["Nose", "Dilated nasal vessels cause stuffiness or runny nose."], ["Eyes", "Weak PDE6 inhibition in the retina can cause a blue tinge or light sensitivity; sudden vision loss from NAION is a rare warning."], ["Heart", "A small QT prolongation effect has been seen, so it is used with caution in people with long QT or certain antiarrhythmics."], ["Gut and back", "Indigestion and back pain are reported, possibly related to smooth muscle effects."]],
  "journey": "Vardenafil is absorbed quickly after swallowing, with onset in about 25 to 60 minutes; a high-fat meal can delay absorption. Bioavailability is modest due to first-pass metabolism. It is about 95 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with minor roles for CYP3A5 and CYP2C9, forming an active desethyl metabolite. The half-life is about four to five hours. Most is excreted in the feces, with a small amount in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels markedly.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and nasal congestion come from vasodilation by cGMP in vessels outside the penis. With nitrates, both drugs push cGMP higher through different steps, which can cause profound, dangerous hypotension, so the combination is contraindicated. Alpha blockers can add to blood pressure drops. PDE6 inhibition explains color vision changes. Priapism is a rare effect of excessive cavernosal relaxation and is an emergency. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated supportively.",
  "fun": "Vardenafil and sildenafil look almost the same on paper. The key difference is a swapped ring system in the core of the molecule.",
  "src": ["Levitra (vardenafil) FDA label", "Staxyn (vardenafil ODT) FDA label", "Nobel Prize 1998 Physiology or Medicine"]
},
"avanafil": {
  "origin": "Avanafil was discovered in Japan by Tanabe Seiyaku, now Mitsubishi Tanabe Pharma. The goal was a PDE5 inhibitor that works faster than earlier drugs and is more selective for PDE5 over other phosphodiesterases such as PDE6 in the retina and PDE11. The US company Vivus licensed and developed it, and the FDA approved it as Stendra in 2012. In 2014 labeling was updated to say it could be taken as little as about 15 minutes before sexual activity, the fastest labeled onset in its class.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Sildenafil becomes the first approved PDE5 inhibitor."], ["2012", "FDA approves avanafil (Stendra) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2014", "Stendra labeling is updated to reflect onset in about 15 minutes."]],
  "cell": "Nitric oxide released by nerves and endothelium during sexual stimulation activates soluble guanylate cyclase in cavernosal smooth muscle, raising cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, lowering intracellular calcium and relaxing the smooth muscle so blood can fill the erectile tissue. PDE5 breaks cGMP down and ends the signal. Avanafil competitively inhibits PDE5, so cGMP signaling is prolonged, but only when nitric oxide is being released. It is highly selective for PDE5 over PDE6, found in retinal photoreceptors, and PDE11, found in skeletal muscle and testis. Higher selectivity is linked to fewer color vision changes and less muscle pain than with some older drugs.",
  "body": [["Penis", "Erections form more easily with sexual stimulation, with effect beginning in about 15 minutes in some people."], ["Blood vessels", "Systemic vasodilation causes headache, flushing and small drops in blood pressure."], ["Nose and throat", "Nasal congestion and nasopharyngitis are reported from vessel dilation in the nasal lining."], ["Eyes", "Visual disturbances are less common due to PDE6 selectivity, but NAION is a rare warning for the class."], ["Back and muscles", "Back pain is reported in a small share of people in clinical trials of the drug."], ["Heart", "Blood pressure drops matter in people with heart disease or on nitrates or alpha blockers."]],
  "journey": "Avanafil is absorbed quickly from the gut, with peak levels in about 30 to 45 minutes, which explains its fast onset. A high-fat meal slows absorption. It is about 99 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with a minor role for CYP2C9, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors are avoided. The half-life is about five hours. Metabolites are excreted in feces (about 62 percent) and urine (about 21 percent).",
  "sidefx": "Headache, flushing and nasal congestion come from cGMP-driven vasodilation outside the penis. Combined with nitrates, which raise cGMP by another route, avanafil can cause severe hypotension, so the combination is contraindicated. Alpha blockers and alcohol can add to blood pressure drops. Priapism is a rare emergency from excessive cavernosal relaxation. Sudden hearing loss and NAION are rare class warnings. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated supportively.",
  "fun": "Avanafil has the fastest labeled onset of the US PDE5 inhibitors, at about 15 minutes in some people.",
  "src": ["Stendra (avanafil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"testosterone-undecanoate": {
  "origin": "Testosterone was first isolated in 1935 by Ernst Laqueur's group in Amsterdam from bull testes, and the same year Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ruzicka each reported ways to make it from cholesterol, work that helped earn them the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Plain testosterone works poorly as a pill because the liver destroys most of it on the first pass. Chemists solved this by attaching fatty acid chains (esters). Testosterone undecanoate carries a long 11-carbon chain. The Dutch company Organon launched an oral form (Andriol) outside the US in the 1970s. In the US, the long-acting injection Aveed was approved in 2014, and oral capsules followed: Jatenzo in 2019, then Tlando and Kyzatrex in 2022. These gave men with low testosterone a pill option that does not carry the liver toxicity of the older 17-alpha-methylated oral androgens.",
  "timeline": [["1935", "Laqueur's group isolates testosterone; Butenandt and Ruzicka report its synthesis."], ["1970s", "Organon introduces oral testosterone undecanoate (Andriol) outside the US."], ["2014", "FDA approves Aveed, the long-acting intramuscular injection."], ["2019", "FDA approves Jatenzo, the first oral testosterone undecanoate capsule in the US."], ["2022", "Tlando and Kyzatrex oral capsules are approved."]],
  "cell": "Testosterone undecanoate is a prodrug. Esterases cut off the undecanoate chain and release free testosterone. Testosterone crosses the cell membrane and binds the androgen receptor in the cytoplasm. The receptor sheds its heat shock proteins, pairs up (dimerizes), moves into the nucleus and binds androgen response elements on DNA, turning on genes for muscle protein, red cell production and male sex traits. In tissues such as prostate, skin and hair follicles, 5-alpha reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT), which binds the same receptor more tightly. Aromatase converts some testosterone to estradiol, which acts on estrogen receptors in bone and brain. Testosterone also feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, lowering GnRH, LH and FSH.",
  "body": [["Muscle and bone", "Androgen receptor activation increases muscle protein synthesis and lean mass. Estradiol made from testosterone helps keep bone dense."], ["Brain and libido", "Androgen and estrogen signaling in the brain restore sex drive, energy and mood in men with low levels."], ["Blood", "Testosterone stimulates erythropoietin release and iron use, raising hematocrit, which can become too high (polycythemia)."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, which is why the oral capsules carry a boxed warning about blood pressure increases."], ["Testes", "Feedback suppression of LH and FSH shrinks the testes and lowers sperm production, so therapy can cause infertility."], ["Skin and prostate", "DHT drives oil glands and prostate growth, causing acne and possible worsening of BPH symptoms."]],
  "journey": "The oral capsules work only with food that contains fat. The very fatty ester dissolves in dietary fat and is taken up into intestinal lymph vessels with chylomicrons, entering the blood through the thoracic duct and skipping the liver's first pass. Esterases then free testosterone, which peaks a few hours after a dose and is gone within hours, so capsules are taken twice a day. The Aveed injection forms an oily depot in muscle that releases the ester slowly over weeks. Testosterone is tightly bound to SHBG and albumin, is metabolized in the liver to DHT, estradiol and inactive conjugates, and leaves mainly in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Most effects come from too much androgen action. Extra red cell production raises hematocrit and clot risk. DHT causes acne and prostate growth. Aromatization to estradiol can cause breast enlargement. Feedback suppression of LH and FSH lowers sperm counts. Fluid retention and blood pressure rises add to cardiovascular concerns, and sleep apnea can worsen. The Aveed injection carries a boxed warning for pulmonary oil microembolism (POME), when tiny droplets of the oily depot reach the lungs and cause cough and shortness of breath, and for anaphylaxis, so patients are watched after each injection. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "Because the oral form rides into the body with dietary fat through the lymph system, a fat-free meal can leave blood testosterone far lower than expected.",
  "src": ["Jatenzo (testosterone undecanoate) FDA label", "Aveed (testosterone undecanoate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"flavoxate": {
  "origin": "Flavoxate is a synthetic molecule built on a flavone ring, the same basic chemical skeleton found in many plant pigments. It was developed in the 1960s by the Italian drug company Recordati as a smooth muscle relaxant for the urinary tract. It was later sold in the US under the brand Urispas for the burning, urgency and frequency that come with bladder and urethral irritation. The brand has since been discontinued, but generic tablets remain. It filled a niche as a symptom reliever used alongside antibiotics or other treatment of the cause.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Recordati in Italy develops flavoxate as a urinary smooth muscle relaxant."]],
  "cell": "Flavoxate relaxes the smooth muscle of the bladder and urethra mainly by a direct action on the muscle cells rather than through a single receptor. Proposed parts of this action include blocking calcium entry into smooth muscle cells (weak calcium channel antagonism) and inhibiting phosphodiesterase, both of which reduce the calcium available to the contractile machinery. It also has weak antimuscarinic activity, blocking acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors on the detrusor, and a local anesthetic effect that may quiet irritated sensory nerves in the bladder lining. The net result is less spasm and less urge, while the infection or inflammation that caused the irritation is not treated.",
  "body": [["Bladder and urethra", "Relaxed smooth muscle and calmer sensory nerves ease urgency, frequency, suprapubic pain and painful urination."], ["Mouth and eyes", "Weak antimuscarinic action reduces saliva and can blur vision by relaxing the focusing muscle of the eye."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness, nervousness and confusion can occur, especially in older adults."], ["Heart", "Anticholinergic effects can speed the heart and cause palpitations."], ["Blood", "Rare cases of low white cell counts (leukopenia) have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Flavoxate is taken by mouth and absorbed from the gut, with effects starting in about an hour. The liver converts much of it to an active metabolite, 3-methylflavone-8-carboxylic acid. The kidneys then excrete this metabolite, with more than half of a dose appearing in the urine within a day. Because the effect lasts only several hours, it is taken several times a day.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects follow from its weak antimuscarinic action: dry mouth, blurred vision, fast heart rate and, in people with a narrowed bladder outlet, urinary retention. Blocking muscarinic receptors in the brain explains drowsiness and confusion in older adults, and the reduced ability to sweat raises the risk of heat illness, especially when combined with other anticholinergic drugs. It is avoided in bowel or bladder outlet obstruction and narrow-angle glaucoma. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Flavoxate's flavone backbone links it chemically to the yellow pigments that color many flowers and fruits, which is where the name flavone comes from (Latin flavus, yellow).",
  "src": ["Flavoxate hydrochloride tablets FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"dutasteride-tamsulosin": {
  "origin": "This capsule joins two drugs that attack an enlarged prostate from two sides. The idea for 5-alpha reductase inhibitors came from a 1974 report by Julianne Imperato-McGinley on a group of people in the Dominican Republic born with an inherited lack of type 2 5-alpha reductase. They had small prostates that never enlarged, showing that DHT drives prostate growth. GlaxoSmithKline then developed dutasteride, which blocks both type 1 and type 2 enzymes, and FDA approved it as Avodart in 2001. Tamsulosin came from the Japanese company Yamanouchi and was approved in the US as Flomax in 1997 as a uroselective alpha-1 blocker. The CombAT trial showed the two together worked better than either alone, and FDA approved the combination capsule Jalyn in 2010.",
  "timeline": [["1974", "Imperato-McGinley describes 5-alpha reductase deficiency, linking DHT to prostate growth."], ["1997", "FDA approves tamsulosin (Flomax)."], ["2001", "FDA approves dutasteride (Avodart)."], ["2010", "FDA approves the combination capsule Jalyn."]],
  "cell": "Dutasteride is a 4-azasteroid that looks like testosterone. It binds and inhibits both type 1 and type 2 5-alpha reductase, the enzymes that turn testosterone into dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Serum DHT falls by about 90 percent. With less DHT acting on androgen receptors in prostate cells, growth signals fall, glandular tissue shrinks and PSA drops. Tamsulosin is a competitive antagonist at alpha-1A (and alpha-1D) adrenergic receptors. These Gq-coupled receptors normally raise intracellular calcium in the smooth muscle of the prostate stroma, capsule and bladder neck when norepinephrine binds. Blocking them relaxes that muscle and opens the urethra. The two parts therefore treat the static component (tissue size) and the dynamic component (muscle tone) of obstruction.",
  "body": [["Prostate", "Less DHT shrinks the gland by about a quarter over months, lowering the risk of urinary retention and surgery."], ["Bladder neck and urethra", "Alpha-1A blockade relaxes smooth muscle within days, improving urine flow and reducing straining."], ["Blood vessels", "Some alpha-1 blockade in vessels can cause dizziness and a drop in blood pressure on standing."], ["Sexual function", "Lower DHT reduces libido and erections, and tamsulosin can reduce or stop ejaculation."], ["Breast tissue", "Shifting the androgen to estrogen balance can cause breast enlargement or tenderness."], ["PSA blood test", "Dutasteride roughly halves PSA, so the value must be interpreted with this in mind."], ["Eyes", "Alpha-1A blockade in the iris dilator muscle can cause floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth in one capsule, usually after the same meal each day. Tamsulosin is absorbed over several hours and cleared by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 with a half-life of about half a day; its benefit appears within days. Dutasteride is very lipophilic, highly protein bound and broken down slowly by CYP3A4, with a half-life of about five weeks. Because of this it takes months for prostate shrinkage and can stay in the body for months after stopping. Dutasteride metabolites leave mainly in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Dutasteride side effects come from lowering DHT: less libido, erectile dysfunction and breast changes. The 5-ARI class carries a warning about a higher rate of high-grade prostate cancer in trials. Because it can be absorbed through the skin and could affect a male fetus, women who are or could become pregnant should not handle broken capsules. Tamsulosin's alpha blockade causes dizziness, orthostatic hypotension and fainting, especially with the first doses, plus ejaculation problems and floppy iris syndrome during eye surgery. Priapism is a rare effect. There is no antidote; hypotension is treated with fluids and lying flat.",
  "fun": "Dutasteride's half-life of about five weeks is one of the longest of any common pill, which is why people who take it are asked not to donate blood for months after their last dose.",
  "src": ["Jalyn (dutasteride and tamsulosin) FDA label", "Imperato-McGinley J et al. Science 1974", "Roehrborn CG et al. CombAT trial"]
},
"finasteride-tadalafil": {
  "origin": "Finasteride was developed by Merck after the discovery that people born without type 2 5-alpha reductase have small prostates. Merck chemists designed a 4-azasteroid that blocks the enzyme, and FDA approved it as Proscar for enlarged prostate in 1992, then as Propecia for hair loss in 1997. Tadalafil started as a compound called IC351 from a partnership between ICOS and Glaxo, later developed by Lilly ICOS. It was approved for erectile dysfunction as Cialis in 2003 and for BPH symptoms in 2011. Veru combined the two in one capsule, Entadfi, which FDA approved in December 2021 for starting BPH treatment for up to 26 weeks, covering the time before finasteride alone takes full effect.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "FDA approves finasteride (Proscar) for BPH."], ["2003", "FDA approves tadalafil (Cialis) for erectile dysfunction."], ["2011", "Tadalafil gains approval for BPH symptoms."], ["2021", "FDA approves the finasteride-tadalafil capsule Entadfi."]],
  "cell": "Finasteride is a competitive, mechanism-based inhibitor of type 2 5-alpha reductase, the main form in the prostate. It blocks conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Less DHT on prostate androgen receptors means less growth signaling, so the gland slowly shrinks and PSA falls. Tadalafil inhibits phosphodiesterase type 5 (PDE5), the enzyme that breaks down cyclic GMP. Nitric oxide from nerves and endothelium activates soluble guanylate cyclase to make cGMP. With PDE5 blocked, cGMP builds up, protein kinase G is activated, intracellular calcium falls and smooth muscle relaxes in the prostate, bladder neck and blood vessels. Better blood flow to the pelvic organs and less sensory nerve activity may also help urinary symptoms.",
  "body": [["Prostate", "Less DHT shrinks the gland over months, while PDE5 inhibition relaxes its smooth muscle within weeks."], ["Bladder", "Relaxed bladder neck and less afferent nerve signaling reduce urgency, frequency and weak stream."], ["Blood vessels", "cGMP-driven vasodilation causes headache, flushing and a mild blood pressure drop, which becomes dangerous with nitrates."], ["Sexual function", "Tadalafil supports erections, while finasteride can lower libido and cause ejaculation problems."], ["Muscles", "PDE11 inhibition in skeletal muscle is a proposed reason for the back pain and muscle aches seen with tadalafil."], ["Eyes and ears", "Rare cases of sudden vision loss (NAION) and sudden hearing loss have been reported with PDE5 inhibitors."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth in one capsule. Tadalafil is absorbed in about two hours, is broken down by CYP3A4 and has a long half-life of about 17 hours, which allows once-daily use. Finasteride is also absorbed within a couple of hours, metabolized by CYP3A4 and has a half-life of about 5 to 6 hours, though its effect on DHT lasts much longer because it binds the enzyme very tightly. Metabolites of both leave mainly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise tadalafil levels.",
  "sidefx": "Tadalafil's side effects come from vasodilation and PDE inhibition elsewhere: headache, indigestion from relaxed esophageal sphincter, back pain and muscle aches. Combining it with nitrates or riociguat stacks two cGMP-raising effects and can drop blood pressure severely, so these combinations are contraindicated. Prolonged erections (priapism) are rare. Finasteride's effects come from low DHT: reduced libido, erectile and ejaculation problems, breast tenderness and, in the class, a higher rate of high-grade prostate cancer in trials. Women who may be pregnant should not handle crushed or broken finasteride because it can affect a male fetus. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tadalafil's long half-life earned Cialis the nickname 'the weekend pill' when it launched.",
  "src": ["Entadfi (finasteride and tadalafil) FDA label", "Proscar FDA label", "Cialis FDA label"]
},
"oxybutynin-transdermal": {
  "origin": "Oxybutynin is a synthetic antimuscarinic first developed in the 1960s, and oral tablets were approved in the US as Ditropan in 1975. Oral oxybutynin works well for overactive bladder, but many people stop it because of severe dry mouth. Researchers traced much of this to N-desethyloxybutynin, a metabolite made when the gut wall and liver process the swallowed drug. Delivering the drug through the skin avoids that first pass. Watson Pharmaceuticals developed the Oxytrol patch, approved by FDA in 2003. In 2013 Oxytrol for Women became the first overactive bladder treatment sold over the counter in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1975", "FDA approves oral oxybutynin (Ditropan)."], ["2003", "FDA approves the Oxytrol transdermal patch."], ["2013", "Oxytrol for Women becomes the first OTC overactive bladder product in the US."]],
  "cell": "Oxybutynin is a tertiary amine that competitively blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. In the bladder, parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine onto M3 receptors on detrusor smooth muscle. M3 is Gq-coupled, so binding normally activates phospholipase C, makes IP3 and releases calcium for contraction. Blocking M3 stops these involuntary contractions, so the bladder can fill to a larger volume before urgency strikes. Oxybutynin also blocks M1 receptors and has a weak direct relaxing effect on smooth muscle. Because it is a tertiary amine, it can cross into the brain, which explains its central side effects in older adults.",
  "body": [["Bladder", "Fewer involuntary detrusor contractions increase capacity and reduce urgency, frequency and leakage."], ["Mouth", "Salivary glands use M3 receptors, so saliva falls, though less with the patch than with tablets."], ["Gut", "Slower gut movement from muscarinic blockade causes constipation."], ["Eyes", "Blocking the ciliary muscle and pupil sphincter can blur near vision."], ["Brain", "Central M1 blockade can cause drowsiness, memory problems and confusion, especially in older adults."], ["Skin", "Itching and redness at the patch site are the most common local effects."]],
  "journey": "The patch is applied to the abdomen, hip or buttock and releases oxybutynin steadily through the skin into the blood for three to four days. Blood levels rise over the first one to two days and reach steady state after the second patch. Because the drug enters the blood directly rather than through the gut and liver, much less N-desethyloxybutynin is made than with tablets. Oxybutynin that does reach the liver is metabolized by CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in the urine. After the patch is removed levels fall with a half-life of several hours.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects are classic antimuscarinic effects: dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and urinary retention in people with a weak bladder or outlet obstruction. Less of the metabolite means less dry mouth than with tablets. Brain muscarinic blockade causes sleepiness and confusion and is a reason anticholinergics are listed as risky for older adults. Reduced sweating can lead to heat illness. The adhesive and drug can irritate the skin, so sites are rotated. Physostigmine can reverse severe central anticholinergic toxicity, though this is rarely needed with a patch.",
  "fun": "Oxytrol for Women was the first prescription overactive bladder drug to switch to over-the-counter sale in the US, sold only to women because men's urgency symptoms may come from prostate disease that needs a diagnosis.",
  "src": ["Oxytrol (oxybutynin transdermal system) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"acetohydroxamic-acid": {
  "origin": "Acetohydroxamic acid targets urease, an enzyme with a famous history. In 1926 James B. Sumner crystallized urease from jack beans, the first enzyme ever crystallized, proving that enzymes are proteins and earning a share of the 1946 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Later, doctors learned that bacteria such as Proteus use their own urease to split urea in urine, making it alkaline and building large branching struvite (infection) stones. Hydroxamic acids, which resemble urea, were found to block urease. Acetohydroxamic acid was developed as Lithostat by Mission Pharmacal and approved by FDA in 1983 as an add-on to antibiotics and surgery for chronic urease-producing urinary infections.",
  "timeline": [["1926", "James B. Sumner crystallizes jack bean urease, the first crystallized enzyme."], ["1946", "Sumner shares the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1983", "FDA approves acetohydroxamic acid (Lithostat)."]],
  "cell": "Urease is a nickel-containing enzyme made by bacteria such as Proteus, Klebsiella and some Staphylococcus species. It splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide. Ammonia raises urine pH, and in alkaline urine magnesium, ammonium and phosphate precipitate as struvite, along with calcium phosphate (carbonate apatite). Acetohydroxamic acid looks like urea and binds the active site, where its hydroxamate group chelates the two nickel ions. This blocks the enzyme. Less ammonia means lower urine pH and less supersaturation, so stones grow more slowly and new ones are less likely. It does not kill bacteria, but lower ammonia and pH can help antibiotics work.",
  "body": [["Urinary tract", "Lower urinary ammonia and pH slow the growth of struvite stones and reduce new stone formation."], ["Blood", "It can cause a hemolytic anemia that is usually mild but can be serious, so blood counts are watched."], ["Veins", "Phlebitis and deep vein thrombosis have been reported, including pulmonary embolism."], ["Brain and mood", "Headache is common, and anxiety, depression and tremor can occur."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea, loss of appetite and hair loss occur in some patients."]],
  "journey": "Acetohydroxamic acid is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking within an hour. Part of it is broken down to acetamide. A large fraction is excreted unchanged by the kidneys, which concentrates it in the urine where the target bacteria live. The half-life is several hours with normal kidney function but longer when kidneys are impaired, and it is less effective and more toxic in poor kidney function.",
  "sidefx": "Hydroxamic acids chelate metals and interact with other enzymes, which may explain their effects outside bacteria. Hemolytic anemia, sometimes with reticulocytosis, is a key toxicity and is more likely in kidney disease. Clotting problems such as phlebitis and deep vein thrombosis have been reported. Headache, anxiety and nausea are common. The drug is teratogenic in animals and is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Urease from jack beans was the first enzyme ever crystallized, and the same enzyme family in bacteria is what this drug was designed to block.",
  "src": ["Lithostat (acetohydroxamic acid) FDA label", "Sumner JB. J Biol Chem 1926"]
},
"sodium-ferric-gluconate": {
  "origin": "Intravenous iron began with iron dextran, which works but can cause dangerous allergic reactions, partly due to antibodies against the dextran sugar. Sodium ferric gluconate complex in sucrose, a dextran-free iron-sugar complex, was used in Europe for years before it reached the US. FDA approved it as Ferrlecit in 1999 for iron deficiency in people on hemodialysis receiving epoetin. It mattered because people on dialysis lose iron steadily and often cannot absorb enough from pills, and ESA therapy works only when enough iron is available.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Iron dextran introduces intravenous iron but carries anaphylaxis risk."], ["1999", "FDA approves sodium ferric gluconate complex (Ferrlecit) for dialysis patients."]],
  "cell": "Each particle has a core of ferric oxyhydroxide wrapped in a shell of gluconate and sucrose that keeps the iron from being released as free, reactive iron. After infusion, macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take up the complex. Inside these cells the iron is freed from the shell and either stored as ferritin or exported through ferroportin to transferrin in the blood. Transferrin carries the iron to developing red cells in the marrow, which take it in through transferrin receptor 1. There it is inserted into protoporphyrin IX to make heme for hemoglobin.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "More iron available to red cell precursors means more hemoglobin, especially when ESA therapy is driving production."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation rise as macrophages and the liver store the new iron."], ["Blood vessels", "Infusion can cause flushing, low blood pressure and chest discomfort from free iron and infusion reactions."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and cramps can occur during or after infusion."]],
  "journey": "It is given intravenously, often during dialysis. The complex is cleared from plasma within hours as macrophages take it up. Iron is then released gradually to transferrin and incorporated into red cells over days to weeks. Very little iron is lost in urine, and the body has no active way to excrete iron, so the iron stays in body stores. This is why iron levels are checked to avoid overload.",
  "sidefx": "Hypotension and flushing are thought to come from labile iron released too quickly and from infusion reactions, which is why the infusion is given slowly. Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur, though less often than with high molecular weight iron dextran. Repeated dosing without monitoring can cause iron overload, since the body cannot excrete excess iron. Serious reactions are treated with standard anaphylaxis care; deferoxamine chelation is used only for true iron overload.",
  "fun": "The human body has no regulated way to excrete iron; it controls iron only at the point of absorption, which is why intravenous iron goes straight into storage.",
  "src": ["Ferrlecit (sodium ferric gluconate complex in sucrose) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"methoxy-polyethylene-glycol-epoetin-beta": {
  "origin": "In 1977 Eugene Goldwasser at the University of Chicago purified human erythropoietin (EPO) from the urine of people with aplastic anemia. In the early 1980s Fu-Kuei Lin at Amgen cloned the EPO gene, leading to epoetin alfa in 1989. Epoetin beta, a similar recombinant EPO, was developed in Europe. Roche then attached a large methoxy polyethylene glycol chain to epoetin beta, creating a continuous erythropoietin receptor activator (CERA) that lasts weeks. FDA approved it as Mircera in 2007 for anemia of chronic kidney disease, but patent litigation with Amgen kept it off the US market until 2014. It later gained pediatric approval.",
  "timeline": [["1977", "Eugene Goldwasser purifies human erythropoietin from urine."], ["1989", "FDA approves the first recombinant EPO, epoetin alfa."], ["2007", "FDA approves Mircera."], ["2014", "Mircera launches in the US after patent litigation ends."]],
  "cell": "Mircera binds the erythropoietin receptor (EpoR) on erythroid progenitor cells (CFU-E and proerythroblasts) in the bone marrow. EpoR is a cytokine receptor without its own enzyme activity. Binding brings two receptor chains together and activates JAK2 kinases attached to them. JAK2 phosphorylates the receptor and STAT5, which moves into the nucleus and turns on survival genes such as Bcl-xL. The main effect is to stop programmed cell death of red cell precursors, so more of them mature into reticulocytes and red cells. Compared with epoetin, it binds the receptor with lower affinity and lets go faster, but the huge PEG chain slows its clearance, so it stimulates the receptor steadily for weeks.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "More red cell precursors survive and mature, raising reticulocytes and then hemoglobin over weeks."], ["Blood", "Higher hemoglobin reduces fatigue and the need for transfusions, but thicker blood raises clot risk."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure often rises, linked to increased red cell mass and vascular effects."], ["Heart and brain", "Aiming for high hemoglobin targets increases heart attack, stroke and death, so the lowest effective target is used."], ["Dialysis access", "Higher red cell mass increases clotting of dialysis access grafts."]],
  "journey": "It is given by IV or subcutaneous injection, usually once or twice a month. The large pegylated molecule stays mostly in the blood and is cleared very slowly, with a half-life of more than five days by either route, much longer than epoetin. It is not broken down by liver enzymes like a small molecule; it is removed by receptor uptake and protein breakdown. Reticulocytes rise within days and hemoglobin climbs over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from making more red cells. Rising red cell mass and blood viscosity raise blood pressure and clot risk, and high hemoglobin targets increase heart attack, stroke and death, which is the subject of the boxed warning. Seizures can occur when blood pressure rises quickly. Rarely, antibodies against EPO cause pure red cell aplasia, in which the marrow stops making red cells. In cancer patients, ESAs carry a warning about tumor progression; Mircera is not indicated for anemia from chemotherapy. There is no antidote; the dose is held.",
  "fun": "Mircera was approved by FDA in 2007 but could not be sold in the US for seven years because a court found it infringed Amgen's EPO patents.",
  "src": ["Mircera (methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta) FDA label", "Miyake T, Kung CK, Goldwasser E. J Biol Chem 1977"]
},
"daprodustat": {
  "origin": "Daprodustat grew out of Nobel-winning science on how cells sense oxygen. In the 1990s Gregg Semenza identified hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF), the switch that turns on the EPO gene, and William Kaelin and Peter Ratcliffe showed how prolyl hydroxylase enzymes and the von Hippel-Lindau protein tag HIF for destruction when oxygen is plentiful. They shared the 2019 Nobel Prize. Drug companies reasoned that blocking prolyl hydroxylase would fool the body into making its own EPO. GlaxoSmithKline developed daprodustat, which was approved in Japan in 2020. FDA approved it as Jesduvroq in February 2023 as the first oral treatment for anemia of CKD in adults on dialysis for at least four months.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Semenza, Kaelin and Ratcliffe uncover the HIF oxygen-sensing pathway."], ["2019", "The three share the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2020", "Daprodustat is approved in Japan."], ["2023", "FDA approves Jesduvroq for anemia of CKD in dialysis patients."]],
  "cell": "In normal oxygen, HIF prolyl hydroxylase enzymes (PHD1, PHD2 and PHD3) use oxygen, iron and 2-oxoglutarate to add hydroxyl groups to proline residues on HIF-alpha. The von Hippel-Lindau protein then recognizes hydroxylated HIF-alpha and marks it with ubiquitin for destruction by the proteasome. Daprodustat reversibly blocks these PHD enzymes by competing with 2-oxoglutarate. HIF-alpha (especially HIF-2 alpha) then escapes destruction, pairs with HIF-beta in the nucleus and turns on hypoxia genes. These include EPO in the kidney and liver, and iron handling genes such as DMT1, duodenal cytochrome b and transferrin. Hepcidin also falls, so more iron is absorbed and released from stores.",
  "body": [["Kidney and liver", "HIF activation raises the body's own EPO production into a near-normal range."], ["Bone marrow", "More EPO drives red cell production and a gradual rise in hemoglobin."], ["Gut and iron stores", "Lower hepcidin and more iron transporters improve iron absorption and release for new red cells."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, and thrombotic events and heart failure hospitalizations are concerns."], ["Stomach and gut", "GI erosions and bleeding have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Daprodustat is a tablet taken once a day. It is absorbed within a few hours and is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2C8, so strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil are contraindicated because they greatly raise levels. The parent drug has a short half-life of a few hours, but some active metabolites last longer. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Hemoglobin rises over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Like ESAs, raising hemoglobin increases clot risk, and Jesduvroq carries a boxed warning for death, heart attack, stroke, venous thromboembolism and dialysis access thrombosis. Because HIF controls many genes beyond EPO, including some related to blood vessel growth, there are theoretical concerns about tumor growth and retinal vessel disease. Heart failure hospitalizations and GI erosions occurred more often in trials. It is not approved for patients not on dialysis. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "Daprodustat essentially tricks kidney cells into thinking they are short of oxygen, much like the body's natural response to living at high altitude.",
  "src": ["Jesduvroq (daprodustat) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2019 press release", "Singh AK et al. ASCEND-D trial, NEJM 2021"]
},
"vadadustat": {
  "origin": "Vadadustat was developed by Akebia Therapeutics in the US, based on the hypoxia-inducible factor pathway discovered by Semenza, Kaelin and Ratcliffe (Nobel Prize 2019). Akebia aimed for an oral pill that would raise the body's own EPO more gently than injected ESAs. It was approved in Japan in 2020 through partner Mitsubishi Tanabe. FDA first declined to approve it in 2022 over safety concerns, including liver injury and thrombotic events in patients not on dialysis. After further review, FDA approved it as Vafseo in March 2024 for anemia of CKD in adults who have been on dialysis for at least three months.",
  "timeline": [["2019", "The HIF oxygen-sensing discovery wins the Nobel Prize."], ["2020", "Vadadustat is approved in Japan."], ["2022", "FDA issues a complete response letter declining approval."], ["2024", "FDA approves Vafseo for anemia of CKD in dialysis patients."]],
  "cell": "Vadadustat reversibly inhibits HIF prolyl hydroxylase domain enzymes PHD1, PHD2 and PHD3, competing with their cofactor 2-oxoglutarate. Without hydroxylation, HIF-alpha is not recognized by the von Hippel-Lindau ubiquitin ligase and is not sent to the proteasome. Stabilized HIF-alpha, particularly HIF-2 alpha, moves into the nucleus, pairs with HIF-beta and binds hypoxia response elements. This increases transcription of EPO in kidney interstitial cells and the liver, and of iron handling genes. Hepcidin falls, so ferroportin stays on gut cells and macrophages and more iron reaches transferrin. The combined effect is more red cell production with better iron use.",
  "body": [["Kidney and liver", "Stabilized HIF raises endogenous EPO production, pulsing up after each dose."], ["Bone marrow", "EPO stimulates red cell precursors, increasing hemoglobin over weeks."], ["Iron handling", "Lower hepcidin improves iron absorption and mobilization from stores."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare serious liver injury have been reported, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Heart and vessels", "Thrombotic events, high blood pressure and heart failure are key risks."], ["Brain", "Seizures have been reported, sometimes linked to rising blood pressure."]],
  "journey": "Vadadustat is a tablet taken once daily, peaking in a few hours. It is very highly protein bound. Unlike daprodustat, it is metabolized mainly by glucuronidation (UGT enzymes) rather than CYP450. Its half-life is about 4.5 hours in healthy people and roughly twice that in dialysis patients. Oral iron and phosphate binders containing iron, calcium or aluminum can bind it in the gut, so it is taken apart from them.",
  "sidefx": "Raising hemoglobin and blood viscosity increases thrombosis, and Vafseo carries a boxed warning for death, heart attack, stroke, venous thromboembolism and access thrombosis. Hepatotoxicity, including rare severe cases, is a specific concern for vadadustat. Because HIF also regulates genes for blood vessel growth, malignancy is a theoretical concern. GI erosions and seizures have been seen. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Vadadustat reached patients in Japan four years before FDA approved it in the US.",
  "src": ["Vafseo (vadadustat) FDA label", "Eckardt KU et al. INNO2VATE trials, NEJM 2021"]
},
"lusutrombopag": {
  "origin": "Thrombopoietin (TPO), the main hormone that controls platelet production, was cloned in 1994 by several groups after decades of searching. Recombinant TPO caused antibody problems, so companies designed small molecules that activate the TPO receptor instead. The Japanese company Shionogi developed lusutrombopag, which was approved in Japan in 2015. FDA approved it as Mulpleta in 2018 to raise platelet counts in adults with chronic liver disease who are scheduled for a procedure, offering an alternative to platelet transfusion.",
  "timeline": [["1994", "Thrombopoietin is cloned by several research groups."], ["2015", "Lusutrombopag is approved in Japan."], ["2018", "FDA approves Mulpleta for thrombocytopenia before procedures in chronic liver disease."]],
  "cell": "Lusutrombopag is a small-molecule agonist of the thrombopoietin receptor (c-Mpl). Unlike natural TPO, which binds the outside of the receptor, it binds the transmembrane region, so it does not compete with TPO. Binding activates the receptor-associated JAK2 kinase, which phosphorylates STAT3 and STAT5 and also activates the MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways. These signals tell megakaryocyte progenitors in the bone marrow to multiply and mature. Mature megakaryocytes then shed more platelets into the blood. In liver disease, the damaged liver makes less TPO and the enlarged spleen traps platelets, so boosting production helps raise the count.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocytes multiply and mature, so platelet output increases over about one to two weeks."], ["Blood", "A higher platelet count lowers the need for platelet transfusion before procedures."], ["Portal vein", "More platelets in a slow-flowing portal system can raise the risk of portal vein thrombosis."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect."]],
  "journey": "It is taken by mouth once daily for a short course of about a week, starting before the planned procedure. It is absorbed well and very highly protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by omega-oxidation through CYP4A enzymes, and it leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about a day. Platelets peak about two weeks after starting and drift back toward baseline over several weeks.",
  "sidefx": "The main concern is clotting, especially portal vein thrombosis, because raising platelet counts in people whose liver disease already slows portal blood flow adds to clot risk. Overshooting the platelet count increases this risk. Headache is the most common complaint. There is no reversal agent; the effect fades as platelets turn over.",
  "fun": "Lusutrombopag binds a different part of the TPO receptor than the body's own hormone, so the two can activate the receptor at the same time.",
  "src": ["Mulpleta (lusutrombopag) FDA label", "Kuter DJ. Thrombopoietin receptor agonists review"]
},
"fostamatinib": {
  "origin": "Fostamatinib was developed by Rigel Pharmaceuticals in South San Francisco. Rigel discovered R406, a small molecule that blocks spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK), an enzyme that relays signals from antibody receptors on immune cells. Because R406 was poorly absorbed, Rigel made a phosphate prodrug, fostamatinib. It was first studied in rheumatoid arthritis, but a partnership with AstraZeneca ended after mixed results. Rigel then focused on immune thrombocytopenia (ITP), where antibody-coated platelets are eaten by spleen macrophages. FDA approved it as Tavalisse in 2018 as the first SYK inhibitor for chronic ITP.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Rigel discovers the SYK inhibitor R406 and its prodrug fostamatinib."], ["2013", "AstraZeneca ends development for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["2018", "FDA approves Tavalisse for chronic ITP."]],
  "cell": "In ITP, autoantibodies coat platelets. Macrophages in the spleen and liver recognize the antibody tails with Fc-gamma receptors. When those receptors cluster, they activate spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK) through ITAM motifs. SYK then drives the actin rearrangement and signaling needed to engulf and destroy the platelet. R406, the active form of fostamatinib, competes with ATP at the SYK kinase site and blocks this signal. Macrophages can still see the coated platelets but cannot eat them as well, so platelets survive longer and the count rises. SYK also signals downstream of B cell receptors, which may contribute to its effect.",
  "body": [["Spleen and liver", "Less Fc receptor driven phagocytosis means fewer platelets destroyed and a higher platelet count."], ["Blood vessels", "Off-target kinase effects, including on VEGF receptor signaling, raise blood pressure."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are common."], ["Liver", "ALT and AST can rise, so liver tests are monitored."], ["White blood cells", "Neutrophil counts can fall, raising infection risk, so blood counts are checked."]],
  "journey": "Fostamatinib is a tablet taken twice daily. In the gut, intestinal alkaline phosphatase removes the phosphate group and releases R406, which is absorbed and peaks within a couple of hours. Almost no fostamatinib reaches the blood intact. R406 is highly protein bound and is broken down by CYP3A4 and UGT1A9. Its half-life is about 15 hours. Most of the dose leaves in feces. Responders usually see platelet rises within a few weeks.",
  "sidefx": "R406 is not perfectly selective and inhibits other kinases, including VEGF receptor 2, which is thought to explain the high blood pressure. Diarrhea and liver enzyme rises are common and are managed with dose changes. Blocking SYK in neutrophils and other immune cells helps explain neutropenia and infections. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise R406 levels. It can harm a fetus. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Fostamatinib works without stopping the antibodies; it simply makes spleen macrophages worse at eating the antibody-coated platelets.",
  "src": ["Tavalisse (fostamatinib) FDA label", "Bussel J et al. FIT trials, Am J Hematol 2018"]
},
"crizanlizumab": {
  "origin": "P-selectin is an adhesion molecule stored in platelets and endothelial cells, identified in the 1980s. Researchers learned that in sickle cell disease, P-selectin helps sickle red cells, white cells and platelets stick to vessel walls and to each other, plugging small vessels. The antibody was developed by Selexys Pharmaceuticals as SelG1 and acquired by Novartis in 2016. After the SUSTAIN trial showed fewer pain crises, FDA approved it as Adakveo in 2019 to reduce vaso-occlusive crises in people 16 and older. A later trial, STAND, did not confirm the benefit, and European regulators revoked its authorization in 2023, but it remains FDA approved in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "P-selectin is identified on activated platelets and endothelium."], ["2016", "Novartis acquires Selexys and its antibody SelG1."], ["2017", "SUSTAIN trial results show fewer vaso-occlusive crises."], ["2019", "FDA approves Adakveo."], ["2023", "Europe revokes its authorization after the STAND trial."]],
  "cell": "During inflammation and hypoxia, activated endothelial cells and platelets move P-selectin from storage granules (Weibel-Palade bodies and alpha granules) to their surface. P-selectin binds P-selectin glycoprotein ligand-1 (PSGL-1) on white cells and other ligands on sickle red cells. This tethers cells to the vessel wall and to each other, slowing flow so more cells sickle and form clumps that block small vessels. Crizanlizumab is a humanized IgG2 antibody that binds P-selectin and blocks this interaction. Fewer cells stick, so the chain of adhesion, stasis and vaso-occlusion is less likely to start.",
  "body": [["Small blood vessels", "Less adhesion of red cells, white cells and platelets reduces vessel blockage and pain crises."], ["Bones and joints", "Fewer vaso-occlusive events means fewer episodes of severe bone pain, though joint and back pain occur as side effects."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions with fever, chills and pain can occur."], ["Lab tests", "It can interfere with automated platelet counts in EDTA tubes, causing falsely low readings."]],
  "journey": "Crizanlizumab is given as an IV infusion, with two loading doses two weeks apart and then every four weeks. As an antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and is cleared slowly by protein breakdown and by binding its target. The half-life is about 11 days. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are infusion-related reactions, including fever, chills, nausea and pain, sometimes resembling a pain crisis. Arthralgia and back pain are common. Because P-selectin also has a role in immune cell trafficking, infection is a theoretical concern. Severe reactions are treated by stopping the infusion and giving supportive care. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Crizanlizumab can make a lab platelet count look falsely low because the antibody causes platelets to clump in the EDTA collection tube.",
  "src": ["Adakveo (crizanlizumab-tmca) FDA label", "Ataga KI et al. SUSTAIN trial, NEJM 2017"]
},
"voxelotor": {
  "origin": "Sickle cell disease was first described in Western medicine by James Herrick in 1910, and in 1949 Linus Pauling showed it was a 'molecular disease' caused by abnormal hemoglobin. Hemoglobin S polymerizes only when it gives up oxygen, so researchers reasoned that holding more hemoglobin in the oxygenated state could prevent sickling. Global Blood Therapeutics designed voxelotor to do this, and FDA granted it accelerated approval as Oxbryta in 2019. Pfizer acquired the company in 2022. In September 2024 Pfizer withdrew Oxbryta from the US and worldwide markets after newer data showed more vaso-occlusive crises and deaths, so it is no longer available.",
  "timeline": [["1910", "James Herrick describes sickle-shaped red cells."], ["1949", "Linus Pauling identifies sickle cell disease as a molecular disease of hemoglobin."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Oxbryta."], ["2022", "Pfizer acquires Global Blood Therapeutics."], ["2024", "Oxbryta is withdrawn from the US market in September."]],
  "cell": "Hemoglobin S has a valine in place of glutamate in the beta chain. When it releases oxygen and shifts to the tense (T, deoxy) state, this valine sticks to a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin, forming long polymers that bend the red cell into a sickle. Voxelotor binds covalently but reversibly (as a Schiff base) to the N-terminal valine of one alpha chain. This stabilizes the relaxed (R, oxygenated) state and raises hemoglobin's oxygen affinity, shifting the oxygen dissociation curve to the left. Fewer molecules are in the deoxy state at any time, so polymer formation, sickling and hemolysis fall.",
  "body": [["Red blood cells", "Less sickling means red cells live longer, raising hemoglobin and lowering reticulocytes."], ["Liver and bilirubin", "Less hemolysis lowers indirect bilirubin and LDH."], ["Tissues and oxygen", "Hemoglobin that holds oxygen more tightly may release less oxygen to tissues, a concern raised after withdrawal."], ["Gut and skin", "Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain, headache and rash were common side effects."]],
  "journey": "Voxelotor was a once-daily tablet. After absorption it partitions heavily into red blood cells, where it binds hemoglobin, so levels in whole blood are far higher than in plasma. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, and its half-life in sickle cell disease is about a day and a half. It leaves in both feces and urine.",
  "sidefx": "Common side effects were headache, diarrhea and rash, and hypersensitivity reactions occurred. Post-marketing studies found an imbalance in vaso-occlusive crises and deaths, leading to withdrawal in 2024. One proposed explanation is that a left-shifted oxygen curve raises hemoglobin numbers without improving, or even reducing, oxygen delivery to tissues. It also interfered with some lab measurements of hemoglobin subtypes. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Voxelotor binds so heavily to hemoglobin that most of the drug in the body is carried inside red blood cells rather than in plasma.",
  "src": ["Oxbryta (voxelotor) FDA label (withdrawn)", "FDA Drug Safety Communication, September 2024", "Vichinsky E et al. HOPE trial, NEJM 2019"]
},
"mitapivat": {
  "origin": "Pyruvate kinase deficiency is the most common enzyme defect of glycolysis and causes lifelong hemolytic anemia because red cells, which lack mitochondria, depend on glycolysis for energy. Agios Pharmaceuticals, a company founded on cancer metabolism research, developed mitapivat as the first drug that activates the red cell pyruvate kinase enzyme, including many mutant forms. FDA approved it as Pyrukynd in February 2022 as the first disease-modifying treatment for hemolytic anemia in adults with PK deficiency. In December 2025 FDA also approved it under the name Aqvesme for adults with alpha or beta thalassemia.",
  "timeline": [["1961", "Pyruvate kinase deficiency is described as a cause of hemolytic anemia."], ["2022", "FDA approves mitapivat (Pyrukynd) for PK deficiency."], ["2025", "FDA approves mitapivat (Aqvesme) for thalassemia."]],
  "cell": "Pyruvate kinase catalyzes the last step of glycolysis, moving a phosphate from phosphoenolpyruvate to ADP to make ATP and pyruvate. In red cells this enzyme is the PKR isoform. Mitapivat binds an allosteric site at the interface between subunits of the PKR tetramer, stabilizing the active form. This works on normal enzyme and on many mutant enzymes that are unstable or less active. Faster flux through glycolysis raises ATP and lowers upstream intermediates, including 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG). More ATP helps red cells maintain their membranes and ion pumps, so they survive longer and hemolysis falls. In thalassemia, extra ATP helps red cells cope with damage from excess globin chains.",
  "body": [["Red blood cells", "More ATP lets red cells keep their shape and survive longer, reducing hemolysis and raising hemoglobin."], ["Spleen and liver", "Fewer damaged cells means less clearance by the spleen and less jaundice."], ["Hormones", "Mitapivat inhibits aromatase, lowering estrone and estradiol in men."], ["Liver", "Hepatocellular injury has been reported in thalassemia patients, so liver tests are monitored."], ["Bones and sleep", "Back pain, joint pain and insomnia are among reported side effects."]],
  "journey": "Mitapivat is taken by mouth twice daily and absorbed quickly, peaking within an hour. It is highly protein bound and metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers change levels. Its effective half-life is a few hours. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Hemoglobin rises over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Stopping mitapivat suddenly can trigger acute hemolysis because red cells lose their energy boost at once, so the dose is tapered. Inhibition of aromatase explains lower estrogen levels in men and may contribute to bone effects. Raised urate and liver injury are reported. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Mitapivat came from a company founded to target cancer metabolism, and its first approval ended up being for an inherited red blood cell disease.",
  "src": ["Pyrukynd (mitapivat) FDA label", "Al-Samkari H et al. ACTIVATE trial, NEJM 2022"]
},
"concizumab": {
  "origin": "Tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI) was characterized in the 1980s by George Broze and colleagues as the natural brake on the tissue factor clotting pathway. Researchers proposed that releasing this brake could restore clotting in hemophilia without replacing the missing factor. Novo Nordisk developed concizumab, a humanized antibody against TFPI. Its trials were paused in 2020 after thrombotic events, then resumed with a new dosing approach. FDA approved it as Alhemo in December 2024 for prophylaxis in people with hemophilia A or B with inhibitors, and in July 2025 expanded approval to people without inhibitors. It is a daily injection given with a pen.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "George Broze and colleagues characterize tissue factor pathway inhibitor."], ["2020", "Concizumab trials pause after thrombotic events, then resume."], ["2024", "FDA approves Alhemo for hemophilia A or B with inhibitors."], ["2025", "Approval expands to hemophilia A or B without inhibitors."]],
  "cell": "Clotting starts when tissue factor (TF) at a wound binds factor VIIa and activates factors X and IX. TFPI quickly shuts this down: its Kunitz-2 domain binds and inhibits factor Xa, and its Kunitz-1 domain then locks the TF-VIIa complex. Normally the intrinsic tenase (factor VIIIa with IXa) takes over and amplifies thrombin. In hemophilia A or B, that amplification is missing. Concizumab binds the Kunitz-2 domain of TFPI and blocks it from inhibiting factor Xa. TF-VIIa can then keep producing factor Xa, which makes enough thrombin to form a stable clot without factor VIII or IX. This works whether or not the patient has inhibitors against replacement factors.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "More factor Xa and thrombin generation prevents spontaneous bleeds and joint bleeds."], ["Joints", "Fewer joint bleeds protect against hemophilic arthropathy over time."], ["Blood vessels", "Too much thrombin, especially with other clotting products, can cause arterial or venous clots."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions and hives are common."]],
  "journey": "Concizumab is injected under the skin once daily with a prefilled pen. As an antibody, it is absorbed slowly from the injection site. Its clearance is nonlinear because binding to TFPI on endothelium removes it (target-mediated disposition), so levels depend on how much TFPI is available. Drug levels are checked early to adjust the dose. It is broken down like other proteins and is not handled by liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "The main risk follows directly from the mechanism: with the natural brake removed, adding extra procoagulants (such as high amounts of factor concentrate or bypassing agents for a breakthrough bleed) can cause thrombosis. Thromboembolic events led to the 2020 trial pause. Hypersensitivity and injection site reactions occur. There is no direct antidote; the drug is held and supportive care given.",
  "fun": "Concizumab and marstacimab target the same small Kunitz-2 domain of TFPI, but concizumab is given every day while marstacimab is given once a week.",
  "src": ["Alhemo (concizumab-mtci) FDA label", "Broze GJ Jr. Tissue factor pathway inhibitor review"]
},
"marstacimab": {
  "origin": "Marstacimab is a fully human IgG1 antibody developed by Pfizer against tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI), the natural brake on the tissue factor clotting pathway first characterized in the 1980s. Pfizer aimed for a simpler preventive treatment for hemophilia than repeated IV factor infusions. In the BASIS trial, weekly injections cut bleeding rates compared with on-demand and prophylactic factor therapy. FDA approved it as Hympavzi in October 2024 for routine prophylaxis in people 12 and older with hemophilia A or B without inhibitors, making it the first once-weekly anti-TFPI treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Tissue factor pathway inhibitor is characterized as a natural anticoagulant."], ["2024", "FDA approves Hympavzi for hemophilia A or B without inhibitors."]],
  "cell": "TFPI uses its Kunitz-2 (K2) domain to bind and inhibit factor Xa, and its Kunitz-1 domain to inhibit the tissue factor-factor VIIa complex. This shuts off the extrinsic start of clotting within seconds, leaving amplification to the intrinsic tenase (factor VIIIa-IXa), which is missing in hemophilia. Marstacimab binds the K2 domain and neutralizes TFPI's ability to block factor Xa. The tissue factor-VIIa complex can then keep generating factor Xa, which, with factor Va, converts prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin generation rises enough to support clotting without factor VIII or IX. Lab markers such as D-dimer and prothrombin fragment 1.2 rise as expected.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Sustained factor Xa and thrombin generation prevents many spontaneous bleeds."], ["Joints", "Fewer joint bleeds help protect joints from long-term damage."], ["Blood vessels", "Excess thrombin generation can cause clots, especially with other procoagulant treatments or other clot risks."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions and itching are common."], ["Pregnancy", "It may cause fetal harm, based on animal data and mechanism."]],
  "journey": "Marstacimab is injected under the skin once weekly with a prefilled pen or syringe. It is absorbed slowly over several days. Binding to TFPI on endothelial cells removes it from the blood (target-mediated clearance), so it has no single fixed half-life. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes or kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Because it removes a natural anticoagulant, thromboembolic events are the main safety concern, particularly when other clotting products are used for a breakthrough bleed or when other clot risks exist. Hypersensitivity reactions and injection site reactions occur. Animal data suggest embryofetal risk. There is no antidote; it is held and factor or supportive care given as needed.",
  "fun": "Marstacimab turns a bleeding problem on its head: instead of adding the missing factor, it takes away a brake on clotting.",
  "src": ["Hympavzi (marstacimab-hncq) FDA label", "Matino D et al. BASIS trial"]
},
"fitusiran": {
  "origin": "Fitusiran comes from RNA interference, a natural gene-silencing system described in 1998 by Andrew Fire and Craig Mello in worms, work that won the 2006 Nobel Prize. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals turned RNAi into medicines by attaching small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) to a sugar, GalNAc, that liver cells grab. Alnylam reasoned that people with a natural antithrombin deficiency who also had hemophilia bled less, so lowering antithrombin might rebalance clotting. Fitusiran was developed by Alnylam and then Sanofi. FDA approved it as Qfitlia in March 2025 for prophylaxis in people 12 and older with hemophilia A or B with or without inhibitors, the first siRNA for hemophilia.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2006", "Fire and Mello receive the Nobel Prize."], ["2025", "FDA approves Qfitlia for hemophilia A or B with or without inhibitors."]],
  "cell": "Antithrombin, made by the liver from the SERPINC1 gene, is a natural anticoagulant that inactivates thrombin and factor Xa. Fitusiran is a double-stranded siRNA linked to three GalNAc sugars. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, which pulls the drug inside. The guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC), which finds the matching SERPINC1 mRNA and cuts it. With less mRNA, the liver makes less antithrombin. Lower antithrombin lets more thrombin survive and act, so thrombin generation rises even though factor VIII or IX is missing. Because RISC is reused, one dose silences the gene for weeks to months.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make much less antithrombin; liver enzyme rises can occur."], ["Blood clotting", "With antithrombin lowered to a target range, more thrombin forms and bleeding episodes fall."], ["Blood vessels", "If antithrombin falls too low, or other clot risks are present, arterial or venous clots, including in the brain, can occur."], ["Gallbladder", "Gallstones and gallbladder inflammation have been seen, which is part of the boxed warning."]],
  "journey": "Fitusiran is injected under the skin every one or two months, with timing adjusted by antithrombin levels. It leaves the blood within hours because liver cells take it up so efficiently. Inside the liver it is slowly broken down by nucleases. The drug's plasma half-life is short, but the silencing effect lasts weeks to months because RISC keeps working. Little intact drug is excreted in urine.",
  "sidefx": "The key risk follows from the mechanism: too little antithrombin tips the balance toward clotting. Qfitlia carries a boxed warning for thrombotic events and for acute and recurrent gallbladder disease. Antithrombin activity is monitored to keep it in a target range. Breakthrough bleeds are treated with reduced amounts of factor or bypassing agents to avoid clots. Because the silencing lasts for months, the effect cannot be switched off quickly after a dose. Liver enzyme rises are also monitored.",
  "fun": "Fitusiran was inspired by an observation that people with hemophilia who also inherited an antithrombin or protein C deficiency tended to bleed less.",
  "src": ["Qfitlia (fitusiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998"]
},
"protein-c-concentrate": {
  "origin": "Protein C was discovered in 1976 by Swedish biochemist Johan Stenflo, who found a new vitamin K-dependent protein in bovine plasma and named it for its position as the third peak, peak C, in his chromatography. Researchers soon learned it is a natural anticoagulant. Babies born with severe protein C deficiency develop purpura fulminans, widespread clotting in skin vessels that can be fatal. Fresh frozen plasma was the only option for years. Baxter developed Ceprotin, a purified concentrate made from human plasma, and FDA approved it in 2007 for severe congenital protein C deficiency.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Johan Stenflo discovers protein C."], ["1980s", "Severe congenital protein C deficiency is linked to neonatal purpura fulminans."], ["2007", "FDA approves Ceprotin."]],
  "cell": "Protein C is a vitamin K-dependent zymogen made by the liver. When thrombin binds thrombomodulin on healthy endothelium, thrombin's job changes: instead of making fibrin, it activates protein C, a step made faster by the endothelial protein C receptor. Activated protein C (APC) is a serine protease. With protein S as its cofactor on phospholipid surfaces, APC cuts and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. Without these cofactors, the prothrombinase and tenase complexes slow down and thrombin generation falls. APC also inhibits plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, favoring clot breakdown, and has anti-inflammatory signaling effects. Replacing protein C restores this brake on clotting.",
  "body": [["Small blood vessels", "Restored protein C stops microvascular clots that cause purpura fulminans and skin death."], ["Skin", "It helps prevent warfarin-induced skin necrosis in deficient patients starting warfarin."], ["Blood clotting", "Inactivation of factors Va and VIIIa reduces thrombin generation and new clot formation."], ["Immune system", "Itching, rash and rare allergic reactions can occur, as with other plasma products."]],
  "journey": "Ceprotin is given as an IV injection, so it acts immediately. It circulates as the inactive zymogen until activated on endothelium. It is consumed by activation and cleared like other plasma proteins. The half-life is about 10 hours on average but can be much shorter during active clotting, when protein C is used up quickly, so dosing and monitoring are frequent during acute episodes.",
  "sidefx": "Because it boosts a natural anticoagulant, bleeding can increase when it is combined with other anticoagulants or thrombolytics. Being a human plasma product, it carries a small theoretical risk of transmitting infections despite viral inactivation steps, and it can cause allergic reactions. It contains trace heparin, so it is avoided in people with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. There is no specific reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Protein C got its simple name because it was the third peak (C) in Stenflo's chromatography column.",
  "src": ["Ceprotin (protein C concentrate, human) FDA label", "Stenflo J. J Biol Chem 1976"]
},
"aminolevulinic-acid": {
  "origin": "Photodynamic therapy began around 1900 when Oscar Raab, a medical student in Munich working with Hermann von Tappeiner, found that acridine dye plus light killed microbes. Von Tappeiner coined the term photodynamic. Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) is the first committed building block of heme, made naturally in every cell. In 1990 James Kennedy, Roy Pottier and colleagues in Canada showed that ALA applied to skin causes abnormal cells to build up the light-sensitive pigment protoporphyrin IX, which light then activates. DUSA Pharmaceuticals developed the Levulan Kerastick, approved by FDA in 1999 for actinic keratoses with blue light. Ameluz gel was approved in 2016, and oral ALA (Gleolan) was approved in 2017 to make malignant glioma glow pink under blue light during surgery.",
  "timeline": [["c. 1900", "Raab and von Tappeiner describe the photodynamic effect."], ["1990", "Kennedy and Pottier report topical ALA photodynamic therapy."], ["1999", "FDA approves Levulan Kerastick for actinic keratoses."], ["2016", "FDA approves Ameluz gel."], ["2017", "FDA approves oral ALA (Gleolan) for glioma visualization."]],
  "cell": "Normally the heme pathway is held back by feedback: heme inhibits ALA synthase, the first enzyme. Supplying ALA directly bypasses this control. Cells take up ALA and convert it through porphobilinogen and other intermediates to protoporphyrin IX (PpIX) in the mitochondria. Ferrochelatase then inserts iron to make heme, but this step is slow, and dysplastic and tumor cells have relatively low ferrochelatase and high uptake, so PpIX accumulates in them. When light near 417 nm (blue) or red light hits PpIX, it absorbs energy and transfers it to oxygen, forming singlet oxygen and other reactive oxygen species. These damage membranes and mitochondria and kill the abnormal cells, while normal skin accumulates less PpIX.",
  "body": [["Skin lesions", "Actinic keratosis cells that build up PpIX are destroyed by light, and the lesions crust and clear."], ["Treated skin", "Stinging, burning, redness and swelling occur during and after illumination."], ["Whole skin", "For about two days the treated area stays light sensitive, so sunlight and bright light are avoided."], ["Brain tumor (oral form)", "Glioma cells accumulate PpIX and fluoresce pink under blue light, helping surgeons see tumor edges."]],
  "journey": "For actinic keratoses, the solution is painted onto lesions and left for several hours, sometimes under occlusion, then the skin is exposed to blue light. Very little ALA reaches the blood from the skin, and the small amount absorbed is cleared within hours. Locally, PpIX builds up over hours and is then converted to heme or cleared, so photosensitivity fades over about two days. Oral ALA for glioma is absorbed quickly and taken a few hours before surgery.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come directly from light-activated damage: stinging, burning, redness, swelling, scaling and crusting at treated sites. Pain during illumination reflects reactive oxygen species activating nerve endings. Until PpIX clears, sun exposure can cause a severe phototoxic burn. Severe hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote; light avoidance and cooling help.",
  "fun": "With oral ALA, surgeons switch the microscope to blue light and malignant glioma tissue glows a vivid pink-violet against normal brain.",
  "src": ["Levulan Kerastick (aminolevulinic acid) FDA label", "Gleolan FDA label", "Kennedy JC, Pottier RH, Pross DC. J Photochem Photobiol B 1990"]
},
"givosiran": {
  "origin": "Acute hepatic porphyrias are inherited defects in heme synthesis. During an attack, the liver's ALA synthase 1 (ALAS1) ramps up, and toxic intermediates ALA and porphobilinogen flood the body, causing severe abdominal pain and nerve damage. Treatment was hemin infusion, which works by feedback. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, a pioneer of RNA interference drugs, designed givosiran to switch off ALAS1 directly in the liver. FDA approved it as Givlaari in November 2019, the first treatment for acute hepatic porphyria to prevent attacks and the first GalNAc-conjugated siRNA to reach patients.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2018", "FDA approves the first siRNA drug, patisiran."], ["2019", "FDA approves givosiran (Givlaari) for acute hepatic porphyria."]],
  "cell": "Givosiran is a double-stranded siRNA linked to a GalNAc sugar cluster. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, which draws the drug into liver cells. The antisense strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC), which binds and cleaves ALAS1 mRNA. Less ALAS1 enzyme means less ALA made at the start of the heme pathway. Because the downstream defect in porphyria (such as low porphobilinogen deaminase) is a bottleneck, lowering input prevents the buildup of ALA and porphobilinogen, which are thought to be neurotoxic. Attacks become less frequent and chronic symptoms improve.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Hepatic ALAS1 falls, cutting production of ALA and porphobilinogen; transaminases can rise."], ["Nerves", "Lower neurotoxic intermediates mean fewer attacks of abdominal pain, weakness and neuropathy."], ["Kidneys", "Rises in creatinine and declines in kidney function have been reported."], ["Blood", "Homocysteine can rise, possibly because heme-dependent enzymes in the liver are affected."], ["Pancreas", "Acute pancreatitis has been reported, sometimes severe."]],
  "journey": "Givosiran is injected under the skin once a month by a health care professional. It is absorbed and taken up by the liver within hours, with a plasma half-life of about six hours. Nucleases in the liver break it down, forming an active metabolite with one fewer nucleotide. A small fraction leaves unchanged in urine. Because RISC keeps working, ALA and porphobilinogen stay lowered for weeks after each dose.",
  "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises reflect the drug's concentration in hepatocytes. Kidney toxicity, raised homocysteine and pancreatitis are reported, and the reasons are not fully understood. Injection site reactions and nausea are common. Anaphylaxis can occur, so patients are observed. There is no reversal agent; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "Some historians have speculated that King George III's bouts of madness were due to porphyria, though this theory is debated.",
  "src": ["Givlaari (givosiran) FDA label", "Balwani M et al. ENVISION trial, NEJM 2020"]
},
"danicopan": {
  "origin": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is a rare blood disease in which red cells lack the protective proteins CD55 and CD59, so complement attacks them. The C5 antibodies eculizumab (2007) and ravulizumab (2018) stopped red cells from bursting in the bloodstream, but many patients stayed anemic because C3 fragments still coated their cells and the spleen and liver removed them (extravascular hemolysis). Achillion Pharmaceuticals developed danicopan, an oral factor D inhibitor, and Alexion (now part of AstraZeneca) acquired Achillion in 2019. FDA approved danicopan as Voydeya in March 2024 as an add-on to ravulizumab or eculizumab for extravascular hemolysis in adults with PNH, the first factor D inhibitor.",
  "timeline": [["2007", "FDA approves eculizumab for PNH."], ["2019", "Alexion acquires Achillion and danicopan."], ["2024", "FDA approves Voydeya as add-on therapy for PNH."]],
  "cell": "The alternative complement pathway runs continuously at a low level. C3b deposited on a cell binds factor B, and factor D, a serine protease, cleaves bound factor B into Bb. The resulting C3bBb is the alternative pathway C3 convertase, which splits more C3 and creates an amplification loop. Danicopan reversibly binds and blocks factor D, so this convertase cannot form. In PNH, red cells lacking CD55 (which normally breaks up convertases) and CD59 get coated with C3 fragments. C5 inhibitors stop membrane attack complex formation but not C3 coating. By blocking upstream at factor D, danicopan reduces C3 deposition, so macrophages in the spleen and liver remove fewer red cells.",
  "body": [["Red blood cells", "Less C3 coating means fewer red cells removed by the spleen and liver, raising hemoglobin."], ["Spleen and liver", "Extravascular hemolysis falls, lowering the need for transfusions."], ["Immune system", "Blocking the alternative pathway weakens defense against encapsulated bacteria such as meningococcus."], ["Liver tests and lipids", "Raised liver enzymes and higher cholesterol have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Danicopan is a tablet taken three times a day with food, alongside a C5 inhibitor. It peaks in a few hours and has a half-life of about eight hours, which is why it is taken several times daily. It is highly protein bound and metabolized by several liver pathways, with CYP3A4 a minor route. It leaves mainly in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Because complement is key for killing encapsulated bacteria, blocking factor D raises the risk of serious infections, especially Neisseria meningitidis, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae. Voydeya carries a boxed warning for these and requires vaccination and a REMS program. Stopping it suddenly can let hemolysis rebound. Headache, vomiting and raised liver enzymes are reported. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Factor D circulates at the lowest concentration of any complement protein, making it an efficient bottleneck to target with a pill.",
  "src": ["Voydeya (danicopan) FDA label", "Lee JW et al. ALPHA trial, Lancet Haematol 2023"]
},
"tbo-filgrastim": {
  "origin": "Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) was purified in the mid-1980s, with work by Karl Welte and Malcolm Moore at Memorial Sloan Kettering and others, and Amgen cloned the human gene. Amgen's filgrastim (Neupogen) was approved in 1991. Tbo-filgrastim is a non-glycosylated G-CSF made in E. coli, developed by a company later acquired by Teva. It was approved in Europe in 2008 as a biosimilar. In the US the biosimilar pathway did not yet exist, so Teva filed a full biologics license application, and FDA approved it as Granix in 2012. It is therefore a separate biologic, not a legally interchangeable biosimilar of filgrastim.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "G-CSF is purified and its gene cloned."], ["1991", "FDA approves filgrastim (Neupogen)."], ["2008", "Tbo-filgrastim is approved in Europe."], ["2012", "FDA approves Granix through a full biologics license application."]],
  "cell": "Tbo-filgrastim binds the G-CSF receptor (CSF3R) on myeloid progenitor cells and mature neutrophils. Binding causes two receptor chains to pair, activating JAK1 and JAK2 kinases, which phosphorylate STAT3 and STAT5. Other pathways such as PI3K-AKT and MAPK are also turned on. These signals drive neutrophil precursors in the marrow to multiply, mature faster and survive longer. G-CSF also lowers CXCL12 retention signals in the marrow, so mature neutrophils are released into the blood. It can enhance neutrophil function, such as phagocytosis. The result is a faster rise in neutrophil counts after chemotherapy.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil precursors multiply and mature faster, and expansion of the marrow causes bone pain."], ["Blood", "Absolute neutrophil count rises within a day or two, shortening the time spent with severe neutropenia."], ["Spleen", "Neutrophil production in the spleen can enlarge it and, rarely, cause rupture."], ["Lungs", "Neutrophil accumulation can cause ARDS in rare cases."], ["Kidneys", "Glomerulonephritis has been reported with G-CSF products."]],
  "journey": "Tbo-filgrastim is injected under the skin once daily, starting a day after chemotherapy. It peaks within several hours and has a half-life of about three to four hours. It is cleared by binding to neutrophils (receptor-mediated uptake) and by the kidneys. As neutrophil counts rise, more drug is removed, which acts as a natural brake on its levels.",
  "sidefx": "Bone pain is the most common effect, caused by rapid marrow expansion and is often treated with simple analgesics. Splenic enlargement and rupture, ARDS, capillary leak syndrome and glomerulonephritis are rare but serious effects linked to neutrophil activation and expansion. In sickle cell disease, G-CSF can trigger severe sickle crises. Allergic reactions occur. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "Tbo-filgrastim was sold as a biosimilar in Europe but had to be approved as a new biologic in the US because the US biosimilar law was not yet in place.",
  "src": ["Granix (tbo-filgrastim) FDA label", "Welte K et al. PNAS 1985"]
},
"factor-ix": {
  "origin": "In 1952 Rosemary Biggs and colleagues in Oxford reported that some people with hemophilia lacked a different clotting factor than the classic factor VIII. They named it Christmas disease after their first patient, Stephen Christmas, and the paper appeared in the Christmas issue of the BMJ. The missing protein became known as factor IX. Early treatments used plasma and then prothrombin complex concentrates in the 1970s, which carried factors II, VII, IX and X and could cause clots. In the 1980s plasma products transmitted HIV and hepatitis to many people with hemophilia. This led to purified, virus-inactivated products such as AlphaNine SD, whose name reflects a solvent detergent step that destroys enveloped viruses.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Biggs and colleagues describe Christmas disease (hemophilia B)."], ["1970s", "Prothrombin complex concentrates are used to treat hemophilia B."], ["1980s", "HIV and hepatitis transmission through plasma products drive viral inactivation methods."], ["1990s", "High-purity plasma-derived factor IX products become available."]],
  "cell": "Factor IX is a vitamin K-dependent serine protease zymogen made by the liver. At a wound, factor XIa or the tissue factor-factor VIIa complex cleaves factor IX into active factor IXa. On the surface of activated platelets, factor IXa binds its cofactor factor VIIIa with calcium, forming the intrinsic tenase complex. This complex activates factor X far faster than tissue factor-VIIa alone, creating the large burst of thrombin needed for a stable clot. In hemophilia B, low factor IX means this amplification fails. Infused factor IX replaces the missing zymogen, restoring tenase activity and thrombin generation.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Restored factor IX corrects the aPTT and allows normal thrombin burst and stable clots."], ["Joints and muscles", "Treating or preventing bleeds protects joints from blood-induced damage."], ["Immune system", "Some people form neutralizing antibodies (inhibitors), and factor IX inhibitors are linked to anaphylaxis and kidney problems."], ["Blood vessels", "Older, less pure products with other factors raised the risk of clotting and DIC."]],
  "journey": "Factor IX is given by IV infusion and acts immediately. A large share of infused factor IX moves out of the blood into tissues and binds collagen IV in vessel walls, so recovery in plasma is lower than with factor VIII. The half-life is about 18 to 24 hours. It is cleared like other plasma proteins by catabolism.",
  "sidefx": "The most serious immune problem is inhibitor development; in hemophilia B, inhibitors often come with allergic reactions or anaphylaxis, and repeated exposure can cause nephrotic syndrome. Clots and DIC were more common with impure complex concentrates containing activated factors. Mild headache, nausea and chills occur. As a plasma product, it carries a small theoretical infection risk despite viral inactivation. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Analysis of DNA from the remains of the Russian royal family in 2009 showed that the 'royal disease' passed down from Queen Victoria was hemophilia B, a factor IX deficiency.",
  "src": ["AlphaNine SD (coagulation factor IX, human) FDA label", "Biggs R et al. BMJ 1952", "Rogaev EI et al. Science 2009"]
},
"von-willebrand-factor": {
  "origin": "In 1926 the Finnish physician Erik von Willebrand described a bleeding disorder in a family from the Aland Islands that affected both males and females, unlike hemophilia. It became known as von Willebrand disease, now the most common inherited bleeding disorder. The missing protein, von Willebrand factor (VWF), was identified decades later. Plasma-derived VWF/factor VIII concentrates such as Humate-P became the standard treatment. Baxalta (later Takeda) developed vonicog alfa, a recombinant VWF made in Chinese hamster ovary cells, and FDA approved it as Vonvendi in 2015, the first recombinant VWF product.",
  "timeline": [["1926", "Erik von Willebrand describes a bleeding disorder in an Aland Islands family."], ["1970s", "Von Willebrand factor is identified as distinct from factor VIII."], ["2015", "FDA approves Vonvendi, the first recombinant VWF."]],
  "cell": "VWF is a large multimeric glycoprotein made by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes. At sites of injury under high shear, VWF binds exposed subendothelial collagen through its A3 domain, and the shear force stretches it so its A1 domain grabs platelet glycoprotein Ib. This tethers platelets to the wound so they can activate and aggregate through GPIIb/IIIa. VWF also binds and protects factor VIII in the blood, preventing its rapid breakdown. Larger multimers are more active. Recombinant VWF contains ultra-large multimers because it is not exposed to ADAMTS13 during manufacture; ADAMTS13 in the patient's blood then trims them. Vonvendi contains no factor VIII, but the patient's own factor VIII rises within hours as VWF protects it.",
  "body": [["Platelets and vessel wall", "Restored VWF lets platelets stick to injured vessels, stopping mucosal and surgical bleeding."], ["Blood clotting", "Endogenous factor VIII rises over 6 to 24 hours because VWF stabilizes it."], ["Immune system", "Itching, allergic reactions and rare neutralizing antibodies can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Thromboembolic events can occur, especially with high factor VIII levels or other clot risks."]],
  "journey": "Recombinant VWF is given by IV infusion, often with factor VIII for the first dose in an acute bleed if factor VIII is low. It acts immediately on platelet adhesion. ADAMTS13 in the blood cleaves the ultra-large multimers into the normal size range. Its half-life is about a day. It is cleared by protein breakdown.",
  "sidefx": "Because it supports platelet adhesion and raises factor VIII, too much can contribute to clotting, so thromboembolic events are a warning. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis can occur, and some patients develop neutralizing antibodies that reduce its effect. Mild itching, headache and nausea are reported. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "The first patient von Willebrand studied was a girl named Hjordis from the Aland Islands, and several of her sisters had died of bleeding.",
  "src": ["Vonvendi (von Willebrand factor, recombinant) FDA label", "von Willebrand EA. Finska Lakaresallskapets Handlingar 1926"]
},
"anagrelide": {
  "origin": "Anagrelide is an imidazoquinazoline first studied in the 1970s as a drug to stop platelets clumping together. In early human studies, researchers noticed that it also lowered the platelet count itself, at amounts below those needed for the anti-aggregation effect. This surprise turned it into a treatment for essential thrombocythemia and other myeloproliferative disorders with very high platelet counts. FDA approved it as Agrylin in 1997. It gave patients an option that lowers platelets without the broad marrow suppression of hydroxyurea.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Anagrelide is studied as an inhibitor of platelet aggregation."], ["1980s", "Its platelet-lowering effect in humans is recognized and studied in thrombocythemia."], ["1997", "FDA approves Agrylin."]],
  "cell": "Anagrelide lowers platelet production by blocking the late maturation of megakaryocytes in the bone marrow. It reduces megakaryocyte size, ploidy and cytoplasmic maturation in the postmitotic phase, so fewer platelets bud off. The exact molecular target for this effect is not fully known, and it has little effect on red or white cell lines. Separately, anagrelide and especially its active metabolite 3-hydroxy-anagrelide inhibit phosphodiesterase 3 (PDE3). Higher cAMP in platelets reduces aggregation. In vascular smooth muscle, higher cAMP causes vasodilation, and in heart muscle it increases contractility and heart rate.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Megakaryocyte maturation slows, so platelet counts fall over one to two weeks."], ["Platelets", "PDE3 inhibition reduces aggregation, adding to bleeding risk with aspirin or anticoagulants."], ["Heart", "PDE3 effects cause palpitations, tachycardia, arrhythmias, QT prolongation and possible heart failure."], ["Blood vessels", "Vasodilation causes headache, flushing, dizziness and fluid retention."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are common."], ["Lungs", "Rare interstitial lung disease has been reported."]],
  "journey": "Anagrelide is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP1A2 to the active 3-hydroxy-anagrelide and the inactive RL603. The parent half-life is short, about 1.5 hours, and the active metabolite lasts a bit longer, so it is taken several times a day. Metabolites leave in the urine. Platelets rise back toward baseline within days of stopping. Smoking and CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine change its levels.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from PDE3 inhibition rather than the platelet effect. Raised cAMP in the heart explains palpitations, tachycardia, atrial fibrillation and, in some patients, heart failure; QT prolongation and torsades have been reported. Vasodilation explains headache and edema. Bleeding risk rises when combined with aspirin or other antithrombotics. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and supportive care given.",
  "fun": "Anagrelide was meant to be an antiplatelet drug, but its real use came from a side effect: it lowered platelet counts.",
  "src": ["Agrylin (anagrelide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"factor-xiii-concentrate": {
  "origin": "In 1948 Hungarian scientists Koloman Laki and Laszlo Lorand discovered that a plasma factor made fibrin clots insoluble, which became known as the Laki-Lorand factor, later factor XIII. In 1960 Duckert, Jung and Shmerling in Switzerland described the first patient with congenital factor XIII deficiency, a child with severe bleeding including from the umbilical cord. This rare disorder causes delayed bleeding, poor wound healing, miscarriage and a high risk of brain bleeds. CSL Behring developed a plasma-derived concentrate (Fibrogammin in Europe), which FDA approved as Corifact in 2011. A recombinant A-subunit product, Tretten, followed in 2013.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Laki and Lorand discover the fibrin-stabilizing factor."], ["1960", "Duckert and colleagues describe congenital factor XIII deficiency."], ["2011", "FDA approves Corifact."], ["2013", "FDA approves recombinant factor XIII A-subunit (Tretten)."]],
  "cell": "Factor XIII circulates as a tetramer of two A subunits, which carry the enzyme, and two B subunits, which carry and protect them. Thrombin cleaves an activation peptide from the A subunits, and in the presence of calcium the B subunits fall away, leaving active factor XIIIa. Factor XIIIa is a transglutaminase. It forms covalent gamma-glutamyl-epsilon-lysyl bonds between fibrin gamma chains and alpha chains, turning a loose fibrin network into a strong cross-linked mesh. It also attaches alpha2-antiplasmin to fibrin, making the clot resistant to plasmin. Without it, clots form but break down early, causing delayed bleeding.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Cross-linked fibrin makes clots stronger and slower to dissolve, preventing delayed bleeding."], ["Brain", "Regular prophylaxis lowers the high risk of intracranial hemorrhage in deficient patients."], ["Wounds", "Factor XIII helps wound healing by cross-linking matrix proteins."], ["Pregnancy", "It is needed to keep the placenta attached, so deficient women may miscarry without replacement."], ["Immune system", "Rash, itching and rare anaphylaxis can occur."]],
  "journey": "Corifact is given by IV infusion, usually once a month as prophylaxis. It acts immediately. Factor XIII has a very long half-life of about a week in adults, a bit shorter in children, which allows monthly dosing. It is used up as clots form and broken down like other plasma proteins.",
  "sidefx": "Because it strengthens clots, too much factor XIII or use in people with other clot risks can contribute to thrombosis. Some patients develop inhibitors against factor XIII. Hypersensitivity reactions, rash, itching, joint pain and headache are reported. As a plasma product, it has a small theoretical infection risk. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Umbilical stump bleeding in the first days of life is a classic first sign of severe congenital factor XIII deficiency.",
  "src": ["Corifact (factor XIII concentrate, human) FDA label", "Laki K, Lorand L. Science 1948"]
},
"efanesoctocog-alfa": {
  "origin": "Factor VIII was cloned in 1984 by teams at Genentech and Genetics Institute, and the first recombinant factor VIII was approved in 1992. Extended half-life versions, such as Fc fusion products, came later but hit a limit: factor VIII in the blood is carried by the patient's own von Willebrand factor, so it cannot outlast VWF's half-life of about 15 hours. Bioverativ (part of Sanofi) and Sobi designed efanesoctocog alfa to break this ceiling by adding a piece of VWF, an Fc region and XTEN polypeptides. FDA approved it as Altuviiio in February 2023 as a once-weekly factor VIII for hemophilia A.",
  "timeline": [["1984", "The factor VIII gene is cloned."], ["1992", "FDA approves the first recombinant factor VIII."], ["2014", "Fc fusion extended half-life factor VIII is approved."], ["2023", "FDA approves Altuviiio, the first factor VIII to break the VWF half-life ceiling."]],
  "cell": "Factor VIII is a cofactor. When activated by thrombin, factor VIIIa binds factor IXa on activated platelet membranes, forming the intrinsic tenase complex that activates factor X much faster, producing the thrombin burst needed for a stable clot. In hemophilia A, this complex fails. Efanesoctocog alfa is a single-chain factor VIII fused to the D'D3 domain of VWF, which normally binds factor VIII, so it does not bind the patient's own VWF and is not cleared along with it. An Fc region binds the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn), which rescues the protein from breakdown in endothelial cells and recycles it into the blood. Two XTEN polypeptides add bulk and slow clearance. When activated, the add-on parts are released and factor VIIIa works normally.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Factor VIII activity stays in the normal to near-normal range for much of the week after a dose."], ["Joints", "High sustained factor levels prevent most joint bleeds and protect joint health."], ["Immune system", "Inhibitor formation and hypersensitivity are possible, as with other factor VIII products."], ["Muscles and joints", "Headache, joint pain and back pain are reported side effects."]],
  "journey": "Altuviiio is given by IV injection once weekly for prophylaxis. It acts immediately. Because it is decoupled from endogenous VWF and recycled by FcRn, its half-life is about 47 hours in adults, about three to four times that of standard factor VIII. It is eventually broken down by protein catabolism.",
  "sidefx": "The main immune risk is forming neutralizing antibodies (inhibitors) that block factor VIII, though this has been uncommon in previously treated patients. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis can occur. Headache and joint pain are reported. Very high factor VIII levels could theoretically add to clot risk in people with other risk factors. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Altuviiio's design pulls factor VIII away from the patient's own VWF, which normally caps how long any factor VIII product can last in the blood.",
  "src": ["Altuviiio (antihemophilic factor recombinant, Fc-VWF-XTEN fusion protein-ehtl) FDA label", "von Drygalski A et al. XTEND-1 trial, NEJM 2023"]
},
"crovalimab": {
  "origin": "Crovalimab was developed by Chugai Pharmaceutical, a member of the Roche group in Japan, using its SMART-Ig recycling antibody technology. Standard C5 antibodies like eculizumab (2007) and ravulizumab (2018) are given by IV infusion. Chugai engineered crovalimab to release C5 inside cells so the same antibody can be recycled and reused, allowing small subcutaneous injections. It also binds a C5 variant common in some people of East Asian ancestry that eculizumab cannot bind. It was first approved in China in early 2024, and FDA approved it as Piasky in June 2024 for PNH in patients 13 and older.",
  "timeline": [["2007", "FDA approves eculizumab, the first C5 inhibitor, for PNH."], ["2024", "Crovalimab is approved in China and then by FDA in June as Piasky."]],
  "cell": "In PNH, red cells lack the GPI-anchored proteins CD55 and CD59, so complement C5 convertases form and the membrane attack complex (C5b-9) punches holes in them. Crovalimab is a humanized IgG1 that binds complement C5 at a site different from eculizumab and blocks its cleavage into C5a and C5b. Without C5b, the membrane attack complex cannot form and intravascular hemolysis stops. Its binding is pH-dependent: it binds C5 tightly in blood but releases it in the acidic endosome. The C5 is degraded in lysosomes, while the free antibody binds FcRn and is recycled back to the blood to bind more C5. It also binds the R885H C5 variant that resists eculizumab.",
  "body": [["Red blood cells", "Blocking the membrane attack complex stops intravascular hemolysis, lowering LDH and raising hemoglobin."], ["Blood vessels", "Less free hemoglobin preserves nitric oxide and lowers the high clot risk of PNH."], ["Immune system", "Blocking C5 weakens defense against Neisseria species, especially meningococcus."], ["Switching patients", "Drug-target-drug complexes can form when switching from another C5 antibody, causing type III hypersensitivity reactions."]],
  "journey": "Treatment starts with an IV loading dose, followed by weekly subcutaneous injections for several weeks and then injections every four weeks. Because it is recycled through FcRn and pH-dependent C5 release, its half-life is very long, about 53 days. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by protein catabolism rather than liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Because the terminal complement pathway is needed to kill Neisseria bacteria, C5 blockade sharply raises the risk of meningococcal infection, the subject of a boxed warning; vaccination and a REMS program are required. Switching from eculizumab or ravulizumab can cause immune complexes of antibody, C5 and the other antibody, leading to type III hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, joint pain and fever. Infusion and injection site reactions and upper respiratory infections occur. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "One crovalimab molecule can capture, release and recapture C5 many times, which is why monthly injections of a small volume are enough.",
  "src": ["Piasky (crovalimab-akkz) FDA label", "Roth A et al. COMMODORE trials"]
},
"ferric-maltol": {
  "origin": "Oral iron salts such as ferrous sulfate have been used since the 19th century but often cause stomach upset, partly because free iron irritates the gut. Maltol is a natural compound found in larch bark, pine needles and roasted malt, also used as a flavor enhancer. Researchers found that ferric iron bound tightly to three maltol molecules forms a stable, neutral complex that can deliver iron with less free iron in the gut. The UK company Shield Therapeutics developed ferric maltol, approved in Europe in 2016 as Feraccru. FDA approved it as Accrufer in 2019 for iron deficiency in adults.",
  "timeline": [["1830s", "Blaud's pills of ferrous sulfate popularize oral iron therapy."], ["2016", "Ferric maltol (Feraccru) is approved in Europe."], ["2019", "FDA approves Accrufer for iron deficiency in adults."]],
  "cell": "In ferric maltol, one ferric iron atom is held by three maltol molecules. This neutral, fat-soluble complex keeps iron from being released as free ferric ions in the gut lumen, where it would form insoluble hydroxides or irritate the lining. At the duodenal surface the complex releases its iron, which follows the normal path: duodenal cytochrome b reduces ferric to ferrous iron, DMT1 carries it into the enterocyte, and ferroportin exports it to the blood, where hephaestin oxidizes it back to ferric iron for transferrin. Because the body's normal controls (hepcidin acting on ferroportin) still apply, absorption adjusts to need. Transferrin then delivers iron to the marrow for hemoglobin and to ferritin stores.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "More iron available for red cell production raises hemoglobin within weeks."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation gradually rise as stores refill."], ["Gut", "Gas, diarrhea, constipation, abdominal pain and dark stools can occur, though the complex is designed to be gentler."], ["Bowel disease", "In inflammatory bowel disease, it may be linked to symptom flares in some patients."]],
  "journey": "Ferric maltol is a capsule taken twice daily on an empty stomach. The complex dissociates at the gut wall; iron is absorbed through the normal duodenal pathway and bound to transferrin, while maltol is absorbed separately and rapidly glucuronidated in the liver and excreted by the kidneys. Iron itself has no regulated route of excretion and is stored. Hemoglobin rises over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Gut side effects such as gas, diarrhea, constipation and abdominal pain come from iron and the maltol complex in the bowel. Stools often darken from unabsorbed iron. Long-term use without monitoring could cause iron overload. Accidental ingestion by children can cause iron poisoning, which damages the gut and then the liver; severe poisoning is treated with deferoxamine.",
  "fun": "Maltol, the iron carrier in this drug, gives freshly baked bread and caramel part of their sweet aroma.",
  "src": ["Accrufer (ferric maltol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"ferric-pyrophosphate-citrate": {
  "origin": "People on hemodialysis lose iron with every treatment through blood left in the circuit and testing. Intravenous iron-sugar complexes help, but they must first be processed by macrophages, which in chronic kidney disease are often blocked by high hepcidin from inflammation. Nephrologist Ajay Gupta developed soluble ferric pyrophosphate, an iron salt that can hand iron straight to transferrin, and Rockwell Medical brought it to market. FDA approved Triferic, added to the dialysate, in 2015, and an IV form, Triferic AVNU, in 2020. It was the first iron product designed to replace ongoing dialysis losses without building up ferritin stores.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Soluble ferric pyrophosphate is developed for dialysis iron replacement."], ["2015", "FDA approves Triferic, added to dialysate."], ["2020", "FDA approves Triferic AVNU for IV use."]],
  "cell": "Ferric pyrophosphate citrate is a water-soluble complex in which ferric iron is held by pyrophosphate and citrate. When it reaches the blood, it donates iron directly to apotransferrin, the empty form of the iron carrier. This bypasses the reticuloendothelial macrophages that must normally break down iron-carbohydrate complexes. In CKD, inflammation raises hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin and traps iron inside macrophages, a key cause of poor iron use. By loading transferrin directly, the drug avoids this hepcidin block. Transferrin then delivers iron to erythroid precursors through transferrin receptor 1 for heme synthesis.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Directly delivered iron supports steady hemoglobin production, which helps maintain hemoglobin with ESA therapy."], ["Iron stores", "Because iron goes to transferrin rather than macrophages, ferritin stores do not build up."], ["Blood vessels", "Hypotension and peripheral edema are reported during dialysis treatment."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms and pain in the arms or legs are reported."], ["Dialysis access", "Bleeding at the arteriovenous fistula site has been reported."]],
  "journey": "Triferic is added to the bicarbonate concentrate of dialysate so iron crosses the dialyzer membrane into the blood during each session; Triferic AVNU is infused into the dialysis circuit. Iron is transferred to transferrin within minutes to hours, and serum iron returns toward baseline with a half-life of about 1.5 hours. The iron is used for red cells or stored, since the body has no active route to excrete iron.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects such as headache, low blood pressure, edema and muscle cramps overlap with symptoms common during dialysis. Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur. Iron overload is possible without monitoring. There is no specific antidote; true iron overload would be treated with chelation.",
  "fun": "Triferic is the only iron product that can be given through the dialysis fluid itself, crossing the dialyzer membrane like other dissolved salts.",
  "src": ["Triferic and Triferic AVNU (ferric pyrophosphate citrate) FDA labels", "Gupta A et al. CRUISE trials"]
},
"pentazocine": {
  "origin": "Pentazocine is a benzomorphan opioid synthesized in the late 1950s by chemists at Sterling-Winthrop as part of an effort to find a strong painkiller with less addiction risk than morphine. FDA approved it as Talwin in 1967. Because it partly blocks mu receptors, it was first thought to have little abuse potential. In the 1970s, however, people began injecting crushed pentazocine tablets mixed with the antihistamine tripelennamine, a combination called 'Ts and Blues'. In response, the manufacturer added naloxone to the tablets, creating Talwin NX in the early 1980s. Naloxone does nothing when swallowed but blocks pentazocine if the tablet is injected. The brand has been discontinued and the injectable form is no longer marketed, but generic pentazocine-naloxone tablets remain.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Sterling-Winthrop chemists synthesize pentazocine."], ["1967", "FDA approves Talwin."], ["1970s", "Injection misuse of pentazocine with tripelennamine spreads."], ["1980s", "Pentazocine-naloxone tablets (Talwin NX) are introduced."]],
  "cell": "Pentazocine is a mixed agonist-antagonist. It is an agonist at kappa-opioid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled and, like mu receptors, reduce cAMP, open potassium channels and close calcium channels on pain-transmitting neurons, especially in the spinal cord. At mu-opioid receptors it acts as a weak partial agonist or antagonist. This gives a ceiling effect: past a certain point, more drug does not add much pain relief or respiratory depression. Kappa activation in the brain causes dysphoria and hallucinations. The added naloxone is a mu antagonist with poor oral bioavailability, so it only matters if the tablet is injected.",
  "body": [["Spinal cord and brain", "Kappa agonism reduces pain transmission, giving moderate pain relief with a ceiling."], ["Mood and perception", "Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, anxiety, confusion and hallucinations."], ["Heart and vessels", "It raises blood pressure, heart rate, pulmonary artery pressure and cardiac workload, unlike morphine."], ["Lungs", "Respiratory depression occurs but tends to plateau at higher amounts."], ["Opioid-dependent patients", "Mu antagonism can precipitate withdrawal in people taking full mu agonists."]],
  "journey": "Pentazocine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, but extensive first-pass metabolism in the liver lets only a fraction reach the blood. It starts working within about half an hour, and its half-life is about 2 to 3 hours. The liver oxidizes and glucuronidates it, and metabolites leave in the urine. Naloxone in the tablet is almost completely destroyed on the first pass when swallowed.",
  "sidefx": "Kappa effects explain the psychotomimetic side effects: dysphoria, hallucinations and confusion. Its sympathetic and cardiac effects raise blood pressure and heart work, making it a poor choice after a heart attack. Partial mu blockade can trigger withdrawal in people dependent on other opioids. Sedation, nausea and dizziness are common. Respiratory depression is possible, especially with other CNS depressants, and naloxone reverses it, though higher amounts may be needed than for pure mu agonists.",
  "fun": "Adding naloxone to pentazocine tablets was one of the first attempts at an abuse-deterrent opioid formulation.",
  "src": ["Pentazocine and naloxone tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"levorphanol": {
  "origin": "In 1946 the German chemist Rudolf Grewe synthesized morphinan, the basic four-ring skeleton of morphine without the oxygen bridge. Chemists at Hoffmann-La Roche used this to make racemorphan, a mix of two mirror-image forms. They found that the left-handed (levo) form, levorphanol, was the potent painkiller, while the right-handed form had cough-suppressing effects; its relative dextromethorphan became a common cough medicine. Levorphanol was introduced in the US as Levo-Dromoran in the early 1950s. It later fell out of favor but has regained interest for its long action and NMDA blocking effect in neuropathic pain. The brand is gone but generic tablets remain.",
  "timeline": [["1946", "Rudolf Grewe synthesizes the morphinan skeleton."], ["1940s", "Hoffmann-La Roche develops racemorphan and separates levorphanol."], ["1950s", "Levorphanol is introduced in the US as Levo-Dromoran."]],
  "cell": "Levorphanol is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled: activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic calcium channels, which reduces release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers. It also activates delta and kappa receptors. Unlike morphine, it blocks NMDA glutamate receptors (noncompetitive antagonism), which may help with neuropathic pain and slow tolerance. It also inhibits reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, strengthening descending pain-inhibiting pathways but adding a risk of serotonin syndrome.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu, delta and kappa activation plus NMDA blockade give strong, long-lasting pain relief."], ["Lungs", "Mu activation in the brainstem blunts the response to carbon dioxide, causing respiratory depression."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut slow movement and cause constipation."], ["Eyes", "Pupils constrict (miosis) from mu-driven parasympathetic activity."], ["Mood and sedation", "Sedation, dizziness and euphoria can occur."]],
  "journey": "Levorphanol is taken by mouth and absorbed well, with onset within an hour. It has a long half-life of about 11 to 16 hours, much longer than its pain-relief duration, so it can build up over several days. The liver metabolizes it by glucuronidation rather than CYP3A4, which means fewer CYP drug interactions. The glucuronide leaves in the urine, so kidney disease can raise levels.",
  "sidefx": "Mu agonism explains constipation, nausea, sedation and respiratory depression. Because its half-life outlasts its pain relief, repeated doses can accumulate and cause delayed or prolonged overdose. Reuptake inhibition adds risk of serotonin syndrome with other serotonergic drugs. Combining it with benzodiazepines or alcohol greatly increases respiratory depression. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, but repeated doses or an infusion may be needed because levorphanol lasts longer than naloxone.",
  "fun": "Levorphanol's mirror image, dextrorphan, is the active metabolite of the cough suppressant dextromethorphan.",
  "src": ["Levorphanol tartrate tablets FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"codeine-acetaminophen": {
  "origin": "Codeine was isolated from opium in 1832 by French chemist Pierre-Jean Robiquet. Acetaminophen was first made by Harmon Northrop Morse in 1878 and tested in patients by Joseph von Mering in the 1880s, but it was ignored until 1948, when Bernard Brodie and Julius Axelrod showed it was the active product of the older drugs acetanilide and phenacetin. McNeil launched Tylenol in 1955. Combining the two gave a moderate pain reliever, and Tylenol with Codeine became one of the most widely used opioid combinations. After deaths in children who were ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers, FDA in 2017 contraindicated codeine in children under 12 and for pain after tonsil or adenoid surgery in those under 18.",
  "timeline": [["1832", "Robiquet isolates codeine from opium."], ["1878", "Harmon Northrop Morse synthesizes acetaminophen."], ["1948", "Brodie and Axelrod identify acetaminophen as the active metabolite of acetanilide."], ["1955", "McNeil launches Tylenol."], ["2017", "FDA contraindicates codeine in children under 12."]],
  "cell": "Codeine itself binds mu-opioid receptors weakly. It works mainly as a prodrug: CYP2D6 in the liver O-demethylates about 5 to 10 percent of it to morphine, a full mu agonist. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled; activation lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels, so pain signals are dampened in the spinal cord and brain. Acetaminophen inhibits prostaglandin synthesis in the central nervous system by acting on the peroxidase site of COX, which works best where peroxide levels are low, as in the brain, explaining its weak anti-inflammatory effect. It also acts through descending serotonergic pathways and a metabolite (AM404) that affects cannabinoid and TRPV1 signaling. The two parts combine opioid and non-opioid pain relief.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Morphine from codeine and central COX inhibition by acetaminophen combine to relieve pain."], ["Cough center", "Codeine suppresses the cough reflex in the brainstem."], ["Lungs", "Mu activation depresses breathing, dangerously so in ultra-rapid metabolizers who make too much morphine."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors slow gut movement, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen overdose forms toxic NAPQI, which can cause severe liver injury."], ["Hypothalamus", "Acetaminophen lowers fever by reducing prostaglandin E2 in the temperature center."]],
  "journey": "Both parts are well absorbed by mouth, with effects in 30 to 60 minutes. Codeine is converted by CYP2D6 to morphine, by CYP3A4 to norcodeine and by glucuronidation to codeine-6-glucuronide. People with low CYP2D6 activity get little pain relief, while ultra-rapid metabolizers make much more morphine. Acetaminophen is mostly glucuronidated and sulfated; a small part is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which glutathione detoxifies. Both have half-lives of a few hours and leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Codeine's effects come from morphine at mu receptors: sedation, constipation, nausea and respiratory depression, which is the boxed warning, especially in ultra-rapid metabolizers and with other CNS depressants. Breastfed infants of ultra-rapid metabolizing mothers can be exposed to high morphine levels. Acetaminophen in excess saturates the safe pathways, and NAPQI depletes glutathione and damages liver cells. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, and N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione to treat acetaminophen overdose.",
  "fun": "Depending on ancestry, roughly 1 to 10 in 100 people are CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers who turn codeine into morphine unusually fast, with the highest rates reported in some North African, Ethiopian and Arab populations.",
  "src": ["Tylenol with Codeine (acetaminophen and codeine) FDA label", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2017", "Brodie BB, Axelrod J. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1948"]
},
"hydrocodone-acetaminophen": {
  "origin": "Hydrocodone was first synthesized from codeine in 1920 by German chemists Carl Mannich and Helene Lowenheim. It was approved in the US in 1943, first mainly as a cough suppressant. Combined with acetaminophen, which Brodie and Axelrod had shown in 1948 to be the active metabolite of older pain relievers, it became products such as Vicodin, Lortab and Norco. For decades it was the most prescribed drug in the US. Because combination products were in Schedule III, they were easy to refill, and they became central to the prescription opioid crisis. In 2014 the DEA moved hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II. FDA also limited the amount of acetaminophen per tablet in prescription combinations to reduce liver injury.",
  "timeline": [["1920", "Mannich and Lowenheim synthesize hydrocodone."], ["1943", "Hydrocodone is approved in the US."], ["2011", "FDA announces limits on acetaminophen content in prescription combination products."], ["2014", "DEA reschedules hydrocodone combination products to Schedule II."]],
  "cell": "Hydrocodone is a semisynthetic phenanthrene opioid and a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, opens G protein-gated inward rectifier potassium (GIRK) channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic N-type calcium channels, cutting the release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers in the spinal cord. In the brain, mu activation also changes the emotional response to pain and releases dopamine in reward pathways, which drives euphoria and addiction. CYP2D6 converts part of hydrocodone to hydromorphone, a more potent mu agonist. Acetaminophen inhibits central prostaglandin synthesis at the COX peroxidase site and acts through descending serotonergic pathways, adding non-opioid pain relief.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation and central COX inhibition together relieve moderate to severe pain."], ["Brainstem breathing center", "Mu activation reduces the response to carbon dioxide, causing slow, shallow breathing that can be fatal."], ["Reward system", "Dopamine release in reward pathways causes euphoria and can lead to misuse and addiction."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut wall slow motility, causing constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in excess forms toxic NAPQI and can cause acute liver failure."], ["Ears", "Hearing loss has been reported with long-term high-level misuse."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth and absorbed within about an hour. Hydrocodone is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and partly by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, so CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels dangerously. Its half-life is about 4 hours. Acetaminophen is mainly glucuronidated and sulfated, with a small part oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI, which glutathione neutralizes. Both leave mainly in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the most dangerous effect and is worse with benzodiazepines, alcohol and other CNS depressants, as the boxed warning states. Constipation, nausea and drowsiness come from mu activation in the gut and brain. Tolerance and physical dependence develop with regular use, and stopping suddenly causes withdrawal. Acetaminophen toxicity happens when the glucuronide and sulfate pathways are overwhelmed and NAPQI depletes glutathione, damaging liver cells. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, and N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for acetaminophen overdose.",
  "fun": "Before 2014 rescheduling, hydrocodone-acetaminophen was the most prescribed medication in the United States.",
  "src": ["Norco (hydrocodone bitartrate and acetaminophen) FDA label", "DEA Final Rule, Federal Register 2014", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"oxycodone-acetaminophen": {
  "origin": "Oxycodone was synthesized in 1916 by Martin Freund and Edmund Speyer at the University of Frankfurt from thebaine, an opium alkaloid. It was introduced in Germany the next year. In the US it was first combined with aspirin (Percodan), and in 1976 Endo Laboratories introduced Percocet, oxycodone combined with acetaminophen. Percocet became one of the most common opioid prescriptions for acute pain after injuries and surgery. Like other opioid combinations, it is a Schedule II drug with boxed warnings about addiction, respiratory depression and liver injury from acetaminophen.",
  "timeline": [["1916", "Freund and Speyer synthesize oxycodone from thebaine."], ["1917", "Oxycodone is introduced in Germany."], ["1976", "Percocet (oxycodone and acetaminophen) is introduced in the US."]],
  "cell": "Oxycodone is a semisynthetic full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Through Gi/o proteins it inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons and closes presynaptic calcium channels, blocking pain signal transmission in the dorsal horn and modifying pain perception in the brain. It has some kappa activity as well. Its metabolite oxymorphone, made by CYP2D6, is more potent, but most of the effect comes from oxycodone itself. Acetaminophen inhibits central prostaglandin synthesis at the COX peroxidase site and works through descending serotonergic pathways. Together they give stronger pain relief than either alone.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Combined opioid and non-opioid action relieves moderate to severe acute pain."], ["Brainstem", "Mu activation depresses breathing, the main cause of death in overdose."], ["Reward system", "Dopamine release produces euphoria, a driver of misuse."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation, nausea and vomiting."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in excess produces NAPQI and can cause severe liver injury."], ["Hormones", "Long-term opioid use can cause adrenal insufficiency and low sex hormones."]],
  "journey": "Oxycodone is well absorbed by mouth, with higher bioavailability than morphine, and starts working within 30 minutes. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 to noroxycodone and partly by CYP2D6 to oxymorphone. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels and cause overdose. Its half-life is about 3.5 hours. Acetaminophen is glucuronidated and sulfated, with a small CYP2E1 fraction forming NAPQI. Both leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Mu agonism explains constipation, nausea, sedation, itching and the boxed warning for respiratory depression, which is greatly worsened by benzodiazepines and alcohol. Repeated use leads to tolerance, dependence and possible addiction. Opioids suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary axes, which can cause adrenal insufficiency. Too much acetaminophen saturates normal pathways and NAPQI injures the liver. Naloxone reverses opioid overdose, and N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen poisoning.",
  "fun": "Oxycodone was first made in Germany in 1916 in the hope of creating an opioid with less addiction potential than heroin.",
  "src": ["Percocet (oxycodone and acetaminophen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"ziconotide": {
  "origin": "Ziconotide comes from the venom of Conus magus, the magician's cone snail, a fish-hunting marine snail that harpoons prey and paralyzes it with a cocktail of small peptides. Starting in the 1970s, Baldomero Olivera and colleagues at the University of Utah studied these conotoxins and found that some precisely block nerve calcium channels. One of them, omega-conotoxin MVIIA, blocks N-type calcium channels used by pain-transmitting neurons. The company Neurex developed a synthetic version, later carried on by Elan, and FDA approved it as Prialt in 2004 for severe chronic pain, given only by intrathecal infusion into the spinal fluid. It was the first drug from cone snail venom and a non-opioid option for patients who cannot tolerate opioids.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Baldomero Olivera's lab begins studying cone snail venom peptides."], ["1980s", "Omega-conotoxins are shown to block N-type calcium channels."], ["2004", "FDA approves ziconotide (Prialt) for intrathecal use."]],
  "cell": "Ziconotide is a 25-amino acid peptide held in shape by three disulfide bonds. It binds and blocks N-type voltage-gated calcium channels (CaV2.2), which are concentrated on the central terminals of primary afferent pain fibers in the superficial dorsal horn of the spinal cord. When a pain signal arrives, these channels normally open, letting calcium in to trigger release of glutamate, substance P and CGRP onto second-order neurons. By plugging the channel pore, ziconotide stops this release, so the pain signal is not passed on. It does not act on opioid receptors, so it does not cause tolerance, withdrawal or respiratory depression.",
  "body": [["Spinal cord", "Blocking calcium entry at pain fiber terminals stops pain signals from crossing to spinal neurons."], ["Brain", "Spread into the brain causes dizziness, confusion, memory problems, nystagmus and unsteady gait."], ["Mind and mood", "Hallucinations, paranoia, depression and suicidal thoughts can occur, which is the subject of a boxed warning."], ["Muscle", "Creatine kinase can rise, sometimes with muscle weakness."], ["Breathing", "It does not depress breathing, unlike opioids."]],
  "journey": "Ziconotide is a peptide that would be destroyed in the gut and does not cross into the spinal fluid well from the blood, so it is given by continuous infusion through an implanted pump directly into the spinal fluid (intrathecal). It spreads within the cerebrospinal fluid, with a half-life of about 4.5 hours there. Small amounts reach the blood, where peptidases quickly break it into amino acids. The dose is increased slowly, and pain relief develops over days. Effects fade within hours when the infusion stops.",
  "sidefx": "N-type calcium channels are also used by neurons in the brain, so drug that spreads upward in the spinal fluid causes neurological and psychiatric effects: dizziness, confusion, memory loss, nystagmus, hallucinations and severe depression. The boxed warning covers severe psychiatric symptoms and neurological impairment. The implanted catheter can introduce infection and meningitis. Because it does not act on opioid receptors, it can be stopped without withdrawal. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped.",
  "fun": "A single cone snail's venom can contain more than a hundred different peptides, each fine-tuned to block a specific ion channel or receptor.",
  "src": ["Prialt (ziconotide) FDA label", "Olivera BM et al. Science 1985"]
},
"suzetrigine": {
  "origin": "In 1996 John Wood and colleagues at University College London cloned a sodium channel found only in pain-sensing nerves, now called NaV1.8. Because it is absent from the brain and heart, it promised pain relief without opioid side effects. Many companies tried and failed to make selective blockers. Vertex Pharmaceuticals discovered suzetrigine (VX-548), which binds NaV1.8 with very high selectivity. After trials in pain following bunionectomy and abdominoplasty, FDA approved it as Journavx in January 2025 for moderate to severe acute pain in adults. It was the first new class of pain medicine approved in more than 20 years and is not a controlled substance.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "NaV1.8 (SNS) is cloned from pain-sensing neurons."], ["2010s", "Vertex develops selective NaV1.8 inhibitors."], ["2025", "FDA approves suzetrigine (Journavx) for acute pain."]],
  "cell": "Pain-sensing neurons (nociceptors) in the dorsal root ganglia express NaV1.8, a tetrodotoxin-resistant voltage-gated sodium channel encoded by SCN10A. It carries much of the sodium current during the upstroke of action potentials in these neurons and supports repetitive firing. Suzetrigine binds the second voltage-sensing domain (VSD2) of NaV1.8 rather than the pore. This stabilizes the channel in its closed state (allosteric inhibition), so it opens less in response to depolarization. Nociceptors fire fewer action potentials, and fewer pain signals reach the spinal cord. Because NaV1.8 is largely limited to peripheral nociceptors, it does not affect brain opioid systems or cause sedation or respiratory depression.",
  "body": [["Peripheral pain nerves", "Blocking NaV1.8 reduces firing of nociceptors so fewer pain signals are sent to the spinal cord."], ["Brain", "It does not act in the brain's reward or breathing centers, so it does not cause euphoria, sedation or respiratory depression."], ["Muscles", "Muscle spasms and increased creatine kinase have been reported."], ["Skin", "Itching and rash are among the more common side effects."], ["Kidneys", "Decreases in estimated kidney filtration have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Suzetrigine is taken by mouth, with a larger first dose followed by doses every 12 hours. It is absorbed in a few hours and is highly protein bound. The liver converts it via CYP3A to an active metabolite, M6-SUZ, which is less potent but lasts longer. The parent half-life is about a day. It leaves in feces and urine. Strong CYP3A inhibitors are avoided, grapefruit is avoided, and CYP3A inducers reduce its levels.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects in trials were mostly mild, including itching, muscle spasms, raised creatine kinase and rash. It can reduce the effectiveness of hormonal contraceptives containing progestins, so additional contraception is advised. Because it targets only peripheral nociceptors, it lacks the opioid risks of addiction and respiratory depression. Reduced kidney function raises exposure. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Some people with rare gain-of-function mutations in sodium channels on pain nerves have painful disorders, which helped researchers see these channels as pain targets.",
  "src": ["Journavx (suzetrigine) FDA label", "Akopian AN, Sivilotti L, Wood JN. Nature 1996"]
},
"butorphanol-nasal": {
  "origin": "Butorphanol is a morphinan opioid synthesized in the early 1970s at Bristol Laboratories as part of the search for strong pain relievers with less addiction and breathing risk. FDA approved the injection as Stadol in 1978. Because swallowed butorphanol is mostly destroyed by the liver, the company developed a nasal spray, Stadol NS, approved in 1991 as the first opioid nasal spray, popular for migraine. It was originally not controlled, but reports of misuse and dependence led the DEA to place it in Schedule IV in 1997. The brand has been discontinued, but generic nasal spray remains.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Bristol Laboratories develops butorphanol."], ["1978", "FDA approves Stadol injection."], ["1991", "FDA approves Stadol NS, the first opioid nasal spray."], ["1997", "Butorphanol is placed in Schedule IV."]],
  "cell": "Butorphanol is a mixed agonist-antagonist. It is an agonist at kappa-opioid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled, so it reduces cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels on pain neurons in the spinal cord and brain. At mu-opioid receptors it acts as a partial agonist or antagonist. This gives a ceiling effect: beyond a certain point, more drug adds little pain relief or respiratory depression. In someone dependent on a full mu agonist, its mu antagonism can trigger withdrawal. Kappa activation also causes sedation and dysphoria.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Kappa agonism relieves moderate to severe pain, including migraine headache."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression occurs but plateaus, though it remains dangerous with other depressants."], ["Mood and perception", "Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, confusion and hallucinations."], ["Heart and lungs", "It can raise pulmonary artery pressure and cardiac workload."], ["Nose and mouth", "Nasal congestion and an unpleasant taste are common with the spray."]],
  "journey": "The spray is absorbed across the blood-rich nasal lining directly into the blood, bypassing the liver first pass that makes oral butorphanol ineffective. Pain relief starts within about 15 minutes and peaks within an hour. The liver hydroxylates and dealkylates it, and metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 5 hours and longer in older adults.",
  "sidefx": "Kappa effects explain the drowsiness, dizziness and psychotomimetic effects such as dysphoria and hallucinations. Mu antagonism can precipitate withdrawal in people using other opioids. Although respiratory depression has a ceiling, it can still be severe with benzodiazepines or alcohol. Overuse for migraine can lead to dependence and medication overuse headache. Naloxone reverses its effects, though larger amounts may be needed.",
  "fun": "Stadol NS was the first opioid approved in the US as a nasal spray.",
  "src": ["Butorphanol tartrate nasal spray FDA label", "DEA scheduling notice, 1997"]
},
"buprenorphine-transdermal": {
  "origin": "Buprenorphine was created in 1966 by chemists at the British company Reckitt and Colman, led by John Lewis and Kenneth Bentley, who were looking for a strong painkiller with less abuse potential. Made from thebaine, an opium alkaloid, it was found to be a partial mu agonist that binds very tightly. The injection Buprenex was approved in the US in 1981, and sublingual forms for opioid addiction followed in 2002. Because swallowed buprenorphine is largely destroyed by the liver, Purdue Pharma developed a seven-day skin patch, Butrans, approved by FDA in 2010 for chronic pain requiring long-term opioid treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Reckitt and Colman chemists synthesize buprenorphine."], ["1981", "FDA approves Buprenex injection."], ["2002", "Sublingual buprenorphine is approved for opioid use disorder."], ["2010", "FDA approves Butrans transdermal patch."]],
  "cell": "Buprenorphine is a partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors with very high affinity and slow dissociation. It activates mu receptors enough to relieve pain but produces a lower maximum effect than full agonists, especially on breathing (a ceiling effect). Through Gi/o proteins it lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels on pain neurons. Its tight binding means it can displace full agonists such as morphine and block their effects, which can precipitate withdrawal. It is also an antagonist at kappa-opioid receptors, which may reduce dysphoria. The patch delivers it slowly and steadily.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Steady partial mu agonism provides continuous relief of chronic pain."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression has a ceiling at analgesic levels but is still dangerous with CNS depressants or in children."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QTc interval at higher patch strengths."], ["Gut", "Constipation and nausea occur but are often milder than with full agonists."], ["Skin", "Itching, redness and rash at the patch site are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare hepatitis have been reported."]],
  "journey": "The patch releases buprenorphine through the skin over seven days. Blood levels rise slowly over the first one to two days and reach steady state by about day three. Heat from heating pads, fever or hot baths increases absorption. The liver metabolizes it by CYP3A4 to norbuprenorphine and by glucuronidation, and most leaves in feces. After removal, levels fall by about half within 12 hours, with an apparent half-life of about a day.",
  "sidefx": "Mu partial agonism explains nausea, constipation, dizziness and sleepiness. High receptor affinity means it can trigger withdrawal in people on full agonists and makes naloxone reversal harder: larger, repeated amounts of naloxone may be needed. QTc prolongation is a labeled risk. Accidental exposure of a child to a patch can be fatal, which is part of the boxed warning. Heat on the patch can raise absorption and cause overdose.",
  "fun": "Buprenorphine binds the mu receptor so tightly that it can take hours to dissociate, which is why naloxone has a hard time knocking it off.",
  "src": ["Butrans (buprenorphine transdermal system) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"morphine-extended-release": {
  "origin": "Morphine was isolated from opium by the German pharmacist Friedrich Serturner in the early 1800s, the first alkaloid ever isolated from a plant. He named it after Morpheus, the Greek god of dreams. Its structure was worked out by Robert Robinson in 1925, and Marshall Gates synthesized it in 1952. Immediate-release morphine wore off in a few hours, so people with chronic cancer pain needed frequent doses. Napp Pharmaceuticals in the UK, a sister company of Purdue, developed the Continus controlled-release system, and MS Contin was introduced in the US in 1987. It became a standard long-acting opioid for chronic severe pain.",
  "timeline": [["1804", "Friedrich Serturner begins isolating morphine from opium."], ["1925", "Robert Robinson determines morphine's structure."], ["1952", "Marshall Gates completes the first total synthesis of morphine."], ["1987", "MS Contin is introduced in the US."]],
  "cell": "Morphine is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, with weaker action at kappa and delta receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens GIRK potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons and closes presynaptic calcium channels, reducing release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers. In the brain, it activates descending inhibitory pathways from the periaqueductal gray and rostral ventromedial medulla and changes the emotional experience of pain. Mu activation of reward pathways releases dopamine. Morphine also releases histamine from mast cells. The extended-release tablet uses a matrix that releases drug over 8 to 12 hours.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation blocks pain transmission and changes pain perception."], ["Brainstem breathing center", "Reduced sensitivity to carbon dioxide causes respiratory depression, the main danger."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility and increased sphincter tone cause constipation and can trigger biliary spasm."], ["Skin and vessels", "Histamine release causes flushing, itching, sweating and orthostatic hypotension."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation constricts the pupils (miosis)."], ["Hormones", "Long-term use can cause adrenal insufficiency and low sex hormones."]],
  "journey": "The tablet is swallowed whole and releases morphine slowly in the gut. Only about a quarter to a third reaches the blood because of first-pass glucuronidation in the liver. The liver uses UGT2B7 to make morphine-3-glucuronide, which is inactive for pain but can excite neurons, and morphine-6-glucuronide, which is an active analgesic. Both glucuronides leave in the urine and build up in kidney failure. Morphine's own half-life is a few hours, but the slow release keeps levels steady for 8 to 12 hours. Crushing or chewing the tablet releases the full dose at once.",
  "sidefx": "Mu agonism causes constipation, nausea, sedation and respiratory depression, which is worsened by CNS depressants and by crushing the tablet. Histamine release causes itching and low blood pressure. In kidney failure, buildup of morphine-3-glucuronide can cause myoclonus and seizures, and morphine-6-glucuronide can cause prolonged sedation. Long-term use leads to tolerance, dependence and opioid-induced hyperalgesia. Naloxone reverses toxicity but may need repeated dosing because the tablet keeps releasing drug.",
  "fun": "Morphine was the first active compound ever isolated from a plant, opening the field of alkaloid chemistry.",
  "src": ["MS Contin (morphine sulfate extended-release) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"oxycodone-extended-release": {
  "origin": "Oxycodone was first made in Germany in 1916. In 1995 FDA approved Purdue Pharma's OxyContin, an extended-release oxycodone tablet given every 12 hours. Purdue marketed it aggressively, including claims that its addiction risk was low, and it became one of the drugs most closely linked to the US opioid crisis. People learned to crush the tablets to release the full dose at once. In 2007 Purdue and executives pleaded guilty to federal charges of misbranding. In 2010 FDA approved a reformulated tablet made with a polyethylene oxide matrix that resists crushing and turns to a gel when wet, and in 2020 Purdue pleaded guilty again to federal charges.",
  "timeline": [["1916", "Freund and Speyer synthesize oxycodone."], ["1995", "FDA approves OxyContin."], ["2007", "Purdue pleads guilty to federal misbranding charges."], ["2010", "FDA approves reformulated, crush-resistant OxyContin."], ["2020", "Purdue pleads guilty to further federal charges."]],
  "cell": "Oxycodone is a full mu-opioid receptor agonist. Through Gi/o proteins, it inhibits adenylyl cyclase, opens postsynaptic potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons and blocks presynaptic calcium entry, cutting the release of pain neurotransmitters in the spinal cord dorsal horn. In the brain it activates descending pain control pathways and reward pathways that release dopamine. Its CYP2D6 metabolite oxymorphone is more potent, but most of the effect comes from oxycodone. The extended-release tablet releases drug in two phases over about 12 hours.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation gives sustained relief of severe chronic pain."], ["Brainstem breathing center", "Mu activation depresses breathing, which can be fatal especially early in treatment or after dose increases."], ["Reward system", "Dopamine release causes euphoria and drives addiction."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation and nausea."], ["Throat and gut", "The swollen gel tablet can cause choking or blockage if not swallowed with enough water."], ["Hormones", "Long-term use can suppress adrenal and gonadal hormones."]],
  "journey": "OxyContin is swallowed whole. It releases oxycodone in two phases, with an early peak at about 1 hour and a later peak at 3 to 5 hours, giving 12 hours of effect. Oral bioavailability is relatively high. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4 to noroxycodone and partly by CYP2D6 to oxymorphone; CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise levels and cause overdose. Metabolites and some unchanged drug leave in the urine. Crushing or chewing defeats the extended release.",
  "sidefx": "Mu activation explains constipation, nausea, drowsiness, itching and respiratory depression. The boxed warnings cover addiction, life-threatening respiratory depression, accidental ingestion by children, neonatal opioid withdrawal syndrome, CYP3A4 interactions and risks with benzodiazepines. The abuse-deterrent matrix makes crushing harder, but it can still be misused by swallowing. Naloxone reverses toxicity but may need repeated dosing as the tablet keeps releasing drug.",
  "fun": "The 2010 OxyContin reformulation is studied as a turning point in the opioid crisis, as some people who misused it switched to heroin afterward.",
  "src": ["OxyContin (oxycodone hydrochloride extended-release) FDA label", "US Department of Justice press releases, 2007 and 2020"]
},
"hydrocodone-ibuprofen": {
  "origin": "Ibuprofen was developed at Boots in Nottingham, England, by Stewart Adams and John Nicholson, who patented it in 1961. It reached the UK market in 1969 and the US in 1974. Adams famously took a dose himself to treat a hangover before a speech. Hydrocodone, synthesized in Germany in 1920, had long been paired with acetaminophen. Combining it instead with an NSAID added anti-inflammatory action. Knoll Pharmaceutical introduced the combination as Vicoprofen, approved by FDA in 1997. The Vicoprofen brand has been discontinued, but generic tablets remain, and the product is Schedule II like other hydrocodone combinations.",
  "timeline": [["1920", "Hydrocodone is synthesized in Germany."], ["1961", "Stewart Adams and John Nicholson patent ibuprofen at Boots."], ["1974", "Ibuprofen is approved in the US."], ["1997", "FDA approves Vicoprofen (hydrocodone and ibuprofen)."], ["2014", "Hydrocodone combinations are moved to Schedule II."]],
  "cell": "Hydrocodone is a full mu-opioid receptor agonist. Through Gi/o proteins it lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and blocks presynaptic calcium channels, reducing pain transmission in the spinal cord and brain. CYP2D6 converts part of it to the more potent hydromorphone. Ibuprofen reversibly inhibits both cyclooxygenase-1 and -2, blocking conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2. Less PGE2 and prostacyclin at injured tissue means less sensitization of pain nerve endings, less swelling and less fever. The combination treats pain both centrally (opioid) and at the injury (NSAID).",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation reduces pain transmission and perception."], ["Injured tissue", "COX inhibition lowers prostaglandins, reducing swelling and nerve sensitization."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation in the brainstem can cause respiratory depression."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins raises the risk of ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Blocking renal prostaglandins can reduce filtration, cause fluid retention and raise potassium."], ["Heart and vessels", "NSAID effects raise blood pressure and the risk of heart attack and stroke."]],
  "journey": "Both parts are absorbed by mouth within about an hour or two. Hydrocodone is metabolized by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, with a half-life of about 4 hours. Ibuprofen is highly protein bound and oxidized mainly by CYP2C9, with a half-life of about 2 hours. Metabolites of both leave in the urine. The product is meant for short-term use.",
  "sidefx": "The opioid part causes sedation, constipation, nausea and respiratory depression, worsened by CNS depressants. The NSAID part causes stomach ulcers and bleeding, acute kidney injury, fluid retention and increased cardiovascular risk, all from blocked prostaglandins. Together they carry boxed warnings for both opioid risks and NSAID cardiovascular and GI risks. Naloxone reverses opioid effects; there is no antidote for ibuprofen, and care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Stewart Adams, co-inventor of ibuprofen, tested an early dose on his own hangover before giving a speech in Moscow.",
  "src": ["Hydrocodone bitartrate and ibuprofen tablets FDA label", "Adams SS. The propionic acids: a personal perspective, J Clin Pharmacol 1992"]
},
"benzhydrocodone-acetaminophen": {
  "origin": "Benzhydrocodone was developed by KemPharm, a company in Iowa that designs prodrugs by chemically linking drugs to other molecules. KemPharm attached hydrocodone to benzoic acid, creating a compound that is inactive until enzymes in the gut cut it apart. The aim was to make the drug harder to misuse by snorting or injecting. FDA approved the combination with acetaminophen as Apadaz in February 2018 for short-term management of acute pain. FDA advisory committees did not support an abuse-deterrent label, and it was approved without one. It is a Schedule II drug.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "KemPharm develops benzhydrocodone as a hydrocodone prodrug."], ["2018", "FDA approves Apadaz without abuse-deterrent labeling."]],
  "cell": "Benzhydrocodone itself has little activity. After it is swallowed, enzymes in the intestinal tract cleave the bond between hydrocodone and benzoic acid, releasing hydrocodone. Hydrocodone is a full mu-opioid receptor agonist that acts through Gi/o proteins to lower cAMP, open potassium channels and close presynaptic calcium channels, dampening pain signal transmission in the spinal cord and brain. Acetaminophen acts centrally by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis at the COX peroxidase site and through serotonergic pathways, with little anti-inflammatory effect. The combination provides opioid and non-opioid pain relief.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Hydrocodone and acetaminophen act together to reduce acute pain."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation can cause respiratory depression, worsened by alcohol and sedatives."], ["Gut", "Slowed motility causes constipation and nausea."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in excess forms NAPQI, which can cause acute liver failure."], ["Skin", "Itching occurs, and acetaminophen rarely causes serious skin reactions."]],
  "journey": "After oral dosing, benzhydrocodone is converted in the gut to hydrocodone, which reaches its peak within about an hour and a half. Hydrocodone is metabolized by CYP3A4 to norhydrocodone and by CYP2D6 to hydromorphone, with a half-life of about 4 hours. Benzoic acid is conjugated with glycine to hippuric acid and excreted in urine. Acetaminophen is glucuronidated and sulfated, with a small CYP2E1 fraction forming NAPQI. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are those of hydrocodone: nausea, sleepiness, constipation, itching and respiratory depression, with boxed warnings for addiction, respiratory depression, CYP3A4 interactions and use with benzodiazepines. The prodrug design does not prevent misuse by swallowing. Acetaminophen can cause liver failure in overdose and rare serious skin reactions. Naloxone reverses opioid effects, and N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen overdose.",
  "fun": "The benzoic acid released from benzhydrocodone is the same compound used as a food preservative.",
  "src": ["Apadaz (benzhydrocodone and acetaminophen) FDA label", "FDA advisory committee briefing, 2016"]
},
"tramadol-acetaminophen": {
  "origin": "Tramadol was synthesized in 1962 by the German company Grunenthal and launched in Germany in 1977. Ortho-McNeil brought it to the US as Ultram, approved in 1995, initially as an uncontrolled drug thought to have low abuse potential. The combination with acetaminophen, Ultracet, was approved in 2001 for short-term acute pain. After reports of misuse, dependence and overdose, the DEA placed tramadol in Schedule IV in 2014. FDA has also restricted its use in children because of the risk of breathing problems in ultra-rapid CYP2D6 metabolizers.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Grunenthal synthesizes tramadol."], ["1977", "Tramadol is launched in Germany."], ["1995", "FDA approves tramadol (Ultram)."], ["2001", "FDA approves Ultracet."], ["2014", "DEA places tramadol in Schedule IV."]],
  "cell": "Tramadol is a racemic mixture with two kinds of action. The parent drug is a weak mu-opioid agonist, but CYP2D6 converts it to O-desmethyltramadol (M1), which binds mu receptors far more strongly and provides most of the opioid effect. Separately, the (+) enantiomer inhibits serotonin reuptake and the (-) enantiomer inhibits norepinephrine reuptake, strengthening descending inhibitory pain pathways from the brainstem to the spinal cord. Acetaminophen adds central COX inhibition and serotonergic effects. The opioid and monoamine actions together give moderate pain relief.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu activation by M1 and increased serotonin and norepinephrine reduce pain."], ["Brain excitability", "Tramadol lowers the seizure threshold, so seizures can occur even at usual doses."], ["Serotonin system", "Combining it with SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs or triptans can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Breathing", "Mu activation can depress breathing, especially in ultra-rapid metabolizers."], ["Metabolism", "Low blood sugar and low sodium (hyponatremia) have been reported."], ["Liver", "Acetaminophen in overdose forms toxic NAPQI and can cause liver failure."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are absorbed well by mouth, with effects within an hour. Tramadol is converted by CYP2D6 to active M1 and by CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 to inactive N-desmethyltramadol. M1 peaks about 3 hours after a dose. Tramadol and M1 have half-lives of about 6 to 7 hours. CYP2D6 poor metabolizers get less pain relief, and ultra-rapid metabolizers get more M1 and more risk. Acetaminophen is mainly glucuronidated and sulfated. Both leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Mu activation causes nausea, constipation, dizziness and respiratory depression. Increased serotonin and norepinephrine explain sweating, serotonin syndrome and the lowered seizure threshold. Hypoglycemia and hyponatremia have been reported. Withdrawal can include both opioid symptoms and unusual symptoms such as anxiety and panic. Acetaminophen overdose damages the liver. Naloxone reverses opioid effects but may increase seizure risk in tramadol overdose; benzodiazepines treat seizures, and N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen toxicity.",
  "fun": "In 2013 researchers reported finding tramadol in the roots of an African tree, but later work showed it came from contamination, because farmers had given tramadol to their cattle.",
  "src": ["Ultracet (tramadol and acetaminophen) FDA label", "Kusari S et al. Angew Chem 2014"]
},
"follitropin": {
  "origin": "Fertility drugs began with gonadotropins extracted from urine. In the 1950s Piero Donini at the Italian company Serono purified gonadotropins from the urine of postmenopausal women, and Bruno Lunenfeld used this product, Pergonal, to achieve a pregnancy in 1962. To get enough urine, Serono collected it from convents of nuns in Italy. Recombinant DNA technology later allowed pure follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) to be made in Chinese hamster ovary cells. Serono's follitropin alfa (Gonal-f) and Organon's follitropin beta (Follistim) were approved in the US in the late 1990s, giving consistent FSH without depending on urine collection.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Piero Donini purifies urinary gonadotropins at Serono."], ["1962", "Bruno Lunenfeld reports a pregnancy after gonadotropin treatment."], ["1990s", "Recombinant FSH (follitropin alfa and beta) is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "FSH is a glycoprotein hormone with an alpha subunit shared with LH, hCG and TSH and a hormone-specific beta subunit. Recombinant FSH binds the FSH receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on ovarian granulosa cells. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. This drives granulosa cells to multiply, rescues growing follicles from atresia, and induces aromatase, which converts androgens from theca cells into estradiol. Given in higher amounts than the natural cycle, FSH lets several follicles grow at once. In men, FSH acts on Sertoli cells in the testes to support sperm production, usually with hCG.",
  "body": [["Ovaries", "Multiple follicles grow and mature, producing more estradiol."], ["Uterus", "Rising estradiol thickens the endometrium in preparation for implantation."], ["Testes", "In men with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, FSH supports Sertoli cells and sperm production."], ["Fluid balance", "Overstimulated ovaries can release vasoactive factors such as VEGF that cause ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS)."], ["Abdomen", "Enlarged ovaries cause bloating, pain and a risk of ovarian torsion."]],
  "journey": "Follitropin is injected under the skin, usually daily with a pen. It is absorbed slowly, peaks after about half a day, and has a half-life of about a day or more, so levels build with repeated doses. It is broken down like the body's own glycoproteins and cleared mainly by the kidneys. Follicles grow over days, guided by ultrasound and estradiol levels, and then a separate hCG or GnRH agonist trigger completes oocyte maturation.",
  "sidefx": "The main risks come from overstimulation. Many follicles growing at once release VEGF and other factors that make vessels leaky, causing fluid shifts into the abdomen and lungs (OHSS), with risk of blood clots and kidney problems. Enlarged ovaries can twist (torsion). Multiple pregnancy is more likely when several eggs are released. Ectopic pregnancy and injection site reactions also occur. There is no antidote; OHSS is managed by withholding the trigger, fluid management and supportive care.",
  "fun": "Before recombinant FSH, a major supplier of fertility hormone was the urine of postmenopausal nuns in Italian convents.",
  "src": ["Gonal-f (follitropin alfa) FDA label", "Lunenfeld B. Historical perspectives in gonadotrophin therapy, Hum Reprod Update 2004"]
},
"menotropins": {
  "origin": "Menotropins, also called human menopausal gonadotropin (hMG), are FSH and LH purified from the urine of postmenopausal women, whose pituitaries make large amounts of gonadotropins. In the 1950s Piero Donini at Serono in Italy developed the purification method, and the product Pergonal led to the first pregnancy in 1962 under Bruno Lunenfeld. Serono collected the urine from Italian convents. Pergonal became a mainstay of ovulation induction and early IVF. Today the main US product is Menopur from Ferring, a highly purified hMG.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Piero Donini develops urinary gonadotropin extraction at Serono."], ["1962", "The first pregnancy after Pergonal treatment is reported."], ["2000s", "Highly purified hMG (Menopur) becomes available in the US."]],
  "cell": "Menotropins supply both FSH and LH activity, with some LH activity coming from hCG present in the product. FSH binds Gs-coupled FSH receptors on granulosa cells, raising cAMP and driving follicle growth and aromatase expression. LH activity binds LH/hCG receptors on theca cells, stimulating androgen production. Granulosa cell aromatase converts those androgens into estradiol (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model). Having both activities can support estradiol production, especially in women with low natural LH. Higher than physiologic FSH lets several follicles develop at once.",
  "body": [["Ovaries", "FSH and LH activity together drive multiple follicles to grow and make estradiol."], ["Uterus", "Rising estradiol thickens the endometrium before implantation."], ["Fluid balance", "Overstimulation can cause OHSS, with fluid shifting into the abdomen and chest."], ["Abdomen", "Ovarian enlargement causes bloating and pain and can lead to torsion."], ["Skin", "Injection site pain and swelling are common."]],
  "journey": "Menotropins are injected under the skin, usually daily. FSH levels rise within hours and build with repeated doses, with a half-life of roughly a day. The glycoproteins are broken down like natural hormones and cleared partly by the kidneys. Follicle growth is followed by ultrasound and estradiol levels, and a separate hCG trigger completes maturation.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects come from ovarian overstimulation: OHSS, ovarian torsion and multiple pregnancy. In OHSS, many follicles release VEGF and other factors that make vessels leak, leading to ascites, low blood volume, clots and kidney failure in severe cases. As a urine-derived protein product, it can cause allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis. Treatment of OHSS is supportive.",
  "fun": "Menotropins come from a natural source: after menopause, the pituitary makes very high levels of FSH and LH, which spill into the urine.",
  "src": ["Menopur (menotropins) FDA label", "Lunenfeld B. Hum Reprod Update 2004"]
},
"choriogonadotropin-alfa": {
  "origin": "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) was discovered in 1927 by German scientists Selmar Aschheim and Bernhard Zondek, who found that urine from pregnant women made the ovaries of immature mice mature. This became the first pregnancy test. For decades, hCG for fertility treatment was purified from pregnancy urine. Serono developed choriogonadotropin alfa, a recombinant hCG made in Chinese hamster ovary cells, and FDA approved it as Ovidrel in 2000. It is used as the 'trigger shot' that mimics the natural LH surge to complete egg maturation before IVF retrieval or timed intercourse.",
  "timeline": [["1927", "Aschheim and Zondek discover hCG and create a pregnancy test."], ["1930s", "Urinary hCG is used in medicine."], ["2000", "FDA approves recombinant hCG (Ovidrel)."]],
  "cell": "hCG shares its alpha subunit with LH, FSH and TSH, and its beta subunit is very similar to LH. It binds the LH/choriogonadotropin receptor (LHCGR), a Gs-coupled receptor on granulosa and theca cells of mature follicles. Rising cAMP mimics the natural midcycle LH surge. This restarts meiosis in the oocyte, which had been paused in prophase I, causes the cumulus cells to expand, and starts enzyme changes that rupture the follicle about 36 hours later. hCG also drives luteinization, turning the follicle into a corpus luteum that makes progesterone to support early pregnancy. Its longer half-life than LH keeps this signal going.",
  "body": [["Ovaries", "The final maturation of eggs and ovulation occur about 36 hours after the trigger."], ["Corpus luteum", "hCG sustains progesterone production to prepare and support the uterine lining."], ["Fluid balance", "hCG strongly drives VEGF release from many follicles, a key cause of OHSS."], ["Pregnancy test", "It can cause a false-positive pregnancy test for about 10 days after the injection."], ["Skin", "Pain and bruising at the injection site are common."]],
  "journey": "Choriogonadotropin alfa is given as a single subcutaneous injection. It is absorbed slowly, peaks about 12 to 24 hours later, and has a terminal half-life of about a day or more. It can stay detectable for about 10 days. It is cleared mostly by glycoprotein breakdown, with a small fraction excreted in urine.",
  "sidefx": "The biggest risk is OHSS, because hCG's long-lasting LH signal makes many follicles release VEGF, increasing vascular permeability and causing fluid shifts, ascites and possible clots. Using a GnRH agonist trigger instead can lower this risk in some IVF cycles. Multiple pregnancy and ectopic pregnancy are possible. Injection site pain is common. Treatment of OHSS is supportive.",
  "fun": "The first hCG pregnancy test, the Aschheim-Zondek test, involved injecting a woman's urine into immature female mice and checking their ovaries days later.",
  "src": ["Ovidrel (choriogonadotropin alfa) FDA label", "Aschheim S, Zondek B. Klin Wochenschr 1928"]
},
"ganirelix": {
  "origin": "Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) was isolated and its structure determined in 1971 by teams led by Andrew Schally and Roger Guillemin, who shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. Scientists then made many modified versions: agonists that suppress the pituitary after an initial flare, and antagonists that block it immediately. Early antagonists caused histamine release and allergic reactions, so later generations were redesigned to avoid this. Ganirelix is one of these third-generation antagonists. Organon brought it to market for IVF, and FDA approved it in 1999 to prevent premature LH surges during ovarian stimulation. Generic versions such as Fyremadel followed.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "Schally and Guillemin's teams determine the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize."], ["1999", "FDA approves ganirelix acetate injection."]],
  "cell": "GnRH is released from the hypothalamus in pulses and binds GnRH receptors, which are Gq-coupled, on pituitary gonadotroph cells. Activation raises IP3 and calcium and triggers release of LH and FSH. Ganirelix is a synthetic decapeptide with several unnatural amino acids that binds the GnRH receptor tightly but does not activate it. As a competitive antagonist, it blocks natural GnRH right away, so LH (and, to a lesser degree, FSH) falls within hours with no initial flare. During ovarian stimulation, rising estradiol could otherwise trigger an early LH surge and premature ovulation. Blocking the receptor prevents this until the planned trigger, and the effect reverses within about two days after stopping.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "LH release is quickly suppressed, preventing a premature LH surge."], ["Ovaries", "Follicles keep growing under injected FSH without ovulating too early."], ["Skin", "Redness, swelling and itching at the injection site are common."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."]],
  "journey": "Ganirelix is injected under the skin once daily, usually starting mid-way through stimulation. It is absorbed rapidly, peaking within about an hour or two, with high bioavailability. Its half-life is about 13 hours after one injection and a bit longer with repeated doses. Peptidases break it into fragments, which leave in feces and urine. Pituitary function recovers within about 48 hours after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local injection site reactions. Headache and nausea occur. OHSS, abdominal pain and multiple pregnancy reflect the overall stimulation cycle rather than ganirelix itself. Some prefilled syringe covers have contained natural rubber latex, a concern in latex allergy. Rare hypersensitivity reactions occur. There is no antidote; effects reverse quickly after stopping.",
  "fun": "GnRH antagonists work within hours, while GnRH agonists first cause a surge of LH and FSH (a flare) before suppressing the pituitary.",
  "src": ["Ganirelix acetate injection FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Science 1971"]
},
"cetrorelix": {
  "origin": "Cetrorelix came out of the GnRH research of Andrew Schally, who shared the 1977 Nobel Prize for determining the structure of hypothalamic hormones. Schally's laboratory at Tulane University in New Orleans worked with the German company Asta Medica to design GnRH antagonists that block the pituitary without causing histamine release. Cetrorelix was one of these third-generation antagonists. Serono brought it to the US as Cetrotide, approved by FDA in 2000, to prevent premature ovulation during controlled ovarian stimulation. Generic versions are now available.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's team determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2000", "FDA approves Cetrotide."]],
  "cell": "Cetrorelix is a synthetic decapeptide that competitively binds GnRH receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs. These Gq-coupled receptors normally respond to pulsed GnRH by releasing LH and FSH. Because cetrorelix occupies the receptor without activating it, LH and FSH secretion falls within hours, with no initial flare like that seen with GnRH agonists. During ovarian stimulation, this prevents the premature LH surge that could cause early ovulation or luteinization. The suppression is dose-dependent and reverses after the drug is stopped.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "LH and FSH release fall quickly, blocking a premature LH surge."], ["Ovaries", "Follicles can be stimulated with injected gonadotropins and timed for retrieval without ovulating early."], ["Skin", "Injection site reactions such as redness, swelling and itching are common."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."]],
  "journey": "Cetrorelix is injected under the skin, usually once daily during the later days of stimulation. It is absorbed quickly, peaking within about an hour, with high bioavailability. Its half-life is several hours after one injection and longer with daily dosing. Peptidases break it down, and fragments and some unchanged drug leave in feces and urine. Pituitary function recovers after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are mainly injection site reactions, headache and nausea. OHSS and multiple pregnancy are risks of the overall stimulation cycle. Rare hypersensitivity reactions occur, though newer antagonists release much less histamine than early GnRH antagonists. It is contraindicated in pregnancy. There is no antidote; effects fade as the drug is cleared.",
  "fun": "Early GnRH antagonists caused so much histamine release that they produced allergic-type reactions, which is why cetrorelix and ganirelix were redesigned with different amino acids.",
  "src": ["Cetrotide (cetrorelix acetate) FDA label", "Schally AV. Nobel lecture 1977"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"ethinyl-estradiol-norgestimate": {
  "origin": "This pill joins two lab-made hormones. Ethinyl estradiol was first made in 1938 by Hans Herloff Inhoffen and Walter Hohlweg at Schering AG in Berlin. Adding an ethinyl group to natural estradiol slowed its breakdown in the liver, so it worked well by mouth. The first birth control pill, Enovid, reached the US market in 1960. Early pills used progestins that also acted a bit like testosterone, which could worsen acne and lipids. Researchers at Ortho Pharmaceutical (Johnson & Johnson) developed norgestimate as a more selective progestin with low androgenic activity. The FDA approved Ortho-Cyclen and the triphasic Ortho Tri-Cyclen in 1992, and in 1997 Ortho Tri-Cyclen became one of the first pills labeled to treat acne. Generic versions such as Sprintec and Tri-Sprintec are now among the most used pills in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1938", "Inhoffen and Hohlweg at Schering AG synthesize ethinyl estradiol."], ["1960", "Enovid becomes the first FDA-approved oral contraceptive."], ["1992", "FDA approves Ortho-Cyclen and Ortho Tri-Cyclen with norgestimate."], ["1997", "Ortho Tri-Cyclen gains an FDA label for moderate acne."]],
  "cell": "Ethinyl estradiol binds estrogen receptors (ER-alpha and ER-beta) and norgestimate's active metabolites bind progesterone receptors. Both are nuclear receptors that change gene transcription. In the hypothalamus and pituitary, steady progestin plus estrogen exert negative feedback. Progestin slows the pulse frequency of GnRH and blocks the mid-cycle LH surge, so the dominant follicle never ruptures. Estrogen suppresses FSH, so fewer follicles start to grow, and it stabilizes the endometrium to reduce breakthrough bleeding. Progestin also thickens cervical mucus so sperm cannot pass, and makes the endometrium thin and less receptive. Norgestimate itself is a prodrug: it is converted to norelgestromin and norgestrel (levonorgestrel), which carry the progestin activity. Its low androgenic profile, plus the estrogen-driven rise in sex hormone binding globulin (SHBG) that lowers free testosterone, explains the benefit for acne.",
  "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Feedback from both hormones lowers FSH and blocks the LH surge, so ovulation does not happen."], ["Ovaries", "Follicles stay small and quiet, which lowers the risk of ovarian cysts and, over years, ovarian cancer."], ["Uterus and cervix", "The lining stays thin, periods are lighter and more regular, and thick cervical mucus blocks sperm."], ["Skin", "More SHBG and less free testosterone reduce oil production, which helps acne."], ["Liver", "Ethinyl estradiol strongly stimulates the liver to make clotting factors, angiotensinogen and binding proteins, which explains clot and blood pressure risks."], ["Blood vessels", "More clotting factors and less natural anticoagulant activity raise the risk of venous clots, stroke and heart attack, especially in smokers over 35."]],
  "journey": "Both hormones are swallowed and absorbed well from the gut. Ethinyl estradiol peaks in about one to two hours, is partly broken down in the gut wall and liver by CYP3A4 and sulfation, and recycles through bile and the gut. Norgestimate is quickly deacetylated to norelgestromin and then to norgestrel, which binds SHBG and albumin and lasts one to two days. Ethinyl estradiol lasts about 15 to 20 hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Daily dosing at the same time keeps levels steady. Drugs that induce CYP3A4, such as rifampin and some antiseizure drugs, can lower levels and cause pill failure.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, breast tenderness and headache come mostly from estrogen acting on the brain and breast tissue. Breakthrough bleeding happens while the endometrium adjusts to a thin, steady state. Serious risks trace to the liver: ethinyl estradiol increases clotting factors, so deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke and heart attack become more likely, especially with smoking, migraine with aura or older age. More angiotensinogen can raise blood pressure. Estrogen makes bile more saturated with cholesterol, which favors gallstones, and rarely drives liver adenomas. There is no antidote; the pill is stopped and clots are treated with anticoagulants.",
  "fun": "Norgestimate's main working metabolite is levonorgestrel, the same progestin found in many other pills and in emergency contraception.",
  "src": ["Ortho Tri-Cyclen and Tri-Sprintec FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"segesterone-ethinyl-estradiol-ring": {
  "origin": "Annovera came from the Population Council, a nonprofit research group in New York that has worked on contraception since the 1950s. Its scientists developed segesterone acetate, also called Nestorone, a progestin related to 19-norprogesterone. It is strongly active on progesterone receptors but has almost no androgen or estrogen effect, and it is poorly active when swallowed because the liver breaks it down quickly, which makes it suited to delivery through the vaginal wall. The Council paired it with ethinyl estradiol in a soft silicone ring that a person can remove, wash and reuse. After years of development and clinical trials, the FDA approved Annovera in August 2018 as the first contraceptive that provides a full year of protection while controlled by the user without a clinic procedure.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "The Population Council begins work on reproductive health research."], ["2018", "FDA approves Annovera, the first one-year reusable contraceptive vaginal ring."]],
  "cell": "Segesterone acetate binds the progesterone receptor strongly and selectively, with very little binding to androgen receptors and no binding to SHBG. Ethinyl estradiol binds estrogen receptors. Together they exert negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary: GnRH pulses slow, the mid-cycle LH surge is blocked and FSH falls, so follicles do not mature and ovulation does not occur. The progestin thickens cervical mucus so sperm cannot pass and thins the endometrium. The estrogen stabilizes the lining, which limits irregular bleeding. Both hormones diffuse out of the silicone ring at a slow, near-constant rate across the vaginal epithelium, so blood levels stay steady instead of rising and falling like a daily pill.",
  "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Steady hormone levels suppress LH and FSH, so the ovaries do not release an egg."], ["Vagina", "The ring sits against the vaginal wall and the hormones pass directly into nearby blood vessels; some people notice discharge or discomfort."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick mucus blocks sperm and the endometrium stays thin; the scheduled 7 days without the ring allow a withdrawal bleed."], ["Liver", "Ethinyl estradiol still reaches the liver through the blood and raises clotting factors and angiotensinogen, even though the drug skipped first-pass metabolism."], ["Blood vessels", "Higher clotting factor levels raise the risk of venous clots, stroke and heart attack, especially in smokers over 35."]],
  "journey": "The hormones leave the ring by diffusion and are absorbed through the vaginal lining into the blood. This bypasses the gut and the first pass through the liver. Levels rise in the first days of each cycle and then hold fairly steady for the 21 days the ring is in place. Segesterone acetate is cleared quickly from blood, with a half-life of about four and a half hours, but the ring keeps resupplying it. Ethinyl estradiol lasts about 15 hours. The liver breaks both down mainly with CYP3A4, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. One ring is used for 13 cycles, about a year.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, nausea and mood change reflect estrogen and progestin effects on the brain. Vaginal discharge and discomfort come from a foreign device resting against the mucosa, and the ring can slip out. Irregular bleeding happens as the lining adjusts. The serious risks come from estrogen acting on the liver: more clotting factors mean a higher risk of deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke and heart attack. Toxic shock syndrome is a rare risk with any device left in the vagina, linked to toxin-producing Staphylococcus aureus. There is no antidote; the ring is removed and complications are treated directly.",
  "fun": "Segesterone acetate is weak when swallowed because the liver breaks it down so fast, which is one reason it was chosen for a ring.",
  "src": ["Annovera FDA label", "Population Council", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"drospirenone-only": {
  "origin": "Drospirenone was developed by Schering AG in Germany as a progestin that behaves like the body's own progesterone more closely than older progestins. It is a chemical relative of spironolactone, so it blocks mineralocorticoid (aldosterone) and androgen receptors as well as acting on progesterone receptors. It first reached the US in the combined pill Yasmin, approved in 2001. Older progestin-only pills used norethindrone and had to be taken within a narrow three-hour window each day. The company Exeltis developed Slynd, a drospirenone-only pill with 24 active days, to give an estrogen-free option with a more forgiving schedule. The FDA approved Slynd in 2019.",
  "timeline": [["2001", "Drospirenone reaches the US in the combined pill Yasmin."], ["2019", "FDA approves Slynd, a drospirenone-only pill without estrogen."]],
  "cell": "Drospirenone binds the progesterone receptor, a nuclear receptor that changes gene transcription. At the hypothalamus and pituitary this reduces GnRH pulse frequency and blocks the mid-cycle LH surge, so ovulation is suppressed. Unlike older progestin-only pills, which rely mostly on cervical mucus, drospirenone reliably stops ovulation. It also thickens cervical mucus so sperm cannot pass and makes the endometrium thin. Because it is a spironolactone analog, drospirenone also blocks mineralocorticoid receptors in the kidney's collecting duct, which promotes sodium and water loss and potassium retention, and blocks androgen receptors, which can help acne. With no estrogen, it does not drive the liver to make extra clotting factors in the way combined pills do.",
  "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Progestin feedback blocks the LH surge so an egg is not released."], ["Cervix and uterus", "Thick mucus blocks sperm and the lining becomes thin; without estrogen support, bleeding is often irregular."], ["Kidneys", "Antimineralocorticoid action leads to mild sodium loss and potassium retention, so potassium can rise in kidney disease or with other potassium-raising drugs."], ["Skin", "Antiandrogen activity can lessen oily skin and acne, though some users still develop acne."], ["Blood and liver", "No estrogen means less stimulation of liver clotting factor production, which is why it is an option when estrogen must be avoided."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth, drospirenone is well absorbed and peaks in about two to six hours. It binds albumin but not SHBG. Its half-life is about 30 hours, which is longer than norethindrone, so a late pill is less likely to let ovulation return. The liver breaks it down, partly by CYP3A4, into inactive acid and sulfate metabolites that leave in urine and feces. Strong CYP3A4 inducers can lower levels and reduce effectiveness.",
  "sidefx": "Irregular or unscheduled bleeding is the most common problem, because a progestin alone keeps the endometrium thin and unstable without estrogen support. Headache, breast pain, nausea and mood changes reflect progestin effects on the brain and breast. High potassium can occur because drospirenone blocks aldosterone at the kidney, especially with kidney disease, ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium-sparing diuretics. If pregnancy does happen, progestin slowing of tubal movement makes an ectopic pregnancy relatively more likely. Clots are rare. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Drospirenone is derived from 17-alpha-spirolactone, the same chemical family as the diuretic spironolactone.",
  "src": ["Slynd FDA label", "Yasmin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"estetrol-drospirenone": {
  "origin": "Estetrol (E4) is a natural estrogen made only by the human fetal liver during pregnancy. It was discovered in 1965 by the group of Egon Diczfalusy at the Karolinska Institute in Sweden, but for decades nobody knew what to do with it. In the 2000s the Belgian company Mithra and its partners studied E4 as a drug and found it acts on estrogen receptors in a more selective way than estradiol or ethinyl estradiol, with less effect on liver proteins. Paired with Schering's progestin drospirenone, it became Nextstellis, marketed in the US by Mayne Pharma. The FDA approved it in April 2021, the first new estrogen in a US birth control pill in decades.",
  "timeline": [["1965", "Diczfalusy's group at the Karolinska Institute discovers estetrol in pregnancy."], ["2000s", "Mithra and partners begin developing estetrol as a medicine."], ["2021", "FDA approves Nextstellis, estetrol with drospirenone."]],
  "cell": "Estetrol binds estrogen receptor alpha in the nucleus and turns on gene transcription like other estrogens. It acts weakly on the receptor's membrane-initiated signaling pathway, and in some tissues it can even oppose estradiol, so it is described as a native estrogen with selective tissue action. Drospirenone binds the progesterone receptor and slows GnRH pulses and blocks the LH surge, so ovulation stops. Estetrol adds FSH suppression and stabilizes the endometrium. Drospirenone also thickens cervical mucus and, as a spironolactone analog, blocks mineralocorticoid and androgen receptors. In studies estetrol caused smaller rises in liver-made proteins such as SHBG, angiotensinogen and clotting factors than ethinyl estradiol, which is thought to matter for clot risk, although the label still carries the same clot warnings as other combined pills.",
  "body": [["Hypothalamus and pituitary", "Combined estrogen and progestin feedback lowers FSH and blocks the LH surge, preventing ovulation."], ["Uterus and cervix", "Thick mucus blocks sperm; estetrol keeps the thin lining stable so bleeding is more predictable."], ["Liver", "Estetrol changes liver protein production less than ethinyl estradiol in studies, but some rise in clotting factors still occurs."], ["Kidneys", "Drospirenone blocks aldosterone, causing mild sodium loss and potassium retention."], ["Blood vessels", "Like all estrogen-containing pills, it carries warnings for venous clots, stroke and heart attack, especially in smokers over 35."]],
  "journey": "Both parts are well absorbed by mouth and peak within about half an hour to two hours. Estetrol is not bound to SHBG and is broken down mainly by glucuronidation and sulfation rather than CYP enzymes, then leaves in urine. Drospirenone is partly handled by CYP3A4 and leaves in urine and feces. Their half-lives are long, about 27 hours for estetrol and 34 hours for drospirenone, which supports steady daily levels.",
  "sidefx": "Bleeding irregularities, headache, breast pain and mood changes reflect the shift in hormone signaling. Acne and weight gain are reported. Drospirenone's aldosterone blockade can raise potassium, which matters with kidney disease or potassium-raising drugs. Estrogen exposure carries a boxed warning about cigarette smoking and serious cardiovascular events, because estrogens raise clotting factor production and can cause deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, stroke and heart attack. Blood pressure can rise. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Estetrol has four hydroxyl groups, which is where the name and the E4 label come from (estradiol is E2, estriol is E3).",
  "src": ["Nextstellis FDA label", "Diczfalusy group, Karolinska Institute, 1965 estetrol reports", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"elagolix-estradiol-norethindrone": {
  "origin": "Elagolix was discovered by Neurocrine Biosciences in San Diego as a small-molecule blocker of the GnRH receptor that can be swallowed. Earlier ways to shut down ovarian hormones used injected GnRH agonists such as leuprolide, which first cause a flare before suppression. Neurocrine partnered with AbbVie, and elagolix alone was approved as Orilissa in 2018 for endometriosis pain. Fibroids grow under estrogen, so lowering estrogen reduces bleeding, but low estrogen also causes hot flashes and bone loss. AbbVie therefore combined elagolix with low-dose estradiol and norethindrone acetate as add-back therapy. The FDA approved this regimen as Oriahnn in May 2020 for heavy menstrual bleeding from uterine fibroids.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's lab describes the structure of GnRH, later honored with a Nobel Prize."], ["2018", "FDA approves elagolix alone as Orilissa for endometriosis pain."], ["2020", "FDA approves Oriahnn, elagolix with estradiol and norethindrone acetate add-back, for fibroid bleeding."]],
  "cell": "Elagolix is a nonpeptide competitive antagonist of the GnRH receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on pituitary gonadotroph cells. Blocking it stops GnRH from triggering phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium signaling, so the pituitary releases less LH and FSH within hours, with no initial flare. Lower gonadotropins mean the ovaries make less estradiol and progesterone. Uterine fibroids (leiomyomas) depend on these hormones, so bleeding drops. The add-back estradiol binds estrogen receptors to keep levels in a range that protects bone and limits hot flashes, while norethindrone acetate, a progestin, protects the endometrium from unopposed estrogen. The goal is a middle zone of estrogen exposure: low enough to calm fibroid bleeding, high enough to limit side effects.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "GnRH receptors are blocked, so LH and FSH fall quickly and stay low while the drug is taken."], ["Ovaries", "Less stimulation means less estrogen and progesterone production and usually no ovulation."], ["Uterus", "Fibroid-related bleeding decreases because the endometrium and fibroids get less hormone drive; the progestin keeps the lining from overgrowing."], ["Bones", "Lower estrogen can reduce bone mineral density over time, which is why add-back hormones are included and treatment length is limited."], ["Blood vessels", "The estrogen part brings the usual estrogen warnings for clots, stroke and heart attack."], ["Brain", "Lower estrogen can cause hot flashes, and mood changes including depression have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth twice daily, elagolix is absorbed quickly, peaks in about an hour and has a short half-life of about four to six hours, so hormone suppression reverses within days to weeks after stopping. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A, with minor CYP2D6, CYP2C8 and UGT pathways, and it is a substrate of OATP1B1 and P-glycoprotein, so many drug interactions exist. It leaves mainly in feces. The morning capsule also delivers estradiol and norethindrone acetate, which are absorbed and processed like other hormone therapy; norethindrone acetate is converted to norethindrone.",
  "sidefx": "Hot flushes and headaches come from lower estrogen. Irregular bleeding reflects shifting hormone levels in the lining. Bone loss happens because estrogen normally restrains bone breakdown by osteoclasts; this is why treatment duration is limited. The estrogen and progestin add-back carry a boxed warning for blood clots and cardiovascular events, since estrogen increases liver clotting factor production. Depression and suicidal thoughts have been reported. Liver enzyme rises can occur, and blood pressure can climb. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "Unlike injected GnRH agonists, elagolix lowers hormones without a first-week flare because it blocks the receptor rather than overstimulating it.",
  "src": ["Oriahnn FDA label", "Orilissa FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"pneumococcal-polysaccharide-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Streptococcus pneumoniae was one of the first bacteria shown to have many capsule types, and early in the 20th century researchers learned that antibodies against a specific capsule sugar protect against that type. Crude capsule vaccines were tested in the 1940s but were abandoned when penicillin arrived. Robert Austrian later showed that pneumococcal disease still killed many people despite antibiotics, which revived interest. Working with Merck, he helped develop a 14-valent purified capsular polysaccharide vaccine licensed in the US in 1977. In 1983 it was expanded to 23 serotypes as Pneumovax 23, which remains in use for adults and high-risk people, alongside newer conjugate vaccines.",
  "timeline": [["1977", "A 14-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["1983", "The 23-valent vaccine, Pneumovax 23, is licensed."]],
  "cell": "PPSV23 contains purified capsular polysaccharides from 23 serotypes. These long, repeating sugar chains are T-cell independent antigens. They cross-link many B-cell receptors at once on mature B cells, especially marginal zone B cells in the spleen, which triggers antibody production without help from CD4 T cells. The result is mostly IgM and IgG2 antibodies against the capsule. When pneumococci later enter the body, these antibodies coat the capsule and activate complement, so neutrophils and macrophages can engulf and kill the bacteria (opsonophagocytosis). Because there is no T-cell help, the response does not build strong memory B cells, repeat doses do not boost well, and children under 2 respond poorly since their marginal zone B cells are immature.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "B cells in the spleen and lymph nodes make serotype-specific antibodies over 2 to 3 weeks."], ["Lungs and blood", "Capsule antibodies help clear pneumococci before they cause bacteremia, meningitis or invasive pneumonia."], ["Spleen", "The spleen is the main site for clearing coated bacteria, which is why people without a working spleen are a key group for pneumococcal vaccines."], ["Injection site", "Pain, swelling and redness are common because the polysaccharides trigger local inflammation."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle or under the skin. Antigen drains to regional lymph nodes and the spleen, where B cells respond. Antibody levels rise over about 2 to 3 weeks and peak in about a month. The polysaccharides are slowly broken down by immune cells. Protection lasts several years but wanes, especially in older adults. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion.",
  "sidefx": "Pain, swelling, redness and warmth at the site come from local inflammation as innate immune cells respond to the injected sugars. Headache, tiredness and muscle aches reflect cytokines released as the immune system reacts. Large local reactions that look like cellulitis can occur, especially with repeat doses, likely because existing antibodies form immune complexes at the site. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.",
  "fun": "There are more than 90 known pneumococcal capsule types, so vaccines target the ones that cause the most serious disease.",
  "src": ["Pneumovax 23 FDA label", "CDC Pink Book: Pneumococcal Disease", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]
},
"typhoid-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Typhoid fever is caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. In 1896 Almroth Wright in England, and Richard Pfeiffer and Wilhelm Kolle in Germany, developed killed whole-cell typhoid vaccines, which were widely used in the military but caused strong reactions. In the 1930s researchers described the Vi (virulence) capsular antigen of S. Typhi. Two better tolerated vaccines followed. Ty21a, a live attenuated oral strain made by chemical mutagenesis in Switzerland in the 1970s, was licensed in the US as Vivotif in 1989. The injected Vi capsular polysaccharide vaccine, Typhim Vi, was licensed in the US in 1994. Both are used mainly for travelers to areas where typhoid is common.",
  "timeline": [["1896", "Almroth Wright and, separately, Pfeiffer and Kolle develop killed typhoid vaccines."], ["1970s", "The live attenuated oral Ty21a strain is developed in Switzerland."], ["1989", "Oral Ty21a vaccine (Vivotif) is licensed in the US."], ["1994", "Vi polysaccharide vaccine (Typhim Vi) is licensed in the US."]],
  "cell": "Typhim Vi contains purified Vi capsular polysaccharide from the Ty2 strain. Like other unconjugated polysaccharides, it is a T-cell independent antigen. Its repeating sugar units cross-link B-cell receptors and drive antibody production without CD4 T-cell help, producing mostly IgG and IgM against Vi. The Vi capsule normally hides S. Typhi from complement and phagocytes. Anti-Vi antibodies coat the bacterium, activate complement and allow phagocytes to kill it, which blocks invasion and bloodstream spread. Without T-cell help, memory is limited and booster doses are given every couple of years for continued risk. Vivotif works differently: the live weakened Ty21a strain briefly colonizes the gut and triggers mucosal IgA and cell-mediated immunity.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "B cells make anti-Vi antibodies over about 2 weeks, giving protection for about 2 years."], ["Gut", "Antibodies help stop S. Typhi from invading the intestinal lining and spreading to lymph tissue and blood."], ["Injection site", "Pain, tenderness and firmness at the site come from local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Headache, malaise and muscle aches are mild and short-lived signs of immune activation."]],
  "journey": "Typhim Vi is injected into muscle. The antigen travels to lymph nodes and the spleen, where B cells respond. Antibodies appear in about 2 weeks and peak in 2 to 4 weeks. Protection lasts about 2 years. The polysaccharide is processed by immune cells, not metabolized like a drug.",
  "sidefx": "Pain, tenderness and induration at the injection site come from local innate immune activation. Headache, malaise and muscle aches reflect cytokine release. Fever is uncommon. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine. Because the Vi vaccine contains no living bacteria, it cannot cause typhoid.",
  "fun": "Mary Mallon, known as Typhoid Mary, was an apparently healthy carrier in early 1900s New York who was linked to many typhoid cases.",
  "src": ["Typhim Vi FDA label", "Vivotif FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Typhoid and Paratyphoid Fever"]
},
"yellow-fever-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Yellow fever is a flavivirus spread by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes. In 1900 Walter Reed's US Army commission in Cuba confirmed mosquito transmission, building on Carlos Finlay's idea. In the 1930s Max Theiler and colleagues at the Rockefeller Foundation in New York passed the Asibi strain of the virus many times through mouse and chicken embryo tissues. By 1937 they had a weakened strain, 17D, that caused only mild infection but strong lasting immunity. Theiler received the 1951 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for this work. Substrains of 17D, including 17D-204 used in YF-Vax, are still the basis for yellow fever vaccines worldwide.",
  "timeline": [["1900", "Walter Reed's commission confirms that mosquitoes spread yellow fever."], ["1937", "Max Theiler and colleagues develop the live attenuated 17D strain."], ["1951", "Theiler receives the Nobel Prize for the yellow fever vaccine."]],
  "cell": "YF-Vax contains live attenuated yellow fever virus (17D-204). After injection under the skin, the virus infects dendritic cells and other local cells and replicates at a low level. Viral RNA is sensed by innate receptors such as TLR7, TLR8, RIG-I and MDA5, which trigger type I interferon and inflammatory signals. Dendritic cells carry viral antigen to lymph nodes, where they activate CD4 helper T cells, CD8 cytotoxic T cells and B cells. B cells, with T-cell help, make neutralizing antibodies, mostly against the viral envelope (E) protein, which block the virus from entering cells. Because the virus replicates, the immune system sees antigen over days in a natural way, which gives strong and very long-lasting immunity, often lifelong after one dose.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "A mild controlled infection produces neutralizing antibodies by about 10 days and long-lived memory B and T cells."], ["Whole body", "Headache, muscle aches and low fever 5 to 10 days later reflect interferon and cytokine release as the virus replicates."], ["Liver and organs (rare)", "In rare cases the vaccine virus spreads widely and causes multiorgan illness that resembles wild yellow fever (YEL-AVD), more often in older adults and people with thymus disease."], ["Nervous system (rare)", "Rarely, the virus or the immune response affects the brain or nerves (YEL-AND), causing encephalitis or Guillain-Barre syndrome."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected under the skin. The live virus multiplies locally and in lymph nodes, and low levels can appear in the blood for a few days. Neutralizing antibodies appear by about 10 days and peak in 2 to 4 weeks. The immune system clears the virus completely. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion. Immunity lasts decades and is likely lifelong for most people.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, muscle aches, low fever and soreness, often 5 to 10 days later, match the time the virus is replicating and interferon is high. The serious adverse events come from the virus being live. In YEL-AVD, the vaccine virus spreads to the liver and other organs and can cause organ failure; risk is higher in people over 60 and those with thymus disorders, because their early immune control is weaker. YEL-AND involves the nervous system. Because the virus is grown in chicken embryos, people with severe egg allergy can react. There is no antidote; care is supportive. The vaccine is avoided in severely immunocompromised people.",
  "fun": "Many countries require proof of yellow fever vaccination for entry, recorded on the International Certificate of Vaccination, often called the yellow card.",
  "src": ["YF-Vax FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Yellow Fever", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1951"]
},
"japanese-encephalitis-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Japanese encephalitis is a mosquito-borne flavivirus first recognized in large outbreaks in Japan. The virus was isolated in Japan in the 1930s. Early vaccines were made from virus grown in mouse brains and inactivated; one of these was used in the US for travelers for years before being discontinued. In China, scientists developed the live attenuated SA14-14-2 strain. The Austrian company Intercell (now Valneva) took this strain, grew it in Vero cells and inactivated it to make a purified vaccine with an aluminum adjuvant. The FDA approved it as Ixiaro in 2009 for adults, later extending it to children.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "Japanese encephalitis virus is isolated in Japan."], ["2009", "FDA approves Ixiaro, a Vero cell-derived inactivated JE vaccine."]],
  "cell": "Ixiaro contains whole Japanese encephalitis virus that has been killed with formaldehyde so it cannot replicate, adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide. The aluminum adjuvant holds antigen at the injection site and activates innate immune signals, including the NLRP3 inflammasome, which draws in antigen-presenting cells. Dendritic cells take up viral proteins and present them on MHC class II to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. Those T cells help B cells make neutralizing IgG antibodies, mainly against the envelope (E) protein, which coats the virus and blocks it from attaching to and fusing with host cells. Because the virus does not replicate, two doses are needed to build adequate antibody levels and memory.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "After two doses, B cells make neutralizing antibodies that block the virus before it can reach the brain."], ["Brain", "Protection matters because JE infection can cause encephalitis with lasting neurologic damage or death."], ["Injection site", "Pain and tenderness come from local inflammation driven by antigen and the aluminum adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Headache, muscle aches, fatigue and fever in young children reflect a short burst of immune signaling."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle. The aluminum-bound antigen is released slowly and taken up by immune cells that travel to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies appear mainly after the second dose and peak within a few weeks. Protection lasts at least 1 to 2 years after the primary series, and a booster extends it. The killed virus is broken down by immune cells.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site pain and tenderness come from the local inflammation that the adjuvant is designed to cause. Headache, muscle aches, fatigue and fever reflect cytokines from immune activation. Because the virus is inactivated, it cannot cause infection. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare, and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine. The older mouse brain-derived vaccine had more allergic and neurologic reactions, which was one reason it was replaced.",
  "fun": "Pigs and wading birds are the main hosts that amplify Japanese encephalitis virus; people are dead-end hosts.",
  "src": ["Ixiaro FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Japanese Encephalitis", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]
},
"cholera-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae, first seen by Filippo Pacini in 1854 and isolated in pure culture by Robert Koch in 1883. Cholera toxin causes massive watery diarrhea. In the 1980s Myron Levine, James Kaper and colleagues at the University of Maryland Center for Vaccine Development engineered a strain, CVD 103-HgR, by deleting the gene for the toxic A subunit of cholera toxin while keeping the harmless B subunit. A Swiss product based on this strain was used abroad. Later, PaxVax developed a US version, and the FDA approved it as Vaxchora in 2016, the only cholera vaccine licensed in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1854", "Filippo Pacini describes the cholera bacterium."], ["1883", "Robert Koch isolates Vibrio cholerae."], ["1980s", "University of Maryland researchers build the attenuated CVD 103-HgR strain."], ["2016", "FDA approves Vaxchora, a single-dose oral cholera vaccine."]],
  "cell": "Vaxchora contains live V. cholerae O1 classical Inaba bacteria that lack the gene for the cholera toxin A subunit, so they cannot cause the toxin-driven diarrhea. After being swallowed with a buffer that protects them from stomach acid, the bacteria briefly colonize the small intestine. M cells over Peyer's patches sample them and pass antigens to dendritic cells. These activate T cells and B cells in gut lymphoid tissue. B cells make IgA and serum vibriocidal antibodies against the O1 lipopolysaccharide, and antibodies against the cholera toxin B subunit. Vibriocidal antibodies, with complement, kill V. cholerae, and mucosal antibodies block bacteria from attaching to the gut lining. Serum vibriocidal antibody is the measured marker of protection.",
  "body": [["Gut", "The weakened bacteria briefly live in the small intestine, which trains local IgA defenses against cholera."], ["Immune system", "Vibriocidal antibodies rise by about 10 days, matching the timing of protection."], ["Stomach and bowel", "Mild abdominal pain, nausea, loss of appetite and loose stools reflect the bacteria colonizing the gut."], ["Whole body", "Tiredness and headache come from a short-lived immune response."]],
  "journey": "The dose is drunk as a suspension made with a bicarbonate buffer, which neutralizes stomach acid long enough for the bacteria to reach the small intestine. They multiply there briefly and are then cleared by the immune response. Vaccine bacteria are shed in stool for at least 7 days, so hand hygiene matters around close contacts. Antibiotics taken shortly before or after can kill the vaccine strain, and chloroquine may blunt the response, which is why the label sets timing rules.",
  "sidefx": "Tiredness, headache, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, poor appetite and diarrhea come from live bacteria growing briefly in the gut and the local inflammation that follows. Because the toxin A gene is deleted, severe cholera-type diarrhea does not occur. Shedding in stool means the strain could reach close contacts. Allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.",
  "fun": "John Snow's 1854 removal of the Broad Street pump handle in London is a founding story of epidemiology and involved cholera.",
  "src": ["Vaxchora FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Cholera", "Levine MM, Kaper JB. Lancet 1988"]
},
"chikungunya-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Chikungunya virus is an alphavirus spread by Aedes mosquitoes. It was first identified during an outbreak in what is now Tanzania in 1952; the name comes from a Makonde word that describes the stooped posture caused by severe joint pain. Large outbreaks spread across the Indian Ocean, Asia and the Americas in the 2000s and 2010s. Two US vaccines followed. Ixchiq, a live attenuated vaccine from Valneva, was approved in 2023, but the FDA suspended its US license in August 2025 over serious adverse events. Vimkunya, from Bavarian Nordic, was approved by the FDA in February 2025. It uses virus-like particles first developed at the US National Institutes of Health, which look like the virus but contain no genetic material.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Chikungunya virus is identified during an outbreak in present-day Tanzania."], ["2013", "Chikungunya spreads in the Caribbean and the Americas."], ["2023", "FDA approves Ixchiq, a live attenuated chikungunya vaccine."], ["2025", "FDA approves the virus-like particle vaccine Vimkunya and suspends the Ixchiq license."]],
  "cell": "Vimkunya contains virus-like particles (VLPs) built from the chikungunya capsid protein and the E1 and E2 envelope glycoproteins. These proteins self-assemble into particles with the same outer shape as the virus but no RNA, so they cannot replicate. The VLPs are adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide adjuvant. The repeating, organized pattern of E2 and E1 on the particle surface strongly cross-links B-cell receptors, and dendritic cells take up the particles and present them to CD4 helper T cells. With T-cell help, B cells make neutralizing IgG, largely against E2, which blocks the virus from attaching to and entering host cells such as fibroblasts and joint tissue cells.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies appear within about 1 to 3 weeks after a single injection."], ["Joints", "Protection matters because chikungunya infection can cause severe joint pain that sometimes lasts months."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the site comes from local inflammation driven by antigen and adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache and muscle aches are short-lived signs of immune activation."]],
  "journey": "Vimkunya is injected into the deltoid muscle. The aluminum-bound particles are taken up by immune cells and carried to nearby lymph nodes, where the antibody response begins. Antibodies rise over about 3 weeks. The particles are broken down by immune cells. How long protection lasts is still being studied.",
  "sidefx": "Pain at the site, fatigue, headache and muscle aches come from normal innate immune activation and cytokine release. Because the VLPs contain no viral genetic material, they cannot cause infection, which is a key difference from the suspended live vaccine Ixchiq. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.",
  "fun": "Chikungunya is usually translated as 'that which bends up', a description of people stooped over by joint pain.",
  "src": ["Vimkunya FDA label", "FDA safety communication on Ixchiq, 2025", "CDC Yellow Book: Chikungunya"]
},
"bcg-vaccine": {
  "origin": "BCG stands for Bacillus Calmette-Guerin, named for Albert Calmette and Camille Guerin at the Pasteur Institute in Lille, France. Starting in 1908 they grew a strain of Mycobacterium bovis, the cattle tuberculosis germ, on a medium of potato, glycerin and ox bile, and passed it 230 times over about 13 years. The strain lost its ability to cause disease but still triggered immunity. It was first given to a human infant in 1921. Over time labs around the world kept different daughter strains. The TICE substrain used in the US came from work at the University of Illinois. BCG remains one of the most widely used vaccines in the world, but in the US it is used only for selected people with high, unavoidable TB exposure.",
  "timeline": [["1908", "Calmette and Guerin begin serial culture of Mycobacterium bovis in Lille."], ["1921", "BCG is first given to a human infant."], ["1930", "A contamination disaster in Lubeck, Germany, kills many infants, later traced to virulent TB in a vaccine batch, not BCG itself."]],
  "cell": "BCG is live attenuated Mycobacterium bovis. It lacks a genomic region called RD1, which encodes the ESX-1 secretion system that virulent M. tuberculosis uses to escape the phagosome and damage host cells. After percutaneous delivery, BCG is taken up by macrophages and dendritic cells. Mycobacterial cell wall components activate TLR2, TLR4 and other innate receptors. Dendritic cells present antigens to CD4 T cells, driving a Th1 response with interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages to kill intracellular mycobacteria. CD8 T cells and memory T cells also form. This cell-mediated immunity is the main defense against TB. BCG protects young children against severe forms such as TB meningitis and miliary TB better than it protects adults against lung TB.",
  "body": [["Skin", "A papule forms, becomes a pustule and then a small ulcer that heals with a scar over weeks."], ["Lymph nodes", "Nearby nodes may swell as immune cells respond; rarely they form pus (suppurative lymphadenitis)."], ["Immune system", "T cells learn to recognize mycobacteria, and the tuberculin skin test usually turns positive within 2 to 3 months."], ["Bones (rare)", "Rarely the bacteria seed bone and cause osteitis."], ["Whole body (rare)", "In people with weak cell-mediated immunity, BCG can spread and cause disseminated infection."]],
  "journey": "The reconstituted vaccine is placed on the skin and pushed in with a multiple puncture device. The bacteria live and multiply briefly in the skin and nearby lymph nodes, then the immune system controls and clears them over weeks to months. Tuberculin conversion appears in about 2 to 3 months. Protection varies and wanes over 10 to 20 years. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion.",
  "sidefx": "The local papule, pustule, ulcer and scar are the expected result of live bacteria triggering inflammation in the skin. Swollen lymph nodes reflect bacteria draining to the nodes. Osteitis and disseminated BCG infection happen when the immune system cannot contain the live organism, which is why the vaccine is avoided in immunocompromised people. BCG is sensitive to standard TB drugs other than pyrazinamide, because M. bovis is naturally resistant to pyrazinamide, which matters if treatment is needed. BCG can also turn a tuberculin skin test positive, complicating TB screening, while interferon-gamma release assays are less affected.",
  "fun": "A separate form of TICE BCG is placed into the bladder to treat early bladder cancer by provoking a local immune attack.",
  "src": ["BCG Vaccine (TICE) FDA label", "CDC: BCG Vaccine Fact Sheet", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]
},
"anthrax-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Anthrax is caused by Bacillus anthracis. In 1876 Robert Koch used anthrax to show that a specific microbe causes a specific disease. In 1881 Louis Pasteur publicly tested an attenuated anthrax vaccine on sheep at Pouilly-le-Fort, France. Modern human vaccines came from the US in the 1950s and 1960s, when researchers found that a cell-free filtrate from an avirulent strain could protect against the toxin. The current anthrax vaccine adsorbed (AVA) was licensed in 1970 and later sold as BioThrax by Emergent BioSolutions. In 2023 the FDA approved Cyfendus, an adjuvanted version for use after exposure together with antibiotics.",
  "timeline": [["1876", "Robert Koch links Bacillus anthracis to anthrax."], ["1881", "Pasteur demonstrates an anthrax vaccine in sheep at Pouilly-le-Fort."], ["1970", "Anthrax vaccine adsorbed is licensed in the US."], ["2001", "Anthrax letters in the US raise interest in post-exposure vaccination."], ["2023", "FDA approves Cyfendus for post-exposure prophylaxis with antibiotics."]],
  "cell": "Anthrax toxin has three parts: protective antigen (PA), lethal factor and edema factor. PA binds receptors on host cells (such as ANTXR2), is cut by furin, and forms a pore that carries lethal factor and edema factor into the cell, where they disrupt signaling and kill immune cells. The vaccine is a cell-free filtrate rich in PA, adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide. Antigen-presenting cells process PA and activate CD4 helper T cells, which help B cells make IgG against PA. These antibodies block PA from binding its receptor and forming the pore, so the toxins cannot get into cells. Cyfendus adds an extra adjuvant (a CpG oligonucleotide that activates TLR9) to speed and strengthen the antibody response for post-exposure use.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "After 2 to 3 doses, B cells make anti-PA antibodies that neutralize anthrax toxin; boosters keep levels up."], ["Blood and tissues", "Neutralizing antibodies stop toxin from entering immune and vascular cells, which prevents the shock seen in inhalation anthrax."], ["Injection site", "Tenderness, pain, redness, swelling and limited arm motion are common because of the adjuvant and local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Muscle aches, headache and fatigue reflect cytokine release after vaccination."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle for pre-exposure use or under the skin for post-exposure use. Aluminum-bound antigen stays at the site and is slowly taken up by immune cells that travel to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies build over the series. After exposure, antibiotics clear germinating spores while the vaccine builds antibodies against toxin from any spores that germinate later. The antigen is processed by the immune system, not metabolized like a drug.",
  "sidefx": "Pain, tenderness, redness, swelling and limited arm movement come from strong local inflammation caused by the aluminum adjuvant and protein antigen. Larger local reactions can occur, sometimes more with later doses as antibodies build up. Muscle aches, headache and fatigue reflect immune activation. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine. The vaccine contains no live bacteria and cannot cause anthrax.",
  "fun": "The name anthrax comes from the Greek word for coal, because of the black scab seen in skin anthrax.",
  "src": ["BioThrax FDA label", "Cyfendus FDA label", "CDC: Anthrax Vaccination"]
},
"influenza-vaccine-high-dose": {
  "origin": "Influenza virus was first isolated from humans in 1933 in London. In the 1940s Thomas Francis Jr. and Jonas Salk at the University of Michigan helped develop inactivated influenza vaccines grown in fertilized chicken eggs, and the first was licensed in the US in 1945, initially for the military. Older adults respond weakly to standard flu shots because the immune system ages, a change called immunosenescence. Sanofi Pasteur reasoned that more antigen could overcome this. Fluzone High-Dose, with four times the hemagglutinin of a standard dose, was licensed by the FDA in 2009 for people 65 and older. A large randomized trial later showed it prevented more influenza than standard-dose vaccine in this age group. A quadrivalent version followed, and the product returned to a trivalent form after B/Yamagata viruses stopped circulating.",
  "timeline": [["1933", "Influenza virus is first isolated from humans."], ["1945", "The first inactivated influenza vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["2009", "FDA licenses Fluzone High-Dose for adults 65 and older."], ["2014", "A large trial shows better protection than standard-dose vaccine in older adults."]],
  "cell": "The vaccine contains split, formaldehyde-inactivated influenza viruses, standardized by their hemagglutinin (HA) content, for the season's A/H1N1, A/H3N2 and B strains. Dendritic cells and macrophages take up HA and neuraminidase and present peptides on MHC class II to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. With T-cell help, B cells, including memory B cells from earlier flu exposures, make IgG against the HA head. These antibodies block HA from binding sialic acid receptors on airway cells, which prevents viral entry (measured as hemagglutination inhibition). In older adults fewer naive cells and weaker T-cell help blunt this response. Giving more antigen recruits more B cells and produces higher antibody titers. Because HA changes from year to year (antigenic drift), the strains are updated and the vaccine is repeated each season.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Higher antigen content produces higher antibody levels in older adults within about 2 weeks."], ["Lungs and airways", "Antibodies block flu virus from infecting airway cells, which lowers the risk of influenza, pneumonia and hospitalization."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling are more common than with standard-dose vaccine because more antigen causes more local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Muscle aches, headache and malaise reflect cytokine release and fade within days."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid muscle. Antigen is taken up by immune cells and carried to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies rise in about 2 weeks and peak by 2 to 4 weeks. Protection wanes over the season, and the circulating strains change, so the shot is given every year. The viral proteins are processed by immune cells, not metabolized like a drug. The virus is inactivated, so it cannot cause flu.",
  "sidefx": "Pain, redness and swelling at the site, along with muscle aches, headache and malaise, come from innate immune activation and are somewhat more frequent than with standard-dose vaccine because of the larger antigen amount. Fainting can happen around any injection. Guillain-Barre syndrome has been linked very rarely to influenza vaccines, likely through an immune cross-reaction with nerve components. Anaphylaxis is rare and is treated with epinephrine.",
  "fun": "Each year experts choose the vaccine strains months ahead, based on global surveillance, so egg-based production can be ready by fall.",
  "src": ["Fluzone High-Dose FDA label", "DiazGranados CA et al. N Engl J Med 2014", "CDC Pink Book: Influenza"]
},
"tick-borne-encephalitis-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Tick-borne encephalitis (TBE) is a flavivirus infection spread mainly by Ixodes ticks, and sometimes by unpasteurized milk, in forested parts of Europe and Asia. The virus was first isolated in the Soviet Far East in the 1930s. In Austria, where TBE was common, Christian Kunz and colleagues developed an inactivated whole-virus vaccine with the company Immuno AG, introduced in the 1970s and later known as FSME-Immun. Mass vaccination in Austria sharply reduced cases. Pfizer acquired the product, and the FDA approved it for US travelers as TicoVac in August 2021, the first TBE vaccine licensed in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "Tick-borne encephalitis virus is first isolated in the Soviet Far East."], ["1970s", "An inactivated TBE vaccine developed in Austria comes into use."], ["2021", "FDA approves TicoVac for people 1 year and older."]],
  "cell": "TicoVac contains whole TBE virus (Neudorfl strain) grown in chick embryo cells and killed with formaldehyde, adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide. The adjuvant holds the antigen at the site and activates innate immune signals. Dendritic cells take up viral proteins, especially the envelope (E) protein, and present them to CD4 helper T cells in lymph nodes. Those T cells help B cells switch to IgG and form memory. Neutralizing antibodies against the E protein bind the virus and block attachment and membrane fusion, so the virus cannot infect cells or reach the brain. Because it does not replicate, a series of doses is needed to reach protective levels.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Antibodies rise after the second dose, and a third dose gives longer protection."], ["Brain and meninges", "Protection matters because TBE infection can cause meningitis, encephalitis and lasting neurologic problems."], ["Injection site", "Pain and tenderness are common because of local inflammation from the adjuvant."], ["Whole body", "Headache, fatigue, muscle aches and fever in young children reflect a brief immune response."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected into muscle. The aluminum-bound antigen is released slowly and carried by immune cells to lymph nodes. Antibodies appear after the second dose and peak within weeks after the second or third dose. Protection lasts at least 3 years after the primary series, and boosters are used for continued risk. The killed virus is broken down by immune cells.",
  "sidefx": "Pain and tenderness at the site, headache, fatigue and muscle aches come from normal innate immune activation and cytokine release. Fever is more frequent in young children, whose immune systems react strongly to new antigens. Because the virus is killed, it cannot cause infection. Allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine. The virus is grown in chick embryo cells, so severe egg allergy is a consideration.",
  "fun": "In parts of Europe TBE has been linked to raw milk or cheese from infected goats, not just tick bites.",
  "src": ["TicoVac FDA label", "CDC Yellow Book: Tick-Borne Encephalitis"]
},
"pneumococcal-21-valent-conjugate-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Plain polysaccharide pneumococcal vaccines do not work well in young children and give limited memory. In the 1980s John Robbins and Rachel Schneerson at the US National Institutes of Health showed that linking a capsule sugar to a carrier protein made it a T-cell dependent antigen, first for Haemophilus influenzae type b. This conjugate idea led to pneumococcal conjugate vaccines, starting with a 7-valent vaccine in 2000. Those vaccines were designed around serotypes that cause disease in children. Merck designed Capvaxive (V116) for adults instead, choosing 21 serotypes, including several not found in earlier vaccines, that cause most invasive disease in adults. The FDA approved it in June 2024.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "NIH researchers Robbins and Schneerson develop the conjugate vaccine concept."], ["2000", "The first pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, PCV7, is licensed in the US."], ["2024", "FDA approves Capvaxive, a 21-valent conjugate vaccine designed for adults."]],
  "cell": "Each of the 21 capsular polysaccharides is chemically linked to CRM197, a nontoxic mutant of diphtheria toxin. A B cell whose receptor recognizes a capsule sugar takes up the whole conjugate, digests the protein part and presents carrier peptides on MHC class II. CD4 helper T cells that recognize those peptides then give the B cell help through CD40 ligand and cytokines. This drives germinal center reactions, class switching to IgG, affinity maturation and memory B cells, which a plain polysaccharide cannot do. The resulting IgG coats the pneumococcal capsule, fixes complement and allows neutrophils and macrophages to engulf and kill the bacteria (opsonophagocytic activity).",
  "body": [["Immune system", "T-cell help produces high-affinity IgG and memory B cells against each of the 21 capsule types."], ["Lungs and blood", "Antibodies help clear pneumococci early, which lowers the risk of pneumonia, bacteremia and meningitis."], ["Spleen", "Opsonized bacteria are cleared by phagocytes in the spleen and liver."], ["Injection site", "Pain, swelling and redness come from local inflammation."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache and muscle aches reflect brief cytokine release."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is injected into the deltoid muscle. Antigen-presenting cells carry the conjugates to nearby lymph nodes, where germinal centers form. Antibodies rise within about 2 weeks and peak around a month. Memory B cells persist for years. The protein and sugar parts are broken down by immune cells, not metabolized like a drug.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site pain, swelling and redness, fatigue, headache and muscle aches come from innate immune activation and cytokines. Fainting can happen around any injection. Fever is uncommon. Severe allergic reactions, including to the diphtheria-derived carrier, are rare and are treated with epinephrine. The vaccine contains no live bacteria.",
  "fun": "Capvaxive includes serotypes such as 15A, 23B and 35B that were not covered by earlier pneumococcal vaccines.",
  "src": ["Capvaxive FDA label", "CDC: Pneumococcal Vaccine Recommendations", "Plotkin's Vaccines"]
},
"meningococcal-abcwy-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Neisseria meningitidis was identified by Anton Weichselbaum in 1887. Serogroups A, C, W and Y have polysaccharide capsules that work well in conjugate vaccines. Serogroup B was harder, because its capsule sugar resembles a molecule on human nerve cells and does not trigger a useful response. Researchers turned to surface proteins instead, using a genome-based approach called reverse vaccinology, pioneered by Rino Rappuoli and colleagues. This led to the MenB vaccines Trumenba (Pfizer, based on factor H binding protein) and Bexsero (GSK, 4CMenB). Combining a MenACWY conjugate with a MenB component cut the number of shots. The FDA approved Pfizer's Penbraya in October 2023 and GSK's Penmenvy in February 2025.",
  "timeline": [["1887", "Anton Weichselbaum identifies Neisseria meningitidis."], ["2000", "Reverse vaccinology work begins to identify MenB protein antigens from the genome."], ["2014", "FDA approves the first MenB vaccine, Trumenba."], ["2023", "FDA approves Penbraya, a pentavalent MenABCWY vaccine."], ["2025", "FDA approves Penmenvy, a second MenABCWY vaccine."]],
  "cell": "The vaccine has two parts. The MenACWY part contains capsular polysaccharides from groups A, C, W and Y, each linked to a carrier protein (tetanus toxoid in Penbraya, CRM197 in Penmenvy). The carrier lets CD4 T cells help capsule-specific B cells, which produces IgG and memory. The MenB part contains protein antigens instead of sugar. In Penbraya these are two lipidated variants of factor H binding protein (fHbp). Meningococci use fHbp to grab human factor H and shut down complement on their surface. Antibodies against fHbp and against the capsules let complement assemble the membrane attack complex and lyse the bacteria. This serum bactericidal activity, measured with human complement, is the marker of protection.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "B cells make bactericidal antibodies against all five serogroups within 1 to 2 weeks."], ["Blood and meninges", "Antibodies with complement kill meningococci before they cause meningitis or septicemia, which can kill within hours."], ["Injection site", "Pain at the site is common, especially from the MenB protein component."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, headache, muscle aches and chills reflect cytokine release."]],
  "journey": "The lyophilized MenACWY part is reconstituted with the liquid MenB part and injected into the deltoid. Immune cells carry the antigens to nearby lymph nodes. Antibodies rise within 1 to 2 weeks and peak in about a month. Protection against groups A, C, W and Y lasts several years; MenB antibodies wane faster. The antigens are processed by immune cells.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site pain, fatigue, headache, muscle aches and chills come from innate immune activation, and the lipidated MenB proteins are especially reactogenic. Fever and fainting can occur. Because the vaccine has no live bacteria, it cannot cause meningococcal disease. Severe allergic reactions are rare and are treated with epinephrine.",
  "fun": "The MenB capsule is avoided as a vaccine target because it chemically resembles polysialic acid on human nerve cell adhesion molecules.",
  "src": ["Penbraya FDA label", "Penmenvy FDA label", "CDC: Meningococcal Vaccine Recommendations"]
},
"rsv-monoclonal-clesrovimab": {
  "origin": "Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) is the leading cause of bronchiolitis and hospitalization in infants. A failed formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine in the 1960s made some infants sicker, which slowed vaccine work for decades. Passive protection came first: palivizumab, an antibody given monthly to high-risk infants, was approved in 1998. Later, structural work showed that the RSV fusion (F) protein has different shapes before and after fusion. This guided new antibodies. Merck developed clesrovimab, a human IgG1 antibody with a long half-life, so one shot could cover a whole RSV season. The FDA approved it as Enflonsia in June 2025 for infants entering their first RSV season.",
  "timeline": [["1956", "RSV is first isolated, from chimpanzees, and soon after from children."], ["1960s", "A formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine trial leads to enhanced disease in infants."], ["1998", "FDA approves palivizumab for RSV prevention in high-risk infants."], ["2025", "FDA approves clesrovimab (Enflonsia) as a single-dose infant RSV antibody."]],
  "cell": "RSV enters airway epithelial cells using its F glycoprotein, which changes shape from a prefusion to a postfusion form to fuse the viral and cell membranes. Clesrovimab binds a conserved site on F (antigenic site IV), which is present on both forms. Binding blocks the shape change needed for fusion, so the virus cannot enter cells or spread by fusing neighboring cells into syncytia. This is passive immunization: the infant receives ready-made antibody rather than making its own. Changes in the Fc region increase binding to the neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) at acidic pH, so more antibody is rescued from breakdown inside cells and recycled to the blood, which extends its half-life to about 44 days and lets one dose last about 5 months.",
  "body": [["Lungs and airways", "Antibody in airway fluid and blood blocks RSV from infecting the small airways, lowering the risk of bronchiolitis and pneumonia."], ["Immune system", "Protection begins within days, without needing the infant's immature immune system to respond."], ["Injection site", "Redness and swelling at the thigh are the most common reactions."], ["Skin", "Rash can occur, likely as a mild immune reaction to the antibody."]],
  "journey": "Clesrovimab is injected into the anterolateral thigh. It is absorbed slowly from muscle into the lymph and blood, reaching peak levels in about a week, with protective levels within days. It spreads into tissue fluid, including the lungs. Like other antibodies, it is broken down by proteolysis in cells throughout the body, not by the liver or kidneys. FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of about 44 days, so one dose covers about 5 months.",
  "sidefx": "Redness and swelling at the injection site come from local reaction to a protein injection. Rash may reflect a mild immune response to the antibody. Because clesrovimab is a human protein, serious hypersensitivity is rare but possible, and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine. It is used with caution in infants with bleeding disorders because any intramuscular injection can cause bleeding into muscle.",
  "fun": "An antibody shot like this gives immunity in days, but it fades over months because the infant's body is not making the antibody itself.",
  "src": ["Enflonsia FDA label", "CDC: RSV Immunization for Infants", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"smallpox-vaccine-acam2000": {
  "origin": "In 1796 Edward Jenner in England took material from a cowpox sore on milkmaid Sarah Nelmes and scratched it into the arm of a boy, James Phipps, who then resisted smallpox. The word vaccine comes from vacca, Latin for cow. Over time the virus used became vaccinia, a related orthopoxvirus whose exact origin is uncertain. A worldwide vaccination campaign led WHO to declare smallpox eradicated in 1980. The older US vaccine, Dryvax, was grown on calf skin. After 2001, concern about bioterrorism led the US to stockpile a modern version. Acambis cloned virus from Dryvax and grew it in Vero cell culture to make ACAM2000, which the FDA approved in 2007. In 2024 its label was expanded to include mpox.",
  "timeline": [["1796", "Edward Jenner inoculates James Phipps with cowpox material."], ["1980", "WHO declares smallpox eradicated."], ["2007", "FDA approves ACAM2000, replacing Dryvax."], ["2024", "FDA expands the ACAM2000 label to include mpox prevention."]],
  "cell": "ACAM2000 is live, replicating vaccinia virus. It is placed into the skin with a bifurcated needle, where it infects keratinocytes and replicates, forming a local lesion. Innate sensors detect the viral DNA and proteins, triggering interferon and inflammation. Dendritic cells carry viral antigens to lymph nodes and activate CD4 T cells, CD8 cytotoxic T cells and B cells. Neutralizing antibodies target vaccinia surface proteins, and T cells kill infected cells. Orthopoxviruses share many proteins, so immunity to vaccinia cross-protects against variola (smallpox) and mpox. A successful 'take', with a papule, vesicle and pustule at the site, shows the virus replicated and the immune system responded.",
  "body": [["Skin", "A papule appears at 3 to 4 days, turns into a pustule, then scabs and leaves a scar over 2 to 3 weeks."], ["Lymph nodes", "Nearby nodes swell as immune cells respond to the replicating virus."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies and T cells develop that protect against smallpox and mpox for years."], ["Heart", "Myocarditis and pericarditis can occur, mostly in young men, likely from immune-mediated inflammation."], ["Eyes", "Touching the site and then the eye can spread live virus and cause eye infection."]],
  "journey": "The vaccine is delivered by pricking the skin many times with a bifurcated needle. The live virus replicates in the skin, and the lesion contains infectious virus until the scab falls off, so the site is covered and contaminated dressings are handled as infectious waste. The virus usually stays local and is cleared by the immune system within weeks. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion.",
  "sidefx": "Itching, local reaction, swollen nodes, fever and muscle aches come from live virus replicating and the immune response. Because the virus is live and shed from the site, it can spread to other body areas (autoinoculation), especially the eye, or to close contacts. Myocarditis and pericarditis carry a boxed warning. In people with weak cell-mediated immunity the virus can keep growing (progressive vaccinia), and in eczema it can spread widely on the skin (eczema vaccinatum); encephalitis and fetal vaccinia also occur. Vaccinia immune globulin intravenous (VIGIV) is used for certain serious complications, and the antiviral tecovirimat may be used under special access.",
  "fun": "Smallpox is the only human disease ever eradicated worldwide.",
  "src": ["ACAM2000 FDA label", "CDC: Smallpox Vaccine", "Jenner E. An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae, 1798"]
},
"cysteamine-ophthalmic": {
  "origin": "Cystinosis is a rare inherited disease in which the amino acid cystine builds up inside lysosomes and forms crystals in the kidneys, eyes and other organs. In the 1970s researchers, including Jess Thoene and Jerry Schneider, showed that cysteamine, a simple aminothiol, could deplete cystine from cystinotic cells. Oral cysteamine (Cystagon) was approved in 1994 and changed the course of kidney disease. But oral cysteamine does not reach the cornea, which has no blood vessels, so corneal crystals kept growing and caused painful light sensitivity. Eye drops were developed, first through the US National Institutes of Health. The FDA approved Cystaran in 2012 and the more viscous Cystadrops in 2020.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Researchers show that cysteamine depletes cystine from cystinotic cells."], ["1994", "FDA approves oral cysteamine (Cystagon) for cystinosis."], ["2012", "FDA approves Cystaran cysteamine eye drops."], ["2020", "FDA approves Cystadrops, a more viscous cysteamine eye drop."]],
  "cell": "In cystinosis, mutations in the CTNS gene disable cystinosin, the transporter that normally moves cystine out of the lysosome. Cystine is poorly soluble, so it piles up and crystallizes, including in the cornea. Cysteamine enters cells and lysosomes and reacts with cystine in a thiol-disulfide exchange. This splits cystine into cysteine and a cysteine-cysteamine mixed disulfide. The mixed disulfide resembles lysine and leaves the lysosome through a cationic amino acid transporter (PQLC2), and free cysteine leaves through its own transporter. Over months, this lowers crystal load in the corneal cells, which reduces light sensitivity and pain.",
  "body": [["Cornea", "Crystal density falls slowly over months, which eases photophobia, pain and the risk of corneal erosions."], ["Eye surface", "The drops can sting and cause redness or irritation because cysteamine is a reactive thiol."], ["Vision", "Less scattered light from crystals can improve comfort and glare."], ["Rest of body", "Very little reaches the blood, so the drops do not treat kidney or other organ disease; oral cysteamine is still needed."]],
  "journey": "The drops are placed on the eye and act locally in the cornea. Cysteamine is quickly oxidized and loses activity, which is why drops are needed frequently through the day and why unopened bottles are kept frozen or cold. Only tiny amounts are absorbed into the blood. What is not absorbed drains through the tear ducts. There is no meaningful systemic metabolism or excretion to track.",
  "sidefx": "Light sensitivity, redness, eye pain and irritation come from the chemically reactive thiol drug touching the corneal surface, and from the disease itself. Headache may follow frequent dosing and eye strain. Visual field changes have been reported. There is no antidote; the drops are rinsed out or stopped if irritation is severe.",
  "fun": "Cysteamine has a strong sulfur smell, and people taking it by mouth can develop bad breath and body odor; the eye drops avoid that.",
  "src": ["Cystaran FDA label", "Cystadrops FDA label", "Gahl WA et al. N Engl J Med 2002 (cystinosis review)"]
},
"olezarsen": {
  "origin": "Olezarsen came from Ionis Pharmaceuticals in California, a company built around antisense drugs, short strands of modified DNA that bind a specific messenger RNA. Apolipoprotein C-III (apoC-III) was known to slow the clearance of triglyceride-rich particles, and people born with loss-of-function APOC3 variants have low triglycerides. Ionis first made volanesorsen, an apoC-III antisense drug that worked but caused low platelets; it was not approved in the US but was approved in Europe. Olezarsen adds a GalNAc sugar cluster that steers the drug to liver cells, allowing much smaller doses. The FDA approved it as Tryngolza in December 2024 for familial chylomicronemia syndrome, the first approval of the drug.",
  "timeline": [["2008", "Studies of an Amish population link APOC3 loss-of-function variants to low triglycerides."], ["2019", "Volanesorsen, an earlier apoC-III antisense drug, is approved in Europe but not in the US."], ["2024", "FDA approves olezarsen (Tryngolza) for familial chylomicronemia syndrome."]],
  "cell": "Olezarsen is a gapmer antisense oligonucleotide with 2'-O-methoxyethyl (2'-MOE) wings and a central DNA gap. A GalNAc cluster on one end binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, so the drug is taken up mainly by liver cells. Inside, it pairs with APOC3 messenger RNA. The DNA-RNA hybrid is recognized by RNase H1, which cuts the mRNA so apoC-III protein is not made. ApoC-III normally inhibits lipoprotein lipase and blocks the liver from clearing triglyceride-rich lipoproteins through receptors. With less apoC-III, lipoprotein lipase works better and the liver clears chylomicron and VLDL remnants faster, even in people who lack normal lipase pathways. Triglyceride levels fall, and so does the risk of pancreatitis.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make much less apoC-III, which speeds up removal of triglyceride-rich particles."], ["Blood", "Triglycerides drop substantially over weeks of monthly dosing."], ["Pancreas", "Lower chylomicron levels reduce the risk of acute pancreatitis, a major danger of very high triglycerides."], ["Injection site", "Redness, swelling or pain are common reactions to the injected oligonucleotide."], ["Platelets", "Mild drops in platelet count can occur, but much less than with the earlier non-GalNAc drug."]],
  "journey": "Olezarsen is injected under the skin monthly. It is absorbed into the blood within hours, binds plasma proteins strongly and is quickly taken up by the liver through ASGPR. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Instead, nucleases slowly cut it into shorter pieces in tissues, and those pieces leave in the urine. Its tissue half-life is weeks, which allows once-monthly dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from local immune and inflammatory responses to the oligonucleotide. Joint pain has been reported. Platelet counts can fall mildly, a known class effect of 2'-MOE antisense drugs; GalNAc targeting keeps systemic exposure low, which helps limit this. Liver enzyme rises reflect drug concentrated in hepatocytes. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "The GalNAc sugar works like a postal code: the asialoglycoprotein receptor that reads it is found almost only on liver cells.",
  "src": ["Tryngolza FDA label", "Pollin TI et al. Science 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"sutimlimab": {
  "origin": "Cold agglutinin disease is a rare autoimmune anemia in which IgM antibodies stick to red blood cells in cool parts of the body and turn on complement. For years there was no approved drug. Sutimlimab was developed from a humanized antibody against complement C1s, first by True North Therapeutics, which was acquired by Bioverativ; Sanofi then acquired Bioverativ in 2018 and completed development. The FDA approved sutimlimab as Enjaymo in February 2022, the first approved treatment for cold agglutinin disease. It was a proof that selectively blocking the classical complement pathway could stop a disease while leaving the other pathways working.",
  "timeline": [["2018", "Sanofi acquires Bioverativ, the developer of sutimlimab."], ["2022", "FDA approves sutimlimab (Enjaymo) for hemolysis in cold agglutinin disease."]],
  "cell": "In cold agglutinin disease, IgM autoantibodies bind the I antigen on red cells at cool temperatures in the fingers, toes and nose. IgM strongly binds C1q, which activates the C1 complex. C1s, a serine protease, then cleaves C4 and C2 to form the C3 convertase. C3b coats the red cells, and as they warm, IgM falls off but C3b stays. Liver macrophages recognize C3b and remove the cells (extravascular hemolysis); a smaller part proceeds to the membrane attack complex and intravascular hemolysis. Sutimlimab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds C1s and blocks it from cleaving C4. This stops the classical pathway at its start, so red cells are no longer coated with C3b. The lectin and alternative complement pathways keep working.",
  "body": [["Blood", "Hemolysis slows, so hemoglobin rises and transfusion needs fall within weeks."], ["Liver", "Fewer C3b-coated red cells are cleared by Kupffer cells, which is the main way red cells are destroyed in this disease."], ["Energy", "Fatigue and shortness of breath improve as hemoglobin rises and anemia corrects."], ["Immune system", "Classical pathway blockade can make some infections, especially with encapsulated bacteria, harder to clear."], ["Skin and fingers", "Raynaud phenomenon and acrocyanosis can still occur, because the drug does not stop red cells from clumping in the cold."]],
  "journey": "Sutimlimab is given by IV infusion, with loading doses followed by doses every 2 weeks. It stays mostly in the blood and tissue fluid. Like other antibodies, it is broken down into peptides and amino acids by cells throughout the body and is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes. Part of its clearance comes from binding its target, so its half-life is concentration dependent and lasts weeks. Hemolysis returns after the drug is stopped.",
  "sidefx": "Infections, including urinary and respiratory infections, happen because the classical complement pathway helps fight bacteria; vaccination against encapsulated bacteria such as meningococcus and pneumococcus is recommended. Headache, high blood pressure and infusion reactions can occur. Raynaud phenomenon and acrocyanosis persist because agglutination in the cold is a direct IgM effect, not complement. Because the classical pathway helps clear immune complexes, lupus-like autoimmunity is a theoretical and reported concern. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped.",
  "fun": "People with cold agglutinin disease can notice symptoms worsen with cold drinks or winter weather, because the antibodies bind best in the cold.",
  "src": ["Enjaymo FDA label", "Roth A et al. N Engl J Med 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"lumacaftor-ivacaftor": {
  "origin": "In 1989 teams led by Lap-Chee Tsui, John Riordan and Francis Collins identified the CFTR gene, whose defects cause cystic fibrosis. The most common mutation, F508del, makes a misfolded protein that the cell destroys before it reaches the surface. The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation funded drug screening at Aurora Biosciences, later part of Vertex Pharmaceuticals, in a model called venture philanthropy. Screening found ivacaftor, a potentiator approved alone as Kalydeco in 2012 for gating mutations, and lumacaftor, a corrector that helps F508del protein fold. Neither worked well alone for F508del, but together they gave a modest benefit. The FDA approved the combination as Orkambi in July 2015, the first treatment aimed at the basic defect in people with two copies of F508del.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Tsui, Riordan and Collins identify the CFTR gene."], ["2012", "FDA approves ivacaftor (Kalydeco) for CF with the G551D mutation."], ["2015", "FDA approves lumacaftor-ivacaftor (Orkambi) for people homozygous for F508del."]],
  "cell": "CFTR is an ATP-gated chloride and bicarbonate channel in the apical membrane of epithelial cells in the airways, pancreas, gut, sweat glands and reproductive tract. The F508del mutation causes the protein to misfold in the endoplasmic reticulum, where quality control sends most of it to the proteasome. The little that reaches the surface opens poorly. Lumacaftor is a corrector: it binds the first membrane-spanning domain and stabilizes folding so more CFTR moves through the Golgi to the cell surface. Ivacaftor is a potentiator: it binds CFTR at the membrane and increases the time the channel spends open. More channels that open more often move more chloride and bicarbonate out of the cell. Water follows, so airway surface liquid and mucus become thinner and easier to clear, and sweat chloride falls.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Thinner mucus and better clearance give a modest improvement in lung function and fewer pulmonary exacerbations."], ["Airways (early)", "Chest tightness and shortness of breath can occur when starting, especially with low lung function."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride drops within about 2 weeks, a marker that CFTR is working better."], ["Pancreas and gut", "Better chloride and bicarbonate secretion can help gut function, though most patients still need enzyme replacement."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, and advanced liver disease can worsen."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been reported in children, so eye exams are done."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth with fat-containing food, which greatly increases absorption. They peak in about 4 hours and are about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. Lumacaftor is barely metabolized and lasts about 26 hours. Ivacaftor is broken down extensively by CYP3A and lasts about 9 hours. Most leaves in the feces. Lumacaftor is a strong CYP3A inducer, so it lowers ivacaftor levels and those of many other drugs, including hormonal contraceptives, which may become unreliable.",
  "sidefx": "Chest tightness and shortness of breath at the start are thought to reflect changes in airway surface and mucus as CFTR function shifts. Nausea, diarrhea and flatulence relate to effects on gut fluid secretion. Liver enzyme rises and, in people with cirrhosis, liver decompensation reflect drug handling by the liver. Blood pressure can rise. Cataracts in children have been seen with CFTR modulators. Many problems arise from lumacaftor's strong CYP3A induction, which lowers the levels of other drugs. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation's investment in Vertex was later sold for royalty rights worth more than 3 billion US dollars, which it put back into research.",
  "src": ["Orkambi FDA label", "Riordan JR et al. Science 1989", "Wainwright CE et al. N Engl J Med 2015"]
},
"tezacaftor-ivacaftor": {
  "origin": "Tezacaftor was developed by Vertex Pharmaceuticals as a second-generation CFTR corrector after lumacaftor. Lumacaftor helped F508del protein fold, but it caused chest tightness and was a strong CYP3A inducer that weakened ivacaftor and many other drugs. Tezacaftor works at a similar site on CFTR but causes fewer respiratory side effects and fewer drug interactions. In trials, tezacaftor plus ivacaftor improved lung function in people with two copies of F508del and in people with one F508del copy plus a residual function mutation. The FDA approved it as Symdeko in February 2018. Tezacaftor later became part of the triple combination with elexacaftor (Trikafta), which helps many more people.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "The CFTR gene is identified."], ["2018", "FDA approves tezacaftor-ivacaftor (Symdeko)."], ["2019", "Tezacaftor is included in the triple combination elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor."]],
  "cell": "CFTR is an apical chloride and bicarbonate channel in epithelial cells. F508del CFTR misfolds in the endoplasmic reticulum and is degraded, and what reaches the surface opens poorly. Tezacaftor is a type I corrector that binds the first membrane-spanning domain and stabilizes the protein during folding, so more CFTR is trafficked to the cell membrane. Ivacaftor is a potentiator that binds CFTR at the surface and increases its open probability. The extra channels move more chloride and bicarbonate out of the cell. Water follows by osmosis, so airway surface liquid rehydrates, mucus thins and cilia clear it better. For residual function mutations, which already reach the surface but conduct poorly, ivacaftor's potentiation plus extra protein from tezacaftor adds to the benefit.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Better airway hydration improves lung function and lowers the rate of pulmonary exacerbations."], ["Sweat glands", "Sweat chloride falls within about 2 weeks as more CFTR works in the sweat duct."], ["Sinuses", "Sinus congestion is a reported side effect, even as mucus becomes thinner."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, so they are checked during treatment."], ["Eyes", "Cataracts have been reported in children taking CFTR modulators."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are among the more common reported side effects."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are taken by mouth with fat-containing food, which raises absorption. Tezacaftor peaks in about 2 to 4 hours and ivacaftor in about 4 to 6 hours. Both are about 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks them down mainly with CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, and they leave mostly in the feces. Tezacaftor's half-life is about 15 hours, with a long terminal phase from metabolites. Unlike lumacaftor, tezacaftor is not a strong enzyme inducer, but strong CYP3A inhibitors raise levels a lot, so the schedule is changed when they are used.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, sinus congestion and dizziness are the most common effects and are generally mild. Liver enzyme rises reflect metabolism in the liver, and rare severe liver injury has been reported. Cataracts have been seen in children taking CFTR modulators. Compared with lumacaftor, chest tightness and drug interactions from enzyme induction are much less of a problem. CYP3A inducers such as rifampin can drop levels so far that the drug no longer works. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Symdeko is taken as two different tablets: a combination tablet in the morning and plain ivacaftor in the evening.",
  "src": ["Symdeko FDA label", "Taylor-Cousar JL et al. N Engl J Med 2017", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"fluticasone-nasal": {
  "origin": "Fluticasone propionate was developed by Glaxo in the UK in the early 1980s as a topical corticosteroid designed to be very potent where it is applied but almost completely inactivated when swallowed. Its fluorine atoms and 17-carbothioate ester give it strong glucocorticoid receptor binding, and nearly all swallowed drug is destroyed on the first pass through the liver. In the US, Flonase nasal spray was approved in 1994 for allergic rhinitis. It became one of the most prescribed nasal steroids. In 2014 the FDA approved fluticasone propionate nasal spray for over-the-counter sale, and the related fluticasone furoate (Flonase Sensimist) also became available without a prescription.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo develops fluticasone propionate as a topical corticosteroid."], ["1994", "FDA approves Flonase nasal spray."], ["2014", "FDA approves fluticasone propionate nasal spray for over-the-counter use."]],
  "cell": "Fluticasone is a lipophilic glucocorticoid that crosses cell membranes in the nasal lining and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor with very high affinity. The drug-receptor complex moves to the nucleus. There it represses the transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1 (transrepression), turning down genes for cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also turns on anti-inflammatory genes such as annexin-1 and MAP kinase phosphatase-1 (transactivation). Over days, the nose has fewer eosinophils, mast cells and T cells, less vascular leak and less mucus secretion. This explains why it controls congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose, and why the full effect takes days to build rather than minutes.",
  "body": [["Nasal lining", "Fewer inflammatory cells and less swelling reduce congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Eyes", "Less nasal inflammation also lessens allergic eye symptoms through nasal-ocular reflexes."], ["Sinuses and nose", "Nosebleeds, dryness and burning occur because steroids thin the nasal mucosa a little and the spray irritates it."], ["Throat", "Some spray drips back and may cause throat irritation or local yeast infection."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings with long-term corticosteroid use."], ["Growth", "Children may grow slightly more slowly with long-term use, so the lowest effective dose is used."]],
  "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, most of the drug stays on the nasal lining. A small part is absorbed directly, and much is swallowed. The swallowed part is almost all destroyed by CYP3A4 in the gut wall and liver on its first pass, so oral bioavailability is under about 1 percent and blood levels are mostly undetectable. Absorbed drug is converted by CYP3A4 to an inactive 17-beta-carboxylic acid metabolite and leaves mainly in the feces. Effects start within 12 hours to several days and build over 1 to 2 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Nosebleeds, nasal burning and dryness come from the spray's direct contact and from steroids reducing the turnover of nasal tissue; aiming away from the septum helps prevent septal injury. Local immune suppression explains nasal or throat yeast infection. Long-term steroid exposure carries warnings for glaucoma, cataracts and reduced growth in children. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, especially ritonavir, block fluticasone breakdown so much that blood levels rise and can cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression. There is no antidote; the drug is tapered or changed.",
  "fun": "Fluticasone is so thoroughly destroyed on first pass that swallowed drug adds almost nothing to blood levels.",
  "src": ["Flonase FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"budesonide-nasal": {
  "origin": "Budesonide was developed in the 1970s by the Swedish company Draco, part of Astra (now AstraZeneca). Chemists designed it to bind the glucocorticoid receptor strongly while being cleared quickly by the liver, so that drug absorbed into the blood would have less effect on the rest of the body. Unlike fluticasone and beclomethasone, budesonide has no halogen atoms. It was introduced first for asthma and allergic rhinitis in Europe, then as Rhinocort in the US in the 1990s. In 2015 the FDA approved budesonide nasal spray for over-the-counter sale as Rhinocort Allergy. Large pregnancy registries have studied budesonide more than other nasal steroids, which is why it is often chosen in pregnancy.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Draco (Astra) develops budesonide in Sweden."], ["1990s", "Budesonide nasal spray is marketed in the US as Rhinocort."], ["2015", "FDA approves budesonide nasal spray for over-the-counter use."]],
  "cell": "Budesonide enters nasal epithelial and immune cells and binds the glucocorticoid receptor. The complex moves into the nucleus, where it represses pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1, reducing production of cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also increases transcription of anti-inflammatory proteins. Inside cells, budesonide forms reversible fatty acid esters, which act as a local reservoir that slowly releases active drug and supports once-daily dosing. Over days, fewer eosinophils, mast cells and lymphocytes enter the nasal lining, blood vessels leak less, and mucus glands calm down, so congestion, sneezing and runny nose improve.",
  "body": [["Nasal lining", "Allergic inflammation settles over days, easing congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Nose and throat", "Nosebleeds, irritation and sore throat come from direct contact and mild thinning of the mucosa."], ["Pregnancy", "Registry data on budesonide are the most reassuring among nasal steroids, which shapes its common use in pregnancy."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings with long-term corticosteroid exposure."], ["Growth", "Long-term use in children may slightly slow growth."]],
  "journey": "After spraying, most of the drug stays on the nasal lining, and some is swallowed. Budesonide that is swallowed or absorbed is broken down extensively by CYP3A4 in the liver on its first pass, to metabolites with weak activity. Its systemic half-life is about 2 to 3 hours. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Symptom relief may start within about 10 hours to a day, with full effect in about 2 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Nosebleeds, nasal irritation and cough come from the spray hitting the nasal lining and from steroids thinning it slightly over time. Local immune suppression allows Candida infection. Septal perforation is rare and linked to spraying toward the septum. Glaucoma, cataracts and slowed growth in children are class warnings for corticosteroids. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise budesonide levels and add systemic steroid effects. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Budesonide's intracellular fatty acid esters act like a slow-release depot inside the very cells it treats.",
  "src": ["Rhinocort Allergy label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"beclomethasone-nasal": {
  "origin": "Beclomethasone was first made in the 1960s, and its dipropionate ester was one of the first potent corticosteroids that could be given topically to the airways with limited effect on the rest of the body. Glaxo in the UK introduced it as an asthma inhaler in the early 1970s and as the Beconase nasal spray soon after. This showed that steroids could treat airway inflammation locally, changing care for asthma and allergic rhinitis. In the US, Beconase AQ became a long-used aqueous spray. In 2012 the FDA approved Qnasl, a beclomethasone nasal aerosol from Teva that delivers a dry mist instead of a liquid spray.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Beclomethasone dipropionate is developed as a topical corticosteroid."], ["1972", "Glaxo introduces beclomethasone as an asthma inhaler in the UK."], ["2012", "FDA approves Qnasl, a dry beclomethasone nasal aerosol."]],
  "cell": "Beclomethasone dipropionate is a prodrug. Esterases in the nasal lining and lungs remove one propionate group to form beclomethasone 17-monopropionate, which binds the glucocorticoid receptor far more strongly than the parent. The drug-receptor complex enters the nucleus, where it suppresses NF-kappa B and AP-1 driven transcription of inflammatory cytokines, chemokines and adhesion molecules, and switches on anti-inflammatory genes. Over days this lowers the number of eosinophils, mast cells and T cells in the nasal mucosa and reduces vascular leak and mucus production, which relieves congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose.",
  "body": [["Nasal lining", "Allergic inflammation decreases, easing congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose."], ["Nasal polyps", "Beconase AQ is labeled to help prevent nasal polyps from coming back after surgery."], ["Nose", "Nasal discomfort and nosebleeds reflect local irritation and slight thinning of the lining."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings with long-term corticosteroid use."], ["Growth", "Long-term use in children can slightly slow growth."]],
  "journey": "Beclomethasone is sprayed or puffed onto the nasal lining, where esterases convert it to the active 17-monopropionate. Some of the dose is swallowed. The active form is cleared by CYP3A4 in the liver, with a systemic half-life of about 2.7 hours, and leaves mainly in the feces. Effects start in about 1 to 3 days and reach their peak in 1 to 2 weeks. The dry aerosol form leaves less liquid dripping down the throat or out of the nose.",
  "sidefx": "Nasal discomfort, nosebleeds and headache come from direct contact with the nasal mucosa. Ulceration and septal perforation are rare, linked to steroid-related thinning and spraying toward the septum. Local immune suppression allows Candida infection. Glaucoma, cataracts and slowed growth are steroid class warnings. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Beclomethasone helped prove that inhaled and nasal steroids could replace many courses of oral steroids for airway disease.",
  "src": ["Qnasl FDA label", "Beconase AQ FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"ciclesonide-nasal": {
  "origin": "Ciclesonide was discovered by the German company Byk Gulden (later Altana Pharma) as a corticosteroid prodrug that is activated mainly where it is needed. The idea was to send in an inactive molecule that local esterases in the airway convert to the active drug, des-ciclesonide, while the parent compound does little elsewhere. Development partners brought it to the US, where the FDA approved Omnaris nasal spray in 2006 for allergic rhinitis. Zetonna, a dry nasal aerosol form, followed in 2012. Ciclesonide is also sold as the asthma inhaler Alvesco.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Byk Gulden in Germany develops ciclesonide as a corticosteroid prodrug."], ["2006", "FDA approves Omnaris ciclesonide nasal spray."], ["2012", "FDA approves Zetonna, a ciclesonide nasal aerosol."]],
  "cell": "Ciclesonide itself binds the glucocorticoid receptor weakly. Esterases in the nasal epithelium remove its isobutyryl group to form des-ciclesonide, which binds the receptor about 100 times more strongly. Des-ciclesonide also forms reversible fatty acid conjugates inside cells, which hold it in the tissue and support once-daily dosing. Bound to the glucocorticoid receptor, it enters the nucleus and suppresses NF-kappa B and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes while turning on anti-inflammatory genes. This reduces eosinophils, mast cells and cytokines in the nasal lining, which calms congestion, sneezing, itching and runny nose over days. Very high plasma protein binding and rapid liver clearance limit effects on the rest of the body.",
  "body": [["Nasal lining", "Local activation calms allergic inflammation, easing congestion, sneezing and runny nose."], ["Nose", "Nosebleeds and nasal irritation can occur, though the hypotonic spray is designed to feel gentle."], ["Throat", "Nasopharyngitis is a reported side effect."], ["Eyes (long term)", "Glaucoma and cataracts are listed warnings for corticosteroids."], ["Growth", "As with other nasal steroids, long-term use in children can slightly slow growth."]],
  "journey": "After spraying, ciclesonide is converted in the nasal lining by esterases to des-ciclesonide. Very little reaches the blood, and what does is 99 percent or more bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks des-ciclesonide down with CYP3A4, and metabolites leave mainly in the feces. Data from the inhaled form suggest a systemic half-life of about 6 to 7 hours. Effects begin in 24 to 48 hours and peak over 1 to 2 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, nosebleeds and nasopharyngitis come from local irritation and steroid effects on the nasal lining. Ulceration and septal perforation are rare. Candida infection reflects local immune suppression. Glaucoma, cataracts and growth effects are corticosteroid class warnings, though very little ciclesonide reaches the blood. Hypersensitivity reactions are rare. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Ciclesonide is a prodrug that is mostly switched on in the airway itself, a design meant to keep its action local.",
  "src": ["Omnaris FDA label", "Zetonna FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"ipratropium-nasal": {
  "origin": "Ipratropium descends from plant medicines. Atropine comes from deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna) and related plants, and smoking Datura leaves was an old remedy for asthma. Atropine dries secretions but reaches the brain and causes many side effects. In the 1970s Boehringer Ingelheim in Germany made ipratropium by adding an isopropyl group to the nitrogen of an atropine-like molecule. This gave it a permanent positive charge, so it is poorly absorbed through membranes and stays where it is applied. It was first used as the Atrovent inhaler for lung disease, and the nasal spray was approved in the US in the 1990s for runny nose.",
  "timeline": [["1831", "Atropine is isolated from belladonna plants."], ["1970s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops ipratropium bromide."], ["1990s", "FDA approves Atrovent nasal spray for rhinorrhea."]],
  "cell": "Glands in the nasal lining make watery mucus mainly in response to parasympathetic nerves that release acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors. M3 receptors are Gq coupled: they activate phospholipase C, raise IP3 and intracellular calcium, and drive gland secretion. Ipratropium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors (M1, M2 and M3). Blocking M3 on seromucous glands stops this signal, so the glands secrete less fluid. Because it is a quaternary ammonium compound with a permanent charge, it crosses membranes poorly, stays mostly on the nasal surface and barely enters the brain. It reduces runny nose but does little for congestion, sneezing or itching, which are driven by histamine and other mediators.",
  "body": [["Nasal glands", "Less acetylcholine signaling means less watery discharge within about 15 minutes."], ["Nasal lining", "Dryness, irritation and nosebleeds occur because the lining loses some of its normal moisture."], ["Mouth and throat", "Some drug reaches the throat and causes dry mouth or taste change."], ["Eyes", "If sprayed into the eyes, it blocks the pupil and focusing muscles, causing dilated pupils, blurred vision and, rarely, acute angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Bladder", "Rarely, systemic anticholinergic effects can worsen urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."]],
  "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, ipratropium acts on the surface. Because it carries a permanent charge, less than 20 percent of a nasal dose is absorbed into the blood. What is absorbed is partly broken down by ester hydrolysis in the liver and leaves in urine and feces, with a systemic half-life of about 1.6 hours. Its local effect starts in about 15 minutes and lasts about 4 to 8 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Nasal dryness, irritation and nosebleeds come from reduced gland secretion in the nose. Dry mouth and taste changes happen when spray drains to the throat. Eye exposure blocks muscarinic receptors in the iris sphincter and ciliary muscle, causing pupil dilation and blurred vision, and in people with narrow angles this can trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma. Urinary retention is rare because little drug is absorbed. Hypersensitivity can rarely cause angioedema or bronchospasm. There is no specific antidote for the mild effects; physostigmine is reserved for serious anticholinergic toxicity, which is very unlikely from nasal use.",
  "fun": "The name belladonna means beautiful lady; atropine-containing drops were once used to widen pupils for cosmetic effect.",
  "src": ["Atrovent Nasal Spray FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"olopatadine-nasal": {
  "origin": "Olopatadine was discovered by Kyowa Hakko Kogyo in Japan as an antihistamine related to doxepin's tricyclic structure. Alcon licensed it and first developed it as an eye drop, Patanol, approved in the US in 1996, which became one of the most used allergy eye drops. Alcon then developed a nasal spray form, Patanase, which the FDA approved in 2008 for seasonal allergic rhinitis. Olopatadine was later combined with the steroid mometasone in the nasal spray Ryaltris.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves olopatadine eye drops (Patanol)."], ["2008", "FDA approves Patanase olopatadine nasal spray."]],
  "cell": "Histamine released from mast cells activates H1 receptors on nasal nerves, blood vessels and glands. H1 is Gq coupled: it raises intracellular calcium and turns on NF-kappa B. On sensory nerves this triggers itching and sneezing; on blood vessels it increases permeability and dilation, causing swelling and congestion; on glands it adds to secretion. Olopatadine is a selective H1 receptor inverse agonist that stabilizes the receptor in its inactive state, so these signals are blocked. It also stabilizes mast cells in lab studies, reducing release of histamine and other mediators. Applied in the nose, it acts within about 30 minutes because it does not need to change gene expression like a steroid.",
  "body": [["Nasal nerves", "Blocking H1 receptors on sensory nerves quickly reduces itching and sneezing."], ["Nasal blood vessels", "Less histamine-driven leak and dilation eases runny nose and some congestion."], ["Mouth", "A bitter taste is common because some spray drains to the back of the throat and tongue."], ["Nose", "Nosebleeds and, rarely, ulceration or septal perforation can occur from local irritation."], ["Brain", "Some people feel drowsy, though less than with older oral antihistamines."]],
  "journey": "Sprayed into the nose, olopatadine acts locally, but a fair amount is absorbed and swallowed, with blood levels peaking in 30 minutes to an hour. It is about 55 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes very little of it, and most leaves unchanged in the urine. Its systemic half-life is about 8 to 12 hours, which supports twice-daily dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Bitter taste comes from the drug draining onto taste buds. Headache, nosebleeds and throat pain reflect local effects on the nasal and throat lining. Somnolence happens because some drug reaches the blood and a little crosses into the brain, where H1 receptors maintain wakefulness. Nasal ulceration and septal perforation are rare. Hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Olopatadine first became popular as an eye drop before it was turned into a nasal spray.",
  "src": ["Patanase FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"phenylephrine-oral": {
  "origin": "Phenylephrine is a synthetic relative of epinephrine, made and patented in the 1930s. It became widely used as a nasal spray, eye drop and IV vasopressor. As an oral decongestant, it was reviewed and accepted in the FDA's OTC drug review of cold products that began in the 1970s. Its big moment came after the Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act of 2005 moved pseudoephedrine behind the pharmacy counter, because pseudoephedrine can be used to make methamphetamine. Many companies reformulated products with oral phenylephrine. However, studies found that oral phenylephrine works poorly because the gut and liver destroy most of it. In 2023 an FDA advisory committee concluded that oral phenylephrine is not effective as a decongestant, and in 2024 the FDA proposed removing it from the OTC monograph.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "Phenylephrine is synthesized and patented."], ["1970s", "Oral phenylephrine is accepted in the FDA OTC review of cold products."], ["2006", "Pseudoephedrine moves behind the counter, and many products switch to oral phenylephrine."], ["2023", "An FDA advisory committee concludes oral phenylephrine is not effective as a decongestant."], ["2024", "FDA proposes removing oral phenylephrine from the OTC monograph."]],
  "cell": "Phenylephrine is a direct-acting, selective alpha-1 adrenergic agonist. Alpha-1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq coupled: binding activates phospholipase C, raises IP3 and intracellular calcium, and causes the muscle to contract. In the nose, constricting the large venous sinusoids in the turbinates reduces blood pooling and swelling, which opens the airway. Phenylephrine has little action on beta receptors and does not release norepinephrine from nerves. When given by mouth, most of the drug is inactivated by MAO-A and sulfation in the gut wall before it reaches the blood, which is the main reason its decongestant effect is weak in clinical studies.",
  "body": [["Nasal blood vessels", "Alpha-1 activation can shrink swollen nasal tissue, though by mouth this effect is small in studies."], ["Blood vessels", "Constriction of vessels elsewhere can raise blood pressure, which matters in hypertension."], ["Heart", "Higher blood pressure can trigger a reflex slowing of the heart rate; palpitations can still occur."], ["Brain", "Nervousness, restlessness, insomnia and headache are reported."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 receptors in the bladder neck and prostate contract, which can cause urinary retention."]],
  "journey": "Swallowed phenylephrine is absorbed but then heavily broken down in the gut wall and liver by MAO-A and sulfate conjugation. Only a small fraction, often cited as under about 40 percent and possibly far less, reaches the circulation as active drug. Its effect, if any, starts in 15 to 30 minutes and lasts about 4 hours, with a half-life of about 2 to 3 hours. Inactive metabolites leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Nervousness, restlessness and insomnia reflect sympathetic stimulation. Raised blood pressure and palpitations come from alpha-1 constriction of blood vessels throughout the body. Urinary retention happens because alpha-1 receptors tighten the bladder outlet. With MAO inhibitors, phenylephrine is not broken down normally, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. A dangerous blood pressure rise is treated with an alpha blocker such as phentolamine.",
  "fun": "Phenylephrine works well as a nasal spray, where it acts directly on the nasal lining; the debate is about the pill.",
  "src": ["FDA Nonprescription Drugs Advisory Committee, September 2023", "FDA proposed order on oral phenylephrine, 2024", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"epinephrine-auto-injector": {
  "origin": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the hormone of the adrenal medulla. In 1901 Jokichi Takamine and his assistant Keizo Uenaka isolated it in pure form, and it was sold as Adrenalin by Parke-Davis. It soon became a treatment for asthma attacks and allergic reactions. The auto-injector came from military technology: Sheldon Kaplan developed a spring-loaded injector to deliver nerve agent antidotes to soldiers. That design was adapted for epinephrine, and the EpiPen was approved by the FDA in 1987, letting patients and families treat anaphylaxis in the moments before help arrives. Later devices include Auvi-Q, which talks users through the steps, and generic auto-injectors.",
  "timeline": [["1901", "Jokichi Takamine isolates epinephrine, sold as Adrenalin."], ["1970s", "Sheldon Kaplan develops a spring-loaded auto-injector for the military."], ["1987", "FDA approves the EpiPen."], ["2012", "FDA approves Auvi-Q, a voice-guided epinephrine auto-injector."]],
  "cell": "Epinephrine activates alpha-1, alpha-2, beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors. Alpha-1 receptors (Gq) on blood vessels raise intracellular calcium and constrict vessels, which raises blood pressure and reduces swelling of the airway and skin. Beta-1 receptors (Gs) on the heart increase cAMP, which raises heart rate and contraction force. Beta-2 receptors (Gs) on bronchial smooth muscle raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A, which relaxes the airways. Beta-2 signaling on mast cells and basophils also raises cAMP and reduces further release of histamine, tryptase and leukotrienes. This combination reverses the core problems of anaphylaxis: vasodilation and leaky vessels, airway swelling and bronchospasm, and continued mediator release.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 constriction raises blood pressure and reduces fluid leak, swelling of the lips, tongue and throat, and hives."], ["Airways", "Beta-2 relaxation opens narrowed bronchi and eases wheeze."], ["Heart", "Beta-1 stimulation increases heart rate and output, which supports blood pressure but can cause palpitations."], ["Mast cells", "Higher cAMP in mast cells slows further release of allergy chemicals."], ["Metabolism", "Epinephrine raises blood sugar and can lower potassium as it drives potassium into cells."], ["Brain and muscles", "Anxiety, tremor and headache come from widespread adrenergic stimulation."]],
  "journey": "The auto-injector pushes epinephrine into the thigh muscle, which has rich blood flow, so it is absorbed within minutes and peaks in about 5 to 10 minutes. Injecting into the thigh gives faster peaks than under the skin of the arm. In the blood, epinephrine is cleared in minutes by uptake into nerves and by the enzymes COMT and MAO in the liver and other tissues. Metabolites such as metanephrine and vanillylmandelic acid leave in the urine. Because the effect lasts only about 10 to 20 minutes, symptoms can return, which is why emergency care follows use.",
  "sidefx": "Anxiety, tremor, palpitations, pallor and headache come from broad adrenergic stimulation. Strong beta-1 effects raise heart oxygen demand, so arrhythmias and angina can occur, especially in people with heart disease. Very high levels, mostly from accidental IV injection or overdose, can cause severe hypertension, pulmonary edema, ventricular arrhythmias or brain bleeding. Injecting into a finger can cause intense local vessel constriction; this is sometimes treated with phentolamine. In anaphylaxis, no condition is considered an absolute reason to withhold epinephrine, per anaphylaxis guidelines.",
  "fun": "The British name adrenaline and the American name epinephrine both mean 'on the kidney', from Latin and Greek.",
  "src": ["EpiPen FDA label", "Takamine J. Adrenalin, the active principle of the suprarenal glands, 1901", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"sublingual-grass-pollen-extract": {
  "origin": "In 1873 the English physician Charles Blackley showed that grass pollen causes hay fever, partly by testing pollen on himself. In 1911 Leonard Noon and John Freeman at St Mary's Hospital in London reported that injecting small, rising amounts of grass pollen extract could protect against hay fever, the start of allergen immunotherapy. For a century this meant regular allergy shots. Researchers in Europe then found that holding allergen under the tongue could build tolerance with less risk. The French company Stallergenes developed Oralair, a tablet with standardized pollen from five grasses (sweet vernal, orchard, perennial rye, Timothy and Kentucky blue). The FDA approved Oralair in April 2014, the same month it approved ALK's Timothy grass tablet Grastek, bringing sublingual tablet immunotherapy to the US.",
  "timeline": [["1873", "Charles Blackley shows that grass pollen causes hay fever."], ["1911", "Noon and Freeman report the first pollen immunotherapy injections."], ["1980s", "Sublingual immunotherapy is studied in Europe as an alternative to shots."], ["2014", "FDA approves Oralair and Grastek sublingual grass pollen tablets."]],
  "cell": "In grass allergy, Th2 cells drive B cells to make IgE against grass pollen proteins, such as group 1 and group 5 allergens. IgE sits on mast cells and basophils, so pollen exposure cross-links it and releases histamine and other mediators. With daily sublingual exposure, allergen is taken up by oral dendritic cells (including Langerhans-type cells) that tend to promote tolerance. They carry it to nearby lymph nodes and favor regulatory T cells that make IL-10 and TGF-beta. Over months the response shifts away from Th2: specific IgG4 and IgA antibodies rise and act as blocking antibodies that catch pollen before it reaches IgE on mast cells, and mast cell and basophil reactivity falls. Clinical benefit grows over the first season and can persist after several years of treatment.",
  "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Itching, irritation and swelling under the tongue are common because the allergen meets mast cells right where the tablet dissolves."], ["Nose", "Sneezing, runny nose and congestion during grass season decrease as tolerance builds."], ["Eyes", "Allergic eye symptoms during grass season also improve."], ["Immune system", "Regulatory T cells and blocking IgG4 increase, which turns down the allergic response over months to years."], ["Esophagus", "Rarely, ongoing allergen exposure triggers eosinophilic esophagitis, with trouble swallowing or chest pain."], ["Airways", "Throat swelling and asthma flares can occur, so severe or unstable asthma is a contraindication."]],
  "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue, and the allergen proteins contact the oral mucosa, where immune cells sample them. The rest is swallowed and digested like food protein. The proteins are not absorbed and metabolized like a drug; their effect comes from training the immune system. Treatment begins about 4 months before grass season and continues through it, and benefit builds over weeks to months.",
  "sidefx": "Oral itching, throat irritation, mouth and tongue swelling and ear itching come from the allergen activating IgE-coated mast cells in the mouth. These usually fade over the first weeks. More serious reactions, including laryngopharyngeal swelling, asthma flares and anaphylaxis, happen when the allergic response is stronger or the allergen spreads. The label carries a boxed warning for severe allergic reactions, and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed for use if one occurs. The first dose is observed in a clinic. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects a chronic allergic response in the esophagus.",
  "fun": "Noon and Freeman's 1911 paper was published in The Lancet, and their method remains the basis for allergy shots.",
  "src": ["Oralair FDA label", "Noon L. Lancet 1911", "Middleton's Allergy"]
},
"dust-mite-allergen-extract": {
  "origin": "People long knew that house dust triggered asthma and rhinitis, but not why. In 1964 Dutch researchers Reindert Voorhorst and Frits Spieksma showed that tiny mites living in house dust, Dermatophagoides pteronyssinus and related species, were the main source of the allergen. Mite proteins come mostly from their fecal pellets. Standardized mite extracts were then used for allergy shots. The Danish company ALK-Abello developed a sublingual tablet with extract of both D. farinae and D. pteronyssinus, standardized in SQ-HDM units. The FDA approved it as Odactra in March 2017, the first sublingual tablet for house dust mite allergy in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1964", "Voorhorst and Spieksma identify house dust mites as the main house dust allergen."], ["2017", "FDA approves Odactra, a house dust mite sublingual tablet."]],
  "cell": "House dust mite allergens include group 1 proteins such as Der p 1 and Der f 1, which are cysteine proteases, and group 2 proteins such as Der p 2. The proteases can loosen tight junctions between airway cells, and Der p 2 resembles a partner of TLR4, which helps explain why mites are such strong allergens. In mite allergy, Th2 cells drive IgE that arms mast cells. Daily sublingual dosing exposes oral dendritic cells, which favor tolerance, to these proteins. This promotes regulatory T cells that make IL-10 and TGF-beta, shifts the T-cell balance away from Th2, raises blocking IgG4 antibodies and lowers mast cell and basophil reactivity. Over months, mite exposure at home causes less nasal and eye inflammation.",
  "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Itching, irritation and swelling of the mouth and tongue occur because the tablet's allergen activates local mast cells."], ["Nose", "Year-round congestion, sneezing and runny nose from mites decrease over months of treatment."], ["Eyes", "Allergic conjunctivitis from mites can also improve."], ["Immune system", "Regulatory T cells and blocking antibodies grow, damping the allergic response."], ["Esophagus", "Rarely, eosinophilic esophagitis develops."], ["Airways", "Asthma flares or throat swelling can occur, so severe or unstable asthma is a contraindication."]],
  "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue within seconds. The allergen proteins contact the oral mucosa, where immune cells take them up; the rest is swallowed and digested as protein. Nothing is metabolized like a conventional drug. Benefit builds over several months of daily dosing and continues with ongoing treatment, which is usually planned over years.",
  "sidefx": "Throat irritation, oral and ear itching, swelling of the mouth and tongue, and stomach upset come from allergen meeting IgE-armed mast cells in the mouth and gut. These are most common early. Serious reactions such as laryngopharyngeal edema, asthma exacerbation and anaphylaxis are why the label has a boxed warning, the first dose is observed in a clinic and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects a chronic allergic process in the esophagus.",
  "fun": "Dust mites do not drink water; they absorb moisture from the air, which is why they thrive in humid bedrooms.",
  "src": ["Odactra FDA label", "Voorhorst R, Spieksma FTM et al. 1964", "Middleton's Allergy"]
},
"grass-pollen-allergen-extract": {
  "origin": "Timothy grass (Phleum pratense) is one of the most common causes of hay fever in North America and Europe, and its allergens cross-react with those of many other northern grasses. Building on a century of pollen immunotherapy that began with Leonard Noon and John Freeman's 1911 injection work in London, the Danish company ALK-Abello developed a sublingual tablet with standardized Timothy grass pollen extract. It was approved in Europe as Grazax in 2006. In the US it was approved by the FDA in April 2014 as Grastek, initially marketed with Merck, for grass pollen allergic rhinitis in children and adults.",
  "timeline": [["1911", "Noon and Freeman report grass pollen immunotherapy injections in London."], ["2006", "ALK's Timothy grass tablet is approved in Europe as Grazax."], ["2014", "FDA approves Grastek, the Timothy grass sublingual tablet."]],
  "cell": "The tablet delivers Timothy grass allergens, especially Phl p 1 and Phl p 5. Because grasses of the Pooideae subfamily share similar proteins, tolerance to Timothy grass carries over to related grasses. In the mouth, dendritic cells take up the allergen in a tolerance-favoring setting and present it to T cells in nearby lymph nodes. Over months this builds regulatory T cells that release IL-10 and TGF-beta, reduces the Th2 cytokines IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, and drives B cells to make allergen-specific IgG4. IgG4 binds pollen proteins before they reach IgE on mast cells and also blocks IgE-facilitated allergen presentation. The net result is fewer symptoms when grass pollen is inhaled.",
  "body": [["Mouth", "Mouth and lip itching or swelling are common as the allergen meets IgE-armed mast cells under the tongue."], ["Ears and throat", "Ear itching and throat irritation reflect shared sensory nerves and nearby mast cells."], ["Nose and eyes", "Seasonal sneezing, runny nose, congestion and itchy eyes decrease as tolerance builds."], ["Immune system", "Regulatory T cells and IgG4 rise, shifting the response away from allergy."], ["Esophagus", "Eosinophilic esophagitis is a rare complication of daily allergen exposure."]],
  "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue, and the proteins stay in contact with the oral mucosa for about a minute before swallowing. The swallowed portion is digested as protein. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion. Treatment starts at least 12 weeks before grass season, and multi-year courses give benefit that can last after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Mouth itching, throat irritation, ear itching and tongue or lip swelling happen because the allergen is placed directly where mast cells sit. Systemic allergic reactions and anaphylaxis are possible because the patient is allergic to the very material in the tablet. The label has a boxed warning, the first dose is given under observation, and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects ongoing allergic inflammation in the esophagus.",
  "fun": "Timothy grass is reportedly named after Timothy Hanson, who promoted it as a hay crop in the American colonies in the 1700s.",
  "src": ["Grastek FDA label", "Noon L. Lancet 1911", "Middleton's Allergy"]
},
"ragweed-allergen-extract": {
  "origin": "Short ragweed (Ambrosia artemisiifolia) is the main cause of late summer and fall hay fever in North America. A single plant can release enormous amounts of light pollen that travels far on the wind. In the 1960s researchers at Johns Hopkins, including Te Piao King and Philip Norman, purified the major ragweed allergen, then called antigen E and now known as Amb a 1, and used it to standardize extracts and study immunotherapy. Standardized ragweed extracts were used for allergy shots for decades. ALK-Abello developed a sublingual tablet standardized to Amb a 1 content, which the FDA approved as Ragwitek in April 2014.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Johns Hopkins researchers purify the major ragweed allergen Amb a 1 (antigen E)."], ["2014", "FDA approves Ragwitek, a short ragweed sublingual tablet."]],
  "cell": "Amb a 1 is a pectate lyase protein in ragweed pollen. In ragweed allergy, Th2 cells drive IgE against it, which arms mast cells in the nose and eyes. Each daily tablet places Amb a 1 under the tongue, where oral dendritic cells take it up in a setting that favors tolerance. They present it in nearby lymph nodes to T cells, promoting regulatory T cells that secrete IL-10 and TGF-beta. Over time Th2 activity falls, B cells make allergen-specific IgG4 that blocks pollen from cross-linking IgE, and mast cells and basophils become less reactive. This reduces sneezing, congestion and itchy eyes during ragweed season.",
  "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Throat irritation and mouth itching or swelling are common as the allergen meets local mast cells."], ["Ears", "Ear itching is common and likely relates to shared sensory nerves in the throat."], ["Nose and eyes", "Fall hay fever symptoms decrease once tolerance builds."], ["Immune system", "Blocking IgG4 and regulatory T cells increase over the treatment period."], ["Esophagus", "Rarely, eosinophilic esophagitis develops from daily allergen exposure."]],
  "journey": "The tablet dissolves under the tongue and the allergen contacts the oral mucosa for about a minute before swallowing. The swallowed part is digested as protein. There is no drug-style metabolism or excretion. Treatment starts at least 12 weeks before ragweed season and continues through it.",
  "sidefx": "Throat irritation, mouth and ear itching and oral swelling are local allergic reactions to the tablet's allergen. Systemic reactions and anaphylaxis can occur because the person is allergic to the material being given. The label has a boxed warning for severe allergic reactions, the first dose is observed in a clinic and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects chronic allergic inflammation in the esophagus.",
  "fun": "The genus name Ambrosia, the food of the Greek gods, is an odd fit for a plant that causes so much misery.",
  "src": ["Ragwitek FDA label", "King TP, Norman PS. Biochemistry 1962", "Middleton's Allergy"]
},
"peanut-allergen-powder": {
  "origin": "Peanut allergy rose sharply in Western countries in the late 20th century and rarely goes away. Researchers studied oral immunotherapy, giving tiny, gradually increasing amounts of peanut protein to build tolerance, but home-made doses were hard to standardize. Aimmune Therapeutics, founded in 2011, developed a pharmaceutical-grade peanut powder with tightly controlled amounts of key peanut proteins. In January 2020 the FDA approved it as Palforzia, the first FDA-approved treatment for any food allergy, for children 4 through 17. The approval was later extended to children as young as 1. A separate line of research, the 2015 LEAP study, showed that early peanut introduction in infancy can prevent peanut allergy from developing.",
  "timeline": [["2015", "The LEAP study shows early peanut introduction prevents many cases of peanut allergy."], ["2020", "FDA approves Palforzia, the first FDA-approved treatment for a food allergy."], ["2024", "FDA extends Palforzia to children starting at age 1."]],
  "cell": "In peanut allergy, B cells make IgE against peanut proteins, mainly Ara h 1, Ara h 2 and Ara h 3, and that IgE sits on mast cells and basophils. Even small exposures cross-link IgE and trigger histamine, tryptase and leukotriene release, which can cause anaphylaxis. Oral immunotherapy starts below the reaction threshold and slowly increases. Repeated exposure is thought to make mast cells and basophils less responsive (desensitization), and over longer periods to promote regulatory T cells, lower Th2 signals and raise peanut-specific IgG4, which binds peanut proteins before they reach IgE. The goal is to raise the amount of peanut that causes a reaction, so accidental exposure is less dangerous. It is not a cure, and protection fades if daily dosing stops.",
  "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Itching and throat irritation are common because peanut protein contacts mast cells as it is eaten."], ["Stomach and gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are common, especially during up-dosing, as gut mast cells react."], ["Skin", "Hives and itching are signs of a mild allergic reaction to the dose."], ["Airways", "Cough and wheezing can occur, and asthma must be well controlled."], ["Esophagus", "Eosinophilic esophagitis can develop with daily allergen exposure."], ["Immune system", "Tolerance to peanut builds over months, raising the amount needed to cause a reaction."]],
  "journey": "The powder is mixed into soft food and eaten. Peanut proteins contact the mouth and gut lining, where immune cells sample them, and are then digested like any food protein. Nothing is metabolized like a drug. Desensitization builds over about 6 months of careful up-dosing, and daily maintenance is needed to keep it; missed days lower tolerance.",
  "sidefx": "Because the product is peanut protein given to people with peanut allergy, allergic reactions are expected. Mouth and throat itching, abdominal pain, vomiting, hives and cough come from IgE-armed mast cells reacting to the dose. Anaphylaxis can occur, which is why the product is available only through a REMS program, dose increases happen in a clinic and an epinephrine auto-injector is prescribed. Exercise, hot showers, illness and missed doses can lower the reaction threshold. Eosinophilic esophagitis reflects chronic allergic inflammation in the esophagus.",
  "fun": "Palforzia's powder is measured in peanut protein, and the full maintenance amount is roughly equal to one peanut.",
  "src": ["Palforzia FDA label", "Du Toit G et al. N Engl J Med 2015 (LEAP)", "PALISADE Group. N Engl J Med 2018"]
},
"dexchlorpheniramine": {
  "origin": "Chlorpheniramine is an alkylamine antihistamine developed by Schering Corporation in the late 1940s, during the first wave of antihistamines that followed Daniel Bovet's antihistamine research in France in the 1930s and 1940s. Chlorpheniramine is a racemic mixture of two mirror-image forms. Researchers found that nearly all the H1-blocking activity comes from the dextrorotatory (S) form. Separating this form gave dexchlorpheniramine, which is about twice as potent as racemic chlorpheniramine, so less drug is needed for the same effect. It was marketed as Polaramine and is now available as Ryclora oral solution and in some prescription cough and cold liquids.",
  "timeline": [["1937", "Daniel Bovet and colleagues describe the first antihistamine compounds in France."], ["1940s", "Schering develops chlorpheniramine."], ["1957", "Bovet receives the Nobel Prize, partly for antihistamine discovery."]],
  "cell": "Histamine from mast cells activates H1 receptors, which are Gq coupled. This activates phospholipase C, raises IP3 and calcium and turns on NF-kappa B. The result is itching and sneezing through sensory nerves, leaky and dilated vessels causing hives and nasal swelling, and gland secretion. Dexchlorpheniramine is an H1 inverse agonist that binds the receptor and holds it in its inactive state, blocking these effects. As a first-generation antihistamine, it is lipophilic and crosses the blood-brain barrier, where it blocks H1 receptors that maintain wakefulness, causing drowsiness. It also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors to some degree, which dries secretions and explains dry mouth and urinary retention.",
  "body": [["Nose and eyes", "Blocking H1 receptors reduces sneezing, runny nose and itchy, watery eyes."], ["Skin", "Hives and itching decrease because histamine can no longer drive vessel leak and nerve firing."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness and dizziness are common because it enters the brain; children and some others may become agitated instead."], ["Mouth and airways", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth and thicker secretions."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade can cause urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."], ["Anaphylaxis", "It is an add-on after epinephrine, treating itching and hives but not airway or blood pressure problems."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth, dexchlorpheniramine is well absorbed and starts working within 15 to 60 minutes, peaking in about 2 to 3 hours. It is about 70 percent protein bound and spreads widely, including into the brain. The liver breaks it down by N-demethylation, partly via CYP2D6, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is long, about 20 to 30 hours, even though doses are given every 4 to 6 hours for symptom control.",
  "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and impaired thinking come from blocking H1 receptors in the brain that normally keep people alert. Dry mouth, thick secretions, constipation and urinary retention come from muscarinic blockade. Older adults are more likely to become confused because of these anticholinergic effects. Young children can show paradoxical excitation. In overdose, anticholinergic toxicity and sodium channel effects can cause seizures and arrhythmias. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, and physostigmine is sometimes used for severe anticholinergic delirium.",
  "fun": "Dexchlorpheniramine is an example of a chiral switch: selling only the active mirror-image form of an older drug.",
  "src": ["Ryclora prescribing information", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1957"]
},
"cetirizine-pseudoephedrine": {
  "origin": "This tablet combines two drugs with very different histories. Pseudoephedrine comes from the Ephedra plant (ma huang), used in Chinese medicine for thousands of years. Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi isolated ephedrine from it in 1885, and pseudoephedrine, a stereoisomer, was described soon after. Cetirizine was developed by UCB in Belgium as the active carboxylic acid metabolite of the older antihistamine hydroxyzine, giving strong H1 blockade with less drowsiness. Zyrtec was approved in the US in 1995 and marketed by Pfizer. The combination Zyrtec-D was approved in 2001 and moved to over-the-counter sale in the late 2000s, behind the pharmacy counter because of 2006 federal limits on pseudoephedrine sales.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ephedra (ma huang) is used in traditional Chinese medicine."], ["1885", "Nagai Nagayoshi isolates ephedrine from Ephedra."], ["1995", "FDA approves cetirizine (Zyrtec)."], ["2001", "FDA approves Zyrtec-D, cetirizine with pseudoephedrine."], ["2006", "The Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act moves pseudoephedrine behind the counter."]],
  "cell": "Cetirizine is a selective peripheral H1 receptor inverse agonist. It holds the Gq-coupled H1 receptor in its inactive state, so histamine cannot trigger itching, sneezing, vessel leak or gland secretion. Its carboxyl group makes it zwitterionic and a P-glycoprotein substrate, which limits brain entry, though it is somewhat more sedating than loratadine or fexofenadine. Pseudoephedrine is a sympathomimetic with mixed action: it mainly enters nerve terminals and causes release of norepinephrine, and it also has some direct agonist activity. Norepinephrine acts on alpha-1 receptors on the venous sinusoids of the nasal turbinates, contracting them so swollen tissue shrinks and the nasal airway opens. Cetirizine handles histamine symptoms and pseudoephedrine handles congestion, which antihistamines alone treat poorly.",
  "body": [["Nose", "Cetirizine reduces sneezing and runny nose, and pseudoephedrine shrinks swollen nasal vessels to relieve congestion."], ["Eyes and skin", "H1 blockade reduces itchy, watery eyes and itching."], ["Brain", "Pseudoephedrine stimulates the brain, causing insomnia or nervousness, while cetirizine causes mild drowsiness in some people."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Released norepinephrine can raise blood pressure and heart rate and cause palpitations."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation tightens the bladder outlet, which can cause urinary retention with prostate enlargement."]],
  "journey": "The extended-release tablet releases cetirizine quickly, peaking in about an hour, while pseudoephedrine is released slowly and peaks in about 4 to 6 hours. Both are minimally metabolized and leave mostly unchanged in the urine. Cetirizine is about 93 percent protein bound with a half-life of about 8 hours. Pseudoephedrine's half-life is about 5 to 8 hours and changes with urine pH: acidic urine speeds its elimination and alkaline urine slows it. Kidney impairment raises levels of both.",
  "sidefx": "Insomnia, nervousness and headache come from pseudoephedrine stimulating the central nervous system. High blood pressure and palpitations come from norepinephrine release acting on alpha and beta receptors. Urinary retention reflects alpha-1 tightening of the bladder neck. Drowsiness and dry mouth come from cetirizine's H1 blockade. With MAO inhibitors, released norepinephrine is not broken down, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. Overdose can cause arrhythmias. There is no specific antidote; severe hypertension is treated with drugs such as phentolamine.",
  "fun": "Pseudoephedrine sales are limited by law because it can be chemically converted to methamphetamine.",
  "src": ["Zyrtec-D label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act of 2005"]
},
"loratadine-pseudoephedrine": {
  "origin": "Loratadine was developed by Schering-Plough as a second-generation antihistamine that does not readily enter the brain, so it causes little drowsiness. The FDA approved Claritin in 1993, and it became one of the best-selling allergy drugs. A combination with the decongestant pseudoephedrine, Claritin-D, followed in 12-hour and 24-hour forms. In 2002 loratadine and Claritin-D became available over the counter. Pseudoephedrine itself traces back to the Ephedra plant used in Chinese medicine; after the 2006 Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act, Claritin-D moved behind the pharmacy counter with ID and purchase limits.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Ephedra (ma huang), the source of ephedrine alkaloids, is used in Chinese medicine."], ["1993", "FDA approves loratadine (Claritin)."], ["2002", "Loratadine and Claritin-D become available over the counter."], ["2006", "Federal law moves pseudoephedrine products behind the counter."]],
  "cell": "Loratadine is a peripheral H1 receptor inverse agonist and a prodrug in part: the liver converts it to desloratadine, which is even more potent and lasts longer. Holding the Gq-coupled H1 receptor in its inactive state prevents histamine from causing itching, sneezing, vessel leak and secretions. Loratadine and desloratadine are P-glycoprotein substrates that are pumped out of the brain, so drowsiness is uncommon at labeled doses. Pseudoephedrine enters sympathetic nerve terminals and releases norepinephrine, with some direct receptor action. Norepinephrine activates alpha-1 receptors on nasal venous sinusoids, which constrict and shrink the swollen nasal lining. Together the two drugs treat both histamine symptoms and nasal congestion.",
  "body": [["Nose", "Loratadine reduces sneezing and runny nose; pseudoephedrine relieves congestion by shrinking nasal vessels."], ["Eyes and skin", "H1 blockade eases itchy, watery eyes and itching."], ["Brain", "Pseudoephedrine can cause insomnia and nervousness; loratadine rarely causes drowsiness."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation can raise blood pressure and heart rate."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation of the bladder outlet can cause urinary retention."]],
  "journey": "Loratadine is absorbed quickly, peaking in about 1 to 1.5 hours, and is extensively metabolized by CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to desloratadine, which has a half-life of about 27 hours. It is highly protein bound, and metabolites leave in urine and feces. Pseudoephedrine is released from the extended-release tablet over hours, peaking around 4 to 6 hours. It is barely metabolized and leaves in the urine, with a half-life of about 5 to 8 hours that depends on urine pH.",
  "sidefx": "Insomnia, nervousness and headache come from pseudoephedrine's stimulation of the central nervous system. Raised blood pressure and palpitations come from norepinephrine release. Urinary retention reflects alpha-1 effects on the bladder neck. Dry mouth may come from both drugs. With MAO inhibitors, norepinephrine accumulates and can cause a hypertensive crisis. Overdose can cause arrhythmias. There is no antidote; severe hypertension is treated with drugs such as phentolamine.",
  "fun": "Claritin's switch to over-the-counter status in 2002 was unusual because an insurance company had petitioned the FDA to make it nonprescription.",
  "src": ["Claritin-D label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"fexofenadine-pseudoephedrine": {
  "origin": "Fexofenadine came from a safety problem. Terfenadine (Seldane), one of the first nonsedating antihistamines, could cause the dangerous heart rhythm torsades de pointes when its breakdown by CYP3A4 was blocked by drugs such as ketoconazole or erythromycin. Researchers found that terfenadine's active metabolite, fexofenadine, gave the antihistamine benefit without blocking cardiac hERG potassium channels. Hoechst Marion Roussel developed fexofenadine, approved in the US in 1996 as Allegra, and terfenadine was withdrawn from the US market in the late 1990s. The combination with pseudoephedrine, Allegra-D, followed. Fexofenadine went over the counter in 2011, and Allegra-D is sold behind the pharmacy counter because of federal pseudoephedrine limits.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "FDA approves fexofenadine (Allegra)."], ["1998", "Terfenadine is removed from the US market after fexofenadine becomes available."], ["2011", "Fexofenadine and Allegra-D become available over the counter."]],
  "cell": "Fexofenadine is a peripheral H1 receptor inverse agonist. By holding the Gq-coupled H1 receptor inactive, it blocks histamine-driven itching, sneezing, vascular leak and secretion. It is a zwitterion and a P-glycoprotein substrate, so it is pumped out of the brain and causes essentially no sedation. Unlike its parent terfenadine, it does not block hERG potassium channels in the heart. Pseudoephedrine mainly releases norepinephrine from sympathetic nerve endings and has some direct agonist action. Norepinephrine contracts alpha-1 receptors on the venous sinusoids of the nasal turbinates, shrinking the swollen lining and opening the airway. The pairing covers histamine symptoms plus congestion.",
  "body": [["Nose", "Fexofenadine reduces sneezing and runny nose; pseudoephedrine relieves stuffiness by shrinking nasal blood vessels."], ["Eyes and skin", "H1 blockade eases itchy eyes and itching."], ["Brain", "Fexofenadine does not cause drowsiness, but pseudoephedrine can cause insomnia and nervousness."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Pseudoephedrine can raise blood pressure and heart rate; fexofenadine itself does not affect the QT interval."], ["Bladder", "Alpha-1 stimulation can cause urinary retention, especially with prostate enlargement."]],
  "journey": "Fexofenadine is absorbed by transporters in the gut, including OATP1A2, and peaks in about 2 hours. Fruit juices such as grapefruit, orange and apple inhibit these transporters and can cut absorption, which is why it is taken with water. Only about 5 percent is metabolized; most leaves through bile into the feces, with some in urine, and its half-life is about 14 hours. Pseudoephedrine is released slowly from the extended-release layer, peaks around 6 hours, is barely metabolized and leaves in urine with a half-life of about 5 to 8 hours that depends on urine pH.",
  "sidefx": "Insomnia, nervousness and headache come from pseudoephedrine stimulating the brain. Raised blood pressure and palpitations reflect norepinephrine release. Urinary retention comes from alpha-1 effects on the bladder neck. Nausea and dry mouth are reported. With MAO inhibitors, a hypertensive crisis can occur because norepinephrine is not broken down. Overdose can cause arrhythmias. There is no antidote; severe hypertension is treated with drugs such as phentolamine.",
  "fun": "Fexofenadine is a case where a drug's own metabolite turned out to be the safer medicine.",
  "src": ["Allegra-D label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"sodium-picosulfate": {
  "origin": "Sodium picosulfate is a stimulant laxative developed in Germany in the 1960s. It belongs to the same family as bisacodyl, and both are converted in the gut to the same active compound. On its own it was sold in Europe as a laxative. Combined with magnesium oxide and citric acid, which react in water to form magnesium citrate, it became a bowel cleansing preparation used in the UK and elsewhere for decades. Ferring Pharmaceuticals brought this combination to the US as Prepopik powder, approved by the FDA in 2012, followed by the ready-to-drink Clenpiq in 2017. Its appeal was a much smaller volume to drink than traditional polyethylene glycol preps. Prepopik is now discontinued in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Sodium picosulfate is developed in Germany as a stimulant laxative."], ["2012", "FDA approves Prepopik, picosulfate with magnesium citrate, for colonoscopy prep."], ["2017", "FDA approves Clenpiq, a ready-to-drink version."]],
  "cell": "Sodium picosulfate is a prodrug. It passes through the stomach and small intestine mostly unchanged. In the colon, bacterial sulfatase enzymes remove its sulfate groups to form BHPM (bis-(p-hydroxyphenyl)-pyridyl-2-methane), the same active metabolite as bisacodyl. BHPM acts on the colon lining and its enteric nerves to increase propulsive contractions, and it reduces water absorption while increasing water and electrolyte secretion into the lumen, partly through prostaglandin signaling. Magnesium oxide and citric acid form magnesium citrate. Magnesium ions are poorly absorbed, so they hold water in the bowel by osmosis and may also release cholecystokinin, which adds to secretion and motility. The stimulant and osmotic actions together produce large-volume watery stools that clear the colon.",
  "body": [["Colon", "Strong contractions and fluid in the lumen produce watery diarrhea that empties the bowel for colonoscopy."], ["Gut", "Cramping, bloating, nausea and vomiting come from rapid stretching and contraction of the bowel."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Large fluid losses can cause dehydration, low sodium and low potassium, which is why clear liquids are taken with it."], ["Kidneys", "Absorbed magnesium is cleared by the kidneys, so kidney disease can lead to high magnesium."], ["Brain", "Low sodium can cause headache, confusion and, rarely, seizures."], ["Heart", "Electrolyte shifts can trigger arrhythmias in people at risk."]],
  "journey": "The solution is swallowed in two portions, each followed by clear liquids. Picosulfate is barely absorbed and travels to the colon, where bacteria activate it to BHPM, which is also poorly absorbed. Bowel movements usually begin within about 2 to 3 hours of the first dose. Some magnesium is absorbed into the blood and cleared by the kidneys; the rest, along with picosulfate and BHPM, leaves in the stool. Antibiotics that alter gut bacteria could in theory reduce activation.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, cramping, bloating and vomiting reflect intense bowel stimulation and distension. Dehydration, low sodium and low potassium come from large fluid and salt losses in the stool, and low sodium can lead to seizures. High magnesium occurs when the kidneys cannot clear absorbed magnesium. Fainting can follow fluid loss. Arrhythmias relate to electrolyte changes. Ischemic colitis is rare and may come from reduced blood flow during intense bowel activity. Aspiration is a risk in people who cannot swallow safely. There is no antidote; fluids and electrolytes are replaced.",
  "fun": "Picosulfate needs gut bacteria to work: it is switched on only after colonic bacteria cut off its sulfate groups.",
  "src": ["Clenpiq FDA label", "Prepopik FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
},
"lidocaine-patch": {
  "origin": "Lidocaine was synthesized in 1943 by Swedish chemists Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist at Stockholm University while they studied compounds related to gramine, a barley alkaloid. It was more stable than ester anesthetics such as procaine and caused fewer allergic reactions. Astra marketed it as Xylocaine in 1948, and it became the most widely used local anesthetic in the world. Decades later, researchers found that applying lidocaine to the skin could calm the damaged nerves of postherpetic neuralgia, the long-lasting pain after shingles. The Lidoderm 5 percent patch was approved by the FDA in 1999 for this condition. Later products include ZTlido and lower-strength over-the-counter patches.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine in Stockholm."], ["1948", "Astra launches lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["1999", "FDA approves the Lidoderm 5 percent patch for postherpetic neuralgia."], ["2018", "FDA approves ZTlido, a lower-load lidocaine topical system."]],
  "cell": "Lidocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve membranes. The uncharged form crosses the membrane, then the charged form binds a site inside the channel pore from the cytoplasmic side. It binds best to channels that are open or inactivated, so nerves that fire often are blocked more (use-dependent block). After shingles, damaged pain fibers in the skin develop extra sodium channels, including Nav1.7 and Nav1.8, and fire spontaneously, which drives burning pain and allodynia. Lidocaine from the patch reaches these superficial fibers in low concentrations that quiet the abnormal firing without fully numbing the skin. The patch itself also acts as a barrier that protects sensitive skin from clothing touch.",
  "body": [["Skin nerves", "Abnormal firing in damaged pain fibers drops, which reduces burning pain and touch-evoked pain."], ["Skin", "Redness, swelling, itching or burning at the site come from the adhesive and local drug effect."], ["Touch sensation", "The patch reduces pain but usually does not make the skin fully numb."], ["Heart and brain", "Very little reaches the blood at labeled use, but excess patches, broken skin or heat can raise levels enough to affect heart rhythm and the brain."]],
  "journey": "Lidocaine diffuses from the patch into the skin, where it acts on local nerve endings. Only a small fraction, around 3 percent of the drug in the patch with labeled use, enters the blood, and peak blood levels are low, near the end of the wear period. Absorbed lidocaine is broken down in the liver by CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 to MEGX and glycinexylidide, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its systemic half-life is about 1.5 to 2 hours. Heating pads, broken skin, more patches or longer wear raise absorption. A used patch still contains most of its lidocaine.",
  "sidefx": "Redness, swelling, itching and burning at the site come from the adhesive and drug on the skin. Allergic reactions, blisters and skin discoloration are uncommon. Systemic toxicity happens only if enough lidocaine reaches the blood, such as from many patches, heat, broken skin or a child chewing a used patch. Then sodium channel block in the brain causes numb lips, ringing ears, confusion and seizures, and in the heart causes slow rhythms and arrhythmias. Lidocaine can oxidize hemoglobin, causing methemoglobinemia, which is treated with methylene blue. Severe local anesthetic toxicity is treated with lipid emulsion therapy and supportive care.",
  "fun": "A used Lidoderm patch still holds most of its lidocaine, which is why used patches are folded sticky sides together before disposal.",
  "src": ["Lidoderm FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Lofgren N. Studies on Local Anesthetics: Xylocaine, 1948"]
},
"dyclonine": {
  "origin": "Dyclonine is a local anesthetic from the mid-20th century with an unusual structure. Most local anesthetics are either esters (like benzocaine and procaine) or amides (like lidocaine), but dyclonine is a ketone. This made it useful for people allergic to both classes. It was once sold as Dyclone solution for numbing the mouth and airway before procedures, but that product was discontinued. Today dyclonine is mainly found in over-the-counter sore throat lozenges such as Sucrets, where it numbs the throat for short periods.",
  "timeline": [["20th century", "Dyclonine is introduced as a topical anesthetic that is neither an ester nor an amide."]],
  "cell": "Like other local anesthetics, dyclonine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the membranes of sensory nerve endings. It enters the nerve and binds a site in the channel pore, stabilizing the channel in its inactivated state. Sodium cannot rush in, so the nerve cannot generate the action potentials that carry pain signals to the brain. Small, unmyelinated pain fibers near the surface of the mouth and throat lining are blocked easily, which is why the throat feels numb within minutes. Because dyclonine is a ketone, it is not broken down by plasma esterases into para-aminobenzoic acid, the trigger for ester anesthetic allergies, and it does not share the amide structure.",
  "body": [["Mouth and throat", "Sensory nerves in the mucosa are blocked, so sore throat and mouth pain ease within minutes."], ["Swallowing", "Numbness of the throat can blunt the gag and swallowing reflexes, which matters in people at risk of choking."], ["Mouth lining", "Mild local irritation or a stinging feeling can occur where the lozenge dissolves."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions are rare, and the ketone structure avoids cross-allergy with esters and amides."]],
  "journey": "The lozenge dissolves slowly, and dyclonine acts directly on the surface of the mouth and throat. Its effect starts within minutes and lasts up to about an hour. Some drug is swallowed, but the amount that reaches the blood from a lozenge is small. Its systemic metabolism and excretion are not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Oral numbness is the expected effect of sodium channel block in the mucosa. Hot foods and drinks can cause burns because the numb area does not sense heat. Throat numbness can blunt swallowing reflexes and raise aspiration risk in people with impaired swallowing. Mild local irritation and rare allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur. There is no specific antidote; very large exposures would be treated like other local anesthetic toxicity.",
  "fun": "Dyclonine is often listed as the only common local anesthetic that is neither an ester nor an amide.",
  "src": ["Sucrets Sore Throat lozenge label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"chlorhexidine-oral-rinse": {
  "origin": "Chlorhexidine was discovered in the early 1950s at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in Manchester, England, where chemists were making bisbiguanide compounds while searching for antimalarial drugs. The compound turned out to be a powerful antiseptic, and it was introduced in the UK in 1954. It became standard for skin antisepsis, wound care and hand scrubs. Around 1970 researchers in Denmark, including Harald Loe and colleagues, showed that chlorhexidine mouth rinses could prevent dental plaque and gingivitis. In the US, chlorhexidine gluconate oral rinse was approved as Peridex in the 1980s.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Chlorhexidine is discovered at ICI in England during antimalarial research."], ["1954", "Chlorhexidine is introduced as an antiseptic in the UK."], ["1970", "Loe and Schiott report that chlorhexidine rinses prevent plaque."], ["1980s", "Chlorhexidine oral rinse (Peridex) is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Chlorhexidine is a cationic bisbiguanide. At the pH of the mouth it carries strong positive charges that bind to the negatively charged bacterial cell surface, including phosphate groups in the cell wall and membrane. At lower concentrations it disrupts membrane integrity, so potassium and other small molecules leak out and the bacteria stop growing (bacteriostatic). At higher concentrations it causes the cytoplasm to coagulate and the cell dies (bactericidal). Chlorhexidine also binds to tooth enamel, the pellicle, plaque and the oral mucosa and is released slowly over hours. This property, called substantivity, gives about 12 hours of antibacterial action and prevents bacteria from rebuilding plaque.",
  "body": [["Teeth and gums", "Less plaque means less gum inflammation, so gums bleed and swell less over weeks."], ["Teeth and fillings", "Brown staining of teeth, tongue and fillings is common because chlorhexidine binds surfaces and reacts with dietary pigments."], ["Taste", "Taste can be altered for a few hours because chlorhexidine binds to taste receptor areas."], ["Tartar", "Calculus can increase on teeth during use."], ["Mouth lining", "Superficial peeling and soreness of the mucosa can occur."]],
  "journey": "The rinse is swished for 30 seconds and spit out. About a third of the dose stays bound in the mouth and is released slowly over hours. Little is swallowed, and what is swallowed is poorly absorbed from the gut and leaves in the feces. Toothpaste, eating or rinsing right after use can inactivate it, because ingredients such as sodium lauryl sulfate are anionic and neutralize the positively charged drug.",
  "sidefx": "Staining of teeth, tongue and restorations happens because chlorhexidine binds tooth surfaces and the pellicle and traps pigments from coffee, tea and wine. Altered taste reflects binding near taste cells. More tartar forms, possibly because chlorhexidine changes how salivary proteins and minerals deposit. Mucosal peeling and soreness reflect direct irritation. Severe allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, are rare but recognized with chlorhexidine products and are treated with epinephrine.",
  "fun": "Chlorhexidine was found by chemists hunting for malaria drugs, not antiseptics.",
  "src": ["Peridex FDA label", "Davies GE et al. Br J Pharmacol 1954", "Loe H, Schiott CR. J Periodontal Res 1970"]
},
"triamcinolone-dental-paste": {
  "origin": "Triamcinolone was made at Lederle Laboratories in the 1950s as part of the wave of synthetic corticosteroids that followed the discovery of cortisone's effects. Adding a 9-alpha fluorine and a 16-alpha hydroxyl group gave strong anti-inflammatory action with little salt retention. Making the acetonide derivative increased potency on the skin and mucous membranes. To keep a steroid on wet oral tissues, it was combined with Orabase, an adhesive base of gelatin, pectin and carboxymethylcellulose that sticks to the mouth lining. Kenalog in Orabase was used for decades and has been discontinued, and generic triamcinolone acetonide dental pastes and brands such as Oralone continue its use for mouth ulcers.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Philip Hench and colleagues first treat a patient with cortisone at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1950", "Hench, Kendall and Reichstein receive the Nobel Prize for work on adrenal hormones."], ["1950s", "Lederle Laboratories develops triamcinolone."]],
  "cell": "Triamcinolone acetonide diffuses into cells of the oral mucosa and binds the cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor. The complex moves to the nucleus, where it represses the transcription factors NF-kappa B and AP-1, lowering production of cytokines such as IL-1, TNF-alpha and IL-6, chemokines and adhesion molecules. It also induces anti-inflammatory proteins such as annexin-1, which reduces phospholipase A2 activity and the release of arachidonic acid, so fewer prostaglandins and leukotrienes are made. Fewer white cells enter the ulcer, vessels leak less, and pain and swelling ease. The adhesive paste base forms a film over the sore, protecting it from food, teeth and saliva while holding the steroid in place.",
  "body": [["Mouth ulcers", "Less inflammation and a protective film reduce pain and may help ulcers heal faster."], ["Mouth lining", "Local burning, irritation or a gritty feel come from the paste and its contact with a raw ulcer."], ["Taste", "Taste can be altered while the paste is in place."], ["Local immunity", "Steroid action can allow oral thrush or worsen herpes sores, because local immune defenses are dampened."], ["Rest of body", "Very little is absorbed, so effects on the adrenal glands are rare with short courses."]],
  "journey": "The paste is pressed onto a dried ulcer and forms a slippery film that adheres for a few hours. Triamcinolone acts in the tissue directly under it. Only a small amount is absorbed through the mucosa or swallowed. Absorbed drug is broken down in the liver by CYP3A4 and leaves in urine and bile, with a systemic half-life of about 2 to 5 hours. Bedtime use lengthens contact because saliva flow and movement are lower during sleep.",
  "sidefx": "Local burning, irritation and gritty texture come from the paste on a sensitive, open sore. Oral candidiasis and worsened herpetic lesions happen because the steroid suppresses local immune cells that normally control yeast and viruses; this is why it is not used on viral or fungal sores. Allergic contact reactions to the base can occur. Suppression of the body's own cortisol production is rare with short courses because very little drug reaches the blood. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Pressing, not rubbing, matters: rubbing makes the paste crumble instead of forming its protective film.",
  "src": ["Triamcinolone acetonide dental paste labeling", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1950"]
},
"tedizolid": {
  "origin": "The oxazolidinone antibiotics were discovered at DuPont in the 1980s, and Pharmacia and Upjohn developed linezolid, approved in 2000 as the first of the class. Linezolid works well against MRSA and other resistant gram-positive bacteria, but it can cause low blood counts and nerve damage with longer use. Tedizolid was discovered by Dong-A Pharmaceutical in South Korea and developed by Trius Therapeutics, which was later acquired by Cubist and then Merck. Changes to the side chain gave it stronger ribosome binding, allowing once-daily dosing and a short course. The FDA approved tedizolid as Sivextro in June 2014 for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Oxazolidinone antibiotics are discovered at DuPont."], ["2000", "FDA approves linezolid, the first oxazolidinone."], ["2014", "FDA approves tedizolid (Sivextro) for skin and skin structure infections."]],
  "cell": "Tedizolid phosphate is a prodrug that phosphatases quickly convert to tedizolid. Tedizolid binds the 23S ribosomal RNA of the bacterial 50S subunit at the peptidyl transferase center. This interferes with correct positioning of the first aminoacyl-tRNA and blocks formation of a functional 70S initiation complex, so protein synthesis cannot begin. Without new proteins, bacteria stop growing; the effect is mainly bacteriostatic against staphylococci and enterococci. Its D-ring and hydroxymethyl side chain add extra binding contacts, which makes it more potent than linezolid and keeps activity against many strains carrying the cfr gene, which methylates 23S rRNA and causes linezolid resistance. It covers MRSA, streptococci and enterococci but not most gram-negative bacteria.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Bacterial growth stops in infected skin, which lets immune cells clear cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common; changes to gut bacteria can allow C. difficile colitis."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are among the more common side effects."], ["Bone marrow", "Drops in hemoglobin, platelets or neutrophils can occur, as with other oxazolidinones."], ["Nerves and eyes", "Peripheral and optic nerve damage are class concerns with longer use, likely from effects on mitochondrial ribosomes."]],
  "journey": "Tedizolid phosphate is given by mouth or by IV infusion. Oral bioavailability is very high, around 90 percent, so the two routes are nearly interchangeable. Phosphatases in the blood and tissues convert it to active tedizolid. It is about 70 to 90 percent protein bound and spreads into skin and soft tissue. The liver converts it to an inactive sulfate conjugate without using CYP enzymes, and about 82 percent leaves in the feces with the rest in urine. Its half-life is about 12 hours, supporting once-daily dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, diarrhea, vomiting and headache come from effects on the gut and its bacteria. Clostridioides difficile colitis can follow, as with most antibiotics. Bacterial ribosomes resemble mitochondrial ribosomes, so oxazolidinones can slow mitochondrial protein synthesis in human cells, which is thought to explain bone marrow suppression and peripheral and optic neuropathy with longer use. Tedizolid is a weak, reversible MAO inhibitor, but serotonin syndrome has been less of a concern than with linezolid. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tedizolid's standard course for skin infections is only 6 days.",
  "src": ["Sivextro FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"sulfadiazine": {
  "origin": "The sulfa drug era began when Gerhard Domagk at Bayer found in 1932 that the red dye Prontosil protected mice from streptococcal infection. Researchers at the Pasteur Institute then showed that Prontosil was broken down in the body to sulfanilamide, the true active drug. Domagk received the 1939 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Chemists made thousands of related sulfonamides. In 1940 Richard Roblin and colleagues at American Cyanamid made sulfadiazine, which was more potent and better tolerated than earlier sulfas and was widely used in World War II. Today it is mainly used with pyrimethamine for toxoplasmosis and for rheumatic fever prevention in people allergic to penicillin.",
  "timeline": [["1932", "Gerhard Domagk discovers the antibacterial effect of Prontosil."], ["1935", "Researchers show Prontosil is converted to sulfanilamide."], ["1939", "Domagk is awarded the Nobel Prize for sulfa drug discovery."], ["1940", "Sulfadiazine is synthesized at American Cyanamid."]],
  "cell": "Bacteria and protozoa such as Toxoplasma gondii must make their own folate, while humans take folate from food. Sulfadiazine is a structural analog of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA). It competitively inhibits dihydropteroate synthase, the enzyme that joins PABA to a pteridine to make dihydropteroic acid, an early step toward dihydrofolate. Without folate, the organism cannot make enough thymidine, purines and some amino acids, so DNA synthesis and growth stop; the effect is bacteriostatic. Pyrimethamine blocks a later step, dihydrofolate reductase, so the pair causes a sequential blockade that is much stronger against Toxoplasma than either drug alone. Leucovorin (folinic acid) is given with pyrimethamine to protect human bone marrow, because the parasite cannot use it well.",
  "body": [["Brain and eyes", "With pyrimethamine, it stops Toxoplasma multiplying in brain and retinal tissue, which is key in toxoplasmic encephalitis and chorioretinitis."], ["Heart (prevention)", "Long-term use helps prevent streptococcal infections that could trigger rheumatic fever."], ["Kidneys", "The drug and its acetyl metabolite can crystallize in urine, causing crystalluria, blood in the urine and kidney injury, especially with low fluid intake."], ["Skin", "Rash and photosensitivity are common; rarely, severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome occur."], ["Blood", "Hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency, low white counts and, rarely, agranulocytosis or aplastic anemia can occur."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis is uncommon but can be severe."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth, sulfadiazine is well absorbed and peaks in about 3 to 6 hours. It is about 38 to 48 percent protein bound and spreads well into tissues, including the cerebrospinal fluid, which helps in brain infections. The liver acetylates it to an N4-acetyl metabolite that is less soluble. Both the parent drug and the metabolite leave through the kidneys, with a half-life of about 10 to 12 hours that lengthens in kidney disease. Generous fluid intake keeps urine dilute to reduce crystal formation.",
  "sidefx": "Crystalluria and kidney injury happen because sulfadiazine and its acetyl metabolite are poorly soluble in acidic urine; fluids and sometimes urine alkalinization help prevent this. Rashes, fever and severe skin reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis reflect immune reactions to sulfonamide metabolites. Hemolytic anemia in G6PD deficiency is caused by oxidative stress on red cells. In newborns, sulfonamides push bilirubin off albumin, which can cause kernicterus. Bone marrow suppression is worsened when combined with pyrimethamine, which is why leucovorin is given. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Prontosil was a dye, and patients taking it sometimes turned reddish; the active part turned out to be colorless sulfanilamide.",
  "src": ["Sulfadiazine tablets prescribing information", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1939"]
},
"tinidazole": {
  "origin": "The nitroimidazole story began with azomycin, a natural 2-nitroimidazole from Streptomyces bacteria found in the 1950s. Chemists at Rhone-Poulenc in France made metronidazole from this lead, and it was introduced around 1960 for trichomoniasis. Tinidazole, a close relative with a longer half-life, was developed by Pfizer in the late 1960s and used for decades in Europe, Asia and other regions. It was not sold in the US until 2004, when the FDA approved Tindamax for trichomoniasis, giardiasis and amebiasis. A bacterial vaginosis indication followed in 2007.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Azomycin, a natural nitroimidazole from Streptomyces, is identified."], ["1960", "Metronidazole is introduced for trichomoniasis."], ["1960s", "Pfizer develops tinidazole."], ["2004", "FDA approves tinidazole (Tindamax) in the US."], ["2007", "Tinidazole is approved in the US for bacterial vaginosis."]],
  "cell": "Tinidazole is a prodrug that enters cells by passive diffusion. In anaerobic bacteria and protozoa such as Trichomonas, Giardia and Entamoeba, low-redox electron carriers such as ferredoxin (reduced by pyruvate-ferredoxin oxidoreductase) donate electrons to its nitro group. This forms short-lived nitro radical anions and other reactive intermediates. These damage DNA by causing strand breaks and loss of helical structure, which kills the organism. Human cells and aerobic bacteria lack these low-redox systems and oxygen quickly reoxidizes any radical formed, so the drug is selectively toxic to anaerobes. Constant reduction inside anaerobes also keeps a concentration gradient that draws more drug into the cell.",
  "body": [["Vagina and genital tract", "Killing Trichomonas and anaerobic bacteria clears trichomoniasis and bacterial vaginosis."], ["Intestine", "It kills Giardia and Entamoeba in the gut, and it reaches the liver for amebic abscess."], ["Mouth", "A metallic or bitter taste is common, partly because the drug is secreted into saliva."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy and, rarely, seizures are linked to nitroimidazole effects on nerve tissue."], ["Vaginal flora", "Killing normal bacteria can allow vaginal yeast overgrowth."]],
  "journey": "Tinidazole is almost completely absorbed by mouth and peaks in about 1.5 to 2 hours. Taking it with food lessens stomach upset. It is only about 12 percent protein bound and spreads widely, including into the cerebrospinal fluid, saliva and breast milk, and it crosses the placenta. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4. About 20 to 25 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, with the rest as metabolites in urine and feces. Its half-life is about 12 to 14 hours, longer than metronidazole, which allows single-dose or short regimens.",
  "sidefx": "Metallic or bitter taste, nausea, loss of appetite and fatigue are common and relate to the drug's presence in saliva and the gut. Peripheral neuropathy and seizures reflect toxicity to nerve tissue, possibly from reactive metabolites. Vaginal yeast infection follows disruption of normal flora. Alcohol during and for several days after treatment can cause a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing, vomiting and palpitations. The label carries a boxed warning based on carcinogenicity seen in rodents with metronidazole. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Metronidazole was originally developed only for trichomoniasis; its activity against anaerobic bacteria was found by chance when a patient's gum infection cleared.",
  "src": ["Tindamax FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"secnidazole": {
  "origin": "Secnidazole is a 5-nitroimidazole, a relative of metronidazole and tinidazole, that has been used outside the US for decades, particularly in Europe, Latin America and Africa, for intestinal and genital protozoal infections. Its standout feature is a long half-life, which allows a single dose to work. US companies developed a granule form to give a single-dose oral treatment for bacterial vaginosis. The FDA approved it as Solosec in September 2017. In 2021 the FDA added trichomoniasis to its label, making it a single-dose option for both conditions.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Metronidazole's success leads to development of related nitroimidazoles."], ["2017", "FDA approves secnidazole (Solosec) for bacterial vaginosis."], ["2021", "FDA adds trichomoniasis to the Solosec label."]],
  "cell": "Secnidazole is a prodrug that diffuses into microbial cells. Inside anaerobic bacteria such as Gardnerella and Prevotella and protozoa such as Trichomonas vaginalis, low-redox electron carriers like ferredoxin reduce its nitro group. This produces nitro radical anions and other reactive intermediates that break DNA strands and destabilize the helix, so the organism dies. Aerobic human cells cannot reduce the drug efficiently, and any radical formed is quickly reoxidized by oxygen, so human cells are largely spared. Its long half-life keeps drug levels above the minimum inhibitory concentration for days after one dose.",
  "body": [["Vagina", "Killing the overgrown anaerobic bacteria clears bacterial vaginosis and lets normal lactobacilli recover."], ["Genital tract", "It kills Trichomonas vaginalis to treat trichomoniasis, and sexual partners are treated as well."], ["Vaginal flora", "Disruption of normal bacteria can allow yeast overgrowth, causing vulvovaginal candidiasis and itching."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea, abdominal pain and vomiting reflect effects on the gut and its flora."], ["Taste and head", "Altered taste and headache are reported, as with other nitroimidazoles."]],
  "journey": "The granules are sprinkled on soft food and eaten. Secnidazole is well absorbed and peaks in about 4 hours. It is less than 5 percent protein bound and spreads widely through body fluids. The liver metabolizes only a small amount through CYP-mediated oxidation. About 15 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 17 hours, the longest of the common nitroimidazoles, so one dose covers several days.",
  "sidefx": "Vulvovaginal candidiasis and vaginal itching happen because killing normal flora gives yeast room to grow. Headache, nausea, diarrhea, abdominal pain and taste change are typical nitroimidazole effects on the gut and nervous system. Like related drugs, it carries cautions about alcohol and potential for neurologic effects, and carcinogenicity seen in rodents with other nitroimidazoles has shaped labeling. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Secnidazole's half-life is long enough that a single dose is the whole treatment course.",
  "src": ["Solosec FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"lefamulin": {
  "origin": "Lefamulin belongs to the pleuromutilins, a family that began with a mushroom. In 1951 researchers described pleuromutilin, an antibacterial compound made by the gilled fungus then called Pleurotus mutilus (now Clitopilus). For decades the family was used only in animals: semisynthetic versions such as tiamulin and valnemulin treated respiratory and gut infections in pigs and poultry. A topical version, retapamulin, reached human skin in 2007. Chemists at the Austrian company Nabriva Therapeutics, which grew out of Sandoz research in Vienna, modified the core so it could be given by vein and by mouth with good blood levels. The FDA approved lefamulin as Xenleta in 2019, making it the first systemic pleuromutilin for people and the first new antibiotic class for community-acquired pneumonia in many years.",
  "timeline": [["1951", "Pleuromutilin is described from the fungus Pleurotus (Clitopilus) mutilus."], ["1970s", "Semisynthetic pleuromutilins such as tiamulin enter veterinary use."], ["2007", "Retapamulin ointment becomes the first pleuromutilin approved for human skin infections."], ["2019", "FDA approves lefamulin (Xenleta) IV and tablets for community-acquired bacterial pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Lefamulin binds the peptidyl transferase center (PTC) of the bacterial 50S ribosomal subunit, on domain V of the 23S rRNA. Its tricyclic core sits in a pocket that overlaps both the A site and the P site, and its side chain reaches into the P site. The pocket closes tightly around the drug (an induced fit), so transfer RNAs carrying amino acids cannot line up correctly and peptide bonds cannot form. Protein synthesis stops at the earliest steps. Because this binding site and mode differ from macrolides, lincosamides, tetracyclines and oxazolidinones, cross-resistance with those classes is uncommon. The effect is mainly bacteriostatic against many organisms, and it covers typical pneumonia pathogens (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Staphylococcus aureus) and atypical ones (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella).",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Lefamulin concentrates in the lining fluid of the airways, where it stops both typical and atypical pneumonia bacteria from making proteins, so the infection is controlled while immune cells clear it."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval on the ECG by slowing repolarization, which adds to the effect of other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Gut", "The oral tablets often cause nausea, vomiting and diarrhea, and like any antibiotic it can disturb gut flora and allow C. difficile to grow."], ["Veins", "The IV form can irritate the vein at the infusion site, which is why it comes with a citrate-buffered diluent."], ["Liver", "The liver breaks the drug down through CYP3A4, so strong inhibitors or inducers of that enzyme change blood levels."]],
  "journey": "Lefamulin can be given by vein or as a tablet. Oral absorption is modest and food lowers it, so tablets are taken on an empty stomach. In the blood it is highly protein bound, yet it reaches good levels in the airway lining fluid. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, and it is also a P-glycoprotein substrate. Most of the drug and its metabolites leave in the feces, with only a small amount in urine. The half-life is about eight hours, which supports twice-daily dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea come largely from direct irritation of the gut and from changes in gut bacteria; disruption of normal flora also explains the risk of C. difficile colitis. QT prolongation reflects an effect on cardiac potassium currents during repolarization, so combining it with other QT-prolonging drugs or giving it with low potassium raises the risk of torsades de pointes. Infusion-site reactions are local vein irritation. The drug harmed fetal development in animal studies, so the label warns about use in pregnancy. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "The first pleuromutilin drugs were built for farm animals, so this antibiotic class spent nearly half a century treating pigs before it was given to people by mouth or vein.",
  "src": ["Xenleta (lefamulin) FDA label", "Kavanagh F et al. Antibiotic substances from basidiomycetes, 1951", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"cycloserine": {
  "origin": "Cycloserine is a natural antibiotic made by soil bacteria. In the mid-1950s several groups, including researchers at Merck and at Eli Lilly, independently isolated it from Streptomyces species such as Streptomyces orchidaceus and Streptomyces garyphalus. It turned out to be a simple molecule, a ring-shaped copy of the amino acid D-alanine, and it was soon made synthetically. It was active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and Lilly marketed it as Seromycin. Its psychiatric and seizure side effects kept it from first-line use, but it remained an important reserve drug for drug-resistant tuberculosis.",
  "timeline": [["1954", "Cycloserine is isolated from Streptomyces cultures by several research groups."], ["1955", "Clinical studies begin using cycloserine against tuberculosis."], ["1990s", "Rising multidrug-resistant TB renews interest in cycloserine as a second-line drug."]],
  "cell": "Cycloserine is a structural analog of D-alanine. Bacteria need D-alanyl-D-alanine as the end of the peptide stem that cross-links peptidoglycan. Cycloserine competitively inhibits two enzymes in that pathway: alanine racemase, which converts L-alanine into D-alanine, and D-alanine:D-alanine ligase, which joins two D-alanines together. Without D-alanyl-D-alanine, the cytoplasmic building block (UDP-muramyl pentapeptide) cannot be completed and the wall cannot be built or repaired. This acts earlier in cell wall synthesis than beta-lactams or vancomycin. In the human brain, cycloserine also acts at the glycine co-agonist site of the NMDA glutamate receptor (as a partial agonist), which helps explain its neuropsychiatric effects.",
  "body": [["Lungs and TB lesions", "In drug-resistant tuberculosis it blocks wall building in the bacilli, adding to other drugs in the regimen so resistance is less likely to emerge."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain well and changes NMDA receptor signaling, causing headache, drowsiness, irritability, depression, psychosis and seizures."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy can occur, and pyridoxine is commonly given alongside to lower neurologic toxicity."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys remove most of the drug unchanged, so poor kidney function raises levels and toxicity."], ["Urinary tract", "High urine concentrations explain its use for some urinary tract infections."]],
  "journey": "Cycloserine is taken by mouth and absorbed well, reaching peak levels within a few hours. It spreads widely through body fluids and tissues, including the cerebrospinal fluid, where levels approach those in blood. Very little is metabolized. Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine by glomerular filtration, so the half-life of about ten hours stretches out when the kidneys are impaired. Blood levels are often measured to keep exposure in a safer range.",
  "sidefx": "The main problems are in the brain. Because cycloserine reaches high levels in the central nervous system and acts on NMDA receptors, it can cause anxiety, confusion, depression, psychosis, suicidal thinking and seizures, and the risk rises with higher blood levels, kidney disease and alcohol use. Combining it with ethionamide or isoniazid adds central nervous system toxicity. Pyridoxine is often given to reduce neurotoxicity. There is no specific antidote; seizures are treated with standard anticonvulsant care.",
  "fun": "Researchers later tested the NMDA effect of cycloserine on purpose, studying it as an add-on to exposure therapy for anxiety disorders, a rare case of an antibiotic side effect becoming a research tool in psychiatry.",
  "src": ["Seromycin (cycloserine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "WHO consolidated guidelines on drug-resistant tuberculosis"]
},
"aminosalicylic-acid": {
  "origin": "Para-aminosalicylic acid (PAS) came from clever reasoning by the Swedish chemist Jorgen Lehmann. In 1940 Frederick Bernheim reported that salicylate increased the oxygen use of tuberculosis bacteria, which suggested they consumed it. Lehmann guessed that a slightly changed salicylate might jam that process instead. He had PAS made in 1944, found it blocked TB growth and published in 1946. In the late 1940s British Medical Research Council trials showed that giving PAS with streptomycin sharply reduced streptomycin resistance. This was one of the first clear proofs that combining TB drugs prevents resistance, a principle still central to TB care. Today PAS is a second-line drug, supplied in the US as delayed-release granules (Paser).",
  "timeline": [["1940", "Bernheim reports that salicylate stimulates oxygen uptake by TB bacteria."], ["1944", "Jorgen Lehmann has PAS synthesized and finds it inhibits TB."], ["1946", "Lehmann publishes the first clinical results with PAS."], ["1950", "MRC trial shows PAS plus streptomycin limits drug resistance."]],
  "cell": "PAS is a structural analog of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), the starting material for folate in bacteria. Older teaching described it simply as a competitive inhibitor of dihydropteroate synthase. Newer work shows it is actually a prodrug inside Mycobacterium tuberculosis: dihydropteroate synthase accepts PAS in place of PABA, and dihydrofolate synthase then converts the product into a hydroxyl-dihydrofolate analog. That false folate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, so the bacterium runs short of reduced folates needed to make thymidine, purines and some amino acids. Growth slows (bacteriostatic). Human cells take in preformed folate from the diet and do not use PABA, which gives selectivity.",
  "body": [["TB bacteria", "It slows the growth of tuberculosis bacilli and helps protect partner drugs from resistance in multidrug-resistant TB regimens."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea are common and are the main reason people struggle to take it."], ["Thyroid", "Long-term use can block thyroid hormone production and cause goiter or hypothyroidism, worse when combined with ethionamide."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis can occur, often as part of a hypersensitivity reaction in the first weeks."], ["Nutrition", "It can reduce absorption of vitamin B12, folate and fat from the gut."], ["Blood", "Rarely it causes agranulocytosis or hemolytic anemia, especially in people with G6PD deficiency."]],
  "journey": "The granules are coated with an acid-resistant layer, so they pass the stomach intact and release drug in the more neutral small intestine; they are sprinkled on acidic food to keep the coat working. Peak levels arrive about six hours after a dose. PAS spreads into most tissues and fluids but enters the cerebrospinal fluid poorly unless the meninges are inflamed. The liver and gut wall inactivate it by N-acetylation. More than 80 percent leaves in the urine as parent drug and acetylated metabolite. The parent half-life is only about an hour, so it is taken several times a day.",
  "sidefx": "Gut upset comes from direct irritation and high drug loads. Hypothyroidism happens because PAS interferes with iodine use in the thyroid, an effect added to by ethionamide. Hepatitis and fever with rash are largely hypersensitivity reactions. Reduced B12 and folate absorption reflects effects on the intestinal lining. Hemolysis in G6PD deficiency reflects oxidant stress on red cells. PAS competes with isoniazid for acetylation, so isoniazid levels rise. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the drug.",
  "fun": "When PAS was introduced it was taken in large amounts every day, and patients often called it one of the hardest parts of TB treatment to swallow.",
  "src": ["Paser (aminosalicylic acid) FDA label", "Lehmann J. Para-aminosalicylic acid in the treatment of tuberculosis. Lancet, 1946", "Medical Research Council trial of streptomycin and PAS, BMJ 1950"]
},
"clofazimine": {
  "origin": "Clofazimine began as a dye. In 1957 a team led by Vincent Barry at the Medical Research Council laboratories in Dublin, Ireland, made a series of red riminophenazine compounds while searching for TB drugs. One of them, coded B663, became clofazimine. It was disappointing in tuberculosis at first but proved very active against Mycobacterium leprae, the cause of leprosy. It also calmed the painful inflammatory leprosy reaction called erythema nodosum leprosum. Clofazimine, sold as Lamprene, became one of the three core drugs in the World Health Organization multidrug therapy for leprosy, and the FDA approved it in 1986. US commercial supply later ended, and today it is available in the US through the National Hansen's Disease Program and FDA expanded access. It has also returned as a key drug for drug-resistant tuberculosis.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Vincent Barry's team in Dublin synthesizes clofazimine (B663)."], ["1960s", "Clofazimine is shown to be effective against leprosy."], ["1981", "A WHO study group recommends multidrug therapy for leprosy, which includes clofazimine for multibacillary disease."], ["1986", "FDA approves Lamprene for leprosy."], ["2010s", "Clofazimine is added to many drug-resistant TB regimens."]],
  "cell": "Clofazimine is very fat-soluble and collects in mycobacterial membranes and inside macrophages, where mycobacteria live. Its exact mechanism is not fully settled. Leading ideas are that it binds guanine-rich mycobacterial DNA and interferes with replication, and that it is reduced by the bacterial respiratory enzyme NDH-2 and then reoxidized, a redox cycle that releases reactive oxygen species which damage the bacterium. It also disrupts membrane function and potassium transport. The overall effect is slow killing or growth inhibition. Separately, clofazimine has anti-inflammatory and immune-modulating effects, including reduced neutrophil and lymphocyte activity, which is why it helps erythema nodosum leprosum.",
  "body": [["Skin", "The red drug builds up in skin and fat, giving a pink to reddish-brown or almost black discoloration that fades slowly over months to years after stopping. Dry, scaly skin is common."], ["Body fluids", "Urine, sweat, tears, sputum and feces can turn pink, red or brown because the dye is excreted in them."], ["Gut", "Crystals can deposit in the bowel wall and lymph nodes, causing pain and, rarely, obstruction or bleeding."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval, especially combined with bedaquiline, fluoroquinolones or macrolides."], ["Eyes", "Crystal deposits in the conjunctiva and cornea can cause dry eye or discoloration."], ["Mood", "Depression has been reported, partly linked to the visible skin changes."]],
  "journey": "Clofazimine is taken by mouth with food, which improves its uneven absorption. Because it dissolves in fat, it leaves the blood and is stored in fat, skin, macrophages of the liver, spleen and lymph nodes, and the bowel wall, where it can form crystals. Release from these stores is very slow. The liver metabolizes only a little. Most leaves in the feces through bile or as unabsorbed drug, with small amounts in urine, sweat and skin oils. With repeated dosing the half-life is about 70 days, so it stays in the body for months.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect comes from the drug piling up as a colored, fat-loving compound. Skin and fluid discoloration happen because the dye stains the tissues and fluids it reaches. Crystal-storing macrophages in the bowel wall and spleen explain abdominal pain, rare obstruction and splenic infarction. QT prolongation reflects effects on cardiac repolarization and adds to other QT drugs. There is no antidote, and effects fade slowly because of the long tissue half-life.",
  "fun": "Clofazimine is a true dye; its color is strong enough that it visibly tints skin, and the change can last for months after the last dose.",
  "src": ["Lamprene (clofazimine) FDA label", "Barry VC et al. A new series of phenazines with high antituberculosis activity. Nature, 1957", "National Hansen's Disease Program guidance"]
},
"cefditoren": {
  "origin": "Cefditoren was developed by the Japanese company Meiji Seika as an oral third-generation cephalosporin. The active molecule is not absorbed well from the gut, so chemists attached a pivoxil ester (a pivalate group) to make cefditoren pivoxil, a prodrug that crosses the intestinal wall and then releases the active drug. It was first used in Japan in the 1990s, and the FDA approved it in the US as Spectracef in 2001 for mild to moderate respiratory and skin infections.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Meiji Seika develops cefditoren pivoxil and launches it in Japan."], ["2001", "FDA approves Spectracef (cefditoren pivoxil) tablets."]],
  "cell": "Cefditoren is a beta-lactam. It binds penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), the transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. Its beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the peptide stem, so the enzyme forms a stable acyl bond with the drug and is inactivated. Without cross-linking, the wall weakens while the bacterium keeps growing, and autolysins break it down further, so the cell bursts. Cefditoren has high affinity for PBPs of Streptococcus pneumoniae, including some penicillin-intermediate strains, and is stable against many common beta-lactamases of Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. It does not cover MRSA or Pseudomonas.",
  "body": [["Airways and lungs", "It kills common pneumonia and bronchitis bacteria such as pneumococcus, H. influenzae and Moraxella."], ["Throat and skin", "It treats strep throat and mild uncomplicated skin infections caused by streptococci and methicillin-susceptible staphylococci."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, and changes in gut flora can allow C. difficile overgrowth."], ["Muscles and metabolism", "The released pivalate binds carnitine and is excreted in urine, so carnitine stores can drop with longer use."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys clear the active drug, so kidney impairment raises levels."]],
  "journey": "Cefditoren pivoxil is swallowed as a tablet and is absorbed better with a moderate or high-fat meal. Antacids and acid blockers lower absorption. In the intestinal wall, esterases split the ester, releasing active cefditoren and pivalate. Cefditoren is highly bound to albumin, peaks in about two to three hours and is not appreciably metabolized. The kidneys remove it unchanged by filtration and tubular secretion, with a short half-life of under two hours, which is why it is taken twice daily.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis come from disruption of normal gut bacteria. Allergic reactions, from rash to rare anaphylaxis, reflect the shared beta-lactam structure. Carnitine depletion is specific to pivoxil prodrugs: the pivalate released must be joined to carnitine to be excreted, so carnitine is lost in the urine, and the drug is avoided in people with carnitine deficiency. The tablets contain sodium caseinate, a milk protein, so true milk-protein allergy is a contraindication (lactose intolerance is not). There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Spectracef (cefditoren pivoxil) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ceftibuten": {
  "origin": "Ceftibuten is an oral third-generation cephalosporin discovered in Japan in the 1980s and developed for the US market by Schering-Plough, which sold it as Cedax. It was approved by the FDA in the mid-1990s as a once-daily capsule and a fruit-flavored suspension for children. Its structure is unusual for a cephalosporin because it lacks the typical side group at the 3 position, which helps make it stable to many beta-lactamases and well absorbed by mouth without a prodrug ester.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ceftibuten is discovered and developed in Japan."], ["1990s", "FDA approves Cedax capsules and oral suspension."]],
  "cell": "Ceftibuten binds penicillin-binding proteins in the bacterial cell membrane, with particularly strong binding to PBP3 of gram-negative bacteria, which is needed for septum formation during division. Binding inactivates the transpeptidase, so new peptidoglycan cannot be cross-linked. Bacteria form long filaments, the wall weakens and the cells lyse. It is stable against many plasmid-mediated beta-lactamases, giving it good activity against Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis (including beta-lactamase producers) and Streptococcus pyogenes. Activity against Streptococcus pneumoniae and Staphylococcus aureus is weaker than many other cephalosporins, which limits its uses.",
  "body": [["Middle ear", "It reaches the middle ear fluid and treats otitis media caused by H. influenzae, Moraxella and streptococci."], ["Throat", "It kills group A streptococci in strep throat and tonsillitis."], ["Airways", "It treats bacterial flares of chronic bronchitis caused by susceptible gram-negative organisms."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and abdominal pain can occur, and C. difficile colitis is possible after any antibiotic."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine, so kidney function affects levels."]],
  "journey": "Ceftibuten is absorbed well from the gut without needing a prodrug. Food lowers absorption of the suspension, which is taken on an empty stomach. It peaks in two to three hours and is moderately protein bound. A small portion converts to a less active trans isomer. Most of the dose leaves unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about two to two and a half hours. Even so, it is effective once daily for the infections it is used for.",
  "sidefx": "Gut upset and C. difficile colitis come from changes in the normal gut bacteria. Rash and rare anaphylaxis reflect beta-lactam hypersensitivity. Mild rises in liver enzymes can occur. Because the kidneys clear it, reduced kidney function raises levels and side effect risk. There is no antidote; serious reactions are managed by stopping the drug.",
  "src": ["Cedax (ceftibuten) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"butenafine": {
  "origin": "Butenafine was developed in Japan by Kaken Pharmaceutical. It is a benzylamine, a close chemical cousin of the allylamine antifungals naftifine and terbinafine. The FDA approved it as a prescription cream (Mentax) in the late 1990s, and in 2001 it became available over the counter as Lotrimin Ultra for athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Kaken Pharmaceutical develops butenafine in Japan."], ["1996", "FDA approves butenafine cream (Mentax)."], ["2001", "Butenafine becomes available over the counter as Lotrimin Ultra."]],
  "cell": "Butenafine inhibits squalene epoxidase, an enzyme early in the fungal pathway that builds ergosterol, the main sterol of fungal cell membranes. Blocking this step has two effects. Ergosterol runs short, so the membrane becomes leaky and its enzymes work poorly. Squalene, the substrate, piles up inside the cell to toxic levels and disrupts membranes. The second effect makes butenafine fungicidal against dermatophytes such as Trichophyton, Microsporum and Epidermophyton. The human version of squalene epoxidase is much less sensitive, which gives selectivity.",
  "body": [["Skin", "It kills dermatophyte fungi in the outer skin layer, clearing athlete's foot, jock itch and ringworm."], ["Stratum corneum", "It binds to the outer dead skin layer and stays there after the last application, so fungi keep being killed for some time after a short course."], ["Application site", "Burning, stinging, itching or redness can occur where it is applied."]],
  "journey": "Butenafine is applied to the skin and stays mostly in the stratum corneum, the outer dead layer, where it forms a reservoir. Only a small amount passes into the bloodstream, and any that does is metabolized by the liver. Because so little reaches the blood, it has no meaningful effects elsewhere in the body.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are local. Burning, stinging and redness are direct irritation of inflamed skin. Contact dermatitis is an allergic response to the drug or cream ingredients. Systemic effects are not expected because blood levels are very low. There is no antidote, and none is needed for topical use.",
  "src": ["Lotrimin Ultra (butenafine) Drug Facts label", "Mentax (butenafine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"naftifine": {
  "origin": "Naftifine was the first allylamine antifungal, and it was found by chance. In the 1970s chemists at the Sandoz Research Institute in Vienna, including Anton Stutz, were working on compounds for the nervous system when one of them showed strong antifungal activity. That compound became naftifine. Studying how it worked revealed a new antifungal target, squalene epoxidase. The same team then refined the structure and created terbinafine, which became one of the most widely used antifungals in the world. Naftifine itself was approved in the US as Naftin cream in 1988.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Sandoz researchers in Vienna discover the antifungal activity of naftifine."], ["1988", "FDA approves Naftin (naftifine) cream."], ["1990s", "The allylamine work leads to terbinafine, a close relative of naftifine."]],
  "cell": "Naftifine blocks squalene epoxidase, the fungal enzyme that turns squalene into squalene 2,3-epoxide on the way to ergosterol. Ergosterol is the sterol that keeps fungal membranes stable. With the enzyme blocked, ergosterol falls and squalene builds up inside the cell. The lack of ergosterol makes the membrane leaky, and the excess squalene is toxic and disrupts membranes, so dermatophytes are killed (fungicidal). Naftifine also has some anti-inflammatory activity, which may help itch and redness in tinea.",
  "body": [["Skin", "It kills dermatophytes that cause athlete's foot between the toes, jock itch and ringworm of the body."], ["Inflamed skin", "Mild anti-inflammatory activity may ease redness and itching as the infection clears."], ["Application site", "Burning, stinging, dryness or redness can occur where it is applied."]],
  "journey": "Naftifine is spread on the skin as a cream or gel and penetrates the outer layers, where it lingers as a reservoir. A small fraction is absorbed into the blood. That absorbed drug is partly metabolized by the liver and leaves in the urine and feces, with a half-life of about two to three days. The amounts in blood are far too low to cause body-wide effects.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects are mostly local irritation, because the cream or gel sits on skin that is already inflamed. Allergic contact dermatitis is uncommon. Systemic side effects are not expected because so little reaches the blood. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Naftifine was discovered while researchers were looking for drugs that act on the nervous system, and its accidental antifungal activity opened up a whole new class of medicines.",
  "src": ["Naftin (naftifine) FDA label", "Stutz A, Petranyi G. Naftifine-related allylamine antimycotics. J Med Chem, 1984"]
},
"oxiconazole": {
  "origin": "Oxiconazole is an imidazole antifungal, part of the azole family that grew from work in the late 1960s showing that imidazole rings could block fungal sterol production. It was developed in the late 1970s and early 1980s and was approved in the US in the late 1980s as Oxistat cream, followed by a lotion. It was designed as a topical drug for dermatophyte infections and tinea versicolor.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Early imidazole antifungals such as clotrimazole and miconazole are developed."], ["1980s", "Oxiconazole cream is approved in the US as Oxistat."]],
  "cell": "Oxiconazole inhibits lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme (CYP51). The nitrogen atom on the imidazole ring binds the heme iron of the enzyme, so it cannot remove a methyl group from lanosterol. Ergosterol production falls and toxic 14-alpha-methylated sterols build up in the membrane. The membrane becomes disordered and leaky, and membrane-bound enzymes fail. This is mainly fungistatic, though high skin concentrations can kill fungi. Oxiconazole covers dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Epidermophyton, Microsporum) and Malassezia, the yeast behind tinea versicolor.",
  "body": [["Skin", "It stops dermatophytes and Malassezia from building their membranes, clearing athlete's foot, jock itch, ringworm and (with the cream) tinea versicolor."], ["Application site", "Itching, burning and irritation can occur, and maceration can develop if the skin stays wet."]],
  "journey": "Oxiconazole is applied once or twice a day and moves into the outer layers of the skin, where it reaches high concentrations. Less than 0.3 percent of the applied amount is absorbed into the body, so blood levels are negligible.",
  "sidefx": "Because so little enters the blood, side effects are local. Itching and burning reflect irritation of inflamed skin, and contact dermatitis is an allergic response to the drug or base. The liver enzyme interactions seen with oral azoles are not a concern at these tiny systemic levels. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Oxistat (oxiconazole) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"sertaconazole": {
  "origin": "Sertaconazole was developed by the Spanish company Ferrer Internacional in Barcelona in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Its designers added a benzothiophene ring to the classic imidazole antifungal structure, which makes it very fat-soluble and gives it a second way to damage fungal membranes. It was used in Europe first, and the FDA approved it in the US as Ertaczo cream in 2003 for athlete's foot between the toes.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ferrer Internacional develops sertaconazole in Spain."], ["2003", "FDA approves Ertaczo (sertaconazole) cream for interdigital tinea pedis."]],
  "cell": "Like other imidazoles, sertaconazole binds the heme iron of lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51) and blocks ergosterol production, so methylated sterols accumulate and the fungal membrane becomes disordered (a fungistatic effect). Its benzothiophene ring is thought to resemble the amino acid tryptophan and to insert directly into the fungal membrane, forming pores that let cell contents such as ATP leak out. This direct membrane damage can kill fungi at higher concentrations. Laboratory studies also suggest anti-inflammatory and anti-itch activity, including reduced release of inflammatory mediators.",
  "body": [["Skin between the toes", "It clears dermatophyte infection in interdigital athlete's foot by blocking membrane building and damaging the membrane directly."], ["Inflamed skin", "Proposed anti-inflammatory effects may ease itching as the infection clears."], ["Application site", "Contact dermatitis, dry skin and burning can occur where it is applied."]],
  "journey": "Sertaconazole is applied to dry skin twice daily and stays in the outer layers of the skin, helped by its high fat solubility. Studies after repeated application found no detectable drug in the blood, so it acts only where it is applied.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are local. Burning and dryness are irritation of skin that is already inflamed. Contact dermatitis and sensitization are allergic responses. Systemic effects do not occur because the drug is not measurably absorbed. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Ertaczo (sertaconazole) FDA label", "Croxtall JD, Plosker GL. Sertaconazole: a review. Drugs, 2009"]
},
"ethionamide": {
  "origin": "Ethionamide grew out of the search for drugs related to isoniazid, the breakthrough TB drug of 1952. Isoniazid is a derivative of isonicotinic acid, and chemists in France made a series of thioamide versions of that core. In 1956 a group working with the French company Theraplix reported that ethionamide (2-ethylthioisonicotinamide) was strongly active against tuberculosis. It was too hard on the stomach to become a first-line drug, but it became an important second-line agent and is sold in the US as Trecator for drug-resistant TB.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Isoniazid is introduced, prompting a search for related compounds."], ["1956", "Ethionamide is reported as an antituberculosis thioamide in France."], ["1990s", "Ethionamide becomes a standard part of many multidrug-resistant TB regimens."]],
  "cell": "Ethionamide is a prodrug. Inside Mycobacterium tuberculosis, the flavin monooxygenase EthA (whose production is controlled by the repressor EthR) oxidizes it to a reactive intermediate. That intermediate joins with NAD to form an ethionamide-NAD adduct, which tightly inhibits InhA, the enoyl-acyl carrier protein reductase of the type II fatty acid synthase. InhA is needed to lengthen the long fatty acid chains that become mycolic acids, the waxy core of the mycobacterial cell wall. Without mycolic acids the wall cannot be built and growth stops (mostly bacteriostatic). Isoniazid hits the same enzyme but is activated by a different enzyme, KatG. So bacteria with katG mutations often stay sensitive to ethionamide, while mutations in the inhA promoter cause resistance to both drugs.",
  "body": [["TB bacteria", "It blocks mycolic acid production so the bacilli cannot build their protective cell wall, which helps treat TB that resists isoniazid or rifampin."], ["Stomach and gut", "Nausea, vomiting, metallic taste, drooling and loss of appetite are very common and often limit use."], ["Liver", "It can inflame the liver, from mild enzyme rises to serious hepatitis."], ["Thyroid", "It resembles the antithyroid drug methimazole and can cause hypothyroidism and goiter, more so with PAS."], ["Nerves and eyes", "Peripheral neuropathy and, rarely, optic neuritis can occur; pyridoxine is given alongside."], ["Brain and mood", "Depression, drowsiness and, rarely, psychotic reactions can occur, adding to the effects of cycloserine."], ["Blood sugar", "It can lower blood glucose, which matters in people with diabetes."]],
  "journey": "Ethionamide is taken by mouth, often with food or at bedtime to ease nausea. It is absorbed well, peaks in about an hour and spreads widely through the body, including into the cerebrospinal fluid, which makes it useful in TB meningitis. The liver extensively metabolizes it, including to ethionamide sulfoxide, which is also active. Less than 1 percent leaves unchanged; the rest is excreted in the urine as metabolites. The half-life is about two hours.",
  "sidefx": "The gut effects are thought to come partly from direct action on the brain's vomiting centers and partly from local irritation. Hepatitis reflects liver injury from reactive metabolites. Hypothyroidism happens because its thioamide structure, like methimazole, interferes with thyroid hormone synthesis. Neuropathy may relate to interference with pyridoxine (vitamin B6) use, so B6 is given. Combining it with cycloserine or isoniazid adds nerve and brain toxicity. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Trecator (ethionamide) FDA label", "Vannelli TA et al. The antituberculosis drug ethionamide is activated by a flavoprotein monooxygenase. J Biol Chem, 2002", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"indacaterol": {
  "origin": "Indacaterol was designed by Novartis in the 2000s to be a truly once-daily beta2 agonist for COPD. Older long-acting agents such as salmeterol and formoterol last about twelve hours. Novartis chemists built a molecule that stays in the lipid layers of airway smooth muscle cell membranes and keeps stimulating receptors for about a day. It was approved in Europe in 2009 and by the FDA in 2011 as Arcapta Neohaler, the first ultra-long-acting beta agonist in the US. It was also combined with the anticholinergic glycopyrrolate as Utibron Neohaler. US availability of these brands has since become limited.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Novartis develops indacaterol as a once-daily beta2 agonist."], ["2009", "Indacaterol is approved in the European Union for COPD."], ["2011", "FDA approves Arcapta Neohaler for COPD maintenance."], ["2015", "FDA approves Utibron Neohaler (indacaterol and glycopyrrolate)."]],
  "cell": "Indacaterol is a selective agonist at beta2-adrenergic receptors on airway smooth muscle. Binding activates the stimulatory G protein Gs, which turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cyclic AMP. Cyclic AMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates targets that lower intracellular calcium and inactivate myosin light chain kinase, while opening calcium-activated potassium channels. The muscle relaxes and the airway widens. Indacaterol is a high-efficacy agonist with a fast onset like formoterol. Its long action is thought to come from its affinity for lipid raft regions of the cell membrane, which act as a local depot that keeps feeding drug to the receptor for about 24 hours.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Relaxed smooth muscle keeps the bronchi open for about a day, easing breathlessness and improving exercise ability in COPD."], ["Heart", "Some drug reaches beta receptors in the heart, which can raise heart rate and, rarely, trigger arrhythmias."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Beta2 stimulation can cause tremor and muscle cramps."], ["Potassium and glucose", "Beta2 stimulation drives potassium into cells and releases glucose, so blood potassium can fall and blood sugar can rise."], ["Throat", "A brief cough right after inhaling the powder is common."]],
  "journey": "The dry powder is inhaled from a pierced capsule, and much of it deposits in the airways while the swallowed part is absorbed from the gut. Plasma levels peak about fifteen minutes after a dose, and bronchodilation starts within about five minutes. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely. The liver clears it through UGT1A1 glucuronidation and CYP3A4 hydroxylation, and it is a P-glycoprotein substrate. Most leaves in the feces, with less than 2 percent in urine. The effective half-life is about two days, and steady state comes after about two weeks of daily use.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from beta2 stimulation outside the lungs: tremor, fast heart rate, low potassium and high blood sugar. Cough after inhalation is local airway irritation. Paradoxical bronchospasm is a rare reaction to the inhaled powder. Long-acting beta agonists used alone in asthma were linked to asthma-related deaths, so indacaterol is approved only for COPD and is not a rescue inhaler. Nonselective beta blockers block its effect. Overdose is treated with supportive care; a cardioselective beta blocker may be used with great caution.",
  "src": ["Arcapta Neohaler (indacaterol) FDA label", "GOLD Report"]
},
"glycopyrronium-topical": {
  "origin": "Glycopyrronium (glycopyrrolate) is a synthetic anticholinergic that was first approved in the US in 1961 as Robinul tablets for peptic ulcer disease, and it later became a standard drug in anesthesia to dry secretions and protect against slow heart rates. Because it is a quaternary ammonium compound, it carries a permanent positive charge and does not easily cross membranes such as the blood-brain barrier. The company Dermira used that property to create a topical treatment for underarm sweating. A pre-moistened cloth containing glycopyrronium tosylate was approved by the FDA in 2018 as Qbrexza, the first topical anticholinergic for primary axillary hyperhidrosis.",
  "timeline": [["1961", "FDA approves glycopyrrolate tablets (Robinul) for peptic ulcer."], ["1970s", "Injectable glycopyrrolate becomes common in anesthesia."], ["2018", "FDA approves Qbrexza cloths for primary axillary hyperhidrosis."]],
  "cell": "Eccrine sweat glands are unusual: they are driven by sympathetic nerves, but those nerves release acetylcholine, which acts on M3 muscarinic receptors on the gland cells. M3 receptors signal through Gq, activating phospholipase C, raising IP3 and releasing intracellular calcium, which drives the secretion of sweat. Glycopyrronium is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic receptors. Applied to the underarm skin, it diffuses to the glands, occupies the M3 receptors and blocks acetylcholine, so the gland cells are not signaled to secrete and sweat output falls.",
  "body": [["Underarm sweat glands", "Blocking muscarinic receptors on eccrine glands reduces underarm sweating within the first week of daily use."], ["Mouth", "Some absorbed drug blocks salivary gland receptors and causes dry mouth, the most common side effect."], ["Eyes", "Drug left on the fingers can reach the eye and dilate the pupil, causing blurred vision, sometimes in one eye only."], ["Bladder", "Absorbed drug can relax the bladder muscle and cause urinary hesitation or retention."], ["Temperature control", "Less sweating means less cooling, so heat illness is possible in hot environments."], ["Skin", "Pain, redness and itching can occur where it is applied."]],
  "journey": "The cloth is wiped across each underarm once daily, and the drug moves into the skin around the sweat glands. Only a small amount is absorbed into the blood. Because the molecule is permanently charged, very little crosses into the brain. Any glycopyrronium that reaches the blood is metabolized little and is cleared mainly by the kidneys, largely unchanged. The local effect lasts about a day per application.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects are classic anticholinergic effects from the small amount absorbed: dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation and urinary hesitation. Pupil dilation and blurred vision usually mean drug on the hands touched the eye, which is why hands are washed after use. Reduced sweating lowers heat loss. Effects add to other anticholinergic drugs. Physostigmine reverses anticholinergic toxicity in general, but it does little for the peripheral effects of a quaternary drug and is rarely needed with topical use.",
  "src": ["Qbrexza (glycopyrronium) FDA label", "Robinul (glycopyrrolate) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"penciclovir": {
  "origin": "Penciclovir was developed in the 1980s by researchers at Beecham Pharmaceuticals in the UK (later SmithKline Beecham) as an acyclovir-like guanosine analog with a longer-lasting active form. It is absorbed poorly by mouth, so the company also made famciclovir, an oral prodrug that the body converts into penciclovir. For skin use, penciclovir itself was formulated as a cream. The FDA approved penciclovir cream as Denavir in 1996 for recurrent cold sores on the lips and face.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Beecham researchers develop penciclovir and its oral prodrug famciclovir."], ["1994", "FDA approves famciclovir, the oral prodrug of penciclovir."], ["1996", "FDA approves Denavir (penciclovir) cream for cold sores."]],
  "cell": "Penciclovir is a guanosine analog. In herpes simplex-infected skin cells, the viral thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate, a step that uninfected cells do poorly, so the drug concentrates where the virus is. Cellular kinases then add two more phosphates to make penciclovir triphosphate. The triphosphate competes with deoxyguanosine triphosphate for the viral DNA polymerase and slows viral DNA synthesis. Unlike acyclovir, penciclovir keeps a hydroxyl group that allows limited chain extension, so it is not an obligate chain terminator. Its triphosphate lasts much longer inside infected cells (about 7 to 20 hours) than acyclovir triphosphate, which supports its use in a short, frequent course.",
  "body": [["Lips and facial skin", "It slows viral copying in infected skin cells, so cold sores heal somewhat faster and pain and viral shedding end sooner, especially if started at the first tingle."], ["Application site", "Mild redness or irritation can occur where the cream is applied."], ["Rest of the body", "With topical use, so little enters the blood that effects elsewhere are not expected."]],
  "journey": "Penciclovir cream is applied to the lesion every two hours while awake, starting at the earliest sign. The drug moves into the outer skin cells, where virus-infected cells trap it by phosphorylation. Blood levels after topical use are too low to measure. When penciclovir does reach the blood (as from oral famciclovir), it is cleared mainly by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are minor and local, such as redness or a mild reaction at the site, because the drug needs viral thymidine kinase for activation and barely reaches the blood. Headache and altered taste have been reported in trials but were similar to placebo cream. There is no antidote and none is needed.",
  "src": ["Denavir (penciclovir) FDA label", "Famvir (famciclovir) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"sinecatechins": {
  "origin": "Sinecatechins comes from green tea, the leaves of Camellia sinensis. Green tea polyphenols called catechins, especially epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG), had long been studied for antioxidant and antitumor effects in the lab. The German company MediGene developed a partially purified extract of green tea leaves as an ointment for genital warts. In 2006 the FDA approved it as Veregen, the first botanical drug approved under the FDA's botanical drug guidance. A botanical drug is a mixture from a plant rather than a single purified chemical, so its quality is controlled by a standardized manufacturing process and chemical profile.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Green tea from Camellia sinensis is used as a drink in China."], ["1990s", "Green tea catechins are studied for antiviral, antioxidant and antitumor effects."], ["2006", "FDA approves Veregen, the first botanical drug under its botanical guidance, for external genital warts."]],
  "cell": "The exact way sinecatechins clears warts is not known. The extract is mostly catechins, with EGCG the largest part. Laboratory studies suggest several actions. Catechins are antioxidants and may change how keratinocytes infected with human papillomavirus grow, slowing proliferation and encouraging apoptosis (programmed cell death). They may inhibit transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1 that support infected cell growth. They may also affect local immune signaling, including activation of immune cells that recognize infected tissue. Clinical trials show the ointment clears warts better than its vehicle, but how much each action contributes is not established.",
  "body": [["Genital and perianal skin", "Over weeks, warts shrink and clear in many people, likely through effects on infected skin cells and local immunity."], ["Application site", "Redness, itching, burning, pain, swelling and erosions are very common and reflect local inflammation as warts are attacked."], ["Foreskin and urethra", "In uncircumcised males, inflammation under the foreskin can cause phimosis, and lesions near the urethral opening can lead to meatal narrowing."], ["Barrier contraceptives", "The ointment can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms."]],
  "journey": "The ointment is dabbed on the warts several times a day for up to about four months and is not washed off between applications. It acts in the skin. Studies found negligible levels of catechins in the blood, similar to what is seen after drinking green tea, so body-wide effects are not expected.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local inflammation, which may be part of how the treatment works: redness, itching, burning, swelling and sometimes ulceration or blisters. Swollen lymph nodes in the groin can reflect this local immune activity. Phimosis and urethral narrowing happen when inflammation scars delicate tissue. The ointment's oily base weakens latex. It does not cure HPV infection, so new warts can appear. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Veregen was the first prescription drug approved by the FDA under its botanical drug pathway, which allows a complex plant mixture to be approved as a drug rather than requiring a single purified molecule.",
  "src": ["Veregen (sinecatechins) FDA label", "FDA Guidance for Industry: Botanical Drug Development", "Tatti S et al. Sinecatechins in the treatment of external genital warts. Br J Dermatol, 2010"]
},
"cantharidin": {
  "origin": "Cantharidin is a natural poison made by blister beetles, especially the emerald beetle Lytta vesicatoria, known as Spanish fly. The beetles release it as a defense, and it blisters skin on contact. Dried, powdered beetles were used in medicine since ancient times to raise blisters, a common treatment in older medicine, and they were also infamous as a dangerous supposed aphrodisiac that caused deaths. In 1810 the French chemist Pierre Robiquet isolated cantharidin as the active compound. For decades dermatologists used compounded cantharidin to treat warts and molluscum without an FDA-approved product. In 2023 the FDA approved Ycanth, a standardized cantharidin solution from Verrica Pharmaceuticals, as the first approved treatment for molluscum contagiosum.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Powdered blister beetles are used to raise blisters on the skin."], ["1810", "Pierre Robiquet isolates cantharidin from blister beetles."], ["1950s", "Dermatologists begin using cantharidin to treat warts and molluscum."], ["2023", "FDA approves Ycanth, the first approved treatment for molluscum contagiosum."]],
  "cell": "Cantharidin inhibits protein phosphatase 2A and, to a lesser degree, protein phosphatase 1, enzymes that remove phosphate groups from many cell proteins. In the epidermis, keratinocytes are joined by desmosomes. When phosphatases are blocked, desmosomal proteins stay abnormally phosphorylated, the desmosomes break down and the cells separate from each other. This process, called acantholysis, opens a fluid-filled blister inside the epidermis, just above the basal layer. The blister lifts off the molluscum lesion, which contains the poxvirus-filled cells, and the area heals without scarring in most cases because the deeper dermis is not damaged. Cantharidin does not directly act on the virus.",
  "body": [["Skin at treated bumps", "A blister forms within about a day, lifting the molluscum lesion away; it crusts and heals over about a week."], ["Surrounding skin", "Pain, itching, redness and temporary dark or light spots can occur, especially if the liquid spreads beyond the lesion."], ["Eyes and mucous membranes", "Accidental contact causes severe irritation and blistering, so it is kept away from the eyes, lips and mucosa."], ["Gut and kidneys if swallowed", "Ingestion causes severe burns of the mouth and gut, bleeding and kidney failure, and can be fatal."]],
  "journey": "A health professional paints the solution onto each lesion with a single-use applicator, and the liquid dries into a film. A purple dye marks treated spots. The drug is washed off with soap and water after about 24 hours, or sooner if blistering is severe. Very little is absorbed through intact skin. When cantharidin is swallowed, it is absorbed from the gut and excreted by the kidneys, where it damages the tubules and bladder lining.",
  "sidefx": "Blistering is the intended effect, but the same phosphatase block can blister too much skin if the solution spreads or is left on too long, causing pain, infection and pigment changes. It is extremely toxic if swallowed: it blisters the mouth, esophagus and stomach, causes gut bleeding and injures the kidneys and urinary tract, which is why historic Spanish fly poisonings were deadly. The product is flammable. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Cantharidin is often nicknamed beetle juice by dermatologists, and the beetles themselves were once a feared poison despite their reputation as a love potion.",
  "src": ["Ycanth (cantharidin) FDA label", "Moed L et al. Cantharidin revisited. Arch Dermatol, 2001", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"tioconazole": {
  "origin": "Tioconazole is an imidazole antifungal developed by Pfizer researchers in the UK in the 1970s, part of the wave of azole antifungals that followed clotrimazole and miconazole. It was used topically for skin and nail infections and was formulated as a single-dose vaginal ointment. In the US it became an over-the-counter one-day treatment for vaginal yeast infections, sold as Vagistat-1 and Monistat 1.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Pfizer researchers develop tioconazole."], ["1990s", "Single-dose tioconazole vaginal ointment becomes available over the counter in the US."]],
  "cell": "Tioconazole blocks lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme that removes a methyl group from lanosterol on the way to ergosterol. The imidazole nitrogen binds the heme iron in the enzyme. Ergosterol levels fall and toxic methylated sterols build up, so the Candida membrane becomes disordered and leaky. At the high concentrations reached in the vagina, azoles can also directly damage the membrane and kill the yeast. The ointment acts on Candida albicans and many other Candida species.",
  "body": [["Vagina", "The ointment stays in contact with the vaginal lining for several days and kills Candida, so itching, discharge and burning improve over one to three days."], ["Vulva", "Local burning, itching or irritation can occur, especially when the skin is already inflamed."], ["Barrier contraceptives", "The ointment base can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms."]],
  "journey": "A single applicator of ointment is inserted into the vagina at bedtime. The drug stays in the vaginal fluid and lining for days, which is why one dose is enough. Only a small amount is absorbed into the blood, and any absorbed drug is handled by the liver. Systemic levels are very low.",
  "sidefx": "Burning, itching and irritation are local effects on inflamed tissue. Allergic reactions are uncommon. Although systemic absorption is small, case reports have linked vaginal azoles with increased INR and bleeding in people taking warfarin, likely because the small absorbed amount still inhibits CYP2C9. The oily base can weaken latex. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Monistat 1 (tioconazole) Drug Facts label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mecamylamine": {
  "origin": "Mecamylamine came from the era when high blood pressure was treated by blocking the autonomic ganglia. Early ganglion blockers such as hexamethonium were charged molecules that were poorly absorbed by mouth. In the mid-1950s Merck researchers introduced mecamylamine, a secondary amine that is well absorbed orally, as Inversine in 1956. It lowered blood pressure strongly, but because it blocks all autonomic ganglia it caused many side effects, and it was largely replaced by newer drugs in the 1960s and later. It remains available in the US as Vecamyl. Because it crosses into the brain and blocks nicotinic receptors, it has also been studied in smoking cessation and Tourette syndrome.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Hexamethonium and other ganglion blockers are used for severe hypertension."], ["1956", "Merck introduces mecamylamine as Inversine."], ["1960s", "Newer antihypertensives begin replacing ganglion blockers."], ["2013", "Mecamylamine is relaunched in the US as Vecamyl."]],
  "cell": "Mecamylamine is a noncompetitive antagonist of neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (the Nn type). These receptors are ligand-gated cation channels on the postganglionic neurons of both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia. Normally, acetylcholine from preganglionic fibers opens these channels and fires the postganglionic neuron. Mecamylamine blocks the open channel, so the signal is not passed on. Losing sympathetic tone dilates arterioles and veins, which lowers blood pressure, especially when standing, when reflex vasoconstriction is needed most. The effect on any organ depends on which branch normally dominates there: in blood vessels the sympathetic system dominates, while in the gut, bladder, eye and salivary glands the parasympathetic system dominates, so those organs show anticholinergic-like effects.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Loss of sympathetic tone widens arteries and veins, so blood pressure falls sharply, most noticeably on standing."], ["Heart", "Heart rate often rises modestly because vagal tone to the SA node is also blocked."], ["Gut", "Loss of parasympathetic drive slows the bowel, causing constipation and, at worst, paralytic ileus."], ["Bladder", "The bladder contracts poorly, causing hesitancy or retention."], ["Eyes", "The pupils dilate and focusing for near vision is impaired, causing blurred vision."], ["Mouth and sexual function", "Saliva production falls, and impotence and reduced libido are common."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain, where it can cause tremor, confusion or mental changes."]],
  "journey": "Mecamylamine is taken by mouth and absorbed almost completely from the gut. It acts within about half an hour to two hours, with peak effect in about three to five hours. Because it is not permanently charged, it crosses into the brain and placenta. It is metabolized very little and is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. Acidic urine speeds its removal, and alkaline urine slows it, so drugs such as sodium bicarbonate or acetazolamide raise levels. Its action lasts many hours.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect follows from blocking both branches of the autonomic nervous system at once. Orthostatic hypotension and fainting come from loss of sympathetic reflexes. Dry mouth, constipation, ileus, urinary retention and blurred vision come from loss of parasympathetic tone. Impotence reflects blocked parasympathetic erection and sympathetic ejaculation. Brain effects such as tremor and confusion come from central nicotinic blockade. There is no specific antidote; severe low blood pressure is treated with fluids, positioning and vasopressors.",
  "fun": "Mecamylamine is one of the few drugs that blocks both the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems at the same time, which is why it is a favorite example in autonomic pharmacology teaching.",
  "src": ["Vecamyl (mecamylamine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ketorolac-nasal": {
  "origin": "Ketorolac was developed by the Syntex company in California in the 1980s as a strong NSAID that could be given by injection as an alternative to opioids for short-term pain. The injection was approved in the US in 1989 as Toradol, and tablets followed. To offer opioid-level relief without needles, the drug was later formulated as a nasal spray. The FDA approved intranasal ketorolac as Sprix in 2010 for short-term management of moderate to moderately severe pain in adults, with the same five-day total limit as other ketorolac forms.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Syntex develops ketorolac as a potent NSAID analgesic."], ["1989", "FDA approves ketorolac injection (Toradol)."], ["2010", "FDA approves Sprix ketorolac nasal spray."]],
  "cell": "Ketorolac is a nonselective inhibitor of cyclooxygenase, blocking both COX-1 and COX-2, with somewhat more potency at COX-1. These enzymes convert arachidonic acid, released from cell membranes by phospholipase A2, into prostaglandin H2, the precursor of PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. At sites of injury, PGE2 sensitizes nociceptors so they fire more easily; in the spinal cord, prostaglandins enhance pain transmission. Blocking their production reduces this peripheral and central sensitization, giving strong pain relief. Less thromboxane in platelets reduces aggregation, and less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever.",
  "body": [["Pain pathways", "Lower prostaglandin levels make pain nerve endings and spinal pain pathways less sensitive, giving relief comparable to moderate opioid doses for short-term pain."], ["Nose", "The spray can cause nasal discomfort, burning and watery eyes as it contacts the nasal lining."], ["Stomach", "Loss of COX-1 prostaglandins weakens the stomach's protective mucus and blood flow, so ulcers and bleeding can occur, even with nasal use."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins help keep kidney blood flow up when it is stressed; blocking them can cause acute kidney injury, low urine output and high potassium."], ["Platelets", "Less thromboxane means platelets clump less, raising bleeding risk."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs, it raises the risk of heart attack and stroke and can raise blood pressure and cause fluid retention."]],
  "journey": "The spray is absorbed through the rich blood supply of the nasal lining, bypassing first-pass liver metabolism and reaching the blood quickly, with pain relief in about twenty to thirty minutes. Blood levels peak in under an hour. Ketorolac is more than 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver hydroxylates and glucuronidates it, and about 90 percent leaves in the urine. The half-life is about five to six hours, longer in older adults and kidney disease.",
  "sidefx": "Ketorolac is one of the most potent COX-1 blockers, which explains its high rate of stomach bleeding and its effects on platelets. Kidney injury happens because prostaglandins are needed to keep the afferent arterioles open when blood volume is low. High potassium follows from reduced renin release. Bronchospasm can occur in aspirin-sensitive asthma, where blocking COX shunts arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes. Cardiovascular risk relates to less prostacyclin in vessel walls. Treatment is limited to five days total for these reasons. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Sprix (ketorolac tromethamine) nasal spray FDA label", "Toradol (ketorolac) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"fenoprofen": {
  "origin": "Fenoprofen is a propionic acid NSAID, in the same family as ibuprofen and naproxen. It was developed by Eli Lilly in the late 1960s and early 1970s, and the FDA approved it as Nalfon in 1976 for arthritis and pain. It never became as common as ibuprofen, but it remains available as generic capsules and tablets. It is noted in pharmacology texts for a relatively high association with kidney problems such as interstitial nephritis and nephrotic syndrome.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "British researchers introduce ibuprofen, starting the propionic acid NSAID family."], ["1976", "FDA approves Nalfon (fenoprofen) for arthritis and pain."]],
  "cell": "Fenoprofen reversibly inhibits both cyclooxygenase isoforms, COX-1 and COX-2. These enzymes turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, which becomes PGE2, prostacyclin and thromboxane A2. In inflamed joints, less PGE2 means less vasodilation, swelling and nociceptor sensitization, so pain and stiffness ease. Less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever. Reduced platelet thromboxane mildly impairs aggregation, but because the binding is reversible this effect fades as the drug leaves, unlike aspirin.",
  "body": [["Joints", "Lower prostaglandin levels reduce pain, swelling and stiffness in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis."], ["Pain nerves", "Less PGE2 makes pain nerve endings less sensitive, giving relief for mild to moderate pain."], ["Stomach", "Loss of protective prostaglandins can cause indigestion, ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "It can reduce kidney blood flow and is linked with interstitial nephritis and nephrotic syndrome, sometimes with heavy protein loss."], ["Ears", "Tinnitus and reduced hearing can occur, as with other NSAIDs at higher levels."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs it raises the risk of heart attack and stroke and can cause fluid retention."]],
  "journey": "Fenoprofen is taken by mouth and absorbed well; food slows absorption a little. It peaks in about one to two hours and is about 99 percent bound to albumin. The liver converts it to glucuronide conjugates and a 4-hydroxy metabolite, and about 90 percent leaves in the urine as conjugates. The half-life is about three hours, so it is taken several times a day.",
  "sidefx": "Stomach ulcers and bleeding come from loss of COX-1 prostaglandins that protect the stomach lining. Kidney injury has two causes: reduced prostaglandin support of kidney blood flow, and an immune reaction in the kidney tissue (interstitial nephritis), which can come with nephrotic-range protein loss. High potassium follows from reduced renin. Heart attack and stroke risk relates to the imbalance between prostacyclin and thromboxane. Asthma attacks can occur in aspirin-sensitive people. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Nalfon (fenoprofen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"meclofenamate": {
  "origin": "Meclofenamate belongs to the fenamates, NSAIDs built on an anthranilic acid (N-phenylanthranilic acid) core. Researchers at Parke-Davis in Michigan developed the fenamate family in the 1960s, including mefenamic acid and flufenamic acid, and then meclofenamic acid. Meclofenamate sodium was approved in the US around 1980 as Meclomen for arthritis and was later also labeled for menstrual cramps and heavy menstrual bleeding. The Meclomen brand has been discontinued, but generic capsules remain.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Parke-Davis researchers develop the fenamate NSAIDs."], ["1980", "Meclofenamate sodium (Meclomen) is approved in the US for arthritis."]],
  "cell": "Meclofenamate inhibits both cyclooxygenase isoforms, COX-1 and COX-2, so arachidonic acid is not converted to prostaglandin H2 and downstream PGE2, PGF2-alpha, prostacyclin and thromboxane fall. Fenamates are unusual because they also appear to block prostaglandin receptors directly, so they reduce both the making and the action of prostaglandins. Lab studies also show some inhibition of 5-lipoxygenase. In the uterus, PGF2-alpha and PGE2 drive strong contractions and pain in dysmenorrhea, and prostaglandins in the endometrium promote heavy bleeding; reducing them eases cramps and reduces menstrual blood loss. In joints, less PGE2 means less swelling and pain.",
  "body": [["Uterus", "Fewer prostaglandins mean weaker, less painful uterine contractions and less menstrual blood loss."], ["Joints", "Less inflammation and pain in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is notably common with fenamates, along with nausea, indigestion, ulcers and bleeding."], ["Kidneys", "Reduced prostaglandin support of kidney blood flow can cause acute kidney injury and high potassium."], ["Blood", "Platelet function is reduced, and it enhances the effect of warfarin; rare hemolytic anemia has been reported."], ["Heart and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs it raises the risk of heart attack, stroke and fluid retention."]],
  "journey": "Meclofenamate is taken by mouth and absorbed quickly, peaking within about half an hour to two hours. More than 99 percent is bound to albumin. The liver oxidizes it to an active 3-hydroxymethyl metabolite that lasts longer than the parent and then glucuronidates it. About two thirds leaves in the urine and the rest in the feces. The parent half-life is about one to three hours.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea is a hallmark of fenamates and may reflect effects on intestinal fluid and prostaglandin balance; it can be severe enough to stop the drug. Ulcers and bleeding come from loss of protective COX-1 prostaglandins in the stomach. Kidney injury and high potassium follow from blocked kidney prostaglandins. Bleeding risk rises with warfarin because of both platelet effects and protein-binding interactions. Cardiovascular risk relates to less vascular prostacyclin. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Meclofenamate sodium capsules FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"mometasone-sinus-implant": {
  "origin": "The mometasone sinus implant joined two older ideas: a potent topical steroid and a dissolvable stent. Mometasone furoate was developed by Schering-Plough in the 1980s and became widely used in nasal sprays and skin products. Intersect ENT, a California company, designed bioabsorbable steroid-releasing sinus implants. Its first product, a small stent placed during sinus surgery, was cleared in 2011. Sinuva, a larger spring-like implant that an ENT physician places in the office for nasal polyps that return after ethmoid surgery, was approved by the FDA as a drug in 2017. It offered an option between daily sprays and repeat surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Schering-Plough develops mometasone furoate."], ["2011", "Intersect ENT's first steroid-releasing sinus stent is cleared for use after sinus surgery."], ["2017", "FDA approves Sinuva sinus implant for recurrent nasal polyps after ethmoid surgery."]],
  "cell": "Mometasone furoate is a lipophilic glucocorticoid with very high affinity for the glucocorticoid receptor in the cytoplasm of nasal and sinus lining cells and immune cells. Binding releases the receptor from heat shock proteins, and the drug-receptor complex moves into the nucleus. There it switches on anti-inflammatory genes (transactivation, such as lipocortin-1 and MAP kinase phosphatase-1) and represses pro-inflammatory transcription factors such as NF-kappa B and AP-1 (transrepression). The result is fewer cytokines (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13), less eosinophil survival and recruitment, fewer mast cells and less tissue swelling. Nasal polyps in chronic rhinosinusitis are driven largely by this type 2 eosinophilic inflammation, so they shrink.",
  "body": [["Ethmoid sinus and polyps", "Steady local steroid release over about 90 days shrinks polyps and swelling, opening blocked sinus passages."], ["Nose", "Patients often notice less congestion and obstruction and may avoid repeat surgery."], ["Nasal lining", "Nosebleeds and local irritation can occur, and the implant can move or become infected."], ["Rest of the body", "Plasma levels are very low and mostly unmeasurable after the first weeks, so body-wide steroid effects are unlikely."]],
  "journey": "The implant is collapsed in a delivery tool, pushed through the nostril into the ethmoid sinus under endoscopic guidance and allowed to spring open against the sinus walls. Its polymer slowly dissolves and releases mometasone directly onto the polyp tissue for up to 90 days, with the highest tissue levels in the early weeks. Only a small amount reaches the blood. What does enter the blood is extensively metabolized by liver CYP3A4 and excreted mainly in bile and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local: nosebleeds, irritation, and implant migration or infection, which may require removal. Because steroids suppress local immunity, infections can occur. Systemic steroid effects such as adrenal suppression are unlikely because blood levels are very low, but strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir could raise exposure. Very rarely, intranasal steroids are linked to glaucoma or cataracts. There is no antidote; the implant can be removed.",
  "src": ["Sinuva (mometasone furoate) sinus implant FDA label", "Kern RC et al. Bioabsorbable steroid-eluting implants for recurrent nasal polyps. Int Forum Allergy Rhinol, 2018"]
},
"fluticasone-exhalation-nasal": {
  "origin": "Fluticasone propionate was developed by Glaxo in the 1980s and became famous as Flonase nasal spray, approved in the US in 1994. Standard sprays deposit much of the drug at the front of the nose, away from the sinus openings. The company OptiNose built a breath-powered exhalation delivery system: the user blows into a mouthpiece, which closes the soft palate and pushes the spray high and deep into the nasal passages. The FDA approved this product as Xhance in 2017 for nasal polyps and later added chronic rhinosinusitis without polyps.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Glaxo develops fluticasone propionate."], ["1994", "FDA approves Flonase fluticasone nasal spray."], ["2017", "FDA approves Xhance exhalation delivery system for nasal polyps."], ["2024", "FDA expands Xhance approval to chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps."]],
  "cell": "Fluticasone propionate is a highly lipophilic glucocorticoid with strong affinity for the glucocorticoid receptor. In cells of the nasal and sinus lining and in immune cells, binding sends the receptor into the nucleus, where it turns on anti-inflammatory genes and shuts down NF-kappa B and AP-1 driven inflammatory genes. Cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13 fall, eosinophils and mast cells decline, and blood vessel leakiness and mucus production drop. The device matters because chronic sinus disease sits in the upper and back parts of the nose around the sinus drainage pathways, and the exhalation system delivers more drug to those regions than a standard spray.",
  "body": [["Sinus drainage pathways", "More steroid reaches the middle meatus and upper nasal areas, reducing swelling so sinuses can drain and ventilate."], ["Nasal polyps", "Eosinophilic inflammation is suppressed, so polyps shrink and congestion improves."], ["Nasal lining", "Nosebleeds, irritation and, rarely, septal ulcers or perforation can occur from local steroid and device contact."], ["Eyes", "Long-term intranasal steroid use is associated with a small risk of glaucoma and cataracts."], ["Adrenal glands", "Usually little effect, but with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as ritonavir, fluticasone levels can rise enough to cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression."]],
  "journey": "The user places the sealing nosepiece in one nostril and blows into the mouthpiece, which closes the soft palate and carries the spray along the nasal passage; little reaches the throat or lungs. The drug acts on the nasal lining. Any swallowed fluticasone is almost entirely removed by first-pass metabolism in the liver, and absorbed drug is converted by CYP3A4 to an inactive carboxylic acid metabolite. It leaves mainly in the feces through bile. Symptom relief builds over days to weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Local steroid effects explain nosebleeds, irritation, nasal Candida infection and septal ulcers or perforation, as steroids thin the lining and slow healing. Systemic effects are uncommon because of low absorption and high first-pass metabolism, but blocking CYP3A4 with ritonavir or cobicistat prevents breakdown and can cause Cushing syndrome and adrenal suppression. Long-term use may slightly raise eye pressure or speed cataracts. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Xhance (fluticasone propionate) FDA label", "Flonase (fluticasone propionate) FDA label"]
},
"prothrombin-complex-concentrate": {
  "origin": "Prothrombin complex concentrates were first made in the 1960s and 1970s by fractionating donated human plasma. The early products were three-factor concentrates used mostly for hemophilia B, before purified factor IX existed. In Europe, four-factor concentrates containing factors II, VII, IX and X were used for decades to reverse vitamin K antagonists such as warfarin. In the US, CSL Behring's Kcentra became the first FDA-approved four-factor PCC in 2013 for urgent warfarin reversal with major bleeding, and a second product, Octapharma's Balfaxar, was approved in 2023. Viral inactivation steps and added anticoagulant proteins have made modern products safer than early concentrates. Four-factor PCC is also widely used, outside its labeled indication, to help manage bleeding with factor Xa inhibitors.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Plasma-derived concentrates of vitamin K-dependent factors are developed."], ["1990s", "Four-factor PCCs are used in Europe for warfarin reversal."], ["2013", "FDA approves Kcentra, the first four-factor PCC in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves Balfaxar, a second four-factor PCC."]],
  "cell": "Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase in the liver, so the liver makes factors II, VII, IX and X (and proteins C and S) without their gamma-carboxyglutamate groups. Without those groups, the factors cannot bind calcium and phospholipid on activated platelets, and clotting fails. Four-factor PCC delivers fully carboxylated, functional factors II, VII, IX and X purified from donor plasma, along with the natural anticoagulant proteins C and S, and in some products heparin and antithrombin to limit unwanted activation. Once infused, these factors join the coagulation cascade at once: factor VIIa with tissue factor activates IX and X, the tenase and prothrombinase complexes form, and prothrombin is converted to thrombin, which turns fibrinogen into fibrin. The INR falls within minutes. Because the factors are replaced directly, it works much faster than vitamin K, which needs the liver to make new factors over many hours.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Replacing missing vitamin K-dependent factors restores thrombin generation and brings the INR down within about half an hour."], ["Bleeding sites", "Stopping bleeding in the brain, gut or after trauma depends on restoring clot formation quickly, which PCC does with a small volume."], ["Circulation", "The small infusion volume avoids the fluid overload that large amounts of plasma can cause."], ["Veins and arteries", "Pushing the system toward clotting can cause unwanted clots such as DVT, PE, stroke or heart attack."], ["Immune system", "As a plasma-derived product, allergic reactions are possible, and there is a small theoretical risk of transmitting infections."]],
  "journey": "PCC is reconstituted and given by vein, so the factors are in the blood immediately and the INR corrects within about thirty minutes. Each factor then behaves like the body's own: factor VII fades in about four hours, factor IX in about a day, factor X in about a day and a half, and factor II over two to three days. They are used up in clotting and broken down like other plasma proteins. Because factor VII wears off first while warfarin is still present, vitamin K is given at the same time so the liver can keep making functional factors.",
  "sidefx": "The main risk is thrombosis. Restoring clotting factors quickly in patients who were anticoagulated for a reason, such as atrial fibrillation or a mechanical valve, can lead to stroke, heart attack, DVT or PE, and combining PCC with factor VIIa or antifibrinolytics adds to this. Disseminated intravascular coagulation is a rare, serious form. Hypersensitivity and, for heparin-containing products, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia are possible. Headache and nausea are common. There is no reversal agent for PCC itself.",
  "src": ["Kcentra (prothrombin complex concentrate, human) FDA label", "Balfaxar FDA label", "Sarode R et al. Efficacy and safety of a 4-factor PCC in patients on vitamin K antagonists. Circulation, 2013"]
},
"hypertonic-saline-3-percent": {
  "origin": "Saltwater infusions date back to 1832, when the Scottish physician Thomas Latta injected salt solution into the veins of cholera patients. The idea of using concentrated salt to pull water out of the brain came later. In 1919 Lewis Weed and Paul McKibben at Johns Hopkins showed that injecting concentrated salt solution into animals reduced brain volume and cerebrospinal fluid pressure, while dilute solutions raised it. Hypertonic saline was used for severe hyponatremia for decades, and from the 1980s and 1990s it gained wide use, alongside mannitol, to lower intracranial pressure after brain injury. Today 3 percent sodium chloride is a standard premixed solution, about 3.3 times the salt of normal saline.",
  "timeline": [["1832", "Thomas Latta gives intravenous salt solution to cholera patients."], ["1919", "Weed and McKibben show concentrated salt solutions shrink the brain and lower CSF pressure in animals."], ["1980s", "Hypertonic saline is studied for raised intracranial pressure and trauma resuscitation."]],
  "cell": "Water moves freely across most cell membranes, but sodium is largely kept outside cells by the sodium-potassium pump. When 3 percent saline raises plasma sodium, the fluid outside cells becomes more concentrated (higher osmolality). Water then moves by osmosis from inside cells into the blood. In the brain, the intact blood-brain barrier is nearly impermeable to sodium, so the osmotic gradient pulls water out of brain tissue into the capillaries, shrinking swollen brain and lowering intracranial pressure. In hyponatremia, the same shift reverses the cell swelling that causes confusion and seizures. Hypertonic saline also expands blood volume and may improve blood flow in small vessels and reduce inflammation, effects that are still being studied.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Water is drawn out of swollen brain tissue, lowering intracranial pressure within minutes and reversing swelling from hyponatremia."], ["Blood volume", "Water pulled from cells into the bloodstream expands circulating volume and can raise blood pressure."], ["Electrolytes", "Sodium and chloride rise; the extra chloride can cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, and potassium can fall."], ["Lungs and heart", "Rapid volume expansion can cause fluid overload and pulmonary edema in people with heart failure."], ["Veins", "The concentrated solution can irritate veins and injure tissue if it leaks out."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys gradually excrete the extra sodium and chloride."]],
  "journey": "Three percent saline is given by vein and sometimes by intraosseous line in emergencies. It acts almost immediately: sodium rises and water shifts within minutes, and intracranial pressure falls within about fifteen to thirty minutes. It is not metabolized. Sodium and chloride spread through the extracellular fluid and are removed by the kidneys. The intracranial pressure effect lasts a few hours, while the sodium change persists until the kidneys excrete it. Brain cells adapt to new sodium levels over a day or two, which is why the speed of change matters.",
  "sidefx": "Osmotic demyelination syndrome is the most feared risk. In chronic hyponatremia, brain cells have shed solutes to avoid swelling. If sodium is then raised too quickly, water leaves those cells faster than they can rebuild solutes, and myelin in the pons and elsewhere is damaged, causing trouble speaking and swallowing, paralysis or a locked-in state. Hypernatremia and fluid overload come from giving more salt than needed. Hyperchloremic acidosis comes from the high chloride load. Rebound intracranial pressure can occur as the effect wears off. Vein irritation reflects the concentrated solution. If sodium rises too fast, it can be lowered again with free water and desmopressin under expert care.",
  "src": ["3% Sodium Chloride Injection USP FDA label", "Weed LH, McKibben PS. Pressure changes in the cerebro-spinal fluid following intravenous injection of solutions of various concentrations. Am J Physiol, 1919", "Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines"]
},
"ifosfamide": {
  "origin": "Ifosfamide is a close chemical relative of cyclophosphamide. Both were created at the German company Asta-Werke, where Norbert Brock and colleagues designed nitrogen mustard prodrugs that would be inactive until converted in the body. Cyclophosphamide came first in the late 1950s; ifosfamide, which moves one chloroethyl group to a different nitrogen, followed in the 1960s. Ifosfamide caused severe bleeding from the bladder, which limited its use until mesna, a drug that neutralizes the toxic metabolite in urine, was developed by the same company. The FDA approved ifosfamide (Ifex) in 1988, together with mesna, for testicular cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1958", "Cyclophosphamide is introduced by Asta-Werke."], ["1960s", "Ifosfamide is synthesized as a cyclophosphamide analog."], ["1970s", "Mesna is developed to protect the bladder from acrolein."], ["1988", "FDA approves ifosfamide (Ifex) and mesna."]],
  "cell": "Ifosfamide is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 hydroxylate it to 4-hydroxyifosfamide, which breaks down into ifosforamide mustard and acrolein. Ifosforamide mustard forms reactive aziridinium ions that alkylate DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. Because it has two reactive arms, it can bond two guanines together, forming DNA interstrand and intrastrand cross-links. Cross-linked DNA cannot be unzipped for replication or transcription, so the cell triggers p53 and apoptosis. It acts in all phases of the cell cycle (cell cycle nonspecific). A larger share of ifosfamide than of cyclophosphamide is diverted down a side pathway (N-dechloroethylation), which produces chloroacetaldehyde, linked to brain and kidney toxicity.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "Cross-linked DNA kills rapidly dividing cancer cells in testicular cancer, sarcomas and some lymphomas."], ["Bone marrow", "White cell, platelet and red cell production fall, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment."], ["Bladder", "Acrolein in urine damages the bladder lining and causes hemorrhagic cystitis unless mesna and fluids are given."], ["Brain", "Encephalopathy with confusion, sleepiness, hallucinations or coma can occur, likely from chloroacetaldehyde."], ["Kidneys", "Damage to the proximal tubules causes Fanconi syndrome, with loss of phosphate, potassium, glucose and bicarbonate, and can cause rickets in children."], ["Gut and hair", "Nausea, vomiting and hair loss are common."]],
  "journey": "Ifosfamide is given by vein and must be activated by the liver over hours. Activation speeds up with repeated doses because ifosfamide induces its own metabolism. Ifosforamide mustard enters tumor cells throughout the body. The half-life is about seven to fifteen hours depending on dose. The parent drug and metabolites, including acrolein and chloroacetaldehyde, leave in the urine, which is why bladder toxicity occurs and why kidney function matters.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, hair loss and gut upset come from killing dividing normal cells. Hemorrhagic cystitis is caused by acrolein concentrating in the bladder; mesna is a thiol that binds acrolein in the urine and inactivates it. Encephalopathy and kidney tubule damage are linked to chloroacetaldehyde. CYP inducers can shift metabolism and raise toxic metabolites. Methylene blue is used, outside its labeled indications, to treat or prevent ifosfamide encephalopathy. Secondary cancers can follow DNA damage.",
  "fun": "Mesna was designed to act only in the bladder: in the blood it pairs with itself into an inactive form, and the kidneys split it back into its active thiol right where acrolein collects.",
  "src": ["Ifex (ifosfamide) FDA label", "Mesnex (mesna) FDA label", "Brock N. Oxazaphosphorine cytostatics: past, present, future. Cancer Res, 1989"]
},
"melphalan": {
  "origin": "Melphalan was designed in the early 1950s at the Chester Beatty Research Institute in London by Frank Bergel and John Stock. Their idea was to attach a nitrogen mustard to phenylalanine, the amino acid that pigment cells use to make melanin, hoping cells such as melanoma cells would take it up eagerly. The L form of this molecule became melphalan (L-phenylalanine mustard). It turned out to be most useful in multiple myeloma, where it became a mainstay of treatment, and the FDA approved Alkeran in 1964. High-dose melphalan with autologous stem cell rescue later became the standard transplant regimen for myeloma, and a propylene glycol-free IV form, Evomela, was approved in 2016.",
  "timeline": [["1953", "Bergel and Stock synthesize melphalan at the Chester Beatty Research Institute."], ["1964", "FDA approves Alkeran (melphalan) tablets."], ["1980s", "High-dose melphalan with stem cell rescue is developed for myeloma."], ["2016", "FDA approves Evomela, a captisol-based IV melphalan."]],
  "cell": "Melphalan is a bifunctional nitrogen mustard. Its chloroethyl arms spontaneously form reactive aziridinium ions, which attack DNA bases, mainly the N7 position of guanine. With two arms, one molecule can link two guanines on opposite strands (interstrand cross-links), which blocks the DNA from separating for replication and transcription. The cell recognizes the damage, stalls and dies through apoptosis. Because melphalan looks like phenylalanine, it enters cells through amino acid transporters such as LAT1. It works in all phases of the cell cycle, and plasma cells and bone marrow stem cells are especially sensitive, which is why it is used both against myeloma and to clear the marrow before transplant.",
  "body": [["Myeloma and plasma cells", "DNA cross-linking kills malignant plasma cells, lowering abnormal proteins and bone damage in multiple myeloma."], ["Bone marrow", "At high doses it wipes out normal marrow, so stem cells are reinfused to rebuild blood counts."], ["Mouth and gut", "Damage to dividing lining cells causes mucositis, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea."], ["Immune system", "Low white cell counts cause serious infection risk until counts recover."], ["Hair and fertility", "Hair loss and damage to the ovaries or testes can occur."], ["Long term", "DNA damage can lead to secondary leukemia or myelodysplasia years later."]],
  "journey": "Oral melphalan is absorbed unevenly, and food lowers absorption. IV melphalan reaches the blood immediately. It enters cells through amino acid transporters. Melphalan does not need liver activation; it breaks down by spontaneous chemical hydrolysis into inactive products, so its half-life is short, about one to two hours. Breakdown products leave in the urine and feces. Kidney impairment raises exposure and toxicity.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis, hair loss and infection come from killing dividing normal cells, and with high-dose transplant regimens they are expected until the infused stem cells engraft. Nausea reflects both gut damage and effects on the brain's vomiting center. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur with IV use. Secondary leukemia reflects permanent DNA damage to marrow stem cells. There is no antidote; supportive care includes growth factors, transfusions and infection prevention.",
  "src": ["Alkeran (melphalan) FDA label", "Evomela (melphalan) FDA label", "Bergel F, Stock JA. Cyto-active amino-acid and peptide derivatives. J Chem Soc, 1954"]
},
"bendamustine": {
  "origin": "Bendamustine was created in 1963 in Jena, East Germany, by Werner Ozegowski and Dieter Krebs at the Institute for Microbiology and Experimental Therapy. They combined a nitrogen mustard with a benzimidazole ring, hoping it might also act like a purine analog. It was used in East Germany for decades under the name Cytostasan, but was largely unknown to the rest of the world until after German reunification, when it was studied more widely. In 2008 the FDA approved it as Treanda for chronic lymphocytic leukemia and indolent B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Faster-infusing formulations such as Bendeka followed.",
  "timeline": [["1963", "Ozegowski and Krebs synthesize bendamustine in Jena, East Germany."], ["1970s", "Bendamustine is used clinically in East Germany as Cytostasan."], ["1990s", "After reunification, bendamustine is studied widely in Western trials."], ["2008", "FDA approves Treanda for CLL and indolent B-cell lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Bendamustine has a nitrogen mustard group with two chloroethyl arms, which form aziridinium ions that alkylate DNA at guanine and create interstrand and intrastrand cross-links. Its benzimidazole ring resembles a purine. Compared with other alkylators, bendamustine causes more durable DNA double-strand breaks, activates a DNA damage response that relies more on base excision repair than on alkyltransferase repair, and induces apoptosis through p53. It can also cause mitotic catastrophe, a form of cell death when cells try to divide with damaged DNA. This distinct pattern may explain why it works in some lymphomas that resist other alkylating agents.",
  "body": [["Lymphoma and CLL cells", "DNA damage kills malignant B cells in CLL and indolent lymphomas, often in combination with rituximab."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils and platelets drop, with low points around the third week."], ["Immune system", "Lymphocyte counts fall deeply and for a long time, raising the risk of opportunistic infections such as herpes zoster and, rarely, PML."], ["Skin", "Rashes are common, and severe reactions such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome can occur, especially with allopurinol."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid tumor cell death can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]],
  "journey": "Bendamustine is given by vein and is more than 95 percent protein bound. It is broken down mainly by hydrolysis into inactive products, with a smaller part oxidized by CYP1A2 into active metabolites (gamma-hydroxybendamustine and M4). The parent drug has a short half-life of about forty minutes. Most of the drug and its products leave in the feces. CYP1A2 inhibitors such as ciprofloxacin and fluvoxamine can raise levels, and smoking can lower them.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, nausea and infections come from killing dividing cells and the lymphocytes it targets. Long-lasting lymphopenia explains opportunistic infections. Infusion reactions and rash reflect immune reactions to the drug; combining it with allopurinol has been linked to more severe skin reactions. Tumor lysis syndrome follows rapid breakdown of a large tumor load. Leaking out of the vein can injure tissue. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Bendamustine spent about four decades as a little-known East German drug before Western trials turned it into a widely used lymphoma treatment.",
  "src": ["Treanda (bendamustine) FDA label", "Cheson BD, Rummel MJ. Bendamustine: rebirth of an old drug. J Clin Oncol, 2009"]
},
"busulfan": {
  "origin": "Busulfan was developed in the early 1950s at the Chester Beatty Research Institute in London by Alexander Haddow and Geoffrey Timmis, who studied alkyl sulfonates as a new kind of alkylating agent. In 1953 David Galton reported that it controlled chronic myeloid leukemia, and the oral tablet Myleran was approved in the US in 1954. For decades it was the main drug for CML until targeted drugs such as imatinib replaced it. Because it is so toxic to marrow stem cells, busulfan became a key part of conditioning before stem cell transplant. An IV form, Busulfex, was approved in 1999, giving more predictable blood levels than tablets.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Haddow and Timmis develop alkyl sulfonates including busulfan."], ["1953", "Galton reports busulfan controls chronic myeloid leukemia."], ["1954", "FDA approves Myleran (busulfan) tablets."], ["1999", "FDA approves Busulfex (IV busulfan) for transplant conditioning."]],
  "cell": "Busulfan is a bifunctional alkyl sulfonate. Its two methanesulfonate groups are good leaving groups, so the four-carbon chain between them reacts with DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. One molecule can bond to two guanines, forming intrastrand cross-links (and some interstrand cross-links), and it also forms DNA-protein cross-links. Busulfan is especially toxic to early hematopoietic stem cells, which divide slowly and are less able to repair this damage, while it spares lymphocytes relatively. This makes it strongly myeloablative but only modestly immunosuppressive, so it is combined with drugs such as cyclophosphamide or fludarabine before transplant.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "It destroys marrow stem cells, emptying the marrow so donor or stored stem cells can engraft."], ["Brain", "It crosses into the brain and can cause seizures, so seizure prevention is given beforehand."], ["Liver", "Damage to small liver veins can cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease), with painful liver swelling, jaundice and fluid gain."], ["Lungs", "Long-term use can cause pulmonary fibrosis, known as busulfan lung, sometimes years later."], ["Skin", "It can darken the skin (hyperpigmentation)."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Fertility", "Ovarian and testicular failure are common after high doses."]],
  "journey": "IV busulfan is infused through a central line and reaches predictable blood levels; oral busulfan is absorbed well but more variably. It crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid at levels close to those in plasma. The liver conjugates it with glutathione, mainly through glutathione S-transferase A1, and the products leave in the urine. The half-life is about two to three hours. Blood levels are often measured during conditioning to adjust exposure, and drugs that deplete glutathione or inhibit metabolism, such as acetaminophen, itraconazole or metronidazole, can raise levels.",
  "sidefx": "Myeloablation is the intended effect, so infection and bleeding risks are high until engraftment. Seizures happen because busulfan reaches the brain easily. Veno-occlusive disease reflects injury to the endothelium of liver sinusoids, and the risk is higher with higher exposure and with cyclophosphamide. Busulfan lung and skin darkening are classic long-term effects of the oral drug. Glutathione depletion by acetaminophen slows clearance. There is no antidote; defibrotide treats severe veno-occlusive disease.",
  "src": ["Busulfex (busulfan) injection FDA label", "Myleran (busulfan) FDA label", "Galton DAG. Myleran in chronic myeloid leukaemia. Lancet, 1953"]
},
"carmustine": {
  "origin": "Carmustine, also called BCNU, came out of the US National Cancer Institute screening program. In the 1960s chemists at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, led by John Montgomery and Thomas Johnston, studied nitrosoureas after one showed activity against leukemia in mice. BCNU was notable because it was lipid soluble and crossed the blood-brain barrier, making it useful for brain tumors. The FDA approved IV carmustine as BiCNU in 1977. In 1996 the FDA approved Gliadel, a dissolvable wafer containing carmustine that surgeons place in the brain cavity after removing a glioma, developed by Henry Brem at Johns Hopkins and Robert Langer at MIT.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Southern Research Institute chemists develop carmustine (BCNU) in the NCI program."], ["1977", "FDA approves BiCNU (IV carmustine)."], ["1996", "FDA approves Gliadel carmustine wafer for glioblastoma after surgery."]],
  "cell": "Carmustine does not need enzymes to activate it. In the body it breaks down spontaneously into two reactive pieces. A chloroethyl diazonium ion alkylates DNA, especially the O6 position of guanine; over the next hours, this chloroethyl adduct reacts with the cytosine on the opposite strand, forming an interstrand cross-link that blocks replication and kills the cell. An isocyanate piece carbamoylates lysine residues on proteins, which can inactivate DNA repair enzymes and glutathione reductase. Tumor cells that make the repair enzyme O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT) can remove the chloroethyl group before the cross-link forms, which is a key resistance mechanism. Carmustine is cell cycle nonspecific.",
  "body": [["Brain tumors", "Its fat solubility lets it cross the blood-brain barrier and kill glioma cells; the wafer delivers high local doses after surgery."], ["Bone marrow", "Marrow suppression is delayed, with platelets lowest at about four to five weeks and white cells at five to six weeks."], ["Lungs", "Pulmonary infiltrates and fibrosis can develop, sometimes many years later, and the risk rises with total exposure."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Liver enzyme rises and kidney damage can occur."], ["Eyes", "Optic neuroretinitis has been reported, especially with intra-arterial use."], ["Infusion site", "The alcohol diluent and the drug can cause flushing, burning and low blood pressure with fast infusion."]],
  "journey": "IV carmustine is distributed quickly and, being lipid soluble, enters the brain and cerebrospinal fluid well. The parent drug lasts only about fifteen to thirty minutes because it breaks down spontaneously and is also metabolized by the liver; its active pieces work within that time. Metabolites leave mostly in the urine, and some is exhaled as carbon dioxide. Gliadel wafers release carmustine into the surrounding brain over a few weeks as the polymer dissolves, with little reaching the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Delayed marrow suppression reflects damage to marrow stem cells, which shows up only as mature cells age out. Pulmonary fibrosis is thought to come from carbamoylation and oxidative injury of lung cells, and it can appear years later, especially after chest radiation. Nausea is from effects on the brain's vomiting center. Flushing and low blood pressure come from fast infusion and the alcohol diluent. Gliadel wafers can cause seizures, brain swelling and healing problems near the cavity. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["BiCNU (carmustine) FDA label", "Gliadel (carmustine wafer) FDA label", "Brem H et al. Placebo-controlled trial of safety and efficacy of intraoperative controlled delivery of carmustine. Lancet, 1995"]
},
"thiotepa": {
  "origin": "Thiotepa is one of the oldest chemotherapy drugs still in use. It came from 1950s research at American Cyanamid's Lederle Laboratories, which studied ethylenimine compounds related to the nitrogen mustards. Chemists found that molecules with several three-membered aziridine rings could alkylate DNA directly. Thiotepa (triethylenethiophosphoramide) was approved in the US in 1959 and was used for breast, ovarian and bladder cancers and for fluid buildup around the lungs caused by cancer. Later it became valued in high-dose transplant conditioning because it crosses into the brain. In 2017 the FDA approved Tepadina for conditioning before stem cell transplant in children with beta-thalassemia.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Lederle Laboratories develops ethylenimine alkylating agents including thiotepa."], ["1959", "FDA approves thiotepa."], ["1980s", "High-dose thiotepa is studied in stem cell transplant regimens."], ["2017", "FDA approves Tepadina for conditioning in pediatric beta-thalassemia transplant."]],
  "cell": "Thiotepa carries three aziridine (ethylenimine) rings. Each ring can open and attach an ethyl group to DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. With several reactive rings, a single molecule can link two DNA bases, forming interstrand and intrastrand cross-links that block DNA strand separation, stall replication and trigger apoptosis. Liver CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 convert thiotepa to TEPA, its oxygen analog, which also alkylates DNA and lasts longer. Some TEPA releases aziridine, which may add to activity. Thiotepa is cell cycle nonspecific. It is also a strong inhibitor of CYP2B6, which matters when it is combined with cyclophosphamide.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "It is strongly myeloablative, so it is used to empty the marrow before transplant; counts fall to their lowest at about two to three weeks."], ["Brain", "It crosses the blood-brain barrier, which is useful for brain lymphoma regimens but can cause confusion or other central nervous system effects at high doses."], ["Skin", "It is excreted in sweat, so it can darken, redden, burn or peel the skin, especially in skin folds and under dressings."], ["Bladder", "Placed directly in the bladder, it treats superficial bladder cancer with little absorption."], ["Liver", "High doses can cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease)."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and vomiting are common."]],
  "journey": "Thiotepa can be given by vein, into the bladder or into body cavities such as the pleural space. IV thiotepa spreads widely, including into the cerebrospinal fluid. The liver converts it through CYP3A4 and CYP2B6 to TEPA, an active metabolite that lasts longer than the parent drug. The half-life of thiotepa is about one and a half to four hours. Metabolites leave in the urine. Part of the drug is also excreted through the skin in sweat. Some drug is absorbed from the bladder, especially if the lining is damaged.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and infection come from killing dividing cells, and at transplant doses they are expected until engraftment. Skin darkening and peeling occur because the drug comes out in sweat and damages skin cells directly, which is why frequent bathing and changing of dressings is advised during high-dose treatment. Central nervous system toxicity reflects its good brain penetration. Veno-occlusive disease reflects injury to liver sinusoid lining cells. Secondary cancers can occur after DNA damage. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Tepadina (thiotepa) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"dacarbazine": {
  "origin": "Dacarbazine (DTIC) was created at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, around 1959, as part of the National Cancer Institute program. Chemists including Y. Fulmer Shealy and John Montgomery were making imidazole compounds that resembled 5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide, an intermediate in purine synthesis, hoping to make an antimetabolite. Instead, the triazene group turned out to release a methylating agent, so dacarbazine acts as an alkylating drug. The FDA approved it in 1975 for metastatic melanoma, and it became the D in the ABVD regimen for Hodgkin lymphoma. Its chemistry later inspired temozolomide, an oral drug that produces the same active compound without liver activation.",
  "timeline": [["1959", "Dacarbazine is synthesized at the Southern Research Institute."], ["1975", "FDA approves dacarbazine for metastatic melanoma."], ["1975", "The ABVD regimen including dacarbazine is reported for Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["1999", "Temozolomide, a related drug that forms the same active compound, is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Dacarbazine is a prodrug. In the liver, CYP1A2 and CYP2E1 (and CYP1A1) remove a methyl group (N-demethylation), producing MTIC (monomethyl triazenoimidazole carboxamide). MTIC breaks down into a methyldiazonium ion, a highly reactive methylating agent, and an inactive imidazole carboxamide (AIC). The methyldiazonium ion adds methyl groups to DNA, mainly at N7 and O6 of guanine. O6-methylguanine is the key lesion: during replication it pairs with thymine instead of cytosine, and the mismatch repair system repeatedly tries and fails to fix it, causing DNA breaks and apoptosis. Tumors with high levels of the repair enzyme MGMT, which removes the methyl group, are more resistant.",
  "body": [["Melanoma and lymphoma", "DNA methylation kills melanoma cells and, combined with other drugs in ABVD, Hodgkin lymphoma cells."], ["Stomach and brain", "Severe nausea and vomiting are common because it is highly emetogenic."], ["Bone marrow", "White cell and platelet counts fall, with the low point about two to four weeks after treatment."], ["Whole body", "A flu-like illness with fever, muscle aches and tiredness can follow a dose."], ["Liver", "A rare but serious reaction causes clots in the hepatic veins and liver cell death."], ["Skin", "Sun sensitivity, facial flushing and hair loss can occur."], ["Veins", "It irritates veins, causing pain and burning, especially if infused quickly or leaked."]],
  "journey": "Dacarbazine is given by vein and must be activated in the liver. It is weakly protein bound and does not enter the brain well. The terminal half-life is about five hours. About 40 percent leaves unchanged in the urine, and the inactive breakdown product AIC is also excreted in urine. Because CYP1A2 activates it, smoking and enzyme inducers can increase activation while CYP1A2 inhibitors can reduce it. The drug is light sensitive and is protected from light.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are among the worst of any chemotherapy drug, likely from direct effects on the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Marrow suppression comes from DNA damage in dividing blood cell precursors. The flu-like syndrome may reflect cytokine release. Hepatic vein thrombosis with liver necrosis is rare and thought to involve damage to small liver veins. Photosensitivity relates to light-sensitive breakdown products. Vein irritation is direct chemical injury. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Dacarbazine for injection FDA label", "Bonadonna G et al. ABVD regimen in Hodgkin disease. Cancer, 1975"]
},
"streptozocin": {
  "origin": "Streptozocin is a natural product from the soil bacterium Streptomyces achromogenes. Scientists at the Upjohn Company isolated it around 1959 while searching for new antibiotics. In the early 1960s researchers found that it destroyed the insulin-making beta cells of the pancreas in animals, causing diabetes. That property made it a classic laboratory tool for creating diabetic animals, and it also suggested a cancer use. Because it selectively targets beta cells, it was tested in pancreatic islet cell tumors and approved by the FDA in 1982 as Zanosar for metastatic islet cell carcinoma.",
  "timeline": [["1959", "Streptozocin is isolated from Streptomyces achromogenes at Upjohn."], ["1963", "Its diabetes-causing effect on pancreatic beta cells is reported in animals."], ["1982", "FDA approves Zanosar for metastatic pancreatic islet cell carcinoma."]],
  "cell": "Streptozocin is a methylnitrosourea attached to glucosamine, a sugar. The sugar part lets it enter cells through the glucose transporter GLUT2, which is abundant on pancreatic beta cells, liver cells and kidney tubule cells. Inside the cell, the nitrosourea breaks down into a methyldiazonium ion, which methylates DNA, especially at the N7 and O6 positions of guanine. The DNA damage activates poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP), which uses up NAD and ATP; the beta cell runs out of energy and dies. Streptozocin also releases nitric oxide and creates free radicals that add to the damage. Unlike most nitrosoureas, it causes relatively little cross-linking and little marrow toxicity.",
  "body": [["Pancreatic islet tumors", "It kills neuroendocrine tumor cells that, like beta cells, take it in through glucose transporters."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney tubule cells also take it up, so proteinuria, rising creatinine, low phosphate and renal tubular damage are common and cumulative."], ["Blood sugar", "Destroying insulin-producing cells can first release insulin and cause hypoglycemia, then later lead to high blood sugar."], ["Stomach and brain", "Severe nausea and vomiting are common, from effects on the brain vomiting center."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise, as liver cells also carry GLUT2."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood counts usually fall only mildly, unlike most other nitrosoureas."]],
  "journey": "Streptozocin is given by vein and spreads quickly to the liver, kidneys and pancreas, the organs with high GLUT2 levels. The parent drug disappears from plasma quickly, with a half-life of about thirty-five minutes, while its metabolites last longer. It is metabolized in the liver and kidneys. Most of the drug leaves in the urine as metabolites, which is part of why kidney injury is its main toxicity.",
  "sidefx": "Kidney damage is the dose-limiting toxicity because tubule cells absorb streptozocin through GLUT2 and are injured by the same DNA methylation and energy depletion that kill beta cells; the damage builds with each course, so kidney function is followed closely and other nephrotoxins add to it. Sudden insulin release from dying beta cells can cause hypoglycemia, and loss of beta cells can cause diabetes. Nausea is from effects on the vomiting center. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver cell uptake. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Streptozocin is one of the most widely used chemicals in diabetes research, because a single injection in rodents reliably destroys beta cells and creates a model of type 1 diabetes.",
  "src": ["Zanosar (streptozocin) FDA label", "Rakieten N et al. Studies on the diabetogenic action of streptozotocin. Cancer Chemother Rep, 1963"]
},
"trabectedin": {
  "origin": "Trabectedin comes from the sea. In 1969 US National Cancer Institute screening found that extracts of the Caribbean mangrove tunicate (sea squirt) Ecteinascidia turbinata had antitumor activity. The active compound was present in tiny amounts, and its structure was finally solved in 1990 by the group of Kenneth Rinehart at the University of Illinois, and independently by researchers at Harbor Branch Oceanographic Institution. The Spanish company PharmaMar developed it as ET-743. Harvesting tons of sea squirts gave only grams of drug, so chemists built it in the lab; E. J. Corey published a full synthesis in 1996, and PharmaMar later made it from a bacterial starting material. It was approved in Europe in 2007 and by the FDA in 2015 as Yondelis for liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "NCI screening finds antitumor activity in Ecteinascidia turbinata extracts."], ["1990", "The structure of ecteinascidin 743 is determined."], ["1996", "E. J. Corey's group reports a total synthesis."], ["2007", "Trabectedin is approved in the European Union for soft tissue sarcoma."], ["2015", "FDA approves Yondelis for liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma."]],
  "cell": "Trabectedin binds the minor groove of DNA and forms a covalent bond with the N2 amino group of guanine, preferring certain sequences. This bends the DNA toward the major groove. The distorted DNA traps the transcription-coupled nucleotide excision repair machinery, which tries to cut the lesion but creates toxic DNA breaks, and it stalls RNA polymerase II, which is then degraded, so transcription of many genes stops. Cells with defective homologous recombination are especially sensitive. In myxoid liposarcoma, trabectedin displaces the abnormal FUS-CHOP fusion transcription factor from its target genes, which helps tumor cells mature. It also kills tumor-associated macrophages and monocytes, reducing the supportive inflammatory environment around the tumor.",
  "body": [["Sarcoma", "Disrupting transcription and DNA repair slows or shrinks liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma, especially myxoid liposarcoma."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes rise early in each cycle; dexamethasone premedication lowers this risk."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils fall, with the low point about ten to fifteen days after a dose."], ["Muscle", "It can damage muscle, raising CPK and rarely causing rhabdomyolysis."], ["Heart", "Cardiomyopathy and heart failure can occur."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak syndrome, with swelling and low blood pressure, is a rare serious effect."], ["Infusion site", "Leakage outside a vein causes tissue necrosis, so a central line is used."]],
  "journey": "Trabectedin is given as a 24-hour infusion through a central line. It is about 97 percent protein bound and spreads widely into tissues, giving a very large volume of distribution. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Most leaves in the feces, with little in urine. The terminal half-life is very long, about a week.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression reflects DNA damage in blood cell precursors. Liver injury reflects hepatocyte exposure during metabolism and is reduced by dexamethasone, which also helps prevent nausea. Muscle injury and cardiomyopathy may reflect effects on high-energy, transcription-dependent cells. Capillary leak relates to damage to the vessel lining. Extravasation necrosis is direct tissue toxicity. CYP3A inhibitors raise all of these risks. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Early supplies of trabectedin required harvesting about a ton of sea squirts to obtain roughly a gram of drug, which pushed chemists to find ways to make it in the lab.",
  "src": ["Yondelis (trabectedin) FDA label", "Rinehart KL et al. Ecteinascidins 729, 743, 745. J Org Chem, 1990", "Corey EJ et al. Enantioselective total synthesis of ecteinascidin 743. J Am Chem Soc, 1996"]
},
"lurbinectedin": {
  "origin": "Lurbinectedin is a fully synthetic relative of trabectedin, the sea squirt compound. Scientists at the Spanish company PharmaMar modified one part of the trabectedin structure (replacing a tetrahydroisoquinoline unit with a tetrahydro-beta-carboline) to change how the drug distributes in the body and acts on transcription. Small cell lung cancer relies heavily on certain transcription programs, and lurbinectedin showed activity in patients whose disease had come back after platinum chemotherapy. In 2020 the FDA gave it accelerated approval as Zepzelca, marketed in the US by Jazz Pharmaceuticals, for metastatic small cell lung cancer after platinum therapy. It was later approved for maintenance treatment with atezolizumab in extensive-stage disease.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "PharmaMar designs lurbinectedin as a synthetic trabectedin analog."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Zepzelca for metastatic small cell lung cancer after platinum."]],
  "cell": "Lurbinectedin binds guanine residues in the minor groove of DNA, especially in GC-rich regions near gene promoters, forming covalent adducts that bend the helix. RNA polymerase II stalls on these adducts, is tagged and degraded by the proteasome, and transcription of many genes stops, including oncogenic transcription programs that small cell lung cancer depends on, such as those driven by the transcription factor ASCL1. The adducts also trigger DNA double-strand breaks and cell death, particularly in cells that rely on homologous recombination for repair. Like trabectedin, lurbinectedin reduces tumor-associated macrophages and the inflammatory signals they release.",
  "body": [["Small cell lung cancer", "Shutting down transcription programs that tumor cells depend on, and breaking their DNA, shrinks tumors in some patients after platinum therapy."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common, with the low point about one to two weeks after a dose, and febrile neutropenia and sepsis can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise as the liver handles the drug."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, constipation and diarrhea are common."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue and decreased appetite are frequent."], ["Infusion site", "Leakage outside the vein can cause tissue necrosis, so a central line is preferred."]],
  "journey": "Lurbinectedin is given as a one-hour infusion. It is about 99 percent protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong or moderate CYP3A inhibitors (including grapefruit) raise levels and inducers lower them. About 89 percent leaves in the feces and a small fraction in urine. The terminal half-life is about two days.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenia and other cytopenias come from DNA damage in dividing blood cell precursors, and they set the main safety limits. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver exposure. Nausea and fatigue are common with DNA-damaging chemotherapy. Extravasation necrosis is direct tissue toxicity. Rhabdomyolysis has been reported. CYP3A inhibitors raise all of these risks. There is no antidote; growth factor support is used for neutropenia.",
  "src": ["Zepzelca (lurbinectedin) FDA label", "Trigo J et al. Lurbinectedin as second-line treatment for SCLC. Lancet Oncol, 2020"]
},
"treosulfan": {
  "origin": "Treosulfan is a sulfonate alkylating agent related to busulfan. It was developed in Germany and was used there for decades, first against ovarian cancer, before researchers recognized that it could empty the bone marrow with less liver and lung toxicity than busulfan. European transplant centers adopted treosulfan with fludarabine as a reduced-toxicity conditioning regimen, and it was approved for that use in the European Union. The FDA approved treosulfan as Grafapex in 2025, in combination with fludarabine, as conditioning before allogeneic stem cell transplant for AML and MDS.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Treosulfan is used in Germany for ovarian cancer."], ["2000s", "Treosulfan plus fludarabine is studied as transplant conditioning in Europe."], ["2025", "FDA approves Grafapex (treosulfan) with fludarabine for transplant conditioning in AML and MDS."]],
  "cell": "Treosulfan is a prodrug. Under the normal pH and temperature of the body, without enzymes, it converts step by step into a monoepoxide and then L-diepoxybutane. These epoxides are reactive alkylating agents that attack DNA, mainly at guanine, and form interstrand and intrastrand cross-links. The cross-links block replication and transcription and trigger apoptosis. Treosulfan is strongly toxic to hematopoietic stem cells and also kills lymphocytes, which supports both clearing the recipient's marrow and suppressing the immune system so donor cells can engraft. Combining it with fludarabine adds further immune suppression.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "It destroys the patient's marrow so donor stem cells can take its place, leaving profound low counts until engraftment."], ["Immune system", "Killing lymphocytes helps prevent rejection of the donor graft but raises infection risk."], ["Skin", "Darkening, redness and peeling of the skin can occur, especially in skin folds and the diaper area of young children."], ["Mouth and gut", "Stomatitis, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, but serious liver vessel injury appears less common than with busulfan."], ["Brain", "Seizures have been reported, particularly in infants."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain is common during and after conditioning."]],
  "journey": "Treosulfan is given by vein over about two hours on several days. It converts nonenzymatically into its active epoxides in the blood and tissues, so it does not depend on liver enzymes. It has a half-life of about two hours. A portion leaves unchanged in the urine, and the rest is excreted as breakdown products.",
  "sidefx": "Profound cytopenias, infection and graft failure risk are part of the intended marrow destruction. Mucositis and gut upset come from killing dividing lining cells. Skin toxicity reflects drug reaching the skin and damaging dividing cells there, which is why skin hygiene in the diaper area matters for young children. Seizures may reflect central nervous system exposure. Secondary cancers can follow DNA damage. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Grafapex (treosulfan) FDA label", "Beelen DW et al. Treosulfan or busulfan plus fludarabine as conditioning in AML or MDS. Lancet Haematol, 2020"]
},
"carboplatin": {
  "origin": "Carboplatin was created to make cisplatin easier to tolerate. Cisplatin's anticancer effect was discovered by Barnett Rosenberg at Michigan State University in 1965, when platinum electrodes in a bacterial culture stopped the bacteria from dividing. Cisplatin was powerful but very hard on the kidneys and nerves and caused severe vomiting. In the 1970s researchers at the Institute of Cancer Research in London, working with the platinum company Johnson Matthey and with Bristol-Myers, searched for gentler analogs. They found carboplatin, which replaces cisplatin's chloride groups with a more stable dicarboxylate ring. Hilary Calvert and colleagues later created the Calvert formula, which uses kidney function to plan carboplatin exposure. The FDA approved carboplatin (Paraplatin) in 1989.",
  "timeline": [["1965", "Barnett Rosenberg discovers that platinum compounds stop cell division."], ["1978", "FDA approves cisplatin."], ["1970s", "Carboplatin is developed in London as a less toxic cisplatin analog."], ["1989", "FDA approves carboplatin (Paraplatin)."], ["1989", "Calvert and colleagues publish a kidney function-based dosing formula."]],
  "cell": "Carboplatin is a platinum compound whose cyclobutanedicarboxylate ring comes off slowly in the body (aquation). The activated platinum then binds DNA, mainly at the N7 position of guanine. Most adducts are intrastrand cross-links between neighboring guanines, with fewer interstrand cross-links. These bend the DNA, block replication and transcription, and are recognized by damage-sensing proteins, leading to cell cycle arrest and apoptosis. It is cell cycle nonspecific. Carboplatin forms the same DNA adducts as cisplatin, but because its ring leaves more slowly, fewer reactive species form in the kidney and nerves, so it is less nephrotoxic, neurotoxic and emetogenic, while marrow suppression becomes the main limit. Cells resist through better DNA repair, reduced uptake and higher glutathione.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "DNA cross-links kill ovarian, lung, head and neck and other cancer cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets and neutrophils fall, with the low point around three weeks, and thrombocytopenia is the dose-limiting effect."], ["Stomach and brain", "Nausea and vomiting occur, though less than with cisplatin."], ["Kidneys", "It is less toxic to the kidneys than cisplatin, but it still causes loss of magnesium, potassium, calcium and sodium."], ["Nerves and ears", "Peripheral neuropathy and hearing loss are possible but less common than with cisplatin."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions become more common after many cycles, sometimes severe."]],
  "journey": "Carboplatin is given by vein. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes; instead it slowly releases its ring through aquation. About 70 percent leaves in the urine within a day, mainly by glomerular filtration, so total exposure depends directly on kidney function, which is the basis of the Calvert formula. The half-life of free carboplatin is a few hours with normal kidneys, while platinum bound irreversibly to proteins lingers for days. Aluminum reacts with carboplatin, so aluminum needles and sets are avoided.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, especially of platelets, reflects DNA damage in dividing marrow cells and is predictable from kidney function and exposure. Electrolyte loss comes from kidney tubule effects. Nausea is less than with cisplatin because fewer reactive species reach the gut and brain quickly. Hypersensitivity reactions rise after six or more courses as the immune system becomes sensitized to platinum. Nerve and ear damage reflect platinum adducts in those cells. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Platinum's anticancer effect was discovered by accident when an electric current through platinum electrodes made E. coli grow into long filaments instead of dividing.",
  "src": ["Carboplatin injection FDA label", "Rosenberg B et al. Inhibition of cell division in Escherichia coli by electrolysis products from a platinum electrode. Nature, 1965", "Calvert AH et al. Carboplatin dosage: prospective evaluation of a simple formula. J Clin Oncol, 1989"]
},
"oxaliplatin": {
  "origin": "Oxaliplatin was first synthesized in 1976 by Yoshinori Kidani at Nagoya City University in Japan, who was exploring platinum compounds that carry a diaminocyclohexane (DACH) group. The bulky DACH ring made oxaliplatin active against tumors that resisted cisplatin. The Swiss company Debiopharm developed it, and it was first approved in France in 1996. The FDA approved oxaliplatin (Eloxatin) in 2002 for colorectal cancer in combination with fluorouracil and leucovorin, the FOLFOX regimen, and it later became standard after surgery for stage III colon cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Yoshinori Kidani synthesizes oxaliplatin in Japan."], ["1996", "Oxaliplatin is first approved in France."], ["2002", "FDA approves oxaliplatin (Eloxatin) for colorectal cancer."], ["2004", "FDA approves oxaliplatin with fluorouracil and leucovorin after surgery for stage III colon cancer."]],
  "cell": "Oxaliplatin is a third-generation platinum compound. In the body its oxalate group is replaced by water and chloride, forming reactive platinum species that bind DNA at the N7 position of guanine and create mainly intrastrand cross-links. Because the platinum carries the bulky DACH ring, the adducts are larger and distort DNA differently from cisplatin and carboplatin adducts. Mismatch repair proteins do not recognize these adducts well, so tumors with defective mismatch repair, which resist cisplatin, can still respond. The adducts block DNA replication and transcription and trigger apoptosis. Oxaliplatin also induces immunogenic cell death and inhibits ribosome production, which may add to its effect. The released oxalate chelates calcium and affects voltage-gated sodium channels in nerves, which drives its acute neurotoxicity.",
  "body": [["Colon and rectal cancer", "DNA adducts kill colorectal cancer cells, especially combined with fluorouracil and leucovorin."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Acute cold-triggered tingling of the hands, feet and mouth occurs within hours; repeated cycles cause a cumulative, longer-lasting sensory neuropathy."], ["Throat", "Cold air or drinks can trigger a frightening sense of throat tightness (pharyngolaryngeal dysesthesia) without true airway blockage."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils and platelets fall moderately."], ["Liver and spleen", "Injury to liver sinusoids can enlarge the spleen and lower platelets over time."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions, sometimes severe, can occur after multiple cycles."]],
  "journey": "Oxaliplatin is given by vein, mixed only in dextrose because chloride solutions make it unstable. It transforms quickly and nonenzymatically into reactive platinum species, and much of the platinum binds irreversibly to plasma proteins and red blood cells. No CYP enzymes are involved. About half of the platinum leaves in the urine. Free platinum lingers with a very long terminal half-life of more than two weeks, reflecting slow release from tissues.",
  "sidefx": "Acute neuropathy is thought to come from oxalate binding calcium and altering voltage-gated sodium channels, making nerves overexcitable, especially with cold; this is why cold drinks and cold air trigger it. Chronic neuropathy reflects platinum building up in dorsal root ganglion neurons. Marrow suppression and gut effects reflect damage to dividing cells. Sinusoidal liver injury may relate to damage to the cells lining liver vessels. Hypersensitivity becomes more common with repeated exposure. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Eloxatin (oxaliplatin) FDA label", "Andre T et al. Oxaliplatin, fluorouracil and leucovorin as adjuvant treatment for colon cancer (MOSAIC). N Engl J Med, 2004"]
},
"pemetrexed": {
  "origin": "Pemetrexed was designed in the 1990s through a collaboration between Edward C. Taylor, an organic chemist at Princeton University, and Eli Lilly. Taylor had been working on folate-like molecules built on a pyrrolo-pyrimidine core. The goal was an antifolate that would block several folate enzymes at once and be retained inside cancer cells. Early trials showed severe toxicity in some patients, and researchers found that people with low folate and vitamin B12 status were most at risk. Giving folic acid and B12 alongside made the drug much safer. The FDA approved pemetrexed (Alimta) in 2004 for mesothelioma with cisplatin, and later for nonsquamous non-small cell lung cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Edward C. Taylor and Eli Lilly develop pemetrexed."], ["1999", "Vitamin supplementation is introduced to reduce pemetrexed toxicity."], ["2004", "FDA approves Alimta for malignant pleural mesothelioma with cisplatin."], ["2008", "FDA approves pemetrexed with cisplatin as first-line treatment for nonsquamous NSCLC."]],
  "cell": "Pemetrexed enters cells mainly through the reduced folate carrier. Inside, folylpolyglutamate synthetase adds several glutamate groups, which traps the drug in the cell and makes it a more potent inhibitor. The polyglutamated drug blocks several folate-dependent enzymes: thymidylate synthase (TS), its main target, which makes thymidine for DNA; dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), which regenerates reduced folate; and glycinamide ribonucleotide formyltransferase (GARFT), needed for purine synthesis. Cells run short of thymidine and purines, DNA synthesis stalls in S phase and cells die. Nonsquamous lung cancers tend to express less thymidylate synthase than squamous tumors, which helps explain why they respond better.",
  "body": [["Lung and pleura", "Blocking DNA building blocks kills nonsquamous lung cancer and mesothelioma cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with the low point about eight to ten days after a dose; vitamin supplements reduce this."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and loss of appetite reflect damage to dividing lining cells."], ["Skin", "Rash is common and is reduced by dexamethasone premedication."], ["Kidneys", "It is cleared by the kidneys, so poor kidney function raises exposure; kidney injury can occur."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is an uncommon but serious effect."]],
  "journey": "Pemetrexed is given by vein over a short infusion. About 81 percent is protein bound. It is not significantly metabolized. The kidneys clear 70 to 90 percent unchanged in the urine within a day, by filtration and tubular secretion. The half-life is about three and a half hours with normal kidney function. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen reduce its clearance in people with reduced kidney function.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and diarrhea come from starving dividing normal cells of DNA building blocks. Low folate and B12 make this much worse, because the cells have less reserve to recover, so folic acid and B12 are given before and during treatment. Rash appears to be an inflammatory skin reaction, which dexamethasone prevents. Kidney injury raises levels and toxicity. Leucovorin can be used to rescue normal cells in severe toxicity or overdose.",
  "src": ["Alimta (pemetrexed) FDA label", "Vogelzang NJ et al. Pemetrexed plus cisplatin in mesothelioma. J Clin Oncol, 2003"]
},
"pralatrexate": {
  "origin": "Pralatrexate was designed by researchers at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center and SRI International, including Francis Sirotnak and Joseph DeGraw, as an improved version of methotrexate. Their goal was an antifolate that cancer cells would take up and trap far better than methotrexate. Allos Therapeutics developed it, and in 2009 the FDA approved it as Folotyn for relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphoma, making it the first drug approved for that disease.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Aminopterin, an early antifolate, produces remissions in childhood leukemia."], ["1990s", "Pralatrexate is designed at Memorial Sloan Kettering and SRI International."], ["2009", "FDA approves Folotyn, the first drug for relapsed peripheral T-cell lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Pralatrexate is a folate analog. It has very high affinity for reduced folate carrier type 1 (RFC-1), the main transporter that brings folates into cells, and many cancer cells overexpress this carrier. Once inside, folylpolyglutamate synthetase adds glutamate groups, which traps the drug in the cell. Pralatrexate is both a better substrate for RFC-1 and for this enzyme than methotrexate, so it builds up to higher levels in tumor cells. The polyglutamated drug competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), so the cell cannot regenerate tetrahydrofolate. Without reduced folates, thymidylate and purine synthesis stop, DNA synthesis fails in S phase and the lymphoma cells die.",
  "body": [["Lymphoma cells", "High uptake and trapping of the drug in malignant T cells starves them of DNA building blocks, shrinking some peripheral T-cell lymphomas."], ["Mouth", "Mucositis is the main dose-limiting side effect, often appearing in the first cycle; folic acid and B12 reduce it."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets especially fall, along with neutrophils and red cells."], ["Skin", "Rash is common, and severe skin reactions such as toxic epidermal necrolysis can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme levels can rise during treatment and are checked regularly."], ["Metabolism", "Tumor lysis syndrome can occur with rapid tumor breakdown."]],
  "journey": "Pralatrexate is given as a short IV push. It is about two thirds protein bound. It is not significantly metabolized by CYP enzymes. About a third leaves unchanged in the urine and some in the feces. The plasma half-life is about twelve to eighteen hours, while polyglutamated drug persists longer inside cells. Probenecid, NSAIDs and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole can slow its renal clearance.",
  "sidefx": "Mucositis, low platelets and other cytopenias come from starving rapidly dividing normal cells of folate-dependent building blocks. Folic acid and vitamin B12 are given before and during treatment because a folate-poor state makes normal cells more vulnerable. Skin reactions reflect damage to dividing skin cells and immune mechanisms. Reduced kidney clearance raises exposure. Leucovorin can be used to bypass the DHFR block in normal cells in severe toxicity.",
  "src": ["Folotyn (pralatrexate) FDA label", "O'Connor OA et al. Pralatrexate in relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphoma (PROPEL). J Clin Oncol, 2011"]
},
"gemcitabine": {
  "origin": "Gemcitabine was first made in the early 1980s by Larry Hertel, a chemist at Eli Lilly, who was building fluorinated sugars and attached two fluorine atoms to deoxycytidine. The compound was first explored as an antiviral, but lab testing showed strong activity against cancer cells, including solid tumors that resisted cytarabine, its closest relative. A landmark trial showed that gemcitabine eased symptoms and improved survival in advanced pancreatic cancer compared with fluorouracil, and the FDA approved it as Gemzar in 1996. It later became part of standard regimens for lung, breast, ovarian, bladder and biliary cancers.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Larry Hertel at Eli Lilly synthesizes gemcitabine."], ["1996", "FDA approves Gemzar for advanced pancreatic cancer."], ["1998", "FDA approves gemcitabine with cisplatin for non-small cell lung cancer."], ["2013", "Gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel is approved for metastatic pancreatic cancer."]],
  "cell": "Gemcitabine (2',2'-difluorodeoxycytidine) enters cells through nucleoside transporters, mainly hENT1. Deoxycytidine kinase adds the first phosphate, which is the rate-limiting step, and other kinases make the diphosphate and triphosphate. The diphosphate (dFdCDP) inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, lowering the cell's supply of normal deoxynucleotides, especially dCTP. The triphosphate (dFdCTP) competes with dCTP and is built into DNA. After it is added, DNA polymerase adds one more normal nucleotide and then stops, a process called masked chain termination, which hides the drug from proofreading repair. Lower dCTP levels also mean less competition for dFdCTP and less breakdown of the drug, a self-potentiation loop. DNA synthesis stalls and cells die, mainly in S phase and at the G1/S boundary.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "Blocking DNA synthesis kills pancreatic, lung, breast, ovarian and bladder cancer cells."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with the low point about one week to ten days after a dose."], ["Whole body", "A flu-like illness with fever, chills and muscle aches is common, especially after the first doses."], ["Lungs", "Rarely it causes pneumonitis, capillary leak or acute respiratory distress."], ["Kidneys and blood", "Hemolytic uremic syndrome, a rare but serious form of small vessel damage, can cause kidney failure."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes often rise mildly and are usually monitored during treatment."], ["Skin and swelling", "Rash and swelling of the legs (edema) are common."]],
  "journey": "Gemcitabine is given by vein, usually over thirty minutes. Infusion time matters, because longer infusions increase marrow toxicity. In the blood and liver, cytidine deaminase quickly converts it to an inactive metabolite, dFdU, so the plasma half-life is short, about forty to ninety minutes. Inside tumor cells, the active triphosphate persists much longer. More than 90 percent of the dose leaves in the urine, mostly as dFdU.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression reflects stalled DNA synthesis in dividing blood precursors. The flu-like syndrome likely relates to cytokine release. Rare lung injury, capillary leak and hemolytic uremic syndrome appear to involve damage to the endothelium, the lining of small blood vessels. Gemcitabine is a strong radiosensitizer, so radiation given at the same time can cause severe tissue injury. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Gemzar (gemcitabine) FDA label", "Burris HA et al. Gemcitabine versus fluorouracil in advanced pancreatic cancer. J Clin Oncol, 1997"]
},
"azacitidine": {
  "origin": "Azacitidine was synthesized in 1964 by the Czech chemists Alois Piskala and Frantisek Sorm in Prague, who made cytidine analogs in which a nitrogen replaces carbon 5 of the base. It was first tested as a standard cytotoxic chemotherapy drug for leukemia. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, researchers including Peter Jones and Shirley Taylor discovered that it blocks DNA methylation and can switch silenced genes back on. Low-dose schedules were then studied in myelodysplastic syndromes. In 2004 the FDA approved azacitidine (Vidaza) as the first drug for MDS, and a later trial showed it improved overall survival. An oral form, Onureg, is a separate product for maintenance in AML.",
  "timeline": [["1964", "Piskala and Sorm synthesize azacitidine in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980", "Jones and Taylor show azacitidine inhibits DNA methylation and changes gene expression."], ["2004", "FDA approves Vidaza, the first drug for myelodysplastic syndromes."], ["2009", "A phase 3 trial shows azacitidine improves survival in higher-risk MDS."], ["2020", "FDA approves oral azacitidine (Onureg) for AML maintenance."]],
  "cell": "Azacitidine is a cytidine analog with a nitrogen at position 5 of the ring. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by uridine-cytidine kinase. Most of it (about 80 to 90 percent) is built into RNA, where it disrupts protein synthesis and kills cells. A smaller part is converted by ribonucleotide reductase into the deoxy form and built into DNA. In DNA, when DNA methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) tries to methylate the abnormal base, the enzyme becomes trapped in a covalent bond and is then degraded. As cells divide without DNMT1, methylation marks are lost, so tumor suppressor and differentiation genes that were silenced by promoter methylation can switch back on. At higher exposures, the trapped DNMT adducts and RNA damage cause direct cytotoxicity.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Abnormal MDS cells are killed or pushed to mature, and over months many patients make more normal blood cells and need fewer transfusions."], ["Blood counts", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells often fall first, especially in the early cycles, before any improvement."], ["Injection sites", "Under the skin it causes redness, bruising and pain at injection sites."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, constipation and diarrhea are common."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely it causes renal tubular acidosis, rising creatinine and low potassium."], ["Liver", "In people with widespread liver tumors, it can worsen liver function."]],
  "journey": "Azacitidine is given under the skin or by vein over several days each cycle. Under the skin it is absorbed quickly and peaks in about half an hour. It is unstable in solution and breaks down by spontaneous hydrolysis and by cytidine deaminase, so its half-life is short, under an hour. Its effects on methylation, though, accumulate as cells divide, and responses take several cycles, often months. Most of the drug and its breakdown products leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood counts happen because azacitidine kills dividing marrow cells, normal and abnormal, before normal blood production recovers; infections and bleeding follow from this. Nausea reflects effects on the gut and vomiting center. Injection site reactions are local irritation. Kidney effects reflect toxicity to tubule cells. Liver problems are more likely when the liver is already heavily involved by tumor. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Vidaza (azacitidine) FDA label", "Jones PA, Taylor SM. Cellular differentiation, cytidine analogs and DNA methylation. Cell, 1980", "Fenaux P et al. Azacitidine versus conventional care in higher-risk MDS. Lancet Oncol, 2009"]
},
"decitabine": {
  "origin": "Decitabine (5-aza-2'-deoxycytidine) was synthesized in 1964 by Alois Piskala and Frantisek Sorm in Prague, alongside its relative azacitidine. Unlike azacitidine, decitabine is a deoxyribose nucleoside, so it is built only into DNA, not RNA. It was first tested at high cytotoxic doses for leukemia. Work in the 1980s showed it is a powerful inhibitor of DNA methylation, and lower-dose schedules were developed to reactivate silenced genes in abnormal marrow cells. The FDA approved decitabine (Dacogen) in 2006 for myelodysplastic syndromes. In 2020 an oral tablet, Inqovi, combined decitabine with cedazuridine, which blocks the gut and liver enzyme that would otherwise destroy decitabine.",
  "timeline": [["1964", "Piskala and Sorm synthesize decitabine in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980s", "Decitabine is shown to be a potent inhibitor of DNA methylation."], ["2006", "FDA approves Dacogen for myelodysplastic syndromes."], ["2020", "FDA approves Inqovi, oral decitabine with cedazuridine."]],
  "cell": "Decitabine enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxycytidine kinase to its monophosphate and then to decitabine triphosphate. DNA polymerase builds it into newly copied DNA in place of cytosine during S phase. When DNA methyltransferase 1 (DNMT1) tries to methylate the abnormal base, it forms a covalent bond and is trapped, and the cell then degrades the enzyme. With DNMT1 depleted, methylation patterns are not copied to the new strands as cells divide, so promoters of silenced tumor suppressor and differentiation genes become unmethylated and active again. Abnormal cells mature or die. At higher exposures, the trapped DNMT-DNA adducts themselves cause DNA damage and cytotoxicity. Because it acts only during DNA synthesis, it affects dividing cells.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Over several cycles, abnormal MDS clones shrink or mature, and normal blood production can improve."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia are common, especially early, before counts recover."], ["Immune system", "Low white cell counts raise the risk of fever, pneumonia and other infections."], ["Bleeding", "Low platelets cause bruising, petechiae and bleeding."], ["Gut and whole body", "Nausea, constipation, diarrhea, fatigue and fever are common."], ["Metabolism", "High blood sugar has been reported during treatment."]],
  "journey": "IV decitabine is infused over one or three hours depending on the schedule. It is barely protein bound. It is quickly broken down by cytidine deaminase in the liver and other tissues, giving a plasma half-life of only about half an hour, while its effect on methylation builds across cycles. Only a small fraction leaves unchanged in the urine. Taken by mouth alone, it would be destroyed by cytidine deaminase in the gut and liver, which is why the oral product pairs it with the deaminase inhibitor cedazuridine.",
  "sidefx": "Cytopenias reflect damage to dividing marrow cells, both normal and abnormal, and they lead to infections and bleeding until counts recover. Gut effects and fatigue are common with chemotherapy. Decitabine does not have the RNA effects of azacitidine, but its marrow effects are similar. It can harm a developing fetus because it disrupts DNA methylation, which is vital in early development. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Dacogen (decitabine) FDA label", "Inqovi (decitabine and cedazuridine) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"fludarabine": {
  "origin": "Fludarabine came from the work of John Montgomery and colleagues at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama. The antiviral and anticancer nucleoside vidarabine (ara-A) was quickly destroyed by the enzyme adenosine deaminase. In the 1960s Montgomery's team added a fluorine atom at the 2 position of the adenine base, which made the molecule resistant to adenosine deaminase. A phosphate was added to make it soluble for IV use, creating fludarabine phosphate. The FDA approved it as Fludara in 1991 for B-cell chronic lymphocytic leukemia. It became widely used with cyclophosphamide and rituximab for CLL, and today it is a central part of lymphodepletion before CAR-T cell therapy and of conditioning before stem cell transplant.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Montgomery's team at Southern Research Institute synthesizes 2-fluoro-ara-A."], ["1991", "FDA approves Fludara for B-cell CLL."], ["2010s", "Fludarabine with cyclophosphamide becomes standard lymphodepletion before CAR-T therapy."]],
  "cell": "Fludarabine phosphate is a prodrug. In the blood it is quickly dephosphorylated to 2-fluoro-ara-A, which enters cells through nucleoside transporters. Inside, deoxycytidine kinase phosphorylates it, and other kinases make the active triphosphate, F-ara-ATP. F-ara-ATP inhibits several enzymes: ribonucleotide reductase (reducing deoxynucleotide supplies), DNA polymerases and DNA primase, and DNA ligase. It is built into DNA and halts chain elongation, and it is also built into RNA and impairs RNA synthesis. Importantly, it interferes with DNA repair, so it kills resting (non-dividing) lymphocytes as well as dividing cells by triggering apoptosis. Lymphocytes have high deoxycytidine kinase activity, which concentrates the active drug in them.",
  "body": [["Leukemia and lymphoma cells", "Active drug accumulates in malignant B lymphocytes and kills both dividing and resting cells in CLL and indolent lymphomas."], ["Immune system", "It causes deep, long-lasting loss of CD4 T cells and other lymphocytes, lasting months to over a year, which is useful before CAR-T or transplant but raises infection risk."], ["Infections", "Opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis pneumonia, herpes zoster and cytomegalovirus are more common, so prevention is often used."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with low points about two weeks after a dose."], ["Red blood cells", "Autoimmune hemolytic anemia can develop or worsen."], ["Brain and nerves", "At high doses it can cause severe neurotoxicity, including blindness, coma and death; lower doses can cause milder confusion or neuropathy."], ["Blood transfusions", "Because of severe T-cell suppression, irradiated blood products are used to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease."]],
  "journey": "Fludarabine phosphate is given by vein and is converted almost immediately in the blood to 2-fluoro-ara-A. That form spreads to tissues and enters cells, where it is phosphorylated to the active triphosphate, which stays inside lymphocytes for many hours. About 60 percent leaves in the urine, so kidney impairment raises exposure and toxicity. The half-life of 2-fluoro-ara-A is about twenty hours.",
  "sidefx": "Profound lymphocyte loss is the main effect and also the main risk: it explains opportunistic infections and the need for irradiated blood. Marrow suppression reflects damage to dividing precursors. Autoimmune hemolytic anemia may come from disrupted immune regulation as T cells are depleted. Neurotoxicity is dose-related and reflects nucleoside analog toxicity in neurons. Combining it with pentostatin caused fatal lung toxicity in trials, so they are not used together. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Fludarabine phosphate injection FDA label", "Montgomery JA, Hewson K. Nucleosides of 2-fluoroadenine. J Med Chem, 1969", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"cladribine": {
  "origin": "Cladribine (2-chlorodeoxyadenosine, 2-CdA) was developed into a drug by Dennis Carson and colleagues at the Scripps Clinic in California in the early 1980s. They were inspired by a rare disease: children with inherited adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency lose their lymphocytes because toxic deoxyadenosine products build up in them. Carson's team reasoned that a deoxyadenosine analog that ADA could not break down would selectively kill lymphocytes. Adding a chlorine to deoxyadenosine did exactly that. A single seven-day infusion produced long-lasting complete remissions in most patients with hairy cell leukemia, and the FDA approved cladribine (Leustatin) in 1993. An oral form, Mavenclad, was approved in 2019 for relapsing multiple sclerosis.",
  "timeline": [["1972", "Inherited adenosine deaminase deficiency is linked to severe combined immunodeficiency."], ["1980s", "Dennis Carson's group at Scripps develops cladribine as a lymphocyte-targeting drug."], ["1990", "A single seven-day course is reported to produce durable remissions in hairy cell leukemia."], ["1993", "FDA approves Leustatin for hairy cell leukemia."], ["2019", "FDA approves oral cladribine (Mavenclad) for relapsing multiple sclerosis."]],
  "cell": "Cladribine is a deoxyadenosine analog with a chlorine at position 2, which makes it resistant to adenosine deaminase. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxycytidine kinase to the monophosphate, then to the active triphosphate (Cd-ATP). Lymphocytes have high deoxycytidine kinase activity and low activity of 5'-nucleotidase, the enzyme that removes the phosphate, so the drug accumulates selectively in them. Cd-ATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, is built into DNA and causes DNA strand breaks. In resting cells, repair of these breaks uses up NAD and ATP through PARP activation, and the cell dies of energy depletion and apoptosis. This lets cladribine kill both dividing and resting lymphocytes, including the slow-growing cells of hairy cell leukemia.",
  "body": [["Hairy cell leukemia", "Malignant B cells trap the active drug and die, so a single course often produces long remissions."], ["Immune system", "CD4 T cells and B cells fall sharply and stay low for months, which in multiple sclerosis reduces the immune attack on the nervous system."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil counts fall, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment, often causing fever."], ["Infections", "Low lymphocytes raise the risk of opportunistic infections such as herpes zoster."], ["Nerves and kidneys", "Very high doses, beyond those used today, caused severe nerve damage and kidney failure in early studies."], ["Skin", "Rash and injection site reactions can occur."]],
  "journey": "IV cladribine is given as a continuous infusion for seven days. Oral cladribine, used in multiple sclerosis, is absorbed moderately. About 20 percent is protein bound. Cladribine enters cells and is trapped as its phosphorylated forms, especially in lymphocytes, where it persists well beyond the plasma half-life of about five hours. The kidneys clear much of the drug, so kidney impairment raises exposure.",
  "sidefx": "Lymphocyte depletion explains both the benefit and the infection risk, and irradiated blood products are commonly used to prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease. Neutropenic fever is common in the first weeks of treatment. Neurotoxicity and kidney injury were seen with much higher doses and reflect nucleoside analog toxicity in those tissues. Tumor lysis can occur with a high tumor burden. Because it damages DNA, it may increase cancer risk, which is noted for the oral MS product. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Cladribine injection FDA label", "Mavenclad (cladribine) FDA label", "Piro LD, Carrera CJ, Carson DA et al. Lasting remissions in hairy-cell leukemia induced by a single infusion of 2-chlorodeoxyadenosine. N Engl J Med, 1990"]
},
"clofarabine": {
  "origin": "Clofarabine was designed at the Southern Research Institute in Birmingham, Alabama, by John Montgomery and John Secrist and colleagues, building on their work with fludarabine and with cladribine. It combines features of both: a chlorine at position 2 of the adenine (like cladribine) and a fluorine on the sugar (which makes it stable in stomach acid and resistant to phosphorylase enzymes). It was developed by Ilex Oncology and Genzyme, and in 2004 the FDA approved it as Clolar for children and young adults with relapsed or refractory acute lymphoblastic leukemia after at least two prior regimens. It was the first new leukemia drug approved specifically for children in more than a decade.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Southern Research Institute chemists design clofarabine as a next-generation purine analog."], ["2004", "FDA approves Clolar for relapsed or refractory pediatric acute lymphoblastic leukemia."]],
  "cell": "Clofarabine is a deoxyadenosine analog resistant to adenosine deaminase and to purine nucleoside phosphorylase. It enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxycytidine kinase and then other kinases to clofarabine triphosphate. The triphosphate strongly inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, depleting deoxynucleotide pools, and competes with dATP for DNA polymerases, so it is built into DNA and stops chain elongation. It also damages mitochondria, causing release of cytochrome c and apoptosis-inducing factor, which triggers apoptosis directly. This combination kills both dividing and some non-dividing leukemia cells.",
  "body": [["Leukemia cells", "Active drug accumulates in leukemic lymphoblasts and kills them, clearing the marrow in some children with relapsed ALL."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall sharply, with serious infection risk."], ["Blood vessels", "Systemic inflammatory response syndrome and capillary leak, with fever, low blood pressure, fluid shifts and breathing trouble, can occur, probably from cytokine release as leukemia cells die."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes and bilirubin often rise, and veno-occlusive disease can occur, especially after transplant."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury can occur, and kidney function affects clearance."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and enterocolitis are common."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and, rarely, severe skin reactions can occur."]],
  "journey": "Clofarabine is given by vein over two hours on several days in a row. About 47 percent is protein bound. It is not heavily metabolized by the liver; instead it is phosphorylated inside cells, where the triphosphate persists. About half of the dose leaves unchanged in the urine, so kidney impairment raises exposure. The plasma half-life is about five hours.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression and infections reflect killing of dividing marrow cells. Capillary leak and the inflammatory syndrome are thought to come from cytokines released as large numbers of leukemia cells die; tumor lysis syndrome follows the same rapid cell death, so fluids are given during dosing. Liver and kidney toxicity reflect direct nucleoside analog injury and are made worse by other liver- or kidney-toxic drugs. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Clolar (clofarabine) FDA label", "Jeha S et al. Phase II study of clofarabine in pediatric patients with refractory or relapsed ALL. J Clin Oncol, 2006"]
},
"nelarabine": {
  "origin": "Nelarabine grew out of purine research at Burroughs Wellcome, the company where Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings pioneered purine-based drugs. Children born without the enzyme purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) lose their T cells, because deoxyguanosine builds up and is converted to dGTP, which is toxic to T cells. Researchers reasoned that a guanine analog resistant to PNP could selectively kill T cells. Ara-G (9-beta-D-arabinofuranosylguanine) fit that idea but was very hard to dissolve. The Burroughs Wellcome team made nelarabine, a soluble prodrug that the body converts to ara-G. Glaxo Wellcome and later GlaxoSmithKline developed it, and the FDA approved it as Arranon in 2005 for T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia and T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma that relapsed after at least two regimens.",
  "timeline": [["1975", "Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency is linked to selective T-cell immunodeficiency."], ["1990s", "Nelarabine is developed as a soluble prodrug of ara-G."], ["2005", "FDA approves Arranon for relapsed T-cell ALL and lymphoblastic lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Nelarabine is a prodrug. In the blood, adenosine deaminase removes its methoxy group, converting it to ara-G. Ara-G enters cells through nucleoside transporters and is phosphorylated by deoxyguanosine kinase and deoxycytidine kinase, then by other kinases, to ara-GTP. T cells, especially malignant T lymphoblasts, build up much more ara-GTP than B cells, probably because of higher kinase activity and slower breakdown. Ara-GTP competes with dGTP for DNA polymerase and is built into DNA, which halts DNA synthesis and triggers apoptosis. This T-cell selectivity mirrors the T-cell loss seen in inherited PNP deficiency.",
  "body": [["Leukemia and lymphoma cells", "Malignant T lymphoblasts trap ara-GTP and die, producing responses in relapsed T-cell ALL and lymphoblastic lymphoma."], ["Brain", "Drowsiness, confusion, seizures and ataxia can occur, and the risk is higher after prior intrathecal chemotherapy or craniospinal radiation."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Numbness, tingling, weakness and, rarely, ascending paralysis resembling Guillain-Barre syndrome can occur and may not fully reverse."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with the low point about one to two weeks after treatment."], ["Gut and whole body", "Nausea, diarrhea, fatigue and fever are common."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid tumor breakdown can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]],
  "journey": "Nelarabine is given by vein over one to two hours. It is converted quickly in the blood by adenosine deaminase to ara-G, so nelarabine itself lasts only about thirty minutes, while ara-G has a half-life of about three hours. Ara-G spreads into tissues, including the central nervous system, and is phosphorylated inside cells, where ara-GTP persists. Ara-G is also broken down to guanine. Part of the drug and its products leave in the urine. Pentostatin, an adenosine deaminase inhibitor, blocks conversion to ara-G, so the two are not combined.",
  "sidefx": "Neurotoxicity is the dose-limiting and most serious effect. It appears to come from ara-G reaching the brain and nerves and damaging neurons and myelin, and it can be severe or permanent, which is why neurologic checks are done before each dose. Marrow suppression reflects DNA synthesis inhibition in dividing precursors. Tumor lysis follows rapid leukemia cell death. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped for serious neurologic effects.",
  "src": ["Arranon (nelarabine) FDA label", "Giblett ER et al. Nucleoside-phosphorylase deficiency in a child with severely defective T-cell immunity. Lancet, 1975", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"pentostatin": {
  "origin": "Pentostatin (2'-deoxycoformycin) is a natural product of the bacterium Streptomyces antibioticus. Scientists at Parke-Davis isolated it from fermentation broth in the early 1970s while searching for compounds that would protect the antiviral drug vidarabine from being destroyed by adenosine deaminase. It turned out to be one of the most potent adenosine deaminase inhibitors known. Researchers then realized that blocking this enzyme could mimic inherited adenosine deaminase deficiency, a condition in which lymphocytes die. Pentostatin proved highly effective in hairy cell leukemia, and the FDA approved it as Nipent in 1991.",
  "timeline": [["1972", "Inherited adenosine deaminase deficiency is linked to severe combined immunodeficiency."], ["1974", "Pentostatin is reported from Streptomyces antibioticus fermentation."], ["1980s", "Pentostatin produces high response rates in hairy cell leukemia."], ["1991", "FDA approves Nipent for hairy cell leukemia."]],
  "cell": "Pentostatin is a transition-state analog that binds adenosine deaminase (ADA) so tightly that inhibition is nearly irreversible. ADA normally breaks down adenosine and deoxyadenosine. With ADA blocked, deoxyadenosine accumulates and lymphocytes, which have high deoxycytidine kinase and low 5'-nucleotidase activity, convert it to dATP. High dATP inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, starving the cell of other deoxynucleotides needed for DNA synthesis and repair. Deoxyadenosine also inactivates S-adenosylhomocysteine hydrolase, disrupting cellular methylation reactions. DNA strand breaks accumulate, PARP activation depletes NAD and ATP, and the cells die by apoptosis. Because this works in resting cells, it is effective against slow-growing hairy cell leukemia.",
  "body": [["Hairy cell leukemia", "Toxic deoxyadenosine products build up in malignant B cells and kill them, producing long remissions."], ["Immune system", "Lymphocyte counts, especially CD4 T cells, fall and stay low for months, raising infection risk."], ["Kidneys", "It is cleared by the kidneys and can cause kidney toxicity, so hydration is given and kidney function guides use."], ["Brain", "At high doses, lethargy, confusion and coma have occurred."], ["Eyes", "Keratoconjunctivitis and other eye irritation can occur."], ["Lungs", "Combined with fludarabine it caused severe or fatal lung toxicity, so the two are not given together."], ["Gut and whole body", "Nausea, vomiting, fever, rash and fatigue are common."]],
  "journey": "Pentostatin is given by vein as a bolus or short infusion, with IV fluids before and after. It is barely protein bound and spreads through body water, including some entry into the cerebrospinal fluid. It is metabolized very little. The kidneys excrete most of it unchanged, with a half-life of about six hours that lengthens with kidney impairment. ADA inhibition lasts for days, much longer than the drug stays in plasma, because binding is so tight.",
  "sidefx": "Lymphocyte depletion explains both its benefit and the infections that follow. Kidney and nervous system toxicity are dose related and were severe at the high doses used in early studies. Combining it with fludarabine caused fatal pulmonary toxicity, and ADA inhibition also blocks the activation of nelarabine. It also strengthens the toxicity of vidarabine, the drug it was first meant to protect. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Pentostatin was found while scientists were looking for a helper drug to protect an antiviral, and it became a leukemia treatment by mimicking a rare inherited immune disease.",
  "src": ["Nipent (pentostatin) FDA label", "Woo PWK et al. A new adenosine deaminase inhibitor from Streptomyces antibioticus. J Heterocycl Chem, 1974", "Giblett ER et al. Adenosine-deaminase deficiency in two patients with severely impaired cellular immunity. Lancet, 1972"]
},
"floxuridine": {
  "origin": "Floxuridine (5-fluoro-2'-deoxyuridine, FUDR) was created alongside fluorouracil in the late 1950s. Charles Heidelberger at the University of Wisconsin noticed that tumor cells used the base uracil more than normal cells did and reasoned that a fluorinated uracil could block DNA synthesis. Working with Robert Duschinsky and colleagues at Hoffmann-La Roche, he made fluorouracil in 1957 and then floxuridine, its deoxyribose nucleoside. Because the liver removes most floxuridine on its first pass, it became ideal for delivering very high doses directly into the hepatic artery to treat colorectal cancer that had spread to the liver, while sparing the rest of the body. It is used through implanted pumps and catheters in specialized centers.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Heidelberger and Duschinsky report fluorouracil as an anticancer agent."], ["1958", "Floxuridine, the deoxyribonucleoside of fluorouracil, is described."], ["1980s", "Implanted pumps allow continuous hepatic arterial floxuridine for liver metastases."]],
  "cell": "Floxuridine is a prodrug of the active nucleotide FdUMP. Thymidine kinase phosphorylates it directly to FdUMP, a shorter path than fluorouracil takes. FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase together with the folate cofactor 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, forming a stable three-part complex that locks the enzyme. Thymidylate synthase normally makes thymidine monophosphate from deoxyuridine monophosphate, so the cell runs short of thymidine for DNA (thymineless death), and uracil is mistakenly added to DNA, causing breaks during repair. Floxuridine is also partly converted to fluorouracil, whose metabolites are incorporated into RNA and add to toxicity. The effect is greatest in S phase. Leucovorin raises the folate cofactor and stabilizes the complex, increasing the effect.",
  "body": [["Liver metastases", "Delivered into the hepatic artery, which feeds liver tumors, it reaches very high concentrations in tumor tissue and kills colorectal cancer cells there."], ["Liver and bile ducts", "High local drug levels can inflame the liver and scar the bile ducts (sclerosing cholangitis), sometimes permanently."], ["Stomach and duodenum", "If drug leaks into arteries that feed the stomach, ulcers and gastritis can develop."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, cramps and mouth sores can occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Blood counts fall less than with systemic fluoropyrimidines because the liver captures most of the drug."], ["Catheter and pump", "The catheter can clot, move or become infected."]],
  "journey": "Floxuridine is infused continuously through a catheter placed in the hepatic artery, usually by an implanted pump. The liver extracts most of the drug on its first pass, often more than 90 percent, so liver tumor levels are very high while blood levels stay low. The liver breaks it down to fluorouracil and inactive metabolites through the DPD pathway. Metabolites leave in the urine, and some carbon is breathed out as carbon dioxide. It clears from plasma quickly.",
  "sidefx": "Because the liver captures the drug, the main toxicities are in the liver and bile ducts: chemical hepatitis and biliary sclerosis come from high local concentrations damaging bile duct cells, which depend heavily on the hepatic artery for blood. Peptic ulcers come from drug reaching stomach and duodenal branches, which is why catheter position is confirmed before infusion. Gut and marrow toxicity reflect thymidylate synthase inhibition in dividing cells. Uridine triacetate is an FDA-approved antidote for fluorouracil or capecitabine overdose and severe early toxicity.",
  "src": ["Floxuridine for injection FDA label", "Heidelberger C et al. Fluorinated pyrimidines, a new class of tumour-inhibitory compounds. Nature, 1957"]
},
"daunorubicin-cytarabine-liposome": {
  "origin": "For decades, newly diagnosed AML was treated with the 7+3 regimen, cytarabine for seven days and daunorubicin for three days, developed in the early 1970s. Researchers at Celator Pharmaceuticals in Vancouver, Canada, studied many ratios of the two drugs in the lab and found that a 1:5 molar ratio of daunorubicin to cytarabine was especially synergistic. The challenge was keeping that ratio after injection, because free drugs leave the blood at different rates. Their CombiPlex technology packed both drugs together inside liposomes that keep the ratio fixed and deliver it to leukemia cells. Jazz Pharmaceuticals acquired Celator in 2016, and in 2017 the FDA approved the product, CPX-351, as Vyxeos for therapy-related AML and AML with myelodysplasia-related changes, the first approval to show a survival advantage over 7+3 in these high-risk groups.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Daunorubicin is isolated from Streptomyces bacteria, and cytarabine enters leukemia treatment."], ["1973", "The 7+3 combination of cytarabine and daunorubicin is reported for AML."], ["2000s", "Celator Pharmaceuticals develops CPX-351 using a fixed 1:5 drug ratio in liposomes."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vyxeos for therapy-related AML and AML with myelodysplasia-related changes."]],
  "cell": "Vyxeos is a bilamellar liposome that holds daunorubicin and cytarabine at a fixed 1:5 molar ratio. The liposomes circulate intact, collect in the bone marrow and are taken up more by leukemia cells than by normal marrow cells. Inside the cell, the liposomes break down and release both drugs. Daunorubicin, an anthracycline, intercalates between DNA base pairs and traps topoisomerase II after it cuts DNA, leaving double-strand breaks; it also generates free radicals. Cytarabine is phosphorylated to ara-CTP, which competes with dCTP and is built into DNA, blocking DNA polymerase and chain elongation in S phase. Together at this ratio, the two drugs are synergistic, meaning they kill more leukemia cells than either one would at the same doses.",
  "body": [["Leukemia cells", "Liposomes concentrate both drugs in AML cells in the marrow, killing them and improving survival in high-risk AML compared with 7+3."], ["Bone marrow", "All blood counts fall deeply and recover more slowly than after 7+3, with high risk of infection and bleeding."], ["Bleeding", "Low platelets can cause serious bleeding, including in the brain."], ["Heart", "Daunorubicin can damage heart muscle, causing heart failure or arrhythmias, and the risk adds to any previous anthracycline exposure."], ["Mouth, gut and skin", "Mucositis, nausea, diarrhea, rash and swelling are common."], ["Immune system", "Allergic reactions to the liposomal product can occur."]],
  "journey": "Vyxeos is given by vein over ninety minutes on a few days of each cycle. The liposomes keep both drugs in the bloodstream far longer than free drugs would, so the total-drug half-lives are about thirty-one hours for daunorubicin and forty hours for cytarabine. Most of each drug stays inside liposomes in the plasma. Once released, daunorubicin is reduced to daunorubicinol by carbonyl reductase and leaves in urine and bile, and cytarabine is deaminated to inactive ara-U by cytidine deaminase and leaves in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Deep and prolonged cytopenias come from killing both leukemic and normal marrow cells, so fevers, infections and bleeding are common. Heart damage comes from daunorubicin: anthracyclines produce free radicals and affect topoisomerase II-beta in heart muscle cells, and the risk rises with the total lifetime anthracycline dose. Mucositis and diarrhea reflect damage to dividing gut lining cells. Because the doses and liposomal form differ from free drugs, the product is not interchangeable with other daunorubicin or cytarabine products. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Vyxeos (daunorubicin and cytarabine) liposome FDA label", "Lancet JE et al. CPX-351 versus 7+3 in older adults with newly diagnosed secondary AML. J Clin Oncol, 2018"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"doxorubicin-liposomal": {
  "origin": "Doxorubicin itself came from soil. In the late 1960s scientists at the Italian company Farmitalia, led by Federico Arcamone, grew a mutant strain of Streptomyces peucetius and found it made a red pigment, doxorubicin, that worked against far more cancers than its parent drug daunorubicin. Its big problem was heart damage. In the 1980s and early 1990s researchers including Yechezkel Barenholz and Alberto Gabizon, working with the company Liposome Technology (later Sequus), packed doxorubicin inside tiny fat bubbles (liposomes) coated with polyethylene glycol. The PEG coat hides the bubbles from the immune cells that would normally clear them, so they circulate for days and leak into tumors through their leaky blood vessels. The FDA approved the product as Doxil in 1995 for AIDS-related Kaposi sarcoma. It later gained approval for ovarian cancer and, with bortezomib, multiple myeloma.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "Farmitalia reports doxorubicin from a mutant Streptomyces peucetius strain."], ["1980s", "Long-circulating PEG-coated (stealth) liposomes are developed."], ["1995", "FDA approves Doxil for AIDS-related Kaposi sarcoma, the first FDA-approved nanodrug."], ["1999", "Approval expands to ovarian cancer after platinum therapy."], ["2007", "Approval for multiple myeloma in combination with bortezomib."]],
  "cell": "The drug inside the liposome is ordinary doxorubicin. The PEG-coated liposome is too large to leave normal capillaries but slips out through the gaps in tumor vessels, which is called the enhanced permeability and retention effect. Once in the tumor, doxorubicin is slowly released. Its flat ring system slides between DNA base pairs (intercalation). It traps topoisomerase II on DNA after the enzyme has cut both strands, so the breaks cannot be resealed. Double-strand breaks pile up and trigger p53-driven apoptosis. The quinone part of the molecule also cycles electrons with iron and oxygen to make free radicals that damage membranes. The drug acts in any phase but hits S phase hardest. Because the liposome keeps free drug levels low and steady, heart-damaging peaks are blunted.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "Liposomes collect in tumor tissue through leaky vessels, giving a longer, more focused exposure to doxorubicin than the free drug."], ["Skin of hands and feet", "Long-circulating liposomes leak into small capillaries of the palms and soles, especially where pressure and friction occur, causing redness, peeling and pain (hand-foot syndrome)."], ["Mouth and gut", "Rapidly dividing lining cells are injured, producing stomatitis and nausea."], ["Bone marrow", "Dividing blood precursors are killed, lowering neutrophils, red cells and platelets."], ["Heart", "Free-radical injury to heart muscle cells can still cause cardiomyopathy, with risk rising with the total lifetime anthracycline amount, though peak-related damage is less than with conventional doxorubicin."], ["Immune system", "Liposomes can activate complement during the first infusion, causing flushing, chest tightness and back pain."]],
  "journey": "Given only by IV infusion. Most of the drug stays inside the liposomes in the bloodstream, so the volume of distribution is small, close to blood volume. The PEG coat lets the liposomes avoid the liver and spleen macrophages for a long time, with a half-life of about two to three days. Liposomes slowly deposit in tumors and skin, where doxorubicin leaks out. Released drug is metabolized like regular doxorubicin, mainly to doxorubicinol, and leaves mostly through bile. It cannot be swapped for conventional doxorubicin on an equal basis because the two behave so differently.",
  "sidefx": "Hand-foot syndrome is the signature effect and comes from long-lived liposomes leaking drug into the skin of the palms and soles. Mucositis comes from the same slow exposure of mouth lining cells. Marrow suppression reflects killing of dividing blood precursors. Cardiomyopathy comes from iron-driven free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta injury in heart cells; total lifetime anthracycline exposure is tracked for that reason. Infusion reactions are thought to come from complement activation by the liposomes (CARPA), which is why the first infusion starts slowly. There is no specific antidote; dexrazoxane is used to protect the heart with conventional doxorubicin in some settings.",
  "fun": "During a severe Doxil shortage in 2011 and 2012, the FDA allowed temporary import of a similar liposomal doxorubicin from India called Lipodox.",
  "src": ["Doxil (doxorubicin HCl liposome injection) FDA label", "Barenholz Y. Doxil: the first FDA-approved nano-drug. J Control Release 2012", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"daunorubicin": {
  "origin": "Daunorubicin was the first anthracycline. In the late 1950s and early 1960s, scientists at the Italian company Farmitalia screened soil microbes for cancer-fighting compounds. A Streptomyces peucetius strain from soil near Castel del Monte in the Apulia region made a bright red substance. The name joins the Dauni, an ancient people of Apulia, with the French word rubis, meaning ruby. At about the same time a French team at Rhone-Poulenc found the same molecule and called it rubidomycin. It proved strongly active against acute leukemias. Combined with cytarabine in the 7+3 regimen, it became the backbone of AML induction for decades. A later liposome product combining daunorubicin and cytarabine (Vyxeos) was approved in 2017.",
  "timeline": [["1963", "Italian and French teams report the red Streptomyces antibiotic daunorubicin (rubidomycin)."], ["1970s", "The 7+3 regimen of cytarabine plus daunorubicin becomes standard AML induction."], ["1979", "FDA approves daunorubicin (Cerubidine)."], ["2017", "FDA approves a liposomal daunorubicin and cytarabine combination for certain AML."]],
  "cell": "Daunorubicin has a flat four-ring anthraquinone core attached to an amino sugar. The flat rings slide between DNA base pairs (intercalation), and the sugar sits in the minor groove. This locks topoisomerase II onto DNA right after it has made a double-strand cut, stabilizing the cleavage complex so the break cannot be resealed. When replication or transcription runs into these complexes, permanent double-strand breaks form and the cell triggers apoptosis. The quinone ring can also accept an electron and pass it to oxygen, especially with iron, forming superoxide and hydroxyl radicals that damage lipids and DNA. It works in all phases but most in S phase. Leukemia blasts, which divide rapidly and rely heavily on topoisomerase II, are especially sensitive.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Leukemic blasts and normal blood precursors are both killed, which is the goal of induction but leaves a period of very low counts with a nadir around 10 to 14 days."], ["Heart", "Free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta injury damage heart muscle cells, causing cardiomyopathy that becomes more likely as the lifetime total rises, especially after chest radiation."], ["Mouth and gut", "Dividing lining cells are injured, causing mucositis, nausea and vomiting."], ["Hair follicles", "Rapidly dividing follicle cells stop growing, so hair falls out."], ["Urine", "The red drug and its metabolites color the urine red for a day or two; this is harmless."], ["Kidneys and metabolism", "Rapid killing of leukemia cells releases uric acid, potassium and phosphate, which can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]],
  "journey": "Given only into a running IV line, because it badly damages tissue if it leaks. It spreads rapidly into tissues and binds tightly to DNA, so tissue levels are much higher than blood levels. Aldo-keto reductase enzymes in the liver and other tissues turn it into daunorubicinol, which is also active. Both are cleared through bile and urine. The parent drug's half-life is about 18 hours, and daunorubicinol lasts longer. Liver or kidney impairment raises exposure and calls for dose reduction per the label.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and hair loss come from killing any rapidly dividing cell. Heart damage comes from two effects in heart muscle: iron-catalyzed free radicals that injure mitochondria and membranes, and blocking of topoisomerase II-beta, which disrupts mitochondrial gene expression. Heart cells cannot regenerate, so the damage adds up over a lifetime. Leakage outside the vein binds DNA in local tissue and causes spreading necrosis; dexrazoxane (Totect) is an FDA-approved treatment for anthracycline extravasation. Red urine is just drug color. Tumor lysis is handled with hydration and uric acid lowering.",
  "fun": "Daunorubicin's name is a little geography lesson: the Dauni were a pre-Roman people of the same Apulian region where the drug-making microbe was found.",
  "src": ["Daunorubicin hydrochloride injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Weiss RB. The anthracyclines: will we ever find a better doxorubicin. Semin Oncol 1992"]
},
"epirubicin": {
  "origin": "Epirubicin was made by chemists at Farmitalia in Milan in the 1970s, in the same group led by Federico Arcamone that developed doxorubicin. They wanted an anthracycline that killed tumors as well as doxorubicin but was gentler on the heart. They flipped the orientation of a single hydroxyl group on the sugar (the 4-prime position), making a mirror-image form called an epimer. That small change lets the liver attach glucuronic acid to the drug, so it is cleared faster. Epirubicin was used in Europe for years before the FDA approved it as Ellence in 1999 for adjuvant treatment of node-positive breast cancer after surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Farmitalia chemists create epirubicin by changing the sugar of doxorubicin."], ["1980s", "Epirubicin enters clinical use in Europe."], ["1999", "FDA approves Ellence for adjuvant treatment of node-positive breast cancer."]],
  "cell": "Like other anthracyclines, epirubicin intercalates between DNA base pairs and stabilizes the topoisomerase II-DNA cleavage complex. Topoisomerase II normally cuts both DNA strands to untangle them and then reseals the cut. With epirubicin bound, the enzyme stays stuck on the cut ends, leaving double-strand breaks that trigger apoptosis when the cell tries to replicate or divide. The quinone ring also forms free radicals with iron and oxygen. It is cell-cycle nonspecific, with the most effect in S and G2. The 4-prime epimer sugar changes its handling by the body more than its action on DNA.",
  "body": [["Breast tumor cells", "Rapidly dividing cancer cells accumulate DNA breaks and die, lowering the chance of relapse after surgery."], ["Bone marrow", "White cells drop with a nadir around 10 to 14 days, raising infection risk."], ["Heart", "Cumulative exposure can damage heart muscle, though at equal anticancer effect it is somewhat less cardiotoxic than doxorubicin."], ["Mouth, gut and hair", "Dividing lining and follicle cells are harmed, causing mucositis, nausea and hair loss."], ["Ovaries", "Damage to ovarian follicles can cause amenorrhea and hot flashes in premenopausal women."], ["Bone marrow, late", "DNA damage in stem cells can rarely lead to secondary AML or MDS years later."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion. It spreads widely into tissues and binds DNA, giving a large volume of distribution. The liver converts it to epirubicinol and, uniquely among anthracyclines, to glucuronide conjugates, which are inactive and cleared quickly. Most is eliminated through bile, with a smaller part in urine. The half-life is about 33 hours. Liver impairment raises exposure because the liver does most of the work.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, hair loss and mucositis come from attacking dividing cells. Heart damage comes from iron-driven free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta blockade in heart muscle, building up with total lifetime dose. Secondary AML and MDS arise because topoisomerase II poisons can create chromosome breaks and translocations in marrow stem cells. Extravasation causes severe tissue injury because the drug binds local DNA; dexrazoxane (Totect) is an FDA-approved treatment for anthracycline extravasation. Red urine is just the color of the drug.",
  "fun": "The only structural difference between epirubicin and doxorubicin is the direction one hydroxyl group points on the sugar ring.",
  "src": ["Ellence (epirubicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"idarubicin": {
  "origin": "Idarubicin was made at Farmitalia in Italy in the 1970s by the Arcamone group, the same chemists behind doxorubicin. They removed the methoxy group from one ring of daunorubicin, creating 4-demethoxydaunorubicin. Losing that group made the molecule more fat-soluble, so it enters cells faster and in greater amounts. That made it more potent and, unusually for an anthracycline, it can even be absorbed when taken by mouth (an oral form was used outside the US). The FDA approved IV idarubicin as Idamycin in 1990 for adult AML, where it is used in place of daunorubicin with cytarabine.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Farmitalia chemists report 4-demethoxydaunorubicin (idarubicin)."], ["1990", "FDA approves Idamycin for acute myeloid leukemia in adults."]],
  "cell": "Idarubicin intercalates between DNA base pairs and stabilizes the topoisomerase II-DNA cleavage complex, leaving double-strand breaks that trigger apoptosis. Because it is more lipophilic than daunorubicin, it crosses cell membranes more readily and reaches higher levels inside leukemic cells. It is also less easily pumped out by P-glycoprotein, which helps in some resistant leukemias. Its main metabolite, idarubicinol, is nearly as active and lasts much longer, extending the cytotoxic effect. It is cell-cycle nonspecific.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Leukemic and normal blood cells are killed, giving deep cytopenias with a nadir around 10 to 14 days."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and abdominal pain come from injury to dividing lining cells."], ["Heart", "Like other anthracyclines it can damage heart muscle, causing heart failure or arrhythmia."], ["Liver and kidneys", "These clear the drug; impairment raises exposure and toxicity, so the label calls for dose changes."], ["Hair and urine", "Hair loss is common and urine turns red from the drug color."]],
  "journey": "Given IV over a few minutes into a running line. It spreads widely and rapidly into tissues because it is so fat-soluble. Liver and other tissue enzymes reduce it to idarubicinol, an active metabolite with a half-life of about two days or longer, compared with about 22 hours for the parent. Both are cleared mainly in bile, with some in urine. Idarubicinol also crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression, mucositis and hair loss reflect killing of dividing cells. Heart toxicity comes from free radicals and topoisomerase II-beta injury in heart muscle. Secondary AML and MDS come from DNA breaks in stem cells. If it leaks out of the vein it binds tissue DNA and causes severe necrosis; dexrazoxane (Totect) is an FDA-approved treatment for anthracycline extravasation. Tumor lysis from rapid blast killing is managed with hydration and uric acid lowering.",
  "fun": "Removing just one small methoxy group made idarubicin several times more potent than its parent, daunorubicin.",
  "src": ["Idamycin PFS (idarubicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mitoxantrone": {
  "origin": "Mitoxantrone is fully synthetic. In the 1970s chemists at Lederle Laboratories (American Cyanamid) and other groups were studying anthraquinone compounds, a chemical family related to textile dyes, looking for drugs that act like anthracyclines without their heart toxicity. They made the anthracenedione mitoxantrone, which keeps the flat DNA-binding rings but lacks the sugar and the quinone structure that drives much of anthracycline free-radical formation. The FDA approved it as Novantrone in 1987 for AML, later for pain in advanced prostate cancer, and in 2000 for worsening forms of multiple sclerosis. The Novantrone brand has since been discontinued, but generic mitoxantrone remains available.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Lederle chemists synthesize mitoxantrone from the anthraquinone family."], ["1987", "FDA approves Novantrone for acute nonlymphocytic leukemia."], ["1996", "Approval for pain from advanced hormone-refractory prostate cancer."], ["2000", "FDA approves mitoxantrone for secondary progressive and worsening relapsing MS."]],
  "cell": "Mitoxantrone's flat three-ring core intercalates into DNA, and its side chains bind the phosphate backbone, crosslinking strands electrostatically. It inhibits topoisomerase II, stabilizing the enzyme-DNA cleavage complex and causing strand breaks that lead to apoptosis. It forms fewer free radicals than anthracyclines because it resists one-electron reduction. In multiple sclerosis its effect is immune suppression: it kills or inhibits dividing B cells, T cells and macrophages, reduces antigen presentation and lowers the release of inflammatory cytokines, so fewer immune cells attack myelin.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils fall with a nadir around 10 to 14 days, raising infection risk."], ["Immune system", "Fewer active lymphocytes and macrophages enter the brain and spinal cord, which slows MS relapses and disability progression."], ["Heart", "Despite fewer free radicals, it still causes cumulative cardiomyopathy, sometimes years after treatment, so MS use has a lifetime limit and LVEF checks."], ["Urine and eyes", "The dark blue drug turns urine blue-green and can tint the whites of the eyes bluish for a short time."], ["Reproductive system", "Menstrual changes and amenorrhea are common in women treated for MS."], ["Bone marrow, late", "Topoisomerase II damage in stem cells can lead to treatment-related AML."]],
  "journey": "Given IV only, never intrathecally. It distributes very widely and binds tissue DNA, giving a huge volume of distribution and slow release back into the blood. The liver oxidizes and conjugates it, and most leaves through bile and feces, with only a small amount in urine. The terminal half-life is long, a median of about 75 hours. Liver impairment raises levels.",
  "sidefx": "Cardiotoxicity likely reflects topoisomerase II-beta injury in heart muscle and some residual oxidative stress, and it adds up with total lifetime exposure, so MS treatment is capped. Secondary AML arises because topoisomerase II poisons cause chromosome translocations in stem cells. Marrow suppression and infections reflect killing of dividing blood cells. Blue-green urine and bluish sclera are just drug color. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Mitoxantrone is a deep blue dye-like molecule, a family resemblance to the anthraquinone dyes chemists started from.",
  "src": ["Mitoxantrone injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"valrubicin": {
  "origin": "Valrubicin is a semisynthetic relative of doxorubicin first studied in the 1970s under the code name AD-32 by researchers in the United States. Chemists added a trifluoroacetyl group to the sugar and a valerate ester to the side chain, making the molecule much more fat-soluble. That lets it soak quickly into the cells lining the bladder when instilled through a catheter, while very little reaches the blood. The FDA approved it as Valstar in 1998 for carcinoma in situ of the bladder that had not responded to BCG, in patients for whom immediate bladder removal was not an option. It was taken off the market in 2002 because of a manufacturing problem and returned in 2009.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Valrubicin (AD-32) is developed as a lipophilic doxorubicin derivative."], ["1998", "FDA approves Valstar for BCG-refractory carcinoma in situ of the bladder."], ["2002", "Valstar is withdrawn because of a manufacturing issue."], ["2009", "Valstar returns to the US market."]],
  "cell": "Valrubicin enters urothelial cells quickly because it is very lipophilic. Inside, it interferes with incorporation of nucleosides into DNA and RNA and disrupts the topoisomerase II-DNA cleavage complex, causing chromosomal damage. Unlike doxorubicin, it intercalates into DNA only weakly; its main effects come from topoisomerase II interference and metabolic disruption. Damaged cells arrest in G2 and die. Because it stays in the bladder, it acts mainly on the bladder lining, including the flat carcinoma in situ cells.",
  "body": [["Bladder lining", "Cancerous and normal urothelial cells are exposed directly during the dwell, killing carcinoma in situ cells in some patients."], ["Bladder function", "Irritation of the lining causes frequency, urgency, painful urination, spasms and sometimes blood in the urine for a few days."], ["Urine", "The red drug colors urine red for about a day after treatment."], ["Bone marrow", "Normally unaffected because little drug is absorbed, but a bladder perforation can allow absorption and marrow suppression."]],
  "journey": "Instilled into the bladder through a urinary catheter and held for about two hours before the patient voids. It enters the bladder wall but very little crosses into the blood when the bladder is intact. Almost all the drug leaves in the voided urine, largely unchanged. That is why systemic effects like hair loss or marrow suppression are rare. Treatment is delayed after a bladder resection so the drug does not leak through raw tissue.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local: the drug irritates and kills bladder lining cells, causing cystitis-like symptoms. If the bladder wall is perforated or compromised, the drug can reach the bloodstream and cause anthracycline effects such as marrow suppression. A key risk is not the drug itself but delay: if the cancer does not respond, putting off cystectomy can allow it to spread, so response is checked about three months after therapy.",
  "fun": "Valrubicin is used inside the bladder rather than in the bloodstream because its fat-soluble design lets it soak into the bladder lining quickly while very little is absorbed.",
  "src": ["Valstar (valrubicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"dactinomycin": {
  "origin": "Dactinomycin, also called actinomycin D, comes from soil bacteria. In 1940 Selman Waksman and H. Boyd Woodruff at Rutgers University isolated actinomycin from Streptomyces (Actinomyces) antibioticus. It was the first antibiotic isolated from an actinomycete, but it was far too toxic to use against infections. In the 1950s Sidney Farber and colleagues in Boston showed it was active against Wilms tumor, a childhood kidney cancer, making it one of the first antibiotics used as a cancer drug. Merck marketed it as Cosmegen, approved by the FDA in 1964. It remains part of standard regimens for Wilms tumor, rhabdomyosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma and gestational trophoblastic disease.",
  "timeline": [["1940", "Selman Waksman and H. Boyd Woodruff isolate actinomycin from Streptomyces."], ["1950s", "Sidney Farber's group shows activity in Wilms tumor."], ["1964", "FDA approves Cosmegen (dactinomycin)."]],
  "cell": "Dactinomycin has a flat phenoxazone ring with two cyclic peptide loops. The ring intercalates between guanine-cytosine base pairs, and the peptide loops sit in the minor groove, anchoring it firmly. This blocks DNA-dependent RNA polymerase from moving along the template, so transcription stops, especially of ribosomal RNA. Without new RNA and protein, cells cannot grow or divide and they die. It also causes single-strand DNA breaks, possibly through topoisomerase interference. It is largely cell-cycle nonspecific. It also makes cells more sensitive to radiation.",
  "body": [["Tumor cells", "Blocking transcription starves fast-growing pediatric tumors of new proteins, killing them."], ["Bone marrow", "White cells and platelets fall, with a nadir around one to three weeks."], ["Mouth and gut", "Severe mucositis, diarrhea, nausea and vomiting reflect injury to rapidly renewing lining cells."], ["Liver", "Damage to the small hepatic veins can cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease), with liver enlargement, fluid buildup and jaundice."], ["Skin", "Hair loss and acne-like rashes occur, and previously irradiated skin can flare (radiation recall)."]],
  "journey": "Given IV. It leaves the blood very quickly and concentrates in tissues, where it binds tightly to DNA. Very little is metabolized. It is slowly excreted unchanged in bile and urine, with a terminal half-life of about 36 hours. Doses are measured in micrograms because it is so potent. It does not cross into the brain well.",
  "sidefx": "Because it stops RNA synthesis in all fast-renewing cells, the main toxicities are marrow suppression, mucositis, diarrhea and hair loss. Liver veno-occlusive disease comes from injury to sinusoidal endothelial cells. It is a strong vesicant, so leakage outside a vein causes tissue necrosis. Because it sensitizes cells to radiation, it can worsen radiation injury and cause radiation recall. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Actinomycin D is also a standard lab tool: biologists add it to cultured cells to shut off transcription and measure how long RNA molecules last.",
  "src": ["Cosmegen (dactinomycin) FDA label", "Waksman SA, Woodruff HB. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1940", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"mitomycin": {
  "origin": "Mitomycin C was discovered in Japan. In the mid-1950s scientists at Kyowa Hakko, including Toju Hata and Shigetoshi Wakaki, isolated a family of mitomycins from the soil bacterium Streptomyces caespitosus. Mitomycin C proved the most useful against tumors. The FDA approved it in 1974, and it was sold as Mutamycin. Over the years it found new roles outside the bloodstream: it is applied to the eye during glaucoma surgery (Mitosol, approved 2012) to stop scarring, and gel forms for urothelial cancer were approved as Jelmyto in 2020 for upper tract tumors and Zusduri in 2025 for certain bladder tumors.",
  "timeline": [["1956", "Kyowa Hakko scientists report mitomycins from Streptomyces caespitosus."], ["1974", "FDA approves mitomycin for gastric and pancreatic cancer."], ["2012", "FDA approves Mitosol for use during glaucoma surgery."], ["2020", "FDA approves Jelmyto, a gel for low-grade upper tract urothelial cancer."], ["2025", "FDA approves Zusduri for certain low-grade bladder tumors."]],
  "cell": "Mitomycin is a prodrug. Inside cells, enzymes such as NAD(P)H quinone oxidoreductase (DT-diaphorase) and cytochrome P450 reductase reduce its quinone ring. This unmasks two reactive sites that alkylate DNA, mainly at the N2 position of guanine in CpG sequences. Because it has two reactive sites, it forms crosslinks between the two DNA strands. Crosslinked strands cannot separate for replication or transcription, so the cell dies. Reduction is favored in low-oxygen conditions, so mitomycin is somewhat more active in hypoxic tumor cells. It is cell-cycle nonspecific, with most effect in late G1 and S. In the eye, it stops fibroblasts from dividing so scar tissue does not close the surgical drainage channel.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Platelets and white cells fall late, around four to six weeks, and the effect builds up with repeated cycles."], ["Kidneys and blood vessels", "Endothelial injury can cause hemolytic uremic syndrome with broken red cells, low platelets and kidney failure."], ["Lungs", "Rarely it causes interstitial pneumonitis and fibrosis."], ["Gut and skin", "Nausea, mouth sores and hair loss occur; leaking from a vein causes deep tissue necrosis."], ["Bladder and urinary tract", "Local gels or instillations kill tumor cells on the lining and cause local irritation, with little systemic exposure."], ["Eye", "Applied during surgery, it prevents fibroblast scarring but can thin the tissue around the surgical site."]],
  "journey": "For systemic use it is given IV and cleared from the blood very quickly, with a plasma half-life of under an hour. It is activated and broken down mainly in the liver and other tissues, and only a small part leaves unchanged in urine. Even though it leaves the blood quickly, its DNA crosslinks persist, which explains delayed marrow effects. When put into the bladder, the upper urinary tract or on the eye, only small amounts are absorbed.",
  "sidefx": "Delayed, cumulative marrow suppression reflects DNA crosslinks in slowly cycling stem cells. Hemolytic uremic syndrome and lung injury are thought to come from damage to endothelial cells, possibly through reactive oxygen species formed during redox cycling of the quinone. It is a vesicant, so extravasation causes severe necrosis. With urothelial gels, ureteric narrowing can occur from local scarring. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "The same drug used for stomach cancer is dabbed on the eye for a few minutes during glaucoma surgery to keep the new drainage channel from scarring shut.",
  "src": ["Mitomycin for injection FDA label", "Jelmyto (mitomycin) FDA label", "Mitosol (mitomycin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"irinotecan": {
  "origin": "Irinotecan traces back to a Chinese tree, Camptotheca acuminata, sometimes called the happy tree. In 1966 Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani at the Research Triangle Institute in North Carolina isolated camptothecin from its bark as part of a National Cancer Institute plant screening program. Camptothecin was active against tumors but too toxic and insoluble, and early trials were stopped. In 1985 Leroy Liu and colleagues showed its target was topoisomerase I, which revived interest. Japanese scientists at Yakult Honsha and Daiichi made a water-soluble prodrug, irinotecan, in the 1980s. The FDA approved it as Camptosar in 1996 for colorectal cancer, where it became a core part of FOLFIRI.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani isolate camptothecin from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["1980s", "Japanese researchers synthesize irinotecan, a soluble camptothecin prodrug."], ["1985", "Topoisomerase I is identified as the target of camptothecin."], ["1996", "FDA approves Camptosar for metastatic colorectal cancer."]],
  "cell": "Irinotecan itself is a weak drug. Carboxylesterase enzymes, mainly in the liver, cut off its bulky side chain to release SN-38, which is about 100 to 1,000 times more potent. Topoisomerase I relieves DNA twisting by cutting one strand, letting it rotate and then resealing it. SN-38 wedges into the enzyme-DNA complex and blocks the resealing step. When a replication fork runs into this trapped complex, the single-strand nick becomes a lethal double-strand break, so the drug is S-phase specific. The liver then inactivates SN-38 by attaching glucuronic acid using UGT1A1.",
  "body": [["Tumor cells", "Dividing colorectal and other cancer cells collect replication-linked DNA breaks and die."], ["Gut, early", "Within hours of infusion, irinotecan inhibits acetylcholinesterase, producing a cholinergic syndrome of cramping, sweating, watery eyes, salivation and diarrhea."], ["Gut, late", "Days later, SN-38 released in the gut injures the lining and causes secretory diarrhea that can be severe and dehydrating."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils drop, with a nadir around one to three weeks."], ["Hair and energy", "Hair loss and fatigue are common, reflecting injury to follicle cells and the general toll of treatment."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, interstitial lung disease occurs."]],
  "journey": "Given IV. Carboxylesterase in the liver converts some irinotecan into active SN-38, which peaks about an hour after the infusion. CYP3A4 turns some irinotecan into inactive products, so strong CYP3A4 inducers or inhibitors change exposure. UGT1A1 glucuronidates SN-38 into inactive SN-38 glucuronide, which goes into bile. In the intestine, bacterial beta-glucuronidase can cut off the glucuronide and release active SN-38 again, right next to the gut lining. Half-lives are roughly 6 to 12 hours for irinotecan and 10 to 20 hours for SN-38.",
  "sidefx": "Early diarrhea is cholinergic, from acetylcholinesterase inhibition, and is treated with atropine. Late diarrhea comes from SN-38 being reactivated by gut bacteria and damaging crypt cells, and it calls for prompt loperamide and fluids. Neutropenia comes from killing dividing marrow cells. People with low UGT1A1 activity, such as those homozygous for UGT1A1*28 (the variant behind Gilbert syndrome), clear SN-38 slowly and have more neutropenia, so the label discusses lower starting doses. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "People with Gilbert syndrome, which only causes mild harmless jaundice, can get severe toxicity from irinotecan because the same enzyme handles both bilirubin and SN-38.",
  "src": ["Camptosar (irinotecan) FDA label", "Wall ME, Wani MC et al. J Am Chem Soc 1966", "Hsiang YH, Liu LF et al. J Biol Chem 1985", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"irinotecan-liposome": {
  "origin": "Liposomal irinotecan builds on the camptothecin story that began with the happy tree, Camptotheca acuminata, and the 1966 isolation of camptothecin by Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani. The aim was to keep irinotecan in the bloodstream longer and deliver more of it to tumors. Researchers at Hermes Biosciences in California developed a way to load irinotecan densely inside small liposomes, and Merrimack Pharmaceuticals carried the product forward. The FDA approved it as Onivyde in 2015 for metastatic pancreatic cancer after gemcitabine-based therapy, given with fluorouracil and leucovorin. In 2024 it was approved for first-line metastatic pancreatic cancer in the NALIRIFOX regimen. It is now marketed by Ipsen.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["2000s", "Hermes Biosciences develops liposome-encapsulated irinotecan."], ["2015", "FDA approves Onivyde for pancreatic cancer after gemcitabine."], ["2024", "FDA approves Onivyde as first-line therapy in NALIRIFOX."]],
  "cell": "Inside the bloodstream, irinotecan stays trapped in the liposomes, protected from early conversion and clearance. The liposomes collect in tumor tissue through leaky vessels, where tumor-associated macrophages are thought to take them up and release irinotecan. Carboxylesterase then converts irinotecan to SN-38. SN-38 traps topoisomerase I on DNA after it nicks one strand, so the nick cannot be resealed. When a replication fork collides with this complex, a double-strand break forms and the cell dies. The drug is S-phase specific. The liposome changes where and how long the drug acts, not the target.",
  "body": [["Pancreatic tumor", "Longer exposure inside the tumor increases DNA damage in dividing cancer cells."], ["Gut", "SN-38 injures intestinal lining cells, causing diarrhea that can be severe, sometimes with early cholinergic cramping."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils fall and can lead to serious infections and sepsis."], ["Mouth and appetite", "Stomatitis, nausea, vomiting and poor appetite are common."], ["Lungs", "Rarely, interstitial lung disease develops."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion. Most irinotecan circulates inside liposomes, giving much longer exposure than conventional irinotecan. Liposomes slowly release drug, especially in tumors and in tissues with many macrophages. Released irinotecan is converted to SN-38 by carboxylesterase and broken down by CYP3A4, and SN-38 is cleared by UGT1A1 glucuronidation and biliary excretion. It is not interchangeable with conventional irinotecan. People homozygous for UGT1A1*28 start at a lower dose per the label.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from SN-38 killing dividing marrow cells, and the boxed warning highlights fatal neutropenic sepsis. Diarrhea comes from SN-38 damage to intestinal crypt cells, with early diarrhea partly from cholinergic effects that respond to atropine and late diarrhea treated with loperamide. Slow UGT1A1 clearance raises SN-38 levels and toxicity. Premedication with a corticosteroid and antiemetic is used before each dose. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Onivyde (irinotecan liposome) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Wang-Gillam A et al. NAPOLI-1, Lancet 2016"]
},
"topotecan": {
  "origin": "Topotecan is a descendant of camptothecin, the alkaloid that Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani isolated in 1966 from the bark of the Chinese happy tree, Camptotheca acuminata. Camptothecin itself failed early trials because it was insoluble and toxic, and the water-soluble salt form used was much less active. Once topoisomerase I was identified as its target in 1985, chemists at SmithKline Beecham designed topotecan, a semisynthetic camptothecin with a side chain that makes it water-soluble while keeping the active lactone ring. The FDA approved it as Hycamtin in 1996 for ovarian cancer after initial chemotherapy. It later gained approvals for small cell lung cancer, including an oral capsule, and for cervical cancer with cisplatin.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["1985", "Topoisomerase I is identified as camptothecin's target."], ["1996", "FDA approves Hycamtin for ovarian cancer."], ["2006", "Approval for cervical cancer in combination with cisplatin."], ["2007", "Oral topotecan capsules are approved for relapsed small cell lung cancer."]],
  "cell": "Topoisomerase I relaxes supercoiled DNA by nicking one strand, letting it swivel, then resealing it. Topotecan binds the enzyme-DNA complex at the nick and blocks resealing, trapping a covalent topoisomerase I-DNA cleavable complex. When a replication fork reaches this trapped complex, the single-strand nick turns into a double-strand break, which the cell cannot repair well, so it undergoes apoptosis. This makes the drug S-phase specific. Its active form has a closed lactone ring; at body pH the ring opens reversibly into a less active carboxylate form.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils, platelets and red cells fall, with a nadir around 7 to 11 days; this is the dose-limiting toxicity."], ["Tumor cells", "Dividing ovarian, lung and cervical cancer cells accumulate replication-linked DNA breaks and die."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea occur, and rarely neutropenic colitis with fever and abdominal pain."], ["Hair", "Hair loss is common because follicle cells divide rapidly."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease is a rare but serious effect."]],
  "journey": "Given IV, or by mouth as capsules with about 40 percent bioavailability. In the blood, the lactone ring opens and closes depending on pH. Unlike irinotecan, topotecan needs no activation and is not mainly handled by the liver. A small amount is N-demethylated by CYP enzymes, but most is cleared by the kidneys, so kidney impairment raises exposure and calls for dose reduction per the label. The half-life is short, about two to three hours. It crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid fairly well.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression is the main problem and comes directly from killing dividing blood precursors; the label limits starting treatment to adequate baseline neutrophil and platelet counts. Diarrhea and mucositis reflect injury to gut lining cells. Neutropenic colitis happens when a damaged gut wall meets very low neutrophils. Kidney impairment increases toxicity because the kidneys clear the drug. There is no antidote; growth factors and transfusions support recovery.",
  "src": ["Hycamtin (topotecan) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"vinblastine": {
  "origin": "Vinblastine comes from the Madagascar periwinkle, Catharanthus roseus, a plant used in folk medicine in several countries to treat diabetes. In the 1950s Robert Noble and Charles Beer at the University of Western Ontario tested periwinkle extracts for blood sugar effects. Instead, the extracts made rats' white cell counts plunge. They isolated the responsible alkaloid, vincaleukoblastine, later called vinblastine, and reported it in 1958. At nearly the same time, Gordon Svoboda's group at Eli Lilly was isolating periwinkle alkaloids, including vincristine. Vinblastine was approved in the early 1960s as Velban and became a key drug for Hodgkin lymphoma (in ABVD) and testicular cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Noble and Beer find that periwinkle extract lowers white cell counts in rats."], ["1958", "Vinblastine (vincaleukoblastine) is reported."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly markets vinblastine as Velban."], ["1970s", "ABVD, which includes vinblastine, is developed for Hodgkin lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Microtubules are hollow tubes made of alpha- and beta-tubulin that form the mitotic spindle. Vinblastine binds beta-tubulin at the vinca site, near the growing plus end. This blocks addition of new tubulin dimers, so microtubules cannot polymerize, and at higher levels it causes tubulin to clump into paracrystals. Without a working spindle, chromosomes cannot line up and separate, so cells stall in metaphase (M phase) and undergo apoptosis. Neurons also depend on microtubules for axonal transport, but vinblastine affects them less than vincristine does.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "White cell counts drop sharply with a nadir around 5 to 10 days; this limits dosing."], ["Nerves", "Interference with axonal microtubules can cause numbness, tingling and loss of reflexes, though less than with vincristine."], ["Gut", "Autonomic nerve effects slow the bowel, causing constipation and sometimes ileus."], ["Jaw and bones", "Jaw pain and bone pain occur, likely from nerve effects."], ["Kidneys and sodium", "Inappropriate release of antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) can make the kidneys hold water and lower blood sodium."], ["Hair", "Hair loss is common because hair follicle cells divide rapidly."]],
  "journey": "Given only IV, often as a short push into a running line. It leaves the blood quickly and binds tightly to tissues and platelets. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as azole antifungals raise toxicity. Most leaves in bile and feces. The terminal half-life is about a day. It does not enter the brain well. Liver impairment raises exposure.",
  "sidefx": "Marrow suppression comes from blocking mitosis in dividing blood precursors. Neuropathy, constipation and jaw pain come from disrupted axonal microtubules. The deadliest risk is giving it into the spinal fluid by mistake: the drug destroys nerve tissue and causes ascending paralysis and death, so syringes carry a 'for IV use only, fatal if given by other routes' label and it is often dispensed in a small IV bag. It is a vesicant; leakage causes tissue damage, often managed with hyaluronidase and warm compresses. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Vinblastine was found by accident: researchers were testing periwinkle as a diabetes remedy and noticed it wiped out white blood cells instead.",
  "src": ["Vinblastine sulfate injection FDA label", "Noble RL, Beer CT, Cutts JH. Ann N Y Acad Sci 1958", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"vinorelbine": {
  "origin": "Vinorelbine is a semisynthetic vinca alkaloid designed in France. In the late 1970s Pierre Potier and colleagues at the Institut de Chimie des Substances Naturelles, part of the French national research center CNRS, worked on modifying the periwinkle alkaloids that gave vinblastine and vincristine. By changing the catharanthine part of the molecule, they made vinorelbine, which binds mitotic spindle microtubules more than the microtubules in nerve axons. Developed with Pierre Fabre, it was launched in France in 1989. The FDA approved it as Navelbine in 1994 for non-small cell lung cancer, and it is widely used for breast cancer as well.",
  "timeline": [["1979", "Pierre Potier's group at CNRS in France prepares vinorelbine."], ["1989", "Vinorelbine is launched in France."], ["1994", "FDA approves Navelbine for non-small cell lung cancer."]],
  "cell": "Vinorelbine binds beta-tubulin at the vinca binding site near the microtubule plus end and blocks addition of new tubulin, preventing microtubule polymerization. The mitotic spindle cannot form properly, so cells arrest in metaphase (M phase) and die by apoptosis. Compared with vincristine, it has relatively more effect on mitotic microtubules than on the axonal microtubules that carry cargo along nerves, which explains its lower nerve toxicity. Its main dose-limiting effect is on dividing granulocyte precursors.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Granulocytes fall sharply with a nadir around 7 to 10 days, raising infection risk."], ["Nerves", "Mild numbness and tingling can occur, but much less than with vincristine."], ["Gut", "Constipation is common and, rarely, paralytic ileus develops from autonomic nerve effects."], ["Veins", "It irritates veins, causing phlebitis, so the vein is flushed after each dose."], ["Lungs", "Rare acute shortness of breath, interstitial pneumonitis and ARDS can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme elevations occur, and liver impairment raises drug levels."]],
  "journey": "Given IV as a short infusion (an oral form exists outside the US). It spreads widely into tissues, including lung tissue, giving a large volume of distribution. The liver metabolizes it mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can increase toxicity. Most is excreted in bile and feces, with a smaller share in urine. The terminal half-life is roughly 28 to 44 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Severe neutropenia, the boxed warning, comes from mitotic arrest of granulocyte precursors and can lead to septic shock. Constipation and neuropathy reflect microtubule disruption in autonomic and sensory nerves. Phlebitis and extravasation injury arise because the drug is a vesicant that damages vein walls. Like the other vincas, it is fatal if given by any route other than IV. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Navelbine (vinorelbine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"paclitaxel-protein-bound": {
  "origin": "Paclitaxel came from the bark of the Pacific yew, Taxus brevifolia. A US Department of Agriculture botanist, Arthur Barclay, collected the bark in 1962 for a National Cancer Institute plant screening program, and Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani reported paclitaxel's structure in 1971. In 1979 Susan Horwitz at Albert Einstein College of Medicine showed it worked in a new way, by freezing microtubules. Conventional paclitaxel is almost insoluble and must be dissolved in castor-oil-based Cremophor, which causes allergic reactions. American BioScience (later Abraxis BioScience), founded by Patrick Soon-Shiong, bound paclitaxel to human albumin to form nanoparticles that need no solvent. The FDA approved this nab-paclitaxel as Abraxane in 2005 for metastatic breast cancer, followed by non-small cell lung cancer in 2012 and pancreatic cancer in 2013.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Pacific yew bark is collected for the National Cancer Institute screening program."], ["1971", "Wall and Wani report the structure of paclitaxel."], ["1979", "Susan Horwitz shows paclitaxel stabilizes microtubules."], ["2005", "FDA approves Abraxane for metastatic breast cancer."], ["2012", "Approval for non-small cell lung cancer."], ["2013", "Approval for metastatic pancreatic cancer with gemcitabine."]],
  "cell": "Paclitaxel binds the beta-tubulin subunit on the inside of microtubules. Instead of blocking assembly like the vinca alkaloids, it does the opposite: it promotes assembly and stops microtubules from coming apart. Normal mitosis needs microtubules to grow and shrink rapidly. Frozen, overly stable microtubules cannot form a working spindle, so the spindle checkpoint holds cells in G2/M, and they die by apoptosis. The albumin carrier may help delivery to tumors through albumin transport pathways such as gp60 and SPARC, though how much this matters in people is still debated.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils drop with a nadir around days 8 to 15, which is the main dose-limiting effect."], ["Nerves", "Stabilized microtubules disrupt transport along long sensory axons, causing numbness, tingling and pain in the hands and feet."], ["Muscles and joints", "Aches in muscles and joints are common in the days after treatment."], ["Hair", "Nearly complete hair loss is typical because follicle cells are blocked in mitosis."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur, especially with gemcitabine."], ["Immune system", "Without Cremophor, severe allergic infusion reactions are much less common than with conventional paclitaxel."]],
  "journey": "Given IV as a short infusion. The albumin nanoparticles break apart quickly in the blood, releasing paclitaxel bound to albumin, which spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks paclitaxel down mainly through CYP2C8 and CYP3A4, so drugs that strongly block or induce these enzymes change its levels. Most leaves through bile in the feces. The terminal half-life is about a day. It is not interchangeable with conventional paclitaxel.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from mitotic arrest in dividing marrow cells, so the label requires adequate neutrophil counts before each dose. Sensory neuropathy comes from rigid microtubules blocking axonal transport, and it tends to improve after the drug is held. Infections and sepsis, notably in pancreatic cancer, follow neutropenia. Because it contains human albumin, there is a remote theoretical risk of transmitting viral disease. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "One early-1990s estimate held that treating a single patient with paclitaxel took the bark of several mature yew trees, which pushed chemists to make it from renewable yew needles and later by plant cell culture.",
  "src": ["Abraxane (paclitaxel protein-bound) FDA label", "Schiff PB, Fant J, Horwitz SB. Nature 1979", "Wani MC et al. J Am Chem Soc 1971"]
},
"docetaxel": {
  "origin": "Docetaxel came from a search for a sustainable taxane. Paclitaxel needed bark from the slow-growing Pacific yew, which was scarce. In the 1980s Pierre Potier's team at the CNRS Institut de Chimie des Substances Naturelles in Gif-sur-Yvette, France, worked with 10-deacetylbaccatin III, a taxane core that can be extracted from the needles of the common European yew, Taxus baccata. Needles regrow, so the source was renewable. By adding a new side chain, they made docetaxel, which turned out to be about twice as potent as paclitaxel at binding microtubules in lab studies. Rhone-Poulenc Rorer developed it, and the FDA approved it as Taxotere in 1996. It is now used for breast, lung, prostate, gastric, and head and neck cancers.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Pierre Potier's CNRS team synthesizes docetaxel from European yew needle extract."], ["1996", "FDA approves Taxotere for advanced breast cancer."], ["2004", "Approval with prednisone for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]],
  "cell": "Docetaxel binds the taxane site on beta-tubulin inside the microtubule. It pushes tubulin to assemble and blocks disassembly, producing rigid, nonfunctional microtubule bundles. The mitotic spindle cannot reorganize, so the spindle assembly checkpoint holds cells in M phase (G2/M), and they undergo apoptosis. It also promotes phosphorylation of the anti-apoptotic protein bcl-2, removing a survival signal. In prostate cancer, microtubule stabilization also hinders transport of the androgen receptor into the nucleus.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophils drop, with a nadir at about one week, and febrile neutropenia is common."], ["Fluid balance", "Capillaries become leaky over repeated cycles, causing swelling, weight gain, pleural effusions and ascites; dexamethasone premedication reduces this."], ["Skin and nails", "Nail discoloration and loss, rash and hand-foot changes occur."], ["Nerves", "Sensory neuropathy develops as with other taxanes."], ["Eyes", "Excess tearing from tear duct narrowing and rarely cystoid macular edema occur."], ["Hair", "Hair loss is common, and some patients have persistent alopecia."]],
  "journey": "Given IV as a one-hour infusion. It binds tightly to plasma proteins and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors increase exposure, and liver impairment sharply raises the risk of fatal toxicity. About three quarters leaves in the feces through bile and only a small amount in urine. The terminal half-life is about 11 hours. The formulation contains polysorbate 80 and alcohol, and the alcohol content can cause intoxication-like effects.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenia and infections come from mitotic arrest of marrow precursors. Fluid retention is a cumulative capillary leak that corticosteroid premedication reduces. Hypersensitivity reactions are partly linked to the polysorbate 80 solvent. Neuropathy comes from rigid axonal microtubules. Toxic deaths are much more likely with abnormal liver tests because the liver clears the drug, which is why the boxed warning centers on liver function. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Docetaxel was made from needles instead of bark, so yew trees did not have to be cut down to produce it.",
  "src": ["Taxotere (docetaxel) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Gueritte-Voegelein F et al. J Med Chem 1991"]
},
"cabazitaxel": {
  "origin": "Cabazitaxel was developed by Sanofi as a next-generation taxane for tumors that had stopped responding to docetaxel. A common way tumors resist taxanes is by pumping them out with P-glycoprotein (MDR1). Sanofi chemists modified the docetaxel structure, adding two methoxy groups, which made the molecule a poor substrate for this pump. Like docetaxel, it is made semisynthetically from 10-deacetylbaccatin III extracted from yew needles. In the TROPIC trial, cabazitaxel with prednisone improved survival in men with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer that had progressed after docetaxel, and the FDA approved it as Jevtana in 2010.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Sanofi develops cabazitaxel to overcome P-glycoprotein-mediated taxane resistance."], ["2010", "The TROPIC trial shows a survival benefit after docetaxel."], ["2010", "FDA approves Jevtana for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]],
  "cell": "Cabazitaxel binds beta-tubulin at the taxane site and stabilizes microtubules by promoting assembly and blocking disassembly. The rigid microtubules prevent the mitotic spindle from working, so cells arrest at G2/M and die by apoptosis. Its key difference from docetaxel is low affinity for P-glycoprotein, the ATP-driven efflux pump that many resistant cancer cells overexpress, so it stays inside those cells. The same property lets it cross the blood-brain barrier better than other taxanes.",
  "body": [["Prostate cancer cells", "Docetaxel-resistant tumor cells that pump out other taxanes are still killed during mitosis."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common and can be fatal; neutrophils bottom out in the second week of the cycle."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting come from injury to dividing gut lining cells and can cause dehydration."], ["Kidneys and bladder", "Dehydration, sepsis or urinary obstruction can cause kidney failure, and cystitis with blood in the urine can occur, including radiation recall."], ["Immune system", "Severe hypersensitivity reactions can occur, partly linked to the polysorbate 80 solvent."]],
  "journey": "Given IV as a one-hour infusion, with antihistamine, corticosteroid and H2 blocker premedication. It spreads widely into tissues and binds plasma proteins. The liver metabolizes most of it through CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, with a little CYP2C8, so strong CYP3A inhibitors and inducers alter its levels. About three quarters is excreted in feces and very little in urine. Its terminal half-life is long, about four days.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenic deaths, in the boxed warning, come from mitotic arrest of granulocyte precursors; prophylactic growth factor support is often used. Severe hypersensitivity is linked to polysorbate 80. Diarrhea comes from killing dividing intestinal cells. Kidney failure often follows dehydration or sepsis. Liver impairment raises exposure because the liver clears the drug. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Jevtana (cabazitaxel) FDA label", "de Bono JS et al. TROPIC trial, Lancet 2010", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"eribulin": {
  "origin": "Eribulin comes from the sea. In 1986 Japanese chemists Daisuke Uemura and Yoshimasa Hirata isolated halichondrin B from the marine sponge Halichondria okadai. It was extremely potent against cancer cells, but sponges made only tiny amounts. In 1992 Yoshito Kishi's lab at Harvard completed a total synthesis of halichondrin B. Working with Kishi, scientists at the Japanese company Eisai found that the right half of the molecule carried most of the activity and designed a simpler, fully synthetic version, eribulin. Even simplified, it takes dozens of chemical steps to make, making it one of the most complex drugs produced by total synthesis. The FDA approved it as Halaven in 2010 for metastatic breast cancer and in 2016 for liposarcoma.",
  "timeline": [["1986", "Uemura and Hirata isolate halichondrin B from the sponge Halichondria okadai."], ["1992", "Yoshito Kishi's group at Harvard completes the total synthesis of halichondrin B."], ["2010", "FDA approves Halaven for metastatic breast cancer."], ["2016", "FDA approves eribulin for unresectable or metastatic liposarcoma."]],
  "cell": "Eribulin works differently from both taxanes and vinca alkaloids. It binds to the plus ends of microtubules with high affinity and suppresses the growth phase of dynamic instability, while leaving the shortening phase largely untouched. It also traps free tubulin in nonproductive aggregates. Without normal growth at the plus ends, the mitotic spindle cannot form, and cells arrest at G2/M. The block is irreversible, so cells die by apoptosis even after the drug is gone. In lab and animal studies it also remodels tumor blood vessels and reverses epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition, which may limit spread.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is the most common serious effect, with a nadir around day 13."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy develops from disrupted axonal microtubules, though often milder than with taxanes."], ["Heart rhythm", "It can prolong the QT interval, especially with low potassium or magnesium."], ["Hair and energy", "Hair loss and fatigue are common, along with nausea and constipation."], ["Tumor tissue", "Breast cancer and liposarcoma cells stall in mitosis and die."]],
  "journey": "Given IV over two to five minutes. It spreads widely into tissues and has a long half-life of about 40 hours. Unlike taxanes, it is barely metabolized and CYP3A4 plays little role, so drug interactions are few. Most of it is excreted unchanged in the feces, with a small amount in urine. Liver and moderate or severe kidney impairment raise exposure and call for lower doses per the label.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenia comes from mitotic arrest of marrow precursors, so blood counts are checked before each dose. Neuropathy comes from impaired microtubule growth in long nerve axons. QT prolongation was seen in studies and is more likely with low potassium or magnesium, heart failure or other QT drugs. It is not a vesicant like the vincas. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Early isolation work processed about 600 kilograms of sponge to obtain roughly a third of a gram of halichondrin B, which is why a fully synthetic version was the only practical route.",
  "src": ["Halaven (eribulin) FDA label", "Hirata Y, Uemura D. Pure Appl Chem 1986", "Aicher TD, Kishi Y et al. J Am Chem Soc 1992"]
},
"ixabepilone": {
  "origin": "Ixabepilone is based on epothilones, natural products made by a soil myxobacterium, Sorangium cellulosum. In 1987 Gerhard Hofle and Hans Reichenbach at the German Research Centre for Biotechnology (GBF) in Braunschweig isolated epothilones, first noting antifungal activity. In 1995 researchers at Merck showed epothilones stabilize microtubules like paclitaxel, even in taxane-resistant cells. Natural epothilone B is unstable in the body because enzymes break its lactone ring. Bristol-Myers Squibb chemists replaced the ring oxygen with nitrogen, making a lactam that resists breakdown. This ixabepilone was approved by the FDA as Ixempra in 2007 for metastatic or locally advanced breast cancer resistant to other drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1987", "Hofle and Reichenbach isolate epothilones from Sorangium cellulosum."], ["1995", "Epothilones are shown to stabilize microtubules like paclitaxel."], ["2007", "FDA approves Ixempra for resistant metastatic or locally advanced breast cancer."]],
  "cell": "Ixabepilone binds beta-tubulin at or near the taxane site, promoting microtubule assembly and preventing disassembly. Hyperstable microtubules cannot form a working mitotic spindle, so cells arrest at G2/M and undergo apoptosis. It is less affected than taxanes by common resistance mechanisms: it is a poor substrate for P-glycoprotein and MRP-1 efflux pumps and retains activity in cells that overexpress beta-III tubulin, an isotype linked to taxane resistance.",
  "body": [["Breast cancer cells", "Tumors resistant to anthracyclines and taxanes can still be arrested in mitosis and killed."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral sensory neuropathy is common and is the main dose-limiting effect."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia with a nadir in the second week raises infection risk."], ["Muscles and joints", "Muscle and joint aches, fatigue and hair loss are common."], ["Heart", "Rarely it is linked to myocardial ischemia and ventricular dysfunction."]],
  "journey": "Given IV as a three-hour infusion after premedication with H1 and H2 blockers, because the solvent contains polyoxyl 35 castor oil (Cremophor). It spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down extensively through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers alter exposure. Most leaves in the feces, with some in urine. The half-life is about 52 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Neuropathy comes from rigid microtubules disrupting transport along long sensory nerves. Neutropenia reflects mitotic arrest of marrow precursors. Hypersensitivity reactions are linked to the castor oil solvent, so premedication is required. The boxed warning against combining it with capecitabine in liver impairment exists because reduced CYP3A4 clearance raises drug levels and caused neutropenia-related deaths. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Ixempra (ixabepilone) FDA label", "Gerth K, Bedorf N, Hofle G, Reichenbach H. J Antibiot 1996", "Bollag DM et al. Cancer Res 1995"]
},
"arsenic-trioxide": {
  "origin": "Arsenic has a long and grim history as a poison, but also as a medicine. Arsenic compounds appear in ancient Greek and Chinese medicine. In 1786 the English physician Thomas Fowler introduced Fowler's solution, a potassium arsenite tonic, and in the late 1800s doctors noticed it lowered white cell counts in leukemia. The modern story began in China. In the 1970s Zhang Tingdong and colleagues at Harbin Medical University found that an arsenic trioxide preparation could put patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) into remission. In the 1990s Shanghai researchers, including Chen Zhu and Wang Zhen-yi, showed how it works on the PML-RARA protein. A 1998 New England Journal of Medicine study from Memorial Sloan Kettering confirmed the results, and the FDA approved Trisenox in 2000. In 2018 it was approved with tretinoin as first-line therapy for low-risk APL, a chemotherapy-free regimen that cures most patients.",
  "timeline": [["1786", "Thomas Fowler introduces Fowler's solution, an arsenic tonic."], ["1970s", "Zhang Tingdong's group in Harbin reports arsenic remissions in APL."], ["1998", "Memorial Sloan Kettering study of arsenic trioxide in relapsed APL is published."], ["2000", "FDA approves Trisenox for relapsed or refractory APL."], ["2018", "FDA approves arsenic trioxide with tretinoin as first-line therapy for low-risk APL."]],
  "cell": "APL is driven by the t(15;17) translocation, which fuses the PML gene to the retinoic acid receptor alpha gene (RARA). The PML-RARA fusion protein blocks myeloid differentiation, so cells pile up as promyelocytes. Arsenic binds directly to cysteine residues in the PML part of the fusion protein. This causes the protein to clump and become tagged with SUMO and then ubiquitin, which marks it for destruction by the proteasome. With PML-RARA gone, the blasts partially mature into neutrophil-like cells. Arsenic also triggers apoptosis by damaging mitochondria, increasing reactive oxygen species and activating caspases. Tretinoin attacks the RARA side of the same fusion protein, so the two drugs work together.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic promyelocytes mature and die, and white counts often rise first (leukocytosis) as cells differentiate."], ["Lungs and whole body", "Differentiating cells release cytokines and stick to vessel walls, causing differentiation syndrome with fever, shortness of breath, lung infiltrates, fluid gain and low blood pressure."], ["Heart", "It prolongs the QT interval, which can lead to torsade de pointes or AV block."], ["Blood clotting", "Clearing APL cells resolves the dangerous bleeding and clotting disorder (DIC) typical of APL."], ["Nervous system", "It can cause peripheral neuropathy and encephalopathy, including Wernicke encephalopathy."], ["Liver and metabolism", "Liver enzyme rises, high blood sugar and low potassium and magnesium are common."]],
  "journey": "Given IV. In the blood, arsenic trioxide dissolves into arsenious acid, the active trivalent form. It distributes widely into tissues, including the liver, kidneys, heart, lungs, hair and nails. The liver reduces and methylates it into monomethylarsonic acid and dimethylarsinic acid, which are less toxic. CYP enzymes are not involved. Most is excreted in urine, so kidney impairment can raise exposure. The half-life of trivalent arsenic is about 10 to 14 hours, but arsenic lingers in tissues much longer.",
  "sidefx": "Differentiation syndrome comes from the rapid maturation of many leukemic cells that release cytokines and migrate into the lungs and other tissues; it is treated with high-dose corticosteroids. QT prolongation reflects arsenic effects on cardiac potassium and calcium channels and is worse with low potassium or magnesium, so electrolytes and ECGs are monitored. Encephalopathy, including thiamine-related Wernicke encephalopathy, has been reported. For acute arsenic poisoning, chelating agents such as dimercaprol or succimer are used, but they are not used to reverse therapeutic effects.",
  "fun": "Fowler's solution was so widely used in the 1800s that Charles Darwin is thought to have taken it for skin problems.",
  "src": ["Trisenox (arsenic trioxide) FDA label", "Soignet SL et al. N Engl J Med 1998", "Lo-Coco F et al. N Engl J Med 2013", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"pegaspargase": {
  "origin": "Asparaginase therapy started with an odd observation. In 1953 John Kidd at Cornell found that guinea pig serum made lymphomas melt away in mice, but the serum of horses or rabbits did not. In 1961 J. D. Broome showed the active factor was the enzyme L-asparaginase. Scientists then found that the bacterium Escherichia coli makes a similar enzyme in large amounts, and E. coli asparaginase (Elspar) was approved by the FDA in 1978 for acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Because the bacterial protein triggered allergic reactions and was cleared quickly, Enzon attached polyethylene glycol chains to it. This PEG-asparaginase, Oncaspar, was approved in 1994 for patients allergic to native asparaginase, and in 2006 for first-line ALL. It is now the standard asparaginase in US ALL regimens.",
  "timeline": [["1953", "John Kidd finds guinea pig serum makes lymphomas regress in mice."], ["1961", "J. D. Broome identifies L-asparaginase as the active factor."], ["1978", "FDA approves native E. coli asparaginase (Elspar)."], ["1994", "FDA approves Oncaspar for patients allergic to native asparaginase."], ["2006", "Oncaspar is approved for first-line treatment of ALL."]],
  "cell": "Pegaspargase is E. coli L-asparaginase with many PEG chains attached. The enzyme stays in the bloodstream and breaks down the amino acid L-asparagine into aspartic acid and ammonia. Most normal cells can make their own asparagine using asparagine synthetase. Leukemic lymphoblasts have very low asparagine synthetase activity, so they depend on asparagine from the blood. When it is gone, they cannot make proteins, stop in G1 and die by apoptosis. The enzyme also breaks down some glutamine. The PEG chains shield the protein from antibodies and slow its clearance, so one dose works for about two weeks or more.",
  "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Starved of asparagine, ALL lymphoblasts stop making proteins and die."], ["Liver", "The liver's own protein production also falls, lowering albumin, clotting factors and anticoagulant proteins, and causing fatty liver and enzyme rises."], ["Blood clotting", "Lower antithrombin, protein C and protein S alongside lower fibrinogen raise the risk of both clots, including cerebral venous sinus thrombosis, and bleeding."], ["Pancreas", "It can cause pancreatitis, sometimes severe or necrotizing."], ["Blood sugar", "Less insulin production and possibly insulin resistance cause high blood sugar, especially with steroids."], ["Immune system", "The bacterial protein can still trigger allergic reactions or silent inactivating antibodies."]],
  "journey": "Given IV or IM. It is a large protein that stays mainly in the blood and lymph, with little entry into tissues. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes; it is slowly broken down by proteases and taken up by the reticuloendothelial system. The PEG coat stretches the half-life to about five to six days, so asparaginase activity lasts about two to three weeks. Serum asparaginase activity can be measured to confirm the drug is working.",
  "sidefx": "Most toxicities come from asparagine and glutamine depletion in normal organs that make a lot of protein. The liver makes less albumin, fibrinogen and natural anticoagulants, which explains clots, bleeding and hepatotoxicity. Reduced insulin synthesis causes high blood sugar. Pancreatitis is a direct toxic effect on the pancreas. Allergic reactions come from antibodies against the bacterial protein; these can also neutralize the drug silently. Patients who react are usually switched to Erwinia-derived asparaginase. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The first clue to asparaginase came from guinea pigs: only their serum, among many animals tested, made lymphomas shrink in mice.",
  "src": ["Oncaspar (pegaspargase) FDA label", "Kidd JG. J Exp Med 1953", "Broome JD. Nature 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"calaspargase-pegol": {
  "origin": "Calaspargase pegol is the newest step in the asparaginase story that began in 1953, when John Kidd found that guinea pig serum shrank lymphomas in mice, and in 1961, when J. D. Broome traced that effect to the enzyme L-asparaginase. E. coli asparaginase and then pegaspargase (PEG-linked E. coli asparaginase) became pillars of ALL treatment. Pegaspargase attaches its PEG chains through a succinimidyl succinate linker, which can break down over time. Calaspargase pegol uses the same E. coli enzyme but a more stable succinimidyl carbonate linker, so the PEG coat stays on longer and enzyme activity lasts longer. The FDA approved it as Asparlas, marketed by Servier, in 2018 as part of multi-agent therapy for ALL in patients from 1 month to 21 years old.",
  "timeline": [["1953", "John Kidd observes lymphoma regression from guinea pig serum."], ["1961", "J. D. Broome identifies L-asparaginase as the active factor."], ["1994", "Pegaspargase, an earlier PEGylated E. coli asparaginase, is FDA approved."], ["2018", "FDA approves Asparlas (calaspargase pegol-mknl) for ALL in patients 1 month to 21 years."]],
  "cell": "Calaspargase pegol is E. coli L-asparaginase with PEG chains linked by succinimidyl carbonate bonds. In the blood, the enzyme splits L-asparagine into aspartic acid and ammonia, and it also breaks down some glutamine. Normal cells can make asparagine with asparagine synthetase, but ALL lymphoblasts have very little of this enzyme. Without asparagine from the blood, they cannot build proteins, arrest in G1 and die by apoptosis. The more stable linker keeps the PEG shield attached, slowing clearance and reducing exposure of the enzyme to antibodies, so asparagine stays depleted for longer between doses.",
  "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Lymphoblasts starved of asparagine stop making proteins and die."], ["Liver", "Reduced protein synthesis lowers albumin, fibrinogen and natural anticoagulants and can cause fatty liver, liver enzyme rises and high bilirubin."], ["Blood clotting", "Imbalanced clotting and anticoagulant proteins increase the risk of both thrombosis and bleeding."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis can occur and may be severe."], ["Blood sugar", "Lower insulin production can cause high blood sugar, especially with steroids."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies to the bacterial protein can cause allergic reactions or silently inactivate the drug."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion. Like other asparaginases, it is a large protein that stays mostly in the bloodstream. It is not cleared by the kidneys or by liver CYP enzymes; instead it is slowly broken down by proteases and removed by the reticuloendothelial system. The stable linker gives it a long half-life of about 16 days, so asparaginase activity stays high for about three weeks or longer, allowing less frequent dosing than pegaspargase. Patients are watched for one hour after each dose for allergic reactions.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects mirror its mechanism. Depleting asparagine and glutamine slows protein synthesis in the liver, which explains clotting problems, bleeding and liver toxicity. Reduced insulin synthesis raises blood sugar. Pancreatitis is a known direct toxicity. Hypersensitivity comes from antibodies to the E. coli protein; patients who react are generally switched to an Erwinia-derived asparaginase, which the antibodies do not recognize. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Asparlas (calaspargase pegol-mknl) FDA label", "Broome JD. Nature 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"asparaginase-erwinia-recombinant": {
  "origin": "Asparaginase from the plant-disease bacterium Erwinia chrysanthemi (now often called Dickeya chrysanthemi) has been used for decades as a backup for patients allergic to E. coli asparaginase, because the two enzymes look different to the immune system. A native Erwinia product, Erwinaze, was FDA approved in 2011, but it was made in limited supply and shortages were common. To fix that, Jazz Pharmaceuticals developed a recombinant version, produced by inserting the Erwinia enzyme gene into the bacterium Pseudomonas fluorescens. The FDA approved it as Rylaze in 2021 for ALL and lymphoblastic lymphoma in patients who developed hypersensitivity to E. coli-derived asparaginase. It was reviewed under the FDA's Real-Time Oncology Review program.",
  "timeline": [["1961", "L-asparaginase is identified as the antileukemic factor in guinea pig serum."], ["2011", "FDA approves native Erwinia asparaginase (Erwinaze)."], ["2021", "FDA approves Rylaze, a recombinant Erwinia asparaginase made in Pseudomonas fluorescens."]],
  "cell": "Rylaze is an L-asparaginase with the amino acid sequence of the Erwinia chrysanthemi enzyme. In the blood it breaks L-asparagine into aspartic acid and ammonia, and it breaks down glutamine somewhat more than the E. coli enzyme does. ALL lymphoblasts lack enough asparagine synthetase to make their own asparagine, so when blood levels drop they cannot build proteins, arrest in G1 and undergo apoptosis. Because its structure differs from E. coli asparaginase, antibodies a patient made against the E. coli enzyme generally do not bind or neutralize it, so it keeps working after an allergy.",
  "body": [["Leukemic cells", "Asparagine starvation kills lymphoblasts that cannot make their own asparagine."], ["Liver", "Lower protein production can raise liver enzymes and bilirubin and reduce clotting proteins."], ["Blood clotting", "Changes in fibrinogen and natural anticoagulants raise the risk of clots and bleeding."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis can occur, likely from direct toxicity to pancreatic cells that make large amounts of protein."], ["Blood sugar", "Reduced insulin synthesis can raise blood sugar."], ["Injection site and immune system", "IM injection can cause local pain, and the protein can still cause allergic reactions."]],
  "journey": "Given as an IM injection. It peaks in the blood in about one to two days. Without a PEG coat, it has a short half-life of about 18 hours, so it is given frequently, such as every 48 hours or on a Monday-Wednesday-Friday schedule, to keep asparaginase activity high. Like other protein drugs, it is broken down by proteases and the reticuloendothelial system, not by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Like all asparaginases, its toxicities come from depleting asparagine and glutamine, which slows protein synthesis in the liver and pancreas. That explains liver toxicity, low clotting proteins with clots or bleeding, high blood sugar and pancreatitis. Hypersensitivity can still occur because it is a bacterial protein, though cross-reactivity with E. coli asparaginase antibodies is low. Nausea and fatigue are common. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The Erwinia bacterium that supplies this enzyme's gene is best known as a cause of soft rot in plants such as potatoes and chrysanthemums.",
  "src": ["Rylaze (asparaginase erwinia chrysanthemi (recombinant)-rywn) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"romidepsin": {
  "origin": "Romidepsin is a natural product from a soil bacterium. In the early 1990s scientists at the Japanese company Fujisawa Pharmaceutical isolated it, then called FR901228 or FK228, from Chromobacterium violaceum, a bacterium known for its violet pigment. It showed striking activity against tumor cells, and its mechanism was later found to be inhibition of histone deacetylases. The US National Cancer Institute helped carry it through early trials, and Gloucester Pharmaceuticals took it to approval. The FDA approved it as Istodax in 2009 for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL) after at least one prior systemic therapy. A peripheral T-cell lymphoma indication granted in 2011 was withdrawn in 2021 after a confirmatory trial did not show benefit.",
  "timeline": [["1994", "Fujisawa scientists report FR901228 (romidepsin) from Chromobacterium violaceum."], ["1998", "Romidepsin is shown to inhibit histone deacetylases."], ["2009", "FDA approves Istodax for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."], ["2021", "The peripheral T-cell lymphoma indication is withdrawn in the US."]],
  "cell": "DNA in the nucleus is wrapped around histone proteins. Histone deacetylases (HDACs) remove acetyl groups from histone tails, which tightens chromatin and silences genes. Romidepsin is a prodrug with an internal disulfide bond. Inside the cell, glutathione reduces the disulfide, freeing a thiol group that binds the zinc ion at the active site of class I HDACs, mainly HDAC1 and HDAC2. With these enzymes blocked, acetylated histones build up, chromatin opens and silenced genes, including the cell-cycle brake p21, are switched back on. Malignant T cells then arrest in G1 and G2/M and undergo apoptosis. Acetylation of non-histone proteins, such as transcription factors, also contributes.",
  "body": [["Malignant T cells", "Lymphoma cells in the skin and blood stop dividing and die, which can shrink skin plaques and tumors."], ["Bone marrow", "Platelets, neutrophils and red cells fall, raising bleeding and infection risk."], ["Gut and taste", "Nausea, vomiting, poor appetite and altered taste are very common."], ["Heart", "It causes T-wave and ST-segment changes on ECG and can prolong the QT interval, especially with low potassium or magnesium."], ["Immune system", "Serious infections occur, including reactivation of Epstein-Barr virus and hepatitis B."], ["Energy", "Fatigue is one of the most frequent complaints."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion over four hours. It binds strongly to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. It is also a P-glycoprotein substrate. The half-life is short, about three hours, but its effects on gene expression outlast its presence in the blood. Elimination is mostly by liver metabolism.",
  "sidefx": "HDAC inhibition affects many normal cells, not just cancer cells. Cytopenias come from effects on dividing blood precursors. Nausea and fatigue are common class effects. ECG changes and QT prolongation are typical of HDAC inhibitors, so potassium and magnesium are kept in range before each dose. Infections and viral reactivation reflect immune suppression and depletion of normal T cells. Tumor lysis syndrome can occur in heavily burdened disease. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Romidepsin's producer, Chromobacterium violaceum, makes a striking purple pigment called violacein that makes its colonies look violet.",
  "src": ["Istodax (romidepsin) FDA label", "Ueda H et al. J Antibiot 1994", "Nakajima H et al. Exp Cell Res 1998"]
},
"belinostat": {
  "origin": "Belinostat is a synthetic histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor. It was first developed in the early 2000s as PXD101 by the British company Prolifix, later part of the Danish company TopoTarget. Its design draws on the hydroxamic acid chemistry of earlier HDAC inhibitors such as vorinostat, which grip the zinc ion inside the enzyme. Spectrum Pharmaceuticals later took it through registration. Based on response rates in the BELIEF trial, the FDA granted accelerated approval to Beleodaq in 2014 for relapsed or refractory peripheral T-cell lymphoma (PTCL), a group of lymphomas with few treatment options.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Belinostat (PXD101) is developed by Prolifix and TopoTarget."], ["2014", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Beleodaq for relapsed or refractory PTCL."]],
  "cell": "Belinostat is a hydroxamic acid. Its hydroxamate group binds the zinc ion in the catalytic pocket of histone deacetylases, blocking class I, II and IV HDACs, so it is called a pan-HDAC inhibitor. With HDACs blocked, acetyl groups build up on histones and on many other proteins, including p53, tubulin and the chaperone HSP90. Open chromatin allows silenced tumor suppressor genes to be expressed again. The combined effects cause cell-cycle arrest, failure of DNA repair and apoptosis, which malignant T cells seem especially sensitive to.",
  "body": [["Malignant T cells", "Lymphoma cells arrest and die, shrinking tumors in a subset of patients with PTCL."], ["Bone marrow", "Thrombocytopenia, neutropenia and anemia occur, raising bleeding and infection risk."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation are common."], ["Liver", "Liver toxicity can occur, so liver tests are checked before each cycle."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue and fever are frequent, reflecting broad effects on many cell types and immune signaling."]],
  "journey": "Given IV over 30 minutes. It binds strongly to plasma proteins. The liver clears it mainly by glucuronidation through UGT1A1, with minor CYP2A6, CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 involvement. People who are homozygous for the UGT1A1*28 variant clear it more slowly and start at a reduced dose per the label. The half-life is short, about one hour. Metabolites leave mostly in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Broad HDAC inhibition affects normal cells, especially blood precursors, which explains cytopenias. Nausea, vomiting and fatigue are common class effects. Serious infections, including fatal sepsis and pneumonia, follow low blood counts and immune suppression. Liver toxicity may relate to hepatic metabolism of the drug. Tumor lysis syndrome can occur with large tumor burden. Slow UGT1A1 clearance raises exposure and toxicity. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Beleodaq (belinostat) FDA label", "O'Connor OA et al. BELIEF trial, J Clin Oncol 2015"]
},
"bortezomib": {
  "origin": "Bortezomib grew out of research on the ubiquitin-proteasome system, the cell's protein disposal machinery, whose discovery earned Aaron Ciechanover, Avram Hershko and Irwin Rose the 2004 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. In the mid-1990s chemist Julian Adams and colleagues at a small Massachusetts company, MyoGenics (renamed ProScript), designed boronic acid peptides that block the proteasome. Many experts doubted that blocking such a basic cell process could be safe. ProScript was bought by Millennium Pharmaceuticals, which developed the compound, PS-341. Myeloma cells turned out to be unusually sensitive. The FDA granted accelerated approval as Velcade in 2003 for relapsed multiple myeloma, making it the first proteasome inhibitor. It was approved for first-line myeloma in 2008 and for mantle cell lymphoma in 2006.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ciechanover, Hershko and Rose work out ubiquitin-mediated protein degradation."], ["1995", "Julian Adams's team at ProScript designs PS-341 (bortezomib)."], ["2003", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Velcade for relapsed multiple myeloma."], ["2006", "Approval for relapsed mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2008", "Approval for previously untreated multiple myeloma."]],
  "cell": "Cells tag unwanted proteins with chains of ubiquitin and feed them into the 26S proteasome, a barrel-shaped complex whose 20S core contains protease sites. Bortezomib's boron atom binds reversibly to the threonine in the beta5 subunit, which has chymotrypsin-like activity. With the proteasome blocked, misfolded and regulatory proteins pile up. IkappaB, which normally holds NF-kappaB inactive, is not degraded, so NF-kappaB survival signaling drops. Pro-apoptotic proteins such as Bax, Noxa and p53 accumulate. Myeloma plasma cells make huge amounts of immunoglobulin, so misfolded protein builds up fast in their endoplasmic reticulum, triggering the unfolded protein response, ER stress and apoptosis.",
  "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Overwhelmed by unfolded protein, plasma cells die and M-protein levels fall."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Painful sensory neuropathy is common, especially with IV use; subcutaneous dosing reduces it."], ["Blood pressure", "Autonomic nerve injury and other effects cause orthostatic hypotension."], ["Platelets", "Platelet counts fall in a cyclic pattern because proteasome inhibition reversibly slows platelet release from megakaryocytes."], ["Immune system", "Reduced T-cell function increases herpes zoster reactivation, so antiviral prophylaxis is common."], ["Gut and heart", "Diarrhea, constipation and nausea are common; heart failure and lung toxicity are less common but serious."]],
  "journey": "Given IV or subcutaneously. Subcutaneous dosing gives lower peak levels with similar total exposure, which lowers neuropathy. It distributes widely into tissues but does not enter the brain much. The liver breaks it down by oxidative deboronation through CYP3A4, CYP2C19 and CYP1A2, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers alter exposure. The half-life lengthens with repeated doses, ranging from about 40 to 193 hours. Proteasome activity recovers within about 72 hours, which sets the twice-weekly or weekly schedules.",
  "sidefx": "Neuropathy is thought to come from proteasome inhibition in sensory neurons and possibly off-target inhibition of other proteases such as HtrA2/Omi; it is length dependent and often painful. Thrombocytopenia is cyclic and reversible because megakaryocytes need proteasome activity to release platelets. Hypotension reflects autonomic neuropathy. Herpes zoster reactivation reflects impaired cellular immunity. Rapid myeloma cell death can cause tumor lysis syndrome. Giving it intrathecally is fatal. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Bortezomib was the first drug approved that works by blocking the proteasome, a target many scientists once thought was too essential to touch safely.",
  "src": ["Velcade (bortezomib) FDA label", "Adams J et al. Cancer Res 1999", "Richardson PG et al. N Engl J Med 2003", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"carfilzomib": {
  "origin": "Carfilzomib traces back to a natural product. In the early 1990s, researchers in Japan isolated epoxomicin, an antitumor compound from an actinomycete soil bacterium. In 1999 Craig Crews and colleagues at Yale University showed that epoxomicin binds the proteasome very selectively and permanently, through its epoxyketone group. Crews's lab made improved analogs, including YU-101, and cofounded the company Proteolix to develop them. Proteolix optimized the molecule into carfilzomib. Onyx Pharmaceuticals acquired Proteolix and took it to approval, and the FDA granted accelerated approval to Kyprolis in 2012 for relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma. Amgen later acquired Onyx.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "Epoxomicin is reported from an actinomycete in Japan."], ["1999", "Craig Crews's lab at Yale shows epoxomicin selectively and irreversibly inhibits the proteasome."], ["2000s", "Proteolix is founded to develop epoxyketone proteasome inhibitors."], ["2012", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Kyprolis for relapsed and refractory multiple myeloma."]],
  "cell": "Carfilzomib is a tetrapeptide epoxyketone. Its epoxyketone binds the N-terminal threonine of the beta5 (chymotrypsin-like) subunit of the 20S proteasome and the immunoproteasome, forming a stable covalent ring. This bond is irreversible, so proteasome activity returns only when cells make new proteasomes. Polyubiquitinated proteins pile up, the unfolded protein response is overwhelmed, and NF-kappaB signaling falls because IkappaB is not degraded. Myeloma plasma cells, which make huge amounts of antibody protein, undergo ER stress and apoptosis. Carfilzomib is more selective than bortezomib and hits fewer non-proteasome proteases, which is thought to explain its much lower rate of neuropathy.",
  "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells die from proteasome blockade, and M-protein falls, including in some bortezomib-resistant disease."], ["Heart", "New or worsening heart failure, ischemia and arrhythmias can occur."], ["Blood vessels and pressure", "Hypertension, including crisis, and pulmonary hypertension occur, possibly from endothelial and nitric oxide effects."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury can result from tumor lysis, thrombotic microangiopathy or direct effects."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath is common, and ARDS and lung toxicity can occur."], ["Peripheral nerves", "Neuropathy is much less common than with bortezomib."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, with hydration and dexamethasone premedication early in treatment. It binds strongly to plasma proteins. It is cleared very quickly, with a half-life of an hour or less, mostly by peptidases and epoxide hydrolysis outside the liver; CYP enzymes play only a minor role, so CYP drug interactions are few. Even though it leaves the blood quickly, proteasome inhibition persists because the binding is permanent. Metabolites leave mainly in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Cardiac and vascular effects are the major concern. Proposed mechanisms include proteasome inhibition in heart muscle cells, which depend on the proteasome to clear damaged proteins, and reduced nitric oxide signaling in blood vessels, which raises blood pressure. Kidney injury comes from tumor lysis, thrombotic microangiopathy and fluid shifts. Infusion reactions involve cytokine release, which dexamethasone premedication blunts. Neuropathy is rare because carfilzomib spares the off-target proteases bortezomib blocks. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Kyprolis (carfilzomib) FDA label", "Meng L, Crews CM et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1999", "Siegel DS et al. Blood 2012"]
},
"imetelstat": {
  "origin": "Imetelstat goes after telomerase, the enzyme that rebuilds the protective caps (telomeres) at the ends of chromosomes. Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider discovered telomerase in 1984 using the pond organism Tetrahymena, and they shared the 2009 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Jack Szostak. Most normal adult cells have little telomerase, but many cancer cells switch it on to keep dividing. Scientists at Geron Corporation in California designed imetelstat, a short lipid-linked oligonucleotide that blocks the enzyme's RNA template. After decades of trials, the FDA approved it as Rytelo in 2024 for adults with lower-risk myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) and transfusion-dependent anemia who did not respond to or could not receive erythropoiesis-stimulating agents. It was the first telomerase inhibitor approved.",
  "timeline": [["1984", "Elizabeth Blackburn and Carol Greider discover telomerase activity."], ["2000s", "Geron develops imetelstat, a lipid-conjugated telomerase template antagonist."], ["2009", "Blackburn, Greider and Szostak share the Nobel Prize for telomere and telomerase discoveries."], ["2024", "FDA approves Rytelo for lower-risk MDS with transfusion-dependent anemia."]],
  "cell": "Telomerase is made of a reverse transcriptase protein (hTERT) and an RNA component (hTR) whose template sequence is copied to add TTAGGG repeats onto chromosome ends. Imetelstat is a 13-base thio-phosphoramidate oligonucleotide that pairs exactly with the hTR template region, blocking it so the enzyme cannot extend telomeres. A lipid tail helps it enter cells. Malignant myeloid stem and progenitor cells in MDS have high telomerase activity and short telomeres, so they are more dependent on the enzyme. Blocking it drives these malignant clones into apoptosis, which lets normal red cell production recover and reduces the need for transfusions.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Malignant MDS clones shrink, and healthier progenitors restore red cell production, so many patients stop needing regular transfusions."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is common and often severe early in treatment because the drug also affects megakaryocyte precursors and the malignant clone that is still making platelets."], ["White blood cells", "Neutropenia is common in the first cycles, raising infection risk."], ["Liver", "Mild liver enzyme rises can occur because the liver takes up a large share of the drug."], ["Clotting tests", "It can prolong the aPTT lab test, an effect seen with this type of oligonucleotide."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion every four weeks, after premedication with an antihistamine and a corticosteroid. It binds strongly to plasma proteins and is taken up into tissues, including the bone marrow, liver, kidneys and spleen. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes; instead nucleases cut it into shorter pieces. Very little intact drug appears in urine. The half-life is several days. Responses build over weeks as red cell production recovers.",
  "sidefx": "Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia are on-target effects: the drug kills malignant progenitors that make many of the patient's blood cells and also affects normal dividing progenitors, so counts drop early, usually in the first cycles, and recover. Counts are checked weekly at first. Infusion reactions are reduced with premedication. aPTT prolongation is a lab effect rather than a true bleeding disorder. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Telomerase was discovered in Tetrahymena, a single-celled pond organism with thousands of tiny chromosomes, which made telomere biology easier to study.",
  "src": ["Rytelo (imetelstat) FDA label", "Greider CW, Blackburn EH. Cell 1985", "Platzbecker U et al. IMerge trial, Lancet 2024"]
},
"temsirolimus": {
  "origin": "Temsirolimus is built from rapamycin, a molecule discovered in soil from Easter Island, which islanders call Rapa Nui. A 1964 Canadian scientific expedition collected the soil, and in 1972 Suren Sehgal at Ayerst Research Laboratories in Montreal isolated rapamycin (sirolimus) from the bacterium Streptomyces hygroscopicus. It turned out to be a powerful immunosuppressant and to slow tumor growth. Its target was later named mTOR, the mechanistic target of rapamycin. Wyeth chemists made temsirolimus (CCI-779), an ester prodrug of sirolimus that is soluble enough for IV use. The FDA approved it as Torisel in 2007 for advanced renal cell carcinoma, making it the first mTOR inhibitor approved for cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1964", "A Canadian expedition collects soil samples on Easter Island (Rapa Nui)."], ["1972", "Suren Sehgal at Ayerst isolates rapamycin from Streptomyces hygroscopicus."], ["1990s", "mTOR is identified as the cellular target of rapamycin."], ["2007", "FDA approves Torisel for advanced renal cell carcinoma."]],
  "cell": "Temsirolimus and its active metabolite sirolimus bind the intracellular protein FKBP-12. The drug-FKBP-12 complex attaches to mTOR and inhibits mTOR complex 1 (mTORC1). mTORC1 normally senses growth signals and nutrients and turns on protein synthesis by phosphorylating p70 S6 kinase and 4E-BP1. With mTORC1 blocked, 4E-BP1 stays bound to the translation factor eIF4E, and making of key proteins falls, including cyclin D1 and hypoxia-inducible factors HIF-1 alpha and HIF-2 alpha. Less cyclin D causes G1 arrest. Less HIF means less VEGF, which reduces blood vessel growth in kidney cancers, where VHL loss makes HIF signaling especially important.",
  "body": [["Kidney tumor", "Tumor cells grow more slowly and fewer new blood vessels form, which helps control advanced renal cell carcinoma."], ["Metabolism", "mTOR blockade disrupts insulin signaling and lipid handling, raising blood sugar, cholesterol and triglycerides."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease (noninfectious pneumonitis) can develop."], ["Mouth and skin", "Mouth sores, rash and nail changes come from slowed renewal of epithelial cells."], ["Immune system", "Immune suppression raises infection risk, including Pneumocystis pneumonia."], ["Wound healing", "Reduced cell growth and angiogenesis slow healing of wounds and surgical sites."]],
  "journey": "Given IV once weekly, after an antihistamine premedication. In the liver, CYP3A4 converts it largely to sirolimus, which is active and has a longer half-life, about 55 hours versus about 17 hours for temsirolimus. Both bind extensively to red blood cells and plasma proteins. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise and inducers lower sirolimus levels. Most is excreted in the feces, with very little in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from mTOR's role in normal growth and metabolism. Blocking insulin and nutrient signaling causes high blood sugar and lipids. Slowed epithelial renewal causes mouth sores and rash. Immune suppression, the same effect that makes sirolimus a transplant drug, increases infections. Pneumonitis is a class effect of mTOR inhibitors. Reduced angiogenesis and cell growth impair wound healing and may contribute to bowel perforation. Infusion reactions are reduced by antihistamine premedication. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Rapamycin is named after Rapa Nui, the native name for Easter Island, where the soil sample that produced it was collected.",
  "src": ["Torisel (temsirolimus) FDA label", "Vezina C, Kudelski A, Sehgal SN. J Antibiot 1975", "Hudes G et al. N Engl J Med 2007"]
},
"pertuzumab": {
  "origin": "Pertuzumab came from Genentech, the company that also made trastuzumab (Herceptin). Researchers there studied how HER2, a receptor overexpressed in about one in five breast cancers, signals. HER2 has no known ligand of its own; it works mainly by pairing (dimerizing) with partner receptors such as HER3. Genentech scientists developed a mouse antibody called 2C4 that blocked the pairing arm of HER2, then humanized it to make pertuzumab. Because it binds a different part of HER2 than trastuzumab, the two work together. The FDA approved Perjeta in 2012 for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer, based on the CLEOPATRA trial. In 2013 it became the first drug to receive accelerated approval for neoadjuvant (before surgery) breast cancer treatment. A subcutaneous combination with trastuzumab and hyaluronidase, Phesgo, was approved in 2020.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Genentech develops the 2C4 antibody against the HER2 dimerization domain."], ["2012", "FDA approves Perjeta for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2013", "Accelerated approval for neoadjuvant treatment, the first based on pathologic complete response."], ["2017", "Approval for adjuvant treatment of high-risk early HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2020", "FDA approves Phesgo, a subcutaneous pertuzumab, trastuzumab and hyaluronidase combination."]],
  "cell": "HER2 belongs to the EGFR family of receptor tyrosine kinases. It signals by forming pairs (dimers) with EGFR, HER3 or HER4, and the HER2-HER3 pair is an especially strong driver of PI3K/AKT survival signaling. Pertuzumab binds extracellular subdomain II of HER2, the dimerization arm, and physically blocks ligand-driven pairing. Without dimerization, the tyrosine kinases do not cross-phosphorylate, so MAPK and PI3K/AKT pathways stay quiet, and cells stop growing and die. As an IgG1 it also recruits NK cells for antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. Trastuzumab binds subdomain IV and blocks ligand-independent signaling, so together they shut down HER2 more completely.",
  "body": [["HER2-positive tumors", "Breast cancer cells lose a major growth signal, and in combination with trastuzumab and a taxane tumors shrink and survival improves."], ["Heart", "HER2 signaling helps heart muscle cells cope with stress, so blocking it can lower ejection fraction and cause heart failure, usually reversible."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common because EGFR family signaling helps regulate gut lining fluid handling."], ["Skin and hair", "Rash and hair loss (largely from the chemotherapy partner) are common."], ["Pregnancy", "HER2 is needed for fetal kidney development, so exposure can cause low amniotic fluid and fetal harm."]],
  "journey": "Given as an IV infusion every three weeks, or subcutaneously as part of Phesgo, in which hyaluronidase temporarily breaks down tissue barriers so a large volume can be absorbed. Like other IgG antibodies, it is too large to be filtered by the kidneys and is not metabolized by liver enzymes. It is slowly broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, and the neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of about 18 days.",
  "sidefx": "Heart dysfunction comes from blocking HER2 signals that help cardiomyocytes survive stress; it is monitored with regular LVEF checks and usually reverses when treatment is held. Diarrhea is an EGFR-family class effect on the gut. Fetal toxicity reflects HER2's role in fetal kidney development, so pregnancy is checked before starting. Infusion reactions are generally mild. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Perjeta (pertuzumab) FDA label", "Phesgo FDA label", "Baselga J et al. CLEOPATRA, N Engl J Med 2012"]
},
"margetuximab": {
  "origin": "Margetuximab was developed by the Maryland company MacroGenics. It comes from the same mouse antibody, 4D5, that Genentech humanized to make trastuzumab, so it binds the same spot on HER2. MacroGenics kept the antibody in a chimeric form and engineered its Fc tail, the part that talks to immune cells, to bind more strongly to the activating receptor CD16A on natural killer cells and less to the inhibitory receptor CD32B. The idea was to strengthen immune killing, including in people whose CD16A gene variant normally binds antibodies weakly. Based on the SOPHIA trial, the FDA approved Margenza in 2020, with chemotherapy, for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer after two or more prior anti-HER2 regimens.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "The mouse anti-HER2 antibody 4D5 is developed at Genentech."], ["2010s", "MacroGenics engineers the Fc region of a chimeric 4D5 antibody to enhance immune cell binding."], ["2020", "FDA approves Margenza for pretreated metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."]],
  "cell": "Margetuximab binds HER2 extracellular subdomain IV, the same epitope as trastuzumab. This blocks ligand-independent HER2 signaling, reduces cleavage of HER2's outer part (shedding), and lowers MAPK and PI3K/AKT growth signals. Its main design feature is the Fc region. Five amino acid changes increase binding to CD16A (FcgammaRIIIa), including its lower-affinity 158F form, and reduce binding to CD32B (FcgammaRIIb), an inhibitory receptor. Natural killer cells and macrophages are therefore more strongly activated, enhancing antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity against HER2-positive cancer cells.",
  "body": [["HER2-positive tumors", "Cancer cells lose HER2 growth signals and are targeted more strongly by NK cells."], ["Heart", "Like other HER2 drugs, it can lower ejection fraction and cause heart failure because HER2 signals help heart cells handle stress."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions such as fever, chills and rash are common, especially with the first infusion."], ["Gut and energy", "Fatigue, nausea, diarrhea and vomiting are common, partly from the chemotherapy partner."], ["Pregnancy", "Blocking HER2 can harm the fetus, causing low amniotic fluid and kidney problems."]],
  "journey": "Given IV every three weeks. As a large IgG antibody it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not cleared by the kidneys or by CYP enzymes; it is broken down slowly into peptides and amino acids. The neonatal Fc receptor protects it from breakdown, giving a half-life of about 19 days. Drug interactions through liver enzymes are not expected.",
  "sidefx": "Left ventricular dysfunction is a HER2 class effect, so LVEF is checked before and every three months during therapy. Infusion reactions come from immune activation and cytokine release, likely increased by the enhanced Fc design. Fetal harm reflects HER2's role in fetal kidney development. Gut effects and fatigue are largely shared with the chemotherapy partner. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Margenza (margetuximab-cmkb) FDA label", "Rugo HS et al. SOPHIA trial, JAMA Oncol 2021"]
},
"obinutuzumab": {
  "origin": "Obinutuzumab came from a Swiss idea about sugar. Antibodies carry a sugar chain on their Fc tail, and researchers found that removing one sugar, fucose, makes the tail grip the CD16A receptor on natural killer cells much more strongly. Pablo Umana and colleagues at GlycArt Biotechnology in Zurich developed a way to make antibodies with less fucose by engineering the cells that produce them. Roche acquired GlycArt in 2005 and used the method to build GA101, a humanized type II anti-CD20 antibody. Named obinutuzumab, it was approved by the FDA as Gazyva in 2013, with chlorambucil, for previously untreated chronic lymphocytic leukemia. It was the first drug approved after receiving the FDA's new Breakthrough Therapy designation. It later gained approvals for follicular lymphoma and, in 2025, for lupus nephritis.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "GlycArt Biotechnology develops glycoengineering to reduce antibody fucose."], ["2013", "FDA approves Gazyva with chlorambucil for untreated CLL, the first approval for a Breakthrough Therapy-designated drug."], ["2016", "Approval for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma with bendamustine."], ["2017", "Approval for previously untreated follicular lymphoma."], ["2025", "FDA approves obinutuzumab for active lupus nephritis."]],
  "cell": "CD20 is a membrane protein on pre-B and mature B cells, including most B-cell lymphoma and CLL cells, but not on stem cells or plasma cells. Obinutuzumab binds CD20 in a different orientation from rituximab, which makes it a type II antibody. Type II antibodies do not cluster CD20 into lipid rafts, so they activate complement less but cause more direct, non-apoptotic cell death. Its Fc region has reduced fucose, which raises its binding to FcgammaRIIIa (CD16A) on NK cells and macrophages and strongly boosts antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity and phagocytosis. The result is deeper B-cell depletion than with rituximab in CLL.",
  "body": [["Malignant B cells", "CLL and lymphoma cells are killed by NK cells, macrophages and direct cell death, often lowering blood counts of leukemia cells within days."], ["Normal B cells", "Healthy B cells are depleted for months, lowering antibody production and raising infection risk."], ["Immune system on first infusion", "Rapid killing of many cells releases cytokines, causing fever, chills, low blood pressure and breathing trouble during the first infusion."], ["Kidneys and metabolism", "Rapid tumor breakdown can cause tumor lysis syndrome, with high potassium, phosphate and uric acid."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia occur, sometimes late after treatment ends."], ["Liver", "Hepatitis B can reactivate when B cells and antibodies are depleted, leading to severe hepatitis."]],
  "journey": "Given IV, starting with a split first dose to limit infusion reactions. As an IgG antibody it stays mainly in the blood and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids rather than by the liver or kidneys. Early on, clearance is faster because many CD20-positive cells bind and remove it (target-mediated clearance). As B cells disappear, clearance slows and the terminal half-life is about 26 to 36 days. B-cell depletion lasts many months after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as large numbers of B cells are killed quickly; premedication with acetaminophen, an antihistamine and a corticosteroid reduces them. Tumor lysis syndrome reflects rapid cell death in high-burden CLL. Hepatitis B reactivation and progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), the boxed warnings, happen because B-cell depletion removes immune control of latent viruses, so HBV screening is done first. Neutropenia may involve immune effects on the marrow. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Gazyva (obinutuzumab) FDA label", "Goede V et al. CLL11, N Engl J Med 2014", "Mossner E et al. Blood 2010"]
},
"ramucirumab": {
  "origin": "Ramucirumab came from the New York biotech company ImClone Systems, which developed a fully human antibody (code name IMC-1121B) against VEGF receptor 2, the main receptor that drives blood vessel growth in tumors. The idea that tumors need new blood vessels to grow was championed by Judah Folkman in the 1970s. Unlike bevacizumab, which mops up the VEGF-A protein, ramucirumab blocks the receptor itself. Eli Lilly acquired ImClone in 2008 and completed development. The FDA approved Cyramza in 2014 for advanced gastric or gastroesophageal junction cancer after prior chemotherapy. It later gained approvals for non-small cell lung cancer, colorectal cancer and hepatocellular carcinoma with high alpha-fetoprotein.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "Judah Folkman proposes that tumors depend on angiogenesis."], ["2000s", "ImClone develops IMC-1121B, a human antibody against VEGFR2."], ["2008", "Eli Lilly acquires ImClone."], ["2014", "FDA approves Cyramza for advanced gastric cancer."], ["2019", "Approval for hepatocellular carcinoma with high alpha-fetoprotein levels."]],
  "cell": "Vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (VEGFR2, also called KDR) is a receptor tyrosine kinase on endothelial cells. When VEGF-A, VEGF-C or VEGF-D binds, the receptor pairs up, phosphorylates itself and turns on signals (PLC-gamma, MAPK and PI3K/AKT) that make endothelial cells divide, migrate, survive and become leaky. Ramucirumab is a fully human IgG1 that binds the extracellular domain of VEGFR2 and blocks all three ligands from attaching. Without this signal, new tumor vessels do not form, existing abnormal vessels regress, and tumors lose blood supply. It is usually combined with chemotherapy, and vessel normalization may also help chemotherapy reach the tumor.",
  "body": [["Tumor blood vessels", "Fewer new vessels grow, which slows tumor growth, especially with chemotherapy."], ["Blood pressure", "Less VEGF signaling lowers nitric oxide production in vessel walls, so blood pressure rises."], ["Kidneys", "VEGF supports the filtering cells of the glomerulus, so blocking it causes protein in the urine and, rarely, nephrotic syndrome."], ["Bleeding and healing", "Weakened vessel repair raises the risk of bleeding, nosebleeds, poor wound healing and bowel perforation."], ["Arteries", "Arterial clots such as heart attack and stroke are uncommon but serious."], ["Liver in cirrhosis", "In patients with advanced cirrhosis, worsening liver function and encephalopathy have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, usually every two or three weeks, after an H1 antihistamine premedication. As a large IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes or cleared by the kidneys; cells break it down into peptides and amino acids. The neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of about two weeks. No CYP drug interactions are expected.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from blocking VEGF signals that normal blood vessels need. Lower nitric oxide and reduced capillary density raise blood pressure. Glomerular injury causes proteinuria. Impaired vessel maintenance and repair causes bleeding, poor wound healing and GI perforation, so it is held around surgery. Endothelial dysfunction raises arterial clot risk. Reversible posterior encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) can follow severe blood pressure changes. The boxed warning for bleeding, perforation and wound healing was removed in 2020, but these remain warnings. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Cyramza (ramucirumab) FDA label", "Fuchs CS et al. REGARD trial, Lancet 2014", "Folkman J. N Engl J Med 1971"]
},
"cetuximab": {
  "origin": "Cetuximab began in the early 1980s in San Diego, where John Mendelsohn, Gordon Sato and colleagues at the University of California made mouse antibodies against the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR), including one called 225. They showed that blocking EGFR could stop cancer cells from growing, a new idea at the time. The antibody was turned into a mouse-human chimera (C225) and developed by ImClone Systems. In 2001 the FDA refused to review ImClone's first application, and a related insider trading case became a national news story. After more trials, the FDA approved Erbitux in 2004 for metastatic colorectal cancer and in 2006 for head and neck cancer with radiation. In 2009 its use in colorectal cancer was limited to tumors without KRAS mutations, an early example of a genetic test guiding a cancer drug.",
  "timeline": [["1983", "Mendelsohn and Sato's group at UC San Diego develops anti-EGFR mouse antibody 225."], ["2001", "The FDA declines to file ImClone's first cetuximab application."], ["2004", "FDA approves Erbitux for metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2006", "Approval for head and neck squamous cell cancer with radiation."], ["2009", "Labeling limits colorectal use to KRAS wild-type tumors."]],
  "cell": "EGFR (HER1, ERBB1) is a receptor tyrosine kinase on many epithelial cells and cancers. When EGF or TGF-alpha binds, EGFR pairs with another receptor, phosphorylates itself and switches on RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways that drive growth and survival. Cetuximab binds domain III of the EGFR extracellular region and blocks the ligand from binding. Without signaling, cells arrest in G1, make less VEGF and are more easily killed by radiation. The antibody-receptor complex is pulled inside and degraded, lowering EGFR on the surface. As an IgG1 it also recruits NK cells for antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. If RAS is mutated, the pathway is switched on below the receptor, so blocking EGFR does not help.",
  "body": [["Tumor cells", "Colorectal and head and neck cancer cells lose EGFR-driven growth signals and become more sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy."], ["Skin", "EGFR keeps hair follicles and skin cells maturing normally, so blocking it causes an acne-like rash, dry skin, itching and nail fold infections."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "EGFR in the kidney tubule drives magnesium reabsorption, so blocking it causes magnesium wasting and low magnesium, which can lower calcium and potassium."], ["Immune system", "Severe infusion reactions can occur, sometimes on the first dose."], ["Heart and lungs", "Cardiopulmonary arrest has been reported with radiation or platinum-based chemotherapy, and interstitial lung disease can occur."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and mucositis, especially with radiation, are common."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly or every two weeks. As a large IgG antibody, it stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is cleared in two ways: general protein breakdown and binding to EGFR on cells, which pulls it inside to be degraded. That target-mediated clearance means levels rise more than proportionally at higher exposure. At steady state the half-life is about 112 hours (roughly five days). It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Skin toxicity is an on-target effect, because EGFR is essential for normal skin and follicle biology; the acneiform rash is common enough that it is used as a sign the drug is active. Low magnesium comes from blocked EGFR in the kidney's thick ascending limb and distal tubule. Severe infusion reactions are often anaphylactic and are linked to preexisting IgE antibodies against galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose (alpha-gal), a sugar on cetuximab made in mouse cells. Cardiopulmonary arrest may relate to electrolyte changes. There is no antidote; premedication with an H1 antagonist is used.",
  "fun": "Anaphylaxis to cetuximab was most common in the southeastern US, and the cause was traced to alpha-gal antibodies, which can follow lone star tick bites and also cause red meat allergy.",
  "src": ["Erbitux (cetuximab) FDA label", "Chung CH et al. N Engl J Med 2008 (alpha-gal)", "Mendelsohn J. Clin Cancer Res 1997"]
},
"panitumumab": {
  "origin": "Panitumumab was one of the first fully human antibodies made using transgenic mice. Scientists at the California company Abgenix engineered the XenoMouse, whose own antibody genes were switched off and replaced with human antibody genes. When these mice were immunized with EGFR, they made fully human anti-EGFR antibodies, avoiding the mouse protein that can trigger immune reactions. Amgen, which later acquired Abgenix, developed panitumumab. The FDA approved Vectibix in 2006 for EGFR-expressing metastatic colorectal cancer that progressed after chemotherapy. In 2009 the label was limited to tumors without KRAS mutations, and later RAS (KRAS and NRAS) testing became required.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Abgenix develops the XenoMouse, a mouse that makes human antibodies."], ["2006", "FDA approves Vectibix for metastatic colorectal cancer after chemotherapy."], ["2009", "Use is limited to KRAS wild-type colorectal cancer."], ["2014", "Approval for first-line RAS wild-type colorectal cancer with FOLFOX."]],
  "cell": "Panitumumab binds domain III of the EGFR extracellular region and competitively blocks EGF and TGF-alpha. Without ligand, EGFR cannot pair up and autophosphorylate, so the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT pathways stay off. Tumor cells arrest in G1, make less VEGF and undergo apoptosis, and the receptor is internalized and degraded. Unlike cetuximab, panitumumab is an IgG2, which binds Fc receptors on NK cells poorly, so its effect relies mainly on signal blockade rather than immune killing. KRAS or NRAS mutations activate the pathway downstream of EGFR, so the drug does not work in those tumors.",
  "body": [["Colorectal tumors", "RAS wild-type colon cancer cells lose a key growth signal and stop growing or shrink."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin, cracks, itching and nail fold infections occur in about 90 percent of patients because EGFR is needed for normal skin and follicle renewal."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Blocking EGFR in the kidney tubule causes magnesium wasting, with low magnesium, calcium and potassium."], ["Eyes", "Dry eye, keratitis and eyelash changes come from EGFR's role in the cornea and lid."], ["Sun sensitivity", "Skin reactions worsen with sunlight, so sun protection helps."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease and pulmonary fibrosis are rare but serious."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two weeks. As an IgG2 antibody it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids. At low levels, binding to EGFR and being pulled inside cells adds to clearance (target-mediated clearance). The half-life is about seven and a half days. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so liver drug interactions are not expected.",
  "sidefx": "Skin toxicity, the boxed warning, is on target: EGFR controls normal skin cell maturation and hair follicle function, so blocking it causes inflammation, rash and cracking that can become infected or, rarely, lead to necrotizing fasciitis. Low magnesium comes from blocked tubular reabsorption. As a fully human IgG2, it causes fewer infusion reactions than cetuximab, though severe ones can occur. Adding it to bevacizumab plus chemotherapy increased toxicity and deaths, so that combination is avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Vectibix (panitumumab) FDA label", "Jakobovits A et al. Nat Biotechnol 2007", "Amado RG et al. J Clin Oncol 2008"]
},
"necitumumab": {
  "origin": "Necitumumab was developed by ImClone Systems, the company behind cetuximab, as a fully human IgG1 antibody against EGFR (code name IMC-11F8). After Eli Lilly acquired ImClone in 2008, Lilly tested it in lung cancer. The SQUIRE trial added necitumumab to gemcitabine and cisplatin for previously untreated metastatic squamous non-small cell lung cancer and showed a modest improvement in survival. A separate trial in non-squamous lung cancer was stopped early because of more clots and deaths. The FDA approved Portrazza in 2015 for squamous non-small cell lung cancer only, in combination with gemcitabine and cisplatin.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "ImClone develops IMC-11F8, a fully human anti-EGFR IgG1."], ["2008", "Eli Lilly acquires ImClone."], ["2015", "SQUIRE trial results are published, and the FDA approves Portrazza for squamous NSCLC."]],
  "cell": "Necitumumab binds the ligand-binding domain III of EGFR, blocking EGF and TGF-alpha. The receptor cannot pair, autophosphorylate or turn on RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK and PI3K-AKT signaling, so tumor cells grow and divide less and make less VEGF. The antibody also causes the receptor to be internalized and degraded. As an IgG1 it can recruit NK cells to kill EGFR-expressing cells through antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity. Squamous lung cancers often express high levels of EGFR, which made them the target population.",
  "body": [["Squamous lung tumors", "EGFR-dependent cancer cells grow more slowly, producing a modest survival gain with chemotherapy."], ["Electrolytes", "Blocking EGFR in the kidney tubules causes magnesium wasting, often with low calcium and potassium."], ["Heart", "Cardiopulmonary arrest and sudden death occurred in about 3 percent of patients, possibly linked to electrolyte losses."], ["Blood vessels", "Venous clots, including pulmonary embolism, and arterial events such as stroke are more common."], ["Skin", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and nail fold infections come from EGFR's role in normal skin."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion on days 1 and 8 of each three-week cycle, before gemcitabine and cisplatin. As an IgG1 antibody it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid. Clearance is through protein breakdown and binding to EGFR (target-mediated clearance). The half-life is about two weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Low magnesium is on target, from blocked EGFR-driven magnesium reabsorption in the kidney, and cisplatin adds to the loss. The boxed warning for cardiopulmonary arrest is thought to relate partly to these electrolyte disturbances, so electrolytes are checked and replaced. Clots may reflect effects on endothelium and the combination with cisplatin; it is not used in non-squamous lung cancer because of more clots and deaths there. Skin toxicity is an EGFR class effect. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Portrazza (necitumumab) FDA label", "Thatcher N et al. SQUIRE trial, Lancet Oncol 2015"]
},
"amivantamab": {
  "origin": "Amivantamab is a bispecific antibody, meaning each molecule has two different arms. It was created with Genmab's DuoBody platform, which lets two half-antibodies with different targets swap pieces and join, and was developed by Janssen (Johnson & Johnson). One arm targets EGFR and the other targets MET, the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor. Some lung cancers have unusual EGFR mutations called exon 20 insertions that resist standard EGFR pills, and MET activation is a common way tumors escape EGFR drugs. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Rybrevant in 2021 for non-small cell lung cancer with EGFR exon 20 insertions after platinum chemotherapy, the first therapy approved for this group. In 2024 it was approved with the EGFR pill lazertinib as first-line therapy for common EGFR mutations.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "Janssen and Genmab develop an EGFR-MET bispecific antibody with DuoBody technology."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Rybrevant for EGFR exon 20 insertion NSCLC."], ["2024", "Approvals for first-line exon 20 disease with chemotherapy and, with lazertinib, for common EGFR mutations."]],
  "cell": "One arm of amivantamab binds EGFR and the other binds MET. By blocking EGF from EGFR and hepatocyte growth factor from MET, it shuts down signaling through both receptors, which share downstream MAPK and PI3K/AKT pathways. Because it binds the outside of the receptor, it works regardless of which mutation is in the kinase domain inside, including exon 20 insertions. The bound receptors are pulled into the cell and degraded. Its Fc region has low fucose, which strengthens binding to Fc receptors on NK cells and macrophages and drives antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and trogocytosis, in which immune cells nibble away receptor-bearing membrane from tumor cells.",
  "body": [["Lung tumors", "EGFR-mutant lung cancer cells lose both EGFR and MET survival signals and are attacked by immune cells."], ["Skin and nails", "Acne-like rash, dry skin and painful nail fold infections are very common because EGFR is needed for normal skin renewal."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions are very common with the first dose, so the first dose is split over two days."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and may be fatal."], ["Veins", "With lazertinib, deep vein clots and pulmonary emboli are common, so preventive anticoagulation is recommended early in treatment."], ["Eyes, mouth and fluid", "Keratitis, mouth sores, swelling and low albumin occur, the last partly reflecting MET's role in the liver and blood vessels."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, starting weekly and then every two or three weeks. As an IgG1 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. Clearance is through protein breakdown and through binding to EGFR and MET on cells (target-mediated clearance). The half-life is about 11 days. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so drug interactions through liver enzymes are not expected.",
  "sidefx": "Skin and nail problems are on-target EGFR effects. Infusion reactions, mostly with the first dose, come from cytokine release and are reduced by premedication with an antihistamine, antipyretic and glucocorticoid and by splitting the first dose. Interstitial lung disease is an EGFR-class effect. The clot risk with lazertinib is not fully explained, which is why prophylactic anticoagulation is recommended for the first four months. Low albumin and swelling are linked to MET blockade. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Rybrevant (amivantamab-vmjw) FDA label", "Park K et al. CHRYSALIS, J Clin Oncol 2021", "Cho BC et al. MARIPOSA, N Engl J Med 2024"]
},
"daratumumab": {
  "origin": "Daratumumab was made by the Danish biotech company Genmab using transgenic mice that produce fully human antibodies. Genmab chose CD38, a protein found at very high levels on myeloma plasma cells. In 2012 Genmab licensed it to Janssen, which ran the trials. Even as a single drug it shrank myeloma that had resisted proteasome inhibitors and immunomodulatory drugs, which was striking for an antibody. The FDA approved Darzalex in 2015 for heavily pretreated multiple myeloma, the first anti-CD38 antibody. It moved into combinations and first-line care, and in 2020 a subcutaneous form with hyaluronidase, Darzalex Faspro, shortened administration from hours to minutes.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Genmab develops daratumumab, a human anti-CD38 antibody."], ["2012", "Genmab licenses daratumumab to Janssen."], ["2015", "FDA approves Darzalex for multiple myeloma after at least three prior lines."], ["2020", "FDA approves Darzalex Faspro for subcutaneous use."]],
  "cell": "CD38 is a transmembrane glycoprotein that works as an enzyme on the cell surface, turning NAD into signaling molecules such as cyclic ADP-ribose, and also as an adhesion receptor. It is expressed at very high levels on myeloma plasma cells. Daratumumab binds CD38 and kills the cell in several ways: it activates complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity), recruits NK cells (antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity), triggers macrophages to engulf the cell (phagocytosis) and, when cross-linked by Fc receptors, triggers apoptosis. It also removes CD38-positive immune suppressor cells, such as regulatory T cells, regulatory B cells and myeloid-derived suppressor cells, which allows T cells that recognize the tumor to expand.",
  "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells are killed, so M-protein in the blood and urine falls over weeks."], ["Immune system", "Removing suppressor cells boosts T-cell activity, while NK cells, which also express CD38, are reduced."], ["Airways", "Infusion reactions often affect the airways, with nasal congestion, throat irritation, cough and bronchospasm, especially with the first dose."], ["Blood bank testing", "It binds CD38 on reagent red cells, making antibody screens and crossmatches falsely positive for months."], ["Lab tests for myeloma", "As an IgG kappa antibody, it can show up on protein electrophoresis and immunofixation and mimic residual myeloma."], ["Bone marrow and infections", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and infections such as pneumonia occur, especially with other myeloma drugs."]],
  "journey": "Given IV or subcutaneously with hyaluronidase, which loosens tissue so a larger volume can be absorbed under the skin. Schedules start weekly and then spread out to every two to four weeks. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. Clearance is faster when many CD38-positive cells are present and slows as the tumor shrinks. With weekly dosing the half-life is about 18 days.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as CD38-bearing cells are attacked, and they are reduced by premedication with a corticosteroid, antihistamine and acetaminophen; patients with COPD may get bronchodilators afterward. Blood bank interference occurs because red cells carry small amounts of CD38; labs can treat test cells with dithiothreitol (DTT) to remove it, so a type and screen is done before the first dose. Neutropenia, infections and hepatitis B reactivation reflect immune effects. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Because daratumumab makes red cell antibody screens falsely positive, blood banks are told before treatment starts so they can treat test cells to work around it.",
  "src": ["Darzalex (daratumumab) FDA label", "Darzalex Faspro FDA label", "Lokhorst HM et al. N Engl J Med 2015"]
},
"isatuximab": {
  "origin": "Isatuximab was discovered by the US company ImmunoGen and developed by Sanofi as a second anti-CD38 antibody for multiple myeloma. It binds a different spot on CD38 than daratumumab, one that also blocks CD38's enzyme activity and lets it trigger cell death directly. Sanofi tested it with pomalidomide and dexamethasone in the ICARIA-MM trial, which improved progression-free survival in relapsed myeloma. The FDA approved Sarclisa in 2020 for that combination. Later approvals added combination with carfilzomib and dexamethasone (2021) and with bortezomib, lenalidomide and dexamethasone for newly diagnosed patients not eligible for transplant (2024).",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "ImmunoGen discovers the anti-CD38 antibody later named isatuximab, developed by Sanofi."], ["2020", "FDA approves Sarclisa with pomalidomide and dexamethasone for relapsed myeloma."], ["2021", "Approval with carfilzomib and dexamethasone."], ["2024", "Approval with VRd for newly diagnosed transplant-ineligible myeloma."]],
  "cell": "Isatuximab is a chimeric IgG1 that binds a specific epitope on CD38, a surface enzyme highly expressed on myeloma plasma cells. Binding at this site inhibits CD38's ADP-ribosyl cyclase activity. It kills myeloma cells through antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by NK cells, antibody-dependent phagocytosis by macrophages and complement-dependent cytotoxicity, and unlike daratumumab it can trigger apoptosis directly without needing cross-linking by Fc receptors. It also activates NK cells and removes CD38-positive regulatory T cells, which helps the immune system attack the tumor. Pomalidomide further stimulates NK and T cells, so the two work together.",
  "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Plasma cells are killed and M-protein declines over weeks."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common, especially with pomalidomide, raising infection risk."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions, mostly with the first dose, cause shortness of breath, cough and chills."], ["Lungs and airways", "Upper respiratory infections and pneumonia are common in treated patients."], ["Lab tests", "It interferes with red cell antibody screening and can mimic M-protein on electrophoresis and immunofixation."], ["Second cancers", "Skin cancers and other second primary malignancies were seen more often in trials."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly at first and then every two weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids. Clearance includes binding to CD38 on tumor cells, so it falls as the tumor shrinks, and the half-life lengthens over time, with a median terminal half-life of about four weeks reported. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions reflect cytokine release as CD38-bearing cells are attacked and are reduced by premedication with dexamethasone, acetaminophen, an H2 blocker and diphenhydramine. Neutropenia and infections come from immune and marrow effects. Interference with blood bank testing comes from CD38 on red cells, so a type and screen is done before the first dose. The excess of second cancers in trials is not fully explained. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Sarclisa (isatuximab-irfc) FDA label", "Attal M et al. ICARIA-MM, Lancet 2019"]
},
"elotuzumab": {
  "origin": "Elotuzumab targets SLAMF7 (also called CS1), a surface protein that researchers found on nearly all myeloma cells and on natural killer cells. The antibody (originally HuLuc63) was developed by PDL BioPharma, then by Bristol-Myers Squibb in partnership with AbbVie. On its own it did little against myeloma, but with lenalidomide, which boosts NK cells, it worked well. The ELOQUENT-2 trial showed better progression-free survival when elotuzumab was added to lenalidomide and dexamethasone, and the FDA approved Empliciti in 2015. It was the first immunostimulatory antibody approved for myeloma. A combination with pomalidomide and dexamethasone was approved in 2018.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "PDL BioPharma develops HuLuc63, an anti-CS1 (SLAMF7) antibody."], ["2015", "FDA approves Empliciti with lenalidomide and dexamethasone for relapsed myeloma."], ["2018", "Approval with pomalidomide and dexamethasone."]],
  "cell": "SLAMF7 is a signaling receptor on myeloma cells and NK cells. Elotuzumab works in two ways. First, it binds SLAMF7 on NK cells and directly activates them through the adaptor protein EAT-2. Second, it coats myeloma cells, and its Fc tail engages CD16 on NK cells, triggering antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. On myeloma cells, which lack EAT-2, SLAMF7 binding does not activate them. Lenalidomide and pomalidomide increase NK cell numbers and activity, which is why elotuzumab works with them but has little effect alone.",
  "body": [["Myeloma cells", "Activated NK cells kill plasma cells, and M-protein falls over weeks."], ["Immune system", "NK cell activation and immune changes increase infections such as pneumonia and herpes zoster."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and blood pressure changes can occur, mostly early, and are reduced by premedication."], ["Lab tests", "As an IgG kappa antibody, it can appear on serum protein electrophoresis and immunofixation and mimic myeloma protein."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, diarrhea, cough and constipation are common, partly from the combined drugs."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme elevations can occur, so liver tests are checked periodically."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly at first and then every two to four weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids. Clearance is partly through binding to SLAMF7 on cells, so it is nonlinear: clearance falls at higher exposure. The half-life is long at labeled doses. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from immune activation and are reduced by premedication with dexamethasone, an H1 and H2 blocker and acetaminophen. Infections reflect immune effects plus the combined myeloma drugs and steroids. Second primary malignancies were reported more often in trials. Lab interference comes from the antibody itself showing up as an IgG kappa band. Embryo-fetal toxicity warnings come from its partners lenalidomide and pomalidomide. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Empliciti (elotuzumab) FDA label", "Lonial S et al. ELOQUENT-2, N Engl J Med 2015"]
},
"alemtuzumab": {
  "origin": "Alemtuzumab was born in the pathology department at the University of Cambridge, which gave it its original name, Campath (Cambridge Pathology). In the early 1980s Herman Waldmann and colleagues made rat antibodies against lymphocytes, aiming to remove T cells from donor marrow before transplants. One, Campath-1, bound a target later named CD52. In 1988 Lutz Riechmann, Greg Winter and Waldmann humanized it by grafting the rat binding loops onto a human antibody, making Campath-1H one of the first humanized antibodies. The FDA approved Campath in 2001 for B-cell chronic lymphocytic leukemia. In 2012 it was withdrawn from the commercial market for CLL (it remains available through a distribution program) and repurposed at a different schedule as Lemtrada, approved in 2014 for relapsing multiple sclerosis.",
  "timeline": [["1983", "Herman Waldmann's Cambridge group develops the Campath-1 rat antibodies."], ["1988", "Riechmann, Winter and Waldmann report humanized Campath-1H."], ["2001", "FDA approves Campath for B-cell CLL."], ["2012", "Campath is withdrawn from commercial sale and offered through a distribution program."], ["2014", "FDA approves Lemtrada for relapsing multiple sclerosis."]],
  "cell": "CD52 is a small protein anchored to the cell surface by a GPI anchor. It is found on mature B and T lymphocytes, NK cells, monocytes and macrophages, but not on blood stem cells. Alemtuzumab binds CD52 and destroys these cells by recruiting NK cells and macrophages (antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity) and by activating complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). This causes rapid, profound lymphocyte depletion. In CLL it clears malignant B cells. In MS, the immune system rebuilds over months from stem cells, and the new repertoire seems less prone to attack myelin. B cells return within months, but CD4 T cells take a year or more, which shapes both benefit and risk.",
  "body": [["Lymphocytes", "B and T cells fall to very low levels within days and recover slowly, CD4 cells taking more than a year."], ["Immune defense", "Severe immune suppression leads to opportunistic infections such as CMV, Pneumocystis pneumonia, herpes viruses and Listeria."], ["Infusion reactions", "Massive cytokine release during early infusions causes fever, rigors, rash, low blood pressure and breathing trouble."], ["Blood cells", "Pancytopenia, autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia can occur."], ["Thyroid and kidneys (MS use)", "As the immune system rebuilds, autoimmune thyroid disease is common and anti-GBM kidney disease can occur."], ["Brain and vessels (MS use)", "Stroke and arterial dissection have been reported within days of infusion."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion. It binds quickly to CD52-bearing cells, and early on many target cells clear it fast, so the half-life after the first dose is only about 11 hours. As lymphocytes disappear, clearance slows and the half-life rises to about six days with repeated dosing. It is broken down like other antibodies into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its effect on lymphocytes lasts months to years, far longer than the drug itself.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from cytokine release as large numbers of lymphocytes are destroyed at once; premedication with steroids, antihistamines and acetaminophen reduces them. Opportunistic infections follow profound T-cell depletion, so antiviral and Pneumocystis prophylaxis and CMV monitoring are used. Autoimmune diseases after MS treatment are thought to come from unbalanced immune reconstitution, in which B cells return well before regulatory T cells. Cytopenias include both marrow effects and autoimmunity. Lemtrada is available only through a REMS program. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The name Campath comes from Cambridge Pathology, the university department where the antibody was first made.",
  "src": ["Campath (alemtuzumab) FDA label", "Lemtrada (alemtuzumab) FDA label", "Riechmann L, Clark M, Waldmann H, Winter G. Nature 1988"]
},
"dinutuximab": {
  "origin": "Dinutuximab targets GD2, a sugar-containing fat (ganglioside) that coats neuroblastoma cells, a childhood cancer of the developing nervous system. In the 1980s Ralph Reisfeld and colleagues at the Scripps Research Institute in La Jolla made a mouse antibody against GD2, then a mouse-human chimeric version called ch14.18. For years the National Cancer Institute made the antibody itself to supply trials. In a Children's Oncology Group trial published in 2010, adding ch14.18 with GM-CSF and IL-2 to isotretinoin after intensive therapy improved survival in high-risk neuroblastoma. United Therapeutics took over manufacturing, and the FDA approved Unituxin in 2015, the first antibody approved for neuroblastoma.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ralph Reisfeld's Scripps group develops anti-GD2 antibodies, leading to chimeric ch14.18."], ["2010", "Children's Oncology Group trial shows improved survival with ch14.18 immunotherapy."], ["2015", "FDA approves Unituxin for high-risk neuroblastoma."]],
  "cell": "GD2 is a disialoganglioside that sits in the outer membrane of neuroblastoma cells and other neuroectodermal tumors, at much higher levels than on most normal tissues. Dinutuximab binds GD2 and marks the cell for destruction. Its IgG1 Fc tail recruits neutrophils, macrophages and NK cells to kill the cell by antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity, and it activates complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). GM-CSF boosts neutrophils and macrophages, and IL-2 activates NK cells, which is why it is given with these cytokines. Normal peripheral pain nerve fibers also carry GD2, so the antibody activates complement on these nerves and causes severe pain.",
  "body": [["Neuroblastoma cells", "Remaining tumor cells after intensive therapy are cleared by immune cells, lowering the chance of relapse."], ["Pain nerves", "Binding to GD2 on sensory nerve fibers triggers complement and causes severe neuropathic pain in the abdomen, limbs and back during infusions."], ["Blood vessels", "Cytokine release causes capillary leak, with swelling, low blood pressure and fluid shifts."], ["Eyes and nerves", "Blurred vision, light sensitivity, dilated pupils and, rarely, transverse myelitis can occur."], ["Electrolytes", "Low potassium, sodium and calcium are common, partly from capillary leak and fluid shifts."], ["Immune system", "Serious infusion reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion over several hours on consecutive days in each cycle, with GM-CSF or IL-2 and followed by oral isotretinoin. As an IgG antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids and is not cleared by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 10 days. Immune killing happens over days as effector cells are recruited.",
  "sidefx": "Neuropathic pain, part of the boxed warning, is on target: GD2 sits on pain fibers, and antibody plus complement activates them. Strong opioids and other pain medicines are given before and during infusions. Infusion reactions and capillary leak come from immune activation and cytokines, made stronger by IL-2. Neurologic eye effects and rare transverse myelitis or posterior encephalopathy (RPLS) reflect nerve and vessel effects. Electrolyte losses follow fluid shifts. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "For years before approval, the National Cancer Institute itself manufactured the ch14.18 antibody so children in trials could receive it.",
  "src": ["Unituxin (dinutuximab) FDA label", "Yu AL et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]
},
"naxitamab": {
  "origin": "Naxitamab grew from decades of work by Nai-Kong Cheung at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center in New York. In the 1980s his group made a mouse antibody against GD2 called 3F8 and treated children with neuroblastoma with it. Later they humanized the antibody, making hu3F8, to lower immune reactions against mouse protein and allow repeated courses. Y-mAbs Therapeutics licensed it and developed it as naxitamab. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Danyelza in 2020, with GM-CSF, for children 1 year and older and adults with relapsed or refractory high-risk neuroblastoma in the bone or bone marrow that has partly responded or stabilized with earlier therapy.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Nai-Kong Cheung's group at Memorial Sloan Kettering develops the anti-GD2 mouse antibody 3F8."], ["2010s", "The antibody is humanized as hu3F8 and licensed to Y-mAbs Therapeutics."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Danyelza for relapsed or refractory high-risk neuroblastoma in bone or marrow."]],
  "cell": "Naxitamab is a humanized IgG1 that binds GD2, a disialoganglioside highly expressed on neuroblastoma and other neuroectodermal tumors. Once bound, its Fc tail recruits granulocytes, macrophages and NK cells to kill the tumor cell through antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity and activates complement (complement-dependent cytotoxicity). GM-CSF, given alongside, increases the number and activity of granulocytes and macrophages, strengthening this killing. GD2 is also on peripheral pain fibers, so the antibody causes intense neuropathic pain during infusions. It is not cell-cycle specific.",
  "body": [["Neuroblastoma in bone and marrow", "Tumor cells coated with antibody are destroyed by immune cells, producing responses in disease that resisted other treatments."], ["Pain nerves", "Binding to GD2 on sensory fibers activates complement and causes severe pain during infusions, often in the abdomen, bones and limbs."], ["Airways and immune system", "Serious infusion reactions such as bronchospasm, stridor, hives and low blood pressure are common."], ["Blood pressure", "High blood pressure and fast heart rate can occur during and soon after infusions."], ["Nervous system", "Rare transverse myelitis, posterior encephalopathy (RPLS), urinary retention and pupil changes can occur."]],
  "journey": "Given as an IV infusion over about half an hour to an hour on days 1, 3 and 5 of each cycle, with GM-CSF injections under the skin before and during the cycle. It is usually given in an outpatient setting with premedication. As an IgG antibody, it is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 8 days.",
  "sidefx": "Severe neuropathic pain is on target, from GD2 on sensory nerve fibers, so patients receive opioids and other pain medicines around each infusion. Serious infusion reactions come from immune activation and cytokine release, and premedication with antihistamines, corticosteroids and acetaminophen reduces them. Hypertension is common and is checked during and after infusions. Transverse myelitis and RPLS reflect nerve and vessel effects. Both neurotoxicity and infusion reactions are in the boxed warning. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Danyelza (naxitamab-gqgk) FDA label", "Cheung NK et al. J Clin Oncol 1987"]
},
"mogamulizumab": {
  "origin": "Mogamulizumab came from the Japanese company Kyowa Kirin (formerly Kyowa Hakko Kirin). It targets CCR4, a chemokine receptor found on malignant T cells in adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma and cutaneous T-cell lymphomas. Kyowa's POTELLIGENT technology produces antibodies in cells that lack the enzyme that adds fucose to the antibody's sugar chain, which makes immune cells kill antibody-coated targets much more strongly. Mogamulizumab was first approved in Japan in 2012 for adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma. Based on the MAVORIC trial, which compared it with vorinostat, the FDA approved Poteligeo in 2018 for adults with relapsed or refractory mycosis fungoides or Sezary syndrome after at least one prior systemic therapy.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Kyowa Hakko Kirin develops POTELLIGENT defucosylated antibody technology."], ["2012", "Mogamulizumab is approved in Japan for adult T-cell leukemia-lymphoma."], ["2018", "FDA approves Poteligeo for mycosis fungoides and Sezary syndrome."]],
  "cell": "CCR4 is a receptor for the chemokines CCL17 and CCL22 that guides T cells into the skin. Malignant T cells in mycosis fungoides and Sezary syndrome express high levels of it. Mogamulizumab binds CCR4 and marks these cells for destruction. Because its Fc region lacks fucose, it binds FcgammaRIIIa (CD16A) on NK cells far more tightly, strongly enhancing antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity. Regulatory T cells also carry CCR4, so they are depleted too. This loss of immune brakes can boost anti-tumor immunity but also drives many of its autoimmune side effects.",
  "body": [["Malignant T cells in blood and skin", "Cancerous T cells are killed by NK cells, often clearing blood involvement in Sezary syndrome before skin lesions improve."], ["Skin", "Drug eruptions are common and can look like disease progression; severe reactions such as SJS and TEN can occur."], ["Immune balance", "Depleting regulatory T cells can cause autoimmune problems such as myositis, myocarditis, hepatitis, pneumonitis and thyroiditis."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and nausea occur mostly with the first infusion."], ["Transplant", "Patients who later receive an allogeneic stem cell transplant have more severe graft-versus-host disease."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, weekly in the first cycle and then every two weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The half-life is about 17 days. Because regulatory T cells take time to recover, its immune effects outlast the drug.",
  "sidefx": "Most serious effects trace back to loss of CCR4-positive regulatory T cells, which normally keep the immune system from attacking the body. That explains the drug rashes, autoimmune organ inflammation and worse graft-versus-host disease after transplant. Because a mogamulizumab rash can mimic lymphoma, a skin biopsy is often used to tell them apart. Infusion reactions are reduced by premedication with diphenhydramine and acetaminophen. Infections follow immune changes. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Poteligeo (mogamulizumab-kpkc) FDA label", "Kim YH et al. MAVORIC, Lancet Oncol 2018"]
},
"tafasitamab": {
  "origin": "Tafasitamab began at Xencor, a California company that engineers antibody Fc regions. Xencor designed an anti-CD19 antibody (XmAb5574) with two amino acid changes in its Fc tail that make it bind immune cell Fc receptors much more strongly. MorphoSys, a German company, licensed it in 2010 and developed it as MOR208, later partnering with Incyte. In the single-arm L-MIND trial, tafasitamab plus lenalidomide produced durable responses in relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) in people who could not have a stem cell transplant. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Monjuvi in 2020 for that use. It later gained approval for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma with lenalidomide and rituximab.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Xencor engineers XmAb5574, an Fc-enhanced anti-CD19 antibody."], ["2010", "MorphoSys licenses the antibody and develops it as MOR208."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Monjuvi with lenalidomide for relapsed or refractory DLBCL."], ["2025", "Approval for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma with lenalidomide and rituximab."]],
  "cell": "CD19 is a B-cell surface protein present from early B-cell development through mature B cells, and it remains on most B-cell lymphomas, including some that have lost CD20. Tafasitamab binds CD19. Its Fc region carries two amino acid substitutions that increase binding to Fc gamma receptors, especially FcgammaRIIIa on NK cells and receptors on macrophages. This strengthens antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cellular phagocytosis, and binding can also trigger some direct apoptosis. Lenalidomide boosts NK cell numbers and activity and has its own anti-lymphoma effects, so the two combine well.",
  "body": [["Lymphoma cells", "CD19-positive DLBCL and follicular lymphoma cells are killed by NK cells and macrophages, shrinking tumors."], ["Normal B cells", "Healthy B cells are depleted too, lowering antibody production and raising infection risk."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia and anemia are common, largely with lenalidomide."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and rash can occur, mostly with the first infusions."], ["Lungs and infections", "Respiratory infections and pneumonia are common serious effects."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, more often in the first cycles and then every two weeks. As an IgG antibody, it stays in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The half-life is about 17 days. CYP drug interactions are not expected; lenalidomide is cleared separately by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia reflect combined effects of B-cell depletion, immune activation and lenalidomide's marrow suppression. Infections follow low neutrophils and loss of normal B cells. Infusion reactions come from cytokine release and are reduced with premedication for the first infusions. Lenalidomide adds its own boxed warnings for fetal harm, blood count drops and clots. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Monjuvi (tafasitamab-cxix) FDA label", "Salles G et al. L-MIND, Lancet Oncol 2020", "Horton HM et al. Cancer Res 2008"]
},
"zolbetuximab": {
  "origin": "Zolbetuximab was created at Ganymed Pharmaceuticals in Mainz, Germany, a company cofounded by Ugur Sahin and Ozlem Tureci, who later founded BioNTech. Their team searched for proteins that are normally hidden in healthy tissue but exposed on cancer cells. They found claudin 18.2, a tight-junction protein in the stomach lining that becomes accessible on many gastric cancer cells. Ganymed developed the antibody IMAB362, and Astellas acquired the company in 2016. Two phase 3 trials, SPOTLIGHT and GLOW, showed longer survival when zolbetuximab was added to chemotherapy. The FDA approved Vyloy in 2024 for first-line treatment of HER2-negative, CLDN18.2-positive gastric or gastroesophageal junction adenocarcinoma, the first claudin-targeted drug.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Ganymed Pharmaceuticals identifies claudin 18.2 as a cancer target and develops IMAB362."], ["2016", "Astellas acquires Ganymed."], ["2023", "SPOTLIGHT and GLOW phase 3 results are published."], ["2024", "FDA approves Vyloy, the first claudin 18.2-directed therapy."]],
  "cell": "Claudins are proteins that seal the tight junctions between epithelial cells. In the normal stomach, claudin 18.2 is tucked into these junctions and is hard for antibodies to reach. When stomach cells become cancerous, cell polarity and junctions are disrupted, so claudin 18.2 becomes exposed on the cell surface. Zolbetuximab, a chimeric IgG1, binds it and triggers antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by NK cells and complement-dependent cytotoxicity. Chemotherapy appears to increase claudin 18.2 exposure and immune cell recruitment, adding to the effect. Tumors are tested with an FDA-approved immunohistochemistry test to confirm claudin 18.2 positivity.",
  "body": [["Gastric and GEJ tumors", "Claudin 18.2-positive cancer cells are attacked by immune cells and complement, improving survival with chemotherapy."], ["Stomach lining", "Binding to claudin 18.2 in normal gastric mucosa causes severe nausea and vomiting, often during the first infusion."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Repeated vomiting can cause dehydration and low potassium."], ["Appetite and weight", "Poor appetite and low albumin are common."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia occurs, largely from the chemotherapy partner."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two or three weeks, matched to the chemotherapy schedule. As an IgG antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is long, measured in weeks. Nausea is worst at the start of the first infusion, so infusion rates can be slowed.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting are on target: the antibody reaches claudin 18.2 in the normal stomach lining and causes local injury and inflammation, especially early in treatment. Antiemetic premedication with combinations such as an NK1 and a 5-HT3 antagonist is used, and the infusion rate is slowed or interrupted when vomiting occurs. Dehydration and low potassium follow vomiting. Hypersensitivity comes from immune reactions to the antibody. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Zolbetuximab's inventors, Ugur Sahin and Ozlem Tureci, later led BioNTech, the company that developed one of the first mRNA COVID-19 vaccines.",
  "src": ["Vyloy (zolbetuximab-clzb) FDA label", "Shitara K et al. SPOTLIGHT, Lancet 2023", "Shah MA et al. GLOW, Nat Med 2023"]
},
"nivolumab": {
  "origin": "Nivolumab rests on a discovery made in Kyoto. In 1992 Tasuku Honjo's lab at Kyoto University identified PD-1 (programmed death-1), a receptor on T cells, and later showed it acts as a brake on immune responses. Tumors exploit this brake by displaying its ligand, PD-L1. The Japanese company Ono Pharmaceutical and the US company Medarex developed a fully human anti-PD-1 antibody, and Bristol-Myers Squibb acquired Medarex in 2009. Nivolumab was approved in Japan in July 2014 and by the FDA as Opdivo in December 2014 for advanced melanoma. It went on to approvals in many cancers, including lung, kidney, head and neck, Hodgkin lymphoma and others. Honjo shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with James Allison for checkpoint discoveries.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab identifies PD-1."], ["2009", "Bristol-Myers Squibb acquires Medarex, co-developer of nivolumab with Ono."], ["2014", "Nivolumab is approved in Japan in July and by the FDA in December for melanoma."], ["2018", "Honjo and James Allison share the Nobel Prize for immune checkpoint discoveries."], ["2024", "FDA approves a subcutaneous nivolumab with hyaluronidase (Opdivo Qvantig)."]],
  "cell": "PD-1 is an inhibitory receptor that appears on T cells after they are activated, especially during long-lasting stimulation such as chronic infection or cancer. When PD-1 binds PD-L1 or PD-L2 on tumor or immune cells, it recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which removes phosphate groups from T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling molecules. T cells then stop dividing, make less cytokine and become exhausted. Nivolumab, a fully human IgG4 antibody, binds PD-1 and blocks both ligands. This releases the brake mainly in the effector phase, inside tumors and peripheral tissues, so tumor-specific T cells recover function and kill cancer cells. The IgG4 format has little ability to recruit Fc-driven killing, so it does not deplete the T cells it binds.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "Reactivated T cells attack cancer cells, which can produce durable responses, sometimes after initial swelling of tumors from immune cell infiltration."], ["Lungs", "T-cell attack on lung tissue can cause pneumonitis with cough and shortness of breath."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune-mediated colitis causes diarrhea, and hepatitis causes liver enzyme rises."], ["Endocrine glands", "The thyroid, pituitary, adrenal glands and pancreas can be attacked, causing hypothyroidism, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency or type 1 diabetes that may be permanent."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and vitiligo are common; severe reactions such as SJS and TEN are rare."], ["Heart, kidneys and nerves", "Myocarditis, nephritis and encephalitis are uncommon but can be serious."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two to four weeks, or subcutaneously with hyaluronidase. As an IgG4 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The neonatal Fc receptor recycles it, giving a half-life of about 25 days. Its blockade of PD-1 and its immune effects can last months after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated: releasing the PD-1 brake lets T cells attack normal tissues as well as tumors. Any organ can be affected, most often skin, gut, liver, lungs and endocrine glands. Most inflammatory effects are treated by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with other immune suppressants such as infliximab for resistant colitis. Endocrine damage often needs lifelong hormone replacement rather than steroids. Myocarditis is rare but can be fatal, especially in combination with CTLA-4 blockade. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Honjo's lab originally named PD-1 for programmed death because they thought it was involved in cell death; its real job as an immune brake was worked out later.",
  "src": ["Opdivo (nivolumab) FDA label", "Ishida Y, Agata Y, Shibahara K, Honjo T. EMBO J 1992", "Robert C et al. N Engl J Med 2015"]
},
"nivolumab-relatlimab": {
  "origin": "This product joins two immune checkpoint blockers in one vial. Nivolumab blocks PD-1, discovered by Tasuku Honjo's Kyoto lab in 1992. Relatlimab blocks LAG-3 (lymphocyte activation gene 3), a second T-cell brake identified in 1990 by Frederic Triebel and colleagues in France. Bristol-Myers Squibb developed relatlimab and tested the fixed combination in the RELATIVITY-047 trial in untreated advanced melanoma, where it roughly doubled progression-free survival compared with nivolumab alone. The FDA approved Opdualag in 2022 for unresectable or metastatic melanoma in adults and children 12 years and older. It was the first LAG-3 blocking drug and the third distinct checkpoint pathway (after CTLA-4 and PD-1/PD-L1) to be targeted.",
  "timeline": [["1990", "Frederic Triebel's group identifies LAG-3."], ["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab identifies PD-1."], ["2014", "Nivolumab is first approved for melanoma."], ["2022", "FDA approves Opdualag, the first LAG-3 blocking antibody combination."]],
  "cell": "Exhausted T cells inside tumors often carry several inhibitory receptors at once. PD-1, when bound by PD-L1 or PD-L2, recruits SHP-2 and shuts down T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling. LAG-3 is a CD4-like receptor that binds MHC class II, and also ligands such as FGL1, and dampens T-cell proliferation and cytokine production through its own intracellular motif. The two pathways act in complementary ways. Nivolumab (human IgG4) blocks PD-1, and relatlimab (human IgG4) blocks LAG-3 from binding its ligands. Releasing both brakes restores the function of exhausted CD8 and CD4 T cells more than PD-1 blockade alone, increasing tumor killing.",
  "body": [["Melanoma", "Reinvigorated T cells attack melanoma, doubling the time before progression in trials compared with nivolumab alone."], ["Heart", "Myocarditis is more frequent than with nivolumab alone, so troponin is often checked."], ["Endocrine glands", "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and hypophysitis can occur."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Lungs and kidneys", "Pneumonitis and nephritis can develop when T cells infiltrate lung and kidney tissue."], ["Skin and joints", "Rash, itching and musculoskeletal pain are common."]],
  "journey": "Given as a single IV infusion every four weeks containing both antibodies in a fixed ratio. Both are IgG4 antibodies that stay mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. They are broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. Half-lives are similar, about 26 days for nivolumab and 27 days for relatlimab. Immune effects persist for months.",
  "sidefx": "All side effects are immune-mediated: releasing two T-cell brakes lets T cells attack normal tissue. Rates of serious immune effects were higher than with nivolumab alone but lower than with nivolumab plus ipilimumab in trials. Myocarditis is a key concern. Treatment involves holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with other immune suppressants for steroid-resistant cases. Endocrine gland damage usually needs lifelong hormone replacement. There is no reversal agent.",
  "src": ["Opdualag (nivolumab and relatlimab-rmbw) FDA label", "Tawbi HA et al. RELATIVITY-047, N Engl J Med 2022", "Triebel F et al. J Exp Med 1990"]
},
"ipilimumab": {
  "origin": "Ipilimumab was the first immune checkpoint inhibitor. CTLA-4 was identified in 1987 by Pierre Golstein's group in Marseille, France, and was later shown to be a brake on T-cell activation. In 1996 James Allison's lab at the University of California, Berkeley showed that an antibody blocking CTLA-4 made tumors in mice shrink, introducing the idea of releasing immune brakes to fight cancer. Medarex developed a fully human anti-CTLA-4 antibody (MDX-010), and Bristol-Myers Squibb took it through phase 3 trials. In 2010 it became the first drug ever shown to improve overall survival in metastatic melanoma, and the FDA approved Yervoy in 2011. Allison shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Tasuku Honjo.",
  "timeline": [["1987", "CTLA-4 is identified by Pierre Golstein's group."], ["1996", "James Allison's lab shows CTLA-4 blockade causes tumor rejection in mice."], ["2010", "Phase 3 trial shows improved survival in metastatic melanoma."], ["2011", "FDA approves Yervoy, the first immune checkpoint inhibitor."], ["2015", "FDA approves the nivolumab plus ipilimumab combination for melanoma."], ["2018", "Allison and Honjo share the Nobel Prize for checkpoint discoveries."]],
  "cell": "T cells need two signals to activate: the antigen signal through the T-cell receptor and a co-stimulatory signal when CD28 binds B7 molecules (CD80 and CD86) on antigen-presenting cells. Soon after activation, T cells put CTLA-4 on their surface. CTLA-4 binds CD80 and CD86 much more tightly than CD28 does, outcompeting it and pulling B7 off antigen-presenting cells, which shuts activation down. Regulatory T cells also use CTLA-4 constantly. Ipilimumab, a fully human IgG1, binds CTLA-4 and blocks it. This happens mainly in lymph nodes during the priming phase, so more T cells are activated, more diverse T-cell clones expand and travel to tumors. Its IgG1 tail may also deplete CTLA-4-rich regulatory T cells inside tumors.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "A larger and broader army of T cells attacks melanoma and other cancers, with some patients having very long-lasting responses."], ["Colon", "Enterocolitis with diarrhea is the classic early serious effect and can lead to perforation."], ["Pituitary", "Hypophysitis is more common than with PD-1 drugs, causing headache, fatigue and low levels of pituitary hormones."], ["Liver", "Immune hepatitis from T cells attacking liver cells raises liver enzymes."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and vitiligo are common as T cells react to skin and pigment cells."], ["Other organs", "Thyroid, adrenal, nerve, eye, kidney, lung and heart inflammation can occur."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion, usually every three weeks for a limited number of doses, alone or with nivolumab. As an IgG1 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. The half-life is about 15 days. Immune effects begin within weeks and can continue for months after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated. Because CTLA-4 blockade broadens T-cell priming in lymph nodes, it unleashes more widespread autoimmunity than PD-1 blockade, and serious effects are more frequent and rise with dose. Colitis, hepatitis, skin reactions and hypophysitis are most typical. Treatment is to hold the drug and give corticosteroids, adding drugs such as infliximab for steroid-resistant colitis. Hormone deficits often need lifelong replacement. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "James Allison played harmonica in a blues band made up of immunologists called the Checkpoints.",
  "src": ["Yervoy (ipilimumab) FDA label", "Leach DR, Krummel MF, Allison JP. Science 1996", "Hodi FS et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]
},
"tremelimumab": {
  "origin": "Tremelimumab is a fully human anti-CTLA-4 antibody originally developed by Pfizer (code name CP-675,206), building on James Allison's 1996 finding that blocking CTLA-4 made mouse tumors regress. In a phase 3 melanoma trial it did not improve survival compared with chemotherapy, and its development stalled while ipilimumab went on to approval. MedImmune, part of AstraZeneca, later acquired the rights and tested it with its anti-PD-L1 antibody durvalumab. In the HIMALAYA trial for unresectable liver cancer, a single priming dose of tremelimumab plus regular durvalumab (the STRIDE regimen) improved survival over sorafenib. The FDA approved Imjudo in 2022 for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma with durvalumab, and later that year for metastatic non-small cell lung cancer with durvalumab and chemotherapy.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "Allison's lab shows CTLA-4 blockade causes tumor rejection in mice."], ["2000s", "Pfizer develops tremelimumab; a phase 3 melanoma trial does not show a survival benefit."], ["2010s", "AstraZeneca's MedImmune develops tremelimumab with durvalumab."], ["2022", "FDA approves Imjudo with durvalumab for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma and for metastatic NSCLC."]],
  "cell": "CTLA-4 is an inhibitory receptor that T cells raise after activation. It binds the co-stimulatory molecules CD80 and CD86 on antigen-presenting cells more tightly than CD28 does, cutting off the second signal T cells need to fully activate. Tremelimumab, a human IgG2, binds CTLA-4 and blocks it from binding CD80 and CD86, so more CD28 co-stimulation occurs during T-cell priming in lymph nodes and more tumor-specific T cells expand. Durvalumab blocks PD-L1 in the tumor, releasing the brake on T cells in the effector phase. In the STRIDE regimen, one priming dose of tremelimumab is meant to expand T cells while limiting the cumulative toxicity that repeated CTLA-4 blockade causes.",
  "body": [["Liver and lung tumors", "Expanded and reactivated T cells attack cancer cells, improving survival with durvalumab."], ["Liver tissue", "Immune hepatitis can be hard to separate from underlying liver disease in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["Gut", "Colitis causes diarrhea and, rarely, perforation."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroid disease, adrenal insufficiency and hypophysitis can occur."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common as activated T cells infiltrate the skin."], ["Lungs, kidneys and heart", "Pneumonitis, nephritis and rare myocarditis can develop."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion: a single dose with the first durvalumab dose in liver cancer, or several doses with chemotherapy in lung cancer. As an IgG2 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid and is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, not by the liver or kidneys. The half-life is about 16 to 18 days. Immune effects begin within weeks and last well beyond the drug's presence.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated, from T cells released by CTLA-4 and PD-L1 blockade attacking normal tissues. A single priming dose was chosen partly to limit the higher rates of colitis and other immune effects seen with repeated CTLA-4 blockade. Management is to hold therapy and give corticosteroids, with additional immune suppressants if needed. Endocrine damage often needs lifelong hormone replacement. There is no reversal agent.",
  "src": ["Imjudo (tremelimumab-actl) FDA label", "Abou-Alfa GK et al. HIMALAYA, NEJM Evid 2022", "Ribas A et al. J Clin Oncol 2013"]
},
"atezolizumab": {
  "origin": "Atezolizumab blocks PD-L1, the partner that tumors use to switch off PD-1 on T cells. PD-L1 was described in 1999 by Lieping Chen's lab at the Mayo Clinic (as B7-H1), and in 2000 Gordon Freeman, Tasuku Honjo and colleagues showed it binds PD-1. Genentech, part of Roche, engineered a humanized anti-PD-L1 IgG1 with an altered Fc region so it would not kill the PD-L1-positive immune cells it binds. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Tecentriq in 2016 for advanced urothelial (bladder) cancer, making it the first PD-L1 inhibitor approved. Its bladder and breast cancer indications were later withdrawn after confirmatory trials fell short, but it remains approved for lung cancer, small cell lung cancer, liver cancer, melanoma and alveolar soft part sarcoma. A separate subcutaneous product with hyaluronidase, Tecentriq Hybreza, was approved in 2024.",
  "timeline": [["1999", "Lieping Chen's lab describes B7-H1, later named PD-L1."], ["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to bind PD-1 and inhibit T cells."], ["2016", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Tecentriq for urothelial cancer, the first PD-L1 inhibitor."], ["2019", "Approval with carboplatin and etoposide for extensive-stage small cell lung cancer."], ["2020", "Approval with bevacizumab for unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["2024", "FDA approves subcutaneous Tecentriq Hybreza."]],
  "cell": "Many tumor cells and tumor-infiltrating immune cells display PD-L1 (CD274), often in response to interferon-gamma released by attacking T cells. PD-L1 binds PD-1 on T cells, shutting them down, and also binds B7.1 (CD80), another inhibitory interaction. Atezolizumab binds PD-L1 and blocks both interactions, restoring cytotoxic T-cell activity against the tumor. It leaves PD-L2 free to bind PD-1, which some think may help preserve immune balance in the lung. An N298A substitution removes the sugar on its Fc region, so it does not trigger antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity and does not deplete PD-L1-expressing activated T cells.",
  "body": [["Tumors", "T cells in the tumor recover the ability to kill cancer cells, producing durable responses in some patients."], ["Lungs", "Immune pneumonitis can cause cough, shortness of breath and low oxygen."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroiditis, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency and type 1 diabetes can occur and may be permanent."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common; severe reactions are rare."], ["Kidneys, heart and nerves", "Nephritis, myocarditis and neurologic inflammation are uncommon but serious."]],
  "journey": "Given by IV infusion every two, three or four weeks, depending on the schedule. As an IgG1 antibody, it stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is broken down by cells into peptides and amino acids, with no meaningful liver or kidney clearance, so CYP drug interactions are not expected. The half-life is about 27 days. PD-L1 blockade lasts weeks, and immune side effects can appear months after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are immune-mediated: blocking PD-L1 releases T cells that can attack normal tissues. The most common affected organs are skin, thyroid, lungs, gut and liver. Inflammatory effects are managed by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, adding other immune suppressants for resistant cases. Endocrine gland damage often needs lifelong hormone replacement. Infusion reactions are usually mild. There is no reversal agent.",
  "src": ["Tecentriq (atezolizumab) FDA label", "Dong H, Zhu G, Tamada K, Chen L. Nat Med 1999", "Freeman GJ et al. J Exp Med 2000"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"durvalumab": {
  "origin": "Durvalumab grew out of the discovery of the PD-1 'brake' on T cells. Tasuku Honjo's lab in Kyoto described PD-1 in 1992, and its partner PD-L1 was identified around 1999 to 2000 by Lieping Chen's group and by Gordon Freeman and colleagues. Tumors were found to coat themselves in PD-L1 to switch off attacking T cells. MedImmune, the biologics arm of AstraZeneca, developed durvalumab as a fully human antibody against PD-L1 and engineered its Fc tail so it would not kill the PD-L1-carrying immune cells it binds. The FDA first approved it as Imfinzi in 2017. Its landmark came in 2018, when the PACIFIC trial showed that giving it after chemoradiation for unresectable Stage III lung cancer improved survival, which changed standard care for that stage of disease.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "Tasuku Honjo's lab in Kyoto describes the PD-1 gene."], ["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to bind PD-1 and turn T cells off."], ["2017", "FDA grants first approval of durvalumab (Imfinzi)."], ["2018", "FDA approves durvalumab after chemoradiation for Stage III NSCLC based on the PACIFIC trial."], ["2018", "Honjo shares the Nobel Prize for checkpoint discoveries with James Allison."]],
  "cell": "Activated T cells display PD-1. Tumor cells and immune cells in the tumor display PD-L1 (CD274), often in response to interferon-gamma from the T cells themselves. When PD-L1 engages PD-1, the PD-1 tail recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which strips phosphates from the T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling molecules. The T cell stops dividing, makes less cytokine and becomes 'exhausted'. Durvalumab is a human IgG1 kappa antibody that binds PD-L1 and blocks it from engaging both PD-1 and CD80 (B7.1). With the brake released, tumor-specific T cells regain proliferation, cytokine release and killing. A triple Fc mutation (L234F, L235E, P331S) reduces Fc-gamma receptor and complement binding, so the antibody does not destroy PD-L1-positive immune cells by ADCC or CDC. It acts on the immune system, not on the cancer cell cycle.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Reawakened cytotoxic T cells infiltrate and kill cancer cells. Responses can take weeks to months and may follow early swelling of tumors."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur from T cells attacking lung tissue, and the risk is a special concern after chest radiation, which already inflames the lung."], ["Gut and liver", "Loss of self-tolerance can cause colitis with diarrhea and immune hepatitis with rising liver enzymes."], ["Endocrine glands", "The thyroid, pituitary, adrenal glands and pancreatic islet cells can be inflamed, causing hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency or new type 1 diabetes, which are often permanent."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common. Rare severe reactions such as SJS/TEN can occur."], ["Heart and kidneys", "Rare myocarditis and nephritis reflect the same immune attack on normal organs."]],
  "journey": "Durvalumab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the blood completely. As a large IgG protein it stays mostly in the blood and the fluid between cells. It is not handled by liver CYP enzymes or cleared by the kidneys. Instead, cells throughout the body take it up and break it down into peptides and amino acids, while the FcRn receptor recycles some of it and extends its life. Its half-life at steady state is about 18 days, and the blockade of PD-L1 lasts for weeks. Immune side effects can appear months after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "The main toxicities are immune-mediated. Removing the PD-L1 brake lets T cells attack normal tissues that also rely on PD-1 signaling for tolerance, which explains pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, skin reactions and rare myocarditis. These are treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants. Lost hormone gland function is replaced with hormones. Infusion reactions come from the protein itself. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "The PACIFIC trial was one of the first to show that an immunotherapy given after curative-intent chemoradiation could improve overall survival in Stage III lung cancer.",
  "src": ["Imfinzi (durvalumab) FDA label", "Antonia SJ et al. PACIFIC trial, N Engl J Med 2017", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"avelumab": {
  "origin": "Avelumab was developed by the German company Merck KGaA (known as EMD Serono in the US) in alliance with Pfizer. It is a fully human anti-PD-L1 antibody that, unlike several of its relatives, keeps a normal IgG1 Fc tail. In March 2017 it became the first drug approved by the FDA for metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma, a rare and aggressive skin cancer often linked to Merkel cell polyomavirus. In 2020 the JAVELIN Bladder 100 trial showed that starting avelumab as maintenance right after successful platinum chemotherapy for advanced bladder cancer helped people live longer, which created a new maintenance approach for urothelial cancer.",
  "timeline": [["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to switch off T cells through PD-1."], ["2017", "FDA approves avelumab (Bavencio) for metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma, the first drug for this cancer."], ["2020", "FDA approves avelumab as first-line maintenance for advanced urothelial carcinoma after JAVELIN Bladder 100."]],
  "cell": "Avelumab is a human IgG1 lambda antibody that binds PD-L1 (CD274) on tumor cells and immune cells. It blocks PD-L1 from engaging PD-1 and B7.1 (CD80) on T cells. Normally PD-1 engagement recruits the phosphatase SHP-2 and dampens T-cell receptor and CD28 signals, so T cells stop dividing and killing. Blocking the ligand releases that brake, and tumor-specific cytotoxic T cells resume proliferation, interferon-gamma release and perforin and granzyme killing. Because avelumab keeps a native Fc region, it can also recruit natural killer cells to kill PD-L1-positive tumor cells by antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity, which has been shown in laboratory studies. The effect is immune, not cell-cycle specific.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Released T cells and possibly NK cells attack cancer cells. Merkel cell carcinoma, which often carries viral antigens, responds well."], ["Immune system", "The native Fc tail and the protein itself make infusion reactions with fever, chills and flushing relatively common, especially early."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis can cause diarrhea, and immune hepatitis can raise liver enzymes."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can result from T cells attacking hormone-making cells."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis causes cough and shortness of breath from immune inflammation of lung tissue."], ["Kidneys", "Immune nephritis is uncommon but can reduce kidney function."]],
  "journey": "Avelumab is given by IV infusion over about an hour, so all of it reaches the blood. It distributes mainly in plasma and tissue fluid. Like other IgG antibodies it is broken down by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body, not by liver enzymes or kidney filtration. Its half-life is shorter than many checkpoint antibodies, about 6 days, which is why it is given every 2 weeks. Premedication is used before early infusions because reactions are common.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions with fever, chills, flushing, low blood pressure and breathing trouble come from the immune response to an infused protein and are more frequent with avelumab than with some related antibodies. The immune-mediated side effects, including pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, endocrine gland damage, nephritis and rare myocarditis, occur because PD-L1 blockade also releases T cells against normal tissue. These are managed with corticosteroids and holding the drug. Hormone loss is replaced. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Merkel cell carcinoma is linked to a polyomavirus in most cases, so the tumor shows foreign viral proteins that make it a natural target for a reawakened immune system.",
  "src": ["Bavencio (avelumab) FDA label", "Powles T et al. JAVELIN Bladder 100, N Engl J Med 2020", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"cemiplimab": {
  "origin": "Cemiplimab was made by Regeneron Pharmaceuticals, developed together with Sanofi. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, which have human antibody genes in place of their own, so the antibodies these mice make are fully human. The target was PD-1, the T-cell brake first described by Tasuku Honjo's lab in 1992. In September 2018 the FDA approved cemiplimab (Libtayo) as the first drug for advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, a sun-related skin cancer that can be disfiguring when surgery or radiation are not options. Later approvals added basal cell carcinoma after hedgehog inhibitors and non-small cell lung cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2018", "FDA approves cemiplimab (Libtayo) for advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma."], ["2021", "FDA approvals extend cemiplimab to advanced basal cell carcinoma and PD-L1-high NSCLC."]],
  "cell": "Cemiplimab is a human IgG4 antibody with a stabilizing hinge mutation (S228P) that binds the PD-1 receptor (CD279) on T cells. It blocks PD-1 from engaging both of its ligands, PD-L1 and PD-L2. Normally ligand binding recruits SHP-2 to the PD-1 tail, which dephosphorylates T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling proteins, slowing T-cell division, cytokine release and killing. With PD-1 blocked, tumor-specific T cells recover these functions. Skin squamous cell cancers carry very high numbers of UV-induced mutations, so they display many abnormal proteins (neoantigens) that T cells can recognize once the brake is released. The IgG4 backbone limits Fc effector functions so the antibody does not deplete the T cells it binds.",
  "body": [["Skin tumors", "Reactivated T cells attack heavily mutated skin cancers, and lesions can shrink over weeks to months."], ["Lungs", "Immune pneumonitis can cause cough and breathlessness."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and immune hepatitis causes liver enzyme rises."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroid inflammation, adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can develop and may be permanent."], ["Transplanted organs", "Releasing T cells can trigger rejection of a solid organ transplant, a real concern because many people with skin cancer are transplant recipients."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common immune effects on normal skin."]],
  "journey": "Cemiplimab is given by IV infusion, so it is fully available in the blood. It stays mainly in plasma and tissue fluid and is broken down slowly by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body, with FcRn recycling extending its life. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes and is not cleared by the kidneys. The half-life is about 20 days, and receptor blockade lasts weeks. Immune side effects can start long after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "The key side effects are immune-mediated inflammation of normal organs: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, severe skin reactions and rare myocarditis. They happen because PD-1 also keeps T cells tolerant of healthy tissue. Treatment is holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with hormone replacement when a gland is destroyed. Organ transplant rejection can occur for the same reason. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma has one of the highest mutation counts of any human cancer because of lifelong sun exposure, which helps explain why it responds so well to PD-1 blockade.",
  "src": ["Libtayo (cemiplimab-rwlc) FDA label", "Migden MR et al. N Engl J Med 2018", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"dostarlimab": {
  "origin": "Dostarlimab was discovered by AnaptysBio and developed by Tesaro, which was bought by GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) in 2019. It is a humanized anti-PD-1 antibody. The FDA first approved it as Jemperli in April 2021 for mismatch repair-deficient (dMMR) endometrial cancer that had come back after platinum chemotherapy, and later for dMMR solid tumors of any origin and, with chemotherapy, for primary advanced endometrial cancer. It drew wide attention in 2022 when a small trial at Memorial Sloan Kettering reported that every patient with dMMR locally advanced rectal cancer had a complete clinical response.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2019", "GSK acquires Tesaro, the developer of dostarlimab."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for dMMR recurrent or advanced endometrial cancer."], ["2022", "Cercek and colleagues report complete clinical responses in all patients in a small dMMR rectal cancer trial."], ["2023", "FDA approves dostarlimab with carboplatin and paclitaxel for primary advanced endometrial cancer."]],
  "cell": "Dostarlimab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds PD-1 (CD279) on T cells and blocks its interaction with PD-L1 and PD-L2. PD-1 ligation normally recruits the phosphatase SHP-2, which turns down T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling and leaves T cells exhausted. Blocking PD-1 restores T-cell proliferation, cytokine release and cytotoxic killing. Mismatch repair-deficient tumors cannot fix small DNA copying errors, so they build up thousands of mutations and make many abnormal proteins called neoantigens. These tumors are full of T cells already primed to see them, which is why releasing the PD-1 brake works especially well in dMMR and MSI-high cancers. It is not cell-cycle specific.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "T cells recognize the many neoantigens in dMMR tumors and destroy cancer cells, sometimes completely."], ["Endocrine glands", "Hypothyroidism is common, and adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can occur from immune attack on hormone-making cells."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis causes diarrhea, and immune hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is an uncommon but serious immune inflammation of the lung."], ["Joints and skin", "Joint pain and rash reflect immune activity in normal tissues."], ["Blood (with chemotherapy)", "When combined with carboplatin and paclitaxel, low blood counts, nerve damage and hair loss come mostly from the chemotherapy."]],
  "journey": "Dostarlimab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the blood fully. It stays largely in plasma and tissue fluid and is degraded slowly by protein catabolism in cells, protected in part by FcRn recycling. Liver enzymes and the kidneys play no meaningful role. The half-life is about 23 days, long enough to allow dosing every 3 weeks at first and then every 6 weeks. PD-1 blockade lasts for weeks after a dose.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from the immune system turned loose on normal tissue: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroid and other gland damage, nephritis, skin reactions and rare myocarditis. PD-1 normally helps keep self-reactive T cells quiet, so blocking it lowers that barrier. Management is holding the drug and giving corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants. Glands that stop working need lifelong hormone replacement. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "In the 2022 rectal cancer study, patients were able to avoid planned surgery, radiation and chemotherapy because their tumors disappeared on dostarlimab alone.",
  "src": ["Jemperli (dostarlimab-gxly) FDA label", "Cercek A et al. N Engl J Med 2022", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"retifanlimab": {
  "origin": "Retifanlimab was originally developed by MacroGenics and licensed to Incyte, which carried it through approval. It is a humanized IgG4 anti-PD-1 antibody. In March 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Zynyz for metastatic or recurrent locally advanced Merkel cell carcinoma, a rare skin cancer. In 2025 it was approved for squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal, both with carboplatin and paclitaxel and as a single agent after platinum chemotherapy, giving that uncommon cancer one of its first approved immunotherapies.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2017", "Incyte licenses retifanlimab from MacroGenics."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for Merkel cell carcinoma."], ["2025", "FDA approves retifanlimab for squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal."]],
  "cell": "Retifanlimab is a humanized IgG4 antibody that binds PD-1 (CD279) on activated T cells and blocks its binding to PD-L1 and PD-L2. When PD-1 is engaged, it recruits SHP-2, which dephosphorylates key molecules downstream of the T-cell receptor and CD28 and shuts down T-cell activation. Blocking PD-1 restores T-cell proliferation, interferon-gamma release and killing of tumor cells. Merkel cell carcinoma and anal squamous cell carcinoma are often driven by viruses (Merkel cell polyomavirus and human papillomavirus), so they display viral proteins that T cells can recognize once the brake is lifted. The IgG4 format limits Fc effector activity against the T cells it binds.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Reactivated T cells kill tumor cells that display viral or mutated proteins."], ["Endocrine glands", "Thyroiditis, adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can follow immune attack on hormone-making tissue."], ["Gut and liver", "Colitis causes diarrhea, and immune hepatitis raises liver enzymes."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can develop from immune inflammation of lung tissue."], ["Skin", "Itching and rash are common, reflecting immune activity in normal skin."], ["Blood (with chemotherapy)", "With carboplatin and paclitaxel, low blood counts and nerve damage come mostly from the chemotherapy."]],
  "journey": "Retifanlimab is given by IV infusion, so it reaches the blood completely. It stays mostly in plasma and tissue fluid and is broken down slowly by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body. CYP enzymes and kidney filtration do not play a meaningful role. Like other IgG4 antibodies, its half-life is measured in weeks, and PD-1 blockade lasts weeks after a dose. Immune reactions can appear months later.",
  "sidefx": "Its serious side effects are immune-mediated: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroid and adrenal damage, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, severe skin reactions and rare myocarditis. These arise because PD-1 also restrains T cells that react against healthy tissue. They are managed by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with hormone replacement for failed glands. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Zynyz (retifanlimab-dlwr) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"toripalimab": {
  "origin": "Toripalimab was developed by Shanghai Junshi Biosciences in China and first approved there in 2018. It is a humanized IgG4 anti-PD-1 antibody. Junshi partnered with Coherus BioSciences for the US market. In October 2023 the FDA approved it as Loqtorzi for nasopharyngeal carcinoma, a cancer of the upper throat behind the nose that is strongly linked to Epstein-Barr virus and is much more common in Southern China and Southeast Asia. It was the first drug FDA approved specifically for this cancer and one of the first PD-1 antibodies developed in China to reach the US market.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2018", "Toripalimab is approved in China."], ["2023", "FDA approves toripalimab (Loqtorzi) for nasopharyngeal carcinoma."]],
  "cell": "Toripalimab is a humanized IgG4 kappa antibody that binds PD-1 (CD279) on T cells and blocks its interaction with PD-L1 and PD-L2. PD-1 engagement normally recruits SHP-2, which dephosphorylates T-cell receptor and CD28 signaling proteins and shuts down T-cell activity. Blocking it restores proliferation, cytokine release and tumor cell killing. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma usually carries Epstein-Barr virus and is heavily infiltrated by immune cells, and it often expresses PD-L1, so releasing T cells against virus-infected tumor cells is effective. In combination with cisplatin and gemcitabine, chemotherapy kills tumor cells and releases antigens that T cells can then target.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Reactivated T cells attack EBV-infected nasopharyngeal cancer cells."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism is common, partly from immune thyroiditis and partly from prior neck radiation."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis and hepatitis can cause diarrhea and liver enzyme rises."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can cause cough and shortness of breath."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching reflect immune activity in normal skin."], ["Blood and gut (with chemotherapy)", "Nausea, vomiting and low blood counts come mostly from cisplatin and gemcitabine."]],
  "journey": "Toripalimab is given by IV infusion, so all of it enters the bloodstream. It distributes mostly in plasma and tissue fluid. Like other IgG antibodies, it is broken down by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body and is not handled by liver CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is measured in weeks, and PD-1 blockade persists for weeks between doses.",
  "sidefx": "The serious side effects are immune-mediated inflammation of normal organs, including pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, endocrine gland damage, nephritis, skin reactions and rare myocarditis. These follow from PD-1 blockade, which removes a brake that keeps self-reactive T cells quiet. Corticosteroids and holding the drug are the main treatments, with hormone replacement when a gland fails. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is so closely tied to Epstein-Barr virus that viral DNA in the blood is used to track the disease.",
  "src": ["Loqtorzi (toripalimab-tpzi) FDA label", "Mai HQ et al. JUPITER-02 trial, Nat Med 2021"]
},
"tislelizumab": {
  "origin": "Tislelizumab was developed by BeiGene, a company founded in Beijing in 2010 (later renamed BeOne Medicines). It is a humanized IgG4 anti-PD-1 antibody whose Fc region was specifically engineered to avoid binding Fc-gamma receptors on macrophages, a design meant to stop macrophages from eating the very T cells the antibody is trying to help. It was first approved in China in 2019. The FDA approved it as Tevimbra in March 2024 for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma after prior chemotherapy, and later for first-line esophageal and gastric cancers with chemotherapy.",
  "timeline": [["2010", "BeiGene is founded in Beijing."], ["2019", "Tislelizumab receives its first approval in China."], ["2024", "FDA approves tislelizumab (Tevimbra) for previously treated esophageal squamous cell carcinoma."]],
  "cell": "Tislelizumab binds PD-1 (CD279) on T cells with high affinity and blocks its binding to PD-L1 and PD-L2. That prevents SHP-2 from being recruited to the PD-1 tail, so T-cell receptor and CD28 signals stay on and T cells keep dividing, making cytokines and killing tumor cells. Studies of other anti-PD-1 antibodies suggested that macrophages carrying Fc-gamma receptors could grab antibody-coated T cells and remove them, which might blunt the effect. Tislelizumab's Fc was engineered to minimize this binding, limiting antibody-dependent phagocytosis of PD-1-positive T cells. The effect is immune and not cell-cycle specific.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Released T cells attack esophageal and gastric cancer cells, especially when PD-L1 is present."], ["Thyroid and other glands", "Hypothyroidism is common, and adrenal insufficiency, hypophysitis and type 1 diabetes can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can reflect immune hepatitis."], ["Gut", "Immune colitis causes diarrhea and abdominal pain."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can cause cough and breathlessness."], ["Blood and nerves (with chemotherapy)", "Low blood counts, nerve damage and diarrhea with combination regimens mostly come from the chemotherapy."]],
  "journey": "Tislelizumab is given by IV infusion, so it is fully available in the blood. It distributes mainly in the plasma and tissue fluid and is cleared slowly by protein catabolism in cells throughout the body. It is not a CYP substrate and is not filtered by the kidneys. The half-life is measured in weeks, so PD-1 blockade lasts across the dosing interval and beyond.",
  "sidefx": "The main dangers are immune-mediated: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroid and adrenal damage, type 1 diabetes, nephritis, severe skin reactions and myocarditis. They occur because the same PD-1 brake protects normal tissues from self-reactive T cells. Treatment is holding the drug and giving corticosteroids or other immunosuppressants, plus hormone replacement as needed. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Tevimbra (tislelizumab-jsgr) FDA label", "Shen L et al. RATIONALE-302 trial, J Clin Oncol 2022"]
},
"penpulimab": {
  "origin": "Penpulimab was developed by Akeso, a biotechnology company in Zhongshan, China. It is a humanized IgG1 anti-PD-1 antibody whose Fc region was engineered to remove Fc-gamma receptor binding, so it blocks PD-1 without triggering immune cells to attack the T cells it coats. It was first approved in China. In 2025 the FDA approved penpulimab-kcqx for recurrent or metastatic non-keratinizing nasopharyngeal carcinoma, both with platinum and gemcitabine and alone after prior therapy. Its US label uses its nonproprietary name.",
  "timeline": [["1992", "PD-1 is described by Tasuku Honjo's lab."], ["2021", "Penpulimab is first approved in China."], ["2025", "FDA approves penpulimab-kcqx for nasopharyngeal carcinoma."]],
  "cell": "Penpulimab binds PD-1 (CD279) on activated T cells and blocks it from engaging PD-L1 and PD-L2. When PD-1 is engaged, SHP-2 phosphatase is recruited and turns off T-cell receptor and CD28 signals, so the T cell stops dividing, makes less cytokine and kills less. Blocking PD-1 restores these functions against tumor cells. Although penpulimab is an IgG1, its Fc region was engineered so it does not bind Fc-gamma receptors, which prevents ADCC and antibody-dependent phagocytosis of the PD-1-positive T cells. Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is usually Epstein-Barr virus positive and rich in immune cells, so it responds to checkpoint blockade. It is not cell-cycle specific.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Reactivated T cells target EBV-associated nasopharyngeal cancer cells."], ["Thyroid", "Hypothyroidism is common, from immune thyroiditis often added to damage from prior neck radiation."], ["Gut and liver", "Immune colitis and hepatitis can cause diarrhea and liver enzyme rises."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can be serious and sometimes fatal."], ["Pancreas and adrenals", "Immune attack can cause type 1 diabetes with ketoacidosis or adrenal insufficiency."], ["Nerves and muscle", "Rare myasthenia gravis and myocarditis can occur and may occur together."]],
  "journey": "Penpulimab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the blood completely. It stays mostly in plasma and tissue fluid and is broken down into peptides and amino acids by protein catabolism throughout the body. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes and is not cleared by the kidneys. Its half-life is measured in weeks, and PD-1 blockade persists for weeks after each dose.",
  "sidefx": "Like other PD-1 blockers, penpulimab releases T cells against normal organs, causing pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, nephritis, hypophysitis, adrenal insufficiency, type 1 diabetes and rare myocarditis or myasthenia gravis. Severe skin reactions can occur. These are managed by holding the drug and giving corticosteroids, with hormone replacement for failed glands. Nausea and low blood counts in combination regimens come mostly from the chemotherapy. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Penpulimab-kcqx FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"cosibelimab": {
  "origin": "Cosibelimab is a fully human anti-PD-L1 antibody developed by Checkpoint Therapeutics, a US company that licensed it from the Dana-Farber Cancer Institute. Unlike several PD-L1 antibodies, it keeps a working IgG1 Fc region. The FDA approved it as Unloxcyt in December 2024 for metastatic or locally advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma when surgery or radiation cannot cure the disease. It was the first PD-L1 blocker approved for this sun-related skin cancer, which had previously been treated with PD-1 blockers.",
  "timeline": [["2000", "PD-L1 is shown to switch off T cells through PD-1."], ["2024", "FDA approves cosibelimab (Unloxcyt) for advanced cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma."]],
  "cell": "Cosibelimab is a human IgG1 antibody that binds PD-L1 (CD274) on tumor and immune cells. It blocks PD-L1 from engaging PD-1 and B7.1 (CD80). PD-L1 to PD-1 binding normally recruits SHP-2 to the T-cell side and turns down T-cell receptor and CD28 signals, so T cells stop attacking. Removing that signal reactivates tumor-specific cytotoxic T cells. Because cosibelimab keeps a functional Fc region, it can also flag PD-L1-positive tumor cells for antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity by NK cells. Skin squamous cell carcinomas carry very many UV-induced mutations, giving T cells many targets once they are released.",
  "body": [["Skin tumors", "Reactivated T cells, possibly helped by NK cells, shrink sun-damaged skin cancers."], ["Thyroid and other glands", "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and hypophysitis can follow immune attack on hormone-making cells."], ["Gut and liver", "Diarrhea from colitis and liver enzyme rises from hepatitis can occur."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis from immune inflammation of lung tissue can be serious or fatal."], ["Skin", "Rash reflects immune activity in normal skin."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain and fatigue are common."]],
  "journey": "Cosibelimab is given by IV infusion, so it enters the bloodstream fully. It distributes in plasma and tissue fluid and binds PD-L1 on cells, which can speed its removal when target levels are high. It is broken down by protein catabolism rather than by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Its half-life is measured in weeks, and ligand blockade persists between doses.",
  "sidefx": "Its serious side effects are immune-mediated: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, nephritis, endocrine gland damage, severe skin reactions, rare myocarditis and type 1 diabetes with ketoacidosis. Blocking PD-L1 lifts a brake that also protects healthy tissue from self-reactive T cells. Holding the drug and giving corticosteroids is the main treatment, with hormone replacement for lost gland function. Infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Unloxcyt (cosibelimab-ipdl) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"blinatumomab": {
  "origin": "Blinatumomab is the first approved bispecific T-cell engager, or BiTE. The idea came from German researchers, including Gert Riethmuller and Peter Kufer in Munich, who designed small proteins made of two antibody binding pieces joined by a short linker: one piece grabs a cancer cell and the other grabs a T cell. The Munich company Micromet developed the CD19-targeted version, and Amgen bought Micromet in 2012. In December 2014 the FDA granted accelerated approval to blinatumomab (Blincyto) for relapsed or refractory Philadelphia chromosome-negative B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia. It later gained approvals for measurable residual disease and for consolidation, and it opened the door to a whole family of T-cell engagers.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Researchers in Munich develop single-chain bispecific antibodies that link T cells to tumor cells."], ["2008", "Early trial of blinatumomab in lymphoma reports tumor regressions at very low concentrations."], ["2012", "Amgen acquires Micromet, the developer of blinatumomab."], ["2014", "FDA grants accelerated approval for relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."], ["2018", "FDA approves blinatumomab for ALL in remission with measurable residual disease."]],
  "cell": "Blinatumomab is a fusion of two single-chain variable fragments (scFv) joined by a flexible linker, about 54 kDa, with no Fc region. One arm binds CD19, present on almost all normal and malignant B cells. The other binds CD3 epsilon, part of the T-cell receptor complex. By bridging the two cells it forms an artificial immune synapse. This activates the T cell without needing antigen presentation on MHC or a matching T-cell receptor, so any nearby cytotoxic T cell can be recruited. The activated T cell releases perforin and granzymes into the B cell, triggering apoptosis, and then can move on to kill more targets (serial killing). T cells also release cytokines such as IL-6, IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha and multiply, which drives both the antileukemic effect and cytokine release syndrome.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic B blasts are killed, and minimal residual disease can be cleared. Normal B cells are also destroyed."], ["Immune system", "Loss of normal B cells lowers antibody levels (hypogammaglobulinemia), raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release from activated T cells causes fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen, called cytokine release syndrome."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity can cause tremor, confusion, trouble speaking, encephalopathy and seizures, thought to involve T-cell and cytokine effects on the brain and its blood vessels."], ["Pancreas", "Pancreatitis has been reported, sometimes severe."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid killing of many leukemia cells can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]],
  "journey": "Because it is small and has no Fc region to be recycled by FcRn, blinatumomab is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about 2 hours. It is therefore given as a continuous IV infusion through a portable pump for weeks at a time in each cycle. Steady state is reached within about a day. It is broken down by protein catabolism into small peptides and amino acids, with a small part filtered by the kidneys. Effects fade within hours after the infusion stops, which helps when toxicity needs to be controlled.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome happens because many T cells are activated at once and flood the blood with IL-6, IFN-gamma and other cytokines. Stepping up the dose and giving corticosteroid premedication lowers this risk. Severe CRS is treated by stopping the infusion, giving tocilizumab (an IL-6 receptor blocker) and corticosteroids. Neurologic toxicity is managed by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. B-cell loss causes low antibody levels and infections. Tumor lysis can follow rapid leukemia cell death.",
  "fun": "Blinatumomab works at extraordinarily low concentrations because each recruited T cell can kill target after target, which is why it is dosed in micrograms rather than the much larger amounts typical of antibodies.",
  "src": ["Blincyto (blinatumomab) FDA label", "Bargou R et al. Science 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"teclistamab": {
  "origin": "Teclistamab was developed by Janssen, the pharmaceutical company of Johnson & Johnson. It targets B-cell maturation antigen (BCMA), a protein found almost only on plasma cells and myeloma cells, which made it an ideal myeloma target. Unlike the small, short-lived BiTE format of blinatumomab, teclistamab is a full-size IgG4 bispecific antibody, so it lasts long enough to be given as an injection under the skin. In October 2022 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Tecvayli, making it the first BCMA-directed bispecific T-cell engager approved for relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma. Later it was approved with daratumumab for earlier relapse.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified as a receptor on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for teclistamab (Tecvayli), the first BCMA bispecific for myeloma."]],
  "cell": "Teclistamab is a humanized IgG4 bispecific antibody with one arm that binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells and myeloma cells and one arm that binds CD3 on T cells. Bridging the two forms an immune synapse that activates the T cell without needing MHC presentation. The T cell releases perforin and granzymes, killing the myeloma cell by apoptosis, and secretes cytokines such as IL-6, IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha while multiplying. Because normal plasma cells also carry BCMA, they are destroyed too, which lowers immunoglobulin levels. The IgG4 backbone and Fc changes reduce unwanted Fc effector activity, and the full-length format gives it a long half-life.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are killed by redirected T cells, lowering M-protein and marrow plasma cells."], ["Immune system", "Normal plasma cells are depleted, so antibody levels fall and serious bacterial, viral and opportunistic infections become common."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen is most common after early step-up doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS (immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity) can cause confusion, word-finding problems and rarely seizures."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common, partly from marrow inflammation and disease."], ["Skin", "Injection-site redness and swelling come from local immune activation."]],
  "journey": "Teclistamab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph into the blood, reaching peak levels several days after a dose. As a full IgG antibody it is protected by FcRn recycling, giving a half-life of days to weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Treatment starts with step-up doses to lower the cytokine surge, and later dosing can be spaced out once a response is established.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from sudden mass activation of T cells, which release IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids, and premedication and step-up dosing reduce it. Neurotoxicity (ICANS) is managed with corticosteroids. Because BCMA is also on healthy plasma cells, antibody levels fall and infections, including pneumonia and PML, are a leading cause of harm, so immunoglobulin replacement and infection prevention are used. Low blood counts add to infection risk.",
  "src": ["Tecvayli (teclistamab-cqyv) FDA label", "Moreau P et al. MajesTEC-1, N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"talquetamab": {
  "origin": "Talquetamab was developed by Janssen (Johnson & Johnson) to give myeloma a second T-cell target besides BCMA. It is aimed at GPRC5D, an orphan G protein-coupled receptor that is highly expressed on myeloma cells but, outside of them, mainly in hard keratinized tissues such as hair follicles, nails and the tongue. In August 2023 the FDA granted accelerated approval to talquetamab (Talvey), the first GPRC5D-directed therapy, for multiple myeloma after at least four prior lines of therapy. Because its target differs, it can work in people whose myeloma has escaped BCMA-directed treatment.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "GPRC5D is identified as a protein highly expressed on myeloma cells."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for talquetamab (Talvey), the first GPRC5D-directed therapy."]],
  "cell": "Talquetamab is a humanized IgG4 bispecific antibody. One arm binds GPRC5D on myeloma cells and the other binds CD3 on T cells. Linking the two builds an immune synapse that activates the T cell independent of MHC, leading to perforin and granzyme release and apoptosis of the myeloma cell, plus cytokine release and T-cell expansion. GPRC5D is also present in keratinized tissues, including the papillae of the tongue, nail beds and the skin, so T cells are also directed at these normal cells. That on-target, off-tumor effect explains its unusual taste, nail and skin side effects.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells carrying GPRC5D are killed, including in disease resistant to BCMA therapy."], ["Mouth and tongue", "Taste changes, dry mouth and trouble swallowing are very common because GPRC5D is present in tongue papillae."], ["Nails and skin", "Nail thinning or loss, skin peeling and rash come from T-cell attack on keratinized tissue."], ["Weight and nutrition", "Taste loss and swallowing problems can lead to marked weight loss."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever and low blood pressure follows early step-up doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion and, rarely, seizures."]],
  "journey": "Talquetamab is injected under the skin and absorbed slowly through the lymph into the blood over several days. As a full IgG antibody it is recycled by FcRn and lasts days to weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism rather than by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Treatment begins with step-up doses before full dosing to limit cytokine release, and dosing can be weekly or every 2 weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome results from mass T-cell activation and IL-6 release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is managed with corticosteroids. The taste, mouth, nail and skin problems are on-target effects: T cells are drawn to GPRC5D in normal keratinized cells. Infections arise from immune disruption, low blood counts and loss of normal plasma cells, though infection rates have been somewhat lower than with BCMA-directed bispecifics.",
  "fun": "Talquetamab's taste and nail side effects are a textbook example of 'on-target, off-tumor' toxicity, where the drug hits the right protein in the wrong tissue.",
  "src": ["Talvey (talquetamab-tgvs) FDA label", "Chari A et al. MonumenTAL-1, N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"elranatamab": {
  "origin": "Elranatamab was developed by Pfizer as a BCMA-directed bispecific T-cell engager for multiple myeloma. It is built on a humanized IgG2 backbone and is given as a fixed dose under the skin. In August 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Elrexfio for multiple myeloma that had relapsed after at least four prior lines of therapy, including a proteasome inhibitor, an immunomodulatory drug and an anti-CD38 antibody. It became the second BCMA bispecific available in the US, after teclistamab.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for elranatamab (Elrexfio)."]],
  "cell": "Elranatamab is a humanized IgG2 bispecific antibody. One arm binds BCMA (TNFRSF17), which is expressed on plasma cells and myeloma cells and supports their survival. The other arm binds CD3 on T cells. Linking them creates an immune synapse that activates the T cell regardless of its natural specificity and without MHC involvement. The T cell releases perforin and granzymes to kill the myeloma cell, then secretes cytokines and multiplies. Normal plasma cells carrying BCMA are destroyed too, so immunoglobulin production falls. The IgG2 format with Fc changes limits Fc-mediated effects, while the full-size structure gives it a long half-life.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are destroyed, lowering paraprotein levels."], ["Immune system", "Loss of normal plasma cells causes low antibody levels and a high rate of serious infections such as pneumonia and sepsis."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen is most common after the first step-up doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, sleepiness and trouble speaking."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common."], ["Skin", "Injection-site reactions reflect local immune activation."]],
  "journey": "Elranatamab is injected under the skin and reaches the blood slowly, with peak levels several days after a dose. FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not CYP metabolism or kidney filtration. Step-up dosing in the first week lowers the cytokine surge, after which it is given on a regular schedule that can be spaced out in responders.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome follows mass activation of T cells and IL-6 release, and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. Because BCMA is on healthy plasma cells, antibody levels drop and infections, including opportunistic ones and PML, are common and can be fatal. Immunoglobulin replacement and infection prevention are part of care. Low blood counts add to the risk.",
  "src": ["Elrexfio (elranatamab-bcmm) FDA label", "Lesokhin AM et al. MagnetisMM-3, Nat Med 2023"]
},
"linvoseltamab": {
  "origin": "Linvoseltamab was developed by Regeneron Pharmaceuticals using its VelocImmune mice, which make fully human antibodies, and its platform for building bispecific antibodies. It targets BCMA on myeloma cells and CD3 on T cells. Unlike the other BCMA bispecifics in the US, it is given by IV infusion. Its first FDA review in 2024 was delayed because of issues at a third-party manufacturing site, not because of its clinical data. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Lynozyfic in 2025 for relapsed or refractory multiple myeloma after at least four prior lines of therapy.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2024", "Initial FDA review is delayed by findings at a third-party manufacturing site."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for linvoseltamab (Lynozyfic)."]],
  "cell": "Linvoseltamab is a fully human IgG4 bispecific antibody. One arm binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells and myeloma cells, and the other binds CD3 on T cells. By holding the two cells together it forms an immune synapse that activates T cells without MHC involvement. Activated T cells release perforin and granzymes, killing the myeloma cell, and secrete cytokines while expanding. Normal plasma cells are also killed because they express BCMA, so immunoglobulin production falls. The IgG4 format with Fc changes limits Fc effector functions.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are killed by redirected T cells."], ["Immune system", "Normal plasma cell loss lowers antibody levels, and infections such as pneumonia are a major risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome usually appears within about a day of a step-up dose."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, sleepiness and trouble speaking."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common."], ["Muscles and lungs", "Musculoskeletal pain, cough and upper respiratory infections are common."]],
  "journey": "Linvoseltamab is given by IV infusion, so it is fully in the blood at the end of the infusion. As a full IgG antibody it is recycled by FcRn and has a half-life of weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Step-up doses are given first, often with hospital monitoring, and then dosing becomes less frequent once a good response is reached.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by rapid activation of many T cells and IL-6 release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. Loss of normal plasma cells and low blood counts make infections common and sometimes fatal, so immunoglobulin replacement and preventive antimicrobials are often used. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Lynozyfic (linvoseltamab-gcpt) FDA label", "Bumma N et al. LINKER-MM1, J Clin Oncol 2024"]
},
"epcoritamab": {
  "origin": "Epcoritamab was created by the Danish biotechnology company Genmab using its DuoBody platform, which pairs two different antibody halves into one stable bispecific IgG1. It was co-developed with AbbVie. In May 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Epkinly for relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy. It was the first bispecific T-cell engager for lymphoma given as an injection under the skin. Approvals for follicular lymphoma followed, alone and with lenalidomide and rituximab.",
  "timeline": [["1999", "Genmab is founded in Copenhagen."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for epcoritamab (Epkinly) in DLBCL."], ["2024", "FDA approves epcoritamab for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Epcoritamab is a humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody with a silenced Fc region. One arm binds CD20 (MS4A1) on normal and malignant B cells. The other binds CD3 on T cells. The bridge forms a cytolytic synapse that activates T cells independent of MHC. The T cells release perforin and granzymes, killing CD20-positive lymphoma cells, and secrete cytokines while multiplying. Its CD20 binding site differs from that of rituximab, so it can be combined with rituximab. Silencing the Fc prevents the antibody from triggering ADCC or complement against the T cells it binds.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "Lymphoma cells are killed, and responses can be deep and lasting in some patients."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are depleted, lowering antibodies and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome most often follows the first full dose in cycle 1."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion and aphasia, and rarely seizures."], ["Skin", "Injection-site reactions come from local immune activity."], ["Metabolism", "Rapid killing of bulky disease can cause tumor lysis syndrome."]],
  "journey": "Epcoritamab is injected under the skin. It is absorbed slowly through the lymphatic system, which brings it straight to the lymph nodes where much of the lymphoma lives, and peaks in the blood several days later. FcRn recycling gives it a half-life of weeks. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not by liver enzymes or the kidneys. Step-up dosing in the first cycle lowers the cytokine surge.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from mass T-cell activation and IL-6 release, is lowered by step-up dosing and corticosteroid premedication, and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell depletion and low blood counts lead to infections, including PML. Tumor lysis can follow rapid lymphoma cell death. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Epkinly (epcoritamab-bysp) FDA label", "Thieblemont C et al. EPCORE NHL-1, J Clin Oncol 2023"]
},
"glofitamab": {
  "origin": "Glofitamab was developed by Roche and its US company Genentech. Its design is unusual: a 2:1 format with two arms that bind CD20 and one arm that binds CD3, which gives it strong grip on lymphoma cells. To lower the risk of a dangerous cytokine surge, each course begins with one dose of obinutuzumab, an anti-CD20 antibody, to clear B cells from the blood before glofitamab is given. In June 2023 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Columvi for relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy. It is given for a fixed number of cycles rather than indefinitely.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "Roche develops a 2:1 CD20 by CD3 bispecific antibody format."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval for glofitamab (Columvi) in large B-cell lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "Glofitamab is a humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody with two CD20-binding regions and one CD3 epsilon-binding region, plus a silenced Fc. Binding CD20 with two arms gives high avidity, so it holds onto B cells tightly even when CD20 levels are low. The CD3 arm then engages a T cell, forming an immune synapse that activates the T cell independent of MHC. The T cell releases perforin and granzymes and kills the CD20-positive cell, while releasing cytokines and expanding. Obinutuzumab pretreatment depletes circulating and some tissue B cells first, which lowers the number of targets that trigger T cells all at once and reduces cytokine release syndrome.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "Lymphoma cells are killed, and responses can be complete and lasting after a fixed course."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are depleted for months, lowering antibodies and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome usually begins within the first day after the first step-up dose."], ["Tumor sites", "Tumor flare can cause pain and swelling where lymphoma sits as T cells pour in."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, and rarely seizures."], ["Blood counts and minerals", "Lymphopenia, neutropenia and low phosphate are common."]],
  "journey": "Glofitamab is given by IV infusion, starting with step-up doses after a single obinutuzumab pretreatment. It is fully available in the blood and spreads into lymphoid tissue. As an IgG it is recycled by FcRn, with a terminal half-life of about a week. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not CYP enzymes or the kidneys. The course is fixed at 12 cycles, but B-cell depletion persists for months after.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by mass T-cell activation and is reduced by obinutuzumab pretreatment, step-up dosing and corticosteroid premedication. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is managed with corticosteroids. Tumor flare reflects immune cells flooding tumor sites. B-cell loss and neutropenia lead to serious infections. Low phosphate likely reflects cell shifts during immune activation.",
  "fun": "The 2:1 design means each glofitamab molecule can grip two CD20 proteins at once, which boosts its hold on lymphoma cells.",
  "src": ["Columvi (glofitamab-gxbm) FDA label", "Dickinson MJ et al. N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"mosunetuzumab": {
  "origin": "Mosunetuzumab was developed by Genentech, part of Roche. It is a full-length humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody that binds CD20 on B cells and CD3 on T cells. In December 2022 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Lunsumio for relapsed or refractory follicular lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy, making it the first CD20 by CD3 bispecific T-cell engager approved in the US. It is given for a fixed number of cycles, a time-limited approach that allows treatment-free intervals. A subcutaneous form, Lunsumio Velo, was later approved.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "Genentech develops a full-length CD20 by CD3 bispecific antibody."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for mosunetuzumab (Lunsumio), the first CD20 by CD3 bispecific in the US."]],
  "cell": "Mosunetuzumab binds CD20 (MS4A1) on normal and malignant B cells with one arm and CD3 epsilon on T cells with the other. This pulls the cells together into a cytolytic synapse that activates T cells independent of MHC. The T cells release perforin and granzymes, killing the CD20-positive B cells, and release cytokines as they expand. The Fc region is modified to reduce Fc-gamma receptor binding, which limits nonspecific immune activation and protects T cells from being killed. Follicular lymphoma cells reliably carry CD20, making them a good target.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "Follicular lymphoma cells are killed, and many patients reach complete remission after a fixed course."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are depleted for months, lowering antibodies and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is most common after the larger doses early in cycle 1."], ["Tumor sites", "Tumor flare can cause pain and swelling at lymphoma sites."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching can occur from immune activation."], ["Blood counts and minerals", "Lymphopenia, neutropenia and low phosphate are common."]],
  "journey": "Mosunetuzumab is given by IV infusion with step-up doses in the first cycle, or by injection under the skin in its newer form. Once in the blood it distributes into lymphoid tissue. As a full IgG it is recycled by FcRn, giving a terminal half-life of about 16 days. It is broken down by protein catabolism rather than CYP metabolism or kidney clearance. The course lasts 8 cycles for complete responders or up to 17 cycles otherwise.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by T-cell activation releasing IL-6 and other cytokines. Step-up dosing and premedication reduce it, and it is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell depletion and neutropenia cause serious infections. Tumor flare comes from immune cells entering tumor sites. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Lunsumio (mosunetuzumab-axgb) FDA label", "Budde LE et al. Lancet Oncol 2022"]
},
"tarlatamab": {
  "origin": "Tarlatamab was developed by Amgen using its BiTE platform, the same technology behind blinatumomab, but with an added Fc piece that extends its half-life. It targets DLL3 (delta-like ligand 3), a protein that is rarely on the surface of normal cells but is found on most small cell lung cancers. An earlier DLL3-directed antibody-drug conjugate from another company had failed in trials, so DLL3 had looked like a hard target. In May 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to tarlatamab (Imdelltra) for extensive-stage small cell lung cancer that progressed after platinum chemotherapy. It was the first T-cell engager approved for a common solid tumor.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "DLL3 is identified as a surface target on small cell lung cancer."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for tarlatamab (Imdelltra), the first DLL3-directed T-cell engager."]],
  "cell": "Tarlatamab is a half-life extended BiTE. It contains two single-chain variable fragments, one binding DLL3 and one binding CD3 epsilon, fused to a single-chain Fc that slows its clearance. DLL3 normally inhibits Notch signaling inside cells during development and is mostly kept inside the cell, but small cell lung cancer and other neuroendocrine tumors display it on their surface. When tarlatamab links a DLL3-positive tumor cell to a T cell, the T cell is activated without MHC involvement and releases perforin and granzymes, killing the tumor cell. Activated T cells also release cytokines and multiply.",
  "body": [["Lungs and tumor sites", "Small cell lung cancer cells displaying DLL3 are killed by redirected T cells."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen occurs mostly after the first two doses."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, tremor, trouble speaking and rarely seizures."], ["Mouth and appetite", "Taste changes and loss of appetite are common."], ["Blood and salts", "Anemia, lymphopenia and low sodium are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur during treatment and are monitored."]],
  "journey": "Tarlatamab is given by IV infusion. The Fc extension lets it last days rather than hours, so it can be given every 2 weeks instead of by continuous infusion like blinatumomab. It is broken down by protein catabolism and is not cleared by CYP enzymes or the kidneys. Because CRS and neurotoxicity are most likely early, the first doses are given with a period of monitoring and IV fluids.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome happens when many T cells are activated at once and release IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids, and early doses are given with monitoring. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. Serious infections and neutropenia can occur. Low sodium and taste changes are common. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "DLL3 is mostly hidden inside normal cells, which is why a T-cell engager aimed at it can spare most healthy tissue.",
  "src": ["Imdelltra (tarlatamab-dlle) FDA label", "Ahn MJ et al. DeLLphi-301, N Engl J Med 2023"]
},
"zanidatamab": {
  "origin": "Zanidatamab was created by Zymeworks, a Canadian biotechnology company, using its Azymetric platform for building bispecific antibodies. Instead of linking a cancer cell to a T cell, it binds two different sites on the same target, HER2. One arm binds the site used by trastuzumab and the other binds the site used by pertuzumab. Zymeworks licensed it to Jazz Pharmaceuticals. In November 2024 the FDA granted it accelerated approval as Ziihera for previously treated HER2-positive biliary tract cancer. It was later approved with chemotherapy for HER2-positive gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Trastuzumab is approved as the first HER2-targeted antibody."], ["2022", "Jazz Pharmaceuticals licenses zanidatamab from Zymeworks."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for zanidatamab (Ziihera) in HER2-positive biliary tract cancer."]],
  "cell": "HER2 (ERBB2) is a receptor tyrosine kinase. When it is overexpressed, it pairs with itself or with HER3 and other family members, driving PI3K/AKT and MAPK growth signals. Zanidatamab is a humanized IgG1 biparatopic antibody: one arm binds extracellular domain 4 (the trastuzumab epitope) and the other binds domain 2 (the pertuzumab epitope, the dimerization arm). Because the two arms can bind different HER2 molecules at once, the antibodies cross-link receptors into large clusters on the cell surface. These clusters are pulled into the cell and degraded, lowering surface HER2 and shutting down its signaling. The Fc region also triggers complement-dependent cytotoxicity and ADCC.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "HER2-driven biliary and gastroesophageal cancer cells lose their growth signal and are tagged for immune killing."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect and can be severe, likely from HER-family blockade in the intestinal lining."], ["Heart", "Heart muscle uses HER2 signaling to cope with stress, so blocking it can lower ejection fraction."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions with fever and chills can occur during or after infusions."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common during treatment and are monitored."], ["Pregnancy", "HER2 blockade can harm fetal kidney development and lower amniotic fluid."]],
  "journey": "Zanidatamab is given by IV infusion every 2 or 3 weeks. It stays mainly in the blood and tissue fluid, binds HER2 on tumor cells and is taken into cells. Like other IgG antibodies it is recycled by FcRn and broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. CYP enzymes and the kidneys do not clear it. Its half-life is long, measured in days to weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea comes from HER-family signaling loss in the gut lining and can lead to dehydration and kidney injury, so it is treated early with fluids and antidiarrheals. Lower heart function reflects loss of HER2 survival signals in heart muscle cells, so heart function is checked before and during treatment. Infusion reactions come from the protein and its Fc activity. Embryo-fetal toxicity occurs because HER2 is needed for fetal development. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Ziihera (zanidatamab-hrii) FDA label", "Harding JJ et al. HERIZON-BTC-01, Lancet Oncol 2023"]
},
"zenocutuzumab": {
  "origin": "Zenocutuzumab was developed by Merus, a Dutch company whose Biclonics platform makes full-length bispecific antibodies. It was built to treat cancers driven by NRG1 gene fusions, rare changes where part of the neuregulin 1 gene fuses to another gene and produces a protein that constantly switches on HER3. These fusions occur in a small fraction of lung, pancreatic and other cancers and had no targeted treatment. In December 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to zenocutuzumab (Bizengri) for NRG1 fusion-positive non-small cell lung cancer and pancreatic adenocarcinoma, the first therapy approved for this genetic driver.",
  "timeline": [["2014", "NRG1 gene fusions are described as cancer drivers in lung adenocarcinoma."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for zenocutuzumab (Bizengri), the first therapy for NRG1 fusion cancers."]],
  "cell": "NRG1 fusion proteins carry the EGF-like domain of neuregulin 1, the natural ligand of HER3 (ERBB3). When the fusion ligand binds HER3, HER3 pairs with HER2, and the HER2-HER3 dimer is one of the strongest activators of PI3K/AKT growth signaling. Zenocutuzumab is a humanized IgG1 bispecific antibody. Its HER2 arm docks on HER2, which positions its HER3 arm to block the ligand-binding site on HER3. This 'dock and block' mechanism stops NRG1 fusion proteins from activating HER3 and prevents HER2-HER3 pairing, shutting down PI3K/AKT and MAPK signals. The low-fucose Fc also enhances ADCC by NK cells.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "NRG1 fusion-driven lung and pancreatic cancer cells lose their main growth signal."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea reflect HER-family blockade in the gut lining."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur, especially with the first dose."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and can be fatal."], ["Heart", "Blocking HER2 signals can reduce heart muscle function."], ["Minerals", "Low magnesium is common. HER-family signaling helps kidney tubules reabsorb magnesium, which may explain this effect."]],
  "journey": "Zenocutuzumab is given by IV infusion every 2 weeks, so all of it reaches the blood. It distributes into plasma and tissue fluid and binds HER2 and HER3 on cells. It is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids, not by liver enzymes or kidney filtration. Its half-life is measured in days.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions come from the protein and its enhanced Fc activity and are reduced with premedication. Diarrhea and low magnesium reflect HER-family signaling loss in the gut lining and kidney. Reduced heart function reflects HER2 blockade in heart muscle. Pneumonitis is a serious immune-related lung injury. Embryo-fetal harm is expected from HER2 and HER3 blockade. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Bizengri (zenocutuzumab-zbco) FDA label", "Schram AM et al. N Engl J Med 2025"]
},
"tebentafusp": {
  "origin": "Tebentafusp was developed by Immunocore, a company spun out of the University of Oxford, using its ImmTAC platform (immune-mobilizing monoclonal T-cell receptors against cancer). Instead of an antibody, its targeting end is an engineered soluble T-cell receptor, which can see protein fragments from inside cells as they are displayed on HLA molecules. In January 2022 the FDA approved tebentafusp (Kimmtrak) for HLA-A*02:01-positive adults with unresectable or metastatic uveal melanoma, an eye cancer with few options. It was the first approved T-cell receptor therapeutic, the first drug approved for this cancer, and the first bispecific T-cell engager to improve overall survival in a solid tumor.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Immunocore develops soluble T-cell receptor drugs from Oxford research."], ["2021", "A randomized trial shows tebentafusp improves overall survival in metastatic uveal melanoma."], ["2022", "FDA approves tebentafusp (Kimmtrak), the first T-cell receptor therapeutic."]],
  "cell": "Cells chop internal proteins into peptides and display them on HLA class I molecules. Melanocytes and melanoma cells display a peptide from gp100, a melanosome protein. Tebentafusp has two parts. One is a soluble T-cell receptor engineered to bind the gp100 peptide-HLA-A*02:01 complex with very high affinity, far tighter than a natural T-cell receptor. The other is an anti-CD3 single-chain variable fragment. When the TCR end binds a melanoma cell, the anti-CD3 end recruits any nearby T cell, activating it to release cytokines and perforin and granzymes that kill the cell. Because recognition depends on HLA-A*02:01, it only works in people with that tissue type.",
  "body": [["Liver (main metastatic site)", "Uveal melanoma spreads mainly to the liver, where redirected T cells attack tumor cells."], ["Skin", "Rash, itching and dry skin are very common because normal skin melanocytes also show gp100, and T cells attack them."], ["Hair and skin color", "Loss of pigment in skin and hair can occur from melanocyte destruction."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, chills and low blood pressure is common within hours of the first three infusions."], ["Liver enzymes", "Liver enzyme rises can occur, especially with liver metastases."], ["Eyes", "Melanocytes in the eye can be affected, though serious eye problems are uncommon."]],
  "journey": "Tebentafusp is given by IV infusion once a week. It is a relatively small protein without a full antibody Fc, so it is cleared quickly, with a half-life of roughly 6 to 8 hours. It is broken down by protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids. Because cytokine release syndrome is most likely with the first three doses, these are given in a hospital or with overnight monitoring.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome occurs because the drug activates many T cells at once, releasing IL-6 and other cytokines, and is treated with fluids, corticosteroids and, if needed, tocilizumab. Skin rash, itching and depigmentation happen because normal melanocytes also display the gp100 peptide, an on-target effect. Liver enzyme rises reflect inflammation around liver metastases. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tebentafusp can only be used if a blood test shows the HLA-A*02:01 tissue type, which is present in roughly half of people of European descent.",
  "src": ["Kimmtrak (tebentafusp-tebn) FDA label", "Nathan P et al. N Engl J Med 2021"]
},
"ado-trastuzumab-emtansine": {
  "origin": "T-DM1 joins two stories. The first is trastuzumab, the HER2 antibody developed at Genentech and approved in 1998. The second is maytansine, a very potent cell poison isolated in the early 1970s by S. Morris Kupchan and colleagues from an East African shrub of the genus Maytenus. Maytansine was too toxic to use as ordinary chemotherapy. ImmunoGen, a Massachusetts company, developed derivatives called maytansinoids, including DM1, and a way to attach them to antibodies. Genentech linked DM1 to trastuzumab through a stable linker, so the poison would be released mainly inside HER2-positive cancer cells. The FDA approved it as Kadcyla in February 2013 for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer, the first ADC approved for a solid tumor, and in 2019 for early breast cancer with disease left after presurgical therapy.",
  "timeline": [["1972", "Kupchan and colleagues report maytansine from a Maytenus shrub."], ["1998", "FDA approves trastuzumab for HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2013", "FDA approves ado-trastuzumab emtansine (Kadcyla) for metastatic HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2019", "FDA approves T-DM1 for residual early breast cancer after the KATHERINE trial."]],
  "cell": "The trastuzumab part, a humanized IgG1, binds subdomain IV of the HER2 receptor on tumor cells. The whole complex is then pulled into the cell by endocytosis and sent to lysosomes. Because the MCC linker is a stable thioether that cannot be cut by enzymes, the payload is freed only when the antibody itself is digested, releasing lysine-MCC-DM1. DM1 binds tubulin at the vinca region and stops microtubules from assembling. Without a working mitotic spindle, the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. The charged lysine-MCC-DM1 does not easily cross membranes, so there is little bystander killing of nearby cells. The antibody also keeps its own actions: blocking HER2 signaling and recruiting NK cells for ADCC. On average about 3.5 DM1 molecules are attached to each antibody.",
  "body": [["Breast tumor", "HER2-positive cancer cells take up the conjugate and die during cell division."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common, and rare severe liver injury and nodular regenerative hyperplasia can occur, likely from DM1 effects on liver cells."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is a hallmark effect, thought to come from DM1 damaging megakaryocytes, the platelet-making cells."], ["Heart", "Blocking HER2 can reduce left ventricular function, because heart cells use HER2 for stress survival."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy can occur because microtubules are needed for transport along long nerve fibers."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis is uncommon but can be fatal."]],
  "journey": "T-DM1 is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate stays mostly in the blood and tissue fluid and has a half-life of about 4 days. It is taken up by HER2-positive cells and digested in lysosomes, releasing DM1-containing catabolites. Any free DM1 is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 and CYP3A5 and leaves the body mainly in bile and feces. Free DM1 in the blood is highly protein bound. The antibody portion is broken down to peptides and amino acids like other proteins.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from the payload. DM1 stops microtubules, which explains low platelets, nerve damage and liver injury in cells that are exposed to the drug. Bleeding risk rises when platelets are low. Heart dysfunction comes from the trastuzumab part blocking HER2 in heart muscle. Embryo-fetal harm comes from both parts. T-DM1 must not be substituted for trastuzumab or for trastuzumab deruxtecan because the names are similar and the drugs are different. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Maytansine was once tested as a stand-alone cancer drug but was too toxic. Attaching it to an antibody turned a failed chemotherapy into a targeted one.",
  "src": ["Kadcyla (ado-trastuzumab emtansine) FDA label", "Kupchan SM et al. J Am Chem Soc 1972", "Verma S et al. EMILIA trial, N Engl J Med 2012"]
},
"trastuzumab-deruxtecan": {
  "origin": "Trastuzumab deruxtecan was developed by the Japanese company Daiichi Sankyo, later in partnership with AstraZeneca. Daiichi Sankyo designed a new payload, DXd, from exatecan, a derivative of camptothecin, the topoisomerase I poison first isolated in 1966 by Monroe Wall and Mansukh Wani from the Chinese 'happy tree', Camptotheca acuminata. They attached about eight DXd molecules to each trastuzumab antibody with a cleavable linker. The FDA first approved it as Enhertu in December 2019 for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer. In 2022 the DESTINY-Breast04 trial showed it worked in 'HER2-low' breast cancer, creating a new treatment category for tumors once thought to be HER2-negative. Approvals followed in HER2-mutant lung cancer, gastric cancer, HER2-ultralow breast cancer and HER2-positive solid tumors of any origin.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Wall and Wani isolate camptothecin from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["2019", "FDA approves trastuzumab deruxtecan (Enhertu) for HER2-positive metastatic breast cancer."], ["2022", "FDA approves T-DXd for HER2-low breast cancer and HER2-mutant NSCLC."], ["2024", "FDA grants a tumor-agnostic approval for HER2-positive (IHC 3+) solid tumors."]],
  "cell": "The trastuzumab IgG1 binds HER2 on tumor cells and the conjugate is taken into the cell and moved to lysosomes. There, cathepsin enzymes cut the tetrapeptide (GGFG) linker, which is designed to be stable in blood, releasing DXd. DXd blocks topoisomerase I, the enzyme that nicks one DNA strand to relieve twisting during replication. DXd traps the enzyme on DNA, so when a replication fork runs into the trapped complex during S phase, it becomes a double-strand break and the cell dies by apoptosis. DXd crosses membranes easily, so after release it can diffuse into neighboring cancer cells and kill them even if they have little or no HER2. This bystander effect, together with a high drug-to-antibody ratio of about 8, helps explain its activity in HER2-low tumors.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "HER2-positive and HER2-low cancer cells and their neighbors are killed through DNA damage."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis is the most important serious toxicity and can be fatal; the cause is not fully understood."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia and anemia come from DXd damaging rapidly dividing blood-forming cells."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common, and the drug is considered moderately to highly emetogenic, along with diarrhea or constipation."], ["Hair", "Hair loss reflects damage to dividing hair follicle cells."], ["Heart", "Reduced left ventricular function can occur from HER2 blockade."]],
  "journey": "T-DXd is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The intact conjugate circulates with a half-life of about 6 to 7 days. After uptake into HER2-expressing cells, DXd is released in lysosomes. Free DXd in the blood is short-lived and highly protein bound, about 97 percent. DXd is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 in the liver and leaves mostly through bile into feces. The antibody portion is broken down into peptides and amino acids.",
  "sidefx": "Topoisomerase I poisoning by DXd damages DNA in any dividing cell it reaches, explaining neutropenia, anemia, nausea, hair loss and gut effects. Interstitial lung disease is a boxed warning; the exact cause is unclear, but it may involve uptake of the conjugate by lung immune cells. Suspected ILD is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. Heart dysfunction comes from HER2 blockade. It must not be substituted for trastuzumab or ado-trastuzumab emtansine. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The DESTINY-Breast04 results drew a standing ovation at the 2022 ASCO meeting because they changed the care of about half of all breast cancers, those with low HER2 levels.",
  "src": ["Enhertu (fam-trastuzumab deruxtecan-nxki) FDA label", "Modi S et al. DESTINY-Breast04, N Engl J Med 2022", "Wall ME, Wani MC et al. J Am Chem Soc 1966"]
},
"sacituzumab-govitecan": {
  "origin": "Sacituzumab govitecan was developed by Immunomedics, a New Jersey company founded by David Goldenberg, a pioneer of antibody-targeted cancer therapy. Its antibody targets Trop-2, a surface protein found on many epithelial cancers, and its payload is SN-38, the active form of the chemotherapy irinotecan. SN-38 traces back to camptothecin, isolated in 1966 from the Chinese 'happy tree', Camptotheca acuminata. SN-38 is too poorly soluble and too toxic to give alone, but attached to an antibody it could be delivered to tumors. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Trodelvy in April 2020 for metastatic triple-negative breast cancer, a hard-to-treat subtype, and Gilead Sciences bought Immunomedics later that year. It was later approved for HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["1996", "FDA approves irinotecan, whose active metabolite is SN-38."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval for sacituzumab govitecan (Trodelvy) in triple-negative breast cancer."], ["2020", "Gilead Sciences acquires Immunomedics."], ["2023", "FDA approves it for pretreated HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody (hRS7) binds Trop-2 (TACSTD2), a transmembrane protein overexpressed on many carcinomas. About 7 to 8 SN-38 molecules are attached through the CL2A linker, which is sensitive to acid and hydrolysis. Some SN-38 is released inside the tumor cell after the conjugate is internalized, and some is released in the acidic tumor environment, where it can enter nearby cells (bystander effect). SN-38 binds the topoisomerase I-DNA complex and stops the enzyme from resealing the DNA nick. When a replication fork collides with this trapped complex during S phase, a double-strand break forms and the cell undergoes apoptosis.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Trop-2-expressing breast cancer cells and nearby cancer cells are killed by DNA damage."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common and can be severe, because SN-38 damages dividing white cell precursors."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is common and can be severe, as SN-38 injures the rapidly dividing lining of the intestine, the same toxicity seen with irinotecan."], ["Stomach and appetite", "Nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite are common."], ["Hair", "Hair loss comes from injury to dividing follicle cells."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity and infusion reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur."]],
  "journey": "Sacituzumab govitecan is given by IV infusion on days 1 and 8 of a 3-week cycle. Unlike many ADCs, it has a short half-life, about 16 hours for the conjugate, because the linker releases SN-38 steadily. Free SN-38 is very highly bound to albumin. The liver inactivates SN-38 by glucuronidation through UGT1A1 to SN-38 glucuronide, which leaves mainly through bile into the gut. People with reduced UGT1A1 activity, such as those homozygous for the UGT1A1*28 allele, clear SN-38 more slowly and have more neutropenia.",
  "sidefx": "Both boxed warnings, neutropenia and diarrhea, come from SN-38 damaging rapidly dividing cells in the marrow and gut lining. In the gut, bacteria can also cut the glucuronide back into active SN-38, which adds to intestinal injury. Late diarrhea is treated with loperamide and fluids, and early cholinergic-type diarrhea may be treated with atropine. Growth factors and dose delays are used for neutropenia. People with reduced UGT1A1 function are at higher risk. Infusion reactions come from the protein. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Trop-2 got its name as a 'trophoblast antigen' because it was first found on trophoblast cells of the placenta, which, like cancer cells, invade surrounding tissue.",
  "src": ["Trodelvy (sacituzumab govitecan-hziy) FDA label", "Bardia A et al. ASCENT trial, N Engl J Med 2021"]
},
"datopotamab-deruxtecan": {
  "origin": "Datopotamab deruxtecan comes from Daiichi Sankyo, developed with AstraZeneca. It uses the same DXd payload and cleavable linker technology as trastuzumab deruxtecan, but its antibody targets Trop-2, the same surface protein targeted by sacituzumab govitecan. Daiichi Sankyo chose a lower drug-to-antibody ratio of about 4 to balance potency and tolerability. The FDA approved it as Datroway in January 2025 for previously treated HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer, and in 2025 it received accelerated approval for EGFR-mutated non-small cell lung cancer after EGFR-targeted therapy and platinum chemotherapy.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Camptothecin, ancestor of the DXd payload, is isolated from Camptotheca acuminata."], ["2019", "Trastuzumab deruxtecan proves the DXd ADC platform."], ["2025", "FDA approves datopotamab deruxtecan (Datroway) for HR-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for EGFR-mutated NSCLC."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds Trop-2 (TACSTD2) on tumor cells and the conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes. Lysosomal enzymes cleave the GGFG tetrapeptide linker and release DXd, an exatecan-derived topoisomerase I inhibitor. DXd traps topoisomerase I on DNA, so when replication forks hit the trapped complexes during S phase they become double-strand breaks, triggering apoptosis. DXd is membrane permeable and can diffuse into nearby cancer cells, killing them even if they express little Trop-2. The drug-to-antibody ratio is about 4, half that of trastuzumab deruxtecan.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Trop-2-expressing breast and lung cancer cells and their neighbors are killed through DNA damage."], ["Mouth", "Stomatitis (mouth sores) is the most common side effect, likely because Trop-2 is present in the lining of the mouth and the payload damages those cells."], ["Eyes", "Dry eye and keratitis can occur, as the corneal surface also expresses Trop-2."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and can be fatal."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common."], ["Hair", "Hair loss reflects injury to dividing follicle cells."]],
  "journey": "Datopotamab deruxtecan is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4 to 5 days. After uptake into Trop-2-positive cells, DXd is released in lysosomes. Free DXd in blood is short-lived and about 97 percent protein bound. DXd is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and leaves through bile into feces. The antibody portion is broken down to peptides and amino acids.",
  "sidefx": "Stomatitis and eye surface problems reflect Trop-2 on normal mucosal and corneal cells, where the conjugate delivers its payload. Steroid mouthwash and regular eye care are used to reduce these. Interstitial lung disease is the most serious risk and is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. Nausea, hair loss and low blood counts come from DXd damaging dividing cells. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Datroway (datopotamab deruxtecan-dlnk) FDA label", "TROPION-Breast01 trial (Bardia A et al.)"]
},
"enfortumab-vedotin": {
  "origin": "Enfortumab vedotin was developed by Astellas and Seagen (formerly Seattle Genetics, now part of Pfizer). Its antibody targets Nectin-4, a cell adhesion protein found on almost all urothelial (bladder) cancers. Its payload, monomethyl auristatin E (MMAE), is a synthetic relative of dolastatin 10, a compound isolated in 1987 by George R. Pettit of Arizona State University from the sea hare Dolabella auricularia, and later traced to cyanobacteria the sea hare eats. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Padcev in December 2019 for advanced urothelial cancer after prior therapy. In 2023 the EV-302 trial showed that enfortumab vedotin with pembrolizumab nearly doubled overall survival compared with platinum chemotherapy, replacing chemotherapy as first-line treatment after decades.",
  "timeline": [["1987", "George R. Pettit reports dolastatin 10 from the sea hare Dolabella auricularia."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval for enfortumab vedotin (Padcev)."], ["2023", "FDA approves enfortumab vedotin with pembrolizumab as first-line therapy for advanced urothelial cancer."]],
  "cell": "The fully human IgG1 antibody binds Nectin-4 on urothelial cancer cells. The conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes, where proteases cut the valine-citrulline linker and release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, so the mitotic spindle cannot form, the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. MMAE can cross membranes, so it also kills neighboring tumor cells (bystander effect). Dying cells release danger signals that can prime an immune response (immunogenic cell death), which may help explain why it works so well with pembrolizumab.",
  "body": [["Urothelial tumor", "Nectin-4-rich bladder and urinary tract cancer cells die during division."], ["Skin", "Rash is very common and can progress to SJS/TEN, because Nectin-4 is present in normal skin keratinocytes and sweat glands."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral sensory neuropathy is common because microtubules are needed to transport material along long nerve fibers."], ["Blood sugar", "Hyperglycemia and diabetic ketoacidosis can occur, even in people without diabetes; the exact cause is unclear."], ["Eyes", "Dry eye, blurred vision and keratitis can occur."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is uncommon but can be severe or fatal."]],
  "journey": "Enfortumab vedotin is given by IV infusion on a weekly schedule within each cycle. The conjugate has a half-life of about 3.4 days, and free MMAE about 2.4 days. After uptake, MMAE is released in lysosomes. Free MMAE is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and is excreted mostly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE levels and side effects. The antibody portion is broken down into peptides and amino acids.",
  "sidefx": "Skin reactions arise because Nectin-4 is expressed in normal skin, so the conjugate delivers MMAE to skin cells; severe reactions such as SJS/TEN carry a boxed warning. Nerve damage comes from MMAE disrupting microtubules in nerve axons. High blood sugar and DKA are recognized risks, so glucose is checked. Eye surface toxicity and pneumonitis occur less often. Management is holding or stopping the drug and treating the specific problem, such as corticosteroids for severe skin reactions. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The sea hare that supplied dolastatin 10 is a soft-bodied marine snail. The compound was present in such tiny amounts that huge numbers of animals had to be collected to isolate enough to solve its structure.",
  "src": ["Padcev (enfortumab vedotin-ejfv) FDA label", "Powles T et al. EV-302, N Engl J Med 2024", "Pettit GR et al. J Am Chem Soc 1987"]
},
"tisotumab-vedotin": {
  "origin": "Tisotumab vedotin was developed by the Danish company Genmab together with Seagen (now part of Pfizer). Its antibody targets tissue factor, the protein that starts the extrinsic clotting cascade, which many cancers, including cervical cancer, display in large amounts. It carries the MMAE payload derived from dolastatin 10, a compound first found in a sea hare. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Tivdak in September 2021 for recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer that progressed on or after chemotherapy, and later converted it to full approval after a confirmatory trial showed improved survival.",
  "timeline": [["1987", "Dolastatin 10, the parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for tisotumab vedotin (Tivdak) in cervical cancer."], ["2024", "FDA grants full approval after the innovaTV 301 trial."]],
  "cell": "The fully human IgG1 antibody binds tissue factor (TF, CD142) on tumor cells. The conjugate is internalized, and lysosomal proteases cleave the valine-citrulline linker to release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule assembly, causing G2/M arrest and apoptosis. Freed MMAE can enter nearby cells and kill them too (bystander effect). The antibody also recruits immune cells by ADCC and blocks TF-factor VIIa signaling that tumors use for growth, while being designed to have little effect on normal clotting.",
  "body": [["Cervical tumor", "TF-rich cancer cells are killed during division."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis, dry eye and keratitis are common and can lead to corneal ulcers, because TF and the conjugate affect the eye surface."], ["Nose and bleeding", "Nosebleeds are common, and serious bleeding can occur, possibly related to TF targeting and vessel effects."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy comes from MMAE disrupting microtubule transport in nerves."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis is uncommon but can be severe."], ["Hair and skin", "Hair loss and rash can occur, reflecting payload effects on dividing cells."]],
  "journey": "Tisotumab vedotin is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4 days and free MMAE about 2.6 days. MMAE released in cells or in blood is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and excreted mostly in feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE exposure. The antibody portion is broken down by protein catabolism.",
  "sidefx": "Eye toxicity carries a boxed warning. It is thought to come from the conjugate reaching the conjunctiva and cornea, so eye exams, lubricating drops, steroid drops and cooling eye pads during infusion are used to lower risk. Bleeding, mostly nosebleeds, may relate to TF targeting in vessels. Neuropathy reflects MMAE damage to nerve microtubules. Pneumonitis can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Tivdak (tisotumab vedotin-tftv) FDA label", "Coleman RL et al. innovaTV 204, Lancet Oncol 2021"]
},
"mirvetuximab-soravtansine": {
  "origin": "Mirvetuximab soravtansine was developed by ImmunoGen, the Massachusetts company that pioneered maytansinoid payloads, a family that traces back to maytansine, isolated in the early 1970s from an African Maytenus shrub. Its antibody targets folate receptor alpha (FRalpha), a protein that helps cells take in folate and is overexpressed on many ovarian cancers but scarce on most normal tissues. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Elahere in November 2022 for FRalpha-positive, platinum-resistant ovarian cancer, a setting with few effective options, and full approval in 2024 after the MIRASOL trial showed longer survival. AbbVie acquired ImmunoGen in 2024.",
  "timeline": [["1972", "Maytansine is reported from a Maytenus shrub."], ["2022", "FDA grants accelerated approval for mirvetuximab soravtansine (Elahere)."], ["2024", "FDA grants full approval after the MIRASOL trial."], ["2024", "AbbVie acquires ImmunoGen."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds folate receptor alpha (FOLR1) on ovarian cancer cells. The conjugate is internalized and processed in lysosomes, and the cleavable disulfide (sulfo-SPDB) linker releases DM4, a maytansinoid. DM4 is converted to S-methyl-DM4, and both bind tubulin and block microtubule assembly. The mitotic spindle fails, the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. These payload forms can cross membranes and kill nearby tumor cells (bystander effect). Patients are selected with an FDA-approved test showing high FRalpha on the tumor.",
  "body": [["Ovarian tumor", "FRalpha-rich cancer cells die during division."], ["Eyes", "Blurred vision and keratopathy (changes in the corneal surface) are very common and are thought to come from off-target uptake of the payload by corneal epithelial cells."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy reflects payload damage to nerve microtubules."], ["Gut", "Nausea, abdominal pain and diarrhea are common."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur and are checked during treatment."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonitis can occur and can be severe."]],
  "journey": "Mirvetuximab soravtansine is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks, with the amount based on adjusted ideal body weight. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4.8 days. DM4 is released in cells and is metabolized by CYP3A4 to S-methyl-DM4. The payload is highly protein bound and is eliminated mainly in feces. The antibody portion is broken down into peptides and amino acids.",
  "sidefx": "Eye toxicity carries a boxed warning. Off-target uptake of the maytansinoid by rapidly renewing corneal epithelial cells causes blurred vision and keratopathy, so eye exams, lubricating drops and steroid eye drops are used. Symptoms usually improve after holding the drug. Neuropathy comes from microtubule damage in nerves. Pneumonitis is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Elahere (mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx) FDA label", "Moore KN et al. MIRASOL, N Engl J Med 2023"]
},
"brentuximab-vedotin": {
  "origin": "Brentuximab vedotin was developed by Seattle Genetics (later Seagen, now part of Pfizer), with Takeda as partner outside the US. Its target, CD30, was first identified in the early 1980s by German researchers studying the Reed-Sternberg cells of Hodgkin lymphoma, then named the Ki-1 antigen. An earlier naked CD30 antibody had shown little effect, so Seattle Genetics attached MMAE, a potent synthetic relative of dolastatin 10, a compound first isolated from a sea hare. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Adcetris in August 2011 for relapsed Hodgkin lymphoma and systemic anaplastic large cell lymphoma. It was the first new Hodgkin lymphoma drug approved in the US in decades, and it later moved into first-line treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1982", "German researchers describe the Ki-1 (CD30) antigen on Reed-Sternberg cells."], ["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2011", "FDA grants accelerated approval for brentuximab vedotin (Adcetris)."], ["2018", "FDA approves brentuximab with AVD for previously untreated Stage III or IV Hodgkin lymphoma."]],
  "cell": "The chimeric IgG1 antibody (cAC10) binds CD30 (TNFRSF8), which is highly expressed on Reed-Sternberg cells of classical Hodgkin lymphoma and on anaplastic large cell lymphoma and some other T-cell lymphomas. The conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes, where proteases cut the valine-citrulline linker to release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, so the cell arrests in G2/M and undergoes apoptosis. Free MMAE can diffuse into neighboring cells, including the many supporting immune cells around Reed-Sternberg cells (bystander effect).",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes", "CD30-positive Hodgkin and T-cell lymphoma cells are killed during division."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral sensory neuropathy is common and builds with each dose, because MMAE disrupts microtubule transport in long nerves; motor neuropathy can also occur."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia is common, especially with chemotherapy, raising infection risk."], ["Pancreas", "Acute pancreatitis is rare but can be fatal."], ["Brain", "Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a JC virus brain infection, is a rare boxed warning risk."], ["Liver and lungs", "Hepatotoxicity and pulmonary toxicity can occur, and the lung risk is greatly raised with bleomycin."]],
  "journey": "Brentuximab vedotin is given by IV infusion every 2 to 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 4 to 6 days and free MMAE about 3 to 4 days. MMAE is metabolized by CYP3A4 and is excreted mostly in feces, with some in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise MMAE levels. Severe kidney or liver impairment raises MMAE exposure. The antibody part is broken down by protein catabolism.",
  "sidefx": "Neuropathy, the most common dose-limiting problem, comes from MMAE disrupting microtubules that carry material along nerve fibers; it usually improves after the drug is stopped or reduced. Neutropenia reflects payload effects on marrow. Use with bleomycin is contraindicated because of severe lung toxicity. PML occurs from JC virus reactivation in an immunosuppressed person. Pancreatitis and severe skin reactions are rare. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Reed-Sternberg cells are often a small minority of the cells in a Hodgkin lymph node; most of the mass is a crowd of immune cells drawn in by the cancer cells.",
  "src": ["Adcetris (brentuximab vedotin) FDA label", "Younes A et al. N Engl J Med 2010", "Schwab U, Stein H et al. Nature 1982"]
},
"polatuzumab-vedotin": {
  "origin": "Polatuzumab vedotin was developed by Genentech, part of Roche, using ADC technology licensed from Seattle Genetics. Its antibody targets CD79b, part of the B-cell receptor complex found on almost all mature B-cell lymphomas. Like brentuximab and enfortumab, it carries MMAE, a synthetic relative of the sea hare compound dolastatin 10. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Polivy in June 2019 with bendamustine and rituximab for relapsed or refractory diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. In 2023, based on the POLARIX trial, it was approved as part of Pola-R-CHP for previously untreated DLBCL, the first change to standard first-line DLBCL treatment in about 20 years.",
  "timeline": [["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval for polatuzumab vedotin (Polivy) in relapsed DLBCL."], ["2023", "FDA approves Pola-R-CHP for previously untreated DLBCL."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds CD79b, a signaling component of the B-cell receptor complex. CD79b is naturally internalized, so the conjugate is efficiently carried into the B cell and sent to lysosomes. Proteases there cut the valine-citrulline linker and release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, causing G2/M arrest and apoptosis. Freed MMAE can also kill nearby cells (bystander effect). In Pola-R-CHP, polatuzumab replaces vincristine, another microtubule poison, which helps avoid stacking two drugs with similar nerve toxicity.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "CD79b-positive lymphoma cells are killed during division."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells also carry CD79b and are depleted, adding to infection risk."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy comes from MMAE disrupting microtubule transport in nerves."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia, anemia and low platelets are common, especially with chemotherapy."], ["Infections", "Pneumonia, sepsis and rarely PML can occur."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and hepatotoxicity can occur."]],
  "journey": "Polatuzumab vedotin is given by IV infusion once every 21-day cycle for a fixed number of cycles. Antibody-conjugated MMAE has a half-life of about 12 days. Released MMAE is metabolized by CYP3A4 and leaves mostly through bile into feces, with a small amount in urine. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE exposure. The antibody is broken down by protein catabolism.",
  "sidefx": "Neuropathy and low blood counts reflect the MMAE payload: microtubule disruption in nerve axons and in dividing marrow cells. Infections arise from neutropenia, B-cell depletion and chemotherapy, so preventive antimicrobials and growth factors are used. Infusion reactions come from the protein. Tumor lysis can follow rapid lymphoma cell death. PML is rare. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Polivy (polatuzumab vedotin-piiq) FDA label", "Tilly H et al. POLARIX, N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"loncastuximab-tesirine": {
  "origin": "Loncastuximab tesirine was developed by ADC Therapeutics, a company based in Lausanne, Switzerland. Its payload belongs to the pyrrolobenzodiazepines (PBDs), a family whose first member, anthramycin, was discovered in the 1960s from the soil bacterium Streptomyces refuineus. PBDs bind DNA with great precision, and chemists later joined two PBD units into dimers that cross-link both DNA strands. ADC Therapeutics linked such a dimer to an antibody against CD19, a protein on nearly all B-cell lymphomas. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Zynlonta in April 2021 for relapsed or refractory large B-cell lymphoma after two or more lines of therapy.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Anthramycin, the first pyrrolobenzodiazepine, is discovered from Streptomyces bacteria."], ["2000s", "PBD dimers are developed as ultra-potent DNA cross-linking payloads."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval for loncastuximab tesirine (Zynlonta)."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds CD19 on B cells, and the conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes. Proteases there cut the valine-alanine linker of tesirine and release SG3199, a PBD dimer. SG3199 slides into the minor groove of DNA and covalently binds guanine bases on opposite strands, forming interstrand cross-links. Because these cross-links cause little distortion of the DNA helix, they are hard for repair enzymes to detect. The cross-links block DNA replication and transcription, leading to cell death. This action does not depend on a specific phase of the cell cycle.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "CD19-positive lymphoma cells die from unrepaired DNA cross-links."], ["Fluid balance", "Edema, pleural and pericardial effusions and ascites can develop, a recognized PBD payload effect thought to involve vascular leak."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity and rash can occur, so sun exposure is limited."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia, low platelets and anemia come from payload effects on marrow cells."], ["Liver", "Increased GGT and other liver enzymes are common."], ["Infections", "Serious infections can occur with low counts and B-cell depletion."]],
  "journey": "Loncastuximab tesirine is given by IV infusion every 3 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of about 2 to 3 weeks. After uptake into CD19-positive cells, the PBD payload is released. Free SG3199 is highly protein bound and is partly metabolized by CYP3A4. The antibody part is broken down by protein catabolism, and minor amounts are eliminated in bile and urine.",
  "sidefx": "Fluid build-up, skin reactions and photosensitivity are linked to the PBD payload. Dexamethasone premedication is used to reduce edema and effusions. Low blood counts come from DNA cross-linking in dividing marrow cells. Infections follow low counts and loss of normal B cells. Embryo-fetal harm is expected because the payload damages DNA. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Zynlonta (loncastuximab tesirine-lpyl) FDA label", "Caimi PF et al. LOTIS-2, Lancet Oncol 2021"]
},
"inotuzumab-ozogamicin": {
  "origin": "Inotuzumab ozogamicin carries calicheamicin, one of the most potent natural cell poisons known. Calicheamicin was discovered in the 1980s by scientists at Lederle Laboratories from the soil bacterium Micromonospora echinospora, found in a chalky soil in Texas called caliche, which gave the compound its name. It was so toxic that it could only be used when attached to an antibody. Wyeth (which absorbed Lederle) developed antibody-calicheamicin conjugates, and Pfizer completed inotuzumab after acquiring Wyeth. It targets CD22, found on most B-cell leukemia blasts. The FDA approved Besponsa in August 2017 for relapsed or refractory B-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukemia in adults, later extended to children 1 year and older.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Lederle scientists discover calicheamicin from Micromonospora echinospora in Texas soil."], ["2009", "Pfizer acquires Wyeth, developer of calicheamicin conjugates."], ["2017", "FDA approves inotuzumab ozogamicin (Besponsa) for relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG4 antibody binds CD22 on B-lineage leukemia cells, and the conjugate is rapidly internalized. In the acidic lysosome, the acid-labile hydrazone linker breaks and releases N-acetyl-gamma-calicheamicin. Inside the cell, reduction of its trisulfide group (by glutathione) triggers a chemical rearrangement called Bergman cyclization of its enediyne core, forming a highly reactive diradical. Calicheamicin sits in the DNA minor groove, and this diradical tears hydrogen atoms from the sugar backbone of both strands, causing double-strand DNA breaks. The cell arrests and dies by apoptosis. This does not depend on a specific cell-cycle phase.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "CD22-positive leukemic blasts are killed, often leading to remission and measurable residual disease negativity."], ["Liver", "Hepatic veno-occlusive disease (sinusoidal obstruction syndrome) is a hallmark risk, as calicheamicin injures the cells lining liver sinusoids, blocking blood flow and causing liver swelling, fluid gain and jaundice."], ["Platelets and white cells", "Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia are common, raising bleeding and infection risk."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation can occur, so ECGs and electrolytes are monitored."], ["After transplant", "Non-relapse deaths after stem cell transplant are increased, largely from VOD."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions can occur, and premedication is used."]],
  "journey": "Inotuzumab ozogamicin is given by IV infusion on a weekly schedule within each cycle. It binds CD22 and is cleared partly by uptake into target cells, so its clearance changes as leukemia cells are removed. Its half-life at steady state is about 12 days. Calicheamicin is broken down mainly by nonenzymatic reduction, and the antibody by protein catabolism. Very little is cleared by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "VOD/SOS carries a boxed warning. Calicheamicin damages sinusoidal endothelial cells in the liver, which swell and block blood flow, causing liver enlargement, fluid retention and rising bilirubin. Risk is highest when stem cell transplant follows, especially with conditioning regimens that contain two alkylating agents. Defibrotide is a treatment used for severe VOD. Low counts reflect marrow toxicity and leukemia. QT prolongation is monitored with ECGs. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Calicheamicin is named after caliche, the hard calcium-rich soil of Texas where the bacterium that makes it was found.",
  "src": ["Besponsa (inotuzumab ozogamicin) FDA label", "Kantarjian HM et al. N Engl J Med 2016", "Lee MD et al. J Am Chem Soc 1987"]
},
"gemtuzumab-ozogamicin": {
  "origin": "Gemtuzumab ozogamicin was the first antibody-drug conjugate ever approved. It was developed by Wyeth, combining an antibody against CD33, a protein on most acute myeloid leukemia blasts, with calicheamicin, a powerful DNA-breaking toxin discovered by Lederle scientists in the 1980s in a soil bacterium from Texas. The FDA granted accelerated approval to Mylotarg in May 2000 for older adults with relapsed AML. A later confirmatory trial did not show benefit and found more early deaths, so Pfizer voluntarily withdrew it in 2010. Further studies showed that smaller, split (fractionated) doses were safer and effective, and the FDA approved it again in September 2017 for newly diagnosed and relapsed CD33-positive AML.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Calicheamicin is discovered in a Texas soil bacterium."], ["2000", "FDA approves gemtuzumab ozogamicin (Mylotarg), the first antibody-drug conjugate."], ["2010", "Mylotarg is voluntarily withdrawn from the US market after a confirmatory trial fails."], ["2017", "FDA re-approves gemtuzumab ozogamicin with a lower fractionated dosing schedule."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG4 antibody binds CD33 (Siglec-3), a surface protein on myeloid cells and on blasts in most AML. CD33 is internalized after binding, carrying the conjugate into the cell. In the acidic lysosome, the hydrazone linker breaks and releases N-acetyl-gamma-calicheamicin. Reduction of calicheamicin's trisulfide by glutathione triggers Bergman cyclization of its enediyne core, producing a diradical that sits in the DNA minor groove and abstracts hydrogens from the sugar backbone. This causes double-strand DNA breaks, cell-cycle arrest and apoptosis. Normal myeloid precursors that carry CD33 are also killed, which explains prolonged low blood counts.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "CD33-positive leukemic blasts are destroyed, increasing remission rates when combined with chemotherapy."], ["Platelets and white cells", "Normal CD33-positive myeloid cells are also killed, so neutropenia and thrombocytopenia can be prolonged."], ["Liver", "Hepatic veno-occlusive disease (sinusoidal obstruction syndrome) can occur from calicheamicin injury to the cells lining liver sinusoids."], ["Bleeding", "Low platelets can lead to serious hemorrhage, including in the brain."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur, so premedication is used."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and fever are common as dividing lining cells are injured."]],
  "journey": "Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is given by IV infusion, usually as split doses on specific days. It is cleared partly by binding to CD33 on blasts, so levels and half-life are higher after the second dose when fewer blasts remain. Calicheamicin is inactivated mainly by nonenzymatic reduction, and the antibody is broken down by protein catabolism. Very little leaves through the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "VOD/SOS carries a boxed warning: calicheamicin damages sinusoidal endothelial cells in the liver, causing blocked blood flow, liver enlargement, fluid gain and jaundice. Defibrotide is a treatment used for severe VOD. Prolonged low counts reflect killing of normal CD33-positive myeloid cells. Bleeding comes from low platelets. Infusion reactions come from the protein and from cytokine release as blasts die. The lower, fractionated dosing used today was chosen because it lowered these early toxicities. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Mylotarg's withdrawal and return is a classic story of how changing the dose schedule, not the molecule, can turn a failed drug into a useful one.",
  "src": ["Mylotarg (gemtuzumab ozogamicin) FDA label", "Castaigne S et al. ALFA-0701, Lancet 2012"]
},
"belantamab-mafodotin": {
  "origin": "Belantamab mafodotin was developed by GlaxoSmithKline (GSK). It targets BCMA, a protein on myeloma cells, and carries MMAF, an auristatin related to the sea hare compound dolastatin 10. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Blenrep in August 2020 for heavily pretreated myeloma, the first anti-BCMA therapy approved. In November 2022 GSK withdrew it from the US market after the DREAMM-3 confirmatory trial did not meet its main goal. Later trials combining it with standard myeloma drugs, including DREAMM-7 with bortezomib and dexamethasone, showed benefit, and it returned to the US market in 2025 in combination with bortezomib and dexamethasone, with a safety program for its eye effects.",
  "timeline": [["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of the auristatins, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval for belantamab mafodotin (Blenrep), the first BCMA-directed therapy."], ["2022", "Blenrep is withdrawn from the US market after DREAMM-3."], ["2025", "Blenrep returns to the US market with bortezomib and dexamethasone."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells and myeloma cells. The conjugate is internalized and fully digested in lysosomes, because its maleimidocaproyl linker cannot be cut, releasing cysteine-mcMMAF. MMAF inhibits tubulin polymerization, so the cell arrests in G2/M and undergoes apoptosis. MMAF carries a charged group that makes it poorly membrane permeable, so it stays trapped in the target cell, with little bystander killing. The antibody's Fc is afucosylated, which strengthens binding to Fc-gamma receptor IIIa on NK cells and enhances ADCC, and it also blocks BCMA survival signals.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "BCMA-positive myeloma cells are killed by the payload and by immune effector cells."], ["Eyes", "Keratopathy (changes in the corneal epithelium), blurred vision, dry eye and light sensitivity are very common, because corneal cells take up the conjugate nonspecifically."], ["Platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is common and can lead to bleeding."], ["Immune system", "Infections such as pneumonia and upper respiratory infections are common in treated myeloma."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea can occur during treatment cycles."], ["Liver", "Liver toxicity with enzyme rises can occur and is monitored."]],
  "journey": "Belantamab mafodotin is given by IV infusion once every 3-week cycle, or less often depending on eye findings. The conjugate has a half-life of about 2 weeks. The antibody is broken down by protein catabolism, while released MMAF is barely metabolized and is eliminated mainly in feces. Because MMAF is not a major CYP substrate, drug interactions are few.",
  "sidefx": "Eye toxicity carries a boxed warning. Corneal epithelial cells take up the conjugate by a target-independent route, and the MMAF payload injures them, producing microcyst-like changes, blurred vision and, rarely, severe vision loss or corneal ulcers. Regular eye exams before each dose are required, and doses are held or spaced out when changes appear; most eye changes recover. Low platelets reflect marrow effects and disease. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Blenrep (belantamab mafodotin-blmf) FDA label", "Hungria V et al. DREAMM-7, N Engl J Med 2024"]
},
"telisotuzumab-vedotin": {
  "origin": "Telisotuzumab vedotin was developed by AbbVie. Its antibody targets c-Met, the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor, which is overexpressed in a subset of non-small cell lung cancers. Earlier attempts to block c-Met with antibodies had not succeeded, so AbbVie used the antibody instead as a delivery vehicle for MMAE, the auristatin payload derived from the sea hare compound dolastatin 10. In 2025 the FDA granted accelerated approval to telisotuzumab vedotin (Emrelis) for previously treated advanced nonsquamous non-small cell lung cancer with high c-Met protein overexpression, the first c-Met-directed antibody-drug conjugate.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "The MET gene and its receptor, c-Met, are discovered."], ["1987", "Dolastatin 10, parent of MMAE, is reported from a sea hare."], ["2025", "FDA grants accelerated approval for telisotuzumab vedotin (Emrelis)."]],
  "cell": "The humanized IgG1 antibody binds c-Met (the MET receptor tyrosine kinase) on tumor cells. The conjugate is internalized and moved to lysosomes, where proteases cut the valine-citrulline linker and release MMAE. MMAE binds tubulin and blocks microtubule polymerization, so the cell arrests in G2/M and dies by apoptosis. Freed MMAE can enter neighboring tumor cells (bystander effect). Patients are selected using an FDA-approved test showing high c-Met protein, meaning strong staining in at least half of tumor cells. Protein overexpression differs from MET exon 14 skipping mutations, which are treated with MET kinase inhibitors.",
  "body": [["Lung tumor", "c-Met-rich cancer cells are killed during division."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy is the most common side effect, from MMAE disrupting microtubule transport in nerves."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur and can be fatal."], ["Eyes", "Keratitis and blurred vision can occur from payload effects on the eye surface."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema (swelling of the legs and feet) is common."], ["General", "Fatigue and decreased appetite are common."]],
  "journey": "Telisotuzumab vedotin is given by IV infusion every 2 weeks. The conjugate has a half-life of several days. Released MMAE is metabolized mainly by CYP3A4 and leaves mostly through bile into feces. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise MMAE exposure. The antibody part is broken down by protein catabolism.",
  "sidefx": "Neuropathy reflects MMAE damage to the microtubules that move material along nerve fibers, and it is managed by holding or reducing doses. Pneumonitis is the most serious risk and is treated by stopping the drug and giving corticosteroids. Eye surface problems and infusion reactions can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Emrelis (telisotuzumab vedotin-tllv) FDA label", "LUMINOSITY trial (Camidge DR et al.)"]
},
"tagraxofusp": {
  "origin": "Tagraxofusp borrows its killing power from diphtheria toxin, the poison made by Corynebacterium diphtheriae that Emile Roux and Alexandre Yersin showed in 1888 could cause disease on its own. Researchers later learned to cut off the part of the toxin that binds normal cells and replace it with a human hormone, so the toxin would enter only cells carrying that hormone's receptor. Academic work led by Arthur Frankel and colleagues joined truncated diphtheria toxin to interleukin-3, aiming at cells with the IL-3 receptor (CD123). Stemline Therapeutics developed it, and in December 2018 the FDA approved tagraxofusp (Elzonris) for blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm (BPDCN), a rare, aggressive blood cancer whose cells carry very high levels of CD123. It was the first drug approved for BPDCN and the first CD123-directed therapy.",
  "timeline": [["1888", "Roux and Yersin show that diphtheria bacteria release a toxin."], ["1990s", "Researchers build fusion toxins that replace diphtheria toxin's binding domain with human cytokines."], ["2018", "FDA approves tagraxofusp (Elzonris), the first treatment for BPDCN."]],
  "cell": "Tagraxofusp is a recombinant fusion protein of human IL-3 and a truncated diphtheria toxin that keeps the catalytic (A) and translocation domains but lacks the native receptor-binding domain. The IL-3 portion binds CD123, the IL-3 receptor alpha chain, which is strongly expressed on BPDCN cells. The complex is taken into the cell by endocytosis. In the acidic endosome, the translocation domain inserts into the membrane and moves the catalytic fragment into the cytoplasm. There the catalytic domain uses NAD+ to ADP-ribosylate diphthamide, a modified histidine on elongation factor 2 (EF-2). Inactivated EF-2 can no longer move ribosomes along messenger RNA, so protein synthesis stops and the cell dies by apoptosis. A single toxin molecule can inactivate many EF-2 molecules. It works regardless of cell-cycle phase.",
  "body": [["Blood and skin cancer sites", "CD123-rich BPDCN cells in skin, marrow and lymph nodes are killed."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak syndrome can occur because the toxin injures endothelial cells, letting fluid and albumin seep into tissues."], ["Fluid balance", "Low albumin, edema and rapid weight gain are early signs of capillary leak."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common and may reflect injury to liver cells."], ["Platelets", "Low platelets can occur, raising bleeding risk."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions and fever can occur."]],
  "journey": "Tagraxofusp is given by IV infusion once daily for 5 days in each cycle. It is a small protein that is cleared very quickly, with a half-life of under an hour, which is why it is given on consecutive days. It is broken down by protein catabolism and not by CYP enzymes, and little is cleared unchanged by the kidneys. The first cycle is given in a hospital so that early capillary leak can be detected.",
  "sidefx": "Capillary leak syndrome carries a boxed warning. Diphtheria toxin damages endothelial cells, so plasma fluid and albumin leak out of vessels, causing edema, weight gain, low albumin and low blood pressure, and it can be fatal. Albumin, weight and blood pressure are monitored, and doses are held with albumin replacement and diuretics when warning signs appear. Liver enzyme rises reflect liver injury. Hypersensitivity comes from the foreign protein. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "BPDCN was long confused with leukemia or lymphoma. Its cells are now known to come from plasmacytoid dendritic cells, the immune cells that make large amounts of interferon during viral infections.",
  "src": ["Elzonris (tagraxofusp-erzs) FDA label", "Pemmaraju N et al. N Engl J Med 2019"]
},
"denileukin-diftitox": {
  "origin": "Denileukin diftitox combines interleukin-2 with fragments of diphtheria toxin, an idea pioneered by John R. Murphy and colleagues at Boston University, who showed that replacing the toxin's own binding domain with IL-2 would steer it to activated T cells carrying IL-2 receptors. The original product, Ontak, was developed by Seragen and approved by the FDA in 1999 for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma. It was the first fusion protein toxin approved for cancer. Ontak was taken off the market in 2014 because of manufacturing problems. A new, more purified version made by Citius Pharmaceuticals was approved in August 2024 as Lymphir for relapsed or refractory cutaneous T-cell lymphoma.",
  "timeline": [["1888", "Roux and Yersin show that diphtheria bacteria release a toxin."], ["1980s", "Murphy and colleagues create IL-2 to diphtheria toxin fusion proteins."], ["1999", "FDA approves denileukin diftitox (Ontak) for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."], ["2014", "Ontak is discontinued because of manufacturing problems."], ["2024", "FDA approves a new formulation, denileukin diftitox-cxdl (Lymphir)."]],
  "cell": "Denileukin diftitox is a recombinant fusion of human IL-2 and the catalytic (A) and translocation (B) fragments of diphtheria toxin. The IL-2 portion binds the IL-2 receptor, especially the high-affinity complex containing CD25 (IL-2R alpha), which is present on malignant T cells in many cutaneous T-cell lymphomas and on regulatory T cells. After binding, the complex is taken up by endocytosis. In the acidic endosome the translocation fragment moves the catalytic fragment into the cytoplasm, where it ADP-ribosylates diphthamide on elongation factor 2 using NAD+. This stops protein synthesis and the cell dies. Depleting regulatory T cells may also lift some immune suppression against the tumor.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Malignant T cells in skin patches, plaques and tumors are killed, so lesions improve."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak syndrome occurs because the toxin damages endothelial cells, letting fluid escape into tissues."], ["Fluid balance", "Edema, low albumin and weight gain are common signs of vascular leak."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions with fever, chills and low blood pressure are common, especially with the first doses."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are common and usually appear in the first cycles."], ["Eyes", "Vision changes, including loss of color vision, have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Denileukin diftitox is given by IV infusion once daily for 5 days in each cycle. Like other small fusion proteins it is cleared quickly, with a half-life of about an hour or less. It is broken down by protein catabolism, not by liver enzymes, and little is removed unchanged by the kidneys. Antibodies against the drug commonly develop over repeated cycles.",
  "sidefx": "Capillary leak syndrome, which carries a boxed warning, comes from diphtheria toxin injury to endothelial cells, so fluid and albumin leak out and cause edema, low blood pressure and weight gain. Albumin and weight are monitored and doses are held when leak develops. Infusion reactions come from the foreign protein and cytokine release, and premedication is used. Liver enzyme rises and vision changes can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Lymphir (denileukin diftitox-cxdl) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"fulvestrant": {
  "origin": "Fulvestrant was discovered at Imperial Chemical Industries (ICI) in England, the same company that developed tamoxifen. A team led by Alan Wakeling searched for a 'pure' antiestrogen that, unlike tamoxifen, would have no estrogen-like effects at all. They found that adding a long side chain to the 7-alpha position of estradiol produced a molecule that blocked the receptor completely and caused it to be destroyed. The compound, first called ICI 182,780, was reported in 1991. ICI's pharmaceutical business became Zeneca and then AstraZeneca, which brought fulvestrant (Faslodex) to FDA approval in 2002 for hormone receptor-positive advanced breast cancer. It was the first selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD).",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "ICI develops tamoxifen, the first major antiestrogen."], ["1991", "Wakeling and colleagues report ICI 182,780, a pure antiestrogen."], ["2002", "FDA approves fulvestrant (Faslodex) for advanced breast cancer."], ["2017", "FDA approves fulvestrant as first-line therapy for endocrine-naive advanced breast cancer."]],
  "cell": "In hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, estradiol binds estrogen receptor alpha (ER-alpha), which pairs up (dimerizes), moves into the nucleus and switches on genes for cell growth, such as cyclin D1. Fulvestrant is a steroidal estradiol analog with a long 7-alpha alkylsulfinyl side chain. It binds ER-alpha competitively with high affinity. The bulky side chain disrupts the receptor's shape, blocking dimerization, nuclear localization and the activation functions AF-1 and AF-2. The misfolded receptor is then tagged and destroyed by the proteasome, so the cell has far less ER to respond to any estrogen. Unlike tamoxifen, it has no partial agonist activity, including in the endometrium.",
  "body": [["Breast cancer", "Tumor cells lose estrogen receptor signaling and stop growing or shrink."], ["Endometrium", "Because it is a pure antagonist, it does not stimulate the uterine lining as tamoxifen can."], ["Blood vessels and brain", "Hot flashes reflect loss of estrogen signaling in temperature-regulating centers."], ["Injection sites", "Injection-site pain is common, and large-volume gluteal injections can rarely irritate the sciatic nerve."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises can occur because the liver metabolizes the drug."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain and fatigue are common."]],
  "journey": "Fulvestrant is given as an intramuscular injection into the buttock, as two injections at each dosing visit. It is not effective by mouth. The oil-based depot releases drug slowly, with peak levels about 7 days after injection and a long half-life of about 40 days. Steady state comes within about a month because of a loading schedule. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, including by CYP3A4 and conjugation, and it leaves mostly in feces through bile, with very little in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Hot flashes, fatigue and joint pain come from loss of estrogen signaling. Injection-site pain and rare sciatic nerve irritation come from the large intramuscular depot. Because it is given into muscle, it can cause bleeding or bruising in people with low platelets or on anticoagulants. Liver enzyme rises reflect its hepatic metabolism. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Fulvestrant's discovery helped inspire a new generation of oral SERDs designed to destroy the estrogen receptor without the need for large injections.",
  "src": ["Faslodex (fulvestrant) FDA label", "Wakeling AE et al. Cancer Res 1991", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"goserelin": {
  "origin": "Goserelin grew out of the discovery of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). In 1971 Andrew Schally's lab determined the structure of this 10-amino-acid hypothalamic hormone, and Roger Guillemin's lab worked on hypothalamic hormones in parallel; they shared the 1977 Nobel Prize. Researchers then found that giving GnRH continuously, rather than in natural pulses, paradoxically shut the pituitary down. Chemists at ICI in England made goserelin, a more stable and potent GnRH analog, and designed a biodegradable implant that releases it steadily. The FDA approved Zoladex in 1989 for advanced prostate cancer. It was later approved for advanced breast cancer, endometriosis and endometrial thinning.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "Andrew Schally's lab determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1977", "Schally and Guillemin share the Nobel Prize for hypothalamic hormones."], ["1989", "FDA approves the goserelin implant (Zoladex) for advanced prostate cancer."]],
  "cell": "Native GnRH is released from the hypothalamus in pulses and binds Gq-coupled GnRH receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells, causing LH and FSH release. Goserelin is a synthetic decapeptide GnRH agonist with amino acid changes that resist breakdown and bind the receptor more strongly. At first it acts like a strong burst of GnRH, so LH, FSH and testosterone or estradiol rise for about 1 to 2 weeks (the flare). With continuous rather than pulsatile stimulation, gonadotroph cells internalize and downregulate their receptors and uncouple them from signaling. LH and FSH fall, and testosterone drops to castrate levels in men and estradiol to postmenopausal levels in women within about 2 to 4 weeks. This is called medical castration.",
  "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Hormone-sensitive prostate cancer cells lose testosterone and stop growing or shrink."], ["Breast and uterus", "Low estradiol slows hormone-sensitive breast cancer and shrinks endometriosis and the uterine lining."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes and sweating come from low sex hormones affecting the temperature-regulating center."], ["Bones", "Low sex hormones speed bone loss, raising fracture risk over time."], ["Metabolism and heart", "Androgen deprivation can raise blood sugar and weight and is linked to diabetes and cardiovascular risk."], ["Sexual function", "Low libido and erectile dysfunction in men, and vaginal dryness in women, follow hormone loss."]],
  "journey": "Goserelin is placed as a small solid implant under the skin of the abdominal wall with a special syringe. The implant is made of a biodegradable polymer that slowly releases the peptide over 28 days or 3 months, depending on the product. Released goserelin itself has a short half-life of a few hours, but the steady release maintains suppression. The peptide is broken down by hydrolysis and leaves mainly in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Hot flashes, sexual dysfunction, bone loss and metabolic changes come directly from medical castration. The early flare in testosterone can temporarily worsen prostate cancer, causing bone pain, urinary blockage or spinal cord compression in men with spine metastases, which is why an antiandrogen is often started first. Injection of the implant can rarely injure abdominal wall vessels and cause serious bleeding, especially in thin people or those on anticoagulants. There is no antidote; effects reverse slowly as the implant is used up.",
  "fun": "The paradox that a hormone 'gas pedal' can act as a brake when pressed constantly was one of the key insights of reproductive endocrinology.",
  "src": ["Zoladex (goserelin implant) FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Science 1971", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"triptorelin": {
  "origin": "Triptorelin is one of the GnRH agonists that followed the 1971 discovery of the structure of gonadotropin-releasing hormone in Andrew Schally's lab. Researchers found that swapping the glycine at position 6 of GnRH for a D-amino acid made the peptide resist breakdown and bind its receptor more tightly. Triptorelin carries D-tryptophan at that position. It was developed in Europe and brought to market in long-acting depot forms by the Swiss company Debiopharm and its partners. In the US it was approved as Trelstar in 2000 for palliative treatment of advanced prostate cancer, and a separate product, Triptodur, was later approved for central precocious puberty in children.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "Schally's lab determines the structure of GnRH."], ["1970s", "D-amino acid substitutions at position 6 produce long-acting GnRH agonists such as triptorelin."], ["2000", "FDA approves triptorelin (Trelstar) for advanced prostate cancer."], ["2017", "FDA approves Triptodur for central precocious puberty."]],
  "cell": "Triptorelin is a synthetic GnRH decapeptide with D-tryptophan at position 6, which slows its breakdown and increases its affinity for the pituitary GnRH receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on gonadotroph cells. At first it acts as a strong agonist, causing a surge of LH and FSH and a rise in testosterone during the first week (the flare). Continuous, non-pulsatile exposure then causes the gonadotrophs to internalize and downregulate their GnRH receptors and uncouple them from signaling. LH and FSH secretion falls, and testosterone drops to castrate levels in about 2 to 4 weeks. Prostate cancer cells that depend on testosterone stop growing.",
  "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Testosterone falls to castrate levels, so hormone-sensitive prostate cancer stops growing or shrinks."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes and sweating follow loss of testosterone."], ["Bones", "Long-term low testosterone causes bone loss and raises fracture risk."], ["Metabolism", "Weight gain and higher blood sugar can occur with androgen deprivation."], ["Heart", "Androgen deprivation is linked to cardiovascular events and can prolong the QT interval."], ["Mood and sexual function", "Depression, low libido and erectile dysfunction can occur."]],
  "journey": "Triptorelin is given as an intramuscular injection of a slow-release depot. Peak blood levels occur within hours, and then the depot keeps releasing drug for 4, 12 or 24 weeks depending on the product strength. Triptorelin itself has a short terminal half-life of a few hours after IV dosing, so the depot design is what keeps suppression steady. It is broken down by peptidases, not by CYP enzymes, and is eliminated by both the kidneys and the liver.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from low testosterone: hot flashes, sexual dysfunction, bone loss, weight gain and higher blood sugar. The early testosterone flare can briefly worsen prostate cancer, causing bone pain, urinary blockage or spinal cord compression in men with spinal metastases, so close monitoring or an antiandrogen is used early. QT prolongation and cardiovascular risks are linked to androgen deprivation. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Triptorelin is also used in other formulations outside prostate cancer, including as a trigger for egg maturation in IVF, because a single burst of GnRH agonist causes a natural LH surge.",
  "src": ["Trelstar (triptorelin pamoate) FDA label", "Schally AV et al. Science 1971", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"degarelix": {
  "origin": "GnRH agonists such as leuprolide lower testosterone only after a temporary surge, which can be dangerous in men with advanced prostate cancer. Researchers therefore worked for decades on GnRH antagonists that would block the receptor directly. Early antagonists caused allergic reactions by releasing histamine. Degarelix was designed as a peptide antagonist with much less histamine release, and it forms a slow-release gel depot under the skin. An earlier antagonist, abarelix, reached the US market but was withdrawn, largely because of allergic reactions. Degarelix was developed by the Danish company Ferring Pharmaceuticals and approved by the FDA in December 2008 as Firmagon for advanced prostate cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1971", "The structure of GnRH is determined in Andrew Schally's lab."], ["1990s", "Researchers develop GnRH antagonists with less histamine release."], ["2008", "FDA approves degarelix (Firmagon) for advanced prostate cancer."]],
  "cell": "Degarelix is a synthetic decapeptide that binds GnRH receptors on pituitary gonadotroph cells competitively and reversibly. Unlike an agonist, it does not activate the receptor at all, so there is no initial burst of LH and FSH release. LH and FSH fall immediately, and with less LH reaching the testes, Leydig cells make far less testosterone. Testosterone reaches castrate levels within about 3 days, much faster than with GnRH agonists. Because there is no flare, there is no early worsening of prostate cancer. After injection the drug forms a gel depot under the skin that slowly releases it.",
  "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Rapid testosterone suppression stops hormone-sensitive prostate cancer growth, which is useful when quick control is needed."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling at the injection site are very common because the drug forms a depot under the skin."], ["Brain and skin", "Hot flashes come from loss of testosterone."], ["Bones", "Long-term androgen deprivation causes bone loss and fracture risk."], ["Heart", "Androgen deprivation can prolong the QT interval and is linked to cardiovascular risk."], ["Metabolism and liver", "Weight gain is common, and liver enzyme rises can occur."]],
  "journey": "Degarelix is injected under the skin of the abdomen. Once injected, it forms a gel depot from which it is released slowly. Peak blood levels occur within about 2 days, and maintenance doses last about 28 days. The apparent half-life after the starting dose is about 53 days, reflecting slow release from the depot. It is about 90 percent protein bound. It is broken down by peptide hydrolysis in the hepatobiliary system, not by CYP enzymes, and leaves mostly in feces with some in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Injection-site reactions come from the depot forming under the skin. Hot flashes, weight gain, bone loss and sexual side effects reflect testosterone suppression. QT prolongation is a risk of androgen deprivation itself, so caution is used with other QT-prolonging drugs and with electrolyte problems. Hypersensitivity can occur, though histamine release is lower than with older antagonists. There is no antidote; effects fade as the depot is used up.",
  "fun": "Because there is no flare, degarelix and other antagonists do not need an antiandrogen started beforehand to prevent early tumor growth.",
  "src": ["Firmagon (degarelix) FDA label", "Klotz L et al. BJU Int 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tisagenlecleucel": {
  "origin": "Tisagenlecleucel was the first CAR-T cell therapy approved in the US. The idea of giving T cells an artificial receptor came from Zelig Eshhar in Israel, who described 'T-bodies' in 1989. Later researchers added costimulatory domains, including the 4-1BB domain used in this product, from work by Dario Campana and colleagues at St. Jude Children's Research Hospital. At the University of Pennsylvania, Carl June, David Porter, Bruce Levine and colleagues treated adults with CLL in 2010, and in 2012 Stephan Grupp's team at the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia treated Emily Whitehead, a 6-year-old girl with relapsed ALL, who went into a remission that has lasted for years. Novartis developed the therapy, and the FDA approved Kymriah in August 2017 for children and young adults with relapsed ALL. It was also the first gene therapy approved in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Zelig Eshhar describes T cells with chimeric antigen receptors."], ["2004", "Campana's lab reports a CD19 CAR with a 4-1BB costimulatory domain."], ["2011", "Penn researchers report durable remissions in CLL with CD19 CAR-T cells."], ["2012", "Emily Whitehead becomes the first child with ALL treated with this CAR-T therapy."], ["2017", "FDA approves tisagenlecleucel (Kymriah), the first CAR-T and first US gene therapy."]],
  "cell": "T cells collected from the patient are transduced with a lentiviral vector carrying a chimeric antigen receptor gene. The CAR has an anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment on the outside, a CD8 hinge and transmembrane region, and inside the cell a 4-1BB (CD137) costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. When the CAR binds CD19 on a B cell, CD3-zeta delivers the activation signal and 4-1BB delivers the second signal that supports survival and memory. This works without MHC antigen presentation. Activated CAR-T cells kill CD19-positive cells with perforin and granzymes, release cytokines such as IL-6 and IFN-gamma and multiply many times over. The 4-1BB domain is linked to slower expansion and longer persistence than CD28-based products.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic and lymphoma B cells are destroyed, often leading to deep remission."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are also destroyed (B-cell aplasia), which can last as long as the CAR-T cells persist, causing low antibody levels and infections."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, low blood pressure and low oxygen occurs as CAR-T cells expand."], ["Brain", "ICANS can cause confusion, trouble speaking, tremor, seizures and rarely cerebral edema."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low blood counts can persist for weeks after infusion, partly from lymphodepleting chemotherapy."], ["Immune activation", "Rarely, HLH/MAS, an extreme inflammatory state, can develop."]],
  "journey": "The patient's T cells are collected by leukapheresis, shipped to a manufacturing site, engineered and expanded over weeks, then frozen and sent back. Before infusion, lymphodepleting chemotherapy (usually fludarabine and cyclophosphamide) clears space for the new cells. After a single IV infusion, the CAR-T cells travel through blood to the marrow, lymph nodes and even the cerebrospinal fluid. They expand rapidly when they meet CD19, peaking around 10 days, and then contract, but some can persist for months to years. There is no metabolism in the usual sense.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by massive CAR-T activation and release of IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab, an IL-6 receptor antibody, and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity (ICANS) is thought to involve cytokines and endothelial activation in the brain and is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell aplasia causes low antibody levels, treated with immunoglobulin replacement. Prolonged low counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning also notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.",
  "fun": "The lentiviral vector used to make these CAR-T cells is derived from HIV, with the genes needed to cause disease removed, so a disabled virus is used to rewire T cells against cancer.",
  "src": ["Kymriah (tisagenlecleucel) FDA label", "Maude SL et al. N Engl J Med 2014", "Eshhar Z et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1993"]
},
"axicabtagene-ciloleucel": {
  "origin": "Axicabtagene ciloleucel grew out of work at the National Cancer Institute, where Steven Rosenberg and James Kochenderfer built an anti-CD19 CAR with a CD28 costimulatory domain. In a 2010 report, Kochenderfer and colleagues described a patient with advanced follicular lymphoma whose disease regressed after treatment, one of the first CD19 CAR-T successes. Kite Pharma licensed the technology and developed it for large B-cell lymphoma. The FDA approved Yescarta in October 2017, the second CAR-T therapy and the first for adults with non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Gilead Sciences bought Kite the same year. In 2022 the ZUMA-7 trial led to its approval as second-line therapy for large B-cell lymphoma that relapses early.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Zelig Eshhar describes the chimeric antigen receptor concept."], ["2010", "Kochenderfer and Rosenberg at NCI report regression of lymphoma with anti-CD19 CAR-T cells."], ["2017", "FDA approves axicabtagene ciloleucel (Yescarta) for large B-cell lymphoma."], ["2017", "Gilead Sciences acquires Kite Pharma."], ["2022", "FDA approves Yescarta as second-line therapy for early relapsing LBCL."]],
  "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a gammaretroviral vector carrying a CAR gene. The CAR contains a murine anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment (FMC63), linked to a CD28 hinge, transmembrane and costimulatory domain and a CD3-zeta signaling domain. Binding CD19 on B cells triggers CD3-zeta signaling, while CD28 provides strong costimulation. This drives rapid T-cell activation and proliferation, release of cytokines including IL-6, IFN-gamma and GM-CSF, and MHC-independent killing of CD19-positive cells with perforin and granzymes. CD28-based CARs tend to expand faster and to higher peaks than 4-1BB-based CARs, which is linked to faster responses but more early toxicity.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes and tumor", "CD19-positive lymphoma cells are destroyed, and about 4 in 10 patients in key trials had long-lasting remissions."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are destroyed, causing low antibody levels and infections."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, fast heart rate and low blood pressure is very common, usually starting within 2 to 3 days."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity (ICANS), including confusion, trouble speaking, tremor and seizures, is more common than with 4-1BB products."], ["Bone marrow", "Low blood counts can last for weeks after treatment."], ["Infections", "Febrile neutropenia and bacterial, viral and fungal infections can occur."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded at a central facility, then frozen and shipped back, a process taking a few weeks. The patient receives lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, then a single IV infusion of the cells. The CAR-T cells travel to lymph nodes, marrow and other tissues, expand rapidly with a peak between 7 and 14 days, and then contract. Some persist for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from rapid activation and expansion of CAR-T cells releasing IL-6 and other cytokines. It is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated mainly with corticosteroids and supportive care, and seizure prevention may be used. B-cell aplasia lowers antibody levels, so immunoglobulin replacement may be needed. Prolonged low blood counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.",
  "src": ["Yescarta (axicabtagene ciloleucel) FDA label", "Kochenderfer JN et al. Blood 2010", "Neelapu SS et al. ZUMA-1, N Engl J Med 2017"]
},
"brexucabtagene-autoleucel": {
  "origin": "Brexucabtagene autoleucel was developed by Kite Pharma (part of Gilead) using the same CD28-based anti-CD19 CAR as axicabtagene ciloleucel, which came from National Cancer Institute research. The difference lies in manufacturing: an extra T-cell enrichment step removes circulating cancerous B cells from the collected blood, which matters in diseases like mantle cell lymphoma and ALL where tumor cells often circulate. In July 2020 the FDA approved Tecartus for relapsed or refractory mantle cell lymphoma, the first CAR-T therapy for that disease. In October 2021 it became the first CAR-T approved for adults with relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL.",
  "timeline": [["2010", "NCI researchers report early success with CD28-based anti-CD19 CAR-T cells."], ["2020", "FDA approves brexucabtagene autoleucel (Tecartus) for mantle cell lymphoma."], ["2021", "FDA approves Tecartus for adults with relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."]],
  "cell": "The patient's T cells are enriched, then transduced with a gammaretroviral vector carrying the same CAR as axi-cel: a murine anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment (FMC63), a CD28 costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. Enrichment removes CD19-positive tumor cells from the starting product, so they do not interfere with manufacturing by activating the CAR-T cells too early. After infusion, CAR binding to CD19 drives strong T-cell activation and expansion, release of cytokines and MHC-independent killing of CD19-positive B cells by perforin and granzymes.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes, blood and marrow", "Mantle cell lymphoma and ALL cells are destroyed, often leading to deep remissions."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are lost, lowering antibody levels and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever and low blood pressure is very common."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity is frequent and often severe, especially in ALL, with confusion, trouble speaking, tremor and seizures."], ["Bone marrow", "Low blood counts can last for weeks or longer."], ["Infections", "Bacterial, viral and fungal infections are common during recovery."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, enriched, engineered and expanded over a few weeks, then frozen and shipped back. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are given as a single IV infusion. CAR-T cells home to marrow, lymph nodes and blood, peak in about 1 to 2 weeks, then contract while some persist for months. They are not metabolized like small-molecule drugs.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome results from rapid CAR-T activation and cytokine release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids and supportive care and is especially common in adult ALL. B-cell aplasia causes low antibody levels, sometimes needing immunoglobulin replacement. Prolonged low counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.",
  "src": ["Tecartus (brexucabtagene autoleucel) FDA label", "Wang M et al. ZUMA-2, N Engl J Med 2020"]
},
"lisocabtagene-maraleucel": {
  "origin": "Lisocabtagene maraleucel came from research at the Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center, Memorial Sloan Kettering and Seattle Children's Research Institute, which founded Juno Therapeutics in 2013. A key idea was to give CD8 killer T cells and CD4 helper T cells in a defined 1:1 ratio, aiming for more consistent results from patient to patient. Celgene bought Juno in 2018 and was itself acquired by Bristol Myers Squibb in 2019. The FDA approved Breyanzi in February 2021 for relapsed or refractory large B-cell lymphoma. Later approvals made it the first CAR-T approved for chronic lymphocytic leukemia in 2024, along with follicular, mantle cell and marginal zone lymphoma.",
  "timeline": [["2013", "Juno Therapeutics is founded by Seattle and New York cancer centers."], ["2018", "Celgene acquires Juno."], ["2019", "Bristol Myers Squibb acquires Celgene."], ["2021", "FDA approves lisocabtagene maraleucel (Breyanzi) for large B-cell lymphoma."], ["2024", "Breyanzi becomes the first CAR-T approved for CLL/SLL."]],
  "cell": "CD8 and CD4 T cells are separated from the patient's blood and each is transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a CAR: an anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment (FMC63), an IgG4 hinge, a CD28 transmembrane region, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. A truncated EGFR tag is also expressed as a marker. The two cell types are given in equal numbers. CD19 binding activates the CAR-T cells to expand, release cytokines and kill CD19-positive cells without MHC presentation. CD4 cells help CD8 cells expand and persist. The 4-1BB domain is linked to steadier expansion and lower rates of severe CRS than CD28-based CARs.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes, blood and marrow", "CD19-positive lymphoma and CLL cells are destroyed."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are also destroyed, lowering antibody levels and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is common but usually lower grade, starting about 5 days after infusion on average."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity, including confusion and tremor, can occur and occasionally is severe."], ["Bone marrow", "Low blood counts can last for weeks after infusion."], ["Infections", "Serious infections can occur during recovery."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, separated into CD8 and CD4 cells, engineered and expanded over a few weeks, then frozen and returned as two components. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the CD8 and CD4 components are infused IV one after the other. The cells home to lymph nodes, marrow and blood, peak in about 10 to 14 days, then contract while some persist for months. Its lower rate of severe early toxicity has allowed treatment in outpatient settings at experienced centers.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome follows CAR-T activation and IL-6 release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell aplasia causes low antibody levels. Prolonged low counts raise infection risk. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy. Severe CRS is less common than with CD28-based products, consistent with the slower 4-1BB signaling.",
  "src": ["Breyanzi (lisocabtagene maraleucel) FDA label", "Abramson JS et al. TRANSCEND NHL 001, Lancet 2020"]
},
"idecabtagene-vicleucel": {
  "origin": "Idecabtagene vicleucel was developed by bluebird bio in partnership with Celgene, later Bristol Myers Squibb. It applied the CAR-T approach to multiple myeloma by targeting BCMA (B-cell maturation antigen), a protein found on plasma cells and myeloma cells but not on most other tissues. In March 2021 the FDA approved Abecma, the first CAR-T cell therapy for multiple myeloma, for patients whose disease had relapsed after several prior lines of therapy. It was the first cell-based gene therapy approved for this disease.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "BCMA is identified on mature B cells and plasma cells."], ["2013", "Researchers identify BCMA as a promising CAR-T target in myeloma."], ["2021", "FDA approves idecabtagene vicleucel (Abecma), the first CAR-T for multiple myeloma."]],
  "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a CAR made of a murine anti-BCMA single-chain variable fragment, a CD8 hinge and transmembrane domain, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. When the CAR binds BCMA (TNFRSF17) on plasma cells, the T cell is activated without MHC presentation. It releases perforin and granzymes, killing the myeloma cell, secretes cytokines and multiplies. Because normal plasma cells carry BCMA, they are also eliminated, which lowers antibody production.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are destroyed, often lowering paraprotein to very low levels."], ["Immune system", "Normal plasma cells are lost, causing low antibody levels and infections."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is very common and often starts within a day of infusion."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low neutrophils, platelets and red cells can last for weeks to months, partly from lymphodepleting chemotherapy and inflammation."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity can occur, including confusion and tremor."], ["Immune activation", "HLH/MAS, an extreme inflammatory state, can occur and be fatal."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded over a few weeks, then frozen and shipped back. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are given as one IV infusion. They home to the marrow and lymphoid tissues, expand to a peak around 11 days and then contract, with some persisting for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome is caused by CAR-T activation and cytokine release and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. Prolonged low blood counts are a major risk and can lead to bleeding and infection. Loss of normal plasma cells causes low antibody levels, treated with immunoglobulin replacement. HLH/MAS reflects runaway immune activation. A boxed warning notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.",
  "src": ["Abecma (idecabtagene vicleucel) FDA label", "Munshi NC et al. KarMMa, N Engl J Med 2021"]
},
"ciltacabtagene-autoleucel": {
  "origin": "Ciltacabtagene autoleucel was developed by Legend Biotech, a company based in Nanjing, China, and later in partnership with Janssen (Johnson & Johnson). Its CAR is unusual: instead of a standard antibody fragment, it uses two single-domain antibodies (VHH fragments) of the kind made naturally by camels and llamas, each binding a different part of BCMA, which gives a very strong grip on myeloma cells. Early results from China were striking. The FDA approved Carvykti in February 2022 for heavily pretreated multiple myeloma, and in 2024 for earlier use in lenalidomide-refractory disease after at least one prior line.",
  "timeline": [["1993", "Belgian researchers report that camels make antibodies with only heavy chains."], ["2017", "Legend Biotech reports early cilta-cel results from China."], ["2022", "FDA approves ciltacabtagene autoleucel (Carvykti) for relapsed multiple myeloma."], ["2024", "FDA approves Carvykti after at least one prior line in lenalidomide-refractory myeloma."]],
  "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a CAR with two BCMA-binding single-domain antibodies (camelid VHH) arranged in tandem, a CD8 hinge and transmembrane domain, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. The two binders recognize different epitopes on BCMA, increasing avidity. When the CAR binds BCMA on plasma cells, the T cell is activated without MHC, expands strongly, releases cytokines and kills BCMA-positive cells with perforin and granzymes. Normal plasma cells are also eliminated, lowering antibody production.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Myeloma cells are destroyed, and many patients reach deep, lasting remission."], ["Immune system", "Loss of normal plasma cells lowers antibody levels and raises infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is very common, with a median onset around 7 days, later than with many CAR-T products."], ["Brain and nerves", "Delayed neurotoxicity can appear weeks to months later, including parkinsonism, cranial nerve palsies (often facial) and Guillain-Barre syndrome."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low blood counts can be prolonged for weeks after infusion."], ["Immune activation", "HLH/MAS, a runaway inflammatory state, can occur."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded over several weeks, then frozen and sent back. Bridging therapy may be given while waiting. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are infused IV once. They home to the marrow and expand to a peak around 13 days before contracting, with some persisting for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome follows CAR-T activation and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Early neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. Delayed movement and nerve disorders, such as parkinsonism, are a distinctive boxed warning risk; they may be linked to high CAR-T expansion and possibly low-level BCMA in parts of the brain, and they may not fully recover. Prolonged low counts and low antibody levels raise infection risk. A boxed warning also notes rare T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.",
  "fun": "The two binding domains in cilta-cel's CAR are modeled on single-domain antibodies first discovered in camels, which make antibodies built from heavy chains alone.",
  "src": ["Carvykti (ciltacabtagene autoleucel) FDA label", "Berdeja JG et al. CARTITUDE-1, Lancet 2021", "Hamers-Casterman C et al. Nature 1993"]
},
"obecabtagene-autoleucel": {
  "origin": "Obecabtagene autoleucel was developed by Autolus Therapeutics, a company spun out of University College London, based on work by Martin Pule and colleagues. They designed a new anti-CD19 binder, CAT19, that lets go of its target quickly (a fast off-rate). The idea was that a gentler, more natural-like T-cell engagement would reduce overactivation and toxicity while helping the CAR-T cells persist longer. In November 2024 the FDA approved Aucatzyl for adults with relapsed or refractory B-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukemia, a group that often tolerates other CAR-T products poorly.",
  "timeline": [["2014", "Autolus Therapeutics is founded from University College London research."], ["2024", "FDA approves obecabtagene autoleucel (Aucatzyl) for adult relapsed or refractory B-cell ALL."]],
  "cell": "The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a second-generation CAR: the CAT19 anti-CD19 single-chain variable fragment, a 4-1BB costimulatory domain and the CD3-zeta signaling domain. CAT19 binds CD19 strongly enough to trigger killing but releases it quickly. This fast off-rate is designed to mimic the brief contacts of natural T-cell receptors, so each CAR-T cell is not overstimulated. When engaged, the cells kill CD19-positive cells without MHC, release cytokines and expand. The aim is less cytokine release and less exhaustion, which could support persistence.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow and blood", "Leukemic B blasts are destroyed, and many adults reach remission."], ["Immune system", "Normal B cells are also destroyed, lowering antibody levels and raising infection risk."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome is common, though severe CRS has been less frequent than with some other CD19 CAR-T products in adult ALL."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity (ICANS), including confusion and seizures, can occur."], ["Bone marrow (normal)", "Low blood counts can be prolonged for weeks after infusion."], ["Infections", "Serious infections are a leading risk during recovery."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded, then frozen and returned. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the dose is split: part is infused first and the rest about 10 days later, with the size of the first part adjusted to the amount of leukemia in the marrow. This tailoring aims to lower toxicity in people with high disease burden. CAR-T cells expand over the first weeks and can persist for months.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome comes from CAR-T activation and release of IL-6 and other cytokines and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. Neurotoxicity is treated with corticosteroids. B-cell loss and prolonged low counts cause infections, which can be fatal. HLH/MAS can occur. A boxed warning notes rare secondary T-cell cancers after CAR-T therapy.",
  "src": ["Aucatzyl (obecabtagene autoleucel) FDA label", "Roddie C et al. FELIX trial, N Engl J Med 2024"]
},
"afamitresgene-autoleucel": {
  "origin": "Afamitresgene autoleucel was developed by Adaptimmune, a company formed from research at the University of Oxford on engineering T-cell receptors with enhanced binding. Unlike CAR-T products, it gives T cells a modified natural T-cell receptor, which lets them see protein fragments from inside cancer cells. Its target is MAGE-A4, a 'cancer-testis antigen' that is made by many tumors, including most synovial sarcomas, but is silent in normal adult tissues except the testis. In August 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to Tecelra for synovial sarcoma, the first engineered T-cell receptor therapy approved in the US and the first new treatment for this rare soft tissue cancer in more than a decade.",
  "timeline": [["1991", "The first MAGE cancer-testis antigen is discovered in Belgium."], ["2008", "Adaptimmune is founded to develop engineered T-cell receptors."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for afamitresgene autoleucel (Tecelra), the first engineered TCR therapy."]],
  "cell": "Cells chop up internal proteins and display the fragments on HLA class I molecules. MAGE-A4 is an intracellular protein, so an antibody or CAR cannot reach it, but a T-cell receptor can see its peptide when it is displayed on HLA-A*02. The patient's T cells are transduced with a lentiviral vector encoding a T-cell receptor engineered for high affinity to a MAGE-A4 peptide bound to certain HLA-A*02 types. When these T cells meet a tumor cell displaying the complex, the TCR signals through the natural CD3 machinery, activating the T cell to release cytokines and kill with perforin and granzymes. Because recognition is HLA-restricted, patients must have a matching HLA-A*02 type and a MAGE-A4-positive tumor.",
  "body": [["Tumor", "Synovial sarcoma cells displaying MAGE-A4 are killed, and tumors can shrink."], ["Whole body (cytokines)", "Cytokine release syndrome with fever, fast heart rate and low blood pressure is common."], ["Bone marrow", "Prolonged low lymphocytes, neutrophils and platelets occur, largely from lymphodepleting chemotherapy."], ["Brain", "Neurotoxicity (ICANS) can occur, though usually less than with CD19 CAR-T."], ["Infections", "Low blood counts raise the risk of serious infections."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common."]],
  "journey": "T cells are collected by leukapheresis, engineered and expanded, then frozen and returned. After lymphodepleting chemotherapy with fludarabine and cyclophosphamide, the cells are given as one IV infusion. They expand in the first 1 to 2 weeks and travel through the blood into tumors. Some persist for months. They are not metabolized like drugs.",
  "sidefx": "Cytokine release syndrome results from mass T-cell activation and is treated with tocilizumab and corticosteroids. ICANS is treated with corticosteroids. Prolonged low blood counts come from the lymphodepleting regimen and immune activation, raising infection and bleeding risk. Because the cells are HLA-restricted and MAGE-A4 is absent from most normal tissues, off-tumor injury has been limited. A boxed warning notes the risk of severe or fatal CRS.",
  "fun": "MAGE stands for melanoma-associated antigen, and the first member of the family was discovered by Thierry Boon's team in Brussels as a protein that patients' own T cells could recognize.",
  "src": ["Tecelra (afamitresgene autoleucel) FDA label", "D'Angelo SP et al. SPEARHEAD-1, Lancet 2024", "van der Bruggen P et al. Science 1991"]
},
"lifileucel": {
  "origin": "Lifileucel is built on decades of work by Steven Rosenberg at the National Cancer Institute. In the 1980s his team found that lymphocytes taken from inside melanoma tumors, called tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes (TILs), could be grown in large numbers with interleukin-2 and returned to patients. A 1988 report showed tumor regressions in some people with metastatic melanoma. For many years TIL therapy was offered only at a few academic centers. Iovance Biotherapeutics developed a centralized manufacturing process to make it widely available. In February 2024 the FDA granted accelerated approval to Amtagvi for advanced melanoma after PD-1 therapy, the first cellular therapy approved for a solid tumor.",
  "timeline": [["1986", "Rosenberg's team describes the potency of tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes in animals."], ["1988", "Rosenberg and colleagues report melanoma regressions with TIL therapy in patients."], ["1992", "FDA approves high-dose IL-2 (aldesleukin) for metastatic kidney cancer."], ["2024", "FDA grants accelerated approval for lifileucel (Amtagvi), the first cell therapy for a solid tumor."]],
  "cell": "Melanomas are often infiltrated by T cells that already recognize the tumor, but inside the tumor they are outnumbered and suppressed. A piece of tumor is removed surgically, and these TILs, mostly CD8 and CD4 T cells, are expanded in the lab with IL-2 into billions of cells. The cells are not genetically modified. Their natural, polyclonal T-cell receptors recognize many targets, including neoantigens created by the many UV-induced mutations in melanoma, presented on MHC class I and II. Before infusion, lymphodepleting chemotherapy removes competing lymphocytes and regulatory T cells and raises homeostatic cytokines such as IL-7 and IL-15. After infusion, high-dose IL-2 (aldesleukin) supports their expansion, and the TILs then kill tumor cells with perforin, granzymes and cytokines.",
  "body": [["Melanoma tumors", "The returned T cells attack tumor cells at many sites, and some responses last years."], ["Whole body (IL-2 and cytokines)", "Fever, chills and fast heart rate are very common, mostly from IL-2 and the immune response."], ["Blood vessels", "Capillary leak from IL-2 can cause low blood pressure, edema, low oxygen and kidney injury."], ["Bone marrow", "Lymphodepleting chemotherapy causes severe, sometimes prolonged low blood counts with febrile neutropenia."], ["Heart", "Arrhythmias can occur during the IL-2 period."], ["Skin and hair", "Rash, hair loss and sometimes vitiligo-like pigment loss can occur as T cells also target normal melanocytes."]],
  "journey": "A tumor is surgically removed and shipped to a central facility, where TILs are grown over a few weeks, then frozen and returned. The patient receives a week of nonmyeloablative lymphodepleting chemotherapy with cyclophosphamide and fludarabine, then a single IV infusion of TILs, followed by a short course of high-dose IL-2 to support expansion. The cells spread through the blood and home to tumor sites. IL-2 itself has a short half-life of about 85 minutes. Responding TILs can persist for months.",
  "sidefx": "Many acute side effects come from high-dose IL-2, which causes fever, chills and capillary leak with low blood pressure, edema, low oxygen and kidney injury, and from the lymphodepleting chemotherapy, which causes prolonged low blood counts and infection risk. Treatment is given in hospitals with intensive care support. A boxed warning covers treatment-related deaths, prolonged severe cytopenia, severe infection, cardiopulmonary problems and kidney impairment. Corticosteroids are avoided where possible because they can blunt the T cells.",
  "fun": "Each lifileucel product is unique to one person, grown from the T cells that were already fighting inside that person's own tumor.",
  "src": ["Amtagvi (lifileucel) FDA label", "Rosenberg SA et al. N Engl J Med 1988", "Chesney J et al. J Immunother Cancer 2022"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"sipuleucel-t": {
  "origin": "Sipuleucel-T grew out of a simple idea: if the immune system could be taught to see a protein that prostate cancer cells carry, it might attack the tumor on its own. Researchers at the Seattle-area company Dendreon chose prostatic acid phosphatase (PAP), an enzyme made by most prostate cancers, and fused it to GM-CSF, an immune growth signal, to make the lab protein PA2024. Each patient's own white blood cells are collected, mixed with PA2024 so the antigen-presenting cells load up on PAP, and then given back. In 2010 the IMPACT trial showed a modest survival gain in men with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, and the FDA approved Provenge that year. It was the first autologous cellular immunotherapy approved for cancer in the US, and it opened the door for later personalized cell therapies.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Dendreon develops the PAP-GM-CSF fusion antigen approach for prostate cancer."], ["2010", "The IMPACT trial reports a survival benefit in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2010", "FDA approves Provenge, the first autologous cellular immunotherapy for cancer."]],
  "cell": "About three days before each infusion, the patient's peripheral blood mononuclear cells are collected by leukapheresis. In the lab they are cultured with PA2024, a fusion of prostatic acid phosphatase and GM-CSF. The GM-CSF part binds GM-CSF receptors on antigen-presenting cells such as dendritic cells, which activates them and helps them take up the attached PAP. The cells break PAP into peptides and display them on MHC class I and II molecules, along with co-stimulatory signals. Upregulation of CD54 (ICAM-1) is used as the potency marker. After infusion, these activated antigen-presenting cells prime helper and cytotoxic T cells that recognize PAP. The primed T cells can then find and kill PAP-expressing prostate cancer cells. The effect is active immunity, so it builds over weeks and may extend survival without shrinking tumors on scans.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Primes T cells and antibody responses against prostatic acid phosphatase, a protein on most prostate cancer cells, creating antigen-specific antitumor immunity."], ["Prostate cancer", "Survival benefit is seen without consistent tumor shrinkage or PSA drop, because the immune effect works slowly and indirectly."], ["Whole body (infusion)", "Cytokines released by the activated cells cause chills, fever, fatigue and body aches, usually within a day of each infusion."], ["Veins and catheters", "Repeated leukapheresis may need a central line, which carries a risk of catheter infection and clots."], ["Heart and vessels", "Thromboembolic events, stroke and heart attack have been reported in trials, though a direct cause is not fully defined."]],
  "journey": "Sipuleucel-T is a living cell product, so it has no classic pharmacokinetics. The patient's own cells are collected, activated in a central lab for a few days, shipped back in an insulated container and infused through a vein. The activated cells circulate and travel to lymphoid tissue, where they present PAP to T cells. The cells themselves are not metabolized by liver enzymes. Their job is done once T cells are primed, and the resulting immune response develops over weeks after the full course.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are infusion reactions. The activated cells and the GM-CSF in the antigen release cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF, which reset the body's temperature set point and cause chills, fever, fatigue, back pain and joint aches. Rarely the reaction includes high blood pressure or low oxygen. Catheter infections come from the venous access needed for repeated collection. Because the product is made only for one person, matching the patient to the bag before infusion is critical. There is no antidote; reactions are managed with supportive care.",
  "fun": "Each Provenge dose is made for only one person, using that person's own blood cells, so the whole supply chain from collection to infusion runs on a tight clock of a few days.",
  "src": ["Provenge (sipuleucel-T) FDA label", "Kantoff PW et al. N Engl J Med 2010 (IMPACT trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"talimogene-laherparepvec": {
  "origin": "Talimogene laherparepvec, or T-VEC, began as a herpes simplex virus type 1 strain called JS1, taken from a cold sore. Scientists at the British biotech company BioVex, founded by Robert Coffin, engineered the virus to grow in cancer cells but not in healthy cells and added the gene for human GM-CSF to rally the immune system. Amgen bought BioVex in 2011. After the OPTiM trial showed durable responses in injected melanoma lesions, the FDA approved Imlygic in October 2015. It was the first oncolytic virus therapy approved in the United States, turning a decades-old idea, that viruses can kill tumors, into a licensed treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "BioVex scientists begin engineering herpes simplex virus as a cancer-killing agent."], ["2011", "Amgen acquires BioVex and its oncolytic virus program."], ["2015", "FDA approves Imlygic, the first oncolytic viral therapy in the US."]],
  "cell": "T-VEC is a live, attenuated HSV-1 with two key deletions and one insertion. Deleting ICP34.5 removes the gene the virus normally uses to overcome the cell's protein kinase R (PKR) antiviral defense. Healthy cells with working PKR and interferon pathways shut down viral protein synthesis, but many tumor cells have defective pathways, so the virus replicates selectively in them. Deleting ICP47 keeps the infected cell able to load viral and tumor antigens onto MHC class I, so cytotoxic T cells can see it. The inserted human GM-CSF gene is expressed as the virus multiplies. Viral replication ends in lysis, which spills tumor antigens and GM-CSF into the area. GM-CSF recruits and matures dendritic cells, which carry tumor antigens to lymph nodes and prime T cells. This can shrink both injected lesions and, sometimes, lesions that were never injected.",
  "body": [["Injected tumors", "Direct viral lysis and local inflammation destroy injected melanoma nodules in the skin, under the skin and in lymph nodes."], ["Immune system", "Released tumor antigens and GM-CSF prime T cells, which can cause regression of some uninjected lesions."], ["Whole body", "Viral replication and cytokines cause flu-like illness, fever, chills and fatigue, mostly after the first injections."], ["Skin", "Cellulitis, ulceration or necrosis can occur at injection sites; vitiligo may appear as T cells attack normal melanocytes that share antigens."], ["Herpes risk", "As a live HSV-1, it can cause herpetic lesions in patients or close contacts and is dangerous in people with weak immune systems."]],
  "journey": "T-VEC is injected straight into skin, subcutaneous and nodal lesions, using a fanning pattern to spread it through the tumor. Most of the virus stays in and around the injected tissue, where it multiplies in tumor cells. Small amounts of viral DNA can be detected for a short time in blood and urine, and live virus can be on the injection site surface, which is why the site is covered. The virus is not broken down by liver enzymes; the immune system clears it. Responses take months to appear and can last for years.",
  "sidefx": "Flu-like symptoms come from the body's antiviral response to a replicating virus and from GM-CSF. Injection site pain, cellulitis and necrosis reflect local tumor lysis and inflammation. Because it is a live herpes virus, it can spread to others through contact with the site and can cause disseminated herpes in immunocompromised people, which is why pregnant or immunocompromised staff do not handle it. The virus remains sensitive to acyclovir, which can be used to treat herpetic complications, though that also blocks the therapy. Vitiligo reflects immune attack on normal pigment cells.",
  "fun": "The parent virus strain was isolated from a cold sore, so a common nuisance virus became the first approved cancer-killing virus in the US.",
  "src": ["Imlygic (talimogene laherparepvec) FDA label", "Andtbacka RH et al. J Clin Oncol 2015 (OPTiM trial)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"nadofaragene-firadenovec": {
  "origin": "Interferon alfa has long been known to slow bladder cancer, but bladder lining cells lose it quickly when it is simply poured in. Researchers set out to make the bladder produce its own interferon. They packed the human interferon alfa-2b gene into a non-replicating adenovirus vector and paired it with Syn3, a surfactant that helps the virus enter bladder cells. The program passed through several companies before Ferring Pharmaceuticals completed development. In December 2022 the FDA approved Adstiladrin for BCG-unresponsive non-muscle invasive bladder cancer with carcinoma in situ. It was the first gene therapy approved for bladder cancer and gave patients who had failed BCG an option other than removing the bladder.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Early studies test an adenovirus carrying the interferon alfa-2b gene with the Syn3 enhancer in the bladder."], ["2022", "FDA approves Adstiladrin, the first gene therapy for bladder cancer."]],
  "cell": "Nadofaragene firadenovec is a recombinant adenovirus serotype 5 with the genes needed for replication removed, so it cannot copy itself. The Syn3 surfactant in the formulation loosens the glycosaminoglycan layer and helps the vector enter urothelial cells. Once inside, the vector delivers the human interferon alfa-2b gene to the nucleus, where it stays as an episome without integrating into chromosomes. The cells then transcribe and secrete interferon alfa-2b. The interferon binds type I interferon receptors (IFNAR1 and IFNAR2) on tumor and immune cells, activating JAK1 and TYK2 and STAT signaling. This switches on genes that slow cell division, promote apoptosis, block new vessel growth and increase MHC expression. It also activates NK cells and T cells in the bladder wall. Because transduced cells keep making interferon for weeks, one instillation lasts a long time.",
  "body": [["Bladder lining", "Transduced urothelial cells secrete interferon alfa-2b that slows growth and triggers death of carcinoma in situ and papillary tumor cells."], ["Local immune system", "Interferon recruits and activates NK cells, T cells and dendritic cells in the bladder wall and boosts antigen display on tumor cells."], ["Urinary tract", "Instillation and local inflammation cause bladder spasm, urgency, painful urination, blood in urine and discharge."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, chills and fever reflect small amounts of interferon effect and the local immune reaction."]],
  "journey": "The suspension is instilled through a urinary catheter and held in the bladder for about an hour while the patient changes position so the whole lining is coated. The vector enters lining cells, and almost none reaches the bloodstream. Interferon levels in the bladder rise within days and stay up for weeks as the transduced cells keep working, which allows dosing every three months. The vector does not replicate and is cleared as the lining cells turn over. Vector DNA is shed in urine for several days, so urine is handled with care.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local. The catheter and the irritant effect of the instillation cause discharge, spasm, urgency and dysuria, and inflammation from interferon causes some blood in the urine. Anticholinergic medication beforehand helps with spasm. Chills, fever and fatigue reflect interferon's flu-like effects. Urinary infections relate to catheterization. The main serious risk is not a toxicity but delay: if the cancer does not respond, waiting too long to remove the bladder can allow it to become muscle-invasive. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Adstiladrin (nadofaragene firadenovec-vncg) FDA label", "Boorjian SA et al. Lancet Oncol 2021", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"bcg-live-intravesical": {
  "origin": "BCG stands for Bacillus Calmette-Guerin, named for Albert Calmette and Camille Guerin of the Pasteur Institute in Lille, France. Starting in 1908 they grew a strain of Mycobacterium bovis, the cattle tuberculosis germ, on a potato, bile and glycerin medium and transferred it 230 times over 13 years until it lost its ability to cause disease. It was first given as a tuberculosis vaccine in 1921. In 1929 Raymond Pearl noted less cancer at autopsy in people who had tuberculosis, which sparked interest in BCG against tumors. In 1976 Alvaro Morales and colleagues in Canada reported that BCG placed into the bladder prevented bladder cancer from coming back. Around 1990 the FDA approved intravesical BCG, and it remains the standard immunotherapy for high-risk non-muscle invasive bladder cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1908", "Calmette and Guerin begin serial passage of Mycobacterium bovis in Lille."], ["1921", "BCG is first given as a tuberculosis vaccine."], ["1929", "Raymond Pearl reports less cancer at autopsy in people with tuberculosis."], ["1976", "Morales and colleagues report intravesical BCG for superficial bladder cancer."], ["c. 1990", "FDA approves intravesical BCG for bladder carcinoma in situ."]],
  "cell": "Live attenuated M. bovis is instilled into the bladder and sticks to the urothelium by binding fibronectin, exposed where the lining is damaged. Urothelial and tumor cells take up the bacteria. Pattern-recognition receptors such as Toll-like receptors 2 and 4 sense mycobacterial cell wall components, and infected cells release IL-6, IL-8 and other chemokines. Neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, NK cells and T cells pour into the bladder wall. A Th1-type response develops, with IL-2, IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha, forming granulomas. Cytotoxic T cells, NK cells and activated macrophages kill tumor cells through perforin, granzymes, TRAIL and other pathways. Repeated instillations amplify this local immune response, which explains both its antitumor effect and the cystitis it causes.",
  "body": [["Bladder", "Intense local inflammation and immune cell influx destroy carcinoma in situ and lower the chance that papillary tumors return after surgery."], ["Urinary tract", "Cystitis causes burning, frequency, urgency and blood in urine, usually peaking a day or two after each treatment."], ["Whole body", "Fever, malaise and flu-like symptoms reflect cytokines released by the immune response."], ["Prostate and testes", "BCG can spread locally and cause granulomatous prostatitis or epididymitis."], ["Lungs, liver, joints", "Rarely BCG enters the blood and causes pneumonitis, hepatitis, reactive arthritis or a life-threatening disseminated infection."]],
  "journey": "BCG is instilled through a catheter by gravity and held in the bladder for about two hours before it is voided. It acts on the bladder surface and wall, and normally very little enters the bloodstream. Trauma to the lining, such as from a difficult catheterization, can let organisms into the blood, which is why treatment is delayed after bleeding or recent surgery. The bacteria are not metabolized; the immune system kills and clears them. Live organisms are shed in urine for hours to days, so voided urine is disinfected.",
  "sidefx": "The irritative bladder symptoms are the intended immune reaction showing itself as cystitis. Fever and malaise come from cytokines entering the circulation. BCG is a live mycobacterium, so if it escapes the bladder it can infect the prostate, epididymis, lungs, liver or bloodstream, and disseminated BCG sepsis can be fatal. BCG is naturally resistant to pyrazinamide, so systemic infections are treated with other antituberculosis drugs such as isoniazid, rifampin and ethambutol, sometimes with steroids. Handling precautions protect staff because it is infectious.",
  "fun": "The same bacterium used in the bladder is the BCG tuberculosis vaccine, which has been given to billions of people worldwide, making it one of the most widely used vaccines in history.",
  "src": ["TICE BCG FDA label", "Morales A et al. J Urol 1976", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"nogapendekin-alfa-inbakicept": {
  "origin": "Interleukin-15 is a natural cytokine that makes natural killer cells and killer T cells grow, but on its own it lasts only minutes in the body. Scientists at Altor BioScience, led by Hing Wong, created a souped-up complex: a mutant IL-15 (the N72D superagonist) bound to the sushi domain of its partner receptor IL-15R alpha, fused to an antibody Fc tail for stability. The compound, called ALT-803 and later N-803, moved to ImmunityBio, the company founded by Patrick Soon-Shiong. Given into the bladder together with BCG, it produced lasting complete responses in BCG-unresponsive carcinoma in situ. The FDA approved it as Anktiva in April 2024.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Altor BioScience develops the IL-15 superagonist complex ALT-803."], ["2010s", "The program moves to ImmunityBio as N-803."], ["2024", "FDA approves Anktiva with BCG for BCG-unresponsive bladder carcinoma in situ."]],
  "cell": "Nogapendekin alfa is an IL-15 variant with an asparagine to aspartate change at position 72 that increases its activity. Inbakicept is a dimer of the IL-15 receptor alpha sushi domain fused to a human IgG1 Fc. Together they mimic trans-presentation, the natural way IL-15 is held out to immune cells by IL-15R alpha on dendritic cells and macrophages. The complex binds the shared IL-2/IL-15 receptor beta (CD122) and common gamma chain (CD132) on NK cells and CD8 T cells. This activates JAK1 and JAK3, which phosphorylate STAT5, along with PI3K-AKT and MAPK pathways. The result is proliferation, survival and greater cytotoxic activity of NK and memory CD8 T cells, with little expansion of regulatory T cells because it does not use CD25. Combined with BCG, which brings immune cells into the bladder, it strengthens their killing of tumor cells.",
  "body": [["Bladder", "Activated NK cells and CD8 T cells in the bladder wall attack carcinoma in situ and papillary tumor cells alongside the BCG response."], ["Urinary tract", "Instillation with BCG causes dysuria, frequency, blood in urine and urinary infections."], ["Whole body", "Fatigue, chills, fever and muscle aches reflect immune activation, but blood levels stay low because little is absorbed."]],
  "journey": "The protein is mixed with BCG and instilled through a urinary catheter, then held in the bladder before voiding. It acts mainly on immune cells in and under the bladder lining. Very little crosses into the bloodstream, so systemic exposure is minimal and its blood kinetics are not well characterized. Like other proteins, any that is absorbed is broken down into amino acids. The immune effect builds over induction and maintenance courses.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the combined bladder instillation: catheter trauma and BCG cystitis cause burning, frequency and hematuria, and catheterization brings a risk of urinary infection. Systemic immune activation is small because little reaches the blood. Because it is given with live BCG, BCG infection risks and handling precautions apply. As with other bladder-sparing options, waiting too long to remove the bladder if the cancer does not respond can allow progression. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Anktiva (nogapendekin alfa inbakicept-pmln) FDA label", "Chamie K et al. NEJM Evidence 2023 (QUILT 3.032)"]
},
"aldesleukin": {
  "origin": "In 1976 Doris Morgan, Francis Ruscetti and Robert Gallo at the US National Cancer Institute found that a factor made by stimulated lymphocytes kept T cells growing in culture. They called it T cell growth factor; it was later named interleukin-2. Steven Rosenberg at the NCI saw a way to use it against cancer. Once Cetus Corporation produced large amounts of recombinant IL-2 in E. coli, Rosenberg's team gave high doses to patients, and in 1984 a woman with metastatic melanoma had a complete, lasting remission. The FDA approved aldesleukin (Proleukin) for metastatic kidney cancer in 1992 and for metastatic melanoma in 1998. It was one of the first proofs that the immune system alone could cure some advanced cancers.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Morgan, Ruscetti and Gallo describe T cell growth factor, later called IL-2."], ["1983", "The IL-2 gene is cloned, allowing recombinant production."], ["1984", "Rosenberg's team at the NCI sees a durable complete response with high-dose IL-2."], ["1992", "FDA approves aldesleukin for metastatic renal cell carcinoma."], ["1998", "FDA approves aldesleukin for metastatic melanoma."]],
  "cell": "Aldesleukin is a recombinant IL-2 that lacks the first alanine and has serine instead of cysteine at position 125, which prevents misfolding. It binds the IL-2 receptor on T cells and NK cells. The high-affinity receptor has three chains: CD25 (alpha), CD122 (beta) and CD132 (common gamma). The intermediate-affinity form has only CD122 and CD132 and is found on NK cells and resting memory T cells, which high doses reach. Binding brings JAK1 (on CD122) and JAK3 (on CD132) together, and they phosphorylate STAT5, along with PI3K-AKT and MAPK signaling. This drives clonal expansion of activated cytotoxic T cells and NK cells and creates lymphokine-activated killer cells. The activated cells release a second wave of cytokines, including IFN-gamma, TNF-alpha and IL-1. These kill tumor cells in some patients but also damage blood vessel lining, which leads to capillary leak.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "T cells and NK cells multiply and become more cytotoxic, producing durable responses in a minority of patients with kidney cancer or melanoma."], ["Blood vessels", "Cytokines and activated lymphocytes injure the endothelium, so fluid and protein leak into tissues: capillary leak syndrome with low blood pressure, edema and weight gain."], ["Heart", "Low blood pressure and cytokines cause fast heart rate, atrial arrhythmias and, rarely, myocardial ischemia."], ["Lungs", "Leaked fluid causes pulmonary edema and pleural effusions, leading to shortness of breath."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood pressure and reduced renal blood flow cause oliguria and rising creatinine, usually reversible."], ["Brain", "Lethargy, confusion and somnolence can progress to coma, so mental status is watched closely."], ["Neutrophils", "Neutrophil chemotaxis is impaired, raising the risk of bacterial infections, especially of central lines."]],
  "journey": "Aldesleukin is given as a short IV infusion in the hospital. It spreads quickly into the blood and extracellular fluid, with a distribution half-life of about 13 minutes and an elimination half-life of about 85 minutes. The kidneys filter it, and proximal tubule cells break it down into amino acids. Because it is cleared so fast, it is given repeatedly in scheduled hospital courses. Fever and chills start within hours, and most toxicities fade within days after the last dose.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects are the immune cascade spilling over. Cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-1 reset the brain's temperature set point, causing fever and rigors. Vessel injury causes capillary leak, which explains hypotension, edema, effusions, kidney injury and organ hypoperfusion. Impaired neutrophil function raises infection risk. IL-2 can unmask autoimmune disease, especially thyroiditis. There is no specific antidote. Care is supportive with fluids and vasopressors, and stopping doses reverses most effects. Dexamethasone can blunt toxicity but also blunts the antitumor effect.",
  "fun": "Steven Rosenberg treated 66 patients with IL-2 related therapies before seeing his first major response, and that first patient remained cancer-free for decades.",
  "src": ["Proleukin (aldesleukin) FDA label", "Morgan DA, Ruscetti FW, Gallo R. Science 1976", "Rosenberg SA. J Immunol 2014 (IL-2: the first effective immunotherapy)"]
},
"lutetium-lu-177-dotatate": {
  "origin": "Lutetium Lu 177 dotatate is a guided missile built on the hormone somatostatin, which Roger Guillemin's group described in 1973. Many neuroendocrine tumors are covered with somatostatin receptors, especially subtype 2. In the 1980s Sandoz made octreotide, a stable somatostatin analog, and radiolabeled versions were soon used to image these tumors. Researchers led by Eric Krenning at Erasmus Medical Center in Rotterdam then attached therapeutic radioisotopes. Their lutetium-177 version of the analog dotatate, studied from around 2000, shrank tumors and extended control. The French company Advanced Accelerator Applications ran the NETTER-1 trial, and the FDA approved Lutathera in January 2018 for somatostatin receptor-positive gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors. Novartis acquired the company that year.",
  "timeline": [["1973", "Somatostatin is described by Roger Guillemin's laboratory."], ["1980s", "Octreotide is developed, and radiolabeled analogs are used to image neuroendocrine tumors."], ["2000s", "Erasmus MC in Rotterdam treats patients with lutetium-177 dotatate."], ["2018", "FDA approves Lutathera after the NETTER-1 trial."]],
  "cell": "Dotatate (DOTA-Tyr3-octreotate) is a somatostatin analog that binds somatostatin receptor subtype 2 (SSTR2) with high affinity. The DOTA cage tightly holds lutetium-177. After binding, the receptor-ligand complex is internalized, so the radioactive atom is trapped inside the tumor cell. Lutetium-177 decays by emitting beta particles (electrons) that travel up to about 2 millimeters in tissue. These electrons ionize water and DNA, creating free radicals and single and double-strand DNA breaks. Cells that cannot repair the damage die, regardless of where they are in the cell cycle. Because the range spans many cell widths, neighboring tumor cells that lack the receptor are also hit (the crossfire effect). Lutetium-177 also gives off gamma photons, which allow imaging of where the drug went.",
  "body": [["Neuroendocrine tumors", "Concentrated radiation in SSTR2-positive tumors slows growth and extends progression-free survival in gut and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors."], ["Bone marrow", "Circulating drug irradiates the marrow, lowering lymphocytes, platelets, red cells and neutrophils; a small risk of MDS or leukemia follows."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys filter the peptide and partly reabsorb it in the proximal tubule, so a co-infused amino acid solution of lysine and arginine is given to block this uptake."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting come mostly from the amino acid infusion rather than the radiation."], ["Hormones", "Damaged tumor cells can release stored hormones and cause a neuroendocrine hormonal crisis with flushing, diarrhea or blood pressure swings."], ["Liver", "Liver metastases receive radiation, and liver enzymes can rise."]],
  "journey": "It is infused into a vein, with amino acids before and during to protect the kidneys. It leaves the blood quickly, with an effective blood half-life of a few hours, and binds receptor-rich tumors within hours. The drug is not metabolized; lutetium-177 simply decays, with a physical half-life of about 6.65 days, so the tumor receives radiation over weeks. Unbound drug is filtered by the kidneys, and most of it leaves in the urine within two days, which is why urine is handled with radiation precautions.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow where the radioactivity goes. Marrow exposure causes low blood counts and, rarely, myelodysplastic syndrome or acute leukemia years later. The kidneys handle the peptide, so radiation nephropathy is possible, which the amino acid infusion reduces. The concentrated amino acids cause nausea and can cause high potassium. Hormone release from dying tumors causes crises. Long-acting somatostatin analogs are paused before treatment because they compete for the same receptor. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "The same tumor-seeking molecule can carry an imaging isotope like gallium-68 for a PET scan or lutetium-177 for treatment, a pairing called theranostics.",
  "src": ["Lutathera (lutetium Lu 177 dotatate) FDA label", "Strosberg J et al. N Engl J Med 2017 (NETTER-1)", "Kwekkeboom DJ, Krenning EP et al. J Clin Oncol 2008"]
},
"lutetium-lu-177-vipivotide-tetraxetan": {
  "origin": "Prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) is an enzyme, glutamate carboxypeptidase II, packed onto the surface of most prostate cancer cells. Researchers at the German Cancer Research Center (DKFZ) and Heidelberg University designed small molecules that grab PSMA tightly, and around 2015 they described PSMA-617, a ligand with a chelator that can hold lutetium-177. Early compassionate use in Germany produced striking drops in PSA. Endocyte licensed the compound, Novartis acquired Endocyte in 2018, and the VISION trial showed longer survival in men with PSMA-positive metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer. The FDA approved Pluvicto in March 2022.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "PSMA is characterized as a prostate cancer surface enzyme."], ["2015", "Heidelberg and DKFZ researchers describe the ligand PSMA-617."], ["2018", "Novartis acquires Endocyte, which held rights to PSMA-617."], ["2022", "FDA approves Pluvicto for PSMA-positive metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."]],
  "cell": "Vipivotide tetraxetan (PSMA-617) has a glutamate-urea-lysine motif that fits the active site of PSMA (glutamate carboxypeptidase II, the FOLH1 gene product), a linker, and a DOTA chelator holding lutetium-177. After binding, PSMA is internalized, carrying the radionuclide into the cancer cell. Lutetium-177 emits beta particles that travel up to about 2 millimeters, ionizing water and DNA and causing single and double-strand breaks. Cells that cannot repair the damage die by apoptosis or mitotic catastrophe, independent of cell-cycle phase. The crossfire effect also irradiates nearby tumor cells with lower PSMA levels. Gamma photons allow imaging. Normal tissues that express PSMA, such as salivary and lacrimal glands and kidney tubules, also take up the drug.",
  "body": [["Prostate cancer", "Radiation concentrated in PSMA-positive metastases in bone, lymph nodes and organs lowers PSA and extends survival."], ["Salivary and tear glands", "These glands express PSMA, so radiation causes dry mouth and dry eyes, sometimes long-lasting."], ["Bone marrow", "Radiation to the marrow, often already crowded with bone metastases, causes anemia, low platelets and low white counts."], ["Kidneys", "Tubular uptake and urinary excretion expose the kidneys to radiation and can cause kidney injury."], ["Gut", "Nausea, loss of appetite and constipation are common during treatment."]],
  "journey": "Pluvicto is given by IV injection. It clears from the blood quickly, with a terminal blood half-life of about 41 hours, and binds PSMA-positive tissue within hours. It is not metabolized; lutetium-177 decays with a physical half-life of about 6.65 days, delivering radiation over days to weeks. The unbound drug is filtered by the kidneys and leaves mainly in urine. Patients follow radiation safety steps for a few days to limit exposure to others.",
  "sidefx": "Each main side effect tracks a tissue that holds the drug. PSMA in salivary and lacrimal glands explains xerostomia and dry eye. Marrow exposure explains the cytopenias, which are worse when bone metastases already crowd out normal blood cells. Renal handling explains kidney toxicity, so hydration is encouraged. Radiation exposure can harm sperm, so contraception is discussed. There is no antidote; care is supportive, with transfusions or growth factors for low counts.",
  "fun": "Before treatment, a PSMA PET scan using a gallium-68 or fluorine-18 version of a similar ligand shows which tumors will light up, so doctors can see the target before they hit it.",
  "src": ["Pluvicto (lutetium Lu 177 vipivotide tetraxetan) FDA label", "Sartor O et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (VISION)", "Benesova M et al. J Nucl Med 2015"]
},
"radium-ra-223-dichloride": {
  "origin": "Marie and Pierre Curie discovered the element radium in 1898. Radium is chemically similar to calcium, so the body treats it like calcium and deposits it in bone. Radium-223 is a short-lived isotope that gives off alpha particles, which are powerful but travel less than a tenth of a millimeter. Norwegian researchers Roy Larsen and Oyvind Bruland saw that this could hit bone metastases while sparing most of the marrow, and they founded the company Algeta to develop it, later partnering with Bayer. The ALSYMPCA trial showed longer survival in men with prostate cancer that had spread to bone, and the FDA approved Xofigo in May 2013. It was the first alpha-emitting radiopharmaceutical approved in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1898", "Marie and Pierre Curie discover radium."], ["1990s", "Norwegian researchers propose radium-223 as a bone-seeking alpha emitter."], ["2013", "FDA approves Xofigo, the first alpha-emitting therapy, for prostate cancer bone metastases."]],
  "cell": "Radium-223 is a calcium mimetic. It forms complexes with hydroxyapatite in bone, concentrating where bone is being rapidly built and broken down, such as osteoblastic metastases. As it and its daughter isotopes decay, they release four alpha particles. Alpha particles are helium nuclei with high linear energy transfer: they deposit a lot of energy over a very short path of less than 100 micrometers, only a few cell widths. This creates clustered double-strand DNA breaks that cells cannot easily repair, killing tumor cells, osteoblasts and osteoclasts right next to the deposit. The damage does not depend on oxygen or cell-cycle phase. The short range limits exposure of the nearby marrow compared with beta-emitting bone agents.",
  "body": [["Bone metastases", "Alpha particles kill tumor cells and abnormal bone-forming cells in osteoblastic lesions, slowing disease and extending survival."], ["Bone marrow", "Some marrow lies close to the deposits, so anemia, low platelets and low neutrophils occur, especially with widespread bone disease."], ["Gut", "The small intestine excretes radium, so nausea, diarrhea and vomiting are common."], ["Skeleton", "Fractures are more common when combined with abiraterone and prednisone, so that combination is avoided."]],
  "journey": "Radium-223 is given by slow IV injection. It leaves the blood within hours, going mostly to bone and to the intestine. It is not metabolized; it simply decays with a physical half-life of about 11.4 days, so bone deposits receive radiation over weeks. Unlike most radiopharmaceuticals, it leaves the body mainly in the feces through the small intestine, with only a small amount in urine. This is why stool and body fluids are handled carefully for a time after each injection.",
  "sidefx": "The cytopenias come from alpha radiation reaching marrow cells next to bone deposits. Because the intestine excretes the drug, the gut lining is irradiated, causing nausea and diarrhea. Peripheral edema has also been reported. The fracture and death risk seen with abiraterone is not fully explained, but it led to a warning against that combination. There is no antidote; supportive care includes transfusions and holding treatment until counts recover.",
  "fun": "In the 1920s, workers who painted watch dials with radium paint, the Radium Girls, developed bone damage because radium settles in bone like calcium, the same property Xofigo uses on purpose.",
  "src": ["Xofigo (radium Ra 223 dichloride) FDA label", "Parker C et al. N Engl J Med 2013 (ALSYMPCA)"]
},
"sodium-iodide-i-131": {
  "origin": "The thyroid is the only tissue that greedily pulls iodine from the blood to make thyroid hormone. In 1938 Glenn Seaborg and John Livingood produced iodine-131. Saul Hertz, a physician at Massachusetts General Hospital, worked with MIT physicist Arthur Roberts to study radioactive iodine uptake, and in 1941 he gave the first therapeutic radioiodine to a patient with hyperthyroidism. In the 1940s Samuel Seidlin used it to treat metastatic thyroid cancer. After World War II, reactors made I-131 widely available, and it became one of the first targeted radiation treatments and one of the founding drugs of nuclear medicine. It is still a standard treatment for Graves disease, toxic nodules and differentiated thyroid cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1938", "Seaborg and Livingood produce iodine-131."], ["1941", "Saul Hertz gives the first therapeutic radioiodine for hyperthyroidism."], ["1946", "Seidlin reports radioiodine treatment of metastatic thyroid cancer."], ["1951", "FDA approves sodium iodide I-131 for thyroid conditions."]],
  "cell": "Iodide-131 behaves chemically like ordinary iodide. The sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane of thyroid follicular cells uses the sodium gradient made by the Na-K ATPase to pump iodide into the cell. TSH increases NIS expression. Inside, the iodide moves to the follicle lumen, where thyroid peroxidase oxidizes it and attaches it to tyrosines on thyroglobulin (organification), trapping it in the gland. As I-131 decays, it emits beta particles with a mean range of about half a millimeter that break DNA and kill follicular cells over the next weeks, and gamma rays that allow imaging. Well-differentiated thyroid cancers often keep NIS, so they also take up the isotope, which allows ablation of remnant tissue and treatment of iodine-avid metastases.",
  "body": [["Thyroid", "Radiation destroys overactive thyroid tissue in hyperthyroidism and ablates remaining tissue after thyroid cancer surgery, often leading to permanent hypothyroidism."], ["Thyroid cancer metastases", "Iodine-avid spread to lungs, lymph nodes or bone takes up the isotope and is irradiated."], ["Salivary glands", "These glands also express NIS, so they concentrate the iodine and can become swollen and painful, with dry mouth and taste change."], ["Bone marrow", "Circulating radiation can lower blood counts, especially after large cumulative treatment for cancer."], ["Neck", "Radiation thyroiditis can cause neck tenderness and a short burst of released thyroid hormone."]],
  "journey": "Sodium iodide I-131 is swallowed as a capsule or solution and is almost completely absorbed from the gut. It travels in the blood as free iodide. The thyroid traps it within hours, with peak uptake around 24 hours. Iodide that the thyroid does not take is filtered by the kidneys and leaves in the urine within days, with smaller amounts in saliva, sweat and breast milk. The trapped iodine is not otherwise broken down; it decays with a physical half-life of about eight days. Patients follow radiation precautions for a few days, and breastfeeding must stop.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow the tissues that collect iodide. The thyroid takes the most, so thyroid destruction and hypothyroidism are expected, and early thyroiditis can briefly release hormone and worsen symptoms. Salivary and tear glands have NIS, causing sialadenitis and dry mouth. High cumulative activity can suppress marrow, cause radiation pneumonitis in diffuse lung metastases, and slightly raise the risk of later leukemia. Pregnancy is a contraindication because the fetal thyroid takes up iodine after about 10 to 12 weeks. There is no reversal agent once taken; hydration and frequent voiding speed clearance.",
  "fun": "Saul Hertz's 1941 treatment is often called the birth of nuclear medicine and of the idea of a targeted cancer theranostic.",
  "src": ["Sodium iodide I-131 FDA label", "Hertz S, Roberts A. JAMA 1946", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"samarium-sm-153-lexidronam": {
  "origin": "Bone metastases often cause severe pain, and in the 1980s researchers looked for radioactive atoms that would settle in bone and calm it. A team at the University of Missouri, home to a powerful research reactor, worked with Dow Chemical to attach samarium-153 to EDTMP, a phosphonate that sticks to growing bone like the bisphosphonates. The product was licensed to Cytogen and approved by the FDA as Quadramet in 1997 for pain from osteoblastic bone metastases. It was used for years, especially in prostate and breast cancer. It has since been reported discontinued by its US manufacturer, and no current US label was found on DailyMed, so it is largely of historical and educational interest in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "University of Missouri and Dow Chemical researchers develop samarium-153 EDTMP."], ["1997", "FDA approves Quadramet for bone metastasis pain."], ["2010s", "The US product is reported discontinued and no current label is listed."]],
  "cell": "Lexidronam is EDTMP (ethylenediamine tetramethylene phosphonate), a chelator with four phosphonate groups. Phosphonates bind calcium in hydroxyapatite, especially where osteoblasts are actively laying down new mineral, as in osteoblastic metastases. The EDTMP carries samarium-153 into these areas. Samarium-153 emits beta particles with a mean soft tissue range of about 0.6 millimeter that cause DNA strand breaks in nearby tumor cells and bone cells. This damage, along with effects on cells that release pain mediators, reduces bone pain. It is a bone-seeking radionuclide rather than a receptor drug, and its effect does not depend on cell-cycle phase. It also gives off gamma photons that allow imaging.",
  "body": [["Bone metastases", "Beta radiation to osteoblastic lesions relieves bone pain for weeks to months in many patients."], ["Bone marrow", "Marrow near bone deposits is irradiated, so platelets and white cells fall, with the lowest point about three to five weeks later."], ["Pain", "A short flare of bone pain can occur in the first days before relief begins."]],
  "journey": "It was given as a slow IV injection. The bone took up the drug within hours, in proportion to how active each area was, matching the pattern seen on a bone scan. The part that did not bind bone was filtered by the kidneys and left in the urine within hours. The bound samarium was not metabolized; it decayed with a physical half-life of about 46 hours, so most of its radiation was delivered within about a week. Pain relief usually began within about a week.",
  "sidefx": "Bone marrow suppression is the main toxicity because the marrow sits next to the bone surface where the drug lands. The short pain flare is thought to reflect local inflammation as the radiation acts. Radiation exposure means pregnancy must be avoided. Pre-existing low counts or extensive prior chemotherapy raised the risk of severe pancytopenia. There is no antidote; supportive care with transfusions was used for marrow suppression.",
  "src": ["Quadramet (samarium Sm 153 lexidronam) historical FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"dexrazoxane": {
  "origin": "Dexrazoxane was first made as a possible cancer drug. In the late 1960s Andrew Creighton and colleagues at the Imperial Cancer Research Fund in London synthesized a family of bisdioxopiperazines, ring-closed relatives of the chelator EDTA designed to slip into cells. Racemic razoxane (ICRF-159) had modest antitumor activity, and its d-isomer, ICRF-187, became dexrazoxane. In the 1970s and 1980s Eugene Herman and others found that it protected animal hearts from doxorubicin damage. Clinical trials confirmed less cardiomyopathy, and the FDA approved Zinecard in 1995 to protect the heart in women receiving doxorubicin for metastatic breast cancer. In 2007 a second product, Totect, was approved to treat anthracycline extravasation.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Creighton and colleagues at the Imperial Cancer Research Fund make bisdioxopiperazines including ICRF-187."], ["1970s", "Animal studies show protection against anthracycline heart damage."], ["1995", "FDA approves Zinecard as a cardioprotectant with doxorubicin."], ["2007", "FDA approves Totect for anthracycline extravasation."]],
  "cell": "Dexrazoxane is a cyclic, uncharged EDTA analog that crosses cell membranes easily. Inside heart cells it is hydrolyzed to the open-ring form ADR-925, a strong metal chelator. Anthracyclines such as doxorubicin bind iron, and the iron-drug complex cycles electrons to oxygen, making superoxide and hydroxyl radicals that damage mitochondria and membranes in cardiomyocytes, which have weak antioxidant defenses. ADR-925 strips iron from this complex and binds free iron, cutting radical production. Dexrazoxane is also a catalytic inhibitor of topoisomerase II. It locks the enzyme in a closed clamp before it cuts DNA, and it causes degradation of topoisomerase II beta, the form in quiescent heart cells. Because doxorubicin poisons topoisomerase II beta to create DNA double-strand breaks in the heart, removing the enzyme blocks that injury.",
  "body": [["Heart", "Less iron-driven oxidative injury and topoisomerase II beta damage means less anthracycline cardiomyopathy and a slower fall in ejection fraction."], ["Tissue at an extravasation site", "Given IV after an anthracycline leaks into tissue, it limits the spreading tissue death that would otherwise need surgery."], ["Bone marrow", "Topoisomerase II inhibition in dividing marrow cells adds to chemotherapy myelosuppression, lowering neutrophils and platelets."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise during treatment."]],
  "journey": "Dexrazoxane is given as a short IV infusion shortly before the anthracycline dose. It distributes into body water, including heart cells, and is not significantly bound to plasma proteins. It is hydrolyzed inside cells and in plasma to the open-ring chelating form ADR-925. A large share is excreted unchanged in the urine, and the elimination half-life is about two to two and a half hours. Kidney impairment raises levels.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects add to those of the chemotherapy it is given with. Its topoisomerase II inhibition in dividing marrow cells deepens neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. Because topoisomerase II inhibitors can cause chromosome breaks, secondary AML and MDS have been reported. Injection-site pain and liver enzyme rises also occur. Early concern that it might reduce tumor response has not been confirmed in most analyses. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Zinecard and Totect (dexrazoxane) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Hasinoff BB. Cardiovasc Toxicol 2007"]
},
"rasburicase": {
  "origin": "Most mammals carry urate oxidase, or uricase, an enzyme that turns uric acid into soluble allantoin. Humans and other great apes lost working uricase through gene mutations millions of years ago, which is why we can get gout and kidney stones from uric acid. Scientists borrowed the enzyme from the mold Aspergillus flavus. A purified, non-recombinant mold uricase was used in France and Italy from the 1970s. Sanofi then produced a recombinant version in yeast, called rasburicase. The FDA approved Elitek in 2002 for children with cancer at risk of tumor lysis syndrome and extended it to adults in 2009. It gave doctors a way to destroy uric acid already in the blood, rather than just stop new production.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Non-recombinant Aspergillus uricase is used in Europe for hyperuricemia."], ["2002", "FDA approves Elitek for pediatric tumor lysis prevention."], ["2009", "FDA extends approval to adults."]],
  "cell": "Rasburicase is a recombinant Aspergillus flavus urate oxidase expressed in Saccharomyces cerevisiae. It is a tetramer that oxidizes uric acid in the blood, using molecular oxygen, to 5-hydroxyisourate, which breaks down to allantoin. Allantoin is five to ten times more water soluble than uric acid, so it is filtered and excreted without crystallizing in renal tubules. Each reaction makes hydrogen peroxide as a by-product. Red cells normally neutralize hydrogen peroxide with glutathione, which is kept in reduced form by NADPH from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD). In G6PD deficiency this defense fails, so peroxide oxidizes hemoglobin and membranes, causing hemolysis and methemoglobinemia. Unlike allopurinol, which blocks xanthine oxidase and prevents new uric acid, rasburicase removes uric acid that already exists.",
  "body": [["Blood", "Plasma uric acid falls sharply within hours as the enzyme converts it to allantoin."], ["Kidneys", "Less uric acid reaching the tubules means fewer urate crystals and less acute kidney injury during tumor lysis."], ["Red cells", "Hydrogen peroxide made by the reaction can cause hemolysis and methemoglobinemia, especially with G6PD deficiency."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign fungal protein it can cause allergic reactions and anaphylaxis, and antibodies form with repeated use."]],
  "journey": "Rasburicase is given by IV infusion. It works in the bloodstream, staying mostly in the plasma volume. Uric acid falls within about four hours of the first dose. As a protein it is broken down by normal protein catabolism into peptides and amino acids rather than by liver enzymes, and its clearance does not depend on the kidneys. The half-life is about 16 to 21 hours. The enzyme keeps working in a blood sample at room temperature, so samples for uric acid are collected in prechilled tubes and kept on ice, or the result will be falsely low.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warnings come straight from the chemistry and the source of the protein. The hydrogen peroxide made with each reaction overwhelms red cell defenses in G6PD deficiency, causing severe hemolysis and methemoglobinemia, so patients at risk are screened first. Methylene blue does not work well, and can be harmful, in G6PD deficiency. Being a fungal protein, it can trigger anaphylaxis. Nausea, vomiting and fever are common. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and reactions are treated supportively.",
  "fun": "Losing uricase may have helped our ancestors: one theory is that higher uric acid helped great apes keep blood pressure up on a low-salt diet or acted as an antioxidant.",
  "src": ["Elitek (rasburicase) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"palifermin": {
  "origin": "In 1989 Jeffrey Rubin and Stuart Aaronson at the US National Cancer Institute discovered keratinocyte growth factor (KGF, later called FGF-7), a protein made by connective tissue cells that tells nearby epithelial cells to grow. Because it acts on the linings of the mouth and gut, Amgen developed a truncated recombinant version, palifermin, to protect these linings from the damage of high-dose chemotherapy and radiation. In a trial in patients with blood cancers receiving total body irradiation and chemotherapy before stem cell transplant, it reduced the incidence and length of severe oral mucositis. The FDA approved Kepivance in December 2004, the first drug approved to prevent oral mucositis.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Rubin and Aaronson at the NCI discover keratinocyte growth factor."], ["2004", "FDA approves Kepivance to reduce severe oral mucositis in stem cell transplant patients."]],
  "cell": "Palifermin is human KGF with the first 23 amino acids removed, made in E. coli, which makes it more stable. It binds the KGF receptor, the FGFR2 IIIb isoform, a receptor tyrosine kinase found mainly on epithelial cells. Binding causes receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation, which activates RAS-MAPK, PI3K-AKT and PLC-gamma pathways. These signals drive epithelial proliferation and differentiation and turn on protective programs, including antioxidant enzymes and anti-apoptotic proteins. The result is a thicker, more resilient lining of the tongue, cheeks and gut. When chemotherapy and radiation later kill dividing cells, the thicker lining is less likely to break down into painful ulcers, and it heals faster.",
  "body": [["Mouth", "A thicker, more resistant lining lowers the incidence and length of severe oral mucositis after high-dose therapy."], ["Tongue and taste", "Epithelial growth thickens or discolors the tongue and changes taste or sensation in the mouth."], ["Skin", "KGF receptors in the skin cause rash, redness, itching and swelling."], ["Pancreas", "Amylase and lipase can rise, usually without symptoms."], ["Tumors", "Because some tumor cells carry the KGF receptor, there is a theoretical concern about stimulating growth of epithelial cancers."]],
  "journey": "Palifermin is given by IV bolus for three days before and three days after the conditioning regimen, but not on the same day as chemotherapy. Levels drop quickly at first and then more slowly, with a half-life of about four and a half hours. It is a protein, so it is broken down by proteolysis, and the kidneys are not the main route of clearance. Its effect on the epithelium lasts several days after each block of doses. It is not given within 24 hours of chemotherapy because freshly stimulated, dividing epithelial cells would be more sensitive to the chemotherapy.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects reflect growth of epithelium where the receptor is found. Thickening of the tongue and mouth lining causes dysesthesia and taste change. Skin effects such as rash, redness and edema come from receptor activation in skin. Rising amylase and lipase reflect effects on the pancreas and salivary glands. Giving it too close to chemotherapy can worsen mucositis because stimulated cells are more vulnerable. Tumor growth stimulation remains a theoretical concern. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Kepivance (palifermin) FDA label", "Rubin JS et al. PNAS 1989", "Spielberger R et al. N Engl J Med 2004"]
},
"amifostine": {
  "origin": "Amifostine was born from Cold War fears of nuclear war. In 1959 the US Army began an antiradiation drug program at the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research, and chemists synthesized and screened thousands of sulfur-containing compounds in mice for protection against radiation. One compound, WR-2721, stood out. It turned out to protect normal tissues better than tumors, which made it interesting for cancer treatment. The FDA approved it as Ethyol in 1995 to reduce cumulative kidney toxicity from cisplatin in advanced ovarian cancer, and in 1999 to reduce dry mouth from radiation for head and neck cancer.",
  "timeline": [["1959", "The US Army antiradiation drug program begins at Walter Reed."], ["1960s", "WR-2721 is identified as a strong radioprotector in animals."], ["1995", "FDA approves Ethyol to reduce cisplatin kidney toxicity."], ["1999", "FDA approves Ethyol to reduce radiation-induced xerostomia."]],
  "cell": "Amifostine is an inactive organic thiophosphate prodrug. Alkaline phosphatase on cell membranes removes the phosphate to make the free thiol WR-1065, which enters cells. Normal tissues have more of this enzyme activity, better blood flow and a more neutral pH than many tumors, which are often hypoxic and acidic, so normal cells make and take up more WR-1065. Inside, the thiol scavenges reactive oxygen species made by radiation and certain chemotherapy, donates hydrogen to repair damaged DNA, and binds the reactive species of cisplatin and alkylating agents before they attack DNA. WR-1065 is further oxidized to disulfides that may also be protective. The net effect is selective protection of normal cells, such as kidney tubules and salivary glands.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "WR-1065 binds reactive cisplatin species in tubular cells, reducing cumulative kidney injury."], ["Salivary glands", "Scavenging radiation-generated free radicals in gland tissue reduces acute and late dry mouth."], ["Blood vessels", "It relaxes vascular smooth muscle, so blood pressure can drop during the infusion."], ["Gut and brain", "Nausea and vomiting are common, and sleepiness can occur."], ["Parathyroid and calcium", "It can lower parathyroid hormone release, causing low calcium."], ["Skin", "Rash and rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis occur."]],
  "journey": "Amifostine is given IV over a short time shortly before chemotherapy or radiation. It leaves the plasma very fast, with a half-life of about eight minutes, as membrane alkaline phosphatase converts it to WR-1065 and tissues take it up. The active thiol is then oxidized to disulfides inside cells. Very little of the parent drug is excreted in urine. Its protective window is short, which is why timing relative to treatment matters.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure, the main limiting effect, comes from relaxation of blood vessel smooth muscle, so patients lie flat and are hydrated with blood pressure checked often. Nausea and vomiting are frequent and are prevented with antiemetics. Sulfur compounds cause a metallic taste, flushing and sneezing. Low calcium reflects reduced parathyroid hormone release. Serious skin reactions and anaphylaxis are rare but require stopping the drug. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Of the thousands of compounds screened in the Walter Reed program, WR-2721 was the one that made it to the pharmacy shelf decades later as a cancer supportive drug.",
  "src": ["Ethyol (amifostine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"levoleucovorin": {
  "origin": "In 1948 researchers found a factor in liver that the bacterium Leuconostoc citrovorum needed to grow. They called it citrovorum factor, and it proved to be folinic acid (5-formyltetrahydrofolate), later called leucovorin. Doctors soon learned that it could rescue normal cells from methotrexate and, from the 1980s, that it boosted fluorouracil against colon cancer. Leucovorin is a 50-50 mix of two mirror-image forms, and only the l-form is active. Spectrum Pharmaceuticals developed the pure active isomer, levoleucovorin, and the FDA approved Fusilev in 2008 for methotrexate rescue after high-dose therapy in osteosarcoma, with colorectal cancer use with fluorouracil added in 2011.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Citrovorum factor, later known as leucovorin, is described as a bacterial growth factor."], ["1950s", "Leucovorin is used to rescue normal cells from methotrexate."], ["2008", "FDA approves levoleucovorin (Fusilev) for methotrexate rescue."], ["2011", "Approval is extended to use with fluorouracil in colorectal cancer."]],
  "cell": "Levoleucovorin is the active (6S) isomer of 5-formyltetrahydrofolate, already in reduced form. Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), so cells cannot regenerate tetrahydrofolate, and they run out of the folate cofactors needed for purine synthesis and for thymidylate synthase to make dTMP. DNA synthesis stops in S phase. Levoleucovorin enters cells through the reduced folate carrier and is converted to 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate and other active folates without needing DHFR, so normal cells can make DNA again. This is rescue. With fluorouracil, the active metabolite FdUMP binds thymidylate synthase. Extra 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate from levoleucovorin forms a tight ternary complex with FdUMP and the enzyme, prolonging inhibition and making fluorouracil more effective, and more toxic.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Restoring folate cofactors lets marrow cells resume DNA synthesis after high-dose methotrexate, preventing severe cytopenias."], ["Gut and mouth", "Mucosa is protected from methotrexate, but with fluorouracil the stronger enzyme block causes more stomatitis and diarrhea."], ["Kidneys", "Rescue lowers methotrexate injury to cells, though high methotrexate levels still require hydration and urine alkalinization."], ["Tumor", "Combined with fluorouracil it increases killing of colorectal cancer cells."]],
  "journey": "Levoleucovorin is given IV, either on a timed schedule after methotrexate or just before fluorouracil. It is quickly converted in the body to 5-methyltetrahydrofolate and other reduced folates, which circulate and enter cells. Its half-life is about five to six hours, and the active 5-methyl metabolite lasts a little longer. Folate metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Because it is already the active isomer, the amount needed is half that of racemic leucovorin.",
  "sidefx": "With methotrexate rescue, levoleucovorin has few side effects of its own. With fluorouracil, the main problems are fluorouracil toxicities made stronger by a tighter thymidylate synthase block: stomatitis, diarrhea, dehydration and myelosuppression, which can be severe in older adults. Hypersensitivity and rare seizures have been reported. It is not given intrathecally. It cannot rescue cells if given too late after methotrexate, and it competes with methotrexate for cell entry, so timing matters. For methotrexate toxicity with poor clearance, glucarpidase can also be used to break down methotrexate.",
  "src": ["Fusilev and Khapzory (levoleucovorin) FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"eflapegrastim": {
  "origin": "The story of eflapegrastim starts with the colony-stimulating factors, which Donald Metcalf and colleagues in Melbourne began describing in the 1960s, and with Amgen's recombinant G-CSF filgrastim, approved in 1991. Filgrastim lasts only hours, so pegfilgrastim later added a polyethylene glycol tail for once-per-cycle use. The Korean company Hanmi Pharmaceutical took a different approach with its LAPSCOVERY platform, linking G-CSF to a human antibody Fc fragment through a short PEG linker to slow clearance. Spectrum Pharmaceuticals licensed it, and the FDA approved Rolvedon in September 2022 to reduce febrile neutropenia in patients receiving myelosuppressive chemotherapy.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Metcalf and colleagues describe colony-stimulating factors in Melbourne."], ["1991", "FDA approves filgrastim, the first recombinant G-CSF."], ["2022", "FDA approves eflapegrastim (Rolvedon) for chemotherapy-induced neutropenia."]],
  "cell": "Eflapegrastim is a recombinant human G-CSF analog joined by a short PEG linker to a human IgG4 Fc fragment. The G-CSF part binds the G-CSF receptor (CSF3R) on myeloid progenitor cells and neutrophils. Receptor dimerization activates JAK1 and JAK2, which phosphorylate STAT3 and STAT5, along with PI3K-AKT and MAPK pathways. These signals increase proliferation of neutrophil precursors, speed their maturation, and prevent apoptosis. G-CSF also cleaves CXCR4 retention signals in the marrow and pushes mature neutrophils into the blood. The Fc and linker make the molecule large, so it escapes kidney filtration, and Fc binding to the neonatal Fc receptor may also help it persist. It is cleared mostly by binding to G-CSF receptors on neutrophils, so as neutrophil counts recover, clearance speeds up.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Neutrophil precursors multiply and mature faster, so the neutrophil low point after chemotherapy is shallower and shorter."], ["Blood", "Absolute neutrophil count rises within about a day; very high white counts can occur."], ["Bones", "Marrow expansion stretches the bone lining and causes bone pain, especially in the pelvis, back and long bones."], ["Spleen", "Myeloid cells can expand in the spleen, rarely causing splenic enlargement or rupture."], ["Lungs", "Neutrophils activated in the lung can rarely cause ARDS."]],
  "journey": "Eflapegrastim is injected under the skin once per chemotherapy cycle, at least a day after chemotherapy. It is absorbed slowly from the injection site. Its large size keeps it out of kidney filtration. It is cleared mainly by neutrophils that bind and internalize it, plus general protein breakdown, so its half-life is long when neutrophils are low and shortens as counts rise. This self-regulating clearance lets one dose cover a cycle.",
  "sidefx": "Bone pain comes from rapid marrow expansion. Leukocytosis is an overshoot of the intended effect. Splenic rupture can occur because myeloid cells crowd the spleen; left shoulder or upper abdominal pain is a warning sign. ARDS reflects activated neutrophils collecting in the lung. People with sickle cell disease can have crises because more white cells promote vessel blockage. Capillary leak and glomerulonephritis are rare class effects. It is not given within the 14 days before or 24 hours after chemotherapy because it would make dividing marrow cells more vulnerable. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Rolvedon (eflapegrastim-xnst) FDA label", "Metcalf D. Blood 2008 (hematopoietic cytokines review)"]
},
"sargramostim": {
  "origin": "In 1966 Ray Bradley and Donald Metcalf in Melbourne, and separately Dov Pluznik and Leo Sachs in Israel, showed that blood cell precursors could form colonies in soft agar if a growth factor was present. One of these colony-stimulating factors, granulocyte-macrophage CSF (GM-CSF), was purified and its gene was cloned in the 1980s. The Seattle company Immunex produced a recombinant human GM-CSF in yeast, called sargramostim. The FDA approved Leukine in 1991 to speed recovery of white cells after bone marrow transplant. In 2018 it was also approved to improve survival after a dangerous whole-body radiation exposure, the hematopoietic syndrome of acute radiation syndrome.",
  "timeline": [["1966", "Bradley and Metcalf, and Pluznik and Sachs, develop the colony assay that reveals colony-stimulating factors."], ["1980s", "The GM-CSF gene is cloned and recombinant protein is produced."], ["1991", "FDA approves Leukine (sargramostim) for marrow transplant recovery."], ["2018", "FDA approves sargramostim for acute radiation syndrome."]],
  "cell": "Sargramostim is a glycosylated recombinant human GM-CSF made in Saccharomyces cerevisiae, with leucine in place of proline at position 23. It binds the GM-CSF receptor, which has a ligand-specific alpha chain (CSF2RA) and a common beta chain shared with IL-3 and IL-5 receptors. Binding brings JAK2 molecules together, which phosphorylate STAT5 and activate MAPK and PI3K pathways. This stimulates proliferation and differentiation of granulocyte, macrophage and eosinophil progenitors, and it enhances the functions of mature cells, including neutrophil and macrophage killing, antigen presentation by dendritic cells, and cytokine release. That broader action, compared with G-CSF, explains both its uses and its fluid-retaining side effects.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "More neutrophil, monocyte and eosinophil precursors are made, speeding white cell recovery after transplant or chemotherapy."], ["Immune function", "Mature neutrophils, macrophages and dendritic cells become more active at fighting infection and presenting antigens."], ["Blood vessels", "Cytokine effects make vessels leaky, causing fluid retention, edema, effusions and rarely capillary leak syndrome."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath can occur as activated white cells collect in the lungs, especially during infusion."], ["First dose", "A first-dose reaction with flushing, low blood pressure, rapid heart rate and low oxygen can occur."], ["Whole body", "Fever, bone pain, fatigue and diarrhea reflect cytokine release and marrow expansion."]],
  "journey": "Sargramostim can be given by IV infusion or by injection under the skin. Subcutaneous doses peak in about one to four hours. The half-life is about one hour IV and two and a half to three hours subcutaneously, so it is given daily. It is broken down by proteolysis and taken up by cells carrying the receptor, and the kidneys are not the main route. White counts rise within days.",
  "sidefx": "GM-CSF acts on many myeloid cells and activates them, so side effects are broader than with G-CSF. Cytokines released by activated macrophages cause fever and malaise. Vessel leak causes fluid retention, pleural and pericardial effusions and weight gain, so caution is needed with heart failure or fluid overload. The first-dose reaction reflects sudden neutrophil activation and pooling in the lungs. Supraventricular arrhythmias can occur. Like other CSFs, it is not given within 24 hours of chemotherapy. There is no antidote; stopping it reverses most effects.",
  "fun": "Sargramostim is stockpiled by the US government for radiation emergencies because it can help the marrow recover after a large radiation exposure.",
  "src": ["Leukine (sargramostim) FDA label", "Metcalf D. Blood 2008", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"plerixafor": {
  "origin": "Plerixafor began as an HIV drug. In the late 1980s Erik De Clercq and colleagues at the Rega Institute in Belgium noticed that a commercial sample of cyclam, a ring-shaped metal binding compound, blocked HIV, and the activity came from a contaminant: two cyclam rings joined together, a bicyclam. The optimized bicyclam, AMD3100, was later found to block CXCR4, the co-receptor some HIV strains use to enter cells. In HIV trials it caused a surprising rise in white blood cells. Researchers realized it was releasing stem cells from the bone marrow. The Canadian company AnorMED redirected it to stem cell mobilization, Genzyme acquired AnorMED in 2006, and the FDA approved Mozobil in December 2008 for use with G-CSF in non-Hodgkin lymphoma and multiple myeloma.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "De Clercq's group finds anti-HIV activity in a bicyclam contaminant of cyclam."], ["1997", "AMD3100 is shown to block the CXCR4 chemokine receptor."], ["2000s", "HIV studies reveal leukocytosis, leading to stem cell mobilization trials."], ["2008", "FDA approves Mozobil for stem cell mobilization."]],
  "cell": "Hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells are anchored in the bone marrow niche by CXCR4, a G protein-coupled chemokine receptor on their surface, which binds stromal cell-derived factor-1 alpha (SDF-1 alpha, also called CXCL12) made by marrow stromal cells and osteoblasts. CXCL12 binding activates Gi signaling, adhesion molecules such as VLA-4, and chemotaxis that keep the cells in place. Plerixafor is a positively charged bicyclam that binds acidic residues in the CXCR4 pocket and reversibly blocks CXCL12 binding. Without this retention signal, stem cells detach and move into the blood. CD34-positive cell counts peak about 10 to 14 hours after a dose. G-CSF works in a complementary way, partly by lowering marrow CXCL12 and cleaving adhesion molecules, so the combination yields more cells for apheresis. Plerixafor is not cytotoxic.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Stem and progenitor cells are released from the marrow niche into the circulation."], ["Blood", "CD34-positive cells and total white cells rise for about a day, providing enough stem cells for collection and later transplant."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and nausea are common, possibly from CXCR4 blockade on gut cells."], ["Spleen", "Mobilized cells can pool in the spleen, causing enlargement and, rarely, rupture."], ["Circulation", "Vasovagal reactions and orthostatic hypotension can occur, often soon after injection."]],
  "journey": "Plerixafor is injected under the skin in the evening, so that the CD34 peak lines up with apheresis the next morning. It is absorbed quickly, with plasma levels peaking in 30 to 60 minutes. Up to about half is bound to plasma proteins. It is not metabolized and is not a CYP substrate. About 70 percent leaves unchanged in the urine within a day, with a half-life of about three to five hours in normal kidney function, so the dose is reduced in kidney impairment.",
  "sidefx": "Gastrointestinal effects such as diarrhea and nausea are the most common, and injection-site redness reflects local irritation. Leukocytosis is the expected effect of releasing white cells. Splenic enlargement and rare rupture come from mobilized cells pooling in the spleen. In leukemia, it can also mobilize leukemic cells, so it is not used for that purpose. Vasovagal reactions and anaphylaxis can occur shortly after a dose. It causes fetal harm in animals. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Plerixafor's discovery started with a contaminant: the anti-HIV activity in a bottle of cyclam came from a tiny amount of a joined double-ring impurity.",
  "src": ["Mozobil (plerixafor) FDA label", "De Clercq E. Nat Rev Drug Discov 2003"]
},
"motixafortide": {
  "origin": "Motixafortide belongs to a family of CXCR4-blocking peptides with an unusual ancestor: polyphemusin II, a defense peptide from the blood cells of the horseshoe crab. In the 1990s Japanese chemists, including the groups of Nobutaka Fujii and Hirokazu Tamamura in Kyoto, shortened and stabilized these peptides into potent CXCR4 inhibitors called the T-140 series. A refined cyclic peptide from this line, BKT140, was developed by the Israeli company Biokine Therapeutics and then BioLineRx as BL-8040. In the GENESIS trial, a single dose with filgrastim let most patients with multiple myeloma collect enough stem cells in one or two apheresis sessions. The FDA approved Aphexda in September 2023.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Japanese chemists develop T-140 peptides from horseshoe crab polyphemusin as CXCR4 blockers."], ["2010s", "BioLineRx develops the cyclic peptide BL-8040, later named motixafortide."], ["2023", "FDA approves Aphexda with filgrastim for stem cell mobilization in multiple myeloma."]],
  "cell": "Motixafortide is a synthetic cyclic peptide of 14 amino acids that binds CXCR4, the chemokine receptor that holds hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells in the marrow niche. It binds with high affinity and dissociates slowly, so it occupies the receptor much longer than plerixafor. By blocking binding of the ligand CXCL12 (SDF-1 alpha), it stops Gi-coupled signaling, integrin activation and the chemotactic pull that keep these cells anchored. The stem cells leave the marrow and enter the blood, where CD34-positive counts rise within hours and stay high through the apheresis window. Combined with filgrastim, which increases the number of progenitors and loosens other retention signals, it produces large stem cell yields from a single dose. It is not cytotoxic.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Stem and progenitor cells are released in large numbers because the CXCR4 retention signal is blocked for many hours."], ["Blood", "CD34-positive cells and white cells rise markedly, allowing enough stem cells to be collected in fewer sessions."], ["Skin and mast cells", "Injection-site pain, redness, itching and flushing are common, likely from mast cell histamine release by the cationic peptide."], ["Immune system", "Systemic hypersensitivity with hives, flushing or low blood pressure can occur, so premedication is used."]],
  "journey": "Motixafortide is given as a subcutaneous injection, after several days of filgrastim and about 10 to 14 hours before the first apheresis. It is absorbed from under the skin and has a plasma half-life of about two hours, but because it stays bound to CXCR4 for a long time, its effect lasts much longer than its blood levels. As a peptide it is broken down by proteases into smaller peptides and amino acids.",
  "sidefx": "Injection-site reactions, itching and flushing are thought to reflect mast cell degranulation and histamine release triggered by the positively charged peptide, which is why antihistamines, an H2 blocker and a leukotriene blocker are given beforehand. Systemic hypersensitivity and rare anaphylactic shock can follow. Leukocytosis is part of the intended effect. Like plerixafor, it can mobilize tumor cells, so it is not used in leukemia. There is no antidote; reactions are treated supportively.",
  "fun": "Horseshoe crabs, whose blue blood is also used to test drugs for bacterial toxins, supplied the starting peptide for this drug family.",
  "src": ["Aphexda (motixafortide) FDA label", "Crees ZD et al. Nat Med 2023 (GENESIS)", "Tamamura H et al. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1998"]
},
"trilaciclib": {
  "origin": "Trilaciclib grew from basic research on how blood stem cells depend on the enzymes CDK4 and CDK6 to leave the resting G1 phase. Norman Sharpless and colleagues at the University of North Carolina showed in mice that briefly pausing these stem cells with a CDK4/6 inhibitor protected them from chemotherapy. The company G1 Therapeutics, founded in North Carolina in 2008 based on this work, designed trilaciclib as a short-acting IV CDK4/6 inhibitor. Because small cell lung cancer cells usually lack the Rb protein, the drug pauses the marrow but not the tumor. The FDA approved Cosela in February 2021 to reduce chemotherapy-induced myelosuppression in extensive-stage small cell lung cancer, the first approved drug of its kind.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Sharpless and colleagues show that CDK4/6 inhibition protects mouse marrow from chemotherapy."], ["2008", "G1 Therapeutics is founded to develop the approach."], ["2021", "FDA approves Cosela, the first myeloprotective CDK4/6 inhibitor."]],
  "cell": "To move from G1 into S phase, a cell uses cyclin D bound to cyclin-dependent kinases 4 and 6. These kinases phosphorylate the retinoblastoma protein (Rb), which then releases the E2F transcription factors that switch on S-phase genes. Trilaciclib reversibly inhibits CDK4 and CDK6, keeping Rb unphosphorylated and E2F bound, so hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells pause in G1. Most cytotoxic chemotherapy, such as platinum, etoposide and topotecan, kills cells in S phase or mitosis, so paused marrow cells are spared. The pause lasts only hours, so cells resume dividing once the chemotherapy is gone. Small cell lung cancer cells usually have lost Rb, so they do not depend on CDK4/6 and are not arrested, keeping them sensitive to chemotherapy.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "A brief G1 pause protects stem and progenitor cells, lowering severe neutropenia and the need for red cell and platelet transfusions."], ["Blood cells", "Fewer episodes of severe neutropenia mean less need for G-CSF and fewer chemotherapy delays."], ["Veins", "The IV infusion can cause phlebitis and thrombophlebitis at the infusion site."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease or pneumonitis can occur, as with other CDK4/6 inhibitors."], ["Electrolytes", "Low calcium, potassium and phosphate are common lab findings."]],
  "journey": "Trilaciclib is given as a short IV infusion within four hours before chemotherapy on each treatment day. It spreads widely into tissues and is about 70 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, and the products leave mostly in the feces with little in the urine. The half-life is about 14 hours, but the G1 arrest in marrow cells is transient and wears off in hours.",
  "sidefx": "Fatigue and electrolyte changes are common. The infusion is irritating to veins, causing phlebitis. Interstitial lung disease is a class effect of CDK4/6 inhibitors and may reflect immune or direct effects on lung cells. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Because it inhibits drug transporters such as OCT2 and MATE, it can raise levels of drugs like dofetilide and cisplatin cleared by those transporters. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Cosela (trilaciclib) FDA label", "Johnson SM et al. J Clin Invest 2010", "Weiss JM et al. Ann Oncol 2019"]
},
"fosaprepitant": {
  "origin": "In 1931 Ulf von Euler and John Gaddum found a substance in horse brain and gut extracts that contracted intestine and lowered blood pressure. They called it substance P. Decades later it was shown to be a neuropeptide that acts on neurokinin-1 (NK1) receptors, including in the brainstem centers that control vomiting. Merck chemists developed aprepitant, an oral NK1 antagonist approved in 2003 that especially helped delayed vomiting after chemotherapy. Aprepitant does not dissolve well in water, so Merck made fosaprepitant, a water-soluble phosphate prodrug for IV use. The FDA approved Emend for injection in January 2008, allowing a single IV dose on the day of chemotherapy.",
  "timeline": [["1931", "Von Euler and Gaddum discover substance P."], ["1980s", "Substance P is shown to act on NK1 receptors."], ["2003", "FDA approves oral aprepitant."], ["2008", "FDA approves IV fosaprepitant (Emend for injection)."]],
  "cell": "Fosaprepitant is a phosphoryl prodrug. Phosphatases in the blood and tissues remove the phosphate within about 30 minutes, releasing aprepitant. Aprepitant crosses the blood-brain barrier and binds the human NK1 receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor for substance P, with high affinity and selectivity. Chemotherapy injures gut cells, which release substance P and serotonin, and substance P is also released centrally in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema. Blocking NK1 receptors there prevents substance P from activating phospholipase C and the calcium signals that drive the vomiting reflex. The NK1 pathway dominates the delayed phase of chemotherapy-induced emesis, days two to five, which is why NK1 antagonists add so much to 5-HT3 antagonists and dexamethasone. It also augments their effect in the acute phase.",
  "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocking NK1 receptors in the nucleus tractus solitarius and area postrema prevents acute and especially delayed nausea and vomiting after chemotherapy."], ["Gut", "Fewer vomiting episodes, with occasional diarrhea or constipation."], ["Infusion site", "The infusion can irritate the vein, causing pain, redness or thrombophlebitis, and rarely necrosis with extravasation."], ["Liver enzymes", "Aprepitant moderately inhibits and then induces CYP3A4 and induces CYP2C9, changing levels of drugs like dexamethasone and warfarin."]],
  "journey": "Fosaprepitant is infused IV before chemotherapy and is converted to aprepitant within about 30 minutes. Aprepitant is over 95 percent bound to plasma proteins and crosses into the brain. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP3A4, with smaller roles for CYP1A2 and CYP2C19, and the metabolites leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about 9 to 13 hours, and a single dose covers the delayed phase.",
  "sidefx": "The drug itself is usually well tolerated; many reported effects such as fatigue and neutropenia come from the chemotherapy. Infusion-site reactions come from the formulation irritating the vein, and hypersensitivity can include anaphylaxis. The most important issues are interactions. Inhibiting CYP3A4 raises levels of dexamethasone (so its dose is lowered) and some chemotherapy drugs. Inducing CYP2C9 lowers warfarin levels and INR for a couple of weeks, and lowers hormonal contraceptive effect. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The P in substance P simply stood for the powder the extract was dried into, not for pain.",
  "src": ["Emend for injection (fosaprepitant) FDA label", "von Euler US, Gaddum JH. J Physiol 1931", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"palonosetron": {
  "origin": "In the late 1980s and early 1990s the first 5-HT3 antagonists, ondansetron and granisetron, transformed chemotherapy nausea, but they mostly helped the first day. Chemists at Syntex in California made a new 5-HT3 blocker with a very different structure and much higher receptor affinity, coded RS-25259. The Swiss company Helsinn Healthcare developed it as palonosetron. With a half-life of about 40 hours, it protected into the delayed phase. The FDA approved Aloxi in 2003 for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, and later for postoperative nausea and vomiting.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Syntex researchers make RS-25259, later named palonosetron."], ["2003", "FDA approves Aloxi for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting."], ["2008", "FDA approves palonosetron for postoperative nausea and vomiting."]],
  "cell": "The 5-HT3 receptor is a ligand-gated cation channel, not a G protein-coupled receptor. Chemotherapy damages enterochromaffin cells in the gut lining, which release serotonin. Serotonin binds 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferent nerve endings, opening the channel and sending signals to the nucleus tractus solitarius and the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, which also has 5-HT3 receptors. Palonosetron competitively blocks these receptors with very high affinity. It also binds an allosteric site with positive cooperativity, triggers receptor internalization, and inhibits crosstalk between 5-HT3 and NK1 receptor signaling. Together with its long half-life, this extends protection beyond the first day into the delayed phase, unlike earlier drugs in the class.",
  "body": [["Gut and vagus nerve", "Blocking 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents prevents serotonin released by damaged gut cells from triggering the vomiting reflex."], ["Brainstem", "Blocking receptors in the area postrema and nucleus tractus solitarius reduces nausea from chemotherapy and after surgery."], ["Bowel", "Reduced serotonin signaling in the gut slows motility, causing constipation."], ["Head", "Headache is the most common side effect, as with other drugs in the class."], ["Heart", "Unlike older 5-HT3 blockers, it has little clinically meaningful effect on the QT interval at approved doses."]],
  "journey": "Palonosetron is given as a single IV injection about 30 minutes before chemotherapy or before anesthesia. It works within about 30 minutes and has a long half-life of about 40 hours. About 62 percent is bound to plasma proteins. Roughly half is metabolized in the liver, mainly by CYP2D6 with help from CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, into nearly inactive metabolites. About 40 percent leaves unchanged in the urine and the rest as metabolites.",
  "sidefx": "Headache and constipation come from 5-HT3 blockade: serotonin normally helps drive gut motility, and changes in cranial vessel or nerve signaling may explain headache. Dizziness and fatigue occur. Serotonin syndrome is possible when combined with other serotonergic drugs, because blocking one receptor type may shunt serotonin to others. Hypersensitivity and rare anaphylaxis occur, including in people allergic to other 5-HT3 antagonists. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Aloxi (palonosetron) FDA label", "Rojas C et al. Anesth Analg 2008", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"luspatercept": {
  "origin": "Luspatercept came from work at Acceleron Pharma in Cambridge, Massachusetts, on the activin receptor family, which controls muscle, bone and blood cell growth. Acceleron fused the outer part of a modified activin receptor type IIB to an antibody Fc and found that, in animals, this ligand trap sharply raised red blood cells by helping late red cell precursors mature, a different step from erythropoietin. Acceleron developed it with Celgene, later part of Bristol Myers Squibb. The FDA approved Reblozyl in November 2019 for anemia in beta thalassemia requiring regular transfusions, and in April 2020 for anemia in lower-risk myelodysplastic syndromes.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Acceleron develops activin receptor ligand traps and notes their effects on red cells."], ["2019", "FDA approves Reblozyl for transfusion-dependent beta thalassemia."], ["2020", "FDA approves Reblozyl for anemia in lower-risk myelodysplastic syndromes."]],
  "cell": "Luspatercept is a fusion protein made of a modified extracellular domain of human activin receptor type IIB linked to human IgG1 Fc. It acts as a ligand trap, binding members of the TGF-beta superfamily such as GDF11, activin B and GDF8 before they reach their receptors. In ineffective erythropoiesis, as in beta thalassemia and MDS, these ligands signal through activin receptors to phosphorylate Smad2 and Smad3, which pile up and block late-stage red cell precursors from maturing. Trapping the ligands lowers Smad2/3 signaling, so late erythroblasts can mature into red cells. This is why it is called an erythroid maturation agent: erythropoiesis-stimulating agents act mainly on early progenitors through the EPO receptor, while luspatercept acts later in the pathway.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Late red cell precursors mature more effectively, so more red cells reach the blood and fewer transfusions are needed."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin rises gradually over several weeks as the newly matured red cells accumulate."], ["Blood vessels", "Blood pressure can rise, and a higher red cell mass may contribute to clotting events such as DVT, pulmonary embolism and stroke."], ["Bones and muscles", "Bone pain, joint pain and muscle aches are common, possibly reflecting activity of TGF-beta family ligands in these tissues."], ["Extramedullary masses", "In beta thalassemia, stimulated blood-forming tissue outside the marrow can grow and press on the spinal cord."]],
  "journey": "Luspatercept is injected under the skin every three weeks. It is absorbed slowly, with blood levels peaking about a week after injection. Like antibodies, it is a large protein, so it is not filtered by the kidneys or metabolized by liver enzymes; it is broken down into peptides and amino acids by general protein catabolism. Its half-life is about 11 days. Hemoglobin response develops over several weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Higher red cell mass and changes in vascular signaling explain rises in blood pressure and an increase in thromboembolic events, especially in splenectomized thalassemia patients or those with other clot risks. Extramedullary hematopoietic masses can expand because the drug stimulates red cell production wherever it happens. Musculoskeletal pain, headache and fatigue are common. It caused fetal harm in animals, consistent with the role of these ligands in development. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "A close relative built on the same activin receptor idea, sotatercept, is used for pulmonary arterial hypertension, showing how one signaling family touches both blood and blood vessels.",
  "src": ["Reblozyl (luspatercept-aamt) FDA label", "Cappellini MD et al. N Engl J Med 2020 (BELIEVE)", "Fenaux P et al. N Engl J Med 2020 (MEDALIST)"]
},
"zoledronic-acid": {
  "origin": "Bisphosphonates started as industrial chemicals. In the 19th century chemists made them, and in the 20th century they were used to keep calcium scale out of pipes and textile equipment. In the 1960s Herbert Fleisch and colleagues, working in Davos and Bern, showed that pyrophosphate, a natural inhibitor of calcium crystal formation, controlled bone mineral, and that stable bisphosphonate look-alikes could block bone resorption. Over the next decades, adding nitrogen to the side chain made the drugs far more potent. Zoledronic acid, with a nitrogen-containing imidazole ring, was developed by Ciba-Geigy, which became Novartis. The FDA approved Zometa in 2001 for hypercalcemia of malignancy, later for bone metastases and myeloma, and approved Reclast in 2007 as a once-yearly infusion for osteoporosis.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Fleisch and colleagues show that bisphosphonates inhibit bone resorption."], ["1990s", "Nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates are found to inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase."], ["2001", "FDA approves Zometa for hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["2007", "FDA approves Reclast as a once-yearly osteoporosis treatment."]],
  "cell": "Zoledronic acid has a P-C-P backbone that binds calcium in hydroxyapatite, so it concentrates on bone surfaces where resorption is active. When osteoclasts dissolve bone, they take up the drug by endocytosis. Inside, the nitrogen-containing imidazole side chain inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, an enzyme of the mevalonate (cholesterol synthesis) pathway. Without farnesyl and geranylgeranyl pyrophosphate, small GTPases such as Ras, Rho, Rac and Rab cannot be prenylated and attached to membranes. These proteins are needed for the osteoclast's ruffled border, cytoskeleton and vesicle traffic. The osteoclasts stop resorbing bone and undergo apoptosis. Less bone breakdown means less calcium released into the blood and fewer fractures and skeletal events. In circulating gamma delta T cells, the buildup of upstream isoprenoids triggers activation, which explains the acute phase reaction.",
  "body": [["Bone", "Osteoclast activity drops, so bone breakdown slows, bone density rises and fractures and skeletal-related events from metastases decrease."], ["Blood calcium", "Less calcium released from bone lowers serum calcium in hypercalcemia of malignancy over several days."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete the drug unchanged; high peak levels can injure tubules, so infusion time and kidney function matter."], ["Immune system", "The first infusion often causes an acute phase reaction with fever, muscle aches and flu-like symptoms."], ["Jaw", "Long-term suppression of bone turnover can lead to osteonecrosis of the jaw, especially after dental extractions."], ["Eyes", "Rarely causes eye inflammation such as uveitis or scleritis, which needs prompt eye evaluation."]],
  "journey": "Zoledronic acid is given by IV infusion over at least 15 minutes. Plasma levels fall quickly as the drug binds bone or is filtered by the kidneys. It is not metabolized. About 40 percent is excreted unchanged in the urine within a day, and the rest is bound to bone, where it can remain for years and is released very slowly, giving a terminal half-life of about 146 hours from plasma. Calcium falls over several days, and the effect on bone lasts a year or more.",
  "sidefx": "The acute phase reaction comes from isoprenoid buildup that activates gamma delta T cells and releases cytokines. Kidney toxicity reflects high drug concentration in tubular cells, so dosing depends on kidney function and infusion is not rushed. Hypocalcemia follows from blocking bone resorption, especially with low vitamin D. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures are thought to come from long-term suppression of normal bone remodeling, so old microdamage is not repaired. Ocular inflammation is rare. There is no antidote; calcium and vitamin D are used for hypocalcemia.",
  "fun": "Bisphosphonates bind bone so tightly that a single dose of zoledronic acid can still be detected in the skeleton years later.",
  "src": ["Zometa and Reclast (zoledronic acid) FDA labels", "Fleisch H. Bisphosphonates in Bone Disease", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"pamidronate": {
  "origin": "Pamidronate, also known as APD, was one of the first nitrogen-containing bisphosphonates. It was synthesized in the 1970s and studied closely by researchers at Leiden University in the Netherlands, who used it to treat Paget disease and cancer-related high calcium. Adding an amino group to the side chain made it far more potent than etidronate, the first clinical bisphosphonate. Ciba-Geigy marketed it as Aredia, and the FDA approved it in 1991 for hypercalcemia of malignancy, later adding Paget disease, osteolytic bone metastases from breast cancer and myeloma bone lesions. The Aredia brand has been discontinued, but generic pamidronate remains available.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Pamidronate (APD) is synthesized and studied, notably in Leiden."], ["1991", "FDA approves Aredia for hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["1990s", "Approvals expand to Paget disease, breast cancer bone metastases and myeloma."]],
  "cell": "Pamidronate's P-C-P backbone binds hydroxyapatite on bone surfaces, especially where resorption is active. Osteoclasts take it up as they dissolve bone. Its aminopropyl side chain inhibits farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in the mevalonate pathway, though less potently than zoledronic acid. Without farnesyl and geranylgeranyl groups, small GTPases such as Ras, Rho and Rab cannot be prenylated and anchored to membranes. The osteoclast loses its ruffled border, cytoskeletal organization and vesicle transport, stops resorbing bone and undergoes apoptosis. The result is less calcium released from bone, lower serum calcium, and fewer skeletal complications in metastatic disease. Isoprenoid buildup also activates gamma delta T cells, causing a fever reaction.",
  "body": [["Bone", "Slowed osteoclast resorption reduces bone pain, fractures and other skeletal complications from metastases and myeloma, and calms Paget disease."], ["Blood calcium", "Serum calcium falls within one to two days and reaches its lowest point about a week later."], ["Kidneys", "The drug is excreted unchanged by the kidneys and can damage them, including a form of collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis."], ["Immune system", "Fever and flu-like symptoms after the first infusion reflect an acute phase reaction."], ["Electrolytes", "Low phosphate, magnesium and potassium are common."], ["Jaw and eyes", "Osteonecrosis of the jaw and eye inflammation such as uveitis or scleritis are rare but recognized."]],
  "journey": "Pamidronate is given by slow IV infusion over several hours. It quickly leaves the blood to bind bone or be filtered by the kidneys. It is not metabolized. The portion not taken up by bone is excreted unchanged in urine, with a plasma elimination half-life of about 28 hours, while the bone-bound drug stays for a long time. Serum calcium usually returns to normal within days and stays normal for about two weeks or longer.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from its two homes, bone and kidney. Blocking resorption lowers calcium, phosphate and magnesium. High concentrations in the kidney injure tubules and glomeruli, so it is infused slowly and renal function is checked before each dose. The acute phase reaction comes from gamma delta T cell activation. Osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical fractures reflect long-term suppression of bone remodeling. Infusion site reactions occur. There is no antidote; calcium and vitamin D correct hypocalcemia.",
  "src": ["Pamidronate disodium injection FDA label", "Fleisch H. Bisphosphonates in Bone Disease", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"crotalidae-polyvalent-immune-fab": {
  "origin": "Antivenom therapy began in the 1890s, when Albert Calmette at the Pasteur Institute and others showed that serum from animals immunized with venom could protect against snakebite, building on Emil von Behring and Shibasaburo Kitasato's 1890 work on antitoxins. For decades US rattlesnake bites were treated with a whole-serum horse antivenom made by Wyeth from the 1950s, which caused frequent allergic reactions and serum sickness. Researchers then developed a purer product: sheep were immunized with venoms from four North American pit vipers, and their antibodies were cut with the enzyme papain into small Fab fragments, removing the Fc portion that drives many reactions. The FDA approved CroFab in October 2000, and it became the standard US treatment for rattlesnake, copperhead and cottonmouth envenomation.",
  "timeline": [["1890", "Von Behring and Kitasato describe antitoxin serum therapy."], ["1890s", "Calmette develops one of the first snake antivenoms."], ["1950s", "Wyeth's equine whole-IgG crotalid antivenom comes into use in the US."], ["2000", "FDA approves CroFab, an ovine Fab antivenom."]],
  "cell": "Sheep are immunized with venom from the western diamondback (Crotalus atrox), eastern diamondback (Crotalus adamanteus), Mojave rattlesnake (Crotalus scutulatus) and cottonmouth (Agkistrodon piscivorus). Their IgG is purified and digested with papain into Fab fragments of about 50 kDa, each with one antigen-binding site and no Fc. The fragments bind venom components including snake venom metalloproteinases that break down capillary basement membranes and cause swelling and bleeding, phospholipase A2 enzymes that damage cell membranes and muscle, and thrombin-like enzymes that consume fibrinogen. Binding blocks their active sites or traps them in complexes, and the small size helps Fab reach venom in the tissues and redistribute it into the blood, where the complexes are filtered by the kidneys. Fab does not repair damage that has already occurred.",
  "body": [["Bite site and limb", "Neutralizing metalloproteinases and phospholipases stops progression of swelling, pain and tissue injury."], ["Blood clotting", "Binding thrombin-like enzymes reverses low fibrinogen and prolonged clotting times."], ["Platelets", "Low platelet counts from venom recover as toxins are neutralized."], ["Circulation", "Systemic effects such as low blood pressure and vomiting resolve."], ["Immune system", "Sheep proteins can cause hives, itching or anaphylaxis, and delayed serum sickness one to three weeks later."]],
  "journey": "CroFab is reconstituted and given by IV infusion, with repeat doses until the envenomation is controlled, then maintenance doses. The Fab fragments spread from the blood into tissue fluid and bind venom. Fab is small enough for the kidneys to filter, and both free Fab and Fab-venom complexes are cleared renally, with an elimination half-life of about 12 to 23 hours. Venom from the bite site can keep entering the blood longer than Fab persists, which is why coagulopathy can recur days later.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions arise because the body sees sheep protein as foreign; removing the Fc portion and the immunogenic parts lowers but does not remove this risk. People allergic to papain or papaya may react. Serum sickness is a type III immune complex reaction days to weeks later. Recurrent coagulopathy and low platelets happen because Fab is cleared faster than venom depots in the tissue, so follow-up labs are checked. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine and stopping the infusion.",
  "fun": "The sheep used to make CroFab are raised in Australia, which is free of many livestock diseases.",
  "src": ["CroFab (crotalidae polyvalent immune fab) FDA label", "Dart RC et al. Arch Intern Med 2001", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"crotalidae-immune-fab2-equine": {
  "origin": "Anavip was developed by Instituto Bioclon in Mexico, a company with a long history of making antivenoms for Latin American snakes and scorpions. Horses are immunized with venom from two Latin American pit vipers, the fer-de-lance Bothrops asper and the South American rattlesnake Crotalus durissus, and their antibodies are digested with pepsin to make F(ab')2 fragments. Because pit viper venoms share many toxin families, these antibodies cross-neutralize North American rattlesnake venom. The product was used in Latin America before US trials showed it worked against rattlesnake bites and had less late return of coagulopathy than CroFab. The FDA approved Anavip in May 2015 for North American rattlesnake envenomation, and its label later broadened to North American pit vipers.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Calmette develops early snake antivenoms using immunized animals."], ["2000s", "Instituto Bioclon's equine F(ab')2 antivenom is studied in US rattlesnake bites."], ["2015", "FDA approves Anavip for North American rattlesnake envenomation."]],
  "cell": "Horses are immunized with Bothrops asper and Crotalus durissus venoms, and their IgG is digested with pepsin. Pepsin removes the Fc portion but leaves the two antigen-binding arms joined, making F(ab')2 fragments of about 110 kDa with two binding sites each. These fragments bind and neutralize conserved pit viper toxins: metalloproteinases that break down capillary walls and cause swelling and bleeding, phospholipase A2 that damages membranes and muscle, and thrombin-like enzymes that consume fibrinogen. Two binding sites allow them to cross-link toxins into larger complexes that are removed by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the fragment is too big for fast kidney filtration, it stays in the blood for days, matching the slow release of venom from the bite site and lowering late recurrence of coagulopathy.",
  "body": [["Bite site and limb", "Neutralizing local toxins stops progression of swelling, pain and tissue damage."], ["Blood clotting", "Binding thrombin-like enzymes reverses venom-induced low fibrinogen and prolonged clotting, with less late recurrence."], ["Platelets", "Venom-induced thrombocytopenia improves as toxins are neutralized."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause itching, rash, hives, fever, anaphylaxis or delayed serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "Anavip is reconstituted and given as an IV infusion, with additional doses if signs of envenomation continue. The large F(ab')2 fragments mostly stay in the blood and nearby tissue fluid, where they bind venom as it leaks from the bite. They are too large for rapid kidney filtration and are broken down slowly by protein catabolism, with a half-life of about 133 hours, about five to six days. This long presence covers the time venom keeps entering the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because horse proteins are foreign; removing Fc and purifying the product reduces but does not remove the risk. People with horse allergy or prior equine antivenom are at higher risk. Serum sickness, a delayed immune complex reaction, can cause rash, fever and joint pain. Because the fragments last longer, late coagulopathy is less common than with Fab products, but labs are still followed. Anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.",
  "src": ["Anavip (crotalidae immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "Bush SP et al. Ann Emerg Med 2015", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"north-american-coral-snake-antivenin": {
  "origin": "The eastern coral snake, Micrurus fulvius, is an elapid, a relative of cobras, and its venom causes paralysis rather than swelling. Wyeth Laboratories produced North American Coral Snake Antivenin by immunizing horses with Micrurus fulvius venom and purifying their antibodies, and it was licensed in the US in 1967. Because coral snake bites are rare and the product was hard to make profitably, Wyeth stopped routine production in the early 2000s. To avoid running out, the FDA repeatedly extended the expiration dates of existing lots after testing. Pfizer, which acquired Wyeth, later made new lots. It remains the only FDA-approved US coral snake antivenom, and supply has often been limited.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Early antivenoms are made from the serum of immunized animals."], ["1967", "Wyeth's North American Coral Snake Antivenin is licensed in the US."], ["2000s", "Production stops and the FDA extends expiration dates to preserve supply."], ["2010s", "Pfizer produces new lots of the antivenin."]],
  "cell": "Coral snake venom contains alpha-neurotoxins, small three-finger toxin proteins that bind the alpha subunits of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. This blocks acetylcholine from opening the receptor channel, so muscles cannot depolarize, causing descending flaccid paralysis similar to a nondepolarizing paralytic drug. The antivenin is whole equine IgG raised against Micrurus fulvius venom. Its antibodies bind free toxin in the blood and tissues, preventing new binding to receptors. Toxin that has already bound tightly to receptors is not easily removed, so the antivenin stops paralysis from progressing but does not quickly reverse it. Recovery depends on the body making new receptors. This is why treatment is given early, often before symptoms appear, when a bite is confirmed.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing alpha-neurotoxins prevents further blockade of nicotinic receptors and stops paralysis from progressing."], ["Eyes and face", "Ptosis, double vision and slurred speech are early signs of venom action that treatment aims to halt."], ["Breathing", "By preventing diaphragm and respiratory muscle paralysis, it can avoid the need for mechanical ventilation."], ["Immune system", "Whole horse IgG commonly causes allergic reactions, anaphylaxis and delayed serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenin is reconstituted and given by slow IV infusion. Whole IgG is large and stays mainly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not filtered by the kidneys; it is removed slowly by the reticuloendothelial system and normal antibody catabolism, so it lasts several days. It binds toxin soon after infusion. Because coral snake symptoms can be delayed for many hours, early treatment of a confirmed bite is common.",
  "sidefx": "Because it is whole horse immunoglobulin with its Fc portion, allergic reactions are more frequent than with fragment antivenoms. Immediate reactions range from hives to anaphylaxis, so epinephrine and airway equipment are kept ready. Serum sickness, an immune complex reaction to the foreign protein, causes rash, fever and joint pain one to three weeks later and is treated with steroids and antihistamines. If antivenin is not available, care is supportive with airway protection and ventilation until new receptors form.",
  "fun": "The rhyme often used to tell coral snakes from harmless mimics only works for North American species and is not reliable elsewhere.",
  "src": ["North American Coral Snake Antivenin FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"black-widow-spider-antivenin": {
  "origin": "Black widow spiders, Latrodectus mactans and related species, produce alpha-latrotoxin, one of the most potent nerve toxins known for its size. In the early 20th century researchers learned to make antivenom by injecting horses with small amounts of spider venom and collecting their serum. Merck's Antivenin (Latrodectus mactans) has been licensed in the US since the 1930s and is still made by immunizing horses with venom extracted from spiders. It is whole equine serum protein, so it carries a real allergy risk, and it is usually reserved for severe envenomation with pain and muscle spasm not controlled by other treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1900s", "Horse serum antivenoms are developed for venomous animal bites."], ["1930s", "Merck's black widow antivenin is licensed in the US."], ["1990s", "Neurexins and latrophilins are identified as receptors for alpha-latrotoxin."]],
  "cell": "Alpha-latrotoxin is a large protein that binds receptors on presynaptic nerve terminals, mainly neurexins and latrophilins (adhesion G protein-coupled receptors). After binding, it forms tetramers that insert into the membrane and create cation-permeable pores. Calcium floods in, and the toxin also directly triggers vesicle fusion, causing massive release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction and norepinephrine and other transmitters at autonomic synapses. The result is painful muscle cramping and rigidity, sweating, high blood pressure and fast heart rate. The antivenin is equine IgG against Latrodectus mactans venom. It binds circulating toxin and blocks it from reaching nerve terminals, and it can relieve symptoms quickly when toxin is still active.",
  "body": [["Muscles", "Neutralizing toxin relieves severe cramps, abdominal rigidity and back and chest pain caused by excess acetylcholine release."], ["Autonomic system", "Sweating, high blood pressure and fast heart rate from norepinephrine release settle as toxin is bound."], ["Pain", "Symptom relief often begins within one to three hours, sooner than with opioids and benzodiazepines alone."], ["Immune system", "Horse serum protein can cause allergic reactions, anaphylaxis and delayed serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenin is reconstituted and given IV, often diluted and infused slowly, or by IM injection. Whole IgG stays in the blood and tissue fluid and is cleared slowly by the reticuloendothelial system over several days. It binds toxin that has not yet reached nerve terminals. A skin test may be considered by some clinicians, but it does not reliably predict reactions.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions come from the foreign horse proteins in a whole serum product, and anaphylaxis, including deaths, has been reported. People with horse allergy, asthma or prior equine products are at higher risk, so epinephrine is kept ready. Serum sickness one to three weeks later causes rash, fever and joint pain. Because black widow bites are rarely fatal, its risks are weighed against severity. Care without antivenin relies on opioids and benzodiazepines.",
  "fun": "Only the larger female black widow can deliver a dangerous bite; males are small and rarely bite people.",
  "src": ["Antivenin (Latrodectus mactans) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"centruroides-scorpion-immune-fab2": {
  "origin": "In the southwestern United States, especially Arizona, the bark scorpion Centruroides sculpturatus causes thousands of stings each year, and young children can develop severe neurotoxic symptoms. A goat-derived antivenom produced in Arizona was used locally for years but was never FDA approved, and production ended. Instituto Bioclon in Mexico, where Centruroides stings are a major public health problem, made an equine F(ab')2 antivenom against several Mexican Centruroides species. Clinical work led by researchers at the University of Arizona, including Leslie Boyer, showed that it quickly reversed severe scorpion envenomation in children. The FDA approved Anascorp in August 2011, the first FDA-approved scorpion antivenom.",
  "timeline": [["2009", "A trial shows rapid symptom resolution in children with severe scorpion stings."], ["2011", "FDA approves Anascorp, the first US scorpion antivenom."]],
  "cell": "Centruroides venom contains small neurotoxins that bind voltage-gated sodium channels on nerve cells. Beta toxins shift channel activation to more negative voltages, and other toxins delay inactivation, so channels open too easily and stay open longer. Neurons fire repeatedly, releasing acetylcholine and catecholamines. This causes roving eye movements, muscle fasciculations, involuntary jerking, excess saliva, agitation and breathing trouble. Anascorp is made by immunizing horses with venom from C. noxius, C. limpidus limpidus, C. limpidus tecomanus and C. suffusus suffusus, then digesting the IgG with pepsin to remove Fc and make F(ab')2 fragments. These fragments cross-neutralize the closely related toxins of the Arizona bark scorpion, binding them so they cannot reach the sodium channels.",
  "body": [["Nervous system", "Neutralizing sodium channel toxins stops repetitive nerve firing, so roving eye movements, jerking and fasciculations resolve."], ["Airway and breathing", "Less hypersalivation and muscle incoordination reduce airway risk and the need for sedation or intubation."], ["Behavior", "Agitation and restlessness in children settle, usually within hours."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause rash, itching, fever, vomiting, anaphylaxis or delayed serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "Anascorp is reconstituted and given as an IV infusion, sometimes followed by more vials if symptoms persist. The F(ab')2 fragments distribute into the blood and tissue fluid and bind venom. Symptoms often resolve within about one to four hours. The fragments are too large for rapid kidney filtration and are broken down slowly, with a half-life of about 159 hours, so they remain for days.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects mainly reflect reaction to foreign horse protein, even though the Fc portion is removed. Vomiting, fever, rash, nausea and itching are the most common. Serum sickness, an immune complex reaction days to weeks later, causes rash, fever and joint pain. Anaphylaxis is possible, particularly in people with horse allergy or prior equine products, so epinephrine is kept ready. Without antivenom, severe cases are managed with sedation and airway support.",
  "src": ["Anascorp (Centruroides scorpion immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "Boyer LV et al. N Engl J Med 2009", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"nimodipine": {
  "origin": "Nimodipine was developed by the German company Bayer in the 1970s as part of its work on dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, a family that also includes nifedipine. Chemists made it more lipid soluble so it could cross the blood-brain barrier and act on brain vessels. Trials in the 1980s, including a landmark British trial, showed that it reduced death and disability from delayed brain ischemia after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage. The FDA approved Nimotop capsules in 1988 for this use. In 2013 an oral solution, Nymalize, was approved, partly to reduce the danger of nurses drawing capsule liquid into syringes and giving it by vein. The Nimotop brand has since been discontinued, with generic capsules and Nymalize still available.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Bayer develops nimodipine as a cerebrovascular-selective dihydropyridine."], ["1988", "FDA approves Nimotop for subarachnoid hemorrhage."], ["1989", "The British aneurysm nimodipine trial reports improved outcomes."], ["2013", "FDA approves Nymalize oral solution."]],
  "cell": "Nimodipine blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels (Cav1.2) by binding the dihydropyridine site on the alpha-1 subunit, favoring the inactivated state of the channel. In vascular smooth muscle, calcium entering through these channels binds calmodulin, activating myosin light chain kinase and contraction. Blocking entry relaxes arterial smooth muscle. Its high lipophilicity lets it cross the blood-brain barrier, and it acts preferentially on cerebral arterioles. After subarachnoid hemorrhage, it improves outcomes even though large-vessel angiographic vasospasm is not clearly reduced. Proposed explanations include dilation of small vessels and collateral flow, and neuroprotection by limiting calcium overload in ischemic neurons, which otherwise activates proteases, phospholipases and cell death pathways.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Fewer delayed ischemic neurologic deficits after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage, improving survival and recovery."], ["Systemic blood vessels", "Arterial dilation lowers blood pressure, which can reduce brain perfusion if it falls too far."], ["Heart", "Reflex tachycardia or sometimes bradycardia can occur; little direct depression of the heart at usual doses."], ["Head", "Headache and flushing come from dilation of cranial and skin vessels."], ["Gut", "Nausea and other stomach upset are common."]],
  "journey": "Nimodipine is given by mouth or through a feeding tube every few hours, starting within 96 hours of the bleed. It is absorbed quickly, but extensive first-pass CYP3A4 metabolism in the gut wall and liver leaves bioavailability around 13 percent. It is over 95 percent protein bound and crosses into the brain. Its half-life is short, one to two hours, with a terminal phase of about eight to nine hours. Inactive metabolites leave in urine and bile. CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them.",
  "sidefx": "Hypotension, the main dose-limiting effect, comes from relaxing systemic arteries, and it matters because the injured brain needs adequate pressure for blood flow. Headache and flushing reflect vessel dilation. The boxed warning is about route: capsule contents injected IV have caused cardiovascular collapse and cardiac arrest, because a large dose acts instantly on heart and vessels. Oral syringes that cannot connect to IV lines are used. There is no specific antidote; calcium and vasopressors are used for severe hypotension.",
  "src": ["Nymalize (nimodipine) FDA label", "Pickard JD et al. BMJ 1989", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tirofiban": {
  "origin": "In the 1980s researchers learned that the final step in platelet clumping is fibrinogen bridging glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors on neighboring platelets, and that fibrinogen grips the receptor through a short sequence, arginine-glycine-aspartate (RGD). Snake venom proteins called disintegrins, such as echistatin, also use RGD to block the receptor. Chemists at Merck set out to build a small non-peptide molecule that mimicked this sequence. Starting from the amino acid tyrosine, they designed tirofiban, a compact RGD mimic. The FDA approved Aggrastat in 1998 for acute coronary syndromes, and it later passed to Medicure. It joined abciximab and eptifibatide as the GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Fibrinogen binding to GP IIb/IIIa through the RGD sequence is described."], ["1990s", "Merck designs tirofiban as a non-peptide RGD mimic."], ["1998", "FDA approves Aggrastat for acute coronary syndromes."]],
  "cell": "When platelets are activated by any stimulus, whether thrombin, ADP, thromboxane A2 or collagen, inside-out signaling changes the shape of glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (integrin alphaIIb-beta3) so it can bind fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor. Each fibrinogen molecule can bridge two platelets, building the platelet plug. Tirofiban is a tyrosine-derived non-peptide that mimics the RGD sequence and binds the receptor's ligand pocket reversibly. Blocking the receptor stops fibrinogen and von Willebrand factor binding, so platelets cannot cross-link, regardless of what activated them. That makes it a final common pathway inhibitor. Because binding is reversible and the drug is cleared fairly fast, platelet function returns within about four to eight hours after the infusion stops.",
  "body": [["Platelets", "Aggregation is strongly inhibited, preventing new platelet-rich clots in narrowed or stented coronary arteries."], ["Heart", "Fewer heart attacks and ischemic events in acute coronary syndromes and during PCI."], ["Bleeding sites", "Access sites, gut, urinary tract and brain can bleed because platelets cannot form plugs."], ["Platelet count", "Rarely platelets fall suddenly and profoundly within hours, likely from antibodies that recognize the drug-bound receptor."]],
  "journey": "Tirofiban is given IV as a bolus followed by an infusion, and platelet inhibition begins within minutes. About 65 percent is bound to plasma proteins. It is minimally metabolized and leaves the body largely unchanged in urine and bile. The half-life is about two hours, and kidney impairment slows clearance, so the infusion is reduced when kidney function is low. Platelet function recovers toward baseline within about four to eight hours after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main risk because platelets cannot form plugs, and it is greater with heparin, aspirin and P2Y12 inhibitors given together. Acute thrombocytopenia can occur within hours, thought to be immune-mediated as antibodies bind receptors altered by drug binding, so counts are checked early. There is no specific antidote. Because binding is reversible and clearance is fast, stopping the infusion restores platelet function in hours; platelet transfusion is less effective than with abciximab because free drug can bind new platelets.",
  "fun": "Tirofiban is a small molecule that comes off its receptor quickly, so unlike the antibody abciximab, platelet function returns within hours of stopping it.",
  "src": ["Aggrastat (tirofiban) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"cosyntropin": {
  "origin": "ACTH, the pituitary hormone that drives the adrenal glands, was identified in the 1930s, and its full structure of 39 amino acids was worked out in the 1950s. Researchers found that the first 24 amino acids carry all of its biological activity, while the rest varies between species and triggers allergy. In the early 1960s Robert Schwyzer and colleagues at Ciba in Basel synthesized this 24-amino-acid peptide, tetracosactide, one of the early landmarks of peptide synthesis. In the US it is sold as cosyntropin (Cortrosyn). It replaced animal-derived ACTH for the ACTH stimulation test, the standard test for adrenal insufficiency.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "ACTH is identified as a pituitary hormone that stimulates the adrenal cortex."], ["1950s", "The amino acid sequence of ACTH is determined."], ["1960s", "Schwyzer's group at Ciba synthesizes ACTH 1-24 (tetracosactide)."]],
  "cell": "Cosyntropin is identical to amino acids 1 to 24 of human ACTH. It binds the melanocortin 2 receptor (MC2R), a Gs-coupled receptor on cells of the adrenal cortex, which needs the accessory protein MRAP to reach the cell surface. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating protein kinase A. Within minutes, PKA increases cholesterol delivery to mitochondria through the StAR protein and activates side-chain cleavage of cholesterol to pregnenolone, the rate-limiting step of steroid synthesis. Cortisol output from the zona fasciculata rises within 30 to 60 minutes. A healthy adrenal gland responds with a clear cortisol rise. In primary adrenal insufficiency, the gland is damaged and cannot respond. In long-standing secondary insufficiency, the gland has atrophied without ACTH and also responds poorly.",
  "body": [["Adrenal cortex", "Rapid stimulation of cortisol synthesis and release, used to test whether the adrenal glands work."], ["Blood cortisol", "Cortisol rises and peaks 30 to 60 minutes after the dose in a normal gland."], ["Aldosterone", "A smaller, transient rise in aldosterone may occur, since ACTH also has a modest effect on the zona glomerulosa."], ["Skin and vessels", "Flushing and mild injection site reactions can occur."]],
  "journey": "Cosyntropin is given by IV or IM injection, and blood cortisol is measured before and 30 and 60 minutes after. The peptide is cleared from plasma quickly, with a half-life of about 15 minutes, and is broken down by proteolysis into fragments that leave mainly in urine. The cortisol rise lasts a few hours. Because it is a short peptide used once for testing, interactions mostly involve drugs that change cortisol measurement, such as glucocorticoids or estrogen that raises cortisol-binding globulin.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are few because it is used once and acts briefly. Flushing and nausea can occur. Because the allergenic C-terminal portion of animal ACTH is absent, allergic reactions are uncommon, but hypersensitivity and rare anaphylaxis have been reported, especially in people with prior reactions to ACTH. Results can be misleading after recent glucocorticoid use or with conditions that change cortisol-binding globulin. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Cortrosyn (cosyntropin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"oxacillin": {
  "origin": "Oxacillin descends from Alexander Fleming's 1928 discovery that a Penicillium mold killed staphylococci. By the 1950s many hospital staph strains made penicillinase, an enzyme that cuts penicillin's beta-lactam ring. In 1957 scientists at Beecham Research Laboratories in England isolated 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the bare penicillin core, which let chemists attach new side chains. They produced methicillin in 1959, and in the early 1960s a series of isoxazolyl penicillins, including oxacillin, cloxacillin and dicloxacillin, whose bulky side chains shield the ring from penicillinase. Oxacillin became a standard IV drug for methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, and its name is still used in lab testing, where oxacillin resistance defines MRSA.",
  "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin killing staphylococci."], ["1957", "Beecham scientists isolate 6-aminopenicillanic acid."], ["1959", "Methicillin, the first penicillinase-resistant penicillin, is introduced."], ["1960s", "Isoxazolyl penicillins including oxacillin enter clinical use."]],
  "cell": "Oxacillin is a beta-lactam whose strained four-membered ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of peptidoglycan precursors. Penicillin-binding proteins (transpeptidases) attack the ring and become covalently acylated, so they cannot form the peptide cross-links that give the cell wall strength. Without cross-linking, autolysins break down the wall, and the cell bursts from internal pressure. The killing is time-dependent, so effect depends on how long levels stay above the MIC. The bulky phenyl isoxazolyl side chain blocks staphylococcal penicillinase from reaching the ring. MRSA resists oxacillin because the mecA gene encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase with low affinity for beta-lactams that keeps building the wall when other PBPs are blocked. Its gram-negative activity is poor because it does not pass well through porins.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Kills methicillin-susceptible S. aureus and streptococci causing cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Blood and heart valves", "Used for MSSA bacteremia and endocarditis, where it often works better than vancomycin for susceptible strains."], ["Bones and joints", "Treats MSSA osteomyelitis and septic arthritis."], ["Liver", "Can raise liver enzymes or cause hepatitis, more often than nafcillin."], ["Kidneys and blood", "Rarely causes interstitial nephritis or neutropenia, usually after prolonged use."]],
  "journey": "Oxacillin is given IV or IM because oral absorption is poor. It spreads into most tissues, including bone and inflamed joints, and crosses inflamed meninges to a limited degree. It is about 94 percent protein bound. Part is metabolized in the liver, and the drug and metabolites leave through both kidneys and bile, so dose changes are less often needed in kidney disease. The half-life is short, about 20 to 30 minutes, so it is dosed every four to six hours or by continuous infusion.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions, from rash to anaphylaxis, occur because the beta-lactam ring opens and binds body proteins, forming antigens; cross-reactivity applies to other penicillins. Liver enzyme rises reflect hepatic handling. Interstitial nephritis is an immune reaction in the kidney. Neutropenia occurs with long courses. Hypokalemia can result from the large sodium load delivered with the drug. Disrupting gut flora can allow C. difficile to overgrow. Phlebitis comes from irritation of the vein. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.",
  "src": ["Oxacillin for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]
},
"piperacillin": {
  "origin": "Piperacillin was created in the 1970s by Toyama Chemical in Japan as part of a push to extend penicillins against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and other gram-negative rods. It is a ureidopenicillin, built on the ampicillin skeleton with a polar piperazine side chain that helps it pass through gram-negative porins and bind their penicillin-binding proteins. It was sold as a single agent in the US (Pipracil) from the early 1980s. Because many bacteria destroy it with beta-lactamases, it was later paired with the inhibitor tazobactam, approved as Zosyn in 1993, which became one of the most widely used hospital antibiotics. No single-agent piperacillin product is currently marketed in the US; it is available only as piperacillin-tazobactam.",
  "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin's antibacterial effect."], ["1970s", "Toyama Chemical develops piperacillin."], ["1980s", "Single-agent piperacillin enters US use."], ["1993", "FDA approves piperacillin-tazobactam (Zosyn)."]],
  "cell": "Piperacillin is a beta-lactam that acylates penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP3 of gram-negative rods, which builds the septum during division. Acylated PBPs cannot cross-link peptidoglycan. Blocking PBP3 causes long filaments before the cells lyse. The killing is time-dependent, so effect depends on the time levels stay above the MIC, which is why extended infusions are sometimes used. The polar ureido-piperazine side chain improves passage through porins in the outer membrane and increases affinity for gram-negative PBPs, giving activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacterales, streptococci, some enterococci and anaerobes. However, it is hydrolyzed by many beta-lactamases, including staphylococcal penicillinase, ESBLs and AmpC, which is why it is paired with tazobactam, an irreversible inhibitor of many class A enzymes.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired pneumonia caused by Pseudomonas and other gram-negative rods."], ["Abdomen", "With its anaerobic and gram-negative coverage it treats intra-abdominal infections."], ["Blood", "Can interfere with platelet function and cause bleeding, especially in kidney failure."], ["Electrolytes", "Acts as a non-absorbable anion in the kidney tubule, causing potassium loss and hypokalemia."], ["Kidneys and marrow", "Rarely causes interstitial nephritis and, with long courses, neutropenia."]],
  "journey": "Piperacillin is given IV. It distributes into most tissues and fluids, including lung, bile and abdominal tissue, and into inflamed meninges to some extent. It is about 30 percent protein bound and is minimally metabolized. Most is excreted unchanged by the kidneys through filtration and tubular secretion, and some is excreted in bile. The half-life is about one hour, so dosing is frequent, and doses are adjusted in kidney impairment.",
  "sidefx": "As with all penicillins, the opened beta-lactam binds body proteins and can trigger allergy, from rash to anaphylaxis. Bleeding comes from interference with platelet aggregation. Hypokalemia occurs because the drug acts as an unreabsorbed anion in the distal tubule, pulling potassium out. Interstitial nephritis is an immune reaction. Long courses can cause neutropenia. Gut flora disruption allows C. difficile. With vancomycin, piperacillin-tazobactam has been linked to higher rates of acute kidney injury. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Zosyn (piperacillin and tazobactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]
},
"cefoxitin": {
  "origin": "The cephalosporin story began in 1945, when Giuseppe Brotzu in Sardinia found that a fungus, later called Acremonium (Cephalosporium), from sea water near a sewage outlet produced antibiotic substances. In the early 1970s researchers at Merck and at Eli Lilly found a related group, the cephamycins, made by Streptomyces bacteria. Merck chemically modified cephamycin C from Streptomyces lactamdurans to create cefoxitin. Its methoxy group gave stability against many beta-lactamases and strong activity against anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis. Marketed as Mefoxin around 1978, it became widely used for surgical prophylaxis and mixed abdominal and pelvic infections. It is now available as a generic.",
  "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers cephalosporin-producing fungus in Sardinia."], ["1971", "Cephamycins are reported from Streptomyces species."], ["1978", "Cefoxitin is introduced in the US as Mefoxin."]],
  "cell": "Cefoxitin is a beta-lactam that binds and acylates penicillin-binding proteins, blocking the transpeptidation that cross-links peptidoglycan. Weakened walls and autolysin activity cause cell lysis, so it is bactericidal and time-dependent. What sets it apart is the 7-alpha-methoxy group of the cephamycins, which sterically blocks many beta-lactamases from attacking the ring. This gives it activity against anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis and some beta-lactamase-producing gram-negatives. It also strongly induces chromosomal AmpC beta-lactamase in organisms such as Enterobacter, which can lead to resistance to other cephalosporins. It is inactive against enterococci (their PBPs bind it poorly), MRSA (PBP2a) and Pseudomonas. Because it induces mecA expression, the cefoxitin disk is used in labs to detect MRSA.",
  "body": [["Abdomen and pelvis", "Covers gut gram-negatives and anaerobes, so it is used for intra-abdominal and gynecologic infections and surgical prophylaxis."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats mixed aerobic and anaerobic infections such as diabetic foot or bite wounds."], ["Gut", "Disrupts normal flora, allowing diarrhea and C. difficile infection."], ["Blood", "Can cause a positive Coombs test, eosinophilia and rarely hemolytic anemia."], ["Kidneys", "Rarely causes allergic interstitial nephritis with fever, rash and rising creatinine."]],
  "journey": "Cefoxitin is given IV. It distributes into most tissues and fluids, including the peritoneal fluid and pelvic tissues, but it does not reach the cerebrospinal fluid well enough for meningitis. It is about 65 to 80 percent protein bound and is minimally metabolized. Most is excreted unchanged in the urine through filtration and tubular secretion, and probenecid slows this. The half-life is about 45 to 60 minutes, so doses are repeated every six to eight hours, and during long surgery it is re-dosed.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because the opened beta-lactam binds proteins; cross-reactivity with penicillins is low but possible, largely determined by side-chain similarity. Phlebitis comes from vein irritation. C. difficile diarrhea follows disruption of gut anaerobes. Positive Coombs tests and rare hemolysis come from drug binding to red cell membranes. Interstitial nephritis is immune-mediated. Cefoxitin can falsely raise creatinine on some lab methods. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Cefoxitin for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]
},
"ceftazidime": {
  "origin": "Ceftazidime is a third-generation cephalosporin, part of the family that began with Giuseppe Brotzu's 1945 discovery of an antibiotic-making fungus off the coast of Sardinia. In the late 1970s chemists at Glaxo in Britain added an aminothiazolyl oxime and a carboxypropyl group to the cephalosporin core, which gave strong activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a major cause of hospital and cystic fibrosis infections. It was introduced in the early 1980s and approved in the US in the mid-1980s as Fortaz, with Tazicef as another brand. For years it was a mainstay antipseudomonal drug. Its weakness against newer beta-lactamases later led to its pairing with avibactam.",
  "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers cephalosporin-producing fungus in Sardinia."], ["1970s", "Glaxo chemists develop ceftazidime."], ["1980s", "Ceftazidime enters clinical use as an antipseudomonal cephalosporin."], ["2015", "Ceftazidime is paired with avibactam as Avycaz."]],
  "cell": "Ceftazidime binds penicillin-binding protein 3 (PBP3) of gram-negative bacilli with high affinity. PBP3 builds the septum when bacteria divide, so blocking it causes long filaments before lysis. Like other beta-lactams, the ring acylates the PBP active site, stopping peptidoglycan cross-linking, and the weakened wall bursts. Killing is time-dependent. The aminothiazolyl oxime group adds stability against some beta-lactamases and gram-negative potency, and the carboxypropyl group adds activity against Pseudomonas and helps passage through porins. It is hydrolyzed by ESBLs, AmpC and carbapenemases. It binds staphylococcal PBPs weakly, so its gram-positive activity is poor, and it has no useful anaerobic activity.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Treats Pseudomonas and other gram-negative pneumonias, including in cystic fibrosis."], ["Brain and meninges", "Crosses inflamed meninges well enough to treat gram-negative meningitis, such as after neurosurgery."], ["Urinary tract", "High urine levels help treat complicated urinary infections."], ["Fever with neutropenia", "Its Pseudomonas coverage made it a traditional choice for febrile neutropenia."], ["Brain toxicity", "In kidney failure, high levels can cause confusion, myoclonus, encephalopathy and seizures."]],
  "journey": "Ceftazidime is given IV or IM. It distributes widely into tissues and fluids, including bone, lung and inflamed meninges. It is less than 10 percent protein bound and is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete about 80 to 90 percent unchanged by glomerular filtration. The half-life is about two hours in normal kidney function and much longer in kidney failure, so doses are adjusted to kidney function.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because the opened beta-lactam binds body proteins. Ceftazidime shares its side chain with aztreonam, so cross-reactivity is possible between them. Neurotoxicity occurs when kidney failure lets levels build up, as beta-lactams in high concentrations block GABA-A receptors in the brain. Phlebitis comes from vein irritation, and C. difficile follows disruption of gut flora. A positive Coombs test reflects drug binding to red cells. Dialysis removes it. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Fortaz (ceftazidime) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]
},
"ceftolozane-tazobactam": {
  "origin": "Ceftolozane was discovered by the Japanese company Astellas, which was searching for a cephalosporin that could beat Pseudomonas aeruginosa even when the bacteria pumped drugs out or closed their porins. Its structure is related to ceftazidime but with a bulkier side chain that makes it stable against the AmpC enzyme of Pseudomonas. The US company Calixa licensed it and paired it with tazobactam, a beta-lactamase inhibitor discovered at Taiho Pharmaceutical in Japan and used since the 1990s with piperacillin. Cubist Pharmaceuticals acquired Calixa, and the FDA approved Zerbaxa in December 2014 for complicated abdominal and urinary infections, later adding hospital-acquired pneumonia. Merck acquired Cubist in 2015.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Taiho Pharmaceutical discovers tazobactam."], ["2000s", "Astellas discovers ceftolozane, later developed by Calixa and Cubist."], ["2014", "FDA approves Zerbaxa for complicated intra-abdominal and urinary infections."], ["2019", "FDA approves Zerbaxa for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Ceftolozane is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins, with high affinity for P. aeruginosa PBP1b, PBP1c and PBP3. Acylating these enzymes stops peptidoglycan cross-linking, and the cell lyses. Its side chain makes it a poor substrate for Pseudomonas AmpC, and its potency is little affected by loss of the OprD porin or by upregulated efflux pumps, the main ways Pseudomonas resists other drugs. That explains its activity against many multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas strains. Tazobactam is a penicillanic acid sulfone that acts as a suicide inhibitor: it acylates the active-site serine of many class A beta-lactamases, including most ESBLs, and some class C enzymes, permanently disabling them. Tazobactam extends ceftolozane's coverage to ESBL-producing Enterobacterales. Neither part inhibits carbapenemases such as KPC or metallo-beta-lactamases.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, especially with multidrug-resistant Pseudomonas."], ["Abdomen", "With metronidazole, treats complicated intra-abdominal infections."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections and pyelonephritis, including ESBL producers."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and C. difficile infection can follow disruption of gut flora."], ["Kidneys", "Both parts are cleared by the kidneys, and lower effectiveness was seen with reduced kidney function in trials."]],
  "journey": "Zerbaxa is given IV over one hour every eight hours. Both components are minimally protein bound and distribute into body tissues, including the lung lining fluid. Ceftolozane is not metabolized and leaves unchanged by glomerular filtration, with a half-life of about two and a half to three hours. Tazobactam is partly hydrolyzed to an inactive metabolite and is cleared by both filtration and tubular secretion, with a half-life of about one hour. Doses are adjusted for kidney function.",
  "sidefx": "Like other beta-lactams, the drug can cause allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, through protein binding by the opened ring. Broad gram-negative activity disrupts gut flora, causing diarrhea and C. difficile colitis. Hypokalemia can occur. In kidney impairment, accumulating beta-lactams can affect the brain. Lower cure rates seen with moderate kidney impairment in trials are thought to relate partly to dosing as kidney function changes. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Zerbaxa (ceftolozane and tazobactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"ceftazidime-avibactam": {
  "origin": "By the 2000s ceftazidime was losing ground to ESBLs and carbapenemases. Avibactam came from a new chemical class, the diazabicyclooctanes, discovered by scientists at the French company Hoechst Marion Roussel and developed by its spin-off Novexel. Unlike older beta-lactamase inhibitors, it is not itself a beta-lactam and it can inhibit KPC carbapenemases. AstraZeneca and Forest Laboratories, later Actavis and Allergan, developed it with ceftazidime, and the FDA approved Avycaz in February 2015 under the Qualified Infectious Disease Product program for complicated abdominal and urinary infections. Hospital-acquired pneumonia was added in 2018. It became a key drug for carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales that make KPC.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ceftazidime enters clinical use."], ["1990s", "Diazabicyclooctane inhibitors, including avibactam, are discovered in France."], ["2015", "FDA approves Avycaz for complicated intra-abdominal and urinary infections."], ["2018", "FDA approves Avycaz for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Ceftazidime binds PBP3 of gram-negative bacilli, blocking septal peptidoglycan cross-linking and causing filamentation and lysis. It is time-dependent and bactericidal. Avibactam protects it. As a diazabicyclooctane, it covalently acylates the active-site serine of beta-lactamases, but unlike tazobactam, the reaction is reversible: the ring re-closes and avibactam is released intact to inhibit another enzyme. It inhibits Ambler class A enzymes, including ESBLs and KPC carbapenemases, class C AmpC enzymes, and some class D enzymes such as OXA-48. It does not inhibit class B metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM, VIM and IMP, which use zinc rather than serine, so it is combined with aztreonam in some cases. Resistance can arise from KPC mutations such as D179Y that reduce avibactam binding.",
  "body": [["Abdomen", "With metronidazole, treats complicated intra-abdominal infections including resistant gram-negatives."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections and pyelonephritis caused by ESBL and KPC producers."], ["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia from susceptible gram-negative bacteria."], ["Brain", "In kidney impairment, accumulation can cause confusion, myoclonus, seizures or encephalopathy."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and C. difficile infection can follow disruption of gut flora."]],
  "journey": "Avycaz is given IV over two hours every eight hours. Both drugs have low protein binding and similar distribution into tissues, including lung lining fluid. Neither is significantly metabolized. Both leave mainly unchanged in the urine by glomerular filtration, with half-lives of about two to three hours, so they stay matched. Doses are reduced when kidney function falls.",
  "sidefx": "Allergic reactions occur because opened beta-lactams bind body proteins; avibactam is not a beta-lactam, but ceftazidime can still cross-react, especially with aztreonam, which shares a side chain. Neurotoxicity, including seizures and status epilepticus, occurs mainly with kidney impairment or dosing errors, because high beta-lactam levels block brain GABA-A receptors. Broad spectrum explains C. difficile colitis. A positive Coombs test may occur. There is no antidote, though dialysis removes both components.",
  "src": ["Avycaz (ceftazidime and avibactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Ehmann DE et al. PNAS 2012"]
},
"cefiderocol": {
  "origin": "Bacteria need iron, and to get it they release siderophores, small molecules that grab iron and are then pulled back into the cell by dedicated transporters. Scientists at the Japanese company Shionogi used this as a Trojan horse. They attached a catechol iron-binding group to a cephalosporin, so bacteria would actively pump the antibiotic in along with iron. The result, cefiderocol, gets past outer membrane barriers that keep other drugs out and is stable against many carbapenemases, including metallo-beta-lactamases. The FDA approved Fetroja in November 2019 for complicated urinary infections in patients with limited options, and in 2020 for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia. It was the first siderophore antibiotic approved in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers cephalosporin-producing fungus in Sardinia."], ["2000s", "Shionogi designs cefiderocol, a siderophore cephalosporin."], ["2019", "FDA approves Fetroja for complicated urinary tract infections."], ["2020", "FDA approves Fetroja for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Cefiderocol has a catechol group on its C-3 side chain that binds ferric iron (Fe3+) like a natural siderophore. Gram-negative bacteria recognize this iron complex and actively transport it across the outer membrane through TonB-dependent iron transporters, such as CirA and Fiu in E. coli and PiuA in Pseudomonas. This raises drug levels in the periplasm, in addition to passive entry through porins, so loss of porins or overactive efflux pumps matter less. In the periplasm it binds penicillin-binding proteins, mainly PBP3, blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking and causing lysis. Its side chains, similar to those of ceftazidime and cefepime, make it stable against many serine beta-lactamases, including KPC and OXA-48, and against metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM and VIM. It is time-dependent and bactericidal against many carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter and Stenotrophomonas.",
  "body": [["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections and pyelonephritis caused by carbapenem-resistant gram-negative bacteria."], ["Lungs", "Treats hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia from resistant gram-negatives."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and C. difficile infection follow disruption of gut flora."], ["Blood and electrolytes", "Low potassium, raised liver tests and low platelets can occur."], ["Brain", "Seizures and other neurotoxicity can occur, especially with kidney impairment."]],
  "journey": "Cefiderocol is given IV as a three-hour infusion every eight hours, which keeps levels above the MIC for longer. It is about 40 to 60 percent bound to albumin and distributes into lung lining fluid. It is minimally metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about two to three hours. Doses are lowered in kidney impairment and raised in augmented renal clearance, which is common in young critically ill patients.",
  "sidefx": "Allergy and C. difficile colitis follow the same patterns as other cephalosporins. Seizures reflect beta-lactam blockade of brain GABA-A receptors when levels are high. In a trial of patients with carbapenem-resistant infections, all-cause death was higher with cefiderocol than with best available therapy, especially in Acinetobacter infections, and the reason is not fully explained; this is a labeled warning. It can interfere with some urine dipstick tests. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Cefiderocol's iron-grabbing design turns the bacteria's own iron-scavenging system into an entry gate, the same Trojan horse idea nature already uses in some antibiotic-producing microbes.",
  "src": ["Fetroja (cefiderocol) FDA label", "Bassetti M et al. Lancet Infect Dis 2021 (CREDIBLE-CR)"]
},
"ertapenem": {
  "origin": "Carbapenems trace back to thienamycin, which Merck scientists isolated in 1976 from the soil bacterium Streptomyces cattleya. Thienamycin was powerful but unstable, so Merck made imipenem, approved in 1985 with cilastatin, and later meropenem appeared from another company. Merck then designed ertapenem to last longer in the body. A benzoic acid side chain makes it highly bound to albumin, which slows its clearance and allows once-daily dosing, though it also lost activity against Pseudomonas and Acinetobacter. The FDA approved Invanz in November 2001 for community-acquired abdominal, pelvic, skin, lung and urinary infections, and later for prevention of infection after colorectal surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1985", "FDA approves imipenem-cilastatin, the first carbapenem."], ["2001", "FDA approves ertapenem (Invanz) as a once-daily carbapenem."]],
  "cell": "Ertapenem is a carbapenem, a beta-lactam with a carbon in place of sulfur and a double bond in the five-membered ring. It binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP2 and PBP3 in E. coli, blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking and causing lysis. The trans-hydroxyethyl side chain makes it stable against most serine beta-lactamases, including ESBLs and AmpC, which is why it is commonly used for ESBL-producing Enterobacterales. Its negatively charged benzoic acid side chain increases protein binding and slows elimination, but it reduces passage through Pseudomonas porins and affinity for some PBPs. As a result it has poor activity against Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter and Enterococcus. Carbapenemases such as KPC and NDM destroy it.",
  "body": [["Abdomen and pelvis", "Treats community-acquired intra-abdominal and pelvic infections, covering gram-negatives and anaerobes such as Bacteroides."], ["Urinary tract", "Treats complicated urinary infections, including those from ESBL producers."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats complicated skin infections, including diabetic foot infections without Pseudomonas."], ["Brain", "Can lower the seizure threshold, especially in kidney impairment or existing brain disease."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection follow disruption of normal flora."]],
  "journey": "Ertapenem is given once daily IV, or IM where it is about 90 percent absorbed. It is highly and concentration-dependently bound to albumin, about 85 to 95 percent, which keeps it in the blood and gives a half-life of about four hours. Unlike imipenem, it is fairly stable to kidney dehydropeptidase-I, though some is hydrolyzed to an inactive open-ring metabolite. About 80 percent leaves in the urine as unchanged drug and metabolite, and about 10 percent in feces. Doses are reduced in severe kidney impairment.",
  "sidefx": "Seizures come from carbapenem blockade of brain GABA-A receptors, which is more likely when kidney failure raises levels. Carbapenems also sharply lower valproic acid levels, which can trigger seizures, so the combination is avoided. Allergic reactions occur, though cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy is low. Broad spectrum explains C. difficile and yeast infections such as vaginitis. Liver enzymes can rise. There is no antidote; it is removed by hemodialysis.",
  "src": ["Invanz (ertapenem) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Kahan JS et al. J Antibiot 1979"]
},
"tobramycin": {
  "origin": "Tobramycin comes from the soil bacterium Streptomyces tenebrarius, which makes a group of related antibiotics called the nebramycin complex. Scientists at Eli Lilly described the complex in the 1960s, and tobramycin was isolated as one of its components. It proved especially strong against Pseudomonas aeruginosa and was introduced in the US in the 1970s as an injection. In 1997 an inhaled form, TOBI, was approved for people with cystic fibrosis, whose lungs are chronically infected with Pseudomonas, delivering high drug levels to the airways with little reaching the blood. Ophthalmic forms are also used for eye infections.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Streptomycin, the first aminoglycoside, is discovered."], ["1960s", "Eli Lilly scientists describe the nebramycin complex from Streptomyces tenebrarius."], ["1970s", "Tobramycin injection enters US clinical use."], ["1997", "FDA approves inhaled tobramycin (TOBI) for cystic fibrosis."]],
  "cell": "Tobramycin is a polycationic aminoglycoside. It first binds negatively charged lipopolysaccharide and displaces magnesium and calcium bridges in the gram-negative outer membrane, then crosses the inner membrane by an oxygen-dependent active transport driven by the electron transport chain. This is why anaerobes are resistant and why beta-lactams, which damage the wall, increase uptake (synergy). Inside, it binds irreversibly to the A site of 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit. It blocks formation of the initiation complex and causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made. Misfolded proteins inserted into the membrane create pores that let in more drug, speeding killing. Killing is concentration-dependent with a post-antibiotic effect, which supports once-daily extended-interval dosing. Resistance comes mainly from aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Inhaled tobramycin suppresses chronic Pseudomonas infection in cystic fibrosis, and IV forms treat serious gram-negative pneumonia."], ["Blood and organs", "Treats serious gram-negative infections, often with a beta-lactam for synergy."], ["Kidneys", "Accumulates in proximal tubule cells and causes acute tubular injury, rising creatinine and loss of magnesium, potassium and calcium."], ["Inner ear", "Damages cochlear and vestibular hair cells, causing tinnitus, high-frequency hearing loss and balance problems, which can be permanent."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Blocks presynaptic calcium entry, reducing acetylcholine release and rarely causing respiratory paralysis."], ["Eyes", "Topical drops treat bacterial conjunctivitis with little drug reaching the blood."]],
  "journey": "Tobramycin is poorly absorbed from the gut, so it is given IV, IM, by inhalation or as eye drops. After IV dosing it stays mostly in extracellular fluid, with low protein binding, and penetrates poorly into the brain and cerebrospinal fluid. It is not metabolized and is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two hours that grows much longer in kidney failure. Blood levels guide dosing. Inhaled tobramycin gives high airway levels, and only a small fraction reaches the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity happens because tubular cells take up the drug through megalin receptors and it builds up in lysosomes, injuring the cells; this is usually reversible. Ototoxicity comes from accumulation in inner ear fluid and free radical damage to hair cells, which do not regrow, so it is often permanent; certain mitochondrial rRNA gene variants raise this risk. Neuromuscular blockade comes from blocking presynaptic calcium channels and can be reversed with IV calcium. Once-daily dosing and level monitoring lower toxicity. Inhaled forms can cause bronchospasm.",
  "src": ["Tobramycin injection and TOBI FDA labels", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"amikacin": {
  "origin": "Amikacin was made in 1972 by Hiroshi Kawaguchi and colleagues at Bristol-Banyu Research Institute in Japan. They started from kanamycin, an aminoglycoside discovered by Hamao Umezawa in 1957 from Streptomyces kanamyceticus. By then, many bacteria resisted kanamycin and gentamicin by making enzymes that add chemical groups to the drug. Kawaguchi's team attached an L-hydroxyaminobutyric acid side chain that blocks most of these enzymes. The resulting semisynthetic drug, amikacin, became the aminoglycoside held in reserve for resistant gram-negative infections and an important drug for mycobacteria. In 2018 the FDA approved Arikayce, a liposome inhaled form, for refractory Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Hamao Umezawa discovers kanamycin."], ["1972", "Kawaguchi and colleagues synthesize amikacin from kanamycin."], ["1970s", "Amikacin injection enters clinical use."], ["2018", "FDA approves Arikayce for refractory MAC lung disease."]],
  "cell": "Amikacin is a polycationic aminoglycoside. It binds the gram-negative outer membrane and then crosses the inner membrane by oxygen-dependent active transport, so anaerobes are resistant, and cell wall agents increase its uptake. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the A site of 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit, blocking the initiation complex and causing mRNA misreading. Faulty proteins damage the membrane and increase drug uptake, causing rapid, concentration-dependent killing with a post-antibiotic effect. Its L-hydroxyaminobutyric acid (AHBA) side chain on the 1-amino group sterically blocks most aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, including acetyltransferases, adenylyltransferases and phosphotransferases, so it often remains active against strains resistant to gentamicin and tobramycin. It is also active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacteria.",
  "body": [["Blood and organs", "Treats serious gram-negative infections resistant to other aminoglycosides, often with a beta-lactam."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled liposomal amikacin treats refractory MAC lung disease, and IV forms are part of some regimens for drug-resistant tuberculosis and nontuberculous mycobacteria."], ["Kidneys", "Builds up in proximal tubule cells, causing usually reversible tubular injury and rising creatinine."], ["Inner ear", "Damages cochlear hair cells more than vestibular cells, causing high-frequency hearing loss that can be permanent."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Can reduce acetylcholine release and rarely cause respiratory paralysis."], ["Airways (inhaled)", "Inhaled form can cause hoarseness, cough and bronchospasm and a rare hypersensitivity pneumonitis."]],
  "journey": "Amikacin is not absorbed from the gut, so it is given IV, IM or by inhalation. After injection it stays mostly in extracellular fluid, with low protein binding. It is not metabolized and is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two to three hours that lengthens a lot in kidney failure, so levels guide dosing. The inhaled liposomal form keeps most drug in the lungs, where liposomes are taken up by macrophages that harbor mycobacteria, and blood levels stay low.",
  "sidefx": "Like other aminoglycosides, amikacin is taken up by kidney tubule cells through megalin and accumulates, causing tubular injury. Inner ear hair cells are damaged by drug buildup and free radicals and do not regrow, so hearing loss is often permanent, especially with long courses and in people with certain mitochondrial rRNA variants. Neuromuscular blockade comes from blocking presynaptic calcium entry and is reversed with calcium. Inhaled use irritates airways. Toxicity rises with duration, high trough levels and other nephrotoxic drugs.",
  "src": ["Amikacin injection and Arikayce FDA labels", "Kawaguchi H et al. J Antibiot 1972", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"streptomycin": {
  "origin": "Streptomycin was the first effective drug against tuberculosis. In 1943 Albert Schatz, a graduate student in Selman Waksman's lab at Rutgers University, isolated it from Streptomyces griseus, a soil bacterium, while screening microbes for antibiotics. At the Mayo Clinic, William Feldman and Corwin Hinshaw showed it cured tuberculosis in guinea pigs and then treated the first human patients in 1944 and 1945. In 1948 the British Medical Research Council published a trial of streptomycin for pulmonary tuberculosis, one of the first randomized controlled trials in medicine. Waksman received the 1952 Nobel Prize, and a later dispute over Schatz's credit became a famous story about recognition in science. Streptomycin is still used for some tuberculosis regimens, plague and tularemia.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Albert Schatz in Selman Waksman's lab isolates streptomycin from Streptomyces griseus."], ["1944", "Feldman and Hinshaw at the Mayo Clinic begin treating tuberculosis with streptomycin."], ["1948", "The British MRC publishes one of the first randomized controlled trials, testing streptomycin for tuberculosis."], ["1952", "Selman Waksman receives the Nobel Prize for the discovery."]],
  "cell": "Streptomycin enters bacteria by oxygen-dependent active transport, so anaerobes are resistant. Inside, it binds irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit at a site formed by 16S rRNA and the ribosomal protein S12. This distorts the decoding site, interferes with the initiation complex and causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made. Some inserted into the membrane worsen damage and drug uptake, and killing is bactericidal and concentration-dependent. Resistance arises from mutations in rpsL, the gene for S12, or in rrs, the 16S rRNA gene, or from modifying enzymes. It is active against Mycobacterium tuberculosis in extracellular, actively dividing populations, as well as Yersinia pestis, Francisella tularensis and Brucella. It does not reach bacteria inside cells well.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Kills actively dividing M. tuberculosis in cavities as part of multidrug regimens, and treats pneumonic plague."], ["Lymph nodes and blood", "Treats plague, tularemia and, with other drugs, brucellosis."], ["Vestibular system", "Damages vestibular hair cells more than cochlear cells, causing vertigo and ataxia that can be permanent."], ["Hearing", "Can cause tinnitus and high-frequency hearing loss."], ["Kidneys", "Less nephrotoxic than other aminoglycosides, but can still raise creatinine."], ["Skin and nerves", "Rash, eosinophilia and tingling around the mouth can occur."]],
  "journey": "Streptomycin is not absorbed from the gut, so it is given by deep IM injection, and occasionally IV. Peak levels occur one to two hours after injection. It stays mainly in extracellular fluid, with about 35 percent protein binding, and enters cerebrospinal fluid poorly. It is not metabolized and is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about two and a half hours that lengthens in kidney failure and older adults, so doses are adjusted.",
  "sidefx": "Vestibular toxicity is the classic problem: streptomycin concentrates in inner ear fluid and generates free radicals that kill vestibular hair cells, which do not regrow, leading to permanent dizziness and imbalance. Hearing loss occurs by the same process in the cochlea. Neuromuscular blockade comes from blocking presynaptic calcium entry and can cause respiratory paralysis, especially with anesthesia or myasthenia; calcium can reverse it. Kidney injury reflects tubular uptake. It crosses the placenta and can cause congenital deafness. Hypersensitivity and painful injections are common.",
  "fun": "Waksman coined the word antibiotic in its modern sense to describe substances like streptomycin made by microbes to kill other microbes.",
  "src": ["Streptomycin for injection FDA label", "Schatz A, Bugie E, Waksman SA. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1944", "Medical Research Council. BMJ 1948"]
},
"tigecycline": {
  "origin": "Tetracyclines date to 1948, when chlortetracycline was found in Streptomyces aureofaciens at Lederle Laboratories. Over decades, bacteria learned to resist them by pumping them out with Tet efflux pumps or by making ribosomal protection proteins that knock the drug off the ribosome. In the 1990s researchers at Lederle, later part of Wyeth, added a bulky glycylamido group to minocycline, creating a new subclass called glycylcyclines. The leading compound, tigecycline, escaped both resistance mechanisms. The FDA approved Tygacil in June 2005 for complicated skin and intra-abdominal infections, later adding community-acquired pneumonia. In 2010 and 2013 the FDA added warnings, including a boxed warning, after analyses found higher all-cause mortality than with comparator drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Chlortetracycline, the first tetracycline, is discovered at Lederle Laboratories."], ["1990s", "Lederle and Wyeth scientists develop glycylcyclines from minocycline."], ["2005", "FDA approves Tygacil (tigecycline)."], ["2013", "FDA adds a boxed warning for increased all-cause mortality."]],
  "cell": "Tigecycline binds the 30S ribosomal subunit at the A site, formed by 16S rRNA. This blocks aminoacyl-tRNA from docking, so new amino acids cannot be added and peptide elongation stops. The effect is mostly bacteriostatic. It binds the ribosome about five times more tightly than older tetracyclines. Its 9-tert-butylglycylamido side chain on the minocycline core adds steric bulk that keeps it from being pumped out by Tet efflux pumps and makes it resistant to ribosomal protection proteins such as TetM. This gives activity against MRSA, VRE, ESBL-producing Enterobacterales, Acinetobacter and anaerobes. It is pumped out by multidrug efflux systems such as MexXY-OprM in Pseudomonas aeruginosa and is weak against Proteus, Providencia and Morganella.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Treats complicated skin infections, including MRSA, as a broad-spectrum option."], ["Abdomen", "Covers aerobes and anaerobes in complicated intra-abdominal infections."], ["Gut and stomach", "Nausea and vomiting are very common, more than with older tetracyclines."], ["Liver and pancreas", "Can raise liver tests, cause hepatotoxicity and, rarely, acute pancreatitis."], ["Clotting", "Can lower fibrinogen and prolong PT and aPTT."], ["Teeth and skin", "Like other tetracyclines, it can discolor developing teeth and cause photosensitivity."]],
  "journey": "Tigecycline is given IV only, every 12 hours. It has a very large volume of distribution, moving into tissues such as bone, lung, gallbladder and colon, which leaves blood levels low. That limits its use in bloodstream infections. It is 71 to 89 percent protein bound. It is minimally metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged, about 59 percent through bile and feces and about 33 percent in urine. The half-life is long, about 27 hours after one dose and 42 hours after multiple doses. Dose is reduced in severe liver impairment.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting likely come from direct effects on the gut and central receptors. Higher all-cause mortality in trials, the basis of its boxed warning, is thought to reflect poorer cure rates, especially in ventilator-associated pneumonia, perhaps because blood levels are low. Liver injury and pancreatitis are recognized class effects. Low fibrinogen and coagulopathy are reported. As a tetracycline, it binds calcium in growing teeth and bones and can cause tooth discoloration in children and fetal harm. Disrupting gut flora allows C. difficile. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Tygacil (tigecycline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "FDA Drug Safety Communication 2013"]
},
"dalbavancin": {
  "origin": "Dalbavancin is a modern cousin of vancomycin, which was found in a soil sample from Borneo in the 1950s. Its parent compound, A40926, is a teicoplanin-like glycopeptide made by the actinomycete Nonomuraea. In the 1990s chemists at the Italian company Biosearch Italia modified A40926, adding a lipid tail and an amide group that let the drug anchor to bacterial membranes and stay in the body far longer. The program passed to Vicuron, Pfizer and then Durata Therapeutics, which completed trials showing that one or two infusions could treat serious skin infections. The FDA approved Dalvance in May 2014, one of the first products approved under the GAIN Act's Qualified Infectious Disease Product designation, for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Vancomycin is discovered from a Borneo soil organism."], ["1990s", "Biosearch Italia develops dalbavancin from the glycopeptide A40926."], ["2014", "FDA approves Dalvance for acute bacterial skin and skin structure infections."]],
  "cell": "Dalbavancin binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of lipid II, the peptidoglycan building block, at the outer surface of the cell membrane. Bound this way, the precursor cannot be used by transglycosylases to extend glycan chains or by transpeptidases to cross-link peptides, so the cell wall cannot be built and the bacterium dies. Its lipophilic side chain inserts into the bacterial membrane, holding the drug close to its target, and the drug also forms dimers that bind more tightly. This raises potency against Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, and streptococci. It is bactericidal and concentration-dependent. It cannot cross the outer membrane of gram-negative bacteria. VanA enterococci replace D-Ala-D-Ala with D-Ala-D-lactate, which it binds poorly, so they are resistant.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "One or two infusions treat cellulitis, abscesses and wound infections caused by MRSA, MSSA and streptococci."], ["Bones and blood (off-label)", "Because levels last weeks, it is studied for osteomyelitis and some bloodstream infections."], ["Liver", "ALT can rise, sometimes several times above normal."], ["Infusion", "Rapid infusion can cause flushing, hives and itching, similar to vancomycin flushing syndrome."], ["Gut", "Nausea, diarrhea and C. difficile infection can occur."]],
  "journey": "Dalbavancin is given IV, either as one infusion or as two doses a week apart. It is about 93 percent bound to albumin, which, with its membrane anchoring, gives an extremely long half-life of about 346 hours, about two weeks. It reaches skin, bone and blister fluid and stays there for weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes; a small hydroxy metabolite forms. About a third leaves unchanged in the urine and about a fifth in feces. Doses are lowered in severe kidney impairment without dialysis.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are mild: nausea, headache and diarrhea. Infusion reactions with flushing and urticaria resemble vancomycin flushing syndrome from histamine release and are reduced by infusing over 30 minutes. ALT elevations occur more often than with comparators. Hypersensitivity can cross-react with other glycopeptides. Because the drug lasts so long, any adverse reaction cannot be quickly reversed by stopping it. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "With a half-life of about two weeks, a single infusion of dalbavancin can replace roughly two weeks of daily antibiotic treatment.",
  "src": ["Dalvance (dalbavancin) FDA label", "Boucher HW et al. N Engl J Med 2014 (DISCOVER trials)"]
},
"amphotericin-b-liposomal": {
  "origin": "Amphotericin B was discovered in 1955 by scientists at the Squibb Institute, including Gold, Stout, Pagano and Donovick, from Streptomyces nodosus, a bacterium in a soil sample from the Orinoco River region of Venezuela. It became the first broad and reliable treatment for life-threatening fungal infections, but the original deoxycholate form often damaged the kidneys and caused severe fevers and chills, earning it the nickname amphoterrible. In the 1980s and 1990s, researchers packed the drug into small, single-layer liposomes, which carry it to infected tissues while sparing human cells. Vestar, later NeXstar Pharmaceuticals, developed AmBisome, and the FDA approved it in 1997. It also became a key drug for visceral leishmaniasis.",
  "timeline": [["1955", "Squibb scientists discover amphotericin B from Streptomyces nodosus from Venezuelan soil."], ["1950s", "Conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate enters clinical use."], ["1997", "FDA approves AmBisome, liposomal amphotericin B."]],
  "cell": "Amphotericin B is a polyene macrolide with a rigid row of double bonds on one side and hydroxyl groups on the other. It binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes, much more tightly than cholesterol, the human sterol. Several drug molecules arrange with ergosterol to form pores across the membrane, and the drug may also pull ergosterol out of the membrane as a sterol sponge. Potassium, sodium and other small molecules leak out, and the fungal cell dies, so it is fungicidal. It also causes oxidative damage. In AmBisome, the drug is held in small unilamellar liposomes with cholesterol. The liposomes circulate longer, are taken up by macrophages and reach infected tissue, where fungal cells pull the drug out. Less free drug binds human kidney tubule membranes, lowering kidney toxicity and infusion reactions. Leishmania parasites also contain ergosterol-like sterols, which explains its antiprotozoal use.",
  "body": [["Fungal infections", "Fungicidal against Candida, Aspergillus, Cryptococcus, Mucorales and endemic fungi such as Histoplasma, used for invasive infections and fever with neutropenia."], ["Leishmania", "Kills Leishmania parasites in macrophages in visceral leishmaniasis."], ["Kidneys", "Less nephrotoxic than conventional amphotericin, but it can still constrict renal arterioles and injure tubules, raising creatinine."], ["Electrolytes", "Tubular pore formation leaks potassium and magnesium into urine, causing low potassium and magnesium."], ["Infusion reactions", "Fever, chills and back or chest pain can occur during infusion from cytokine release and liposome-related reactions."], ["Liver and blood", "Liver enzymes can rise, and anemia can develop from reduced erythropoietin."]],
  "journey": "Liposomal amphotericin is given only IV. The liposomes stay in the blood longer than conventional drug, and are taken up by the liver, spleen and macrophages, then release drug at sites of infection. It is highly bound in the blood. It is not significantly metabolized and is cleared slowly, as drug redistributes from tissues, with an initial half-life of about 7 to 10 hours and a terminal half-life of about 100 to 150 hours. Little reaches the urine or cerebrospinal fluid, but it is still used for cryptococcal meningitis because it reaches brain tissue.",
  "sidefx": "Because amphotericin also binds cholesterol in human membranes, though weakly, kidney tubule cells become leaky, losing potassium and magnesium, and renal vessels constrict, lowering filtration. Saline loading and replacing electrolytes reduce this. Fever and chills come from cytokine release by monocytes. Back or chest pain during infusion is a liposome-related reaction. Anemia reflects less erythropoietin. A key danger is confusing products: conventional amphotericin given at liposomal doses has caused fatal cardiac arrest. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Amphotericin B got its name from its amphoteric chemistry, meaning it can act as both an acid and a base.",
  "src": ["AmBisome (amphotericin B liposome) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"artesunate": {
  "origin": "Artesunate comes from sweet wormwood, Artemisia annua, known in China as qinghao. A 4th-century text by Ge Hong described soaking the herb in cold water and drinking the juice for fevers. During the Vietnam War, China launched Project 523 to find new malaria drugs. In 1971 and 1972 Tu Youyou and her team tested a low-temperature extract, inspired by Ge Hong's cold-water method, and isolated artemisinin, which cleared malaria in mice, monkeys and people. Chinese chemists then made artesunate in the late 1970s, a water-soluble derivative that can be injected. Large trials showed it lowered death from severe malaria compared with quinine. The CDC supplied it in the US under an investigational program, and the FDA approved Artesunate for Injection (Amivas) in May 2020. Tu Youyou shared the 2015 Nobel Prize.",
  "timeline": [["4th century", "Ge Hong describes qinghao for fevers in a Chinese medical text."], ["1972", "Tu Youyou's team isolates artemisinin under Project 523."], ["1970s", "Chinese chemists synthesize artesunate."], ["2015", "Tu Youyou shares the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."], ["2020", "FDA approves Artesunate for Injection for severe malaria."]],
  "cell": "Artesunate is a prodrug that plasma esterases quickly hydrolyze to dihydroartemisinin (DHA). The key part of the molecule is an endoperoxide bridge. Inside red cells, the malaria parasite digests hemoglobin in its food vacuole, freeing heme with ferrous iron (Fe2+). Heme iron cleaves the endoperoxide bridge, producing reactive oxygen and carbon-centered free radicals. These radicals alkylate many parasite proteins and heme, damage membranes, and disrupt targets proposed to include the calcium pump PfATP6. Because it acts on many stages, from young ring forms to mature schizonts, it clears parasites faster than any other antimalarial and prevents rings from maturing into sticky forms that block small vessels. Resistance in Southeast Asia is linked to kelch13 mutations that let ring stages survive.",
  "body": [["Blood (parasites)", "Rapidly kills Plasmodium in red cells, clearing parasitemia faster than quinine and lowering death in severe malaria."], ["Brain and organs", "Stopping parasites before they mature reduces blockage of small vessels, which drives cerebral malaria and organ failure."], ["Red cells", "Delayed hemolysis can occur one to several weeks later as once-infected red cells, pitted by the spleen, are cleared."], ["Kidneys and liver", "Hemoglobinuria, kidney injury and jaundice are seen, often as part of severe malaria itself."]],
  "journey": "Artesunate is given IV, usually at set intervals, followed by a full oral course of another antimalarial. It is converted within minutes by plasma esterases to dihydroartemisinin, which is about 93 percent protein bound. DHA peaks within 15 to 25 minutes. Artesunate has a half-life under 15 minutes, and DHA about half an hour to an hour and a half. DHA is glucuronidated by UGT1A9 and UGT2B7 and leaves in the urine. Because it is cleared so fast, a partner drug is needed to finish off the remaining parasites.",
  "sidefx": "Post-artesunate delayed hemolysis happens because the drug kills parasites inside red cells, and the spleen removes the parasite but returns the damaged red cells to the blood. These once-infected cells die about one to three weeks later, causing anemia, so hemoglobin is checked for weeks afterward. Hemoglobinuria, kidney injury and jaundice often reflect severe malaria. Allergic reactions and anaphylaxis are rare. In animals, artemisinins can harm early embryos, though they are used in pregnancy for severe malaria. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Tu Youyou volunteered to be one of the first people to take the artemisinin extract to test its safety before it was given to patients.",
  "src": ["Artesunate for Injection FDA label", "Tu Y. Nat Med 2011 (The discovery of artemisinin)", "Dondorp AM et al. Lancet 2010 (AQUAMAT)"]
},
"rabies-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "In 1885 Louis Pasteur gave the first rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister, a boy bitten by a rabid dog. Vaccines take one to two weeks to produce antibodies, which can be too slow after a severe bite. In 1954 researchers at the Pasteur Institute of Iran, working with the World Health Organization, treated people bitten by a rabid wolf and showed that adding antirabies serum to vaccine saved more lives than vaccine alone. Early serum came from horses and caused serum sickness, so human rabies immune globulin, purified from donors with high antibody levels, was developed and licensed in the US in the 1970s. Today HyperRAB, Imogam Rabies-HT and Kedrab supply this passive immunity, given once at the start of post-exposure prophylaxis together with vaccine.",
  "timeline": [["1885", "Louis Pasteur gives the first rabies vaccine to Joseph Meister."], ["1954", "A study in Iran after rabid wolf bites shows serum plus vaccine works better than vaccine alone."], ["1970s", "Human rabies immune globulin is licensed in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Kedrab, another human rabies immune globulin."]],
  "cell": "Rabies immune globulin is pooled human IgG from donors hyperimmunized with rabies vaccine. Its antibodies bind the rabies virus glycoprotein, the spike that lets the virus attach to nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction and other receptors before entering nerve endings. Once inside a nerve, the virus travels by retrograde axonal transport to the brain, where antibodies cannot reach it. By neutralizing virus at the wound before it enters nerves, RIG blocks infection. Antibody-coated virus is also cleared through Fc-mediated uptake by phagocytes and complement. This passive protection bridges the gap of about 7 to 14 days until the vaccine causes the body to make its own antibodies. Larger or repeated doses can bind vaccine antigen and suppress the active response, which is why it is given only once and only up to day 7 after the first vaccine dose.",
  "body": [["Wound site", "Infiltrating the wound with antibody neutralizes virus before it can enter peripheral nerves."], ["Blood", "Any remaining antibody given intramuscularly provides circulating neutralizing antibody for weeks."], ["Immune system", "Provides passive protection while the vaccine builds active immunity; too much can blunt that vaccine response."], ["Injection site", "Pain, tenderness and soreness at the wound and injection sites are common."]],
  "journey": "As much of the dose as anatomically feasible is infiltrated into and around the wound, and any remainder is injected IM at a site away from the vaccine. Wound infiltration gives immediate local neutralization. From the IM site, antibody is absorbed slowly, and serum levels peak in about two to seven days. It is broken down like the body's own IgG by catabolism, with a half-life of about three weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local pain and tenderness from injecting volume into tissue, plus headache or low-grade fever. Because it is human IgG rather than horse serum, serum sickness is rare. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are possible, especially in people with IgA deficiency who have anti-IgA antibodies. Rarely, immune globulins can cause thrombosis. As a blood product, it carries a theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents, minimized by donor screening and viral inactivation. Giving vaccine and RIG in the same syringe or site would neutralize the vaccine, so they are kept apart.",
  "fun": "Joseph Meister later worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute in Paris for many years.",
  "src": ["HyperRAB, Imogam Rabies-HT and Kedrab FDA labels", "CDC ACIP rabies post-exposure prophylaxis recommendations", "Baltazard M et al. Bull World Health Organ 1955"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"tetanus-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "The idea behind tetanus immune globulin goes back to 1890, when Emil von Behring and Shibasaburo Kitasato, working in Robert Koch's institute in Berlin, showed that serum from animals immunized against tetanus toxin could protect other animals. This was the birth of antitoxin therapy, and von Behring received the first Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1901 for serum therapy. For decades the antitoxin came from horses, which often caused serum sickness and allergic reactions. Starting in the 1960s, antitoxin made from the plasma of human donors with high anti-tetanus antibody levels replaced horse serum in the United States. Today's product, sold as HyperTET, is pooled human IgG with a high titer of antibody to tetanospasmin.",
  "timeline": [["1884","Arthur Nicolaier produces tetanus in animals with soil samples, pointing to a soil microbe."],["1890","Von Behring and Kitasato show that antitoxin serum protects animals against tetanus toxin."],["1901","Von Behring receives the first Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for serum therapy."],["1960s","Human tetanus immune globulin replaces horse antitoxin in the US."]],
  "cell": "Clostridium tetani grows in dirty, low-oxygen wounds and releases tetanospasmin. The toxin binds to motor nerve endings, is taken up and carried backward along the axon to the spinal cord. There it crosses into inhibitory interneurons and cleaves synaptobrevin, a SNARE protein needed to release GABA and glycine. Without this inhibition, motor neurons fire without control, causing rigidity and spasms. Tetanus immune globulin is human IgG that binds the toxin while it is still free in the blood and tissue fluid. The antibody-toxin complex can no longer attach to nerve endings and is cleared by phagocytes. It cannot reach toxin already inside neurons, which is why it prevents worsening but does not reverse spasms that have started.",
  "body": [["Wound and tissue fluid","Antibodies given near the time of injury catch toxin as it leaks out of the wound, before it can bind to nerve terminals."],["Nervous system","By blocking new toxin uptake, it limits how many inhibitory synapses are lost, which can reduce the severity and length of spasms."],["Immune system","It provides borrowed (passive) immunity for about four weeks while the tetanus toxoid vaccine teaches the person's own immune system to make antibody."],["Injection site","Soreness and tenderness come from the volume of protein injected into muscle."]],
  "journey": "Tetanus immune globulin is injected into muscle. The IgG is absorbed slowly from the muscle into the blood, reaching peak levels in about two days. It spreads through the blood and tissue fluid, where it meets the toxin. It is not metabolized by the liver or cleared by the kidneys. Instead it is broken down like the person's own IgG, with a half-life of about three to four weeks, giving roughly a month of protection.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are local pain and low-grade fever from injecting a large amount of foreign protein into muscle. Because it is a human product rather than horse serum, serum sickness is uncommon. Rare allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can happen with any plasma protein. Like other immune globulins, it carries a small risk of blood clots. Because it is made from pooled human plasma, donor screening and viral inactivation steps reduce the risk of passing on infections.",
  "fun": "Before human antitoxin, horse antitoxin was so common that serum sickness, a delayed reaction to horse proteins, was first described in detail in 1905 by Clemens von Pirquet and Bela Schick in children treated with horse serum.",
  "src": ["HyperTET S/D (tetanus immune globulin) FDA label","CDC Pink Book: Tetanus","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"hepatitis-b-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "Hepatitis B immune globulin grew out of the discovery of the hepatitis B virus itself. In the mid-1960s Baruch Blumberg found an unusual protein in the blood of an Australian Aboriginal man, which he called the Australia antigen. Later work showed it was the surface coat of the hepatitis B virus (HBsAg), and Blumberg shared the 1976 Nobel Prize for this work. Once scientists could measure antibody to that antigen (anti-HBs), they could collect plasma from donors with very high levels and purify their IgG. Hyperimmune globulins of this kind were developed in the 1970s. Today HBIG (HyperHEP B, Nabi-HB, HepaGam B) is given with hepatitis B vaccine to newborns of infected mothers and after needlestick or sexual exposures, and to liver transplant recipients with hepatitis B.",
  "timeline": [["1965","Baruch Blumberg reports the Australia antigen, later shown to be hepatitis B surface antigen."],["1970s","Hyperimmune globulin rich in anti-HBs is developed for post-exposure protection."],["1976","Blumberg shares the Nobel Prize for discoveries about hepatitis B."],["1981","The first hepatitis B vaccine, made from plasma, is licensed in the US."]],
  "cell": "The hepatitis B virus enters liver cells when its surface protein (HBsAg) binds to a bile acid transporter on hepatocytes called NTCP. HBIG contains large amounts of anti-HBs IgG. These antibodies coat circulating virions and free HBsAg particles, blocking the part of the surface protein the virus needs to bind and enter liver cells. Coated virus is also tagged for removal by phagocytes in the liver and spleen. This is passive immunity: the antibody works at once but fades over weeks. Given together with the vaccine, it covers the gap while the person's own B cells learn to make anti-HBs. It does not clear virus already living inside infected hepatocytes.",
  "body": [["Blood","Antibodies bind free virus and surface antigen in the blood right after exposure, lowering the amount of virus that reaches the liver."],["Liver","With fewer virions reaching hepatocytes, infection is less likely to take hold. In newborns, HBIG plus vaccine prevents most mother-to-child transmission."],["Immune system","It supplies borrowed antibody for several weeks and does not block the vaccine from working when given at a different site."],["Transplanted liver","In liver transplant for hepatitis B, ongoing HBIG helps keep the virus from infecting the new liver."]],
  "journey": "HBIG is usually injected into muscle, where it is absorbed slowly into the blood over several days. Some products can be given by vein, as in liver transplant, which gives immediate high levels. The antibody spreads through blood and tissue fluid. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes or filtered by the kidneys. It is broken down by cells like the body's own IgG, with a half-life of about three weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Pain at the injection site, headache and muscle aches come from the injected protein and the immune response to it. Allergic reactions are rare but possible with any plasma product, and people with IgA deficiency who have antibodies to IgA are at higher risk. Like other immune globulins, it carries a small risk of blood clots. Donor screening and viral inactivation steps lower the risk of passing on infections.",
  "fun": "Blumberg was not looking for a virus at all. He was studying inherited differences in blood proteins among people around the world when he stumbled on the Australia antigen.",
  "src": ["HyperHEP B (hepatitis B immune globulin) FDA label","Nabi-HB FDA label","CDC Pink Book: Hepatitis B"]
},
"varicella-zoster-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "In the late 1960s researchers including Philip Brunell showed that plasma from people recovering from shingles (zoster) had high levels of antibody against the varicella zoster virus and could protect exposed high-risk children from severe chickenpox. This zoster immune globulin was later replaced by varicella zoster immune globulin (VZIG), made from donor plasma with high antibody titers, which was licensed in the US in 1981. When the US maker stopped producing VZIG in the mid-2000s, a Canadian product, VARIZIG, became available under an investigational protocol and was then FDA approved in 2012. It protects people who cannot safely receive the live varicella vaccine, such as some immunocompromised patients, pregnant women and certain newborns.",
  "timeline": [["1954","Thomas Weller grows varicella zoster virus in cell culture."],["1969","Zoster immune globulin from convalescent plasma is shown to protect exposed children."],["1981","Varicella zoster immune globulin (VZIG) is licensed in the US."],["2012","FDA approves VARIZIG after US supply of the older product ended."]],
  "cell": "Varicella zoster virus spreads by breathing in virus particles. It first infects cells in the airway and nearby lymph nodes, then spreads through the blood, carried by infected T cells, to the skin and organs during an incubation period of about two weeks. VARIZIG is purified human IgG rich in antibodies to the virus envelope glycoproteins. These antibodies bind virus particles outside cells, blocking attachment and fusion, and mark infected cells for destruction by complement and natural killer cells. Given during the incubation period, it lowers the amount of virus that reaches the skin and organs. It provides passive protection only and does not treat chickenpox once the rash has appeared.",
  "body": [["Blood","Antibodies neutralize virus during the early spread through the blood, which lowers the chance of widespread infection."],["Lungs","Less virus spread means a lower risk of varicella pneumonia, a dangerous complication in pregnant women and adults."],["Brain","Reducing viral spread lowers the risk of encephalitis in high-risk patients."],["Skin","Infection, if it still happens, tends to be milder with fewer lesions."],["Immune system","It provides borrowed protection for a few weeks without training the person's own immunity."]],
  "journey": "VARIZIG is injected into muscle and absorbed slowly into the blood, with peak levels in about five days. It works best when given as soon as possible after exposure, within the time window in the label. The antibody spreads through blood and tissue fluid and is broken down by cells like the body's own IgG, with a half-life of about three and a half weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Pain at the injection site and headache come from injecting a concentrated protein solution. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur with any plasma-derived product, and the risk is higher in people with IgA deficiency who have anti-IgA antibodies. Immune globulins carry a small risk of blood clots, more so in older people and those with clotting risk factors. Antibody from VARIZIG can interfere with live vaccines given soon after, so those vaccines are timed later.",
  "fun": "Shingles patients were the original donors. Their reactivated virus boosts their antibody levels so high that their plasma made the first protective globulin.",
  "src": ["VARIZIG (varicella zoster immune globulin) FDA label","CDC Pink Book: Varicella","Brunell PA et al. NEJM 1969"]
},
"botulism-antitoxin-heptavalent": {
  "origin": "Botulism was first described in detail in the 1820s by the German physician Justinus Kerner, who studied deaths from spoiled sausages (botulus is Latin for sausage). In 1895, after a funeral meal in Belgium sickened many guests, Emile van Ermengem isolated the bacterium now called Clostridium botulinum. Horse antitoxins against some toxin types were used for decades. Because botulinum toxin is a potential bioterrorism agent, the US government sponsored development of an antitoxin covering all seven classic serotypes, A through G. The horse IgG is cut with enzymes to remove most of the Fc portion, making it 'despeciated' and less likely to cause reactions. This heptavalent botulism antitoxin (BAT) was approved by the FDA in 2013 and is supplied through the CDC and the Strategic National Stockpile.",
  "timeline": [["1820s","Justinus Kerner describes sausage poisoning, later called botulism."],["1895","Emile van Ermengem isolates Clostridium botulinum after a Belgian outbreak."],["1940s","Horse antitoxins against some toxin types come into use."],["2013","FDA approves heptavalent botulism antitoxin (BAT)."]],
  "cell": "Botulinum toxin has a heavy chain and a light chain. The heavy chain binds receptors on cholinergic nerve terminals and the toxin is taken in by endocytosis. The light chain, a zinc protease, then escapes into the cytoplasm and cuts SNARE proteins: SNAP-25 for types A and E, and synaptobrevin for types B, D, F and G. Without intact SNAREs, vesicles of acetylcholine cannot fuse with the membrane, so nerves cannot signal muscles. BAT is a mix of horse antibody fragments (Fab and F(ab')2) against all seven types. The fragments bind toxin still circulating in the blood, block it from attaching to nerve terminals, and allow it to be cleared. Toxin already inside nerve endings is not reached. Recovery needs new nerve sprouts and SNARE proteins, which takes weeks to months.",
  "body": [["Nerve terminals","By stopping more toxin from entering nerve endings, BAT halts the spread of paralysis but cannot undo the paralysis already present."],["Muscles","Weakness typically starts in the cranial nerves (double vision, drooping eyelids, trouble swallowing) and descends. Early antitoxin limits how far it goes."],["Breathing","Limiting paralysis of the diaphragm and chest muscles can reduce how long a patient needs a ventilator."],["Autonomic nerves","Botulism also blocks autonomic cholinergic nerves, causing dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention. Antitoxin stops further loss."],["Immune system","Horse proteins can trigger allergic reactions or delayed serum sickness, even after the Fc portion is removed."]],
  "journey": "BAT is given by slow infusion into a vein so that antibody reaches circulating toxin right away. The smaller Fab pieces clear faster, partly through the kidneys, while the larger F(ab')2 pieces last longer. Depending on the serotype, half-lives range from under a day to more than a day. The fragments are broken down as proteins. Because toxin binds nerve endings quickly, giving it early matters more than its exact half-life.",
  "sidefx": "The main risks come from the horse source. Immediate allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur because the immune system sees horse protein as foreign. Delayed serum sickness, with fever, rash and joint pain one to three weeks later, happens when antibodies against horse protein form immune complexes. Infusion reactions such as fever and chills are common. Slow heart rate has been seen during infusion. Staff are prepared to treat anaphylaxis with epinephrine during the infusion.",
  "fun": "Botulinum toxin is one of the most potent toxins known, yet the same molecule, in tiny medical amounts, is used as Botox to relax wrinkles and treat muscle spasms.",
  "src": ["BAT (botulism antitoxin heptavalent, equine) FDA label","CDC Botulism clinical guidance","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"esketamine": {
  "origin": "Esketamine is the mirror-image S form of ketamine. Ketamine was made in 1962 by chemist C. L. Stevens at Parke-Davis as a safer replacement for phencyclidine (PCP), and it was approved as an anesthetic in 1970. In 2000 a small study at Yale led by Robert Berman, Dennis Charney and John Krystal found that a single ketamine infusion lifted depression within hours, a striking result because standard antidepressants take weeks. Janssen developed the S-enantiomer, which binds NMDA receptors more strongly, as a nasal spray. The FDA approved it as Spravato in 2019 for treatment-resistant depression, the first new mechanism for depression in decades, and later for depressive symptoms in adults with major depression and suicidal thoughts. It is given only in certified clinics under a REMS program.",
  "timeline": [["1962","Ketamine is synthesized at Parke-Davis."],["1970","FDA approves ketamine as an anesthetic."],["2000","Yale researchers report a rapid antidepressant effect of ketamine."],["2019","FDA approves esketamine nasal spray (Spravato) for treatment-resistant depression."],["2020","Label expands to depressive symptoms in adults with major depression and suicidal ideation or behavior."]],
  "cell": "Esketamine blocks the NMDA receptor, an ion channel opened by glutamate. It enters the open channel and binds at the PCP site inside the pore, so it is a non-competitive blocker. A leading theory is that it blocks NMDA receptors on GABA interneurons more than on pyramidal neurons. With the brakes off, pyramidal neurons release a burst of glutamate, which activates AMPA receptors. AMPA signaling triggers release of BDNF, which activates TrkB receptors and the mTOR pathway. This drives new protein synthesis and the growth of new dendritic spines and synapses in the prefrontal cortex, reversing synapse loss seen with chronic stress. The same NMDA block causes the short-lived dissociation and perceptual changes.",
  "body": [["Brain: mood circuits","Rapid growth of new synapses in the prefrontal cortex is thought to underlie mood improvement that can start within a day."],["Brain: perception","NMDA block disconnects normal sensory processing, causing dissociation, a dreamlike or detached feeling, that peaks soon after a dose and fades within about two hours."],["Brain: alertness","Sedation and dizziness are common, which is why patients are observed and do not drive until the next day."],["Heart and blood vessels","Blood pressure rises temporarily, peaking around 40 minutes, from increased sympathetic activity."],["Bladder","Ketamine-related drugs can irritate the bladder lining, and cystitis is a listed risk."],["Gut","Nausea and vomiting are common, which is why patients avoid food before a session."]],
  "journey": "Esketamine is sprayed into the nose, where it is absorbed through the nasal lining into the blood, skipping first-pass liver breakdown. Bioavailability is under half. It is fat-soluble and enters the brain within minutes. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP2B6 and CYP3A4, to noresketamine and other metabolites, which leave in urine. Dissociation and sedation fade in about two hours, and the terminal half-life is about 7 to 12 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Dissociation, dizziness and altered perception come directly from NMDA block in sensory and cortical circuits. Sedation and, rarely, breathing suppression are more likely when combined with benzodiazepines, opioids or alcohol. Blood pressure rises because ketamine-like drugs boost sympathetic outflow and block norepinephrine reuptake, so it is avoided in people with aneurysms or bleeding in the brain. The euphoric and dissociative effects create abuse potential, which is why it is Schedule III and given only under supervision. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Spravato is the only antidepressant patients take in a clinic and then must sit and be watched for at least two hours before going home.",
  "src": ["Spravato (esketamine) FDA label","Berman RM et al. Biol Psychiatry 2000","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"brexanolone": {
  "origin": "Brexanolone is a medical version of allopregnanolone, a steroid the body makes from progesterone. In the 1980s researchers discovered that certain progesterone breakdown products act on brain GABA-A receptors, calming nerve activity. Allopregnanolone levels rise sharply in pregnancy and fall after birth, and that drop was proposed as one trigger for postpartum depression. Sage Therapeutics developed a stable IV form, dissolving the steroid in a cyclodextrin carrier. In 2019 the FDA approved it as Zulresso, the first drug approved specifically for postpartum depression. It required a continuous infusion over about 60 hours in a monitored facility because of sudden sedation. After the oral neurosteroid zuranolone was approved in 2023, Zulresso was discontinued and has no current US label.",
  "timeline": [["1986","Researchers show steroid metabolites can act on GABA-A receptors."],["2019","FDA approves brexanolone (Zulresso) for postpartum depression."],["2023","Oral zuranolone is approved for postpartum depression."],["2020s","Zulresso is discontinued in the US."]],
  "cell": "GABA-A receptors are chloride channels that quiet neurons. There are two main kinds: synaptic receptors, which respond to bursts of GABA, and extrasynaptic receptors (often containing delta subunits), which create a steady background inhibition called tonic inhibition. Brexanolone binds a neurosteroid site in the transmembrane part of the receptor, separate from the benzodiazepine site. It is a positive allosteric modulator, so it makes GABA open the channel more effectively, letting more chloride in and hyperpolarizing the neuron. Because it acts on extrasynaptic receptors that benzodiazepines do not affect, it boosts tonic inhibition. The theory is that this rapidly resets overactive stress and mood networks that were destabilized by the fall in allopregnanolone after childbirth.",
  "body": [["Brain: mood","Depressive symptoms improved within a day or two in trials, much faster than standard antidepressants, and the benefit lasted to 30 days."],["Brain: alertness","Enhanced GABA signaling causes sleepiness and, in some patients, sudden loss of consciousness, which is why oxygen levels were monitored throughout."],["Breathing","Heavy sedation can lead to low oxygen levels, especially with other sedatives."],["Skin and blood vessels","Flushing and hot flashes were reported during the infusion, along with dry mouth."],["Breast milk","Small amounts pass into milk, but the oral bioavailability in an infant is low."]],
  "journey": "Brexanolone was given only by continuous IV infusion over about 60 hours, with the rate stepped up and then down. It is very fat-soluble and more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins, and it spreads widely, including into the brain. It is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Instead it undergoes keto-reduction, glucuronidation and sulfation into inactive metabolites. These leave in both feces and urine. The half-life is about nine hours.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly all key side effects come from boosting GABA-A inhibition throughout the brain. Sleepiness, dizziness and sudden loss of consciousness follow from strong neuronal quieting, and low oxygen can happen if breathing slows. Because of this, the label carried a boxed warning and the drug was given only in certified settings with continuous pulse oximetry. Other sedatives, such as alcohol, opioids and benzodiazepines, add to these effects. There is no specific reversal agent; stopping the infusion lets symptoms resolve within about 15 to 60 minutes.",
  "fun": "Brexanolone is chemically the same as a hormone every pregnant woman makes, which is why it was described as replacing what the brain lost rather than adding something new.",
  "src": ["Zulresso (brexanolone) FDA label","Meltzer-Brody S et al. Lancet 2018","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"surfactant-beractant": {
  "origin": "In 1959 Mary Ellen Avery and Jere Mead at Harvard showed that the lungs of premature babies who died of respiratory distress syndrome lacked surfactant, a soapy film that keeps the tiny air sacs open. That discovery made replacing surfactant a goal. In 1980 Tetsuro Fujiwara and colleagues in Japan reported the first successful treatment of premature babies with a surfactant extracted from cow lungs and enriched with lipids. That preparation became the basis of beractant, developed in the US by Abbott (Ross Laboratories) as Survanta. The FDA approved Survanta in 1991. Surfactant therapy, along with steroids before birth, sharply lowered deaths from respiratory distress in premature infants.",
  "timeline": [["1959","Avery and Mead link respiratory distress in premature babies to surfactant deficiency."],["1980","Fujiwara reports successful treatment of premature babies with bovine-derived surfactant."],["1991","FDA approves beractant (Survanta)."]],
  "cell": "Alveoli are lined by a thin layer of water. Water molecules pull on each other, creating surface tension that tends to collapse small air sacs, especially at the end of a breath. By the law of Laplace, the pressure needed to keep a bubble open rises as the radius shrinks, so small alveoli empty into larger ones. Type II pneumocytes normally secrete surfactant, mostly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC) with surfactant proteins SP-B and SP-C. These proteins help the lipids spread and form a film at the air-water surface. Premature lungs have too few mature type II cells. Beractant, a cow lung extract with SP-B and SP-C plus added DPPC, palmitic acid and tripalmitin, spreads across the alveolar surface and lowers surface tension. Alveoli stay open, lung compliance rises and gas exchange improves.",
  "body": [["Lungs","Air sacs stay open at the end of each breath, so the lungs become less stiff and the baby needs less ventilator pressure."],["Blood oxygen","Oxygen levels often improve within minutes, allowing the inspired oxygen to be lowered."],["Airway","The liquid is placed through the breathing tube, so it can briefly block the tube or cause reflux into it during dosing."],["Heart","Brief slow heart rate and oxygen drops can occur during the dose as the airway is temporarily filled with liquid."],["Lung blood vessels","As lung pressures change quickly, rare bleeding into the lungs and air leaks such as pneumothorax can occur."]],
  "journey": "Beractant is dripped directly into the trachea through an endotracheal tube, often in portions with the baby positioned to spread it to all parts of the lungs. It stays in the lungs and joins the baby's own surfactant pool. Type II cells take up and recycle the lipids, and alveolar macrophages break down the rest. Almost none reaches the general circulation. Effects on oxygen appear within minutes and last for hours, and repeat doses can be given if needed.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the way it is given. Filling the airway with liquid can briefly cause a slow heart rate and drop in oxygen, and the tube can become blocked. Improved lung compliance happens fast, so ventilator settings may suddenly be too high, which can overinflate the lungs and cause air leaks or pneumothorax. Pulmonary hemorrhage may relate to rapid changes in lung blood flow as pressures fall. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Mary Ellen Avery's discovery started with a simple observation: foam from healthy lung tissue was stable, but foam from the lungs of babies who died of respiratory distress was not.",
  "src": ["Survanta (beractant) FDA label","Avery ME, Mead J. Am J Dis Child 1959","Fujiwara T et al. Lancet 1980"]
},
"nitric-oxide-inhaled": {
  "origin": "In 1980 Robert Furchgott showed that blood vessels relax only when their inner lining (endothelium) is intact, and he called the unknown signal endothelium-derived relaxing factor. In 1987 groups led by Louis Ignarro and Salvador Moncada identified it as nitric oxide, a simple gas. Ferid Murad had already shown that nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase. Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad shared the 1998 Nobel Prize. In 1991 Warren Zapol's group at Massachusetts General Hospital showed that breathing nitric oxide relaxed lung blood vessels without dropping systemic blood pressure. The FDA approved inhaled nitric oxide (INOmax) in 1999 for term and near-term newborns with hypoxic respiratory failure and pulmonary hypertension, reducing the need for heart-lung bypass (ECMO).",
  "timeline": [["1980","Furchgott describes endothelium-derived relaxing factor."],["1987","Ignarro and Moncada identify it as nitric oxide."],["1991","Zapol's group shows inhaled nitric oxide is a selective lung vasodilator."],["1998","Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad share the Nobel Prize for nitric oxide signaling."],["1999","FDA approves inhaled nitric oxide (INOmax) for newborns."]],
  "cell": "Nitric oxide is a small gas that passes straight through cell membranes. Breathed in, it reaches only alveoli that are ventilated and diffuses into the smooth muscle of nearby small arteries. There it binds the heme group of soluble guanylate cyclase, which converts GTP to cyclic GMP. Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers calcium inside the cell and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. The muscle relaxes and the vessel widens. Because only vessels next to air-filled alveoli widen, blood is steered toward lung units that can pick up oxygen, which improves ventilation-perfusion matching. When nitric oxide reaches the blood, hemoglobin binds and inactivates it within seconds, so systemic vessels are barely affected.",
  "body": [["Lung blood vessels","Pulmonary arteries relax, lowering pulmonary artery pressure and reducing the strain on the right heart."],["Gas exchange","Blood is redirected to ventilated alveoli, so oxygen levels rise and less blood shunts past the lungs."],["Heart: shunts in newborns","Lower lung pressure reduces right-to-left flow through the ductus arteriosus and foramen ovale in newborns with pulmonary hypertension."],["Blood","Nitric oxide reacts with hemoglobin to form methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen, so levels are monitored."],["Systemic circulation","Because hemoglobin inactivates it so fast, blood pressure usually changes little."],["Left heart","In left heart failure, more blood returning to the left heart can raise pressures and cause pulmonary edema."]],
  "journey": "Nitric oxide is delivered as a gas mixed into the ventilator circuit, from a cylinder or a bedside generator. It is absorbed across the alveoli in seconds and acts on nearby vessels. In the blood it combines with oxyhemoglobin to form methemoglobin and nitrate. Red cell enzymes convert methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, and nitrate is excreted in the urine. Its effect starts within minutes and stops within minutes after it is turned off.",
  "sidefx": "Methemoglobinemia happens because nitric oxide oxidizes hemoglobin iron. Nitric oxide also reacts with oxygen to form nitrogen dioxide, which irritates the airways, so its level is monitored in the circuit. Stopping it suddenly can cause rebound pulmonary hypertension and a sharp fall in oxygen, because the body's own nitric oxide production is suppressed during treatment, so it is weaned slowly. In patients with left ventricular failure, more lung blood flow can overload the left heart and cause pulmonary edema. Methemoglobinemia can be treated with methylene blue.",
  "fun": "Nitric oxide was named Molecule of the Year by the journal Science in 1992, a remarkable honor for a gas once known mainly as an air pollutant.",
  "src": ["INOmax (nitric oxide) FDA label","Furchgott RF, Zawadzki JV. Nature 1980","Frostell C, Zapol WM et al. Circulation 1991"]
},
"basiliximab": {
  "origin": "Basiliximab came from decades of work on how T cells drive organ rejection. In the mid-1970s researchers discovered interleukin-2 (IL-2), a growth signal T cells use to multiply. The high-affinity IL-2 receptor includes an alpha chain, CD25 (once called the Tac antigen), that appears mainly on activated T cells. Blocking it promised to stop T cells that were reacting to a transplant while leaving resting T cells alone. Novartis built basiliximab as a chimeric antibody, with mouse binding regions joined to a human IgG1 frame to reduce immune reactions against it. The FDA approved it as Simulect in 1998 for preventing acute rejection in kidney transplants, given as induction around the time of surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1976","A T-cell growth factor, later named interleukin-2, is described."],["1981","The Tac antigen, later known as CD25, is identified on activated T cells."],["1998","FDA approves basiliximab (Simulect) for kidney transplant induction."]],
  "cell": "When a T cell recognizes donor antigens, it becomes activated and makes both IL-2 and the IL-2 receptor alpha chain, CD25. CD25 joins the beta and gamma chains to form the high-affinity IL-2 receptor. IL-2 binding activates JAK1 and JAK3 and STAT5 signaling, along with mTOR, pushing the T cell from the G1 phase into the S phase of the cell cycle so it divides into a clone. Basiliximab binds CD25 with high affinity and blocks IL-2 from binding. Without this signal, activated T cells cannot expand. The antibody does not kill T cells, so it does not deplete lymphocytes or cause cytokine release. CD25 remains saturated for about four to six weeks, covering the early period when rejection risk is highest.",
  "body": [["Immune system","Only T cells that have been activated by the new organ are held back, so overall immune defense is less affected than with depleting antibodies."],["Transplanted kidney","Fewer activated T cells reach the graft, which lowers the rate of acute rejection in the first weeks after transplant."],["Infection risk","Infections are similar to placebo in trials, but they remain a concern because it is always used with other immunosuppressants."],["Gut and general","Constipation, nausea, swelling and high blood pressure were common in trials but occurred at similar rates with placebo, reflecting transplant care in general."]],
  "journey": "Basiliximab is given by IV infusion or injection, usually once before transplant surgery and once a few days later. It stays mostly in the blood and lymph fluid, where it binds CD25 on T cells. Like other IgG antibodies, it is not metabolized by the liver or cleared by the kidneys. It is broken down by cells into amino acids. Its half-life is about a week in adults, and CD25 stays blocked for about a month or more.",
  "sidefx": "Because it blocks only one receptor and does not destroy cells, basiliximab causes far fewer reactions than older depleting antibodies. Its chimeric design lowers but does not eliminate the chance of allergic reactions, and rare severe hypersensitivity with anaphylaxis, capillary leak or pulmonary edema has been reported, especially on re-exposure. Long-term suppression of T cells together with other drugs raises the risk of infection and post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder, a lymphoma linked to Epstein-Barr virus. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "The -ximab ending signals a chimeric antibody: part mouse, part human. Fully human antibodies end in -umab.",
  "src": ["Simulect (basiliximab) FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"antithymocyte-globulin": {
  "origin": "Anti-thymocyte globulin is an old idea: inject human immune cells into an animal, collect the antibodies it makes, and give them back to people to wipe out their T cells. Antilymphocyte serums were studied in the 1960s, including in early kidney transplant programs. Upjohn developed an equine product, Atgam, made by immunizing horses with human thymus cells; the FDA approved it in 1981 for kidney transplant rejection, and it is also used for aplastic anemia. A rabbit product, Thymoglobulin, made by immunizing rabbits with human thymocytes, was approved in the US in 1998 for treating acute kidney transplant rejection. It is widely used for induction at the time of transplant, a use backed by guidelines.",
  "timeline": [["1960s","Antilymphocyte serums are studied to prevent transplant rejection."],["1981","FDA approves equine anti-thymocyte globulin (Atgam)."],["1998","FDA approves rabbit anti-thymocyte globulin (Thymoglobulin)."],["2017","Thymoglobulin label expands to prophylaxis of rejection in kidney transplant."]],
  "cell": "Rabbit ATG is polyclonal: it contains many different antibodies that recognize many molecules on T cells, including CD2, CD3, CD4, CD8, CD11a, CD18, CD25, CD45 and HLA class I and II. Once they coat T cells, several things happen. In the blood, the Fc part of the antibodies activates complement, punching holes in the cell membrane and lysing the cell. In the spleen and lymph nodes, macrophages with Fc receptors engulf coated cells (opsonization). Some antibodies also trigger apoptosis. The result is deep and long-lasting T-cell depletion. Binding to adhesion molecules and receptors also blocks T-cell activation and trafficking. With fewer T cells, there are fewer to attack the graft.",
  "body": [["Blood","Lymphocyte counts fall within hours and T-cell numbers can stay low for months. White cell and platelet counts can also drop because of cross-reacting antibodies."],["Immune system","Broad T-cell depletion reduces acute rejection but raises the risk of infections such as cytomegalovirus and fungal infections."],["Whole body","Fever, chills and low blood pressure with the first doses come from cytokines released as T cells are destroyed."],["Transplanted kidney","Fewer T cells means less attack on the new organ, both at induction and during a rejection episode."],["Joints and skin","Serum sickness, with fever, rash and joint pain days later, can follow because the body makes antibodies against the rabbit protein."]],
  "journey": "ATG is given by slow IV infusion through a large vein, usually over several days. It binds quickly to lymphocytes in blood and lymphoid tissue. The free rabbit IgG falls with a half-life of about two to three days at first, but it can be detected for weeks, with a terminal half-life of about a month. It is broken down as protein, not by the liver or kidneys. Patients are often given steroids, acetaminophen and antihistamines first to blunt infusion reactions.",
  "sidefx": "The first-dose reaction is cytokine release: as T cells are destroyed they release TNF, IL-6 and other cytokines, causing fever, chills, rigors and sometimes low blood pressure or breathing problems. Low white cell and platelet counts happen because some antibodies bind those cells too. Foreign animal protein can cause anaphylaxis or delayed serum sickness. Profound, long T-cell depletion raises the risk of serious infection and Epstein-Barr virus driven lymphoma (PTLD). There is no antidote; reactions are treated supportively.",
  "fun": "Each lot of ATG is a little different because it comes from a group of animals' immune responses rather than from a single cloned cell, which is why it is called polyclonal.",
  "src": ["Thymoglobulin (anti-thymocyte globulin, rabbit) FDA label","Atgam (lymphocyte immune globulin, equine) FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"intravenous-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "IVIG rests on two milestones. During World War II, Edwin Cohn at Harvard developed cold ethanol fractionation, a way to split donated plasma into albumin, gamma globulin and other parts. In 1952 Ogden Bruton, an Army pediatrician, described a boy with repeated infections and no gamma globulin in his blood, the first recognized primary immunodeficiency, and treated him with injections of gamma globulin. Early products could only be given into muscle because they clumped and caused severe reactions in a vein. Processing changes in the late 1970s and early 1980s made IgG safe for IV use, and IV immune globulin was licensed in the US in the early 1980s. In 1981 Paul Imbach reported that IVIG raised platelets in children with immune thrombocytopenia, opening its use as an immune modulator.",
  "timeline": [["1940s","Edwin Cohn develops cold ethanol fractionation of plasma."],["1952","Ogden Bruton describes agammaglobulinemia and treats it with gamma globulin."],["1981","Imbach reports IVIG raises platelet counts in childhood ITP."],["1980s","IV immune globulin products are licensed in the US."]],
  "cell": "IVIG is IgG pooled from thousands of donors, so it holds antibodies against a huge range of germs. In antibody deficiency it simply replaces missing IgG: the antibodies neutralize toxins and viruses and coat bacteria (opsonization) so phagocytes can eat them. At high levels it also changes immune activity in several ways. It saturates Fc gamma receptors on spleen macrophages, so they clear fewer antibody-coated platelets in ITP. It saturates FcRn, the receptor that normally rescues IgG from breakdown, which speeds removal of harmful autoantibodies. Anti-idiotype antibodies in the pool can bind and neutralize autoantibodies. It also mops up activated complement fragments, increases expression of the inhibitory receptor Fc gamma RIIB, and shifts cytokine production toward calmer patterns.",
  "body": [["Immune defense","In people who cannot make antibodies, regular infusions restore IgG levels and greatly reduce pneumonia, sinus and other bacterial infections."],["Platelets","In ITP, blocking spleen Fc receptors slows the destruction of antibody-coated platelets, so counts rise within days."],["Nerves","In Guillain-Barre syndrome and CIDP, it lowers the activity of autoantibodies and complement that damage peripheral nerve myelin."],["Heart and vessels","In Kawasaki disease, it reduces vessel inflammation and the risk of coronary artery aneurysms."],["Blood thickness","A large protein load raises blood viscosity, which contributes to the risk of clots."],["Kidneys","Products stabilized with sucrose have been linked to kidney injury from osmotic damage to tubule cells."]],
  "journey": "IVIG is infused into a vein, so levels peak at the end of the infusion. The IgG first stays in the blood, then over a few days moves into tissue fluid until about half is outside the bloodstream. It is not metabolized by the liver or cleared by the kidneys. It is recycled by FcRn and slowly broken down by cells of the reticuloendothelial system, with a half-life of about three to five weeks, which is why replacement is given every three to four weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, chills, flushing and muscle aches during or after infusion come partly from immune complexes and cytokine release, and often relate to infusion speed. Aseptic meningitis, a severe headache with neck stiffness, may come from antibodies and inflammation in the meninges. Hemolysis happens because donor anti-A and anti-B antibodies coat the patient's red cells. Clots, including stroke and heart attack, relate to higher blood viscosity and procoagulant factors, leading to a boxed warning for thrombosis and kidney injury. People with IgA deficiency and anti-IgA antibodies can have anaphylaxis. TRALI, a lung injury, is rare.",
  "fun": "A single lot of IVIG can contain plasma from more than 10,000 donors, so it carries a sampling of the immune memories of a whole population.",
  "src": ["Gammagard Liquid (immune globulin) FDA label","Bruton OC. Pediatrics 1952","Imbach P et al. Lancet 1981"]
},
"rho-d-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "In 1939 and 1940 Philip Levine, Karl Landsteiner and Alexander Wiener described the Rh blood group and linked it to hemolytic disease of the newborn, in which an Rh-negative mother makes antibodies that destroy her Rh-positive baby's red cells. In the early 1960s two teams worked on prevention. In New York, Vincent Freda, John Gorman and William Pollack (at Ortho) tested anti-D antibody given to Rh-negative men and then mothers. In Liverpool, Cyril Clarke and Ronald Finn did similar work. Giving anti-D just after delivery cleared fetal red cells before the mother's immune system could learn to react. The FDA licensed RhoGAM in 1968. Its use, later extended to during pregnancy, made Rh disease rare in countries with routine prophylaxis.",
  "timeline": [["1940","Landsteiner and Wiener describe the Rh factor; Levine links it to newborn hemolytic disease."],["1960s","Teams in New York and Liverpool show anti-D can prevent Rh sensitization."],["1968","FDA licenses RhoGAM."],["1990s","IV anti-D is approved to treat immune thrombocytopenia in Rh-positive patients."]],
  "cell": "During pregnancy and especially at delivery, a small amount of fetal blood can enter the mother's circulation. If the mother is RhD-negative and the baby is RhD-positive, her immune system can see the D antigen as foreign and form memory B cells. In a later pregnancy, IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and destroys fetal red cells. Rho(D) immune globulin is IgG anti-D from donors. It binds the D antigen on fetal red cells in the mother's blood. Coated cells are removed by spleen macrophages through Fc receptors, and the antibody also suppresses the mother's primary B-cell response, so no anti-D memory forms. In ITP, IV anti-D coats the patient's own Rh-positive red cells, which keep spleen Fc receptors busy and spare antibody-coated platelets.",
  "body": [["Mother's immune system","It prevents the mother from forming her own long-lasting anti-D antibodies, which protects future pregnancies."],["Fetus and newborn","Without maternal anti-D, later Rh-positive babies are spared hemolysis, anemia, jaundice and hydrops fetalis."],["Spleen","Antibody-coated red cells are cleared in the spleen. In ITP, this busy spleen clears fewer platelets."],["Blood (ITP use)","IV anti-D causes mild breakdown of the patient's own red cells, which lowers hemoglobin a little and, rarely, causes severe intravascular hemolysis."],["Injection site","Soreness and swelling come from the volume of protein injected into muscle."]],
  "journey": "Rho(D) immune globulin is given by injection into muscle or, for some products, into a vein. After an IM injection, levels peak in about two to seven days. It spreads through the blood and binds fetal red cells there. It is not metabolized by liver enzymes or cleared by the kidneys. It is broken down like the body's own IgG, with a half-life of about three to four weeks, which gives protection for about 12 weeks. That is why a dose at 28 weeks covers the rest of pregnancy.",
  "sidefx": "Local pain and mild fever come from injecting a protein solution. Because the drug works by coating red cells, a mild drop in hemoglobin can occur. When IV anti-D is used for ITP in Rh-positive patients, many red cells are coated at once, and rarely this causes intravascular hemolysis with kidney failure and disseminated intravascular coagulation, which led to a boxed warning. Allergic reactions are rare, with higher risk in IgA-deficient people with anti-IgA. It must not be given to the newborn.",
  "fun": "Some of the first anti-D donors were mothers who had already been sensitized, and a few men with very high anti-D levels donated plasma for decades, helping protect millions of babies.",
  "src": ["RhoGAM (Rho(D) immune globulin) FDA label","WinRho SDF FDA label","Freda VJ, Gorman JG, Pollack W. Transfusion 1964"]
},
"tropicamide": {
  "origin": "Tropicamide belongs to a family that began with the deadly nightshade plant, Atropa belladonna. In Renaissance Italy women reportedly used belladonna drops to widen their pupils, which gave the plant its name, meaning beautiful woman. Atropine, the plant's main alkaloid, was isolated in the 1830s and became the classic pupil-dilating drug, but its effect can last a week or more. Eye doctors wanted something that dilated the pupil for an exam and wore off the same day. Tropicamide, a synthetic antimuscarinic, was developed in the 1950s and became the standard short-acting drop for eye exams. It is sold as Mydriacyl and in generics, and in Paremyd with hydroxyamphetamine.",
  "timeline": [["c. 1500s","Belladonna is reportedly used to dilate pupils for beauty."],["1830s","Atropine is isolated from Atropa belladonna."],["1950s","Tropicamide is developed as a short-acting synthetic mydriatic."]],
  "cell": "Two muscles in the eye respond to acetylcholine from parasympathetic nerves. The sphincter pupillae in the iris narrows the pupil, and the ciliary muscle contracts to focus on near objects (accommodation). Both use M3 muscarinic receptors, which are Gq-coupled: acetylcholine binding activates phospholipase C, makes IP3, releases calcium and contracts the muscle. Tropicamide competitively blocks these muscarinic receptors. The sphincter relaxes, and the opposing dilator muscle, driven by sympathetic alpha-1 receptors, widens the pupil (mydriasis). The ciliary muscle also relaxes, so the eye cannot focus up close (cycloplegia). Tropicamide binds and leaves the receptor more quickly than atropine, so its effect lasts hours rather than days.",
  "body": [["Iris","The pupil widens within about 20 to 40 minutes, letting the clinician see the retina and optic nerve."],["Ciliary muscle","Near focusing is temporarily lost, causing blurred near vision for a few hours."],["Light sensitivity","A wide pupil lets in too much light, so bright light feels uncomfortable until it wears off."],["Eye drainage angle","In people with narrow angles, the bunched-up iris can block fluid drainage and trigger acute angle-closure glaucoma."],["Whole body","Drops that drain through the tear duct into the nose can be absorbed; in small children this can rarely cause behavior changes or cardiorespiratory effects."]],
  "journey": "Tropicamide is put on the eye as a drop. It passes through the cornea into the front of the eye and reaches the iris and ciliary body. Dilation starts in 15 to 20 minutes and peaks in 20 to 40 minutes. The pupil usually returns to normal in about 4 to 8 hours, sometimes up to a day. Some of the drop drains through the tear duct into the nose and throat, where a little is absorbed, but blood levels are very low. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye reduces this.",
  "sidefx": "Stinging is from the drop itself. Blurred vision and light sensitivity follow directly from blocked pupil and ciliary muscles. In eyes with a narrow drainage angle, a dilated pupil can push the iris against the angle and suddenly raise eye pressure, causing angle-closure glaucoma. When drops are absorbed through the nose, especially in infants and small children, blocked muscarinic receptors in the brain and heart can cause agitation, psychotic reactions, fast heart rate or, rarely, collapse. Severe anticholinergic toxicity can be reversed with physostigmine in a monitored setting.",
  "fun": "Tropicamide's quick offset is why many eye clinics can dilate your eyes in the morning and let you drive home by evening.",
  "src": ["Mydriacyl (tropicamide) FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"proparacaine": {
  "origin": "Eye anesthesia began in 1884, when Karl Koller, a young doctor in Vienna, showed that cocaine drops numbed the cornea so surgery could be done on an awake patient. Cocaine was addictive and damaged the corneal surface, so chemists made safer ester anesthetics, starting with procaine in 1905. Proparacaine was introduced in the 1950s as a topical eye anesthetic that stung less than earlier drugs. It is sold as Alcaine and in generics, and combined with fluorescein as Fluoracaine for measuring eye pressure. Today it is one of the most common drops in eye clinics and emergency departments for exams, foreign body removal and tonometry.",
  "timeline": [["1884","Karl Koller shows cocaine drops numb the eye for surgery."],["1905","Alfred Einhorn's procaine is introduced as a safer ester local anesthetic."],["1950s","Proparacaine is introduced as a topical eye anesthetic."]],
  "cell": "Pain from the cornea travels along very fine nerve endings from the trigeminal nerve. To send a signal, these nerves open voltage-gated sodium channels, letting sodium rush in and carry an action potential to the brain. Proparacaine is a weak base. Its uncharged form passes through the nerve membrane. Inside the nerve it picks up a proton and the charged form binds a site inside the sodium channel pore, mostly when the channel is open or inactivated. Blocked channels cannot open, so the nerve cannot fire and pain signals stop. The cornea's nerve endings sit very close to the surface, which is why a single drop works within seconds.",
  "body": [["Cornea","The cornea becomes numb within about 30 seconds, allowing tonometry, removal of foreign bodies and other procedures."],["Conjunctiva","The lining of the eye also numbs, with brief redness and stinging when the drop goes in."],["Blink reflex","With the cornea numb, the blink reflex is lost, so the eye is unprotected from dust and rubbing for about 15 to 20 minutes."],["Corneal surface","Repeated use slows the healing of the corneal surface cells and can break down the epithelium."]],
  "journey": "Proparacaine is dropped onto the eye and acts right at the surface. Numbness starts in about 20 to 30 seconds and lasts about 15 to 20 minutes. Very little reaches the blood. What is absorbed is an ester and is quickly broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase, and the products leave in the urine. Because so little enters the body, systemic effects are very rare.",
  "sidefx": "Stinging and redness come from the drop itself. Loss of the blink reflex is a direct result of numbing the cornea, which is why the eye is protected afterward. Local anesthetics are toxic to corneal epithelial cells and slow their movement and repair. Used over and over, as when patients are sent home with drops, this can cause keratitis, ulcers, scarring and even corneal perforation, which is why it is meant for in-office use only. Allergic reactions, including contact dermatitis of the eyelids, are uncommon.",
  "fun": "Proparacaine is one of the few eye medicines that is often kept in the refrigerator, because it can darken and lose strength at room temperature over time.",
  "src": ["Alcaine (proparacaine) FDA label","Koller K. 1884 report on cocaine anesthesia of the eye","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tetracaine": {
  "origin": "Tetracaine is an ester local anesthetic built on the same para-aminobenzoic acid core as procaine, the first synthetic local anesthetic, introduced in 1905. Procaine was safe but weak and short-acting. In the late 1920s chemists in Germany added a butyl group to the molecule, making it more fat-soluble, more potent and longer acting. The new drug, also called amethocaine, was introduced around 1930 and was used for spinal anesthesia, topical anesthesia of the airway and the eye. Today the eye drop form is used to numb the cornea for exams and minor procedures, and other topical and spinal forms exist.",
  "timeline": [["1884","Karl Koller shows cocaine numbs the eye."],["1905","Procaine is introduced as the first widely used synthetic local anesthetic."],["c. 1930","Tetracaine is introduced as a more potent ester local anesthetic."]],
  "cell": "Tetracaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the sensory nerve endings of the cornea and conjunctiva. The uncharged drug crosses the nerve membrane, becomes charged inside the cell, and binds a site inside the channel pore when the channel is open or inactivated. With sodium unable to enter, the nerve cannot produce an action potential, so pain signals do not reach the brain. Its butyl side chain makes it much more fat-soluble than procaine, so it enters the nerve membrane more easily and binds more strongly, which explains its greater potency. As an ester of para-aminobenzoic acid, any drug that reaches the blood is broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase.",
  "body": [["Cornea","The cornea numbs within seconds, allowing tonometry, foreign body removal and other quick procedures."],["Conjunctiva","The lining of the eye also numbs, but tetracaine usually stings more on instillation than proparacaine."],["Blink reflex","Because the cornea is numb, the blink reflex is lost for about 15 to 20 minutes, so the eye can be injured without the person noticing."],["Corneal surface","Like all topical anesthetics, it slows healing of the corneal surface, especially with repeated use."]],
  "journey": "Tetracaine eye drops act right on the surface of the eye, with onset in about 10 to 20 seconds and effect for about 15 to 20 minutes. Only tiny amounts reach the blood. The absorbed drug is broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase to para-aminobutylbenzoic acid and other products, which leave in the urine. When tetracaine is used on larger mucous surfaces such as the airway, more can be absorbed, and it is broken down more slowly than other esters.",
  "sidefx": "Stinging and burning are from the drug and its acidity. Loss of the blink reflex follows from corneal numbness. Repeated use is toxic to corneal epithelial cells and can lead to keratitis, scarring and opacification, which is why the drops are not sent home with patients. Allergic reactions are possible because ester anesthetics form para-aminobenzoic acid compounds that some people react to. When large amounts are absorbed from mucous membranes, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity, which is treated with supportive care and lipid emulsion.",
  "fun": "Tetracaine is far more potent than procaine. Adding a single four-carbon chain to the molecule made the difference.",
  "src": ["Tetracaine hydrochloride ophthalmic solution FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"factor-viia-recombinant": {
  "origin": "Recombinant factor VIIa was developed to solve a hard problem in hemophilia. Some patients with hemophilia A or B form inhibitors, antibodies that destroy replacement factor VIII or IX. In the late 1970s and 1980s, the Swedish physician Ulla Hedner, working with Walter Kisiel, proposed that high levels of activated factor VII could bypass factors VIII and IX entirely. Plasma-derived factor VIIa was first used in a patient in 1983. Novo Nordisk then produced it with recombinant DNA in hamster kidney cells, and it was first given in 1988. The FDA approved NovoSeven in 1999 for bleeding in hemophilia with inhibitors. A second product, eptacog beta (Sevenfact), made in transgenic rabbits, was approved in 2020.",
  "timeline": [["1983","Plasma-derived factor VIIa is first used to treat a hemophilia patient with inhibitors."],["1988","Recombinant factor VIIa is first given to a patient."],["1999","FDA approves NovoSeven for hemophilia with inhibitors."],["2020","FDA approves eptacog beta (Sevenfact)."]],
  "cell": "Clotting starts when blood vessel injury exposes tissue factor. Factor VIIa bound to tissue factor activates factors X and IX. Normally, factors VIIIa and IXa then form the tenase complex on activated platelets, which makes most of the factor Xa needed for a large thrombin burst. In hemophilia this step fails. At the high levels reached with therapy, recombinant factor VIIa does two things. It binds tissue factor at the injury. It also binds directly to the phospholipid surface of activated platelets, where it activates factor X without needing tissue factor or factors VIII and IX. Factor Xa with factor Va converts prothrombin to thrombin, and the thrombin burst turns fibrinogen into fibrin. Because activated platelets gather only where there is injury, the effect is mostly local.",
  "body": [["Blood at the injury site","A local thrombin burst forms a fibrin clot that controls bleeding even when factors VIII or IX are blocked by inhibitors."],["Platelets","Thrombin also activates more platelets, strengthening the platelet plug."],["Laboratory tests","PT and INR shorten quickly, but this lab change does not prove that bleeding is controlled."],["Arteries","Excess thrombin generation can form clots in arteries, causing stroke or heart attack, especially in patients with vascular disease."],["Veins and lungs","Venous clots and pulmonary emboli can occur, particularly with use outside approved indications."]],
  "journey": "Recombinant factor VIIa is given as an IV bolus, so it acts within minutes. It stays mostly in the blood. It is inactivated by natural inhibitors, antithrombin and tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and cleared mainly by the liver. Its half-life is short, about two to three hours in adults and shorter in children, which is why doses are often repeated every few hours until bleeding stops.",
  "sidefx": "The main risk is clotting in the wrong place. Because high levels of factor VIIa generate thrombin on any activated platelet or exposed tissue factor, patients with atherosclerosis, crush injury or sepsis can form arterial or venous clots, and there is a boxed warning for thrombosis with use outside approved indications. Disseminated intravascular coagulation has been reported. Fever, headache and blood pressure changes are common. Some patients form antibodies that reduce response. There is no specific reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Factor VII has the shortest half-life of all the clotting factors in the body, only a few hours, which is why warfarin first lowers factor VII and raises the INR within a day or two.",
  "src": ["NovoSeven RT (coagulation factor VIIa, recombinant) FDA label","Sevenfact FDA label","Hedner U. Semin Hematol 2006"]
},
"fibrinogen-concentrate": {
  "origin": "Fibrinogen, also called factor I, was the first clotting protein ever described; 19th century scientists noticed that blood clotted because a soluble plasma protein turned into insoluble fibrin threads. For decades the only way to replace it was cryoprecipitate or fresh frozen plasma, which vary in strength and carry transfusion risks. Fibrinogen concentrates, purified from pooled human plasma and treated to inactivate viruses, were used in Europe for years before reaching the US. The FDA approved CSL Behring's RiaSTAP in 2009 for bleeding in people with congenital fibrinogen deficiency, and Octapharma's Fibryga in 2017. It allows a known amount of fibrinogen to be given quickly without thawing plasma.",
  "timeline": [["1800s","Scientists identify fibrinogen as the protein that becomes fibrin in clots."],["1960s","Cryoprecipitate becomes a standard source of fibrinogen."],["2009","FDA approves RiaSTAP for congenital fibrinogen deficiency."],["2017","FDA approves Fibryga."]],
  "cell": "Fibrinogen is a large protein made of three pairs of chains. At the final step of clotting, thrombin cuts off small pieces called fibrinopeptides A and B. The remaining fibrin monomers stick to each other end to end and side to side, forming long strands. Factor XIIIa, also activated by thrombin, then cross-links these strands into a strong, stable mesh that holds the clot together. Fibrinogen also has a second role: it bridges activated platelets by binding their glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors, linking platelets into a plug. Fibrinogen concentrate simply adds back this substrate, so when thrombin is made at a wound there is enough raw material to form a firm clot.",
  "body": [["Blood","Plasma fibrinogen rises right away, and clot strength improves on viscoelastic tests such as TEG or ROTEM."],["Bleeding sites","With enough fibrinogen, clots form faster and are firmer, which helps stop bleeding in people with low fibrinogen."],["Platelets","More fibrinogen links platelets together more effectively through IIb/IIIa receptors."],["Veins and arteries","Too much fibrinogen can favor unwanted clots, such as deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism and heart attack."]],
  "journey": "Fibrinogen concentrate is a powder mixed with sterile water and given into a vein, so the plasma level rises immediately. It stays mostly in the blood. It is used up when clots form and the rest is broken down slowly like the body's own fibrinogen. The half-life is about three days in adults, so one dose can keep levels up for several days.",
  "sidefx": "Clot-related problems are the main serious risk because fibrinogen is the building block of clots. Raising it too high, especially in people with other clotting risks, can contribute to venous or arterial thrombosis. Allergic reactions and anaphylaxis can occur with any plasma protein. Fever and headache are common. Because it is made from human plasma, there is a very small theoretical risk of transmitting infections, which donor screening and viral inactivation lower.",
  "fun": "Fibrinogen is one of the most plentiful proteins in plasma, and its level rises with inflammation, which is part of why the erythrocyte sedimentation rate goes up: fibrinogen makes red cells stack and fall faster.",
  "src": ["RiaSTAP (fibrinogen concentrate) FDA label","Fibryga FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"antithrombin": {
  "origin": "In 1939 researchers including Kenneth Brinkhous found that heparin needed a helper protein in plasma to work, which they called heparin cofactor. That protein is antithrombin. In 1965 the Norwegian doctor Olav Egeberg described a family with frequent blood clots and low antithrombin, the first known inherited thrombophilia. This showed antithrombin is one of the body's main natural anticoagulants. A purified human plasma product, Thrombate III, was approved in the US in 1991 for people with hereditary antithrombin deficiency, to prevent or treat clots around surgery, childbirth or other high-risk times.",
  "timeline": [["1939","Brinkhous and colleagues show heparin needs a plasma cofactor."],["1965","Egeberg describes inherited antithrombin deficiency in a Norwegian family."],["1991","Thrombate III is approved in the US for hereditary antithrombin deficiency."]],
  "cell": "Antithrombin is a serpin, a serine protease inhibitor made by the liver. It acts like a molecular trap. Its reactive loop looks like the normal target of thrombin and factor Xa. When one of these enzymes cuts the loop, antithrombin snaps into a new shape and locks the enzyme in a tight, permanent one-to-one complex. It also inhibits factors IXa, XIa and XIIa to a lesser degree. On its own the reaction is slow. Heparin and the heparan sulfate on blood vessel walls carry a special pentasaccharide sequence that binds antithrombin and changes its shape, speeding inhibition about a thousandfold. People with antithrombin deficiency make too much thrombin and form clots, and they may not respond well to heparin.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting","Restoring antithrombin lowers thrombin and factor Xa activity, reducing the risk of venous clots in deficient patients."],["Response to heparin","In people with low antithrombin, adding it back restores the effect of heparin."],["Bleeding","Combined with heparin, extra antithrombin can increase bleeding, so heparin may be adjusted."],["Liver and spleen","Antithrombin-enzyme complexes are removed by the liver and reticuloendothelial system."]],
  "journey": "Antithrombin is given into a vein, so the level rises at once. It stays mostly in the blood and the lining of vessels. It is used up as it forms complexes with clotting enzymes, which are then cleared by the liver. Its half-life is about two and a half to almost four days, but it is shorter during surgery, active clotting or heparin use, when more is consumed.",
  "sidefx": "Because antithrombin adds to the body's anticoagulant power, bleeding is a risk, especially with heparin, which speeds its action greatly. Dizziness, chest discomfort, nausea and a foul taste have been reported during infusion. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can occur with plasma proteins. As a plasma product, it carries a very small risk of transmitting infections. There is no specific reversal agent, although stopping heparin removes the accelerating effect.",
  "fun": "Heparin is not really an anticoagulant on its own. It works almost entirely by supercharging antithrombin, so heparin barely works in someone with very low antithrombin.",
  "src": ["Thrombate III (antithrombin III, human) FDA label","Egeberg O. Thromb Diath Haemorrh 1965","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"hemin": {
  "origin": "The porphyrias are inherited disorders of heme production. In acute intermittent porphyria, a partial lack of the enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase lets toxic precursors build up during attacks, causing severe abdominal pain, nerve damage and mental changes. Researchers reasoned that if heme normally switches off its own production, giving heme might calm an attack. In 1971 Herbert Bonkowsky and colleagues reported that IV hematin lowered the precursors in patients with acute porphyria. Abbott developed the product as Panhematin, and in 1983 it became the first drug approved under the new US Orphan Drug Act. It is now made by Recordati Rare Diseases.",
  "timeline": [["1971","Bonkowsky and colleagues report that IV hematin lowers porphyrin precursors in acute porphyria."],["1983","Panhematin becomes the first drug approved under the US Orphan Drug Act."],["2019","Givosiran, an RNA-based drug, is approved for acute hepatic porphyria."]],
  "cell": "Heme is built in eight enzyme steps. The first and rate-limiting step in the liver is made by delta-aminolevulinic acid synthase 1 (ALAS1). Normally, heme acts as a brake on ALAS1: it reduces the gene's expression, destabilizes its messenger RNA, and blocks the enzyme's transport into mitochondria. In acute intermittent porphyria, a downstream enzyme is weak. Drugs, fasting, hormones or stress raise the demand for heme, which turns up ALAS1, and the precursors delta-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen (PBG) pile up. These are thought to be toxic to nerves. Hemin is a heme analog. Taken up by liver cells, it refills the free heme pool and switches ALAS1 back down, so ALA and PBG production falls.",
  "body": [["Liver","The liver makes fewer heme precursors, so urinary ALA and PBG fall within a few days."],["Gut and abdomen","Severe abdominal pain, caused by effects on autonomic nerves, usually eases as precursor levels fall."],["Nerves","Early treatment can limit the progression of motor neuropathy and weakness."],["Brain and autonomic system","Confusion, anxiety, fast heart rate and high blood pressure tend to settle as the attack resolves."],["Veins","The infusion is irritating, and phlebitis at the IV site is common."],["Blood clotting","Hemin can briefly prolong clotting times and lower platelets."]],
  "journey": "Hemin is a powder mixed with sterile water, or more often with human albumin to make it more stable, and infused into a vein. In the blood it binds hemopexin and albumin, which carry it to the liver. Liver cells take it up, where it represses ALAS1. It is broken down by heme oxygenase into biliverdin, iron and carbon monoxide. Bilirubin from biliverdin leaves in bile, and the iron joins body stores. The half-life is about 11 hours but varies.",
  "sidefx": "Phlebitis happens because hemin and its breakdown products irritate the vein lining, which is one reason it is mixed with albumin and given through a large vein. Hemin breakdown products can interfere with clotting factors and platelets, briefly prolonging PT and aPTT. In overdose it can cause acute kidney injury. Repeated courses can add to iron stores. Allergic reactions are uncommon. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Some historians have suggested that King George III's episodes of madness were caused by porphyria, though this idea is still debated.",
  "src": ["Panhematin (hemin for injection) FDA label","Bonkowsky HL et al. PNAS 1971","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector": {
  "origin": "This kit pairs two antidotes with separate histories. Atropine comes from the deadly nightshade plant and was isolated in the 1830s; it blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. Organophosphate nerve agents were discovered in Germany in 1936, when Gerhard Schrader, searching for insecticides, made tabun. These agents block acetylcholinesterase. In 1955 Irwin Wilson and Sara Ginsburg at Columbia University designed pralidoxime, an oxime that could pull the organophosphate off the enzyme and restore it. The US military used the two as separate auto-injectors in the Mark I kit. DuoDote, a single auto-injector holding both drugs, was approved by the FDA in 2006 for civilian first responders and EMS treating nerve agent or organophosphate insecticide poisoning.",
  "timeline": [["1830s","Atropine is isolated from Atropa belladonna."],["1936","Gerhard Schrader synthesizes tabun, the first nerve agent."],["1955","Wilson and Ginsburg develop pralidoxime to reactivate acetylcholinesterase."],["1980s","Mark I kits with separate atropine and pralidoxime injectors are issued to US troops."],["2006","FDA approves DuoDote, a combined auto-injector."]],
  "cell": "Organophosphates phosphorylate the serine at the active site of acetylcholinesterase, so the enzyme can no longer break down acetylcholine. Acetylcholine builds up at muscarinic receptors (glands, smooth muscle, heart), nicotinic receptors (skeletal muscle and autonomic ganglia) and in the brain. Atropine is a competitive antagonist at muscarinic M1 to M5 receptors. It does not touch the enzyme or nicotinic receptors, but it blocks the excess acetylcholine at muscarinic sites, drying secretions, opening the airways and raising the heart rate. Pralidoxime is a quaternary oxime. It binds near the blocked active site and attacks the phosphorus, pulling the phosphoryl group off the serine and freeing the enzyme. This only works before 'aging', when the bound agent loses a chemical group and becomes permanent. Restored enzyme helps most at nicotinic neuromuscular junctions, easing weakness.",
  "body": [["Airways and lungs","Atropine dries the flood of airway secretions and relaxes bronchospasm, which are the main causes of death in nerve agent poisoning."],["Heart","Atropine reverses the slow heart rate caused by excess vagal acetylcholine and usually causes a faster rate."],["Glands","Sweating, drooling, tearing and runny nose dry up as muscarinic receptors are blocked."],["Skeletal muscle","Pralidoxime restores the enzyme at nerve-muscle junctions, reducing twitching, weakness and paralysis of breathing muscles."],["Gut and bladder","Atropine reduces cramps, vomiting, diarrhea and involuntary urination."],["Brain","Atropine enters the brain and can help some central effects, but pralidoxime is charged and enters poorly, so seizures are treated with benzodiazepines."]],
  "journey": "DuoDote is injected into the thigh muscle through clothing. Both drugs are absorbed from muscle within minutes. Atropine spreads widely, crosses into the brain and is partly broken down by the liver, with much excreted unchanged in urine; its half-life is about two to four hours. Pralidoxime stays mostly outside the brain because it carries a permanent positive charge. It is barely metabolized and leaves through the kidneys mostly unchanged, with a half-life of about one to two and a half hours.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from blocking muscarinic receptors beyond what is needed: dry mouth, blurred vision, flushing, fast heart rate, urinary retention and, with excess, confusion, delirium and high body temperature because the person cannot sweat. In someone who has not been poisoned, atropine toxicity can be dangerous, especially in heat. Pralidoxime can cause dizziness, blurred vision and, given quickly, muscle weakness or high blood pressure. Severe anticholinergic toxicity is managed supportively.",
  "fun": "The endpoint used for atropine in organophosphate poisoning is drying of lung secretions, not the size of the pupils, because pupils can stay pinpoint even after enough atropine.",
  "src": ["DuoDote (atropine and pralidoxime chloride) FDA label","Wilson IB, Ginsburg S. Biochim Biophys Acta 1955","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"sodium-nitrite": {
  "origin": "Sodium nitrite as a cyanide antidote came out of animal studies in the early 1930s. In 1933 K. K. Chen, Charles Rose and George Clowes at Eli Lilly reported that dogs given lethal cyanide survived when treated with nitrites followed by sodium thiosulfate. Their idea was to create methemoglobin on purpose, because methemoglobin grabs cyanide more tightly than the cell's own enzymes do. The combination of amyl nitrite, sodium nitrite and sodium thiosulfate became the classic cyanide antidote kit for decades. For many years it was sold without a modern FDA label. Formal FDA approval came in the early 2010s, both as Nithiodote (sodium nitrite with sodium thiosulfate) and as separate sodium nitrite injection. Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit), approved in 2006, is now often preferred, especially for smoke inhalation.",
  "timeline": [["1933","Chen, Rose and Clowes at Eli Lilly show nitrite plus thiosulfate protects dogs from cyanide."],["1930s","The nitrite-thiosulfate cyanide antidote kit comes into clinical use."],["2006","Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) is approved as an alternative cyanide antidote."],["2010s","Sodium nitrite receives formal FDA approval, alone and in Nithiodote."]],
  "cell": "Cyanide kills by binding the ferric iron in cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), the last enzyme of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Cells can no longer use oxygen to make ATP, even though plenty of oxygen is in the blood. They switch to anaerobic metabolism, and lactic acid builds up. Sodium nitrite oxidizes the iron in hemoglobin from the ferrous (Fe2+) to the ferric (Fe3+) state, forming methemoglobin. Methemoglobin's ferric iron has a higher affinity for cyanide than cytochrome oxidase does, so it pulls cyanide out of the mitochondria, forming cyanmethemoglobin. Freed cytochrome oxidase restarts oxidative phosphorylation. Nitrite also produces nitric oxide, which may help through other effects. The cyanide in cyanmethemoglobin is then removed with sodium thiosulfate.",
  "body": [["Blood","A portion of hemoglobin becomes methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen and gives the skin a bluish color."],["Mitochondria","Cytochrome oxidase is freed from cyanide, so cells can use oxygen again and lactic acidosis starts to improve."],["Blood vessels","Nitrite and nitric oxide relax vascular smooth muscle, which lowers blood pressure and can cause flushing and headache."],["Heart","Low blood pressure can cause a fast heart rate, dizziness or fainting."],["Red cells in G6PD deficiency","Oxidant stress can break down red cells in people with G6PD deficiency."]],
  "journey": "Sodium nitrite is given slowly into a vein, so it reaches red cells within minutes. Methemoglobin rises over the next 30 to 60 minutes. Nitrite has a half-life of about 45 minutes. Part is converted to other nitrogen compounds and a large share is excreted unchanged in the urine. Red cell enzymes (methemoglobin reductase) slowly convert methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin over hours.",
  "sidefx": "The main dangers come from the same actions that make it work. Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so too much worsens tissue hypoxia, which is a major concern in smoke inhalation victims who already have carbon monoxide poisoning. That is why hydroxocobalamin is preferred in fires. Vasodilation causes low blood pressure, headache and fainting. In G6PD deficiency, the oxidant stress can cause hemolysis. Excess methemoglobinemia can be treated with methylene blue, though this also releases cyanide, so it is used with care.",
  "fun": "The nitrite strategy turns a poisoning into a treatment: doctors deliberately cause one condition, methemoglobinemia, to cure another, cyanide poisoning.",
  "src": ["Sodium Nitrite Injection FDA label","Nithiodote FDA label","Chen KK, Rose CL, Clowes GHA. 1933 cyanide antidote studies"]
},
"sodium-thiosulfate": {
  "origin": "Sodium thiosulfate has been known for centuries as a simple sulfur salt, used in photography as 'hypo' to fix images. In the 1890s and early 1900s scientists noticed that the body changes cyanide into thiocyanate, and in the early 1930s the liver enzyme that does this was described and named rhodanese. In 1933 K. K. Chen and colleagues at Eli Lilly showed that adding sodium thiosulfate to nitrite treatment rescued dogs from lethal cyanide, creating the classic cyanide antidote kit. It received formal FDA approval in the early 2010s, alone and as part of Nithiodote. In 2022 a separate product, Pedmark, was approved to lower the risk of hearing loss from cisplatin chemotherapy in children with certain solid tumors.",
  "timeline": [["1930s","The enzyme rhodanese, which turns cyanide into thiocyanate, is described."],["1933","Chen and colleagues show thiosulfate plus nitrite protects dogs from cyanide."],["2010s","Sodium thiosulfate receives formal FDA approval for cyanide poisoning."],["2022","FDA approves Pedmark to reduce cisplatin-related hearing loss in children."]],
  "cell": "The body has a natural cyanide disposal system. Rhodanese (thiosulfate sulfurtransferase), an enzyme found mainly in mitochondria of the liver and kidney, moves a sulfur atom from a donor onto cyanide, making thiocyanate. Thiocyanate is much less toxic and leaves in the urine. Normally the supply of sulfur donors is small, so the pathway is quickly overwhelmed. Sodium thiosulfate supplies large amounts of sulfur for rhodanese, speeding cyanide removal many times over. The reaction is fairly slow to start, which is why it is paired with faster agents such as nitrite or hydroxocobalamin. In cisplatin protection, thiosulfate is thought to bind and inactivate platinum in the inner ear, where it reaches the hair cells.",
  "body": [["Mitochondria","As cyanide is converted to thiocyanate, cytochrome oxidase is freed and cells can use oxygen again."],["Blood chemistry","Lactic acidosis gradually improves as aerobic metabolism resumes."],["Kidneys","Thiosulfate and thiocyanate are cleared by the kidneys; in kidney failure thiocyanate can build up."],["Blood pressure","Thiosulfate itself can lower blood pressure, and the drop is more marked when given with nitrite."],["Inner ear","In children receiving cisplatin, it reduces damage to cochlear hair cells and lowers the rate of hearing loss."],["Gut","Nausea, vomiting and a salty taste are common during infusion."]],
  "journey": "Sodium thiosulfate is given into a vein. It spreads through the fluid outside cells but enters cells poorly, which limits how fast it works. The kidneys remove it quickly, and its half-life is under an hour. The thiocyanate it forms with cyanide lasts much longer, especially if the kidneys are weak. Some thiosulfate is oxidized to sulfate. For cisplatin protection, timing after the cisplatin dose is important so it does not reduce the anticancer effect.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and a salty taste come from the large salt load. Low blood pressure can happen, mostly when given with sodium nitrite. Thiocyanate, the end product, can build up in kidney failure and cause confusion, weakness and psychosis. The sodium load can matter in people with heart failure. With Pedmark, high sodium and low potassium can occur. There is no specific antidote for thiosulfate itself; thiocyanate can be removed by dialysis.",
  "fun": "The same chemical photographers used to fix images, called hypo, also fixes cyanide into a harmless form in the body.",
  "src": ["Sodium Thiosulfate Injection FDA label","Pedmark (sodium thiosulfate) FDA label","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"amyl-nitrite": {
  "origin": "Amyl nitrite was first made in 1844 by the French chemist Antoine Jerome Balard. Later researchers noticed that breathing its vapor caused flushing and a racing pulse. In 1867 the Scottish physician Thomas Lauder Brunton used inhaled amyl nitrite to relieve angina, reasoning that it lowered pressure in the vessels. This was one of the first drug treatments for angina, before nitroglycerin. In 1933 it became part of the cyanide antidote kit developed at Eli Lilly, used first as a quick bridge before IV sodium nitrite. It came in fragile glass capsules, called pearls, that were crushed and inhaled. Its use in cyanide kits has largely ended, and it remains available as a prescription inhalant. Related alkyl nitrites are misused as 'poppers'.",
  "timeline": [["1844","Antoine Jerome Balard first synthesizes amyl nitrite."],["1867","Thomas Lauder Brunton uses inhaled amyl nitrite to relieve angina."],["1933","Amyl nitrite becomes part of the nitrite-thiosulfate cyanide antidote kit."],["1970s","Recreational misuse of alkyl nitrites as poppers becomes widespread."]],
  "cell": "Amyl nitrite is an organic nitrite that releases nitric oxide in vascular smooth muscle. Nitric oxide activates soluble guanylate cyclase, which converts GTP to cyclic GMP. Cyclic GMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers calcium inside the cell and activates myosin light chain phosphatase. The muscle relaxes. Veins dilate more than arteries, so blood pools in the veins and less returns to the heart (lower preload), which lowers the heart's oxygen demand and relieves angina. Nitrite also oxidizes hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methemoglobin, which binds cyanide as cyanmethemoglobin. Inhaled amyl nitrite produces only modest methemoglobin, which is why it was used only as a bridge in cyanide poisoning.",
  "body": [["Veins","Veins widen and blood pools, reducing the work of the heart, which explains the relief of angina."],["Arteries","Arteries in the head and skin widen, causing flushing, a throbbing headache and a fall in blood pressure."],["Heart","The drop in blood pressure triggers a reflex fast heart rate and pounding pulse."],["Brain","Low blood pressure can cause dizziness or fainting, especially when standing."],["Blood","Some hemoglobin becomes methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen."]],
  "journey": "Amyl nitrite is a volatile liquid. When the capsule is crushed, the vapor is breathed in and absorbed through the lungs within seconds. The effect starts within about 30 seconds and lasts only about three to five minutes. It is rapidly broken down by hydrolysis into isoamyl alcohol and nitrite, and the products leave in the urine. Swallowing it is much more dangerous than inhaling it.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, dizziness and fast heart rate all come from sudden vasodilation and the body's reflex response. Low blood pressure can cause fainting. Combined with PDE-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil, which also raise cyclic GMP, it can cause a severe and dangerous drop in blood pressure. Large amounts, especially if swallowed, can cause life-threatening methemoglobinemia, which is treated with methylene blue. The vapor is highly flammable.",
  "fun": "The name poppers comes from the popping sound the glass ampules made when they were crushed between the fingers.",
  "src": ["Amyl Nitrite Inhalant FDA label","Brunton TL. Lancet 1867","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"prussian-blue": {
  "origin": "Prussian blue was one of the first synthetic pigments. It was made by accident around 1706 in Berlin by the color maker Johann Jacob Diesbach, and painters soon used its deep blue. Much later, scientists found that its crystal lattice could trap cesium and thallium ions. Beginning in the 1960s, it was studied as a treatment for thallium poisoning and radioactive cesium. Its best known use came in 1987 in Goiania, Brazil, when a stolen radiotherapy source spread cesium-137 and Prussian blue was given to many contaminated people. After the 2001 terrorist attacks, the FDA reviewed the evidence and in 2003 approved Radiogardase (insoluble Prussian blue capsules) for internal contamination with radioactive cesium or with thallium. It is kept in the Strategic National Stockpile.",
  "timeline": [["c. 1706","Johann Jacob Diesbach makes Prussian blue pigment in Berlin."],["1960s","Prussian blue is studied for removing thallium and cesium from the body."],["1987","Prussian blue is used for many people after the cesium-137 accident in Goiania, Brazil."],["2003","FDA approves Radiogardase for cesium and thallium contamination."]],
  "cell": "Prussian blue is insoluble ferric hexacyanoferrate. It is not absorbed. Its crystal is a cube-shaped lattice with spaces that fit monovalent ions of the right size. Cesium and thallium ions (similar in size to potassium) fit into these spaces and are held tightly, while other ions are released in exchange. Normally, cesium and thallium in the body are secreted into the gut in bile and intestinal fluid and then reabsorbed, a recycling loop called enterohepatic and enteroenteric circulation. Prussian blue sits in the gut lumen and grabs these ions as they enter, breaking the loop. The bound metal leaves in the stool. This shortens the time cesium-137 stays in the body from about 110 days to about 30 days, and thallium from about eight days to three.",
  "body": [["Gut","It acts entirely inside the intestine, binding cesium and thallium before they can be reabsorbed."],["Whole body radiation","Faster removal of cesium-137 lowers the total internal radiation dose to tissues and bone marrow."],["Nerves and hair (thallium)","Lowering thallium levels limits nerve damage, hair loss and other toxicity."],["Stool","Stools turn blue, which is harmless and expected."],["Bowel habits","Constipation is common and slows the drug's work, so a fiber-rich diet or laxatives may be used."],["Electrolytes","It can bind some potassium, and low potassium has been reported."]],
  "journey": "Prussian blue is taken by mouth as capsules. It is not absorbed through the gut wall, is not metabolized and passes through the intestines unchanged. It works for as long as it is in the gut, binding cesium and thallium secreted into the bowel, and leaves in the stool with the bound metal. Treatment lasts at least 30 days and is guided by measuring radioactivity in the body or urine and stool.",
  "sidefx": "Because it stays in the gut, side effects are mostly local. Constipation and stomach upset are common, and blue stool is expected. Because the lattice can also exchange potassium, low potassium and other electrolyte shifts can occur, especially with long use. Patients who handle their stool, including caregivers of contaminated people, may be exposed to radioactive material in it. There is no antidote, and none is needed.",
  "fun": "The same pigment is the blue in many 18th and 19th century paintings, including Hokusai's famous print The Great Wave off Kanagawa.",
  "src": ["Radiogardase (Prussian blue insoluble capsules) FDA label","REAC/TS radiation emergency guidance","IAEA report on the Goiania radiological accident"]
},
"calcium-disodium-edta": {
  "origin": "EDTA (ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid) was first made in Germany in the 1930s by the chemist Ferdinand Munz, who wanted a substitute for citric acid to soften water for the textile industry. Its claw-like grip on metal ions caught the interest of doctors. In the early 1950s, physicians treating lead poisoning, including workers and children, found that EDTA could pull lead into the urine. Giving plain disodium EDTA also stripped calcium from the blood and could cause fatal hypocalcemia, so the calcium salt, calcium disodium EDTA, was developed. It became a standard treatment for lead poisoning, sold as Calcium Disodium Versenate. The brand has been discontinued, and generic edetate calcium disodium injection remains available.",
  "timeline": [["1930s","Ferdinand Munz first synthesizes EDTA in Germany."],["1950s","Calcium disodium EDTA comes into use for lead poisoning."],["2000s","Deaths from mix-ups with edetate disodium lead to FDA safety warnings."]],
  "cell": "EDTA has four acid groups and two nitrogen atoms that wrap around a metal ion like a cage. Each metal binds with a different strength. Calcium disodium EDTA comes already loaded with calcium. Lead binds EDTA much more tightly than calcium, so when the drug meets lead, the lead takes calcium's place. The result is a stable, water-soluble lead chelate that stays in the fluid outside cells. The kidneys filter it out into the urine. Because the drug already carries calcium, it does not pull calcium from the blood. EDTA mostly stays outside cells, so it removes lead from the blood and soft tissues and from bone surfaces, rather than from deep inside cells. It also binds zinc, iron, manganese and copper, which explains some side effects.",
  "body": [["Blood","Blood lead falls during treatment, but it often rebounds days later as lead shifts out of bone, so repeat courses may be needed."],["Kidneys","The kidneys carry the chelated lead out in urine. High lead loads and the drug can injure the tubules, causing protein and blood in the urine."],["Bone","Lead stored in bone is slowly mobilized into the blood, which is why blood levels rebound."],["Brain","In lead encephalopathy, moving lead can briefly worsen brain swelling, so it is usually given after dimercaprol, which is started first."],["Trace minerals","Zinc and other trace metals are also removed, and zinc levels can fall."],["Injection site","IM injection is painful, so a local anesthetic is often mixed in."]],
  "journey": "Calcium disodium EDTA is given into a vein or into muscle. It is poorly absorbed by mouth, so oral use is not effective. It stays mainly in the fluid outside cells. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out quickly, with a half-life of about 20 to 60 minutes after IV dosing and almost all gone within a day. Lead excretion in urine rises within an hour of starting treatment.",
  "sidefx": "Kidney damage is the main dose-limiting toxicity. The kidneys handle a large load of chelated lead and drug, and tubular cells can be injured, so kidney function and urine are checked. Loss of zinc and other trace metals comes from the drug's broad binding. In lead encephalopathy, shifting lead can raise brain pressure, which is why dimercaprol is given first. The most dangerous error is confusing it with edetate disodium, a different salt that binds calcium and caused deaths from sudden hypocalcemia and cardiac arrest. Edetate disodium is no longer marketed in the US.",
  "fun": "EDTA is also in many everyday products, from mayonnaise and shampoo to the purple-top blood tubes in labs, where it keeps blood from clotting by binding calcium.",
  "src": ["Edetate calcium disodium injection FDA label","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"pentetate-calcium-trisodium": {
  "origin": "Pentetate, or DTPA (diethylenetriaminepentaacetic acid), is a larger cousin of EDTA with a stronger grip on heavy metals. It was developed as a chelating agent in the mid-20th century. In the 1950s and 1960s, scientists in the nuclear field studied it for removing plutonium and other transuranic elements from workers who had been internally contaminated. For decades it was used in the US under an investigational protocol run through the government. After the 2001 terrorist attacks raised concern about radiological 'dirty bombs', the FDA approved Ca-DTPA and Zn-DTPA (made by Hameln Pharma) in 2004 for internal contamination with plutonium, americium or curium. Both are stocked in the Strategic National Stockpile.",
  "timeline": [["1950s","DTPA is developed as a chelating agent."],["1960s","DTPA is used to remove plutonium from contaminated nuclear workers under investigational use."],["2004","FDA approves Ca-DTPA for plutonium, americium and curium contamination."]],
  "cell": "DTPA has five acid groups and three nitrogen atoms that wrap tightly around a metal ion. Ca-DTPA comes loaded with calcium. Transuranic elements such as plutonium, americium and curium bind DTPA far more strongly than calcium does, so they replace it. The result is a stable, water-soluble chelate that the kidneys filter out into the urine. DTPA stays mostly in the blood and fluid outside cells. It works best on metal that is still circulating, before it settles into bone and liver, where it is held tightly and is hard to reach. It does not bind uranium or neptunium well. In the first 24 hours, Ca-DTPA removes about ten times more plutonium than Zn-DTPA, which is why it is preferred as the first dose when available.",
  "body": [["Blood and tissue fluid","Circulating plutonium, americium and curium are captured and carried out in urine, lowering the long-term radiation dose."],["Bone and liver","Removing metal before it deposits protects bone surfaces and liver cells from years of radiation exposure."],["Trace minerals","Ca-DTPA also strips zinc, magnesium and manganese from the body, which is why later doses switch to Zn-DTPA."],["Lungs","When given by nebulizer for inhaled contamination, it can trigger cough and wheezing, especially in people with asthma."],["Pregnancy","Zinc depletion from Ca-DTPA can harm a developing fetus, so Zn-DTPA is preferred in pregnancy."]],
  "journey": "Ca-DTPA is given into a vein, or breathed in from a nebulizer if contamination was by inhalation. It spreads through the fluid outside cells and does not enter cells much. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out quickly, with a half-life of about 90 minutes, and almost all of a dose is gone within 24 hours, carrying the bound metal with it. Urine and stool are radioactive during treatment.",
  "sidefx": "The main problem is loss of essential trace metals. Because DTPA binds zinc, magnesium and manganese, repeated Ca-DTPA can cause deficiencies, so it is used for the first dose and then replaced by Zn-DTPA. Zinc depletion can harm a fetus, explaining the teratogenicity warning. Nebulized use can cause bronchospasm. Nausea, headache, a metallic taste and lightheadedness are reported. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The patient's urine after DTPA treatment becomes the main way doctors measure how much radioactive metal was removed, and caregivers must handle it as radioactive waste.",
  "src": ["Pentetate calcium trisodium injection (Ca-DTPA) FDA label","REAC/TS radiation emergency guidance","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"pentetate-zinc-trisodium": {
  "origin": "Zn-DTPA is the zinc form of pentetate, the same chelator as Ca-DTPA but loaded with zinc instead of calcium. DTPA was studied in the 1950s and 1960s for removing plutonium and similar elements from contaminated nuclear workers. Researchers found that the calcium form worked faster in the first day, but repeated doses stripped the body of zinc and other trace metals. Loading the molecule with zinc solved that problem for longer treatment. Both forms were used under an investigational protocol for decades. In 2004 the FDA approved Zn-DTPA and Ca-DTPA, made by Hameln Pharma, for internal contamination with plutonium, americium or curium, as part of preparations for radiological emergencies.",
  "timeline": [["1950s","DTPA is developed as a chelating agent."],["1960s","DTPA is used under investigational protocols for plutonium contamination."],["2004","FDA approves Zn-DTPA for plutonium, americium and curium contamination."]],
  "cell": "Like Ca-DTPA, Zn-DTPA wraps tightly around metal ions with its five acid groups and three nitrogens. The zinc in Zn-DTPA is bound less tightly than plutonium, americium or curium would be, so these transuranic elements displace the zinc and form stable, water-soluble chelates. The kidneys filter out the chelates in urine. Because the molecule already carries zinc, it does not pull much zinc or manganese out of the body. Zinc is held more tightly than calcium, so the exchange is a bit slower, and Zn-DTPA removes less plutonium than Ca-DTPA in the first 24 hours. After the first day, the two work about equally well, so Zn-DTPA is used for continued treatment and as the first choice in pregnancy.",
  "body": [["Blood and tissue fluid","It captures circulating transuranic elements and moves them into urine, lowering long-term radiation dose."],["Bone and liver","Removing metal before it settles in bone and liver protects these tissues from long-term radiation."],["Trace minerals","Because it carries its own zinc, it causes little net loss of zinc and manganese, making it safer for longer courses."],["Lungs","Nebulized use for inhaled contamination can cause cough and wheezing, especially with asthma."],["Pregnancy","It is preferred over Ca-DTPA in pregnancy because it does not deplete the zinc a fetus needs."]],
  "journey": "Zn-DTPA is given into a vein, or by nebulizer for inhaled contamination. It stays in the fluid outside cells. It is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it out quickly, with a half-life of about 90 minutes, and nearly all of a dose is cleared in a day, taking the bound metal with it. Because effects depend on catching circulating metal, the earlier it is started the better.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild: nausea, headache, lightheadedness and a metallic taste. Because the zinc salt does not strip trace metals, mineral deficiency is much less of a problem than with Ca-DTPA. Nebulized use can cause bronchospasm in people with asthma. Urine and stool become radioactive and need careful handling. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Swapping a single metal atom, calcium for zinc, turns the same drug from a fast first-day treatment into a safer long-term one.",
  "src": ["Pentetate zinc trisodium injection (Zn-DTPA) FDA label","REAC/TS radiation emergency guidance","Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"lipid-emulsion": {
  "origin": "Giving fat into a vein was dangerous for decades because early emulsions caused fever, clotting problems and organ damage. In the early 1960s the Swedish physician and nutrition researcher Arvid Wretlind developed Intralipid, a stable emulsion of soybean oil with egg phospholipid and glycerin. It allowed patients who could not eat to receive essential fatty acids and calories by vein. It reached the US market in the 1970s. A new use came from anesthesia. In 1998 Guy Weinberg and colleagues showed that lipid emulsion rescued rats from bupivacaine overdose. In 2006 the first human case reports described patients in cardiac arrest from local anesthetic toxicity who recovered after lipid infusion. Its use for this purpose is off-label but is part of anesthesia society guidelines.",
  "timeline": [["1961","Arvid Wretlind develops Intralipid in Sweden."],["1970s","Intralipid becomes available in the US for parenteral nutrition."],["1998","Weinberg and colleagues show lipid emulsion reverses bupivacaine toxicity in rats."],["2006","First human case reports of lipid rescue in local anesthetic cardiac arrest are published."]],
  "cell": "As nutrition, the emulsion's tiny fat droplets behave like natural chylomicrons. Lipoprotein lipase on blood vessel walls breaks the triglycerides into free fatty acids and glycerol. Cells burn the fatty acids for energy or store them, and the emulsion supplies essential fatty acids such as linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid. In poisoning, several mechanisms are proposed. The lipid sink idea is that a large fat phase in the blood soaks up fat-soluble drugs like bupivacaine, pulling them out of heart and brain tissue. The lipid shuttle idea is that it carries the drug to the liver and muscle for storage or breakdown. Fatty acids may also directly fuel heart muscle, whose energy use is blocked by bupivacaine, and may influence calcium handling, improving contractility.",
  "body": [["Heart","In local anesthetic toxicity, it can improve contractions and restore circulation, often within minutes."],["Blood fats","Plasma triglycerides rise sharply, which can interfere with some lab tests."],["Nutrition","As part of parenteral nutrition, it provides calories and essential fatty acids, preventing fatty acid deficiency."],["Liver","Long-term use, especially in infants, can cause fatty liver and cholestasis."],["Pancreas","Very high triglycerides can trigger pancreatitis."],["Lungs","Fat overload can affect lung gas exchange and, rarely, cause an ARDS-like picture."]],
  "journey": "Lipid emulsion is given into a vein, as a bolus and infusion for toxicity or as a slower infusion for nutrition. The droplets circulate like chylomicrons. Lipoprotein lipase breaks them down into fatty acids and glycerol, which are burned for energy in many tissues or stored in fat. Some droplets are taken up by the liver and spleen. Triglycerides clear from the blood with a half-time of about 30 to 60 minutes when the body's clearance is not overwhelmed.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from too much fat in the blood. Very high triglycerides can cause pancreatitis, interfere with lab tests and, with large doses, cause fat overload syndrome with fever, liver enlargement, low platelets and lung problems. Premature infants clear fat slowly, and deaths have been reported from fat buildup in their lungs. Allergic reactions can occur in people allergic to egg or soy. Because it is a rich nutrient, infected IV lines are a concern. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped.",
  "fun": "Intralipid looks like milk for the same reason milk is white: billions of tiny fat droplets scatter light.",
  "src": ["Intralipid (lipid injectable emulsion) FDA label","Weinberg GL et al. Anesthesiology 1998","ASRA Practice Advisory on Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity"]
},
"remifentanil": {
  "origin": "Remifentanil was designed on purpose to wear off fast. In the late 1980s and early 1990s chemists at Glaxo set out to build an opioid as potent as fentanyl but with an ester side chain that blood enzymes would cut quickly. The idea, called soft drug design, was that the drug would be broken down so fast that it would not build up no matter how long it was infused. The result was remifentanil, approved by the FDA in 1996 as Ultiva. It changed anesthesia practice by letting anesthesiologists give deep opioid analgesia during surgery and have patients wake up within minutes after the infusion stopped.",
  "timeline": [["1960","Paul Janssen's team synthesizes fentanyl, the parent of the anilidopiperidine opioids."],["1990s","Glaxo chemists design remifentanil with an ester linkage for rapid breakdown."],["1996","FDA approves remifentanil (Ultiva)."]],
  "cell": "Remifentanil is a selective full agonist at the mu-opioid receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. When it binds, the G protein inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cyclic AMP. The G protein beta-gamma subunits open potassium channels (GIRK), which hyperpolarize neurons, and close voltage-gated calcium channels at nerve terminals, which reduces release of pain transmitters like glutamate and substance P. In the spinal cord dorsal horn and in brain areas such as the periaqueductal gray, this blocks pain signals. In the brainstem it lowers the drive to breathe. Its key feature is the methyl ester side chain, which nonspecific esterases in blood and tissues (not pseudocholinesterase) quickly hydrolyze to a nearly inactive carboxylic acid.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord","Strong pain relief during surgery, which lets less of other anesthetics be used."],["Breathing","Mu receptors in the brainstem reduce the response to carbon dioxide, so breathing slows or stops at higher levels."],["Heart","Increased vagal tone causes slow heart rate, and blood pressure often falls."],["Chest wall muscles","Fast boluses can cause muscle rigidity, making ventilation difficult."],["Gut","Nausea and vomiting are common after anesthesia."],["Pain after surgery","Because it wears off in minutes, patients need a longer-acting pain plan before it stops. Some develop heightened pain sensitivity after high-dose use."]],
  "journey": "Remifentanil is given only by IV infusion or bolus. It reaches the brain in about a minute and acts quickly. Esterases in blood and tissues break it down so fast that its context-sensitive half-time is about three to four minutes, whether it has been running for one hour or ten. This organ-independent breakdown means kidney or liver failure barely changes its offset. The inactive acid metabolite is excreted by the kidneys. Effects fade within about five to ten minutes after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Respiratory depression and apnea come straight from mu-receptor action on brainstem breathing centers. Slow heart rate and low blood pressure come from vagal stimulation and reduced sympathetic tone. Chest wall rigidity occurs with rapid boluses. Because it wears off so fast, patients can wake suddenly in severe pain if other analgesics have not been given. Naloxone reverses its effects, but its short duration often makes that unnecessary once the infusion stops. It contains glycine, so it is not given into the spine.",
  "fun": "Remifentanil is one of the clearest examples of soft drug design: a drug built from the start to be inactivated quickly and predictably by the body.",
  "src": ["Ultiva (remifentanil) FDA label","Miller's Anesthesia","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"sufentanil": {
  "origin": "Sufentanil came from the lab of Paul Janssen at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, which created fentanyl in 1960 and then a series of related opioids. Sufentanil was synthesized in the 1970s as a thienyl analog of fentanyl and proved to be several times more potent, with very stable effects on the heart. The FDA approved it for IV and epidural use as Sufenta in 1984. It became popular in heart surgery and in epidural pain relief during labor. In 2018 the FDA approved a small sublingual tablet, Dsuvia, for acute pain in medically supervised settings, a decision that drew debate because of the potency of the drug.",
  "timeline": [["1960","Paul Janssen's team synthesizes fentanyl."],["1970s","Sufentanil is synthesized at Janssen Pharmaceutica."],["1984","FDA approves sufentanil (Sufenta) injection."],["2018","FDA approves sublingual sufentanil tablet (Dsuvia) for supervised settings."]],
  "cell": "Sufentanil is a highly selective full agonist at mu-opioid receptors. Binding activates Gi/o proteins, which inhibit adenylyl cyclase and lower cyclic AMP. The beta-gamma subunits open GIRK potassium channels, hyperpolarizing neurons, and close voltage-gated calcium channels at presynaptic terminals, reducing release of glutamate and substance P from pain fibers. In the spinal cord, brainstem and higher brain centers, this blocks the transmission and perception of pain. Sufentanil binds mu receptors more tightly than fentanyl and is very fat-soluble, so it reaches the brain and spinal cord quickly. It causes very little histamine release, which helps keep blood pressure stable.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord","Profound pain relief and sedation, used in anesthesia and in epidurals during labor."],["Breathing","Mu receptors in the brainstem reduce the drive to breathe, causing slow breathing or apnea at higher levels."],["Heart","Increased vagal tone slows the heart rate. Because it releases little histamine, blood pressure is generally stable."],["Chest wall muscles","Fast IV doses can cause muscle rigidity that makes ventilation difficult."],["Skin","Itching is common, especially with epidural use, from mu receptors in the spinal cord."],["Newborns","When used in labor, it can cross the placenta and depress the baby's breathing."]],
  "journey": "Sufentanil is given into a vein, into the epidural space or under the tongue. Because it is very fat-soluble, it enters the brain within minutes and then redistributes into fat and muscle, which ends the effect of a single dose in about 30 to 60 minutes. It is about 92 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down through CYP3A4, by N-dealkylation and O-demethylation, and metabolites leave in urine and stool. The elimination half-life is about two and a half to three hours.",
  "sidefx": "Respiratory depression is the main danger, caused by mu-receptor action on the brainstem, and it is worse with other sedatives. Slow heart rate comes from vagal stimulation. Chest wall rigidity occurs with rapid boluses. Itching and urinary retention are especially common with epidural use. Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors can raise its levels and prolong breathing suppression. Naloxone reverses its effects, though repeated naloxone may be needed because sufentanil can outlast it.",
  "fun": "Sufentanil is so potent that the Dsuvia tablet is only about 3 millimeters across.",
  "src": ["Sufenta (sufentanil citrate) FDA label","Dsuvia (sufentanil sublingual tablet) FDA label","Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"nalbuphine": {
  "origin": "Nalbuphine came from a long search for a strong painkiller with less addiction and less breathing suppression than morphine. Chemists built it from the morphine-like phenanthrene skeleton, related to oxymorphone and naloxone, at Endo Laboratories in the 1960s. The goal was a mixed agonist-antagonist: a drug that activates kappa-opioid receptors for pain relief while blocking mu receptors, which drive euphoria and respiratory depression. Nalbuphine was approved in the US in 1979 as Nubain. It is not a federally controlled substance. The Nubain brand has been discontinued, and generic injection remains, used for moderate to severe pain, in labor, and to reverse itching or breathing suppression from mu opioids.",
  "timeline": [["1960s","Nalbuphine is developed at Endo Laboratories."],["1979","FDA approves nalbuphine (Nubain) injection."]],
  "cell": "Nalbuphine acts on two opioid receptors in opposite ways. At kappa-opioid receptors, it is an agonist. Kappa receptors are Gi/o-coupled; activation lowers cyclic AMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels in pain neurons of the spinal cord and brain, producing pain relief and sedation. At mu-opioid receptors it is an antagonist or very weak partial agonist. It binds mu receptors but barely activates them. This creates a ceiling: above a certain level, more drug does not further slow breathing, because the mu receptors that control breathing are not fully activated. In a person taking a full mu agonist such as morphine or fentanyl, nalbuphine displaces it from mu receptors, which can reverse breathing suppression and itching or cause withdrawal in someone dependent on opioids.",
  "body": [["Spinal cord and brain","Kappa activation relieves moderate to severe pain, though it may be less effective for very severe pain than full mu agonists."],["Breathing","Breathing slows but shows a ceiling effect, so it is less likely than full mu agonists to cause severe respiratory depression when used alone."],["Sedation","Sleepiness is the most common side effect, from kappa action in the brain."],["Mood and perception","Kappa activation can cause dysphoria, anxiety or hallucinations, especially at higher levels."],["Heart and blood pressure","It has little effect on heart rate or blood pressure, unlike some other mixed agonist-antagonists."],["Skin","Its mu-blocking effect can relieve itching caused by spinal or epidural morphine."]],
  "journey": "Nalbuphine is given into a vein, muscle or under the skin. By vein it starts working in two to three minutes; by muscle or skin in under 15 minutes. It is not used by mouth because the liver destroys most of it on first pass. It is about half bound to plasma proteins and crosses the placenta. The liver inactivates it mainly by glucuronidation, and metabolites leave in feces and urine. The half-life is about five hours, with pain relief lasting three to six hours.",
  "sidefx": "Sedation, dizziness and sweating come from kappa activity in the brain. Dysphoria and hallucinations also come from kappa receptors, which are linked to aversive feelings. Breathing suppression is limited by the ceiling effect, but it can still be serious when combined with alcohol, benzodiazepines or other sedatives. In people dependent on mu opioids, blocking mu receptors can trigger acute withdrawal. In labor, it can slow the fetal heart rate. Naloxone reverses its effects, although higher amounts may be needed.",
  "fun": "Nalbuphine can be used to treat the itching that morphine causes, by blocking mu receptors just enough while still providing its own kappa-based pain relief.",
  "src": ["Nalbuphine hydrochloride injection FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"methohexital": {
  "origin": "Barbiturates were first made in the late 1800s and early 1900s, and in 1934 John Lundy at the Mayo Clinic introduced thiopental, the first widely used IV anesthetic, which put patients to sleep in seconds. Chemists then looked for an even shorter-acting barbiturate. Methohexital, an oxybarbiturate (it has an oxygen where thiopental has a sulfur), was developed by Eli Lilly and introduced as Brevital in the late 1950s. It wakes patients faster than thiopental. Unlike most barbiturates, it can lower the seizure threshold, which made it the classic anesthetic for electroconvulsive therapy (ECT). After thiopental left the US market around 2011, methohexital became the main barbiturate anesthetic still available.",
  "timeline": [["1934","John Lundy introduces thiopental at the Mayo Clinic."],["1950s","Eli Lilly introduces methohexital (Brevital)."],["2011","Thiopental is no longer available in the US, leaving methohexital as the main barbiturate anesthetic."]],
  "cell": "Methohexital binds a barbiturate site on the GABA-A receptor, a chloride channel made of five subunits. At low concentrations it is a positive allosteric modulator: it makes the channel stay open longer each time GABA binds (benzodiazepines instead increase how often it opens). More chloride flows in and the neuron is hyperpolarized and harder to fire. At higher concentrations, methohexital opens the channel directly even without GABA, which is why barbiturates can cause much deeper depression than benzodiazepines. It also blocks AMPA glutamate receptors, cutting excitatory signaling. The result is quick loss of consciousness. It wakes the patient fast because it redistributes from the brain to muscle and fat within minutes.",
  "body": [["Brain","Consciousness is lost within about a minute, and the patient wakes in five to seven minutes after a single dose as the drug leaves the brain."],["Seizure threshold","Unlike thiopental, it can lower the seizure threshold and cause twitching or seizures, which makes it useful for ECT."],["Breathing","Breathing slows or stops briefly because GABA signaling suppresses the brainstem breathing centers."],["Heart and vessels","Veins dilate and the heart pumps less forcefully, so blood pressure falls and the heart rate rises as a reflex."],["Airway","It can trigger hiccups, coughing, laryngospasm or bronchospasm."],["Blood vessels at injection","Injection can be painful, and accidental injection into an artery can cause severe tissue damage."]],
  "journey": "Methohexital is usually given into a vein, though it can be given into muscle or rectally, mostly in children. It is very fat-soluble and passes into the brain within seconds. It then quickly moves from the brain to muscle and fat, which ends the effect of a single dose in minutes. The liver slowly breaks it down by demethylation and oxidation, and metabolites leave in the urine. Its elimination half-life is about four hours, much shorter than thiopental's, so repeated doses build up less.",
  "sidefx": "Respiratory depression and apnea come from GABA-A enhancement in the brainstem. Low blood pressure comes from vein dilation and reduced heart contraction. Excitatory effects such as muscle twitching, hiccups and seizures are linked to its effects on the cortex and are unusual for a barbiturate. Barbiturates induce the liver enzyme ALA synthase and can trigger attacks of acute porphyria, so they are avoided in porphyria. Injection into an artery can cause severe vasospasm and gangrene. There is no reversal agent; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Methohexital is one of the few anesthetics that can make seizures more likely, the opposite of most barbiturates, which is why it became the gold standard for ECT.",
  "src": ["Brevital (methohexital) FDA label","Miller's Anesthesia","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"chloroprocaine": {
  "origin": "Chloroprocaine is a close cousin of procaine, the first widely used synthetic local anesthetic, which Alfred Einhorn created in 1904 and which was introduced in 1905. Adding a chlorine atom to procaine's ring made a drug that is broken down in the blood even faster. Chloroprocaine was introduced in 1952 as Nesacaine. It is the shortest-acting ester local anesthetic, which makes it useful for epidural anesthesia in labor and for short procedures. Early epidural formulations with preservatives were linked to nerve injury and back pain, which led to preservative-free versions. In 2017 the FDA approved a preservative-free spinal form, Clorotekal, for short surgeries.",
  "timeline": [["1904","Alfred Einhorn synthesizes procaine."],["1952","Chloroprocaine is introduced as Nesacaine."],["1980s","Nerve injury reports with older formulations lead to preservative-free products."],["2017","FDA approves a spinal form of chloroprocaine (Clorotekal)."]],
  "cell": "Chloroprocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve fibers. The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane; inside the cell, it becomes charged and binds a site within the channel pore. It binds most strongly when the channel is open or inactivated, so it blocks busy nerves best. When sodium cannot rush in, the nerve cannot fire an action potential. Small fibers, such as sympathetic fibers and pain and temperature fibers (C and A-delta), are blocked first, followed by touch and then motor fibers. The ester bond is quickly hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase into para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) and 2-chloro-4-aminobenzoic acid, which is why its effects end fast and its systemic toxicity is low.",
  "body": [["Nerves in the blocked area","Pain, temperature, touch and then movement are lost, allowing surgery or pain relief in labor."],["Sympathetic nerves","Neuraxial block relaxes blood vessels in the lower body, so blood pressure can drop."],["Fetus","Because it is broken down within seconds in the blood, very little reaches the baby during labor."],["Heart and brain","If enough reaches the blood, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity, but its quick breakdown makes this less likely than with amide drugs."],["Blood","Very rarely it can cause methemoglobinemia."]],
  "journey": "Chloroprocaine is injected near nerves, into the epidural space or into the spinal fluid. It works within about 6 to 12 minutes and lasts about 30 to 60 minutes. Whatever reaches the blood is broken down by pseudocholinesterase extremely quickly, with a plasma half-life of only about 20 seconds in adults. The breakdown products leave in the urine. People with pseudocholinesterase deficiency break it down more slowly.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure in neuraxial use comes from blocking sympathetic nerves. Back pain was linked to older epidural formulations containing EDTA. If injected into a vessel, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity, with seizures and heart rhythm problems, though rapid breakdown limits this. Allergic reactions are more common with esters than amides because they form PABA, a known allergen. Methemoglobinemia is rare. LAST is treated with lipid emulsion and supportive care, and methemoglobinemia with methylene blue.",
  "fun": "Chloroprocaine's half-life in blood is measured in seconds, which makes it one of the safest local anesthetics for a mother and baby during labor.",
  "src": ["Nesacaine (chloroprocaine) FDA label","Clorotekal FDA label","Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"mepivacaine": {
  "origin": "After lidocaine was introduced in Sweden in the late 1940s, Swedish chemists kept exploring the amide family of local anesthetics. In 1956 Bo af Ekenstam and colleagues at the Swedish company AB Bofors made a series of pipecolyl xylidide compounds. Mepivacaine was the first to reach patients, introduced in 1957. The same series later produced bupivacaine and ropivacaine. Mepivacaine causes less vasodilation than lidocaine, so it can be used without epinephrine, which made it popular in dentistry for patients who should avoid epinephrine. It is sold as Carbocaine, Polocaine and Scandonest.",
  "timeline": [["1948","Lidocaine is introduced in Sweden as the first amide local anesthetic."],["1956","Bo af Ekenstam's group synthesizes mepivacaine and related compounds."],["1957","Mepivacaine is introduced into clinical use."]],
  "cell": "Mepivacaine is an amide local anesthetic that blocks voltage-gated sodium channels. The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane. Inside the nerve, it picks up a proton, and the charged form binds a site inside the channel pore from the inner side, mostly in the open and inactivated states. Blocked channels stop sodium from entering, so the nerve cannot depolarize and no action potential is sent. Small pain and temperature fibers are blocked first, then touch and motor fibers. Mepivacaine has less direct effect on the smooth muscle of blood vessels than lidocaine, so it causes less vasodilation. That keeps it at the site longer, which is why it works reasonably well even without epinephrine.",
  "body": [["Nerves in the blocked area","Pain and touch are lost in the area, allowing dental or minor surgery."],["Mouth and lips (dental use)","Soft tissue numbness can last two to three hours, so children may bite their lips or cheeks without noticing."],["Blood vessels","Less vessel widening than lidocaine means it stays near the nerve longer without epinephrine."],["Brain","If blood levels get too high, it can cause dizziness, ringing in the ears and seizures."],["Heart","Very high levels can slow conduction and depress the heart."]],
  "journey": "Mepivacaine is injected near nerves, such as in the mouth for dental work. It works within about three to five minutes in dental use. Some is absorbed into the blood, where it binds proteins. The liver breaks it down by N-demethylation and hydroxylation, and metabolites, plus a small unchanged amount, leave in the urine. The half-life is about two to three hours in adults, but around nine hours in newborns, whose livers are immature.",
  "sidefx": "Numbness that outlasts the procedure is a direct result of nerve block, and lip biting is a concern in children. If too much reaches the blood, sodium channel block in the brain causes local anesthetic systemic toxicity: early signs like dizziness and a metallic taste, then seizures, and at higher levels heart rhythm problems and cardiac arrest. Allergy to amide anesthetics is rare. Methemoglobinemia can occur rarely. LAST is treated with lipid emulsion and supportive care.",
  "fun": "Mepivacaine is often called the dentist's choice for patients who should avoid epinephrine, because it numbs well enough on its own.",
  "src": ["Carbocaine (mepivacaine) FDA label","Scandonest FDA label","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ropivacaine": {
  "origin": "Ropivacaine was designed in response to a safety problem. Bupivacaine, introduced in the 1960s, gives long-lasting nerve block but is very toxic to the heart, and in 1979 an editorial by George Albright described cardiac arrests that were hard to resuscitate after bupivacaine. Researchers at the Swedish company Astra turned to a close relative from the same pipecolyl xylidide series made by Bo af Ekenstam in the 1950s. They developed it as a pure S-enantiomer, because the S form of these drugs is less toxic to the heart, and with a propyl side chain instead of bupivacaine's butyl. The result was ropivacaine, approved by the FDA in 1996 as Naropin. It is widely used for epidurals and nerve blocks.",
  "timeline": [["1957","Bo af Ekenstam's group synthesizes the pipecolyl xylidide family that includes ropivacaine."],["1979","Albright warns of cardiac arrest after bupivacaine, spurring safer alternatives."],["1996","FDA approves ropivacaine (Naropin)."]],
  "cell": "Ropivacaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from the inside of the nerve. The uncharged drug crosses the membrane, becomes charged and binds within the channel pore when it is open or inactivated. Sodium entry stops and the nerve cannot fire. Ropivacaine is less fat-soluble than bupivacaine because of its shorter side chain. It enters thin, unmyelinated pain fibers well but penetrates the thick myelinated motor fibers less, so at low concentrations it blocks pain more than movement (sensory-motor separation). In the heart, the pure S-enantiomer binds cardiac sodium channels less tightly and leaves them faster than the R form in bupivacaine, which lowers its cardiotoxicity.",
  "body": [["Nerves in the blocked area","Long-lasting pain relief lets it be used for surgery and for hours of pain control after surgery."],["Motor nerves","At low concentrations, patients can often still move their legs, helpful for labor epidurals."],["Sympathetic nerves","Neuraxial block relaxes blood vessels and can lower blood pressure."],["Heart","It is less cardiotoxic than bupivacaine, but high blood levels can still cause rhythm problems and cardiac arrest."],["Brain","High blood levels can cause dizziness, tinnitus, a metallic taste and seizures."],["Blood vessels at the site","It causes mild vasoconstriction at low concentrations, which helps it stay near the nerve."]],
  "journey": "Ropivacaine is injected near nerves, into the epidural space or into tissue. Onset is about 10 to 30 minutes, and effect lasts hours, up to about 10 hours for major nerve blocks. Absorbed drug is about 94 percent bound to plasma proteins, mainly alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP1A2 to 3-hydroxy-ropivacaine, with a minor CYP3A4 role, and metabolites leave in the urine. The half-life is about two hours by vein and about four hours after epidural use, because absorption from the site is slow.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure and slow heart rate with neuraxial use come from sympathetic block. If too much reaches the blood, sodium channel block in the brain and heart causes local anesthetic systemic toxicity: early agitation and tinnitus, then seizures, and then arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, though the risk is lower than with bupivacaine. Strong CYP1A2 inhibitors such as fluvoxamine can raise levels. LAST is treated with lipid emulsion and supportive care.",
  "fun": "Ropivacaine is one of the first local anesthetics developed as a single mirror-image form specifically to make it safer for the heart.",
  "src": ["Naropin (ropivacaine) FDA label","Albright GA. Anesthesiology 1979","Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"articaine": {
  "origin": "Articaine was made in 1969 by the chemist H. Rusching and colleagues at the German company Hoechst, under the name carticaine. It is unusual among amide local anesthetics because it has a thiophene ring instead of a benzene ring and an extra ester group. It entered dental practice in Germany in 1976 and became the most used dental anesthetic in much of Europe. In the US, the FDA approved articaine with epinephrine as Septocaine in 2000. It is now one of the most common dental anesthetics in the US as well, valued for fast onset and good spread through bone.",
  "timeline": [["1969","Articaine (carticaine) is synthesized at Hoechst in Germany."],["1976","Articaine enters dental use in Germany."],["2000","FDA approves articaine with epinephrine (Septocaine)."]],
  "cell": "Articaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from the inside of the nerve, the same way other amide anesthetics do. Its thiophene ring makes it more fat-soluble, helping it spread through bone and soft tissue to reach dental nerves. Once inside the nerve, the charged form binds the channel pore, stopping sodium entry and preventing action potentials, so pain signals from the tooth cannot reach the brain. Articaine also has an ester side chain. Plasma carboxylesterases cut this chain quickly, turning about 90 percent of the drug into inactive articainic acid. The epinephrine in the cartridge stimulates alpha-1 receptors on nearby blood vessels, narrowing them and slowing removal of the anesthetic, which prolongs numbness and reduces bleeding.",
  "body": [["Teeth and jaw","Fast, deep numbness of the teeth, allowing fillings, extractions and root canals."],["Gums and soft tissue","Soft tissue numbness lasts several hours, longer than the numbness of the tooth itself."],["Blood vessels","Epinephrine narrows local vessels, reducing bleeding in the surgical area."],["Heart","Some epinephrine reaches the blood, which can raise heart rate and blood pressure."],["Nerves","Rarely, numbness or tingling can persist for weeks after a lower jaw nerve block."],["Blood","Very rarely, it can cause methemoglobinemia."]],
  "journey": "Articaine is injected into the mouth near the target nerve. Onset is about one to six minutes. Pulpal (tooth) anesthesia lasts about an hour, and soft tissue numbness lasts longer. Absorbed articaine is broken down quickly in the blood by carboxylesterases, mainly to articainic acid, with only a small amount handled by the liver. Metabolites leave in the urine. Its half-life is short, about 20 to 45 minutes, so systemic levels fall quickly.",
  "sidefx": "Pain and swelling at the injection site come from the injection itself. Prolonged numbness or tingling may reflect local nerve irritation; some studies suggest it is more common with higher-concentration anesthetics in lower jaw blocks, though this is debated. If injected into a vessel or given in excess, it can cause local anesthetic systemic toxicity with seizures and arrhythmias. Epinephrine can cause fast heart rate, high blood pressure and rhythm problems in sensitive patients. The product contains sulfite as a preservative for epinephrine, which can trigger reactions in sulfite-sensitive people.",
  "fun": "Because blood enzymes break it down so fast, articaine's half-life is much shorter than that of lidocaine.",
  "src": ["Septocaine (articaine and epinephrine) FDA label","Malamed SF. Handbook of Local Anesthesia","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"atracurium": {
  "origin": "Muscle relaxants for surgery trace back to curare, the arrow poison used by Indigenous peoples of South America. Its active part, tubocurarine, was first used in anesthesia in 1942. Early relaxants depended on the liver and kidneys for removal, which caused problems in very sick patients. In the 1970s and early 1980s John Stenlake and colleagues at the University of Strathclyde in Glasgow designed atracurium to break down on its own in the body, using a chemical reaction called Hofmann elimination that happens at normal body temperature and pH. Developed with Burroughs Wellcome, it was approved in the US in 1983 as Tracrium. Its success led to cisatracurium, a purified isomer. The Tracrium brand has been discontinued, and generic atracurium remains.",
  "timeline": [["1942","Harold Griffith and Enid Johnson first use curare in anesthesia in Montreal."],["1970s","John Stenlake's group designs atracurium to undergo Hofmann elimination."],["1983","FDA approves atracurium (Tracrium)."],["1995","Cisatracurium, a single isomer of atracurium, is approved."]],
  "cell": "At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine released from the nerve binds nicotinic receptors on the muscle end plate. These receptors are ion channels made of two alpha, one beta, one delta and one epsilon subunit; acetylcholine must bind both alpha subunits to open them. Sodium rushes in and the muscle contracts. Atracurium is a bulky benzylisoquinolinium compound that competes with acetylcholine for the alpha sites but does not open the channel, so the end plate cannot depolarize and the muscle relaxes (nondepolarizing block). Atracurium breaks down by Hofmann elimination, a spontaneous chemical reaction at body pH and temperature, and by ester hydrolysis in plasma. Both produce laudanosine and a quaternary acrylate. Cholinesterase inhibitors such as neostigmine raise acetylcholine to outcompete it.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle","Muscles relax and become limp, allowing intubation and surgery. Small muscles such as the eyes are affected first, then limbs, then the diaphragm."],["Breathing","The diaphragm and chest muscles are paralyzed, so breathing must be supported."],["Skin and blood vessels","Atracurium releases histamine from mast cells, causing flushing and a drop in blood pressure, especially with rapid or large doses."],["Airways","Histamine can cause bronchospasm, a concern in people with asthma."],["Brain","It does not affect awareness or pain, so patients must be fully sedated before paralysis."],["Liver and kidneys","Because its breakdown does not depend on these organs, it can be used in liver or kidney failure."]],
  "journey": "Atracurium is given only into a vein. Paralysis begins in about two minutes and peaks in three to five minutes. Its breakdown happens in the plasma by Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, with a half-life of about 20 minutes, regardless of liver or kidney function. Effects last about 20 to 35 minutes. The metabolite laudanosine is cleared by the liver and kidneys, and it can build up with long infusions.",
  "sidefx": "Flushing, low blood pressure and fast heart rate come from histamine released from mast cells, which is why it is given slowly. Bronchospasm is also histamine-related. Prolonged weakness can follow long use in critical illness. Laudanosine can cross into the brain and, at high levels in animals, cause seizures, though this is rarely a problem in people. Because it does not affect consciousness, awareness under paralysis can happen if sedation is inadequate. Anaphylaxis is a known risk with neuromuscular blockers. Neostigmine with an antimuscarinic can reverse the block once some recovery has begun.",
  "fun": "Atracurium's breakdown is pure chemistry. Like a timer, it falls apart on its own at body temperature, which is why the vials are kept in the refrigerator.",
  "src": ["Atracurium besylate injection FDA label","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics","Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"remimazolam": {
  "origin": "Remimazolam was built to do for benzodiazepines what remifentanil did for opioids. In the 1990s scientists at Glaxo Wellcome added an ester side chain to a midazolam-like structure so that tissue esterases would quickly turn it into an inactive compound. The idea was a sedative that wears off fast and predictably. Glaxo did not develop it further, and the program was later picked up by other companies, including the German company PAION. After trials in procedural sedation, the FDA approved it as Byfavo in 2020 for sedation during procedures lasting 30 minutes or less, such as colonoscopy and bronchoscopy. It is a Schedule IV controlled substance.",
  "timeline": [["1990s","Glaxo Wellcome scientists design remimazolam as an ester-based soft benzodiazepine."],["2000s","Development moves to other companies, including PAION."],["2020","FDA approves remimazolam (Byfavo) for procedural sedation."]],
  "cell": "Remimazolam binds the benzodiazepine site on the GABA-A receptor, at the interface between the alpha and gamma subunits. It is a positive allosteric modulator: it does not open the channel by itself, but it increases how often the channel opens when GABA binds. More chloride enters the neuron, hyperpolarizing it and making it less likely to fire. This produces sedation, anxiety relief and memory loss for the procedure, but no pain relief. Its key feature is a carboxylic ester side chain. Carboxylesterase-1, mainly in the liver, hydrolyzes it to CNS7054, an inactive metabolite with much weaker binding. Because this breakdown is fast and does not depend on CYP enzymes, its effect ends quickly.",
  "body": [["Brain","Rapid sedation and anterograde amnesia let patients tolerate procedures and not remember them."],["Breathing","GABA effects on breathing centers can slow breathing or cause low oxygen, especially with opioids."],["Heart and blood pressure","Blood pressure can fall, though in trials this was often less marked than with propofol."],["Recovery","Most patients are alert again within about 10 to 20 minutes after the last dose."],["Pain","It has no pain-relieving action, so opioids such as fentanyl are usually given with it."]],
  "journey": "Remimazolam is given only into a vein. It begins working in one to three minutes, and peak sedation comes about three minutes after a dose. It is more than 91 percent bound to albumin. Carboxylesterase-1 in the liver and other tissues breaks it down quickly to inactive CNS7054, which leaves mainly in the urine. The half-life is about 37 to 53 minutes. Severe liver disease can slow recovery because less carboxylesterase is available.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure, low oxygen and slow breathing come from GABA-A enhancement in the brainstem, and these effects add up with opioids and other sedatives. Prolonged sedation can occur in severe liver impairment. Because the formulation contains dextran 40, people allergic to dextran should avoid it. Flumazenil, a benzodiazepine antagonist, can reverse sedation, though its own short action may require repeat doses.",
  "fun": "Remimazolam's design shows how adding a single ester group can turn a familiar drug into one with a whole new pharmacokinetic profile.",
  "src": ["Byfavo (remimazolam) FDA label","Kilpatrick GJ et al. Anesthesiology 2007","Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"ephedrine": {
  "origin": "Ephedrine comes from Ephedra plants, known in Chinese medicine as ma huang, which have been used for thousands of years for cough, asthma and colds. In 1885 the Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi isolated ephedrine from the plant. Its medical value was recognized in 1924 when K. K. Chen and Carl Schmidt at Peking Union Medical College showed that it acted much like adrenaline but lasted longer and worked by mouth. It became a major asthma drug before modern inhalers. Ephedra supplements were banned in the US in 2004 after reports of heart attacks and strokes, and oral ephedrine is tightly regulated because it can be used to make methamphetamine. Injectable ephedrine, including FDA-approved Akovaz since 2016, is mainly used to treat low blood pressure during anesthesia.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient","Ephedra (ma huang) is used in Chinese medicine for breathing problems."],["1885","Nagai Nagayoshi isolates ephedrine from Ephedra."],["1924","Chen and Schmidt describe ephedrine's adrenaline-like effects."],["2004","FDA bans dietary supplements containing ephedra alkaloids."],["2016","FDA approves ephedrine sulfate injection (Akovaz)."]],
  "cell": "Ephedrine acts in two ways. Directly, it is an agonist at alpha-1, beta-1 and beta-2 adrenergic receptors. Alpha-1 activation (Gq) narrows blood vessels. Beta-1 activation (Gs) raises cyclic AMP in heart muscle, increasing heart rate and force. Beta-2 activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle. Indirectly, ephedrine is taken up into sympathetic nerve endings by the norepinephrine transporter and into vesicles by VMAT, where it displaces stored norepinephrine, which leaks out and stimulates receptors. This indirect action means repeated doses work less and less (tachyphylaxis) as norepinephrine stores run low. It also works poorly in people whose stores are depleted, such as those taking reserpine.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","Alpha-1 stimulation narrows vessels, raising blood pressure."],["Heart","Beta-1 stimulation increases heart rate and contractility, raising cardiac output."],["Lungs","Beta-2 stimulation relaxes airway muscle, which is why it was once a common asthma drug."],["Brain","It crosses into the brain and causes alertness, restlessness, anxiety and insomnia."],["Uterus and placenta","In obstetric anesthesia, it raises blood pressure while generally preserving blood flow to the uterus."],["Bladder","Alpha effects on the bladder outlet can cause urinary retention."]],
  "journey": "Injectable ephedrine is given into a vein and acts within about a minute. It is not broken down by MAO or COMT, unlike adrenaline, which is why it lasts longer and works by mouth. A small amount is demethylated by the liver, but most is excreted unchanged by the kidneys. Acidic urine speeds its removal. The half-life is about three to six hours, and the pressor effect of a single IV dose lasts about 10 to 60 minutes.",
  "sidefx": "Fast heart rate, palpitations and high blood pressure come from beta-1 and alpha-1 stimulation. Arrhythmias and heart ischemia can follow when the heart works harder than its blood supply allows. CNS stimulation causes restlessness, anxiety and insomnia. Tachyphylaxis means repeated doses become less effective. With MAO inhibitors, released norepinephrine is not broken down, which can cause a hypertensive crisis. There is no specific antidote; treatment is supportive, with short-acting vasodilators or beta blockers for severe effects.",
  "fun": "Ephedrine's structure is so close to methamphetamine that ephedrine and pseudoephedrine products are regulated as List I chemicals to prevent illicit drug production.",
  "src": ["Akovaz (ephedrine sulfate) FDA label","Chen KK, Schmidt CF. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1924","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"albumin-human": {
  "origin": "Albumin therapy was born during World War II. The US military needed a stable blood product that could be shipped to battlefields without refrigeration. At Harvard, Edwin Cohn developed cold ethanol fractionation, a method to separate plasma into its protein parts, including albumin, by carefully changing temperature, alcohol and acidity. Albumin was heated to kill viruses, which made it one of the safest plasma products. It was used to treat burned and wounded sailors after the attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941. Today many brands, including Albutein, Plasbumin, AlbuRx and Flexbumin, are used for volume replacement, low albumin states, burns, liver disease and large-volume paracentesis.",
  "timeline": [["1940","Edwin Cohn begins large-scale plasma fractionation work at Harvard."],["1941","Albumin is used to treat casualties after the Pearl Harbor attack."],["1940s","Heat treatment of albumin is introduced to inactivate viruses."]],
  "cell": "Albumin is a single protein chain of about 66.5 kDa, made in the liver. It is too large to pass easily through normal capillary walls. It creates most of the colloid oncotic pressure in plasma, the force that pulls water back into vessels and balances the hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out (Starling forces). Giving 5 percent albumin, which has an oncotic pressure similar to plasma, adds volume roughly equal to the amount given. Giving 25 percent albumin draws extra water from the tissues into the blood, about three to four times its own volume. Albumin also binds and carries many substances, including bilirubin, fatty acids, calcium, hormones and drugs. In septic or burned patients with leaky capillaries, albumin escapes into the tissues and the effect is shorter.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","More fluid stays in the vessels, raising blood volume, blood pressure and cardiac output."],["Tissues","In low-albumin states, 25 percent albumin can pull fluid out of swollen tissues."],["Liver disease","After large-volume paracentesis, albumin helps prevent circulatory problems and kidney injury in cirrhosis."],["Kidneys","Better blood volume improves blood flow to the kidneys in some patients."],["Lungs","Too much volume can overload the circulation and cause pulmonary edema, especially in heart failure."],["Drug binding","Albumin carries many drugs, so its level affects how much free drug is active in the blood."]],
  "journey": "Albumin is given into a vein. It stays mainly in the blood at first and then slowly moves into the tissue fluid, with about 40 percent of the body's albumin inside vessels at any time. It returns to the blood through the lymphatic system. It is not cleared by the kidneys under normal conditions. Cells throughout the body take it up and break it down into amino acids. Its half-life is about 15 to 20 days.",
  "sidefx": "Fever, chills and hives come from rare reactions to the protein or trace contaminants. Circulatory overload and pulmonary edema happen because albumin pulls fluid into vessels, especially with 25 percent albumin in people with weak hearts. Because it is made from heat-treated plasma, the risk of transmitting viruses is extremely low. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, are rare. There is no antidote; overload is treated with diuretics and supportive care.",
  "fun": "Albumin is the most abundant protein in human plasma. Its name comes from the Latin albus, meaning white, the same root as albumen, the white of an egg.",
  "src": ["Albutein (albumin human) FDA label","Plasbumin FDA label","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"hetastarch": {
  "origin": "Hetastarch is a man-made colloid built from amylopectin, the branched starch in waxy corn. Plain starch is broken down in minutes by amylase in the blood, so chemists attached hydroxyethyl groups to the glucose units to slow that breakdown and keep the molecules in the vessels longer. Hydroxyethyl starch was studied as a plasma substitute in the 1960s, and hetastarch (Hespan) reached the US market in the early 1970s as a cheaper, shelf-stable alternative to albumin. Hextend, hetastarch in a balanced electrolyte solution, was approved in 1999. Large trials later linked hydroxyethyl starches to kidney injury, bleeding and higher death rates in critically ill and septic patients. The FDA added a boxed warning in 2013 and in 2021 strengthened it to say these products should not be used unless adequate alternatives are unavailable.",
  "timeline": [["1960s","Hydroxyethyl starch is studied as a synthetic plasma volume expander."],["1970s","Hetastarch (Hespan) becomes available in the US."],["1999","Hextend, hetastarch in a lactated electrolyte solution, is approved."],["2013","FDA adds a boxed warning on mortality, kidney injury and bleeding."],["2021","FDA strengthens warnings to limit use to when alternatives are unavailable."]],
  "cell": "Hetastarch molecules are large, highly branched starch chains with an average size of about 600 kDa. Hydroxyethyl groups on the glucose units block amylase from cutting the chains quickly. Like albumin, these large molecules stay in the blood and create colloid oncotic pressure that holds water inside vessels, expanding blood volume. Amylase slowly trims large molecules into smaller ones, which the kidneys filter out. The largest pieces are taken up by macrophages and other cells and stored in tissues such as the liver, skin and kidney tubules for a long time. Large starch molecules also lower factor VIII and von Willebrand factor levels and coat platelets, which impairs clotting. Uptake into kidney tubule cells causes osmotic swelling and injury.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","Blood volume increases and lasts about a day or more, raising blood pressure and cardiac output in hypovolemia."],["Blood clotting","Lower factor VIII and von Willebrand factor and impaired platelets prolong bleeding, especially after large volumes."],["Kidneys","Starch taken up by tubule cells causes swelling (osmotic nephrosis) and acute kidney injury, especially in sepsis."],["Skin","Starch stored in skin nerves can cause severe itching that starts weeks later and lasts months."],["Pancreas lab tests","Serum amylase rises because amylase binds to starch, which can falsely suggest pancreatitis."],["Whole body","In septic and critically ill patients, trials showed a higher risk of death."]],
  "journey": "Hetastarch is infused into a vein, where it acts at once. Its volume effect lasts about a day or more. Amylase in the blood breaks large molecules into smaller ones. Smaller pieces are filtered by the kidneys into the urine within days, while the largest molecules are taken up by tissues and released very slowly, so traces can remain for weeks or longer. That long tissue storage explains delayed itching.",
  "sidefx": "Bleeding comes from dilution of clotting factors, lower factor VIII and von Willebrand factor, and coated platelets. Kidney injury comes from starch uptake into tubule cells, causing osmotic swelling. Itching comes from starch stored in skin nerves. These effects explain the boxed warning against use in critically ill adults, including sepsis, unless no adequate alternative exists. Anaphylactoid reactions can occur. There is no antidote; it is stopped and the patient is supported.",
  "fun": "Hetastarch is made from the starch of waxy corn, the same kind of starch used to thicken foods.",
  "src": ["Hespan (hetastarch) FDA label","FDA Safety Communication on hydroxyethyl starch products, 2013 and 2021","Myburgh JA et al. NEJM 2012 (CHEST trial)"]
},
"dextran-40": {
  "origin": "Dextran is a sugar polymer made by bacteria. In the 1800s sugar refiners were plagued by slimy, gummy masses in their syrups, which turned out to be dextran made by the bacterium Leuconostoc mesenteroides. In the 1940s Swedish scientists Anders Gronwall and Bjorn Ingelman at Uppsala University showed that partly broken-down dextran could be used as a plasma substitute, and a larger dextran product (Macrodex) became available in the late 1940s. A smaller version with an average size of about 40 kDa, low molecular weight dextran, was introduced later to improve blood flow in small vessels. Dextran 40 (LMD) is used for plasma volume expansion and as an adjunct to prevent clots in some surgeries.",
  "timeline": [["1800s","Sugar refiners notice slimy dextran masses made by bacteria."],["1940s","Gronwall and Ingelman develop dextran as a plasma substitute in Sweden."],["1960s","Low molecular weight dextran 40 comes into use for improving microcirculation."]],
  "cell": "Dextran 40 is a branched chain of glucose units linked mainly by alpha-1,6 bonds, made by bacterial fermentation of sugar. The chains are large enough to stay in the blood at first and create colloid oncotic pressure, which holds water in the vessels and expands blood volume. Because many molecules are small enough to be filtered by the kidneys, the effect is shorter than with larger colloids. Dextran also coats red cells, platelets and the vessel wall. This makes red cells less likely to stack together, lowers blood viscosity, reduces platelet sticking and lowers factor VIII activity. Together these effects improve flow in small vessels and make clots less likely.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","Blood volume increases quickly, with the effect lasting several hours."],["Microcirculation","Lower viscosity and less red cell clumping improve blood flow through small vessels."],["Blood clotting","Coated platelets and lower factor VIII reduce clotting and prolong bleeding time."],["Kidneys","The kidneys filter most of it. In dehydration, dextran can concentrate in the tubules and cause kidney failure."],["Lab tests","Dextran can interfere with blood typing and crossmatching, so samples are drawn before it is given."]],
  "journey": "Dextran 40 is given into a vein and acts at once. Its half-life is about two to three hours. About 70 percent of a dose is filtered by the kidneys within a day. Larger molecules are taken up by tissues and slowly broken down by dextranase enzymes into glucose, which the body then uses. The volume effect lasts about six hours, shorter than albumin.",
  "sidefx": "Severe anaphylactoid reactions happen because many people have antibodies against bacterial dextrans from earlier exposure; these antibodies form immune complexes when dextran is infused. Bleeding risk comes from its antiplatelet and anti-factor VIII effects. Kidney failure can occur when highly concentrated dextran in the tubules raises urine viscosity and injures tubule cells, especially in dehydration. Fluid overload is possible in heart failure. There is no specific reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Dextran molecules form the same gooey film that bacteria use to stick to teeth, contributing to dental plaque.",
  "src": ["LMD (dextran 40) FDA label","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics","Gronwall A, Ingelman B. Acta Physiol Scand 1944"]
},
"lactated-ringers": {
  "origin": "In the early 1880s Sydney Ringer, a physician in London, was studying how frog hearts beat outside the body in a salt solution. His hearts kept beating well, until he realized his lab assistant had been using London tap water, which contained calcium and potassium. When distilled water was used, the hearts stopped. This accident led Ringer to show that calcium and potassium in the right balance are essential for heart contraction, and Ringer's solution was born. In 1932 the American pediatrician Alexis Hartmann added sodium lactate to treat acidosis in sick children, creating lactated Ringer's, also called Hartmann's solution. Today it is one of the most used IV fluids in trauma, surgery and burns.",
  "timeline": [["1882","Sydney Ringer begins publishing studies of salt solutions on frog hearts."],["1883","Ringer reports that calcium and potassium are needed for heart contraction."],["1932","Alexis Hartmann adds lactate to Ringer's solution to treat acidosis."]],
  "cell": "Lactated Ringer's is an isotonic crystalloid with sodium, potassium, calcium, chloride and lactate in amounts close to plasma. Because it has a similar effective osmolality to plasma, it does not shift water into or out of cells. It distributes throughout the fluid outside cells: the small ions pass freely through capillary walls, so within about 30 to 60 minutes only about a quarter of an infused volume stays in the blood vessels and the rest moves into the tissue fluid. The lactate is taken up mainly by the liver and converted through pyruvate to glucose or carbon dioxide, consuming a hydrogen ion and generating bicarbonate. Because it has less chloride than normal saline, it causes less hyperchloremic acidosis.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","Volume increases at first, raising preload, cardiac output and blood pressure in dehydration or blood loss."],["Tissue fluid","Most of the fluid moves into the tissues within an hour, so large volumes cause edema."],["Acid-base balance","Lactate becomes bicarbonate in the liver, so it causes less acidosis than normal saline."],["Kidneys","Better blood volume improves kidney blood flow and urine output."],["Blood transfusions","Its calcium can bind citrate in stored blood and cause clots, so it is not mixed directly with some blood products."],["Lab lactate","Lactate from the fluid is quickly cleared with normal liver function and does not usually cause lactic acidosis."]],
  "journey": "Lactated Ringer's is given into a vein. It reaches all of the fluid outside cells within about 30 to 60 minutes, with about a quarter staying in the vessels. The liver converts lactate to bicarbonate within about 20 minutes in healthy people. The kidneys excrete the extra water and electrolytes over hours.",
  "sidefx": "Fluid overload, swelling and pulmonary edema happen because most of the infused fluid moves into the tissues and lungs, especially in heart or kidney failure. Its sodium is a little lower than plasma, so large volumes can lower sodium. Its potassium can add to high potassium in severe kidney failure. Calcium can react with citrate in blood products and with some drugs, such as ceftriaxone in newborns. Severe liver failure slows lactate conversion. Allergic reactions are very rare.",
  "fun": "One of medicine's most common IV fluids exists because a lab assistant used tap water instead of distilled water.",
  "src": ["Lactated Ringer's Injection USP FDA label","Ringer S. J Physiol 1883","Hartmann AF, Senn MJE. J Clin Invest 1932"]
},
"plasma-lyte": {
  "origin": "Plasma-Lyte belongs to the family of balanced crystalloids that began with Sydney Ringer's salt solutions in the 1880s and Alexis Hartmann's lactated Ringer's in 1932. Later, Baxter developed Multiple Electrolytes Injection Type 1, sold as Plasma-Lyte A and Plasma-Lyte 148, with a mix of electrolytes designed to resemble plasma more closely. It contains magnesium instead of calcium and uses acetate and gluconate instead of lactate as buffers. Because it has no calcium, it can run in the same IV line as blood products. Recent large trials comparing balanced fluids with saline in critically ill adults have increased interest in fluids like Plasma-Lyte.",
  "timeline": [["1880s","Sydney Ringer shows calcium and potassium are needed in salt solutions for heart function."],["1932","Alexis Hartmann creates lactated Ringer's, the first buffered balanced crystalloid."],["2018","The SMART trial reports fewer kidney complications with balanced crystalloids than saline in critically ill adults."]],
  "cell": "Plasma-Lyte is an isotonic crystalloid with sodium, potassium, magnesium, chloride, acetate and gluconate, and a pH near 7.4. Its osmolality is close to plasma, so it does not shift water into or out of cells. Small ions cross capillaries freely, so it spreads through all the fluid outside cells, with only about a quarter staying in the blood vessels after redistribution. Its chloride is close to plasma levels, much lower than saline, so it does not cause hyperchloremic acidosis. Acetate is taken up by muscle and other tissues and converted to bicarbonate. Gluconate is only partly metabolized and some is excreted unchanged. The bicarbonate made helps prevent acidosis.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","Volume increases at first, raising preload, cardiac output and blood pressure in hypovolemia."],["Tissue fluid","Most of the fluid moves into the tissues within an hour, which can cause swelling with large volumes."],["Acid-base balance","Acetate and gluconate produce bicarbonate, and its lower chloride avoids the acidosis seen with saline."],["Kidneys","Lower chloride may reduce kidney vasoconstriction compared with saline, which is one theory for fewer kidney complications in trials."],["Blood transfusions","Because it contains no calcium, it does not cause clotting when run with blood products."],["Heart","Acetate can relax vessels, and rapid large infusions have been linked to lower blood pressure in some settings."]],
  "journey": "Plasma-Lyte is given into a vein. It spreads through all of the fluid outside cells within about 30 to 60 minutes. Acetate is converted to bicarbonate in muscle and liver within minutes, so it does not depend on the liver as much as lactate does. Gluconate is metabolized more slowly and partly excreted. The kidneys clear the extra water and electrolytes over hours.",
  "sidefx": "Fluid overload, swelling and pulmonary edema come from large volumes of fluid spreading into tissues, especially in heart or kidney failure. Its potassium and magnesium can build up in severe kidney failure. Gluconate can cause falsely positive results on some fungal galactomannan tests, a lab problem reported with older products. There is no specific antidote; overload is treated with fluid restriction and diuretics.",
  "fun": "Plasma-Lyte's makers tried to make an IV fluid that looks as much like plasma as possible, including a pH near the body's own.",
  "src": ["Plasma-Lyte A Injection pH 7.4 FDA label","Semler MW et al. NEJM 2018 (SMART trial)","Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"normal-saline": {
  "origin": "Saline as a medical treatment began with cholera. In 1832, during a cholera epidemic in Britain, the Scottish doctor Thomas Latta injected salt water into the veins of dying patients and saw some recover, one of the first uses of IV fluid. Later in the 1800s, physiologists, including the Dutch scientist Hartog Hamburger, studied red cells in salt solutions and found that about 0.9 percent sodium chloride did not make them swell or shrink. This concentration became known as 'normal' saline, although it is not truly normal: it has more chloride than plasma. In the 20th century, sterile, sealed bags made it the most used IV fluid in the world. Recent trials show balanced fluids may cause fewer kidney complications in critically ill adults.",
  "timeline": [["1832","Thomas Latta gives IV salt solutions to cholera patients."],["1880s","Hamburger's red cell studies help define 0.9 percent saline as isotonic."],["1970s","Flexible plastic IV bags become widely used."],["2018","The SMART trial compares saline with balanced crystalloids in critically ill adults."]],
  "cell": "Normal saline contains 154 mEq per liter each of sodium and chloride, with an osmolarity of about 308 mOsm per liter, close to plasma. Because it does not change the osmolality of the fluid outside cells much, it does not shift water into or out of cells. Sodium and chloride cross capillaries freely, so the fluid spreads through all the extracellular space, leaving about a quarter in the blood vessels after 30 to 60 minutes. Its chloride content is much higher than plasma. Large volumes raise blood chloride. By the strong ion difference concept, this lowers bicarbonate and causes a non-anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. High chloride in the kidney's macula densa may also narrow kidney arterioles and lower filtration.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels","Volume rises at first, increasing preload, cardiac output and blood pressure in dehydration or blood loss."],["Tissue fluid","Most of the fluid moves into the tissues, so large volumes cause swelling."],["Acid-base balance","High chloride lowers bicarbonate and can cause hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis."],["Kidneys","High chloride may reduce kidney blood flow and filtration, a possible reason for more kidney complications in some trials."],["Sodium levels","Large volumes can raise sodium in people who lose free water; overly fast correction of chronic low sodium can damage the brain."],["Blood transfusions","Because it has no calcium, it is the standard fluid to run with blood products."]],
  "journey": "Normal saline is given into a vein. It distributes through all the extracellular fluid within about 30 to 60 minutes, so only about a quarter stays in the blood. It is not metabolized. The kidneys gradually excrete the extra sodium, chloride and water, which can take longer than with balanced fluids because high chloride may reduce kidney blood flow.",
  "sidefx": "Fluid overload and pulmonary edema come from large volumes spreading into tissues and lungs. Hyperchloremic acidosis follows from its high chloride content. Raising sodium too fast in someone with chronic low sodium can cause osmotic demyelination, as brain cells that adapted to low sodium shrink too quickly. Infiltration of the IV can cause swelling in tissues. Air embolism is a risk with pressurized bags. There is no antidote; care involves adjusting fluids and using diuretics.",
  "fun": "Normal saline is not normal at all: its chloride level is about 50 percent higher than that of human plasma.",
  "src": ["0.9% Sodium Chloride Injection USP FDA label","Latta T. Lancet 1832","Semler MW et al. NEJM 2018 (SMART trial)"]
},
"sodium-chloride-23-4-percent": {
  "origin": "Concentrated sodium chloride was first made as a pharmacy additive: a small vial added to IV bags or parenteral nutrition to supply sodium. Its second use comes from brain research. In 1919 Lewis Weed and Paul McKibben at Johns Hopkins showed that injecting concentrated salt solutions into the blood shrank the brain and lowered cerebrospinal fluid pressure, while dilute solutions made it swell. This was the start of osmotherapy. Mannitol became the standard osmotic agent in the 1960s, but hypertonic saline gained new interest in neurocritical care. Undiluted 23.4 percent saline given through a central line to lower dangerous brain pressure is an off-label use. Because accidental undiluted injection has killed patients, it is treated as a high-alert medication.",
  "timeline": [["1919","Weed and McKibben show hypertonic solutions shrink the brain and lower CSF pressure."],["1960s","Mannitol becomes the standard osmotic therapy for raised intracranial pressure."],["1990s","Concentrated hypertonic saline is increasingly studied for brain swelling."]],
  "cell": "23.4 percent saline holds about 4 mEq per mL each of sodium and chloride, with an osmolarity around 8,000 mOsm per liter, more than 25 times that of plasma. When diluted, it simply replaces sodium and chloride. Given in small volumes through a central line, it causes an abrupt rise in plasma sodium and osmolality. The blood-brain barrier is mostly impermeable to sodium, so the brain cannot quickly match the change. Water moves out of brain cells and tissue into the hypertonic blood by osmosis, shrinking brain volume and lowering intracranial pressure. The rise in plasma osmolality also draws water from cells and tissues throughout the body into the vessels, expanding plasma volume and improving blood pressure.",
  "body": [["Brain","Water moves out of the brain, lowering intracranial pressure within minutes. Where the blood-brain barrier is damaged, the effect is weaker."],["Blood volume","Fluid shifts from cells and tissues into vessels, raising blood volume and blood pressure."],["Sodium levels","Serum sodium and osmolality rise quickly and must be monitored."],["Kidneys","The sodium load is excreted by the kidneys, which may cause large urine volumes and potassium loss."],["Veins","The extreme concentration damages vein walls, so it must go through a central line."],["Acid-base balance","The chloride load can cause hyperchloremic acidosis."]],
  "journey": "For most uses, 23.4 percent saline is diluted into larger IV fluids or parenteral nutrition before it is given. For brain swelling, a small undiluted amount is pushed through a central line, and the rise in sodium is immediate. The sodium and chloride spread throughout the fluid outside cells. They are not metabolized. The kidneys excrete them over hours to days. The effect on brain pressure from a bolus can last hours.",
  "sidefx": "The extreme concentration is the danger. Accidental undiluted injection into a peripheral vein or the wrong patient can cause sudden severe hypernatremia, brain shrinkage, bleeding in the brain and death, which is why it is a high-alert drug stored separately. Leakage into tissue can cause tissue death. Very fast rises in sodium can damage the brain through osmotic demyelination, especially in people with chronic low sodium. Hemolysis can occur near the infusion site. Fluid overload and pulmonary edema are possible. Treatment is supportive.",
  "fun": "23.4 percent saline is so concentrated that it is several times saltier than seawater.",
  "src": ["23.4% Sodium Chloride Injection USP FDA label","Weed LH, McKibben PS. Am J Physiol 1919","ISMP list of high-alert medications"]
},
"potassium-phosphate": {
  "origin": "Phosphorus was discovered in 1669 by the German alchemist Hennig Brand, who boiled down urine looking for gold and found a substance that glowed in the dark. Much later, scientists learned how important phosphate is in the body. In 1929 Karl Lohmann, and separately Cyrus Fiske and Yellapragada Subbarow, discovered ATP, the cell's energy molecule, built around phosphate. Low phosphate became a recognized problem when parenteral nutrition and refeeding of starved people showed that sudden feeding can drive phosphate into cells and cause dangerous drops. Potassium phosphates injection was long used in the US without a formal approval, and FDA-approved products became available in the late 2010s. It replaces phosphate when patients cannot take it by mouth.",
  "timeline": [["1669","Hennig Brand discovers phosphorus by distilling urine."],["1929","Lohmann, and separately Fiske and Subbarow, discover ATP."],["1970s","Low phosphate is recognized as a complication of parenteral nutrition and refeeding."],["2010s","FDA-approved potassium phosphates injection products become available."]],
  "cell": "Inorganic phosphate is a building block of ATP and creatine phosphate, the cell's energy currency. It is also part of DNA, RNA, cell membrane phospholipids and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG) in red cells, which helps hemoglobin release oxygen to tissues. Phosphate also buffers acid in the urine. When phosphate is low, cells cannot make enough ATP. Muscle, including the diaphragm and heart, becomes weak, and red cells release oxygen poorly. Potassium phosphates injection supplies both phosphate and potassium. Phosphate enters cells through sodium-phosphate cotransporters and refills cell stores, restoring ATP and 2,3-DPG. The added potassium refills the cell's main positive ion, which is often low at the same time.",
  "body": [["Muscles","Restored ATP improves muscle strength, including the diaphragm, which can help patients wean from a ventilator."],["Heart","Better energy supply improves heart contraction. The potassium also affects heart rhythm."],["Red blood cells","More 2,3-DPG helps hemoglobin release oxygen to tissues."],["Calcium balance","Phosphate binds calcium. Rapid infusion can lower blood calcium and cause tetany."],["Kidneys","The kidneys filter phosphate and reabsorb part of it in the proximal tubule. Calcium phosphate can deposit in the kidneys at high levels."],["Nerves","Severe low phosphate can cause confusion and seizures, which improve as levels are restored."]],
  "journey": "Potassium phosphates injection is diluted and infused into a vein. Phosphate and potassium move into cells, so blood phosphate can drift back down over hours after the infusion ends. The phosphate joins the body's pool inside cells and bone. The kidneys filter phosphate and potassium and excrete what is not reabsorbed. In kidney failure, both can build up.",
  "sidefx": "High potassium is the most dangerous risk, because rapid infusion raises blood potassium faster than cells can absorb it, which can cause arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Phosphate binds calcium, so too much or too fast lowers blood calcium, causing tetany, and can deposit calcium phosphate in tissues, kidneys and vessels. Kidney injury can result from these deposits. Phlebitis comes from the irritating solution. There is no antidote; calcium is given for hypocalcemia or hyperkalemia effects on the heart.",
  "fun": "Phosphorus means light bearer in Greek, named for the eerie glow Hennig Brand saw from the substance he extracted from urine.",
  "src": ["Potassium Phosphates Injection FDA label","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics","ASPEN Parenteral Nutrition Handbook"]
},
"sodium-phosphate": {
  "origin": "Sodium phosphates injection supplies the same phosphate as potassium phosphates but with sodium instead of potassium, which matters when a patient has high potassium or kidney problems. Interest in phosphate replacement grew with discoveries about red cells and energy. In 1967 Reinhold and Ruth Benesch, and separately Chanutin and Curnish, showed that 2,3-diphosphoglycerate controls how readily hemoglobin releases oxygen. Doctors also learned that phosphate falls sharply during treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis and during refeeding, as insulin drives phosphate into cells. Sodium phosphates injection has been used in US hospitals for decades to correct low phosphate in patients who cannot take it by mouth.",
  "timeline": [["1929","Scientists discover ATP, the cell's phosphate-based energy molecule."],["1967","Benesch and Benesch show 2,3-DPG controls oxygen release from hemoglobin."],["1970s","Low phosphate is recognized as a complication of IV feeding and diabetic ketoacidosis treatment."]],
  "cell": "Sodium phosphates injection provides inorganic phosphate in a mix of monobasic and dibasic salts. Inside cells, phosphate is needed to make ATP from ADP, so it powers muscles, nerves and the heart. Red cells use it to make 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, which binds hemoglobin and shifts the oxygen curve to the right, so oxygen is released more easily to tissues. Phosphate is also part of membrane phospholipids and nucleic acids and helps the kidney excrete acid. Phosphate enters cells through sodium-phosphate cotransporters. Insulin and glucose drive phosphate into cells, which is why levels drop during feeding and diabetic ketoacidosis treatment. The sodium in the product adds to the body's sodium load.",
  "body": [["Muscles","ATP restoration improves weakness, including breathing muscle weakness and rhabdomyolysis risk."],["Red blood cells","More 2,3-DPG improves oxygen delivery and red cells become less fragile."],["Calcium balance","Phosphate binds calcium, so too much can lower blood calcium and cause tetany and arrhythmias."],["Fluid and sodium","The sodium load can cause fluid retention and raise sodium, a concern in heart failure."],["Kidneys","High levels can cause calcium phosphate deposits in the kidney and acute kidney injury."],["Nerves and brain","Correcting severe low phosphate can relieve confusion, numbness and seizures."]],
  "journey": "Sodium phosphates injection is diluted and given into a vein. Phosphate moves into cells and bone, so blood levels fall again within hours. Insulin, glucose and alkalosis speed this shift. The kidneys filter phosphate and reabsorb part of it, excreting the rest in urine along with the sodium. In kidney failure, phosphate builds up and dosing must be very cautious.",
  "sidefx": "High phosphate binds calcium in the blood, which lowers calcium and can cause tetany, seizures and heart rhythm problems. The calcium-phosphate product can rise enough to deposit crystals in kidneys, vessels and soft tissues, leading to kidney injury. The sodium load can cause high sodium and fluid overload. Phlebitis occurs from the irritating solution. There is no antidote; calcium is given for symptomatic hypocalcemia.",
  "fun": "Each red cell contains about as many molecules of 2,3-DPG as hemoglobin, making phosphate a key player in getting oxygen to tissues.",
  "src": ["Sodium Phosphates Injection FDA label","Benesch R, Benesch RE. Biochem Biophys Res Commun 1967","ASPEN Parenteral Nutrition Handbook"]
},
"amino-acids-parenteral": {
  "origin": "Feeding protein by vein began in 1937, when Robert Elman at Washington University in St. Louis gave patients a protein hydrolysate (broken-down casein) by IV. Full IV nutrition seemed impossible for decades because the concentrated solutions needed destroyed small veins. In the 1960s Stanley Dudrick and Jonathan Rhoads at the University of Pennsylvania solved this by placing catheters in large central veins, where blood flow dilutes concentrated fluids quickly. In 1968 they reported that beagle puppies grew normally on IV nutrition alone, followed by a human infant. Crystalline amino acid solutions later replaced hydrolysates. Today products like Travasol, Clinisol, Trophamine and Clinimix provide the protein part of parenteral nutrition.",
  "timeline": [["1937","Robert Elman gives IV protein hydrolysates to patients."],["1968","Dudrick and Rhoads report growth on total parenteral nutrition in puppies and an infant."],["1970s","Crystalline amino acid solutions replace protein hydrolysates."],["1980s","Pediatric amino acid formulas such as Trophamine are developed for infants."]],
  "cell": "Amino acid injections supply all the essential amino acids the body cannot make, plus many nonessential ones, in their L form. Cells use these to build new proteins through ribosomal synthesis, including muscle, enzymes, immune proteins and wound repair proteins. To build protein rather than burn it, the body also needs enough nonprotein energy from dextrose and lipid. When energy is adequate, nitrogen balance turns positive, meaning more protein is built than broken down. Amino acids also serve as building blocks for neurotransmitters, nucleotides, glutathione and other molecules, and can be used for gluconeogenesis. Excess amino acids are deaminated in the liver, and their nitrogen is converted to urea through the urea cycle.",
  "body": [["Muscles","Muscle breakdown slows and lean body mass is better preserved in patients who cannot eat."],["Wounds and skin","Supplying protein supports collagen synthesis and wound healing."],["Liver","Long-term parenteral nutrition can cause fatty liver and cholestasis, especially in infants."],["Kidneys and urea","Breakdown of extra amino acids raises blood urea nitrogen, which can be a problem in kidney failure."],["Blood sugar","When given with dextrose, blood sugar can rise, requiring insulin."],["Metabolism","Refeeding a starved patient can drive phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells, causing dangerous drops."]],
  "journey": "Amino acid solutions are usually mixed with dextrose, lipids, electrolytes and vitamins and infused into a vein. Concentrated mixes go through a central line, while dilute ones may use a peripheral vein. The amino acids enter cells through transporters and are used for protein synthesis or energy. The liver removes excess amino groups and makes urea, which the kidneys excrete. Effects continue only while the infusion runs.",
  "sidefx": "Many complications relate to the route and the mixture rather than the amino acids themselves. Catheter-related bloodstream infection happens because the nutrient-rich fluid and central line give bacteria and fungi a place to grow. Refeeding syndrome happens when nutrition drives electrolytes into cells. Raised BUN and ammonia occur when amino acid breakdown outpaces the liver and kidneys. Liver problems relate to long-term use. Precipitates of calcium and phosphate in the mixture have caused deadly lung emboli, which is why in-line filters are used.",
  "fun": "Stanley Dudrick's beagle puppies grew so well on IV nutrition alone that they matched their littermates who ate normally.",
  "src": ["Travasol (amino acids) FDA label","Dudrick SJ, Wilmore DW, Vars HM, Rhoads JE. Surgery 1968","ASPEN Parenteral Nutrition Handbook"]
},
"chlorothiazide": {
  "origin": "Chlorothiazide was the first thiazide diuretic. In the 1950s a team at Merck Sharp & Dohme, including pharmacologist Karl Beyer and chemists James Sprague, Frederick Novello and John Baer, was building on sulfonamide drugs that inhibited carbonic anhydrase and caused weak diuresis. They wanted a drug that made the kidneys excrete chloride along with sodium. In 1957 they made chlorothiazide, which caused strong sodium and chloride loss. It was approved in 1958 as Diuril and quickly became one of the first well-tolerated oral drugs for high blood pressure and swelling. The team received a Lasker Award in 1975. Today the IV form, Sodium Diuril, is used in hospitals, often with loop diuretics.",
  "timeline": [["1957","Merck scientists synthesize chlorothiazide."],["1958","FDA approves chlorothiazide (Diuril)."],["1975","Beyer, Sprague, Novello and Baer receive a Lasker Award for thiazide diuretics."]],
  "cell": "Chlorothiazide is secreted into the proximal tubule by organic anion transporters and travels with the urine to the distal convoluted tubule. There it binds and blocks the sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC, SLC12A3) on the inner (luminal) membrane. Normally NCC reabsorbs about 5 to 10 percent of filtered sodium. With it blocked, more sodium, chloride and water stay in the urine. More sodium then reaches the collecting duct, where ENaC reabsorbs it in exchange for potassium and hydrogen ions, so potassium and acid are lost (hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis). Lower sodium inside the distal cells boosts calcium reabsorption through the basolateral sodium-calcium exchanger, reducing urine calcium. It also mildly inhibits carbonic anhydrase.",
  "body": [["Kidneys","More sodium, chloride and water are lost in urine, which reduces fluid volume and swelling."],["Blood pressure","Lower blood volume first, and later reduced vascular resistance, bring blood pressure down."],["Potassium and acid","More sodium reaching the collecting duct drives potassium and hydrogen loss, causing low potassium and metabolic alkalosis."],["Calcium","Calcium reabsorption increases, lowering urine calcium and sometimes raising blood calcium."],["Uric acid","It competes with uric acid for secretion and increases reabsorption, which can trigger gout."],["Blood sugar and lipids","Low potassium reduces insulin release, raising blood sugar. Lipids may also rise."]],
  "journey": "IV chlorothiazide acts within about 15 minutes and peaks in about 30 minutes, with effects lasting about two hours. By mouth it is absorbed incompletely. It is not metabolized. It is secreted into the tubule by organic anion transporters and leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 45 to 120 minutes. It must reach the tubule lumen to work, so it is less effective in severe kidney failure.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from its site of action. Extra sodium delivery to the collecting duct causes potassium and magnesium loss and metabolic alkalosis. Low sodium occurs because it blocks dilution of urine in the distal tubule while thirst and ADH continue. Higher calcium, uric acid and glucose follow from changes in tubular transport and insulin release. Fluid loss can cause dizziness and low blood pressure. Low potassium can trigger arrhythmias, especially with digoxin. As a sulfonamide, it can cause allergic reactions. There is no antidote; electrolytes are replaced.",
  "fun": "Chlorothiazide was the parent of the thiazides. Hydrochlorothiazide, one of the most prescribed drugs in the world, was introduced shortly after as a more potent relative.",
  "src": ["Sodium Diuril (chlorothiazide sodium) FDA label","Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics","Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"alvimopan": {
  "origin": "Alvimopan was built to solve a frustrating problem after bowel surgery. Opioids given for pain, plus the stress of surgery itself, make the gut stop moving for days, a state called postoperative ileus. Chemists at Eli Lilly first made the molecule in the 1990s as a mu-opioid blocker that was too large and too charged to cross into the brain. It was later licensed to Adolor Corporation, which developed it with GlaxoSmithKline. The FDA approved it as Entereg in 2008 for short-term hospital use after partial bowel resection. Because a long-term study in patients with chronic opioid constipation showed more heart attacks, it was approved only for short courses and only through hospitals enrolled in a restricted program.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Eli Lilly chemists create alvimopan as a gut-restricted opioid antagonist."], ["2008", "FDA approves Entereg for postoperative ileus after bowel resection, with a restricted hospital program."]],
  "cell": "Alvimopan is a competitive antagonist at mu-opioid receptors. These receptors sit on neurons of the enteric nervous system, especially the myenteric plexus. When an opioid activates them, Gi/o signaling lowers cAMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, so the neurons release less acetylcholine. Less acetylcholine means weaker coordinated contractions and more fluid absorption, which stalls transit. Alvimopan binds the same receptors with high affinity and keeps opioids from activating them. Its zwitterionic structure and size keep it from crossing the blood-brain barrier, so receptors in the brain and spinal cord stay available to the opioid and pain relief is kept. Gut bacteria convert some of it to an active amide metabolite that also blocks the receptor.",
  "body": [["Stomach and small bowel", "With enteric mu receptors blocked, normal propulsive waves come back sooner, so patients tolerate solid food earlier after surgery."], ["Colon", "Restored motility shortens the time to the first bowel movement, which is a main marker of recovered gut function."], ["Brain and spinal cord", "Very little drug reaches the central nervous system, so opioid pain relief and its central effects are not reversed."], ["Heart", "A higher rate of heart attacks was seen with long-term use in a chronic opioid study, which is why use is limited to a short hospital course."], ["Gut in opioid-tolerant patients", "People already used to opioids can get cramping and diarrhea because suddenly blocking gut receptors acts like a local withdrawal."]],
  "journey": "Alvimopan is a capsule taken by mouth before surgery and continued for a short time after. Only a small portion is absorbed, which suits a drug meant to work on the gut wall. It reaches its peak in about two hours. In the intestine, gut bacteria hydrolyze it to an active amide metabolite, so antibiotics or bowel preparation can change how much metabolite forms. Most of the drug leaves in bile and feces, with a small part in the urine. The parent drug has a terminal half-life of about 10 to 17 hours.",
  "sidefx": "The main concern is myocardial infarction, seen in a long-term trial in people taking opioids for chronic pain. The reason is not fully understood, but it led to a boxed warning and a restricted hospital-only program limited to a short course. Dyspepsia, back pain, low potassium and urinary retention are common reported effects. In people already tolerant to opioids, blocking gut receptors can trigger cramping and diarrhea, a local withdrawal-like effect. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Alvimopan was among the first drugs designed on purpose to block opioid effects in the gut while leaving pain relief in the brain untouched, a class now called PAMORAs.",
  "src": ["Entereg (alvimopan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"cefotetan": {
  "origin": "Cefotetan belongs to the cephamycins, a branch of the cephalosporin family that came from soil bacteria. In the early 1970s researchers at Merck found cephamycin C, a beta-lactam made by Streptomyces bacteria that carried an extra methoxy group which protected it from many beta-lactamase enzymes. That idea led to cefoxitin and later to cefotetan, which was developed by the Japanese company Yamanouchi and introduced in the US in the 1980s as Cefotan. It became popular for surgical prophylaxis and pelvic infections because it covers gut anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis and lasts long enough for less frequent dosing.",
  "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu isolates the cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["1970s", "Merck researchers describe cephamycins from Streptomyces with a protective methoxy group."], ["1980s", "Cefotetan is introduced in the US as Cefotan."]],
  "cell": "Cefotetan is a beta-lactam that binds penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan strands in the bacterial cell wall. The beta-lactam ring mimics the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall precursor, so the enzyme acylates itself on the drug and is inactivated. Without cross-links, the wall weakens while autolysins keep cutting it, and the growing cell bursts. The 7-alpha-methoxy group shields the ring from many beta-lactamases, including those of Bacteroides fragilis. Its N-methylthiotetrazole (NMTT) side chain is the chemical reason for two off-target effects: it inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase in the liver and blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Gram-negative rods, streptococci and gut anaerobes are killed as their cell walls fail; MRSA, enterococci and Pseudomonas are not covered."], ["Blood clotting", "The NMTT side chain lowers recycling of vitamin K, so clotting factors II, VII, IX and X fall and the INR can rise, especially in malnourished patients."], ["Red blood cells", "Cefotetan can coat red cells and trigger antibody-mediated hemolysis, which is more common with this drug than with most cephalosporins and can be fatal."], ["Liver and alcohol metabolism", "Blocking aldehyde dehydrogenase lets acetaldehyde build up after alcohol, causing flushing, nausea and racing heart (a disulfiram-like reaction)."], ["Gut flora", "Broad killing of normal colon bacteria makes room for Clostridioides difficile."]],
  "journey": "Cefotetan is given IV or by deep IM injection because it is not absorbed by mouth. It is highly protein bound and spreads into most tissues, including pelvic and abdominal sites. It is barely metabolized, though part of it changes into a tautomer form in the body. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged, with some in bile. Its half-life of about 3 to 4.6 hours is longer than many cephalosporins, which allows twice-daily dosing, and kidney disease slows its removal.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea and C. difficile infection come from wiping out normal gut flora. The NMTT side chain explains low prothrombin and bleeding, because it blocks vitamin K recycling, and vitamin K can correct it. The same side chain causes the disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol. Immune hemolytic anemia happens when the drug binds red cells and antibodies attack them; it can be severe and is a classic reason to stop the drug. Allergy is shared with other beta-lactams. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Cefotetan, cefoxitin and cefazolin are a well-known look-alike, sound-alike trio, even though they cover quite different bacteria.",
  "src": ["Cefotetan for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"cefotaxime": {
  "origin": "Cefotaxime traces back to 1945, when the Italian scientist Giuseppe Brotzu found that a mold growing near a sewage outlet in Sardinia, later called Cephalosporium (now Acremonium), made a substance that killed bacteria. Oxford chemists then isolated cephalosporin C, whose core could be modified. In the 1970s chemists at Hoechst in Germany, working with Roussel Uclaf, added an aminothiazole ring and a methoxyimino group to that core. The result, cefotaxime, was the first third-generation cephalosporin, stable against many beta-lactamases and able to reach the cerebrospinal fluid. It was approved in the US as Claforan in 1981 and became a standard treatment for meningitis and newborn infections. The Claforan brand has since been discontinued, but generic cefotaxime remains.",
  "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers a cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["1950s", "Oxford researchers isolate cephalosporin C."], ["1970s", "Hoechst and Roussel Uclaf develop cefotaxime."], ["1981", "FDA approves Claforan, the first third-generation cephalosporin in the US."]],
  "cell": "Cefotaxime acylates penicillin-binding proteins, mainly PBP1a, PBP1b and PBP3. These enzymes perform the last cross-linking step in peptidoglycan synthesis. The drug's beta-lactam ring looks like the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall building block, so the enzyme binds it and becomes stuck. Without cross-links the wall cannot hold the cell's internal pressure, autolysins keep breaking bonds, and the bacterium lyses. Killing depends on the time drug levels stay above the MIC. The methoxyimino side chain blocks many common beta-lactamases, but extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs) and AmpC enzymes can still destroy it.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria, Haemophilus and many Enterobacterales; it has no reliable activity against Pseudomonas, enterococci, MRSA or Listeria."], ["Brain and meninges", "Inflamed meninges let enough drug into the cerebrospinal fluid to treat bacterial meningitis."], ["Newborns", "Unlike ceftriaxone, it does not push bilirubin off albumin and is not linked to calcium precipitates, which made it a classic choice in neonates."], ["Gut flora", "Killing normal bowel bacteria can allow C. difficile overgrowth and diarrhea."], ["Blood and kidneys", "Rarely it causes low white cells, eosinophilia or allergic interstitial nephritis, which are immune reactions."], ["Heart", "Very rapid bolus through a central line has been linked to arrhythmias."]],
  "journey": "Cefotaxime is given IV or IM because the gut does not absorb it. It spreads into body fluids and crosses inflamed meninges. The liver removes an acetyl group to make desacetylcefotaxime, which is also active and works together with the parent drug. Both are cleared by the kidneys. The parent drug has a short half-life of about one hour, so it is given several times a day, and the half-life is longer in newborns and in kidney failure.",
  "sidefx": "Pain or phlebitis at the injection site comes from local irritation. Rash and anaphylaxis are immune reactions to the beta-lactam structure. Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis come from disrupting normal gut bacteria. Neutropenia, eosinophilia, interstitial nephritis and a positive Coombs test are immune-mediated and usually reverse when the drug is stopped. Arrhythmia has been reported after very fast central injection. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Brotzu found his mold near a sewage outlet because he reasoned that seawater there seemed to clean itself of germs faster than expected.",
  "src": ["Cefotaxime for injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"meropenem-vaborbactam": {
  "origin": "This combination pairs an older antibiotic with a new protector. The carbapenem family began with thienamycin, which Merck scientists found in 1976 in the soil bacterium Streptomyces cattleya. Meropenem, a more stable carbapenem developed by Sumitomo in Japan, was approved in the US in 1996. By the 2000s, Klebsiella strains carrying the KPC carbapenemase were spreading and destroying carbapenems. Scientists at Rempex Pharmaceuticals, part of The Medicines Company, designed vaborbactam, a cyclic boronic acid that traps serine beta-lactamases such as KPC. The FDA approved the pair as Vabomere in 2017 for complicated urinary tract infections.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin, the first carbapenem, from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1996", "FDA approves meropenem in the US."], ["2017", "FDA approves Vabomere (meropenem-vaborbactam)."]],
  "cell": "Meropenem binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP2 and PBP3, and blocks the transpeptidation that cross-links peptidoglycan. The weakened wall bursts as autolysins act, so the bacterium dies. KPC enzymes are serine beta-lactamases that attack the beta-lactam ring with an active-site serine. Vaborbactam is not a beta-lactam. Its boron atom forms a reversible covalent bond with that serine, mimicking the transition state, and holds the enzyme in an inactive state. This protects meropenem from Ambler class A (including KPC) and class C enzymes. Vaborbactam does nothing against metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM, VIM, IMP), which use zinc instead of serine, or against OXA-48.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "KPC-producing Klebsiella and other Enterobacterales become susceptible again because their carbapenemase is blocked."], ["Kidneys and urinary tract", "Both drugs are cleared by the kidneys and reach high levels in urine, which suits kidney and bladder infections."], ["Brain", "Carbapenems can lower the seizure threshold, probably by interfering with GABA-A signaling, though meropenem does this less than imipenem."], ["Gut flora", "Broad killing of gut bacteria can lead to diarrhea and C. difficile colitis."], ["Veins", "The long infusion can irritate the vein and cause phlebitis."]],
  "journey": "Both parts are given together as an IV infusion over about three hours, which keeps meropenem above the MIC for longer. Meropenem is barely protein bound, while vaborbactam is about one-third bound. Meropenem is partly broken to an inactive open-ring form; vaborbactam is not metabolized. Both leave mainly through the kidneys, each with a half-life of about one to two hours, so doses are adjusted in kidney disease.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, phlebitis, nausea and diarrhea are the most common effects. Diarrhea and C. difficile reflect disruption of normal flora. Seizures can occur, especially in kidney failure or with brain disease, because carbapenems interfere with inhibitory GABA signaling. Allergy is possible in people with beta-lactam allergy. Meropenem also sharply lowers valproic acid levels, which can lead to breakthrough seizures. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Vaborbactam was the first boron-based beta-lactamase inhibitor approved, using a chemical trick that resembles how some cancer drugs trap enzymes.",
  "src": ["Vabomere (meropenem and vaborbactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"imipenem-relebactam": {
  "origin": "Imipenem was the first carbapenem used in patients. Merck scientists found thienamycin in 1976 in Streptomyces cattleya, but it was too unstable to make into a drug. They created imipenem, a stable derivative, and found that a kidney enzyme called dehydropeptidase-I broke it down. Their answer was to add cilastatin, which blocks that enzyme, and Primaxin (imipenem-cilastatin) was approved in 1985. Decades later, carbapenem-resistant bacteria made imipenem less reliable. Merck then added relebactam, a diazabicyclooctane beta-lactamase inhibitor, and the three-drug combination was approved as Recarbrio in 2019.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Merck scientists discover thienamycin from Streptomyces cattleya."], ["1985", "FDA approves Primaxin (imipenem-cilastatin), the first carbapenem."], ["2019", "FDA approves Recarbrio (imipenem, cilastatin and relebactam)."], ["2020", "Recarbrio gains approval for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Each of the three parts has a separate job. Imipenem binds penicillin-binding proteins, especially PBP2, and stops peptidoglycan cross-linking, so the bacterial cell wall fails and the cell bursts. Cilastatin has no antibacterial effect; it blocks renal dehydropeptidase-I on the brush border of proximal tubule cells, which would otherwise break down imipenem and leave little active drug in the urine. Relebactam is a non-beta-lactam inhibitor that forms a reversible covalent bond with the active serine of Ambler class A enzymes (including KPC) and class C AmpC enzymes. It also helps against Pseudomonas that combines AmpC with porin loss. It does not block metallo-beta-lactamases or OXA-48.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Many imipenem-resistant Enterobacterales and Pseudomonas aeruginosa become treatable because their serine beta-lactamases are inhibited."], ["Kidneys", "Cilastatin protects imipenem from tubular breakdown, which keeps urine levels up and avoids tubular toxicity from breakdown products."], ["Brain", "Imipenem has the highest seizure risk among carbapenems, linked to blocking GABA-A receptors, especially at high levels or in kidney failure."], ["Gut flora", "Broad activity disturbs normal bowel bacteria, so diarrhea and C. difficile can follow."], ["Blood", "Anemia and low potassium have been reported in treated patients."]],
  "journey": "The three drugs are given together by IV infusion. Imipenem and relebactam each have a half-life of about one hour and are mostly cleared by the kidneys. Without cilastatin, kidney dehydropeptidase-I would hydrolyze much of the imipenem. Cilastatin is more protein bound than the other two. Because all three depend on the kidneys, doses are adjusted when kidney function falls.",
  "sidefx": "Seizures are the classic concern with imipenem and come from interference with inhibitory GABA-A signaling in the brain; the risk rises with kidney failure and brain lesions. Diarrhea and C. difficile colitis reflect loss of normal flora. Phlebitis is local irritation. Cross-allergy with other beta-lactams is possible. Like other carbapenems it lowers valproic acid levels and can cause breakthrough seizures. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Cilastatin is one of the few drugs added to a product only to protect another drug from the patient's own kidney enzyme.",
  "src": ["Recarbrio (imipenem, cilastatin and relebactam) FDA label", "Primaxin FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"sulbactam-durlobactam": {
  "origin": "Sulbactam was made by Pfizer in the late 1970s as a beta-lactamase inhibitor and was paired with ampicillin as Unasyn, approved in the US in 1986. Doctors later noticed that sulbactam itself kills Acinetobacter baumannii, a hospital germ that often resists nearly everything. But Acinetobacter makes OXA-type enzymes that destroy sulbactam. Scientists at Entasis Therapeutics, a company spun out of AstraZeneca, designed durlobactam, a diazabicyclooctane inhibitor that blocks those enzymes. After a trial in patients with carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter infections, the FDA approved the combination as Xacduro in 2023 for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia caused by this organism.",
  "timeline": [["1986", "FDA approves ampicillin-sulbactam (Unasyn)."], ["2023", "FDA approves Xacduro (sulbactam-durlobactam) for Acinetobacter pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "In most bacteria sulbactam acts only as a decoy for beta-lactamases. In Acinetobacter it binds PBP1 and PBP3, the transpeptidases that build and divide the cell wall, so it is a true antibiotic against this organism and kills it. Acinetobacter fights back with beta-lactamases, especially class D OXA carbapenemases plus class A and C enzymes. Durlobactam forms a reversible covalent bond with the active serine of these class A, C and D enzymes, keeping sulbactam intact. Durlobactam also binds PBP2 in Acinetobacter, adding to the effect. Neither part works against metallo-beta-lactamases, which use zinc instead of serine.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Treats pneumonia caused by carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii-calcoaceticus complex, which often leaves few other options."], ["Bacteria", "The activity is narrow; it is designed for Acinetobacter, so it is typically given with another drug such as imipenem-cilastatin to cover other germs."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises are among the most common lab changes."], ["Blood and electrolytes", "Anemia and low potassium were seen in treated critically ill patients."], ["Gut flora", "Diarrhea and C. difficile can follow broad antibiotic use."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are given together by IV infusion. They are lightly metabolized and are cleared mainly by the kidneys, each with a half-life of about two hours. Kidney function changes doses, and patients with augmented renal clearance can need more frequent dosing. Both reach the lining fluid of the lungs, where the infection lives.",
  "sidefx": "Liver enzyme rises, diarrhea, anemia and low potassium were the most common findings in trials. C. difficile reflects disruption of normal gut bacteria. As beta-lactam-type molecules, both can trigger allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, in people allergic to beta-lactams. Arrhythmias were reported in very sick trial patients. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Sulbactam spent decades known only as a helper drug before clinicians realized it was one of the few beta-lactams that kills Acinetobacter on its own.",
  "src": ["Xacduro (sulbactam and durlobactam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"aztreonam-avibactam": {
  "origin": "Aztreonam came from a 1981 Squibb discovery of monobactams, single-ring beta-lactams made by soil bacteria such as Chromobacterium violaceum. Squibb made a synthetic version, aztreonam, approved as Azactam in 1986. Aztreonam has an unusual strength: metallo-beta-lactamases such as NDM cannot destroy it. The problem was that the same bacteria usually also carry other enzymes that do. Avibactam, a diazabicyclooctane inhibitor, was already in use with ceftazidime (Avycaz, 2015). Pfizer and AbbVie paired it with aztreonam so that avibactam blocks the other enzymes while aztreonam escapes the metallo-enzyme. Approved in Europe in 2024, it was approved by the FDA as Emblaveo in 2025 for complicated intra-abdominal infections, used with metronidazole.",
  "timeline": [["1981", "Squibb scientists report monobactams from soil bacteria."], ["1986", "FDA approves aztreonam (Azactam)."], ["2015", "Avibactam is first approved, paired with ceftazidime."], ["2025", "FDA approves Emblaveo (aztreonam-avibactam)."]],
  "cell": "Aztreonam binds penicillin-binding protein 3 of gram-negative rods and blocks cell wall cross-linking at the division site, so cells form long filaments and then lyse. Metallo-beta-lactamases (class B: NDM, VIM, IMP) use zinc to break beta-lactam rings, but they do not hydrolyze aztreonam well. Bacteria that carry metallo-enzymes usually also make serine enzymes such as ESBLs, AmpC, KPC or OXA-48, which do destroy aztreonam. Avibactam forms a reversible covalent bond with the active serine of those class A, class C and some class D enzymes. The combination lets aztreonam reach its PBP3 target intact in strains that defeat almost every other beta-lactam.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Active against Enterobacterales that make metallo-beta-lactamases plus serine enzymes; it has no useful activity against gram-positive bacteria or anaerobes."], ["Abdomen", "Used for complicated intra-abdominal infection, with metronidazole added to cover anaerobes."], ["Liver", "Raised liver enzymes and liver injury have been reported, so liver tests are watched."], ["Blood", "Anemia and changes in platelet counts were reported in trials."], ["Gut flora", "Diarrhea and C. difficile can follow disruption of normal bowel bacteria."]],
  "journey": "Both drugs are given together by IV infusion. Neither is much metabolized. Both are cleared by the kidneys, each with a half-life of about two hours, so doses depend on kidney function. Aztreonam is about half protein bound, avibactam very little.",
  "sidefx": "Anemia, diarrhea, nausea, raised liver enzymes and infusion site reactions are the common effects. Liver injury is a labeled concern and is watched with blood tests. C. difficile comes from loss of normal gut bacteria. Aztreonam rarely cross-reacts with penicillin allergy, but it shares a side chain with ceftazidime, so people allergic to ceftazidime can react. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Before this product existed, clinicians treating metallo-beta-lactamase infections would sometimes give aztreonam and ceftazidime-avibactam side by side just to combine aztreonam with avibactam.",
  "src": ["Emblaveo (aztreonam and avibactam) FDA label", "Azactam (aztreonam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"plazomicin": {
  "origin": "Plazomicin is a modern answer to an old drug class. Aminoglycosides began with streptomycin, isolated in 1943 by Albert Schatz in Selman Waksman's lab at Rutgers from Streptomyces griseus. Over time bacteria learned to disable aminoglycosides with modifying enzymes that add acetyl, phosphate or nucleotide groups. Scientists at Achaogen, a California company, started from sisomicin, a natural aminoglycoside from Micromonospora bacteria, and changed its structure so most of those enzymes could no longer attach. The FDA approved it as Zemdri in 2018 for complicated urinary tract infections. Achaogen went bankrupt in 2019, and the drug's rights were later bought by Cipla.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Albert Schatz and Selman Waksman isolate streptomycin."], ["2018", "FDA approves Zemdri (plazomicin) for complicated urinary tract infections."], ["2019", "Achaogen files for bankruptcy; plazomicin rights later go to Cipla."]],
  "cell": "Plazomicin enters gram-negative bacteria in steps. Its positive charges let it cross the outer membrane, and then an oxygen-dependent transport process pulls it across the inner membrane, which is why aminoglycosides do not work against anaerobes. Inside, it binds the A site of 16S rRNA in the 30S ribosomal subunit. This causes misreading of mRNA, so faulty proteins are made and inserted into the membrane, making it leakier and pulling in more drug. Killing is fast and concentration dependent, with a post-antibiotic effect. Its modified structure blocks most aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, but bacteria with 16S rRNA methyltransferases, which change the binding site, remain resistant.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills many ESBL-producing and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales that resist older aminoglycosides."], ["Kidneys", "Proximal tubule cells take up the drug through megalin receptors and are injured, so kidney function can fall."], ["Inner ear", "Hair cells in the cochlea and vestibular system take up aminoglycosides and can be permanently damaged, causing hearing loss, ringing or imbalance."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It reduces presynaptic acetylcholine release, which can cause weakness and, rarely, breathing paralysis."], ["Fetus", "Aminoglycosides cross the placenta and can cause deafness in the baby."]],
  "journey": "Plazomicin is given once daily by IV infusion because it is not absorbed from the gut. It stays mostly in the blood and extracellular fluid. It is not metabolized and is cleared almost entirely by the kidneys unchanged, with a half-life of about 3.5 hours. Blood level monitoring is used in some patients, and dosing depends on kidney function.",
  "sidefx": "Kidney injury happens because tubule cells actively take up the drug, which damages their lysosomes and mitochondria. Ototoxicity comes from drug building up in inner ear hair cells and creating free radicals; it can be permanent. Neuromuscular blockade occurs because aminoglycosides compete with calcium at nerve endings; calcium can help reverse it. Plazomicin has a boxed warning for nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, neuromuscular blockade and fetal harm. There is no specific antidote; hemodialysis can remove it.",
  "fun": "Once-daily aminoglycoside dosing works because of the post-antibiotic effect: bacteria stay suppressed for hours after levels fall.",
  "src": ["Zemdri (plazomicin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Schatz A, Bugie E, Waksman SA. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med, 1944"]
}
,
"eravacycline": {
  "origin": "Tetracyclines began in 1948, when Benjamin Duggar at Lederle Laboratories described chlortetracycline from the golden soil mold Streptomyces aureofaciens. For decades every tetracycline was made by tweaking natural fermentation products. Bacteria fought back with efflux pumps and ribosome-protection proteins. In the 2000s chemist Andrew Myers and his group at Harvard worked out a way to build tetracyclines completely from scratch, which allowed changes that fermentation chemistry could not make. Tetraphase Pharmaceuticals used this platform to create eravacycline, a fluorocycline with a fluorine at C-7 and a pyrrolidinoacetamido group at C-9. The FDA approved it as Xerava in 2018 for complicated intra-abdominal infections.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "Benjamin Duggar reports chlortetracycline from Streptomyces aureofaciens."], ["2000s", "Andrew Myers' lab at Harvard develops a fully synthetic route to tetracyclines."], ["2018", "FDA approves Xerava (eravacycline) for complicated intra-abdominal infections."]],
  "cell": "Eravacycline enters bacteria and binds the 30S ribosomal subunit. It sits where aminoacyl-tRNA must dock at the A site, so new amino acids cannot be added to the growing protein chain. Protein synthesis stalls and the bacterium stops growing, which is mainly a bacteriostatic effect. Older tetracyclines fail when bacteria pump them out with Tet efflux proteins or knock them off the ribosome with ribosomal protection proteins such as TetM. Eravacycline's C-7 fluorine and C-9 side chain help it bind more tightly and escape both mechanisms. Because it targets ribosomes rather than cell walls, beta-lactamases, including carbapenemases, do not affect it.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Covers ESBL and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales, Acinetobacter, anaerobes, MRSA and VRE, but not Pseudomonas aeruginosa."], ["Abdomen", "Good tissue penetration supports its use for mixed infections inside the abdomen."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are common, similar to other tetracycline-class drugs."], ["Teeth and bones", "Like other tetracyclines it binds calcium and can stain developing teeth and slow bone growth in fetuses and young children."], ["Pancreas and liver", "Pancreatitis and liver enzyme rises have been reported with the class."], ["Veins", "Infusion site reactions and phlebitis are among the most common effects."]],
  "journey": "Eravacycline is given only by IV infusion. It is highly protein bound and spreads widely into tissues. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP3A4 and flavin monooxygenase, so strong CYP3A4 inducers lower its levels. About half leaves in feces and about one-third in urine. Its long half-life of about 20 hours supports twice-daily dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting reflect local and central gut effects common to tetracyclines. Diarrhea and C. difficile come from changes in gut flora. Tooth discoloration and slowed bone growth happen because the drug binds calcium in mineralizing tissue, which is why it is avoided in late pregnancy and young children. Photosensitivity, pancreatitis and liver injury are class effects. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Eravacycline was among the first antibiotics in its class made by total chemical synthesis rather than by modifying a fermentation product.",
  "src": ["Xerava (eravacycline) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"anidulafungin": {
  "origin": "Anidulafungin comes from a fungus that fights other fungi. In the 1970s researchers found echinocandin B, a lipopeptide made by Aspergillus species, including Aspergillus nidulans. It killed Candida but also broke open red blood cells because of its natural fatty tail. Chemists at Eli Lilly replaced that tail with a different side chain, which removed most of the hemolysis. The compound passed through Versicor and Vicuron Pharmaceuticals and then to Pfizer, which brought it to market. The FDA approved it as Eraxis in 2006, making it the third echinocandin after caspofungin and micafungin.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Echinocandin B is discovered in Aspergillus fermentation."], ["2006", "FDA approves Eraxis (anidulafungin) for candidemia and esophageal candidiasis."]],
  "cell": "Fungal cell walls are held together by beta-(1,3)-D-glucan, a sugar polymer that human cells do not make. Anidulafungin noncompetitively inhibits the enzyme that builds it, beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase, by binding its FKS1 subunit in the fungal membrane. Without new glucan, the wall weakens, especially at growing tips and budding sites. The fungus can no longer resist its own internal pressure and swells and bursts. This is fungicidal against Candida. Against Aspergillus, which grows only at hyphal tips, it stops growth (fungistatic) instead. Mutations in FKS1 hot spots cause echinocandin resistance.",
  "body": [["Candida", "Candida cells lyse because their walls fail, which is why it is used for candidemia and invasive candidiasis."], ["Esophagus", "Treats Candida esophagitis when oral options are not suitable."], ["Liver", "Liver enzymes can rise and, rarely, hepatic dysfunction occurs, so liver tests are followed."], ["Infusion-related", "Rapid infusion can release histamine, causing flushing, rash, itching or low blood pressure."], ["Electrolytes", "Low potassium is among the reported effects."]],
  "journey": "Anidulafungin is given by IV infusion because it is not absorbed by mouth. It is more than 99 percent protein bound and spreads into tissues. Unusually, it is not broken down by liver enzymes. Instead it slowly degrades chemically in the blood to an inactive open-ring peptide, which is then cleared in feces. Very little appears in urine. Its long half-life of about 40 to 50 hours allows once-daily dosing, and neither kidney nor liver disease requires dose changes.",
  "sidefx": "Because humans lack glucan synthase, side effects are mild compared with older antifungals. Histamine release during fast infusion explains flushing, rash and low blood pressure, so slower infusion helps. Liver enzyme elevations and rare hepatic dysfunction can occur. Diarrhea, nausea and low potassium are common reports. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "The echinocandins have been called a penicillin for fungi, because they attack a cell wall that human cells simply do not have.",
  "src": ["Eraxis (anidulafungin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"rezafungin": {
  "origin": "Rezafungin was designed to make echinocandins last longer. Scientists at Cidara Therapeutics in San Diego started from anidulafungin and added a choline group to its side chain. This change made the molecule far more chemically stable, so it no longer broke down quickly in the blood. The result was a drug with a half-life of several days that can be given once a week. The FDA approved it as Rezzayo in 2023 for candidemia and invasive candidiasis in adults with limited or no other options.",
  "timeline": [["2006", "Anidulafungin, the parent molecule, is approved in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves Rezzayo (rezafungin), a once-weekly echinocandin."]],
  "cell": "Like other echinocandins, rezafungin inhibits beta-(1,3)-D-glucan synthase by binding its FKS subunit in the fungal cell membrane. This stops production of beta-(1,3)-D-glucan, a key structural polymer of the fungal wall that human cells lack. With the wall weakened, Candida cells cannot hold their internal pressure and burst. The choline-modified side chain does not change the target but resists the chemical ring-opening that degrades anidulafungin. This keeps levels high, and killing is concentration dependent, so large peaks early in treatment are thought to help.",
  "body": [["Candida", "High, sustained levels kill Candida in the bloodstream and deep tissues."], ["Skin", "Photosensitivity was seen with rezafungin, so sun exposure can cause skin reactions."], ["Infusion-related", "Flushing, warmth, low blood pressure and chest tightness can occur during infusion, likely from histamine release."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises have been reported, as with other echinocandins, so liver tests are followed."], ["Electrolytes and blood", "Low potassium, low magnesium and anemia were common in trials of very sick patients."], ["Nerves", "Tremor appeared in animal studies at high exposure and is noted as a potential risk."]],
  "journey": "Rezafungin is given by IV infusion once a week. It is more than 97 percent protein bound. It is chemically stable and is only minimally metabolized. Most of it leaves slowly in feces. Its half-life of about 130 to 150 hours is what allows weekly dosing, and it has few drug interactions because it is not a CYP substrate.",
  "sidefx": "Because the target is absent in human cells, most toxicity is mild. Infusion reactions likely reflect histamine release. Photosensitivity is a labeled warning. Liver test changes can occur, as with other echinocandins. Tremor was seen in animal studies at high levels. Low potassium, fever and gut upset are common in hospitalized patients receiving it. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "A single weekly rezafungin infusion can replace seven daily infusions of an older echinocandin.",
  "src": ["Rezzayo (rezafungin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"cidofovir": {
  "origin": "Cidofovir came from a famous partnership between two chemists. Antonin Holy at the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry in Prague designed acyclic nucleoside phosphonates, molecules that look like nucleotides but already carry a stable phosphate-like group. Erik De Clercq at the Rega Institute in Belgium tested them against viruses and found strong activity against herpesviruses. Gilead Sciences developed cidofovir, and the FDA approved it as Vistide in 1996 for cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis in people with AIDS. The same chemical family later produced tenofovir, a backbone of HIV treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Antonin Holy and Erik De Clercq develop acyclic nucleoside phosphonates."], ["1996", "FDA approves Vistide (cidofovir) for CMV retinitis in AIDS."]],
  "cell": "Many antivirals, such as ganciclovir and acyclovir, need a viral kinase to add the first phosphate. Cidofovir already carries a phosphonate group, so it skips that step. Host cell kinases add two more phosphates, making cidofovir diphosphate. This active form competes with deoxycytidine triphosphate for the viral DNA polymerase (UL54 in CMV) and can be built into the growing viral DNA, slowing further chain elongation. Viral polymerase prefers it over human polymerases, which gives selectivity. Because it does not need viral UL97 or thymidine kinase, it stays active against ganciclovir-resistant CMV and acyclovir-resistant herpes simplex and varicella-zoster. The diphosphate lasts for days inside cells.",
  "body": [["Eyes", "Stops CMV from destroying the retina in people with AIDS; it can also lower eye pressure and cause inflammation of the iris and uvea."], ["Kidneys", "Proximal tubule cells take up cidofovir through the OAT1 transporter and are damaged, causing protein in urine, rising creatinine and sometimes Fanconi syndrome."], ["Bone marrow", "Neutropenia can occur, so blood counts are checked."], ["Blood chemistry", "Tubular injury can cause metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss."], ["Whole body", "Nausea, fever and rash are common, and many are linked to probenecid given with it."]],
  "journey": "Cidofovir is given by IV infusion because it is poorly absorbed by mouth. It is barely protein bound and is not metabolized by the liver. Cells convert it to the active diphosphate. The kidneys clear the parent drug unchanged through filtration and secretion by OAT1, with a plasma half-life of about 2.6 hours, but the active diphosphate lasts for days inside cells, which allows maintenance doses every two weeks. Probenecid is given with each dose to block OAT1 and reduce kidney uptake, and IV saline is given to help protect the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity is the main danger and carries a boxed warning. Proximal tubule cells actively take up cidofovir through OAT1 and the drug builds up and injures them, which explains protein in the urine, Fanconi syndrome and acute kidney failure. Probenecid and IV fluids reduce this. Neutropenia reflects effects on dividing marrow cells. Ocular hypotony and uveitis reflect injury to the eye's fluid-producing tissues. Animal studies showed carcinogenic and fertility effects. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Holy and De Clercq's acyclic nucleoside phosphonates led to cidofovir, adefovir and tenofovir, one of the most productive chemistry partnerships in antiviral history.",
  "src": ["Vistide (cidofovir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "De Clercq E, Holy A. Nat Rev Drug Discov, 2005"]
},
"peramivir": {
  "origin": "Peramivir came from structure-based drug design. In 1983 Peter Colman and colleagues in Australia solved the 3D structure of influenza neuraminidase, the enzyme that lets new virus particles escape infected cells. That map allowed chemists to design molecules that fit the enzyme's active site. Scientists at BioCryst Pharmaceuticals in Alabama designed peramivir in the 1990s, building it on a cyclopentane ring. It was first used widely under an emergency authorization during the 2009 H1N1 pandemic and was approved in Japan in 2010. The FDA approved it as Rapivab in 2014, the first IV neuraminidase inhibitor in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1983", "Peter Colman's group solves the structure of influenza neuraminidase."], ["1990s", "BioCryst designs peramivir by structure-based methods."], ["2009", "Peramivir is used under emergency authorization during the H1N1 pandemic."], ["2014", "FDA approves Rapivab (peramivir)."]],
  "cell": "Influenza viruses bind sialic acid on cell surfaces to get in. When new virions bud out, they would stick to the same sialic acid and clump on the cell surface. Neuraminidase cuts sialic acid off so they can be released and spread. Peramivir is a transition-state analog of sialic acid. It binds the neuraminidase active site of influenza A and B tightly and competitively, so the enzyme cannot clip sialic acid. New virions stay stuck to the infected cell and to each other and cannot spread through the airway. Peramivir comes off the enzyme slowly, which supports single-dose treatment.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Less virus spreads from cell to cell in the respiratory tract, so symptoms resolve somewhat sooner when started within two days."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea is the most common side effect."], ["Blood", "Neutropenia has been seen, especially in children."], ["Brain and behavior", "Confusion, abnormal behavior and delirium have been reported in patients with influenza on neuraminidase inhibitors, though influenza itself can cause these."], ["Skin", "Rare serious skin reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Peramivir is given as a single IV infusion, which helps people who cannot take oral medicine. It is not strongly protein bound and is not significantly metabolized. About 90 percent leaves unchanged in the urine. Its half-life is about 20 hours, and doses are lowered in kidney disease.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea is the most common effect. Because the drug acts on a viral enzyme with no direct human counterpart, serious side effects are uncommon. Allergic and skin reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome, are immune-mediated. Neuropsychiatric events have been reported with this class, mostly in children, though it is unclear how much comes from the drug versus influenza. Live influenza nasal vaccine may not work if given close to treatment, because the drug blocks the vaccine virus too. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Structure-based design of neuraminidase inhibitors was one of the early triumphs of designing drugs from a protein's 3D shape.",
  "src": ["Rapivab (peramivir) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Varghese JN, Laver WG, Colman PM. Nature, 1983"]
},
"anthrax-immune-globulin": {
  "origin": "Anthrax was the first disease proven to be caused by a specific microbe, when Robert Koch traced it to Bacillus anthracis in 1876. In inhalation anthrax the bacteria release toxins that keep killing even after antibiotics clear the germs. After the 2001 anthrax letter attacks in the US, the government funded new antitoxins. Cangene Corporation, later part of Emergent BioSolutions, collected plasma from people vaccinated with anthrax vaccine and purified their antibodies. Because human trials of anthrax are not possible, the FDA approved Anthrasil in 2015 under the Animal Rule, using animal studies to show it works.",
  "timeline": [["1876", "Robert Koch shows that Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax."], ["2001", "Anthrax letter attacks in the US prompt new antitoxin development."], ["2015", "FDA approves Anthrasil under the Animal Rule."]],
  "cell": "Anthrax toxin has three parts. Protective antigen (PA) binds receptors on host cells (ANTXR1 and ANTXR2), is cut by a host protease, and forms a ring that acts as a delivery pore. Lethal factor and edema factor then ride through this pore into the cell. Lethal factor cuts MAP kinase kinases and disrupts immune cells and blood vessels; edema factor is an adenylyl cyclase that floods cells with cAMP and causes swelling. Anthrasil is polyclonal human IgG from vaccinated donors. Its antibodies bind PA and block it from reaching receptors or forming the pore, so the other two toxins cannot get in. This is passive immunity: it neutralizes toxin already circulating but does not kill bacteria.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Neutralizing toxin helps prevent the vascular leak, shock and fluid around the lungs seen in severe anthrax."], ["Immune cells", "Blocking lethal factor entry protects macrophages and other immune cells so the body can keep fighting."], ["Bacteria", "It does not kill Bacillus anthracis, so antibiotics are always given with it."], ["Blood clotting", "Like other IV immune globulins, it can raise the risk of blood clots."], ["Kidneys and blood", "IV immune globulins can cause kidney injury and hemolysis in some patients."]],
  "journey": "Anthrasil is given by IV infusion. It binds free toxin almost immediately. As human IgG, it stays in the blood and tissue fluid for weeks, and the body breaks it down like its own antibodies through normal protein catabolism. It is not cleared by the kidneys or liver in the usual drug sense.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, back pain, nausea and infusion site reactions are common with immune globulin products. Thrombosis carries a boxed warning; IgG can increase blood thickness and activate clotting. The product contains maltose, which some blood glucose meters read as glucose, giving falsely high readings that can hide true hypoglycemia; this is a boxed warning too. Hemolysis comes from donor antibodies against blood group antigens. Aseptic meningitis, kidney injury, TRALI and anaphylaxis are rare immune globulin risks.",
  "fun": "The FDA Animal Rule, used to approve Anthrasil, lets drugs for deadly threats be approved on animal evidence when human trials would be unethical.",
  "src": ["Anthrasil (anthrax immune globulin intravenous) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Koch R. 1876 anthrax etiology paper"]
},
"obiltoxaximab": {
  "origin": "Obiltoxaximab was built for biodefense. Robert Koch showed in 1876 that Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax, and in the 20th century researchers learned that its toxins, not just the bacteria, kill in inhalation anthrax. After the 2001 anthrax attacks, US agencies funded antitoxins. The first, raxibacumab, was approved in 2012. Elusys Therapeutics, a small New Jersey company, developed obiltoxaximab, a chimeric monoclonal antibody against anthrax protective antigen. It was approved as Anthim in 2016 under the FDA Animal Rule, since human anthrax trials are not possible.",
  "timeline": [["1876", "Robert Koch shows that Bacillus anthracis causes anthrax."], ["2012", "Raxibacumab becomes the first approved anthrax monoclonal antibody."], ["2016", "FDA approves Anthim (obiltoxaximab) under the Animal Rule."]],
  "cell": "Anthrax toxin needs protective antigen (PA) to get its harmful parts, lethal factor and edema factor, into cells. PA normally binds the host receptors ANTXR1 and ANTXR2, is cut by furin, forms a heptamer pore and carries lethal and edema factor inside. Obiltoxaximab is a chimeric IgG1 kappa antibody that binds free PA with high affinity and blocks it from binding its receptors. Without PA attached to cells, the toxin cannot enter. Lethal factor would otherwise cleave MAP kinase kinases and cripple immune cells; edema factor would raise cAMP and cause tissue swelling. Like other antitoxins, it neutralizes toxin but does not kill the bacteria.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels and lungs", "Blocking toxin entry helps prevent the shock, vascular leak and pleural effusions that make inhalation anthrax deadly."], ["Immune system", "Immune cells are spared from lethal factor damage, so the body and antibiotics can clear the infection."], ["Brain", "It does not cross the blood-brain barrier well enough to treat anthrax meningitis."], ["Skin and airways", "Itching, hives, cough and upper respiratory symptoms are among the reported effects."]],
  "journey": "Obiltoxaximab is given as a single IV infusion, with premedication with diphenhydramine recommended. It binds circulating PA quickly. As an IgG antibody it is broken down slowly by normal protein catabolism and has a long half-life of about 15 to 23 days, so one dose covers the danger period while antibiotics work.",
  "sidefx": "Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are the key risks and carry a boxed warning, because the antibody is a foreign protein with mouse-derived parts that the immune system can react to. This is why it is given where reactions can be treated and with antihistamine premedication. Headache, itching, hives, cough and infusion site swelling are common. It has no antibacterial effect, so it is used alongside antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Anthim is stockpiled in the US Strategic National Stockpile for use after a possible anthrax attack.",
  "src": ["Anthim (obiltoxaximab) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"penicillin-g-benzathine": {
  "origin": "Penicillin G was the first penicillin used in patients, after Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that Penicillium mold killed staphylococci and Howard Florey and Ernst Chain's Oxford team purified it around 1940. Penicillin G leaves the body in about half an hour, so early treatment meant many shots. To make one injection last, researchers looked for poorly soluble salts that would dissolve slowly from muscle. In the early 1950s Wyeth introduced benzathine penicillin G, which keeps low levels in the blood for weeks. It became the standard treatment for syphilis and the mainstay of preventing repeat rheumatic fever. Its sole US maker has faced repeated supply shortages.",
  "timeline": [["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin's effect on staphylococci."], ["1940", "Florey and Chain's Oxford team purifies penicillin and shows it protects mice."], ["1950s", "Wyeth introduces long-acting benzathine penicillin G (Bicillin)."], ["2020s", "Repeated shortages of benzathine penicillin affect syphilis treatment in the US."]],
  "cell": "After injection, the benzathine salt dissolves slowly and releases penicillin G. Penicillin G acylates penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan in the bacterial wall, by mimicking the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall precursor. Without cross-links the wall weakens, autolysins keep cutting it, and the dividing bacterium lyses. Killing depends on time above the MIC, not peak height. That is why a low but steady level works for organisms that are extremely sensitive, such as Treponema pallidum and Streptococcus pyogenes. Beta-lactamase enzymes destroy penicillin G, so it does not treat beta-lactamase producers.",
  "body": [["Syphilis bacteria", "Treponema pallidum divides slowly and is very sensitive, so weeks of low penicillin levels cure it; it remains the only recommended treatment for syphilis in pregnancy."], ["Throat", "A single shot treats strep throat and, given monthly, prevents repeat infections that would trigger rheumatic fever."], ["Heart", "Preventing repeat strep infection protects heart valves from rheumatic heart disease."], ["Injection site", "The thick depot causes pain and firm swelling in the muscle."], ["Whole body", "During syphilis treatment, dying spirochetes release inflammatory products that can cause fever and chills within hours (Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction)."]],
  "journey": "Benzathine penicillin is given only by deep IM injection. The salt sits in the muscle as a depot and slowly dissolves, releasing penicillin G that peaks in about 13 to 24 hours. Low levels can be found for about two to four weeks. Once released, penicillin G itself has a half-life of only about 30 minutes and is cleared by kidney tubular secretion, so the slow release from the muscle, not slow elimination, explains the long effect. It is never given IV.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site pain comes from the thick suspension in muscle. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is caused by cytokines released as spirochetes die. Accidental injection into a vessel can block small arteries with crystals, causing Nicolau syndrome (tissue death) or cardiorespiratory arrest, which is why it carries a boxed warning against IV use. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, come from immune responses to penicillin breakdown products; epinephrine is the treatment for anaphylaxis.",
  "fun": "To prevent rheumatic fever from coming back, some patients receive a benzathine penicillin shot every three to four weeks for ten years or longer.",
  "src": ["Bicillin L-A (penicillin G benzathine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines"]
},
"amphotericin-b-deoxycholate": {
  "origin": "Amphotericin B came from a soil sample collected near the Orinoco River in Venezuela. In 1955 researchers at the Squibb Institute for Medical Research isolated two antifungal substances from the bacterium Streptomyces nodosus in that soil and named them amphotericin A and B. Amphotericin B was more active but did not dissolve in water, so it was combined with the bile salt deoxycholate to make an IV form. Introduced in the late 1950s as Fungizone, it was for decades the only reliable treatment for deadly fungal infections. Its toxicity earned it the nickname amphoterrible and later drove development of lipid forms, but the conventional deoxycholate form remains in use.",
  "timeline": [["1955", "Squibb researchers isolate amphotericin B from Streptomyces nodosus from Venezuelan soil."], ["1950s", "Conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate (Fungizone) enters clinical use."], ["1990s", "Lipid formulations are introduced to reduce kidney toxicity."]],
  "cell": "Amphotericin B is a polyene with one water-loving side and one fat-loving side. It binds ergosterol, the main sterol in fungal cell membranes. Several molecules gather around ergosterol and form pores through the membrane. Potassium, magnesium and other small molecules leak out, and the fungal cell dies. It may also pull ergosterol out of the membrane and cause oxidative damage. It binds cholesterol in human membranes much less, but not zero, which explains much of its toxicity, especially in kidney tubule cells. It also triggers cytokine release from immune cells and constricts the afferent arteriole of the kidney.",
  "body": [["Fungi", "Kills Candida, Cryptococcus, Aspergillus, Histoplasma, Mucor and many other fungi by punching holes in their membranes."], ["Kidneys", "Afferent arteriole narrowing lowers filtration, and tubule injury wastes potassium and magnesium and causes distal renal tubular acidosis."], ["Immune system", "Release of TNF and IL-1 from immune cells causes fever, shaking chills and headache during the infusion."], ["Blood", "Reduced erythropoietin from injured kidneys causes a slowly developing anemia."], ["Heart", "Very fast infusion or overdose can cause arrhythmias and cardiac arrest, partly from potassium shifts."], ["Veins", "The infusion irritates veins and causes phlebitis."]],
  "journey": "Amphotericin B is given by slow IV infusion because the gut does not absorb it. It is more than 90 percent bound to proteins and lipoproteins and moves into tissues such as liver, spleen and kidney, though little enters cerebrospinal fluid. It is not much metabolized. It leaves the tissues very slowly, with an initial half-life of about a day and a terminal half-life of about 15 days, and traces appear in urine for weeks. Kidney failure does not greatly change its levels, and dialysis does not remove it.",
  "sidefx": "Fever and rigors come from cytokine release by immune cells; premedication with acetaminophen, antihistamines or hydrocortisone may be used. Nephrotoxicity is the main dose-limiting problem: arteriole narrowing lowers filtration, and pores in tubule cells waste potassium and magnesium. Saline loading reduces this. Anemia follows lower erythropoietin. Overdose can cause cardiac arrest, so the conventional and lipid forms must not be confused. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Its old nickname, amphoterrible, reflects how hard the infusion was on patients, yet it is still the reference drug that newer antifungals are compared against.",
  "src": ["Amphotericin B for Injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ceftobiprole": {
  "origin": "Ceftobiprole was developed to bring a cephalosporin that kills MRSA. MRSA resists nearly all beta-lactams because it carries PBP2a, a cell wall enzyme that ignores them. Scientists at Basilea Pharmaceutica, a Swiss company spun out of Roche, developed ceftobiprole with a side chain that fits into PBP2a. It was approved in Canada and several European countries around 2008 but stalled in the US for years. After new trials, the FDA approved it as Zevtera in 2024 for Staphylococcus aureus bloodstream infections, acute bacterial skin infections and community-acquired pneumonia.",
  "timeline": [["1945", "Giuseppe Brotzu discovers the cephalosporin-producing mold in Sardinia."], ["2008", "Ceftobiprole is first approved outside the US, starting with Canada."], ["2024", "FDA approves Zevtera (ceftobiprole medocaril)."]],
  "cell": "Ceftobiprole is given as ceftobiprole medocaril, a water-soluble prodrug that plasma esterases quickly convert to active ceftobiprole. The active drug binds penicillin-binding proteins and blocks transpeptidation, the cross-linking of peptidoglycan. Its large side chain fits into PBP2a of MRSA, whose active site is normally closed to beta-lactams. It also binds PBP2x of penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae and PBP3 of gram-negative rods. Without cross-links, the cell wall fails and the bacteria lyse. It remains vulnerable to ESBLs and carbapenemases.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills MSSA, MRSA, streptococci, many Enterobacterales and some Pseudomonas aeruginosa."], ["Bloodstream", "Approved for Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia, including right-sided endocarditis."], ["Brain", "Like other cephalosporins it can cause seizures, especially when kidney function is poor and levels build up."], ["Mouth and gut", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and an altered sense of taste are common."], ["Electrolytes", "Low sodium has been reported in treated patients, so sodium levels are watched."], ["Gut flora", "Disruption of normal bacteria can lead to C. difficile."]],
  "journey": "Ceftobiprole medocaril is given by IV infusion, and esterases in the blood convert it to active ceftobiprole almost right away. Only about 16 percent is protein bound. Little more metabolism happens. The kidneys clear it, mainly by filtration, largely unchanged, with a half-life of about three hours. Doses are adjusted for kidney function.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and taste changes are the most common effects. Seizures reflect the beta-lactam effect on GABA-A signaling and are more likely when kidney failure raises levels. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are shared with other beta-lactams. C. difficile results from loss of normal gut flora. There is no antidote; hemodialysis can remove some drug.",
  "fun": "Ceftobiprole waited about 16 years between its first approval abroad and its US approval.",
  "src": ["Zevtera (ceftobiprole medocaril) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
,
"onasemnogene-abeparvovec": {
  "origin": "Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) was once the leading genetic cause of death in infants. In 1995 a team in Paris led by Judith Melki identified the cause: loss of the SMN1 gene, which makes survival motor neuron protein. Researchers then found that AAV9, a harmless adeno-associated virus, could cross into the spinal cord after IV injection. At Nationwide Children's Hospital in Ohio, Jerry Mendell and Brian Kaspar used it to carry a working SMN1 gene into motor neurons, and an early trial showed infants surviving and sitting who otherwise would not. The company AveXis, bought by Novartis in 2018, developed it as Zolgensma, approved by the FDA in 2019 as a one-time IV gene therapy. An intrathecal version, Itvisma, was later approved.",
  "timeline": [["1995", "Judith Melki's team identifies SMN1 as the SMA gene."], ["2017", "Mendell and colleagues publish striking results of gene therapy in infants with SMA type 1."], ["2018", "Novartis acquires AveXis."], ["2019", "FDA approves Zolgensma, a one-time IV gene therapy for SMA."]],
  "cell": "In SMA, both copies of SMN1 are deleted or mutated. The nearby SMN2 gene makes mostly an unstable, shortened protein, so motor neurons in the anterior horn of the spinal cord lack enough SMN protein, which is needed to assemble the small nuclear ribonucleoproteins that run mRNA splicing. These neurons slowly die. Onasemnogene abeparvovec is a non-replicating AAV9 capsid holding a full-length human SMN1 gene with a strong, continuous promoter. After infusion the capsid crosses the blood-brain barrier, enters motor neurons and delivers its DNA to the nucleus. The DNA stays there as an episome, mostly without integrating into chromosomes, and makes SMN protein steadily. Because neurons do not divide, the episome is not diluted, so one dose can last.",
  "body": [["Motor neurons", "Steady SMN protein stops further loss of anterior horn cells, so muscles keep their nerve supply."], ["Muscles and breathing", "Treated infants reach milestones like head control and sitting and are much more likely to survive without permanent breathing support, especially when treated before symptoms."], ["Liver", "The liver takes up much of the vector, and the immune response to infected liver cells can cause enzyme rises and even acute liver failure; steroids are given around the infusion."], ["Blood and kidneys", "Thrombocytopenia and thrombotic microangiopathy, with clots in small vessels and kidney injury, have been reported in the weeks after infusion."], ["Heart", "Troponin rises have been seen, so heart markers are checked."]],
  "journey": "Zolgensma is a single IV infusion given over about an hour. The vector spreads through the blood to the liver, heart, muscle and, importantly, the brain and spinal cord. It is not metabolized like a drug; instead its DNA persists inside cells as an episome and makes SMN protein for the long term. Vector DNA is shed in stool, saliva and urine for weeks, so caregivers use careful hand hygiene with diapers. Patients are tested for antibodies to AAV9 beforehand, because existing antibodies would neutralize the vector.",
  "sidefx": "Most serious side effects come from the immune system reacting to the viral capsid and the cells that take it up. Infected liver cells are attacked, causing aminotransferase rises and, rarely, acute liver failure, which carries a boxed warning; corticosteroids are given before and after the infusion to dampen this. Complement activation by the capsid is thought to drive thrombotic microangiopathy with low platelets and kidney injury. Vomiting is common. There is no way to remove the vector once given.",
  "fun": "When approved, Zolgensma was one of the most expensive drugs ever sold, priced at about two million US dollars for a single infusion.",
  "src": ["Zolgensma (onasemnogene abeparvovec-xioi) FDA label", "Lefebvre S, et al. Cell, 1995", "Mendell JR, et al. N Engl J Med, 2017"]
},
"loxapine-inhaled": {
  "origin": "Loxapine is an older antipsychotic of the dibenzoxazepine family, developed in the 1960s and approved in the US as an oral capsule (Loxitane) in the 1970s. Decades later, Alexza Pharmaceuticals in California built the Staccato system, a single-use device that heats a thin film of drug in a fraction of a second to make an aerosol a person breathes in. Inhaled drug reaches the blood almost as fast as an IV injection without needles. The FDA approved inhaled loxapine as Adasuve in 2012 for acute agitation in schizophrenia or bipolar I disorder, under a restricted program because it can cause bronchospasm.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Oral loxapine is approved in the US as Loxitane."], ["2012", "FDA approves Adasuve, inhaled loxapine, with a restricted program for bronchospasm risk."]],
  "cell": "Loxapine blocks dopamine D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, which reduces overactive dopamine signaling linked to agitation and psychosis. Blocking these Gi-coupled receptors removes dopamine's brake on adenylyl cyclase in target neurons. It also blocks serotonin 5-HT2A receptors fairly strongly for a typical antipsychotic. Blocking H1 histamine receptors causes sedation, alpha-1 blockade lowers blood pressure, and muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth. D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway can cause extrapyramidal symptoms, and in the pituitary it raises prolactin. Part of loxapine is converted to amoxapine, a tricyclic antidepressant.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Rapid D2 and 5-HT2A blockade calms agitation within minutes, and H1 blockade adds sedation."], ["Airways", "The aerosol can irritate the airways and trigger bronchospasm, especially in asthma or COPD, which is why a rescue bronchodilator must be on hand."], ["Blood pressure", "Alpha-1 blockade can cause low blood pressure and dizziness on standing."], ["Movement", "D2 blockade in the basal ganglia can cause dystonia, stiffness or restlessness."], ["Mouth", "A bad or bitter taste is the most common side effect, from drug deposited in the mouth."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled loxapine is breathed in through the device in one deep breath. The aerosol deposits deep in the lungs, and the drug crosses the huge alveolar surface, reaching peak blood levels in about two minutes. Calming begins within about ten minutes. It is highly protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP1A2, CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to hydroxylated metabolites and amoxapine, which leave in urine and feces. Its half-life is about six to eight hours.",
  "sidefx": "Bronchospasm is the defining danger and carries a boxed warning: the aerosol irritates sensitive airways, so it is given only in enrolled settings that can treat respiratory distress. A boxed warning also covers higher death rates in older people with dementia-related psychosis, as with all antipsychotics. D2 blockade explains extrapyramidal symptoms and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Alpha-1 blockade causes hypotension, and the drug lowers the seizure threshold. Bronchospasm is treated with an inhaled beta-agonist.",
  "fun": "The Staccato device heats the drug film for less than a second, turning it into vapor that condenses into particles small enough to reach the alveoli.",
  "src": ["Adasuve (loxapine) inhalation powder FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"doxapram": {
  "origin": "Doxapram is an analeptic, a drug that stimulates breathing. Its story builds on the Belgian physiologist Corneille Heymans, who won the 1938 Nobel Prize for showing that the carotid and aortic bodies sense blood chemistry and drive breathing. In the early 1960s chemists at A. H. Robins in Richmond, Virginia, developed doxapram, and it was marketed as Dopram. It was used for breathing depression after anesthesia and drug overdose. Its role has shrunk because ventilation and specific reversal agents such as naloxone are safer and more reliable.",
  "timeline": [["1938", "Corneille Heymans wins the Nobel Prize for work on carotid and aortic body reflexes."], ["1960s", "A. H. Robins develops doxapram, marketed as Dopram."]],
  "cell": "At lower levels doxapram acts mainly on the peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid bodies, and to a lesser degree the aortic bodies. In carotid glomus cells it is thought to block oxygen-sensitive potassium channels, especially TASK-type channels. With fewer potassium channels open, the glomus cells depolarize, calcium enters, and they release transmitters that fire the carotid sinus nerve. The brainstem reads this as low oxygen and increases breathing, mostly by raising tidal volume. At higher levels doxapram directly stimulates the medullary respiratory center and other parts of the brain and spinal cord. It also causes catecholamine release, which raises blood pressure and heart rate.",
  "body": [["Lungs and breathing", "Tidal volume and minute ventilation rise within seconds, which can improve breathing after anesthesia."], ["Heart and vessels", "Catecholamine release raises blood pressure and heart rate and can trigger arrhythmias."], ["Brain", "Broader central stimulation causes arousal, agitation and, at high levels, seizures."], ["Airways", "Cough, laryngospasm and bronchospasm can occur."], ["Skin and body temperature", "Flushing, sweating and sometimes hyperthermia come from sympathetic activation."], ["Bladder", "Urinary retention has been reported, likely from sympathetic stimulation of the bladder outlet."]],
  "journey": "Doxapram is given IV as an injection or infusion. Breathing rises within 20 to 40 seconds and peaks in one to two minutes, but a single injection wears off in about 5 to 12 minutes as the drug redistributes. The liver breaks it down extensively, including to ketodoxapram, an active metabolite, and the products leave in urine. Its elimination half-life is about 3.4 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from stimulating the nervous system beyond the breathing centers. Catecholamine release causes hypertension, tachycardia and arrhythmias. Wider brain stimulation causes agitation, tremor and seizures. It is risky in people with coronary disease, uncontrolled hypertension or seizure disorders, and with MAO inhibitors or sympathomimetics. It does not reverse opioid or sedative effects and is not a substitute for ventilation. There is no antidote; seizures are treated with short-acting anticonvulsants.",
  "fun": "Heymans showed the carotid body's role using dogs whose heads were perfused by the blood of another dog, separating the sensor from the brain.",
  "src": ["Dopram (doxapram hydrochloride) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1938 record"]
},
"emapalumab": {
  "origin": "Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) was first described in 1952 by the Scottish doctors James Farquhar and Albert Claireaux as a familial disease of infants. In HLH, killer T cells and NK cells cannot clear infected cells, so they keep pumping out interferon gamma, which drives macrophages into a destructive frenzy. Researchers at the Swiss company Novimmune developed emapalumab, a fully human antibody against interferon gamma, and the program was later taken over by Sobi. The FDA approved it as Gamifant in 2018, the first drug approved specifically for primary HLH. Its label was later expanded to macrophage activation syndrome in Still disease.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Farquhar and Claireaux describe familial hemophagocytic reticulosis (HLH)."], ["2018", "FDA approves Gamifant (emapalumab), the first drug for primary HLH."], ["2025", "Gamifant label expands to macrophage activation syndrome in Still disease."]],
  "cell": "In primary HLH, gene defects such as PRF1 (perforin) mutations prevent cytotoxic T cells and NK cells from killing infected or activated cells. The immune response never shuts off, and these cells keep releasing interferon gamma. Interferon gamma binds the IFNGR1/IFNGR2 receptor on macrophages, activates JAK1/JAK2 and STAT1, and turns on genes that make macrophages eat blood cells (hemophagocytosis) and release more cytokines and the chemokine CXCL9. Emapalumab is a human IgG1 antibody that binds interferon gamma, both free and receptor-bound, and neutralizes it. This breaks the loop: macrophage activation falls, CXCL9 and ferritin drop, and fever and cytopenias improve, bridging patients to stem cell transplant.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing interferon gamma calms the cytokine storm, lowering fever, ferritin, soluble CD25 and CXCL9."], ["Bone marrow and blood", "With less hemophagocytosis, red cells, white cells and platelets recover."], ["Liver and spleen", "Less macrophage infiltration lets the enlarged liver and spleen shrink and liver tests improve."], ["Infection defense", "Interferon gamma is key against intracellular germs, so blocking it raises the risk of tuberculosis, histoplasmosis, CMV, adenovirus and herpes zoster."], ["Blood pressure", "High blood pressure is a common reported effect."]],
  "journey": "Emapalumab is given by IV infusion several times a week at first. As an IgG antibody it is broken down by normal protein catabolism and by binding to its target. In healthy people its half-life is about 22 days, but in active HLH the large amount of interferon gamma speeds its clearance, shortening the half-life to as little as a few days, so doses are adjusted to response.",
  "sidefx": "The main risk is infection, because interferon gamma activates macrophages to kill intracellular microbes. Without it, latent tuberculosis can reactivate and fungal and viral infections can spread; patients are screened for TB and given prophylaxis against herpes zoster, Pneumocystis and fungi. Live vaccines are avoided. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the antibody. Hypertension, fever and low potassium are common. There is no reversal agent.",
  "fun": "Interferon gamma was originally named immune interferon because it was made by stimulated immune cells rather than by virus-infected cells.",
  "src": ["Gamifant (emapalumab-lzsg) FDA label", "Farquhar JW, Claireaux AE. Arch Dis Child, 1952", "Locatelli F, et al. N Engl J Med, 2020"]
},
"cyclopentolate": {
  "origin": "Cyclopentolate is a synthetic cousin of atropine. Atropine comes from deadly nightshade, Atropa belladonna, whose name means beautiful lady: women in Renaissance Italy were said to use its juice to widen their pupils. Atropine was isolated from the plant in the 1830s, but it dilates the pupil and blocks focusing for a week or more. In the 1950s chemists made cyclopentolate, a shorter-acting antimuscarinic. Sold as Cyclogyl, it became a standard eye drop for dilating pupils and relaxing the focusing muscle during eye exams, especially to measure refraction in children.",
  "timeline": [["1830s", "Atropine is isolated from Atropa belladonna."], ["1950s", "Cyclopentolate is developed as a shorter-acting synthetic antimuscarinic eye drop."]],
  "cell": "The iris has two muscles: the sphincter pupillae, which constricts the pupil, and the dilator pupillae, which widens it. The sphincter and the ciliary muscle, which changes lens shape for near focus, are run by parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors. M3 activates Gq, phospholipase C and IP3, raising calcium and causing contraction. Cyclopentolate competitively blocks these M3 receptors. The sphincter relaxes and the sympathetic dilator acts unopposed, so the pupil widens (mydriasis). The ciliary muscle relaxes and cannot focus up close (cycloplegia), which reveals the eye's true refractive error.",
  "body": [["Iris", "The pupil dilates within 30 to 60 minutes, which lets the examiner see the retina."], ["Ciliary muscle", "Focusing is paralyzed, which gives an accurate glasses measurement in children and relieves painful ciliary spasm in uveitis."], ["Vision", "Blurred near vision and light sensitivity last until the drug wears off, often within a day."], ["Eye pressure", "A widened pupil can block the drainage angle in narrow-angle eyes and trigger angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Brain", "Absorbed drug can cross into the brain and cause restlessness, confusion, ataxia or hallucinations, especially in young children."], ["Whole body", "Absorbed drug can cause fast heart rate, dry mouth, flushing and fever."]],
  "journey": "Cyclopentolate is applied as eye drops. It passes through the cornea into the iris and ciliary body. Some drains through the tear duct into the nose, where it can be absorbed into the blood, which explains whole-body effects. Pressing on the inner corner of the eye reduces this. Recovery from cycloplegia usually happens within about 24 hours, though dilation can last longer. Its systemic metabolism is not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Stinging on instillation is local irritation. Blurred vision and light sensitivity are the direct, expected effects of blocking M3 in the eye. Angle-closure glaucoma can occur in eyes with narrow angles because the dilated iris crowds the drainage angle. Systemic antimuscarinic effects, including tachycardia, flushing, fever, dry mouth and CNS effects such as hallucinations and seizures, come from absorption through the nasal mucosa and are most likely in infants and small children. Physostigmine reverses central anticholinergic toxicity, but care is usually supportive.",
  "fun": "Antimuscarinic toxicity is remembered as hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat and mad as a hatter, and cyclopentolate drops can cause a mild version in small children.",
  "src": ["Cyclogyl (cyclopentolate) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"fluorescein": {
  "origin": "Fluorescein was born in a chemistry lab. In 1871 the German chemist Adolf von Baeyer heated phthalic anhydride with resorcinol and made a dye that glows bright yellow-green under light. In 1882 Paul Ehrlich used it to trace fluid in the eye. Its big clinical moment came in 1961, when Harold Novotny and David Alvis, then medical students in Indiana, published the first method of fluorescein angiography, photographing the dye as it flowed through retinal vessels. Fluorescein strips and drops also became the standard way to find corneal scratches. Today IV forms such as AK-Fluor and Fluorescite are used for angiography.",
  "timeline": [["1871", "Adolf von Baeyer synthesizes fluorescein."], ["1882", "Paul Ehrlich uses fluorescein to study fluid in the eye."], ["1961", "Novotny and Alvis describe fluorescein angiography of the retina."]],
  "cell": "Fluorescein is not a receptor drug; it works through light. The molecule absorbs blue light at about 465 to 490 nm and emits yellow-green light at about 520 to 530 nm. In angiography, IV fluorescein travels through the blood, and a camera with filters records it filling the choroid, then retinal arteries and veins. Healthy retinal vessels have tight junctions, the blood-retinal barrier, that keep fluorescein inside. Where vessels are leaky, such as in diabetic retinopathy or macular degeneration, the dye seeps out and glows. On the eye surface, intact epithelium repels it, but where cells are missing, such as an abrasion or ulcer, the dye pools and glows under cobalt-blue light.",
  "body": [["Eyes", "Shows retinal vessel leaks, blockages and abnormal new vessels in angiography, and outlines corneal scratches and ulcers on the surface."], ["Skin", "IV dye turns the skin yellow for several hours."], ["Kidneys and urine", "The kidneys clear the dye, turning urine bright yellow-orange for a day or more."], ["Stomach and brain", "Nausea and vomiting are common shortly after IV injection."], ["Immune system", "Hives, itching and rarely anaphylaxis or bronchospasm can occur after IV use."], ["Veins and tissues", "If IV dye leaks out of the vein, it can irritate and injure tissue."]],
  "journey": "For angiography fluorescein is injected into an arm vein and reaches the retina within about 7 to 14 seconds. It is moderately bound to plasma proteins. The liver and kidneys convert it to fluorescein glucuronide, which also fluoresces. Both are cleared by the kidneys. Free fluorescein has a half-life of under an hour, the glucuronide several hours. Topical strips and drops stay on the eye surface, and only tiny amounts reach the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting after IV injection are the most common effects and usually pass quickly. Yellow skin and urine are simply the dye being carried and excreted. Hives and itching reflect mild hypersensitivity, and rare anaphylaxis, bronchospasm and cardiac arrest are why emergency equipment is kept nearby. Extravasation can cause painful tissue injury because the solution is alkaline. Allergic reactions are treated with the usual emergency measures, including epinephrine for anaphylaxis.",
  "fun": "Fluorescein was used to turn the Chicago River green for Saint Patrick's Day in the 1960s, and it is still used to trace underground water flow.",
  "src": ["AK-Fluor and Fluorescite (fluorescein injection) FDA labels", "Novotny HR, Alvis DL. Circulation, 1961", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"lidocaine-ophthalmic-gel": {
  "origin": "Eye anesthesia started with cocaine: in 1884 the Viennese ophthalmologist Karl Koller showed that cocaine drops numbed the eye, which opened the field of local anesthesia. Cocaine was toxic and addictive, so chemists looked for safer options. In 1943 Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist in Stockholm synthesized lidocaine, the first amide local anesthetic, and the Swedish company Astra marketed it as Xylocaine in 1948. Much later, a thick gel form for the eye was developed. Akten, lidocaine hydrochloride ophthalmic gel 3.5 percent, was approved by the FDA in 2008 to numb the eye surface during eye procedures.",
  "timeline": [["1884", "Karl Koller shows cocaine numbs the eye."], ["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine."], ["1948", "Astra launches lidocaine as Xylocaine."], ["2008", "FDA approves Akten lidocaine ophthalmic gel."]],
  "cell": "Lidocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in the sensory nerve endings of the cornea and conjunctiva, which belong to the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. The uncharged form crosses the nerve membrane, then picks up a proton inside the cell. The charged form binds a site inside the channel pore, reaching it from the cytoplasmic side, and binds best to open and inactivated channels. With sodium unable to rush in, the nerve cannot fire an action potential, so pain signals do not reach the brain. The corneal nerves are small and close to the surface, so they are blocked quickly. The gel holds lidocaine against the eye longer than drops would.",
  "body": [["Cornea", "The cornea, one of the most densely innervated tissues in the body, becomes numb within about a minute, allowing injections and procedures without pain."], ["Conjunctiva", "The conjunctiva is also numbed, and some redness can appear from the gel and the procedure."], ["Blink reflex and protection", "Loss of corneal sensation also removes the warning pain of scratches or foreign bodies until it wears off."], ["Corneal surface", "Repeated anesthetic exposure can damage and slow healing of the corneal epithelium."], ["Whole body", "Very little reaches the blood, so systemic toxicity is unlikely with ocular use."]],
  "journey": "The gel is placed on the eye surface and lidocaine soaks into the corneal and conjunctival nerve endings. Numbness starts within about a minute and is renewed by reapplying. Only a tiny fraction reaches the blood through conjunctival vessels and the tear duct. Any lidocaine that does is broken down by the liver through CYP1A2 and CYP3A4 to MEGX and glycinexylidide and cleared by the kidneys, with a systemic half-life of about 1.5 to 2 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Redness, burning and headache are the most common reported effects. Loss of corneal sensation means the patient will not feel a scratch or foreign body, so the eye is protected until feeling returns. Anesthetics can damage the corneal epithelium, which is why they are used for procedures, not for ongoing home pain relief. Hypersensitivity is rare. Because so little enters the blood, local anesthetic systemic toxicity is unlikely; if it occurred, lipid emulsion is the standard rescue for local anesthetic toxicity.",
  "fun": "Koller's 1884 cocaine discovery was presented at a Heidelberg meeting by a colleague because Koller could not afford the trip.",
  "src": ["Akten (lidocaine hydrochloride ophthalmic gel) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"phenylephrine-ketorolac-intraocular": {
  "origin": "During cataract and lens replacement surgery, the pupil tends to shrink as the operation goes on, partly from surgical trauma that releases prostaglandins. A small pupil makes surgery harder and riskier. Omeros Corporation in Seattle combined two well-known drugs, the alpha-1 agonist phenylephrine and the NSAID ketorolac, into a solution added to the irrigation fluid that flows through the eye during surgery. The FDA approved it as Omidria in 2014 to keep the pupil dilated during surgery and to reduce early pain after surgery. Each of its parts had a long history: phenylephrine as a decongestant and pressor, and ketorolac as an injectable NSAID first approved in the US in the late 1980s.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Ketorolac is first approved in the US."], ["2014", "FDA approves Omidria for use in ocular irrigating solution during lens surgery."]],
  "cell": "The two parts act on different pupil muscles and pathways. Phenylephrine stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on the iris dilator (radial) muscle. Alpha-1 is Gq-coupled, activating phospholipase C and raising calcium, so the dilator contracts and the pupil widens. Ketorolac inhibits cyclooxygenase-1 and -2. Surgical trauma releases arachidonic acid, which COX turns into prostaglandins such as PGE2. These prostaglandins cause the iris sphincter to constrict independently of cholinergic nerves, and they promote inflammation and pain. Blocking their synthesis keeps the pupil from shrinking and lowers early postoperative pain.",
  "body": [["Iris", "The pupil stays wide during surgery because the dilator is stimulated and prostaglandin-driven constriction is blocked."], ["Eye tissues", "Less prostaglandin production lowers early inflammation and pain after surgery."], ["Eye pressure", "Increased eye pressure and inflammation in the anterior chamber can occur after surgery."], ["Lens capsule", "Posterior capsule opacification has been reported after the operation."], ["Blood pressure", "Any phenylephrine that is absorbed can raise blood pressure by activating vascular alpha-1 receptors."]],
  "journey": "Omidria is diluted into the irrigating solution and flows through the front of the eye during surgery, so it works right where it is needed. Only small amounts reach the bloodstream. Absorbed phenylephrine is broken down by monoamine oxidase and sulfation, with a systemic half-life of about two to three hours. Absorbed ketorolac is metabolized by the liver through glucuronidation and hydroxylation, with a half-life of about five hours. Both are cleared by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Eye irritation, inflammation, increased eye pressure and capsule opacification are the most common reported effects and may reflect the surgery as well as the drug. Absorbed phenylephrine can raise blood pressure through vascular alpha-1 receptors, which matters in people with heart disease. Ketorolac carries the NSAID cross-sensitivity risk in people allergic to aspirin or other NSAIDs. Serious hypersensitivity is rare. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Omidria is not dropped on the eye or injected directly; it is mixed into the fluid that washes through the eye during the entire operation.",
  "src": ["Omidria (phenylephrine and ketorolac intraocular solution) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"exagamglogene-autotemcel": {
  "origin": "Exagamglogene autotemcel, or exa-cel, is the first approved medicine made with CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing. In 2012 Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier showed that the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 system could be programmed to cut DNA at chosen sites, work that won the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Separately, Stuart Orkin's lab at Boston Children's Hospital found that the gene BCL11A shuts off fetal hemoglobin after birth, and later that a red cell-specific enhancer controls it. CRISPR Therapeutics and Vertex Pharmaceuticals used these discoveries to edit patients' own stem cells. In 2019 Victoria Gray of Mississippi became the first US sickle cell patient treated. The FDA approved it as Casgevy for sickle cell disease in December 2023 and for transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia in January 2024.",
  "timeline": [["2008", "Orkin's lab identifies BCL11A as a repressor of fetal hemoglobin."], ["2012", "Doudna and Charpentier describe programmable CRISPR-Cas9 DNA cutting."], ["2019", "Victoria Gray becomes the first US sickle cell patient treated with exa-cel."], ["2023", "FDA approves Casgevy for sickle cell disease."], ["2024", "FDA approves Casgevy for transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia."]],
  "cell": "Before birth, red cells make fetal hemoglobin (HbF, two alpha and two gamma chains). After birth, the transcription factor BCL11A shuts off gamma-globin and switches cells to adult beta-globin. In sickle cell disease the beta-globin is mutated, so deoxygenated HbS polymerizes and sickles red cells; in beta-thalassemia too little beta-globin is made. For exa-cel, a patient's own CD34+ stem cells are collected and edited outside the body with Cas9 and a guide RNA targeting the erythroid-specific enhancer of BCL11A. The cut disrupts the enhancer, so BCL11A falls only in red cell precursors. Gamma-globin turns back on, HbF rises, HbF blocks HbS polymerization and stands in for missing beta-globin.",
  "body": [["Red blood cells", "Many red cells now contain high fetal hemoglobin, so they resist sickling and live longer."], ["Blood vessels and pain", "Most sickle cell patients in trials stopped having severe vaso-occlusive pain crises."], ["Transfusion needs", "Most thalassemia patients in trials stopped needing regular transfusions."], ["Bone marrow", "Busulfan conditioning wipes out the marrow to make room for edited cells, causing weeks of low blood counts until engraftment."], ["Mouth and gut", "Mucositis, nausea and appetite loss mainly come from busulfan."], ["Fertility", "Busulfan conditioning can cause infertility, so fertility preservation is discussed beforehand."]],
  "journey": "The process takes months. Stem cells are mobilized and collected from the blood, edited in a manufacturing facility, and frozen. The patient then gets high-dose busulfan to clear the marrow, and the edited cells are infused IV. They home to the bone marrow and begin making new blood cells. Neutrophils recover around four weeks, and fetal hemoglobin rises over months. Because the edit is permanent in the stem cells, the effect is expected to last for life.",
  "sidefx": "Most short-term side effects come from busulfan conditioning, not the edited cells. Busulfan damages fast-dividing cells, causing mucositis, nausea, low blood counts and infections during the neutropenic period. Platelet recovery can be slow, raising bleeding risk. Busulfan can also cause infertility. Engraftment failure, while rare, would leave the patient without blood cell production. Long-term risks of off-target edits are being studied in follow-up.",
  "fun": "Exa-cel mimics a natural condition, hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin, in which people with sickle cell genes have mild disease because they keep making fetal hemoglobin.",
  "src": ["Casgevy (exagamglogene autotemcel) FDA label", "Frangoul H, et al. N Engl J Med, 2021", "Jinek M, et al. Science, 2012"]
},
"lovotibeglogene-autotemcel": {
  "origin": "Lovotibeglogene autotemcel, or lovo-cel, grew out of decades of work on gene addition using lentiviruses, viral vectors derived from HIV and stripped of their ability to cause disease. Researchers led by Philippe Leboulch developed a lentiviral vector carrying a modified beta-globin gene with an anti-sickling change. The company bluebird bio developed it under the earlier name LentiGlobin. The FDA approved it as Lyfgenia on the same day in December 2023 as exa-cel, giving sickle cell disease its first two gene therapies. It carries a boxed warning for blood cancers.",
  "timeline": [["2010", "A thalassemia patient in France becomes transfusion independent after lentiviral beta-globin gene therapy."], ["2023", "FDA approves Lyfgenia (lovotibeglogene autotemcel) for sickle cell disease."]],
  "cell": "In sickle cell disease, a single mutation in beta-globin (glutamic acid to valine at position 6) makes HbS polymerize when it loses oxygen, which deforms red cells. For lovo-cel, a patient's own CD34+ stem cells are collected and exposed to the BB305 lentiviral vector. The vector inserts, permanently and at varying sites in the genome, a gene for beta-A-T87Q-globin. This is an adult beta-globin with a threonine changed to glutamine at position 87, the same spot that makes gamma-globin resist polymer formation. Red cells made from these stem cells contain HbA-T87Q, which does not join sickle polymers and dilutes HbS, so fewer cells sickle and hemolysis falls.",
  "body": [["Red blood cells", "New red cells carry anti-sickling HbA-T87Q, so they sickle less and survive longer."], ["Blood vessels and pain", "Most treated patients had far fewer vaso-occlusive events in trials."], ["Bone marrow", "Busulfan conditioning clears the marrow so modified cells can engraft, leaving weeks of low counts."], ["Mouth and gut", "Stomatitis and gut effects mainly come from busulfan."], ["Blood cancer risk", "Some patients developed AML or MDS, which led to a boxed warning and lifelong monitoring."]],
  "journey": "Stem cells are collected from the blood after mobilization, transduced with the vector in a central facility, and frozen. The patient receives busulfan conditioning, then the modified cells are infused IV. They home to the marrow and begin making blood. Neutrophils return in about three weeks, and HbA-T87Q rises over months. Because the gene is integrated into stem cell DNA, its effect is expected to be lifelong.",
  "sidefx": "Short-term side effects mostly come from busulfan, which harms fast-dividing cells and causes stomatitis, low blood counts, infections and possible infertility. Platelet recovery can be delayed. The boxed warning for hematologic malignancy reflects AML and MDS cases in trial patients; possible causes include busulfan, the stress of sickle cell disease on stem cells, and insertion of the vector near cancer-related genes. Patients are followed with blood counts for at least 15 years.",
  "fun": "The T87Q change borrows a trick from fetal gamma-globin, whose amino acid at that position helps it resist joining sickle hemoglobin polymers.",
  "src": ["Lyfgenia (lovotibeglogene autotemcel) FDA label", "Kanter J, et al. N Engl J Med, 2022", "Cavazzana-Calvo M, et al. Nature, 2010"]
}
,
"etranacogene-dezaparvovec": {
  "origin": "Hemophilia B, also called Christmas disease after Stephen Christmas, the first patient described with it in 1952, comes from too little clotting factor IX. For decades patients needed frequent factor infusions. In 2009 Italian researchers led by Paolo Simioni in Padua described a family with blood clots caused by a factor IX variant, R338L, that is about eight times more active than normal. This Padua variant made gene therapy far more efficient. The Dutch company uniQure built an AAV5 vector carrying the Padua factor IX gene and licensed it to CSL Behring. The FDA approved it as Hemgenix in 2022, the first gene therapy for hemophilia B, at a launch price of about 3.5 million US dollars.",
  "timeline": [["1952", "Christmas disease (hemophilia B) is described, named after patient Stephen Christmas."], ["2009", "Simioni and colleagues describe the hyperactive factor IX Padua variant."], ["2022", "FDA approves Hemgenix, the first gene therapy for hemophilia B."]],
  "cell": "Factor IX is a vitamin K-dependent protein made in the liver. When activated, factor IXa joins factor VIIIa on platelet surfaces to activate factor X, a key step in making thrombin. In hemophilia B this step is weak, so clots form poorly. Etranacogene dezaparvovec is a non-replicating AAV5 capsid carrying a codon-optimized human factor IX gene with the Padua R338L change, driven by a liver-specific promoter. After IV infusion the capsid enters hepatocytes and the DNA travels to the nucleus, where it stays mostly as an episome rather than joining chromosomes. Hepatocytes then make and secrete Padua factor IX, which is several times more active per molecule, so even modest protein levels restore useful clotting.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Steady factor IX activity improves thrombin generation, so spontaneous bleeds fall and most patients stop routine factor infusions."], ["Joints", "Fewer bleeds into joints help prevent the joint damage that defines severe hemophilia."], ["Liver", "Immune responses to transduced liver cells can raise ALT, and corticosteroids may be needed to protect expression."], ["Immune system", "Infusion reactions and flu-like symptoms can occur in the hours to days after infusion."], ["Cancer risk", "Rare vector integration into DNA raises a theoretical risk of liver cancer, so liver imaging is followed in people with risk factors."]],
  "journey": "Hemgenix is given once by IV infusion. The AAV5 capsid is taken up mainly by the liver. Its DNA persists in hepatocytes and factor IX activity rises over weeks to months, then holds steady for years in trials. Vector DNA is shed in blood, semen and other fluids for a time. Because adult hepatocytes divide slowly, the episomes are not quickly lost, but liver injury or regrowth could reduce expression. The therapy cannot be repeated with the same capsid, because the body makes lasting antibodies against AAV5.",
  "sidefx": "ALT rises are the most common lab effect and reflect immune T cells attacking liver cells that display capsid pieces; steroids are used if enzymes climb. Headache, fatigue, nausea and flu-like symptoms reflect immune activation. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the capsid. Liver cancer risk is theoretical, tied to rare vector integration. Patients are monitored for factor IX levels and for any new inhibitor antibodies.",
  "fun": "The Padua variant was found because a young Italian man had unexplained blood clots, and his family turned out to carry a supercharged factor IX gene.",
  "src": ["Hemgenix (etranacogene dezaparvovec-drlb) FDA label", "Simioni P, et al. N Engl J Med, 2009", "Pipe SW, et al. N Engl J Med, 2023"]
},
"valoctocogene-roxaparvovec": {
  "origin": "Hemophilia A, the most common form of hemophilia, results from too little clotting factor VIII. It famously ran in the royal families of Europe through descendants of Queen Victoria. The factor VIII gene is very large, too big to fit inside an AAV capsid, so researchers used a B-domain-deleted version, a shortened factor VIII that still works. BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed an AAV5 vector carrying this hFVIII-SQ gene with a liver-specific promoter. After approval in Europe in 2022, the FDA approved it as Roctavian in 2023 for adults with severe hemophilia A who do not have antibodies to AAV5. It was the first gene therapy for hemophilia A.",
  "timeline": [["1984", "The human factor VIII gene is cloned."], ["2022", "Roctavian receives conditional approval in Europe."], ["2023", "FDA approves Roctavian, the first gene therapy for severe hemophilia A."]],
  "cell": "Factor VIII is a cofactor that, once activated by thrombin, joins activated factor IX on platelet surfaces to activate factor X strongly. Without it, thrombin forms too slowly, and bleeding into joints and muscles follows. Valoctocogene roxaparvovec is a non-replicating AAV5 capsid holding a B-domain-deleted human factor VIII gene with a liver-specific promoter. After IV infusion it enters hepatocytes, and the DNA forms episomes in the nucleus. Hepatocytes, which do not normally make much factor VIII (that is mostly endothelial cells), are pushed to make and secrete it. Making a large secreted protein can stress the endoplasmic reticulum of liver cells, which may help explain falling expression over time.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Factor VIII activity rises and annual bleeds fall sharply for many patients, often allowing them to stop routine factor VIII prophylaxis."], ["Joints and muscles", "Fewer bleeds help protect joints from hemophilic arthropathy."], ["Liver", "ALT rises are common and may reflect immune attack on transduced cells or cell stress; corticosteroids are often needed."], ["Over time", "Factor VIII levels peak in the first year and then decline gradually over years, so the effect may not be lifelong."], ["Blood vessels", "Thromboembolic events have been reported, particularly in people with clot risk factors."]],
  "journey": "Roctavian is a single IV infusion. Most of the vector goes to the liver, and factor VIII activity rises over weeks to months, peaks within about a year, then slowly declines. Vector DNA is shed in semen and other fluids for weeks to months, so barrier contraception is advised for a time. It cannot be repeated, because patients make lasting antibodies against AAV5. People with prior AAV5 antibodies are screened out because those antibodies would block the vector.",
  "sidefx": "ALT elevation is the most common problem and can lower factor VIII expression if not controlled, so liver tests are checked often and steroids are given when needed; steroids themselves add side effects. Nausea, fatigue and headache follow infusion and immune activation. Infusion reactions are immune responses to the capsid. High factor VIII levels can contribute to blood clots. Long-term liver cancer risk is theoretical, from rare vector integration.",
  "fun": "Factor VIII is so large that its full gene would not fit inside an AAV capsid, so the therapy uses a trimmed version without the B domain, which turns out not to be needed for clotting.",
  "src": ["Roctavian (valoctocogene roxaparvovec-rvox) FDA label", "Ozelo MC, et al. N Engl J Med, 2022"]
},
"alfentanil": {
  "origin": "Alfentanil comes from the family of synthetic opioids designed by Paul Janssen and his company, Janssen Pharmaceutica, in Belgium. After fentanyl in 1960, the Janssen team made a series of related molecules to fine-tune speed and duration. Alfentanil was made in the 1970s as a fentanyl analog that acts faster and wears off sooner, useful for short, painful procedures and anesthesia. It was approved in the US as Alfenta in the 1980s. Its unique chemistry, a low pKa that leaves most of the drug un-ionized in the blood, became a classic textbook example of how ionization controls how fast a drug enters the brain.",
  "timeline": [["1960", "Paul Janssen's team synthesizes fentanyl."], ["1970s", "Janssen Pharmaceutica develops alfentanil."], ["1980s", "FDA approves alfentanil (Alfenta) for anesthesia."]],
  "cell": "Alfentanil is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled. Activation lowers cAMP by inhibiting adenylyl cyclase, opens G protein-coupled inward-rectifying potassium channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels so less glutamate and substance P are released. This damps pain signals in the dorsal horn, brainstem and higher centers. In the brainstem it also reduces the respiratory center's response to carbon dioxide. Alfentanil's pKa is about 6.5, so about 90 percent is un-ionized at body pH. Even though it is less lipid soluble than fentanyl, this large un-ionized fraction lets it cross the blood-brain barrier fast, giving onset in about a minute.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Strong, fast analgesia and sedation for intubation, short procedures or as part of anesthesia."], ["Breathing", "The brainstem becomes less sensitive to carbon dioxide, so breathing slows or stops, which is why it is used where airway support is available."], ["Chest wall muscles", "Rapid large doses can cause chest wall rigidity, which makes ventilation hard and may need a neuromuscular blocker."], ["Heart", "Increased vagal tone slows the heart rate, and blood pressure can drop."], ["Pupils and gut", "Pupils constrict, and nausea, vomiting and slowed gut motility can occur."]],
  "journey": "Alfentanil is given IV. It reaches the brain in about one to one and a half minutes, and a single bolus lasts only about ten minutes as it redistributes. It has a small volume of distribution because it is highly bound to alpha-1 acid glycoprotein and less fat soluble than fentanyl, which keeps it from piling up in fat. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors such as erythromycin can prolong it. Its elimination half-life is about 1.5 to 1.8 hours, and metabolites leave in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Respiratory depression and apnea are direct results of mu receptor activation in the brainstem breathing centers. Chest wall rigidity is thought to come from opioid effects on brainstem and spinal motor pathways. Bradycardia comes from vagal stimulation. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Naloxone, a competitive mu antagonist, reverses opioid effects, though large doses and repeat dosing may be needed and pain returns as the opioid is blocked.",
  "fun": "Alfentanil reaches its peak brain effect faster than fentanyl even though it is less fat soluble, a classic lesson that the un-ionized fraction, not just lipid solubility, controls speed.",
  "src": ["Alfenta (alfentanil) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"prilocaine": {
  "origin": "Prilocaine is one of the amide local anesthetics that followed lidocaine, which Nils Lofgren and Bengt Lundqvist made in Stockholm in 1943. Lofgren continued this work, and in the 1950s he and colleagues at the Swedish company Astra synthesized prilocaine, a toluidine derivative. It entered clinical use around 1960. In the US it is best known as Citanest for dental anesthesia, plain or with epinephrine. It is also one half of EMLA cream, a mix of lidocaine and prilocaine that numbs intact skin. Prilocaine is famous among students for one unusual toxicity: it can turn hemoglobin into methemoglobin.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Lofgren and Lundqvist synthesize lidocaine."], ["1950s", "Lofgren and colleagues at Astra synthesize prilocaine."], ["1960", "Prilocaine enters clinical use."], ["1980s", "EMLA cream (lidocaine and prilocaine) is introduced."]],
  "cell": "Prilocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the nerve cell. The uncharged base form crosses the nerve membrane; inside, it gains a proton, and the charged form binds a site within the channel pore. It binds best to open and inactivated channels, so actively firing nerves are blocked more readily. Sodium cannot enter, the membrane cannot depolarize, and the action potential cannot travel. Small unmyelinated C fibers and thin A-delta pain fibers are blocked first, then temperature, touch and motor fibers. Amidases in the liver, kidney and lungs break prilocaine into o-toluidine, and o-toluidine metabolites oxidize the iron in hemoglobin from ferrous to ferric, making methemoglobin.",
  "body": [["Nerves", "Sensory nerves stop sending pain signals, numbing the injected tooth or tissue."], ["Mouth and lips", "Soft tissue numbness lasts longer than the dental anesthesia, which can lead to lip or tongue biting, especially in children."], ["Blood", "Methemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so high levels cause cyanosis, low pulse oximetry readings and tissue hypoxia."], ["Blood vessels", "Prilocaine causes less vasodilation than lidocaine, so it can work without epinephrine; the epinephrine form further narrows vessels to slow absorption."], ["Brain and heart", "If too much reaches the blood, sodium channel blockade can cause seizures and cardiac depression."]],
  "journey": "Prilocaine is injected near the nerve or into the tissue in dental work. Onset is two to four minutes. It is absorbed into the blood from the injection site, and epinephrine slows this. It is less protein bound than many amides and has a large volume of distribution. Unlike most amides, which are broken down only in the liver, prilocaine is also hydrolyzed in the kidneys and lungs, so it is cleared quickly. The products, including o-toluidine and N-propylalanine, leave in urine, and its half-life is about 1.6 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Methemoglobinemia is prilocaine's signature risk. Its o-toluidine metabolites oxidize hemoglobin iron, so the blood turns chocolate-brown and carries less oxygen. Infants, people with G6PD deficiency or congenital methemoglobinemia, and those taking other oxidizing drugs are more vulnerable. Methylene blue reverses it by helping reduce methemoglobin back to hemoglobin, though it is avoided in G6PD deficiency. Local anesthetic systemic toxicity causes seizures and cardiac arrest from widespread sodium channel blockade and is treated with lipid emulsion and resuscitation.",
  "fun": "Pulse oximeters tend to read around 85 percent in significant methemoglobinemia no matter how much oxygen is given, a classic clue on exams and in practice.",
  "src": ["Citanest Plain Dental (prilocaine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"bupivacaine-liposomal": {
  "origin": "Bupivacaine was synthesized in 1957 by Bo af Ekenstam in Sweden and entered clinical use in the 1960s as a long-acting amide local anesthetic. Even so, a single injection lasts only hours, and surgical pain lasts days. In the 1990s the San Diego company DepoTech developed DepoFoam, tiny multivesicular lipid particles that hold drug in many small chambers and release it slowly. Pacira Pharmaceuticals used this technology to load bupivacaine, and the FDA approved it as Exparel in 2011 for infiltration at the surgical site. Approval was later expanded to certain nerve blocks, as part of efforts to reduce opioid use after surgery.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Bo af Ekenstam synthesizes bupivacaine."], ["1990s", "DepoTech develops DepoFoam multivesicular liposome technology."], ["2011", "FDA approves Exparel (liposomal bupivacaine)."], ["2018", "Exparel is approved for interscalene brachial plexus nerve block."]],
  "cell": "Bupivacaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels on nerve membranes. The uncharged form enters the nerve, gains a proton inside, and the charged form binds within the channel pore, favoring open and inactivated states. Without sodium entry, action potentials cannot start or travel, so pain signals stop at the site. Bupivacaine binds the channel strongly and comes off slowly, which gives a long block. In the heart it also binds cardiac sodium channels tightly, which is why its toxicity is especially dangerous. The liposomes do not change the mechanism. They hold the drug in many tiny water-filled chambers inside lipid walls that break down over days, releasing bupivacaine gradually.",
  "body": [["Surgical site", "Slow release keeps nerves near the wound numb for up to about 72 hours, which can lower opioid needs."], ["Peripheral nerves", "When used as a nerve block, the area supplied by that nerve becomes numb and may be weak."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation are common after surgery and may also reflect other drugs and the operation."], ["Heart", "Bupivacaine that reaches the blood can slow conduction and cause arrhythmias or cardiac arrest at toxic levels."], ["Brain", "High blood levels cause dizziness, metallic taste, ringing in the ears and then seizures."]],
  "journey": "Exparel is injected into the surgical site or around a nerve and is not given IV. A small free fraction acts quickly, then the liposomes release bupivacaine over several days. Blood levels show two peaks: an early one from free drug and a later one around 12 to 36 hours from liposome release. Absorbed bupivacaine is about 95 percent protein bound and is metabolized by liver CYP3A4 to pipecolylxylidine, which leaves in urine. Other local anesthetics, especially lidocaine, can cause an immediate release of bupivacaine from the liposomes, so timing between them matters.",
  "sidefx": "Local anesthetic systemic toxicity is the main danger: bupivacaine that reaches the blood blocks sodium channels in the brain and heart, causing seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Bupivacaine binds cardiac sodium channels more tightly than lidocaine, making resuscitation harder. Lipid emulsion therapy is the specific rescue, thought to soak up the drug. Nerve block use can cause motor weakness and falls. Hypotension and bradycardia reflect autonomic and cardiac effects.",
  "fun": "A single DepoFoam particle looks like a tiny honeycomb under a microscope, with many separate chambers that open one after another.",
  "src": ["Exparel (bupivacaine liposome injectable suspension) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"edrophonium": {
  "origin": "In 1934 the Scottish physician Mary Walker showed that physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor from the Calabar bean, quickly but briefly restored strength in myasthenia gravis. Physostigmine and neostigmine act for hours, so a faster, shorter agent was sought for testing. Edrophonium, a quaternary ammonium compound, was introduced by Hoffmann-La Roche around 1950 as Tensilon. In the early 1950s doctors began using the Tensilon test: a short IV dose that, in myasthenia, briefly improved a drooping eyelid or weak muscles. Antibody tests, nerve studies and the ice pack test have largely replaced it, and US supply has been very limited.",
  "timeline": [["1934", "Mary Walker shows physostigmine improves myasthenia gravis."], ["1950s", "Edrophonium (Tensilon) is introduced, and the Tensilon test enters practice."]],
  "cell": "In myasthenia gravis, antibodies attack nicotinic acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle, so fewer receptors are left to respond. Acetylcholinesterase normally breaks down acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft within milliseconds. Edrophonium binds acetylcholinesterase reversibly through an ionic bond at the anionic site, but unlike neostigmine it does not carbamylate the enzyme. It simply occupies the enzyme briefly. Acetylcholine lasts longer in the cleft and can bind the remaining receptors more times, which raises muscle strength for a few minutes. It also increases acetylcholine at muscarinic sites, causing parasympathetic effects. Its quaternary charge keeps it out of the brain.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "In myasthenia, strength improves within a minute and fades in five to ten minutes, which is the basis of the Tensilon test."], ["Heart", "Extra acetylcholine at muscarinic M2 receptors slows the heart and AV conduction, sometimes severely; atropine is kept at hand."], ["Airways", "Muscarinic activation narrows airways and increases secretions, so bronchospasm can occur, especially in asthma."], ["Glands", "Salivation, sweating and tearing increase."], ["Gut", "Cramping, nausea and diarrhea come from increased muscarinic gut activity."], ["Muscle fibers", "Fasciculations can appear as nicotinic receptors are overstimulated."]],
  "journey": "Edrophonium is given IV and starts working within 30 to 60 seconds. It peaks in about a minute and lasts only five to ten minutes, because it binds the enzyme loosely and comes off quickly. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It is barely metabolized and is cleared mainly by the kidneys unchanged, with a plasma half-life of about one to two hours.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are cholinergic: extra acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors causes bradycardia, bronchospasm, salivation, sweating and cramps, and at nicotinic receptors causes fasciculations. In a patient already overdosed on cholinesterase inhibitors (cholinergic crisis), more acetylcholine can worsen weakness and breathing failure. Atropine blocks the muscarinic effects but not the nicotinic weakness. Because effects are short, most reactions pass quickly, but cardiac arrest has been reported.",
  "fun": "Mary Walker's 1934 finding with physostigmine became known as the miracle at St Alfege's, after the London hospital where she worked.",
  "src": ["Enlon (edrophonium chloride) FDA label", "Walker MB. Lancet, 1934", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"amisulpride": {
  "origin": "Amisulpride is a substituted benzamide, a relative of sulpiride and metoclopramide. It was developed in France by Synthelabo (later part of Sanofi) and has been used for decades in Europe and elsewhere as an oral antipsychotic, but it was never approved in the US for psychosis. Researchers at the British company Acacia Pharma noticed that very low doses blocked the dopamine receptors that trigger nausea without the usual sedation or movement effects. They developed an IV form for postoperative nausea and vomiting, and the FDA approved it as Barhemsys in 2020. It was the first antiemetic approved in the US for treating nausea and vomiting after surgery in patients whose earlier prophylaxis had failed.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Amisulpride is developed in France as an antipsychotic."], ["2020", "FDA approves IV amisulpride (Barhemsys) for postoperative nausea and vomiting."]],
  "cell": "Nausea and vomiting after surgery are driven partly by the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, a brain region outside the blood-brain barrier that samples the blood for toxins and drugs. Dopamine D2 and D3 receptors there send signals to the vomiting center in the medulla. Amisulpride selectively and competitively blocks D2 and D3 receptors, so these signals are damped. It has little affinity for serotonin, histamine, alpha-adrenergic or muscarinic receptors, which is why it causes less sedation, dry mouth and low blood pressure than older dopamine antagonists such as droperidol or prochlorperazine. Blocking D2 in the pituitary raises prolactin.",
  "body": [["Brain (area postrema)", "Blocking dopamine signals in the trigger zone reduces nausea and vomiting after surgery."], ["Heart", "It can prolong the QT interval in a dose-dependent way, so it is avoided in people with long QT and used with care with other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Pituitary", "Prolactin rises because dopamine normally suppresses prolactin release."], ["Movement", "Extrapyramidal effects are uncommon at antiemetic doses but possible as with other D2 blockers."], ["Injection site", "Infusion site pain is among the common effects."]],
  "journey": "Barhemsys is given IV as a short injection and works within minutes. It is only about 25 to 30 percent protein bound and is barely metabolized. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged, with a half-life of about four to five hours. Severe kidney disease raises levels.",
  "sidefx": "QT prolongation happens because amisulpride, like many D2 antagonists, can block cardiac hERG potassium channels at higher levels, delaying repolarization; torsades de pointes is rare. Prolactin rise reflects pituitary D2 blockade. Infusion site pain, chills, low potassium and low blood pressure around surgery have been reported. Extrapyramidal symptoms are possible but uncommon. There is no antidote; acute dystonia from D2 blockade is typically treated with diphenhydramine or benztropine.",
  "fun": "The antiemetic dose of amisulpride is a small fraction of the doses used to treat schizophrenia in Europe, which is why it causes so little sedation.",
  "src": ["Barhemsys (amisulpride) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"oliceridine": {
  "origin": "Oliceridine came from the idea of biased agonism. Opioid receptors signal through G proteins and through a second pathway involving beta-arrestin-2. In 1999 Laura Bohn and colleagues in Robert Lefkowitz and Marc Caron's group at Duke reported that mice lacking beta-arrestin-2 had stronger morphine pain relief, and later work suggested less respiratory depression and constipation. That led Trevena, a company co-founded by Lefkowitz, to design oliceridine, a mu-opioid agonist that favors G protein signaling. After an initial FDA rejection in 2018, it was approved as Olinvyk in 2020 for severe acute pain in hospitals. Later research has questioned how much the beta-arrestin idea explains respiratory depression, and the label carries full opioid warnings.",
  "timeline": [["1999", "Bohn and colleagues report enhanced morphine analgesia in beta-arrestin-2 knockout mice."], ["2018", "FDA issues a complete response letter for oliceridine."], ["2020", "FDA approves Olinvyk (oliceridine) for severe acute pain."]],
  "cell": "Oliceridine binds the mu-opioid receptor and activates its Gi/o protein. This lowers cAMP, opens GIRK potassium channels, which hyperpolarize neurons, and closes presynaptic calcium channels, which reduces release of pain transmitters in the dorsal horn and brain. Compared with morphine, it recruits less beta-arrestin-2, the protein that ends G protein signaling, triggers receptor internalization and starts other signaling. In theory this keeps analgesia while lowering respiratory depression and gut slowing. In practice, oliceridine still causes respiratory depression in a dose-related way. Low intrinsic efficacy may explain part of its profile.",
  "body": [["Brain and spinal cord", "Mu receptor activation reduces pain signaling and the emotional response to pain."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression still occurs, especially with benzodiazepines or other sedatives."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting and constipation occur, though trial rates were somewhat lower than with morphine at similar pain relief."], ["Heart", "QT prolongation was seen at higher cumulative exposure, which limits the total daily amount."], ["Skin", "Itching can occur, as with other opioids."]],
  "journey": "Oliceridine is given IV in the hospital, as a bolus or patient-controlled dose. It works within two to five minutes and lasts one to three hours. It is about 77 percent protein bound. The liver metabolizes it through CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 to inactive metabolites, so CYP2D6 poor metabolizers and people on strong CYP3A4 or CYP2D6 inhibitors have higher levels. The half-life is about 1.3 to 3 hours, and metabolites leave in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Like all mu agonists, it can cause life-threatening respiratory depression, abuse and addiction, and dangerous sedation with benzodiazepines, all boxed warnings. These come from mu receptor effects in the brainstem and reward pathways. Nausea comes from the chemoreceptor trigger zone. QT prolongation limits total exposure. Naloxone reverses its effects as with other opioids.",
  "fun": "Oliceridine was the first drug approved that was designed around the concept of a biased agonist at a G protein-coupled receptor.",
  "src": ["Olinvyk (oliceridine) FDA label", "Bohn LM, et al. Science, 1999", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"dengue-vaccine": {
  "origin": "Dengvaxia was built on the back of another famous vaccine. In the 1930s Max Theiler at the Rockefeller Foundation created the yellow fever 17D strain, a live weakened virus, and won the 1951 Nobel Prize. Decades later the company Acambis developed ChimeriVax, a method of swapping 17D's outer protein genes for those of related viruses. Sanofi Pasteur used this to make a vaccine with the outer genes of all four dengue serotypes. It was first approved in Mexico and the Philippines in 2015. A 2017 analysis showed people never infected before had a higher risk of severe dengue after vaccination, leading to controversy in the Philippines. The FDA approved it in 2019 only for children 9 to 16 with lab-confirmed prior dengue living in endemic US areas. Sanofi later announced it would stop making it because of low demand.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "Max Theiler develops the yellow fever 17D vaccine strain."], ["2015", "Dengvaxia is first approved, in Mexico and the Philippines."], ["2017", "Data show higher severe dengue risk in vaccinated people with no prior infection."], ["2019", "FDA approves Dengvaxia for children 9 to 16 with confirmed prior dengue in endemic areas."]],
  "cell": "Dengvaxia is a live attenuated chimeric virus: the yellow fever 17D backbone provides replication machinery, while the prM and envelope (E) genes come from dengue serotypes 1 to 4. After injection, the vaccine viruses replicate a little, and dendritic cells take up the dengue proteins and present them to T cells, which help B cells make neutralizing antibodies against each serotype. In someone who has had dengue before, the vaccine acts like a second exposure and gives broad, strong protection. In someone who has never had dengue, it acts like a first infection. Then a later natural infection can behave like a second infection, where weakly binding antibodies help the virus enter Fc receptor-bearing cells (antibody-dependent enhancement) and severe dengue becomes more likely.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies to all four dengue serotypes form after the three-dose series."], ["Previously infected children", "In those with prior dengue, it lowers the risk of dengue illness, hospitalization and severe disease."], ["Never-infected recipients", "Without prior infection, a later natural infection is more likely to be severe, which is why lab confirmation of prior infection is required."], ["Injection site and whole body", "Pain at the site, headache, malaise, weakness and muscle aches are common for a few days."]],
  "journey": "Dengvaxia is given as three injections six months apart. The live vaccine viruses replicate at low levels near the injection site and in lymph tissue, then the immune system clears them. Antibodies rise after each dose, and protection is strongest after the third, though it wanes over several years. As a live vaccine, it is avoided in people with weakened immune systems and in pregnancy.",
  "sidefx": "Common reactions such as soreness, headache and aches are the expected signs of immune activation. The serious risk is severe dengue after later natural infection in people who were dengue-naive when vaccinated, explained by antibody-dependent enhancement. Allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur, as with any vaccine. As a live vaccine it can cause problems in immunocompromised people. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Dengvaxia's backbone is the same yellow fever 17D strain that Max Theiler made in the 1930s, one of the most successful vaccines ever created.",
  "src": ["Dengvaxia (dengue tetravalent vaccine, live) FDA label", "CDC dengue vaccine recommendations", "Sridhar S, et al. N Engl J Med, 2018"]
},
"sterile-water": {
  "origin": "Sterile Water for Injection is not a drug but the foundation of many. Early IV infusions in the 1900s were often followed by sudden fevers and chills, and no one knew why. In the 1920s the American biochemist Florence Seibert showed that the culprit was pyrogens, heat-stable substances from bacteria that survived ordinary sterilization in distilled water. She designed a distillation apparatus that kept spray from carrying pyrogens into the product. Her work led to pyrogen-free water standards that made modern IV therapy safe. Today Sterile Water for Injection, USP, is distilled or purified, sterile, nonpyrogenic and free of any solute, and is used mainly to dissolve and dilute other drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1920s", "Florence Seibert shows that bacterial pyrogens cause fevers after IV injections and develops a way to remove them."], ["1940s", "Pyrogen testing becomes part of US Pharmacopeia standards for injections."]],
  "cell": "Sterile water has no pharmacologic target. It works as a solvent. Its importance comes from osmosis. Plain water has an osmolality of zero, while blood is about 285 to 295 mOsm/kg. If it enters the blood without enough solute, water moves across cell membranes from the hypotonic plasma into red blood cells, which have higher osmolality. Red cells swell and burst (hemolysis), releasing potassium and hemoglobin. Free water also lowers plasma sodium and shifts into brain cells, which can cause cerebral edema. That is why sterile water is used only after drugs or solutes are added to make the final solution suitable.",
  "body": [["Drug preparation", "Dissolves powdered drugs and dilutes concentrated ones so they can be given safely."], ["Red blood cells", "If given IV alone in volume, water rushes into red cells and makes them burst."], ["Kidneys", "Released hemoglobin can injure the kidneys, causing acute kidney injury."], ["Blood potassium", "Burst red cells spill potassium into the blood, which can cause dangerous heart rhythms."], ["Brain", "Large amounts of free water lower plasma sodium and swell brain cells, causing headache, confusion, seizures or coma."]],
  "journey": "Sterile water is not given by itself in large IV amounts; it serves as a vehicle. Once a diluted drug enters the blood, the water simply joins total body water and distributes across cells. It is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete excess free water, which they can do efficiently when they work normally.",
  "sidefx": "When used correctly as a diluent, it has no side effects of its own. The harm comes from mistakes: infusing plain sterile water, especially in large volumes, causes hemolysis from osmotic swelling of red cells, hyperkalemia from released potassium, kidney injury from hemoglobin, and hypotonic hyponatremia with brain swelling. There is no antidote; treatment is supportive, with care of potassium, sodium and kidney function. Mix-ups with similar-looking bags of IV fluid are a known cause.",
  "fun": "Florence Seibert, who made pyrogen-free IV water possible, is also known for purifying the tuberculin protein used in the TB skin test.",
  "src": ["Sterile Water for Injection, USP FDA labeling", "Seibert FB. Am J Physiol, 1923", "ISMP safety alerts on sterile water"]
},
"tromethamine": {
  "origin": "Tromethamine, also called THAM or Tris, began as a laboratory buffer. Biochemists used tris(hydroxymethyl)aminomethane to hold the pH of solutions steady, and it is still in many lab buffers today. In the late 1950s researchers, notably Gabriel Nahas, studied it as a way to treat acidosis in patients without adding sodium or making carbon dioxide, problems seen with sodium bicarbonate. It became available in the US as THAM Solution for treating metabolic acidosis, especially during cardiac bypass surgery and cardiac arrest. Its use today is limited and specialized.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Tris buffer is studied as a clinical treatment for acidosis, notably by Gabriel Nahas."], ["1960s", "THAM solution enters clinical use in the US."]],
  "cell": "Tromethamine is not a receptor drug. It is a weak base with a pKa of about 7.8, close to body pH, which makes it a good buffer. It accepts hydrogen ions: THAM plus H+ becomes THAM-H+. It can buffer both metabolic acids and carbonic acid. When it takes a proton from carbonic acid, bicarbonate is left behind, and carbon dioxide falls rather than rises, unlike sodium bicarbonate, which generates CO2. About 30 percent of THAM is un-ionized at body pH and can enter cells, so it also buffers inside cells. The protonated form is excreted by the kidneys, carrying acid out and causing an osmotic diuresis.",
  "body": [["Blood chemistry", "Blood pH rises and PaCO2 falls because hydrogen ions are bound without making carbon dioxide."], ["Breathing", "Lower CO2 reduces the drive to breathe, which can depress ventilation, especially in patients not on a ventilator."], ["Kidneys", "Protonated THAM is excreted in urine, carrying acid out and producing an osmotic diuresis."], ["Blood sugar", "THAM can lower blood glucose, possibly by increasing insulin release and cell glucose uptake."], ["Potassium", "It can raise blood potassium, particularly in kidney failure."], ["Veins and tissues", "The alkaline solution irritates veins and can cause tissue death if it leaks."]],
  "journey": "THAM is given by IV infusion, ideally into a large vein. It works within minutes. It is not metabolized. It distributes into extracellular fluid and partly into cells. The kidneys excrete the protonated form, with a half-life of about five to six hours in normal kidney function; in kidney failure it builds up, so it is used with great caution or avoided.",
  "sidefx": "Respiratory depression comes from lowering CO2 and raising pH, which both reduce the drive to breathe, which is why ventilation support should be available. Hypoglycemia, which can last hours, and hyperkalemia are known metabolic effects. The alkaline solution causes venospasm and phlebitis and, if extravasated, tissue necrosis. Use through the umbilical vein in newborns has been linked to liver necrosis. Accumulation in kidney failure increases toxicity. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Tris is one of the most common buffers in biology labs worldwide, used in everything from DNA gels to protein experiments.",
  "src": ["THAM Solution (tromethamine injection) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nahas GG. Pharmacol Rev, 1962"]
}
,
"multivitamin-infusion": {
  "origin": "The story of IV multivitamins joins two histories. The first is the discovery of vitamins: James Lind's 1747 citrus trial for scurvy, Christiaan Eijkman's 1890s work on beriberi in chickens fed polished rice, and Casimir Funk's 1912 coining of the word vitamine. The second is IV nutrition. In the late 1960s Stanley Dudrick and colleagues at the University of Pennsylvania showed that beagle puppies, then infants, could grow on IV nutrition alone. Patients fed only by vein soon showed vitamin deficiencies, so standardized IV multivitamin products followed. Around 2000 adult formulas such as Infuvite Adult and M.V.I. Adult were updated to add vitamin K and increase several B vitamins, giving 13 vitamins in one product.",
  "timeline": [["1747", "James Lind's trial shows citrus fruit cures scurvy."], ["1912", "Casimir Funk coins the term vitamine."], ["1960s", "Stanley Dudrick and colleagues develop total parenteral nutrition."], ["2000", "Adult IV multivitamin formulas are updated to include vitamin K."]],
  "cell": "Each vitamin feeds a different set of enzymes. Thiamine becomes thiamine pyrophosphate, a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and transketolase, so without it cells cannot burn glucose well, which harms neurons first. Riboflavin forms FAD and FMN, and niacinamide forms NAD and NADP, the electron carriers of metabolism. Pyridoxine becomes pyridoxal phosphate for transaminases and neurotransmitter synthesis. Folic acid and B12 run one-carbon transfers needed for DNA synthesis. Biotin and pantothenic acid support carboxylases and coenzyme A. Vitamin K enables gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. Vitamin A acts in the retina and on nuclear receptors, vitamin D on the vitamin D receptor, vitamin E as a membrane antioxidant, and vitamin C for collagen hydroxylation.",
  "body": [["Brain and nerves", "Thiamine prevents Wernicke encephalopathy, and B6 and B12 protect peripheral nerves."], ["Blood", "Folic acid and B12 prevent megaloblastic anemia, and vitamin K keeps clotting factors working."], ["Skin, gums and wounds", "Vitamin C maintains collagen, preventing scurvy, while niacin prevents pellagra's rash."], ["Eyes", "Vitamin A supports night vision and the surface of the eye."], ["Bones", "Vitamin D supports calcium absorption and bone mineralization."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Excess water-soluble vitamins are cleared in urine; riboflavin turns it bright yellow."]],
  "journey": "The vitamins are added to an IV nutrition bag or an IV fluid and enter the blood directly, bypassing the gut. Water-soluble vitamins (B group and C) circulate and are taken up by cells, and any excess is cleared quickly in urine. Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E and K) bind carrier proteins and are stored in the liver and fat, so they can build up with long use. Some vitamins break down with light, so bags may be protected from light.",
  "sidefx": "Bright yellow urine is harmless and comes from riboflavin. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, have been linked especially to thiamine. Vitamin K in the product can counteract warfarin, so INR is watched. With long-term use, fat-soluble vitamins, especially vitamin A, can accumulate, particularly in kidney failure. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the infusion and supportive care.",
  "fun": "The bright yellow banana bag used in emergency departments for alcohol-related malnutrition gets its color from riboflavin in the multivitamin.",
  "src": ["Infuvite Adult and M.V.I. Adult FDA labels", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"trace-elements": {
  "origin": "As IV nutrition spread after Stanley Dudrick's work in the late 1960s, patients fed only by vein began showing deficiencies of trace metals such as zinc (rash and poor healing), copper (anemia and low white counts) and selenium (heart muscle disease). For years hospitals used older trace element mixes that were marketed without full FDA approval. In the late 2010s the FDA pushed for approved, updated products with amounts that matched modern recommendations. Tralement, a four-element adult product with zinc, copper, manganese and selenium from American Regent, was approved around 2020, followed by Multrys for neonates and children. These products lowered the manganese content of older mixes, which had been linked to brain accumulation.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Total parenteral nutrition is developed, revealing trace element deficiencies."], ["2020", "FDA approves Tralement, an updated four-element trace element product."]],
  "cell": "Each element is a cofactor for specific enzymes. Zinc sits in more than 300 metalloenzymes, including carbonic anhydrase, alkaline phosphatase and DNA and RNA polymerases, and in zinc-finger proteins that control gene transcription, which matters for cell division and healing. Copper is needed for ceruloplasmin, which oxidizes iron so it can load onto transferrin, for cytochrome c oxidase in the mitochondria, for superoxide dismutase and for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin. Manganese supports manganese superoxide dismutase, arginase and pyruvate carboxylase. Selenium is built into selenocysteine in glutathione peroxidase, thioredoxin reductase and the deiodinases that turn T4 into T3.",
  "body": [["Skin and wound healing", "Zinc supports rapid cell division, preventing the scaly rash, hair loss and poor wound healing of zinc deficiency."], ["Blood", "Copper lets iron be used to make red cells, preventing anemia and neutropenia."], ["Heart and muscles", "Selenium protects cells from oxidative damage, preventing cardiomyopathy and muscle weakness."], ["Thyroid", "Selenium-dependent deiodinases activate thyroid hormone."], ["Liver and brain", "Copper and manganese are cleared through bile, so liver disease can let them build up, and manganese can deposit in the brain's basal ganglia."]],
  "journey": "The elements are added to the IV nutrition bag and enter the blood directly. Copper binds ceruloplasmin and zinc binds albumin in the blood. They are taken up by tissues and built into enzymes. Zinc and selenium are cleared mainly in urine, while copper and manganese are cleared mainly through bile into the stool, which is why blocked bile flow or liver disease can cause buildup.",
  "sidefx": "At labeled amounts side effects are uncommon. Copper and manganese can build up in cholestatic liver disease because bile is their exit route; manganese excess can cause parkinsonism-like movement problems. Precipitates in nutrition mixtures, if they form, can lodge in the lung vessels, which is why compatibility and filters matter. Hypersensitivity is rare. There is no antidote; excess is managed by reducing or stopping the elements.",
  "fun": "Zinc deficiency in IV nutrition can cause a rash around the mouth and buttocks that looks like the inherited disorder acrodermatitis enteropathica.",
  "src": ["Tralement (trace elements injection 4) FDA label", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines"]
},
"selenium": {
  "origin": "Selenium was discovered in 1817 by the Swedish chemist Jons Jacob Berzelius, who found it in sludge from a sulfuric acid plant and named it after Selene, the Greek goddess of the moon. For a long time it was known mainly as a toxin, because livestock grazing on selenium-rich plants became sick. In 1957 Klaus Schwarz and Calvin Foltz showed it was an essential nutrient. In China, a deadly heart muscle disease called Keshan disease was linked to low soil selenium and prevented with supplements in the 1970s. In the US, patients on long-term IV nutrition developed selenium deficiency, and Selenious Acid Injection from American Regent later received FDA approval as an IV nutrition additive.",
  "timeline": [["1817", "Jons Jacob Berzelius discovers selenium."], ["1957", "Schwarz and Foltz show selenium is an essential nutrient."], ["1970s", "Selenium supplementation is shown to prevent Keshan disease in China."]],
  "cell": "Selenium is built into the amino acid selenocysteine, sometimes called the 21st amino acid, which is inserted into proteins at a special UGA codon. These selenoproteins include glutathione peroxidases, which use glutathione to break down hydrogen peroxide and lipid peroxides before they damage membranes; thioredoxin reductases, which keep cellular redox balance; and the iodothyronine deiodinases, which convert T4 to the active thyroid hormone T3. Selenoprotein P carries selenium in the blood to tissues. Without selenium, cells, especially heart and skeletal muscle cells, are more open to oxidative injury.",
  "body": [["Heart", "Adequate selenium protects heart muscle from oxidative damage, preventing the cardiomyopathy seen in deficiency."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Deficiency causes muscle pain and weakness, which correct with replacement."], ["Thyroid", "Deiodinases need selenium to activate thyroid hormone."], ["Immune system", "Selenoproteins support immune cell function and antioxidant defense."], ["Hair and nails", "Deficiency can cause whitening of the nail beds, while excess causes brittle nails and hair loss."]],
  "journey": "Selenious acid is added to IV nutrition and enters the blood directly. It is carried mainly by selenoprotein P and albumin and is built into selenoproteins in the liver, muscle and other tissues, which act as long-term stores. Excess selenium is methylated and leaves mainly in urine. With larger excess, some is breathed out as dimethyl selenide, which smells like garlic.",
  "sidefx": "At labeled amounts side effects are rare. Selenium has a narrow margin between need and toxicity. Too much over time causes selenosis: garlic breath, hair loss, brittle nails, skin rash, fatigue and nerve problems, because excess selenium generates reactive oxygen species and interferes with sulfur-containing proteins. Acute poisoning can cause heart and gut toxicity. Precipitates in nutrition mixtures can cause lung emboli. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Berzelius named selenium for the moon because it closely resembled tellurium, an element already named for the Earth.",
  "src": ["Selenious Acid Injection FDA label", "Schwarz K, Foltz CM. J Am Chem Soc, 1957", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines"]
},
"copper": {
  "origin": "Copper was one of the first metals humans used, worked into tools more than ten thousand years ago. Its role in the body came into view much later. In the 1920s and 1930s researchers at the University of Wisconsin showed that rats fed only milk needed copper as well as iron to make hemoglobin. Two genetic diseases revealed its balance: Wilson disease, described by S. A. Kinnier Wilson in 1912, in which copper builds up, and Menkes disease, described by John Menkes in 1962, in which copper cannot be absorbed. Copper deficiency also appeared in patients on long-term IV nutrition and after gastric bypass surgery. Cupric chloride injection is used as a copper additive in IV nutrition.",
  "timeline": [["1912", "S. A. Kinnier Wilson describes the copper overload disease that bears his name."], ["1928", "University of Wisconsin researchers show copper is needed with iron to make hemoglobin."], ["1962", "John Menkes describes the copper deficiency disorder later named for him."]],
  "cell": "Copper is a cofactor that moves electrons for several key enzymes. Ceruloplasmin, made in the liver, is a ferroxidase that converts iron from ferrous to ferric so it can bind transferrin and reach the bone marrow. Cytochrome c oxidase, complex IV of the mitochondrial respiratory chain, uses copper to pass electrons to oxygen and make ATP. Copper-zinc superoxide dismutase removes superoxide radicals. Lysyl oxidase cross-links collagen and elastin. Dopamine beta-hydroxylase turns dopamine into norepinephrine, and tyrosinase starts melanin production. Low copper slows all of these.",
  "body": [["Blood", "Copper lets iron be moved and used, so deficiency causes anemia that does not respond to iron and low white cell counts."], ["Nerves and spinal cord", "Deficiency can damage the spinal cord and nerves, causing a gait problem similar to B12 deficiency."], ["Connective tissue and vessels", "Lysyl oxidase needs copper to cross-link collagen and elastin, which keeps vessels and bones strong."], ["Skin and hair", "Tyrosinase needs copper to make melanin, so deficiency can lighten skin and hair."], ["Liver", "The liver stores copper and excretes extra in bile, so liver disease can lead to buildup and injury."]],
  "journey": "Cupric chloride is added to IV nutrition and goes straight into the blood. The liver takes up copper and builds it into ceruloplasmin, which carries about 95 percent of plasma copper. The liver is the main store. Copper leaves mainly through bile into stool, with only a small amount in urine, so blocked bile flow raises body copper. Stores last a long time, so deficiency develops slowly.",
  "sidefx": "At labeled amounts side effects are uncommon. Excess copper builds up in the liver, especially with cholestasis, and causes liver injury because free copper generates reactive oxygen species. Large excess can cause hemolysis because free copper damages red cell membranes. Hypersensitivity is rare. Chelating agents such as penicillamine are used for chronic copper overload in Wilson disease, but for IV nutrition excess the usual step is reducing or stopping copper.",
  "fun": "Some sea creatures, such as octopuses and horseshoe crabs, use a copper protein called hemocyanin instead of hemoglobin, which makes their blood blue.",
  "src": ["Cupric Chloride Injection, USP FDA labeling", "ASPEN parenteral nutrition guidelines", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"activated-charcoal-sorbitol": {
  "origin": "Charcoal has long been used for poisoning, and two French scientists made the point dramatically. In 1813 the chemist Michel Bertrand swallowed arsenic mixed with charcoal and survived. In 1831 the pharmacist Pierre-Fleury Touery swallowed a large dose of strychnine mixed with charcoal in front of the French Academy of Medicine and suffered no ill effects. In the early 1900s, activating charcoal with steam or chemicals greatly increased its surface area. Sorbitol, a sugar alcohol found in fruits such as rowan berries, was added to some charcoal products as a cathartic to speed the charcoal and poison through the gut. Products such as Actidose with Sorbitol followed. Toxicology groups now say cathartics have not been shown to improve outcomes, and charcoal is used selectively.",
  "timeline": [["1813", "Michel Bertrand survives swallowing arsenic mixed with charcoal."], ["1831", "Pierre-Fleury Touery swallows strychnine with charcoal before the French Academy of Medicine."], ["1900s", "Activation processes greatly increase charcoal's adsorptive surface area."], ["2000s", "Toxicology position statements advise against routine cathartic use."]],
  "cell": "Activated charcoal is not absorbed and has no receptor target. It is carbon heated with steam or gases so it is riddled with tiny pores, giving a huge surface area. Many drug and toxin molecules stick to this surface through weak physical forces (adsorption), so they stay in the gut instead of crossing into the blood. It works best on large, uncharged organic molecules. It binds alcohols, iron, lithium, strong acids and alkalis, and other metals poorly. Sorbitol is a poorly absorbed polyol. It stays in the bowel and draws water in by osmosis, which loosens stool and speeds transit. Gut bacteria ferment some of it, producing gas.",
  "body": [["Stomach and intestines", "Charcoal holds many toxins in the gut lumen so less is absorbed, with the greatest benefit if given within about an hour of ingestion."], ["Bowel", "Sorbitol pulls water into the bowel, causing loose stools and cramping."], ["Fluids and electrolytes", "Repeated sorbitol can cause dehydration and electrolyte problems, including high sodium, especially in children."], ["Lungs", "If vomited and inhaled, charcoal can cause severe aspiration pneumonitis or ARDS, which is why airway protection matters."], ["Stool", "Stools turn black as the unabsorbed charcoal passes through, which is expected and harmless."]],
  "journey": "The product is swallowed or given through a gastric tube. Charcoal begins binding toxin as soon as it touches it, and it is not absorbed or metabolized. It travels through the gut and leaves in the stool, carrying bound toxin. Sorbitol is mostly not absorbed. It pulls water into the bowel and is partly fermented by colon bacteria, producing a bowel movement within hours.",
  "sidefx": "Black stool, vomiting, cramping and diarrhea are expected. Vomiting is a key danger because aspirated charcoal injures the lungs, so it is avoided in patients who cannot protect their airway. Sorbitol's osmotic pull can cause dehydration, hypernatremia and electrolyte shifts, especially with repeated doses or in small children, which is why the sorbitol form is generally used for one dose at most. Charcoal can cause bowel obstruction when gut motility is poor. It is not used for caustics or hydrocarbons.",
  "fun": "Repeated doses of plain charcoal can pull some drugs, such as phenobarbital, back out of the blood across the gut wall, a process nicknamed gut dialysis.",
  "src": ["Actidose with Sorbitol product labeling", "AACT/EAPCCT position papers on single-dose activated charcoal and cathartics", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"alteplase-catheter-clearance": {
  "origin": "Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is a natural human enzyme that dissolves clots. In the late 1970s and early 1980s Desire Collen and colleagues in Leuven, Belgium, purified it from a human melanoma cell line that made large amounts. In 1983 Genentech scientists cloned the gene, and recombinant alteplase was approved as Activase in 1987 for heart attacks. Central venous catheters often become blocked by small clots at the tip or in the lumen. Genentech developed a small-volume form designed to be instilled into the catheter itself, approved as Cathflo Activase in 2001 to restore function to blocked central venous access devices.",
  "timeline": [["1981", "Rijken and Collen report purifying tPA from a human melanoma cell line."], ["1983", "Genentech scientists clone the tPA gene."], ["1987", "FDA approves Activase (alteplase) for heart attacks."], ["2001", "FDA approves Cathflo Activase for occluded central venous catheters."]],
  "cell": "Alteplase is a glycosylated serine protease. It has finger and kringle domains that bind fibrin, so it works best on plasminogen that is already sitting on a fibrin clot. Once bound, it cleaves plasminogen at the Arg561-Val562 bond, turning it into plasmin. Plasmin then breaks fibrin strands into soluble fibrin degradation products, and the clot falls apart. In catheter clearance the drug is placed inside the blocked lumen and left to dwell. It acts on the fibrin clot or fibrin sheath inside or at the tip of the catheter, restoring the ability to draw blood and infuse.",
  "body": [["Catheter lumen", "Fibrin plugs dissolve, and many catheters can be used again within 30 to 120 minutes."], ["Bloodstream", "Only a tiny amount reaches the circulation, so whole-body clot breakdown is minimal."], ["Bleeding risk", "Small risk of bleeding exists, especially in patients with conditions that already raise bleeding risk."], ["Infection", "Clearing an infected clot can release bacteria into the blood, so catheter-related sepsis is a known risk."], ["Catheter itself", "Forceful instillation into a fully blocked catheter can rupture it."]],
  "journey": "Cathflo is reconstituted and gently instilled into the blocked catheter, then left to dwell. Most of it stays in the catheter. After the dwell, it is withdrawn rather than flushed into the patient. Any alteplase that does reach the blood is cleared very quickly by the liver, with a half-life of about five minutes.",
  "sidefx": "Because so little drug reaches the circulation, side effects are uncommon. Bleeding is possible from plasmin's fibrin breakdown. If the catheter is infected, dissolving the clot can release bacteria and cause sepsis. Forcing fluid into a fully blocked line can tear the catheter. Rarely, a dislodged clot can travel as an embolus. Hypersensitivity is rare. Systemic bleeding from alteplase can be countered with antifibrinolytics such as aminocaproic acid or tranexamic acid and with cryoprecipitate.",
  "fun": "The melanoma cell line used to purify tPA, called Bowes, made the enzyme in unusually large amounts, which made the first studies possible.",
  "src": ["Cathflo Activase (alteplase) FDA label", "Pennica D, et al. Nature, 1983", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"polymyxin-b": {
  "origin": "Polymyxin B came from a soil bacterium. In 1947 three research groups in the US and Britain independently reported antibiotics from Bacillus polymyxa (now Paenibacillus polymyxa) that killed gram-negative bacteria. One of these, polymyxin B, became a clinical drug. By the 1970s it fell out of favor because of kidney and nerve toxicity and the arrival of safer antibiotics. In the 2000s, as Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas and Klebsiella strains became resistant to almost everything, polymyxin B came back as a last-resort IV antibiotic.",
  "timeline": [["1947", "Three groups independently report polymyxins from Bacillus polymyxa."], ["1970s", "Use declines because of kidney and nerve toxicity."], ["2000s", "Polymyxins return as last-line agents for multidrug-resistant gram-negatives."]],
  "cell": "Polymyxin B is a cationic cyclic lipopeptide with a fatty acid tail. Gram-negative bacteria have an outer membrane whose lipopolysaccharide (LPS) carries negatively charged phosphate groups on lipid A, held together by calcium and magnesium bridges. Polymyxin B's positive amino groups bind these phosphates and push out the calcium and magnesium. Its fatty tail then inserts into the membrane, acting like a detergent. The outer membrane becomes leaky, the drug reaches and disrupts the inner membrane, and cell contents spill out, killing the bacteria quickly in a concentration-dependent way. It also binds and neutralizes endotoxin. Resistance arises when bacteria add positive groups to lipid A, such as through the plasmid gene mcr-1, so the drug cannot bind.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Rapidly kills Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella and E. coli; Proteus, Serratia, Providencia, Burkholderia and gram-positives are naturally resistant."], ["Kidneys", "Tubule cells take up the drug and are damaged, raising creatinine and causing protein and casts in urine."], ["Nerves", "Numbness around the mouth and in the limbs, dizziness and confusion reflect effects on nerve membranes."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It can block acetylcholine release, causing muscle weakness and, rarely, breathing paralysis."], ["Skin", "Skin darkening (hyperpigmentation) has been reported with polymyxin B."]],
  "journey": "Polymyxin B is given by IV infusion; it is not absorbed by mouth. It is about half protein bound. Unlike colistimethate, it is given in active form and is cleared mostly by nonrenal routes, with only a small fraction in urine, so it reaches lower urine levels and is less favored for urinary infections. Its half-life is variable, often about 6 to 12 hours in critically ill patients.",
  "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity comes from the drug's detergent action on kidney tubule cell membranes after uptake, so kidney function is followed closely. Neurotoxicity, such as tingling and dizziness, reflects similar effects on nerve membranes. Neuromuscular blockade, more likely with anesthetics or other neuromuscular blockers, can stop breathing and carries a boxed warning along with kidney and nerve toxicity. Calcium or neostigmine may not fully reverse it, so ventilation support may be needed. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Polymyxin B is also bound to fibers in cartridges used outside the US to filter endotoxin out of the blood in septic shock.",
  "src": ["Polymyxin B for Injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "International consensus guidelines for polymyxin use, 2019"]
},
"colistimethate": {
  "origin": "Colistin, also called polymyxin E, was discovered in 1949 by Japanese researcher Y. Koyama and colleagues from a soil bacterium, Bacillus polymyxa var. colistinus (now Paenibacillus). Colistin itself was very irritating when injected, so drug makers created colistimethate sodium, a less toxic inactive form that the body converts to colistin. It was marketed in the US as Coly-Mycin M. Like polymyxin B, it fell out of favor in the 1970s due to kidney and nerve toxicity. In the 2000s it returned as a last-line drug for multidrug-resistant gram-negative infections. In 2015 researchers in China reported mcr-1, a plasmid gene that spreads colistin resistance between bacteria, linked to heavy colistin use in farm animals.",
  "timeline": [["1949", "Koyama and colleagues in Japan discover colistin."], ["1970s", "Use declines because of kidney and nerve toxicity."], ["2000s", "Colistin returns as a last-line drug against resistant gram-negatives."], ["2015", "Researchers in China report the transferable colistin resistance gene mcr-1."]],
  "cell": "Colistimethate is an inactive prodrug. In solution and in the body, it is slowly hydrolyzed to colistin, the active form. Colistin is a cationic cyclic lipopeptide. Its positive charges bind the negatively charged phosphate groups on lipid A of lipopolysaccharide in the gram-negative outer membrane, displacing the calcium and magnesium that stabilize it. The fatty acid tail inserts into the membrane, which becomes leaky; colistin then disrupts the inner membrane, cell contents escape and the bacterium dies. Killing is concentration dependent. It also binds endotoxin. Resistance happens when bacteria modify lipid A with positive groups, including through chromosomal changes or the plasmid gene mcr-1.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, Enterobacter and E. coli; Proteus, Serratia, Providencia, Burkholderia and gram-positives are naturally resistant."], ["Urinary tract", "Because the prodrug is cleared by the kidneys and converts to colistin in urine, it reaches higher urine levels than polymyxin B."], ["Kidneys", "Colistin is reabsorbed by tubule cells and damages them, causing rising creatinine and low urine output."], ["Nerves", "Tingling, numbness, dizziness and confusion reflect effects on nerve membranes."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "It can block nerve-to-muscle signaling, leading to weakness, apnea and respiratory arrest."]],
  "journey": "Colistimethate is given IV or IM, and it is also inhaled in some settings. In the blood it is partly cleared by the kidneys as prodrug and partly converts to colistin, which takes time to reach steady levels, so loading doses are used. Colistimethate has a short half-life of about two hours. Colistin itself is cleared mostly by nonrenal routes because the kidney tubules reabsorb it, and its half-life is longer, often 9 to 14 hours in critical illness. Doses are confusing because products are labeled in colistin base activity or international units, which is a known source of errors.",
  "sidefx": "Nephrotoxicity is the main limitation: colistin builds up in proximal tubule cells and damages their membranes and mitochondria. Neurotoxicity, such as tingling and dizziness, reflects similar membrane effects on nerves. Neuromuscular blockade can cause apnea, especially with anesthetics or other neuromuscular blockers. Premixed solutions left standing can convert to more colistin and become more toxic. There is no specific antidote; kidney function and breathing are watched closely.",
  "fun": "The mcr-1 gene was discovered in bacteria from pigs in China, a reminder that antibiotic use in agriculture can affect human medicine.",
  "src": ["Coly-Mycin M Parenteral (colistimethate) FDA label", "Liu YY, et al. Lancet Infect Dis, 2016", "International consensus guidelines for polymyxin use, 2019"]
},
"penicillin-g-procaine": {
  "origin": "After Alexander Fleming's 1928 discovery of penicillin and Florey and Chain's purification around 1940, doctors quickly found that penicillin G left the body within hours, so it had to be injected many times a day. Researchers looked for ways to make a depot in the muscle. Procaine, the local anesthetic synthesized by Alfred Einhorn in 1905, formed a poorly soluble salt with penicillin G. Procaine penicillin, introduced in the late 1940s, released penicillin over about a day and numbed the injection site too. It was widely used for syphilis, gonorrhea and pneumonia. It is also combined with benzathine penicillin in Bicillin C-R, a separate product.",
  "timeline": [["1905", "Alfred Einhorn synthesizes procaine."], ["1928", "Alexander Fleming observes penicillin."], ["1940s", "Procaine penicillin G is introduced as a repository IM penicillin."]],
  "cell": "Penicillin G procaine is a salt of penicillin G and procaine that dissolves slowly from muscle, releasing penicillin G over about 24 hours. Penicillin G binds penicillin-binding proteins, the transpeptidases that cross-link peptidoglycan, because its beta-lactam ring resembles the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of the wall building block. The enzyme is acylated and stops working, the wall weakens, autolysins break it further and the bacterium lyses. Killing is time dependent, so steady moderate levels work well. Beta-lactamases destroy penicillin G. The procaine part blocks sodium channels in local nerves, reducing injection pain, and is hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Kills streptococci, pneumococci, Treponema pallidum and other penicillin-susceptible organisms with moderate levels lasting about a day."], ["Injection site", "Procaine numbs the muscle, but the thick suspension still causes some pain and swelling."], ["Brain", "Procaine reaching the blood can cause a frightening reaction with anxiety, hallucinations, a sense of doom and sometimes seizures (Hoigne syndrome)."], ["Whole body", "In syphilis, dying spirochetes cause fever, chills and aches within hours (Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction)."], ["Kidneys and immune system", "Rarely it causes interstitial nephritis or serum sickness, which are immune reactions."]],
  "journey": "Penicillin G procaine is given only by deep IM injection. The depot releases penicillin G slowly, with peak levels in about one to four hours and useful levels for about 24 hours. Once absorbed, penicillin G has a half-life of about 30 minutes and is cleared mostly unchanged by kidney tubular secretion, which probenecid can slow. Procaine is hydrolyzed by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA and diethylaminoethanol. It must never be given IV.",
  "sidefx": "Allergy, including anaphylaxis, is an immune reaction to penicillin breakdown products and is treated with epinephrine. The procaine reaction (Hoigne syndrome) happens when some suspension enters a vessel and procaine reaches the brain, causing sudden fear, hallucinations and sometimes seizures; it is distinct from allergy and usually passes. People allergic to procaine or with pseudocholinesterase deficiency are at higher risk. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is caused by cytokines released as spirochetes die. Accidental IV injection can cause cardiorespiratory arrest.",
  "fun": "Before benzathine penicillin, procaine penicillin was a workhorse of mass syphilis treatment campaigns in the late 1940s and 1950s.",
  "src": ["Penicillin G Procaine Injectable Suspension FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"quinupristin-dalfopristin": {
  "origin": "Streptogramins come from Streptomyces bacteria. Pristinamycin, an oral streptogramin from Streptomyces pristinaespiralis, was used in France for decades, and virginiamycin was used in animal feed. These natural products were mixtures of two parts, A and B, that worked much better together. Chemists at Rhone-Poulenc Rorer made water-soluble derivatives, quinupristin (from pristinamycin IA, a B component) and dalfopristin (from pristinamycin IIA, an A component), so the pair could be given IV. The FDA approved the combination as Synercid in 1999, at a time of rising vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium, for which it was one of the very few options.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Pristinamycin from Streptomyces pristinaespiralis is used in France."], ["1999", "FDA approves Synercid (quinupristin-dalfopristin)."]],
  "cell": "Both drugs bind the 50S ribosomal subunit at neighboring sites. Dalfopristin, the streptogramin A part, binds the peptidyl transferase center of 23S rRNA and blocks early chain elongation. Its binding changes the ribosome's shape so quinupristin, the streptogramin B part, binds about a hundred times more tightly nearby in the exit tunnel where new protein emerges. Quinupristin blocks later elongation and causes release of incomplete peptides. Each alone is bacteriostatic, but together they are synergistic and bactericidal against most staphylococci and streptococci. Against E. faecium they are bacteriostatic. E. faecalis is naturally resistant because of an efflux pump (Lsa). Resistance can also come from erm methylation, which blocks the B part.",
  "body": [["Bacteria", "Treats vancomycin-resistant E. faecium and some staphylococcal and streptococcal skin infections; it does not cover E. faecalis."], ["Veins", "The infusion strongly irritates veins, causing pain, inflammation and thrombophlebitis, so a central line is often used."], ["Joints and muscles", "Arthralgia and myalgia, sometimes severe, are common, especially with longer courses."], ["Liver", "Bilirubin and liver enzymes can rise, partly because the drugs compete with bilirubin for excretion."], ["Drug interactions", "It inhibits CYP3A4, raising levels of drugs such as cyclosporine, midazolam and some calcium channel blockers."]],
  "journey": "Synercid is given by IV infusion, mixed only in dextrose, because saline makes it incompatible. Both parts are converted by nonenzymatic reactions to active metabolites: quinupristin conjugates with glutathione and cysteine, and dalfopristin is hydrolyzed to pristinamycin IIA. The parent drugs have half-lives under an hour, but a post-antibiotic effect extends their action. Most of the drug and metabolites leave in bile and feces, with a smaller part in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion site pain and thrombophlebitis are the most common problems and come from direct irritation of the vein wall; flushing the line with dextrose rather than saline helps. Arthralgia and myalgia are distinctive, with an uncertain mechanism; they can be severe enough to stop therapy. Hyperbilirubinemia reflects liver handling and competition for excretion. CYP3A4 inhibition explains drug interactions. C. difficile results from loss of normal gut flora. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The name Synercid reflects synergy: neither part alone is very strong, but together they kill bacteria.",
  "src": ["Synercid (quinupristin and dalfopristin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"pentamidine": {
  "origin": "Pentamidine was born from a search for diabetes drugs. In the 1930s researchers noticed that the guanidine compound synthalin lowered blood sugar and also killed trypanosomes, which need a lot of glucose. In the late 1930s British researchers, including chemists at the drug company May and Baker, made a series of aromatic diamidines, and pentamidine stood out against African sleeping sickness. It was used in Africa from the 1940s. It later proved effective against Pneumocystis pneumonia. In 1981, unusual requests to the CDC for pentamidine, then available only from the CDC, helped reveal the start of the AIDS epidemic. An inhaled form, NebuPent, was approved in 1989 to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "British researchers, including May and Baker chemists, develop aromatic diamidines such as pentamidine."], ["1940s", "Pentamidine is used against African sleeping sickness."], ["1981", "Requests for pentamidine at the CDC help reveal the emerging AIDS epidemic."], ["1989", "Inhaled pentamidine (NebuPent) is approved to prevent Pneumocystis pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Pentamidine is an aromatic diamidine with two positively charged ends. Its exact mechanism is not fully known, and it likely acts in several ways. It binds AT-rich regions of the DNA minor groove, especially the kinetoplast DNA in the mitochondria of trypanosomes and Leishmania. It also inhibits topoisomerases and interferes with DNA, RNA, phospholipid and protein synthesis and with mitochondrial function. Susceptible organisms concentrate it through specific transporters, which helps explain selective killing. In people, pentamidine is toxic to pancreatic beta cells: it first causes them to release insulin, which drops blood sugar, and then can destroy them, leading to high blood sugar or diabetes.",
  "body": [["Lungs", "Kills Pneumocystis jirovecii, an alternative treatment for Pneumocystis pneumonia when trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole cannot be used."], ["Pancreas", "Beta cell injury causes early hypoglycemia and later hyperglycemia; pancreatitis can also occur."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Fast IV infusion can drop blood pressure, and QT prolongation can cause torsades de pointes."], ["Kidneys", "Nephrotoxicity raises creatinine and can raise potassium and lower calcium and magnesium."], ["Bone marrow", "White cell and platelet counts can fall."], ["Injection site", "IM injection often causes sterile abscesses and pain."]],
  "journey": "For treatment pentamidine is given by slow IV infusion or, less often, IM; it is not absorbed by mouth. The inhaled form is used for prevention and reaches the lungs with little entering the blood. After IV dosing it binds tissues heavily and builds up in the liver, kidneys, spleen and lungs. Little is metabolized. The initial half-life is about six hours, but it is released from tissues slowly over weeks. A small part leaves in urine unchanged.",
  "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia, which can be sudden and severe, comes from injured beta cells dumping insulin; later beta cell destruction causes hyperglycemia, so blood glucose is checked often. Hypotension during fast infusion reflects vasodilation, so it is given slowly with the patient lying down. QT prolongation and torsades come from blocking cardiac potassium currents. Kidney damage, pancreatitis, low blood counts and electrolyte changes are direct toxic effects. Hypoglycemia is treated with dextrose. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "In 1981 a CDC drug technician, Sandra Ford, noticed a cluster of unusual pentamidine requests for young men with Pneumocystis pneumonia, one of the first clues to AIDS.",
  "src": ["Pentam 300 (pentamidine isethionate) FDA label", "CDC MMWR, June 5, 1981", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"telavancin": {
  "origin": "Telavancin is a semisynthetic cousin of vancomycin. Vancomycin itself came from a soil microbe, now called Amycolatopsis orientalis, found in a soil sample from Borneo and developed by Eli Lilly in the 1950s. As MRSA and vancomycin-resistant strains spread, chemists looked for ways to make the glycopeptide scaffold hit harder. Scientists at Theravance in South San Francisco added a fat-loving (lipophilic) side chain and a polar phosphonate group to the vancomycin core. The fatty tail let the molecule anchor in bacterial membranes, giving it a second way to kill. The FDA approved telavancin as Vibativ in 2009 for complicated skin infections and in 2013 for hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia caused by Staphylococcus aureus.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Vancomycin is isolated from a soil actinomycete and developed by Eli Lilly."], ["2000s", "Theravance designs telavancin by adding a lipophilic side chain to the vancomycin core."], ["2009", "FDA approves telavancin (Vibativ) for complicated skin and skin structure infections."], ["2013", "Approval expands to hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated bacterial pneumonia."]],
  "cell": "Telavancin is a lipoglycopeptide with two actions. First, like vancomycin, its glycopeptide core forms hydrogen bonds with the D-alanyl-D-alanine end of lipid II, the building block that carries new peptidoglycan to the cell wall. Capping that end blocks transglycosylation (linking sugars into chains) and transpeptidation (cross-linking the chains), so the wall cannot be built. Second, its lipophilic decylaminoethyl tail inserts into the bacterial cell membrane near lipid II. This depolarizes the membrane and makes it leaky, so ions escape and the cell loses its energy gradient. Together these effects give rapid, concentration-dependent killing of gram-positive bacteria, including MRSA. Gram-negative bacteria are not affected because their outer membrane keeps the large molecule out.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Kills Staphylococcus aureus, including MRSA, and streptococci in deep skin infections, abscesses and wound infections."], ["Lungs", "Treats hospital and ventilator pneumonia caused by susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, used when other options are not suitable."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the drug leaves through the kidneys, and it can injure them. Kidney injury and worse outcomes were seen in patients who already had moderate or severe kidney impairment."], ["Heart", "It can lengthen the QTc interval on the ECG, which matters when combined with other QT-prolonging drugs."], ["Taste and gut", "A metallic or soapy taste, nausea and vomiting are common and reflect drug effects on taste and the gut."], ["Lab tests", "It binds the phospholipid reagents used in some clotting tests, falsely prolonging PT/INR and aPTT without truly thinning the blood."]],
  "journey": "Telavancin is given only by IV infusion because it is not absorbed from the gut. It stays mostly in the bloodstream and tissues, tightly bound to albumin. It is not significantly broken down by liver CYP enzymes. The kidneys clear most of it unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about eight hours in normal kidney function, which supports once-daily dosing. Reduced kidney function raises levels and lengthens its stay.",
  "sidefx": "Kidney injury likely comes from drug concentrating in kidney tubules, the same organ that clears it, so pre-existing kidney disease raises risk. Taste disturbance and nausea are common. QTc prolongation reflects an effect on cardiac repolarization. Rapid infusion can release histamine and cause flushing and itching (an infusion reaction similar to vancomycin's), which is why it is infused slowly. Animal studies showed fetal harm, so the label carries a warning about pregnancy. Foamy urine has been reported. Like other antibiotics, it can allow C. difficile overgrowth. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Telavancin can make clotting tests look abnormal for hours after a dose. The label notes that blood for these tests is drawn just before the next dose, when drug levels are lowest.",
  "src": ["Vibativ (telavancin) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"landiolol": {
  "origin": "Landiolol was designed in Japan by Ono Pharmaceutical as an ultra-short-acting beta blocker, building on the idea behind esmolol: put an ester bond in the molecule so blood enzymes break it down within minutes. The goal was a beta blocker that could be turned on and off quickly in the ICU and operating room, and that was even more selective for heart beta1 receptors than esmolol so it would lower blood pressure less. It was approved in Japan in the early 2000s and was later used in Europe. The FDA approved it as Rapiblyk in 2024 for short-term control of a fast ventricular rate in supraventricular tachycardia, including atrial fibrillation and flutter.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Esmolol establishes the concept of an esterase-cleared, ultra-short-acting beta blocker."], ["2000s", "Landiolol is approved in Japan for perioperative tachyarrhythmias."], ["2024", "FDA approves landiolol (Rapiblyk) for rate control in supraventricular tachycardia."]],
  "cell": "Landiolol competitively blocks beta1-adrenergic receptors, which are Gs protein-coupled receptors found mainly on heart cells. Normally norepinephrine binding activates Gs, which turns on adenylyl cyclase, raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A. In the SA node this speeds phase 4 depolarization through funny (If) channels and L-type calcium channels; in the AV node it speeds conduction. By blocking the receptor, landiolol lowers cAMP, slowing the SA node's firing rate and lengthening AV nodal conduction and refractoriness. That slows the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation or flutter. Its beta1 selectivity is very high, roughly 255 to 1 over beta2, so it has less effect on bronchial and vascular beta2 receptors. It has no intrinsic sympathomimetic activity.",
  "body": [["Heart rate", "Slows the SA node and the AV node within minutes, lowering a fast heart rate in atrial fibrillation, flutter and other supraventricular tachycardias."], ["Heart muscle", "Reduces contractility somewhat, but less than many beta blockers, which helps in patients with borderline heart function."], ["Blood pressure", "Lower heart rate and cardiac output can drop blood pressure, though its high beta1 selectivity limits this."], ["Lungs", "High selectivity means less beta2 blockade in the airways, but bronchospasm remains possible in reactive airway disease."], ["Whole body", "Because blood esterases break it down so fast, its effects wear off within minutes after the infusion stops."]],
  "journey": "Landiolol is given only as a continuous IV infusion. It acts within minutes and reaches a steady effect within about 10 to 15 minutes of a rate change. Esterases in the blood and liver (pseudocholinesterase and carboxylesterases) cut its ester bond, producing inactive metabolites. Its half-life is only about 3 to 4 minutes, so the effect fades within minutes after stopping. The inactive metabolites leave through the kidneys. Because it does not rely on the liver or kidneys for its own clearance, its short action is fairly predictable.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come straight from beta1 blockade. Slowing the SA and AV nodes too much causes bradycardia or heart block. Lower cardiac output causes hypotension and can worsen heart failure. Some residual beta2 blockade can trigger bronchospasm. In overdose, severe bradycardia, cardiogenic shock and asystole can occur. The main reversal is stopping the infusion, since the drug clears in minutes. For severe beta blocker toxicity, references describe glucagon, which raises cAMP through its own receptor, plus atropine, vasopressors and pacing.",
  "fun": "Landiolol and esmolol both carry a built-in weak ester link that blood enzymes cut, so the drug switches off almost as fast as it is turned on. Landiolol's half-life of a few minutes is even shorter than esmolol's.",
  "src": ["Rapiblyk (landiolol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"bupivacaine-meloxicam": {
  "origin": "This product joins two older drugs in a new delivery system. Bupivacaine was synthesized in Sweden in 1957 by Bo af Ekenstam and became one of the most widely used long-acting local anesthetics. Meloxicam is an oxicam NSAID developed by Boehringer Ingelheim. Heron Therapeutics combined them in a thick, bioerodible polymer that is dripped into the open surgical wound and slowly releases both drugs for about three days. Heron's reasoning was that inflamed, acidic wound tissue makes local anesthetics work poorly, and the NSAID helps by calming that inflammation. The FDA approved it as Zynrelef in 2021 for postsurgical pain after certain operations, with the goal of reducing opioid use.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Bo af Ekenstam synthesizes bupivacaine in Sweden."], ["1990s", "Meloxicam, an oxicam NSAID from Boehringer Ingelheim, enters wide use."], ["2021", "FDA approves bupivacaine and meloxicam extended-release solution (Zynrelef) for postsurgical pain."]],
  "cell": "Bupivacaine is a lipid-soluble amide local anesthetic. The uncharged form crosses the nerve membrane, then picks up a proton inside the cell. The charged form binds a site inside the pore of voltage-gated sodium channels, mostly when the channels are open or inactivated. Blocked channels cannot carry the sodium current needed for an action potential, so pain fibers stop sending signals from the incision. Meloxicam inhibits cyclooxygenase, with a preference for COX-2, lowering prostaglandin production in the wound. Less prostaglandin means less inflammation and less local acidity. Since acidic tissue traps local anesthetic in its charged form outside the nerve, reducing inflammation may help more bupivacaine reach its target. The polymer vehicle releases both drugs slowly over about 72 hours.",
  "body": [["Surgical site", "The gel stays in the wound and keeps nerves near the incision numb for up to about three days while reducing local inflammation."], ["Pain nerves", "Sodium channel blockade stops small pain fibers from firing, so fewer pain signals reach the spinal cord."], ["Brain", "Too much bupivacaine in the blood can cause ringing ears, a metallic taste, confusion and seizures, which are early signs of local anesthetic systemic toxicity."], ["Heart", "High blood levels of bupivacaine can block cardiac sodium channels, causing slow rhythms, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."], ["Kidneys", "Meloxicam can lower kidney blood flow when the body depends on prostaglandins, such as with dehydration."], ["Stomach and vessels", "Like other NSAIDs, meloxicam carries boxed warnings for GI bleeding and for heart attack and stroke."]],
  "journey": "The product is applied once into the wound at the end of surgery, not injected with a needle and not given IV. The polymer slowly erodes and releases both drugs into nearby tissue, and some is absorbed into the blood. Bupivacaine levels peak within the first day, while meloxicam peaks later. Both are highly protein bound. The liver breaks down bupivacaine mainly by CYP3A4 and meloxicam mainly by CYP2C9, and their metabolites leave in urine and, for meloxicam, also feces. Slow release makes their apparent half-lives long.",
  "sidefx": "If enough bupivacaine reaches the blood, it blocks sodium channels in the brain and heart, causing local anesthetic systemic toxicity: seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Giving other local anesthetics in the following days adds to that risk. The rescue for this toxicity is lipid emulsion therapy plus airway and seizure care. Local anesthetics can also cause methemoglobinemia, which is reversed with methylene blue. Meloxicam blocks protective prostaglandins in the kidney and stomach and tilts vessels toward clotting, explaining kidney injury, GI bleeding and cardiovascular warnings. Constipation, nausea and vomiting are common after surgery.",
  "fun": "The applicator is designed to drip the drug onto tissues inside the incision, so the product reaches the wound without a single needle stick.",
  "src": ["Zynrelef (bupivacaine and meloxicam) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"agalsidase-beta": {
  "origin": "Fabry disease was described in 1898 by two dermatologists working separately, Johannes Fabry in Germany and William Anderson in England, who both noticed the dark red skin spots (angiokeratomas) it causes. In 1967 Roscoe Brady and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health showed the cause: missing activity of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-galactosidase A, which lets a fatty substance called globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) build up. Genzyme made the human enzyme in Chinese hamster ovary cells using recombinant DNA. In 2003 the FDA approved agalsidase beta as Fabrazyme, the first enzyme replacement therapy for Fabry disease in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1898", "Johannes Fabry and William Anderson separately describe the disease."], ["1967", "Roscoe Brady's NIH group links Fabry disease to alpha-galactosidase A deficiency."], ["2003", "FDA approves agalsidase beta (Fabrazyme)."]],
  "cell": "Fabry disease comes from variants in the X-linked GLA gene that reduce alpha-galactosidase A activity. Without the enzyme, Gb3 (GL-3) and related lipids pile up in lysosomes, especially in blood vessel endothelium, kidney podocytes, heart muscle and small nerves. Agalsidase beta is recombinant human alpha-galactosidase A carrying mannose-6-phosphate tags on its sugar chains. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors on their surface, and the receptor carries it in endosomes to the lysosome. There, in the acidic environment, the enzyme cuts the terminal alpha-galactose off Gb3, letting the lipid be broken down further. Over time this clears GL-3 deposits from capillary endothelium in the kidney, heart and skin.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Clearing Gb3 from vessel lining cells helps protect small vessels in many organs, which is the root of much of the damage in Fabry disease."], ["Kidneys", "Gb3 builds up in podocytes and tubules, leading to protein in the urine and kidney failure; therapy reduces these deposits."], ["Heart", "Lipid storage in heart muscle causes thickening and arrhythmias. Infusion reactions can stress the heart in advanced cardiomyopathy."], ["Nerves", "Small nerve fiber damage causes burning pain in hands and feet (acroparesthesia); reducing storage may lessen this over time."], ["Skin", "Treatment clears Gb3 from skin capillaries, which was one of the measures used for its approval."], ["Immune system", "Many patients form IgG antibodies against the foreign-looking protein, and some form IgE, which can cause allergic reactions."]],
  "journey": "Agalsidase beta is a large protein, so it is given by IV infusion. It leaves the blood quickly, with a plasma half-life of roughly 45 to 100 minutes, because cells pull it in through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it keeps working long after it is gone from the blood, so infusions every two weeks are enough. It is eventually broken down like any other protein into amino acids. It is not cleared by the liver CYP system or excreted intact by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "The most common problems are infusion reactions such as fever, chills, rigors, flushing and headache. These happen because the immune system sees the recombinant enzyme as foreign and makes antibodies, which can activate complement and release cytokines. True IgE-mediated anaphylaxis is possible, so infusions are given where emergency treatment is available. Slowing the infusion and premedication reduce reactions. Patients with advanced Fabry heart disease may not tolerate the fluid and blood pressure changes of an infusion reaction well. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping or slowing the infusion and treating symptoms.",
  "fun": "Fabry disease is X-linked, but women who carry one faulty GLA copy are not simply carriers. Because of random X inactivation, many have real symptoms.",
  "src": ["Fabrazyme (agalsidase beta) FDA label", "Brady RO et al. N Engl J Med 1967", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"imiglucerase": {
  "origin": "Gaucher disease was first described in 1882 by French medical student Philippe Gaucher, who saw a patient with a huge spleen. In the 1960s Roscoe Brady at the NIH found the cause: a missing lysosomal enzyme, glucocerebrosidase. Brady's team then learned that the enzyme only reached macrophages well if its sugar chains were trimmed to expose mannose, which macrophage mannose receptors recognize. Genzyme first made the enzyme from human placentas as alglucerase (Ceredase), approved in 1991, which took thousands of placentas per patient each year. Imiglucerase (Cerezyme) is the recombinant version made in hamster ovary cells, approved in 1994. It replaced the placental product and became one of the first big successes of enzyme replacement therapy.",
  "timeline": [["1882", "Philippe Gaucher describes the disease in his doctoral thesis."], ["1965", "Roscoe Brady identifies glucocerebrosidase deficiency as the cause."], ["1991", "FDA approves placenta-derived alglucerase (Ceredase)."], ["1994", "FDA approves recombinant imiglucerase (Cerezyme)."]],
  "cell": "In Gaucher disease, variants in the GBA1 gene reduce glucocerebrosidase (acid beta-glucosidase). Glucocerebroside (glucosylceramide), released when old blood cells are broken down, builds up inside macrophage lysosomes. These swollen cells, called Gaucher cells, crowd the spleen, liver and bone marrow. Imiglucerase is recombinant glucocerebrosidase whose sugar chains are trimmed down to expose mannose. Mannose receptors on macrophages bind it and carry it into endosomes and then lysosomes. There it splits glucocerebroside into glucose and ceramide, emptying the storage. It differs from the natural enzyme by one amino acid (arginine to histidine at position 495). It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not treat the brain disease of type 2 Gaucher disease.",
  "body": [["Spleen", "Gaucher cells enlarge the spleen greatly. Clearing stored lipid shrinks it over months."], ["Liver", "The liver also shrinks as macrophage storage clears."], ["Blood", "An enlarged spleen traps and destroys blood cells, and marrow gets crowded. Therapy raises hemoglobin and platelet counts."], ["Bones", "Gaucher cells in marrow cause bone pain, bone crises and fractures. Long-term therapy reduces bone crises."], ["Brain", "The enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not help the neurologic disease of type 2 and only partly addresses type 3."], ["Immune system", "About 15% of patients form IgG antibodies, and some have allergic-type infusion reactions."]],
  "journey": "Imiglucerase is given by IV infusion over one to two hours. Macrophages in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take it up so quickly that its plasma half-life is only minutes. Once inside lysosomes it keeps working, so infusions range from three times weekly to every two weeks. It is broken down like other proteins inside cells and is not cleared through CYP enzymes or excreted intact in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the immune system reacting to a foreign protein. Headache, itching, rash, fever and discomfort at the infusion site are common. About 15% of patients form IgG antibodies, and a few develop hypersensitivity or anaphylaxis, so infusions are given with emergency care available. Patients with antibodies are watched more closely for reactions. There is no antidote; reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms.",
  "fun": "The earlier placental version, alglucerase, needed enzyme from tens of thousands of placentas each year for a single adult patient, which is why the recombinant version mattered so much.",
  "src": ["Cerezyme (imiglucerase) FDA label", "Barton NW et al. N Engl J Med 1991", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"asfotase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Hypophosphatasia was named in 1948 by Canadian pediatrician John Rathbun, who described an infant with soft, poorly mineralized bones and very low alkaline phosphatase in the blood. Later work showed the cause was variants in the ALPL gene, which codes for tissue-nonspecific alkaline phosphatase (TNSALP). Researchers at the Montreal company Enobia Pharma engineered a fusion protein that carries the enzyme to bone using a chain of ten aspartic acids, which sticks to bone mineral. Alexion acquired Enobia in 2011. Clinical trials, many led by Michael Whyte in St. Louis, showed that infants who would likely have died survived and grew. The FDA approved it as Strensiq in 2015, the first treatment for the disease.",
  "timeline": [["1948", "John Rathbun names and describes hypophosphatasia."], ["2000s", "Enobia Pharma develops a bone-targeted alkaline phosphatase fusion protein."], ["2012", "Trial results in infants with life-threatening disease are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2015", "FDA approves asfotase alfa (Strensiq)."]],
  "cell": "TNSALP sits on the surface of bone-forming cells and cleaves inorganic pyrophosphate (PPi), a natural inhibitor of crystal growth. With too little enzyme, PPi builds up and blocks hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate) crystals from forming, so bones and teeth stay soft. TNSALP also removes phosphate from pyridoxal 5-prime-phosphate (PLP, the active form of vitamin B6) so it can cross into cells, including brain cells. Asfotase alfa is a fusion protein made of the TNSALP catalytic domain, the Fc part of human IgG1 (which lengthens its life in blood) and a deca-aspartate tail that binds bone mineral. It concentrates in bone, breaks down PPi and lets mineral deposit normally. It also lowers PLP and phosphoethanolamine, the other built-up substrates.",
  "body": [["Bones", "Breaking down pyrophosphate lets calcium phosphate crystals form, so the skeleton mineralizes, rickets-like changes heal and fractures become less common."], ["Lungs and chest", "In infants, a soft rib cage causes breathing failure. Better rib mineralization improves breathing and survival."], ["Growth", "Children grow better as their growth plates mineralize normally."], ["Teeth", "Early loss of baby teeth is a classic sign because tooth roots cannot anchor without normal mineral."], ["Brain", "Infants can have vitamin B6-dependent seizures because PLP cannot be dephosphorylated to enter the brain; restoring enzyme activity addresses this cause."], ["Skin and fat at injection site", "Repeated injections can cause redness, discoloration, lumps and lipodystrophy, so sites are rotated."]],
  "journey": "Asfotase alfa is injected under the skin several times a week. It is absorbed slowly, peaking about one to two days after a dose. The bone-targeting tail binds hydroxyapatite, so much of the enzyme collects at mineralizing bone. The Fc portion helps protect it from breakdown and gives it a half-life of about five days. Like other proteins, it is eventually broken down into amino acids rather than cleared by the liver or kidneys intact.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site reactions such as redness, pain, swelling and color change are very common, and fat loss or buildup (lipodystrophy) can follow repeated injections in one area. Because the drug promotes mineral deposition, calcium phosphate can form in the wrong places, such as the eyes and kidneys (ectopic calcification and nephrocalcinosis). Craniosynostosis, early fusion of skull sutures, is a known feature of the disease and is watched for during treatment. Antibodies can form against the protein, and hypersensitivity including anaphylaxis can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The bone-targeting tail is ten aspartic acid residues in a row. Their negative charges grab calcium in bone mineral, a little like a magnet for the skeleton.",
  "src": ["Strensiq (asfotase alfa) FDA label", "Whyte MP et al. N Engl J Med 2012", "Rathbun JC. Am J Dis Child 1948"]
},
"patisiran": {
  "origin": "Patisiran rests on RNA interference, a natural gene-silencing process. In 1998 Andrew Fire and Craig Mello showed that double-stranded RNA can switch off a matching gene in worms, work that won the 2006 Nobel Prize. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, founded in 2002, spent years solving the hardest problem: getting fragile RNA into human cells. Their answer for patisiran was a lipid nanoparticle that coats itself with the body's apolipoprotein E and is carried into liver cells. Their target was transthyretin (TTR), a liver-made protein that misfolds into amyloid in hereditary TTR amyloidosis. In 2018 the FDA approved patisiran as Onpattro, the first siRNA medicine ever approved.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference in Caenorhabditis elegans."], ["2002", "Alnylam Pharmaceuticals is founded to develop RNAi medicines."], ["2006", "Fire and Mello receive the Nobel Prize."], ["2018", "FDA approves patisiran (Onpattro), the first siRNA drug, for hATTR polyneuropathy."]],
  "cell": "Transthyretin is a four-part (tetramer) protein made mainly in the liver. It carries thyroxine and retinol-binding protein in the blood. In hereditary ATTR amyloidosis, a TTR gene variant makes the tetramer unstable, so it falls apart into monomers that misfold and stack into amyloid fibrils in nerves, heart and other tissues. Patisiran is a double-stranded siRNA packed in a lipid nanoparticle. In the blood the particle picks up apolipoprotein E and enters liver cells through LDL receptors. Inside, the guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC). RISC finds TTR mRNA by base pairing in its 3-prime untranslated region and cuts it. Because it targets a region shared by normal and variant TTR, both are lowered, by about 80%. Less TTR means less raw material for amyloid.",
  "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Less amyloid forms in nerves, slowing the numbness, weakness and pain of hATTR polyneuropathy, and in trials many patients improved."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Amyloid damages nerves that control blood pressure, bowels and bladder; lowering TTR may slow this damage."], ["Heart", "Amyloid in heart muscle stiffens it; studies showed effects on heart function and markers, though its US label is for polyneuropathy."], ["Liver", "The liver is the site of action, where it silences TTR production in hepatocytes."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "TTR helps carry vitamin A in the blood, so serum vitamin A falls; supplementation is used to avoid night blindness."], ["Immune system", "The lipid particle can trigger infusion reactions, so premedication is given beforehand."]],
  "journey": "Patisiran is given by IV infusion every three weeks after premedication with a steroid, acetaminophen and antihistamines. The lipid nanoparticle travels in the blood, picks up apolipoprotein E and is taken into liver cells. The siRNA itself has a half-life of about three days in plasma, but its silencing effect in the liver lasts much longer, with maximal TTR lowering about 10 to 14 days after a dose. Nucleases break down the RNA, and esterases clear the lipid parts. Less than 1% leaves unchanged in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as flushing, back pain, nausea, shortness of breath and headache happen because the lipid nanoparticles activate parts of the innate immune system. Premedication with a steroid, antihistamines and acetaminophen reduces this. Lowering TTR also lowers its partner, retinol-binding protein, so blood vitamin A falls and eye symptoms such as night blindness are possible without supplements. Upper respiratory infections were more common in trials. There is no antidote; infusion reactions are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion.",
  "fun": "The drug's name is a hint at how it works: patisiran ends in -siran, the stem used for small interfering RNA drugs.",
  "src": ["Onpattro (patisiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998", "Adams D et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (APOLLO)"]
},
"vutrisiran": {
  "origin": "Vutrisiran is Alnylam's second-generation TTR silencer. After patisiran showed that RNA interference could treat hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis, Alnylam worked to drop the lipid particle and the IV infusion. Their solution was GalNAc conjugation: attaching three N-acetylgalactosamine sugars that bind a receptor found almost only on liver cells. Vutrisiran also uses an enhanced chemical stabilization pattern that protects it from breakdown, so it can be given as a small injection under the skin every three months. The FDA approved it as Amvuttra in 2022 for hATTR polyneuropathy, and in 2025 for ATTR cardiomyopathy, both wild-type and hereditary, based on the HELIOS-B trial.",
  "timeline": [["2018", "Patisiran becomes the first approved siRNA drug for hATTR."], ["2022", "FDA approves vutrisiran (Amvuttra) for hATTR polyneuropathy."], ["2024", "HELIOS-B trial reports fewer deaths and cardiovascular events in ATTR cardiomyopathy."], ["2025", "FDA expands approval to ATTR cardiomyopathy."]],
  "cell": "Vutrisiran is a chemically modified double-stranded siRNA attached to a triantennary GalNAc ligand. After injection, GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, and the drug is pulled into the cell by endocytosis. A small fraction escapes the endosome and the guide strand loads into RISC. RISC uses base pairing to find TTR mRNA and cleaves it, so ribosomes cannot make TTR protein. The target sequence is shared by normal and variant TTR, so both drop, by more than 80%. With less TTR circulating, fewer unstable tetramers break into monomers that misfold into amyloid fibrils in nerves and heart.",
  "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Lower TTR slows amyloid buildup in nerves, slowing and in some patients improving the neuropathy of hATTR."], ["Heart", "In ATTR cardiomyopathy it reduced cardiovascular death and heart failure events in HELIOS-B, reflecting less amyloid in heart muscle over time."], ["Liver", "The liver is where it works, silencing TTR production in hepatocytes taken up through ASGPR."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "Lower TTR means lower retinol-binding protein and vitamin A in blood, so vitamin A supplementation is recommended to protect night vision."], ["Joints and limbs", "Joint pain and pain in the arms or legs were reported more often in trials."]],
  "journey": "Vutrisiran is injected under the skin by a health care professional every three months. GalNAc targeting sends it rapidly to the liver, so it leaves the blood within hours (plasma half-life about five hours). Inside liver cells it is very stable and keeps RISC loaded for months, which is why maximum TTR lowering takes about three to four months. Nucleases slowly break it into shorter pieces. About a fifth of a dose leaves unchanged in urine. It is not a CYP substrate.",
  "sidefx": "Vutrisiran is generally well tolerated. Its main expected effect is low vitamin A, because TTR carries retinol-binding protein; without supplements, night blindness and other eye symptoms can occur. Injection site reactions are usually mild. Joint pain, breathlessness and limb pain were reported in trials. Because it uses GalNAc delivery rather than lipid nanoparticles, it does not need premedication and causes few infusion-type reactions. There is no antidote, and its effect wears off slowly over months.",
  "fun": "A single injection silences TTR for about three months, so the drug can keep working in the liver long after it has disappeared from the blood.",
  "src": ["Amvuttra (vutrisiran) FDA label", "Fontana M et al. N Engl J Med 2025 (HELIOS-B)", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tafamidis": {
  "origin": "Tafamidis came from basic chemistry research by Jeffery Kelly at the Scripps Research Institute. Kelly's lab showed that transthyretin amyloid forms when the four-part TTR protein first falls apart, and that this breakup is the slow, rate-limiting step. They also noticed that some people carry a protective TTR variant that holds the tetramer together. That suggested a drug that binds the tetramer and stabilizes it could prevent disease. Kelly's team designed small molecules that fit into TTR's empty thyroxine-binding pockets. The biotech FoldRx developed tafamidis, and Pfizer acquired FoldRx in 2010. Europe approved it in 2011 for polyneuropathy. In 2019 the FDA approved Vyndaqel and Vyndamax for ATTR cardiomyopathy after the ATTR-ACT trial showed fewer deaths and hospital stays.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Jeffery Kelly's lab shows TTR tetramer dissociation is the rate-limiting step in amyloid formation."], ["2000s", "Kelly's group designs small-molecule kinetic stabilizers, leading to tafamidis."], ["2011", "Tafamidis is approved in the European Union for hATTR polyneuropathy."], ["2018", "The ATTR-ACT trial reports lower mortality and cardiovascular hospitalizations."], ["2019", "FDA approves tafamidis (Vyndaqel, Vyndamax) for ATTR cardiomyopathy."]],
  "cell": "Transthyretin circulates as a tetramer of four identical subunits. It has two binding channels for thyroxine at the dimer-dimer interface, but in the blood these sites are almost always empty because other proteins carry most thyroxine. For TTR to form amyloid, the tetramer must first dissociate into monomers, which then partly unfold and assemble into fibrils. Tafamidis binds selectively into the thyroxine-binding sites and bridges the two dimers. This raises the energy barrier for dissociation, a process called kinetic stabilization. With the tetramer held together, fewer monomers are available to misfold. It works on both wild-type and many variant forms of TTR. It does not lower TTR production, so serum TTR levels stay the same or rise.",
  "body": [["Heart", "Less amyloid builds up in heart muscle over time, which slows stiffening and loss of function. In ATTR-ACT, the benefit on death and hospitalizations appeared after about 18 months."], ["Peripheral nerves", "In other countries tafamidis is used to slow polyneuropathy from hereditary TTR amyloidosis."], ["Thyroid hormone", "Although it occupies thyroxine-binding sites on TTR, it does not cause clinical thyroid problems because other proteins carry most thyroxine."], ["Gut and urinary tract", "Diarrhea, upper abdominal pain and urinary tract infections were reported in trials, but overall the drug is well tolerated."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies raised concern for fetal harm, so the label advises against pregnancy during treatment."]],
  "journey": "Tafamidis is taken by mouth once daily and is well absorbed. Vyndamax (tafamidis free acid) peaks about four hours after a dose. In the blood it binds almost entirely to proteins, mostly to transthyretin itself, its own target. It has a long half-life of about two days. The liver conjugates it with glucuronic acid, and it leaves in feces and urine, mostly as the glucuronide. Vyndaqel and Vyndamax are different salt forms and are not interchangeable capsule for capsule.",
  "sidefx": "Tafamidis has few side effects because it simply sits in a protein pocket and stabilizes it, without blocking an enzyme or receptor. Diarrhea, urinary tract infection and upper abdominal pain were more common in trials. Animal studies showed embryo-fetal harm, so pregnancy is avoided during treatment. It does not reverse amyloid already deposited, so disease progression can continue. There is no antidote, and none is generally needed.",
  "fun": "The idea for tafamidis came partly from nature: a TTR variant called T119M, found in some Portuguese families, protects carriers from amyloid disease by making the tetramer extra stable.",
  "src": ["Vyndaqel and Vyndamax (tafamidis) FDA label", "Maurer MS et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (ATTR-ACT)", "Bulawa CE et al. PNAS 2012"]
},
"nitisinone": {
  "origin": "Nitisinone began as herbicide research. Scientists at a British agrochemical company (later part of Zeneca) studied leptospermone, a natural chemical from the bottlebrush plant Callistemon that kept weeds from growing near it. Their synthetic triketone, nitisinone (NTBC), blocked the plant enzyme 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPPD), which plants need to make pigments. When toxicology tests in rats raised blood tyrosine and caused eye lesions, researchers realized it blocked the same enzyme in animals. Swedish physician Sven Lindstedt and colleagues in Gothenburg saw that blocking HPPD could stop the toxic buildup in hereditary tyrosinemia type 1 and treated the first children in 1991, reporting results in The Lancet in 1992. It turned a disease that often killed children through liver failure into a manageable condition. The FDA approved it as Orfadin in 2002.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Agrochemical scientists study leptospermone, a natural herbicide from Callistemon plants."], ["1980s", "Synthetic triketone nitisinone is found to inhibit HPPD."], ["1992", "Lindstedt and colleagues report treating tyrosinemia type 1 with nitisinone in The Lancet."], ["2002", "FDA approves nitisinone (Orfadin)."]],
  "cell": "Tyrosine is broken down in the liver in a series of steps. The second step is 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase (HPPD), which turns 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate into homogentisate. The final step is fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH). In tyrosinemia type 1, FAH is missing, so maleylacetoacetate and fumarylacetoacetate build up and are converted to succinylacetone. These compounds damage DNA and proteins in liver cells and kidney tubules, cause liver failure and liver cancer, and block heme synthesis, which causes porphyria-like nerve crises. Nitisinone is a reversible competitive inhibitor of HPPD. Blocking this upstream step prevents formation of the toxic downstream products. The trade-off is that tyrosine itself rises, so a diet low in tyrosine and phenylalanine is required.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Stopping toxic metabolite buildup prevents acute liver failure, reverses liver damage and greatly lowers the risk of liver cancer, especially when started early."], ["Kidneys", "Protecting tubule cells reverses Fanconi-type losses of phosphate, glucose and amino acids, helping rickets heal."], ["Nerves", "Succinylacetone blocks an enzyme in heme synthesis; lowering it prevents painful porphyria-like neurologic crises."], ["Eyes", "Because tyrosine rises, tyrosine crystals can form in the cornea, causing irritation, light sensitivity and corneal clouding."], ["Skin", "High tyrosine can cause painful thickened plaques on palms and soles."], ["Blood", "Low platelets and low white cells can occur and are monitored."]],
  "journey": "Nitisinone is taken by mouth and is well absorbed, peaking about three hours after a dose. It is highly protein bound and has a long half-life of about two days in healthy volunteers. It is largely stable in the body, with only minor CYP3A4 metabolism. It is eliminated in urine and feces, though data are limited. Succinylacetone in the urine falls within days, while liver recovery takes weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from the predictable rise in tyrosine caused by blocking HPPD. Tyrosine is poorly soluble, so when levels get high it can crystallize in the cornea and cause conjunctivitis, keratitis, photophobia and corneal opacities, and it can cause skin plaques on palms and soles. A strict low-tyrosine, low-phenylalanine diet keeps levels down. Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia can occur. If therapy lapses, toxic metabolites return and liver failure or cancer risk rises again. There is no antidote; diet control is the main way to manage tyrosine.",
  "fun": "The same chemistry is still used on farms: mesotrione, a close relative of nitisinone, is a common corn herbicide that blocks HPPD in weeds.",
  "src": ["Orfadin (nitisinone) FDA label", "Lindstedt S et al. Lancet 1992", "Lock EA et al. J Inherit Metab Dis 1998"]
},
"sapropterin": {
  "origin": "Phenylketonuria was discovered in 1934 by Norwegian physician Asbjorn Folling, who found phenylpyruvic acid in the urine of children with intellectual disability. Later research showed that the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) needs a helper molecule, tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4), to work. In 1999, Japanese researcher Shigeo Kure and colleagues reported that some children with classic PAH-deficient PKU lowered their blood phenylalanine when given BH4. This meant a cofactor could boost a weak but present enzyme. BioMarin developed sapropterin dihydrochloride, a synthetic form of the natural 6R-BH4. The FDA approved it as Kuvan in 2007, the first drug treatment for PKU beyond diet.",
  "timeline": [["1934", "Asbjorn Folling describes phenylketonuria."], ["1950s", "Low-phenylalanine diet therapy is introduced."], ["1999", "Kure and colleagues report BH4-responsive PAH deficiency."], ["2007", "FDA approves sapropterin (Kuvan) for BH4-responsive PKU."]],
  "cell": "Phenylalanine hydroxylase in the liver converts phenylalanine to tyrosine. It needs BH4 as a cofactor and uses oxygen and iron. In PKU, PAH variants reduce enzyme activity, so phenylalanine builds up in the blood and brain, where it interferes with myelin and neurotransmitter production. Sapropterin is synthetic 6R-BH4. In patients who still have some PAH activity, extra BH4 works two ways: it saturates the cofactor site, and it acts as a pharmacologic chaperone, helping misfolded PAH fold correctly and resist breakdown. The result is more conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine. People with null PAH variants (no enzyme at all) usually do not respond. BH4 is also the cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase, tryptophan hydroxylase and nitric oxide synthase.",
  "body": [["Blood and brain", "In responders, blood phenylalanine falls, which protects brain development and function and reduces problems with attention, mood and thinking."], ["Diet", "Lower phenylalanine lets many responders eat more natural protein, though most still follow a modified diet."], ["Liver", "The liver is where PAH lives, so this is where sapropterin does its work."], ["Gut", "Headache, diarrhea, abdominal pain and vomiting are common; gastritis and esophagitis have been reported."], ["Nervous system", "Because BH4 is also a cofactor for dopamine and serotonin synthesis, hyperactivity has been reported."]],
  "journey": "Sapropterin is taken by mouth once daily, dissolved in water or juice or mixed with soft food. Taking it with a meal increases absorption. It peaks about three to four hours later and has a half-life of about seven hours. The body handles it like natural BH4: it is oxidized to dihydrobiopterin and biopterin, recycled and excreted. A trial period with blood phenylalanine checks shows whether a person responds.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild. Headache, runny nose, diarrhea, abdominal pain and vomiting are common. Inflammation of the stomach or esophagus has occurred. Phenylalanine can drop too low (hypophenylalaninemia) in some patients, especially young children, because phenylalanine is still an essential amino acid. Since BH4 supports nitric oxide synthase, caution is used with drugs that affect nitric oxide, such as PDE5 inhibitors. Because BH4 helps make dopamine, hyperactivity can occur. Allergic reactions including anaphylaxis are rare. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Newborn screening for PKU started with Robert Guthrie's blood spot test in the early 1960s, the same heel-prick card used for newborn screening today.",
  "src": ["Kuvan (sapropterin) FDA label", "Kure S et al. J Pediatr 1999", "Folling A. Hoppe-Seyler's Z Physiol Chem 1934"]
},
"metreleptin": {
  "origin": "In 1994 Jeffrey Friedman and his team at Rockefeller University cloned the obese (ob) gene in mice and found it codes for a hormone made by fat cells. They named it leptin, from the Greek word for thin. Leptin tells the brain how much fat the body has stored. Hopes that leptin would treat common obesity faded because most people with obesity already have high leptin and are resistant to it. But people who lack fat tissue, as in generalized lipodystrophy, have very low leptin, which leads to huge appetite, severe diabetes and fatty liver. Researchers at the NIH, including Elif Oral and Phillip Gorden, reported in 2002 that leptin replacement dramatically improved these patients. Amgen originally developed the recombinant protein. The FDA approved metreleptin as Myalept in 2014.",
  "timeline": [["1994", "Jeffrey Friedman's lab clones the ob gene and discovers leptin."], ["2002", "NIH researchers report leptin replacement benefits in lipodystrophy in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2014", "FDA approves metreleptin (Myalept) for generalized lipodystrophy."]],
  "cell": "Leptin is a hormone secreted by fat cells in proportion to fat mass. Metreleptin is recombinant human leptin with an extra methionine at the N-terminus. It binds the leptin receptor (LepR), a class I cytokine receptor found in the hypothalamus and peripheral tissues. Binding activates JAK2, which phosphorylates the receptor and the transcription factor STAT3. In the arcuate nucleus, STAT3 signaling increases POMC neurons (which reduce appetite) and decreases NPY and AgRP neurons (which drive hunger). Leptin also acts on the liver and muscle, directly and through the brain, to increase fat oxidation and improve insulin sensitivity. In lipodystrophy, where there is no fat to store excess lipid, replacing the missing leptin cuts food intake and moves lipid out of liver and muscle.",
  "body": [["Brain (hypothalamus)", "Restores the fullness signal, reducing the constant hunger seen in people with very low leptin."], ["Liver", "Reduces fat in the liver and liver size, lowering liver enzymes over time."], ["Blood sugar", "Improves insulin sensitivity, lowering HbA1c and the need for insulin; hypoglycemia can occur if diabetes drugs are not adjusted."], ["Blood fats", "Triglycerides fall, which lowers the risk of pancreatitis in these patients."], ["Reproductive system", "Leptin helps signal energy sufficiency to the reproductive axis, and menstrual cycles can return."], ["Immune system", "Neutralizing antibodies can form, and T-cell lymphoma has been reported in patients with acquired lipodystrophy."]],
  "journey": "Metreleptin is injected under the skin once daily. It peaks about four hours after a dose and has a half-life of about four hours. Like natural leptin, it is cleared mainly through the kidneys, where it is filtered and broken down. Metabolic effects such as lower triglycerides and HbA1c build over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Hypoglycemia occurs when improved insulin sensitivity meets unchanged insulin or sulfonylurea doses. Weight loss reflects reduced appetite. Neutralizing antibodies can develop and may block both the drug and any leftover natural leptin, leading to severe infections or loss of metabolic control. T-cell lymphoma has been seen in patients with acquired generalized lipodystrophy, many of whom had immune abnormalities before treatment; whether the drug plays a role is unclear. Because of these risks it is available only through a REMS program. Leptin can stimulate immune cells, which may worsen autoimmune diseases. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The original ob/ob mice were so obese they weighed about three times as much as normal mice, and leptin injections made them lose weight quickly.",
  "src": ["Myalept (metreleptin) FDA label", "Zhang Y, Friedman JM et al. Nature 1994", "Oral EA et al. N Engl J Med 2002"]
},
"lanadelumab": {
  "origin": "Hereditary angioedema (HAE) causes sudden, severe swelling because the body makes too much bradykinin. Researchers at Dyax Corporation in Massachusetts used phage display, a method of screening huge libraries of antibodies on the surface of bacteriophages, to find a fully human antibody that blocks active plasma kallikrein, the enzyme that releases bradykinin. Dyax had already developed ecallantide, a small kallikrein inhibitor protein. Shire acquired Dyax in 2016, and lanadelumab was tested in the HELP trial for long-term prevention. In 2018 the FDA approved it as Takhzyro, the first monoclonal antibody for preventing HAE attacks. Shire was then acquired by Takeda.",
  "timeline": [["1888", "William Osler describes the hereditary nature of angioedema."], ["2009", "Ecallantide, an earlier Dyax kallikrein inhibitor, is approved for acute attacks."], ["2018", "HELP trial results and FDA approval of lanadelumab (Takhzyro) for HAE prevention."]],
  "cell": "In HAE, C1 esterase inhibitor (C1-INH) is missing or does not work. C1-INH normally controls plasma kallikrein and factor XIIa in the contact (kallikrein-kinin) system. Without that control, active plasma kallikrein keeps cutting high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on blood vessel endothelium, which loosens the junctions between cells and dilates vessels, so fluid leaks into tissue and causes swelling. Lanadelumab is a fully human IgG1 kappa monoclonal antibody that binds the active site of plasma kallikrein and blocks it. It does not bind the inactive form, prekallikrein. Less kallikrein activity means less bradykinin, less vascular leak and fewer attacks. It does not treat histamine-driven allergic angioedema.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Fewer episodes of swelling in the face, hands, feet and genitals."], ["Airway", "Preventing attacks lowers the chance of laryngeal swelling, the most dangerous HAE symptom."], ["Gut", "Bowel wall swelling causes severe abdominal pain and vomiting; prevention reduces these attacks."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking kallikrein lowers bradykinin-driven leaks in small vessels."], ["Clotting tests", "Because kallikrein takes part in contact activation, the antibody can prolong the aPTT test without causing bleeding."]],
  "journey": "Lanadelumab is injected under the skin, often by the patient, every two to four weeks. It is absorbed slowly, peaking about five days after injection. Like other IgG antibodies, it is protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor, giving it a half-life of about two weeks, and steady state takes about 70 days. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver CYP system or the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "The main side effects are injection site pain, redness and bruising. Upper respiratory infections, headache, rash, muscle pain and dizziness were reported. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur because it is a protein. Bleeding is not a major problem, even though aPTT can be prolonged, because kallikrein is not essential for normal clotting in the body. It is not used for acute attacks; those need fast-acting drugs such as icatibant or C1 inhibitor. There is no specific antidote.",
  "fun": "Phage display, the method used to find lanadelumab's antibody, earned George Smith and Gregory Winter a share of the 2018 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, the same year the drug was approved.",
  "src": ["Takhzyro (lanadelumab) FDA label", "Banerji A et al. JAMA 2018 (HELP)", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"berotralstat": {
  "origin": "Berotralstat came from structure-based drug design at BioCryst Pharmaceuticals in North Carolina. BioCryst chemists used X-ray crystal structures of plasma kallikrein to build a small molecule that fits tightly into the enzyme's active site and is still absorbed when swallowed. Earlier HAE prevention options all needed injections or infusions, so a daily pill was a real change for patients. In December 2020 the FDA approved berotralstat as Orladeyo, the first oral drug to prevent HAE attacks. Approval was later extended to younger children with oral pellets.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "BioCryst designs berotralstat using crystal structures of plasma kallikrein."], ["2020", "FDA approves berotralstat (Orladeyo), the first oral HAE prevention drug."], ["2020s", "Approval is extended to younger children with an oral pellet form."]],
  "cell": "In HAE, missing or faulty C1 esterase inhibitor leaves plasma kallikrein poorly controlled. Kallikrein cuts high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin, which acts on B2 receptors on blood vessel endothelium. This opens gaps between endothelial cells and causes swelling. Berotralstat is a small-molecule, reversible, competitive inhibitor of plasma kallikrein. It sits in the enzyme's active site so kininogen cannot be cut. Lower bradykinin means less B2 receptor signaling, less vascular leak and fewer swelling attacks. It is selective for plasma kallikrein over related serine proteases.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Reduces how often swelling attacks happen in the face, limbs and genitals."], ["Airway", "Fewer attacks overall means fewer chances of dangerous throat swelling, but acute attacks still need on-demand therapy."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, vomiting, diarrhea and reflux are the most common side effects, and taking it with food helps."], ["Heart", "At higher than approved doses it can prolong the QT interval."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises have been seen, and doses are adjusted in liver impairment."]],
  "journey": "Berotralstat is taken by mouth once daily with food. It is absorbed over several hours and is about 99% protein bound. It has a long half-life of about 93 hours, which allows once-daily dosing and steady blood levels. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, and it leaves mostly in feces. It is a moderate inhibitor of CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, so it raises levels of some other drugs.",
  "sidefx": "GI side effects such as abdominal pain, vomiting and diarrhea are common and are thought to be local gut irritation; they often ease over time and are reduced by taking the drug with food. At doses higher than approved, berotralstat prolongs the QT interval, so higher doses are avoided and QT-prolonging combinations are watched. It also inhibits CYP2D6, CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, so it can raise levels of some other drugs, such as digoxin. It does not treat an attack that has already started. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Orladeyo (berotralstat) FDA label", "Zuraw B et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2021 (APeX-2)"]
},
"c1-esterase-inhibitor": {
  "origin": "In 1882 Heinrich Quincke described episodes of sudden swelling, and in 1888 William Osler described families in whom it ran across generations, now known as hereditary angioedema. In 1963 Virginia Donaldson and Richard Evans showed that patients lacked a plasma protein that inhibits the first component of complement, C1 esterase inhibitor. Purified C1-INH from donated human plasma was used in Europe for decades. In the US, Cinryze was approved in 2008 for prevention, Berinert in 2009 for acute attacks, Ruconest in 2014 as a recombinant version made in the milk of transgenic rabbits, and Haegarda in 2017 as a subcutaneous preventive form.",
  "timeline": [["1888", "William Osler describes hereditary angioedema."], ["1963", "Donaldson and Evans show HAE patients lack C1 esterase inhibitor."], ["2008", "FDA approves plasma-derived C1-INH (Cinryze) for prevention."], ["2009", "Berinert is approved for acute attacks."], ["2014", "Ruconest, a recombinant C1-INH from transgenic rabbit milk, is approved."], ["2017", "Haegarda, a subcutaneous C1-INH, is approved for prevention."]],
  "cell": "C1 esterase inhibitor is a serpin, a serine protease inhibitor. It acts like a mousetrap: a target protease cuts a loop on the serpin, and the serpin then snaps shut, trapping the enzyme in a covalent complex that is cleared from the blood. Its targets are C1r and C1s of the classical complement pathway, plasma kallikrein and factor XIIa of the contact system, and to some degree factor XIa. In HAE, low or nonfunctional C1-INH lets factor XIIa and kallikrein run unchecked, cutting high-molecular-weight kininogen into bradykinin. Bradykinin acts on B2 receptors to make blood vessels leaky. Giving C1-INH restores functional levels, controls kallikrein and factor XIIa, lowers bradykinin and stops or prevents swelling. It also limits complement consumption, so C4 levels can rise.",
  "body": [["Airway", "Stopping bradykinin-driven swelling can prevent or reverse life-threatening throat swelling."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Reduces swelling attacks in the face, hands, feet and genitals."], ["Gut", "Bowel swelling during attacks causes severe abdominal pain; replacement shortens or prevents these attacks."], ["Complement system", "Controlling C1r and C1s slows complement consumption, so complement levels such as C4 can rise."], ["Clotting system", "Thrombotic events have been reported, especially with high doses or with indwelling catheters, likely related to effects on contact and clotting pathways."]],
  "journey": "Most products are given by slow IV injection; Haegarda is injected under the skin. After IV use, functional C1-INH rises right away. Plasma-derived Cinryze has a half-life of about two to three days. Berinert's is somewhat shorter, and recombinant Ruconest's is only a few hours because its different sugar chains lead to faster clearance. C1-INH is used up as it traps target enzymes, and the complexes are removed by the liver. It is not cleared through CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, rash and infusion site reactions are common. Hypersensitivity can occur, and Ruconest is avoided in people allergic to rabbits because trace rabbit proteins may be present. Thromboembolic events have been reported, particularly at high doses or in people with catheters or other clot risks. Plasma-derived products carry a very small theoretical risk of transmitting infectious agents, reduced by donor screening and viral inactivation steps. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Ruconest is one of the few medicines made in animal milk: rabbits are engineered to secrete human C1-INH, which is then purified.",
  "src": ["Cinryze, Berinert, Haegarda and Ruconest FDA labels", "Donaldson VH, Evans RR. Am J Med 1963", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"icatibant": {
  "origin": "Bradykinin was discovered in 1949 by Brazilian scientists Mauricio Rocha e Silva, Wilson Beraldo and Gastao Rosenfeld, who found that snake venom released a substance from blood proteins that slowly contracted gut muscle. Later work showed bradykinin drives vascular leak and pain through B2 receptors. In the 1980s and 1990s chemists, including teams at Hoechst in Germany, built peptide antagonists of the B2 receptor by swapping natural amino acids for unnatural ones that resist breakdown. One of these, coded HOE 140, became icatibant. It was approved in Europe in 2008 and by the FDA in 2011 as Firazyr for acute attacks of hereditary angioedema in adults, giving patients a self-injected treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1949", "Rocha e Silva and colleagues discover bradykinin."], ["1990s", "Hoechst researchers develop the B2 receptor antagonist HOE 140 (icatibant)."], ["2008", "Icatibant is approved in the European Union."], ["2011", "FDA approves icatibant (Firazyr) for acute HAE attacks."]],
  "cell": "In HAE, missing or faulty C1 esterase inhibitor leaves plasma kallikrein uncontrolled, so it cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen into large amounts of bradykinin. Bradykinin binds the B2 receptor, a Gq protein-coupled receptor on blood vessel endothelium. Gq activation raises intracellular calcium and leads to nitric oxide and prostacyclin release, causing vasodilation and the opening of gaps between endothelial cells, so fluid leaks into tissues. Icatibant is a synthetic 10-amino-acid peptide that competitively blocks the B2 receptor. It contains nonproteinogenic amino acids that resist the peptidases that normally break down bradykinin quickly. With the receptor blocked, vascular leak stops and swelling resolves. It has no effect on histamine-mediated allergic angioedema.",
  "body": [["Airway", "Blocking bradykinin can stop laryngeal swelling from progressing, though airway attacks still need emergency care."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Swelling of the face, hands and feet begins to go down, with meaningful relief in about two hours in trials."], ["Gut", "Bowel wall swelling resolves, easing severe abdominal pain."], ["Injection site", "Bradykinin-like local reactions such as redness, swelling, burning and itching occur at the injection site in most patients."], ["Liver", "Transient rises in liver enzymes were reported."]],
  "journey": "Icatibant is injected under the skin of the abdomen and is almost fully absorbed. It peaks in the blood in less than an hour. Proteolytic enzymes break it into inactive fragments, not CYP enzymes. Its half-life is about one to two hours, so symptoms can return and a repeat injection is sometimes given. Inactive pieces leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site reactions such as redness, swelling, burning and itching are nearly universal and come from the injection itself and local peptide effects. Fever, dizziness, nausea, headache, rash and raised liver enzymes have been reported. Because it blocks bradykinin, which can protect the heart during ischemia, caution is noted in acute coronary disease. Laryngeal attacks can still progress despite treatment, so patients seek emergency care afterward. There is no antidote, and its short half-life means effects fade within hours.",
  "fun": "Bradykinin got its name from the Greek words for slow and movement, because it made gut muscle contract slowly compared with histamine.",
  "src": ["Firazyr (icatibant) FDA label", "Rocha e Silva M et al. Am J Physiol 1949", "Cicardi M et al. N Engl J Med 2010"]
},
"cholic-acid": {
  "origin": "Cholic acid is one of the two primary bile acids the human liver makes from cholesterol. It was first isolated from ox bile in the 1800s. In the 1980s and 1990s, researchers including Kenneth Setchell at Cincinnati Children's Hospital used mass spectrometry to identify rare inherited defects in bile acid synthesis, where a broken enzyme leaves infants with toxic, abnormal bile acids and severe liver disease. Treating these children with oral cholic acid replaced the missing normal bile acid and switched off production of the toxic ones. After decades of use under research protocols, the FDA approved cholic acid as Cholbam in 2015 for bile acid synthesis disorders and as add-on therapy for peroxisomal disorders.",
  "timeline": [["1800s", "Cholic acid is isolated from animal bile."], ["1980s", "Setchell and colleagues identify inborn errors of bile acid synthesis using mass spectrometry."], ["2015", "FDA approves cholic acid (Cholbam)."]],
  "cell": "Normally the liver converts cholesterol into bile acids, starting with the rate-limiting enzyme CYP7A1 (cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase). Bile acids returning to the liver activate the farnesoid X receptor (FXR), a nuclear receptor. FXR switches on SHP and, in the gut, FGF19, both of which turn down CYP7A1. When a downstream enzyme is missing, the liver makes few normal bile acids, so this brake is off. CYP7A1 keeps running and abnormal intermediates pile up, damaging liver cells and causing cholestasis. Oral cholic acid joins the bile acid pool, activates FXR and restores negative feedback, so production of toxic intermediates drops. It also restores bile flow and micelle formation in the gut, allowing fat and fat-soluble vitamins to be absorbed.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Turning off toxic intermediate production lessens cholestatic liver injury and jaundice, and liver tests can improve."], ["Gut", "Restored bile acid micelles improve fat digestion, reducing steatorrhea; diarrhea is a common side effect."], ["Fat-soluble vitamins", "Better micelles improve absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, preventing deficiencies such as bleeding and rickets."], ["Growth", "Better nutrition helps children grow and gain weight."], ["Nerves", "Peripheral neuropathy has been reported, possibly related to underlying disease or vitamin E status."]],
  "journey": "Cholic acid is taken by mouth with food. It is absorbed in the small intestine and enters the enterohepatic circulation like the body's own bile acids. The liver takes it up and conjugates it with glycine or taurine, then secretes it into bile through the bile salt export pump (BSEP). In the gut, much is reabsorbed in the ileum and recycled; some reaches the colon, where bacteria deconjugate it and turn it into deoxycholic acid. A portion leaves in the feces each cycle. Bile acid resins and aluminum antacids bind it and are separated in time.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea happens because bile acids reaching the colon draw water and stimulate secretion. Reflux, nausea and abdominal pain also occur. If too much is given, or in patients with advanced liver disease, extra bile acid can itself be toxic to liver cells, so liver tests are watched and therapy is stopped if they worsen. Worsening liver function is a particular concern in cirrhosis. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Deoxycholic acid, a gut bacterial product of cholic acid, is also an FDA-approved injection (Kybella) used to dissolve fat under the chin.",
  "src": ["Cholbam (cholic acid) FDA label", "Setchell KDR, Heubi JE. J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr 2006"]
},
"tiopronin": {
  "origin": "Cystinuria is one of the oldest known inherited metabolic diseases; William Hyde Wollaston described cystine stones in 1810, and Archibald Garrod listed it among his inborn errors of metabolism in 1908. Patients lose large amounts of cystine in urine, where it crystallizes into stones. In the 1960s doctors found that penicillamine, a thiol drug, broke cystine into more soluble pieces, but its side effects limited its use. Tiopronin (alpha-mercaptopropionylglycine) is a related thiol compound that was used in Japan before it reached the US. It does the same chemical job and was often better tolerated. The FDA approved it as Thiola in 1988, and an enteric-coated version, Thiola EC, was approved in 2019.",
  "timeline": [["1810", "William Hyde Wollaston describes cystine bladder stones."], ["1908", "Archibald Garrod includes cystinuria among his inborn errors of metabolism."], ["1988", "FDA approves tiopronin (Thiola) for cystinuria."], ["2019", "FDA approves enteric-coated Thiola EC."]],
  "cell": "In cystinuria, variants in SLC3A1 or SLC7A9 break a kidney tubule transporter that reabsorbs cystine and the dibasic amino acids. Cystine is two cysteine molecules joined by a disulfide bond, and it is poorly soluble in acidic urine, so it forms crystals and stones. Tiopronin has a free thiol (sulfhydryl) group. In urine it attacks cystine's disulfide bond in a thiol-disulfide exchange, splitting it and forming a tiopronin-cysteine mixed disulfide. This mixed disulfide is much more water soluble than cystine. As a result, free cystine falls below its solubility limit, new stones form less often and some existing stones may dissolve. There is no receptor involved; the drug works by direct chemistry.",
  "body": [["Kidneys and urinary tract", "Lower free cystine in urine means fewer new stones, fewer procedures and protection of kidney function. High fluid intake and urinary alkalinization are part of the plan."], ["Kidney filters", "Some patients develop protein in the urine or nephrotic syndrome, likely from immune complex damage to glomeruli."], ["Skin", "Rash and itching are common reasons people stop the drug."], ["Gut and taste", "Nausea, diarrhea and taste changes are common."], ["Blood and immune system", "Fever, swollen lymph nodes and anemia can occur; agranulocytosis is a rare thiol-class risk."]],
  "journey": "Tiopronin is taken by mouth in divided doses through the day. It is absorbed from the gut, and the delayed-release form peaks about three to six hours later. Part of it is metabolized to 2-mercaptopropionic acid. The kidneys excrete most of it in the urine, both unchanged and as mixed disulfides with cysteine. This is useful because the urine is where it needs to act. Each dose works for several hours, so it is spread across the day.",
  "sidefx": "Many side effects resemble penicillamine's because both are reactive thiols. Thiols can bind proteins and act as haptens, which can trigger immune reactions: rash, fever, swollen lymph nodes, joint pain and proteinuria from immune complex glomerular disease. Blood counts and urine protein are monitored. Taste disturbance may relate to binding of trace metals like zinc. Nausea and diarrhea are common. There is no antidote; stopping the drug usually reverses immune effects.",
  "fun": "Cystine stones are often visible on plain x-rays because the sulfur atoms make them dense, giving them a smooth, ground-glass look.",
  "src": ["Thiola and Thiola EC (tiopronin) FDA label", "Garrod AE. Inborn Errors of Metabolism, 1909", "Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"]
},
"cysteamine": {
  "origin": "Cystinosis was first described in 1903 by Swiss biochemist Emil Abderhalden, who found cystine crystals in the organs of children from one family. Later researchers showed the defect: a lysosomal transporter, now called cystinosin, that normally moves cystine out of lysosomes. In 1976 Jerry Schneider and Jess Thoene at the University of California, San Diego showed that cysteamine, a simple aminothiol made in the body from coenzyme A breakdown, could empty cystine from patients' cells. Cysteamine became the standard therapy. The FDA approved immediate-release Cystagon in 1994 and delayed-release Procysbi in 2013, which allows dosing every 12 hours. Cysteamine eye drops (Cystaran, Cystadrops) treat corneal crystals separately.",
  "timeline": [["1903", "Emil Abderhalden describes cystinosis."], ["1976", "Thoene and Schneider report that cysteamine depletes cystine from cystinotic cells."], ["1994", "FDA approves cysteamine bitartrate (Cystagon)."], ["2013", "FDA approves delayed-release cysteamine (Procysbi)."]],
  "cell": "In nephropathic cystinosis, variants in the CTNS gene break cystinosin, the transporter that carries cystine out of lysosomes. Cystine builds up and forms crystals inside cells throughout the body, starting with kidney tubule cells. Cysteamine is a small aminothiol that enters lysosomes. Inside, it reacts with cystine in a thiol-disulfide exchange, producing free cysteine and a cysteine-cysteamine mixed disulfide. This mixed disulfide resembles lysine closely enough to exit through a different lysosomal transporter for cationic amino acids, PQLC2. Free cysteine leaves through its own transporter. In this way cysteamine bypasses the broken cystinosin and empties lysosomal cystine. White blood cell cystine levels are used to track the effect.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "Lowering cystine in kidney cells slows loss of kidney function and delays kidney failure, though tubular losses (Fanconi syndrome) often persist."], ["Growth", "Children who start treatment early grow better, since stored cystine harms many tissues."], ["Thyroid and other organs", "Long-term therapy delays later problems such as hypothyroidism, diabetes, muscle weakness and swallowing problems."], ["Eyes", "Oral cysteamine does not reach the cornea well, so separate eye drops are used for corneal crystals."], ["Gut", "Cysteamine raises stomach acid production, causing nausea, vomiting, pain and sometimes ulcers."], ["Breath and skin", "Its sulfur metabolites are released in breath and sweat, causing a strong odor."]],
  "journey": "Cysteamine is taken by mouth. The delayed-release form passes the stomach and is absorbed in the small intestine, peaking about three hours after a dose, and allows dosing every 12 hours. It reaches cells throughout the body, where it enters lysosomes. Its half-life is about four hours. It is metabolized to hypotaurine and taurine, and sulfur-containing products such as dimethylsulfide leave through the breath and sweat. White cell cystine falls within hours of a dose.",
  "sidefx": "Cysteamine stimulates gastrin and stomach acid, which explains nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and GI ulcers or bleeding. Sulfur compounds exhaled and sweated out cause the characteristic breath and body odor. High doses have been linked to skin and bone lesions resembling Ehlers-Danlos syndrome, likely from effects on collagen cross-linking, as well as raised intracranial pressure, seizures, lethargy and low white counts. Doses are adjusted using white cell cystine levels. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Cysteamine is a natural molecule: the body makes it when it breaks down coenzyme A, and it is the very tail end of that vitamin B5-containing molecule.",
  "src": ["Procysbi and Cystagon (cysteamine) FDA labels", "Thoene JG et al. J Clin Invest 1976", "Gahl WA et al. N Engl J Med 2002"]
},
"collagenase-clostridium-histolyticum": {
  "origin": "Clostridium histolyticum is a soil bacterium named for its ability to dissolve tissue; it was found in war wounds during World War I. Its collagenases break down the tough collagen fibers of connective tissue. At Stony Brook University in New York, hand surgeon Lawrence Hurst and researcher Marie Badalamente spent years testing purified collagenase as an alternative to surgery for Dupuytren contracture, in which collagen cords pull fingers into the palm. The technology was licensed to BioSpecifics and developed with Auxilium Pharmaceuticals. The FDA approved it as Xiaflex in 2010 for Dupuytren contracture with a palpable cord and in 2013 for Peyronie disease, making it the first FDA-approved drug for both conditions. Endo later acquired Auxilium.",
  "timeline": [["1910s", "Clostridium histolyticum is identified in infected war wounds."], ["1990s", "Hurst and Badalamente at Stony Brook test collagenase injections for Dupuytren contracture."], ["2009", "CORD I trial results are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2010", "FDA approves Xiaflex for Dupuytren contracture."], ["2013", "FDA approves Xiaflex for Peyronie disease."]],
  "cell": "Xiaflex contains two collagenases from Clostridium histolyticum: class I (AUX-I) and class II (AUX-II). Both are zinc-dependent enzymes that hydrolyze the triple helix of interstitial collagen, mainly types I and III, the main collagens in Dupuytren cords and Peyronie plaques. AUX-I cuts near the ends of the collagen molecule, and AUX-II cuts at sites in the interior, so together they break collagen into smaller pieces faster than either alone. When injected into a cord, they weaken it so a manipulation the next day can rupture it and straighten the finger. In Peyronie disease, they soften the plaque in the tunica albuginea so modeling can reduce curvature. The enzymes do not attack collagen type IV in basement membranes as strongly, but nearby normal structures such as tendons and ligaments can still be affected.",
  "body": [["Hand (Dupuytren cords)", "The enzyme weakens the collagen cord so a gentle extension procedure can snap it and straighten the contracted finger."], ["Penis (Peyronie plaques)", "Plaque softening plus modeling reduces curvature. Because of corporal rupture risk, Peyronie use is restricted to a REMS program."], ["Tendons and ligaments", "If the enzyme reaches nearby tendons or ligaments, it can weaken and rupture them."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Swelling, bruising, pain and skin tears during finger extension are common local effects of collagen breakdown."], ["Immune system", "Most patients develop antibodies after repeated injections, and allergic reactions can occur."]],
  "journey": "The drug is injected directly into the cord or plaque. It acts locally and is quickly broken down by tissue proteases. Blood levels are not detectable after a standard injection into a hand cord, so very little reaches the circulation. Cord weakening happens within a day or so, and the manipulation or modeling step follows.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow directly from collagen breakdown where it is not wanted. Bruising, swelling, pain and bleeding come from local tissue and small vessel damage. Tendon or ligament rupture can happen if enzyme reaches these structures, which is why injection technique matters. Skin can tear during finger extension. In Peyronie disease, weakening the tunica can lead to corporal rupture (penile fracture) or serious hematoma, the basis of its boxed warning. Antibodies and hypersensitivity are possible. People on anticoagulants may bleed more locally. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Dupuytren contracture is sometimes called Viking disease because it is common in people of Northern European descent.",
  "src": ["Xiaflex (collagenase clostridium histolyticum) FDA label", "Hurst LC et al. N Engl J Med 2009 (CORD I)"]
},
"collagenase-peyronie": {
  "origin": "Peyronie disease is named for Francois Gigot de la Peyronie, surgeon to King Louis XV of France, who described it in 1743. Fibrous, collagen-rich scar tissue (a plaque) forms in the tunica albuginea, the tough sheath around the erectile tissue, causing the penis to curve. For centuries options were limited to observation or surgery. Collagenase clostridium histolyticum, first developed for Dupuytren contracture by Lawrence Hurst and Marie Badalamente at Stony Brook University, was tested for Peyronie plaques in the IMPRESS trials. In 2013 the FDA approved Xiaflex for Peyronie disease with a palpable plaque and curvature of at least 30 degrees, the first FDA-approved drug treatment for the condition.",
  "timeline": [["1743", "Francois de la Peyronie describes the disease."], ["2010", "Xiaflex is first approved for Dupuytren contracture."], ["2013", "IMPRESS I and II trial results are published, and FDA approves Xiaflex for Peyronie disease."]],
  "cell": "The plaque in Peyronie disease is made mostly of disorganized type I and III collagen deposited after small injuries to the tunica albuginea. The product contains two Clostridium histolyticum collagenases, AUX-I and AUX-II, which hydrolyze the triple-helical region of these collagens at different sites. Working together, they break the plaque's collagen framework into smaller fragments. This softens and partly dissolves the plaque. A penile modeling procedure after injection then gently stretches the softened area, which helps lengthen the shortened side and reduce curvature. The enzymes act only locally and are broken down quickly by tissue proteases.",
  "body": [["Penile plaque", "Breaking down plaque collagen and modeling the tissue reduces curvature over several treatment cycles."], ["Tunica albuginea", "Weakening of this sheath can lead to corporal rupture (penile fracture), a reason for the boxed warning and REMS program."], ["Blood vessels in the penis", "Local damage to small vessels causes bruising, swelling and hematoma, which are very common."], ["Urethra", "Urethral injury is a rare serious complication if the enzyme reaches nearby tissue."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies form in most patients after repeated injections, and allergic reactions can occur."]],
  "journey": "The enzyme is injected into the plaque in the office. It works locally, and systemic blood levels are not measurable after injection. Tissue proteases break it down within hours to days. A modeling procedure follows, and patients do home stretching between cycles. The effect builds over several treatment cycles.",
  "sidefx": "Because the enzyme breaks down collagen, surrounding tissues can also be weakened. Most men have bruising, swelling or pain, and penile hematoma is common. The most serious risk is corporal rupture, where the weakened tunica tears during an erection or sexual activity, which is why sexual activity is avoided for a period after each cycle and the product is available only through a REMS program. Urethral injury is rare. Anticoagulants increase local bleeding. Hypersensitivity can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The same bottle of enzyme is used for two quite different jobs: snapping finger cords in the hand and softening plaques in the penis, both made of the same tough collagens.",
  "src": ["Xiaflex (collagenase clostridium histolyticum) FDA label", "Gelbard M et al. J Urol 2013 (IMPRESS I and II)"]
},
"pozelimab": {
  "origin": "CHAPLE disease (CD55 deficiency with hyperactivation of complement, angiopathic thrombosis and protein-losing enteropathy) was described in 2017 by Ahmet Ozen, Michael Lenardo and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health, who studied families with severe gut protein loss and blood clots. They showed that loss of CD55, a protein that normally brakes complement on cell surfaces, lets complement attack the gut's lymphatic vessels and blood vessels, and that blocking complement C5 helped. Regeneron developed pozelimab, a fully human monoclonal antibody against C5, using its antibody mouse platform. In 2023 the FDA approved it as Veopoz, the first treatment approved for CHAPLE disease.",
  "timeline": [["2017", "Ozen and colleagues describe CD55 deficiency (CHAPLE disease) in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2023", "FDA approves pozelimab (Veopoz) for CHAPLE disease in patients 1 year and older."]],
  "cell": "The complement system is a cascade of blood proteins that tags and destroys microbes. Several pathways converge on C3 convertases and then C5 convertase, which cuts C5 into C5a and C5b. C5a is a strong inflammatory signal that draws in and activates white cells. C5b starts assembly of the membrane attack complex (C5b-9), a pore that punches holes in cell membranes. CD55, also called decay-accelerating factor, normally sits on our own cells and breaks apart convertases so complement does not attack them. In CHAPLE disease, CD55 is missing, so complement deposits on intestinal lymphatics and vessel lining, damaging them so protein leaks into the gut and clots form. Pozelimab binds C5 and blocks its cleavage, so neither C5a nor the membrane attack complex is made.",
  "body": [["Gut", "Less complement damage to intestinal lymphatics stops protein leaking into the bowel, easing abdominal pain, diarrhea and vomiting."], ["Blood proteins", "Serum albumin and total protein return toward normal as gut protein loss stops."], ["Fluid balance", "With albumin restored, swelling of the face and limbs (edema) decreases."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking terminal complement may reduce the clotting tendency seen in the disease."], ["Immune defense", "Without C5 function the body cannot kill Neisseria bacteria well, so meningococcal infections become a serious risk."], ["Growth", "Children gain weight and grow better as nutrition improves."]],
  "journey": "Pozelimab starts with an IV loading dose and then continues as weekly injections under the skin, which can be given at home after training. It stays in the blood for a long time like other IgG antibodies, protected from breakdown by the neonatal Fc receptor. Its clearance also depends on how much C5 it binds, so its kinetics are partly target driven. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the liver CYP system or the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Blocking C5 removes the membrane attack complex, which is the body's main way of killing Neisseria bacteria. That is why the drug carries a boxed warning for life-threatening meningococcal infections, requires meningococcal vaccination and is available only through a REMS program. Other serious infections can also occur. Upper respiratory infections, hives, hair loss and fractures were reported in the trial. As a protein it can cause hypersensitivity reactions. There is no rapid reversal; stopping the drug lets C5 activity return over time.",
  "fun": "CD55's other name, decay-accelerating factor, describes its job: it speeds the falling apart (decay) of complement convertases on the body's own cells.",
  "src": ["Veopoz (pozelimab) FDA label", "Ozen A et al. N Engl J Med 2017"]
},
"alglucosidase-alfa": {
  "origin": "In 1932 Dutch pathologist Joannes Cassianus Pompe described an infant who died with a massively enlarged heart full of glycogen. In 1963 Belgian biochemist Henri-Gery Hers showed the cause was a missing lysosomal enzyme, acid alpha-glucosidase. This made Pompe disease the first known lysosomal storage disorder. At Duke University, Yuan-Tsong Chen and colleagues produced the human enzyme in Chinese hamster ovary cells and tested it in infants. Genzyme developed the product, and the FDA approved it as Myozyme in 2006. A version made at larger production scale was approved as Lumizyme in 2010. In infants, who often died before age one, it greatly improved survival.",
  "timeline": [["1932", "J. C. Pompe describes glycogen storage in the heart of an infant."], ["1963", "Henri-Gery Hers links Pompe disease to acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency."], ["1990s", "Duke researchers develop recombinant acid alpha-glucosidase."], ["2006", "FDA approves alglucosidase alfa (Myozyme)."], ["2010", "FDA approves larger-scale alglucosidase alfa as Lumizyme."]],
  "cell": "Cells constantly bring a little glycogen into lysosomes, where acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase) breaks the alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 bonds and frees glucose. In Pompe disease, variants in GAA leave the enzyme low or absent. Glycogen piles up in lysosomes, especially in heart, skeletal and breathing muscle. The swollen lysosomes disrupt muscle fibers and autophagy, causing weakness. Alglucosidase alfa is recombinant human acid alpha-glucosidase carrying mannose-6-phosphate sugars. These bind the cation-independent mannose-6-phosphate receptor on muscle cells, which carries the enzyme into endosomes and on to lysosomes. There it hydrolyzes stored glycogen to glucose. Uptake into skeletal muscle is limited by how much mannose-6-phosphate the enzyme carries.",
  "body": [["Heart", "In infants, the thick, glycogen-filled heart muscle shrinks and works better, which is a major reason survival improves."], ["Breathing muscles", "Glycogen storage in the diaphragm causes breathing failure; therapy improves ventilator-free survival in infants and stabilizes breathing in adults."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Walking distance and strength stabilize or improve in late-onset disease, though skeletal muscle responds more slowly than heart."], ["Immune system", "Many patients form antibodies. Infants who make no natural enzyme (CRIM-negative) can form high antibody levels that blunt the effect."], ["Kidneys and skin", "Immune complex reactions such as nephrotic syndrome and necrotizing skin lesions have been reported with high antibody levels."]],
  "journey": "Alglucosidase alfa is given by IV infusion every two weeks, with the rate increased in steps over several hours. It leaves the blood within hours as muscle and other cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it keeps working for days. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as fever, rash, flushing, fast heart rate and blood pressure changes are common because the immune system reacts to the recombinant protein. Anaphylaxis can occur, which is part of its boxed warning. Infants with weak hearts and breathing muscles may not tolerate the fluid volume of an infusion, risking acute heart and lung failure. Heart rhythm problems have been seen during anesthesia in infants with cardiomyopathy. High antibody levels can cause immune complex disease such as nephrotic syndrome and skin lesions. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Pompe disease was the first disease ever linked to a missing lysosomal enzyme, which helped launch the whole field of lysosomal storage disorders.",
  "src": ["Lumizyme (alglucosidase alfa) FDA label", "Hers HG. Biochem J 1963", "Kishnani PS et al. Neurology 2007"]
},
"avalglucosidase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Alglucosidase alfa helped Pompe disease, but skeletal muscle took up the enzyme poorly because few of its sugar chains carried mannose-6-phosphate. Scientists at Sanofi Genzyme designed a next-generation enzyme by chemically attaching synthetic bis-mannose-6-phosphate glycans to the same human acid alpha-glucosidase protein. This gave roughly 15 times more mannose-6-phosphate and much stronger binding to the receptor that pulls the enzyme into muscle. In the COMET trial in late-onset Pompe disease, it was compared head to head with alglucosidase alfa. The FDA approved it as Nexviazyme in 2021 for late-onset Pompe disease in people 1 year and older.",
  "timeline": [["2006", "First enzyme replacement for Pompe disease, alglucosidase alfa, is approved."], ["2010s", "Sanofi Genzyme develops a glycoengineered enzyme with added bis-mannose-6-phosphate."], ["2021", "FDA approves avalglucosidase alfa (Nexviazyme) for late-onset Pompe disease."]],
  "cell": "In Pompe disease, low acid alpha-glucosidase leaves glycogen stuck in lysosomes of heart, skeletal and breathing muscle. To work, a replacement enzyme must reach those lysosomes. The cation-independent mannose-6-phosphate receptor on muscle cells binds mannose-6-phosphate sugars and carries the cargo through endosomes to lysosomes. Avalglucosidase alfa is recombinant human acid alpha-glucosidase with synthetic bis-mannose-6-phosphate glycans chemically conjugated to it. Each bis-M6P glycan binds the receptor with high affinity, so more enzyme enters muscle cells. In the acidic lysosome it hydrolyzes alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 bonds of glycogen, releasing glucose and clearing storage.",
  "body": [["Breathing muscles", "In COMET, forced vital capacity improved at least as much as with alglucosidase alfa, reflecting better diaphragm function."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Walking distance improved, with numerically greater gains than the older enzyme, consistent with better muscle uptake."], ["Heart", "Late-onset Pompe disease affects the heart less than the infant form, but enzyme also reaches heart muscle."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies can form, and infusion reactions such as headache, rash, itching and chills occur."]],
  "journey": "Avalglucosidase alfa is given by IV infusion every two weeks over several hours. Cells take it up from the blood quickly, so its plasma half-life is under two hours. Once in lysosomes it continues to clear glycogen. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "As with other enzyme replacements, the main risks are immune reactions to a foreign-looking protein. Infusion reactions such as headache, rash, itching, nausea and chills are common, and anaphylaxis can occur, which is part of its boxed warning. People with weak heart or breathing muscles may not tolerate the fluid load of an infusion and can develop acute heart and lung failure. Antibodies can reduce effect in some patients. Reactions are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The protein backbone is the same as the older enzyme. The improvement comes entirely from the sugar tags added to it, a strategy called glycoengineering.",
  "src": ["Nexviazyme (avalglucosidase alfa) FDA label", "Diaz-Manera J et al. Lancet Neurol 2021 (COMET)"]
},
"migalastat": {
  "origin": "Migalastat is 1-deoxygalactonojirimycin, an iminosugar that mimics galactose. Iminosugars were first studied as enzyme inhibitors, but in 1999 Jian-Qiang Fan and colleagues reported a surprising finding in Nature Medicine: at low concentrations, this galactose mimic actually increased the activity of some mutant forms of alpha-galactosidase A in Fabry cells. It worked as an active-site-specific chaperone, helping the misfolded enzyme pass quality control. Amicus Therapeutics developed the idea into a pill. Migalastat was approved in Europe in 2016, and the FDA approved it as Galafold in 2018 for adults with Fabry disease who have an amenable GLA variant, the first oral Fabry treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1999", "Fan and colleagues show that a galactose analog can rescue mutant alpha-galactosidase A as a chaperone."], ["2000s", "Amicus Therapeutics develops migalastat as an oral pharmacologic chaperone."], ["2016", "Migalastat is approved in the European Union."], ["2018", "FDA approves migalastat (Galafold) for amenable Fabry disease."]],
  "cell": "Many Fabry disease variants change a single amino acid in alpha-galactosidase A. The enzyme could still work but misfolds, so the endoplasmic reticulum's quality control system sends it for destruction instead of to the lysosome. Migalastat is a galactose look-alike that binds reversibly in the enzyme's active site in the endoplasmic reticulum, where the pH is neutral and substrate is absent. This binding stabilizes the correct fold, so the enzyme passes quality control and travels through the Golgi to the lysosome. In the lysosome, the acidic pH and high concentration of the natural substrate Gb3 (GL-3) push migalastat off the active site, freeing the enzyme to break down Gb3. It only works for amenable variants; it cannot help when the enzyme is absent or its active site is broken.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "More working enzyme reduces Gb3 deposits in kidney capillaries, and in trials kidney function stayed relatively stable."], ["Heart", "Left ventricular mass decreased in some studies, consistent with less storage in heart muscle."], ["Blood vessels", "Gb3 clearance from vessel lining cells is thought to slow small vessel damage throughout the body."], ["Nervous system", "Headache is one of the most common side effects reported."], ["Urinary and respiratory tract", "Urinary tract infections, nasal symptoms and fever were reported more often in trials."]],
  "journey": "Migalastat is taken by mouth every other day, on an empty stomach because food lowers absorption. It peaks about three hours after a dose and is not bound to blood proteins. It reaches tissues throughout the body because it is a small molecule. It is barely metabolized and is cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys, with a half-life of about four hours. Every-other-day dosing leaves time between doses for the drug to clear from lysosomes so the enzyme is free to work.",
  "sidefx": "Migalastat is generally well tolerated because it binds only to its target enzyme and is not a foreign protein, so infusion reactions do not occur. Headache, nasopharyngitis, urinary tract infection, nausea and fever were reported. Because it is cleared by the kidneys, it is not used in severe kidney impairment. Taking it more often than every other day could keep the enzyme's active site occupied and reduce its activity. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "A drug that blocks an enzyme can also save it: at the right concentration and timing, migalastat helps its own target enzyme survive and then lets go once it reaches the lysosome.",
  "src": ["Galafold (migalastat) FDA label", "Fan JQ et al. Nat Med 1999", "Germain DP et al. N Engl J Med 2016"]
},
"eliglustat": {
  "origin": "Eliglustat came from decades of work at the University of Michigan. In the 1980s biochemist Norman Radin built ceramide-like molecules that blocked glucosylceramide synthase, the enzyme that makes the lipid stored in Gaucher disease. Later, James Shayman and colleagues refined these into more potent and selective compounds. Genzyme licensed the work and developed eliglustat as a pill for substrate reduction therapy, an alternative to IV enzyme replacement. The FDA approved it as Cerdelga in 2014 for adults with type 1 Gaucher disease. Its label requires CYP2D6 metabolizer status to be known before use, an early example of pharmacogenomics built into prescribing.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Norman Radin develops ceramide analog inhibitors of glucosylceramide synthase."], ["2000s", "James Shayman's group refines the compounds, leading to eliglustat."], ["2014", "FDA approves eliglustat (Cerdelga) for type 1 Gaucher disease."]],
  "cell": "In Gaucher disease, low glucocerebrosidase lets glucosylceramide build up in macrophage lysosomes. Enzyme replacement adds more enzyme; substrate reduction takes the opposite approach and makes less lipid. Glucosylceramide synthase (UDP-glucose ceramide glucosyltransferase) on the Golgi adds glucose to ceramide, the first committed step in making most glycosphingolipids. Eliglustat is a ceramide analog that potently and specifically inhibits this enzyme. With less glucosylceramide being made, the patient's leftover glucocerebrosidase activity can keep up with breakdown, so storage in Gaucher cells decreases. It does not meaningfully cross the blood-brain barrier, so it is not used for neurologic forms of Gaucher disease.",
  "body": [["Spleen", "Less storage in Gaucher cells shrinks the enlarged spleen over months."], ["Liver", "Liver volume falls as macrophage storage decreases."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin and platelet counts rise as the spleen shrinks and marrow is less crowded."], ["Bones", "Bone marrow infiltration and bone disease improve over the long term."], ["Heart", "At high blood levels it can prolong PR, QRS and QTc intervals by blocking cardiac ion channels, which is why genotype and drug interactions matter."], ["Gut", "Indigestion, nausea, diarrhea and upper abdominal pain are common."]],
  "journey": "Eliglustat is taken by mouth as a capsule, once or twice daily depending on CYP2D6 status. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours in extensive metabolizers, though first-pass metabolism keeps bioavailability low. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2D6, with CYP3A4 as a backup path. Poor metabolizers have much higher levels and a longer half-life. Ultrarapid metabolizers may not reach effective levels, so the drug is not used in them. Metabolites leave in urine and feces. Grapefruit and strong CYP2D6 or CYP3A inhibitors raise its levels.",
  "sidefx": "The main safety concern is the heart. High concentrations block cardiac sodium and potassium channels, prolonging PR, QRS and QTc intervals and raising arrhythmia risk. That is why CYP2D6 genotyping is required, interacting drugs are reviewed, and patients with certain heart conditions or long QT avoid it. Fatigue, headache, nausea, diarrhea, back pain and indigestion are common. There is no antidote; management of toxicity is supportive with heart monitoring.",
  "fun": "Eliglustat is one of the few drugs whose dosing schedule depends directly on a genetic test result: CYP2D6 metabolizer status decides whether it is taken once or twice a day, or not at all.",
  "src": ["Cerdelga (eliglustat) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Mistry PK et al. JAMA 2015 (ENGAGE)"]
},
"miglustat": {
  "origin": "Miglustat is N-butyl-deoxynojirimycin, a synthetic relative of deoxynojirimycin, a glucose-like iminosugar found in mulberry leaves and some bacteria. It was first developed in the late 1980s as a possible HIV drug because it blocks enzymes that trim sugars on viral proteins, but it did not work well enough for that use. In 1994 Frances Platt, Terry Butters and Raymond Dwek at the Oxford Glycobiology Institute showed it also blocks glucosylceramide synthase, and they proposed using it to make less of the lipid stored in Gaucher disease, an idea called substrate reduction therapy. Actelion brought it to market, and the FDA approved it as Zavesca in 2003 for adults with mild to moderate type 1 Gaucher disease who cannot use enzyme replacement.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "N-butyl-deoxynojirimycin is studied as an HIV drug."], ["1994", "Platt, Butters and Dwek show it inhibits glucosylceramide synthase."], ["2003", "FDA approves miglustat (Zavesca) for type 1 Gaucher disease."], ["2023", "A separate miglustat product (Opfolda) is approved for use with cipaglucosidase alfa in late-onset Pompe disease."]],
  "cell": "Miglustat is an N-alkylated iminosugar, a glucose mimic. It competitively and reversibly inhibits glucosylceramide synthase, the Golgi enzyme that attaches glucose to ceramide in the first step of making most glycosphingolipids. Less glucosylceramide production lowers the load on deficient glucocerebrosidase in Gaucher disease, so storage in macrophages slowly falls. It is less potent and less specific than eliglustat. Because it resembles glucose, it also inhibits intestinal disaccharidases such as sucrase-isomaltase, which normally split dietary sugars. Undigested sugars stay in the gut, causing osmotic diarrhea and gas. Unlike eliglustat, it crosses the blood-brain barrier.",
  "body": [["Spleen and liver", "Less glucosylceramide storage gives modest reductions in spleen and liver size."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin and platelet counts improve somewhat."], ["Gut", "Blocking intestinal sugar-splitting enzymes causes diarrhea, gas, bloating and abdominal pain, especially with carbohydrate-rich meals."], ["Weight", "Weight loss is common, largely from carbohydrate malabsorption and GI symptoms."], ["Nerves and brain", "It crosses into the brain; tremor and peripheral neuropathy have been reported and are monitored."]],
  "journey": "Miglustat is taken by mouth as a capsule three times a day. It is well absorbed, peaking in about two to two and a half hours, and does not bind plasma proteins. It distributes widely, including into the brain. It is barely metabolized and leaves mostly unchanged in the urine, with a half-life of about six to seven hours, so kidney function affects levels.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its sugar-like structure. Inhibiting intestinal disaccharidases leaves sugars undigested, so bacteria ferment them and they draw water into the bowel, causing diarrhea, gas and pain. A low-carbohydrate diet and antidiarrheals can help. Weight loss often follows. Tremor and peripheral neuropathy are reported and may relate to its effects in nerve tissue. Low platelets and visual disturbances can occur. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Miglustat began as an HIV drug candidate, failed in that role, and then found a new life treating a lipid storage disease.",
  "src": ["Zavesca (miglustat) FDA label", "Platt FM et al. J Biol Chem 1994", "Cox T et al. Lancet 2000"]
},
"laronidase": {
  "origin": "Hurler syndrome, the severe form of mucopolysaccharidosis type I (MPS I), was described in 1919 by German pediatrician Gertrud Hurler. Milder forms are named for Harold Scheie. In 1968 Elizabeth Neufeld and colleagues at the NIH showed that cells from patients with different MPS disorders could correct each other's storage when grown together, because cells release and take up lysosomal enzymes. This cross-correction idea became the basis for enzyme replacement. Emil Kakkis, working at Harbor-UCLA, produced recombinant human alpha-L-iduronidase and tested it in dogs and then patients. BioMarin and Genzyme developed it together, and the FDA approved it as Aldurazyme in 2003, the first treatment for MPS I other than stem cell transplant.",
  "timeline": [["1919", "Gertrud Hurler describes the severe form of MPS I."], ["1968", "Neufeld and colleagues demonstrate cross-correction of MPS cells."], ["2001", "Kakkis and colleagues report the first enzyme replacement trial for MPS I in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2003", "FDA approves laronidase (Aldurazyme)."]],
  "cell": "MPS I comes from variants in IDUA, which encodes alpha-L-iduronidase. This lysosomal enzyme removes terminal alpha-L-iduronic acid from two glycosaminoglycans (GAGs), dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate. Without it, partly broken-down GAGs pile up in lysosomes in nearly every tissue, thickening connective tissue, heart valves, airways and the cornea, and damaging the brain in severe forms. Laronidase is recombinant human alpha-L-iduronidase bearing mannose-6-phosphate. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and route it to lysosomes, where it removes iduronic acid residues so the GAG chains can be fully broken down. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier in meaningful amounts, so it does not treat the brain disease of severe Hurler syndrome.",
  "body": [["Liver and spleen", "GAG storage in the liver and spleen decreases, so these organs shrink."], ["Lungs and airway", "Forced vital capacity improves as GAG storage in airway and chest tissues declines; airway disease also raises infusion reaction risk."], ["Joints and muscles", "Walking distance improves, though stiff joints and bone changes respond only partly."], ["Urine", "Urinary GAG levels fall within weeks, used as a measure of biochemical response."], ["Brain", "Because the enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, severe Hurler syndrome usually needs stem cell transplant early in life to protect the brain."]],
  "journey": "Laronidase is given by IV infusion once a week over several hours, with the rate increased in steps. Cells pull it from the blood quickly, so its plasma half-life is only a few hours. Inside lysosomes it keeps working through the week. It is broken down by normal protein catabolism and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Most patients form antibodies against the enzyme. Infusion reactions such as rash, flushing, fever, chills and headache are common. Patients with MPS I often have narrowed airways and reduced lung function, which makes reactions such as bronchospasm and low oxygen more dangerous. Anaphylaxis can occur and is part of the boxed warning, so infusions are given where emergency care is available. Reactions are managed by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The first proof that enzyme replacement could work for MPS came from mixing cells from two different patients in a dish: each one supplied the enzyme the other was missing.",
  "src": ["Aldurazyme (laronidase) FDA label", "Fratantoni JC, Hall CW, Neufeld EF. Science 1968", "Kakkis ED et al. N Engl J Med 2001"]
},
"idursulfase": {
  "origin": "Hunter syndrome, mucopolysaccharidosis type II, was described in 1917 by Charles Hunter, a Canadian physician, who reported two brothers with coarse features and stiff joints. It is X-linked, so it affects mostly boys. The missing enzyme, iduronate-2-sulfatase, was identified in the 1970s. Transkaryotic Therapies developed idursulfase using a human cell line, switching on the body's own enzyme gene so the cells made human enzyme with human sugar patterns. Shire acquired the company, and the FDA approved idursulfase as Elaprase in 2006, the first treatment for Hunter syndrome. It improves walking but does not treat the brain disease many patients have.",
  "timeline": [["1917", "Charles Hunter describes the syndrome in two brothers."], ["1970s", "Iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency is identified as the cause."], ["2006", "FDA approves idursulfase (Elaprase)."]],
  "cell": "In Hunter syndrome, variants in the X-linked IDS gene reduce iduronate-2-sulfatase. This lysosomal enzyme removes a sulfate group from the 2-position of iduronic acid at the end of dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate chains. Without it, the next enzymes in the breakdown pathway cannot act, so partly digested GAGs build up in lysosomes. Idursulfase is recombinant human iduronate-2-sulfatase carrying mannose-6-phosphate. It binds mannose-6-phosphate receptors on cells, is taken into lysosomes and removes the 2-O-sulfate, allowing GAG breakdown to continue. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not prevent the neurocognitive decline seen in the severe form.",
  "body": [["Liver and spleen", "Reduced GAG storage shrinks the enlarged liver and spleen."], ["Muscles and joints", "Six-minute walk distance improved in trials, reflecting better mobility."], ["Lungs and airway", "Airway narrowing from GAG storage increases the risk of breathing problems during infusion reactions."], ["Urine", "Urinary GAG levels fall within weeks of starting therapy."], ["Brain", "The enzyme does not reach the brain, so cognitive decline in severe Hunter syndrome is not prevented."]],
  "journey": "Idursulfase is given by IV infusion once a week, usually over about three hours. Cells take it from the blood quickly, so its plasma half-life is under an hour. Inside lysosomes it keeps working through the week. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as headache, fever, rash, itching, hives and blood pressure rise are common, reflecting immune responses to the protein. Anaphylaxis can happen and is part of the boxed warning, and some reactions are biphasic, returning up to 24 hours after the infusion, so patients are watched after treatment. Airway disease and reduced lung function in Hunter syndrome make bronchospasm and low oxygen more dangerous. Antibodies can form and may reduce effect. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Idursulfase is made in human cells rather than hamster cells, so its sugar chains look more like those on the body's own enzyme.",
  "src": ["Elaprase (idursulfase) FDA label", "Muenzer J et al. Genet Med 2006"]
},
"elosulfase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Morquio syndrome was described in 1929 independently by Luis Morquio, a pediatrician in Uruguay, and James Brailsford, a radiologist in England. It causes severe short stature, skeletal deformities and loose joints, but intelligence is usually normal. The type A form, MPS IVA, results from missing N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase (GALNS). BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed recombinant human GALNS made in Chinese hamster ovary cells. The FDA approved it as Vimizim in 2014, the first treatment for Morquio A syndrome.",
  "timeline": [["1929", "Luis Morquio and James Brailsford separately describe the syndrome."], ["1970s", "GALNS deficiency is identified as the cause of MPS IVA."], ["2014", "FDA approves elosulfase alfa (Vimizim)."]],
  "cell": "MPS IVA comes from variants in GALNS. This lysosomal enzyme removes 6-sulfate groups from N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfate and galactose-6-sulfate residues in two GAGs: keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate. Without it, these GAGs build up, especially in cartilage, bone and growth plates, where keratan sulfate is abundant. Elosulfase alfa is recombinant human GALNS with mannose-6-phosphate sugars. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and deliver it to lysosomes, where it removes the 6-sulfates so the GAG chains can be broken down. Cartilage has few blood vessels, which limits how much enzyme reaches growth plate cells, so established skeletal changes improve little.",
  "body": [["Bones and cartilage", "Keratan sulfate storage in cartilage drives skeletal dysplasia; therapy lowers storage but changes established bone shape little."], ["Muscles and endurance", "Six-minute walk distance improved modestly in the pivotal trial."], ["Urine", "Urinary keratan sulfate falls within weeks, a marker of enzyme activity."], ["Airway and lungs", "Narrowed airways and a small chest make breathing problems during infusion reactions more dangerous."], ["Spine", "Instability of the upper neck vertebrae is a serious feature of the disease and is monitored separately."]],
  "journey": "Elosulfase alfa is given by IV infusion once a week over about four hours or longer. It leaves the blood quickly as cells take it up, with a plasma half-life well under an hour after a single dose. In lysosomes it continues to work. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared through CYP enzymes or intact by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as fever, vomiting, headache, nausea, abdominal pain, chills and fatigue are common. Anaphylaxis can occur and is part of the boxed warning. Patients with Morquio A often have small airways and restrictive lung disease, so bronchospasm or low oxygen during a reaction is especially risky, and patients with acute illness may have infusions delayed. Antibodies form in nearly all patients. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Unlike many other MPS disorders, Morquio A usually leaves intelligence unaffected, because keratan sulfate is stored mainly in cartilage rather than the brain.",
  "src": ["Vimizim (elosulfase alfa) FDA label", "Hendriksz CJ et al. J Inherit Metab Dis 2014"]
},
"galsulfase": {
  "origin": "Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome, mucopolysaccharidosis type VI, was described in 1963 by French physicians Pierre Maroteaux and Maurice Lamy. Children have short stature, stiff joints, heart valve disease and clouded corneas, but usually normal intelligence. The missing enzyme is N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase, also called arylsulfatase B. BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed recombinant human arylsulfatase B in Chinese hamster ovary cells. The FDA approved it as Naglazyme in 2005, the first treatment for MPS VI.",
  "timeline": [["1963", "Pierre Maroteaux and Maurice Lamy describe MPS VI."], ["1970s", "Arylsulfatase B deficiency is identified as the cause."], ["2005", "FDA approves galsulfase (Naglazyme)."]],
  "cell": "In MPS VI, variants in ARSB reduce N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase (arylsulfatase B). This lysosomal enzyme removes the 4-sulfate from N-acetylgalactosamine residues at the end of dermatan sulfate and chondroitin-4-sulfate. Without it, dermatan sulfate builds up in connective tissue, heart valves, cornea, bones and joints. Galsulfase is recombinant human arylsulfatase B with mannose-6-phosphate sugars. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and send it to lysosomes, where it removes the 4-sulfate so the GAG can be broken down further. Brain involvement is limited in MPS VI, so the blood-brain barrier is less of a problem than in some other MPS types.",
  "body": [["Muscles and endurance", "Walking distance and stair-climbing capacity improved in trials."], ["Urine", "Urinary GAG levels fall within weeks of starting therapy."], ["Heart", "Dermatan sulfate thickens heart valves; enzyme replacement does not easily reverse established valve disease."], ["Airway and lungs", "Narrow airways and sleep apnea raise the risk of breathing problems during infusion reactions."], ["Eyes and joints", "Corneal clouding and joint stiffness are common disease features and respond slowly, if at all."]],
  "journey": "Galsulfase is given by IV infusion once a week over at least four hours. Cells take it up from the blood very quickly, so its plasma half-life is minutes. In lysosomes it keeps working through the week. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared intact by the kidneys or through CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as rash, itching, hives, fever, chills, headache, nausea and abdominal pain are common because the immune system reacts to the recombinant protein. Anaphylaxis is possible. Patients with MPS VI may have airway narrowing and sleep apnea, so apnea and breathing difficulty during an infusion are a concern, and infusions are given where emergency care is available. Fluid volume can stress weakened hearts and lungs. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Pierre Maroteaux, one of the physicians who described MPS VI, went on to describe many other skeletal dysplasias and is widely regarded as a pioneer of that field.",
  "src": ["Naglazyme (galsulfase) FDA label", "Harmatz P et al. J Pediatr 2006"]
},
"sebelipase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency includes Wolman disease, described in 1956 by Israeli neuropathologist Moshe Wolman, in which infants develop huge livers and spleens, calcified adrenal glands and fatal malabsorption, usually within months. A milder later-onset form is called cholesteryl ester storage disease. Synageva BioPharma developed recombinant human lysosomal acid lipase in an unusual way: it was produced in the egg whites of genetically engineered chickens. Alexion acquired Synageva in 2015, and the FDA approved sebelipase alfa as Kanuma that same year, the first treatment for this disease. The FDA approved the engineered chicken as well, a first for an animal used to make a human drug in eggs.",
  "timeline": [["1956", "Moshe Wolman describes the infant form of LAL deficiency."], ["2015", "FDA approves sebelipase alfa (Kanuma), produced in transgenic chicken egg whites."]],
  "cell": "Lysosomal acid lipase (LAL) breaks down cholesteryl esters and triglycerides that enter cells through LDL receptor uptake. It splits them into free cholesterol, glycerol and free fatty acids in the lysosome. Free cholesterol then leaves the lysosome and signals the cell to make less cholesterol and fewer LDL receptors. In LAL deficiency, variants in LIPA block this step. Lipids pile up in liver cells and macrophages, the liver becomes fatty and scarred, and because cells sense too little free cholesterol, they keep making more and take up more LDL, raising blood LDL. Sebelipase alfa is recombinant human LAL. It is taken into cells through mannose-6-phosphate and mannose receptors and delivered to lysosomes, where it hydrolyzes the stored lipids and restores feedback control.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Lysosomal lipid storage decreases, liver enzymes such as ALT fall and liver fat declines, which may slow fibrosis."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol and triglycerides fall and HDL rises as normal cholesterol feedback returns."], ["Spleen", "Splenic enlargement from lipid-filled macrophages decreases."], ["Gut and growth", "In infants, lipid-laden macrophages in the gut wall cause malabsorption; therapy improves weight gain and growth."], ["Survival", "Infants with rapidly progressive disease survived longer with therapy, a disease previously fatal in the first year."]],
  "journey": "Sebelipase alfa is given by IV infusion, every other week for later-onset disease and more often in infants. It disappears from the blood within minutes because liver cells and macrophages take it up through mannose and mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it continues to break down lipid. It is broken down like other proteins and is not cleared through CYP enzymes or intact by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions such as fever, chills, rash, itching and breathing symptoms are common, and anaphylaxis can occur. Because it is produced in chicken egg whites, people with egg allergies may be at higher risk of hypersensitivity. Antibodies, including neutralizing antibodies, can form. Diarrhea, vomiting, fever and cough were common in infant trials, partly reflecting the illness. Reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion and treating symptoms. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Kanuma's enzyme comes from hens whose DNA was modified so their eggs contain the human enzyme, which is then purified from the egg white.",
  "src": ["Kanuma (sebelipase alfa) FDA label", "Burton BK et al. N Engl J Med 2015", "Abramov A, Schorr S, Wolman M. AMA J Dis Child 1956"]
},
"vosoritide": {
  "origin": "Achondroplasia is the most common form of short-limbed dwarfism. In 1994 two research groups found the cause: a gain-of-function variant (G380R) in the FGFR3 gene, which overactivates a brake on growth plate cells. Researchers in Japan and elsewhere then showed that C-type natriuretic peptide (CNP), a natural growth plate signal, could counter that brake, and mice that overmade CNP grew longer bones. Natural CNP lasts only minutes in the blood, so BioMarin Pharmaceutical designed a 39-amino-acid analog that resists breakdown by neutral endopeptidase. The FDA approved it as Voxzogo in 2021 to increase linear growth in children with achondroplasia age 5 and older with open growth plates, and in 2023 extended it to children of all ages with open growth plates.",
  "timeline": [["1994", "FGFR3 variants are identified as the cause of achondroplasia."], ["2000s", "Studies show CNP signaling can counteract FGFR3 overactivity in growth plates."], ["2021", "FDA approves vosoritide (Voxzogo) for achondroplasia."], ["2023", "Approval is extended to younger children with open growth plates."]],
  "cell": "In growth plates, chondrocytes multiply in columns and then enlarge before being replaced by bone, which lengthens the long bones. FGFR3 is a receptor tyrosine kinase that slows this process. In achondroplasia, the G380R variant keeps FGFR3 overly active, so its downstream MAPK pathway (RAF-1, MEK, ERK1/2) restrains chondrocyte proliferation and differentiation. Vosoritide is a modified C-type natriuretic peptide that binds natriuretic peptide receptor B (NPR-B), a guanylyl cyclase receptor on chondrocytes. Binding raises intracellular cGMP, which activates protein kinase G. PKG inhibits RAF-1, damping the overactive MAPK signal from FGFR3. Released from that brake, chondrocytes multiply and mature, promoting endochondral bone growth. It only works while growth plates are open.",
  "body": [["Growth plates", "Faster chondrocyte proliferation increases annual growth rate in children, measured as added height per year."], ["Blood vessels", "CNP signaling relaxes vascular smooth muscle, so blood pressure can drop briefly after injection, causing dizziness or fainting."], ["Injection site", "Redness, swelling and hives at the injection site are very common."], ["Gut", "Vomiting is among the reported side effects."], ["Body proportions", "Studies are examining whether longer-term use changes body proportions and other complications of achondroplasia."]],
  "journey": "Vosoritide is injected under the skin once daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking about 15 minutes after injection, and is cleared within about half an hour. Neutral endopeptidase and other proteases break it down, though its design slows this compared with natural CNP. Despite the short half-life, a daily pulse of signaling is enough to increase growth. Taking it with food and fluids reduces the drop in blood pressure.",
  "sidefx": "Because CNP signaling through cGMP also relaxes blood vessels, a temporary drop in blood pressure can occur after injection, causing dizziness, fatigue or fainting. Eating and drinking beforehand lessens this. Injection site redness, swelling and hives are common. Vomiting can occur. It has no effect once growth plates close, so it is stopped then. There is no antidote; low blood pressure is treated by lying down and fluids.",
  "fun": "Achondroplasia is usually not inherited from a parent: about 80% of cases come from a new FGFR3 variant, often linked to older paternal age.",
  "src": ["Voxzogo (vosoritide) FDA label", "Savarirayan R et al. Lancet 2020", "Shiang R et al. Cell 1994"]
},
"inotersen": {
  "origin": "Inotersen comes from antisense technology developed over decades at Ionis Pharmaceuticals (formerly Isis Pharmaceuticals) in California. Antisense oligonucleotides are short strands of modified DNA-like chemistry designed to bind a single messenger RNA and mark it for destruction. Ionis used 2-prime-O-methoxyethyl (2-prime-MOE) chemistry to make the strands stable and strong binders. They targeted transthyretin (TTR) mRNA, since liver-made TTR misfolds into amyloid in hereditary TTR amyloidosis. Its affiliate Akcea Therapeutics took it through the NEURO-TTR trial. The FDA approved it as Tegsedi in October 2018, just weeks after patisiran, making TTR amyloidosis the first disease with two approved gene-silencing drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Isis Pharmaceuticals (later Ionis) is founded to develop antisense drugs."], ["2018", "NEURO-TTR trial results are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2018", "FDA approves inotersen (Tegsedi) for hATTR polyneuropathy."]],
  "cell": "Inotersen is a 20-nucleotide antisense oligonucleotide built as a gapmer: a central stretch of DNA-like units flanked by 2-prime-MOE-modified wings. It has a phosphorothioate backbone that resists nucleases and binds plasma proteins. After injection it reaches the liver and enters hepatocytes. There it binds by Watson-Crick base pairing to the 3-prime untranslated region of TTR mRNA. The DNA-RNA hybrid in the central gap is recognized by RNase H1, which cuts the RNA strand. Cut mRNA is degraded, so less TTR protein is made. The target sequence is shared by normal and variant TTR, so both are lowered. Serum TTR falls by roughly 70 to 80%, reducing the supply of protein that forms amyloid in nerves.",
  "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Lower TTR slows amyloid buildup in nerves, slowing the progression of numbness, weakness and pain."], ["Blood platelets", "Platelet counts can drop suddenly and severely, raising the risk of serious bleeding including in the brain; frequent counts are required."], ["Kidneys", "Glomerulonephritis can develop, sometimes needing immunosuppressive treatment, so kidney function and urine protein are checked."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "Lower TTR lowers retinol-binding protein and vitamin A, so supplements are given to protect night vision."], ["Brain and vessels", "Stroke and cervicocephalic arterial dissection have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Inotersen is injected under the skin once a week. It is absorbed within hours and is highly bound to plasma proteins, which slows kidney filtration. It distributes mainly to the liver and kidneys. Endonucleases and exonucleases slowly break it into shorter pieces; it is not a CYP substrate. Pieces leave in the urine. Its tissue half-life is about a month, so TTR lowering builds over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Severe thrombocytopenia and glomerulonephritis are the main risks and carry boxed warnings; the drug is available only through a REMS program with regular monitoring of platelets and kidney function. These effects are thought to involve immune mechanisms and the phosphorothioate backbone's tendency to bind proteins, including on platelets. Low vitamin A follows from lower TTR. Injection site reactions, nausea, headache, fatigue and fever are common. There is no antidote; affected patients stop the drug and receive supportive care.",
  "fun": "Inotersen and patisiran silence the same gene in different ways: inotersen uses a single strand and RNase H1, while patisiran uses a double strand and the RISC machinery.",
  "src": ["Tegsedi (inotersen) FDA label", "Benson MD et al. N Engl J Med 2018 (NEURO-TTR)"]
},
"eplontersen": {
  "origin": "Eplontersen is the next step after inotersen at Ionis Pharmaceuticals. Ionis kept the antisense approach against transthyretin mRNA but attached a cluster of three N-acetylgalactosamine (GalNAc) sugars. GalNAc binds a receptor found almost only on liver cells, so much more drug reaches its target and far less is needed. This allowed monthly self-injection and avoided the platelet and kidney problems that required close monitoring with inotersen. Ionis developed it with AstraZeneca. Based on the NEURO-TTRansform trial, the FDA approved it as Wainua in December 2023 for the polyneuropathy of hereditary TTR amyloidosis.",
  "timeline": [["2018", "Inotersen, the first antisense TTR drug, is approved."], ["2010s", "Ionis develops GalNAc-conjugated antisense chemistry for targeted liver delivery."], ["2023", "FDA approves eplontersen (Wainua) for hATTR polyneuropathy."]],
  "cell": "Eplontersen is a 2-prime-MOE gapmer antisense oligonucleotide joined to a triantennary GalNAc ligand. After injection, GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, and the drug is carried into the cell by endocytosis. Once inside, the ligand is cleaved off and the oligonucleotide pairs with the 3-prime untranslated region of TTR mRNA. The DNA-RNA hybrid formed in the gapmer's central stretch is a substrate for RNase H1, which cuts the mRNA so it is degraded. Both variant and wild-type TTR production fall, by about 80% in serum. With less TTR in the blood, fewer unstable tetramers break into monomers that misfold into amyloid in nerves.",
  "body": [["Peripheral nerves", "Lower TTR slows amyloid deposition in nerves, and in trials the neuropathy stabilized or progressed more slowly."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Reducing amyloid may slow damage to nerves controlling blood pressure, gut and bladder."], ["Liver", "The liver is where it works, through GalNAc-targeted uptake into hepatocytes."], ["Eyes and vitamin A", "TTR carries retinol-binding protein, so serum vitamin A falls; supplements protect against night blindness and dry eye."], ["Gut", "Vomiting was reported more often than with placebo."]],
  "journey": "Eplontersen is self-injected under the skin once a month with an autoinjector. GalNAc targeting delivers it rapidly to the liver; plasma levels peak within hours and then fall as liver cells take it up. It is more than 98% protein bound in plasma. Nucleases break it into shorter oligonucleotides, and it is not a CYP substrate. Fragments leave mainly in the urine. Its terminal half-life is about three weeks, which supports monthly dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Eplontersen's main expected effect is low vitamin A, because lowering TTR also lowers its partner, retinol-binding protein. Without supplements, night blindness and dry eyes can result. Vomiting and injection site reactions occur. Because GalNAc delivery lets much less drug reach other tissues, the severe platelet drops and glomerulonephritis seen with inotersen were not seen in trials, and the label does not carry those boxed warnings. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Eplontersen and inotersen work the same way inside the liver cell. The key difference is the GalNAc sugar tag that steers eplontersen straight to the liver, so far less drug reaches other organs.",
  "src": ["Wainua (eplontersen) FDA label", "Coelho T et al. JAMA 2023 (NEURO-TTRansform)"]
},
"acoramidis": {
  "origin": "Acoramidis was designed at Stanford University in the 2010s by Isabella Graef and Mamoun Alhamadsheh, who wanted a drug that would copy a protective gene variant found in nature. Some people with a dangerous transthyretin (TTR) variant are protected from amyloid disease because they also carry a second variant, T119M, which locks the TTR tetramer together. The Stanford team built a small molecule, first called AG10, that binds TTR's thyroxine pockets and forms hydrogen bonds like those that stabilize T119M. Eidos Therapeutics, later part of BridgeBio Pharma, developed it. After the ATTRibute-CM trial, the FDA approved it as Attruby in November 2024 for ATTR cardiomyopathy, the second TTR stabilizer approved for the heart after tafamidis.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "Graef and Alhamadsheh at Stanford design AG10 to mimic the protective T119M TTR variant."], ["2023", "ATTRibute-CM trial reports benefit in ATTR cardiomyopathy."], ["2024", "FDA approves acoramidis (Attruby) for ATTR cardiomyopathy."]],
  "cell": "Transthyretin circulates as a tetramer of four identical subunits with two thyroxine-binding pockets at the interface between its two dimers. Amyloid forms when the tetramer first dissociates into monomers, which then misfold and assemble into fibrils that deposit in heart muscle. Acoramidis binds selectively and with high affinity in the thyroxine-binding pockets. Inside the pocket it forms hydrogen bonds with serine 117 residues on neighboring subunits, mimicking the extra stability of the T119M variant. This kinetic stabilization raises the energy barrier for dissociation, so the tetramer stays intact. At the levels reached in patients, it stabilizes TTR almost completely. Because stabilized TTR is cleared more slowly, serum TTR levels rise, a sign the drug is working. It does not reduce TTR production.",
  "body": [["Heart", "Less amyloid formation slows heart muscle stiffening and was linked to fewer cardiovascular deaths and hospitalizations in ATTRibute-CM."], ["Blood TTR", "Serum TTR levels rise as stabilized tetramers persist longer in the blood, which is a marker of target engagement."], ["Kidneys", "An early small rise in serum creatinine and fall in eGFR can occur, then levels stabilize; this appears related to kidney handling rather than injury."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea and upper abdominal pain were reported more often than with placebo."], ["Thyroid hormone", "Binding the thyroxine pockets of TTR does not cause clinical thyroid problems, because other proteins carry most thyroxine."]],
  "journey": "Acoramidis is taken by mouth twice daily. It is absorbed quickly, peaking about one hour after a dose, and is about 96% protein bound, largely to TTR itself. It has a half-life of about 27 hours. The liver and other tissues conjugate it with glucuronic acid (UGT enzymes), forming an acyl glucuronide, and it leaves mostly in the urine as that metabolite.",
  "sidefx": "Acoramidis is generally well tolerated because it simply stabilizes a carrier protein without blocking an enzyme or receptor. Diarrhea and upper abdominal pain occur. An early rise in serum creatinine with a small drop in eGFR is seen after starting, and it usually stabilizes; it is thought to reflect a change in how the kidneys handle creatinine and filtration rather than true kidney damage. There is no antidote, and none is generally needed.",
  "fun": "Acoramidis is a rare example of a drug designed to copy a protective human gene variant, letting patients borrow the stability that T119M carriers are born with.",
  "src": ["Attruby (acoramidis) FDA label", "Gillmore JD et al. N Engl J Med 2024 (ATTRibute-CM)", "Penchala SC et al. PNAS 2013"]
},
"pegvaliase": {
  "origin": "Pegvaliase uses an enzyme borrowed from bacteria. Phenylalanine ammonia lyase (PAL) is found in plants, fungi and some bacteria, where it breaks down phenylalanine without needing a cofactor. Researchers had long proposed PAL as a way to treat phenylketonuria (PKU) independent of the patient's own broken enzyme. The challenge was that the body sees PAL as foreign and destroys it. BioMarin Pharmaceutical used PAL from the cyanobacterium Anabaena variabilis and coated it with polyethylene glycol (PEG) to hide it from the immune system and keep it in the blood longer. The FDA approved it as Palynziq in 2018 for adults with PKU whose blood phenylalanine stays too high on existing treatment, with a boxed warning for anaphylaxis.",
  "timeline": [["1934", "Asbjorn Folling describes phenylketonuria."], ["1980s", "Researchers propose phenylalanine ammonia lyase as an enzyme substitution therapy for PKU."], ["2018", "FDA approves pegvaliase (Palynziq) for adults with PKU."]],
  "cell": "In PKU, phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) in the liver does not work well, so phenylalanine builds up in blood and brain. Pegvaliase supplies a different enzyme that does not need PAH or its cofactor BH4. Phenylalanine ammonia lyase removes the amino group from phenylalanine, producing trans-cinnamic acid and a small amount of ammonia. Trans-cinnamic acid is converted in the liver to hippuric acid, which is excreted in the urine. Because it bypasses PAH entirely, pegvaliase works regardless of the patient's PAH genotype. PEGylation, the attachment of many polyethylene glycol chains, shields the bacterial protein from immune recognition and kidney filtration, extending how long it circulates and reducing, though not eliminating, antibody formation.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Lower blood phenylalanine reduces its entry into the brain, which may improve attention, mood and thinking that are affected by high levels."], ["Diet", "Many responders can eat more natural protein because the enzyme breaks down phenylalanine from food."], ["Immune system", "Antibodies to PAL and PEG form in nearly everyone, causing hypersensitivity reactions and anaphylaxis, especially early on."], ["Joints and skin", "Joint pain, itching, rashes and injection site reactions are common, largely from immune complex responses."], ["Blood phenylalanine", "Levels can fall too low (hypophenylalaninemia), so diet and dose are adjusted."]],
  "journey": "Pegvaliase is self-injected under the skin, starting at a low level and slowly increasing over weeks to months to let the immune system adapt. It is absorbed slowly, peaking several hours to a day after injection. PEGylation keeps it circulating for a long time, with a half-life averaging about two days at maintenance but varying widely between people, partly because antibodies affect clearance. It is broken down like other proteins rather than through the liver CYP system or the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "The body treats PAL as a foreign bacterial protein. Antibodies against PAL and PEG lead to injection site reactions, joint pain, rash and itching, and the most serious risk is anaphylaxis, which carries a boxed warning. For this reason the first dose is given under medical supervision, the drug is available only through a REMS program, and patients carry auto-injectable epinephrine. Phenylalanine can drop too low when the drug works well, so dietary protein may need to increase. Epinephrine treats anaphylaxis; there is no specific antidote to the enzyme.",
  "fun": "Phenylalanine ammonia lyase is the same type of enzyme plants use to start making lignin, flavonoids and other compounds, which is how it was first studied.",
  "src": ["Palynziq (pegvaliase) FDA label", "Thomas J et al. Mol Genet Metab 2018 (PRISM-2)"]
},
"carglumic-acid": {
  "origin": "N-acetylglutamate synthase (NAGS) deficiency, the rarest urea cycle disorder, was first described in 1981 by C. Bachmann and colleagues. Without NAGS, the liver cannot make N-acetylglutamate, the switch that turns on the first urea cycle enzyme, so ammonia builds up to dangerous levels. Because natural N-acetylglutamate is quickly broken down, researchers turned to its stable synthetic analog, carglumic acid (N-carbamylglutamate). Orphan Europe (later part of Recordati) developed it as Carbaglu, approved in Europe in 2003. The FDA approved it in 2010 for hyperammonemia from NAGS deficiency, and in 2021 for acute hyperammonemia in propionic and methylmalonic acidemia.",
  "timeline": [["1981", "Bachmann and colleagues describe NAGS deficiency."], ["2003", "Carglumic acid (Carbaglu) is approved in the European Union."], ["2010", "FDA approves carglumic acid for NAGS deficiency."], ["2021", "FDA approves carglumic acid for acute hyperammonemia in propionic and methylmalonic acidemia."]],
  "cell": "The urea cycle in liver mitochondria turns toxic ammonia into urea, which the kidneys excrete. The first step is carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 (CPS1), which joins ammonia and bicarbonate. CPS1 is essentially inactive unless N-acetylglutamate (NAG) binds to it as an obligatory allosteric activator. NAG is made by NAGS from glutamate and acetyl-CoA. In NAGS deficiency, NAG is missing, so CPS1 stays off and ammonia builds up. Carglumic acid is a structural analog of NAG that binds CPS1 and activates it, restoring the urea cycle. In propionic and methylmalonic acidemia, propionyl-CoA and methylmalonyl-CoA inhibit NAGS and reduce NAG production; carglumic acid bypasses that inhibition. It is more resistant to breakdown than natural NAG.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Turning CPS1 back on restores urea production from ammonia, often normalizing ammonia within about a day in NAGS deficiency."], ["Brain", "Lowering ammonia protects against brain swelling, seizures, coma and long-term brain injury caused by hyperammonemia."], ["Diet", "In NAGS deficiency, many patients can relax protein restriction once ammonia is controlled."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea were reported."], ["Immune and blood", "Infections, fever and anemia were reported in clinical use, though some reflect the underlying disease."]],
  "journey": "Carglumic acid is given by mouth or nasogastric tube as tablets dispersed in water. Absorption is incomplete, and it peaks about three hours after a dose. It enters the liver, where it acts in mitochondria. Gut bacteria break down part of the dose, much of an oral dose leaves in feces, and absorbed drug is excreted largely unchanged in urine. Its median half-life is about five to six hours.",
  "sidefx": "Carglumic acid simply replaces a missing activator, so serious side effects are uncommon. Vomiting, abdominal pain, fever, tonsillitis, anemia, diarrhea and headache were reported, many of which may reflect the underlying illness in sick patients. Hypersensitivity is rare. In organic acidemias it is an add-on to other ammonia-lowering treatments, not a replacement for them. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "In NAGS deficiency, carglumic acid can work so well that it acts almost like a cure for the urea cycle problem, as long as patients keep taking it.",
  "src": ["Carbaglu (carglumic acid) FDA label", "Bachmann C et al. N Engl J Med 1981", "Haberle J et al. Orphanet J Rare Dis 2019"]
},
"sodium-phenylbutyrate": {
  "origin": "Sodium phenylbutyrate came from the work of Saul Brusilow and colleagues at Johns Hopkins University, who in the late 1970s and 1980s pioneered alternative pathway therapy for urea cycle disorders. Their insight was that if the urea cycle cannot dispose of nitrogen, other compounds can bind nitrogen-carrying amino acids so they leave in the urine instead. Sodium benzoate traps glycine, and phenylacetate traps glutamine. Phenylacetate had a strong, unpleasant smell, so they used phenylbutyrate, an odorless prodrug the body converts to phenylacetate. The FDA approved sodium phenylbutyrate as Buphenyl in 1996 for chronic management of urea cycle disorders.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Saul Brusilow at Johns Hopkins develops alternative pathway nitrogen scavenging."], ["1996", "FDA approves sodium phenylbutyrate (Buphenyl) for urea cycle disorders."], ["2013", "Glycerol phenylbutyrate is approved as a lower-sodium alternative."], ["2022", "A combination with taurursodiol is approved for ALS, then withdrawn in 2024 after a confirmatory trial failed."]],
  "cell": "Normally the urea cycle packs waste nitrogen into urea. In urea cycle disorders, an enzyme defect blocks this, so ammonia and glutamine (a major nitrogen carrier) build up. Sodium phenylbutyrate is a prodrug. In the liver and other tissues it undergoes beta-oxidation to phenylacetate. Phenylacetate is activated to phenylacetyl-CoA and then joined with glutamine by acetylation to form phenylacetylglutamine. Each phenylacetylglutamine molecule carries two nitrogen atoms, the same as urea, and the kidneys excrete it readily. This removes nitrogen through a route that bypasses the defective urea cycle, lowering glutamine and ammonia. It works best as ongoing therapy alongside protein restriction and essential amino acid supplements.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Keeping ammonia low protects against brain swelling, confusion, seizures and coma from hyperammonemia."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete phenylacetylglutamine, the nitrogen-carrying waste product."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "Each dose carries a large amount of sodium, which can cause swelling and worsen heart failure or high blood pressure; low potassium can occur."], ["Reproductive system", "Menstrual changes and amenorrhea are common in women."], ["Taste and smell", "Bad taste, body odor and decreased appetite are common, partly from phenylacetate."], ["Nervous system", "Very high phenylacetate levels cause sleepiness, confusion, headache and vomiting."]],
  "journey": "Sodium phenylbutyrate is taken by mouth with each meal as tablets or powder. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour. It is converted by beta-oxidation to phenylacetate, which is conjugated with glutamine in the liver and kidneys. Phenylbutyrate and phenylacetate both have short half-lives, roughly one hour each. Phenylacetylglutamine is excreted in urine. Because the drug is cleared quickly, it is taken in divided doses with meals and feedings.",
  "sidefx": "The sodium load explains fluid retention, edema and worsening of high blood pressure or heart failure. Losing phenylacetylglutamine in urine can cause potassium loss. Accumulated phenylacetate is itself neurotoxic, causing sleepiness, confusion and vomiting, which can be hard to tell from hyperammonemia. Menstrual dysfunction, body odor and bad taste are common. Too much nitrogen removal can lower essential amino acids such as branched-chain amino acids. There is no antidote; levels are managed by adjusting dose and monitoring.",
  "fun": "Phenylacetate itself smells so strongly that patients and caregivers found its strong, unpleasant odor hard to live with, which is why the odorless prodrug phenylbutyrate replaced it for daily use.",
  "src": ["Buphenyl (sodium phenylbutyrate) FDA label", "Brusilow SW et al. N Engl J Med 1984", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"glycerol-phenylbutyrate": {
  "origin": "Glycerol phenylbutyrate was developed to improve on sodium phenylbutyrate, which had a bad taste, a high sodium load and many tablets or scoops of powder. Chemists joined three phenylbutyrate molecules to a glycerol backbone, making a triglyceride-like liquid that is nearly tasteless and contains no sodium. Pancreatic lipases in the gut release the phenylbutyrate slowly, giving steadier levels through the day. Hyperion Therapeutics developed it, and the FDA approved it as Ravicti in 2013 for chronic management of urea cycle disorders that are not controlled by diet and amino acid supplements alone. It was later extended to younger children.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "Sodium phenylbutyrate (Buphenyl) is approved for urea cycle disorders."], ["2013", "FDA approves glycerol phenylbutyrate (Ravicti)."]],
  "cell": "Glycerol phenylbutyrate is a triglyceride made of three phenylbutyrate chains on a glycerol backbone. In the small intestine, pancreatic lipases cut the chains loose, releasing phenylbutyrate, which is absorbed. Phenylbutyrate then undergoes beta-oxidation to phenylacetate. Phenylacetate is conjugated with glutamine to form phenylacetylglutamine, which carries two nitrogen atoms per molecule and is excreted in urine. This alternative route removes nitrogen without the urea cycle, lowering glutamine and ammonia in patients whose urea cycle enzymes are defective. Because lipase hydrolysis is gradual, phenylbutyrate is released slowly, smoothing ammonia control over 24 hours.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Steady ammonia control protects against hyperammonemic crises that cause confusion, vomiting, seizures and coma."], ["Gut", "Release depends on pancreatic lipase, so it may work less well in people with pancreatic insufficiency; diarrhea and gas are common."], ["Fluid and electrolytes", "It contains no sodium, which avoids the sodium load of sodium phenylbutyrate."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete phenylacetylglutamine, which carries waste nitrogen out of the body."], ["Nervous system", "High phenylacetate levels can cause headache, sleepiness, confusion and numbness."]],
  "journey": "Glycerol phenylbutyrate is taken by mouth with food, using an oral syringe, or through a feeding tube. In the gut, pancreatic lipases release phenylbutyrate gradually, so levels rise more slowly and are steadier than with the sodium salt. Phenylbutyrate is beta-oxidized to phenylacetate, which is joined with glutamine, and the resulting phenylacetylglutamine leaves in the urine. Divided doses with meals keep ammonia controlled over the day.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea, gas, nausea and decreased appetite come from the oily liquid and gut effects. Headache and fatigue are common. The most serious concern is phenylacetate neurotoxicity: if phenylacetate builds up, it can cause sleepiness, confusion, dizziness, headache and vomiting, similar to signs of high ammonia, so levels may be checked. Hyperammonemia can still occur during illness or high protein intake, which is the underlying disease rather than the drug. Because it needs lipase to activate, pancreatic insufficiency can reduce its effect. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Turning a salt into a triglyceride removed both the sodium and almost all of the taste, which is part of why many families found it easier to use.",
  "src": ["Ravicti (glycerol phenylbutyrate) FDA label", "Diaz GA et al. Hepatology 2013"]
},
"betaine": {
  "origin": "Betaine (trimethylglycine) was first identified in the 1800s in sugar beets, Beta vulgaris, which gave it its name. It is found in many foods and is made in the body from choline. In homocystinuria, very high homocysteine damages blood vessels, eyes, bones and the brain. Researchers in the 1980s found that betaine could lower homocysteine by giving it a methyl group through an alternative pathway. The FDA approved anhydrous betaine powder as Cystadane in 1996 for homocystinuria from CBS deficiency, MTHFR deficiency and cobalamin cofactor defects.",
  "timeline": [["1800s", "Betaine is identified in sugar beets."], ["1962", "Homocystinuria is first described in children in Northern Ireland."], ["1980s", "Betaine is shown to lower homocysteine in homocystinuria."], ["1996", "FDA approves betaine (Cystadane) for homocystinuria."]],
  "cell": "Homocysteine sits at a crossroads in methionine metabolism. It can be turned back into methionine by remethylation or sent down the transsulfuration path by cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS) to make cystathionine and cysteine. The main remethylation route uses methionine synthase, which needs vitamin B12 and methylfolate from MTHFR. In homocystinuria, one of these steps is defective, so homocysteine builds up. Betaine provides a second remethylation route: in the liver and kidney, the enzyme betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase (BHMT) transfers one of betaine's methyl groups to homocysteine, forming methionine and dimethylglycine. This bypasses the folate and B12-dependent pathway and lowers plasma total homocysteine. In CBS deficiency, methionine can rise because the downstream exit through CBS stays blocked.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Lowering homocysteine reduces its damage to vessel walls and lowers the risk of blood clots and strokes."], ["Eyes", "Homocystinuria causes lens dislocation; better control may help protect the eyes over time."], ["Bones", "High homocysteine interferes with collagen cross-linking; lowering it may help bone health."], ["Brain", "In CBS deficiency, very high methionine on betaine can lead to brain swelling (cerebral edema)."], ["Gut", "Nausea, stomach discomfort and diarrhea are reported."], ["Body odor", "An unpleasant body odor can occur, likely from betaine and its metabolites."]],
  "journey": "Betaine powder is dissolved in a drink and taken by mouth, usually twice daily. It is absorbed quickly and peaks in one to two hours. It is mostly metabolized by BHMT in the liver and kidney to dimethylglycine, which is further broken down. Little leaves unchanged in urine. Its half-life is about 14 hours. Homocysteine levels fall within days.",
  "sidefx": "Betaine lowers homocysteine by converting it into methionine. In CBS deficiency, where methionine cannot be cleared downstream, this can push methionine very high. Severe hypermethioninemia has been linked to cerebral edema, which is why methionine levels are monitored. GI upset, nausea and diarrhea are common. Body odor may come from betaine metabolites. There is no antidote; if cerebral edema occurs, betaine is stopped and methionine intake is reduced.",
  "fun": "Betaine is also a natural osmolyte: kidney cells and many sea creatures use it to protect themselves from very salty surroundings.",
  "src": ["Cystadane (betaine anhydrous) FDA label", "Wilcken DE et al. N Engl J Med 1983", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"lumasiran": {
  "origin": "Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 (PH1) is a rare inherited disease in which the liver makes too much oxalate, which forms calcium oxalate stones and crystals that destroy the kidneys. For decades, the only definitive fix was a combined liver and kidney transplant. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals, a pioneer in RNA interference, chose a new target: glycolate oxidase, the liver enzyme that makes glyoxylate, the immediate precursor of oxalate. People who naturally lack glycolate oxidase are generally healthy, suggesting that silencing it would be safe. Using its GalNAc-conjugated siRNA platform, Alnylam developed lumasiran. In November 2020 the FDA approved it as Oxlumo, the first treatment for PH1.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2010s", "Alnylam develops GalNAc-conjugated siRNA for targeted liver delivery."], ["2020", "FDA approves lumasiran (Oxlumo), the first drug for PH1."]],
  "cell": "In PH1, the liver enzyme alanine-glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT, from the AGXT gene) is missing or misdirected. Normally AGT converts glyoxylate to glycine in peroxisomes. Without it, glyoxylate is converted by lactate dehydrogenase to oxalate, which the body cannot break down. Lumasiran is a double-stranded siRNA conjugated to GalNAc. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, so the drug is taken up into liver cells. The guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC), which finds and cuts HAO1 mRNA, the message for glycolate oxidase. With less glycolate oxidase, less glycolate is turned into glyoxylate, so less glyoxylate is available to become oxalate. Glycolate builds up instead, but it is soluble and harmlessly excreted in urine.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "Lower oxalate production reduces urinary oxalate, which should mean fewer kidney stones, less nephrocalcinosis and slower loss of kidney function."], ["Blood oxalate", "Plasma oxalate falls, which matters especially in patients with reduced kidney function, where oxalate deposits in bone, heart and other tissues."], ["Liver", "The drug acts in the liver, silencing glycolate oxidase in hepatocytes."], ["Urine glycolate", "Glycolate rises in blood and urine as an expected, harmless result of the upstream block."], ["Injection site", "Injection site reactions such as redness, pain and itching are the most common side effects."]],
  "journey": "Lumasiran is injected under the skin, monthly at first and then every three months. GalNAc delivers it to the liver within hours, so plasma levels peak about four hours after injection and then fall quickly, with a plasma half-life of about five hours. Inside liver cells it lasts for months, sustaining silencing between doses. Nucleases break it into shorter pieces; it is not a CYP substrate. A small amount leaves intact in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Because GalNAc delivery sends nearly all the drug to the liver and glycolate oxidase loss is well tolerated, side effects are few. Injection site reactions are the most common, and abdominal pain has been reported. The rise in glycolate is expected and not toxic, because glycolate is soluble. There is no antidote; the silencing effect fades over months after stopping.",
  "fun": "The idea of targeting glycolate oxidase came partly from people born without it: they have high glycolate levels but no apparent disease, which made it look like a safe enzyme to switch off.",
  "src": ["Oxlumo (lumasiran) FDA label", "Garrelfs SF et al. N Engl J Med 2021 (ILLUMINATE-A)"]
},
"nedosiran": {
  "origin": "Nedosiran takes a different route to the same goal as lumasiran in primary hyperoxaluria. Instead of blocking an early step, it targets lactate dehydrogenase A (LDHA) in the liver, which catalyzes the final step that converts glyoxylate to oxalate. Because this step is shared by all three types of primary hyperoxaluria, the target looked broadly useful. Dicerna Pharmaceuticals developed nedosiran using its GalXC RNAi platform, which attaches GalNAc sugars to an RNAi molecule for liver delivery. Novo Nordisk acquired Dicerna in 2021. After the PHYOX2 trial, the FDA approved it as Rivfloza in 2023 for PH1 in children 9 years and older and adults with relatively preserved kidney function, and later extended it to younger children.",
  "timeline": [["2020", "Lumasiran is approved as the first RNAi drug for PH1."], ["2021", "Novo Nordisk acquires Dicerna Pharmaceuticals."], ["2023", "FDA approves nedosiran (Rivfloza) for PH1."]],
  "cell": "In primary hyperoxaluria, glyoxylate accumulates in liver cells. Lactate dehydrogenase, especially the LDHA isoform in liver, converts glyoxylate to oxalate, which cannot be broken down and crystallizes with calcium in the kidneys. Nedosiran is a GalNAc-conjugated double-stranded RNAi oligonucleotide. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor (ASGPR) on hepatocytes, carrying it into liver cells. The guide strand loads into RISC, which cleaves LDHA mRNA, so less hepatic LDHA protein is made. With less LDHA, less glyoxylate is converted to oxalate. Silencing is limited mostly to the liver, so lactate dehydrogenase in muscle and other tissues continues to work.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "Lower urinary oxalate should reduce calcium oxalate stones and kidney damage."], ["Liver", "LDHA is silenced in hepatocytes, the main source of oxalate in primary hyperoxaluria."], ["Muscle and other tissues", "Because GalNAc delivery is liver-specific, lactate dehydrogenase in muscle and red blood cells is largely unaffected."], ["Injection site", "Injection site reactions are the most common side effect."]],
  "journey": "Nedosiran is injected under the skin once a month. GalNAc targeting brings it to the liver within hours, so plasma levels peak quickly and fall within hours. Inside hepatocytes it stays active for weeks, sustaining LDHA silencing over the monthly interval. Nucleases break it into shorter pieces, and a small amount leaves intact in urine. It is not a CYP substrate.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are few because nearly all the drug goes to the liver. Injection site reactions such as redness, pain and bruising are the most common. Silencing liver LDHA has not caused problems with lactate handling in trials, likely because other tissues and isoforms carry on. There is no antidote; the effect fades gradually after stopping.",
  "fun": "Nedosiran blocks the last step to oxalate, while lumasiran blocks an earlier step, so two RNAi drugs attack the same disease from different points on the same pathway.",
  "src": ["Rivfloza (nedosiran) FDA label", "Baum MA et al. Kidney Int 2023 (PHYOX2)"]
},
"lonafarnib": {
  "origin": "Lonafarnib started as a cancer drug. Schering-Plough developed it in the 1990s as a farnesyltransferase inhibitor, hoping to block RAS proteins, which need a farnesyl lipid tag to work. It did not succeed in cancer. In 2003 a team led by Francis Collins at the US National Human Genome Research Institute, working with the Progeria Research Foundation founded by Leslie Gordon and Scott Berns, found the cause of Hutchinson-Gilford progeria syndrome: a variant in LMNA that makes an abnormal, permanently farnesylated protein called progerin. Lab studies showed farnesyltransferase inhibitors could reverse nuclear damage in progeria cells. Clinical trials at Boston Children's Hospital began in 2007. Eiger BioPharmaceuticals brought lonafarnib to approval, and the FDA approved it as Zokinvy in 2020, the first treatment for progeria.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering-Plough develops lonafarnib as an anticancer farnesyltransferase inhibitor."], ["2003", "LMNA variant causing progeria is identified by Collins and colleagues."], ["2007", "The first clinical trial of lonafarnib in progeria begins at Boston Children's Hospital."], ["2020", "FDA approves lonafarnib (Zokinvy), the first treatment for progeria."]],
  "cell": "Lamin A is a structural protein of the nuclear envelope. It is made as prelamin A, which ends in a CAAX box that is farnesylated, then trimmed, and finally cleaved by the enzyme ZMPSTE24 to remove the farnesylated tail. In progeria, a single-base change (c.1824C>T) activates a hidden splice site, deleting 50 amino acids that include the ZMPSTE24 cleavage site. The resulting protein, progerin, stays permanently farnesylated and anchored to the inner nuclear membrane. It distorts the nucleus (blebbing), disrupts DNA repair and gene expression and causes cells to age early, especially in blood vessel walls. Lonafarnib inhibits farnesyltransferase, blocking addition of the farnesyl group. Unfarnesylated progerin binds the nuclear membrane less, so nuclear shape improves and cellular damage is reduced.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Progeria causes rapid arterial stiffening and atherosclerosis; lonafarnib slows vascular stiffening, which is thought to explain improved survival."], ["Survival", "Treated children lived longer on average than untreated children in comparison studies."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, diarrhea, nausea and abdominal pain are very common and can limit treatment."], ["Liver and kidneys", "Liver enzyme rises and kidney toxicity have been reported."], ["Eyes", "Retinal toxicity was seen in animal studies and is monitored."], ["Blood", "Myelosuppression and electrolyte changes can occur."]],
  "journey": "Lonafarnib is taken by mouth twice daily with meals. It peaks about two to four hours after a dose and is more than 99% protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4. Lonafarnib is also a strong CYP3A inhibitor, so it raises levels of many other drugs and is affected by CYP3A inhibitors and inducers; grapefruit and Seville oranges are avoided. Its half-life is about four to six hours, and it leaves mostly in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Farnesyltransferase acts on many proteins besides progerin, which helps explain broad side effects. GI effects such as vomiting, diarrhea and nausea are the most common and can cause dehydration and electrolyte changes. Liver enzyme rises, kidney toxicity, bone marrow suppression and retinal toxicity can occur. QTc prolongation is possible. As a strong CYP3A inhibitor it causes many drug interactions, including dangerous rises in levels of certain statins and sedatives. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The Progeria Research Foundation was started by the parents of a boy with progeria, and his mother, physician Leslie Gordon, became a leader of the research that led to the drug's approval.",
  "src": ["Zokinvy (lonafarnib) FDA label", "Eriksson M et al. Nature 2003", "Gordon LB et al. JAMA 2018"]
},
"golodirsen": {
  "origin": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is caused by variants in the dystrophin gene, often deletions that throw the genetic reading frame out of register so no dystrophin is made. In the milder Becker muscular dystrophy, deletions keep the frame intact, and a shorter but partly working dystrophin is produced. In the 1990s and 2000s researchers proposed antisense exon skipping: using short synthetic strands to hide a chosen exon from the splicing machinery, turning a Duchenne-type message into a Becker-type message. Sarepta Therapeutics, building on phosphorodiamidate morpholino (PMO) chemistry developed at its predecessor AVI BioPharma, first won accelerated approval for eteplirsen (exon 51) in 2016. Golodirsen, which targets exon 53, received FDA accelerated approval as Vyondys 53 in December 2019.",
  "timeline": [["1986", "The dystrophin gene is identified as the cause of DMD."], ["2016", "Eteplirsen becomes the first exon-skipping drug approved for DMD."], ["2019", "FDA grants accelerated approval to golodirsen (Vyondys 53) for exon 53-amenable DMD."]],
  "cell": "The dystrophin gene has 79 exons, and its mRNA must be spliced so exons join in frame. Some deletions, for example of exon 52, leave exons that do not fit together in frame, so a premature stop codon ends translation and no functional dystrophin forms. Golodirsen is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer, an uncharged antisense strand with morpholine rings instead of ribose. It binds by base pairing to a sequence in exon 53 of dystrophin pre-mRNA. This blocks splicing factors from recognizing exon 53, so the spliceosome skips it and joins the flanking exons. For amenable deletions, skipping exon 53 restores the reading frame, and muscle cells make an internally shortened, partly functional dystrophin, similar to that in Becker muscular dystrophy. Because morpholinos are uncharged, they are not cleaved by RNase H and resist nucleases.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Small increases in dystrophin were seen in muscle biopsies, the basis of accelerated approval; clinical benefit has not yet been established in confirmatory trials."], ["Heart muscle", "Dystrophin loss also causes cardiomyopathy in DMD, but heart effects of golodirsen are not established."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney toxicity, including proteinuria and glomerulonephritis, was seen in animal studies and with related antisense drugs, so urine protein and kidney function are monitored."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions including rash, fever and bronchospasm have been reported."], ["General", "Headache, fever, falls, abdominal pain, cough and vomiting were common in trials."]],
  "journey": "Golodirsen is given by IV infusion once a week. It distributes into muscle and other tissues, though only a small fraction reaches muscle nuclei. Because morpholinos are uncharged and resist nucleases, it is barely metabolized and is not a CYP substrate. The kidneys clear it largely unchanged, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours. Dystrophin production builds over months of weekly dosing.",
  "sidefx": "Golodirsen concentrates in the kidney as it is filtered and excreted, which is thought to explain proteinuria and kidney injury, so kidney function and urine protein are checked before and during treatment. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur because the infused oligonucleotide may trigger immune responses. Headache, fever, cough, vomiting and falls were common in trials. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Exon skipping turns a severe disease into a milder one by cutting out even more genetic material, which seems backward until you see that keeping the reading frame matters more than keeping every exon.",
  "src": ["Vyondys 53 (golodirsen) FDA label", "Frank DE et al. Neurology 2020", "Hoffman EP, Brown RH, Kunkel LM. Cell 1987"]
},
"viltolarsen": {
  "origin": "Viltolarsen was developed in Japan by Nippon Shinyaku together with the National Center of Neurology and Psychiatry in Tokyo. It uses the same exon-skipping idea as Sarepta's drugs and the same phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer (PMO) chemistry, but with its own sequence targeting exon 53 of the dystrophin pre-mRNA. Japanese researchers designed it to skip exon 53 efficiently. It was approved in Japan in March 2020, and the FDA granted accelerated approval to Nippon Shinyaku's US subsidiary, NS Pharma, as Viltepso in August 2020, for Duchenne muscular dystrophy amenable to exon 53 skipping. It was the second exon 53-skipping drug approved in the US after golodirsen.",
  "timeline": [["2016", "Eteplirsen is the first exon-skipping drug approved in the US."], ["2019", "Golodirsen is approved as the first US exon 53-skipping drug."], ["2020", "Viltolarsen is approved in Japan in March."], ["2020", "FDA grants accelerated approval to viltolarsen (Viltepso) in August."]],
  "cell": "In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, deletions in the dystrophin gene disrupt the reading frame, so muscle cells make no functional dystrophin. Viltolarsen is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino antisense oligomer. Inside the nucleus it binds a target sequence within exon 53 of the dystrophin pre-mRNA. This blocks the splicing machinery from including exon 53, so the spliceosome joins the neighboring exons instead. For patients with deletions amenable to exon 53 skipping, this restores the reading frame and allows production of an internally truncated but partly functional dystrophin, similar to that in Becker muscular dystrophy. The neutral morpholino backbone resists nucleases and does not trigger RNase H cleavage.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Dystrophin levels in muscle biopsies increased in trials, the basis of accelerated approval; clinical benefit is still being confirmed."], ["Kidneys", "Proteinuria and kidney toxicity are possible, so urine protein and kidney function are monitored."], ["Heart", "Decreased ejection fraction has been reported, though DMD itself causes cardiomyopathy."], ["Respiratory tract", "Upper respiratory infections and cough were among the most common side effects in trials."], ["General", "Fever, bruising, joint pain, diarrhea and vomiting were reported."]],
  "journey": "Viltolarsen is given by IV infusion once a week over about an hour. It distributes into muscle and other tissues, though only a small fraction reaches muscle nuclei. Its uncharged morpholino chemistry makes it resistant to breakdown, and it is not a CYP substrate. It is excreted mostly unchanged in urine, with a plasma half-life of about two to three hours.",
  "sidefx": "Like other PMO drugs, viltolarsen is filtered and concentrated by the kidney, which explains the warning for kidney toxicity, including glomerulonephritis seen with related antisense drugs. Kidney function and urine protein are monitored. Upper respiratory infections, injection site reactions, cough, fever and bruising were common. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Viltolarsen was the first exon-skipping drug developed and approved in Japan, a country with a long history of exon-skipping research for DMD.",
  "src": ["Viltepso (viltolarsen) FDA label", "Clemens PR et al. JAMA Neurol 2020"]
},
"casimersen": {
  "origin": "Casimersen is Sarepta Therapeutics' third exon-skipping drug for Duchenne muscular dystrophy. After eteplirsen (exon 51) in 2016 and golodirsen (exon 53) in 2019, Sarepta designed a phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligomer that targets exon 45, which is amenable in roughly 8% of people with DMD. The FDA granted it accelerated approval as Amondys 45 in February 2021, based on an increase in dystrophin in muscle biopsies. Continued approval depends on confirmatory trial evidence of clinical benefit.",
  "timeline": [["2016", "Eteplirsen (exon 51) is approved."], ["2019", "Golodirsen (exon 53) is approved."], ["2021", "FDA grants accelerated approval to casimersen (Amondys 45) for exon 45-amenable DMD."]],
  "cell": "In DMD, deletions in the dystrophin gene shift the reading frame so translation stops early and no functional dystrophin is made. Casimersen is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino antisense oligomer that binds a sequence within exon 45 of dystrophin pre-mRNA. By covering that sequence, it blocks the splicing machinery from including exon 45, so the spliceosome joins the neighboring exons. For patients whose deletions are amenable to exon 45 skipping, this restores an in-frame message. Muscle cells then make an internally truncated, partly functional dystrophin similar to the protein in Becker muscular dystrophy. The neutral morpholino backbone resists nucleases and does not recruit RNase H, so the target RNA is redirected rather than destroyed.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Dystrophin in muscle increased in trials, which was the basis for its accelerated approval; clinical benefit is being studied."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney toxicity has been seen with related antisense drugs and in animals, so urine protein and kidney function are monitored."], ["Immune system", "Hypersensitivity reactions such as rash, fever and bronchospasm have been reported."], ["Respiratory tract", "Upper respiratory infection, cough and throat pain were among the more common effects in trials."], ["General", "Fever, headache and joint pain were reported."]],
  "journey": "Casimersen is given by IV infusion once a week. It distributes into muscle and other tissues, with a small fraction reaching muscle nuclei. It is barely metabolized and is not a CYP substrate. It is excreted largely unchanged by the kidneys, with a plasma half-life of about three to four hours.",
  "sidefx": "Like other PMO oligomers, casimersen is filtered and concentrated by the kidney, which is thought to underlie the risk of kidney toxicity; kidney function and urine protein are checked before and during treatment. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur during or after infusion. Upper respiratory infections, cough, fever, headache and joint pain were common. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Each exon-skipping drug only fits patients with particular deletions, so genetic testing is needed to know which exon, if any, a drug can skip for a given patient.",
  "src": ["Amondys 45 (casimersen) FDA label", "Aartsma-Rus A et al. Hum Mutat 2009"]
},
"vamorolone": {
  "origin": "Corticosteroids such as prednisone and deflazacort slow muscle loss in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, but long-term use causes growth delay, weight gain, bone thinning and other harms. Researchers including Eric Hoffman and Kanneboyina Nagaraju, who founded ReveraGen BioPharma, set out to design a steroid that kept the anti-inflammatory benefit with fewer side effects. They modified the steroid structure by adding a delta-9,11 double bond and removing the 11-beta hydroxyl group, which changed how it signals through the glucocorticoid receptor and turned it into a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist. Santhera Pharmaceuticals partnered on development. After the VISION-DMD trial, the FDA approved vamorolone as Agamree in October 2023 for DMD in patients 2 years and older.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Corticosteroids are shown to slow muscle decline in DMD."], ["2010s", "ReveraGen develops vamorolone as a dissociative steroid."], ["2023", "FDA approves vamorolone (Agamree) for Duchenne muscular dystrophy."]],
  "cell": "Classic glucocorticoids bind the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) and act in two main ways. In transrepression, the GR tethers to and blocks NF-kB and other inflammatory transcription factors, reducing inflammation. In transactivation, GR dimers bind DNA response elements and switch on genes, many of which drive side effects such as bone loss, muscle wasting, high blood sugar and growth suppression. Vamorolone binds GR and favors transrepression, inhibiting NF-kB-driven inflammation, with less transactivation. Lacking the 11-beta hydroxyl group changes how it interacts with steroid receptors, and it acts as an antagonist at the mineralocorticoid receptor rather than an agonist, which may reduce fluid retention and blood pressure effects. It also has membrane-stabilizing effects in muscle in lab studies. In dystrophic muscle, less inflammation slows muscle damage.",
  "body": [["Muscle", "Reduced inflammation in dystrophin-deficient muscle helps preserve function, with improvements in timed motor tests similar to prednisone in trials."], ["Growth", "Children's height growth was less affected than with prednisone in clinical trials."], ["Bones", "Bone formation markers were less suppressed than with prednisone, though long-term fracture data are limited."], ["Adrenal glands", "It still suppresses the HPA axis, so stopping suddenly can trigger adrenal crisis and stress-dose steroids may be needed during illness."], ["Metabolism and weight", "Cushingoid features, weight gain and increased appetite still occur."], ["Brain and behavior", "Behavior problems and mood changes are reported, as with other steroids."]],
  "journey": "Vamorolone is taken by mouth once daily with a meal as a liquid suspension. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about two hours, and is about 88% protein bound. The liver metabolizes it by glucuronidation and CYP3A4. Its half-life is about two hours, though its effects on gene expression last longer. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. When stopping after more than a week, the dose is tapered.",
  "sidefx": "Because vamorolone still activates the glucocorticoid receptor, it suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis, so abrupt stopping can cause adrenal insufficiency or crisis. Immune suppression raises infection risk, and live vaccines are generally avoided. Cushingoid features, weight gain, vomiting, behavior changes and vitamin D deficiency are reported. Blood sugar can rise. Its main promise is fewer growth and bone effects than prednisone, not the absence of steroid side effects. Adrenal crisis is treated with stress-dose hydrocortisone.",
  "fun": "In trials, children taking vamorolone kept growing in height at a more typical rate, while children taking prednisone tended to fall behind.",
  "src": ["Agamree (vamorolone) FDA label", "Guglieri M et al. JAMA Neurol 2022 (VISION-DMD)", "Heier CR et al. EMBO Mol Med 2013"]
},
"givinostat": {
  "origin": "Givinostat was discovered by Italfarmaco, an Italian pharmaceutical company in Milan, as a histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor, originally studied for blood cancers and inflammatory diseases. In the 2000s researchers including Pier Lorenzo Puri showed that HDAC inhibitors improved muscle regeneration and reduced scarring in mdx mice, a model of Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Italfarmaco then developed givinostat for DMD and tested it in the EPIDYS trial, which showed slower decline in stair climbing and less fat replacement of muscle. In March 2024 the FDA approved it as Duvyzat for DMD in patients 6 years and older, the first nonsteroidal drug approved for DMD regardless of genetic variant.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Italfarmaco develops givinostat as an HDAC inhibitor."], ["2006", "HDAC inhibitors are reported to improve muscle in mdx mice."], ["2024", "FDA approves givinostat (Duvyzat), the first nonsteroidal drug for all genetic forms of DMD."]],
  "cell": "Histone deacetylases remove acetyl groups from histones, the proteins that package DNA. Deacetylated histones pack DNA tightly and turn genes off. In dystrophin-deficient muscle, the loss of dystrophin disrupts nitric oxide signaling, and HDAC2 becomes constitutively active. This represses genes that muscle stem cells (satellite cells) and fibro-adipogenic progenitors need for regeneration, and pushes those progenitors toward fibrosis and fat. Givinostat inhibits class I and II HDACs. Increased histone acetylation reopens chromatin at regeneration genes, promoting muscle repair, reducing inflammation and limiting replacement of muscle by fat and scar tissue. It does not restore dystrophin, so it works across all DMD variants.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Slower loss of muscle function and less fat infiltration on MRI were seen in trials, reflecting improved regeneration and less fibrosis."], ["Blood platelets", "Thrombocytopenia is common and dose dependent, increasing bleeding risk; platelet counts are monitored."], ["Blood fats", "Triglycerides can rise, so they are checked during treatment."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting are common."], ["Heart", "QTc prolongation is possible, so ECG checks are done in people at risk."]],
  "journey": "Givinostat is taken by mouth twice daily with food as a liquid suspension. It peaks in about two to three hours and is highly protein bound. It is metabolized in the liver by several non-CYP pathways and has a half-life of about six hours. Metabolites leave in feces and urine. Muscle effects build over months.",
  "sidefx": "HDACs regulate genes in many cell types, not just muscle. In the bone marrow, HDAC inhibition reduces platelet production by interfering with megakaryocyte maturation, explaining thrombocytopenia, the most important side effect, along with possible anemia and neutropenia. Changes in lipid gene regulation can raise triglycerides. GI effects like diarrhea and vomiting are common. QTc prolongation can occur. Doses are reduced in steps for low platelets, diarrhea or high triglycerides. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Givinostat was first tested in blood disorders like polycythemia vera before finding a role in muscle disease, showing how one mechanism can apply to very different conditions.",
  "src": ["Duvyzat (givinostat) FDA label", "Mercuri E et al. Lancet Neurol 2024 (EPIDYS)", "Minetti GC et al. Nat Med 2006"]
},
"leniolisib": {
  "origin": "Activated PI3K-delta syndrome (APDS) was described in 2013 by two research groups, one in the United Kingdom and one at the US National Institutes of Health. They found that gain-of-function variants in PIK3CD (APDS1), and later loss-of-function variants in the regulatory subunit gene PIK3R1 (APDS2), make PI3K-delta overactive in immune cells. Patients have recurrent infections, enlarged lymph nodes and spleen, and a high risk of lymphoma. Leniolisib was discovered by Novartis as a selective PI3K-delta inhibitor, originally for autoimmune disease. NIH researchers tested it in APDS patients, and Pharming Group licensed and developed it. The FDA approved it as Joenja in March 2023, the first treatment aimed at the cause of APDS.",
  "timeline": [["2013", "APDS is described by researchers in the UK and at the NIH."], ["2010s", "Novartis develops leniolisib as a selective PI3K-delta inhibitor."], ["2017", "NIH study shows leniolisib reduces lymphoproliferation in APDS patients."], ["2023", "FDA approves leniolisib (Joenja) for APDS."]],
  "cell": "Phosphoinositide 3-kinase delta (PI3K-delta) is found mostly in white blood cells. It has a catalytic subunit, p110-delta (from PIK3CD), and a regulatory subunit, p85-alpha (from PIK3R1). When B and T cell receptors are activated, PI3K-delta converts PIP2 to PIP3 in the cell membrane. PIP3 recruits and activates AKT, which turns on mTOR and other signals that drive cell growth, survival and differentiation. In APDS, variants keep PI3K-delta switched on. Too much signaling pushes T cells to become exhausted senescent cells and drives B cells into abnormal differentiation, so few naive B cells and poor antibody responses result, along with lymphoproliferation. Leniolisib selectively inhibits p110-delta, lowering PIP3 and dampening AKT and mTOR signaling toward normal, which rebalances lymphocyte populations.",
  "body": [["Lymph nodes", "Overactive lymphocytes enlarge lymph nodes; inhibiting PI3K-delta shrinks them."], ["Spleen", "Spleen size decreases as lymphoproliferation calms."], ["B cells", "Naive B cells increase as signaling normalizes, which may improve antibody responses."], ["T cells", "Fewer senescent and exhausted T cells form."], ["Blood counts", "Neutropenia can occur, so white blood cell counts are monitored during treatment."], ["Skin and gut", "Headache, sinusitis, atopic dermatitis and diarrhea were reported in trials."]],
  "journey": "Leniolisib is taken by mouth twice daily, about 12 hours apart. It is absorbed quickly, peaking about one hour after a dose, and is about 94% protein bound. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers are avoided. Its half-life is about seven hours. It leaves in feces and urine.",
  "sidefx": "Leniolisib is generally well tolerated in APDS, where it corrects overactive signaling rather than suppressing normal immunity. Headache, sinusitis and atopic dermatitis were common. Neutropenia has been reported. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Older, less selective PI3K-delta inhibitors used in cancer caused severe colitis, liver injury and infections, but these were not prominent in the APDS trial, likely because of the different patient group and targeted use. Animal studies showed fetal harm, so pregnancy is avoided. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Leniolisib is a rare case of a targeted drug designed to correct a specific inherited signaling defect, essentially turning down an immune pathway that a gene variant left stuck in the on position.",
  "src": ["Joenja (leniolisib) FDA label", "Rao VK et al. Blood 2017", "Angulo I et al. Science 2013"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"pegunigalsidase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Fabry disease was described in 1898, separately by the dermatologist Johannes Fabry in Germany and William Anderson in England, who both saw children with dark red skin spots called angiokeratomas. Decades later the cause was traced to a missing lysosomal enzyme, alpha-galactosidase A, coded on the X chromosome. The first replacement enzymes, made in animal or human cell lines, arrived in the early 2000s. Pegunigalsidase alfa took a different route. The Israeli company Protalix BioTherapeutics grew the human enzyme in plant cells (tobacco cell culture) in steel bioreactors, then chemically linked the two halves of the enzyme together with polyethylene glycol (PEG). The goal was an enzyme that is more stable and stays in the blood longer. Developed with Chiesi, it was approved by the FDA in 2023 as Elfabrio for adults with Fabry disease.",
  "timeline": [["1898", "Johannes Fabry and William Anderson independently describe the disease."], ["1967", "The missing enzyme activity in Fabry disease is identified as a lysosomal alpha-galactosidase defect."], ["2003", "The first enzyme replacement therapy for Fabry disease is approved in the US."], ["2023", "FDA approves pegunigalsidase alfa (Elfabrio), a plant cell-made, PEGylated enzyme."]],
  "cell": "In Fabry disease, cells lack working alpha-galactosidase A, the lysosomal enzyme that clips the last galactose sugar off globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) and related lipids. Gb3 and its breakdown product lyso-Gb3 pile up inside lysosomes of blood vessel lining cells, kidney podocytes, heart muscle cells and nerves. Pegunigalsidase alfa is a replacement copy of the enzyme. Two enzyme units are cross-linked by PEG into a stable dimer, which slows clearance from blood. Cells take up the enzyme by endocytosis and route it to lysosomes, where the acidic pH suits its activity. There it hydrolyzes the terminal alpha-galactosyl bond, so Gb3 is broken down and storage slowly falls. Less storage means less damage to small vessels and kidney cells over time.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "Clearing Gb3 from glomerular cells and capillary lining is meant to slow the loss of kidney filtration (eGFR) that drives Fabry kidney failure."], ["Blood vessels", "Endothelial cells hold less lipid, which may improve small vessel function in skin, kidney, heart and brain."], ["Heart", "Gb3 stored in heart muscle contributes to thickening and rhythm problems; lowering storage aims to slow that process."], ["Nerves", "Small fiber nerves damaged by storage cause burning pain in the hands and feet; enzyme therapy may lessen this over time."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign protein given by vein, it can trigger infusion reactions and anti-drug antibodies."]],
  "journey": "Pegunigalsidase alfa is given as an intravenous infusion because a protein would be digested if swallowed. The PEG coat and cross-linking help it resist breakdown, so it circulates far longer than older Fabry enzymes, with a half-life of roughly 80 hours. This lets it reach tissues over several days between infusions. It is taken into cells and lysosomes, and in the end it is broken down by normal protein-digesting pathways into peptides and amino acids. It is not excreted by the kidneys as active enzyme.",
  "sidefx": "Because the body sees the enzyme as foreign, the immune system can react. Infusion-associated reactions (fever, chills, rash, flushing) come from immune activation during or soon after the drip. True allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur, which is why the label carries a boxed warning and resuscitation support is kept ready. Some patients form anti-drug antibodies that may affect response. A form of immune-complex kidney inflammation (membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis) was reported in trials. There is no antidote; reactions are handled by slowing or stopping the infusion and giving standard allergy treatment.",
  "fun": "Elfabrio is one of very few human medicines made in plant cells. Protalix's earlier plant-made enzyme, taliglucerase alfa for Gaucher disease, was the first plant cell-produced protein drug approved by the FDA, in 2012.",
  "src": ["Elfabrio (pegunigalsidase alfa-iwxj) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"olipudase-alfa": {
  "origin": "In 1914 the German pediatrician Albert Niemann described an infant with a huge liver and spleen who died young. The pathologist Ludwig Pick later studied the tissue and showed a stored lipid, and the condition became known as Niemann-Pick disease. In 1966 Roscoe Brady and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health showed that the missing piece in types A and B was the enzyme acid sphingomyelinase. Today these forms are called acid sphingomyelinase deficiency (ASMD). Researchers, with Genzyme and later Sanofi, made a recombinant copy of the human enzyme in cultured cells. Animal studies showed a key danger: breaking down large stores of sphingomyelin too fast creates ceramide, which is toxic. That led to a slow dose-escalation plan. Olipudase alfa (Xenpozyme) was approved in Japan in early 2022 and by the FDA in August 2022 as the first treatment for the non-brain signs of ASMD.",
  "timeline": [["1914", "Albert Niemann describes the infantile disease."], ["1966", "Roscoe Brady's NIH group links Niemann-Pick disease to acid sphingomyelinase deficiency."], ["2022", "FDA approves olipudase alfa (Xenpozyme), the first therapy for ASMD."]],
  "cell": "Acid sphingomyelinase lives in lysosomes, where it splits sphingomyelin, a membrane lipid, into ceramide and phosphocholine. When the SMPD1 gene is faulty, sphingomyelin builds up in macrophages and other cells, forming foamy lipid-laden cells in the spleen, liver, lungs and bone marrow. Olipudase alfa carries mannose-6-phosphate tags on its sugar chains. These bind mannose-6-phosphate receptors on cell surfaces, so cells pull the enzyme in and deliver it to lysosomes. Inside, it breaks down the stored sphingomyelin. As storage clears, the spleen and liver shrink, lung gas exchange improves and blood lipids move toward normal. The enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not treat the brain disease of type A.",
  "body": [["Spleen and liver", "Clearing stored lipid from macrophages shrinks the enlarged spleen and liver, which can ease abdominal fullness and low platelet counts from splenic trapping."], ["Lungs", "Less lipid in lung macrophages and tissue improves the lungs' ability to move oxygen into blood, measured as diffusing capacity (DLco)."], ["Blood lipids", "Patients often have high LDL and triglycerides with low HDL; treatment tends to improve this pattern."], ["Liver tests", "Rapid breakdown of stored lipid can release ceramide and cause short-lived rises in liver enzymes after infusions."], ["Brain", "Because the enzyme does not cross into the brain, neurologic disease is not treated."]],
  "journey": "Olipudase alfa is given by intravenous infusion, since a protein would be digested in the gut. From the blood it is taken up mainly by the liver, spleen and other tissues rich in macrophages, through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Like other proteins it is eventually broken down into peptides and amino acids rather than cleared intact by the kidneys. Dosing starts low and rises in steps over weeks to let stored lipid be broken down gradually.",
  "sidefx": "The most important risks come from two sources. First, the immune system may react to a foreign protein, giving infusion reactions (headache, fever, hives, nausea) and rarely anaphylaxis, which is the reason for the boxed warning. Second, clearing a large store of sphingomyelin produces ceramide and other breakdown products that can stress cells, which explains liver enzyme rises within a day or two after infusions and why doses are escalated slowly with liver tests checked. Animal studies showed harm to the fetus. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the infusion and supportive care.",
  "fun": "The dangers of fast lipid breakdown were seen first in ASMD mice given high enzyme doses, which is why Xenpozyme has one of the slowest step-up schedules of any enzyme therapy.",
  "src": ["Xenpozyme (olipudase alfa-rpcp) FDA label", "Brady RO et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1966"]
},
"velmanase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Alpha-mannosidosis was first described in 1967 by the Swedish physician Per-Arne Ockerman, who studied a boy with coarse facial features, hearing loss and learning problems and found mannose-rich sugars stored in his tissues. The cause is a missing lysosomal enzyme, alpha-mannosidase, coded by the MAN2B1 gene. Researchers in Europe made a recombinant copy of the human enzyme and showed it lowered stored sugars in a mouse model. The program was developed by the Italian company Chiesi. Velmanase alfa (Lamzede) was approved in the European Union in 2018 and by the FDA in February 2023 for the non-brain signs of alpha-mannosidosis, making it the first approved treatment for this very rare disease.",
  "timeline": [["1967", "Per-Arne Ockerman describes alpha-mannosidosis."], ["2018", "Velmanase alfa is approved in the European Union."], ["2023", "FDA approves velmanase alfa (Lamzede) for non-CNS signs of alpha-mannosidosis."]],
  "cell": "Lysosomal alpha-mannosidase trims mannose sugars from the ends of glycoproteins being recycled inside lysosomes. Without it, mannose-rich oligosaccharides build up in lysosomes in many tissues, swelling cells and disturbing their function. Velmanase alfa is a recombinant human enzyme that carries mannose-6-phosphate on its sugar chains. Cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and send it to lysosomes. There it hydrolyzes alpha-linked mannose bonds, so the stored oligosaccharides can be broken down and cleared. Blood oligosaccharide levels fall, which is the main lab marker of response. The enzyme does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so it is not expected to treat the brain features.",
  "body": [["Muscles and endurance", "Less storage in tissues was linked in trials with modest gains in walking and stair-climbing endurance."], ["Lungs", "Some patients showed better lung function measures, though gains were modest."], ["Immune system", "Alpha-mannosidosis causes frequent infections from weak immune function; reducing storage may help, and as a foreign protein the drug can also trigger antibodies."], ["Blood markers", "Serum oligosaccharides drop as the stored sugars are broken down."], ["Brain and hearing", "Because the enzyme does not reach the brain, learning problems and hearing loss are not expected to improve."]],
  "journey": "Velmanase alfa is given as a weekly intravenous infusion because a protein cannot survive digestion. It travels in the blood and is pulled into cells of the liver, spleen and other tissues by mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it works until it is broken down by normal protein-recycling pathways into peptides and amino acids. It is not removed by the kidneys as active enzyme.",
  "sidefx": "The main safety issue is the immune system reacting to a foreign protein. Infusion-associated reactions and true hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis in about 5 percent of trial patients, explain the boxed warning and the readiness to stop the infusion and treat. Premedication with antihistamines, fever reducers or steroids may be considered. Anti-drug antibodies can form. Animal data raise concern for harm to a fetus. There is no specific antidote; reactions are treated with standard allergy care.",
  "src": ["Lamzede (velmanase alfa-tycv) FDA label", "Ockerman PA. Lancet 1967"]
},
"palovarotene": {
  "origin": "Fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva (FOP) is a rare genetic disease in which muscle, tendon and ligament slowly turn into bone, often after small injuries or flare-ups. In 2006 Eileen Shore, Frederick Kaplan and colleagues at the University of Pennsylvania found the cause: a single mutation (R206H) in the ACVR1 gene, a bone morphogenetic protein (BMP) receptor. Separate work by Maurizio Pacifici, Masahiro Iwamoto and colleagues showed that drugs activating the retinoic acid receptor gamma could block cartilage formation and prevent extra bone in mice. Palovarotene had originally been developed by Roche for emphysema. Clementia Pharmaceuticals licensed it for FOP, and Ipsen acquired Clementia in 2019. The FDA approved palovarotene (Sohonos) in August 2023 as the first treatment for FOP, to reduce new heterotopic bone in adults and children of certain ages.",
  "timeline": [["2006", "Shore, Kaplan and colleagues identify the ACVR1 R206H mutation in FOP."], ["2011", "Mouse studies show RAR-gamma agonists can prevent heterotopic ossification."], ["2019", "Ipsen acquires Clementia and the palovarotene program."], ["2023", "FDA approves palovarotene (Sohonos), the first FOP therapy."]],
  "cell": "In FOP the ACVR1 (ALK2) receptor carries a gain-of-function mutation, so it overreacts to signals, including activin A, and sends too much signal through the SMAD1/5/8 pathway. This tells soft tissue progenitor cells to become cartilage, which then turns into bone in the same way as normal bone growth (endochondral ossification). Palovarotene is a synthetic retinoid that selectively activates retinoic acid receptor gamma (RAR-gamma), a nuclear receptor. Activated RAR-gamma changes gene transcription and dampens BMP-SMAD signaling. It blocks the early step where progenitor cells become cartilage. Without that cartilage template, less new heterotopic bone forms during and after flare-ups.",
  "body": [["Muscle and soft tissue", "By blocking the cartilage stage, it reduces the amount of new extra bone that forms in muscles, tendons and ligaments."], ["Growth plates", "Retinoid signaling also acts on normal growth cartilage, which can close growth plates early in children who are still growing."], ["Skin and lips", "Like other retinoids, it causes dry skin, chapped lips, peeling, itching and hair loss from effects on skin cell turnover."], ["Spine and bone", "Lower bone density and vertebral fractures were seen, reflecting retinoid effects on normal bone."], ["Eyes", "Night blindness can occur because retinoids interfere with vitamin A use in the retina."], ["Mood", "Depression and other psychiatric effects have been reported with retinoids."]],
  "journey": "Palovarotene is taken by mouth, usually with food, and reaches peak levels in about 4 hours. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or inducers change its levels. Its half-life is about 9 hours, and it leaves the body mainly in feces. Its effect on new bone formation appears over weeks to months.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects are classic retinoid effects. Activating retinoic acid receptors in skin and mucous membranes causes dryness, peeling and hair loss. The same pathway that blocks abnormal cartilage also acts on normal growth plate cartilage, which explains early growth plate closure in children and the boxed warning. Retinoids are powerful teratogens because retinoic acid controls embryo patterning, so pregnancy must be prevented. Retinoid effects on bone explain lower bone density and vertebral fractures, and vitamin A pathway interference explains night blindness. There is no antidote; side effects lessen after stopping.",
  "fun": "The FOP gene was found by studying families, but the field was driven for decades by Frederick Kaplan, who kept a registry of patients around the world. The disease is sometimes called the stone man syndrome.",
  "src": ["Sohonos (palovarotene) FDA label", "Shore EM et al. Nature Genetics 2006"]
},
"mavorixafor": {
  "origin": "WHIM syndrome is named for its four features: warts, hypogammaglobulinemia, infections and myelokathexis (white cells trapped in the bone marrow). In 2003 researchers found that it is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the chemokine receptor CXCR4. CXCR4 drugs had first been studied for HIV, because some HIV strains use CXCR4 to enter cells. Mavorixafor began as AMD070, an oral CXCR4 blocker made at the Canadian company AnorMED, which also created the injectable CXCR4 blocker plerixafor. X4 Pharmaceuticals later developed mavorixafor for WHIM syndrome. The FDA approved it in April 2024 as Xolremdi, the first treatment approved for WHIM syndrome, to raise circulating neutrophils and lymphocytes.",
  "timeline": [["2003", "Gain-of-function CXCR4 mutations are identified as the cause of WHIM syndrome."], ["2000s", "AnorMED develops oral CXCR4 antagonists, including AMD070 (later mavorixafor)."], ["2024", "FDA approves mavorixafor (Xolremdi) for WHIM syndrome."]],
  "cell": "CXCR4 is a G protein-coupled chemokine receptor on white blood cells. Its ligand, CXCL12 (also called SDF-1), is made in large amounts by bone marrow stromal cells and holds cells in the marrow. Normally, after activation, CXCR4 is switched off and pulled into the cell, which lets mature neutrophils leave the marrow. In WHIM syndrome, mutations cut off the receptor's tail, so it cannot be shut off and keeps signaling. Mature neutrophils and lymphocytes stay trapped in the marrow. Mavorixafor binds CXCR4 and blocks CXCL12 binding. With the retention signal reduced, neutrophils and lymphocytes are released into the bloodstream, where they can fight infection.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Blocking the CXCR4 hold signal frees mature white cells that were trapped, correcting myelokathexis."], ["Blood", "Absolute neutrophil and lymphocyte counts rise in the circulation."], ["Immune defense", "More circulating white cells lowered infection rates in the pivotal trial."], ["Heart", "Higher blood levels can prolong the QT interval, so heart rhythm effects are monitored."], ["Skin", "Rash and skin conditions were among reported side effects; warts from HPV are part of the disease itself."]],
  "journey": "Mavorixafor is a capsule taken by mouth once daily on an empty stomach. It reaches peak levels in about 3 hours and is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down, mainly through CYP3A4, and it also inhibits CYP2D6, which can raise levels of drugs cleared by that enzyme. Its half-life is long, about 3 days, and it leaves the body mostly in feces. Neutrophil counts rise within weeks.",
  "sidefx": "QT prolongation is concentration-dependent, so drugs that raise mavorixafor levels (strong CYP3A4 inhibitors) and low potassium increase the risk of a dangerous rhythm. Animal studies showed harm to the fetus, likely because CXCR4 and CXCL12 guide cell movement during development, so contraception is used. Low platelets, rash, nosebleeds, runny nose and dizziness were reported. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "WHIM syndrome is one of the few diseases in which a single patient's cure was traced to chance: researchers at NIH described a woman whose WHIM syndrome disappeared after one blood stem cell lost the mutant CXCR4 gene through chromothripsis, a shattering of a chromosome.",
  "src": ["Xolremdi (mavorixafor) FDA label", "Hernandez PA et al. Nature Genetics 2003"]
},
"sebetralstat": {
  "origin": "Hereditary angioedema (HAE) is a genetic disease in which a lack of working C1 esterase inhibitor lets plasma kallikrein run unchecked, releasing bradykinin and causing sudden swelling of the skin, gut and airway. For years, on-demand treatments had to be injected or infused during an attack. The British-American company KalVista Pharmaceuticals set out to make a small-molecule plasma kallikrein inhibitor that could be swallowed. Sebetralstat came from that program. In the KONFIDENT trial, it sped symptom relief compared with placebo. The FDA approved sebetralstat (Ekterly) in 2025 as the first oral on-demand treatment for HAE attacks in adults and adolescents.",
  "timeline": [["1888", "William Osler describes a hereditary form of angioedema in families."], ["1963", "Virginia Donaldson links HAE to a lack of C1 esterase inhibitor."], ["2025", "FDA approves sebetralstat (Ekterly), the first oral on-demand HAE therapy."]],
  "cell": "In HAE, low or faulty C1 esterase inhibitor fails to restrain the contact (kallikrein-kinin) system. Factor XII becomes activated and turns prekallikrein into plasma kallikrein. Kallikrein then cuts high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin, and it also activates more factor XII in a feedback loop. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on blood vessel lining cells, raising nitric oxide and prostaglandins and opening gaps between endothelial cells, so fluid leaks into tissues. Sebetralstat is a small molecule that fits into the active site of plasma kallikrein and blocks it competitively and reversibly. Bradykinin release stops, the feedback loop is broken, and swelling stops progressing and begins to resolve.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Stopping bradykinin release halts the spread of swelling in the face, hands, feet and genitals."], ["Gut", "Abdominal attacks from bowel wall swelling cause severe pain and vomiting; blocking kallikrein shortens these attacks."], ["Airway", "Laryngeal swelling can block breathing; a dose does not replace emergency care because the airway can close before the drug takes effect."], ["Blood vessels", "Less B2 receptor signaling reduces vessel leakiness."], ["Nervous system", "Headache was the most common side effect in trials."]],
  "journey": "Sebetralstat is a tablet taken by mouth at the first sign of an attack. It is absorbed quickly and reaches peak levels in about an hour, which supports rapid action. The liver breaks it down, mainly by CYP3A4, so strong CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them. Its half-life is short, a matter of hours, which fits an on-demand drug used only during attacks.",
  "sidefx": "Sebetralstat was well tolerated in trials, with headache the most common side effect. At exposures well above normal it can cause a small increase in the QT interval. The biggest danger is not the drug but the disease: a throat attack can block the airway, so emergency care is still needed after a dose. Drug interactions through CYP3A4 can change exposure. There is no specific antidote.",
  "src": ["Ekterly (sebetralstat) FDA label", "Riedl MA et al. KONFIDENT trial, NEJM 2024"]
},
"ecallantide": {
  "origin": "Ecallantide was found by phage display, a lab method in which billions of protein variants are displayed on the surface of viruses that infect bacteria, then fished out by how tightly they bind a target. Scientists at Dyax Corporation in Massachusetts started with a Kunitz domain, a small protease-blocking module from a human protein, and screened variants for binding to plasma kallikrein. The winner was a 60-amino-acid protein that blocks kallikrein very tightly. It is produced in the yeast Pichia pastoris. In December 2009 the FDA approved ecallantide (Kalbitor) for acute attacks of hereditary angioedema, one of the first targeted on-demand HAE drugs in the US. Because of anaphylaxis risk, it must be given by a health professional.",
  "timeline": [["1985", "George Smith describes phage display, later recognized with a Nobel Prize in 2018."], ["1990s", "Dyax uses phage display to engineer a Kunitz-domain kallikrein inhibitor."], ["2009", "FDA approves ecallantide (Kalbitor) for acute HAE attacks."]],
  "cell": "In hereditary angioedema, C1 esterase inhibitor is missing or faulty, so plasma kallikrein is not held in check. Kallikrein cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin, the peptide that causes swelling. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on endothelial cells, triggering nitric oxide and prostaglandin release and loosening cell junctions, so plasma leaks into tissue. Ecallantide is a Kunitz-domain protein that slots into the active site of plasma kallikrein and blocks it potently and reversibly. With kallikrein inhibited, bradykinin production falls, vessel leak slows and the attack stops growing.",
  "body": [["Skin and soft tissue", "Blocking bradykinin release stops swelling of the face, limbs and genitals from spreading."], ["Gut", "Bowel wall swelling causes intense abdominal pain; ecallantide shortens these attacks."], ["Airway", "Throat swelling is life-threatening; drug effect takes time, so airway support may still be needed."], ["Blood clotting tests", "Kallikrein takes part in the contact pathway of clotting tests, so ecallantide can prolong the aPTT in the lab without causing bleeding."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign protein made in yeast, it can trigger antibodies and allergic reactions."]],
  "journey": "Ecallantide is injected under the skin, usually as several injections during an attack. It reaches peak blood levels in about 2 to 3 hours. As a small protein it is broken down by proteases and partly filtered by the kidneys. Its half-life is about 2 hours, which fits its on-demand use.",
  "sidefx": "The key risk is anaphylaxis, seen in about 4 percent of patients in trials, which led to a boxed warning and the rule that it is given only by a health professional ready to treat allergic reactions. The immune system recognizes ecallantide as foreign, and people who form antibodies after repeated doses may have higher risk. Anaphylaxis can look like an HAE attack, which makes it hard to recognize. Headache, nausea, fatigue, fever and injection site reactions are common. A prolonged aPTT is a lab effect, not true bleeding. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine and supportive care.",
  "src": ["Kalbitor (ecallantide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"garadacimab": {
  "origin": "Factor XII was discovered in 1955 when the hematologist Oscar Ratnoff studied a railroad worker named John Hageman. His blood clotted very slowly in a test tube, yet he had no bleeding problem. The missing protein was named Hageman factor, or factor XII. Later research showed that activated factor XII starts the contact system that leads to bradykinin release, the cause of swelling in hereditary angioedema. Because people born without factor XII do not bleed, it looked like a safe target. Researchers working with CSL Behring developed fully human antibodies against activated factor XII using phage display. Garadacimab (Andembry) was approved by the FDA in 2025 for preventing HAE attacks, the first therapy to target factor XIIa.",
  "timeline": [["1955", "Oscar Ratnoff describes Hageman factor (factor XII) in patient John Hageman."], ["2010s", "Fully human anti-factor XIIa antibodies are developed with CSL Behring."], ["2025", "FDA approves garadacimab (Andembry) for HAE prophylaxis."]],
  "cell": "In hereditary angioedema, low C1 esterase inhibitor lets the contact system run unchecked. It starts when factor XII contacts negatively charged surfaces and becomes activated factor XII (FXIIa). FXIIa converts prekallikrein to plasma kallikrein, which cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin. Kallikrein also activates more factor XII, creating a loop. Bradykinin acts on B2 receptors to make blood vessels leaky, causing swelling. Garadacimab is a fully human IgG4 monoclonal antibody that binds the catalytic (protease) domain of FXIIa and blocks its activity. This shuts the cascade at its first step, so less kallikrein and bradykinin are made and attacks become less frequent.",
  "body": [["Skin, gut and airway", "Blocking the very top of the bradykinin cascade lowers how often swelling attacks occur in all affected areas."], ["Blood clotting tests", "The aPTT test depends on factor XII, so results are prolonged by the drug even though bleeding does not increase."], ["Clotting in the body", "People who lack factor XII do not bleed abnormally, which is why blocking it does not cause bleeding in normal life."], ["Injection site", "Monthly injections can cause local redness or pain."]],
  "journey": "Garadacimab is injected under the skin once a month, often with a prefilled pen. As a large antibody it is absorbed slowly through the lymph into the blood. It is recycled by the neonatal Fc receptor, which protects it from breakdown and gives a long half-life of about 17 days. Like other antibodies, it is eventually broken down into peptides and amino acids, not cleared by the kidneys or liver enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Garadacimab is generally well tolerated. Injection site reactions, sore throat and cold symptoms and abdominal pain were the most common effects. Because the aPTT test relies on factor XII, the drug makes this lab result look abnormal, which could be misread as a bleeding risk. Hypersensitivity is possible with any protein drug. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "John Hageman, the man whose blood led to the discovery of factor XII, died in 1968 of a pulmonary embolism, a blood clot, despite lacking the clotting factor named after him.",
  "src": ["Andembry (garadacimab-gxii) FDA label", "Ratnoff OD, Colopy JE. J Clin Invest 1955"]
},
"donidalorsen": {
  "origin": "Donidalorsen comes from Ionis Pharmaceuticals in California, a pioneer of antisense drugs. Antisense oligonucleotides are short, chemically modified strands of DNA-like material designed to stick to a single messenger RNA and get it destroyed. Ionis chose prekallikrein, the protein made in the liver that becomes plasma kallikrein, the enzyme that drives bradykinin release in hereditary angioedema. To steer the drug to the liver, they attached a GalNAc sugar cluster that binds a receptor found almost only on liver cells. This makes the drug more potent, so it can be given less often. In trials, injections every 4 or 8 weeks greatly reduced attacks. The FDA approved donidalorsen (Dawnzera) in 2025 for preventing HAE attacks.",
  "timeline": [["1978", "Paul Zamecnik and Mary Stephenson show a short synthetic DNA strand can block viral RNA, the founding antisense idea."], ["2010s", "GalNAc conjugation is developed to deliver oligonucleotides to liver cells."], ["2025", "FDA approves donidalorsen (Dawnzera) for HAE prophylaxis."]],
  "cell": "Prekallikrein is made by liver cells from the KLKB1 gene and released into blood. In HAE, low C1 esterase inhibitor allows prekallikrein to be converted freely to kallikrein, which releases bradykinin and causes swelling. Donidalorsen is a single-stranded antisense oligonucleotide joined to a GalNAc cluster. The GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, so the drug is pulled into liver cells. Inside, it pairs with the KLKB1 messenger RNA by base pairing. The DNA-RNA hybrid is recognized by RNase H1, an enzyme that cuts the RNA strand. With the message destroyed, liver cells make far less prekallikrein. Lower prekallikrein in blood means less kallikrein and bradykinin can form, so attacks occur less often.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Liver cells take up the drug and reduce their production of prekallikrein."], ["Blood", "Plasma prekallikrein levels fall over weeks, lowering the supply for bradykinin generation."], ["Skin, gut and airway", "With less bradykinin, swelling attacks in all these sites become much less frequent."], ["Platelets and liver tests", "Small drops in platelet counts and mild liver enzyme rises were seen in trials, a pattern known from antisense drugs."], ["Injection site", "Redness or discomfort can occur where the drug is injected."]],
  "journey": "Donidalorsen is injected under the skin. It is absorbed within hours and quickly leaves the blood for the liver, helped by its GalNAc tag, so plasma levels fall fast in the first hours. It then stays in liver tissue for a long time, with a terminal half-life of about a month, which supports dosing every 4 or 8 weeks. It is broken down by nucleases into shorter fragments, which leave in the urine. It is not processed by CYP enzymes.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site reactions come from local inflammation as the oligonucleotide is absorbed. Hypersensitivity, including anaphylaxis, can occur. Small platelet declines and mild liver enzyme increases have been linked to oligonucleotide drugs in general, reflecting their uptake by liver and interaction with proteins in blood. Because it works by lowering protein production, its effect builds and fades slowly over weeks. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Dawnzera (donidalorsen) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"cipaglucosidase-alfa": {
  "origin": "In 1932 the Dutch pathologist J. C. Pompe described a baby girl who died with a massively enlarged heart packed with glycogen. In 1963 the Belgian biochemist Henri-Gery Hers showed the cause: a missing lysosomal enzyme, acid alpha-glucosidase (GAA). Pompe disease thus became the first known lysosomal storage disease. The first enzyme replacement, alglucosidase alfa, was approved in 2006. Amicus Therapeutics designed cipaglucosidase alfa to carry more bis-mannose-6-phosphate sugar tags, so muscle cells could pull in more enzyme. They paired it with miglustat, a small molecule that binds and stabilizes the enzyme in the blood before it reaches muscle. The FDA approved cipaglucosidase alfa (Pombiliti) with miglustat (Opfolda) in September 2023 for adults with late-onset Pompe disease.",
  "timeline": [["1932", "J. C. Pompe describes glycogen storage in the heart of an infant."], ["1963", "Henri-Gery Hers identifies acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency, the first lysosomal storage disease defect."], ["2006", "The first Pompe enzyme therapy, alglucosidase alfa, is approved."], ["2023", "FDA approves cipaglucosidase alfa (Pombiliti) with miglustat (Opfolda)."]],
  "cell": "Acid alpha-glucosidase breaks the alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 bonds of glycogen inside lysosomes, releasing glucose. In Pompe disease, glycogen builds up in lysosomes of skeletal, heart and breathing muscles. Swollen lysosomes and blocked autophagy damage the muscle fibers. Cipaglucosidase alfa is a recombinant human GAA made with many bis-mannose-6-phosphate glycans. These bind tightly to the cation-independent mannose-6-phosphate receptor on muscle cells, which carries the enzyme into lysosomes. There it is activated and digests glycogen. Miglustat, taken by mouth before the infusion, binds the enzyme in blood and helps keep it folded and active at neutral pH until it is taken up. Less glycogen means less muscle damage and better strength and breathing.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Clearing glycogen from limb muscles helped stabilize or improve walking distance in late-onset disease."], ["Breathing muscles", "The diaphragm is often weak in Pompe disease; enzyme therapy aims to protect lung function, measured as forced vital capacity."], ["Heart", "Heart muscle storage is mainly a problem in infant-onset disease; this product is approved for late-onset disease."], ["Immune system", "As a foreign protein, it can trigger infusion reactions and anti-drug antibodies."], ["Pregnancy", "Miglustat can harm a fetus, so contraception is needed during treatment."]],
  "journey": "Miglustat capsules are swallowed about an hour before the infusion, timed so their peak meets the enzyme in the blood. Cipaglucosidase alfa is then given by intravenous infusion every two weeks. The enzyme has a short plasma half-life of a few hours because muscle and other cells take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside cells it is eventually broken down into peptides and amino acids. Miglustat, a small molecule, is mostly excreted unchanged by the kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Infusion reactions and anaphylaxis come from the immune system reacting to the foreign enzyme, which is why the label has a boxed warning and medical support is kept ready. People with weak heart or lung function can be harmed by the infusion volume and the stress of reactions, leading to acute cardiorespiratory failure. Anti-drug antibodies can form. Miglustat is linked to fetal harm in animals and can cause diarrhea, since it also inhibits gut sugar-digesting enzymes. There is no antidote; reactions are managed by stopping the infusion and supportive care.",
  "src": ["Pombiliti (cipaglucosidase alfa-atga) FDA label", "Opfolda (miglustat) FDA label", "Hers HG. Biochem J 1963"]
}
,
"vestronidase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Mucopolysaccharidosis type VII is often called Sly syndrome after William Sly, a pediatrician and biochemist at Saint Louis University, who described the first patient in 1973 and showed the missing enzyme was beta-glucuronidase. Sly's lab went on to help explain how cells capture lysosomal enzymes through the mannose-6-phosphate receptor, work that underpins all enzyme replacement therapy. A mouse model of MPS VII became a key test system for ERT and gene therapy. Ultragenyx Pharmaceutical developed a recombinant human beta-glucuronidase, vestronidase alfa. The FDA approved it as Mepsevii in November 2017, the first treatment for MPS VII, an ultra-rare disease with only a few hundred known patients worldwide.",
  "timeline": [["1973", "William Sly and colleagues describe beta-glucuronidase deficiency (MPS VII)."], ["1980s", "Sly's group and others define mannose-6-phosphate receptor uptake of lysosomal enzymes."], ["2017", "FDA approves vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii), the first MPS VII therapy."]],
  "cell": "Glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) such as dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate and chondroitin sulfate are long sugar chains that cells recycle in lysosomes. Beta-glucuronidase removes glucuronic acid residues from the ends of these chains, one of the steps needed to take them apart. In MPS VII the GUSB gene is faulty, so partly digested GAGs pile up in lysosomes in bones, joints, liver, spleen, heart valves and other tissues. Vestronidase alfa is a recombinant human beta-glucuronidase with mannose-6-phosphate tags. It binds mannose-6-phosphate receptors on cell surfaces and is carried to lysosomes, where it restores the missing step. GAG storage falls, and urine GAG levels drop, the main marker of response. It does not cross the blood-brain barrier.",
  "body": [["Liver and spleen", "Clearing stored GAGs reduces the enlargement of these organs seen in MPS VII."], ["Bones and joints", "GAG storage stiffens joints and deforms bone; enzyme therapy aims to slow this, though existing bone changes improve little."], ["Lungs and walking", "Some patients showed gains in walking distance and breathing measures in the trial."], ["Kidneys and urine", "Urinary GAG excretion falls as tissue storage clears."], ["Brain", "Because the enzyme does not cross into the brain, effects on learning and behavior are not established."]],
  "journey": "Vestronidase alfa is given as an intravenous infusion every two weeks. It leaves the blood quickly, with a plasma half-life of a few hours, because tissues such as the liver and spleen take it up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors. Inside lysosomes it works until normal protein turnover breaks it into peptides and amino acids. It is not removed by the kidneys as intact enzyme.",
  "sidefx": "The main danger is anaphylaxis, which can happen even with the first dose, so the label has a boxed warning and patients are watched during and for an hour after infusion. The immune system recognizes the enzyme as foreign, which also explains infusion reactions such as rash, itching and diarrhea and the formation of anti-drug antibodies. Premedication with an antihistamine is recommended. Leakage of the infusion into tissue can cause local reactions. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The beta-glucuronidase-deficient mouse, a natural model of Sly syndrome, was one of the first animals used to prove that enzyme replacement and gene therapy could correct a lysosomal disease.",
  "src": ["Mepsevii (vestronidase alfa-vjbk) FDA label", "Sly WS et al. J Pediatr 1973"]
},
"arimoclomol": {
  "origin": "Arimoclomol traces back to Hungarian research on hydroxylamine derivatives. The company Biorex developed bimoclomol and then arimoclomol in the 1990s as drugs that boost the heat shock response, the cell's built-in system for refolding damaged proteins. It was first studied for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), but it did not prove effective there. The Danish company Orphazyme then focused on Niemann-Pick disease type C (NPC), a rare inherited disease in which cholesterol and lipids are trapped in lysosomes and the brain degenerates. The program later passed to Zevra Therapeutics. In September 2024 the FDA approved arimoclomol (Miplyffa), used with miglustat, to treat the neurologic signs of NPC. It was the first drug approved in the US for NPC.",
  "timeline": [["1914", "Albert Niemann describes the first form of Niemann-Pick disease."], ["1997", "The NPC1 gene is identified as the cause of most NPC cases."], ["2024", "FDA approves arimoclomol (Miplyffa), with miglustat, the first US NPC therapy."]],
  "cell": "Most NPC cases are caused by mutations in NPC1, a membrane protein that moves cholesterol out of late endosomes and lysosomes. Many mutant NPC1 proteins misfold, are caught by quality control in the endoplasmic reticulum, and are destroyed before reaching the lysosome. The exact mechanism of arimoclomol is not fully understood. It is thought to prolong the activation of heat shock factor 1 (HSF1), a transcription factor switched on by cellular stress. Prolonged HSF1 activity raises production of heat shock proteins such as HSP70. These chaperones may help misfolded NPC1 fold properly and reach the lysosome, and HSP70 may also support lysosomal membrane health. In lab studies this reduced lysosomal unesterified cholesterol. It acts as an amplifier: it boosts the stress response in stressed cells rather than turning it on in healthy ones.",
  "body": [["Brain and nerves", "In the trial, the neurologic decline of NPC (walking, swallowing, speech, fine motor skills) progressed more slowly with arimoclomol."], ["Kidneys", "It blocks the OCT2 transporter that secretes creatinine, so blood creatinine rises without a real loss of kidney function."], ["Skin and airway", "Hives and angioedema can occur as hypersensitivity reactions."], ["Gut and weight", "Diarrhea and weight loss were among the reported side effects."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies showed harm to the fetus."]],
  "journey": "Arimoclomol is taken by mouth as capsules three times a day, which can be opened and mixed with food or given through a feeding tube. It is absorbed quickly, reaching peak levels in about half an hour to 2 hours. It has low binding to plasma proteins. A small part is broken down by glucuronidation and minor oxidation, and most of the drug leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 4 hours, which explains the three-times-daily schedule.",
  "sidefx": "Hypersensitivity reactions such as hives and angioedema reflect immune reactions to the drug. Arimoclomol inhibits OCT2, the kidney transporter that secretes creatinine and also handles some drugs, so creatinine rises 10 to 20 percent early in therapy without a fall in true filtration, and levels of OCT2-handled drugs can rise. Animal data showed fetal toxicity. Respiratory infections, diarrhea and weight loss were also reported. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Miplyffa (arimoclomol) FDA label", "Carstea ED et al. Science 1997"]
},
"levacetylleucine": {
  "origin": "Levacetylleucine is the L form of N-acetyl-leucine, a slightly modified version of the amino acid leucine. A mixture of both forms, acetyl-DL-leucine, has been sold in France since the 1950s under the name Tanganil as a treatment for vertigo. In the 2010s doctors noticed that patients with cerebellar ataxias, including Niemann-Pick disease type C (NPC), seemed to improve on it. Researchers at Oxford University and the company IntraBio studied the compound and found that the L form carried the benefit. IntraBio ran trials of the pure L form in NPC. The FDA approved levacetylleucine (Aqneursa) in September 2024 for the neurologic signs of NPC in adults and children, and its label also covers ataxia in ataxia-telangiectasia.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "Acetyl-DL-leucine (Tanganil) is introduced in France for vertigo."], ["2010s", "Case series report improved ataxia in patients taking acetyl-DL-leucine."], ["2024", "FDA approves levacetylleucine (Aqneursa) for Niemann-Pick disease type C."]],
  "cell": "The mechanism is not fully understood. Plain leucine enters cells through the LAT1 amino acid transporter. Adding an acetyl group changes the molecule's charge so it is carried instead by monocarboxylate transporters such as MCT1, which lets it enter neurons more readily. Inside cells, it is deacetylated back to L-leucine. It is thought to improve glucose and energy (ATP) metabolism in affected neurons, support autophagy and lysosomal function, and reduce inflammation in the brain. In NPC, where cerebellar neurons slowly die from lysosomal lipid storage, these effects are proposed to help neurons work better, which shows up as better coordination and neurologic scores.",
  "body": [["Cerebellum and coordination", "In the trial, patients had better scores for ataxia and neurologic function, reflecting improved cerebellar output."], ["Brain energy", "It is proposed to normalize neuron energy metabolism that is disrupted in lysosomal disease."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, swallowing difficulty and vomiting were among reported side effects."], ["Airways", "Upper respiratory infections were reported in trials."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies suggest fetal harm, so contraception is used during treatment and briefly afterward."]],
  "journey": "Levacetylleucine is a powder (granules) taken by mouth after being mixed with water, juice or food, or given through a feeding tube. It is absorbed quickly, reaching peak levels in about an hour. It is carried into cells by monocarboxylate transporters and is deacetylated to L-leucine, which then joins normal amino acid metabolism. Its half-life is short, about an hour, so it is taken in divided doses through the day. It is a P-glycoprotein substrate, which can cause drug interactions.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are generally mild, with abdominal pain, vomiting and swallowing difficulty reflecting local effects in the gut. The main label warning is embryo-fetal toxicity seen in animals, so pregnancy is avoided. The D form of acetyl-leucine competes with the L form, so the racemic over-the-counter product and N-acetyl-D-leucine should not be combined with it. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Aqneursa (levacetylleucine) FDA label", "Bremova-Ertl T et al. NEJM 2024"]
},
"cevimeline": {
  "origin": "Cevimeline began in Israel as a candidate drug for Alzheimer's disease. In the 1980s the chemist Abraham Fisher and colleagues at the Israel Institute for Biological Research designed a rigid quinuclidine molecule, coded AF102B, to activate M1 muscarinic receptors in the brain and mimic acetylcholine lost in dementia. It did not become a dementia drug, but it was a strong stimulator of salivary glands. Developed further in Japan, it was approved by the FDA in 2000 as Evoxac to treat dry mouth in people with Sjogren syndrome, an autoimmune disease that attacks the salivary and tear glands.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Abraham Fisher's group designs AF102B as an M1 muscarinic agonist for Alzheimer's disease."], ["2000", "FDA approves cevimeline (Evoxac) for dry mouth in Sjogren syndrome."]],
  "cell": "Cevimeline is a direct agonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, with high affinity for the M1 and M3 subtypes. Salivary and tear gland acinar cells carry M3 receptors. When cevimeline binds, M3 activates the Gq protein, which turns on phospholipase C. This splits PIP2 into IP3 and diacylglycerol. IP3 releases calcium from internal stores, and the calcium rise opens chloride and potassium channels and moves water through aquaporins into the gland ducts. The result is more watery saliva and tears. In Sjogren syndrome, some glandular tissue survives, so stimulating the remaining cells increases secretion. Muscarinic receptors in sweat glands, gut, airways, eyes and heart are also stimulated, which explains its side effects.",
  "body": [["Salivary glands", "M3 stimulation increases saliva flow, easing dry mouth, difficulty swallowing and the dental decay that dry mouth causes."], ["Sweat glands", "Sympathetic cholinergic sweat glands use muscarinic receptors, so heavy sweating is the most common side effect."], ["Eyes", "Pupil constriction and changes in focusing muscle can blur vision, especially at night, and reduce depth perception."], ["Airways", "Muscarinic stimulation tightens bronchial smooth muscle and increases secretions, a concern in asthma or COPD."], ["Heart", "M2-like effects on the sinus and AV nodes can slow the heart rate and alter conduction."], ["Gut", "Increased gut motility and secretions cause nausea and diarrhea."]],
  "journey": "Cevimeline is a capsule taken by mouth, usually three times a day. It is absorbed well, with peak levels in about 1.5 to 2 hours, and its effect on saliva starts within about an hour. It binds little to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, mainly to sulfoxide products, and by glucuronidation. The metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Its half-life is about 5 hours. People who are poor CYP2D6 metabolizers, or who take CYP2D6 or CYP3A4 inhibitors, can have higher levels.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly every side effect is the muscarinic effect showing up in the wrong place. Sweat glands cause excess sweating and can lead to dehydration. The eye effects cause blurred night vision. Airway receptors cause bronchospasm in people with lung disease. Heart effects cause slow rate or conduction changes. Gut effects cause nausea and diarrhea. Smooth muscle stimulation can cause biliary or kidney stone colic. Severe cholinergic toxicity can be reversed with atropine, a muscarinic antagonist.",
  "src": ["Evoxac (cevimeline) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"dronedarone": {
  "origin": "Amiodarone, developed in Belgium in the 1960s, is one of the most effective drugs for keeping the heart in normal rhythm, but its iodine content and fat-loving nature cause thyroid, lung, liver and skin damage. Chemists at Sanofi set out to build a safer cousin. They removed the iodine atoms and added a methanesulfonamide group that makes the molecule less fat-soluble, giving it a much shorter half-life. The result was dronedarone. Early trials were mixed: the ANDROMEDA study in patients with severe heart failure was stopped early because more patients died on the drug. The ATHENA trial then showed fewer cardiovascular hospitalizations in atrial fibrillation patients. The FDA approved dronedarone (Multaq) in July 2009. The PALLAS trial in 2011 found harm in permanent atrial fibrillation, which led to a stronger boxed warning.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Amiodarone, the parent compound, is developed in Belgium."], ["2003", "The ANDROMEDA trial in severe heart failure is stopped early for excess deaths."], ["2009", "ATHENA results support FDA approval of dronedarone (Multaq)."], ["2011", "The PALLAS trial shows harm in permanent atrial fibrillation."]],
  "cell": "Dronedarone blocks many channels at once, like amiodarone. It blocks several potassium currents (IKr, IKs, IK1 and the acetylcholine-activated IKACh), which slows repolarization in phase 3 and prolongs the action potential and refractory period. It blocks the fast sodium current, which slows conduction, and the L-type calcium current, which slows the sinus and AV nodes. It also blocks alpha and beta adrenergic receptors in a noncompetitive way, reducing the effect of adrenaline on heart rate. Longer refractory periods make it harder for the chaotic reentry circuits of atrial fibrillation to sustain themselves, helping keep the heart in sinus rhythm, while AV nodal slowing controls rate if AF recurs.",
  "body": [["Atria", "Longer refractory periods in atrial muscle make it harder for atrial fibrillation to start or persist."], ["AV node", "Calcium channel and beta blockade slow AV conduction, keeping the ventricular rate lower during AF."], ["Ventricles", "Longer repolarization shows as a longer QT interval, with a small risk of torsades."], ["Heart pumping", "In decompensated or advanced heart failure, the drug's negative effects on the heart can worsen failure and increase death."], ["Kidneys", "It blocks tubular secretion of creatinine, causing a small creatinine rise without a true drop in kidney function."], ["Liver and lungs", "Rare but serious liver injury and lung inflammation have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Dronedarone is a tablet taken twice daily with meals; food raises absorption a few times over. Even so, much of the dose is removed on its first pass through the liver. It is more than 98 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP3A4, including to an active N-debutyl metabolite, so grapefruit and CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels sharply. It leaves mostly in feces. Its half-life is about 13 to 19 hours, far shorter than amiodarone's weeks, and full effect builds over days.",
  "sidefx": "In heart failure that is decompensated, or in permanent AF, the drug's blocking effects on the heart do more harm than good, doubling the risk of death, stroke and HF hospitalization, which is the boxed warning. Blocking IKr prolongs QT and can rarely trigger torsades. Nodal effects cause slow heart rates. Dronedarone inhibits CYP3A, CYP2D6 and P-glycoprotein, so digoxin, dabigatran and some statins rise. The creatinine bump comes from blocked tubular secretion. Liver injury and lung toxicity are rarer than with amiodarone because there is no iodine and less tissue build-up, but they still occur. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Multaq (dronedarone) FDA label", "Hohnloser SH et al. ATHENA trial, NEJM 2009", "Connolly SJ et al. PALLAS trial, NEJM 2011"]
},
"disopyramide": {
  "origin": "Disopyramide was developed at the US drug company G. D. Searle in the 1960s, when doctors were looking for alternatives to quinidine and procainamide. It was introduced in the US as Norpace in the late 1970s for ventricular arrhythmias. Doctors soon noticed its strong negative effect on heart contraction. That property, a problem in heart failure, became useful in hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, where a hypercontractile heart blocks its own outflow. Disopyramide became a long-standing off-label option for that condition. The CAST trial in 1989 changed its use: other class I drugs increased deaths after heart attack, and class I agents now carry a boxed warning limiting them to life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Disopyramide is developed at G. D. Searle."], ["1970s", "Disopyramide (Norpace) is introduced in the US."], ["1989", "The CAST trial shows excess deaths with class I antiarrhythmics after heart attack."]],
  "cell": "Disopyramide is a class Ia antiarrhythmic. It binds open fast voltage-gated sodium channels and blocks the inward sodium current of phase 0, with intermediate on-off kinetics. This slows how fast the electrical impulse spreads, which widens the QRS complex and slows conduction in atria, ventricles and Purkinje fibers. It also blocks the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr), which slows phase 3 repolarization and lengthens the action potential and QT interval. Longer refractory periods help stop reentry circuits. It also blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors strongly, and it lowers the strength of heart muscle contraction more than most antiarrhythmics.",
  "body": [["Ventricles and conduction", "Sodium channel blockade slows conduction and widens QRS; potassium blockade prolongs QT, suppressing reentrant arrhythmias."], ["Heart muscle strength", "Strong negative inotropy can worsen heart failure but reduces the outflow obstruction in obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."], ["Sinus and AV nodes", "Its anticholinergic effect can speed sinus rate and AV conduction, offsetting some direct slowing."], ["Bladder", "Muscarinic blockade relaxes the bladder detrusor, causing hesitancy and retention, especially in older men with large prostates."], ["Eyes and mouth", "Anticholinergic effects cause dry mouth, blurred vision and can provoke angle-closure glaucoma."], ["Blood sugar", "It can cause hypoglycemia, likely by stimulating insulin release from beta cells."]],
  "journey": "Disopyramide is taken by mouth as immediate- or controlled-release capsules and is absorbed well. It peaks in about 2 hours for the immediate form. Its binding to plasma proteins changes with concentration, so free drug rises more than expected at higher levels. The liver converts part of it by CYP3A4 to a mono-N-dealkylated metabolite with more anticholinergic effect. About half leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about 7 hours and longer in kidney disease.",
  "sidefx": "Anticholinergic effects (dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, blurred vision, glaucoma) come from muscarinic blockade. Its strong negative inotropy explains new or worse heart failure and low blood pressure. Potassium channel block prolongs QT and can cause torsades, and sodium channel block can widen QRS and cause ventricular arrhythmias, part of the class I proarrhythmia seen in CAST. Low blood sugar is a known effect. There is no specific antidote; sodium bicarbonate is used for sodium channel blocker toxicity with wide QRS, and magnesium for torsades.",
  "src": ["Norpace (disopyramide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology", "CAST Investigators. NEJM 1989"]
},
"mavacamten": {
  "origin": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common inherited heart disease, often caused by mutations in sarcomere proteins such as beta-myosin. Many of these mutations make the heart contract too hard. In the 2010s scientists at the San Francisco company MyoKardia, founded on work by heart muscle researchers including James Spudich, screened for small molecules that could gently turn down cardiac myosin. Their lead, MYK-461, prevented heart thickening in mice carrying HCM mutations, as reported in Science in 2016. That compound became mavacamten. The EXPLORER-HCM trial showed better exercise capacity and symptoms. Bristol Myers Squibb bought MyoKardia in 2020, and the FDA approved mavacamten (Camzyos) in April 2022 as the first cardiac myosin inhibitor, for symptomatic obstructive HCM.",
  "timeline": [["1990", "The first HCM-causing mutation, in the beta-myosin heavy chain gene, is reported."], ["2016", "MYK-461 is shown to suppress HCM development in mice."], ["2020", "EXPLORER-HCM trial results are published; BMS acquires MyoKardia."], ["2022", "FDA approves mavacamten (Camzyos), the first cardiac myosin inhibitor."]],
  "cell": "Heart muscle contracts when myosin heads bind actin, use ATP and pull the filaments past each other. Myosin heads can rest in a super-relaxed state, folded back on the thick filament, using little energy and not available to bind actin. HCM mutations tend to free too many heads, so too many cross-bridges form and the heart contracts too hard and relaxes poorly. Mavacamten binds cardiac beta-myosin and allosterically and reversibly inhibits its ATPase activity. It slows phosphate release and stabilizes the super-relaxed state, so fewer heads join cross-bridges. The heart contracts with less force, the thickened septum pulls less into the outflow tract, the left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) gradient falls, and filling pressures drop.",
  "body": [["Left ventricle", "Less hypercontractility lowers the LVOT obstruction gradient and lets the ventricle relax and fill better."], ["Heart pumping", "Ejection fraction falls; if it falls too far, systolic heart failure develops, which is why LVEF is checked by echo."], ["Exercise capacity", "Lower obstruction and filling pressures improved peak oxygen use and symptoms in trials."], ["Lungs", "Lower left heart filling pressures can reduce breathlessness from lung congestion."], ["Brain", "Dizziness and fainting can occur, sometimes from obstruction itself or excessive drop in output."]],
  "journey": "Mavacamten is a capsule taken by mouth once a day. It is absorbed quickly, peaking in about an hour, and is highly bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2C19, with help from CYP3A4 and CYP2C9. Its half-life is long, about 6 to 9 days, and about 23 days in people who are CYP2C19 poor metabolizers, so it takes weeks to reach steady state and its effect lasts weeks after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "The main risk follows directly from the mechanism: turning down myosin too far weakens contraction and causes systolic heart failure, which is the boxed warning. Illness such as infection or new atrial fibrillation can further reduce heart function. Because CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 inhibitors raise levels and inducers lower them, drug interactions are dangerous, so it is available only through a REMS program. It can harm a fetus and may reduce the effect of hormonal contraceptives. There is no antidote; the drug is held and the long half-life means recovery takes time.",
  "src": ["Camzyos (mavacamten) FDA label", "Green EM et al. Science 2016", "Olivotto I et al. EXPLORER-HCM, Lancet 2020"]
},
"vericiguat": {
  "origin": "In the 1980s Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad showed that nitric oxide (NO) is the signal that relaxes blood vessels, by activating the enzyme soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC). They shared the 1998 Nobel Prize. In heart failure, NO is often in short supply and sGC is less responsive. Bayer chemists, building on an earlier compound called YC-1, developed sGC stimulators that work even with little NO. The first, riociguat, was approved for pulmonary hypertension in 2013. Vericiguat was designed for once-daily use in heart failure. Developed by Bayer and Merck, it reduced the combined risk of cardiovascular death or HF hospitalization in the VICTORIA trial, and the FDA approved it as Verquvo in January 2021 for adults with HFrEF after a recent worsening event.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad receive the Nobel Prize for nitric oxide signaling."], ["2013", "Riociguat, the first sGC stimulator, is approved."], ["2020", "VICTORIA trial results are published."], ["2021", "FDA approves vericiguat (Verquvo) for heart failure."]],
  "cell": "Soluble guanylate cyclase is an enzyme inside vascular smooth muscle and heart muscle cells. When NO binds its heme group, sGC turns GTP into cyclic GMP (cGMP). cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and desensitizes the contractile machinery, so smooth muscle relaxes. In heart failure, oxidative stress and endothelial dysfunction reduce NO and the cGMP signal falls, which contributes to stiff vessels and heart muscle, fibrosis and remodeling. Vericiguat binds sGC at a site separate from the NO site. It directly stimulates the enzyme even without NO, and it stabilizes NO binding so the enzyme responds better to the NO that remains. More cGMP means vasodilation and may improve heart muscle relaxation and remodeling.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "More cGMP relaxes arteries and veins, lowering afterload and preload."], ["Heart", "Restoring cGMP signaling may reduce stiffness, hypertrophy and fibrosis in the failing heart, lowering HF hospitalizations."], ["Blood pressure", "Vasodilation causes a modest fall in blood pressure and sometimes symptomatic hypotension."], ["Blood", "Anemia was more common on vericiguat in trials, for reasons not fully explained."], ["Pregnancy", "Animal studies showed fetal harm, so it is not given in pregnancy."]],
  "journey": "Vericiguat is a tablet taken once daily with food, which improves absorption. It peaks in about 4 hours and is about 98 percent bound to plasma proteins. Unlike many heart drugs, it is not broken down by CYP enzymes. Instead it is glucuronidated by UGT1A9 and UGT1A1 to an inactive product. Elimination is split between urine and feces. Its half-life is about 30 hours in HF patients, supporting once-daily dosing, and the dose is stepped up over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is the expected result of vasodilation from more cGMP. Combining it with other sGC stimulators or with PDE5 inhibitors, which also raise cGMP, can drop blood pressure too far, so these combinations are avoided. Long-acting nitrates add NO and also raise this risk. Anemia was seen in trials. The boxed warning is for embryo-fetal toxicity seen in animals, so pregnancy is excluded and contraception used. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Verquvo (vericiguat) FDA label", "Armstrong PW et al. VICTORIA trial, NEJM 2020"]
},
"lanreotide": {
  "origin": "Somatostatin was discovered in 1973 by Paul Brazeau, Roger Guillemin and colleagues at the Salk Institute, who found a hypothalamic peptide that blocked the release of growth hormone. Guillemin later shared the 1977 Nobel Prize for his work on brain peptide hormones. Natural somatostatin lasts only a few minutes in blood, so chemists built shorter, stable analogs. Octreotide came first. Lanreotide, a cyclic octapeptide, was developed in the late 1980s in collaboration between Tulane University researchers and the French company Ipsen. Ipsen created a long-acting gel depot that releases the drug slowly for about four weeks. The FDA approved Somatuline Depot in 2007 for acromegaly, and later for gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors (2014) and carcinoid syndrome (2017).",
  "timeline": [["1973", "Brazeau, Guillemin and colleagues discover somatostatin."], ["1980s", "Lanreotide is developed as a stable somatostatin analog."], ["2007", "FDA approves Somatuline Depot for acromegaly."], ["2014", "Lanreotide is approved for GEP neuroendocrine tumors after the CLARINET trial."], ["2017", "FDA approves lanreotide for carcinoid syndrome diarrhea."]],
  "cell": "Lanreotide mimics somatostatin and binds somatostatin receptors, mainly SSTR2 and SSTR5. These are Gi-coupled receptors. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP, opens potassium channels and reduces calcium entry. Lower calcium and cAMP reduce hormone release by exocytosis. In the pituitary, this lowers growth hormone release, so the liver makes less IGF-1, which treats acromegaly. In neuroendocrine tumor cells, which often carry many SSTR2 receptors, it lowers release of serotonin and other peptides that cause flushing and diarrhea. Receptor signaling also slows cell division and may trigger cell death, which slows tumor growth. It also suppresses insulin, glucagon, gastrin and gallbladder contraction.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "Lower growth hormone and IGF-1 control the overgrowth of acromegaly."], ["Neuroendocrine tumors", "Reduced hormone secretion eases flushing and diarrhea, and antiproliferative effects slow tumor growth."], ["Gallbladder", "Reduced cholecystokinin release and gallbladder contraction cause bile to stagnate, leading to sludge and gallstones."], ["Pancreas", "Suppression of both insulin and glucagon can cause either high or low blood sugar."], ["Gut", "Less pancreatic enzyme secretion and slowed gut motility cause loose stools, fat malabsorption and abdominal pain."], ["Heart and thyroid", "It can slow the heart rate and reduce TSH, causing hypothyroidism."]],
  "journey": "Lanreotide is given as a deep subcutaneous injection every 4 weeks. The drug forms a gel depot under the skin and is released slowly by passive diffusion, so levels rise within the first day and then stay steady. The apparent half-life of the depot is about 23 to 30 days, which reflects slow release rather than slow breakdown. Once released, it is broken down by peptidases, mostly in the gut and liver, and eliminated mainly through bile and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects follow from suppressing gut and pancreatic hormones. Less cholecystokinin and gallbladder emptying cause gallstones and sludge. Less pancreatic enzyme secretion causes fatty stools and diarrhea. Suppressed insulin and glucagon cause high or low blood sugar. Suppressed TSH can cause hypothyroidism. Effects on the sinus node can cause bradycardia, especially with other rate-slowing drugs. Injection site lumps and pain come from the depot. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Somatuline Depot (lanreotide) FDA label", "Brazeau P et al. Science 1973", "Caplin ME et al. CLARINET trial, NEJM 2014"]
},
"diazoxide": {
  "origin": "Diazoxide was made in the late 1950s at the Schering Corporation as part of the thiazide diuretic boom. Chemists created a benzothiadiazine closely related to chlorothiazide but without the sulfonamide group needed for diuresis. To their surprise it lowered blood pressure strongly without causing urine output, and it raised blood sugar. In the 1960s the IV form became a treatment for hypertensive emergencies, and the oral form was found to block insulin release. The oral suspension (Proglycem) became a standard treatment for hypoglycemia caused by too much insulin, such as from insulinomas and congenital hyperinsulinism. Only later did scientists learn that diazoxide works by opening ATP-sensitive potassium channels, the same channels that sulfonylureas close.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Diazoxide is synthesized at Schering as a thiazide relative."], ["1960s", "Its blood pressure-lowering and blood sugar-raising effects are described."], ["1980s", "ATP-sensitive potassium channels are identified as its target in beta cells."]],
  "cell": "Pancreatic beta cells sense glucose through ATP-sensitive potassium (K-ATP) channels, made of Kir6.2 pore subunits and SUR1 regulatory subunits. When glucose rises, ATP rises and closes the channels. The cell depolarizes, voltage-gated calcium channels open, calcium floods in and insulin granules are released. Diazoxide binds SUR1 and holds K-ATP channels open even when ATP is high. Potassium flows out, the cell stays hyperpolarized, calcium channels stay closed and insulin release is blocked. Blood sugar rises. This is the reverse of how sulfonylureas work. Diazoxide also opens K-ATP channels (SUR2 type) in arteriolar smooth muscle, relaxing those vessels, and it promotes kidney sodium and water retention.",
  "body": [["Pancreas", "Opening beta cell K-ATP channels blocks insulin release and treats hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia."], ["Blood sugar", "Less insulin raises glucose and can lead to ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar coma if excessive."], ["Arterioles", "Smooth muscle relaxation dilates arterioles and lowers blood pressure, with reflex tachycardia."], ["Kidneys and fluid", "Sodium and water retention cause edema and can tip people into heart failure; a thiazide is often added to counter it."], ["Hair", "Excess hair growth (hypertrichosis) is common, especially in children, likely from K-ATP opening in hair follicles."], ["Lungs in infants", "Pulmonary hypertension has been reported in infants and newborns."]],
  "journey": "Oral diazoxide suspension is absorbed well, with effects starting within an hour and lasting up to about 8 hours. It is more than 90 percent bound to plasma proteins. Part is broken down in the liver by oxidation and sulfate conjugation, and the drug and metabolites leave through the kidneys. The half-life is long, about 24 to 36 hours in adults and shorter in children, and longer in kidney disease.",
  "sidefx": "Fluid retention is the classic problem: kidney sodium retention expands volume, causing edema and sometimes heart failure, which is why a thiazide is often added. Blocking insulin can overshoot, causing high sugar, ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar coma. Vasodilation can cause low blood pressure and fast heart rate. Hair growth, nausea and taste changes are common. Infants can develop pulmonary hypertension. Low platelets and neutrophils can occur. High uric acid comes from reduced kidney urate excretion. There is no antidote; excess hyperglycemia is treated with insulin.",
  "src": ["Proglycem (diazoxide) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
}
,
"levocarnitine": {
  "origin": "Carnitine was discovered in 1905 by the Russian scientists Vladimir Gulevich and Robert Krimberg, who isolated it from meat extract. They named it from the Latin carnis, meaning flesh. For decades its job was unknown. In the 1950s Gottfried Fraenkel found that mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) needed it as a growth factor, so it was briefly called vitamin BT. Around the same time, Irving Fritz and others showed that carnitine is needed for the body to burn long-chain fatty acids in mitochondria. In the 1970s doctors described children with inherited carnitine deficiency and muscle weakness, low blood sugar and heart failure. Pure levocarnitine, the natural L form, was then approved in the US in the 1980s as Carnitor for primary and secondary carnitine deficiency, later including people on dialysis.",
  "timeline": [["1905", "Gulevich and Krimberg isolate carnitine from meat extract."], ["1950s", "Carnitine is found to be needed by mealworms and named vitamin BT."], ["1955", "Irving Fritz shows carnitine stimulates fatty acid oxidation in liver tissue."], ["1973", "The first human carnitine deficiency cases are reported."]],
  "cell": "Long-chain fatty acids are activated to acyl-CoA in the cytoplasm, but acyl-CoA cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane where beta-oxidation happens. Carnitine is the carrier. Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT I) on the outer membrane moves the fatty acid from CoA to carnitine, forming acylcarnitine. The carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase carries it across the inner membrane, and CPT II inside the matrix moves the fatty acid back to CoA. The fatty acyl-CoA then enters beta-oxidation, producing acetyl-CoA for energy and, in the liver, ketone bodies. Free carnitine shuttles back out. Carnitine also accepts excess or toxic acyl groups that build up in organic acid disorders, forming acylcarnitines that the kidneys excrete, which frees up CoA inside cells.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Restoring fat burning gives muscle more fuel during exercise and fasting, easing weakness and fatigue."], ["Heart", "Heart muscle relies heavily on fatty acids; replacing carnitine can improve cardiomyopathy in primary carnitine deficiency."], ["Liver", "Fat oxidation and ketone production return, which helps prevent low blood sugar during fasting."], ["Kidneys and dialysis", "Dialysis removes carnitine; replacement can correct low levels in patients on dialysis."], ["Metabolic disorders", "Binding toxic acyl groups helps remove them in urine in organic acidemias and valproate toxicity."], ["Body odor", "Gut bacteria turn unabsorbed oral carnitine into trimethylamine, which can give a fishy odor."]],
  "journey": "Oral levocarnitine is absorbed partly through the small intestine, with only a modest fraction of a dose taken in. The unabsorbed part is broken down by gut bacteria. Peak blood levels come about 3 hours after an oral dose. IV levocarnitine bypasses the gut and is used in dialysis patients and acute illness. Carnitine is not bound to plasma proteins. It is taken up into muscle and liver, where most of the body's carnitine is stored. The kidneys filter it and normally reabsorb most of it, and excess and acylcarnitines leave in the urine. The IV half-life is about 17 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Gut upset (nausea, cramps, diarrhea) comes from the large oral amounts in the intestine. Bacteria in the gut metabolize unabsorbed carnitine to trimethylamine, causing a fishy body odor; in severe kidney disease, trimethylamine and its oxide can build up, which is why oral use is avoided there. Seizures have been reported, with or without a prior history, though the mechanism is unclear. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur with the IV form. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Carnitor (levocarnitine) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"andexanet-alfa": {
  "origin": "Direct factor Xa inhibitors such as rivaroxaban and apixaban arrived around 2011 and 2012, but there was no specific way to reverse them in a major bleed. Scientists at Portola Pharmaceuticals in California designed a decoy: a version of human factor Xa that still binds the drugs but cannot clot. They swapped the key serine in the active site for alanine and removed the Gla domain that anchors factor Xa to cell membranes. The ANNEXA-4 study showed it quickly lowered anti-factor Xa activity in bleeding patients. The FDA granted accelerated approval to andexanet alfa (Andexxa) in May 2018. Portola was later acquired by Alexion, part of AstraZeneca. Concerns about blood clots and its confirmatory trial results followed, and andexanet alfa was withdrawn from the US market in 2025. For factor Xa inhibitor reversal in the US, four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) is now the usual option.",
  "timeline": [["2011", "Rivaroxaban, a direct factor Xa inhibitor, is approved in the US."], ["2013", "The design of andexanet as a factor Xa decoy is published."], ["2018", "FDA grants accelerated approval to andexanet alfa (Andexxa)."], ["2025", "Andexanet alfa is withdrawn from the US market."]],
  "cell": "Factor Xa is the enzyme at the meeting point of the clotting pathways. It joins factor Va on activated platelet membranes to form prothrombinase, which turns prothrombin into thrombin. Apixaban and rivaroxaban bind factor Xa's active site and block it. Andexanet alfa is a modified human factor Xa. Its active site serine is replaced by alanine, so it has no enzyme activity, and its Gla domain is removed, so it cannot join the prothrombinase complex. It still binds factor Xa inhibitors with high affinity, so it soaks them up from the blood like a sponge. Free drug falls, and the patient's own factor Xa is released to work again. It also binds tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI), a natural brake on clotting, which raises tissue factor-driven thrombin generation and adds a procoagulant effect.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Anti-factor Xa activity drops by about 90 percent within minutes of the bolus, and thrombin generation rises."], ["Bleeding sites", "Restored clotting is meant to help stop major bleeding, such as intracranial or gastrointestinal hemorrhage."], ["Arteries and veins", "The procoagulant effect, including TFPI inhibition, raises the risk of heart attack, stroke, DVT and pulmonary embolism."], ["Heparin response", "It can bind heparin-antithrombin complexes and blunt heparin, a problem before cardiac surgery."], ["Lab tests", "Anti-factor Xa activity rises again about 2 hours after the infusion ends as the decoy is cleared."]],
  "journey": "Andexanet alfa was given as an intravenous bolus followed by a 2-hour infusion. Its effect starts within minutes because it binds circulating drug directly. When the infusion stops, the decoy is cleared, and factor Xa inhibitor still in tissues moves back into blood, so anti-factor Xa activity rises again. As a protein it is broken down by normal protein catabolism, not by the kidneys or liver enzymes. Its terminal half-life is about 5 to 7 hours.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning covered thromboembolism, ischemic events, cardiac arrest and sudden death. These follow from reversing anticoagulation in patients who were anticoagulated because of high clot risk, plus the drug's own procoagulant TFPI binding. Rebound of anti-factor Xa activity after the infusion explains why bleeding could return. Heparin interference made it unsuitable just before cardiopulmonary bypass. Infusion reactions also occurred. With its withdrawal from the US, 4F-PCC is used instead for factor Xa inhibitor reversal.",
  "src": ["Andexxa (andexanet alfa) FDA label", "Lu G et al. Nature Medicine 2013", "Connolly SJ et al. ANNEXA-4, NEJM 2019"]
},
"aminocaproic-acid": {
  "origin": "Aminocaproic acid came from the work of a Japanese husband-and-wife team, Shosuke Okamoto and Utako Okamoto, who in the 1950s searched for compounds that could stop excessive breakdown of blood clots (fibrinolysis). They found that epsilon-aminocaproic acid, a simple molecule shaped like the amino acid lysine, blocked plasmin formation. Utako Okamoto later discovered tranexamic acid in 1962, about ten times more potent. Aminocaproic acid was approved in the US in the 1960s as Amicar. It became a standard drug for bleeding driven by fibrinolysis, such as after heart surgery, prostate surgery and in some bleeding disorders.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Shosuke and Utako Okamoto identify epsilon-aminocaproic acid as an antifibrinolytic."], ["1962", "Utako Okamoto discovers the more potent tranexamic acid."], ["1960s", "Aminocaproic acid (Amicar) is approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Fibrinolysis is the system that dissolves clots. Plasminogen binds to fibrin through lysine-binding sites on its kringle domains. Once bound, tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) or urokinase converts it to plasmin, which cuts fibrin into fragments and dissolves the clot. Aminocaproic acid is a lysine analog. It occupies the lysine-binding sites on plasminogen, so plasminogen cannot attach to fibrin and is activated much less. At high concentrations it also weakly blocks plasmin directly. The result is that formed clots are not broken down as quickly. It does not make new clots; it only protects the ones that form.",
  "body": [["Clots and bleeding", "Stabilizing fibrin clots reduces bleeding when fibrinolysis is excessive, such as in surgery or certain bleeding disorders."], ["Urinary tract", "The urinary tract is rich in urokinase; blocking it can leave clots in the upper urinary tract, causing obstruction."], ["Muscle", "Long-term use can cause muscle weakness, muscle necrosis and rhabdomyolysis, with high CK."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Rapid IV injection can cause low blood pressure, slow heart rate and arrhythmias."], ["Vessels", "By stopping clot breakdown it can tip the balance toward thrombosis in at-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "Aminocaproic acid can be given by mouth or IV and is absorbed well from the gut, peaking in about 1 to 2 hours. It is not significantly bound to plasma proteins and spreads through body water. Very little is metabolized. Most leaves unchanged in the urine, so levels rise in kidney disease. The half-life is about 2 hours, which is why it is given often or by continuous infusion.",
  "sidefx": "Because it blocks clot breakdown everywhere, clots that form can persist, leading to thrombosis such as heart attack, stroke or pulmonary embolism, especially combined with factor IX complex or anti-inhibitor products. High concentrations in urine can cause clots to stay in the kidney or ureter. Muscle damage with long use is thought to come from a direct toxic effect on muscle. Rapid IV pushes drop blood pressure and slow the heart. Nausea, diarrhea and dizziness are common. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Utako Okamoto had trouble getting doctors in Japan to use tranexamic acid. Decades later, the large CRASH-2 and WOMAN trials showed it saves lives in trauma and childbirth bleeding.",
  "src": ["Amicar (aminocaproic acid) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"emicizumab": {
  "origin": "Hemophilia A is caused by a lack of factor VIII, a cofactor that helps factor IXa activate factor X. Replacing factor VIII requires frequent IV infusions, and about a third of severe patients form inhibitor antibodies that neutralize it. Scientists at Chugai Pharmaceutical in Japan, part of the Roche group, had an idea: build an antibody with two different arms, one grabbing factor IXa and the other factor X, so it holds them together the way factor VIII does. After screening many bispecific antibodies and fine-tuning them, they reported the concept in Nature Medicine in 2012. The result, emicizumab, could be injected under the skin weekly or less often. The FDA approved emicizumab (Hemlibra) in November 2017 for hemophilia A with inhibitors and in 2018 for patients without inhibitors.",
  "timeline": [["2012", "Chugai scientists report a bispecific antibody that mimics factor VIII."], ["2017", "FDA approves emicizumab (Hemlibra) for hemophilia A with inhibitors."], ["2018", "Approval expands to hemophilia A without inhibitors."]],
  "cell": "In normal clotting, activated factor VIII (FVIIIa) acts as a bridge on the platelet surface, holding activated factor IX (FIXa) and factor X together in the tenase complex. This speeds the activation of factor X to factor Xa by many thousand times, driving a burst of thrombin. Emicizumab is a humanized bispecific IgG4 antibody. One arm binds factor IXa and the other binds factor X, placing them close together so FIXa can activate factor X. Thrombin generation is restored and clots can form. Because emicizumab is not factor VIII and has a different structure, factor VIII inhibitor antibodies do not recognize it. Unlike factor VIII, it does not need activation and is not regulated, so it works continuously.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Restored tenase activity raises thrombin generation, providing steady protection against bleeding."], ["Joints", "Fewer spontaneous joint bleeds protect against the joint damage (hemophilic arthropathy) that drives disability."], ["Lab tests", "Emicizumab shortens the aPTT strongly and makes one-stage factor VIII assays and Bethesda inhibitor tests unreliable."], ["Small vessels", "Combined with large amounts of aPCC, it can cause thrombotic microangiopathy and clots."], ["Injection site", "Local reactions are common with subcutaneous injections."]],
  "journey": "Emicizumab is injected under the skin, first with weekly loading doses and then weekly, every two weeks or every four weeks. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph, peaking 1 to 2 weeks after a dose. Like other IgG antibodies it is protected by the neonatal Fc receptor, which gives a long half-life of about 4 weeks. It is cleared by protein catabolism rather than by the kidneys or liver enzymes. Steady protection is reached after several weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Emicizumab is not designed to treat acute bleeding; breakthrough bleeds still need bypassing agents or factor VIII. When patients also received activated prothrombin complex concentrate (aPCC) at high cumulative amounts, the combined procoagulant push caused thrombotic microangiopathy and thrombosis, which is the boxed warning. aPCC contains factor IXa and factor X, the very substrates emicizumab binds. Lab interference occurs because the aPTT reaction is driven by emicizumab instead of factor VIII. Injection site reactions, headache and joint pain are common. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Hemlibra (emicizumab-kxwh) FDA label", "Kitazawa T et al. Nature Medicine 2012"]
},
"treprostinil": {
  "origin": "Prostacyclin was discovered in 1976 by Salvador Moncada, John Vane and colleagues at the Wellcome Research Laboratories in England. It is a natural substance made by blood vessel lining cells that relaxes vessels and stops platelets from sticking. As a drug (epoprostenol), it transformed pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) care in the 1990s, but it breaks down in minutes and needs constant IV infusion through a central line. Treprostinil is a more stable synthetic analog first studied at Burroughs Wellcome. United Therapeutics, founded in 1996 by Martine Rothblatt after her daughter was diagnosed with PAH, licensed and developed it. The FDA approved subcutaneous treprostinil (Remodulin) in 2002, inhaled treprostinil (Tyvaso) in 2009 and the oral tablet (Orenitram) in 2013.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Moncada, Vane and colleagues discover prostacyclin."], ["1996", "Martine Rothblatt founds United Therapeutics."], ["2002", "FDA approves subcutaneous treprostinil (Remodulin)."], ["2009", "Inhaled treprostinil (Tyvaso) is approved."], ["2013", "Oral treprostinil (Orenitram) is approved."]],
  "cell": "In PAH, the small lung arteries narrow and thicken, partly from too little prostacyclin and nitric oxide and too much endothelin and thromboxane. Treprostinil binds the prostacyclin (IP) receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle and platelets. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP. In smooth muscle, cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, so the vessel relaxes. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks activation and aggregation. Prostacyclin signaling also slows smooth muscle proliferation. The net effect is lower pulmonary vascular resistance, less strain on the right ventricle and better cardiac output. Treprostinil also binds some other prostaglandin receptors, such as DP1 and EP2.",
  "body": [["Lung arteries", "Vasodilation and less smooth muscle growth lower pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure."], ["Right heart", "Lower resistance reduces right ventricle workload and raises cardiac output, improving exercise capacity."], ["Systemic vessels", "Systemic vasodilation causes flushing, headache and sometimes low blood pressure."], ["Platelets", "Inhibited aggregation raises bleeding risk, especially with anticoagulants."], ["Jaw and muscles", "Jaw pain, a typical prostacyclin side effect, is common, as are leg and muscle pain."], ["Gut", "Prostaglandin-like effects on the gut cause diarrhea and nausea."]],
  "journey": "Treprostinil can be given by continuous subcutaneous or IV infusion, inhaled several times a day, or swallowed as extended-release tablets. Subcutaneous absorption is nearly complete. It is about 91 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down mainly by CYP2C8, so gemfibrozil raises levels and rifampin lowers them. Metabolites leave mainly in urine. The half-life is about 4 hours, much longer than epoprostenol's minutes, but effects still wane after stopping, so infusions must not be interrupted.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, jaw pain, diarrhea and nausea are all prostacyclin receptor effects on vessels and the gut. Subcutaneous infusion often causes severe pain at the infusion site from local prostaglandin effects. Platelet inhibition raises bleeding risk. Systemic vasodilation can drop blood pressure. IV central lines can lead to bloodstream infections and sepsis. Stopping suddenly removes vasodilation and can cause rebound PAH, a crisis for the right heart. Inhaled forms cause cough and throat irritation. There is no antidote; the infusion rate is adjusted for side effects.",
  "src": ["Remodulin, Tyvaso and Orenitram (treprostinil) FDA labels", "Moncada S et al. Nature 1976"]
},
"riociguat": {
  "origin": "Nitric oxide relaxes blood vessels by activating soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC), a discovery recognized by the 1998 Nobel Prize to Furchgott, Ignarro and Murad. In pulmonary hypertension, NO signaling is weak. In 1994 researchers found that a compound called YC-1 could stimulate sGC directly. Building on that, Bayer scientists, including Johannes-Peter Stasch, created more potent sGC stimulators. Riociguat was the first to reach patients. In the CHEST-1 trial in chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) and PATENT-1 in pulmonary arterial hypertension, it improved walking distance. The FDA approved riociguat (Adempas) in October 2013, and it became the first drug approved for inoperable or persistent CTEPH.",
  "timeline": [["1994", "YC-1 is found to stimulate soluble guanylate cyclase directly."], ["1998", "Nobel Prize awarded for nitric oxide as a signaling molecule."], ["2013", "FDA approves riociguat (Adempas), the first sGC stimulator, for CTEPH and PAH."]],
  "cell": "Soluble guanylate cyclase is the receptor for nitric oxide inside smooth muscle cells. When NO binds its heme group, sGC converts GTP to cyclic GMP (cGMP). cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers calcium, relaxes smooth muscle and limits cell growth and scarring. Riociguat acts on sGC in two ways. It sensitizes sGC to the body's own NO by stabilizing the NO-sGC bond, and it directly stimulates sGC at a separate site, even when NO is low. More cGMP relaxes the pulmonary arteries and slows the abnormal growth of vessel wall cells. This lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and the load on the right ventricle. Its effect does not depend on cGMP breakdown being blocked, which is how PDE5 inhibitors work.",
  "body": [["Lung arteries", "More cGMP relaxes pulmonary arteries and lowers vascular resistance in PAH and CTEPH."], ["Right heart", "Less resistance lowers right ventricle strain and improves output and walking distance."], ["Systemic vessels", "Systemic vasodilation lowers blood pressure, causing dizziness and sometimes symptomatic hypotension."], ["Lungs", "Serious bleeding, including coughing up blood, has been reported; pulmonary edema can occur in pulmonary veno-occlusive disease."], ["Gut", "Dyspepsia, nausea and reflux come from relaxation of gut smooth muscle, including the lower esophageal sphincter."], ["Pregnancy", "It caused fetal harm in animals, which is the boxed warning."]],
  "journey": "Riociguat is a tablet taken three times a day, with nearly complete absorption and peak levels in about 1.5 hours. It is about 95 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by several enzymes, including CYP1A1, CYP3A4, CYP2C8 and CYP2J2. CYP1A1 is induced by tobacco smoke, so smokers have lower levels. Strong CYP and P-gp/BCRP inhibitors raise levels. It leaves in urine and feces, with a half-life of about 12 hours in patients. The dose is stepped up over weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure follows from raising cGMP in systemic vessels. Combining riociguat with PDE5 inhibitors or nitrates, which also raise cGMP, can cause dangerous hypotension, so these combinations are contraindicated. Headache, dizziness and gut symptoms reflect smooth muscle relaxation. Bleeding, including hemoptysis, has occurred. The boxed warning is for embryo-fetal toxicity seen in animals, so pregnancy is excluded and contraception used. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Adempas (riociguat) FDA label", "Ghofrani HA et al. CHEST-1 and PATENT-1, NEJM 2013"]
},
"ambrisentan": {
  "origin": "Endothelin-1 was discovered in 1988 by Masashi Yanagisawa, Tomoh Masaki and colleagues at the University of Tsukuba in Japan. It proved to be one of the most powerful blood vessel constrictors known. People with pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) have high endothelin levels in their lungs. Drug companies quickly built endothelin receptor antagonists. Ambrisentan was discovered at the German company Knoll (later part of Abbott) and was developed by Myogen, a Colorado company that Gilead Sciences bought in 2006. It was designed to block the ETA receptor selectively. After the ARIES trials, the FDA approved ambrisentan (Letairis) in June 2007 for PAH. Later, the AMBITION trial showed that starting it together with tadalafil worked better than either alone.",
  "timeline": [["1988", "Yanagisawa, Masaki and colleagues discover endothelin-1."], ["2001", "Bosentan becomes the first endothelin receptor antagonist approved for PAH."], ["2007", "FDA approves ambrisentan (Letairis) for PAH."], ["2015", "AMBITION trial supports initial combination with tadalafil."], ["2025", "FDA removes the endothelin receptor antagonist pregnancy REMS."]],
  "cell": "Endothelin-1 is made by blood vessel lining cells. It acts on two receptor types. ETA receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq-coupled: they raise intracellular calcium, causing strong constriction, and they drive smooth muscle growth and scarring. ETB receptors on endothelial cells release nitric oxide and prostacyclin and help clear endothelin from blood, although ETB on smooth muscle can also constrict. Ambrisentan is a selective, competitive ETA antagonist. By blocking ETA it stops endothelin-induced constriction and growth of pulmonary artery smooth muscle, while leaving ETB-driven vasodilation and endothelin clearance largely intact. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls, and over time the vessels may remodel less.",
  "body": [["Lung arteries", "ETA blockade relaxes and slows thickening of pulmonary arteries, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance."], ["Right heart", "Lower resistance reduces strain on the right ventricle and improves walking distance and time to worsening."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema and fluid retention are common, possibly from endothelin effects on kidney sodium handling and vessel leak."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin often falls, partly from hemodilution."], ["Nose and sinuses", "Vasodilation in nasal tissue causes congestion and sinusitis."], ["Reproduction", "Endothelin signaling is vital to fetal development, so it causes birth defects; sperm counts can also fall."]],
  "journey": "Ambrisentan is a once-daily tablet that is absorbed quickly, peaking in about 2 hours. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver handles it mainly by glucuronidation (UGTs) and partly by oxidation through CYP3A4 and CYP2C19. It leaves mainly through bile and feces, not the kidneys. The effective half-life is about 9 hours. Cyclosporine raises its levels. Clinical benefit appears over days to weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Endothelin is essential for normal formation of the face, heart and great vessels in the embryo, so blocking it causes severe birth defects. This is the boxed warning, and pregnancy must be excluded with effective contraception used. Fluid retention and edema are common and can worsen heart failure. Lower hemoglobin reflects hemodilution and perhaps effects on red cell production. Vasodilation causes headache, flushing and nasal congestion. In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, dilating arteries upstream of blocked veins can cause pulmonary edema. Liver injury is a class concern but less common than with bosentan. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Letairis (ambrisentan) FDA label", "Yanagisawa M et al. Nature 1988", "Galie N et al. AMBITION, NEJM 2015"]
},
"macitentan": {
  "origin": "Macitentan was created at Actelion, a Swiss company founded in 1997 by Jean-Paul Clozel, Martine Clozel and colleagues who had worked on endothelin at Roche. Actelion's first drug, bosentan, was the first endothelin receptor antagonist approved for pulmonary arterial hypertension in 2001, but it caused liver injury. Actelion chemists set out to make a successor with better tissue penetration, longer receptor binding and less liver toxicity. The result was macitentan. The SERAPHIN trial, one of the first PAH trials built around long-term outcomes rather than walking distance, showed fewer serious events or deaths. The FDA approved macitentan (Opsumit) in October 2013. Johnson & Johnson acquired Actelion in 2017, and a single tablet with tadalafil (Opsynvi) was approved in 2024.",
  "timeline": [["1988", "Endothelin-1 is discovered in Japan."], ["2001", "Actelion's bosentan becomes the first ERA approved for PAH."], ["2013", "SERAPHIN results lead to FDA approval of macitentan (Opsumit)."], ["2017", "Johnson & Johnson acquires Actelion."], ["2024", "Macitentan with tadalafil (Opsynvi) is approved as a single tablet."]],
  "cell": "Endothelin-1 is a peptide made by endothelial cells in excess in PAH. It binds ETA and ETB receptors on pulmonary artery smooth muscle. Both are G protein-coupled receptors that raise intracellular calcium, causing constriction, and activate growth pathways that thicken the vessel wall and promote fibrosis. Macitentan is a dual ETA and ETB antagonist. It competes with endothelin for both receptors. Compared with older antagonists, it binds the receptor for longer (slow dissociation) and penetrates tissue well. It is also converted by CYP3A4 to an active metabolite, aprocitentan, which adds to its effect. Blocking endothelin signaling relaxes the pulmonary arteries and slows the remodeling that drives PAH progression.",
  "body": [["Lung arteries", "Blocking both endothelin receptors reduces constriction and vessel wall thickening, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance."], ["Right heart and outcomes", "Less resistance unloads the right ventricle; in SERAPHIN this meant fewer hospitalizations, disease worsening events and deaths combined."], ["Blood", "Hemoglobin falls, sometimes to anemia, likely from hemodilution and effects on red cell production."], ["Liver", "Liver enzyme rises and rare liver failure can occur, though less often than with bosentan."], ["Fluid balance", "Edema and fluid retention can occur, a class effect of endothelin blockade."], ["Reproduction", "Endothelin is needed for fetal development, so it causes birth defects, and it may lower sperm counts."]],
  "journey": "Macitentan is a once-daily tablet. It is absorbed slowly, peaking in about 8 hours, and is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver converts it mainly by CYP3A4 to aprocitentan, an active metabolite with a long half-life of about 48 hours. The parent drug's half-life is about 16 hours. Strong CYP3A4 inducers such as rifampin lower exposure, and strong inhibitors raise it. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning is embryo-fetal toxicity, since endothelin signaling is needed to form the face, heart and great vessels; pregnancy is excluded and contraception used. Anemia reflects hemoglobin decline from hemodilution and perhaps marrow effects. Liver injury is a class effect of endothelin antagonists, so liver tests are checked. Fluid retention and edema can occur. Vasodilation causes headache and nasal congestion. In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, it can cause pulmonary edema. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Aprocitentan, the active metabolite of macitentan, was later developed as a medicine of its own and approved in 2024 as Tryvio for hard-to-control high blood pressure.",
  "src": ["Opsumit (macitentan) FDA label", "Pulido T et al. SERAPHIN trial, NEJM 2013"]
},
"selexipag": {
  "origin": "Prostacyclin-based drugs are among the most effective treatments for pulmonary arterial hypertension, but natural prostacyclin and its analogs are unstable or need infusions or many inhaled doses. Scientists at Nippon Shinyaku in Kyoto, Japan, designed a different kind of molecule: one that is not built on the prostaglandin skeleton at all but still activates the prostacyclin (IP) receptor selectively. The result was selexipag, a prodrug converted in the body to a more potent active form. Developed with Actelion, it was tested in GRIPHON, at the time the largest PAH outcome trial, which showed fewer disease progression events. The FDA approved selexipag (Uptravi) as an oral tablet in December 2015, and an IV form followed in 2021 for patients temporarily unable to take tablets.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Prostacyclin is discovered by Moncada, Vane and colleagues."], ["2000s", "Nippon Shinyaku designs selexipag, a nonprostanoid IP receptor agonist."], ["2015", "GRIPHON results are published, and FDA approves oral selexipag (Uptravi)."], ["2021", "An IV form of selexipag is approved."]],
  "cell": "Selexipag is hydrolyzed by carboxylesterase 1 in the liver to its active metabolite (ACT-333679), which is about 37 times more potent at the prostacyclin receptor. Both bind the IP receptor on pulmonary artery smooth muscle and platelets. The IP receptor is Gs-coupled: it activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers intracellular calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, relaxing the muscle. In platelets cAMP blocks aggregation, and in smooth muscle it slows proliferation. Because selexipag is not a prostanoid, it is selective for the IP receptor and does not strongly activate other prostaglandin receptors such as EP3, which can constrict vessels and upset the gut.",
  "body": [["Lung arteries", "Raising cAMP relaxes pulmonary arteries and slows vessel wall thickening."], ["Disease course", "In GRIPHON it lowered the risk of a combined endpoint of death or PAH disease progression."], ["Head and face", "Systemic vasodilation causes headache and flushing, especially during dose titration."], ["Jaw and muscles", "Jaw pain, muscle aches and limb pain are classic prostacyclin-receptor side effects."], ["Gut", "Diarrhea, nausea and vomiting are common and often limit the dose."], ["Thyroid and blood", "Hyperthyroidism and anemia have been reported."]],
  "journey": "Selexipag is taken by mouth twice daily, and the dose is raised step by step to the highest tolerated level. It is absorbed well, with the parent drug peaking in 1 to 3 hours and the active metabolite in 3 to 4 hours. Carboxylesterase 1 converts it to the active form, which is then broken down by CYP2C8 and CYP3A4 and by glucuronidation. Strong CYP2C8 inhibitors such as gemfibrozil sharply raise active metabolite levels. The parent half-life is about 1 to 2.5 hours and the active metabolite's about 6 to 13 hours. Both are highly protein bound and leave mainly in feces.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, diarrhea, jaw pain, nausea, muscle pain and flushing all come from IP receptor activation in vessels, gut and muscle; they are most common while doses are being raised. Systemic vasodilation can lower blood pressure. In pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, dilating arteries upstream of blocked veins can cause pulmonary edema. Hyperthyroidism and anemia have been reported. There is no antidote; doses are reduced if side effects are not tolerated.",
  "src": ["Uptravi (selexipag) FDA label", "Sitbon O et al. GRIPHON trial, NEJM 2015"]
},
"inclisiran": {
  "origin": "Inclisiran brings together two discoveries. In 1998 Andrew Fire and Craig Mello showed that double-stranded RNA can silence a matching gene, a process called RNA interference (RNAi), and they shared the 2006 Nobel Prize. In 2003 French researchers led by Catherine Boileau and Marianne Abifadel linked mutations in the PCSK9 gene to high LDL cholesterol, and in 2005 and 2006 Jonathan Cohen and Helen Hobbs in Dallas showed that people with loss-of-function PCSK9 variants have low LDL and fewer heart attacks. Alnylam Pharmaceuticals designed a small interfering RNA against PCSK9 with a GalNAc sugar tag that targets the liver. The Medicines Company licensed and developed it, and Novartis bought that company in 2020. The FDA approved inclisiran (Leqvio) in December 2021, with injections twice a year after the first doses.",
  "timeline": [["1998", "Fire and Mello describe RNA interference."], ["2003", "PCSK9 mutations are linked to familial hypercholesterolemia."], ["2006", "Cohen and Hobbs report that low PCSK9 function protects against heart disease."], ["2021", "FDA approves inclisiran (Leqvio)."]],
  "cell": "PCSK9 is a protein made by liver cells. It binds LDL receptors on the hepatocyte surface, and when the receptor is pulled into the cell with LDL, PCSK9 sends it to the lysosome to be destroyed instead of recycled. Less receptor means less LDL cleared from blood. Inclisiran is a double-stranded siRNA linked to a GalNAc cluster. GalNAc binds the asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocytes, so the drug enters liver cells. There the guide strand loads into the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC). RISC uses the guide to find PCSK9 messenger RNA and cuts it. Each RISC complex can cut many mRNA copies, so PCSK9 production falls for months. With less PCSK9, more LDL receptors recycle to the surface and LDL cholesterol falls by about half.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes make less PCSK9 and keep more LDL receptors on their surface, clearing LDL from blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls by about 50 percent, with effects lasting about 6 months between doses."], ["Arteries", "Lower LDL is expected to slow atherosclerosis; outcome trial results were not available at first approval."], ["Injection site", "Mild pain, redness or rash at the injection site are the most common effects."], ["Joints and airways", "Joint pain, bronchitis and urinary tract infections were slightly more common in trials."]],
  "journey": "Inclisiran is injected under the skin by a health professional, then again at 3 months and every 6 months after that. It peaks in plasma about 4 hours after injection and is mostly gone from blood within a day or two, with a plasma half-life of about 9 hours, because the liver takes it up fast. Inside liver cells RISC-bound siRNA lasts for months, which explains the long effect. It is broken down by nucleases, not CYP enzymes, so drug interactions are minimal. About 16 percent leaves unchanged in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Inclisiran has few side effects because its action is confined to the liver and to one gene. Injection site reactions come from local inflammation. Because it acts through RNA silencing rather than CYP pathways, it has few drug interactions, and it does not cause the muscle symptoms linked to statins. Joint pain, urinary infections and bronchitis were slightly more frequent than with placebo. There is no antidote, and its effect fades only as new PCSK9 production recovers over months.",
  "src": ["Leqvio (inclisiran) FDA label", "Fire A, Mello CC et al. Nature 1998", "Ray KK et al. ORION-10 and ORION-11, NEJM 2020"]
}
,
"bempedoic-acid": {
  "origin": "Bempedoic acid comes from Esperion Therapeutics in Michigan, founded by Roger Newton, a scientist who had helped discover atorvastatin (Lipitor) at Parke-Davis. Many patients stop statins because of muscle aches, so Esperion looked for a way to lower cholesterol production without acting in muscle. Their compound, first called ETC-1002, turned out to block ATP-citrate lyase, an enzyme a few steps before the statin target, and to be activated only in the liver. The FDA approved bempedoic acid (Nexletol) and its combination with ezetimibe (Nexlizet) in February 2020 for lowering LDL. In 2023 the CLEAR Outcomes trial in statin-intolerant patients showed fewer heart attacks and other major cardiovascular events, and the label was updated in 2024 to include cardiovascular risk reduction.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Esperion develops ETC-1002, later named bempedoic acid."], ["2020", "FDA approves bempedoic acid (Nexletol) and bempedoic acid with ezetimibe (Nexlizet)."], ["2023", "CLEAR Outcomes trial shows fewer cardiovascular events in statin-intolerant patients."], ["2024", "The label is updated to include cardiovascular risk reduction."]],
  "cell": "Cholesterol is made in liver cells from acetyl-CoA. ATP-citrate lyase (ACL) is the enzyme that makes cytoplasmic acetyl-CoA from citrate exported out of mitochondria, supplying the pathway that later passes through HMG-CoA reductase, the statin target. Bempedoic acid is a prodrug. In the liver, very-long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase 1 (ACSVL1) attaches CoA to it, forming bempedoyl-CoA, the active form, which inhibits ACL. Less acetyl-CoA means less cholesterol synthesis in hepatocytes. Low intracellular cholesterol activates SREBP-2, which increases LDL receptor production. More LDL receptors pull more LDL from blood, so LDL cholesterol falls. ACSVL1 is essentially absent in skeletal muscle, so the drug is not activated there, which helps explain why muscle side effects are uncommon.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Reduced cholesterol synthesis increases LDL receptor levels, clearing LDL from blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL falls by about 17 to 25 percent alone, more with ezetimibe, and further when added to a statin."], ["Arteries and heart", "In statin-intolerant patients, lower LDL translated into fewer heart attacks and coronary procedures."], ["Kidneys and uric acid", "It competes with uric acid for the kidney transporter OAT2, raising uric acid and gout risk."], ["Tendons", "Tendon rupture, a rare effect, was more common in trials, especially in older adults or with steroids or fluoroquinolones."], ["Muscle", "Because it is not activated in muscle, muscle aches are not more common than placebo."]],
  "journey": "Bempedoic acid is a once-daily tablet. It is absorbed by mouth and peaks in about 3.5 hours. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. In the liver ACSVL1 activates it, and it is also converted to an active metabolite (ESU-1) and glucuronidated. Most of a dose leaves in urine as metabolites, with some in feces. The half-life is about 21 hours. LDL lowering is seen within 2 to 4 weeks. It modestly raises simvastatin and pravastatin levels.",
  "sidefx": "Raised uric acid and gout happen because bempedoic acid and its metabolite inhibit OAT2, a kidney transporter involved in uric acid handling. Small rises in creatinine and BUN have a similar transporter-related cause. Tendon rupture is rare, with an unclear mechanism, and is more likely with steroids, fluoroquinolones, age over 60 or kidney failure. Small drops in hemoglobin and rises in liver enzymes have been seen. Muscle symptoms are uncommon because the drug is not activated in muscle. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Nexletol (bempedoic acid) FDA label", "Nissen SE et al. CLEAR Outcomes, NEJM 2023"]
},
"icosapent-ethyl": {
  "origin": "In the 1970s the Danish doctors Hans Olaf Bang and Jorn Dyerberg studied the Inuit of Greenland, who ate a diet rich in fish and seal fat yet seemed to have little heart disease. They found high levels of omega-3 fatty acids, especially eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA), in their blood. Interest in fish oil for the heart followed, but many trials of mixed EPA and DHA supplements showed little benefit. Amarin, an Irish-American company, developed icosapent ethyl, a highly purified ethyl ester of EPA alone, without DHA. The FDA approved it as Vascepa in July 2012 for very high triglycerides. In 2018 the REDUCE-IT trial showed a 25 percent lower risk of major cardiovascular events in high-risk patients on statins, and in December 2019 the FDA approved it to reduce cardiovascular risk.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Bang and Dyerberg study omega-3 fatty acids in Greenland Inuit."], ["2012", "FDA approves icosapent ethyl (Vascepa) for severe hypertriglyceridemia."], ["2018", "REDUCE-IT shows a 25 percent reduction in major cardiovascular events."], ["2019", "FDA approves icosapent ethyl for cardiovascular risk reduction."]],
  "cell": "The exact mechanism is not fully defined. EPA lowers triglycerides mainly by reducing how much triglyceride-rich VLDL the liver makes and releases. Proposed steps include inhibiting acyl-CoA:1,2-diacylglycerol acyltransferase (DGAT), the enzyme that finishes triglyceride assembly; increasing burning of fatty acids in mitochondria and peroxisomes; reducing new fat synthesis; and increasing lipoprotein lipase activity, which clears triglycerides from circulating particles. The cardiovascular benefit in REDUCE-IT seemed larger than the triglyceride drop alone would predict. EPA built into cell membranes and plaques may stabilize them, reduce inflammation and oxidation, and change platelet function, since EPA-derived eicosanoids are less pro-clotting than those from arachidonic acid. Because it contains no DHA, LDL does not rise significantly.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Less VLDL production and more fatty acid oxidation lower blood triglycerides."], ["Arteries", "EPA in plaques and membranes may reduce inflammation and oxidation, contributing to fewer heart attacks and strokes."], ["Platelets", "EPA shifts eicosanoid production away from thromboxane A2, slightly reducing platelet aggregation and raising bleeding risk."], ["Heart rhythm", "Atrial fibrillation and flutter were more common in REDUCE-IT, especially in people with prior AF."], ["Muscles and joints", "Musculoskeletal pain and joint pain were reported in trials."], ["Fluid balance", "Peripheral edema was reported more often than with placebo."]],
  "journey": "Icosapent ethyl comes as capsules taken with meals. In the intestine, the ethyl ester is cleaved off and free EPA is absorbed through the lymph into the blood. EPA is built into plasma lipids, red cell membranes and tissues, which is why levels build over weeks. It is more than 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. EPA is broken down mainly in the liver by beta-oxidation, like other fats, to carbon dioxide and water. It is not removed by the kidneys. The half-life of EPA in plasma is long, about 89 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Bleeding risk follows from EPA's effects on platelets, and is higher with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs. The higher rate of atrial fibrillation is not fully explained, but omega-3 fatty acids can alter ion channel function in atrial cells. Joint and muscle pain, edema, constipation and gout were also reported. People with fish or shellfish allergy could theoretically react, though the oil is highly purified. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "In REDUCE-IT the placebo was mineral oil, which raised LDL and inflammatory markers a little; some experts argued this made the benefit look bigger, a debate that continues.",
  "src": ["Vascepa (icosapent ethyl) FDA label", "Bhatt DL et al. REDUCE-IT, NEJM 2019"]
},
"indomethacin": {
  "origin": "Indomethacin was discovered at Merck in the early 1960s by a team led by the chemist Tsung-Ying Shen. Inflammation research at the time pointed to serotonin, and the team built indole compounds, related to serotonin, testing hundreds of them in animal models of inflammation. Indomethacin stood out as very potent. It was approved in the US in 1965 as Indocin. In 1971 John Vane showed that aspirin-like drugs work by blocking prostaglandin synthesis, which explained how indomethacin worked. Because prostaglandin E2 keeps the fetal ductus arteriosus open, doctors reasoned that blocking it could close a patent ductus in premature babies. In 1976 two US groups, Heymann, Rudolph and Silverman and Friedman and colleagues, reported success, and IV indomethacin became a standard NICU treatment for PDA.",
  "timeline": [["1963", "Tsung-Ying Shen's group at Merck discovers indomethacin."], ["1965", "Indomethacin (Indocin) is approved in the US."], ["1971", "John Vane shows NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis."], ["1976", "Reports show indomethacin can close a patent ductus arteriosus in preterm infants."]],
  "cell": "Indomethacin reversibly blocks both cyclooxygenase enzymes, COX-1 and COX-2, which turn arachidonic acid into prostaglandin H2, the parent of prostaglandins and thromboxane. In the fetus, the ductus arteriosus is held open by PGE2 and prostacyclin acting on EP4 receptors on ductal smooth muscle, which raise cAMP and relax the muscle. After birth, oxygen and falling prostaglandin levels close it, but in preterm babies it often stays open. By cutting PGE2, indomethacin lets ductal smooth muscle constrict and close. In adults, less PGE2 at injured tissue reduces swelling and pain, less PGE2 in the hypothalamus lowers fever, and less platelet thromboxane A2 weakens aggregation.",
  "body": [["Ductus arteriosus", "Loss of PGE2 lets the ductus constrict and close in preterm infants."], ["Kidneys", "Prostaglandins keep kidney blood flow up when it is stressed; blocking them lowers GFR, causing oliguria, sodium and water retention and high potassium."], ["Gut", "Lower mucosal prostaglandins and mesenteric blood flow raise the risk of bleeding, perforation and necrotizing enterocolitis in neonates and ulcers in adults."], ["Brain", "It reduces cerebral blood flow; in adults it can cause headache and dizziness more often than other NSAIDs."], ["Platelets", "Reduced thromboxane impairs platelet function and raises bleeding risk."], ["Joints and soft tissue", "Strong anti-inflammatory action helps gout flares, ankylosing spondylitis and other inflammatory pain."]],
  "journey": "Indomethacin is absorbed well by mouth, peaking in about 2 hours, and can also be given rectally or IV. For neonates it is given IV. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by O-demethylation, N-deacylation and glucuronidation, and part of it is recycled through bile into the gut and reabsorbed (enterohepatic recirculation). Metabolites leave in urine and feces. The adult half-life is about 4.5 hours. In preterm infants it is much longer and highly variable, about 12 to 20 hours depending on age.",
  "sidefx": "All the main toxicities come from losing prostaglandins. In the kidney, blocked vasodilating prostaglandins lower filtration, causing oliguria, rising creatinine, low sodium and high potassium, which is why urine output is checked before each neonatal dose. In the gut, less mucosal protection and lower mesenteric flow cause bleeding, perforation and NEC. Reduced platelet thromboxane causes bleeding. Adult oral forms carry the NSAID boxed warnings for heart attack and stroke and for GI bleeding. Central nervous system effects such as headache are prominent. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Indocin (indomethacin) FDA label", "Indomethacin for injection FDA label", "Heymann MA, Rudolph AM, Silverman NH. NEJM 1976"]
},
"desflurane": {
  "origin": "Desflurane was one of hundreds of fluorinated compounds made in the 1960s and 1970s by Ross Terrell and colleagues at Airco (Ohio Medical Products), the same program that produced enflurane and isoflurane. It sat on the shelf for years because it was hard to make (it requires elemental fluorine) and boils near room temperature, so a normal vaporizer could not handle it. Interest returned in the 1980s as outpatient surgery grew and anesthesiologists wanted quicker wake-ups. Desflurane, with very low blood solubility, gave just that. A special heated, pressurized vaporizer was built for it, and the FDA approved it as Suprane in 1992. In recent years its very high global warming potential has led many hospitals and some countries to phase it out.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Ross Terrell's group synthesizes fluorinated ethers, including desflurane."], ["1980s", "Desflurane is revisited for fast-recovery anesthesia."], ["1992", "FDA approves desflurane (Suprane)."], ["2020s", "Health systems begin phasing out desflurane for climate reasons."]],
  "cell": "Like other volatile anesthetics, desflurane acts on several ion channels rather than one receptor. It enhances inhibitory GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride flow and hyperpolarizing neurons. It also enhances glycine receptors, especially in the spinal cord. It activates two-pore-domain potassium channels such as TREK-1, which also hyperpolarize neurons. It inhibits excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors and neuronal nicotinic receptors. The combined effect dampens neural activity in the brain to produce unconsciousness and amnesia, while effects in the spinal cord produce immobility to pain. Its very low blood:gas partition coefficient (about 0.42) means only a little needs to dissolve in blood before brain levels rise, so it acts and wears off fast.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Rapid onset of unconsciousness and amnesia, with fast wake-up and return of thinking after it is stopped."], ["Spinal cord", "Glycine and GABA-A effects in the cord prevent movement in response to surgical pain."], ["Airway", "It is pungent and irritates the airway, causing cough, breath holding and laryngospasm, so it is not used for gas induction."], ["Heart and vessels", "It lowers blood pressure by vasodilation; rapid increases in concentration can trigger a sympathetic surge with fast heart rate and higher pressure."], ["Breathing", "It depresses breathing and blunts the response to carbon dioxide."], ["Brain blood flow", "It dilates cerebral vessels, raising cerebral blood flow and possibly intracranial pressure."]],
  "journey": "Desflurane is breathed in and moves quickly from the lungs into the blood and then the brain. Its low solubility in blood and tissues means brain levels track inhaled levels closely, with onset in 1 to 2 minutes. It is almost entirely removed by breathing it out unchanged; only about 0.02 percent is metabolized in the liver, the least of any modern volatile agent. Emergence often happens within about 5 to 10 minutes of stopping. Because it boils at about 23 degrees C, it needs a heated, pressurized vaporizer.",
  "sidefx": "Malignant hyperthermia can occur in genetically susceptible people because volatile agents trigger uncontrolled calcium release through mutant ryanodine receptors in muscle; dantrolene is the antidote. In children with some muscle diseases, it can cause severe hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest. Its pungency explains cough and laryngospasm. Vasodilation lowers blood pressure, and fast concentration increases cause sympathetic surges. It can prolong QT. Dry CO2 absorbents can break it down to carbon monoxide. Postoperative nausea and vomiting are common.",
  "fun": "Desflurane has the highest global warming potential of the common anesthetic gases, and an hour of anesthesia with it has been compared to driving a car hundreds of miles.",
  "src": ["Suprane (desflurane) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"isoflurane": {
  "origin": "Isoflurane was synthesized in 1965 by Ross Terrell at Airco as part of a large search for safer fluorinated ether anesthetics. It is a structural isomer of enflurane, which was introduced first. Isoflurane's release was delayed by a mid-1970s study suggesting it caused liver cancer in mice. That finding could not be confirmed when the work was repeated, and the FDA approved isoflurane as Forane in 1979. It became one of the most widely used inhaled anesthetics in the world because it is stable, cheap, and causes little heart rhythm sensitization or liver toxicity compared with halothane. It is still common in human and veterinary anesthesia and in ICU sedation in some countries.",
  "timeline": [["1965", "Ross Terrell synthesizes isoflurane at Airco."], ["1970s", "A mouse cancer concern delays approval and is later not confirmed."], ["1979", "FDA approves isoflurane (Forane)."]],
  "cell": "Isoflurane acts on several targets at once. It enhances GABA-A receptors, increasing chloride currents that quiet neurons, and enhances glycine receptors in the spinal cord. It opens two-pore-domain potassium channels, hyperpolarizing neurons. It inhibits excitatory NMDA glutamate receptors and neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Together these effects reduce neural activity and communication in the cortex, thalamus and brainstem, producing unconsciousness and amnesia, while spinal cord actions produce immobility. Its blood:gas partition coefficient of about 1.4 is higher than desflurane or sevoflurane, so it takes a bit longer to build up and wash out. Its MAC is about 1.15 percent in middle-aged adults.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Produces unconsciousness and amnesia; above about 1 MAC it dilates cerebral vessels and raises cerebral blood flow."], ["Spinal cord", "Prevents movement in response to surgical stimulation through glycine and GABA-A actions."], ["Heart and vessels", "Lowers blood pressure mainly by reducing systemic vascular resistance, with relatively preserved cardiac output and sometimes a faster heart rate."], ["Airways", "It is a bronchodilator, but its pungent smell irritates the airway during inhaled induction."], ["Breathing", "Depresses ventilation and the response to carbon dioxide and low oxygen."], ["Muscle and uterus", "Relaxes skeletal muscle and the uterus, which can increase bleeding after delivery."]],
  "journey": "Isoflurane is inhaled and taken up from the lungs into the blood, with surgical anesthesia in about 7 to 10 minutes. Its moderate solubility means a bit more must dissolve in blood and tissues before brain levels rise. Nearly all of it is breathed out unchanged. About 0.2 percent is metabolized in the liver by CYP2E1 to trifluoroacetic acid and small amounts of fluoride, far less than halothane. Wake-up typically takes 10 to 20 minutes after stopping, longer after long cases as fat stores release it.",
  "sidefx": "Malignant hyperthermia can be triggered in susceptible people with ryanodine receptor mutations, causing runaway muscle calcium release, rigidity and hyperthermia; dantrolene is the antidote. In some muscle disorders, it can cause hyperkalemic cardiac arrest. Vasodilation lowers blood pressure. Raised cerebral blood flow can increase intracranial pressure. Rare liver injury may relate to immune reactions to trifluoroacetylated liver proteins, the same mechanism as halothane hepatitis but far less common. Dry CO2 absorbents can produce carbon monoxide. Nausea, shivering and cough are common.",
  "src": ["Forane (isoflurane) FDA label", "Miller's Anesthesia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"iloprost": {
  "origin": "Iloprost was developed by the German company Schering AG in the late 1970s and 1980s as a stable synthetic version of prostacyclin, the vessel-relaxing molecule discovered by Moncada and Vane in 1976. Natural prostacyclin lasts only minutes, while iloprost was chemically stabilized. It was first used in Europe as an IV drug for severe peripheral vascular disease and Raynaud phenomenon. An inhaled form for pulmonary arterial hypertension, Ventavis, was approved by the FDA in 2004. Doctors in Europe also used IV iloprost for severe frostbite, and studies suggested it reduced amputations. In February 2024 the FDA approved IV iloprost (Aurlumyn) to reduce the risk of finger or toe amputation in adults with severe frostbite, the first FDA-approved frostbite treatment.",
  "timeline": [["1976", "Prostacyclin is discovered."], ["1980s", "Schering AG develops iloprost, a stable prostacyclin analog."], ["2004", "FDA approves inhaled iloprost (Ventavis) for PAH."], ["2024", "FDA approves IV iloprost (Aurlumyn) for severe frostbite."]],
  "cell": "Iloprost binds the prostacyclin (IP) receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle and platelets. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP. In smooth muscle, cAMP activates protein kinase A, which lowers calcium and inhibits myosin light chain kinase, so arteries and arterioles relax. In platelets, higher cAMP blocks activation, shape change and aggregation. In frostbite, cold injury damages endothelium and leads to vasospasm, platelet clumping and microvascular clots that cut off blood flow after rewarming. Iloprost reopens small vessels, keeps platelets from plugging them, and may protect endothelial cells and reduce inflammation. Better microcirculation preserves tissue that would otherwise die.",
  "body": [["Small vessels", "Vasodilation and platelet inhibition restore blood flow in frostbitten fingers and toes, lowering the chance of amputation."], ["Lung arteries", "Inhaled iloprost dilates pulmonary arteries, lowering pulmonary vascular resistance in PAH."], ["Blood pressure", "Systemic vasodilation can lower blood pressure, sometimes causing dizziness or fainting."], ["Head and face", "Headache and flushing come from dilation of head and neck vessels."], ["Heart", "Palpitations and fast heart rate can occur as a reflex to lower blood pressure."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common prostanoid effects."]],
  "journey": "For frostbite, iloprost is given as an IV infusion over several hours daily for several days, adjusted to tolerability. It reaches steady state within minutes of a rate change, and effects fade within about 30 minutes after stopping because its half-life is short, about 20 to 30 minutes. It is about 60 percent bound to plasma proteins. It is broken down mainly by beta-oxidation of its carboxyl side chain, not by CYP enzymes, and metabolites leave mostly in urine. Moderate or severe liver disease slows its breakdown.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly all side effects come from IP receptor activation in the wrong place. Systemic vasodilation causes headache, flushing, dizziness and low blood pressure. Reflex sympathetic activation causes palpitations and fast heart rate. Prostanoid effects on the gut cause nausea and vomiting. Platelet inhibition raises bleeding risk, especially with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs. These effects are the reason the infusion is started low and adjusted. There is no antidote, but the short half-life means effects fade quickly when the infusion is slowed or stopped.",
  "src": ["Aurlumyn (iloprost) FDA label", "Ventavis (iloprost) FDA label", "Cauchy E et al. NEJM 2011"]
},
"cerliponase-alfa": {
  "origin": "Batten disease, a group of inherited brain disorders also called neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses, is named after the British neurologist Frederick Batten, who described affected children in 1903. CLN2 disease is the late-infantile form, in which toddlers lose speech, walking and vision and develop seizures. In 1997 Peter Lobel, David Sleat and colleagues at Rutgers identified the missing enzyme as tripeptidyl peptidase 1 (TPP1). Because a replacement enzyme cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, BioMarin Pharmaceutical developed a recombinant TPP1 to be delivered directly into the fluid-filled spaces of the brain through a surgically placed reservoir. The FDA approved cerliponase alfa (Brineura) in April 2017, the first treatment for CLN2 and the first enzyme therapy given into the brain's ventricles.",
  "timeline": [["1903", "Frederick Batten describes the disease later named after him."], ["1997", "Tripeptidyl peptidase 1 is identified as the enzyme missing in CLN2 disease."], ["2017", "FDA approves cerliponase alfa (Brineura), the first CLN2 treatment."], ["2024", "Approval expands to younger children, including those before symptoms."]],
  "cell": "Tripeptidyl peptidase 1 (TPP1) is a lysosomal enzyme that clips three-amino-acid pieces off the ends of proteins being recycled. Without it, waxy storage material called ceroid lipopigment builds up in the lysosomes of neurons, and neurons, especially in the cortex and cerebellum, die. Cerliponase alfa is a recombinant human TPP1 proenzyme, an inactive precursor. Delivered into the cerebrospinal fluid, it is taken up by neurons and glia through mannose-6-phosphate receptors and carried to lysosomes. The acidic environment there cuts off the pro-segment and activates the enzyme, which breaks down the stored proteins. Less storage slows neuron death, so loss of motor and language skills slows.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Restoring TPP1 activity in neurons slowed the decline in walking and language compared with natural history data."], ["Heart rhythm", "Slow heart rate and low blood pressure can occur during infusions, possibly from effects on brainstem autonomic centers."], ["Brain fluid", "CSF protein and white cell counts can rise, reflecting irritation from the infusions."], ["Nervous system", "Seizures, irritability and headache were reported, though seizures are also part of CLN2."], ["Device site", "The implanted reservoir and catheter can become infected or fail, leading to meningitis."]],
  "journey": "Cerliponase alfa is given every other week as an infusion into the lateral ventricle of the brain through an implanted reservoir and catheter, followed by a flush. This bypasses the blood-brain barrier completely. It spreads through the cerebrospinal fluid and into brain tissue, with CSF levels peaking at the end of infusion. Only small amounts reach the blood. Like other proteins it is eventually broken down in cells. Its effect is measured over months.",
  "sidefx": "Many risks relate to the delivery route. The implanted device is a foreign body, so infection, meningitis, leaks and blockage can occur, requiring strict sterile technique. Infusing fluid into the ventricles may affect autonomic centers, explaining slow heart rate and low blood pressure, which is why heart monitoring is used in some patients. As a foreign protein, it can trigger hypersensitivity including anaphylaxis, which is the boxed warning. Fever, vomiting and CSF changes reflect irritation of the brain lining. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Brineura (cerliponase alfa) FDA label", "Sleat DE et al. Science 1997", "Schulz A et al. NEJM 2018"]
},
"delandistrogene-moxeparvovec": {
  "origin": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is named after the French neurologist Guillaume Duchenne, who described it in the 1860s. In 1986 and 1987 Louis Kunkel's lab at Boston Children's Hospital identified the gene and its protein, dystrophin, a giant rod-shaped protein that anchors muscle fibers. The dystrophin gene is too large to fit in an adeno-associated virus (AAV) vector, so researchers built shortened micro-dystrophin genes that keep the most important parts. At Nationwide Children's Hospital in Ohio, Jerry Mendell and Louise Rodino-Klapac developed a micro-dystrophin gene in an AAVrh74 vector with a muscle-specific promoter. Sarepta Therapeutics licensed and developed it. The FDA granted accelerated approval to delandistrogene moxeparvovec (Elevidys) in June 2023 for young ambulatory boys and expanded it in 2024. In 2025, reports of deaths from acute liver failure led to stronger safety warnings and limits on its use.",
  "timeline": [["1860s", "Guillaume Duchenne describes the muscular dystrophy named after him."], ["1987", "Louis Kunkel's group identifies dystrophin."], ["2023", "FDA grants accelerated approval to Elevidys for ambulatory boys aged 4 to 5."], ["2024", "FDA expands the Elevidys indication."], ["2025", "Fatal acute liver failure cases lead to stronger safety warnings."]],
  "cell": "In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, mutations in the DMD gene prevent production of dystrophin. Dystrophin links the actin skeleton inside muscle fibers to the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex in the cell membrane, which connects to the matrix outside. Without it, the membrane tears with each contraction, calcium floods in, and fibers die and are replaced by fat and scar. Elevidys is a non-replicating AAVrh74 vector carrying a micro-dystrophin gene controlled by the MHCK7 promoter, which is active in skeletal and heart muscle. After one IV infusion, the vector enters muscle cells and its DNA stays in the nucleus mostly as an episome, not integrated into chromosomes. Muscle cells then make micro-dystrophin, a shortened protein that keeps the actin-binding and membrane-anchoring regions, aiming to stabilize the membrane and slow muscle damage.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Micro-dystrophin is expressed in muscle fibers, intended to protect the membrane and slow functional decline."], ["Heart", "The promoter is active in heart muscle; myocarditis has been reported, possibly from immune reactions to vector or transgene."], ["Liver", "AAV vectors are taken up heavily by the liver, and immune responses can cause acute liver injury, including fatal liver failure."], ["Immune system", "Corticosteroids are given around the infusion to blunt immune reactions; preexisting antibodies to AAVrh74 can block treatment."], ["Muscle inflammation", "Immune-mediated myositis has occurred in patients with certain DMD gene deletions."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, nausea and poor appetite are common in the weeks after infusion."]],
  "journey": "Elevidys is given once as an IV infusion, with the amount based on body weight. The vector spreads through the blood, is taken up by muscle cells throughout the body and also by the liver. Micro-dystrophin expression builds over weeks. The vector DNA persists as episomes in long-lived muscle cells and is meant to last, though it can be lost as damaged fibers die. Vector DNA is shed in urine, saliva and especially stool for weeks. It is not metabolized like a small molecule.",
  "sidefx": "The liver takes up much of the vector, and the immune system can attack transduced liver cells, causing acute liver injury and, in some cases, fatal liver failure, so steroids are given and liver tests followed weekly. Immune responses to the capsid or transgene can also inflame heart muscle (myocarditis) and skeletal muscle (myositis). Vomiting and nausea are common early. Complement activation and thrombocytopenia can occur. Steroids raise infection risk. Because it is a one-time gene transfer, its effects cannot be reversed.",
  "src": ["Elevidys (delandistrogene moxeparvovec-rokl) FDA label", "Hoffman EP, Brown RH, Kunkel LM. Cell 1987"]
},
"fosdenopterin": {
  "origin": "Molybdenum cofactor deficiency (MoCD) is an ultra-rare inherited disease in which newborns develop intractable seizures and severe brain damage within days. In type A, the first step of cofactor synthesis, run by the MOCS1 gene, fails, so the intermediate called cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate (cPMP) is never made. The German biochemist Guenter Schwarz and colleagues, studying this pathway, showed that cPMP given back could rescue mice lacking MOCS1. In 2008 an infant in Australia with MoCD type A received cPMP purified from bacteria, and doctors reported a dramatic biochemical response. A synthetic version, fosdenopterin, was developed and later brought forward by Origin Biosciences (part of BridgeBio). The FDA approved fosdenopterin (Nulibry) in February 2021, the first therapy for MoCD type A, to reduce the risk of death.",
  "timeline": [["2004", "cPMP is shown to rescue MOCS1-deficient mice."], ["2008", "An infant with MoCD type A is first treated with cPMP."], ["2021", "FDA approves fosdenopterin (Nulibry), the first therapy for MoCD type A."]],
  "cell": "Molybdenum cofactor is built in steps. MOCS1 turns GTP into cyclic pyranopterin monophosphate (cPMP). Later steps add sulfur to make molybdopterin, then load molybdenum to make the finished cofactor. The cofactor is needed by sulfite oxidase, xanthine dehydrogenase and aldehyde oxidase. Sulfite oxidase is the critical one: it converts toxic sulfite to sulfate. Without it, sulfite and S-sulfocysteine build up. S-sulfocysteine resembles glutamate and overstimulates NMDA receptors, causing excitotoxic neuron death and seizures. Fosdenopterin is synthetic cPMP. Given by vein, it enters cells and supplies the missing intermediate, so the remaining steps can make cofactor. Sulfite oxidase activity returns, sulfite and S-sulfocysteine fall, and further brain injury is limited.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Lower S-sulfocysteine and sulfite reduce excitotoxic injury, decreasing seizures and slowing encephalopathy when started early."], ["Survival", "Treated patients survived longer than untreated natural history controls."], ["Urine markers", "Urinary S-sulfocysteine and xanthine levels move toward normal, showing the cofactor is working."], ["Skin", "Animal studies suggested phototoxicity, so sun and UV exposure are limited."], ["Central line", "Daily infusions need long-term IV access, which brings a risk of catheter infections and bacteremia."]],
  "journey": "Fosdenopterin is given as a daily IV infusion, ideally starting soon after diagnosis in newborns. It peaks at the end of the infusion and has low binding to plasma proteins. It is taken up by cells and incorporated into molybdenum cofactor, with the rest broken down nonenzymatically. Its half-life is short, about 1.2 to 1.7 hours, and it is mainly cleared by the kidneys. Biochemical improvement appears within days.",
  "sidefx": "Most serious problems relate to the long-term central line needed for daily infusions: catheter infections, bacteremia and line complications. Fever, respiratory infections, vomiting and gastroenteritis were common in this very ill group. Animal studies showed a potential for phototoxicity, so the label calls for limiting sun and artificial UV exposure. The drug cannot reverse brain damage that has already happened, which is why early treatment matters. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Nulibry (fosdenopterin) FDA label", "Veldman A et al. Pediatrics 2010"]
},
"atidarsagene-autotemcel": {
  "origin": "Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) is an inherited disease in which a missing enzyme, arylsulfatase A (ARSA), lets sulfatide lipids build up and destroy myelin, the insulating layer on nerves. Children with early-onset MLD lose the ability to walk, talk and swallow and often die in childhood. Bone marrow transplants helped only some patients. At the San Raffaele Telethon Institute for Gene Therapy (SR-Tiget) in Milan, Italy, Alessandra Biffi, Luigi Naldini and colleagues developed a way to add a working ARSA gene to a child's own blood stem cells with a lentiviral vector. Their 2013 report in Science showed protection in children treated before symptoms. Orchard Therapeutics developed the therapy, approved in the EU as Libmeldy in 2020. The FDA approved atidarsagene autotemcel (Lenmeldy) in March 2024, the first FDA-approved treatment for MLD.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Arylsulfatase A deficiency is identified as the cause of MLD."], ["2013", "Biffi and colleagues report lentiviral stem cell gene therapy for MLD in Science."], ["2020", "The therapy is approved in the EU as Libmeldy."], ["2024", "FDA approves atidarsagene autotemcel (Lenmeldy)."]],
  "cell": "ARSA is a lysosomal enzyme that removes a sulfate group from sulfatides, a lipid of myelin. Without it, sulfatides build up in oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells and macrophages, killing myelin-making cells and causing progressive demyelination. To make Lenmeldy, a child's own CD34+ blood stem and progenitor cells are collected and, in a lab, a lentiviral vector inserts a working ARSA gene into their DNA. The child receives busulfan chemotherapy to clear space in the bone marrow, then gets the modified cells back by IV. The cells engraft and make ARSA at high levels. Some of their progeny become microglia-like cells in the brain. These secrete ARSA, which neighboring brain cells take up through mannose-6-phosphate receptors (cross-correction), so sulfatides can be broken down and myelin is protected.",
  "body": [["Brain and nerves", "Supplying ARSA from engrafted cells and brain microglia slows or prevents demyelination when given before major symptoms."], ["Motor and cognitive function", "Many children treated early kept the ability to walk and develop language, unlike untreated siblings."], ["Bone marrow", "Busulfan conditioning wipes out existing marrow, causing a period of very low blood counts and infection risk."], ["Liver", "Veno-occlusive disease can occur, mainly linked to busulfan conditioning."], ["Blood vessels", "Thrombosis and thromboembolic events, including stroke, have been reported."], ["Long-term", "Because the vector integrates into DNA, there is a theoretical risk of cancer from insertional mutagenesis, so monitoring continues for years."]],
  "journey": "Lenmeldy is a one-time IV infusion of the child's own gene-modified stem cells, given after busulfan conditioning. The cells home to the bone marrow and engraft over weeks, then make blood cells that carry the ARSA gene for life. ARSA activity in blood rises over months, and gene-modified cells reach the brain as microglia. Because the cells divide and persist, there is no metabolism or elimination in the usual sense.",
  "sidefx": "Many early side effects, such as febrile neutropenia, mouth sores and infections, come from busulfan conditioning, which destroys the bone marrow before new cells engraft. Veno-occlusive disease of the liver is also a conditioning effect. Delayed platelet recovery causes bleeding risk, and engraftment failure is possible. Thrombosis and encephalitis have been reported. Insertional oncogenesis is a theoretical risk because the lentiviral vector integrates into chromosomes, so blood cancer monitoring continues for at least 15 years. Hypersensitivity to the DMSO preservative can occur.",
  "fun": "At its US launch, Lenmeldy carried a list price of about 4.25 million dollars, making it one of the most expensive medicines in the world.",
  "src": ["Lenmeldy (atidarsagene autotemcel) FDA label", "Biffi A et al. Science 2013"]
}
,
"alirocumab": {
  "origin": "The PCSK9 story began in 2003, when Marianne Abifadel, Catherine Boileau and colleagues in Paris found that gain-of-function PCSK9 mutations cause familial high cholesterol. In 2005 and 2006, Jonathan Cohen and Helen Hobbs in Dallas showed that people with loss-of-function PCSK9 variants have low LDL cholesterol for life and far fewer heart attacks, and that some healthy people have no working PCSK9 at all. That made PCSK9 an attractive drug target. Regeneron used its VelocImmune mice, engineered to make fully human antibodies, to create alirocumab, developed with Sanofi. The FDA approved alirocumab (Praluent) in July 2015, about a month before evolocumab, making it the first PCSK9 inhibitor approved in the US. In 2018 the ODYSSEY OUTCOMES trial showed fewer cardiovascular events after acute coronary syndrome.",
  "timeline": [["2003", "PCSK9 mutations are linked to familial hypercholesterolemia."], ["2006", "Cohen and Hobbs show low PCSK9 function protects against coronary disease."], ["2015", "FDA approves alirocumab (Praluent), the first US PCSK9 inhibitor."], ["2018", "ODYSSEY OUTCOMES shows fewer cardiovascular events after ACS."]],
  "cell": "LDL receptors on liver cells bind LDL particles from blood and carry them inside. Normally the receptor then releases LDL in the endosome and recycles to the surface to catch more, many times over. PCSK9 is a protein released by the liver that binds the LDL receptor on the cell surface. When PCSK9 is attached, the receptor is sent to the lysosome and destroyed instead of recycling. Alirocumab is a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody that binds free PCSK9 in blood and blocks its binding to LDL receptors. More receptors survive and recycle, so the liver clears more LDL from blood. LDL cholesterol falls by roughly half or more, and lipoprotein(a) falls modestly too. Statins raise PCSK9 levels, which is part of why the two work well together.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Hepatocytes keep more LDL receptors on their surface and take up more LDL from blood."], ["Blood lipids", "LDL cholesterol falls by about 45 to 60 percent, and lipoprotein(a) also falls."], ["Arteries and heart", "Lower LDL slows atherosclerosis; after ACS it reduced heart attacks, strokes and related events."], ["Nose and throat", "Nasopharyngitis and flu-like symptoms were slightly more common in trials."], ["Skin and immune system", "Injection site reactions and, rarely, hypersensitivity reactions such as angioedema and vasculitis can occur."]],
  "journey": "Alirocumab is injected under the skin every 2 or 4 weeks, often by the patient with a pen. It is absorbed slowly through the lymph and peaks in about 3 to 7 days. At lower levels it is cleared mainly by binding PCSK9 (target-mediated clearance); at higher levels, by the usual slow breakdown of antibodies into peptides and amino acids. Its half-life is about 17 to 20 days. LDL falls within 1 to 2 weeks. It is not metabolized by CYP enzymes, so drug interactions are minimal.",
  "sidefx": "Because its action is confined to PCSK9 in blood, side effects are few. Injection site redness, itching and swelling come from local reactions to the injected protein. Allergic reactions, including rare angioedema and hypersensitivity vasculitis, reflect the immune system reacting to the antibody. Cold-like symptoms were slightly more common. Very low LDL levels have not been linked to clear harm in trials, consistent with people born without working PCSK9. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Some of the evidence for PCSK9 inhibition came from a healthy woman in Texas, an aerobics instructor and mother, who had two broken copies of PCSK9 and an LDL cholesterol level far lower than normal.",
  "src": ["Praluent (alirocumab) FDA label", "Abifadel M et al. Nature Genetics 2003", "Schwartz GG et al. ODYSSEY OUTCOMES, NEJM 2018"]
},
"prademagene-zamikeracel": {
  "origin": "Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (RDEB) is a severe inherited skin disease in which the skin blisters and tears with slight friction, leaving chronic wounds and a high risk of skin cancer. It is caused by mutations in COL7A1, the gene for type VII collagen, which forms anchoring fibrils that attach the outer skin to the layer below. At Stanford University, a team including Peter Marinkovich and Jean Tang took patients' own skin cells, added a working COL7A1 gene with a retroviral vector, and grew the cells into sheets that could be grafted onto chronic wounds. Early results showed durable healing in many grafted areas. Abeona Therapeutics developed the therapy, and the FDA approved prademagene zamikeracel (Zevaskyn) in April 2025 for wounds in adults and children with RDEB.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Mutations in COL7A1 are identified as the cause of dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa."], ["2016", "Stanford researchers report early results of gene-corrected skin grafts in RDEB."], ["2025", "FDA approves prademagene zamikeracel (Zevaskyn)."]],
  "cell": "Type VII collagen is made by keratinocytes and fibroblasts and assembles into anchoring fibrils just below the basement membrane. These fibrils loop into the dermis and hold the epidermis in place. In RDEB, faulty or absent type VII collagen means no working anchoring fibrils, so the layers separate and blisters form with minor friction. To make Zevaskyn, a small skin biopsy is taken from the patient. Keratinocytes are grown and transduced with a replication-incompetent gamma-retroviral vector carrying the full-length COL7A1 gene, which integrates into the cells' DNA. The cells are grown into sheets and surgically placed on chronic wounds. The grafted cells make functional type VII collagen, which forms anchoring fibrils and attaches the new skin firmly, so the wounds can close and resist blistering.",
  "body": [["Skin wounds", "Grafted gene-corrected sheets cover chronic wounds and produce type VII collagen, leading to wound closure in many treated areas."], ["Skin structure", "New anchoring fibrils hold the epidermis to the dermis, so treated skin blisters less."], ["Pain and itch", "Healed wounds caused less pain; procedural pain and itching were the most common side effects."], ["Immune system", "Antibiotics and reagents used in manufacture can cause hypersensitivity in sensitive patients."], ["Long-term", "Because the vector integrates into DNA, there is a theoretical risk of cancer from insertional mutagenesis."]],
  "journey": "Zevaskyn is not swallowed or injected. Sheets of the patient's own gene-corrected keratinocytes are placed onto prepared wounds in a surgical procedure under anesthesia. The cells attach and grow as part of the patient's skin over weeks. Because it is a living graft rather than a drug in blood, there is essentially no systemic absorption, distribution or elimination. Wound closure is judged months later, and many grafts remain durable.",
  "sidefx": "Procedural pain and itching come from the surgery and wound healing. Grafts may fail to take. Hypersensitivity can occur to vancomycin, amikacin or other reagents used in making the sheets. Because the gamma-retroviral vector integrates into the cells' DNA, it could in theory activate a cancer gene, so long-term monitoring is needed. Human- and bovine-derived reagents carry a very small theoretical risk of passing on infections. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Zevaskyn (prademagene zamikeracel) FDA label", "Siprashvili Z et al. JAMA 2016"]
},
"ibutilide": {
  "origin": "Ibutilide was developed at the Upjohn Company in Kalamazoo, Michigan, in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Researchers were seeking a class III antiarrhythmic that could be given IV to quickly convert recent-onset atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter back to normal rhythm without the side effects of older drugs. Its chemistry is related to sotalol, but it has an unusual dual action, blocking potassium current and enhancing a late sodium current. The FDA approved ibutilide (Corvert) in 1995 for rapid conversion of AF or flutter of recent onset. It remains one of the few drugs approved specifically for chemical cardioversion, though its risk of torsades means it is given only with continuous ECG monitoring.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Upjohn researchers develop ibutilide."], ["1995", "FDA approves ibutilide (Corvert) for rapid conversion of atrial fibrillation or flutter."]],
  "cell": "Ibutilide prolongs repolarization by two mechanisms. It blocks the rapid component of the delayed rectifier potassium current (IKr, carried by hERG channels), which normally returns the cell to rest in phase 3. It also activates a slow inward sodium current during the plateau (phase 2). Both actions keep the cell depolarized longer, lengthening the action potential and effective refractory period in atrial and ventricular muscle. In atrial flutter and fibrillation, reentry circuits need tissue that has recovered and can be excited again. When refractory periods lengthen, the circuits run into tissue that is still refractory and die out, and sinus rhythm returns. It has little effect on heart rate or blood pressure.",
  "body": [["Atria", "Longer refractory periods break reentry circuits, converting recent-onset AF or flutter to sinus rhythm, flutter more often than AF."], ["Ventricles", "Prolonged repolarization lengthens the QT interval and can trigger torsades de pointes."], ["Heart rate and blood pressure", "Rate and blood pressure change little, unlike with many other antiarrhythmics."], ["Conduction system", "Bundle branch block and AV block can occasionally occur."], ["Head", "Headache is a common, mild side effect, likely from changes in vessel tone."]],
  "journey": "Ibutilide is given IV over about 10 minutes, and a second dose can be given if the rhythm has not converted. It is not used orally because of very high first-pass metabolism. It spreads widely into tissues and is about 40 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by oxidation, not through CYP3A4 or CYP2D6. About 82 percent leaves in urine, mostly as metabolites. The half-life averages about 6 hours with a wide range. Conversion usually happens within about 30 minutes.",
  "sidefx": "The key danger is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (torsades de pointes), usually within an hour of the infusion. It happens because prolonged repolarization allows early afterdepolarizations, extra electrical activity during the plateau, which can trigger torsades. Risk is higher in women, in heart failure, and with low potassium or magnesium. This is the reason for the boxed warning, correction of electrolytes before use and ECG monitoring for at least 4 hours. Magnesium sulfate and electrical defibrillation are used for torsades. Headache and low blood pressure can occur.",
  "src": ["Corvert (ibutilide) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"nesiritide": {
  "origin": "B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) was discovered in 1988 by Tetsuji Sudoh, Kenji Kangawa, Hisayuki Matsuo and colleagues in Japan, who isolated it from pig brain, which is why it was first called brain natriuretic peptide. It was soon found to come mostly from the ventricles of the heart, released when the heart muscle is stretched. The California company Scios made recombinant human BNP using E. coli and called it nesiritide. The FDA approved nesiritide (Natrecor) in 2001 for acute decompensated heart failure with breathlessness at rest. Concerns about kidney function and survival then arose. The large ASCEND-HF trial, published in 2011, found no benefit on death or rehospitalization and more hypotension, so it is not a first-line therapy today.",
  "timeline": [["1988", "BNP is discovered in pig brain by Sudoh, Kangawa, Matsuo and colleagues."], ["2001", "FDA approves nesiritide (Natrecor) for acute decompensated heart failure."], ["2011", "ASCEND-HF shows no mortality or rehospitalization benefit."]],
  "cell": "Natriuretic peptides are the heart's own way of unloading itself. Nesiritide is identical to human BNP. It binds natriuretic peptide receptor-A (NPR-A), a receptor whose inside portion is a guanylate cyclase enzyme. Binding activates the enzyme to make cyclic GMP (cGMP). In vascular smooth muscle, cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes the muscle, dilating veins and arteries. This lowers preload and afterload and reduces pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. In the kidney, cGMP signaling increases GFR and reduces sodium reabsorption, producing modest natriuresis. It also suppresses renin, aldosterone and sympathetic activity.",
  "body": [["Veins", "Venodilation lowers preload and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, easing lung congestion and breathlessness."], ["Arteries", "Arterial dilation lowers afterload and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "It causes modest sodium and water loss, but falling blood pressure can worsen kidney function in some patients."], ["Hormones", "It suppresses renin, aldosterone and norepinephrine, opposing the neurohormonal activation of heart failure."], ["Heart", "Lower filling pressures reduce wall stress; it does not directly stimulate contraction."]],
  "journey": "Nesiritide is given IV, usually as a bolus followed by a continuous infusion. Most of its effect appears within about 15 minutes. It is removed from blood in three ways: binding to the clearance receptor NPR-C, which pulls it into cells for breakdown; breakdown by the enzyme neprilysin; and a small amount of kidney filtration. Its half-life is about 18 minutes, but its effect on blood pressure can last hours after the infusion stops.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure is the main side effect and follows directly from cGMP-mediated vasodilation; it can last longer than expected because the hemodynamic effect outlasts plasma levels. Drops in blood pressure can reduce kidney perfusion, explaining rises in creatinine. Headache, nausea and back pain are common. Hypersensitivity can occur with a recombinant protein. Because nesiritide is identical to BNP, it raises BNP measurements, so NT-proBNP is used to track heart failure during therapy. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and blood pressure supported.",
  "src": ["Natrecor (nesiritide) FDA label", "Sudoh T et al. Nature 1988", "O'Connor CM et al. ASCEND-HF, NEJM 2011"]
},
"fenoldopam": {
  "origin": "Fenoldopam was developed at SmithKline Beckman (later SmithKline Beecham) in the late 1970s and 1980s, during research on dopamine receptors. Dopamine itself dilates kidney blood vessels at low levels but activates beta and alpha receptors at higher levels. Researchers wanted a drug that selectively hit the dopamine D1 receptor, which dilates blood vessels, without these other effects. Fenoldopam, a benzazepine derivative, was the result. An oral form was tested but did not succeed because of poor and variable absorption. The IV form was approved by the FDA in 1997 as Corlopam for short-term, in-hospital management of severe hypertension, including hypertensive emergencies.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Fenoldopam is developed at SmithKline as a selective D1 agonist."], ["1997", "FDA approves IV fenoldopam (Corlopam) for severe hypertension."]],
  "cell": "Fenoldopam is a selective agonist at peripheral dopamine D1 receptors. D1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gs-coupled. Activation turns on adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP, which activates protein kinase A, lowers calcium and relaxes the smooth muscle. This dilates arterioles, especially in the renal, mesenteric and coronary beds, so systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure fall. In the kidney, D1 activation increases renal blood flow and also acts on tubule cells to reduce sodium reabsorption, causing natriuresis and diuresis. It does not act on D2 or beta receptors and does not cross the blood-brain barrier. It has some alpha-2 adrenergic binding.",
  "body": [["Arterioles", "D1-mediated vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Renal arterioles dilate, raising renal blood flow, and tubular D1 effects cause natriuresis and diuresis."], ["Heart", "Falling blood pressure triggers reflex tachycardia; ST and T wave changes can occur."], ["Eyes", "It can raise intraocular pressure, a concern in glaucoma."], ["Electrolytes", "Natriuresis and diuresis can lower potassium within hours."], ["Head and skin", "Vasodilation causes headache and flushing."]],
  "journey": "Fenoldopam is given only as a continuous IV infusion. It acts within about 5 minutes and reaches steady state in about 20 minutes. Its half-life is very short, about 5 minutes, so effects fade within about an hour after the infusion stops. The liver breaks it down by conjugation (methylation, glucuronidation and sulfation) rather than CYP enzymes. About 90 percent leaves in urine as metabolites.",
  "sidefx": "Low blood pressure follows directly from vasodilation. Reflex sympathetic activation causes fast heart rate; combining it with beta blockers can remove that reflex and cause unexpected hypotension. Increased sodium and water loss lowers potassium. Raised intraocular pressure occurs through mechanisms that are not fully clear. Headache and flushing reflect vasodilation of head and skin vessels. The product contains sodium metabisulfite, which can cause reactions in sulfite-sensitive people. There is no antidote; because of the short half-life, effects resolve quickly after stopping.",
  "src": ["Corlopam (fenoldopam) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"terlipressin": {
  "origin": "Vasopressin, the body's antidiuretic hormone, was synthesized in the 1950s by Vincent du Vigneaud, who won the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for his work on peptide hormones. Natural vasopressin is short-acting and has strong effects on the heart. In the 1960s, chemists in Czechoslovakia created terlipressin (triglycyl-lysine vasopressin), a modified form that releases lysine vasopressin slowly and is more selective for V1 receptors. It was used in Europe and elsewhere for decades for bleeding from esophageal varices and later for hepatorenal syndrome, a type of kidney failure in advanced liver disease. In the US it was not available until the CONFIRM trial showed better reversal of hepatorenal syndrome. The FDA approved terlipressin (Terlivaz) in September 2022, the first US-approved treatment for hepatorenal syndrome with rapid reduction in kidney function.",
  "timeline": [["1954", "Vincent du Vigneaud's group synthesizes vasopressin."], ["1960s", "Terlipressin is developed in Czechoslovakia."], ["1980s", "Terlipressin enters clinical use in Europe for variceal bleeding."], ["2021", "CONFIRM trial results are published."], ["2022", "FDA approves terlipressin (Terlivaz) for hepatorenal syndrome."]],
  "cell": "Terlipressin is both an active drug and a prodrug. Tissue peptidases slowly cut off its three glycine residues, releasing lysine vasopressin, which also acts on vasopressin receptors. It is about six times more selective for V1 (V1a) than V2 receptors. V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle are Gq-coupled: activation stimulates phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium release, causing constriction. In cirrhosis, the splanchnic (gut) circulation is massively dilated by nitric oxide and other mediators, which lowers effective blood volume and activates renin and sympathetic systems that clamp down on the kidneys. Terlipressin constricts the splanchnic vessels, shifting blood into the systemic circulation. Mean arterial pressure rises, renal perfusion improves, and kidney vasoconstriction eases, so GFR improves. Splanchnic constriction also lowers portal pressure.",
  "body": [["Splanchnic circulation", "V1-mediated constriction of gut vessels reduces portal inflow and pressure."], ["Systemic circulation", "Shifting blood into the central circulation raises mean arterial pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Better renal perfusion and reduced renin and sympathetic drive improve GFR and lower creatinine."], ["Lungs", "Higher central volume, especially with albumin, can cause fluid overload and serious or fatal respiratory failure."], ["Heart and limbs", "Vasoconstriction can cause cardiac, intestinal, peripheral and skin ischemia, and bradycardia."], ["Gut", "Abdominal pain, nausea and diarrhea reflect intestinal vasoconstriction and increased motility."]],
  "journey": "Terlipressin is given IV as a bolus or infusion. It acts within minutes. Tissue peptidases convert it to lysine vasopressin, which peaks about 1 to 2 hours later. The terlipressin half-life is about an hour and lysine vasopressin's about 3 hours, so effects last several hours. Peptidases break both down, and less than 1 percent is excreted unchanged in urine.",
  "sidefx": "The boxed warning is for serious or fatal respiratory failure. Raising central blood volume while patients also get albumin can flood the lungs, especially in people already overloaded or with severe acute-on-chronic liver failure, so oxygen levels are checked before each dose. Strong V1 vasoconstriction explains ischemia of the heart, gut, limbs and skin, and reflex bradycardia. Abdominal pain and diarrhea reflect gut vasoconstriction and motility. Lowering creatinine can change transplant priority scores. It can harm a fetus. There is no antidote; the drug is stopped and fluids reduced.",
  "src": ["Terlivaz (terlipressin) FDA label", "Wong F et al. CONFIRM trial, NEJM 2021"]
},
"conivaptan": {
  "origin": "Vasopressin receptor antagonists, called vaptans, grew out of the understanding of how vasopressin controls water. In the 1990s researchers at Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical in Japan (later Astellas) developed conivaptan, a nonpeptide molecule that blocks both V1a and V2 vasopressin receptors. Its main use is treating hyponatremia (low blood sodium), which is often driven by too much vasopressin, such as in SIADH and heart failure. The FDA approved IV conivaptan (Vaprisol) in December 2005 for euvolemic hyponatremia in hospitalized patients, making it the first vaptan approved in the US. In 2007 approval expanded to hypervolemic hyponatremia. Oral tolvaptan, a selective V2 blocker, followed in 2009.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Yamanouchi researchers develop conivaptan, a dual V1a/V2 antagonist."], ["2005", "FDA approves conivaptan (Vaprisol), the first US vaptan."], ["2007", "Approval expands to hypervolemic hyponatremia."]],
  "cell": "Vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) acts on V2 receptors on the collecting duct cells of the kidney. V2 is Gs-coupled: it raises cAMP, activating protein kinase A, which moves aquaporin-2 water channels into the cell's luminal membrane. Water then flows from the urine back into the blood, concentrating the urine. When vasopressin is too high, as in SIADH, too much water is kept and sodium is diluted. Conivaptan competitively blocks V2 receptors, so aquaporin-2 stays inside the cell and water is lost in the urine without much sodium or potassium (aquaresis). Serum sodium rises. It also blocks V1a receptors on vascular smooth muscle, where vasopressin causes constriction, so it can lower blood pressure.",
  "body": [["Kidneys", "V2 blockade causes free water excretion, producing dilute urine and raising serum sodium."], ["Blood sodium", "Serum sodium and osmolality rise, which can improve symptoms of hyponatremia such as confusion."], ["Blood vessels", "V1a blockade can relax vessels and cause hypotension."], ["Brain", "Correcting sodium too fast shrinks brain cells too quickly and can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome."], ["Infusion site", "Irritation and phlebitis are very common at the IV site."], ["Fluid balance", "Thirst, frequent urination and hypovolemia can occur from rapid water loss."]],
  "journey": "Conivaptan is given IV as a loading dose followed by a continuous infusion for a few days, through a large vein that is changed often. Serum sodium rises over the first day. It is about 99 percent bound to plasma proteins. The liver breaks it down by CYP3A4, and it is also a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, so it raises levels of many other drugs and is contraindicated with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors. It leaves mostly in feces, with some in urine. Its half-life is about 5 hours.",
  "sidefx": "The most serious risk is overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia. Brain cells adapt to low sodium by losing solutes, and a quick rise in sodium pulls water out of them, which can damage myelin in the pons and elsewhere (osmotic demyelination syndrome). This is why sodium is checked frequently. Rapid water loss can cause hypovolemia and low blood pressure, worsened by V1a blockade. Infusion site reactions are common because the drug irritates veins. Its CYP3A4 inhibition causes drug interactions. There is no antidote; the infusion is stopped and, if sodium has risen too fast, free water may be given.",
  "src": ["Vaprisol (conivaptan) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"glucarpidase": {
  "origin": "Glucarpidase is an enzyme from bacteria. In the 1970s British scientists, including Roger Sherwood, at Porton Down in England isolated an enzyme from a Pseudomonas bacterium that could break down folates and methotrexate. They called it carboxypeptidase G2. Doctors realized it could serve as a rescue for patients whose kidneys failed while receiving high-dose methotrexate, leaving toxic levels in the blood. For years it was available only through special programs. The British company BTG made a recombinant version produced in E. coli, and the FDA approved glucarpidase (Voraxaze) in January 2012 for toxic methotrexate levels in patients with delayed clearance due to impaired kidney function.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "Carboxypeptidase G2 is isolated from a Pseudomonas strain in the UK."], ["1990s", "The enzyme is used under compassionate programs for methotrexate toxicity."], ["2012", "FDA approves glucarpidase (Voraxaze)."]],
  "cell": "Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase, starving cells of reduced folates needed to make DNA. It is mostly cleared by the kidneys, and high doses can damage the kidney tubules, which then slows clearance further and causes severe bone marrow, gut and liver toxicity. Glucarpidase is a recombinant carboxypeptidase G2. It cuts the terminal glutamate off methotrexate, producing glutamate and DAMPA (4-deoxy-4-amino-N10-methylpteroic acid). DAMPA is inactive and is cleared by the liver, so it does not depend on the failing kidneys. Within about 15 minutes, plasma methotrexate falls by 97 percent or more. Because glucarpidase is a large protein, it stays outside cells and does not remove methotrexate already inside cells, so leucovorin rescue is still needed.",
  "body": [["Blood", "Extracellular methotrexate levels fall within minutes, limiting further exposure of tissues."], ["Bone marrow", "Reducing circulating methotrexate lowers the risk of severe neutropenia and other low counts."], ["Gut and mouth", "Lower exposure reduces mucositis and diarrhea from methotrexate damage to dividing lining cells."], ["Kidneys", "Lowering methotrexate gives the injured kidneys time to recover."], ["Leucovorin", "Glucarpidase also breaks down leucovorin, so the two are given at least 2 hours apart."], ["Lab tests", "DAMPA cross-reacts with methotrexate immunoassays, which overstate levels for about 48 hours."]],
  "journey": "Glucarpidase is given as a single IV injection. It stays mainly in the blood, since it is a large protein, and acts within 15 minutes. Its half-life is about 5.6 hours, longer in kidney impairment. It is eventually broken down by proteolysis. Because it cannot enter cells, methotrexate stored in tissues can leak back into blood afterward, causing a rebound in levels.",
  "sidefx": "Glucarpidase is generally well tolerated. Tingling, flushing, nausea, vomiting and headache can occur. Hypersensitivity can happen because it is a bacterial protein, and antibodies can form. It also destroys leucovorin, the folate given to rescue cells, so timing must be separated. The DAMPA it produces interferes with methotrexate immunoassays, so a chromatographic method is used for 48 hours. It does not reverse injury inside cells, so methotrexate toxicity can still occur.",
  "src": ["Voraxaze (glucarpidase) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"uridine-triacetate": {
  "origin": "Doctors have long known that uridine, a natural building block of RNA, can protect normal cells from the chemotherapy drug 5-fluorouracil (5-FU). The problem was that plain uridine is poorly absorbed by mouth and given IV causes fever. Researchers at Wellstat Therapeutics in Maryland, including Reid von Borstel, developed uridine triacetate, a prodrug with acetyl groups that make it far better absorbed. It is then converted to uridine in the body. In December 2015 the FDA approved uridine triacetate (Vistogard) as the first antidote for overdoses of 5-FU or capecitabine and for early-onset severe or life-threatening toxicity. The same compound is also approved, as Xuriden, for hereditary orotic aciduria, a rare disorder of pyrimidine synthesis.",
  "timeline": [["1957", "5-fluorouracil is described by Charles Heidelberger and colleagues."], ["1990s", "Wellstat researchers develop uridine triacetate as an oral uridine prodrug."], ["2015", "FDA approves uridine triacetate (Vistogard) as an antidote for 5-FU and capecitabine toxicity."]],
  "cell": "5-FU is converted inside cells into several toxic metabolites. FdUMP blocks thymidylate synthase and DNA synthesis, while fluorouridine triphosphate (FUTP) is built into RNA in place of uridine triphosphate (UTP), disrupting RNA processing and function. In overdose or severe toxicity, RNA damage in rapidly dividing normal tissue, such as gut lining and bone marrow, is a major cause of injury. Uridine triacetate is deacetylated by esterases to uridine, which floods the blood. Cells take up uridine and convert it, through the pyrimidine salvage pathway, to UTP. The large pool of UTP competes with FUTP for incorporation into RNA, so much less fluorinated RNA is made. This limits cell damage in normal tissues, reducing toxicity to the gut, bone marrow, heart and nervous system.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Less RNA damage in blood-forming cells reduces severe neutropenia and other low blood counts."], ["Gut", "Protecting the gut lining reduces mucositis, diarrhea and the risk of perforation."], ["Heart", "It may reduce 5-FU cardiac toxicity, such as chest pain and arrhythmias."], ["Brain", "It helps reduce 5-FU neurotoxicity such as encephalopathy and confusion."], ["Survival", "In the pivotal studies, most treated overdose patients survived, compared with low survival in historical reports."]],
  "journey": "Uridine triacetate comes as granules taken by mouth, mixed with soft food, every 6 hours over several days. The acetyl groups make it fat-soluble so it is absorbed well. Esterases in the gut and blood remove the acetyl groups, releasing uridine, which peaks about 2 to 3 hours after a dose. Uridine joins normal pyrimidine metabolism, with some cleared in urine. Its half-life is about 2 to 2.6 hours. It works best when started early, within 96 hours of the end of 5-FU.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, vomiting and diarrhea are the main side effects; they likely come from the large oral load in the gut, and also overlap with 5-FU toxicity itself. If vomiting occurs within 2 hours of a dose, the dose is repeated. It does not reverse damage already done, which is why timing is critical. It is not meant for routine, nonsevere 5-FU side effects, since it could reduce anticancer effect. Supportive care such as antiemetics and growth factors continues alongside it.",
  "src": ["Vistogard (uridine triacetate) FDA label", "Ma WW et al. Cancer 2017"]
},
"cangrelor": {
  "origin": "Cangrelor was first developed at AstraZeneca in the 1990s, where chemists modified ATP, a natural blocker of the P2Y12 receptor, to make a stable, potent, IV antiplatelet drug. It was coded AR-C69931MX. The Medicines Company licensed it and ran the CHAMPION trial program. Early trials failed to show benefit, but CHAMPION PHOENIX, published in 2013, showed fewer heart attacks, stent clots and other ischemic events during percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) compared with clopidogrel. The FDA approved cangrelor (Kengreal) in June 2015 as an add-on to PCI for patients not already given an oral P2Y12 blocker. It remains the only IV P2Y12 inhibitor, valued for its fast on and off action.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "AstraZeneca chemists develop cangrelor as an ATP analog P2Y12 blocker."], ["2013", "CHAMPION PHOENIX shows fewer ischemic events during PCI."], ["2015", "FDA approves cangrelor (Kengreal)."]],
  "cell": "When platelets are activated, they release ADP from dense granules. ADP binds two receptors: P2Y1, which starts shape change, and P2Y12, a Gi-coupled receptor that keeps the response going. P2Y12 inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP, and activates PI3-kinase, which together switch GP IIb/IIIa receptors into their active, fibrinogen-binding form and amplify aggregation. Cangrelor is an ATP analog that binds P2Y12 directly and reversibly. It does not need metabolic activation, unlike clopidogrel and prasugrel. With P2Y12 blocked, cAMP stays high, GP IIb/IIIa stays inactive and platelets do not aggregate well. Inhibition is near-complete within 2 minutes and wears off within about an hour of stopping, as the drug is rapidly broken down.",
  "body": [["Platelets", "ADP-driven aggregation is almost fully blocked within minutes, protecting coronary stents during PCI."], ["Coronary arteries", "Lower platelet activation reduces periprocedural heart attacks and stent thrombosis."], ["Bleeding", "Bleeding risk rises, mostly minor, and increases with heparin or bivalirudin."], ["Lungs", "Dyspnea occurs in some patients, likely from P2Y12 or adenosine-related effects on sensory nerves."], ["Transition to oral drugs", "Clopidogrel and prasugrel metabolites cannot bind P2Y12 while cangrelor occupies it, so they are given after the infusion ends."]],
  "journey": "Cangrelor is given only IV, as a bolus followed by an infusion. It acts within 2 minutes. It is about 97 to 98 percent bound to plasma proteins. It is rapidly dephosphorylated in the blood to an inactive nucleoside, so it does not depend on the liver or kidneys. Its half-life is only 3 to 6 minutes, and platelet function returns to normal within about an hour after stopping. Metabolites leave in urine and feces.",
  "sidefx": "Bleeding is the main side effect because platelets cannot aggregate well; it is usually minor but can be serious, especially with other antithrombotics. Its fast offset means bleeding risk drops quickly after stopping, with no reversal agent needed. Dyspnea occurs in some patients, similar to ticagrelor. Hypersensitivity reactions can occur. Because cangrelor blocks the receptor, clopidogrel or prasugrel must be given after the infusion stops, while ticagrelor, which binds a different site, can be given during it.",
  "src": ["Kengreal (cangrelor) FDA label", "Bhatt DL et al. CHAMPION PHOENIX, NEJM 2013"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"angiotensin-ii": {
  "origin": "The story starts in 1898, when Robert Tigerstedt and Per Bergman in Stockholm found that kidney extracts raised blood pressure and named the active substance renin. Around 1939 and 1940 two teams working apart found that renin acts on a blood protein to make a powerful pressor peptide. Eduardo Braun-Menendez and colleagues in Buenos Aires called it hypertensin, while Irvine Page and his group in Indianapolis called it angiotonin. In 1958 the two groups agreed on the blended name angiotensin. The peptide was synthesized in the late 1950s. For decades it was a research tool, and medicine focused on blocking it with ACE inhibitors and ARBs. La Jolla Pharmaceutical then developed synthetic human angiotensin II as a vasopressor, and after the ATHOS-3 trial the FDA approved it as Giapreza in 2017 for adults with septic or other distributive shock.",
  "timeline": [["1898", "Tigerstedt and Bergman describe renin from kidney extracts."], ["1940", "Teams in Buenos Aires and Indianapolis independently describe the pressor peptide."], ["1958", "The names hypertensin and angiotonin are merged into angiotensin."], ["2017", "ATHOS-3 trial results are published and the FDA approves Giapreza for distributive shock."]],
  "cell": "Angiotensin II is an eight-amino-acid peptide identical to the human hormone. It binds the angiotensin II type 1 (AT1) receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on vascular smooth muscle. Gq activates phospholipase C, which splits PIP2 into IP3 and diacylglycerol. IP3 releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and the calcium-calmodulin complex activates myosin light-chain kinase, so the arteriole contracts. Diacylglycerol activates protein kinase C, which sustains the contraction. AT1 activation in the adrenal zona glomerulosa increases aldosterone release, and central effects raise vasopressin release. The net clinical effect is a rise in systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure. Because it works on a different receptor than catecholamines or vasopressin, it adds a third pathway in shock that does not respond well to the others.",
  "body": [["Arterioles", "Strong AT1-driven constriction raises systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure, which can lower the needed amount of other vasopressors."], ["Kidneys", "Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole more than the afferent, which helps hold glomerular filtration pressure when blood pressure is low."], ["Adrenal cortex", "Stimulates aldosterone release, which makes the kidney hold sodium and water and lose potassium."], ["Brain and pituitary", "Increases vasopressin release and thirst, adding to fluid retention and vessel tone."], ["Blood and clotting", "Patients receiving it in trials had more venous and arterial clots, so clot prevention is part of care."], ["Metabolism", "Hyperglycemia and a low platelet count were reported in clinical trial patients."]],
  "journey": "Giapreza is given as a continuous IV infusion because the peptide is broken down almost at once. Peptidases in plasma, red blood cells and tissues, such as aminopeptidase A and ACE2, cut it into smaller fragments like angiotensin (1-7) and angiotensin III. Its plasma half-life is under a minute. Blood pressure begins to rise within about five minutes of starting the infusion, and the effect fades within minutes after it stops. The peptides are broken down to amino acids, so there is no liver enzyme or kidney clearance pathway to adjust for.",
  "sidefx": "The most serious risk is clotting. Angiotensin II activates platelets and increases plasminogen activator inhibitor-1, which favors clot formation, and deep vein thrombosis was more common in the treatment arm of ATHOS-3. Strong arteriolar constriction can cut blood flow to fingers, toes and the gut, causing ischemia. Tachycardia, delirium, hyperglycemia, thrombocytopenia and fungal infections were also reported in trial patients. There is no specific reversal agent, but the short half-life means effects fade quickly once the infusion is lowered or stopped.",
  "fun": "Before the synthetic drug existed, the original pressor substance was made by mixing renin from kidney extracts with blood plasma, letting the enzyme cut its protein substrate in a test tube.",
  "src": ["Giapreza (angiotensin II) FDA label", "Khanna A et al. ATHOS-3, N Engl J Med 2017", "Basso N, Terragno NA. History about the discovery of the renin-angiotensin system, Hypertension 2001"]
},
"iohexol": {
  "origin": "Early iodinated contrast agents were ionic salts. They split into charged particles in solution, so they were several times saltier (more osmotic) than blood and caused pain, heat and fluid shifts. In 1969 the Swedish radiologist Torsten Almen proposed a new idea: build contrast molecules that do not ionize, so each molecule carries its iodine without extra charged particles. The Norwegian company Nyegaard & Co. (later Nycomed, now part of GE HealthCare) took up the idea. Its first nonionic agent, metrizamide, was useful but unstable and costly. Chemists there then developed iohexol, a stable nonionic monomer, which entered clinical use in the early 1980s and was approved in the US as Omnipaque in the mid-1980s. Iohexol helped make low-osmolar contrast the standard for CT and angiography.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "Torsten Almen proposes nonionic, low-osmolar contrast molecules."], ["1970s", "Nyegaard & Co. introduces metrizamide, the first nonionic agent."], ["1980s", "Iohexol enters clinical use and is approved in the US as Omnipaque."]],
  "cell": "Iohexol has no receptor target. Each molecule has a benzene ring carrying three iodine atoms. Iodine has a high atomic number, and its K-edge energy sits close to the energy of diagnostic x-rays, so it absorbs x-ray photons strongly by the photoelectric effect. Any space the drug fills blocks more x-rays and looks bright on CT or fluoroscopy. Hydrophilic side chains with many hydroxyl groups make the molecule water soluble and shield the iodine core, which reduces binding to proteins and cell membranes. Because it does not split into ions, a given iodine concentration needs about half as many particles as an ionic monomer, so osmolality is lower. After injection it moves out of the capillaries into the extracellular space but does not enter cells or cross an intact blood-brain barrier.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Fills the vessels within seconds of a bolus, making arteries and veins bright for CT angiography; a warm feeling comes from mild vasodilation."], ["Organs and tumors", "Leaks into the extracellular space of tissues, so areas with more blood flow or leaky vessels, such as many tumors and inflamed tissue, enhance more."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered freely at the glomerulus and concentrated in the urine, which outlines the collecting system on urography; in at-risk patients it can contribute to kidney injury."], ["Thyroid", "Free iodide in the product and from small breakdown can briefly change thyroid hormone production, mainly in infants and people with thyroid disease."], ["Spinal canal", "Specific concentrations are labeled for intrathecal myelography, where it outlines the spinal cord and nerve roots."], ["Gut", "Swallowed or given rectally, it outlines the bowel and is very poorly absorbed through intact mucosa."]],
  "journey": "Iohexol is injected into a vein or artery, or given into the spinal canal, joints, body cavities or the gut. In the blood it mixes with plasma within seconds and spreads into the extracellular fluid within minutes. It binds very little to proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys clear it unchanged by glomerular filtration alone, with no tubular secretion or reabsorption, which is why measured iohexol clearance is used as a research gold standard for GFR. The half-life is about two hours with normal kidneys and much longer in kidney failure, and hemodialysis removes it.",
  "sidefx": "Most reactions are physiologic or allergic-like. Warmth, metallic taste and nausea come from the osmotic load and mild vessel dilation. Allergic-like reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, happen through direct mast cell activation and complement effects rather than classic IgE allergy in most cases. In people with poor kidney function, contrast can reduce medullary blood flow and stress tubule cells, which explains contrast-associated kidney injury. The iodine load can trigger hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism. Wrong concentrations given intrathecally can cause seizures or death, which is the reason for the boxed warning. There is no antidote; severe reactions are treated with epinephrine and supportive care, and hemodialysis can remove the drug.",
  "fun": "Because iohexol is filtered by the kidney and nothing else, researchers use its clearance from blood to measure kidney function more precisely than creatinine-based estimates.",
  "src": ["Omnipaque (iohexol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Almen T. Contrast agent design, J Theor Biol 1969"]
},
"iopamidol": {
  "origin": "Iopamidol came out of the same push for nonionic contrast that followed Torsten Almen's 1969 proposal. It was first made by chemists in Switzerland in the 1970s and developed by the Italian company Bracco, in Milan. Like iohexol, it is a nonionic monomer with three iodine atoms on a benzene ring and water-loving side chains. It entered clinical use in Europe in the early 1980s and reached the US in the 1980s under the brand name Isovue. A separate low-concentration form, Isovue-M, was made for myelography. Iopamidol became one of the most widely used CT and angiography agents in the world.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "Almen proposes nonionic, low-osmolar contrast agents."], ["1970s", "Bracco develops iopamidol."], ["1980s", "Iopamidol enters clinical use and reaches the US market as Isovue."]],
  "cell": "Iopamidol acts by physics rather than pharmacology. Its three iodine atoms absorb x-ray photons by the photoelectric effect, so the blood or tissue it fills blocks more x-rays and appears bright. The molecule does not dissociate into ions, so it delivers the same iodine with fewer particles than older ionic agents, giving lower osmolality and fewer fluid shifts. Its hydroxyl-rich side chains surround the iodinated ring, which makes it very water soluble and lowers interaction with proteins and cell membranes. After injection it distributes in plasma and then into the extracellular space. It does not enter cells or cross an intact blood-brain barrier, so enhancement in the brain means a barrier is broken.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Opacifies arteries and veins during the first pass, which allows CT angiography and catheter angiography."], ["Solid organs", "Spreads into the extracellular space of organs, showing differences in blood supply between normal and diseased tissue."], ["Brain", "Normally stays out of the brain; tumors, abscesses and other lesions that break the blood-brain barrier enhance."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged and concentrated in urine, producing images of the kidneys and ureters; it causes a mild osmotic diuresis."], ["Heart", "During coronary angiography, contrast briefly reaches the coronary arteries and can cause transient changes in rhythm and contractility."], ["Thyroid", "The iodine load can temporarily alter thyroid hormone production in susceptible people."]],
  "journey": "Iopamidol is given into a vein or artery, or intrathecally as Isovue-M. Within minutes it moves from plasma into the extracellular space. It is not metabolized and binds little to protein. The kidneys filter it unchanged, and most is in the urine within a day when kidney function is normal. The elimination half-life is about two hours and is prolonged in kidney disease. Dialysis removes it.",
  "sidefx": "Heat, flushing and metallic taste come from osmotic and vasodilator effects as the bolus passes. Allergic-like reactions, from hives to bronchospasm and anaphylaxis, mostly arise from direct release of histamine and other mediators from mast cells and basophils. Contrast-associated kidney injury in at-risk patients is linked to reduced medullary blood flow and direct tubule toxicity. Leakage outside the vein can injure skin and soft tissue. Only Isovue-M is meant for the spinal canal; the regular strengths can cause fatal neurotoxicity if given intrathecally. There is no antidote, and reactions are treated supportively, with epinephrine for anaphylaxis.",
  "src": ["Isovue (iopamidol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"iodixanol": {
  "origin": "After nonionic monomers like iohexol lowered osmolality, chemists asked if they could make contrast that matched blood exactly. The answer was a dimer: two triiodinated rings joined together, so each molecule carries six iodine atoms. Fewer particles are needed for the same iodine dose. Researchers at Nycomed in Norway developed iodixanol along these lines, and it entered clinical use in the early 1990s. Sodium and calcium salts are added so the final solution is iso-osmolar with blood. It was marketed as Visipaque and approved in the US in the 1990s. It became especially popular for cardiac catheterization and for patients at higher risk of kidney injury.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "Almen proposes nonionic contrast media."], ["1980s", "Nycomed develops nonionic dimeric agents."], ["1990s", "Iodixanol is introduced as Visipaque and approved in the US."]],
  "cell": "Iodixanol has no receptor. Each molecule is a nonionic dimer of two benzene rings, each carrying three iodine atoms. Iodine absorbs x-rays by the photoelectric effect, so vessels and tissues that hold the drug appear bright. Because one particle carries six iodine atoms instead of three, iodixanol reaches the same iodine concentration with far fewer dissolved particles. Its natural osmolality is actually below blood, so electrolytes are added to bring it to about the same osmolality as plasma. The large molecule is more viscous than monomers. It distributes into the extracellular fluid and does not enter cells or an intact brain.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "Fills arteries with less heat and pain than monomers, which is useful in sensitive procedures like peripheral and cerebral angiography."], ["Heart", "Matching blood osmolality causes less fluid shift and less change in heart rhythm and contractility during coronary injection."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged; it is often chosen for at-risk patients, although large studies show only small or uncertain differences in kidney injury compared with low-osmolar agents."], ["Organs", "Leaks into the extracellular space of tissues, showing areas of abnormal blood supply on CT."], ["Skin", "Delayed rashes, appearing hours to a week later, seem to be more common than with monomers."]],
  "journey": "Iodixanol is injected into an artery or vein. It mixes with blood and then spreads into the extracellular space over minutes. It is not metabolized and binds little to protein. The kidneys filter it unchanged, and the half-life is about two hours with normal kidney function. The higher viscosity slows how fast it can be injected and may slow its passage through kidney tubules. Dialysis removes it.",
  "sidefx": "Because it is iso-osmolar, iodixanol causes less warmth and pain than other agents. Allergic-like reactions still happen through direct mediator release from mast cells and basophils. Delayed skin reactions, thought to be T-cell mediated, appear more often than with monomers and can occasionally be severe. In people with kidney disease, contrast can reduce medullary oxygen delivery and injure tubule cells; the higher viscosity may contribute. It must never be given intrathecally. There is no antidote; anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine and supportive care.",
  "src": ["Visipaque (iodixanol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"diatrizoate": {
  "origin": "Diatrizoate was one of the first iodinated contrast agents built on a fully triiodinated benzene ring. It was introduced in the early 1950s, with Sterling-Winthrop in the US and Schering in Germany among the first companies to market it, and it quickly replaced older, more toxic agents for urography and angiography. For decades its sodium and meglumine salts were standard intravascular contrast. Because these salts split into ions, the solutions are very hypertonic. Once safer nonionic agents arrived in the 1980s, diatrizoate moved out of the blood vessels and into the gut and bladder. Today it is used mainly as Gastrografin and similar oral or rectal solutions, and as Cystografin for bladder studies.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Diatrizoate salts are developed and introduced for urography and angiography."], ["1980s", "Nonionic agents begin replacing it for intravascular use."], ["1990s", "Diatrizoate is used mainly for enteric and bladder imaging."]],
  "cell": "Diatrizoate is an ionic triiodinated benzoic acid. In solution it splits into a negatively charged iodinated anion and a sodium or meglumine cation. The iodine absorbs x-rays by the photoelectric effect, so the bowel or bladder it fills appears bright. Because each molecule makes two particles, the solution is several times more concentrated than plasma. In the gut it is barely absorbed, so it stays in the lumen and pulls water in by osmosis. That extra fluid dilutes the contrast as it moves along, stimulates peristalsis and can soften impacted stool or help move contents past an adhesive small bowel obstruction.",
  "body": [["Stomach and small bowel", "Outlines the bowel and shows leaks after surgery or perforation; a water-soluble agent is safer than barium if it escapes into the abdomen."], ["Colon", "Pulls water into the colon, which helps in studies of obstruction and can loosen meconium or stool."], ["Fluid balance", "Water drawn into the gut can cause dehydration and low blood volume, especially in infants and frail adults."], ["Lungs", "If aspirated, the hypertonic solution pulls fluid into the airways and can cause chemical pneumonitis and pulmonary edema."], ["Bladder", "Given through a catheter, it outlines the bladder and urethra for cystourethrography."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth or given rectally, diatrizoate reaches the stomach and bowel within minutes and the colon in about one to four hours. Very little passes through healthy intestinal lining, so most leaves unchanged in the stool over one to two days. If the bowel wall is inflamed or perforated, more is absorbed, and that portion is filtered unchanged by the kidneys, which can make the urinary tract faintly visible. It is not metabolized.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from its high osmolality. Drawing water into the gut causes diarrhea, cramping and nausea and can lead to dehydration and electrolyte shifts, which is why infants and dehydrated patients need care. If inhaled into the lungs, it pulls fluid into the alveoli and can cause severe pneumonitis or pulmonary edema. Absorbed contrast can rarely cause allergic-like reactions. There is no antidote; treatment is fluids, oxygen and supportive care.",
  "src": ["Gastrografin (diatrizoate meglumine and sodium) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]
},
"barium-sulfate": {
  "origin": "Soon after Wilhelm Roentgen discovered x-rays in 1895, doctors looked for safe ways to see the gut. Early studies used bismuth salts, which were costly and sometimes toxic. In the early 1900s physicians began using barium sulfate, a heavy, chalk-like white powder that is almost totally insoluble in water. Its insolubility is the key: free barium ions are poisonous, but barium sulfate passes through the gut without dissolving. By the 1910s barium meals and enemas were standard. Double-contrast techniques, which coat the mucosa and then add air, were refined later in the century. Barium products were sold for decades before modern drug approval rules, and in the 2010s the FDA required and granted formal approvals for many of them.",
  "timeline": [["1895", "Wilhelm Roentgen discovers x-rays."], ["1910s", "Barium sulfate replaces bismuth salts for gut imaging."], ["1960s", "Double-contrast barium studies with air are refined."], ["2010s", "The FDA formally approves several long-used barium sulfate products."]],
  "cell": "Barium sulfate has no receptor or enzyme target. Barium has a high atomic number (56), so it absorbs x-rays strongly and anything it coats or fills appears white. The sulfate salt has extremely low solubility in water and in stomach acid, so essentially no free barium ions are released or absorbed. Products are made as suspensions with suspending agents so the particles coat the mucosa evenly. Thick, dense suspensions coat the lining for double-contrast studies, while dilute suspensions fill the bowel for CT. Different textures, from thin liquid to pudding, are used in swallowing studies to test how a person handles each consistency.",
  "body": [["Esophagus and swallowing", "Thin to thick textures show how a person swallows and whether material enters the airway during a modified barium swallow."], ["Stomach and small bowel", "Coats the mucosa and fills the lumen, showing ulcers, masses, narrowing and transit time."], ["Colon", "Given as an enema, it outlines the colon and rectum; air added afterward gives fine mucosal detail."], ["Bowel habits", "Barium is not absorbed and can dry out in the colon, causing constipation, white stools and rarely impaction."], ["Peritoneum", "If it leaks through a perforation, barium is not cleared and causes severe inflammation and adhesions, which is why water-soluble contrast is used when a leak is suspected."]],
  "journey": "Barium sulfate is swallowed or given rectally. It coats the gut lining immediately and moves through the bowel at the normal pace of transit, reaching the colon in about one to four hours after a swallow. Essentially none is absorbed into the blood, and it is not metabolized. It leaves the body unchanged in stool over one to three days. Drinking fluids helps it pass.",
  "sidefx": "Because barium is not absorbed, its harms are mechanical. Thickened barium in the colon can cause constipation or impaction, especially in people with slow bowels. If it leaks into the abdomen, it causes intense peritonitis and scarring. If aspirated, the particles irritate the lungs and can cause pneumonitis. Very rarely it enters veins through damaged mucosa and causes embolism. Allergic reactions are uncommon and are usually linked to additives or latex, not barium itself. Barium sulfate must never be confused with soluble barium salts, which are poisons. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "Barium sulfate is so insoluble that the same compound is used as a white pigment in paints and paper.",
  "src": ["E-Z-Paque (barium sulfate) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]
},
"gadobutrol": {
  "origin": "MRI arrived in hospitals in the early 1980s. Researchers soon learned that gadolinium, a rare earth metal with seven unpaired electrons, brightens nearby tissue on T1-weighted images, but free gadolinium is toxic. The fix was to lock it inside a tightly binding organic cage called a chelate. The first approved agent, gadopentetate (Magnevist, 1988), used an open, linear cage. Chemists at Schering AG in Berlin (now Bayer) then designed gadobutrol with a closed, ring-shaped macrocyclic cage for higher stability and made it at twice the usual concentration. It was approved in Europe in the late 1990s and by the FDA as Gadavist in 2011.",
  "timeline": [["1988", "The first gadolinium contrast agent, gadopentetate, is approved in the US."], ["1990s", "Schering develops gadobutrol and launches it in Europe."], ["2011", "The FDA approves gadobutrol as Gadavist."], ["2017", "The FDA adds warnings about gadolinium retention in the body."]],
  "cell": "Gadobutrol acts through physics. The Gd3+ ion has seven unpaired electrons, which makes it strongly paramagnetic. When water molecules briefly bind near the gadolinium, its magnetic field speeds up the relaxation of their hydrogen protons after each radiofrequency pulse. Shorter T1 relaxation makes those areas appear bright on T1-weighted images. The macrocyclic butrol ligand wraps gadolinium in a rigid ring, so the ion is released very slowly, which gives high kinetic stability. The higher concentration delivers a compact bolus, useful for MR angiography and perfusion imaging. The agent stays outside cells and crosses only a broken blood-brain barrier.",
  "body": [["Blood vessels", "During the first pass, the concentrated bolus brightens arteries for MR angiography."], ["Brain and spine", "Leaks only where the blood-brain barrier is broken, so tumors, active multiple sclerosis plaques and infections stand out."], ["Breast and heart", "Shows abnormal blood supply in breast tumors and scarred heart muscle on delayed images."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged at the glomerulus; in severe kidney failure it stays in the body much longer."], ["Skin and connective tissue", "In severe kidney failure, retained gadolinium agents have been linked to nephrogenic systemic fibrosis, though macrocyclic agents carry the lowest risk."]],
  "journey": "Gadobutrol is injected into a vein. It circulates in plasma, then moves into the extracellular space within minutes. It does not bind proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it unchanged, with a half-life of about two hours in people with normal kidney function. Most of it leaves the body in the urine within a day. In kidney failure the half-life is much longer, and dialysis can remove it. Tiny amounts remain in bone, brain and skin for long periods, though less than with linear agents.",
  "sidefx": "Common effects such as headache, nausea and altered taste are mild and brief. Allergic-like reactions are rare and can range from hives to anaphylaxis. The most feared toxicity is nephrogenic systemic fibrosis, a thickening of skin and organs seen when free gadolinium is released in people with severe kidney failure; stable macrocyclic cages make this very rare. Small amounts of gadolinium are retained in the brain and bone, with no proven harm so far. Intrathecal use can cause severe neurotoxicity. There is no antidote; dialysis removes the agent, and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.",
  "src": ["Gadavist (gadobutrol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "FDA Drug Safety Communication on gadolinium retention, 2017"]
},
"gadoterate": {
  "origin": "Gadoterate pairs gadolinium with DOTA, a ring-shaped chelating molecule first made by chemists in the 1970s. The French company Guerbet developed gadoterate meglumine, and it was approved in France as Dotarem in 1989, making it one of the earliest macrocyclic MRI agents. It was widely used in Europe for more than two decades before the FDA approved it for the US in 2013. Its tight DOTA cage made it attractive after nephrogenic systemic fibrosis was linked to less stable gadolinium agents in 2006. Generic versions such as Clariscan followed.",
  "timeline": [["1989", "Guerbet launches gadoterate as Dotarem in France."], ["2006", "Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is linked to gadolinium agents."], ["2013", "The FDA approves Dotarem for use in the US."]],
  "cell": "Gadoterate contains a Gd3+ ion held inside the DOTA macrocycle, a rigid 12-membered ring with four arms that grip the metal. The complex carries a negative charge, so it is balanced by meglumine, which makes it an ionic macrocyclic agent. Gadolinium's seven unpaired electrons create a strong local magnetic field that shortens the T1 relaxation time of nearby water protons, so tissues with more agent look brighter on T1-weighted MRI. The DOTA cage releases gadolinium extremely slowly, giving one of the highest kinetic stabilities of any gadolinium agent. It stays in the extracellular space and only crosses a disrupted blood-brain barrier.",
  "body": [["Brain and spine", "Brightens lesions where the blood-brain barrier is disrupted, such as tumors, abscesses and active inflammation."], ["Body organs", "Distributes in the extracellular space of organs and highlights areas with abnormal blood supply."], ["Blood vessels", "Gives vessel enhancement during the first pass after injection."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged into the urine; kidney failure slows its exit from the body."], ["Skin", "Very stable chelation makes nephrogenic systemic fibrosis extremely rare, though caution is still used in severe kidney disease."]],
  "journey": "Gadoterate is injected into a vein. It spreads through plasma and into the extracellular space within minutes. It does not bind to proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys clear it unchanged by filtration, with a half-life of about one and a half hours in healthy people. Nearly all is gone in urine within a day. It is cleared by dialysis. Trace retention in tissues is lower than with linear agents.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, headache, altered taste and a cold or painful feeling at the injection site are common and brief. Allergic-like reactions are uncommon; mast cell and basophil activation can cause hives, bronchospasm or rarely anaphylaxis. Free gadolinium, if released, can deposit in skin and organs and trigger fibrosis in people with severe kidney failure, but the strong DOTA cage keeps release very low. Intrathecal injection can cause serious neurotoxicity. There is no antidote; dialysis removes the drug and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.",
  "src": ["Dotarem (gadoterate meglumine) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]
},
"gadoxetate": {
  "origin": "Most gadolinium agents stay outside cells. Chemists at Schering AG (now Bayer) wanted an agent that liver cells would take up, so they could see which tissue contained working hepatocytes. They added a fat-loving ethoxybenzyl group to a linear gadolinium chelate, creating gadoxetate (Gd-EOB-DTPA). It was approved in Europe as Primovist in 2004 and by the FDA as Eovist in 2008. It became a key tool for finding and characterizing liver lesions, including small cancers.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Schering researchers develop Gd-EOB-DTPA as a liver-specific agent."], ["2004", "Gadoxetate is approved in Europe as Primovist."], ["2008", "The FDA approves gadoxetate as Eovist."]],
  "cell": "Gadoxetate is a linear gadolinium chelate with an ethoxybenzyl side group. Right after injection it acts like other agents, shortening T1 relaxation of water in the blood and extracellular space, so arterial and portal venous phases can be imaged. Then working hepatocytes take it up through organic anion transporting polypeptides OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 on the sinusoidal membrane. Liver cells pump it into bile through MRP2 on the canalicular membrane. About 20 minutes after injection, normal liver is bright on T1 images (the hepatobiliary phase). Lesions without working hepatocytes or transporters, such as metastases and most hepatocellular carcinomas, stay dark against the bright liver.",
  "body": [["Liver", "Healthy liver tissue takes up the agent and brightens on delayed images, making lesions that lack hepatocytes stand out."], ["Bile ducts", "Excretion into bile outlines the ducts and can show bile leaks after surgery or injury."], ["Blood vessels", "Early phases show arterial and portal venous blood supply to lesions."], ["Kidneys", "About half the dose is filtered into the urine; the rest leaves through bile."], ["Lungs", "Some patients have brief shortness of breath during the arterial phase, which can blur early images."]],
  "journey": "Gadoxetate is injected into a vein. It first spreads into the extracellular space, then is taken up by liver cells over about 20 minutes. Liver cells secrete it unchanged into bile, and the kidneys filter the rest. About half leaves in urine and half in stool. It binds weakly to plasma proteins and is not metabolized. The half-life is about one hour in healthy adults. Because it has two routes out, severe disease in both the liver and kidneys slows clearance.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, headache, altered taste and a feeling of warmth are common and short-lived. Transient breathlessness during the arterial phase is reported and is not fully explained. Allergic-like reactions from mediator release are rare. Its linear cage is less stable than macrocyclic ones, but the very low dose used and dual elimination keep the risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis low; it is still a concern in severe kidney failure. There is no antidote; dialysis can remove it and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.",
  "src": ["Eovist (gadoxetate disodium) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]
},
"gadobenate": {
  "origin": "Gadobenate was developed by the Italian company Bracco in the late 1980s and 1990s. Its chelate, BOPTA, adds a benzyl group to a linear gadolinium cage. That small change makes the molecule stick loosely to albumin, which slows its tumbling in water and boosts how strongly it brightens tissue. A small part is also taken up by liver cells. Gadobenate was approved in Europe in the 1990s and by the FDA as MultiHance in 2004. It was later approved for MR angiography of the kidney and leg arteries and for use in children.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Bracco develops gadobenate and launches it in Europe."], ["2004", "The FDA approves gadobenate as MultiHance for CNS MRI."], ["2010s", "US approval is extended to MR angiography and to young children."]],
  "cell": "Gadobenate holds Gd3+ in a linear BOPTA chelate. The gadolinium shortens the T1 relaxation time of nearby water protons, so tissues containing the agent look brighter. The benzyloxymethyl group causes weak, temporary binding to serum albumin. When the complex is attached to the large albumin molecule, it rotates more slowly, which increases its relaxivity (the strength of T1 shortening per unit of gadolinium). That gives stronger enhancement than other standard-concentration agents. A few percent of the dose is taken up by hepatocytes and secreted into bile, causing mild delayed liver enhancement.",
  "body": [["Brain and spine", "Brightens lesions with a disrupted blood-brain barrier, with somewhat stronger enhancement than conventional agents."], ["Blood vessels", "Higher relaxivity and protein binding help MR angiography of the kidney and peripheral arteries."], ["Liver", "Small uptake by hepatocytes gives a mild delayed liver phase one to two hours after injection."], ["Kidneys", "Most of the dose is filtered into the urine unchanged."], ["Skin", "As a linear agent, it carries a higher theoretical risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in severe kidney failure than macrocyclic agents."]],
  "journey": "Gadobenate is injected into a vein and spreads through plasma and extracellular fluid. It binds weakly and briefly to albumin. It is not metabolized. Most leaves unchanged in urine, and a small part goes into bile and out in stool. The half-life is about one to two hours in people with normal kidneys. Dialysis removes it.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea, headache, injection site reactions and altered taste are the most common. Allergic-like reactions arise from direct mast cell and basophil activation and range from hives to anaphylaxis. Because the linear cage releases gadolinium more easily than ring-shaped cages, retention in brain and bone is greater, and nephrogenic systemic fibrosis is a concern in severe kidney failure. There is no antidote; dialysis removes it and anaphylaxis is treated with epinephrine.",
  "src": ["MultiHance (gadobenate dimeglumine) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]
},
"gadopiclenol": {
  "origin": "After gadolinium retention in the brain was reported in 2014, there was a push to get the same image quality with less gadolinium. Guerbet in France designed gadopiclenol around a pyclen-based macrocyclic cage. The cage leaves room for two water molecules to bind directly to gadolinium instead of one, which roughly doubles to triples its brightening power. That lets radiologists use about half the usual gadolinium dose for similar images. The FDA approved gadopiclenol in 2022. Guerbet markets it as Elucirem, and Bracco markets it under a partnership as Vueway.",
  "timeline": [["2014", "Gadolinium deposits in the brain after repeated MRI are reported."], ["2010s", "Guerbet develops the high-relaxivity agent gadopiclenol."], ["2022", "The FDA approves gadopiclenol as Elucirem and Vueway."]],
  "cell": "Gadopiclenol holds Gd3+ in a macrocyclic chelate built on a pyclen ring. Most gadolinium agents let only one water molecule sit directly on the metal. Gadopiclenol has two such inner-sphere water sites. Each bound water molecule swaps rapidly with surrounding water, spreading gadolinium's T1-shortening effect to many more protons. Its larger size also slows rotation, which raises relaxivity further. The result is about two to three times the T1 relaxivity of conventional agents. The macrocyclic design keeps kinetic stability high. Like other extracellular agents, it stays outside cells and crosses only a broken blood-brain barrier.",
  "body": [["Brain and spine", "Highlights lesions where the blood-brain barrier is disrupted, with diagnostic quality similar to standard agents at half the gadolinium dose."], ["Body organs", "Shows abnormal blood supply in the head and neck, chest, abdomen, pelvis and musculoskeletal system."], ["Kidneys", "Filtered unchanged into the urine; kidney failure slows clearance."], ["Long-term gadolinium load", "Less gadolinium per scan is meant to reduce total retention over many scans, though long-term data are still building."]],
  "journey": "Gadopiclenol is injected into a vein and spreads into the extracellular space within minutes. It does not bind proteins and is not metabolized. The kidneys filter it unchanged, with a half-life of about one and a half hours in adults with normal kidney function. Most is gone in urine within a day. Dialysis can remove it.",
  "sidefx": "Injection site pain, headache, nausea and altered taste are the most common effects. Allergic-like reactions from mediator release can occur, rarely as anaphylaxis. As with all gadolinium agents, there is a warning about nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in severe kidney failure and about gadolinium retention in tissues, though its stable cage and lower dose are designed to lower these risks. Intrathecal use can cause serious harm. There is no antidote; dialysis removes it.",
  "src": ["Elucirem (gadopiclenol) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media", "Kanda T et al. Radiology 2014"]
},
"ferumoxytol": {
  "origin": "Ferumoxytol grew out of research on tiny iron oxide particles for MRI in the 1980s and 1990s. Scientists found that iron oxide nanoparticles coated with sugar polymers are taken up by macrophages and strongly change MRI signal. Advanced Magnetics, a Massachusetts company later renamed AMAG Pharmaceuticals, developed a carbohydrate-coated particle that could deliver a large amount of iron quickly and safely into a vein. The FDA approved it as Feraheme in 2009 to treat iron deficiency anemia in adults with chronic kidney disease. Serious allergic reactions led to a boxed warning in 2015, and in 2018 its use was widened to adults with iron deficiency anemia who could not take or did not respond to oral iron. Its MRI properties are also used off label as a blood-pool contrast agent.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Researchers develop coated iron oxide nanoparticles for MRI."], ["2009", "The FDA approves Feraheme for iron deficiency anemia in chronic kidney disease."], ["2015", "A boxed warning for serious hypersensitivity reactions is added."], ["2018", "Approval is expanded to iron deficiency anemia beyond kidney disease."]],
  "cell": "Ferumoxytol is a superparamagnetic iron oxide core wrapped in a polyglucose sorbitol carboxymethylether coat. The coat keeps the iron from being released as free, reactive iron in the blood. Macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in the liver, spleen and bone marrow engulf the particles. Inside lysosomes, the coat is removed and the iron oxide is broken down. The released iron is either stored as ferritin or exported through ferroportin to bind transferrin in plasma. Transferrin carries iron to red cell precursors in the bone marrow, which use transferrin receptors to take it up and build hemoglobin. In MRI, the magnetic iron core strongly shortens T1 and T2-star relaxation, so it can brighten blood vessels or darken macrophage-rich tissue.",
  "body": [["Bone marrow", "Iron delivered by transferrin lets red cell precursors make hemoglobin, so hemoglobin rises over one to two weeks."], ["Liver and spleen", "Macrophages here take up most of the particles and act as the iron store and processing site."], ["Iron stores", "Ferritin and transferrin saturation rise as iron is released from macrophages."], ["Blood vessels on MRI", "The particles stay in the blood for hours, so they can brighten vessels as a blood-pool contrast agent."], ["Immune system", "Rare immediate reactions with low blood pressure can occur, likely from complement activation by the nanoparticles."], ["MRI after treatment", "Iron in tissues can change MRI signal for up to about three months, which radiologists need to know."]],
  "journey": "Ferumoxytol is given only into a vein. The particles circulate for a long time, with a plasma half-life of about 15 hours, before macrophages in the liver, spleen and bone marrow take them up. The iron is then processed and joins the body's normal iron pool, either stored in ferritin or carried by transferrin to the bone marrow. The body has no active way to excrete iron, so it is not meaningfully eliminated in urine or stool, and hemodialysis does not remove it. Red cell production responds over days to weeks.",
  "sidefx": "The most serious risk is a hypersensitivity reaction, including anaphylaxis and severe low blood pressure, which led to the boxed warning. These reactions are thought to involve complement activation and mediator release triggered by the nanoparticles, sometimes called complement activation-related pseudoallergy. That is why the drug is infused over time with monitoring afterward. Nausea, dizziness, diarrhea and headache are common. Excess iron can build up in tissues if given when not needed. Because the iron changes magnetic properties of tissue, MRI interpretation can be affected for weeks. There is no specific reversal agent; reactions are treated with epinephrine and supportive care.",
  "src": ["Feraheme (ferumoxytol) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"perflutren": {
  "origin": "Ultrasound contrast began with a lucky observation in 1968, when Raymond Gramiak and Pravin Shah at the University of Rochester saw a cloud of echoes in the heart after injecting saline during a heart catheterization. Tiny bubbles in the fluid caused the bright signal. Plain air bubbles dissolved too fast to get through the lungs, so researchers spent the next decades making smaller, longer-lasting bubbles. The first FDA-approved agent, Albunex, used air in albumin shells. Later agents used heavy, poorly soluble gases. Perflutren lipid microspheres, made with octafluoropropane gas in a phospholipid shell, were approved as Definity in 2001 and are now made by Lantheus. A boxed warning about serious heart and lung reactions was added in 2007 and later narrowed as more safety data came in.",
  "timeline": [["1968", "Gramiak and Shah describe echo contrast from injected saline bubbles."], ["1990s", "The first commercial ultrasound contrast agents are approved."], ["2001", "The FDA approves Definity, perflutren lipid microspheres."], ["2007", "A boxed warning about cardiopulmonary reactions is added."]],
  "cell": "Perflutren has no receptor target. Each microsphere is a tiny gas bubble, about the size of a red blood cell or smaller, filled with octafluoropropane and wrapped in a phospholipid shell. Octafluoropropane dissolves poorly in blood, so the bubbles last long enough to pass through the lung capillaries and reach the left heart. Ultrasound waves hitting the bubbles make them shrink and expand. Gas is very compressible, so the bubbles vibrate strongly and unevenly, sending back a powerful echo with harmonic frequencies. The huge difference in acoustic impedance between gas and blood makes the blood pool very bright. This outlines the left ventricle's inner wall so that wall motion and heart size can be judged.",
  "body": [["Heart chambers", "Brightens the left ventricle cavity so the inner wall border is clearly seen, improving assessment of wall motion and ejection fraction."], ["Heart muscle", "Some approaches use it to show blood flow within the heart muscle, though that use is not a labeled indication."], ["Lungs", "Bubbles pass through the lung capillaries; the gas eventually diffuses out and is exhaled."], ["Blood vessels", "Stays within the blood vessels as a true blood-pool agent."], ["Circulation", "Rare reactions with low blood pressure, low oxygen or arrhythmia occur, usually within 30 minutes of injection."]],
  "journey": "Perflutren is given into a vein as a bolus or slow infusion. The bubbles reach the right heart within seconds, pass through the lungs, and fill the left heart. Each bolus gives several minutes of useful contrast. The octafluoropropane gas dissolves out of the bubbles and is breathed out through the lungs; its half-life in blood is only about one to two minutes. The lipid shell joins the body's normal fat metabolism. Nothing needs to be cleared by the liver or kidneys.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, flushing, nausea and back or chest pain are the most common effects. Rare but serious reactions include low blood pressure, hypoxia, arrhythmia, anaphylaxis and cardiopulmonary arrest, which led to the boxed warning and a period of observation after injection. Some reactions appear to be complement-mediated responses to the lipid microspheres, sometimes called complement activation-related pseudoallergy, and some may relate to the patient's underlying heart disease. Polyethylene glycol (PEG) in the shell can trigger allergy in people sensitive to PEG. There is no antidote; reactions are treated with standard resuscitation.",
  "fun": "The first echo contrast ever seen came from ordinary saline: the bright cloud was tiny air bubbles stirred up as the fluid was injected.",
  "src": ["Definity (perflutren lipid microsphere) FDA label", "Gramiak R, Shah PM. Echocardiography of the aortic root, Invest Radiol 1968"]
},
"sulfur-hexafluoride": {
  "origin": "Sulfur hexafluoride lipid microspheres were developed by Bracco Research in Geneva, Switzerland, in the 1990s. The heavy, inert gas sulfur hexafluoride dissolves poorly in blood, so bubbles last long enough to cross the lungs. The product was approved in Europe as SonoVue in 2001 and became widely used in Europe and Asia for heart and liver imaging. The FDA approved it as Lumason in 2014 for echocardiography. In 2016 the FDA approved it for liver lesion imaging in adults and children and for ultrasound of the urinary tract in children, making it the first ultrasound contrast agent approved in the US for liver and pediatric bladder imaging.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Bracco develops sulfur hexafluoride microspheres in Geneva."], ["2001", "The agent is approved in Europe as SonoVue."], ["2014", "The FDA approves Lumason for echocardiography."], ["2016", "FDA approval is expanded to liver imaging and pediatric urinary tract ultrasound."]],
  "cell": "Sulfur hexafluoride microspheres have no receptor target. Each bubble is about the size of a red blood cell and is wrapped in a flexible phospholipid shell stabilized by polyethylene glycol. Under ultrasound the bubbles compress and expand far more than surrounding tissue, producing strong echoes and harmonic signals that contrast-specific software can pick out. Because the bubbles stay inside blood vessels, they map blood flow in real time. In the liver, a lesion's pattern over the arterial, portal venous and late phases helps tell benign masses from cancers. Placed in the bladder, they show if urine flows back toward the kidneys (vesicoureteral reflux) without radiation.",
  "body": [["Heart", "Fills the left ventricle so the wall border can be traced clearly for function and size."], ["Liver", "Real-time enhancement patterns help characterize focal liver lesions, such as hemangiomas, focal nodular hyperplasia or cancers."], ["Urinary tract in children", "Given into the bladder through a catheter, it shows reflux of urine into the ureters without x-ray radiation."], ["Lungs", "Bubbles cross the lung capillaries and the gas is exhaled through the lungs."], ["Circulation", "Rare serious reactions, such as low blood pressure or arrhythmia, can happen soon after injection."]],
  "journey": "Lumason is injected into a vein or, for children's bladder imaging, given into the bladder through a catheter. After IV injection the bubbles reach the heart within seconds and pass through the lungs into the arterial circulation. They remain in the blood vessels, giving several minutes of enhancement. The sulfur hexafluoride gas diffuses out and is breathed out, with nearly all gone within about 15 minutes and a terminal half-life of about 10 minutes. The phospholipid shell enters normal fat pathways. When given into the bladder, very little reaches the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Headache, nausea, altered taste, chest discomfort and a feeling of warmth are the most common. Rare but serious cardiopulmonary reactions, including low blood pressure, arrhythmia and cardiac arrest, can happen, mostly within 30 minutes; they seem to involve complement activation by the microspheres in some patients. Polyethylene glycol in the shell can cause allergy in PEG-sensitive people. After bladder use, mild painful urination can occur from the catheter. There is no antidote; reactions are managed with resuscitation and supportive care.",
  "src": ["Lumason (sulfur hexafluoride lipid-type A microspheres) FDA label", "ACR Manual on Contrast Media"]
},
"fdg-f18": {
  "origin": "In the 1920s Otto Warburg noticed that cancer cells use glucose much faster than normal cells, even when oxygen is available. Decades later, Louis Sokoloff at the US National Institutes of Health developed a method using radioactive carbon-labeled deoxyglucose to map glucose use in the brains of animals. To do this in people, a team at Brookhaven National Laboratory, including Tatsuo Ido, Alfred Wolf and Joanna Fowler, labeled deoxyglucose with fluorine-18 in the mid-1970s. In 1976 researchers at the University of Pennsylvania, including Abass Alavi and Martin Reivich, gave it to human volunteers and imaged the brain. FDG became the workhorse of PET imaging, gaining FDA approval in the 1990s and broader approval for cancer, heart and brain imaging in 2000.",
  "timeline": [["1920s", "Otto Warburg describes high glucose use by cancer cells."], ["1976", "Brookhaven chemists make FDG and Penn researchers image the human brain with it."], ["1977", "Sokoloff publishes the deoxyglucose method for brain glucose use."], ["2000", "The FDA approves FDG for cancer, heart and seizure focus imaging."]],
  "cell": "FDG is glucose with the hydroxyl group on carbon 2 replaced by radioactive fluorine-18. Cells take it in through GLUT transporters, mainly GLUT1 and GLUT3, just as they take in glucose. Hexokinase then adds a phosphate, making FDG-6-phosphate. Normally the next enzyme, phosphoglucose isomerase, needs the missing 2-hydroxyl group, so FDG-6-phosphate cannot move further into glycolysis. Most tissues have little glucose-6-phosphatase to remove the phosphate, and the charged molecule cannot leave the cell, so it is trapped. The amount trapped reflects how fast the cell uses glucose. Fluorine-18 decays by emitting a positron, which meets an electron and produces two 511 keV photons going in opposite directions. The PET scanner detects these pairs and builds a map of glucose use.",
  "body": [["Cancers", "Many tumors use glucose heavily (the Warburg effect) and trap more FDG, so they light up on PET for diagnosis, staging and checking treatment response."], ["Brain", "The brain normally uses a lot of glucose; seizure foci between seizures and areas affected by dementia show reduced uptake."], ["Heart", "Working but poorly perfused heart muscle can switch to glucose and take up FDG, which helps show viable tissue after a heart attack."], ["Inflammation", "Activated white cells also take up FDG, so infection and inflammation can light up and mimic cancer."], ["Kidneys and bladder", "Unlike glucose, FDG is not fully reabsorbed by the kidney, so it collects in the urine and the bladder receives the highest radiation dose."]],
  "journey": "FDG is injected into a vein. It spreads quickly through the blood and is taken up by cells over the next hour, so imaging usually starts about 60 minutes after injection while the patient rests quietly. Fasting beforehand keeps blood glucose low so it does not compete with FDG. Trapped FDG is not broken down further. The fluorine-18 decays with a physical half-life of about 110 minutes. The portion not trapped is filtered by the kidneys into the urine, which is why patients are asked to empty the bladder and drink fluids.",
  "sidefx": "At the tiny tracer amounts used, FDG has no measurable drug effects, and side effects are very rare. The main risk is radiation exposure, which is kept low by the short half-life of fluorine-18 and by encouraging urination. High blood glucose or recent insulin can reduce tumor uptake or shift FDG into muscle and fat, lowering image quality. Inflammation, recent surgery and brown fat can cause false positive uptake. There is no antidote, and none is needed.",
  "fun": "Because FDG has a half-life under two hours, it must be made in a cyclotron and rushed to the scanner, often within the same city or region.",
  "src": ["Fludeoxyglucose F 18 Injection FDA label", "Sokoloff L et al. J Neurochem 1977", "Fowler JS, Ido T. Initial and subsequent approach for the synthesis of 18FDG, Semin Nucl Med 2002"]
},
"tc99m-sestamibi": {
  "origin": "Technetium was the first element made artificially, discovered in 1937 by Carlo Perrier and Emilio Segre in Italy. In the late 1950s, scientists at Brookhaven National Laboratory built a generator that produces technetium-99m from molybdenum-99, which made Tc-99m cheap and available in any hospital. Its 6-hour half-life and clean gamma ray made it ideal for imaging. In the 1980s chemist Alan Davison at MIT and colleagues, working with Alun Jones at Harvard, developed a family of technetium isonitrile complexes. One of them, sestamibi, gave good images of heart muscle blood flow. DuPont marketed it as Cardiolite after FDA approval in 1990. It was later also approved for breast imaging (Miraluma) and is widely used to find overactive parathyroid glands.",
  "timeline": [["1937", "Perrier and Segre discover technetium."], ["1950s", "Brookhaven scientists develop the molybdenum-99/technetium-99m generator."], ["1980s", "Davison and Jones develop technetium isonitrile complexes including sestamibi."], ["1990", "The FDA approves sestamibi as Cardiolite for heart perfusion imaging."]],
  "cell": "Tc-99m sestamibi is a positively charged, fat-soluble complex of one technetium atom surrounded by six methoxyisobutyl isonitrile groups. Because it is lipophilic, it diffuses passively across cell membranes. The negative charge inside the cell, and the even stronger negative charge across the inner mitochondrial membrane, pull the cation into mitochondria, where it is held. Uptake is roughly proportional to blood flow and needs living cells with working mitochondria, so dead or scarred tissue takes up little. Sestamibi barely redistributes after uptake, so images taken later still show blood flow at the time of injection. Tc-99m emits a 140 keV gamma ray detected by SPECT. Sestamibi is also a substrate of P-glycoprotein, so tumors that pump it out may show less uptake.",
  "body": [["Heart muscle", "Areas with normal blood flow take up tracer; areas with reduced flow during stress show less uptake, revealing coronary artery disease."], ["Scarred heart tissue", "Dead muscle after a heart attack takes up little tracer at rest or stress, separating scar from ischemia."], ["Parathyroid glands", "Overactive parathyroid tissue, rich in mitochondria, holds the tracer longer than the thyroid, which helps surgeons find adenomas."], ["Breast", "Some breast cancers take up more tracer than normal breast tissue, which is used in molecular breast imaging."], ["Liver and gut", "The tracer is cleared through bile into the bowel, which can interfere with images of the bottom of the heart."]],
  "journey": "Sestamibi is injected into a vein at rest and during exercise or drug-induced stress. Heart muscle takes it up within minutes, and imaging is done about 15 to 60 minutes later; a fatty meal or milk may be given to speed clearance from the gallbladder. The complex is not metabolized. It leaves the body mainly through the liver into bile and stool, with a smaller amount in urine. Tc-99m decays with a physical half-life of about 6 hours.",
  "sidefx": "At tracer doses, sestamibi has no meaningful drug effect. Many people notice a brief metallic or bitter taste or an odd smell at injection, likely from small amounts reaching taste and smell receptors. Headache and flushing can occur, and allergic reactions are rare. Chest pain, arrhythmias or heart attack during the test come from the stress itself, not the tracer. The main risk is a small radiation dose. There is no antidote, and none is needed.",
  "fun": "Technetium's name comes from the Greek word technetos, meaning artificial, because it was the first element made by people rather than found in nature.",
  "src": ["Cardiolite (technetium Tc 99m sestamibi) FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"tc99m-medronate": {
  "origin": "Technetium-99m became widely available in the 1960s thanks to the molybdenum generator developed at Brookhaven National Laboratory. Bone imaging needed a carrier that would stick to bone mineral. In the early 1970s Gopal Subramanian and John McAfee at Upstate Medical Center in Syracuse, New York, showed that technetium-labeled phosphate compounds collect in bone. Phosphates with a carbon backbone (diphosphonates), related to bisphosphonate drugs, proved more stable and cleared faster from soft tissue. Methylene diphosphonate (MDP, medronate) became the standard bone scan agent by the late 1970s and remains one of the most common nuclear medicine studies in the world.",
  "timeline": [["1937", "Technetium is discovered by Perrier and Segre."], ["1960s", "Tc-99m generators make the isotope widely available."], ["1971", "Subramanian and McAfee introduce technetium-labeled phosphates for bone imaging."], ["1970s", "Tc-99m medronate becomes the standard bone scan agent."]],
  "cell": "Medronate is a diphosphonate, with two phosphonate groups joined by a carbon (a P-C-P backbone). That shape resists breakdown and binds tightly to calcium in hydroxyapatite, the mineral of bone. Labeled with Tc-99m, it attaches to the surface of bone crystals by chemisorption. Uptake depends on two things: how much blood reaches the bone and how active the osteoblasts are in laying down new mineral. Areas with fresh bone formation, such as healing fractures, infection, and many bone metastases, take up more tracer and appear as hot spots. Purely lytic lesions with little new bone formation, such as multiple myeloma, may be missed. Tc-99m emits a 140 keV gamma ray detected by a gamma camera.",
  "body": [["Bone", "Collects where bone turnover and blood flow are high, showing metastases, stress fractures, infection and Paget disease as hot spots."], ["Kidneys and bladder", "About half the tracer that does not bind bone is filtered into the urine, so the kidneys and bladder appear on images."], ["Soft tissue", "Background soft tissue activity fades over a few hours, which is why images are taken two to four hours after injection."], ["Abnormal calcification", "Can collect in soft tissue calcification, damaged heart muscle or some tumors, which may show up as unexpected uptake."]],
  "journey": "Tc-99m medronate is injected into a vein. It spreads through the blood and binds bone within minutes. Over the next few hours, the part not bound to bone is filtered by the kidneys and excreted in the urine, which clears soft tissue background. Patients are encouraged to drink fluids and urinate often to reduce bladder radiation. Images are usually taken two to four hours after injection, sometimes with early flow images right after. The compound is not metabolized, and Tc-99m decays with a half-life of about 6 hours.",
  "sidefx": "At the tiny tracer amounts used, medronate has no measurable drug effect. Rare reactions such as rash, itching or nausea have been reported, and anaphylaxis is very rare. The main risk is a small radiation dose, mostly to the bone surfaces and bladder wall, reduced by hydration and frequent urination. There is no antidote, and none is needed.",
  "src": ["Technetium Tc 99m Medronate (MDP) kit FDA label", "Subramanian G, McAfee JG. A new complex of 99mTc for skeletal imaging, Radiology 1971"]
},
"regadenoson": {
  "origin": "Adenosine and dipyridamole were long used to dilate coronary arteries for heart stress tests in people who could not exercise. Adenosine works well but acts on all four adenosine receptor subtypes, causing AV block, bronchospasm and chest discomfort, and it must be infused continuously. CV Therapeutics, a California company, set out to design a selective agonist of the A2A receptor, the subtype that dilates coronary arterioles. The result, regadenoson, could be given as a single quick injection. It was developed with Astellas and approved by the FDA as Lexiscan in 2008. It quickly became the most common drug used for pharmacologic stress testing in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1929", "Drury and Szent-Gyorgyi describe heart effects of adenosine."], ["1990s", "Adenosine becomes a pharmacologic stress agent for perfusion imaging."], ["2008", "The FDA approves regadenoson as Lexiscan."]],
  "cell": "Regadenoson is a selective, low-affinity agonist at the adenosine A2A receptor. A2A receptors on coronary arteriolar smooth muscle are Gs-coupled. Binding activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP, which activates protein kinase A and opens potassium channels, relaxing the smooth muscle. Coronary blood flow rises two to three times. Normal arteries dilate fully, but arteries narrowed by plaque are already dilated downstream and cannot increase flow further. This creates a difference in flow between healthy and diseased areas, which perfusion tracers like sestamibi reveal. Regadenoson has much weaker action at A1 receptors (AV node), A2B and A3 receptors (airways, mast cells) than adenosine, so AV block and bronchospasm are less common but can still occur.",
  "body": [["Coronary arteries", "Dilates coronary arterioles and raises blood flow so narrowed arteries can be found on perfusion imaging."], ["Heart rate and rhythm", "Heart rate rises from a reflex to vasodilation and sympathetic activation; AV block is less common than with adenosine but can occur."], ["Blood pressure", "Causes a mild drop in blood pressure from systemic vasodilation; rarely it rises."], ["Lungs", "Shortness of breath is common, and bronchospasm can occur, especially in people with asthma or COPD."], ["Brain", "Headache and dizziness are common; seizures have been reported, especially with a history of seizures."], ["Gut", "Nausea and stomach discomfort can occur during the test."]],
  "journey": "Regadenoson is given as a rapid IV injection followed by a saline flush, and the tracer is injected shortly after. Coronary flow rises within a minute and peaks within one to four minutes. Its levels fall in three phases: an early phase of a few minutes, a middle phase of about 30 minutes when the drug effect wears off, and a slower final phase of about two hours. It is barely metabolized and is not handled by CYP enzymes. More than half is excreted unchanged in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from adenosine receptor activation. Flushing, headache and dizziness come from vasodilation. Chest discomfort and shortness of breath are common and are thought to involve A2B receptors and sensory nerve activation. Activity at A1 receptors in the AV node can cause heart block. Bronchospasm is linked to A2B and A3 receptors in the airways. Seizures may relate to adenosine receptor effects in the brain. Myocardial ischemia can occur when flow is diverted from narrowed arteries. Aminophylline, an adenosine receptor antagonist, is used to reverse severe or persistent effects. Caffeine before the test blocks the drug's effect, which is why it is avoided.",
  "src": ["Lexiscan (regadenoson) FDA label", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"aminophylline": {
  "origin": "Aminophylline is theophylline paired with ethylenediamine to make it more water soluble. Theophylline is a methylxanthine found in small amounts in tea, which is where its name comes from (Greek theos, god, and phyllon, leaf, from Thea, the old name for the tea plant). German scientist Albrecht Kossel isolated it from tea leaves in 1888, and it was soon made synthetically. Theophylline was first used as a diuretic in the early 1900s. In the 1920s and 1930s doctors found it relaxed the airways, and IV aminophylline became a standard treatment for severe asthma for decades. Today it is mostly a second-line drug, but it remains useful to reverse the effects of adenosine-receptor stress agents like regadenoson.",
  "timeline": [["1888", "Albrecht Kossel isolates theophylline from tea leaves."], ["1900s", "Theophylline is used as a diuretic."], ["1930s", "IV aminophylline is used to treat severe asthma attacks."], ["2000s", "Aminophylline is used to reverse pharmacologic stress agents."]],
  "cell": "Aminophylline releases theophylline in the body. Theophylline competitively blocks adenosine A1 and A2 receptors. That reverses adenosine-driven effects such as coronary vasodilation from regadenoson, AV node slowing and airway narrowing. It also nonselectively inhibits phosphodiesterases, especially PDE3 and PDE4, which normally break down cAMP. Higher cAMP in airway smooth muscle activates protein kinase A and relaxes the muscle, opening the airways. In inflammatory cells, PDE4 inhibition reduces mediator release. Theophylline also strengthens diaphragm contraction and stimulates the brainstem breathing center. At higher levels it may activate histone deacetylase, reducing inflammatory gene expression. Its therapeutic window is narrow.",
  "body": [["Airways", "Relaxes bronchial smooth muscle through PDE inhibition and adenosine blockade, easing bronchospasm."], ["Heart", "Speeds the heart rate and increases contractility by raising cAMP and blocking adenosine; reverses adenosine-related AV block."], ["Brain", "Stimulates breathing drive, alertness and at high levels can cause seizures by blocking calming adenosine signals."], ["Diaphragm", "Improves diaphragm strength and lowers fatigue."], ["Kidneys", "Causes a mild diuresis through adenosine A1 blockade in the kidney."], ["Gut and metabolism", "Causes nausea, vomiting and acid secretion; at toxic levels it causes low potassium and high blood glucose through catecholamine release."]],
  "journey": "Aminophylline is usually given IV, where it quickly frees theophylline. Theophylline spreads widely, about 40 percent bound to protein, and crosses into the brain and placenta. The liver breaks it down mainly through CYP1A2, with help from CYP2E1 and CYP3A4. Its half-life is about 8 hours in healthy nonsmoking adults, shorter in smokers and children, and much longer in newborns, older adults and people with liver disease or heart failure. Many drugs that inhibit CYP1A2, like ciprofloxacin and fluvoxamine, raise levels. In newborns, part of it is turned into caffeine. Metabolites leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Theophylline toxicity reflects its mechanisms at too-high levels. PDE inhibition and catecholamine release cause nausea, vomiting, tremor, fast heart rate and arrhythmias such as multifocal atrial tachycardia and ventricular ectopy. Beta-adrenergic stimulation shifts potassium into cells, causing hypokalemia, and raises glucose. Blocking adenosine in the brain removes a natural seizure brake, so seizures can be severe and hard to stop. There is no specific antidote; activated charcoal (including repeat doses), benzodiazepines, beta blockers for some arrhythmias, and hemodialysis for severe cases are used.",
  "src": ["Aminophylline injection FDA label", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"potassium-iodide": {
  "origin": "Iodine was discovered in 1811 by French chemist Bernard Courtois, who found violet vapor rising from seaweed ash while making gunpowder ingredients. In 1820 Swiss doctor Jean-Francois Coindet used iodine to shrink goiters. In 1923 Henry Plummer at the Mayo Clinic showed that iodine given before surgery made Graves disease safer to operate on. In 1948 Jan Wolff and Israel Chaikoff described how large amounts of iodide briefly shut down thyroid hormone production, now called the Wolff-Chaikoff effect. During the Cold War, scientists recognized that stable iodide could block radioactive iodine from entering the thyroid. The FDA approved potassium iodide for thyroid protection in radiation emergencies in 1982. After the 1986 Chernobyl disaster, Poland gave KI to millions of children, a response often cited when planning for nuclear emergencies.",
  "timeline": [["1811", "Bernard Courtois discovers iodine in seaweed ash."], ["1923", "Henry Plummer uses iodine to prepare Graves disease patients for surgery."], ["1948", "Wolff and Chaikoff describe iodide's blocking effect on hormone synthesis."], ["1982", "The FDA approves KI for thyroid blocking in radiation emergencies."], ["1986", "Poland gives KI widely to children after the Chernobyl accident."]],
  "cell": "Thyroid follicular cells pull iodide from the blood through the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on their basolateral membrane. A flood of stable iodide from KI competes with radioactive iodide for this transporter, diluting it so far less radioiodine is trapped in the gland. At high iodide levels inside the cell, thyroid peroxidase organification of tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin is inhibited, which is the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, so less T4 and T3 are made. Iodide also blocks proteolysis of thyroglobulin, so stored hormone is released more slowly. In Graves disease, high iodide reduces blood flow to the gland, making surgery less bloody. Normal glands escape the Wolff-Chaikoff effect within days to weeks by lowering NIS expression.",
  "body": [["Thyroid in radiation emergencies", "Fills the thyroid with stable iodide so radioactive iodine is not absorbed, lowering future thyroid cancer risk, especially in children."], ["Thyroid in hyperthyroidism", "Rapidly blocks hormone release and synthesis, helping control thyroid storm and preparing the gland for surgery."], ["Airways", "Saturated solution (SSKI) has been used as an expectorant because iodide is secreted into respiratory mucus."], ["Salivary glands", "Iodide is concentrated in saliva, which can cause gland swelling and a metallic taste."], ["Skin", "Rash and acne-like eruptions can occur from iodide sensitivity."], ["Newborns", "Repeated exposure can cause neonatal hypothyroidism because the infant thyroid escapes the block less effectively."]],
  "journey": "Potassium iodide is taken by mouth as a tablet or solution and is absorbed quickly from the gut. Iodide spreads through extracellular fluid and is concentrated by the thyroid, salivary glands, stomach lining and breast tissue. Thyroid protection begins within about 30 minutes and lasts about a day. It is not metabolized. The kidneys excrete most of it in urine within hours to days, and small amounts appear in breast milk, saliva and sweat.",
  "sidefx": "Stomach upset and metallic taste come from local irritation and secretion into saliva. Iodism, with a burning mouth, runny nose, swollen salivary glands and acne-like rash, reflects iodide building up in glands and skin. In people with nodular goiter or Graves disease, extra iodide can trigger hyperthyroidism (the Jod-Basedow effect). In others, especially with autoimmune thyroiditis, the gland may fail to escape the Wolff-Chaikoff effect, leading to hypothyroidism. Rare allergic reactions, including angioedema, occur. There is no specific antidote; stopping the drug and supportive care are used.",
  "fun": "Iodine's name comes from the Greek iodes, meaning violet, after the purple vapor Courtois saw.",
  "src": ["iOSAT and ThyroSafe (potassium iodide) FDA labels", "FDA Guidance: Potassium Iodide as a Thyroid Blocking Agent in Radiation Emergencies", "Wolff J, Chaikoff IL. J Biol Chem 1948"]
},
"sincalide": {
  "origin": "In 1928 Andrew Ivy and Eric Oldberg at Northwestern University found that a substance from the upper small intestine made the gallbladder contract and named it cholecystokinin (CCK), Greek for 'moves the gallbladder.' In 1943 Alan Harper and Henry Raper found a gut hormone that stimulated pancreatic enzymes, which they called pancreozymin. In the 1960s Swedish chemists Erik Jorpes and Viktor Mutt purified the hormone and showed both actions came from the same peptide. Researchers then found that the last eight amino acids carry its full activity. That synthetic C-terminal octapeptide, sincalide, was developed by Squibb and introduced as Kinevac in the 1970s for gallbladder and pancreatic function testing.",
  "timeline": [["1928", "Ivy and Oldberg describe cholecystokinin."], ["1943", "Harper and Raper describe pancreozymin."], ["1960s", "Jorpes and Mutt show CCK and pancreozymin are the same peptide."], ["1970s", "Synthetic CCK-8 (sincalide) is introduced as Kinevac."]],
  "cell": "Sincalide is the C-terminal eight-amino-acid fragment of cholecystokinin, with a sulfated tyrosine needed for full activity. It binds the CCK type A receptor (CCK1), a Gq-coupled receptor on gallbladder smooth muscle. Gq activates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and diacylglycerol, which raise intracellular calcium and cause the gallbladder to contract. At the same time, CCK relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, partly through inhibitory nerves that release nitric oxide and VIP, so bile flows into the duodenum. On pancreatic acinar cells, CCK receptors trigger release of digestive enzymes. It also speeds gut motility. These actions mimic the hormone's normal response to fatty food in the duodenum.",
  "body": [["Gallbladder", "Contracts and empties within minutes; measuring how much empties on a HIDA scan (ejection fraction) helps diagnose gallbladder dysfunction."], ["Sphincter of Oddi", "Relaxes so bile and pancreatic juice flow into the duodenum."], ["Pancreas", "Stimulates enzyme release, which is used with secretin in pancreatic function testing."], ["Intestines", "Speeds small bowel transit and can cause cramping and an urge to have a bowel movement."], ["Circulation", "Flushing and dizziness can occur, and rarely low blood pressure."]],
  "journey": "Sincalide is given into a vein, either as a slow injection or as an infusion during imaging. The gallbladder begins to contract within minutes, with maximum emptying in about 5 to 15 minutes. Peptidases in blood and tissues break it down quickly, so its effect is short and ends soon after the infusion stops. Its elimination has not been well studied.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect CCK's normal actions taken to an extreme. Strong gallbladder and intestinal contraction cause abdominal cramping, nausea and an urge to defecate, especially with fast injection, which is why slow infusion is preferred. Flushing and dizziness come from mild vasodilation. In people with gallstones, strong contraction may push a stone into the duct. Hypersensitivity and anaphylaxis are rare. There is no antidote; effects fade quickly once it is stopped.",
  "src": ["Kinevac (sincalide) FDA label", "Ivy AC, Oldberg E. Am J Physiol 1928"]
},
"secretin": {
  "origin": "Secretin was the first hormone ever discovered. In 1902 William Bayliss and Ernest Starling at University College London showed that acid in the small intestine caused the pancreas to secrete fluid even when the nerves were cut. They concluded a chemical messenger traveled through the blood and named it secretin. In 1905 Starling coined the word hormone, from the Greek for 'to excite' or 'set in motion.' Erik Jorpes and Viktor Mutt in Stockholm purified secretin in the 1960s and determined its 27-amino-acid sequence. For years, natural porcine secretin was used in testing. A synthetic human secretin, ChiRhoStim, was approved by the FDA in the 2000s for pancreatic function and gastrinoma testing, and later for helping see pancreatic ducts during MRCP.",
  "timeline": [["1902", "Bayliss and Starling discover secretin."], ["1905", "Starling introduces the word hormone."], ["1960s", "Jorpes and Mutt purify and sequence secretin."], ["2000s", "Synthetic human secretin is approved for diagnostic use in the US."]],
  "cell": "Secretin is a 27-amino-acid peptide. It binds the secretin receptor, a class B G protein-coupled receptor, on pancreatic duct cells. The receptor activates Gs, which raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A. PKA opens the CFTR chloride channel on the duct lining. Chloride flows into the duct and is swapped for bicarbonate, and water follows, producing a large volume of bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes stomach acid in the duodenum. In normal stomach G cells, secretin lowers gastrin release. Gastrinoma cells in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome have secretin receptors, and secretin makes them release gastrin, so a rise in gastrin after secretin points to a gastrinoma.",
  "body": [["Pancreas", "Duct cells pour out bicarbonate-rich fluid; measuring the output tests how well the pancreas works in chronic pancreatitis."], ["Pancreatic ducts on MRI", "Extra fluid fills and widens the ducts, improving MRCP images of duct problems."], ["Stomach", "Normally lowers gastrin and acid secretion; gastrinomas react paradoxically by releasing gastrin, which is the basis of the secretin stimulation test."], ["Duodenum", "Fluid fills the duodenum within minutes, which can cause a feeling of fullness or nausea."], ["Liver and bile ducts", "Also increases bicarbonate-rich bile flow from bile duct cells."]],
  "journey": "Synthetic human secretin is given as a quick IV injection. Pancreatic secretion peaks within about 5 to 10 minutes, and in gastrinoma testing gastrin levels rise within about 10 minutes. The peptide is broken down by peptidases, and its half-life is about 45 minutes. The response lasts about 30 to 60 minutes. Its elimination route has not been fully described.",
  "sidefx": "Secretin is usually well tolerated. Nausea, flushing, abdominal discomfort and diarrhea come from sudden fluid release into the gut and mild vasodilation. Allergic reactions, including rare anaphylaxis, can happen with any peptide. Drugs like anticholinergics can blunt the response, and proton pump inhibitors can falsely raise gastrin, which affects testing. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Bayliss and Starling's 1902 secretin discovery launched the field of endocrinology, the study of hormones.",
  "src": ["ChiRhoStim (human secretin) FDA label", "Bayliss WM, Starling EH. J Physiol 1902"]
},
"caffeine-citrate": {
  "origin": "Caffeine was first isolated from coffee beans in 1819 by German chemist Friedlieb Ferdinand Runge, reportedly after Johann Wolfgang von Goethe encouraged him to study coffee. In the 1970s doctors studying preterm babies found that methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine reduced apnea of prematurity, pauses in breathing caused by an immature brainstem. Caffeine was preferred because it has a wider safety margin and a long half-life in newborns, allowing once-daily dosing. The FDA approved caffeine citrate as Cafcit in 1999 for short-term treatment of apnea of prematurity. The Caffeine for Apnea of Prematurity (CAP) trial, published in 2006 and 2007, showed it also reduced bronchopulmonary dysplasia and improved survival without neurodevelopmental disability at 18 to 21 months.",
  "timeline": [["1819", "Friedlieb Runge isolates caffeine from coffee."], ["1970s", "Methylxanthines are shown to reduce apnea in preterm infants."], ["1999", "The FDA approves Cafcit for apnea of prematurity."], ["2006", "The CAP trial shows lower rates of bronchopulmonary dysplasia with caffeine."]],
  "cell": "Caffeine is a methylxanthine that competitively blocks adenosine receptors. Adenosine normally acts on A1 and A2A receptors in the brain to slow neuron firing and depress breathing, and in preterm infants this braking effect is strong. By blocking these receptors in the medullary respiratory centers, caffeine increases breathing drive, raises sensitivity to carbon dioxide and makes breathing more regular. It also increases diaphragm contractility and minute ventilation. Only at much higher concentrations does caffeine inhibit phosphodiesterases and raise intracellular cAMP, or affect calcium release from intracellular stores. The citrate form is simply a salt that dissolves easily and contains half its weight as caffeine base.",
  "body": [["Brainstem", "Blocks adenosine in the breathing centers, which reduces apneic pauses and the need for mechanical ventilation."], ["Lungs and diaphragm", "Increases diaphragm strength and minute ventilation, helping infants come off breathing support sooner."], ["Heart", "Mildly increases heart rate; tachycardia is a sign of excess."], ["Brain development", "In the CAP trial, caffeine-treated infants had better neurodevelopmental outcomes at 18 to 21 months."], ["Kidneys", "Blocks adenosine A1 receptors in the kidney, causing a mild diuresis."], ["Gut", "Can cause feeding intolerance and reflux; necrotizing enterocolitis has been reported, though a cause is not proven."]],
  "journey": "Caffeine citrate is given IV or by mouth to infants and is almost fully absorbed when given orally. It spreads through total body water and easily enters the brain. In preterm newborns, the liver enzyme CYP1A2 is immature, so caffeine is barely metabolized and is cleared mostly unchanged by the kidneys. Its half-life is very long, about 3 to 4 days, which allows once-daily dosing. As the liver matures over the first months of life, demethylation increases and the half-life drops to about 5 hours, similar to adults.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from too much adenosine blockade and stimulation. Tachycardia, jitteriness and irritability reflect central and cardiac stimulation. Feeding intolerance and regurgitation reflect increased gut acid and motility. The mild diuresis can affect fluid balance. At toxic levels, seizures, arrhythmias, high or low blood glucose and metabolic acidosis can occur. Because the half-life is long in newborns, levels build up and stay high for days after stopping. There is no antidote; care is supportive, and in severe cases exchange transfusion or dialysis has been used.",
  "fun": "Preterm infants given caffeine can take 3 to 4 days to clear half of it, compared with about 5 hours for most adults.",
  "src": ["Cafcit (caffeine citrate) FDA label", "Schmidt B et al. Caffeine for Apnea of Prematurity trial, N Engl J Med 2006 and 2007"]
},
"poractant-alfa": {
  "origin": "In 1959 Mary Ellen Avery and Jere Mead at Harvard showed that babies who died of respiratory distress syndrome lacked surfactant, the soapy substance that keeps air sacs from collapsing. Treatment took decades to develop. In 1980 Tetsuro Fujiwara in Japan reported the first successful treatment of preterm babies with a surfactant made from cow lungs. In Sweden, Bengt Robertson and Tore Curstedt at the Karolinska Institute developed a surfactant extract from minced pig lungs. The Italian company Chiesi brought it to market as Curosurf, and multicenter European trials in the late 1980s showed it saved lives. The FDA approved poractant alfa in 1999 for treatment of respiratory distress syndrome in preterm infants.",
  "timeline": [["1959", "Avery and Mead link infant respiratory distress syndrome to surfactant deficiency."], ["1980", "Fujiwara reports the first successful surfactant treatment in preterm infants."], ["1980s", "Robertson and Curstedt develop porcine surfactant at Karolinska."], ["1999", "The FDA approves Curosurf in the US."]],
  "cell": "Poractant alfa is a natural extract of pig lung surfactant. It contains phospholipids, mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC), plus the small hydrophobic proteins SP-B and SP-C. Surfactant is normally made by alveolar type II cells. When given into the trachea, the phospholipids spread quickly across the air-liquid surface of the alveoli. SP-B and SP-C help them adsorb and form a stable film. As the alveolus shrinks during exhalation, the DPPC film packs tightly and lowers surface tension to near zero. By the law of Laplace, lower surface tension prevents small alveoli from collapsing at end-expiration. The lungs become easier to inflate, functional residual capacity rises and gas exchange improves.",
  "body": [["Alveoli", "Keeps air sacs open at the end of each breath, preventing the widespread collapse of respiratory distress syndrome."], ["Lung mechanics", "Compliance improves within minutes to hours, so lower ventilator pressures and oxygen levels are needed."], ["Oxygenation", "Better-open alveoli improve oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange, often quickly after dosing."], ["Heart rate", "Brief bradycardia and drops in oxygen saturation can happen during dosing from airway obstruction or vagal response."], ["Lung injury", "Sudden improvement in compliance can cause overdistension and air leaks such as pneumothorax if ventilator settings are not lowered."], ["Pulmonary blood vessels", "Pulmonary hemorrhage is a rare serious complication, possibly related to shifts in pulmonary blood flow."]],
  "journey": "Poractant alfa is given directly into the lungs through an endotracheal tube or a thin catheter. It spreads through the airways into the alveoli within minutes. Very little enters the blood. The surfactant lipids are recycled by alveolar type II cells, which take them up and reuse them, and are also broken down by alveolar macrophages. Repeat doses may be needed if surfactant is inactivated by plasma proteins leaking into the alveoli.",
  "sidefx": "Most problems occur during dosing. The liquid can block the airway or tube briefly, causing bradycardia and oxygen desaturation, and some may reflux up the tube. When the lungs suddenly become more compliant, the same ventilator pressure can overinflate them and cause pneumothorax, so settings are adjusted quickly. Pulmonary hemorrhage is a rare but serious event. Because poractant comes from pig lungs, it contains animal proteins, but immune reactions are not a major issue. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Curosurf (poractant alfa) FDA label", "Avery ME, Mead J. Am J Dis Child 1959", "Fujiwara T et al. Lancet 1980"]
},
"alprostadil": {
  "origin": "Alprostadil is prostaglandin E1. In the 1930s Ulf von Euler in Sweden studied substances in semen that lowered blood pressure and contracted smooth muscle, and he named them prostaglandins because he thought they came from the prostate. In the late 1950s and early 1960s Sune Bergstrom and colleagues at the Karolinska Institute isolated PGE1 and other prostaglandins and worked out their structures. Bergstrom, Bengt Samuelsson and John Vane shared the 1982 Nobel Prize for prostaglandin research. In the mid-1970s pediatric cardiologists, including Peter Olley and Flavio Coceani in Toronto, showed that PGE1 could keep the ductus arteriosus open in newborns with duct-dependent heart defects. Upjohn's Prostin VR Pediatric was approved in the US in the early 1980s. Later, alprostadil was approved for erectile dysfunction as Caverject injection and MUSE urethral pellets in the 1990s.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "Ulf von Euler describes and names prostaglandins."], ["1960s", "Bergstrom's group isolates PGE1 and determines its structure."], ["1970s", "PGE1 is shown to keep the ductus arteriosus open in newborns."], ["1982", "Bergstrom, Samuelsson and Vane share the Nobel Prize for prostaglandin work."], ["1990s", "Alprostadil is approved for erectile dysfunction."]],
  "cell": "Alprostadil binds prostaglandin E (EP) receptors, mainly the Gs-coupled EP2 and EP4 subtypes, on smooth muscle of the ductus arteriosus and other blood vessels. Gs activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP, which activates protein kinase A. PKA lowers intracellular calcium and reduces myosin light-chain kinase activity, so the muscle relaxes. Before birth, low oxygen and high PGE2 from the placenta keep the ductus open. After birth, oxygen rises and PGE2 falls, and the ductus constricts. Giving PGE1 restores the relaxing signal and reopens or keeps open the ductus, supplying blood to the lungs or body in duct-dependent heart defects. In the penis, the same cAMP pathway relaxes corpus cavernosum smooth muscle, letting blood fill the erectile tissue. It also inhibits platelet aggregation.",
  "body": [["Ductus arteriosus", "Keeps the ductus open, allowing blood to reach the lungs or body in newborns with critical congenital heart defects until surgery."], ["Systemic vessels", "Dilates blood vessels, which can cause flushing and low blood pressure."], ["Brainstem", "Apnea is a common serious effect in newborns, especially those under 2 kg, so breathing must be closely watched."], ["Temperature", "Fever is common, reflecting prostaglandin action on the hypothalamus."], ["Bones and gut", "Long-term infusion can cause thickening of the bone surface (cortical hyperostosis) and gastric outlet obstruction from overgrowth of the stomach lining."], ["Penis", "Relaxes cavernosal smooth muscle to produce an erection; overstimulation can cause priapism."]],
  "journey": "In newborns, alprostadil is given as a continuous IV infusion, or sometimes through an umbilical artery, because it is broken down very fast. Up to about 80 percent is destroyed in a single pass through the lungs by oxidation, and its half-life is about 5 to 10 minutes. The ductus responds within about 15 minutes to a few hours. The inactive metabolites are excreted by the kidneys. If the infusion stops, the ductus can begin to close within hours. For erectile dysfunction it is injected into the penis or placed in the urethra, and little reaches the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from prostaglandin effects in other tissues. Apnea in newborns is thought to come from prostaglandin effects on the breathing centers, and it often appears within the first hour. Vasodilation causes flushing, low blood pressure and sometimes bradycardia or tachycardia. Prostaglandins act on the hypothalamus to cause fever. Long infusions stimulate bone and stomach lining growth, causing cortical hyperostosis and gastric outlet obstruction. In adults, injection into the penis can cause pain, prolonged erection and fibrosis. There is no specific antidote; stopping or lowering the infusion and supportive care, including intubation for apnea, are used.",
  "src": ["Prostin VR Pediatric (alprostadil) FDA label", "Caverject (alprostadil) FDA label", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1982 materials"]
},
"cholecalciferol": {
  "origin": "Vitamin D was discovered while scientists fought rickets, a bone disease common in children in smoky, sunless industrial cities. In 1919 Edward Mellanby in Britain showed that cod liver oil prevented rickets in dogs. In 1922 Elmer McCollum at Johns Hopkins showed the active factor differed from vitamin A and named it vitamin D. Around the same time, researchers found that sunlight or ultraviolet light also cured rickets, and Harry Steenbock at the University of Wisconsin showed in 1923 that UV light could make foods antirachitic. Adolf Windaus in Germany worked out the chemistry of sterols and vitamin D and won the 1928 Nobel Prize in Chemistry; his group identified vitamin D3 in the 1930s. In the US, cholecalciferol is sold mainly as a dietary supplement, which is not FDA approved as a drug.",
  "timeline": [["1919", "Edward Mellanby shows cod liver oil prevents rickets in dogs."], ["1922", "Elmer McCollum names vitamin D."], ["1923", "Harry Steenbock shows UV light can make foods antirachitic."], ["1928", "Adolf Windaus receives the Nobel Prize for sterol and vitamin research."], ["1930s", "Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) is identified."]],
  "cell": "Cholecalciferol is a prohormone that must be activated. It is made in skin when UVB light converts 7-dehydrocholesterol into previtamin D3, or it is absorbed from food and supplements. In the liver, the enzyme CYP2R1 adds a hydroxyl group to make 25-hydroxyvitamin D, the main form measured in blood. In the kidney proximal tubule, 1-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP27B1), stimulated by parathyroid hormone and low phosphate, makes 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), the active hormone. Calcitriol binds the nuclear vitamin D receptor, which pairs with the retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin D response elements on DNA. This increases making of calcium channels (TRPV6) and calcium-binding proteins (calbindin) in gut cells, raising calcium and phosphate absorption. It also acts on bone and kidney and suppresses PTH.",
  "body": [["Intestine", "Raises absorption of calcium and phosphate from food, which supplies minerals for bone."], ["Bone", "Supports mineralization of new bone; deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults."], ["Parathyroid glands", "Calcitriol lowers PTH production, so correcting deficiency reduces secondary hyperparathyroidism."], ["Kidneys", "Increases calcium reabsorption and is the site of final activation; kidney disease reduces activation."], ["Muscles", "Severe deficiency causes muscle weakness and pain, which improve with repletion."], ["Immune system", "Vitamin D receptors are found on immune cells, but the clinical value of extra vitamin D for immune or other outcomes is not well established."]],
  "journey": "Cholecalciferol taken by mouth is absorbed with dietary fat in the small intestine and carried in chylomicrons through the lymph. It is stored in fat tissue and taken to the liver bound to vitamin D-binding protein. The liver converts it to 25-hydroxyvitamin D, which has a half-life of about two to three weeks, so blood levels take weeks to rise. The kidney then makes the active form as needed. Breakdown is through CYP24A1, and metabolites leave mainly through bile in stool.",
  "sidefx": "At usual intake levels, side effects are rare. Taking too much over time causes vitamin D toxicity, in which very high calcitriol activity pushes gut calcium absorption and bone release too high. Hypercalcemia causes nausea, constipation, thirst, frequent urination, confusion, kidney stones and, if severe, kidney failure and arrhythmias. Because vitamin D is stored in fat, toxicity can last for weeks after stopping. There is no specific antidote; treatment is stopping the vitamin, IV fluids, and sometimes corticosteroids or bisphosphonates to lower calcium.",
  "fun": "The skin can make vitamin D3 from sunlight, so many scientists consider it a hormone rather than a true vitamin.",
  "src": ["Office of Dietary Supplements, NIH: Vitamin D fact sheet", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1928 materials"]
},
"melatonin": {
  "origin": "In 1958 dermatologist Aaron Lerner and his team at Yale University isolated melatonin from about 250,000 cow pineal glands. They were looking for a cause of skin pigment disorders, because pineal extracts were known to lighten frog skin. They named the compound melatonin because it lightened melanocytes and is made from serotonin. Scientists later learned it is released at night and helps set the body clock. In 1994 the Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act let melatonin be sold in the US as a dietary supplement. It is not FDA approved as a drug. The related prescription drug ramelteon (Rozerem) is a separate, FDA-approved melatonin receptor agonist.",
  "timeline": [["1958", "Aaron Lerner's team at Yale isolates melatonin from cow pineal glands."], ["1970s", "Melatonin is shown to follow a nighttime release rhythm controlled by light."], ["1994", "DSHEA allows melatonin to be sold as a supplement in the US."], ["2005", "The FDA approves ramelteon, a prescription melatonin receptor agonist."]],
  "cell": "The pineal gland makes melatonin from tryptophan, through serotonin, with the enzyme serotonin N-acetyltransferase controlling the rate. Light hitting special retinal ganglion cells sends signals to the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) in the hypothalamus, which suppresses melatonin by day. At night the signal is released and melatonin rises. Melatonin binds two Gi-coupled receptors: MT1 and MT2. MT1 activation in the SCN dampens SCN neuron firing, which helps sleep onset. MT2 activation shifts the timing of the circadian clock. When taken by mouth, melatonin adds to this signal, mildly promoting sleepiness and, when timed correctly, shifting the clock earlier or later. It also lowers core body temperature slightly.",
  "body": [["Brain (SCN)", "Reduces the alerting signal of the body clock and shifts its timing, which may help jet lag and delayed sleep phase."], ["Sleep", "Shortens time to fall asleep modestly in some studies; effect size is generally small."], ["Body temperature", "Slightly lowers core temperature, part of the natural evening drop that prepares for sleep."], ["Daytime alertness", "Taken at the wrong time or in large amounts, it can cause next-day grogginess."], ["Reproduction", "In seasonal animals, melatonin length signals day length and controls breeding cycles; its role in humans is less clear."]],
  "journey": "Melatonin taken by mouth is absorbed quickly, with peak levels in about one hour, but bioavailability is low and variable because the liver breaks much of it down on first pass. The liver enzyme CYP1A2 converts it to 6-hydroxymelatonin, which is then sulfated or glucuronidated and excreted in urine. Its half-life is short, about 40 to 60 minutes. Drugs that inhibit CYP1A2, like fluvoxamine, can sharply raise melatonin levels. Extended-release products last longer.",
  "sidefx": "Because melatonin acts on natural sleep and clock signals, its side effects are generally mild. Drowsiness, headache, dizziness and nausea are the most common, and next-day sleepiness can occur if taken late or in large amounts. Vivid dreams may reflect changes in sleep structure. Because supplements are not regulated as drugs, studies have found actual melatonin content often differs from the label, and some products contain serotonin. Accidental ingestion by children has risen sharply with gummy products. There is no antidote; effects wear off as the short half-life passes.",
  "fun": "Lerner's team needed about a quarter million cow pineal glands to isolate a tiny amount of melatonin.",
  "src": ["Lerner AB et al. J Am Chem Soc 1958", "NIH National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health: Melatonin", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"bpc-157": {
  "origin": "BPC-157, short for Body Protection Compound-157, is a synthetic 15-amino-acid peptide. It was developed and studied mainly by Predrag Sikiric and colleagues at the University of Zagreb in Croatia starting in the early 1990s. They described it as a fragment of a protein found in human gastric juice and reported that it protected the stomach and helped tissue healing in rats. Most published research comes from this one group and is in animals. It is not FDA approved for any use, and human evidence is very limited. It is sold online as an injectable vial, capsule or nasal spray for injury healing. In 2023 the FDA listed BPC-157 among substances that raise significant safety concerns for compounding, and it is banned in sport by the World Anti-Doping Agency.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Sikiric and colleagues in Zagreb begin publishing animal studies of BPC-157."], ["2000s", "A small development program (PL 14736) tests it for inflammatory bowel disease."], ["2023", "The FDA lists BPC-157 among bulk substances with safety concerns for compounding."]],
  "cell": "How BPC-157 works in humans is not established. In animal and cell studies, it has been proposed to increase signaling through the vascular endothelial growth factor receptor 2 (VEGFR2) pathway, which promotes new blood vessel growth. Studies also suggest it interacts with the nitric oxide system and increases growth hormone receptor expression on tendon cells. Some work points to activation of the FAK-paxillin pathway, which helps cells move and attach during wound healing. These findings come mostly from rodent models of gut, tendon, muscle and nerve injury. Because angiogenesis also supports tumor growth, scientists have raised the theoretical concern that it could promote cancer blood supply.",
  "body": [["Stomach and gut", "Animal studies report protection against ulcers and faster healing of bowel injury; human benefit is not proven."], ["Tendons and muscle", "Rodent studies report faster healing of tendon and muscle injuries, the main reason people seek it."], ["Blood vessels", "Proposed to stimulate angiogenesis, which could aid healing but raises concern about tumor growth."], ["Injection sites", "Injection of unregulated products can cause pain, redness, abscess or cellulitis."], ["Immune system", "Contaminated or impure peptides can cause allergic reactions or infection."]],
  "journey": "How BPC-157 moves through the human body is unknown. Animal data suggest it is stable in gastric juice, which is why it is sold in oral forms, but its absorption, distribution, breakdown and elimination have not been well studied in humans. In animals the plasma half-life appears short. Gray-market products vary in purity and content, so what a user actually receives is uncertain.",
  "sidefx": "Because BPC-157 is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported problems include injection-site pain and redness, nausea, dizziness and headache. The main known risks come from product quality: gray-market vials can be contaminated with bacteria or endotoxin, leading to abscesses or sepsis, or contain the wrong substance or amount. Its proposed angiogenic effect raises a theoretical risk of promoting tumors. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["FDA: Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks (2023)", "World Anti-Doping Agency Prohibited List", "Sikiric P et al. review articles on BPC 157"]
},
"tb-500": {
  "origin": "TB-500 is a synthetic peptide based on thymosin beta-4, a natural 43-amino-acid protein. Thymosin beta-4 was first isolated in 1981 from calf thymus extracts by Teresa Low, Allan Goldstein and colleagues at George Washington University while they studied thymic hormones. Researchers later found it is not mainly a thymic hormone; it is found in nearly all cells and binds actin. The company RegeneRx studied full-length thymosin beta-4 for wound and eye healing. TB-500 is a short fragment sold on the gray market, used in horse racing and among athletes and bodybuilders for injury recovery. It is not FDA approved, human evidence is very limited, and it is banned by the World Anti-Doping Agency. In 2023 the FDA listed thymosin beta-4 fragment among substances with safety concerns for compounding.",
  "timeline": [["1981", "Thymosin beta-4 is isolated from calf thymus by Goldstein's group."], ["1990s", "Thymosin beta-4 is identified as a major actin-sequestering protein."], ["2000s", "Full-length thymosin beta-4 is tested in early human wound and eye studies."], ["2023", "The FDA lists thymosin beta-4 fragment among bulk substances with safety concerns."]],
  "cell": "The mechanism of the TB-500 fragment itself is not established. Full-length thymosin beta-4 binds monomeric G-actin and keeps it from polymerizing into filaments, acting as a buffer that the cell can draw on when it needs to move. In preclinical studies, thymosin beta-4 promoted cell migration, new blood vessel growth, wound closure and reduced inflammation, and it activated repair pathways in injured heart tissue in mice. The fragment sold as TB-500 contains the actin-binding region, and sellers claim it shares these effects, but direct evidence is limited. Because it supports cell movement and angiogenesis, there is a theoretical concern it could promote tumor growth or spread.",
  "body": [["Skin and wounds", "Full-length thymosin beta-4 sped wound healing in animal models; human proof is limited."], ["Muscles and tendons", "Users take it hoping for faster recovery from injuries, a use not supported by solid human trials."], ["Blood vessels", "Proposed to promote angiogenesis, which raises concern about effects on tumors."], ["Heart", "Mouse studies suggested heart repair effects after injury; these have not been confirmed in people."], ["Injection sites", "Unregulated injections can cause abscesses, infection and allergic reactions."]],
  "journey": "How TB-500 is absorbed, distributed, broken down and eliminated in humans is unknown. It is usually injected under the skin or into muscle. Full-length thymosin beta-4 is broken down by peptidases, and some fragments are detectable in urine, which is how anti-doping labs test for it. Product content and purity vary widely.",
  "sidefx": "Because TB-500 is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects include injection-site reactions, headache, fatigue and lightheadedness. The biggest known risks come from poor product quality: contamination can cause abscesses, sepsis or allergic reactions, and vials may contain the wrong amount or a different substance. Its proposed effects on cell migration and angiogenesis raise a theoretical tumor-promoting risk. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Low TL, Hu SK, Goldstein AL. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1981", "FDA: Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks (2023)", "World Anti-Doping Agency Prohibited List"]
},
"ipamorelin": {
  "origin": "Ipamorelin is a synthetic five-amino-acid peptide developed at Novo Nordisk in Denmark in the 1990s. It came from a line of research on growth hormone secretagogues, compounds first made in the 1970s and 1980s that release growth hormone through a then-unknown receptor. That receptor was cloned in 1996, and its natural ligand, ghrelin, was discovered in 1999 by Masayasu Kojima and Kenji Kangawa in Japan. A 1998 report described ipamorelin as the first growth hormone secretagogue selective enough to release GH without raising cortisol or prolactin much. It was later tested by other companies for postoperative ileus, but it was not approved. It is not FDA approved, and it is sold on the gray market, often blended with CJC-1295.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "The growth hormone secretagogue receptor is cloned."], ["1998", "Novo Nordisk researchers describe ipamorelin as a selective GH secretagogue."], ["1999", "Kojima and Kangawa discover ghrelin, the receptor's natural ligand."], ["2000s", "Ipamorelin is tested for postoperative ileus but not approved."]],
  "cell": "Ipamorelin is an agonist at the growth hormone secretagogue receptor type 1a (GHS-R1a), also called the ghrelin receptor. This is a Gq-coupled receptor on somatotroph cells of the anterior pituitary and on hypothalamic neurons. Activation raises intracellular calcium and triggers release of a pulse of growth hormone. It also boosts GHRH signaling and reduces somatostatin tone in the hypothalamus. Growth hormone then acts on the liver to raise IGF-1. Early studies showed ipamorelin had less effect on ACTH, cortisol and prolactin than older secretagogues. Because the ghrelin receptor also affects appetite and gut motility, it can increase hunger and was studied to speed bowel recovery after surgery.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "Releases pulses of growth hormone, which raise IGF-1 levels."], ["Appetite", "Ghrelin receptor activation can increase hunger."], ["Gut", "Increases gut motility, the reason it was studied for postoperative ileus."], ["Metabolism", "GH raises blood glucose and lowers insulin sensitivity, so blood sugar can rise."], ["Fluid balance", "GH and IGF-1 cause salt and water retention, leading to swelling and tingling in the hands."]],
  "journey": "In early studies, ipamorelin was given by IV or subcutaneous injection. Growth hormone peaked about 30 to 60 minutes after dosing, and ipamorelin had a half-life of about two hours. It was mostly cleared in urine. Its breakdown and full human pharmacokinetics have not been fully described, and gray-market products vary in content.",
  "sidefx": "Because ipamorelin is not FDA approved, its long-term safety is unknown. Its known effects follow from raising GH: water retention, swelling, joint aches and numbness or tingling in the hands from fluid around nerves. GH counteracts insulin, so blood glucose may rise. Increased hunger reflects ghrelin receptor activation. Injection-site reactions, headache and flushing are reported. Contaminated products can cause infection or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Raun K et al. Ipamorelin, the first selective growth hormone secretagogue, Eur J Endocrinol 1998", "Kojima M et al. Ghrelin, Nature 1999"]
},
"cjc-1295": {
  "origin": "CJC-1295 is a modified version of growth hormone-releasing hormone. The first 29 amino acids of human GHRH carry its full activity, and that fragment was the basis of the drug sermorelin. GHRH(1-29) breaks down within minutes, so the Canadian company ConjuChem, in Montreal, changed several amino acids to resist breakdown and added a chemical linker called drug affinity complex (DAC). DAC bonds the peptide to albumin in the blood, greatly extending how long it lasts. Early human studies published in 2006 showed it raised GH and IGF-1 for days after one injection. Development did not lead to approval. It is not FDA approved, and products are sold on the gray market with or without DAC.",
  "timeline": [["1982", "Human GHRH is isolated and sequenced."], ["2000s", "ConjuChem develops CJC-1295 with its albumin-binding DAC technology."], ["2006", "Early human studies show prolonged rises in GH and IGF-1."]],
  "cell": "CJC-1295 is an analog of GHRH(1-29) that binds the GHRH receptor on somatotroph cells of the anterior pituitary. This receptor is Gs-coupled: binding raises cAMP, activates protein kinase A and increases both GH gene transcription and release of stored GH. The amino acid substitutions protect it from dipeptidyl peptidase-4 and other enzymes. In the DAC version, a reactive maleimide group forms a covalent bond with albumin after injection, so the peptide circulates for days instead of minutes. GH release remains pulsatile because somatostatin still controls the pituitary, but baseline GH and IGF-1 stay higher. The version without DAC, often called modified GRF(1-29), has a short half-life.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "Stimulates GH production and release, raising GH levels for days with the DAC version."], ["Liver", "Higher GH causes the liver to make more IGF-1, which mediates many growth effects."], ["Muscle and fat", "GH and IGF-1 may increase lean mass and fat breakdown, the reasons people seek it."], ["Metabolism", "GH lowers insulin sensitivity, which can raise blood glucose."], ["Fluid balance", "Water and salt retention can cause swelling and carpal tunnel symptoms."]],
  "journey": "CJC-1295 is injected under the skin. The DAC version binds albumin and has a half-life of about 6 to 8 days in early human studies, so GH and IGF-1 stay raised for days. The version without DAC lasts only about 30 minutes. It is broken down by proteolysis. Full human elimination data are not available, and gray-market products vary in content and purity.",
  "sidefx": "Because CJC-1295 is not FDA approved, long-term safety is unknown. Side effects come from prolonged GH and IGF-1 elevation: water retention, swelling, joint pain, tingling and carpal tunnel symptoms. GH blocks insulin action, raising blood glucose. Flushing, headache and injection-site reactions are reported. Prolonged high IGF-1 is a theoretical concern for tumor growth. Contaminated products can cause infection or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; the long half-life of the DAC version means effects last days.",
  "src": ["Teichman SL et al. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2006", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"sermorelin": {
  "origin": "In 1982 two teams found human growth hormone-releasing hormone in pancreatic tumors that were causing acromegaly. Roger Guillemin's group at the Salk Institute and Wylie Vale's group, also at the Salk Institute, each isolated and sequenced the peptide. Researchers then showed the first 29 amino acids carried full activity. That fragment, GHRH(1-29), became sermorelin. Serono marketed it as Geref, which the FDA approved in the 1990s for testing and treating growth hormone deficiency in children. Geref was discontinued in 2008. There is no currently marketed FDA-approved sermorelin product, though compounded versions are sold.",
  "timeline": [["1982", "Guillemin's and Vale's groups isolate human GHRH from tumors causing acromegaly."], ["1990s", "The FDA approves sermorelin as Geref."], ["2008", "Geref is discontinued in the US."]],
  "cell": "Sermorelin is the 1-29 amino acid fragment of human GHRH. It binds the GHRH receptor, a Gs-coupled receptor on somatotroph cells in the anterior pituitary. This raises cAMP, activates protein kinase A and the transcription factor CREB, and increases GH synthesis and release. Because sermorelin works upstream of GH, the pituitary still responds to somatostatin and IGF-1 negative feedback, which keeps GH release pulsatile and limits extreme levels. GH acts on the liver and other tissues to raise IGF-1, which drives growth in children. Its response depends on a working pituitary, so it was also used as a diagnostic test of pituitary GH reserve.",
  "body": [["Pituitary", "Triggers pulsatile GH release, which keeps natural feedback controls in place."], ["Liver", "GH raises IGF-1 production, the main signal for growth."], ["Growth plates", "In children with GH deficiency, raised GH and IGF-1 increase bone length and height."], ["Metabolism", "GH can reduce insulin sensitivity and raise blood glucose."], ["Injection site", "Pain, redness and swelling at the injection site are the most common effects."]],
  "journey": "Sermorelin is injected under the skin or into a vein. GH rises within minutes and peaks within about 30 to 60 minutes. Sermorelin itself is broken down quickly by peptidases, with a half-life of about 10 to 20 minutes. Its elimination has not been fully described. Compounded products vary in quality and content.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects reflect injection or GH effects. Pain, redness and swelling at the injection site are common. Flushing, headache and dizziness can follow injection. Raised GH can lower insulin sensitivity and raise blood glucose. Antibodies against sermorelin have been reported. Rare allergic reactions occur. Compounded products can be contaminated and cause infection. There is no antidote; effects fade quickly because of the short half-life.",
  "src": ["Geref (sermorelin acetate) FDA label (discontinued)", "Guillemin R et al. Science 1982", "Rivier J, Vale W et al. Nature 1982"]
},
"ghk-cu": {
  "origin": "GHK-Cu is a copper complex of a tiny three-amino-acid peptide: glycine, histidine and lysine. It was discovered in 1973 by Loren Pickart, who found that a factor from the plasma of young people made old liver tissue in the lab behave more like young tissue. He identified it as GHK, a peptide that binds copper tightly. Levels of GHK in plasma fall with age. Since the 1980s it has been widely used in cosmetic skin serums and creams. It is not FDA approved as a drug. It is also sold as an injectable research peptide, a use with no established human safety data.",
  "timeline": [["1973", "Loren Pickart identifies GHK as a plasma factor that binds copper."], ["1980s", "GHK-Cu is studied in wound healing and begins appearing in skin care products."], ["2010s", "Lab studies describe broad effects of GHK on gene expression."]],
  "cell": "How GHK-Cu acts in humans is not established. In lab studies, GHK binds copper(II) with high affinity and can deliver it to cells. Copper is a cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen and elastin, and for superoxide dismutase, an antioxidant enzyme. In cell culture and animal studies, GHK-Cu increases collagen, elastin and glycosaminoglycan production by fibroblasts, attracts immune and endothelial cells to wounds, and promotes tissue remodeling. Gene expression studies suggest GHK changes the activity of many genes involved in repair and inflammation. These are proposed mechanisms from lab work; clinical evidence is mainly small cosmetic studies.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Cosmetic studies suggest improved skin firmness and reduced fine lines with topical use; evidence is limited."], ["Wounds", "Animal studies showed faster wound healing; clinical proof is lacking."], ["Hair", "Some cosmetic products claim hair growth effects; human evidence is weak."], ["Injection sites", "Injected products can cause pain, redness, bruising and a blue-green tint at the site, reportedly from copper."]],
  "journey": "On the skin, GHK-Cu acts mostly in the outer layers, and little reaches the blood. GHK occurs naturally in plasma, where it binds albumin and helps carry copper. It is broken down quickly by peptidases. How injected GHK-Cu is distributed and eliminated in humans is not known.",
  "sidefx": "Topical GHK-Cu is generally well tolerated, though skin irritation can occur. Injected products are not FDA approved, and their safety is unknown. Reported effects include injection-site pain, redness, bruising and a blue-green skin discoloration from copper. Unregulated products can be contaminated, causing infection or sepsis. Theoretically, large amounts of copper could cause toxicity, especially in people with Wilson disease. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Pickart L, Thaler MM. Nature New Biology 1973", "Pickart L, Margolina A. Regenerative and protective actions of the GHK-Cu peptide, Int J Mol Sci 2018"]
},
"retatrutide": {
  "origin": "Retatrutide is a laboratory-designed peptide from Eli Lilly, built on the success of GLP-1 drugs like semaglutide and the dual GIP and GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide. Lilly scientists engineered a single molecule that activates three receptors: GIP, GLP-1 and glucagon. Adding glucagon activity was meant to raise energy use and burn liver fat. A phase 2 trial published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2023 reported large weight loss over 48 weeks. Phase 3 trials (the TRIUMPH program) followed. It is not FDA approved, and it is an investigational drug. Gray-market products labeled as retatrutide are sold illegally, and their content and safety are unknown.",
  "timeline": [["2010s", "Eli Lilly develops retatrutide (LY3437943) as a triple hormone receptor agonist."], ["2023", "Phase 2 results in obesity are published in the New England Journal of Medicine."], ["2020s", "Phase 3 trials test retatrutide in obesity and related conditions."]],
  "cell": "Retatrutide is a single peptide that acts as an agonist at three Gs-coupled receptors. At the GLP-1 receptor in the pancreas, it increases glucose-dependent insulin release and lowers glucagon; in the brain, it reduces appetite; and in the stomach, it slows emptying. At the GIP receptor, it adds insulin release and may reduce nausea and affect fat tissue. At the glucagon receptor in the liver, it increases fat oxidation, reduces liver fat and is thought to raise energy expenditure. Normally glucagon raises blood sugar, but the strong GLP-1 and GIP insulin effects counter this. A fatty acid side chain binds albumin, giving a long half-life.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Reduces appetite and increases fullness, lowering food intake."], ["Pancreas", "Increases glucose-dependent insulin release, lowering blood sugar."], ["Liver", "Glucagon receptor action promotes fat burning and reduces liver fat."], ["Stomach and gut", "Slows stomach emptying and causes nausea, vomiting, diarrhea or constipation, especially early."], ["Heart", "Heart rate increases in a dose-related way, as with other incretin drugs."], ["Gallbladder and pancreas", "Rapid weight loss and incretin effects may raise risks of gallbladder disease and pancreatitis."]],
  "journey": "In trials, retatrutide was injected under the skin once a week. A fatty acid chain binds it to albumin, protecting it from breakdown and kidney filtration, giving a half-life of about six days. It is expected to be broken down by proteolysis like other peptides. Full human elimination data are not yet published.",
  "sidefx": "In trials, most side effects came from incretin effects on the gut: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and constipation, mostly during dose increases. Vomiting and diarrhea can lead to dehydration and acute kidney injury. Heart rate rose, likely from GLP-1 and glucagon receptor effects. Pancreatitis and gallbladder disease are concerns shared with other incretin drugs. Hypoglycemia is mainly a risk with insulin or sulfonylureas. Because it is investigational, its full safety profile is not known, and gray-market products add risks of contamination. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Jastreboff AM et al. Triple-hormone-receptor agonist retatrutide for obesity, N Engl J Med 2023", "ClinicalTrials.gov: TRIUMPH program"]
},
"mots-c": {
  "origin": "MOTS-c is a small peptide encoded not in the cell nucleus but in mitochondrial DNA. In 2015 Changhan Lee, Pinchas Cohen and colleagues at the University of Southern California reported in Cell Metabolism that a short open reading frame within the mitochondrial 12S rRNA gene codes a 16-amino-acid peptide. In mice, MOTS-c improved insulin sensitivity and prevented diet-induced obesity. It belongs to a growing family of mitochondrial-derived peptides that includes humanin. It is not FDA approved, human evidence is very limited, and it is sold on the gray market as a performance or metabolic product.",
  "timeline": [["2001", "Humanin, the first mitochondrial-derived peptide, is described."], ["2015", "Lee, Cohen and colleagues describe MOTS-c in Cell Metabolism."], ["2020s", "Animal studies explore MOTS-c in exercise and aging."]],
  "cell": "How MOTS-c acts in humans is not established. In mouse and cell studies, MOTS-c inhibits the folate cycle and de novo purine synthesis, leading to buildup of AICAR, an intermediate that activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK). AMPK is the cell's energy sensor: it increases glucose uptake and fat oxidation in muscle. Under metabolic stress, MOTS-c has been shown to move into the nucleus and regulate genes related to stress response. These effects improved insulin sensitivity and exercise capacity in mice. Because the evidence is mostly from animals, these mechanisms are proposed rather than proven in people.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "In mice, it increased glucose uptake and exercise capacity through AMPK activation."], ["Metabolism", "Mouse studies showed protection from diet-induced obesity and insulin resistance."], ["Blood sugar", "Theoretical risk of low blood sugar when combined with diabetes drugs."], ["Injection sites", "Injected gray-market products can cause site reactions and infection."]],
  "journey": "How MOTS-c is absorbed, distributed, broken down and eliminated in humans is unknown. It is naturally present at low levels in blood and tissues, where levels appear to rise with exercise and fall with age. Gray-market products are usually injected under the skin, and their content and purity vary.",
  "sidefx": "Because MOTS-c is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects include injection-site reactions, fatigue and palpitations. Its proposed effect on glucose uptake raises a theoretical risk of low blood sugar with diabetes medicines. Contaminated products can cause abscesses, sepsis or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Lee C et al. The mitochondrial-derived peptide MOTS-c promotes metabolic homeostasis, Cell Metab 2015"]
},
"aod-9604": {
  "origin": "AOD-9604 stands for Advanced Obesity Drug 9604. It was developed in the 1990s by Frank Ng and colleagues at Monash University in Melbourne, Australia. They found that the tail end of human growth hormone (amino acids 176 to 191) seemed to drive fat breakdown in animals without GH's effects on growth or blood sugar. AOD-9604 is a modified version of that fragment with an extra tyrosine. The Australian company Metabolic Pharmaceuticals tested it in human obesity trials in the 2000s, but it did not produce meaningful weight loss and development for obesity was stopped. It is not FDA approved, and it is sold on the gray market as a fat-loss peptide.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Frank Ng and colleagues at Monash University develop AOD-9604 from a GH fragment."], ["2000s", "Human obesity trials fail to show meaningful weight loss."], ["2010s", "AOD-9604 is sold on the gray market as a fat-loss peptide."]],
  "cell": "AOD-9604 is a 16-amino-acid peptide based on the C-terminal region of human growth hormone. Unlike full GH, it does not bind the GH receptor in a way that raises IGF-1. In early animal and fat cell studies, it was proposed to stimulate lipolysis (breakdown of stored fat) and inhibit lipogenesis (new fat formation), possibly through beta-3 adrenergic receptor pathways in fat cells. The exact receptor and pathway are not known. Human trials did not confirm a meaningful fat-loss effect.",
  "body": [["Fat tissue", "Proposed to increase fat breakdown and reduce fat storage based on animal studies; human trials did not show meaningful weight loss."], ["Growth and IGF-1", "Does not raise IGF-1 in studies, unlike full growth hormone."], ["Blood sugar", "Early studies did not show the insulin resistance seen with full GH."], ["Injection sites", "Unregulated injected products can cause site reactions and infection."]],
  "journey": "Little is known about how AOD-9604 behaves in the human body. Studies suggest it is broken down by proteolysis and has a short half-life. Human trials used oral and injected forms. Gray-market products vary in content and purity.",
  "sidefx": "In human trials, AOD-9604 was generally well tolerated, with headache and mild injection-site reactions reported. Because it is not FDA approved, long-term safety is unknown. The main known risks come from unregulated products: contamination can cause infection, sepsis or allergic reactions. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Heffernan MA et al. Endocrinology 2001", "FDA: Certain Bulk Drug Substances for Use in Compounding that May Present Significant Safety Risks"]
},
"selank": {
  "origin": "Selank is a synthetic seven-amino-acid peptide developed in Russia. It is based on tuftsin, a natural four-amino-acid peptide from immunoglobulin G, discovered in 1970 by Victor Najjar and Kenji Nishioka at Tufts University and named after it. Scientists at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences, with the Zakusov Institute of Pharmacology in Moscow, added a short Pro-Gly-Pro tail to tuftsin to make it more stable. Selank is registered as a drug in Russia for anxiety. It is not FDA approved, most research comes from Russian groups, and independent human evidence is limited. It is sold online as a nasal spray.",
  "timeline": [["1970", "Najjar and Nishioka discover tuftsin at Tufts University."], ["1990s", "Russian researchers develop Selank from tuftsin."], ["2000s", "Selank is registered as a drug in Russia."]],
  "cell": "How Selank works is not fully established. Studies, mostly from Russian groups, propose several actions. It may modulate GABA-A receptor signaling, giving calming effects similar to benzodiazepines but without strong sedation. It is reported to inhibit enzymes that break down enkephalins, so these natural opioid peptides last longer. It is also reported to change expression of genes related to monoamines like serotonin and to immune cytokines, reflecting tuftsin's immune origin. Its Pro-Gly-Pro tail protects it from rapid breakdown. These mechanisms are proposed and have not been confirmed by independent research.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Claimed to reduce anxiety without sedation; independent human evidence is limited."], ["Immune system", "Studies propose effects on cytokine levels, reflecting its origin from tuftsin."], ["Memory and attention", "Some Russian studies claim improved memory; these claims are not well confirmed."], ["Nose", "Nasal spray use can cause nasal irritation."]],
  "journey": "Selank is given as a nasal spray in Russia. Studies suggest it is absorbed through the nasal lining and may reach the brain, though how much is unclear. Peptidases break it down quickly, and its half-life is reported to be minutes. Full human pharmacokinetics are not established.",
  "sidefx": "Because Selank is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not well characterized in the US. Reported effects include nasal irritation, fatigue and headache. Allergic reactions are possible. Products bought online are unregulated and may be impure or mislabeled. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Najjar VA, Nishioka K. Nature 1970", "Russian state drug register (Selank)"]
},
"semax": {
  "origin": "Semax is a synthetic seven-amino-acid peptide developed in the Soviet Union and Russia in the 1980s and 1990s. Scientists at the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Russian Academy of Sciences, working with Moscow State University, started from ACTH(4-10), a fragment of adrenocorticotropic hormone known to affect attention and memory in animals without stimulating the adrenal glands. They added a Pro-Gly-Pro tail to slow its breakdown. Semax is registered as a drug in Russia, used as a nasal spray for stroke recovery and cognitive problems. It is not FDA approved, most research comes from Russian groups, and independent human evidence is limited.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Russian researchers develop Semax from the ACTH(4-10) fragment."], ["1990s", "Semax is registered as a drug in Russia."], ["2000s", "Russian studies describe effects on BDNF and gene expression in animals."]],
  "cell": "How Semax works is not fully established. It is derived from the ACTH(4-10) sequence, which contains the core melanocortin motif, but it lacks the part of ACTH that stimulates cortisol release. Studies, mostly from Russian groups, report that it raises levels of brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and its receptor TrkB in rat brain, which could support neuron survival and plasticity. It has also been reported to affect dopamine and serotonin systems and to change expression of genes related to inflammation and blood vessels after experimental stroke. Possible action at melanocortin receptors has been proposed. The Pro-Gly-Pro tail resists peptidases. These mechanisms are proposed and largely unconfirmed outside Russia.",
  "body": [["Brain after stroke", "Russian studies claim improved recovery after stroke; independent trials are lacking."], ["Attention and memory", "Claimed to improve focus and memory, mainly based on animal and small human studies."], ["Adrenal glands", "Does not raise cortisol, because it lacks the ACTH region that activates adrenal receptors."], ["Nose", "Nasal spray use can cause nasal irritation."], ["Sleep and mood", "Irritability and insomnia are reported, possibly from stimulating effects."]],
  "journey": "Semax is given as a nasal spray. Studies suggest it is absorbed through the nasal lining and may reach the brain. Peptidases break it down quickly, with a plasma half-life of minutes, though its effects are claimed to last hours, possibly through active fragments such as Pro-Gly-Pro. Full human pharmacokinetics are not established.",
  "sidefx": "Because Semax is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not well described in the US. Reported effects include nasal irritation, headache, irritability and trouble sleeping. Allergic reactions are possible. Products bought online are unregulated and may be impure or mislabeled. There is no antidote.",
  "src": ["Russian state drug register (Semax)", "Dolotov OV et al. Brain Res 2006"]
},
"kisspeptin-10": {
  "origin": "The kisspeptin story began in 1996, when researchers led by Danny Welch at Penn State College of Medicine in Hershey, Pennsylvania, found a gene that suppressed the spread of melanoma. They named it KISS1, a nod to Hershey's famous chocolate Kisses. In 2001 scientists identified its protein products, kisspeptins, as ligands of an orphan receptor called GPR54, and one product was named metastin. In 2003 two groups showed that people with mutations in GPR54 fail to go through puberty, revealing kisspeptin as a master switch for the reproductive axis. Kisspeptin-10 is the shortest active fragment, the last ten amino acids. It is used in research on fertility and puberty. It is not FDA approved and is sold on the gray market.",
  "timeline": [["1996", "The KISS1 gene is discovered in Hershey, Pennsylvania, as a melanoma metastasis suppressor."], ["2001", "Kisspeptins are identified as ligands for the GPR54 receptor."], ["2003", "GPR54 mutations are shown to block puberty in humans."], ["2010s", "Kisspeptin is studied to trigger egg maturation in IVF research."]],
  "cell": "Kisspeptin-10 is an agonist at KISS1R (GPR54), a Gq-coupled receptor on gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) neurons in the hypothalamus. Activation turns on phospholipase C, raising intracellular calcium and depolarizing the neurons, which fire and release GnRH into the pituitary portal blood. GnRH then stimulates pituitary gonadotroph cells to release luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH). LH drives testosterone production by the testes and ovulation in women, and FSH supports sperm and follicle development. Kisspeptin neurons are a key link between sex steroid feedback, energy status and GnRH release. Continuous exposure can desensitize the receptor and suppress the axis.",
  "body": [["Hypothalamus", "Triggers GnRH neurons to release GnRH, the top signal of the reproductive axis."], ["Pituitary", "GnRH drives a short rise in LH and FSH within minutes."], ["Testes", "LH stimulates testosterone production, so testosterone rises briefly."], ["Ovaries", "In research settings, kisspeptin has been used to trigger egg maturation before IVF."], ["Long-term exposure", "Prolonged use may desensitize the receptor and suppress reproductive hormones, the opposite of the intended effect."]],
  "journey": "Kisspeptin-10 has been given IV or under the skin in research. LH rises within minutes and peaks around 30 to 60 minutes later. The peptide is broken down quickly by proteolysis, with a half-life of minutes. Its elimination in humans is not well characterized. Gray-market products vary in content and purity.",
  "sidefx": "Because kisspeptin-10 is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects in studies and use include flushing, headache and injection-site reactions. Repeated or continuous use may disrupt the reproductive hormone axis through receptor desensitization. Allergic reactions and infection from contaminated products are possible. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "The KISS1 gene was named after Hershey's Kisses because it was discovered in Hershey, Pennsylvania.",
  "src": ["Lee JH, Welch DR et al. J Natl Cancer Inst 1996", "de Roux N et al. PNAS 2003", "Seminara SB et al. N Engl J Med 2003"]
},
"melanotan-ii": {
  "origin": "Melanotan II was developed at the University of Arizona in the 1980s and 1990s by a team that included chemist Victor Hruby and biologist Mac Hadley. They wanted a synthetic version of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone (alpha-MSH) that could tan the skin without sunlight and possibly lower skin cancer risk. Melanotan II, a small cyclic peptide, tanned the skin but also caused nausea, appetite loss and spontaneous erections in early studies. The sexual effects led to bremelanotide, which the FDA approved as Vyleesi in 2019 for low sexual desire in premenopausal women. A related compound, afamelanotide (Melanotan I), was approved as Scenesse in 2019 for a rare light-sensitivity disorder. Melanotan II itself is not FDA approved and is sold illegally as tanning injections and nasal sprays.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "University of Arizona researchers develop melanotan peptides for sunless tanning."], ["1990s", "Melanotan II is found to cause spontaneous erections in early studies."], ["2019", "The FDA approves bremelanotide (Vyleesi) and afamelanotide (Scenesse), related melanocortin drugs."]],
  "cell": "Melanotan II is a cyclic analog of alpha-MSH that acts as a nonselective agonist at melanocortin receptors. At MC1R on melanocytes, it activates Gs, raises cAMP and increases tyrosinase activity, shifting melanin production toward dark eumelanin and darkening skin, freckles and moles. At MC3R and MC4R in the hypothalamus and spinal cord, it reduces appetite and activates pathways involved in sexual arousal, which explains spontaneous erections. Melanocortin receptors are also involved in autonomic and blood pressure control. Its broad action at multiple receptor subtypes explains its many side effects.",
  "body": [["Skin", "Darkens skin through increased eumelanin, even without sun exposure."], ["Moles and freckles", "Existing moles can darken and new ones can appear; case reports describe melanoma in users."], ["Appetite and gut", "Reduces appetite and often causes nausea and vomiting, especially after early doses."], ["Sexual function", "Causes spontaneous erections and increased arousal; priapism can occur."], ["Blood vessels and blood pressure", "Causes facial flushing and can raise blood pressure."], ["Brain and kidneys", "Rare case reports describe PRES with seizures, rhabdomyolysis and kidney infarction."]],
  "journey": "Melanotan II is usually injected under the skin or used as a nasal spray. Its half-life in plasma is about one hour in limited study data, but skin darkening builds up over days and lasts weeks. It is broken down by peptidases. Full human pharmacokinetics are not established, and illegal products vary widely in content and purity.",
  "sidefx": "Melanotan II's side effects follow from activating many melanocortin receptors at once. MC1R activation darkens skin and moles, which can hide or mimic melanoma, and melanoma has been reported in users. MC3R and MC4R activation causes nausea, appetite loss, yawning and stretching, flushing and spontaneous erections, sometimes prolonged into priapism. Effects on blood pressure and blood vessels may underlie rare serious events such as PRES, kidney infarction and rhabdomyolysis. Unregulated products add risks of contamination and infection. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "The peptide that gave the world bremelanotide started as a tanning project meant to help prevent skin cancer.",
  "src": ["Hadley ME, Dorr RT. Melanocortin peptide therapeutics, Peptides 2006", "Vyleesi (bremelanotide) FDA label", "FDA warning on melanotan products"]
},
"epitalon": {
  "origin": "Epitalon is a synthetic four-amino-acid peptide (Ala-Glu-Asp-Gly). It was developed in Russia by Vladimir Khavinson and colleagues at the St. Petersburg Institute of Bioregulation and Gerontology. They first worked with epithalamin, a peptide extract from cow pineal glands, in the 1980s, and later designed epitalon as a short synthetic version based on its amino acid makeup. Their studies, done mostly by one research group, claimed effects on aging, melatonin and telomeres. It is not FDA approved, independent human evidence is lacking, and it is sold online as an anti-aging peptide.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Khavinson's group studies epithalamin, a pineal gland extract."], ["1990s", "The synthetic tetrapeptide epitalon is developed."], ["2003", "The group reports telomerase activation in human cell cultures."]],
  "cell": "How epitalon works is not established. Lab studies, mostly from the Khavinson group, report that it activates telomerase in human cell cultures, lengthening telomeres, the protective caps on chromosomes that shorten with each cell division. Animal studies from the same group report it restores nighttime melatonin release in older animals and affects gene expression. These are proposed mechanisms. Independent confirmation is lacking, and telomerase activation also raises a theoretical concern about cancer, because many cancer cells depend on telomerase to keep dividing.",
  "body": [["Pineal gland", "Animal studies from one group claim it normalizes melatonin release in older animals."], ["Cells and aging", "Claimed to lengthen telomeres in cell cultures; no proof of anti-aging effects in people."], ["Lifespan", "Rodent studies from the same group claimed longer lifespan; these have not been independently confirmed."], ["Injection sites", "Unregulated injected products can cause site reactions and infection."]],
  "journey": "How epitalon is absorbed, distributed, broken down and eliminated in humans is unknown. As a small peptide, it is expected to be broken down by peptidases. It is sold as an injectable and in other forms, and product content and purity vary.",
  "sidefx": "Because epitalon is not FDA approved, its side effect profile is not established. Reported effects include injection-site reactions, headache and drowsiness. Allergic reactions and infections from contaminated products are possible. Its proposed telomerase activation raises a theoretical cancer risk. There is no antidote; care is supportive.",
  "src": ["Khavinson VK et al. Bull Exp Biol Med 2003", "Anisimov VN, Khavinson VK. Biogerontology 2010"]
},
"ethanol": {
  "origin": "Ethanol is made when yeasts ferment sugars, and people have made alcoholic drinks for thousands of years. Chemical traces in pottery from Jiahu in China show a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit around 7000 BCE. Wine and beer were central to ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt. Distillation, which concentrates alcohol into spirits, spread in medieval Europe and the Islamic world. Doctors once used alcohol widely as a medicine, and it was given IV as an antidote for methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning before fomepizole became preferred. Today beverage alcohol is legal for adults 21 and older in the US and regulated by the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau, not by the FDA as a drug. Alcohol use is a leading cause of preventable death.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Around 7000 BCE, people at Jiahu in China make a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit."], ["Ancient", "Beer and wine are part of daily life in Mesopotamia and Egypt."], ["1920", "US Prohibition begins under the 18th Amendment."], ["1933", "Prohibition ends with the 21st Amendment."], ["1984", "The National Minimum Drinking Age Act sets age 21 nationwide."]],
  "cell": "Ethanol is a small molecule that acts on several brain targets at once. It is a positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors, especially those with delta subunits, so it increases chloride flow into neurons and makes them less excitable. It also blocks NMDA-type glutamate receptors, reducing excitatory signaling, which contributes to memory blackouts. It releases dopamine in the reward pathway of the nucleus accumbens and releases endorphins, which drive its rewarding effects. With chronic use, the brain adapts by reducing GABA-A function and increasing NMDA receptors. When alcohol is suddenly stopped, the brain is left overexcited, causing tremor, seizures and delirium tremens.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Low levels cause disinhibition and relaxation; higher levels cause slurred speech, poor coordination, memory loss, coma and depressed breathing."], ["Liver", "Metabolism makes NADH and acetaldehyde, leading to fatty liver, alcoholic hepatitis and cirrhosis over time; it also blocks gluconeogenesis, causing low blood sugar."], ["Heart", "Binge drinking can trigger atrial fibrillation (holiday heart); chronic heavy use can cause cardiomyopathy."], ["Blood vessels and skin", "Dilates skin vessels, causing flushing and heat loss that leads to hypothermia in the cold."], ["Kidneys", "Suppresses vasopressin release, causing increased urination and dehydration."], ["Stomach and pancreas", "Irritates the stomach lining and can cause gastritis, bleeding and pancreatitis."]],
  "journey": "Ethanol is absorbed quickly from the stomach and especially the small intestine; food slows absorption by delaying stomach emptying. Peak blood levels come about 30 to 90 minutes after drinking. It spreads through total body water, so people with less body water reach higher levels from the same amount. The liver breaks down over 90 percent: alcohol dehydrogenase converts it to acetaldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase converts that to acetate. In chronic drinkers CYP2E1 also helps. Because these enzymes are saturated at low levels, elimination is zero-order, a fixed amount per hour. Small amounts leave in breath, which is the basis of breathalyzers, and in urine.",
  "sidefx": "Ethanol's harms come from its actions on many organs. Too much GABA-A enhancement and NMDA blockade depress the brainstem, causing coma and respiratory arrest. Vomiting while sedated can lead to aspiration. Acetaldehyde and oxidative stress damage the liver and other tissues, and acetaldehyde is a known carcinogen. Thiamine deficiency in chronic drinkers can cause Wernicke encephalopathy. Withdrawal after chronic use is dangerous because the brain has adapted to its presence. There is no specific antidote for alcohol intoxication; care focuses on airway, breathing, glucose, thiamine and warmth, and benzodiazepines are used for withdrawal.",
  "fun": "The flushing some people get after drinking often comes from a common ALDH2 gene variant that slows acetaldehyde breakdown, the same effect produced by the drug disulfiram.",
  "src": ["McGovern PE et al. Fermented beverages of pre- and proto-historic China, PNAS 2004", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "NIAAA: Alcohol's Effects on the Body"]
},
"nicotine": {
  "origin": "Nicotine comes from the tobacco plant, Nicotiana tabacum, native to the Americas, where Indigenous peoples used tobacco for centuries in ceremonies and medicine. European explorers brought it back in the 1500s. In 1560 Jean Nicot, the French ambassador to Portugal, sent tobacco to the French court, and the plant and its alkaloid were later named after him. In 1828 German chemists Wilhelm Posselt and Karl Reimann at Heidelberg isolated pure nicotine. The 1964 US Surgeon General's report linked smoking to lung cancer. To help smokers quit, Swedish scientists at Leo developed nicotine gum, which the FDA approved as Nicorette in 1984. Patches followed in the early 1990s, and many replacement products became available over the counter in 1996. Tobacco and e-cigarette products are regulated by the FDA as tobacco products, not drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1560", "Jean Nicot sends tobacco to the French court."], ["1828", "Posselt and Reimann isolate nicotine."], ["1964", "The US Surgeon General's report links smoking to lung cancer."], ["1984", "The FDA approves Nicorette nicotine gum."], ["1996", "Nicotine gum and patches become available over the counter."]],
  "cell": "Nicotine is an agonist at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, ligand-gated ion channels made of five subunits. In the brain, the alpha4-beta2 receptor on dopamine neurons of the ventral tegmental area is key: activation lets sodium and calcium in, depolarizes the neuron and releases dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, which reinforces use. Nicotine also activates receptors in autonomic ganglia (alpha3-beta4), stimulating both sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves, and in the adrenal medulla, releasing epinephrine. With repeated exposure, receptors desensitize and the brain makes more of them, which underlies tolerance, dependence and withdrawal. At very high levels, nicotine causes depolarizing blockade, like succinylcholine, at ganglia and the neuromuscular junction.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Releases dopamine and other neurotransmitters, causing alertness, reward and, with repeated use, dependence and withdrawal symptoms."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation and adrenal epinephrine raise heart rate and blood pressure and narrow blood vessels."], ["Gut", "Ganglion stimulation causes nausea, vomiting and increased bowel activity, especially with first exposure or poisoning."], ["Glands", "Increases salivation and sweating through ganglionic stimulation."], ["Muscles", "In poisoning, stimulation then blockade at the neuromuscular junction causes twitching, then weakness and breathing failure."], ["Children", "Small children who swallow e-liquid or gum can be quickly poisoned because the dose is large for their body size."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled nicotine reaches the brain within seconds, which makes smoking and vaping highly addictive. Gum, lozenges and nasal spray are absorbed through the lining of the mouth or nose over minutes, and patches deliver it steadily through the skin over hours. Swallowed nicotine is largely broken down by the liver on first pass. The liver enzyme CYP2A6 converts most nicotine to cotinine, which is often measured to check exposure. Nicotine's half-life is about two hours, while cotinine's is about 16 to 20 hours. Metabolites leave in the urine.",
  "sidefx": "Nicotine's side effects follow from stimulating nicotinic receptors throughout the body. Ganglionic and adrenal stimulation cause nausea, a fast heart rate and high blood pressure. Local irritation explains mouth soreness with gum and skin redness under patches. In poisoning, there is first stimulation (vomiting, salivation, tremor, seizures, fast heart rate) and then depolarizing blockade (bradycardia, low blood pressure, muscle weakness and respiratory failure). Most of tobacco's long-term harms, such as cancer and lung disease, come from other chemicals in smoke rather than nicotine itself. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, with atropine for bradycardia and benzodiazepines for seizures.",
  "fun": "Nicotine is also a natural insecticide made by the tobacco plant to protect itself from being eaten.",
  "src": ["Nicorette and NicoDerm CQ FDA labels", "US Surgeon General's Report on Smoking and Health, 1964", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics"]
},
"anabolic-steroid-misuse": {
  "origin": "In 1889 French physiologist Charles-Edouard Brown-Sequard injected himself with extracts from animal testicles and claimed renewed strength, sparking interest in male hormones. In 1935 Ernst Laqueur's group in Amsterdam isolated testosterone from bull testes, and in the same year Adolf Butenandt and Leopold Ruzicka synthesized it; they shared the 1939 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Chemists then made synthetic anabolic-androgenic steroids such as nandrolone and stanozolol to maximize muscle growth. By the 1950s and 1960s athletes were using them, and the International Olympic Committee banned them in the 1970s. Sprinter Ben Johnson's positive test at the 1988 Olympics drew worldwide attention. The US Anabolic Steroid Control Act of 1990 made them Schedule III controlled substances. FDA-approved testosterone products exist for medical use, but non-medical use is illegal and harmful.",
  "timeline": [["1889", "Brown-Sequard reports self-injection of testicular extracts."], ["1935", "Testosterone is isolated and synthesized."], ["1939", "Butenandt and Ruzicka share the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1988", "Ben Johnson tests positive for stanozolol at the Seoul Olympics."], ["1990", "The Anabolic Steroid Control Act places anabolic steroids in Schedule III."]],
  "cell": "Anabolic-androgenic steroids are lipid-soluble and diffuse into cells, where they bind the androgen receptor, a nuclear hormone receptor. The bound receptor moves to the nucleus, binds androgen response elements on DNA and turns on genes that increase muscle protein synthesis and muscle fiber growth. In some tissues, such as skin and prostate, testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to the stronger androgen dihydrotestosterone. Some steroids are converted by aromatase to estrogens, which can cause breast tissue growth. High androgen levels suppress hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH and FSH through negative feedback, shutting down the body's own testosterone and sperm production. Androgens also stimulate the kidney to make erythropoietin, raising red blood cell counts.",
  "body": [["Muscle", "Increases muscle size and strength, the main reason for misuse; tendons do not strengthen as fast, raising injury risk."], ["Heart and vessels", "Raises blood pressure, lowers HDL cholesterol, raises LDL and can thicken the heart muscle, increasing risk of heart attack, stroke and sudden death."], ["Blood", "Raises red blood cell count (erythrocytosis), thickening blood and increasing clot risk."], ["Reproductive system", "In men, suppresses the body's own testosterone, causing testicular shrinkage and infertility; aromatization can cause breast growth. In women, causes deepening voice, facial hair and menstrual changes."], ["Liver", "Oral 17-alpha-alkylated steroids can cause liver injury, cholestasis, and rarely liver tumors or blood-filled cysts (peliosis hepatis)."], ["Brain and mood", "Can cause irritability, aggression and mood swings; depression and suicidal thoughts can occur during withdrawal."], ["Skin and hair", "Acne and male-pattern hair loss come from androgen action on skin and hair follicles."]],
  "journey": "Misused steroids are taken by mouth or injected into muscle. Injectable esters such as testosterone cypionate or enanthate dissolve in oil and are released slowly from the muscle over days to weeks; the ester is then cut off to free the active steroid. Oral forms are chemically modified (17-alpha-alkylated) so the liver does not destroy them on first pass, which also makes them harder on the liver. In blood, testosterone binds sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin. The liver breaks steroids down, and some are aromatized to estrogens. Metabolites are conjugated and excreted in urine, where anti-doping tests detect them.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects follow from androgen receptor activation in many tissues and from shutting down the body's own hormone system. Negative feedback on the pituitary causes testicular shrinkage, low sperm counts and, after stopping, a period of low testosterone with low mood. Aromatization to estrogen causes gynecomastia. Androgens raise red blood cell production and alter cholesterol, which, with higher blood pressure and heart muscle thickening, increases heart attack, stroke and clot risk. Oral alkylated steroids stress the liver. Injecting with shared or non-sterile equipment spreads infections. There is no antidote; care involves stopping use and treating complications, sometimes with medical help to restart natural hormone production.",
  "src": ["Anabolic Steroid Control Act of 1990", "NIDA: Anabolic Steroids DrugFacts", "Goodman & Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1939 materials"]
},
"vitamin-a": {
  "origin": "Night blindness was treated with liver in ancient Egypt, long before anyone knew why it worked. In 1913 Elmer McCollum and Marguerite Davis at the University of Wisconsin found that rats needed a fat-soluble factor from butter and egg yolk to grow, which they called fat-soluble A. Swiss chemist Paul Karrer worked out the structure of retinol and beta-carotene in the early 1930s and shared the 1937 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. George Wald at Harvard showed how vitamin A forms rhodopsin in the eye and shared the 1967 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Today, vitamin A injection (Aquasol A) is an FDA-approved prescription product, while oral supplements are sold as dietary supplements and are not FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Egyptian texts describe liver as a remedy for night blindness."], ["1913", "McCollum and Davis describe fat-soluble A."], ["1931", "Paul Karrer determines the structure of vitamin A."], ["1937", "Karrer shares the Nobel Prize in Chemistry."], ["1967", "George Wald shares the Nobel Prize for the chemistry of vision."]],
  "cell": "Vitamin A works through two main forms. In the retina, retinol is converted to 11-cis-retinal, which binds the protein opsin to form rhodopsin in rod cells. When light hits rhodopsin, 11-cis-retinal flips to all-trans-retinal, changing the protein's shape and triggering a signaling cascade through transducin and phosphodiesterase that closes cGMP-gated sodium channels and sends a signal to the brain. The retinal must be recycled back, so a lack of vitamin A first causes night blindness. In other tissues, retinol is converted to retinoic acid, which binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RAR) paired with retinoid X receptors (RXR). These complexes bind DNA and control genes for epithelial cell growth and differentiation, immune function and embryonic development.",
  "body": [["Eyes", "Needed for rhodopsin in rod cells; deficiency causes night blindness, dry eyes (xerophthalmia) and, if severe, corneal damage and blindness."], ["Skin and mucous membranes", "Keeps epithelial cells healthy and mucus-producing; deficiency causes dry, rough skin and keratinized linings."], ["Immune system", "Supports immune cell function; vitamin A supplementation lowers death rates from measles in deficient children."], ["Embryo", "Retinoic acid guides development of organs, but too much causes serious birth defects."], ["Liver", "The liver stores most of the body's vitamin A; long-term excess causes liver injury and fibrosis."], ["Brain and bones", "Excess raises pressure inside the skull (pseudotumor cerebri) and can cause bone pain and fractures."]],
  "journey": "Vitamin A from food or supplements is absorbed with dietary fat in the small intestine, packed into chylomicrons and carried through the lymph to the liver. The liver stores it as retinyl esters in stellate cells and releases it bound to retinol-binding protein. Stores can last months. Beta-carotene from plants is converted to retinol in the gut. The injection is given into muscle for people who cannot absorb vitamin A. Metabolites leave through bile and urine.",
  "sidefx": "Because vitamin A is stored in the liver, too much builds up over time. Acute excess causes headache, nausea, vomiting and dizziness, partly from raised pressure in the skull. Chronic excess causes dry skin, hair loss, bone pain, liver injury and pseudotumor cerebri. Retinoic acid controls embryonic gene expression, so excess during pregnancy can cause birth defects of the face, heart and brain. IV vitamin A has caused anaphylaxis, which is why the injection is given into muscle. There is no specific antidote; stopping intake and supportive care are used.",
  "fun": "Polar bear liver contains so much vitamin A that Arctic explorers who ate it developed severe vitamin A poisoning.",
  "src": ["Aquasol A (vitamin A palmitate) FDA label", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin A fact sheet", "Nobel Prize materials 1937 and 1967"]
},
"riboflavin": {
  "origin": "Riboflavin was first seen in 1879, when English chemist Alexander Wynter Blyth extracted a yellow-green fluorescent pigment from milk whey, which he called lactochrome. Its role in nutrition became clear in the 1920s and 1930s, when researchers separated the B vitamin complex into parts. Richard Kuhn in Germany and Paul Karrer in Switzerland determined its structure and synthesized it in the mid-1930s. Its name comes from ribose, a sugar in its structure, and flavus, Latin for yellow. In 2016 the FDA approved Photrexa, a riboflavin eye drop used with UVA light to treat progressive keratoconus, a thinning of the cornea. Oral riboflavin supplements are not FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["1879", "Alexander Wynter Blyth isolates a yellow pigment from whey."], ["1930s", "Kuhn and Karrer determine the structure and synthesize riboflavin."], ["1938", "Richard Kuhn is named the 1938 Nobel laureate in Chemistry for carotenoid and vitamin work."], ["2016", "The FDA approves Photrexa for corneal cross-linking."]],
  "cell": "Riboflavin is converted inside cells to two coenzymes. Flavokinase adds a phosphate to make flavin mononucleotide (FMN), and FAD synthetase adds an adenosine to make flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD). These carry electrons in many oxidation-reduction reactions. FMN is part of complex I and FAD is part of complex II (succinate dehydrogenase) of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. FAD is also needed for fatty acid oxidation, the citric acid cycle, glutathione reductase and the activation of vitamin B6 and folate. In corneal cross-linking, riboflavin soaked into the cornea absorbs UVA light and makes reactive oxygen species that form new chemical bonds between collagen fibers, stiffening the cornea.",
  "body": [["Energy metabolism", "FMN and FAD support the electron transport chain and fatty acid breakdown, so all cells depend on them."], ["Skin and mouth", "Deficiency causes cracks at the corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis), a sore red tongue and skin inflammation."], ["Eyes", "Deficiency can cause eye irritation; in keratoconus, Photrexa with UVA light stiffens the cornea and slows its thinning."], ["Blood", "Riboflavin helps activate other B vitamins and supports red blood cell production."], ["Urine", "Excess is excreted unchanged and turns urine bright yellow."]],
  "journey": "Oral riboflavin is absorbed in the small intestine by a carrier that saturates, so absorption is limited at high amounts. Absorption peaks one to two hours after taking it. In cells it is converted to FMN and FAD. The body stores little, and excess is filtered by the kidneys into the urine within hours, with a half-life of about one hour. In the eye, Photrexa is applied to the cornea, and very little reaches the blood.",
  "sidefx": "Oral riboflavin is very safe because absorption is limited and excess leaves in the urine. The most noticeable effect is bright yellow-orange urine, which is harmless. With Photrexa, UVA light and reactive oxygen species can damage the corneal surface, causing pain, haze or slow healing, and rarely infection. There is no antidote, and none is needed for oral use.",
  "src": ["Photrexa (riboflavin 5'-phosphate ophthalmic) FDA label", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Riboflavin fact sheet"]
},
"ascorbic-acid": {
  "origin": "Scurvy killed huge numbers of sailors on long voyages. In 1747 Scottish naval surgeon James Lind ran one of the first controlled clinical trials and found that citrus fruit cured it. In 1907 Axel Holst and Theodor Frolich in Norway created scurvy in guinea pigs, which, like humans, cannot make vitamin C. In 1928 Hungarian scientist Albert Szent-Gyorgyi isolated hexuronic acid, and in 1932 he and Joseph Svirbely, and separately Charles Glen King in the US, showed it was vitamin C. In 1933 Norman Haworth determined its structure and Tadeus Reichstein synthesized it. Szent-Gyorgyi received the 1937 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Today, Ascor injection is an FDA-approved prescription product for scurvy, while oral supplements are not FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["1747", "James Lind shows citrus cures scurvy in a controlled trial."], ["1907", "Holst and Frolich produce scurvy in guinea pigs."], ["1932", "Szent-Gyorgyi and King identify vitamin C."], ["1933", "Haworth determines its structure and Reichstein synthesizes it."], ["1937", "Szent-Gyorgyi and Haworth receive Nobel Prizes."]],
  "cell": "Ascorbic acid is a reducing agent that keeps metal ions in enzymes in their active state. Its best-known role is in collagen formation: prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases need ferrous iron, and vitamin C keeps that iron reduced. These enzymes add hydroxyl groups to proline and lysine in procollagen, which lets the chains form a stable triple helix. Without vitamin C, collagen is weak, so blood vessels, gums, skin and bone break down. Vitamin C is also a cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase (dopamine to norepinephrine), for carnitine synthesis and for some hormone-processing enzymes. In the gut, it reduces ferric iron to ferrous iron, increasing absorption of iron from plants. It also acts as a water-soluble antioxidant.",
  "body": [["Connective tissue", "Needed to make strong collagen; deficiency causes scurvy, with weak blood vessels, bleeding gums and poor wound healing."], ["Skin", "Scurvy causes bleeding around hair follicles, corkscrew hairs and easy bruising."], ["Bones and teeth", "Collagen loss causes bone pain, loose teeth and, in children, bleeding under the bone lining."], ["Gut and iron", "Increases absorption of non-heme iron from plant foods."], ["Nervous system", "Supports norepinephrine synthesis; fatigue and mood changes are early signs of deficiency."], ["Kidneys", "Excess is excreted in urine and partly converted to oxalate, which can form kidney stones."]],
  "journey": "Oral vitamin C is absorbed in the small intestine by sodium-dependent transporters (SVCT1), which become saturated at high intake, so absorption falls as the amount rises. It spreads widely and is concentrated in white blood cells, the adrenal glands and the eye. It is oxidized to dehydroascorbic acid, which can be recycled, and partly broken down to oxalate. The kidneys reabsorb it until plasma levels are saturated, and excess is excreted in urine. IV vitamin C bypasses gut limits, giving much higher blood levels, and has a half-life of about two hours at high levels.",
  "sidefx": "Large oral amounts pull water into the gut because unabsorbed vitamin C is osmotic, causing diarrhea and cramps. Excess is partly converted to oxalate, which can form kidney stones or, with high IV doses or kidney disease, crystal damage to the kidneys. In people with G6PD deficiency, high IV doses can trigger hemolysis because their red cells cannot handle the oxidative stress. High levels can also interfere with some glucose meters and lab tests. There is no antidote; stopping and hydration are used.",
  "fun": "Humans, guinea pigs and some primates and bats cannot make their own vitamin C because they lack a working gene for the enzyme L-gulonolactone oxidase.",
  "src": ["Ascor (ascorbic acid injection) FDA label", "Lind J. A Treatise of the Scurvy, 1753", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin C fact sheet"]
},
"vitamin-e": {
  "origin": "In 1922 Herbert Evans and Katharine Scott Bishop at the University of California, Berkeley, found that rats fed a purified diet could become pregnant but lost their fetuses. A factor in lettuce and wheat germ prevented this, and they called it factor X, later named vitamin E. In 1936 Evans and colleagues isolated it from wheat germ oil and named it tocopherol, from the Greek words for 'to bear offspring.' Its structure was determined in 1938, and it was later identified as the main fat-soluble antioxidant in cell membranes. In the US, vitamin E is sold as a dietary supplement, which is not FDA approved. Large trials have not shown that high-dose vitamin E prevents heart disease or cancer, and some suggested harms.",
  "timeline": [["1922", "Evans and Bishop discover a dietary factor needed for rat fertility."], ["1936", "Vitamin E is isolated from wheat germ oil and named tocopherol."], ["1938", "The structure of alpha-tocopherol is determined and it is synthesized."], ["2000s", "Large trials do not show heart or cancer benefits from high-dose vitamin E."]],
  "cell": "Alpha-tocopherol is a fat-soluble molecule that sits in cell membranes and lipoproteins. Its chromanol ring has a hydroxyl group that donates a hydrogen atom to lipid peroxyl radicals, stopping the chain reaction of lipid peroxidation that would otherwise damage membranes. This turns vitamin E into a tocopheroxyl radical, which vitamin C and other antioxidants can recycle back to its active form. The liver's alpha-tocopherol transfer protein selects alpha-tocopherol and loads it into lipoproteins, which is why this form dominates in blood. At high levels, vitamin E inhibits protein kinase C and platelet aggregation and can interfere with vitamin K-dependent clotting, which raises bleeding risk.",
  "body": [["Cell membranes", "Protects membrane fats from oxidation, especially in nerves, muscle and red blood cells."], ["Nerves", "Deficiency, usually from fat malabsorption or genetic defects, causes ataxia and loss of sensation from nerve damage."], ["Red blood cells", "Deficiency makes red cells fragile and can cause hemolytic anemia, especially in preterm infants."], ["Blood clotting", "High intake inhibits platelets and antagonizes vitamin K, increasing bleeding, especially with warfarin."], ["Brain", "A large meta-analysis suggested higher hemorrhagic stroke risk with high-dose supplements."]],
  "journey": "Vitamin E is absorbed with dietary fat in the small intestine, which requires bile and pancreatic enzymes. It is packed into chylomicrons and carried through the lymph to the liver. The liver's alpha-tocopherol transfer protein loads alpha-tocopherol into VLDL for delivery to tissues, while other forms are broken down. It is stored in fat, liver and muscle. Its plasma half-life is about two to three days. CYP4F2 starts its breakdown, and metabolites leave mainly through bile in stool, with some in urine.",
  "sidefx": "At normal intake vitamin E is safe. High doses can cause nausea, diarrhea and fatigue. Because vitamin E inhibits platelets and interferes with vitamin K-dependent clotting factors, high intake increases bleeding risk, especially with warfarin or antiplatelet drugs, and has been linked to hemorrhagic stroke. The SELECT trial found more prostate cancer in men taking high-dose vitamin E. There is no specific antidote; vitamin K can help reverse bleeding effects.",
  "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Vitamin E fact sheet", "Evans HM, Bishop KS. Science 1922", "Klein EA et al. SELECT, JAMA 2011"]
},
"biotin": {
  "origin": "In 1927 British scientist Margaret Averil Boas found that rats fed large amounts of raw egg white developed hair loss, skin inflammation and paralysis, a condition called egg white injury. A factor in liver protected them. In 1936 German chemists Fritz Kogl and Benno Tonnis isolated a growth factor from egg yolk that they named biotin. It was later shown to be the same as the protective factor and as vitamin H. In 1942 Vincent du Vigneaud at Cornell determined its structure. Avidin, a protein in raw egg white, binds biotin so tightly that it blocks absorption, explaining egg white injury. Biotin is sold as a dietary supplement, which is not FDA approved. In 2017 the FDA warned that high-dose biotin can interfere with lab tests, including troponin.",
  "timeline": [["1927", "Margaret Boas describes egg white injury in rats."], ["1936", "Kogl and Tonnis isolate biotin from egg yolk."], ["1942", "Vincent du Vigneaud determines biotin's structure."], ["2017", "The FDA warns that biotin can interfere with lab tests."]],
  "cell": "Biotin is a coenzyme for five carboxylase enzymes in humans. Holocarboxylase synthetase attaches biotin covalently to a lysine residue in each enzyme. The biotin then carries carbon dioxide (as a carboxyl group) to the substrate. Acetyl-CoA carboxylase makes malonyl-CoA, the first step in fatty acid synthesis. Pyruvate carboxylase makes oxaloacetate, needed for gluconeogenesis and the citric acid cycle. Propionyl-CoA carboxylase and methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase break down certain amino acids and odd-chain fats. When the enzymes are broken down, biotinidase frees biotin for reuse. Separately, many lab tests use a biotin and streptavidin binding system, so high blood biotin can block the test and give false results.",
  "body": [["Metabolism", "Supports fatty acid synthesis, glucose production and amino acid breakdown through its carboxylase enzymes."], ["Skin and hair", "Deficiency causes hair loss and a scaly red rash around the eyes, nose and mouth; benefits for hair and nails in healthy people are not proven."], ["Nervous system", "Deficiency can cause depression, lethargy and tingling."], ["Lab tests", "High biotin levels can cause falsely high or falsely low results in thyroid, hormone and troponin tests, which can lead to missed heart attacks."]],
  "journey": "Biotin from food is freed from proteins by biotinidase and absorbed in the small intestine by the sodium-dependent multivitamin transporter. Gut bacteria also make some biotin. It circulates in plasma, mostly unbound, and is taken up by cells. Some is broken down, and the rest is excreted in the urine, with a half-life of about two hours. High-dose supplements can raise blood levels for many hours, enough to affect lab tests.",
  "sidefx": "Biotin itself is very safe because excess is excreted in the urine. The main risk is lab test interference. Many immunoassays use biotin and streptavidin to capture their target, and extra biotin in the blood competes in this system, giving falsely high or low results. A falsely low troponin can cause a heart attack to be missed, and falsely abnormal thyroid tests can lead to wrong diagnoses. There is no antidote; telling the lab and stopping supplements before testing are standard approaches.",
  "fun": "Avidin from raw egg white binds biotin so tightly that the pair is used throughout biotechnology as a molecular glue.",
  "src": ["FDA Safety Communication: Biotin interference with lab tests (2017, updated 2019)", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Biotin fact sheet"]
},
"zinc": {
  "origin": "Zinc has been used in ointments since ancient times, and zinc oxide is still used on skin today. In 1869 French scientist Jules Raulin showed that zinc was needed for the growth of the mold Aspergillus niger, the first evidence it was essential to living things. Its importance in humans was shown in the early 1960s by Ananda Prasad, who studied young men in Iran and Egypt with dwarfism, delayed puberty and anemia and showed that zinc deficiency was the cause. In 1973 acrodermatitis enteropathica, a rare inherited zinc absorption disorder, was found to respond to zinc. Zinc sulfate injection is FDA approved for parenteral nutrition, while oral zinc supplements and lozenges are not FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Zinc compounds are used in ointments for wounds and skin."], ["1869", "Jules Raulin shows zinc is needed for Aspergillus growth."], ["1961", "Ananda Prasad describes human zinc deficiency in Iran."], ["1973", "Acrodermatitis enteropathica is found to respond to zinc."]],
  "cell": "Zinc is a trace element with structural, catalytic and regulatory roles. It is the catalytic metal in more than 300 enzymes, including carbonic anhydrase, alkaline phosphatase, alcohol dehydrogenase and superoxide dismutase, where it activates water or stabilizes reaction intermediates. It also holds the shape of zinc-finger proteins, a large family of transcription factors that bind DNA and control gene expression. Zinc is needed for DNA and RNA polymerases, cell division and immune cell development, especially T cells. Cells control zinc levels with ZIP and ZnT transporters and metallothionein. Large zinc intake makes gut cells produce more metallothionein, which traps copper and blocks its absorption.",
  "body": [["Immune system", "Needed for T-cell development and function; deficiency increases infections, and zinc lozenges may shorten colds slightly."], ["Skin and wounds", "Supports skin cell growth and wound healing; deficiency causes rash around the mouth and limbs and poor healing."], ["Growth and puberty", "Needed for cell division and growth; deficiency causes short stature and delayed puberty."], ["Taste and smell", "Deficiency reduces taste and smell; intranasal zinc products have been linked to loss of smell."], ["Gut", "Zinc helps treat diarrhea in children in low-income countries; supplements can cause nausea."], ["Copper balance", "Long-term high zinc intake causes copper deficiency, leading to anemia, low white cells and nerve damage."]],
  "journey": "Oral zinc is absorbed in the small intestine through ZIP4 transporters, with about 20 to 40 percent absorbed from food depending on diet. Phytates in grains and legumes bind zinc and reduce absorption. In blood it is mostly bound to albumin. It is found in all tissues, with the most in muscle and bone. The body controls zinc by adjusting gut absorption and excretion into stool, which is the main route out, with a small amount in urine. IV zinc in parenteral nutrition bypasses the gut.",
  "sidefx": "Zinc salts irritate the stomach, causing nausea and a metallic taste. Long-term high intake induces metallothionein in gut cells, which binds copper and keeps it from being absorbed, causing copper deficiency with anemia, low white blood cell counts and a spinal cord disorder (myelopathy) that can resemble vitamin B12 deficiency. Excess zinc in parenteral nutrition can cause vessel precipitates if not properly mixed. There is no specific antidote; stopping zinc and replacing copper are the main treatments.",
  "fun": "Zinc deficiency causing dwarfism was discovered partly because diets in some regions were high in unleavened bread, whose phytates block zinc absorption.",
  "src": ["Zinc sulfate injection FDA label", "Prasad AS et al. Am J Med 1961", "NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Zinc fact sheet"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"electrolyte-oral-rehydration": {
  "origin": "Oral rehydration solution grew out of a physiology discovery. In the early 1960s Robert Crane and colleagues described sodium-glucose cotransport in the small intestine: glucose and sodium cross the gut wall together. Researchers then found this pathway keeps working even during cholera, when the gut is pouring out fluid. In the 1960s teams at the Cholera Research Laboratory in Dhaka and in Calcutta showed that a simple drink of glucose and salts could replace much of the IV fluid patients needed. During the 1971 Bangladesh war, Dilip Mahalanabis used ORS in refugee camps when IV fluid ran out, and deaths fell sharply. In 1978 The Lancet called it potentially the most important medical advance of the century. WHO and UNICEF promoted a standard formula, and in 2002 they switched to a lower osmolarity version that causes less stool output. In the US, ORS products are sold as medical foods or supplements, not FDA approved drugs.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Robert Crane and colleagues describe sodium-glucose cotransport in the intestine."], ["1960s", "Trials in Dhaka and Calcutta show oral glucose-salt solution can treat cholera."], ["1971", "Dilip Mahalanabis uses ORS in Bangladesh war refugee camps with low death rates."], ["1978", "The Lancet calls ORS potentially the most important medical advance of the century."], ["2002", "WHO and UNICEF recommend a reduced osmolarity ORS formula."]],
  "cell": "The key protein is SGLT1, the sodium-glucose cotransporter on the brush border of small intestine cells. SGLT1 moves two sodium ions and one glucose molecule into the cell together, using the sodium gradient set up by the Na-K ATPase on the basolateral side. Glucose then leaves the cell through GLUT2 into the blood, and sodium is pumped out by the Na-K ATPase. Water follows the absorbed solute by osmosis, both through the cells and between them. Cholera toxin and many diarrheal toxins raise cAMP and open CFTR chloride channels to drive secretion, but they leave SGLT1 intact. That is why a drink with the right ratio of sodium to glucose still gets absorbed when plain water or salty water alone would not. Potassium and a base (citrate) replace losses and help correct acidosis.",
  "body": [["Small intestine", "Glucose-coupled sodium uptake pulls water into the body even while toxins drive secretion into the gut, so net fluid balance improves."], ["Blood volume and circulation", "Restored plasma volume raises blood pressure and improves perfusion, which reverses the fast heart rate and poor capillary refill of dehydration."], ["Kidneys", "Better kidney blood flow restores urine output and lets the kidneys correct acid-base and electrolyte problems."], ["Acid-base balance", "Citrate is converted to bicarbonate in the liver, which helps correct the metabolic acidosis caused by stool bicarbonate loss."], ["Potassium balance", "Added potassium replaces what is lost in diarrheal stool, lowering the risk of weakness and rhythm problems from hypokalemia."]],
  "journey": "ORS is swallowed or given by nasogastric tube. Its sodium and glucose are absorbed in the small intestine within minutes to hours, mainly by SGLT1. The absorbed water expands the blood volume and spreads into the tissues. Glucose is used for energy like any dietary sugar. Excess sodium, potassium and water are handled by the kidneys, which adjust urine output as hydration returns. There is no drug half-life in the usual sense; the effect depends on matching intake to ongoing losses.",
  "sidefx": "Vomiting can happen if large amounts are taken quickly, because a stretched, irritated stomach triggers the vomiting reflex; that is why small frequent sips are used. Homemade mixtures with too much salt cause hypernatremia, which pulls water out of brain cells and can cause seizures; correcting sodium too fast in the other direction can also cause brain swelling. Too much sugar draws water into the gut and worsens diarrhea, which is why sports drinks and juices do not work as ORS. There is no antidote; problems are managed by correcting fluid and sodium carefully.",
  "fun": "ORS is credited with saving tens of millions of lives worldwide since the 1970s, mostly young children with diarrhea.",
  "src": ["WHO Oral Rehydration Salts guidance", "CDC and AAP guidance on gastroenteritis", "Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology"]
},
"lactobacillus-rhamnosus-gg": {
  "origin": "LGG was found by two scientists at Tufts University in Boston, Sherwood Gorbach and Barry Goldin. In the 1980s they searched for a lactobacillus that could survive stomach acid and bile, stick to the gut lining and come from a human source. They isolated a strain from the stool of a healthy adult, and the name GG comes from their initials. The strain was patented and licensed widely, first to Valio in Finland for fermented milk and later sold in the US in Culturelle. It became one of the most studied probiotic strains. In 2020 the genus Lactobacillus was split, and the species is now formally called Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus. It is sold as a dietary supplement and is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Gorbach and Goldin isolate the strain at Tufts University."], ["1990s", "LGG products reach the market in Finland and then other countries."], ["2018", "A large NEJM trial finds no benefit for LGG in children with acute gastroenteritis."], ["2019", "A genomic study links LGG bacteremia in ICU patients to the probiotic capsules they received."], ["2020", "Lactobacillus rhamnosus is renamed Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus."]],
  "cell": "LGG is a live bacterium, not a receptor drug, and its proposed actions come from strain studies. First, it tolerates acid and bile, so many cells reach the intestine alive. Second, it carries SpaCBA pili, hair-like surface proteins that bind intestinal mucus and help it stay near the lining for a while. Third, it secretes two proteins, p40 and p75, that in lab studies activate EGF receptor signaling in epithelial cells, reduce cell death and support tight junctions. Fourth, it ferments sugars to lactic acid and competes with pathogens for space and nutrients. Fifth, it interacts with dendritic cells and other mucosal immune cells through pattern recognition receptors, which may shift cytokine signals. The evidence is strongest for antibiotic-associated diarrhea in children and weak or negative for many other uses.",
  "body": [["Intestinal lining", "Pili-mediated adhesion and the p40 and p75 proteins are proposed to support the epithelial barrier and help it recover from injury."], ["Gut microbiota", "While taken, LGG adds to the bacterial population and may limit overgrowth of harmful bacteria during antibiotic courses."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Contact with gut immune cells may shift cytokine signals, the basis for studies in allergy prevention and daycare infections, with mixed results."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "In critically ill, central-line or severely immunosuppressed patients, the organism can cross into the blood and cause bacteremia or endocarditis."]],
  "journey": "LGG is swallowed as capsules, powders or in dairy foods. Many cells survive stomach acid and bile and reach the small and large intestine alive. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Instead it lives in the gut for a short time, attaching to mucus and multiplying. It shows up in stool within days of starting. After the product is stopped, it usually disappears from stool within about one to two weeks, showing that colonization is temporary. Antibiotics can kill it, so doses are commonly separated by a few hours.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation of sugars in the gut, which produces gas and short-chain fatty acids. The serious risk is invasive infection. In people with a damaged gut barrier, central lines or weak immune systems, live bacteria can enter the blood, and a 2019 study matched LGG in ICU patients' blood to their probiotic capsules. FDA warned in 2023 about invasive infection in preterm infants given probiotics. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics the strain is sensitive to.",
  "fun": "The letters GG honor the two discoverers, Gorbach and Goldin.",
  "src": ["Culturelle product information", "Freedman SB et al. NEJM 2018", "Yelin I et al. Nature Medicine 2019", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"saccharomyces-boulardii": {
  "origin": "The yeast was found by French microbiologist Henri Boulard in the 1920s while he was in Indochina (now Vietnam and its neighbors) during a cholera outbreak. He noticed that some local people chewed the skins of lychee and mangosteen fruit to control diarrhea, and he isolated a yeast from the fruit. The French company Biocodex developed it into a medicine, launched in France as Ultra-Levure in 1962. It is sold in the US as Florastor. Genetic studies later showed it is a variety of baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, rather than its own species. In the US it is a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1920s", "Henri Boulard isolates the yeast from tropical fruit in Indochina."], ["1962", "Biocodex launches it in France as Ultra-Levure."], ["2000s", "Genome studies show it is a variety of Saccharomyces cerevisiae."], ["2020", "AGA gives a conditional, low certainty suggestion for C. difficile prevention during antibiotics."]],
  "cell": "S. boulardii is a live yeast, so antibacterial antibiotics do not kill it. Proposed mechanisms come from strain studies. It secretes a 54 kDa serine protease that cleaves C. difficile toxins A and B and the brush-border receptor that toxin A binds, which reduces toxin-driven fluid secretion and inflammation in animal models. It makes a phosphatase that dephosphorylates bacterial endotoxin (LPS), lowering its inflammatory activity. It also releases polyamines and boosts brush-border enzymes such as lactase and sucrase, a trophic effect on the lining. It raises secretory IgA and dampens NF-kB driven inflammatory signaling in lab studies. Human evidence is moderate for antibiotic-associated diarrhea and low certainty for C. difficile prevention.",
  "body": [["Colon lining", "Breaking down C. difficile toxins protects colon cells from the damage that causes watery diarrhea and colitis in models."], ["Small intestine", "Higher brush-border enzyme activity may help digestion and recovery of the lining after diarrhea."], ["Gut immune system", "More secretory IgA and less inflammatory signaling may calm the gut response to pathogens."], ["Gut microbiota during antibiotics", "Because it is a yeast, it survives antibiotic courses and keeps working while bacteria are being killed."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "In ICU or central-line patients, yeast can enter the blood and cause Saccharomyces fungemia, even by spread from opened capsules nearby."]],
  "journey": "The yeast is swallowed as capsules or powder. It survives the stomach and passes through the gut alive but does not attach permanently. It is not absorbed into the blood in healthy people and is not metabolized like a drug. Stool levels reach a steady state within about three days of regular intake. After stopping, it is cleared from stool within about two to five days. Antifungal drugs such as fluconazole and nystatin can kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Gas, bloating and sometimes constipation reflect fermentation and shifts in gut contents. The serious risk is fungemia. A leaky gut lining, central lines or weak immunity let yeast cells reach the blood. Opening capsules near patients spreads yeast on hands and in the air, which explains cases in patients who never took it. There is no antidote; fungemia is treated with antifungal drugs and line removal.",
  "fun": "It was the first yeast widely sold as a probiotic medicine, and it is often given alongside antibiotics precisely because antibiotics cannot kill it.",
  "src": ["Florastor product information", "McFarland LV. World J Gastroenterol 2010 review", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics"]
},
"lactobacillus-reuteri-dsm-17938": {
  "origin": "The species is named after German microbiologist Gerhard Reuter, who studied gut lactobacilli in the 1960s; it was formally named Lactobacillus reuteri in 1980. The Swedish company BioGaia developed a strain called ATCC 55730, isolated from the breast milk of a mother in Peru, and sold it for years. Researchers then found that it carried plasmids with genes for tetracycline and lincomycin resistance. To remove that risk, scientists cured the strain of those two plasmids and created the daughter strain DSM 17938, described in 2008. It replaced the parent strain in BioGaia Protectis drops. It is now formally Limosilactobacillus reuteri and is sold as a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1980", "Lactobacillus reuteri is formally named, honoring Gerhard Reuter."], ["1980s", "Researchers describe reuterin, the antimicrobial made by L. reuteri."], ["2008", "DSM 17938 is described after removal of two resistance plasmids from ATCC 55730."], ["2018", "An individual participant data meta-analysis finds less crying in breastfed colicky infants."], ["2020", "The species moves to the new genus Limosilactobacillus."]],
  "cell": "DSM 17938 is a live bacterium, not a receptor drug. Its best known product is reuterin, a mix of compounds centered on 3-hydroxypropionaldehyde, made when the bacterium ferments glycerol. Reuterin is a broad antimicrobial that is thought to work by reacting with thiol groups and causing oxidative stress in other microbes. In animal models the strain reduced the firing of gut sensory nerves after distension and changed intestinal motility, which may explain fewer crying episodes in colicky infants. It also interacts with mucosal immune cells and may shift cytokine signals toward a less inflammatory pattern. These mechanisms are proposed from lab and animal work, and human benefit is limited mainly to breastfed infants with colic.",
  "body": [["Infant gut nerves", "Lower visceral pain signaling in animal models is proposed to reduce colic-related crying, seen in breastfed but not formula-fed infants."], ["Gut motility", "Animal data show changes in intestinal contractions, the basis for studies in constipation and functional abdominal pain with mixed results."], ["Gut microbiota", "Reuterin production may inhibit competing microbes while the strain is present."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Immune cell contact may shift cytokine balance, studied in acute diarrhea and preterm infants."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive infection is rare but possible in preterm, critically ill or immunocompromised infants and patients."]],
  "journey": "The drops are an oil suspension placed on a spoon or nipple, and chewable tablets are also sold. The bacteria pass through the stomach and live in the gut for a short time. They are not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. The strain is detectable in stool within days and usually disappears within weeks after stopping, so colonization is temporary. Antibiotics reduce its survival.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and spit-up in trials occurred about as often as with placebo. The main serious concern is that any live probiotic can cross a weak gut barrier and cause bacteremia in fragile patients. FDA warned in 2023 that probiotics given to preterm infants have caused invasive infection, including a death. Removing the resistance plasmids from the parent strain was meant to avoid spreading antibiotic resistance genes to other gut bacteria. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "DSM 17938 was made by taking resistance genes out of a natural strain, an early example of cleaning up a probiotic for safety without genetic engineering of new traits.",
  "src": ["BioGaia Protectis product information", "Rosander A et al. Appl Environ Microbiol 2008", "Sung V et al. Pediatrics 2018", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"lactobacillus-reuteri-atcc-pta-5289": {
  "origin": "ATCC PTA 5289 is an L. reuteri strain from the human mouth that the Swedish company BioGaia selected for oral health in the 2000s. It is combined with DSM 17938 in Prodentis lozenges and gum, and GUM PerioBalance is a licensed version. Earlier gum studies used the parent strain ATCC 55730. Almost all trial evidence is for the two-strain product, so effects cannot be pinned on this strain alone. The details of exactly who isolated the strain and when are not widely published. It is sold as a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and since 2020 the species has been called Limosilactobacillus reuteri.",
  "timeline": [["1980", "Lactobacillus reuteri is formally named, honoring Gerhard Reuter."], ["2000s", "BioGaia launches Prodentis combining ATCC PTA 5289 with DSM 17938 for oral health."], ["2020", "The species moves to the new genus Limosilactobacillus."]],
  "cell": "Proposed mechanisms come from small studies. In the mouth, the lozenge releases live bacteria that contact the plaque biofilm on teeth and gums. Like other L. reuteri strains, it can produce reuterin (3-hydroxypropionaldehyde) from glycerol, a broad antimicrobial that stresses other microbes. It competes with periodontal pathogens such as Porphyromonas gingivalis, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans and Prevotella intermedia for space in the biofilm. Some trials measured lower inflammatory cytokines (such as IL-1 beta and TNF alpha) in gingival crevicular fluid, which may explain less gum bleeding. These effects are local, short-lived and supported mainly by small trials with conflicting results.",
  "body": [["Gums", "Less inflammation and bleeding were seen in some small trials, proposed to come from lower inflammatory cytokines in gum fluid."], ["Dental plaque", "Competition with pathogens and reuterin may shift the plaque community, with lower plaque scores in some studies."], ["Periodontal pockets", "As an add-on to scaling and root planing, some trials showed small extra gains in pocket depth, while others did not."], ["Gut", "Swallowed bacteria pass into the gut and are cleared in stool without lasting effect."]],
  "journey": "Lozenges are dissolved slowly in the mouth, which keeps the bacteria in contact with teeth and gums. The bacteria are detectable in saliva while the product is used. They are not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. Most are swallowed and pass through the gut into stool. Levels in saliva fall within weeks after stopping. Antiseptic rinses such as chlorhexidine can kill them.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects are uncommon. Rare allergy reactions come from product ingredients. Like any live bacteria, oral lactobacilli can in theory enter the blood through inflamed gums in severely immunosuppressed or critically ill patients, but this is rare. There is no antidote; infections would be treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "The lozenge form is the point of the product: slow dissolving keeps live bacteria in the mouth long enough to reach the gum line.",
  "src": ["BioGaia Prodentis product information", "Twetman S et al. Acta Odontol Scand 2009", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"bifidobacterium-lactis-bb-12": {
  "origin": "BB-12 comes from the culture collection of Chr. Hansen, a Danish company that has supplied dairy cultures since the 1870s. The strain has been in the collection since the early 1980s and in yogurts and fermented milks since the mid 1980s, making it one of the oldest commercial probiotic bifidobacteria. It was chosen because it tolerates acid, bile and oxygen better than many bifidobacteria, which helps it survive in food and in the gut. Its species was originally Bifidobacterium lactis and was made a subspecies of Bifidobacterium animalis in 2004. Chr. Hansen merged into Novonesis in 2024. It is a food and supplement ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "BB-12 enters the Chr. Hansen collection and dairy products."], ["2004", "B. lactis is reclassified as Bifidobacterium animalis subsp. lactis."], ["2013", "The ProPrems trial of a three-strain mix including BB-12 reports less NEC in preterm infants."], ["2015", "A large trial reports more frequent stools in adults with low stool frequency."]],
  "cell": "BB-12 is a live bacterium with proposed, strain-level mechanisms. It tolerates gastric acid and bile, so many cells reach the colon alive. It adheres to intestinal mucus, which lengthens its stay near the lining. It ferments carbohydrates through the bifid shunt, a pathway using fructose-6-phosphate phosphoketolase that yields acetate and lactate. These acids lower colonic pH and feed other bacteria that make butyrate. The lower pH and competition for nutrients and binding sites limit pathogens. Short-chain fatty acids may soften stool and speed transit. It also interacts with mucosal immune cells, which may shift cytokine signals. Clinical evidence is strongest for a three-strain combination in preterm infants and for stool frequency in adults.",
  "body": [["Colon", "Acetate and lactate lower the pH and support butyrate producers, which may soften stools and increase their frequency."], ["Gut microbiota", "While taken, it raises fecal bifidobacteria and competes with potential pathogens."], ["Preterm infant gut", "As part of a three-strain mix, it lowered NEC in the ProPrems trial, though it did not reduce sepsis or death."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Immune cell contact may shift cytokine balance, studied in respiratory infections with conflicting results."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive Bifidobacterium infection is rare but reported mainly in preterm and other high-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "BB-12 is swallowed in yogurt, fermented milk, capsules, powders or drops. Many cells survive the stomach and small intestine and reach the colon. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It is detectable in stool within days and usually disappears within weeks after stopping, so its presence is temporary. Heat, moisture and time reduce the number of live cells in a product, and antibiotics reduce its survival in the gut.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation in the colon. Dairy forms contain milk protein and lactose, which matter for milk allergy and lactose intolerance. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when a weak gut barrier lets live bacteria into the blood, mainly in preterm infants and immunocompromised patients; FDA warned in 2023 after a preterm infant died from a Bifidobacterium probiotic. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Chr. Hansen began as a supplier of rennet for cheesemaking in the 1870s, long before anyone sold bacteria as health products.",
  "src": ["Jungersen M et al. Microorganisms 2014 (BB-12 review)", "Jacobs SE et al. Pediatrics 2013 (ProPrems)", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"bifidobacterium-lactis-hn019": {
  "origin": "HN019 was developed in New Zealand in the 1990s from dairy research linked to the New Zealand Dairy Research Institute, later part of Fonterra. It was selected along with L. rhamnosus HN001 for acid and bile tolerance and immune effects in lab tests. The strain is also known as DR10 and is supplied under the HOWARU Bifido name, which passed from Danisco to DuPont and then to IFF. It was later studied for gut transit, immune markers in older adults and infant eczema. In a 2008 New Zealand infant trial, HN019 did not prevent eczema, while HN001 did. It is a supplement and food ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "HN019 is selected from New Zealand dairy research."], ["2008", "A New Zealand infant trial finds no eczema prevention with HN019."], ["2011", "A trial reports faster whole gut transit time in adults."], ["2018", "A larger constipation trial finds benefit only in people with three or fewer bowel movements a week."]],
  "cell": "HN019 is a live bacterium whose mechanisms are proposed from strain studies. It ferments carbohydrates through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate. These short-chain fatty acids lower colonic pH, provide fuel to colon cells and may stimulate colonic motility through effects on enteric nerves and serotonin-producing enterochromaffin cells, which could explain faster transit. In older adults, small studies found higher natural killer cell activity and phagocytosis, suggesting innate immune signaling through pattern recognition receptors on gut immune cells. None of these are receptor drug actions, and the clinical effects are modest and inconsistent.",
  "body": [["Colon motility", "Short-chain fatty acids may speed colonic transit, seen as faster whole gut transit in a 2011 trial."], ["Bowel habits", "A 2018 trial found more bowel movements only in people starting with three or fewer per week."], ["Innate immune system", "Small studies in older adults found higher NK cell activity and phagocytosis while taking it."], ["Skin (infant eczema)", "No eczema prevention was seen with HN019 in the 2008 New Zealand trial."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive infection is rare but reported with bifidobacteria in preterm and other high-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "HN019 is swallowed in capsules, powders or fermented milks, often mixed with other strains or fiber. Many cells survive to the colon. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It appears in stool within days and usually disappears within weeks after stopping. Heat and time reduce live counts in products, and antibiotics reduce its survival.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation in the colon. The rare serious risk with any live bifidobacterium is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system, especially preterm infants, as highlighted by the FDA 2023 warning. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "HN019 and its sister strain HN001 were tested side by side in the same infant trial, and only one of them reduced eczema, a clear example that probiotic effects are strain specific.",
  "src": ["Wickens K et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2008", "Waller PA et al. Scand J Gastroenterol 2011", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"bifidobacterium-longum-35624": {
  "origin": "Strain 35624 was isolated at University College Cork in Ireland, from tissue of the healthy human intestine, by researchers linked to what became the Alimentary Pharmabiotic Centre. It was first named Bifidobacterium infantis 35624, and genome sequencing later showed it belongs to B. longum subsp. longum. After positive irritable bowel syndrome trials in 2005 and 2006, Procter and Gamble launched it in the US as Align in the 2000s. It is sold as a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and current ACG and AGA guidelines do not endorse probiotics for IBS.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "The strain is isolated at University College Cork."], ["2005", "A trial of 75 adults with IBS reports symptom benefit."], ["2006", "A trial of 362 women with IBS shows benefit at the middle of three doses."], ["2000s", "Procter and Gamble markets the strain as Align."], ["2010s", "Genome sequencing reclassifies it as B. longum."]],
  "cell": "Strain 35624 is thought to act mainly as an immune signal rather than by settling in the gut. In an early IBS trial, people taking it had a more normal ratio of IL-10 (an anti-inflammatory cytokine) to IL-12 (a pro-inflammatory cytokine) in blood immune cells. In mouse and cell models it induces regulatory T cells (Foxp3 positive) through dendritic cell signaling, including Toll-like receptor 2 and retinoic acid pathways, which tones down inflammatory responses. Small trials in ulcerative colitis, chronic fatigue syndrome and psoriasis showed lower CRP and some cytokines over six to eight weeks. The link between these immune changes and IBS pain and bloating is proposed, not proven.",
  "body": [["Gut symptoms (IBS)", "Two early trials reported less abdominal pain and bloating, though the larger trial showed benefit only at one of three doses."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Regulatory T cell induction and higher IL-10 are proposed to calm low-grade gut inflammation."], ["Systemic inflammation", "Small trials found lower CRP and inflammatory cytokines in blood while taking it."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Invasive Bifidobacterium infection is rare but possible in high-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "Align capsules are swallowed and the bacteria pass through the stomach to the intestine. The strain is recovered from stool during use but does not permanently colonize, and stool levels return to baseline after stopping. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Heat and time reduce live counts, and antibiotics can kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation. The rare serious risk with live bifidobacteria is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system. New IBS-like symptoms after age 50, weight loss or bleeding point to causes a probiotic does not address. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "The strain was renamed after its genome was read, so older papers call it B. infantis 35624 while newer ones call it B. longum 35624.",
  "src": ["O'Mahony L et al. Gastroenterology 2005", "Whorwell PJ et al. Am J Gastroenterol 2006", "ACG 2021 IBS Guideline", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics"]
},
"bifidobacterium-infantis-evc001": {
  "origin": "EVC001 grew out of research on breast milk at the University of California, Davis. Scientists there studied human milk oligosaccharides (HMOs), complex sugars in breast milk that babies cannot digest. They found that B. longum subsp. infantis carries a full set of genes to import and break down HMOs, so breast milk acts as its food. The company Evolve BioSystems, later renamed Infinant Health, was founded in the 2010s to develop a B. infantis strain, EVC001, as Evivo. A 2017 trial (Frese and colleagues) found it colonized breastfed infants and persisted after a short course. In 2023 FDA reported a preterm infant death from B. longum sepsis matched to Evivo with MCT Oil. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "UC Davis researchers show B. infantis can digest human milk oligosaccharides."], ["2010s", "Evolve BioSystems develops EVC001 as Evivo."], ["2017", "A trial shows lasting colonization in breastfed term infants."], ["2023", "FDA reports a preterm infant death from sepsis linked to Evivo with MCT Oil."]],
  "cell": "EVC001 has the HMO utilization gene cluster, including ABC transporters that import intact milk oligosaccharides and enzymes such as fucosidases, sialidases and beta-galactosidases that take them apart inside the cell. It ferments the sugars through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate. These acids lower stool pH, which disfavors Proteobacteria such as Enterobacteriaceae. In study infants, colonization was linked to lower fecal HMOs, lower stool pH, fewer Gram-negative bacteria, lower fecal endotoxin and lower markers of gut inflammation. Because it depends on HMOs, it is studied in breastfed infants. Clinical outcome data such as fewer infections are very limited.",
  "body": [["Infant colon", "HMO fermentation to acetate and lactate lowers stool pH and makes the gut less friendly to Proteobacteria."], ["Gut microbiota", "The strain can become dominant in breastfed infants and persisted more than 30 days after a three-week course in one study."], ["Gut inflammation", "Lower fecal endotoxin and inflammatory markers were seen in mechanistic studies."], ["Bloodstream (preterm infants)", "In fragile preterm infants, the organism can enter the blood and cause sepsis, including a reported death."]],
  "journey": "The powder is mixed with a small amount of breast milk and fed to the infant. The bacteria pass to the colon, where they feed on HMOs and multiply. Colonization occurs within days in breastfed infants. Nothing is absorbed into the blood as a drug, and it is not metabolized; the bacteria and their products leave in stool. Unlike most probiotics, it can persist for weeks after stopping when breast milk keeps feeding it. Formula provides little HMO, so it does not thrive.",
  "sidefx": "Gas or fussiness is uncommon. The serious risk is that an immature gut barrier in preterm or medically fragile infants lets live bacteria into the blood, causing bacteremia and sepsis. FDA states no probiotic is approved to prevent disease in preterm infants. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Human milk contains large amounts of sugars that the baby cannot digest, which researchers think exist mainly to feed the right gut bacteria.",
  "src": ["Frese SA et al. mSphere 2017", "Sela DA et al. PNAS 2008", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"lactobacillus-acidophilus-ncfm": {
  "origin": "NCFM stands for North Carolina Food Microbiology. The strain was isolated from a human source at North Carolina State University in the 1970s by researchers including Stanley Gilliland and Marvin Speck. It was used in Sweet Acidophilus milk, an unfermented milk with added bacteria, and later became an ingredient strain supplied by Rhodia, Danisco, DuPont and now IFF. In 2005 its full genome was sequenced, one of the first for a probiotic strain. The species name Lactobacillus acidophilus stayed the same in the 2020 reclassification. It is a supplement ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1970s", "NCFM is isolated at North Carolina State University."], ["1970s", "The strain is used in Sweet Acidophilus milk."], ["2005", "The NCFM genome is published."], ["2009", "A trial in children reports fewer cold symptoms with NCFM alone or with B. lactis."]],
  "cell": "NCFM is a live lactic acid bacterium, not a receptor drug. It ferments sugars to lactic acid, which lowers local pH and inhibits some competitors. It binds intestinal epithelial cells and mucus, partly through surface layer proteins (SlpA). In lab studies SlpA binds DC-SIGN on dendritic cells, which shifts cytokine output and T cell responses. It also has bile salt hydrolase activity, which helps it survive bile. Proposed clinical effects on IBS pain, bloating and colds come from these local immune and microbial interactions. A 12-week trial of 391 adults with IBS found no overall difference from placebo.",
  "body": [["Gut lumen", "Lactic acid production and competition for nutrients may limit some harmful microbes while it is present."], ["Gut immune system", "Surface layer protein binding to dendritic cells may shift cytokine responses, studied for colds in children."], ["Bowel symptoms", "Studied for IBS and bloating with small or no overall benefit."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia and endocarditis are rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "NCFM is swallowed in capsules, tablets, powders or dairy foods. Many cells survive acid and bile and reach the intestine. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It is present in the gut within days and usually cleared from stool within days to weeks after stopping. Antibiotics can kill it, and viability depends on storage.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from sugar fermentation by the bacteria. Dairy forms contain milk protein and lactose. The rare serious risk is bacteremia or endocarditis when live bacteria cross a damaged gut barrier or reach damaged heart valves in high-risk patients. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Sweet Acidophilus milk let people get acidophilus bacteria without the sour taste of fermented milk, because the bacteria were added cold and not allowed to ferment.",
  "src": ["Sanders ME, Klaenhammer TR. J Dairy Sci 2001 (NCFM review)", "Altermann E et al. PNAS 2005", "Leyer GJ et al. Pediatrics 2009"]
},
"lactobacillus-casei-shirota": {
  "origin": "Minoru Shirota, a medical microbiologist at Kyoto Imperial University in Japan, wanted to prevent infections that were common in Japan at the time. In 1930 he selected and cultured a lactobacillus strain that could survive stomach acid and bile and reach the intestine alive. In 1935 he began selling it in a fermented milk drink called Yakult, which became a global brand. The strain was long called Lactobacillus casei Shirota and is now classified as Lacticaseibacillus paracasei. Yakult is a food, and the strain is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1930", "Minoru Shirota cultures the strain in Kyoto."], ["1935", "Yakult fermented milk drink goes on sale in Japan."], ["2003", "A trial of 70 adults reports improvement in constipation."], ["2020", "The strain is reclassified as Lacticaseibacillus paracasei Shirota."]],
  "cell": "The Shirota strain is acid- and bile-tolerant, so it reaches the colon alive in large numbers. There it ferments sugars to lactic acid, lowering pH and competing with other microbes. Lactic acid and other products may soften stool and support transit. The strain interacts with gut immune cells; its cell wall components are sensed by pattern recognition receptors on macrophages and dendritic cells, which can raise IL-12 and natural killer cell activity in lab and small human studies. These effects are proposed and modest, and pooled IBS trials showed no benefit.",
  "body": [["Colon", "Lactic acid and fermentation products may soften stools, supported by small constipation trials."], ["Gut microbiota", "While taken, it adds large numbers of lactobacilli and may lower some harmful bacteria."], ["Immune system", "Small trials reported changes in NK cell activity, with mixed results for colds."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "The bacteria are swallowed in the refrigerated milk drink. Many survive the stomach and small intestine and are recovered alive in stool within days. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. After stopping, the strain is cleared from stool within days to weeks. Antibiotics can kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation. The drink contains milk protein and added sugar, which matter for milk allergy and diabetes. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Yakult's name comes from 'jahurto', a word for yogurt in Esperanto.",
  "src": ["Yakult Honsha company history", "Koebnick C et al. Can J Gastroenterol 2003", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"lactobacillus-paracasei-cncm-i-1518": {
  "origin": "This strain was developed by the French food company Danone and deposited at the Pasteur Institute's national collection (CNCM) under the number I-1518. It was long known as Lactobacillus casei DN-114 001, or L. casei Defensis. Danone launched it in the fermented milk drink Actimel in Europe in the 1990s, and later sold it in the US as DanActive. The drink also contains the two standard yogurt cultures. Its health claims drew regulatory scrutiny; EFSA did not accept a proposed claim on antibiotic-associated diarrhea in 2010. It is a food, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Danone launches Actimel with the strain in Europe."], ["2007", "A small BMJ trial reports less antibiotic-associated diarrhea in hospital patients over 50."], ["2010", "Trials in daycare children and adults over 70 report effects on common infections."], ["2010", "EFSA rejects a proposed EU health claim on antibiotic-associated diarrhea."]],
  "cell": "CNCM I-1518 is a live lactic acid bacterium, not a receptor drug. It survives transit and ferments sugars to lactic acid in the gut, lowering pH and competing with other microbes. In lab studies it interacts with intestinal epithelial cells and dendritic cells, dampening NF-kB driven inflammatory signaling in response to pathogens and influencing cytokine release. It is proposed to strengthen innate defenses against common infections. The drink also contains Streptococcus thermophilus and Lactobacillus bulgaricus, which add lactase activity. Clinical evidence is limited and mixed.",
  "body": [["Gut immune system", "Contact with epithelial and dendritic cells is proposed to tune inflammatory signaling, the basis for infection trials."], ["Respiratory and GI infections", "Trials in daycare children and older adults reported some effects on common infections, with modest evidence."], ["Gut during antibiotics", "A small hospital trial reported less antibiotic-associated diarrhea, but the claim was not accepted by EFSA."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "The bacteria are swallowed in a fermented milk drink. Many survive to the intestine and are recovered alive in stool within days. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. After stopping, the strain is cleared from stool within days to weeks. Antibiotics can kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation. The drink contains milk protein and added sugar. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in high-risk patients. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "CNCM is the culture collection of the Pasteur Institute in Paris, where many commercial strains are deposited so that they can be patented and identified.",
  "src": ["Hickson M et al. BMJ 2007", "Merenstein D et al. Eur J Clin Nutr 2010", "EFSA NDA Panel opinion 2010"]
}
,
"lactobacillus-plantarum-299v": {
  "origin": "Lp299v was isolated from healthy human intestinal mucosa by researchers at Lund University in Sweden, including microbiologist Goran Molin and surgeon Bengt Jeppsson, in the early 1990s. They chose it for its ability to stick to the gut lining. The Swedish company Probi was founded in 1991 to commercialize Lund strains, and Lp299v first reached consumers in ProViva fruit drinks in Sweden in 1994. It is now sold in supplements such as Jarrow Ideal Bowel Support. Since 2020 its formal name is Lactiplantibacillus plantarum. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1991", "Probi is founded in Sweden to develop Lund University strains."], ["1994", "Lp299v is launched in ProViva fruit drinks in Sweden."], ["2010s", "Isotope studies report higher nonheme iron absorption with Lp299v."], ["2020", "The species is renamed Lactiplantibacillus plantarum."]],
  "cell": "Lp299v carries a mannose-specific adhesin that binds mannose-containing receptors on intestinal epithelial cells, letting it attach to the mucosa and compete with pathogens such as some E. coli that use the same receptors. It ferments sugars to lactic acid, lowering pH. In lab studies it increased expression of the mucins MUC2 and MUC3, thickening the protective mucus layer. Lower pH and reducing compounds in the gut may help convert ferric iron (Fe3+) to ferrous iron (Fe2+), the form taken up by DMT1 on enterocytes, which explains higher nonheme iron absorption in isotope studies. Effects on IBS symptoms are proposed to come from local barrier and microbial changes; ACG recommends against probiotics for global IBS symptoms.",
  "body": [["Intestinal lining", "Mannose-specific adhesion and higher mucin production are proposed to strengthen the mucus barrier."], ["Iron absorption", "Isotope studies found more nonheme iron absorbed from meals, likely by favoring the ferrous form."], ["Bowel symptoms", "Studied for IBS pain and bloating with mixed trial results."], ["Gut microbiota", "Temporary colonization and lactic acid may crowd out some competing microbes."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "Lp299v is swallowed in capsules or fruit drinks. It survives the stomach and attaches temporarily to the intestinal lining. It is recovered in stool within days. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug, and it is cleared from stool within days to weeks after stopping. Antibiotics can kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from bacterial fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in high-risk patients, and FDA warned in 2023 about probiotics in preterm infants. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Lactiplantibacillus plantarum is one of the bacteria in sauerkraut, sourdough and many other plant ferments.",
  "src": ["Probi company information", "Ducrotte P et al. World J Gastroenterol 2012", "ACG 2021 IBS Guideline"]
},
"lactobacillus-rhamnosus-hn001": {
  "origin": "HN001, also called DR20, came from New Zealand dairy research in the 1990s at what became Fonterra. It was selected with its sister strain B. lactis HN019 for survival and immune effects in lab studies, and it is now supplied by IFF. HN001 became known for a 2008 New Zealand trial led by Kristin Wickens and Julian Crane, in which mothers took it in late pregnancy and while breastfeeding and infants took it for two years; eczema rates in the infants fell. It has also been studied for gestational diabetes and postpartum mood. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "HN001 is selected from New Zealand dairy research."], ["2008", "A New Zealand trial reports less infant eczema with HN001."], ["2017", "A trial reports lower gestational diabetes rates in some subgroups with HN001."], ["2020", "The species is renamed Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus."]],
  "cell": "HN001 is a live lactic acid bacterium whose effects are thought to come from training the developing immune system. In early life the gut-associated lymphoid tissue learns tolerance from microbial signals. Bacterial cell wall components such as lipoteichoic acid and peptidoglycan bind Toll-like receptor 2 and NOD2 on dendritic cells, which may promote regulatory T cells and tilt the Th1 and Th2 balance away from allergy. This is proposed, not established. The strain was not found in the breast milk of supplemented mothers, and clinical effects were seen when the infants themselves received it.",
  "body": [["Infant immune system", "Early microbial signals may promote tolerance, proposed to explain less eczema in the 2008 trial."], ["Skin", "Lower eczema rates were reported in infants who took HN001 directly."], ["Metabolism in pregnancy", "One trial reported lower gestational diabetes in some subgroups, a finding that needs confirmation."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised, critically ill or preterm patients."]],
  "journey": "HN001 is swallowed in capsules or powders. It passes through the stomach and lives in the gut for a short time, appearing in stool within days. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and is cleared from stool after stopping. It was not detected in breast milk of mothers taking it. Antibiotics can kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in people with a weak gut barrier or immune system, including preterm infants, as noted in the FDA 2023 warning. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Wickens K et al. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2008", "Wickens KL et al. Br J Nutr 2017", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"lactobacillus-rhamnosus-gr-1-and-reuteri-rc-14": {
  "origin": "These two strains were developed by Gregor Reid and colleagues at the University of Western Ontario (now Western University) in London, Ontario, Canada, with urologist Andrew Bruce, starting in the 1980s. Reid was looking for lactobacilli that could protect women from urinary and vaginal infections. GR-1 was isolated from the urethra of a healthy woman, and RC-14 from a healthy vagina. RC-14 was first named L. fermentum RC-14 and later identified as L. reuteri. The pair is taken by mouth and sold in products such as Jarrow Fem-Dophilus. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Gregor Reid and Andrew Bruce begin work on GR-1 and RC-14 in Ontario."], ["2000s", "Trials report shifts in vaginal flora with oral intake."], ["2012", "The combination does not meet noninferiority to TMP-SMX for UTI prevention."], ["2020", "The species are renamed Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus and Limosilactobacillus reuteri."]],
  "cell": "After oral intake the strains pass through the gut, and some reach the vagina from the rectal and perineal skin. There they are proposed to act locally. They produce lactic acid, which keeps vaginal pH low and is toxic to many BV-associated anaerobes. RC-14 produces hydrogen peroxide and biosurfactants, surface-active proteins that reduce the ability of uropathogens such as E. coli and Enterococcus to stick to epithelial cells. Both compete with pathogens for binding sites (competitive exclusion). These actions come mainly from lab and small human studies.",
  "body": [["Vagina", "More lactobacillus-dominated flora and lower pH were reported in some trials, which disfavors BV-associated bacteria."], ["Urinary tract", "Biosurfactants may reduce uropathogen adhesion, but the combination did not match TMP-SMX for UTI prevention."], ["Gut", "The strains pass through the gut temporarily before reaching the vaginal area."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "The capsules are swallowed. The bacteria pass through the gut and are shed in stool. Over weeks of daily intake, some move from the perineal skin to the vagina, where changes in flora have been measured. They are not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. The effect fades after stopping. Antibiotics can kill them.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from gut fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in high-risk patients. Because the product did not match standard antibiotic prophylaxis for UTI, relying on it in place of proven therapy could leave infections untreated. There is no antidote; infections are treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "The research program began with the idea that the bacteria of healthy women could be used to protect other women from infection.",
  "src": ["Reid G et al. FEMS Immunol Med Microbiol 2003", "Beerepoot MA et al. Arch Intern Med 2012", "Jarrow Fem-Dophilus product information"]
},
"escherichia-coli-nissle-1917": {
  "origin": "In 1917, during World War I, German physician and bacteriologist Alfred Nissle in Freiburg looked for an E. coli that could fight intestinal pathogens. He isolated a strain from the stool of a soldier who stayed healthy during a severe outbreak of shigella dysentery while his comrades fell ill. Nissle showed in culture that the strain inhibited other bacteria, a property he called antagonism. He began selling it the same year as Mutaflor, which is still made by Ardeypharm in Germany. In 2006 researchers found that it carries the pks genomic island and makes colibactin, a toxin that damages DNA in cell and mouse studies. It is licensed as a medicine in Germany but is not an FDA approved drug in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1917", "Alfred Nissle isolates the strain from a healthy WWI soldier and launches Mutaflor."], ["1990s", "Trials compare EcN with mesalamine for keeping ulcerative colitis in remission."], ["2006", "Researchers show EcN carries the pks island that makes colibactin."]],
  "cell": "EcN is a live, nonpathogenic Gram-negative bacterium of serotype O6:K5:H1. It colonizes the colon and outcompetes other Enterobacteriaceae, mainly for iron. It has several siderophore systems (enterobactin, salmochelin, aerobactin and yersiniabactin) that grab scarce iron. It also makes microcins M and H47, small antibacterial peptides that target related bacteria. Its flagellin binds Toll-like receptor 5 on epithelial cells and induces human beta-defensin 2, an antimicrobial peptide. It up-regulates tight junction proteins such as ZO-1 in lab studies, tightening the barrier. In ulcerative colitis trials it was similar to low-dose mesalamine for maintaining remission. It carries the pks island and makes colibactin, a DNA-alkylating genotoxin whose clinical meaning is unknown.",
  "body": [["Colon microbiota", "Iron competition and microcins crowd out harmful E. coli, Salmonella and other relatives."], ["Colon lining", "Beta-defensin induction and tighter junctions are proposed to strengthen the barrier."], ["Colonic inflammation", "Studied for keeping ulcerative colitis in remission, with results similar to mesalamine in trials."], ["Colon cells (DNA)", "Colibactin can damage DNA in lab and mouse studies, a theoretical long-term concern."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "As a live Gram-negative organism, it can cause bacteremia in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "EcN is swallowed in enteric capsules. It survives to the colon, where it multiplies and colonizes within days. It is not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. It is shed in stool, and fecal colonization usually fades after intake ends. Antibiotics active against E. coli kill it.",
  "sidefx": "Bloating, flatulence and mild abdominal discomfort come from added bacterial activity in the colon. Because it is an E. coli, it can cause bacteremia or sepsis if it crosses a damaged barrier in vulnerable patients. Colibactin-related DNA damage is a theoretical risk under study. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics active against E. coli.",
  "fun": "The strain has been sold continuously since 1917, which makes it one of the oldest commercial probiotics in the world.",
  "src": ["Mutaflor product information (Ardeypharm)", "Kruis W et al. Gut 2004", "Nougayrede JP et al. Science 2006"]
},
"bacillus-coagulans-gbi-30": {
  "origin": "Bacillus coagulans was first described in 1915 by B. W. Hammer, who found it curdling canned evaporated milk, which is where the name 'coagulans' comes from. It forms tough spores and makes lactic acid, so it was later sold as a probiotic. The strain GBI-30, 6086 was developed by Ganeden Biotech, a company based in Ohio, and marketed as GanedenBC30 in supplements and in foods such as baked goods and drinks. Kerry acquired Ganeden in 2017. The species name has since changed to Heyndrickxia coagulans. It has an FDA GRAS notice for food use, but it is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1915", "B. W. Hammer describes Bacillus coagulans from coagulated canned milk."], ["2000s", "Ganeden Biotech develops and markets the GBI-30, 6086 strain."], ["2017", "Kerry acquires Ganeden."], ["2020s", "The species is renamed, now Heyndrickxia coagulans."]],
  "cell": "GBI-30 is sold as spores, a dormant form with a thick protective coat. Spores resist heat, stomach acid and bile, so they survive food processing and the stomach. In the small intestine, moisture and nutrients trigger germination into active cells. The active cells ferment sugars to L-lactic acid, lowering pH and competing with other microbes. Small studies found changes in cytokine release from immune cells exposed to the strain, which is the basis for proposed immune effects. Proposed benefits for gas, bloating and IBS symptoms come from small trials. It is not a receptor drug.",
  "body": [["Small intestine and colon", "Germinated cells make lactic acid and may shift the local microbial community for a short time."], ["Gut symptoms", "Small trials reported less gas and bloating, with limited evidence."], ["Immune system", "Lab and small human studies report changes in cytokine signaling."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Bacteremia is a recognized risk of live bacterial products in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "Spores are swallowed in capsules or foods. They pass through the stomach unharmed, germinate in the intestine, and grow for a time. The organism is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. It does not permanently colonize, and its presence fades after stopping. Its timing of action is not well defined.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating come from fermentation by the germinated bacteria. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in high-risk patients. The GRAS notice addresses safety as a food ingredient, not benefit. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Because its spores survive baking, the strain is added to products like muffins and pasta that would kill most probiotics.",
  "src": ["FDA GRAS Notice for Bacillus coagulans GBI-30, 6086", "Hammer BW. Iowa Agricultural Experiment Station Research Bulletin 1915", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"bacillus-clausii": {
  "origin": "Bacillus clausii spores have been sold in Italy as Enterogermina since the late 1950s, developed by an Italian company whose product line later passed to Sanofi and its consumer health spin-off Opella. The product contains four strains, O/C, N/R, SIN and T, which were selected for resistance to different antibiotics so the bacteria could survive alongside antibiotic treatment. The strains were first labeled Bacillus subtilis and were reclassified as B. clausii in the 2000s; the species is now called Shouchella clausii. It is widely used for childhood diarrhea in Europe, Asia and Latin America but is not marketed in the US and is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "Enterogermina spores go on sale in Italy."], ["2000s", "The strains, first labeled B. subtilis, are identified as Bacillus clausii."], ["2020s", "The species is renamed, now Shouchella clausii."]],
  "cell": "The product contains spores that resist gastric acid and bile. In the intestine they germinate into active cells. Proposed actions come from lab and small human studies. The active cells produce antimicrobial substances, including a lantibiotic, that inhibit some Gram-positive bacteria such as C. difficile and S. aureus in vitro. They compete with pathogens for nutrients and space. They may stimulate mucosal immunity, including IgA and interferon signals. The strains carry chromosomal resistance genes to macrolides, a beta-lactamase, aminoglycosides and chloramphenicol, which let them persist during antibiotic courses; transfer to other bacteria was not shown in studies.",
  "body": [["Intestine", "Germinated cells make antimicrobial compounds and compete with pathogens, proposed to shorten diarrhea."], ["Gut during antibiotics", "Because the strains resist many antibiotics, they survive treatment, and two trials reported fewer antibiotic-related GI side effects."], ["Mucosal immune system", "Lab and small human studies suggest stimulation of local immune defenses."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Case reports describe B. clausii bacteremia, mainly in infants and immunocompromised patients."]],
  "journey": "Spores are swallowed from small oral vials or capsules. They pass through the stomach intact, germinate in the intestine and grow for a time. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. They do not permanently colonize, and the organism fades after stopping. Its timing of action is not well defined.",
  "sidefx": "It is generally well tolerated, with mild stomach upset and rare rash or allergy. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier, reported in infants and immunocompromised patients. Its built-in antibiotic resistance, the same trait that lets it survive antibiotic courses, narrows treatment choices if bacteremia occurs. There is no antidote; infection is treated with an antibiotic the strain is sensitive to.",
  "fun": "Its multiple antibiotic resistances are deliberate: the strains were chosen so they would not be wiped out by the antibiotics a patient was taking.",
  "src": ["Enterogermina product information (Sanofi)", "Senesi S et al. Appl Environ Microbiol 2001", "Ianiro G et al. Curr Pharm Des 2018 review"]
},
"bacillus-subtilis-de111": {
  "origin": "Bacillus subtilis is one of the best known soil bacteria. It was named in 1872 by German botanist Ferdinand Cohn, who studied its heat-resistant spores. It also ferments soybeans into natto, a traditional Japanese food. The strain DE111 was developed as a probiotic ingredient by Deerland Probiotics and Enzymes, a US company now part of ADM, in the 2010s. It is used in capsules, gummies and foods because its spores survive manufacturing and storage. It is a supplement ingredient, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1872", "Ferdinand Cohn names Bacillus subtilis and studies its spores."], ["2010s", "Deerland develops and markets the DE111 strain."], ["2010s", "Small trials study DE111 for stool form and immune markers."]],
  "cell": "DE111 is sold as spores that resist stomach acid and bile. In the intestine they germinate into active cells. Active B. subtilis secretes enzymes (proteases, amylases) and antimicrobial compounds such as lipopeptides (surfactin and related molecules) that can inhibit some competing microbes. Proposed immune effects come from small human studies, including one trial reporting anti-inflammatory changes in immune cell responses. Effects on stool form and blood lipids come from small trials. It is not a receptor drug.",
  "body": [["Intestine", "Germinated cells release enzymes and antimicrobial compounds and may briefly shift the microbial community."], ["Bowel habits", "Small trials reported changes in stool form with limited evidence."], ["Immune system", "One trial reported anti-inflammatory changes in immune cell responses."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Rare B. subtilis bacteremia has been reported with probiotic use in immunocompromised patients."]],
  "journey": "Spores are swallowed in capsules, gummies or foods. They pass through the stomach intact, germinate in the intestine and grow for a time. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. They do not permanently colonize. Timing of action is not well defined.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating are uncommon and come from added microbial activity in the gut. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when bacteria cross a weak gut barrier in immunocompromised patients. Evidence for benefit comes mainly from small trials. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Bacillus subtilis is a favorite model organism of biologists, used for decades to study how bacteria make spores.",
  "src": ["Deerland DE111 product information", "Cohn F. Beitrage zur Biologie der Pflanzen 1872", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"streptococcus-salivarius-k12": {
  "origin": "K12 was isolated by microbiologist John Tagg and colleagues at the University of Otago in Dunedin, New Zealand. Tagg studied why some children rarely got strep throat and found that certain mouth bacteria made bacteriocins, natural antibacterial proteins, that inhibit Streptococcus pyogenes. K12 came from the mouth of a healthy child and produced strong inhibitory activity. BLIS Technologies, named for 'bacteriocin-like inhibitory substances', was formed around 2000 to commercialize it in lozenges. It is sold as a supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and most studies are small and linked to the manufacturer.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "John Tagg's group at the University of Otago isolates K12."], ["2000s", "BLIS Technologies brings K12 lozenges to market."], ["2010s", "Small trials study K12 for strep throat, ear infections and bad breath."]],
  "cell": "K12 is a live oral commensal bacterium. It carries a large plasmid with genes for two lantibiotic bacteriocins, salivaricin A2 and salivaricin B. Lantibiotics are small peptides with unusual lanthionine rings. They bind lipid II or disrupt the membranes of susceptible Gram-positive bacteria, inhibiting cell wall building or causing leakage. In the lab they inhibit Streptococcus pyogenes and some bacteria linked to bad breath. K12 also colonizes the tongue and throat, competing for space with other microbes. Clinical benefit is supported by small, mostly manufacturer-linked studies.",
  "body": [["Mouth and tongue", "Colonization and bacteriocins may reduce odor-producing bacteria, the basis for bad breath claims."], ["Throat", "Inhibition of Streptococcus pyogenes in the lab is the basis for studies on recurrent strep throat."], ["Middle ear", "Studied for ear infections in children, with limited evidence."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Bacteremia is a recognized risk of live bacterial products in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "Lozenges dissolve slowly in the mouth so the bacteria can settle on the tongue and throat. Studies found oral colonization within days. The bacteria are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Most are swallowed with saliva and pass into stool. Colonization usually fades within weeks after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "It is generally well tolerated. The rare serious risk is bloodstream infection in immunocompromised or critically ill patients, a concern with any live bacterial product; viridans-type streptococci from the mouth can also seed damaged heart valves. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "The search for K12 started from a simple question: why do some children almost never get strep throat.",
  "src": ["BLIS K12 product information", "Wescombe PA et al. Future Microbiol 2009", "Tagg JR, Dierksen KP. Trends Biotechnol 2003"]
},
"de-simone-formulation": {
  "origin": "The De Simone Formulation is an eight-strain, high-concentration probiotic mixture created by Italian physician and researcher Claudio De Simone, a professor at the University of L'Aquila. It was sold in the 1990s and 2000s as VSL#3, and most trials published as VSL#3 before 2016 used this formulation, including pouchitis trials led by Paolo Gionchetti in Bologna around 2000. After De Simone's partnership with the VSL#3 brand owner ended in 2016, he licensed the original recipe under new names: Visbiome in the US and Vivomixx in Europe. In 2018 a US federal jury found that VSL#3 made after the split was falsely advertised as the same product. It is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "The eight-strain mix is developed by Claudio De Simone and sold as VSL#3."], ["2000", "A Bologna trial reports fewer pouchitis relapses with the mix."], ["2016", "The partnership ends and the original formulation becomes Visbiome and Vivomixx."], ["2018", "A US jury finds later VSL#3 was falsely advertised as the original."]],
  "cell": "The product contains eight strains of lactobacilli, bifidobacteria and Streptococcus thermophilus in very high numbers. Proposed mechanisms come from lab and small human studies. The strains compete with pathogens and shift the gut community. In pouchitis studies, mucosal IL-10 (an anti-inflammatory cytokine) rose while inflammatory cytokines such as TNF alpha, IL-1 and interferon gamma fell. Lab work showed support of tight junction proteins and less epithelial cell death. In hepatic encephalopathy studies, changes in gut flora were proposed to lower ammonia production. The mix is not a receptor drug, and trial results depend on which formulation was used.",
  "body": [["Ileal pouch", "Maintenance trials showed fewer pouchitis relapses, linked to higher mucosal IL-10 and lower inflammatory cytokines."], ["Colon (ulcerative colitis)", "Studied as an add-on for mild to moderate ulcerative colitis, with some positive trials."], ["Gut barrier", "Lab studies show support of tight junctions and less epithelial injury."], ["Liver and brain", "Studied in hepatic encephalopathy, where changes in gut flora may lower ammonia."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Bacteremia is a recognized risk of live bacterial products in immunocompromised or critically ill patients."]],
  "journey": "The powder or capsules are swallowed, delivering very large numbers of live bacteria. Many survive to the intestine, where the strains can be recovered from stool during use. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Fecal recovery fades after stopping, so ongoing intake is needed for any effect. Antibiotics reduce survival.",
  "sidefx": "Bloating, gas and abdominal discomfort are common early, from fermentation by the large bacterial load. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in immunocompromised or critically ill patients. Because products sold under the same brand changed, older trial results do not automatically apply to every product with that name. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "The 2018 court case is a rare example of a probiotic recipe being treated in court as the key to whether clinical trial results apply to a product.",
  "src": ["Visbiome product information", "Gionchetti P et al. Gastroenterology 2000", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics"]
},
"bifidobacterium-breve-m-16v": {
  "origin": "M-16V comes from Morinaga Milk Industry, a Japanese dairy company that has studied infant bifidobacteria for decades. The strain was isolated from the gut of an infant in Japan. It has been given to preterm infants in many Japanese neonatal units since the 1990s, and later in some Australian units. It is also added to some specialty infant formulas, such as Nutricia's Neocate Syneo, as part of synbiotic mixes with prebiotic fibers. It is not an FDA approved drug, and AAP (2021) did not support routine probiotic use in preterm infants.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "M-16V use in Japanese neonatal units becomes common."], ["2010s", "M-16V is added to some synbiotic specialty infant formulas."], ["2021", "AAP does not support routine probiotic use in preterm infants."], ["2023", "FDA warns about probiotics in preterm infants after a death from a different product."]],
  "cell": "M-16V is an infant-type bifidobacterium. It colonizes the infant colon, which in healthy breastfed babies is normally dominated by bifidobacteria. It ferments oligosaccharides through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate, which lower colonic pH. Lower pH and competition for nutrients are proposed to limit overgrowth of pathogens such as Enterobacteriaceae, which are linked to NEC. Lab and small human studies suggest immune effects, such as shifting the Th1 and Th2 balance and supporting regulatory signals, which is the basis for allergy studies. It is not a receptor drug.",
  "body": [["Preterm infant gut", "Raises fecal bifidobacteria in preterm infants, proposed to reduce pathogen overgrowth linked to NEC."], ["Colon chemistry", "Acetate and lactate lower stool pH and may protect the epithelium."], ["Infant immune system", "Studied in synbiotic formulas for allergic infants, with proposed effects on immune balance."], ["Bloodstream (preterm infants)", "Bifidobacterium bacteremia and sepsis have been reported with probiotic products in preterm infants."]],
  "journey": "The bacteria are given in drops or formula. They pass to the infant colon and colonize over weeks in preterm infants. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. A small study found the strain still detectable in stool weeks after hospital discharge.",
  "sidefx": "It is generally well tolerated in studies. The serious concern is that an immature gut barrier in preterm or immunocompromised infants can let live bacteria into the blood, causing bacteremia or sepsis. FDA warned in 2023 after a preterm infant died from a Bifidobacterium probiotic product (a different product). There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "fun": "Bifidobacteria were first seen in the stool of breastfed infants in 1899 by Henry Tissier at the Pasteur Institute.",
  "src": ["Morinaga M-16V product information", "AAP Clinical Report on probiotics in preterm infants 2021", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"lactobacillus-helveticus-r0052-and-bifidobacterium-longum-r0175": {
  "origin": "Both strains come from the culture collection of Institut Rosell, a Montreal probiotics company founded in the 1930s and later acquired by the Canadian yeast company Lallemand. The blend was marketed in the 2010s as Cerebiome and Probio'Stick for stress and mood, as part of the 'psychobiotic' idea that gut bacteria can affect the brain. Key work included a 2011 French trial by Messaoudi and colleagues in healthy volunteers and a later depression trial. Human results are mixed. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug, and not an established treatment for depression.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "Institut Rosell is founded in Montreal."], ["2011", "A trial in healthy volunteers reports less psychological distress with the blend."], ["2017", "A depression trial reports benefit as an add-on, with higher BDNF in a secondary analysis."]],
  "cell": "The mechanism in humans is not established. Animal studies suggest gut-brain axis effects. In rats under stress, the blend reduced intestinal inflammation and permeability, which could lower inflammatory signals reaching the brain. It also blunted stress-induced activation of the HPA axis (lower corticosterone) and changed signaling in brain areas involved in mood. Vagal nerve signaling from the gut to the brainstem is another proposed route. A depression trial found higher serum BDNF than placebo in a secondary analysis. None of these are receptor drug actions, and the human evidence is limited and mixed.",
  "body": [["Gut lining", "Animal studies show less stress-induced inflammation and leakiness of the intestinal barrier."], ["Stress hormone system", "In animals the blend dampened HPA axis activation; human cortisol effects are inconsistent."], ["Brain and mood", "Some trials reported less distress or depressive symptoms; others found no difference from placebo."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium bacteremia is rare but reported in severely ill or immunocompromised patients."]],
  "journey": "The bacteria are swallowed as capsules or sachets. They pass through the gut and are detected in stool mainly during intake, so presence is temporary. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. Trials measured outcomes after about one to two months of intake.",
  "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation in the gut. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in severely ill or immunocompromised patients, a class effect of live probiotics. Supplement contents can differ from labels because products are not FDA reviewed before sale. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Messaoudi M et al. Br J Nutr 2011", "Kazemi A et al. Clin Nutr 2019", "Lallemand Health Solutions product information"]
},
"akkermansia-muciniphila": {
  "origin": "Akkermansia muciniphila was discovered in 2004 by Muriel Derrien and colleagues in Willem de Vos's laboratory at Wageningen University in the Netherlands. They were looking for gut bacteria that feed on mucin, the main protein of intestinal mucus, and named the new genus after Dutch microbiologist Antoon Akkermans. Later work, especially by Patrice Cani's group at UCLouvain in Belgium, linked lower Akkermansia levels to obesity and type 2 diabetes in mice and people. A 2019 human pilot study found that a pasteurized (killed) form improved insulin sensitivity. The EU approved pasteurized Akkermansia as a novel food in 2022, and the US company Pendulum sells live versions. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["2004", "Derrien and de Vos describe Akkermansia muciniphila at Wageningen University."], ["2013", "Mouse studies link Akkermansia to protection from obesity and metabolic disease."], ["2019", "A human pilot finds improved insulin sensitivity with pasteurized Akkermansia."], ["2022", "The EU authorizes pasteurized Akkermansia as a novel food."]],
  "cell": "Akkermansia lives in the mucus layer of the colon and uses mucin as its food. The proposed mechanism comes mainly from mice. An outer membrane protein, Amuc_1100, binds Toll-like receptor 2 on intestinal epithelial cells. This signaling strengthens tight junctions and the gut barrier, which lowers leakage of bacterial lipopolysaccharide (LPS) into the blood, a process called metabolic endotoxemia. Less LPS means less low-grade inflammation that drives insulin resistance. Akkermansia also stimulates mucus production and may influence GLP-1 secretion. Pasteurization keeps Amuc_1100 active, which may be why the killed form worked in mice and in a small human pilot.",
  "body": [["Gut barrier", "Amuc_1100 signaling through TLR2 tightens the barrier in mice and lowers LPS leakage."], ["Mucus layer", "By feeding on mucin, it stimulates turnover and renewal of the mucus layer."], ["Metabolism", "A small human pilot found better insulin sensitivity and lower insulin and cholesterol with the pasteurized form."], ["Inflammation", "Less LPS in the blood is proposed to lower the low-grade inflammation linked to insulin resistance."]],
  "journey": "Products are swallowed as capsules containing live or pasteurized bacteria. The bacteria pass to the colon. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. Live strains are detected in stool mainly during intake. Trials measured effects after about three months of daily use.",
  "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas can occur. Long-term human safety data are limited. As a mucus-degrading bacterium, there is a theoretical concern about thinning mucus in some disease states, and a theoretical risk of bacteremia with live forms in high-risk patients. Effects of the pasteurized form do not automatically apply to live products. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Akkermansia can make up a few percent of all gut bacteria in healthy adults, even though it was unknown before 2004.",
  "src": ["Derrien M et al. Int J Syst Evol Microbiol 2004", "Depommier C et al. Nature Medicine 2019", "EFSA NDA Panel opinion on pasteurized Akkermansia muciniphila 2021"]
},
"clostridium-butyricum-miyairi-588": {
  "origin": "Clostridium butyricum MIYAIRI 588 comes from Japan, where research on butyrate-producing Clostridium strains was led by the Miyairi laboratory starting in the 1930s. The 588 strain was selected for its spore-forming hardiness and lack of toxin genes, and the company Miyarisan Pharmaceutical developed it as a medicine for diarrhea and gut flora disturbance. It has been used in Japan for decades. In the 2020s, researchers at City of Hope in California tested it with immunotherapy in kidney cancer, and a 2022 Nature Medicine trial reported better response rates in a small group. It is licensed as a medicine in Japan but is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1930s", "The Miyairi laboratory in Japan studies butyrate-producing Clostridium strains."], ["2022", "A small Nature Medicine trial combines CBM588 with nivolumab and ipilimumab in kidney cancer."]],
  "cell": "CBM588 is sold as spores, which survive stomach acid and germinate in the gut. The active cells ferment carbohydrates to butyrate and other short-chain fatty acids. Butyrate is the main fuel for colonocytes, supports tight junctions and mucus production, and inhibits histone deacetylases in immune cells. That HDAC inhibition promotes Foxp3 regulatory T cells, which calm inflammation. The strain also raises levels of Bifidobacterium in some studies, which was the planned endpoint in the kidney cancer trials because gut bacteria may influence response to checkpoint inhibitors. CBM588 is non-toxigenic; some other C. butyricum strains carry botulinum neurotoxin type E genes.",
  "body": [["Colon lining", "Butyrate fuels colon cells and supports the barrier and mucus layer."], ["Gut microbiota", "Changes in gut flora include higher Bifidobacterium in some studies."], ["Immune system", "Butyrate-driven regulatory T cells may calm gut inflammation; effects on cancer immunotherapy are under study."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Strain-related bacteremia has been confirmed in hospitalized, mostly immunocompromised or central-line patients."]],
  "journey": "Spores are swallowed as tablets or powders. They pass through the stomach intact and germinate in the intestine within hours to days. The organism is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. It produces short-chain fatty acids in the colon and leaves in stool. Its presence is temporary and fades after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. The rare serious risk is bacteremia and sepsis when live bacteria cross a damaged barrier, confirmed by genome sequencing in some Japanese hospital patients. Because some C. butyricum strains can make botulinum toxin type E, strain identity matters. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Dizman N et al. Nature Medicine 2022", "Miyarisan product information", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"enterococcus-faecium-sf68": {
  "origin": "SF68 is a strain of Enterococcus faecium developed in Switzerland by Cerbios-Pharma and sold as Bioflorin, licensed as a medicine in Italy, Austria and Switzerland. It was studied mainly in older European, largely Italian, trials for acute diarrhea and antibiotic-associated diarrhea. A version called FortiFlora is sold for dogs and cats. Because E. faecium is also a major hospital pathogen that can carry vancomycin resistance, ESPGHAN does not recommend SF68 for childhood gastroenteritis. It is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["20th century", "SF68 is developed in Switzerland and studied in older European trials for diarrhea."], ["2000s", "ESPGHAN advises against SF68 for childhood gastroenteritis over resistance gene concerns."]],
  "cell": "SF68 is a live lactic acid bacterium. Proposed mechanisms include competitive exclusion of pathogens for binding sites and nutrients, production of lactic acid that lowers pH, and possible bacteriocin production. Animal studies suggest immune modulation, including effects on IgA and cytokine responses. It has no single receptor target. The main concern is not its mechanism but its genus: enterococci readily swap mobile genetic elements, and vancomycin resistance genes (vanA, vanB) spread among E. faecium in hospitals. SF68 itself has not been shown to carry vancomycin resistance, but the theoretical risk of acquiring and transferring it limits its use.",
  "body": [["Gut microbiota", "Competition and lactic acid may limit pathogens, proposed to shorten diarrhea in older trials."], ["Gut immune system", "Animal studies suggest effects on IgA and cytokine responses."], ["Antibiotic resistance ecology", "Enterococci can trade resistance genes, a theoretical risk for spreading vancomycin resistance."], ["Bloodstream and heart valves (rare)", "Enterococci can cause bacteremia and endocarditis, a genus concern in high-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "SF68 is swallowed in capsules or powders. It passes to the intestine and is present there for a short time. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. Its presence is temporary. Diarrhea trials measured days to resolution.",
  "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. The serious concerns are tied to the genus: E. faecium causes hospital infections, including bacteremia and endocarditis, and it can pick up vancomycin resistance genes. These risks are theoretical for SF68 itself. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics chosen by susceptibility testing.",
  "fun": "The same species includes both this probiotic strain and vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus (VRE), a major hospital superbug.",
  "src": ["Bioflorin product information", "ESPGHAN position paper on probiotics for acute gastroenteritis", "Mandell, Douglas and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases"]
},
"lactobacillus-crispatus-ctv-05": {
  "origin": "Lactobacillus crispatus is one of the main bacteria in a healthy vagina, and its presence is linked to low pH and a lower risk of bacterial vaginosis. CTV-05 is a hydrogen peroxide-producing strain isolated from a healthy woman and developed by Osel Inc., a California biotech company, as Lactin-V, a vaginal applicator product. It is studied under FDA oversight as an investigational live biotherapeutic. A 2011 trial (Stapleton and colleagues) studied it for recurrent bladder infections, and a 2020 NEJM phase 2b trial (Cohen and colleagues) found fewer BV recurrences after metronidazole. It is not FDA approved and is not sold in stores.",
  "timeline": [["2000s", "Osel develops L. crispatus CTV-05 as Lactin-V."], ["2011", "A trial reports fewer recurrent UTIs with high-level colonization."], ["2020", "A phase 2b NEJM trial finds lower BV recurrence after metronidazole."]],
  "cell": "CTV-05 recolonizes the vagina after antibiotics clear BV. L. crispatus ferments glycogen breakdown products from the vaginal lining to lactic acid, especially the D-lactic acid isomer, keeping pH low (about 4). The acid, in its protonated form, is toxic to Gardnerella vaginalis and other BV-associated anaerobes. The strain also makes hydrogen peroxide and competes for binding sites on vaginal epithelial cells. Lactic acid may also have anti-inflammatory effects on the epithelium. In the UTI trial, high-level vaginal colonization was linked to fewer recurrences, likely by reducing the vaginal reservoir of E. coli.",
  "body": [["Vagina", "Restores a lactobacillus-dominated flora with low pH, which lowered BV recurrence in trials."], ["Bladder", "By reducing vaginal E. coli reservoirs, high-level colonization was linked to fewer recurrent UTIs."], ["Vaginal lining", "Lactic acid may reduce local inflammation and support the epithelium."], ["Local tissue", "Discharge and irritation occurred at rates similar to placebo in trials."]],
  "journey": "Lactin-V is placed in the vagina with an applicator, usually after an antibiotic course. The bacteria colonize within days. The product acts locally and is not absorbed into the blood or metabolized like a drug. In some users colonization persisted after dosing ended.",
  "sidefx": "Vaginal discharge, itching and irritation in trials occurred at rates similar to placebo, likely reflecting the vehicle and the vaginal application itself. Serious infections were not described. It is investigational and not FDA approved. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Researchers group vaginal microbiomes into 'community state types', and the one dominated by L. crispatus is generally considered the most protective.",
  "src": ["Cohen CR et al. NEJM 2020", "Stapleton AE et al. Clin Infect Dis 2011", "Ravel J et al. PNAS 2011"]
},
"lactobacillus-gasseri": {
  "origin": "Lactobacillus gasseri was formally named in 1980 by Lauer and Kandler, who separated it from L. acidophilus, and it is named after French microbiologist Francis Gasser. It lives naturally in the human gut, mouth and vagina. Several commercial strains were later developed, mostly in Japan: SBT2055 (Snow Brand and Megmilk) for belly fat, OLL2716 (Meiji, LG21 yogurt) for H. pylori, and heat-killed CP2305 (Asahi) for stress and sleep, plus BNR17 from Korea. A bottle labeled only L. gasseri may contain a strain with no research behind it. It is a supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1980", "Lactobacillus gasseri is named as a separate species."], ["2000s", "Japanese companies develop strains such as OLL2716 and SBT2055."], ["2010", "A trial reports lower abdominal fat with SBT2055 fermented milk."]],
  "cell": "Mechanisms are strain specific and not established. For SBT2055, studies suggest it may bind bile acids and lower fat absorption, or change lipid metabolism, linked to smaller fat cells in animals. For OLL2716, lactic acid and other secreted factors suppress H. pylori growth and urease activity in the stomach, lowering its density without eradication. For heat-killed CP2305, gut-brain signaling through the vagus nerve is proposed to lower stress responses and improve sleep measures. None of these are receptor drug actions, and each has its own small research base.",
  "body": [["Abdominal fat", "SBT2055 trials reported small reductions in abdominal fat, which faded after stopping."], ["Stomach", "OLL2716 may lower H. pylori activity and gastric inflammation but does not cure the infection."], ["Brain and sleep", "Heat-killed CP2305 was studied for stress and sleep measures, with small trials."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in severely ill or immunocompromised patients."]],
  "journey": "Products are swallowed in fermented milks, tablets or capsules. Live strains pass through the gut and are present temporarily; heat-killed forms act as non-living cells. None are absorbed or metabolized like a drug, and they leave in stool. Trials measured outcomes after about three to six months, and effects faded after intake stopped in the SBT2055 trial.",
  "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. The rare serious risk with live strains is bacteremia in severely ill or immunocompromised patients. Results belong to the specific strain tested. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Kadooka Y et al. Eur J Clin Nutr 2010", "Lauer E, Kandler O. Zentralbl Bakteriol 1980", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"bio-k-plus-three-strain-blend": {
  "origin": "Bio-K+ was developed in Quebec, Canada, and launched in the 1990s as a fermented milk containing L. acidophilus CL1285 and L. casei LBC80R. It was studied in Canadian hospitals as a way to prevent antibiotic-associated diarrhea and C. difficile. A 2010 trial by Gao and colleagues in the American Journal of Gastroenterology found a dose-related reduction in both. A third strain, L. rhamnosus CLR2, was later added to the product. It is a licensed natural health product in Canada but is not an FDA approved drug in the US. AGA 2020 guidance named the original two-strain combination.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Bio-K+ fermented milk is launched in Quebec."], ["2010", "A trial reports dose-related reductions in antibiotic-associated diarrhea and C. difficile."], ["2020", "AGA gives a conditional suggestion for the two-strain combination in C. difficile prevention."]],
  "cell": "Proposed mechanisms come from lab data. The strains produce lactic acid, which lowers pH and inhibits some pathogens. They compete with C. difficile for nutrients and space. In lab studies, products from the strains suppressed expression of C. difficile toxin genes (tcdA and tcdB), which would reduce toxin-driven colitis. They also interact with gut immune cells. There is no single receptor target. Clinical evidence for preventing antibiotic-associated diarrhea in hospital patients is moderate, and the current product adds a strain not used in older trials.",
  "body": [["Colon during antibiotics", "Fewer cases of antibiotic-associated diarrhea were seen in several hospital trials."], ["C. difficile", "Lab data show suppression of toxin gene expression, proposed to lower infection risk."], ["Gut microbiota", "Lactic acid and competition may limit pathogen overgrowth while antibiotics disrupt normal flora."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus bacteremia is rare but reported in severely ill or immunocompromised patients."]],
  "journey": "The product is swallowed as a fermented milk, plant-based drink or capsules. The bacteria pass to the intestine and are present temporarily. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leave in stool. Trials started it within days of beginning antibiotics.",
  "sidefx": "Mild bloating or gas comes from fermentation. Dairy versions contain milk protein. The rare serious risk is bacteremia in severely ill or immunocompromised patients, a class effect of live probiotics. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Gao XW et al. Am J Gastroenterol 2010", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics", "Bio-K+ product information"]
}
,
"bifidobacterium-bifidum-mimbb75": {
  "origin": "MIMBb75 was characterized by microbiologist Simone Guglielmetti and colleagues at the University of Milan, who selected it in lab tests for its unusually strong binding to intestinal cells. A 2011 trial in Alimentary Pharmacology and Therapeutics with live MIMBb75 reported less IBS symptoms. The German company Synformulas then developed a heat-inactivated version, sold as Kijimea Irritable Bowel PRO, which is regulated as a medical device in the EU. A large 2020 trial in The Lancet Gastroenterology and Hepatology by Andresen and colleagues found the non-living cells improved IBS symptoms. It is not a common US product and is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["2010", "University of Milan researchers describe the strain's strong adhesion and the BopA protein."], ["2011", "A trial with live MIMBb75 reports improvement in IBS symptoms."], ["2020", "A large trial finds heat-inactivated MIMBb75 improves IBS symptoms."]],
  "cell": "The proposed mechanism centers on adhesion rather than colonization. MIMBb75 binds tightly to intestinal epithelial cells, and lab studies identified a surface lipoprotein, BopA, involved in this binding. A layer of bound bacteria or bacterial cell surfaces may physically shield the mucosa from irritants and pathogens. Contact with epithelial and immune cells may dampen mucosal immune activation, including mast cell and cytokine signals, which in turn may reduce visceral hypersensitivity, the heightened gut pain sensation of IBS. Because the 2020 trial used heat-killed cells, the authors proposed that live growth is not essential, which fits a surface-protein mechanism.",
  "body": [["Intestinal lining", "Strong adhesion may form a protective layer on the mucosa, proposed from lab studies."], ["Gut pain signaling", "Less abdominal pain in trials is proposed to reflect calmer mucosal immune activity and less visceral hypersensitivity."], ["Bowel habits", "Global IBS symptoms, including bloating and irregular stools, improved more than with placebo in trials."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "The live form carries a theoretical bacteremia risk in high-risk patients; the heat-inactivated form contains no live bacteria."]],
  "journey": "Capsules are swallowed and pass through the stomach to the intestine, where the bacteria or bacterial cells bind the lining for a time. Nothing is absorbed into the blood as a drug. The cells are digested or pass out in stool. Trials judged symptoms over four to eight weeks, and in the 2011 trial the benefit faded within two weeks after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Mild gas or bloating can occur from fermentation and shifts in gut contents. Live bacterial probiotics carry a rare bacteremia risk in immunocompromised or critically ill patients and those with central lines; this does not apply to the heat-inactivated product. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "This is one of the best known examples of a 'postbiotic', a product of killed bacteria that still showed a clinical effect in a large trial.",
  "src": ["Guglielmetti S et al. Aliment Pharmacol Ther 2011", "Andresen V et al. Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol 2020", "Kijimea product information"]
},
"bifidobacterium-longum-bb536": {
  "origin": "BB536 was isolated in 1969 from the gut of a healthy infant by researchers at Morinaga Milk Industry in Japan. Morinaga chose it for its ability to survive in dairy products and in the gut, and it was added to Japanese yogurts and fermented milks from the 1970s. It became one of the most studied bifidobacteria in Japan, tested in pollen allergy, bowel habits, ulcerative colitis and infections in older adults. It is now sold as an ingredient in supplements in many countries. It is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1969", "Morinaga researchers isolate BB536 from a healthy infant."], ["1970s", "BB536 is added to Japanese dairy products."], ["2000s", "Japanese trials study BB536 in cedar pollen allergy."], ["2000s", "BB536 receives an FDA GRAS notice for food use."]],
  "cell": "BB536 is a live bifidobacterium with proposed mechanisms. In the colon it ferments carbohydrates through the bifid shunt to acetate and lactate. These acids lower pH, provide fuel and signals to epithelial cells, and support barrier function; acetate from bifidobacteria protected mice from a lethal E. coli O157 infection in one well known study. It also interacts with mucosal immune cells. In Japanese pollen allergy trials it shifted cytokines away from a Th2 allergy pattern, and in an elderly trial NK cell activity changed. Effects are inconsistent across trials, and it is not a receptor drug.",
  "body": [["Colon", "Acetate and lactate lower pH and support the epithelial barrier."], ["Bowel habits", "Small trials report changes in stool frequency, with inconsistent results."], ["Immune system and allergy", "Pollen allergy trials reported some relief of eye symptoms and a shift away from Th2 cytokines."], ["Colonic inflammation", "Small ulcerative colitis trials reported partial improvement in scores."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Live bifidobacteria carry a theoretical bacteremia risk in high-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "BB536 is swallowed in yogurts, fermented milks or capsules. Many cells survive to the colon. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. It does not permanently colonize most adults, so its presence is transient. Trials ran from two to fourteen weeks.",
  "sidefx": "Mild gas or bloating comes from fermentation. Live bacterial probiotics carry a rare bacteremia risk in immunocompromised or critically ill patients and those with central lines, and FDA warned in 2023 about probiotics in preterm infants. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Morinaga BB536 product information", "Fukuda S et al. Nature 2011", "Xiao JZ et al. Clin Exp Allergy 2006"]
},
"lactobacillus-johnsonii-la1": {
  "origin": "La1 was selected by scientists at the Nestle Research Center near Lausanne, Switzerland, from Nestle's culture collection. It was first called Lactobacillus acidophilus La1 and later identified as L. johnsonii, a species named in 1992 after microbiologist Jeffrey Johnson. Nestle launched it in LC1 fermented milks in Europe in the 1990s. Trials studied it mainly against Helicobacter pylori, where it lowered bacterial density and gastritis activity but rarely cured the infection, and in Crohn disease after surgery, where it did not prevent relapse. Its genome was published in 2004. It is not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["1990s", "Nestle launches LC1 fermented milks with La1."], ["1992", "Lactobacillus johnsonii is named as a species."], ["2004", "The La1 (NCC 533) genome is published."]],
  "cell": "Proposed mechanisms come mainly from lab studies. La1 produces lactic acid and other secreted factors that inhibit H. pylori growth and its ability to attach to gastric epithelial cells. In the stomach this may lower H. pylori density and reduce gastritis activity. La1 also adheres to intestinal epithelial cells and interacts with mucosal immune cells, which may shift cytokine signals. It is not a receptor drug, and in trials it suppressed H. pylori rather than eradicating it.",
  "body": [["Stomach", "Lower H. pylori density and gastritis activity were seen in trials, without true eradication in most people."], ["Small intestine", "Adhesion to epithelial cells may support the barrier while the strain is present."], ["Gut immune system", "Lab studies show effects on mucosal cytokine signaling."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Live lactobacilli carry a theoretical bacteremia risk in high-risk patients."]],
  "journey": "La1 is swallowed in fermented milks. It passes through the stomach, where it can act on H. pylori, and into the intestine. It is not absorbed or metabolized like a drug and leaves in stool. H. pylori trials ran about three weeks, and in a child trial H. pylori usually returned to prior levels after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Mild gas or bloating comes from fermentation. Fermented milks contain milk protein and lactose. Live bacterial probiotics carry a rare bacteremia risk in immunocompromised or critically ill patients and those with central lines. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Pridmore RD et al. PNAS 2004", "Felley CP et al. Eur J Gastroenterol Hepatol 2001", "Marteau P et al. Gut 2006"]
},
"yogurt-cultures": {
  "origin": "Yogurt has been made for thousands of years in the Middle East, Central Asia and the Balkans, where warm milk soured into a thick, tart food. In 1905 Bulgarian medical student Stamen Grigorov, working in Geneva, identified the rod-shaped bacterium now called Lactobacillus delbrueckii subsp. bulgaricus as the key yogurt microbe. Soon after, Elie Metchnikoff of the Pasteur Institute popularized the idea that Bulgarian peasants lived long lives because they ate soured milk, in his 1907 book The Prolongation of Life. In 1919 Isaac Carasso began selling yogurt in Barcelona, the start of Danone. US standards of identity require yogurt to be made with both L. bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus. Yogurt is a food, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Yogurt making develops in the Middle East, Central Asia and the Balkans."], ["1905", "Stamen Grigorov identifies the yogurt bacterium later named L. bulgaricus."], ["1907", "Elie Metchnikoff links soured milk to long life in The Prolongation of Life."], ["1919", "Isaac Carasso starts selling yogurt in Barcelona, founding Danone."]],
  "cell": "The two cultures work as partners. S. thermophilus grows first and makes formic acid and carbon dioxide that stimulate L. bulgaricus, which in turn breaks milk proteins into peptides and amino acids that feed S. thermophilus. Together they ferment lactose to lactic acid, which drops pH and makes casein proteins gel into yogurt. Medically, the best supported effect is on lactose digestion. The bacteria contain beta-galactosidase (lactase). In the small intestine, bile breaks open the bacterial cells and releases this enzyme, which splits lactose into glucose and galactose in people who make too little of their own lactase. Fermentation also lowers the lactose content, and the semisolid texture slows stomach emptying, giving more time for digestion.",
  "body": [["Small intestine", "Bacterial lactase released by bile digests lactose, so less undigested sugar reaches the colon."], ["Colon", "Less undigested lactose means less fermentation by colon bacteria, so less gas, bloating and diarrhea in lactose maldigesters."], ["Stomach", "The thick texture slows gastric emptying, giving lactase more time to work."], ["Immune system", "People with milk allergy react to milk proteins in yogurt, which can cause hives or anaphylaxis."]],
  "journey": "Yogurt is eaten as food. Many cultures die in stomach acid, and survivors are broken open by bile in the small intestine, releasing their lactase. They do not colonize the gut. Nothing is absorbed as a drug; the bacteria are digested like other food proteins, and any remaining cells leave in stool within hours to a day. Yogurts that are heat-treated after culturing contain few live bacteria.",
  "sidefx": "Most people have no side effects or mild gas. Milk allergy reactions come from milk proteins, not the bacteria, and can include anaphylaxis. Yogurt is not lactose free, so people with severe lactose intolerance may still have symptoms. Infections from these food cultures are very rare. There is no antidote; allergic reactions are treated in the usual way, with epinephrine for anaphylaxis.",
  "fun": "The 'self-digesting' trick of yogurt, where bacteria carry their own lactase, is why many lactose-intolerant people tolerate yogurt better than milk.",
  "src": ["FDA standard of identity for yogurt (21 CFR 131.200)", "EFSA NDA Panel opinion on live yogurt cultures and lactose digestion 2010", "Savaiano DA. Am J Clin Nutr 2014"]
},
"kefir-cultures": {
  "origin": "Kefir comes from the Caucasus Mountains, where people fermented milk in skin bags or containers using kefir grains, small cauliflower-like clumps of bacteria and yeasts held together by a sugar matrix. The grains grow and can be passed on, so the culture has been kept alive for generations, and its exact origin is unknown. Kefir spread through Russia in the early 1900s and later worldwide. Scientists have since found that the grains contain Lactobacillus kefiranofaciens, Lentilactobacillus kefiri, Lactococcus, Leuconostoc, acetic acid bacteria and yeasts. Store-bought kefir is often made with defined starter cultures instead of grains. Kefir is a food, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Kefir is made in the Caucasus Mountains with kefir grains."], ["1900s", "Kefir production spreads across Russia."], ["20th century", "Scientists identify the bacteria, yeasts and the kefiran polysaccharide in kefir grains."]],
  "cell": "Kefir grains ferment lactose with several microbes at once. Lactic acid bacteria make lactic acid, yeasts make carbon dioxide and a little ethanol, and acetic acid bacteria add acetic acid. L. kefiranofaciens produces kefiran, a branched glucose and galactose polysaccharide that forms much of the grain. Medically, kefir aids lactose digestion like yogurt: bacterial lactase and lower lactose content mean less undigested sugar reaches the colon. Lactic acid, bacteriocins and kefiran may inhibit some pathogens and influence gut immunity in lab and animal studies. Human evidence beyond lactose digestion is limited.",
  "body": [["Small intestine", "Bacterial lactase and lower lactose content help digestion, with less gas after a lactose load in a small trial."], ["Gut microbiota", "Live bacteria and yeasts pass through the gut for a short time and may shift the community."], ["Gut immune system", "Kefiran and bacterial products are proposed to modulate immunity, mostly from animal data."], ["Immune system (allergy)", "Milk proteins in kefir can cause allergic reactions in people with milk allergy."]],
  "journey": "Kefir is drunk as food. Some bacteria and yeasts survive the stomach and pass through the gut for a short time. Nothing is absorbed as a drug. The microbes are digested or leave in stool within hours to days. Composition differs widely between grain and commercial kefirs and from batch to batch.",
  "sidefx": "Bloating and gas come from carbon dioxide and fermentation. Milk allergy reactions come from milk proteins. Kefir contains a small amount of alcohol from yeast fermentation. Live bacteria and yeasts carry a rare infection risk in severely immunocompromised patients, and antifungal drugs can kill the yeast part. There is no antidote.",
  "fun": "Kefir grains are not cereal grains at all; they are living clumps of microbes that keep growing and can be shared.",
  "src": ["Rosa DD et al. Nutr Res Rev 2017 (kefir review)", "Hertzler SR, Clancy SM. J Am Diet Assoc 2003", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology (probiotics overview)"]
},
"garden-of-life-critical-care": {
  "origin": "Garden of Life is a US supplement company founded in Florida in 2000, and it has been owned by Nestle Health Science since 2017. Its Dr. Formulated Probiotics line was launched in the 2010s with neurologist David Perlmutter. Critical Care is a high-count capsule with 15 Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains plus prebiotic fiber from potato resistant starch and acacia. The name Critical Care is a brand name, not a hospital or ICU product. This exact blend has no published clinical trials. It is a dietary supplement, not an FDA approved drug.",
  "timeline": [["2000", "Garden of Life is founded in Florida."], ["2017", "Nestle Health Science acquires Garden of Life."], ["2010s", "The Dr. Formulated Probiotics line, including Critical Care, is launched."]],
  "cell": "No mechanism is established for this blend, and effects of probiotics are strain specific. In general, Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium strains are proposed to ferment sugars to lactic acid and acetate, lower gut pH, compete with harmful microbes for nutrients and binding sites, and interact with immune cells through pattern recognition receptors such as TLR2. The prebiotic fibers (resistant starch and acacia) are not digested in the small intestine and are fermented in the colon by resident bacteria into short-chain fatty acids like butyrate, which fuel colon cells. Whether these strains together produce any clinical benefit has not been tested.",
  "body": [["Gut microbiota", "Many strains add to the gut community for a short time while taken."], ["Colon", "Prebiotic fibers are fermented to short-chain fatty acids, which feed colon cells and may cause gas."], ["Gut immune system", "Strains may interact with immune cells, but effects of this blend are unknown."], ["Bloodstream (rare)", "Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium bacteremia is rare but reported in immunocompromised, critically ill or preterm patients."]],
  "journey": "The capsules are swallowed. Bacteria that survive stomach acid and bile pass to the intestine and are present temporarily, showing up in stool within days. They are not absorbed or metabolized like a drug. The fibers reach the colon and are fermented there. The strains are cleared from stool within days to weeks after stopping.",
  "sidefx": "Gas and bloating are common early, from both bacterial fermentation and the prebiotic fiber. The rare serious risk is bacteremia when live bacteria cross a weak gut barrier, mainly in immunocompromised, critically ill or preterm patients. Despite the product name, probiotics in critically ill patients are linked to rare bloodstream infections. There is no antidote; infection is treated with antibiotics.",
  "src": ["Garden of Life product information", "AGA 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on probiotics", "FDA 2023 safety communication on probiotics in preterm infants"]
},
"magnesium-glycinate": {
  "origin": "Magnesium was recognized as an element by Scottish chemist Joseph Black in 1755 and first isolated by Humphry Davy in 1808. Its salts have long been used in medicine, starting with Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate) from a spring in Epsom, England, in the 1600s. Magnesium glycinate, also called bisglycinate, binds one magnesium ion to two molecules of the amino acid glycine. Amino acid chelates like this were developed for supplements in the late 20th century, notably by Albion Laboratories in Utah, with the goal of better absorption and less diarrhea than magnesium oxide. It is sold only as a dietary supplement, with no FDA approved drug product.",
  "timeline": [["1618", "Epsom salt is discovered at a spring in Epsom, England, according to tradition."], ["1755", "Joseph Black recognizes magnesium as an element."], ["1808", "Humphry Davy isolates magnesium metal."], ["20th century", "Amino acid chelates such as magnesium bisglycinate are developed for supplements."]],
  "cell": "Magnesium is a cofactor for more than 300 enzymes. It binds ATP, and the Mg-ATP complex is the real substrate for kinases, the Na-K ATPase and DNA and protein synthesis. It acts as a physiologic calcium channel blocker, competing with calcium at voltage-gated channels and on contractile proteins, which relaxes smooth muscle. It blocks the NMDA receptor channel at resting membrane potential, limiting calcium entry and neuronal excitation. In the heart it stabilizes membranes and supports potassium handling. The glycinate chelate is proposed to be absorbed partly intact through dipeptide transporters, according to manufacturer and small studies, leaving less unabsorbed magnesium in the bowel to draw in water.",
  "body": [["Nerves and brain", "NMDA channel block and calcium competition reduce neuronal excitability, the basis for claims about sleep and stress."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Magnesium balances calcium in muscle contraction, the basis for claims about cramps."], ["Heart", "It supports normal rhythm by stabilizing membranes and potassium handling; very high levels slow conduction."], ["Gut", "Less unabsorbed magnesium means less osmotic laxative effect than magnesium oxide or citrate, according to limited studies."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys control magnesium balance, so kidney impairment can lead to dangerous buildup."], ["Bones", "About half of body magnesium is stored in bone, which acts as a reservoir."]],
  "journey": "Taken by mouth, magnesium is absorbed in the small intestine over several hours by passive paracellular movement and active TRPM6 and TRPM7 channels. The chelated form may also use dipeptide pathways. Only part of a dose is absorbed. In the blood about 30 percent is bound to protein. It is stored in bone and inside cells. It is not metabolized; the kidneys filter it and adjust excretion to keep levels normal. Unabsorbed magnesium leaves in stool, and glycine joins the body's amino acid pool.",
  "sidefx": "Diarrhea, nausea and cramping happen when unabsorbed magnesium pulls water into the bowel by osmosis, reported less often with glycinate than with oxide or citrate. In kidney impairment magnesium can build up. High magnesium blocks calcium channels and acetylcholine release at nerve-muscle junctions, causing loss of reflexes, weakness, low blood pressure, respiratory depression and heart block. IV calcium gluconate or chloride counteracts the effects of severe hypermagnesemia, and dialysis can remove it.",
  "fun": "Magnesium sits at the center of every chlorophyll molecule, so green vegetables are a major dietary source.",
  "src": ["NIH Office of Dietary Supplements Magnesium Fact Sheet", "Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology"]
}
,
"cocaine": {
  "origin": "Cocaine comes from the leaves of the coca shrub, Erythroxylum coca, which people in the Andes of South America have chewed for thousands of years to fight fatigue, hunger and altitude sickness. In 1860 Albert Niemann, a graduate student in Gottingen, Germany, isolated the pure alkaloid and named it cocaine. In 1884 Viennese eye doctor Carl Koller showed that a few drops numbed the eye for surgery, making cocaine the first local anesthetic; the same year Sigmund Freud published his essay Uber Coca. Cocaine was soon in tonics and drinks, and its addictive harms led to the US Harrison Narcotics Act of 1914. Today it is Schedule II. FDA approved cocaine hydrochloride nasal solutions, Goprelto in 2017 and Numbrino in 2020, for numbing the nose during procedures. Street use is illegal.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Andean peoples chew coca leaves."], ["1860", "Albert Niemann isolates cocaine from coca leaves."], ["1884", "Carl Koller introduces cocaine as a local anesthetic for eye surgery."], ["1914", "The Harrison Narcotics Act restricts cocaine in the US."], ["1970", "The Controlled Substances Act places cocaine in Schedule II."], ["2017", "FDA approves Goprelto cocaine nasal solution for nasal anesthesia."]],
  "cell": "Cocaine has two main actions. First, it binds and blocks the presynaptic reuptake transporters for norepinephrine (NET), dopamine (DAT) and serotonin (SERT). These monoamines stay longer in the synapse. Excess norepinephrine at alpha-1 and beta-1 receptors causes vasoconstriction, high blood pressure, fast heart rate and dilated pupils. Excess dopamine in the nucleus accumbens drives euphoria and the strong reinforcement that underlies addiction. Second, cocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve and heart cells, preventing action potentials. In nerves this produces local anesthesia. In the heart it slows phase 0 depolarization, widening the QRS like a class I antiarrhythmic. The nasal solution uses both effects: numbing plus vasoconstriction that shrinks the mucosa and reduces bleeding.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Dopamine buildup in reward circuits causes euphoria and craving; high levels cause agitation, psychosis, hyperthermia and seizures."], ["Heart", "Catecholamine excess raises heart rate and oxygen demand while sodium channel block widens the QRS and promotes dangerous rhythms."], ["Coronary arteries", "Alpha-1 driven vasospasm and faster clotting can cause heart attack even in young people."], ["Blood vessels", "Sudden spikes in blood pressure can tear the aorta or cause stroke and brain bleeding."], ["Nose", "Repeated vasoconstriction starves the nasal septum of blood, which can lead to ulcers and perforation."], ["Muscles and temperature", "Agitation and heat production cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis, which can injure the kidneys."]],
  "journey": "Cocaine is absorbed through mucous membranes, lungs or veins. Smoked or injected, it reaches the brain within seconds and peaks in minutes; through the nose it takes several minutes and lasts longer because vasoconstriction slows its own absorption. It is an ester, so plasma cholinesterase and liver esterases quickly break it into benzoylecgonine and ecgonine methyl ester. Its half-life is about an hour. Urine tests detect benzoylecgonine for about two to four days. With alcohol, the liver makes cocaethylene, which lasts longer and is more toxic to the heart.",
  "sidefx": "Nearly all serious harm comes from too much catecholamine and too much sodium channel block. Vasospasm and high blood pressure cause heart attack, aortic dissection and stroke. Sodium channel block causes wide QRS rhythms. Brain stimulation causes seizures, psychosis and hyperthermia, which leads to muscle breakdown. Street cocaine is often contaminated with fentanyl and levamisole. There is no specific antidote; benzodiazepines are commonly used for agitation, and sodium bicarbonate is used for sodium channel toxicity with wide QRS.",
  "fun": "Coca-Cola contained coca leaf extract when it was invented in 1886; the cocaine was removed in the early 1900s.",
  "src": ["Goprelto (cocaine hydrochloride) nasal solution FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"methamphetamine": {
  "origin": "Methamphetamine was first made in 1893 by Japanese chemist Nagai Nagayoshi from ephedrine, a stimulant he had earlier isolated from the ephedra plant. In 1919 Akira Ogata found a simpler route that produced the crystal form. In 1938 the German company Temmler sold it as Pervitin, and it was widely given to soldiers in World War II to fight fatigue. After the war, misuse spread in Japan and elsewhere. In the US, Abbott's Desoxyn tablets were approved in 1943, and today the tablets are approved for ADHD in children 6 and older. Methamphetamine is Schedule II. Illicit crystal methamphetamine is made in clandestine labs, and its use is illegal and causes addiction, psychosis and organ damage.",
  "timeline": [["1893", "Nagai Nagayoshi synthesizes methamphetamine from ephedrine."], ["1919", "Akira Ogata produces crystalline methamphetamine."], ["1938", "Temmler markets methamphetamine as Pervitin in Germany."], ["1943", "FDA approves Desoxyn."], ["1971", "Methamphetamine is placed in Schedule II."]],
  "cell": "Methamphetamine looks enough like dopamine to be carried into nerve terminals by DAT and NET, and it also diffuses in. Inside, it acts on VMAT2, the vesicular transporter, causing monoamines to leak from storage vesicles into the cytoplasm. It also activates TAAR1, an intracellular receptor, which triggers kinase signaling that phosphorylates DAT and makes it run in reverse. The result is a flood of dopamine, norepinephrine and, to a lesser degree, serotonin pushed out into the synapse, independent of nerve firing. It weakly inhibits MAO, which further raises monoamine levels. Dopamine in reward pathways produces intense euphoria and addiction. Norepinephrine drives tachycardia, hypertension, vasoconstriction and hyperthermia. At therapeutic levels in ADHD, the same mechanism improves attention.",
  "body": [["Brain", "A dopamine surge causes euphoria, alertness and addiction; heavy use causes paranoia, psychosis and long-lasting changes in dopamine terminals."], ["Heart", "Sustained catecholamine stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure and can cause heart attack and cardiomyopathy."], ["Blood vessels", "Severe hypertension and vasoconstriction can cause stroke, brain bleeding and aortic dissection."], ["Temperature and muscles", "Hyperactivity and heat generation cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis, with risk of kidney failure."], ["Mouth and teeth", "Dry mouth, grinding and poor hygiene cause severe dental decay, often called meth mouth."], ["Skin", "Sensations of bugs under the skin lead to picking and sores."]],
  "journey": "Methamphetamine is well absorbed by any route. Smoked or injected, it reaches the brain within seconds; by mouth it takes 15 to 30 minutes. It is lipid soluble and crosses into the brain easily. The liver metabolizes it by CYP2D6 to amphetamine and p-hydroxymethamphetamine, but much leaves unchanged in urine, more when urine is acidic. Its half-life is about 10 to 12 hours, so effects last much longer than cocaine. Urine tests detect it for about two to four days.",
  "sidefx": "The main toxicity is an overdose of monoamine release. Norepinephrine causes dangerous hypertension, arrhythmias, heart attack and stroke. Dopamine excess causes agitation, psychosis and violent behavior. Heat production from agitation and muscle activity causes hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis and multiorgan failure. Long use damages dopamine nerve terminals. Illicit supply is often mixed with fentanyl. The tablet label has a boxed warning for abuse and dependence. There is no specific antidote; benzodiazepines and cooling are standard supportive measures.",
  "fun": "Nagai Nagayoshi is also known for isolating ephedrine from the ephedra plant in 1885, the starting point for both decongestants and methamphetamine.",
  "src": ["Desoxyn (methamphetamine hydrochloride) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"mdma": {
  "origin": "MDMA was first made by the German company Merck in 1912 as a chemical intermediate, and Merck patented the process in 1914, but it was never developed as a medicine. In the 1970s chemist Alexander Shulgin resynthesized it and, with David Nichols, described its effects in humans in 1978. Some therapists then used it in psychotherapy. As recreational use as 'ecstasy' grew in the 1980s, the DEA placed it in Schedule I in 1985. Later clinical trials studied MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD, but in 2024 FDA did not approve the application, issuing a complete response letter. It has no approved medical use.",
  "timeline": [["1912", "Merck chemists synthesize MDMA as an intermediate."], ["1978", "Shulgin and Nichols describe its effects in humans."], ["1985", "The DEA places MDMA in Schedule I."], ["2024", "FDA declines to approve MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD."]],
  "cell": "MDMA is an amphetamine with a methylenedioxy ring. It is taken up by monoamine transporters, with the highest affinity for the serotonin transporter (SERT), then NET and DAT. Inside the neuron it disrupts VMAT2 storage and reverses the transporters, so large amounts of serotonin, plus norepinephrine and dopamine, are pushed into the synapse. The serotonin surge acts on many receptors, including 5-HT2A indirectly, producing mood elevation, emotional closeness and altered perception. MDMA also triggers release of oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH). Vasopressin makes the kidneys hold water, which can drop blood sodium. After the surge, serotonin stores are depleted for days, which explains low mood after use. Norepinephrine release drives tachycardia, hypertension and heat production.",
  "body": [["Brain", "A serotonin surge causes euphoria and empathy; depletion afterward causes low mood; too much serotonin can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Kidneys and sodium balance", "Vasopressin release makes the kidneys retain water, and with heavy water drinking, sodium falls and the brain swells."], ["Temperature control", "Muscle activity, serotonin and norepinephrine raise body heat, and crowded hot settings make hyperthermia worse."], ["Heart and vessels", "Catecholamine release raises heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Jaw and muscles", "Jaw clenching and teeth grinding are common; severe hyperthermia can cause rhabdomyolysis."]],
  "journey": "MDMA is swallowed and absorbed from the gut, with effects starting in 30 to 60 minutes and peaking in about one and a half to three hours. It crosses into the brain easily. The liver metabolizes it mainly by CYP2D6, and MDMA inhibits CYP2D6 itself, so its metabolism is nonlinear and extra doses raise levels more than expected. A main metabolite is MDA, which is also active. The half-life is about eight to nine hours. It is excreted by the kidneys and detected in urine for about one to three days.",
  "sidefx": "Hyponatremia comes from vasopressin release plus drinking lots of water, and it can cause brain swelling, seizures and death, especially in women. Hyperthermia comes from serotonin and norepinephrine effects on heat production plus exertion, and it can cause DIC and rhabdomyolysis. Serotonin syndrome occurs with other serotonergic drugs. Tablets sold as ecstasy or molly often contain cathinones, methamphetamine or PMA, which can be more toxic. There is no specific antidote; cooling, benzodiazepines and careful sodium correction are standard supportive measures.",
  "fun": "Merck's 1912 work on MDMA was aimed at making a drug to stop bleeding, and MDMA itself was just a step along the way.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Shulgin AT, Nichols DE. 1978", "FDA complete response letter announcement for midomafetamine 2024"]
},
"heroin": {
  "origin": "Heroin is made by chemically changing morphine, the main alkaloid of the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum. In 1874 English chemist C. R. Alder Wright at St Mary's Hospital in London first made diacetylmorphine by boiling morphine with acetic anhydride. In 1898 the German company Bayer, through chemists including Felix Hoffmann and pharmacologist Heinrich Dreser, marketed it as Heroin, a cough remedy believed to be safer than morphine. It soon proved highly addictive. The US banned its manufacture in 1924, and it is now Schedule I with no approved US medical use, though diamorphine is still used medically in the UK and some other countries. Most US supply has now been replaced or mixed with fentanyl.",
  "timeline": [["1874", "C. R. Alder Wright synthesizes diacetylmorphine in London."], ["1898", "Bayer markets diacetylmorphine as Heroin."], ["1924", "The US bans heroin manufacture."], ["1970", "The Controlled Substances Act places heroin in Schedule I."], ["2010s", "Fentanyl increasingly replaces or contaminates heroin in the US."]],
  "cell": "Heroin itself binds opioid receptors weakly. Its two acetyl groups make it more lipid soluble than morphine, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier quickly. In blood and brain, esterases remove one acetyl group to form 6-monoacetylmorphine (6-MAM), then the second to form morphine. 6-MAM and morphine are full agonists at the mu-opioid receptor, a Gi/o protein-coupled receptor. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowers cAMP, opens GIRK potassium channels to hyperpolarize neurons and closes voltage-gated calcium channels to cut neurotransmitter release. In the brainstem this blunts the response to carbon dioxide and slows breathing. In the ventral tegmental area it disinhibits dopamine neurons, producing euphoria and reinforcing use. Kappa and delta receptors are also activated to a lesser degree.",
  "body": [["Brainstem breathing centers", "Mu receptor activation blunts the response to rising carbon dioxide, causing slow, shallow breathing and apnea in overdose."], ["Brain reward and alertness", "Dopamine release in reward circuits causes euphoria and addiction; sedation progresses to coma at high levels."], ["Eyes", "Activation of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."], ["Gut", "Reduced motility and secretion cause constipation and nausea."], ["Lungs", "Overdose can cause noncardiogenic pulmonary edema; vomiting while sedated can lead to aspiration."], ["Skin and veins", "Injection introduces bacteria and viruses, causing abscesses, endocarditis, wound botulism, HIV and hepatitis C."]],
  "journey": "Injected or smoked, heroin reaches the brain within seconds; snorted, within minutes. Because it is lipid soluble, it enters the brain faster than morphine, which is why the rush is stronger. In the blood it is deacetylated within minutes to 6-MAM, which has a half-life of about 30 minutes, then to morphine, which lasts two to three hours. The liver converts morphine to glucuronides. The kidneys excrete them. 6-MAM in urine is specific for heroin but lasts only hours; morphine is detected for one to three days.",
  "sidefx": "The deadly effect is respiratory depression from mu receptor activation in the brainstem, leading to hypoxia, brain injury and cardiac arrest. Prolonged immobility while unconscious causes compartment syndrome and rhabdomyolysis. Tolerance drops quickly after abstinence, so overdose risk rises after jail, detox or a hospital stay. Fentanyl contamination greatly increases overdose risk. Naloxone, a mu receptor antagonist, reverses the effects and may need repeating, because its effect can wear off before the opioid's.",
  "fun": "The name Heroin is often said to come from the German word 'heroisch', meaning heroic, because early testers said it made them feel heroic.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"carfentanil": {
  "origin": "Carfentanil was synthesized in 1974 by a team at Janssen Pharmaceutica in Belgium, the company founded by Paul Janssen, whose chemists had made fentanyl in 1960. They were exploring fentanyl analogs and found carfentanil to be among the most potent opioids ever made, roughly 10,000 times morphine and 100 times fentanyl. It was later marketed for veterinary use as Wildnil to immobilize very large animals such as elephants, and it was never approved for humans. In the 2010s it began appearing in the illicit drug supply, causing waves of overdose deaths, including in Ohio in 2016. It is DEA Schedule II.",
  "timeline": [["1974", "Janssen Pharmaceutica chemists synthesize carfentanil."], ["1980s", "Carfentanil is used as a veterinary immobilizing agent for large animals."], ["2016", "Carfentanil causes clusters of overdose deaths in the US, including Ohio."]],
  "cell": "Carfentanil is a full agonist at the mu-opioid receptor with extremely high binding affinity and lipid solubility. It enters the brain rapidly and activates mu receptors at tiny concentrations. Like other mu agonists, it couples to Gi/o proteins, lowers cAMP, opens GIRK potassium channels and closes voltage-gated calcium channels, quieting neurons. In the brainstem pre-Botzinger complex and related centers, this stops the breathing rhythm and blunts the carbon dioxide response. In the cortex and arousal centers it causes coma. High potency means a microgram-level amount can activate enough receptors to cause apnea. Rapid high-level mu stimulation can also cause chest wall rigidity, which makes ventilation harder.",
  "body": [["Brainstem breathing centers", "Massive mu receptor activation causes rapid apnea, often within minutes."], ["Brain and arousal", "Deep sedation progresses quickly to coma."], ["Chest wall", "High-potency opioids can cause muscle rigidity of the chest wall, making breathing and ventilation difficult."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."], ["Brain (after hypoxia)", "Prolonged lack of oxygen during apnea causes hypoxic brain injury."]],
  "journey": "Carfentanil is very lipid soluble and is absorbed quickly through lungs, mucous membranes or veins, reaching the brain within seconds to minutes. It is metabolized in the liver mainly by CYP3A4. Its reported half-life is longer than fentanyl, about seven to eight hours, and its effects can outlast a dose of naloxone. It is excreted in urine and feces. Many standard urine drug screens and fentanyl immunoassays do not detect it.",
  "sidefx": "The main toxicity is respiratory arrest from overwhelming mu receptor activation, leading to hypoxic brain injury and cardiac arrest. Because its receptor binding is so strong, reversal often requires repeated naloxone doses, and its long duration means breathing can stop again after naloxone wears off. According to ACMT and AACT, incidental skin contact is unlikely to cause toxicity for responders. Naloxone is the antidote, alongside ventilation support.",
  "fun": "Analysis by UK scientists reported that the aerosol used by Russian forces to end the 2002 Moscow theater siege contained carfentanil and remifentanil.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "ACMT and AACT position statement on fentanyl and analog exposure for emergency responders", "DEA carfentanil warning 2016"]
},
"nitazenes": {
  "origin": "Nitazenes are 2-benzylbenzimidazole opioids first made in the late 1950s by chemists at the Swiss company CIBA, who were searching for new pain relievers. Compounds such as etonitazene and isotonitazene proved extremely potent, but they were never approved for medical use, partly because of severe respiratory depression. Etonitazene was placed in Schedule I in the US in the 1970s. Starting around 2019, isotonitazene appeared in the illicit drug supply in North America and Europe, followed by metonitazene, protonitazene and other analogs. They are chemically unrelated to fentanyl. Several are now Schedule I, with others under temporary scheduling.",
  "timeline": [["1950s", "CIBA chemists synthesize the benzimidazole opioids now called nitazenes."], ["1970s", "Etonitazene is placed in Schedule I in the US."], ["2019", "Isotonitazene appears in the illicit drug supply."], ["2020", "DEA temporarily schedules isotonitazene."]],
  "cell": "Nitazenes are potent full agonists at the mu-opioid receptor. Although chemically unrelated to fentanyl or morphine, they fit the mu receptor binding pocket and activate it strongly. Activation couples to Gi/o proteins, lowering cAMP, opening GIRK potassium channels and closing voltage-gated calcium channels. In brainstem respiratory centers this blunts the carbon dioxide response and slows breathing; in arousal centers it causes coma. Some analogs, such as etonitazene and N-pyrrolidino etonitazene, are many times more potent than fentanyl in lab tests. Because they are opioid receptor agonists, naloxone can reverse them, though repeated doses may be needed.",
  "body": [["Brainstem breathing centers", "Strong mu activation slows and stops breathing, the main cause of death."], ["Brain and arousal", "Mu activation in arousal centers causes sedation that progresses to coma."], ["Eyes", "Mu activation in the Edinger-Westphal nucleus causes pinpoint pupils."], ["Lungs", "Vomiting while sedated can lead to aspiration."]],
  "journey": "Nitazenes act within minutes when smoked, injected or swallowed, often hidden in fake pills or mixed with other opioids. They are lipid soluble and enter the brain quickly. They are metabolized in the liver, but human pharmacokinetics are not well studied, and duration varies and may be prolonged. They are not detected by fentanyl test strips or standard opioid urine screens and require specialized mass spectrometry testing.",
  "sidefx": "The main danger is respiratory depression and arrest from mu receptor activation, causing hypoxic brain injury and death. Because people often do not know they have taken a nitazene, and because some analogs are extremely potent, overdoses can be sudden and severe. Naloxone reverses the effect but may need repeated doses and prolonged observation.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA temporary scheduling order for isotonitazene 2020", "Ujvary I et al. ACS Chem Neurosci 2021 (nitazene review)"]
},
"xylazine": {
  "origin": "Xylazine was synthesized in 1962 by the German company Bayer as a possible blood pressure medicine for humans. Early human testing showed too much sedation and low blood pressure, so it was developed for animals instead. It was approved for veterinary use as Rompun and is widely used to sedate horses, cattle and other animals. It is not approved for humans. In the early 2000s it appeared in the illicit drug supply in Puerto Rico, and in the 2010s it spread in Philadelphia and other US cities, mixed into fentanyl as 'tranq'. In 2023 the White House Office of National Drug Control Policy declared fentanyl adulterated with xylazine an emerging threat.",
  "timeline": [["1962", "Bayer synthesizes xylazine as a possible antihypertensive."], ["1970s", "Xylazine comes into use as a veterinary sedative."], ["2000s", "Xylazine appears in illicit drug use in Puerto Rico."], ["2023", "ONDCP declares fentanyl adulterated with xylazine an emerging threat."]],
  "cell": "Xylazine is an agonist at alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, similar to clonidine and dexmedetomidine. Alpha-2 receptors are Gi-coupled. On presynaptic noradrenergic neurons, especially in the locus coeruleus of the brainstem, activation lowers cAMP and reduces norepinephrine release. Less norepinephrine means sedation, reduced sympathetic outflow, slow heart rate and low blood pressure. Alpha-2 activation on pancreatic beta cells suppresses insulin release, causing high blood sugar. Xylazine is not an opioid, so naloxone does not reverse its sedation. When combined with fentanyl, the sedative and breathing-suppressing effects add together.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Reduced norepinephrine release from the locus coeruleus causes deep, prolonged sedation."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Lower sympathetic tone causes slow heart rate and low blood pressure."], ["Breathing", "Xylazine alone mildly suppresses breathing, but with opioids the combined depression is dangerous."], ["Pancreas", "Alpha-2 activation on beta cells suppresses insulin and raises blood sugar."], ["Skin", "Repeated exposure causes severe necrotic wounds that can appear away from injection sites, possibly from local vasoconstriction and poor tissue blood flow."]],
  "journey": "Xylazine is usually injected, smoked or snorted with fentanyl. It acts within minutes and causes sedation lasting hours. It is metabolized by the liver, and human pharmacokinetics are not well defined. It is excreted by the kidneys. Standard drug screens do not detect it, though specific tests and test strips exist.",
  "sidefx": "Sedation, slow heart rate and low blood pressure all come from reduced norepinephrine signaling. The combination with fentanyl causes deeper, longer sedation and greater risk of respiratory arrest. Chronic use causes necrotic skin ulcers, abscesses and bone infections, thought to involve vasoconstriction and impaired wound healing. Withdrawal can cause anxiety, high blood pressure and restlessness from rebound sympathetic activity. There is no approved antidote in humans. Naloxone does not reverse xylazine but is still given because fentanyl is almost always present.",
  "fun": "Veterinarians reverse xylazine in animals with alpha-2 antagonists such as yohimbine or atipamezole, which are not approved for this use in humans.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "ONDCP emerging threat designation 2023", "FDA alert on xylazine in illicit drugs 2022"]
},
"lsd": {
  "origin": "LSD was first made in 1938 by Swiss chemist Albert Hofmann at the Sandoz laboratories in Basel. He was making derivatives of lysergic acid, a compound from ergot, a fungus (Claviceps purpurea) that infects rye, hoping to find a circulation or breathing stimulant. LSD-25 seemed uninteresting and was set aside. In April 1943 Hofmann made it again, accidentally absorbed a tiny amount and felt strange effects. On April 19, 1943, he deliberately took a small amount and experienced intense hallucinations while riding his bicycle home, a day now called Bicycle Day. Sandoz distributed LSD as Delysid for research in psychiatry from 1947. Recreational use grew in the 1960s, and it became Schedule I in 1970. It has no approved medical use, though it is studied in clinical trials.",
  "timeline": [["1938", "Albert Hofmann synthesizes LSD at Sandoz."], ["1943", "Hofmann discovers its effects, including on Bicycle Day, April 19."], ["1947", "Sandoz begins supplying LSD as Delysid for research."], ["1970", "LSD is placed in Schedule I."]],
  "cell": "LSD is an ergoline that binds many receptors, but its hallucinogenic effect comes mainly from partial agonism at the serotonin 5-HT2A receptor. 5-HT2A is a Gq-coupled receptor; activation turns on phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG, raising calcium and increasing glutamate release in layer V pyramidal neurons of the cortex. This disrupts normal filtering of sensory input through cortical and thalamic circuits, producing visual distortions, synesthesia and altered sense of self. Crystal structures of LSD bound to the closely related 5-HT2B receptor show it becomes trapped under a 'lid' of protein, which helps explain its long duration and potency at microgram amounts. It also acts at 5-HT1A and dopamine D2 receptors and causes mild sympathetic stimulation.",
  "body": [["Brain (perception)", "5-HT2A activation in the cortex alters perception, causing visual changes, synesthesia and time distortion."], ["Brain (mood and thinking)", "Emotional intensity can swing to panic or paranoia, and it may unmask psychosis in vulnerable people."], ["Eyes", "Sympathetic stimulation causes dilated pupils."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild increases in heart rate and blood pressure occur."], ["Body temperature", "Mild temperature rise is typical; severe hyperthermia is rare with LSD itself."]],
  "journey": "LSD is usually taken by mouth on blotter paper or as a liquid and is absorbed from the gut. Effects begin within 30 to 90 minutes, peak in two to four hours and last 8 to 12 hours. It is metabolized in the liver to inactive products such as 2-oxo-3-hydroxy-LSD. The half-life is about three to four hours, but effects last longer than that because of its tight receptor binding. It is excreted by the kidneys and is not detected on standard drug screens.",
  "sidefx": "Physical toxicity is low at common exposure. The main risks are behavioral: panic, paranoia and risky actions that cause injury. LSD can unmask psychosis in people with a predisposition. Some people have persistent perception changes (HPPD). Massive ingestions have caused hyperthermia, coma and bleeding problems. Blotter sold as LSD may contain NBOMe compounds, which are much more dangerous and can cause seizures and death. There is no antidote; a calm environment and benzodiazepines for severe agitation are standard supportive measures.",
  "fun": "LSD is active in such tiny amounts that a typical blotter dose weighs far less than a grain of salt.",
  "src": ["Hofmann A. LSD: My Problem Child 1980", "Wacker D et al. Cell 2017 (LSD-bound 5-HT2B structure)", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"psilocybin": {
  "origin": "Psilocybin is the active compound in Psilocybe and related mushrooms, used for centuries in religious ceremonies by Indigenous peoples of Mexico, such as the Mazatec. In 1955 American banker and amateur mycologist R. Gordon Wasson took part in a ceremony led by Mazatec healer Maria Sabina, and his 1957 Life magazine article brought the mushrooms to wide attention. In 1958 Albert Hofmann at Sandoz, the discoverer of LSD, isolated and identified psilocybin and psilocin from mushrooms grown from Wasson's samples. It became Schedule I in 1970. FDA gave breakthrough therapy designations in 2018 and 2019 for depression trials. It is not FDA approved. Oregon and Colorado now have state-regulated psilocybin programs.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Indigenous peoples in Mexico use psilocybin mushrooms in ceremonies."], ["1957", "R. Gordon Wasson's Life magazine article describes Mazatec mushroom ceremonies."], ["1958", "Albert Hofmann isolates psilocybin and psilocin."], ["1970", "Psilocybin is placed in Schedule I."], ["2018", "FDA grants breakthrough therapy designation for psilocybin in treatment-resistant depression."]],
  "cell": "Psilocybin is a prodrug. Alkaline phosphatase in the gut and liver removes its phosphate group to form psilocin, which is lipid soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier. Psilocin is a structural cousin of serotonin and acts as an agonist at the 5-HT2A receptor, a Gq-coupled receptor on cortical layer V pyramidal neurons. Activation raises intracellular calcium and glutamate release, disrupting cortical and thalamic filtering of sensory information. Brain imaging shows reduced activity and connectivity in the default mode network, linked to changes in sense of self. Psilocin also binds 5-HT1A and 5-HT2C receptors. Proposed antidepressant effects may involve increased neuroplasticity, but this is under study.",
  "body": [["Brain (perception and self)", "5-HT2A activation alters perception, emotions and sense of self for several hours."], ["Brain (mood)", "Studied for depression, with proposed increases in neuroplasticity; panic and anxiety can occur during effects."], ["Gut", "Nausea is common early, possibly from serotonin receptors in the gut and brainstem."], ["Eyes", "Pupils dilate from sympathetic stimulation."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild increases in heart rate and blood pressure occur."]],
  "journey": "Psilocybin is swallowed in dried mushrooms or edibles. It is absorbed from the gut and quickly converted to psilocin. Effects start within 20 to 40 minutes, peak in one to two hours and last four to six hours. Psilocin is mainly glucuronidated in the liver, with a half-life of about two to three hours. It is excreted by the kidneys and is not detected on standard drug screens.",
  "sidefx": "Physical toxicity is low. The main acute risks are panic, paranoia and dangerous behavior during altered perception. Wild-picked mushrooms may be misidentified toxic species, such as amatoxin mushrooms, which cause delayed liver failure. Some edible products sold as psilocybin have contained other substances; FDA warned in 2024 about Diamond Shruumz products. There is no antidote; a calm environment and benzodiazepines for severe agitation are standard supportive measures.",
  "fun": "Psilocybin mushrooms often bruise blue when handled, because psilocin breaks down into blue-colored compounds when exposed to air.",
  "src": ["Hofmann A et al. Experientia 1958", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "FDA warning on Diamond Shruumz products 2024"]
},
"pcp": {
  "origin": "Phencyclidine was synthesized in 1956 by chemist Victor Maddox at Parke-Davis in Detroit while searching for new pain relievers. Pharmacologists found it produced anesthesia without slowing breathing much, and it was tested in humans as Sernyl in the late 1950s. Many patients woke up with severe agitation, hallucinations and delirium, so human use was dropped in the 1960s, though it was used in veterinary medicine for a time as Sernylan. The search for a safer relative led to ketamine in 1962. PCP became a street drug in the late 1960s and is now Schedule II with no approved use.",
  "timeline": [["1956", "Victor Maddox synthesizes phencyclidine at Parke-Davis."], ["1950s", "PCP is tested in humans as the anesthetic Sernyl."], ["1960s", "Human use stops because of severe emergence delirium."], ["1962", "Ketamine is synthesized as a safer relative."], ["1978", "PCP is moved to Schedule II."]],
  "cell": "PCP is a noncompetitive antagonist of the NMDA glutamate receptor. It enters the open ion channel and binds the 'PCP site' inside the pore, blocking calcium and sodium flow even when glutamate is bound. Blocking NMDA receptors in thalamocortical and limbic circuits disconnects sensory input from conscious processing, producing dissociation, analgesia and a trance-like state. PCP also inhibits dopamine reuptake and acts at sigma receptors, which add stimulation, agitation and psychosis. It increases sympathetic output, raising heart rate and blood pressure. Nystagmus, especially vertical, comes from effects on brainstem eye movement control.",
  "body": [["Brain", "NMDA blockade causes dissociation, hallucinations and psychosis, with agitation and violence that can swing to catatonia or coma."], ["Pain perception", "Strong analgesia means people may not notice serious injuries."], ["Eyes", "Horizontal and vertical nystagmus are classic signs."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure, with risk of brain bleeding."], ["Muscles and temperature", "Agitation and rigidity cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis, which can injure the kidneys."]],
  "journey": "PCP is often smoked on cigarettes or marijuana dipped in liquid PCP, or swallowed. Smoked, it acts in two to five minutes; swallowed, in 30 to 60 minutes. It is very lipid soluble and stores in fat and the brain, which explains fluctuating effects that can last for days. The liver metabolizes it by CYP oxidation. Its half-life varies widely, from about 7 to 46 hours. It is excreted by the kidneys, more in acidic urine because of ion trapping, and is detected in urine for about a week.",
  "sidefx": "Violent, unpredictable behavior comes from NMDA blockade combined with dopamine effects, and insensitivity to pain makes injuries more likely. Rhabdomyolysis and hyperthermia come from agitation and muscle rigidity. Sympathetic stimulation can cause hypertensive emergencies. Patients may appear calm and then become violent. There is no antidote; benzodiazepines, cooling and protection from injury are standard supportive measures.",
  "fun": "Ketamine, now an essential anesthetic and depression treatment, was created as a shorter-acting cousin of PCP.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Domino EF. Anesthesiology 2010 (history of PCP and ketamine)", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"ghb": {
  "origin": "Gamma-hydroxybutyrate occurs naturally in small amounts in the brain. French physician and researcher Henri Laborit synthesized it in 1960 while studying GABA, aiming to make a GABA-like molecule that could cross into the brain. It was used in Europe as an anesthetic and later for alcohol withdrawal. In the US it was sold in the 1980s as a supplement for bodybuilding and sleep, until FDA banned these sales in 1990 after poisonings. Its link to overdoses and drug-facilitated sexual assault led to the Hillory J. Farias and Samantha Reid Date-Rape Drug Prohibition Act of 2000, which put illicit GHB in Schedule I. In 2002 FDA approved sodium oxybate (Xyrem) for narcolepsy, a Schedule III drug with a restricted distribution program. Xywav followed in 2020 and Lumryz in 2023.",
  "timeline": [["1960", "Henri Laborit synthesizes GHB."], ["1990", "FDA bans over-the-counter GHB sales after poisonings."], ["2000", "Federal law places illicit GHB in Schedule I."], ["2002", "FDA approves sodium oxybate (Xyrem) for narcolepsy."], ["2020", "FDA approves Xywav, a lower-sodium oxybate."]],
  "cell": "GHB acts mainly as a weak agonist at the GABA-B receptor, a Gi/o-coupled receptor. Activation opens GIRK potassium channels and closes calcium channels, hyperpolarizing neurons and reducing neurotransmitter release. This causes sedation, amnesia and, at higher levels, coma and slowed breathing. GHB also binds specific high-affinity GHB receptors, whose role is still being worked out. Because GABA-B activation depends steeply on concentration, the gap between sedation and coma is narrow, giving a steep dose-response curve. In narcolepsy, oxybate consolidates nighttime sleep and increases slow-wave sleep, which reduces daytime sleepiness and cataplexy. GBL and 1,4-butanediol are converted to GHB in the body by lactonases and alcohol dehydrogenase.",
  "body": [["Brain (arousal)", "GABA-B activation causes sedation and amnesia, progressing suddenly to coma at high levels."], ["Breathing", "Respiratory depression occurs, especially when combined with alcohol or other depressants."], ["Heart", "Slow heart rate is common in toxicity, reflecting central depression and increased vagal tone."], ["Body temperature", "Hypothermia can occur as central temperature regulation is depressed."], ["Muscles", "Myoclonic jerks can occur and may be mistaken for seizures."], ["Sleep (oxybate)", "In narcolepsy, more slow-wave sleep at night reduces daytime sleepiness and cataplexy."]],
  "journey": "GHB is absorbed quickly from the gut, with effects in 15 to 30 minutes and a peak in 30 to 60 minutes. It crosses into the brain easily. It is metabolized mainly by GHB dehydrogenase to succinic semialdehyde, which enters the Krebs cycle and is broken down to carbon dioxide and water. Less than 1 percent is protein bound. The half-life is short, about 30 to 60 minutes, so people often wake suddenly after two to four hours. It is detectable in blood for about six to eight hours and urine for about 12 hours.",
  "sidefx": "Coma and respiratory depression come from GABA-B activation, and the steep dose-response means a small extra amount can cause sudden unconsciousness. Alcohol greatly increases the risk. Vomiting while unconscious can lead to aspiration. Regular use causes dependence, and withdrawal can resemble severe alcohol withdrawal with delirium. The Xyrem label carries a boxed warning for CNS depression and abuse. According to the label, naloxone and flumazenil are not expected to reverse its effects, so care is supportive, with airway protection.",
  "src": ["Xyrem (sodium oxybate) FDA label", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"cannabis": {
  "origin": "Cannabis sativa has been used for fiber, food and medicine for thousands of years, with early records from China and Central Asia. It entered Western medicine in the 1840s through Irish physician William O'Shaughnessy, who had studied it in India. In the US, the Marihuana Tax Act of 1937 restricted it, and in 1970 marijuana became Schedule I. In 1964 Raphael Mechoulam and Yehiel Gaoni at the Weizmann Institute in Israel isolated and determined the structure of THC, the main psychoactive compound. The CB1 receptor was cloned in 1990, and Mechoulam's group identified anandamide, the body's own cannabinoid, in 1992. FDA approved dronabinol (Marinol) in 1985, nabilone (Cesamet), and cannabidiol (Epidiolex) in 2018. Plant cannabis itself is not FDA approved, though many states allow its use.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Cannabis is used in China and Central Asia for fiber and medicine."], ["1937", "The Marihuana Tax Act restricts cannabis in the US."], ["1964", "Mechoulam and Gaoni isolate and identify THC."], ["1985", "FDA approves dronabinol (Marinol)."], ["1990", "The CB1 cannabinoid receptor is cloned."], ["2018", "FDA approves Epidiolex (cannabidiol) for seizures."]],
  "cell": "THC is a partial agonist at CB1 and CB2 cannabinoid receptors, which are Gi/o-coupled receptors. CB1 is found on presynaptic nerve terminals throughout the brain, especially in the hippocampus, basal ganglia, cerebellum and cortex. When THC activates CB1, it lowers cAMP, closes calcium channels and opens potassium channels, reducing release of both GABA and glutamate. This retrograde signaling system normally fine-tunes synapses using endocannabinoids like anandamide and 2-AG. THC overrides it, causing euphoria, altered perception, impaired short-term memory (hippocampus) and poor coordination (cerebellum). CB1 in the hypothalamus increases appetite, and CB1 in the brainstem reduces nausea. CB2 on immune cells modulates inflammation. CBD does not activate CB1 the same way and has non-intoxicating mechanisms.",
  "body": [["Brain (mood and perception)", "CB1 activation causes euphoria and altered perception; high-potency products can trigger anxiety and psychosis."], ["Brain (memory and coordination)", "Hippocampal and cerebellar CB1 effects impair short-term memory, reaction time and coordination, making driving dangerous."], ["Appetite and nausea", "Hypothalamic and brainstem CB1 effects increase appetite and reduce nausea, the basis for dronabinol and nabilone."], ["Heart and vessels", "Heart rate rises and blood vessels dilate, causing red eyes and sometimes fainting on standing."], ["Gut", "Long heavy use can cause cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome with cycles of severe vomiting."], ["Lungs", "Smoking irritates airways, causing cough and bronchitis."]],
  "journey": "Inhaled THC is absorbed through the lungs and reaches the brain within minutes, with effects lasting two to four hours. Swallowed THC is absorbed slowly and heavily processed by the liver on first pass, with effects starting in 30 minutes to two hours and lasting six to eight hours or longer. The liver converts it by CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 to 11-hydroxy-THC, which is active, then to inactive THC-COOH. THC is about 97 percent protein bound and stores in fat, giving a long terminal half-life. Metabolites leave in feces and urine and can be detected for days to weeks in heavy users.",
  "sidefx": "Most side effects come from CB1 activation in the brain. Anxiety, panic and psychosis are more likely with high-potency products and in adolescents. Impaired coordination and reaction time explain driving accidents. Children who eat edibles can become deeply sedated or comatose because of their small size. Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome is linked to long heavy use. Delta-8 THC and other hemp-derived products are not FDA approved, and FDA has warned about poisonings. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive.",
  "fun": "The discovery of THC led scientists to find a whole endocannabinoid system in the body, named after the plant that revealed it.",
  "src": ["Marinol (dronabinol) FDA label", "Epidiolex (cannabidiol) FDA label", "Gaoni Y, Mechoulam R. J Am Chem Soc 1964", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"synthetic-cannabinoids": {
  "origin": "Many synthetic cannabinoids were first made by researchers studying the cannabinoid system. The best known, JWH-018, was created in 1995 by organic chemist John W. Huffman at Clemson University, whose lab made hundreds of compounds (the JWH series) to study CB1 and CB2 receptors. They were never intended for human use. Around 2008, forensic chemists in Europe found JWH-018 sprayed on herbal products sold as 'Spice'. Products called K2 and Spice spread in the US, and the DEA used emergency scheduling for several compounds in 2011, followed by the Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act of 2012. Manufacturers keep changing structures to stay ahead of laws. A 2018 outbreak in Illinois involved products contaminated with brodifacoum, a rat poison, causing severe bleeding. They have no approved medical use.",
  "timeline": [["1995", "John W. Huffman's lab at Clemson synthesizes JWH-018."], ["2008", "JWH-018 is found in Spice herbal products in Europe."], ["2011", "The DEA emergency schedules several synthetic cannabinoids."], ["2012", "The Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act places many in Schedule I."], ["2018", "An Illinois outbreak links synthetic cannabinoids to brodifacoum poisoning."]],
  "cell": "Unlike THC, which is a partial agonist, most synthetic cannabinoids are full agonists at the CB1 receptor, often with much higher potency. Full activation of Gi/o-coupled CB1 strongly lowers cAMP, closes calcium channels and suppresses release of GABA and glutamate across many brain circuits. Because a partial agonist has a ceiling, THC rarely causes severe toxicity, but full agonists can drive receptor signaling far beyond what THC can reach. Excess suppression of GABA release in some circuits may cause seizures. Some metabolites stay active at CB1, which prolongs effects. Many compounds also hit other targets, and new analogs have unknown pharmacology.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Full CB1 activation causes severe agitation, psychosis and seizures, much more often than THC."], ["Heart", "Fast heart rate, high blood pressure, chest pain and heart attack have been reported."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury has occurred in outbreaks, through uncertain mechanisms."], ["Gut", "Severe vomiting is common and can lead to dehydration and electrolyte problems."], ["Blood clotting", "Brodifacoum contamination in 2018 blocked vitamin K recycling and caused severe bleeding."]],
  "journey": "Synthetic cannabinoids are usually smoked from plant material or paper, so they reach the brain within minutes. Effects last hours, and duration varies by compound. They are metabolized in the liver, and some metabolites remain active at CB1. They are not detected by standard THC drug screens, and testing requires specialized methods.",
  "sidefx": "Severe toxicity comes from full CB1 activation without the ceiling seen with THC. This explains seizures, psychosis and dangerous agitation. Cardiac effects include tachycardia, heart attack and stroke. Acute kidney injury and mass outbreaks occur because products vary and contain unknown compounds. In the 2018 brodifacoum outbreak, vitamin K was used to reverse the bleeding. Otherwise there is no antidote; benzodiazepines for agitation and seizures and supportive care are standard.",
  "fun": "John Huffman publicly warned that his research compounds were never meant for people and that their effects in humans were unknown.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA emergency scheduling order 2011", "Kelkar AH et al. NEJM 2018 (brodifacoum outbreak)"]
},
"synthetic-cathinones": {
  "origin": "Synthetic cathinones are lab-made relatives of cathinone, the stimulant in khat (Catha edulis), a shrub whose leaves are chewed in East Africa and the Arabian Peninsula. Some were first made by pharmaceutical researchers: mephedrone was described in 1929, and MDPV was developed by Boehringer Ingelheim in the 1960s, but neither became a medicine. Around 2010 and 2011, products sold as 'bath salts' or 'plant food' and labeled not for human consumption spread in the US, causing severe poisonings. The DEA used emergency scheduling for mephedrone, MDPV and methylone in 2011, and the Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act of 2012 made many Schedule I. New analogs such as alpha-PVP ('flakka') and eutylone followed. They have no approved medical use.",
  "timeline": [["1929", "Mephedrone is first synthesized."], ["1960s", "Boehringer Ingelheim develops MDPV."], ["2011", "The DEA emergency schedules mephedrone, MDPV and methylone."], ["2012", "The Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act places many in Schedule I."]],
  "cell": "Synthetic cathinones are beta-keto amphetamines, and they act on monoamine transporters in two different ways. Pyrovalerones such as MDPV and alpha-PVP are potent blockers of the dopamine transporter (DAT) and norepinephrine transporter (NET), like cocaine but stronger and longer lasting, with little serotonin effect. Ring-substituted cathinones such as mephedrone and methylone are transporter substrates that are carried into the neuron and cause release of dopamine, norepinephrine and serotonin, similar to MDMA. Either way, synaptic monoamines rise sharply. Dopamine excess drives intense stimulation, paranoia and compulsive redosing. Norepinephrine drives tachycardia, hypertension and hyperthermia. Serotonin effects with releasers can cause hyponatremia and serotonin toxicity.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Dopamine surges cause intense stimulation, paranoia, hallucinations and agitated delirium, and psychosis can last for days."], ["Heart and vessels", "Norepinephrine excess causes tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest."], ["Temperature and muscles", "Extreme agitation and heat generation cause hyperthermia and rhabdomyolysis."], ["Kidneys", "Muscle breakdown products and low blood flow cause acute kidney injury."], ["Sodium balance", "Releaser-type cathinones, like MDMA, can cause hyponatremia."]],
  "journey": "Synthetic cathinones are snorted, swallowed, smoked or injected, and act within minutes. Effects peak within about 30 to 90 minutes and last several hours, though psychosis may persist for days. They are metabolized in the liver, and pharmacokinetics vary by compound. They are often not detected on routine drug screens and require specialized testing.",
  "sidefx": "Toxicity comes from excess dopamine and norepinephrine. Agitated delirium with hyperthermia can lead to rhabdomyolysis, kidney failure and multiorgan failure. Sudden death can occur, especially during struggle or restraint, likely from catecholamine-driven arrhythmias and acidosis. Long psychosis reflects prolonged dopamine effects. There is no antidote; benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care are standard measures.",
  "fun": "The 'bath salts' name was a marketing trick: the products had nothing to do with bathing and were labeled that way to avoid drug laws.",
  "src": ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA emergency scheduling order 2011", "Baumann MH et al. Neuropsychopharmacology 2013"]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"kratom": {
  "origin": "Kratom is the leaf of Mitragyna speciosa, a tree in the coffee family that grows in Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia and Papua New Guinea. For generations farm and dock workers in Southeast Asia chewed the fresh leaves or brewed them as tea to fight fatigue in the heat. The Dutch botanist Pieter Korthals named the genus in the 1800s because the flower parts reminded him of a bishop's mitre. The main alkaloid, mitragynine, was isolated in the early 20th century. Thailand outlawed kratom in 1943 and only relaxed that law in 2021. Kratom spread to the US through online and gas station sales in the 2000s and 2010s. FDA states it is not a lawful dietary supplement ingredient and it has no FDA-approved use. Later research showed that a minor alkaloid, 7-hydroxymitragynine (7-OH), is far stronger at opioid receptors, and concentrated 7-OH products led FDA to recommend scheduling 7-OH in 2025.",
  "timeline": [["1943", "Thailand passes a law banning kratom."], ["2018", "A multistate Salmonella outbreak is linked to kratom products in the US."], ["2021", "Thailand removes kratom from its narcotics list."], ["2025", "FDA recommends federal scheduling of concentrated 7-OH products."]],
  "cell": "Mitragynine is an indole alkaloid that acts as a partial agonist at mu-opioid receptors. These are Gi/o-coupled receptors. Binding lowers cyclic AMP, opens GIRK potassium channels and closes voltage-gated calcium channels, so neurons fire less and release less transmitter. In pain pathways that dampens pain signals, and in the brainstem it slows breathing. Mitragynine is described as G protein biased, recruiting beta-arrestin less than morphine in laboratory studies, but human safety has not been proven. In the liver CYP3A4 converts some mitragynine to 7-hydroxymitragynine, a much more potent mu agonist that likely drives much of the opioid effect. Mitragynine also acts on alpha-adrenergic and serotonin receptors, which helps explain the stimulant feel at lower exposure and the sedating, opioid-like effects at higher exposure.",
  "body": [["Brain and mood", "Lower exposure can feel energizing from adrenergic effects. Higher exposure brings opioid-like calm, euphoria and sedation through mu receptors in reward and arousal circuits."], ["Breathing", "Mu-receptor activity in brainstem breathing centers can slow respiration. Most reported deaths involved kratom taken with other opioids, sedatives or alcohol."], ["Pupils", "Opioid activity on the Edinger-Westphal nucleus produces small pupils."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut wall slow movement, causing constipation, nausea and appetite loss with weight loss."], ["Liver", "Cholestatic liver injury has been reported, likely an idiosyncratic reaction to the alkaloids or contaminants."], ["Nervous system", "Seizures are reported, especially with high exposure or co-ingestants, and regular use leads to dependence with opioid-like withdrawal."]],
  "journey": "Kratom is swallowed as leaf powder, capsules, tea, extracts or tablets. Effects begin within about half an hour and peak in one to two hours. The alkaloids are fat soluble and spread widely into the brain and tissues. The liver breaks mitragynine down mainly through CYP3A4, which also makes the more potent 7-OH. Kratom can slow the metabolism of other drugs that use CYP3A4 and CYP2D6. Metabolites leave through the urine. Mitragynine has a long and variable half-life, roughly a day in some studies. Standard drug screens do not detect it, so specific testing is needed. Product strength varies widely because these are unregulated plant products.",
  "sidefx": "Constipation, nausea, small pupils, sedation and slowed breathing all follow from mu-opioid activation, and repeated exposure causes receptor adaptation, so stopping brings opioid-like withdrawal such as aches, runny nose, irritability and cravings. Stimulant effects from adrenergic receptors explain insomnia and fast heart rate. Liver injury and seizures are less well understood. Contamination is a separate danger: products have carried Salmonella and heavy metals. Babies born to people who used kratom can have neonatal withdrawal. Naloxone can reverse opioid-type breathing depression in case reports, but care is mostly supportive.",
  "fun": "Kratom is a cousin of the coffee plant: both belong to the family Rubiaceae, yet their main effects come from very different chemicals.",
  "src": ["FDA statements on kratom and 7-hydroxymitragynine", "NIDA Kratom DrugFacts", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"dmt": {
  "origin": "N,N-dimethyltryptamine (DMT) occurs naturally in many plants and in small amounts in animals. Indigenous peoples of the Amazon have long brewed ayahuasca from the Banisteriopsis caapi vine and DMT-containing leaves such as Psychotria viridis. The vine contains harmala alkaloids that block the enzyme MAO-A, which is what lets swallowed DMT reach the brain. The Canadian chemist Richard Manske first synthesized DMT in 1931, without knowing it was psychoactive. In 1956 the Hungarian chemist and psychiatrist Stephen Szara injected himself with DMT and described its intense, brief hallucinations. DMT was placed in Schedule I under the 1970 Controlled Substances Act. In the 1990s Rick Strassman at the University of New Mexico ran the first new US human DMT studies in decades. In 2006 the US Supreme Court ruled that a church could use ayahuasca tea in ceremonies. DMT has no FDA-approved use, though it is under study for depression.",
  "timeline": [["1931", "Richard Manske first synthesizes DMT."], ["1956", "Stephen Szara reports the hallucinogenic effects of DMT in himself."], ["1970", "DMT becomes a Schedule I drug under the Controlled Substances Act."], ["1990s", "Rick Strassman conducts DMT studies in volunteers at the University of New Mexico."], ["2006", "The US Supreme Court allows ceremonial ayahuasca use by the UDV church."]],
  "cell": "DMT is a tryptamine shaped much like serotonin. Its key action is agonism at serotonin 5-HT2A receptors, which are Gq-coupled receptors dense on layer V pyramidal neurons of the cerebral cortex. Activation turns on phospholipase C, which makes IP3 and DAG, raising calcium and activating protein kinase C. This boosts glutamate release and cortical excitability and disrupts normal filtering of sensory information through thalamus and cortex loops, producing vivid visual and dissociative experiences. DMT also acts at 5-HT1A and 5-HT2C receptors, sigma-1 receptors and trace amine-associated receptors. MAO-A in the gut, liver and brain breaks DMT down within minutes. In ayahuasca, harmine and harmaline reversibly inhibit MAO-A, so DMT survives first-pass metabolism, and those MAO inhibitors raise serotonin and norepinephrine too.",
  "body": [["Brain", "5-HT2A activation in the cortex causes intense visual hallucinations, altered sense of self and time, and sometimes panic or lasting psychological distress."], ["Heart and vessels", "Sympathetic activation raises heart rate and blood pressure during the experience."], ["Eyes", "Pupils dilate from increased sympathetic tone."], ["Gut (ayahuasca)", "Vomiting and diarrhea are common with ayahuasca, partly from serotonin effects on the gut and the bitter plant brew."], ["Serotonin system", "With ayahuasca the MAO-inhibiting harmala alkaloids raise serotonin, so adding SSRIs, SNRIs, tramadol or linezolid can cause serotonin syndrome."], ["Breathing (5-MeO-DMT)", "The related 5-MeO-DMT has caused apnea and seizures in reports."]],
  "journey": "Smoked or vaped DMT reaches the brain in seconds, peaks in minutes and fades within about half an hour, because MAO-A destroys it so quickly. Swallowed alone, DMT is almost entirely broken down by MAO-A in the gut and liver before it reaches the blood. In ayahuasca the harmala alkaloids block that enzyme, so effects start in 30 to 60 minutes and last several hours. The main breakdown product is indole-3-acetic acid, which leaves in the urine. DMT does not show up on standard drug screens.",
  "sidefx": "Fast heart rate, high blood pressure and dilated pupils reflect serotonin and sympathetic activation. Panic and psychosis-like reactions come from intense 5-HT2A-driven changes in perception, and people can be injured while disoriented. The most dangerous drug interactions come from the MAO inhibitors in ayahuasca: they block the breakdown of serotonin and of tyramine and other amines, so serotonergic medicines can trigger serotonin syndrome and stimulants or tyramine-rich foods can trigger hypertensive reactions. There is no specific antidote; care is supportive, with benzodiazepines for agitation and standard serotonin syndrome management.",
  "fun": "Swallowed DMT alone does almost nothing because gut MAO-A destroys it. Ayahuasca works only because the vine in the brew happens to contain natural MAO inhibitors.",
  "src": ["Szara S. 1956 report on DMT effects", "Strassman RJ et al. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1994", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA Drug Fact Sheet: DMT"]
},
"mescaline": {
  "origin": "Mescaline is the main psychoactive alkaloid of peyote (Lophophora williamsii), a small spineless cactus of the Chihuahuan Desert in southern Texas and northern Mexico. It is also found in San Pedro and Peruvian torch cacti of the Andes. Peyote has been used in Indigenous ceremonies for thousands of years; dried peyote buttons from caves in the lower Pecos region of Texas have been dated to more than 5,000 years old. In 1897 the German chemist Arthur Heffter isolated mescaline from peyote and tested it on himself to show it was the active compound. Ernst Spath synthesized it in 1919, making it one of the first hallucinogens made in a lab. Aldous Huxley's 1954 book The Doors of Perception described his mescaline experience and made it famous. It became Schedule I in 1970, with a federal religious exemption for peyote in Native American Church ceremonies. It has no FDA-approved use.",
  "timeline": [["Ancient", "Peyote is used in Indigenous ceremonies in what is now Texas and Mexico."], ["1897", "Arthur Heffter isolates mescaline and confirms its effects in himself."], ["1919", "Ernst Spath achieves the first chemical synthesis of mescaline."], ["1954", "Aldous Huxley publishes The Doors of Perception."], ["1970", "Mescaline and peyote are placed in Schedule I."], ["1994", "Federal law protects ceremonial peyote use by members of Native American tribes."]],
  "cell": "Mescaline is 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenethylamine, a phenethylamine related in shape to dopamine, norepinephrine and amphetamine. Its hallucinogenic action comes from agonism at serotonin 5-HT2A receptors, with additional 5-HT2C activity. 5-HT2A receptors are Gq-coupled: activation stimulates phospholipase C, producing IP3 and DAG, which raise intracellular calcium and activate protein kinase C in cortical pyramidal neurons. This increases glutamate signaling in the prefrontal cortex and loosens normal sensory gating, producing visual changes, altered thinking and emotional shifts. Mescaline is a low-potency agonist compared with LSD, so much more drug is needed to fill the receptors. Its amphetamine-like structure contributes mild sympathetic stimulation.",
  "body": [["Brain", "5-HT2A activation brings vivid color and pattern changes, altered time sense and strong emotions for many hours. Panic or psychosis-like reactions can occur."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are very common early on, from serotonin receptor effects and the bitter plant material."], ["Heart and vessels", "Mild sympathetic stimulation raises heart rate and blood pressure."], ["Eyes and skin", "Pupils dilate and people may sweat or flush from sympathetic effects."], ["Body as a whole", "Physical toxicity is generally low, but the long duration raises the chance of accidents, trauma and dehydration."]],
  "journey": "Mescaline is swallowed as dried cactus, tea or powder. It is water soluble and absorbed fairly slowly, so effects begin in one to two hours and peak a few hours later. Because it is polar, it crosses into the brain less easily than many drugs, which partly explains why so much is needed. It lasts a long time, often eight to twelve hours. Some is broken down by oxidative deamination to 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenylacetic acid, but much leaves unchanged in the urine. The half-life is about six hours. Standard drug screens do not detect it.",
  "sidefx": "Nausea and vomiting come from serotonin receptor stimulation in the gut and brainstem. Fast heart rate, high blood pressure, sweating and wide pupils come from its amphetamine-like sympathetic effects. Psychological harms such as panic, paranoia and risky behavior stem from strong 5-HT2A-driven changes in perception that last many hours. Combining it with stimulants adds to the sympathetic load, and combining it with serotonergic drugs may raise serotonin toxicity risk. Peyote can be confused with other cacti or plants. There is no antidote; care is supportive, with a calm setting and benzodiazepines for severe agitation.",
  "fun": "Mescaline was named after the Mescalero Apache people, and it was one of the first hallucinogens ever made in a laboratory.",
  "src": ["Heffter A. 1898 report on peyote alkaloids", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "DEA Drug Fact Sheet: Peyote and Mescaline"]
},
"inhalants": {
  "origin": "Inhalants are not one drug but many household and industrial chemicals breathed in for a quick high. Some have long medical histories. Joseph Priestley made nitrous oxide in 1772, and Humphry Davy described its euphoric 'laughing gas' effect in 1800. The dentist Horace Wells tried it for tooth extraction in 1844, and medical nitrous oxide is still an approved anesthetic gas. Amyl nitrite was first made in the 1840s, and in 1867 Thomas Lauder Brunton used it to relieve angina; its glass ampules made a popping sound when crushed, the source of the name 'poppers'. Solvent sniffing of glue, gasoline and paint thinner drew public attention in the US in the late 1950s and 1960s. In 1970 Millard Bass described 'sudden sniffing death,' cardiac death in young people who inhaled volatile hydrocarbons. Today the most common products include aerosol dusters, butane, nitrous oxide 'whippets' and alkyl nitrites. None is FDA approved for nonmedical use.",
  "timeline": [["1772", "Joseph Priestley prepares nitrous oxide."], ["1800", "Humphry Davy publishes his studies of laughing gas."], ["1867", "Thomas Lauder Brunton uses amyl nitrite to relieve angina."], ["1960s", "Glue and solvent sniffing becomes a recognized public health problem in the US."], ["1970", "Millard Bass describes sudden sniffing death in JAMA."]],
  "cell": "Volatile solvents (toluene, gasoline) and propellant gases (butane, propane, difluoroethane) are small fat-soluble molecules that dissolve into nerve cell membranes and act like general anesthetics. They enhance GABA-A and glycine receptor chloride currents, which hyperpolarizes neurons, and they block NMDA glutamate receptors, reducing excitation. The result is quick euphoria, then dizziness, slurred speech and loss of coordination. Halogenated hydrocarbons also sensitize the heart muscle to catecholamines, so a surge of adrenaline can set off ventricular fibrillation. Nitrous oxide is an NMDA antagonist and also irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt atom in vitamin B12, which inactivates methionine synthase and blocks myelin upkeep. Alkyl nitrites release nitric oxide, which activates guanylate cyclase, raises cyclic GMP and relaxes vascular smooth muscle; they also oxidize hemoglobin iron to the ferric state, forming methemoglobin.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Anesthetic-like depression brings brief euphoria, then confusion, slurred speech and unsteady walking. Long-term toluene use damages white matter (leukoencephalopathy) with memory loss and ataxia."], ["Heart", "Solvents and gases make the heart overly sensitive to adrenaline. A startle, chase or exertion can trigger fatal ventricular fibrillation, called sudden sniffing death."], ["Lungs and airway", "Rebreathing from a bag lowers oxygen and can cause asphyxia. Spraying cold propellant can freeze the throat, and vomit can be aspirated."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Toluene causes distal renal tubular acidosis, which can drop potassium to dangerously low levels and cause severe weakness."], ["Nerves and spinal cord", "Repeated nitrous oxide inactivates vitamin B12, causing numbness, weakness and a spinal cord disorder called subacute combined degeneration."], ["Blood", "Nitrites turn hemoglobin into methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen, so the skin looks gray-blue and people become short of breath."], ["Blood vessels", "Nitrites widen vessels, causing flushing, headache and low blood pressure, which can be severe with PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil."]],
  "journey": "Inhalants enter through the lungs, so they reach the brain within seconds. Because they are very fat soluble they pass easily into the brain and then redistribute to body fat, so a single high fades within minutes, which leads to repeated inhaling. Most gases, such as butane and nitrous oxide, leave mostly unchanged in exhaled breath. Toluene is partly exhaled and partly broken down in the liver to hippuric acid, which leaves in the urine. Nitrites are quickly broken down in the blood. Standard drug screens do not detect inhalants.",
  "sidefx": "The anesthetic action on brain membranes explains intoxication and, with heavy exposure, coma. Cardiac sensitization to catecholamines explains sudden sniffing death, which is why a person who is intoxicated should not be startled or chased. Hypoxia from bags and frostbite from cold propellants are physical injuries. Toluene's damage to kidney tubule acid handling explains low potassium and acidosis. Nitrous oxide's oxidation of B12 explains nerve and spinal cord damage, treated with B12 replacement. Nitrite-induced methemoglobinemia can be reversed with methylene blue, which helps convert methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin.",
  "fun": "The name 'poppers' comes from the original glass ampules of amyl nitrite, which made a pop when crushed in a cloth to be sniffed for angina.",
  "src": ["Bass M. Sudden sniffing death. JAMA 1970", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "NIDA Inhalants Research Report", "FDA warnings on nitrous oxide and nitrite products"]
},
"designer-benzodiazepines": {
  "origin": "The benzodiazepine story began at Hoffmann-La Roche, where chemist Leo Sternbach found chlordiazepoxide in 1955; it was marketed as Librium in 1960, followed by diazepam (Valium) in 1963. Drug companies went on to make thousands of related molecules, and many were described in patents and papers but never sold. Designer benzodiazepines are mostly these shelved or slightly altered compounds. Etizolam, a thienodiazepine, was developed in Japan and approved there in the 1980s, but never in the US. Starting in the early 2010s, compounds such as flubromazolam, clonazolam and flualprazolam appeared online as 'research chemicals.' Bromazolam, first described decades earlier but never marketed, became one of the most common in the early 2020s. These drugs are now often pressed into counterfeit pills or mixed with fentanyl ('benzo dope'). In 2023 DEA temporarily placed several of them in Schedule I. None is FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["1955", "Leo Sternbach discovers chlordiazepoxide at Hoffmann-La Roche."], ["1963", "Diazepam is marketed as Valium."], ["1980s", "Etizolam is approved in Japan; it is never approved in the US."], ["2010s", "Unapproved benzodiazepines are sold online as research chemicals."], ["2023", "DEA temporarily schedules etizolam, flualprazolam, clonazolam, flubromazolam and diclazepam."]],
  "cell": "Designer benzodiazepines bind the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, a pocket at the interface between an alpha subunit (alpha1, 2, 3 or 5) and the gamma2 subunit. They are positive allosteric modulators: they do not open the channel themselves but make GABA more effective, increasing how often the chloride channel opens. More chloride flows into the neuron, hyperpolarizing it and making it harder to fire. Alpha1-containing receptors mediate sedation and amnesia, alpha2 and alpha3 anxiety relief and muscle relaxation, and alpha5 memory effects. Many designer compounds carry a triazole ring and halogen atoms (for example bromazolam is a bromine version of alprazolam), which raise binding strength, so tiny amounts are potent. Because they only boost GABA, alone they have a ceiling, but with opioids, alcohol or GHB the depression of brainstem breathing becomes additive.",
  "body": [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition causes sedation, slurred speech, poor coordination and memory blackouts. Disinhibition can lead to risky behavior."], ["Breathing", "With opioids, alcohol or GHB the combined depression of the brainstem can stop breathing. Benzodiazepines can also keep people sedated after naloxone reverses the opioid part."], ["Muscles", "Spinal GABA effects relax muscles and impair balance, raising the risk of falls and aspiration."], ["Nervous system with regular use", "GABA-A receptors adapt to constant stimulation. Stopping suddenly unmasks overexcitement, with anxiety, tremor, insomnia, hallucinations and seizures."], ["Memory", "Alpha5 and alpha1 receptor effects in the hippocampus block forming new memories, so people may not recall events."]],
  "journey": "These drugs are swallowed as pressed pills, powders or blotters, sometimes snorted, and often taken unknowingly in fentanyl. They are fat soluble, are absorbed within about an hour and spread readily into the brain. Most are broken down in the liver, often by CYP3A4, and some have active metabolites. Half-lives vary widely, from a few hours to longer than a day, so sedation can last much longer than the person expects. Metabolites leave in the urine. Standard benzodiazepine immunoassays often miss them, so a negative screen does not rule them out.",
  "sidefx": "Sedation, amnesia and loss of coordination come directly from boosted GABA-A inhibition. The real danger is synergy: opioids reduce the drive to breathe while benzodiazepines blunt the brain's response, so together they cause apnea and death. Long-acting compounds explain prolonged sedation in emergency departments. Tolerance and receptor downregulation explain severe withdrawal, which can include seizures. Counterfeit pills may contain fentanyl, so naloxone is still given for possible opioid exposure, though it does not reverse the benzodiazepine. Flumazenil blocks the benzodiazepine site but can provoke seizures in dependent people, so care is mostly supportive.",
  "fun": "Many designer benzodiazepines were first described decades ago in drug company patents and papers, then sat unused until online sellers revived them.",
  "src": ["DEA temporary scheduling order for designer benzodiazepines, 2023", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"tianeptine": {
  "origin": "Tianeptine was developed by the French drug company Servier and introduced in France in the late 1980s as an antidepressant under the brand name Stablon. It is a tricyclic compound in shape, but it acts quite differently from classic tricyclic antidepressants. It was approved in several European, Asian and Latin American countries but never in the US. For years it was thought to work by speeding up serotonin reuptake, the opposite of SSRIs. In 2014 researchers at Columbia University reported that tianeptine is actually a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors, which explained reports of misuse and dependence. In the US it began to be sold online and in gas stations as a 'supplement,' earning the nickname 'gas station heroin.' FDA has warned repeatedly since 2018 that it is not lawful in dietary supplements and has caused serious harm, and several states have banned it.",
  "timeline": [["1980s", "Servier introduces tianeptine in France as the antidepressant Stablon."], ["2014", "Columbia University researchers show tianeptine is a mu-opioid receptor agonist."], ["2018", "FDA issues its first warnings about tianeptine sold as a supplement."], ["2024", "FDA warns that Neptune's Fix products contain synthetic cannabinoids and other substances."]],
  "cell": "At the high exposures seen with misuse, tianeptine acts as a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors and a weaker agonist at delta-opioid receptors. Mu receptors are Gi/o-coupled: activation lowers cyclic AMP, opens GIRK potassium channels and closes presynaptic calcium channels, reducing neuron firing and transmitter release. In the ventral tegmental area this disinhibits dopamine neurons, producing euphoria and reinforcing repeated use. In the brainstem it depresses the breathing centers. Tianeptine and its active metabolite MC5 also modulate glutamate signaling through AMPA and NMDA receptors, which may underlie its proposed antidepressant effects. The short half-life means effects wear off quickly, driving frequent redosing and rapid dependence.",
  "body": [["Brain and reward", "Mu-opioid activation produces euphoria and sedation, and repeated exposure leads to tolerance, craving and dependence."], ["Breathing", "Brainstem mu activation slows breathing. Overdose can cause coma and respiratory arrest, especially with other depressants."], ["Pupils", "Opioid activity constricts the pupils, part of the classic opioid toxidrome."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut slow movement, causing nausea and constipation."], ["Heart and vessels", "Agitation, fast heart rate and high blood pressure are also reported, especially during withdrawal or with adulterants."]],
  "journey": "Tianeptine is swallowed as pills, powders or liquid shots. It is absorbed quickly, with effects in under an hour and peak levels in about an hour. It is highly bound to plasma proteins. Unlike most drugs, it is broken down mainly by beta-oxidation of its side chain, not by CYP enzymes, forming the active metabolite MC5, which lasts longer than the parent drug. The parent half-life is short, a few hours, and longer in older adults and people with kidney disease. Metabolites leave mainly in the urine. Standard opioid drug screens do not detect it.",
  "sidefx": "Sedation, small pupils, constipation and slowed breathing all come from mu-opioid activation. Tolerance and dependence develop quickly because of short-acting, frequent exposure, and stopping causes opioid-like withdrawal with anxiety, sweating, diarrhea and cravings. Naloxone, the mu-opioid antagonist, has reversed tianeptine overdose in case reports. Some products have been adulterated with other drugs, including synthetic cannabinoids, which can cause agitation, seizures or unexpected toxicity. Combining it with opioids, alcohol or benzodiazepines adds to respiratory depression.",
  "fun": "For decades tianeptine was taught as a drug that sped up serotonin reuptake, and only in 2014 did researchers show its strongest action is at the same receptor morphine uses.",
  "src": ["Gassaway MM et al. Transl Psychiatry 2014", "FDA warnings on tianeptine products 2018-2024", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"phenibut": {
  "origin": "Phenibut (beta-phenyl-gamma-aminobutyric acid) was made in the Soviet Union in the 1960s by chemists in Leningrad (now St. Petersburg). The idea was simple: GABA itself does not cross well from blood into the brain, so adding a phenyl ring might help it get in. The result became a prescription drug in Russia and nearby countries for anxiety, insomnia and other conditions, sold under names such as Noofen and Anvifen. It is a close relative of baclofen, which has an extra chlorine atom on the ring. Phenibut is often said to have been included in Soviet cosmonaut medical kits, though that story is hard to verify. In the 2010s it began to be sold online in the US as a nootropic or supplement, and poison centers saw rising calls for sedation and withdrawal. FDA states it is not a lawful dietary ingredient, and it has never been FDA approved.",
  "timeline": [["1960s", "Phenibut is synthesized in the Soviet Union as a brain-penetrating GABA analog."], ["2010s", "Online sales grow in the US and poison center reports of phenibut toxicity rise."], ["2019", "FDA issues warning letters stating phenibut is not a lawful dietary ingredient."]],
  "cell": "Phenibut has two main targets. First, it is an agonist at GABA-B receptors, the same receptors baclofen activates; mainly the R-enantiomer is active here. GABA-B receptors are Gi/o-coupled metabotropic receptors. Presynaptically they close voltage-gated calcium channels and reduce transmitter release; postsynaptically they open GIRK potassium channels, causing slow, lasting hyperpolarization. Second, both enantiomers bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, like gabapentin and pregabalin, which reduces calcium entry at excitatory nerve terminals and lowers glutamate release. Together these effects calm neural activity, causing relaxation and sedation. With repeated use, GABA-B and calcium channel systems adapt, so stopping leaves the brain in an overexcited state.",
  "body": [["Brain", "GABA-B and alpha-2-delta effects calm neural firing, causing relaxation and drowsiness. In overdose, consciousness can swing between deep sedation and agitation or delirium."], ["Breathing", "Alone it rarely stops breathing, but with alcohol, benzodiazepines or opioids the combined depression can be dangerous."], ["Muscles", "GABA-B activity in the spinal cord relaxes muscle tone, similar to baclofen."], ["Nervous system with regular use", "Tolerance develops quickly. Stopping can cause severe anxiety, insomnia, tremor, hallucinations, psychosis and seizures."], ["Gut", "Nausea is common, and GABA-B receptors in the gut and brainstem may contribute to it."]],
  "journey": "Phenibut is swallowed as powder or capsules. Its effects start slowly, often over a few hours, which can lead people to take more before the first amount takes hold. The phenyl ring helps it cross into the brain better than GABA. It undergoes little metabolism and leaves the body mostly unchanged in the urine, so kidney function affects how long it lasts. The half-life is about five hours, but effects can last many hours. Standard drug screens do not detect it.",
  "sidefx": "Drowsiness, dizziness and confusion come from enhanced GABA-B inhibition and reduced excitatory transmitter release. The alternating agitation and lethargy seen in overdose likely reflect complex GABA-B effects on different brain circuits, similar to baclofen toxicity. Tolerance results from receptor and channel adaptation, which is why withdrawal causes rebound overexcitement with anxiety, psychosis and seizures. There is no specific antidote. Withdrawal has been managed in case reports with baclofen, which acts on the same receptor, and with benzodiazepines.",
  "fun": "Phenibut and baclofen differ by just one chlorine atom on the phenyl ring, and both act on the same GABA-B receptor.",
  "src": ["FDA warning letters on phenibut, 2019", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Lapin I. CNS Drug Reviews 2001"]
},
"csl-polyvalent-snake-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Antivenom began in the 1890s, when Albert Calmette of the Pasteur Institute showed that animals immunized with small, increasing amounts of cobra venom made serum that protected others. In Australia, the Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL), founded in Melbourne in 1916, released its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom, in 1930. Over the next three decades CSL added separate antivenoms for taipans, brown snakes, death adders and black snakes. Because doctors often could not tell which snake had bitten, CSL combined all five into a single polyvalent product in the early 1960s. It is made by immunizing horses, collecting their plasma and digesting the antibodies with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Today CSL Seqirus makes it for Australia and Papua New Guinea. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1916", "Commonwealth Serum Laboratories is founded in Melbourne."], ["1930", "CSL releases its first antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1960s", "CSL combines five Australian snake antivenoms into one polyvalent product."]],
  "cell": "The vial holds a mix of horse antibody fragments raised against five venoms: brown snake, tiger snake, death adder, taipan and black (mulga) snake. Each F(ab')2 fragment has two antigen-binding arms and no Fc tail. The fragments bind many different surface sites on venom toxins, including prothrombin activators that drive consumption coagulopathy, presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxins that destroy motor nerve terminals, postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic receptors, and myotoxins. Binding blocks the toxin's active site or prevents it reaching its target, and the antibody-toxin complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Antivenom works only on free, circulating venom. It cannot rebuild nerve terminals that a presynaptic toxin has already damaged, and it cannot replace clotting factors already consumed; the liver must make new ones.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing prothrombin activators stops further consumption of fibrinogen and factors V and VIII. Clotting tests then recover over hours as the liver makes new factors."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Binding neurotoxins halts progression of paralysis. Postsynaptic weakness can reverse, but presynaptic nerve terminal damage recovers slowly over days."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins limits further muscle breakdown, which reduces the risk of kidney injury from myoglobin."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein is foreign to humans, so early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure or wheezing can occur, and serum sickness can follow up to about two weeks later."], ["Volume and protein load", "Because it covers five venoms, a vial carries more foreign protein than a single-snake antivenom, which is why monovalent products are preferred when the snake is known."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is given only by slow intravenous infusion after dilution, in a monitored hospital. It enters the bloodstream at once and starts binding venom during the infusion. F(ab')2 fragments are about 100 kDa, too large to pass the kidney filter, so they mostly stay in blood and extracellular fluid and are broken down by proteolysis in the reticuloendothelial system. They persist for days, longer than smaller Fab fragments, which helps cover venom slowly leaking from the bite site.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from the human immune system meeting horse protein. Early reactions, from mild hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and through IgE in people already sensitized to horse proteins. Asthma, atopy and prior equine antivenom exposure raise the risk. Adrenaline (epinephrine) is the treatment for anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness is a type III reaction: the body makes antibodies against the horse fragments, immune complexes deposit in joints, skin and vessels, and fever, rash and joint pain appear days later, usually treated with corticosteroids.",
  "fun": "Australia uses a snake venom detection kit on swabs from the bite site to identify the snake group, so the smaller-volume monovalent antivenom can be chosen instead of polyvalent.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Polyvalent Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]
},
"csl-brown-snake-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Australian brown snakes (Pseudonaja), especially the eastern brown snake, cause most snakebite deaths in Australia. They live in farmland and on the edges of cities, where people meet them often. After releasing tiger snake antivenom in 1930, Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne worked through the 1950s to make antivenoms for other dangerous Australian snakes, and brown snake antivenom was introduced in that decade. Horses are immunized with eastern brown snake (Pseudonaja textilis) venom, and their antibodies are digested with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Brown snake venom is among the most toxic in mouse tests, but bites usually deliver only a small amount. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1916", "Commonwealth Serum Laboratories is founded in Melbourne."], ["1950s", "CSL introduces brown snake antivenom."], ["2000s", "Australian studies clarify that venom-induced consumption coagulopathy drives most brown snake deaths."]],
  "cell": "The main danger in brown snake venom is pseutarin C, a prothrombin activator that works like the human factor Xa plus factor Va complex. It converts prothrombin to thrombin in the bloodstream, and the thrombin uses up fibrinogen, factor V and factor VIII. This venom-induced consumption coagulopathy leaves the blood unable to clot. Some patients also develop thrombotic microangiopathy, with damaged red cells, low platelets and kidney injury. Brown snake F(ab')2 fragments bind the prothrombin activator and other venom components, blocking their active sites and forming complexes that the reticuloendothelial system removes. Binding stops further factor consumption, but the antivenom cannot replace factors already used up. Clotting recovers only as the liver makes new fibrinogen and factors, which takes many hours.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Stopping the prothrombin activator halts consumption of fibrinogen. INR and fibrinogen recover over hours as the liver resupplies clotting factors."], ["Heart and circulation", "Brown snake bites can cause early collapse or cardiac arrest within the first hour, likely from venom effects on circulation. Antivenom stops further venom action but supportive care treats the collapse."], ["Brain", "Unclotting blood raises the risk of intracranial bleeding, the most feared complication of brown snake envenomation."], ["Kidneys", "Thrombotic microangiopathy can injure the kidneys. Antivenom does not clearly reverse this once it has started."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness days to two weeks later."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and given by slow IV infusion in a monitored hospital. It enters the bloodstream immediately and binds free venom during the infusion. F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for the kidney to filter, so they stay mostly in the blood and tissue fluid and are cleared over days by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. That long presence helps capture venom still leaking from the bite site.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein, not from the venom. Early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure and wheezing arise through complement activation, mast cell release and, in people sensitized to horse proteins, IgE. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness is a type III immune complex reaction in which the body forms antibodies against the horse fragments, causing fever, rash, joint pain and swollen glands days later; corticosteroids are commonly used for it.",
  "fun": "Brown snake venom's prothrombin activator works so much like human clotting factors that a related brown snake protein has been studied as a possible surgical blood-clotting agent.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Brown Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Isbister GK et al. Australian Snakebite Project publications"]
},
"csl-tiger-snake-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Tiger snake antivenom was Australia's first commercial snake antivenom. Tiger snakes (Notechis scutatus) live in the cooler south of the country, including Victoria and Tasmania, and were a major cause of snakebite deaths in the early 20th century. Research on their venom in Melbourne in the 1920s, including work by the scientist Charles Kellaway at the Walter and Eliza Hall Institute, helped make an antivenom possible. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) released tiger snake antivenom in 1930, raised in horses. Later it was refined into pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments. Because related snakes have similar venoms, it is also used for copperheads, rough-scaled snakes, broad-headed snakes and the red-bellied black snake. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1920s", "Researchers in Melbourne study tiger snake venom and immunization."], ["1930", "CSL releases tiger snake antivenom, Australia's first commercial snake antivenom."], ["1950s", "Further CSL antivenoms follow for other Australian snakes."]],
  "cell": "Tiger snake venom has three main harmful parts. A prothrombin activator (notecarin) converts prothrombin to thrombin and drives consumption coagulopathy. Presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxins such as notexin enter motor nerve terminals, damage them and stop acetylcholine release, causing descending flaccid paralysis. Myotoxins break down skeletal muscle. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their active regions and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing free toxin stops further damage. However, once a presynaptic toxin has destroyed a nerve terminal, the antibody cannot fix it; the terminal must regrow over days. Likewise, clotting factors already consumed must be remade by the liver.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing the prothrombin activator halts fibrinogen consumption. Clotting recovers over hours as the liver makes new factors."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Binding notexin-like neurotoxins stops paralysis from getting worse. Paralysis that has already developed from nerve terminal damage recovers slowly."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins limits muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis), which lowers the risk of myoglobin-related kidney injury."], ["Kidneys", "Less muscle breakdown and less clotting damage help protect the kidneys from acute injury."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can trigger early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and given by slow IV infusion in hospital. It reaches the blood at once and binds circulating venom during the infusion. Its F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. This long presence helps catch venom that keeps seeping from the bite site.",
  "sidefx": "The side effects come from the immune system reacting to horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and through IgE in sensitized people. Asthma, atopy and prior equine antivenom raise the risk. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can cause fever, rash, joint pain and swollen glands up to about two weeks later and is usually treated with corticosteroids.",
  "fun": "Tiger snake antivenom is the usual choice for red-bellied black snake bites because it works well and comes in a smaller volume than black snake antivenom.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Tiger Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]
},
"csl-taipan-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Taipans (Oxyuranus) of northern Australia and Papua New Guinea were once one of the deadliest snakes in the region, and survival after a serious bite was rare. In 1950 Kevin Budden, a 20-year-old amateur snake collector, caught a coastal taipan near Cairns so it could be used for antivenom research. He was bitten and died the next day, but the snake was sent to Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne. Its venom was used to immunize horses, and CSL released taipan antivenom in the mid-1950s. The inland taipan, which has the most toxic venom of any land snake in mouse tests, is also covered. CSL Seqirus makes the antivenom today as horse F(ab')2 fragments. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1950", "Kevin Budden captures a coastal taipan for research and dies from its bite."], ["1950s", "CSL releases taipan antivenom raised in horses."]],
  "cell": "Taipan venom attacks blood, nerves and muscle. A prothrombin activator (oscutarin) converts prothrombin to thrombin and causes consumption coagulopathy. Taipoxin, a three-part presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxin, enters motor nerve terminals, destroys them and blocks acetylcholine release, causing descending flaccid paralysis. Postsynaptic three-finger neurotoxins block muscle nicotinic receptors, and myotoxins damage muscle. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Early antivenom can prevent paralysis, but it cannot undo nerve terminals taipoxin has already destroyed. Consumed clotting factors must be made again by the liver.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing taipoxin halts progressive paralysis of eyelids, swallowing and breathing muscles. Damage already done recovers slowly as nerve terminals regrow."], ["Blood clotting", "Stopping the prothrombin activator halts fibrinogen consumption, and clotting returns as the liver makes new factors."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins limits muscle breakdown and the myoglobin load on the kidneys."], ["Kidneys", "Taipan envenomation can cause thrombotic microangiopathy and kidney injury, which antivenom helps limit but may not reverse."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and infused slowly into a vein in a monitored hospital. It starts binding venom in the blood during the infusion. Its F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for kidney filtration and stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Early use matters most, because neurotoxins move quickly from blood into nerve terminals, where antibodies cannot reach.",
  "sidefx": "Adverse effects come from the horse protein. Early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure and wheezing arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and in sensitized people through IgE. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can cause fever, rash and joint pain up to about two weeks later, usually treated with corticosteroids.",
  "fun": "The snake that killed Kevin Budden was used to make Australia's first taipan antivenom, so his capture eventually saved many lives.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Taipan Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]
},
"csl-death-adder-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Death adders (Acanthophis) look like vipers, with thick bodies and wide heads, but they are elapids, related to cobras. They lie hidden in leaf litter and wiggle the tip of their tail like a worm to lure prey, which is why people often step on them. Before antivenom, death adder bites were frequently fatal. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne introduced death adder antivenom in the 1950s, raised in horses against the common death adder (Acanthophis antarcticus). It is now made by CSL Seqirus as pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments and is also used in Papua New Guinea. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1950s", "CSL introduces death adder antivenom."]],
  "cell": "Death adder venom acts mainly on the neuromuscular junction. Its key toxins are postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins, which bind the muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptor on the motor end plate and block acetylcholine from opening it. Without that signal, muscles cannot contract, producing descending flaccid paralysis: drooping eyelids, double vision, trouble swallowing and, in severe cases, breathing failure. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind the free neurotoxins and can also pull toxin off receptors because that binding is reversible. That makes death adder paralysis different from taipan or tiger snake paralysis: antivenom can actually reverse established weakness rather than just stop it. Unlike most other dangerous Australian snakes, death adder venom does not usually cause coagulopathy.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Removing postsynaptic toxins frees nicotinic receptors, so ptosis, bulbar weakness and limb weakness can improve after antivenom."], ["Breathing", "Reversing neuromuscular block helps the diaphragm and chest muscles recover, which can shorten or prevent mechanical ventilation."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Because the block is postsynaptic, neostigmine, which raises acetylcholine, can sometimes help alongside antivenom."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and infused slowly into a vein in hospital. It binds free neurotoxin in the blood right away and shifts the balance so toxin comes off receptors. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they remain in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Strength usually improves over hours.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions are caused by horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and through IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness is a type III immune complex reaction days later, with fever, rash and joint pain, usually treated with corticosteroids. Some Papua New Guinean death adder bites respond less completely, likely because of differences in venom.",
  "fun": "Death adders hunt by 'caudal luring,' twitching a pale tail tip like a grub to attract lizards and birds close enough to strike.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Death Adder Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Johnston CI et al. Australian Snakebite Project reports on death adder envenoming"]
},
"csl-black-snake-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Black snakes (Pseudechis) include the mulga snake, often called the king brown even though it belongs to the black snake genus. The mulga snake lives across most of mainland Australia and can deliver very large amounts of venom in one bite. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne introduced black snake antivenom in the 1950s, raised in horses mainly against mulga snake (Pseudechis australis) venom. The product is made as pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments and comes in a larger volume than most other CSL snake antivenoms. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1950s", "CSL introduces black snake antivenom raised against mulga snake venom."]],
  "cell": "Black snake venom differs from that of brown, tiger and taipan snakes. Its main toxins are phospholipase A2 myotoxins, which damage the membranes of skeletal muscle fibers and cause rhabdomyolysis, and anticoagulant toxins, which interfere with clotting without the massive factor consumption caused by prothrombin activators. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their enzyme activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing the myotoxins stops new muscle injury, but fibers already damaged must heal or be replaced. Neutralizing the anticoagulant toxins lets clotting tests return toward normal.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Neutralizing myotoxins halts muscle breakdown, with less pain, weakness and rise in creatine kinase."], ["Kidneys", "Less muscle breakdown means less myoglobin reaching the kidneys, lowering the risk of acute kidney injury."], ["Blood clotting", "Binding anticoagulant toxins lets clotting tests improve. This is a milder anticoagulant effect, not the consumptive coagulopathy of brown or tiger snakes."], ["Gut and general", "Black snake envenomation often causes nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and headache, which settle as venom is neutralized."], ["Immune system", "The large volume of horse protein raises the chance of early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and given by slow IV infusion in a monitored hospital. It binds venom in the blood during the infusion. The F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Because the vial volume is large, the infusion carries a sizable protein load.",
  "sidefx": "Side effects come from horse protein. Early reactions such as hives, low blood pressure and wheezing arise from complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. The larger protein volume of this product increases the chance of reactions. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear up to about two weeks later with fever, rash and joint pain, usually treated with corticosteroids.",
  "fun": "The mulga snake is called the king brown, but it is a black snake (Pseudechis), not a brown snake, which is why it needs black snake antivenom.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Black Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]
},
"csl-sea-snake-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Sea snakes live in warm Indo-Pacific waters, including around northern Australia and Southeast Asia, and most bites happen to fishers sorting nets. Many bites are nearly painless, so serious symptoms can come on hours later. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne developed sea snake antivenom in the early 1960s, raising it in horses with venom of the beaked sea snake (Enhydrina schistosa, now often called Hydrophis schistosus), a species responsible for many sea snake deaths in Asia. It is made as pepsin-digested F(ab')2 fragments, and laboratory studies show it neutralizes venoms of several other sea snakes. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first snake antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1960s", "CSL develops sea snake antivenom using beaked sea snake venom."]],
  "cell": "Sea snake venom has two main toxin groups. Phospholipase A2 myotoxins break down the membranes of skeletal muscle fibers, releasing myoglobin, potassium and creatine kinase into the blood. Postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins bind the muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptor and block neuromuscular transmission, causing weakness and paralysis. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind both groups at many sites, blocking their active regions and forming complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing myotoxins stops further muscle injury, and neutralizing neurotoxins allows receptors to work again. Muscle already damaged recovers only as fibers regenerate.",
  "body": [["Skeletal muscle", "Stopping myotoxins halts muscle breakdown, which causes pain, stiffness and weakness hours after the bite."], ["Kidneys", "Less myoglobin in the blood reduces the risk of kidney injury and dark urine."], ["Heart", "Muscle breakdown releases potassium. Halting it lowers the risk of dangerous hyperkalemia and heart rhythm problems."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing postsynaptic neurotoxins helps reverse weakness, drooping eyelids and trouble swallowing or breathing."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is diluted and infused slowly into a vein in hospital. It binds venom in the blood during the infusion. F(ab')2 fragments are too large to pass the kidney filter, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early hives, low blood pressure or wheezing arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain and is usually treated with corticosteroids.",
  "fun": "Sea snakes breathe air but can stay underwater for long periods, and some species can take in a little oxygen through their skin.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Sea Snake Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]
},
"csl-box-jellyfish-antivenom": {
  "origin": "The box jellyfish Chironex fleckeri lives in the tropical waters of northern Australia and nearby seas and is one of the most dangerous animals in the ocean. Its long tentacles carry millions of stinging cells. The species was named in 1956 in honor of Hugo Flecker, a Queensland doctor who investigated deaths from jellyfish stings. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne released box jellyfish antivenom around 1970. Unlike most CSL products it is raised in sheep, using venom collected from the jellyfish's stinging cells. Its benefit is debated: it binds venom strongly in the test tube, but animal studies question how well it prevents cardiovascular collapse. It is carried by the Queensland Ambulance Service and made by CSL Seqirus. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1956", "Chironex fleckeri is named in honor of Hugo Flecker."], ["1970s", "CSL introduces box jellyfish antivenom raised in sheep."]],
  "cell": "Each tentacle carries nematocysts, tiny capsules that fire a harpoon-like thread into skin and inject venom. The most harmful venom proteins (such as CfTX-1 and CfTX-2) form pores in cell membranes. Pores in red cells and heart and vascular cells let potassium and other ions leak out, which can cause sudden hyperkalemia, cardiac rhythm problems, low blood pressure and cardiovascular collapse within minutes. Pores in skin cells and nerves cause severe pain and skin damage. Sheep immunoglobulin binds these toxins and is intended to block them before they reach their targets, after which complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the venom acts so fast, much of the damage can happen before antivenom arrives, which may explain why clinical benefit is uncertain.",
  "body": [["Heart and circulation", "The venom can cause collapse and cardiac arrest within minutes. Antivenom is intended to reduce this, but its real-world benefit is debated."], ["Skin", "Venom causes intense pain and whip-like welts that can scar. Antivenom is used when pain is severe and not controlled by other measures."], ["Nerves", "Pore-forming toxins strongly activate pain nerves, causing severe burning pain."], ["Immune system", "Sheep protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness days later."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is given by IV injection or infusion after dilution, and Queensland ambulance protocols also describe an intraosseous route. It enters the bloodstream immediately. Because it is a whole or large immunoglobulin product it is expected to stay in the blood for days and be cleared by protein breakdown, but its pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from sheep protein. Early hives, low blood pressure or wheezing arise from complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in people sensitized to sheep proteins. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear about five to ten days later with fever, rash and joint pain. A strong stinging sensation during injection is also described. The antivenom does not treat Irukandji syndrome from small box jellyfish.",
  "fun": "Vinegar poured on a Chironex sting does not neutralize injected venom, but it stops the remaining unfired stinging cells on the skin from discharging.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Box Jellyfish Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Queensland Ambulance Service Drug Therapy Protocols", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)"]
},
"csl-redback-spider-antivenom": {
  "origin": "The redback spider (Latrodectus hasselti) is Australia's widow spider, a close relative of the American black widow. Bites cause latrodectism: severe local and spreading pain, sweating, nausea and high blood pressure that can last for days. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne introduced redback antivenom in 1956, raised in horses against venom milked from the spiders. For decades it was one of the most often used antivenoms in Australia. Its benefit was later questioned: the RAVE-II randomized trial, published in 2014, found it gave no more pain relief than placebo, and many clinicians now rely mainly on pain medicines. CSL Seqirus makes it today as horse F(ab')2 fragments. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1956", "CSL introduces redback spider antivenom."], ["2014", "The RAVE-II randomized trial finds no added pain relief over placebo."]],
  "cell": "The key toxin in redback venom is alpha-latrotoxin, a large protein that binds receptors on nerve terminals called neurexins and latrophilins. After binding it inserts into the membrane and forms a pore that lets calcium and other cations flood in. The surge of calcium triggers massive release of neurotransmitters from vesicles, including acetylcholine at muscles and norepinephrine at sympathetic nerves. This explains the pain, muscle cramps, sweating, high blood pressure and fast heart rate of latrodectism. The horse F(ab')2 fragments bind alpha-latrotoxin in tissue fluid and blood, blocking it from reaching receptors, and the complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Toxin already fixed to nerve terminals may be less reachable, which may explain inconsistent clinical results.",
  "body": [["Pain pathways", "Neurotransmitter release at nerve endings causes severe pain that spreads from the bite. Antivenom was intended to relieve it, but trials show little benefit over placebo."], ["Sympathetic nerves", "Norepinephrine release causes sweating (often only near the bite), fast heart rate and high blood pressure."], ["Muscles", "Acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions can cause cramps and muscle stiffness."], ["Gut and general", "Nausea, vomiting, headache and feeling unwell are common with systemic envenoming."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "Australian product information describes intramuscular injection for most cases, with IV use after dilution reserved for life-threatening envenoming. Given IM, the antibody fragments are absorbed slowly into the lymph and blood over hours; given IV, they enter the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Detailed pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions are caused by horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, come from complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people; the IV route is described as more likely to provoke them. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days to weeks later with fever, rash, joint pain and swollen glands.",
  "fun": "Redback and American black widow venoms are so similar that the Australian antivenom has been used for widow spider bites in other countries.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Redback Spider Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Isbister GK et al. RAVE-II trial, Ann Emerg Med 2014", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)"]
},
"funnel-web-spider-antivenom": {
  "origin": "The Sydney funnel-web spider (Atrax robustus) and its relatives in the genus Hadronyche live in eastern Australia. A bite, especially from a male, can cause life-threatening toxicity within an hour, and before antivenom there were recorded deaths, including children. The project to make an antivenom was led by Struan Sutherland at Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne. A key problem was that primates are very sensitive to the venom while many other mammals are not, so animals could be immunized safely. The team immunized rabbits and purified their IgG. The antivenom was released in 1980, and no deaths from funnel-web bites have been recorded in Australia since it came into use. Venom for production is collected by milking spiders, much of it at the Australian Reptile Park. CSL Seqirus makes it today. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1980", "CSL releases funnel-web spider antivenom developed by Struan Sutherland's team."], ["1981", "Funnel-web antivenom is first used successfully in a patient."]],
  "cell": "Funnel-web venom contains delta-hexatoxins (also called delta-atracotoxins, such as robustoxin). These toxins bind voltage-gated sodium channels on nerves and slow the channel's inactivation, so each nerve impulse lasts longer and nerves fire repeatedly. That causes massive release of acetylcholine and catecholamines from both somatic and autonomic nerves. The result is an autonomic storm: tingling around the mouth, muscle twitching, drooling, tearing, heavy sweating, very high blood pressure, fast heart rate and fluid in the lungs. Rabbit IgG binds the delta-hexatoxins in blood and tissue fluid, preventing them from reaching sodium channels, and the complexes are cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the toxin acts on channels rather than destroying nerves, symptoms can reverse quickly once it is neutralized.",
  "body": [["Autonomic nervous system", "Neutralizing delta-hexatoxins stops the flood of acetylcholine and adrenaline, so sweating, drooling, tearing and blood pressure swings settle."], ["Muscles", "Muscle twitching and spasms stop as repetitive nerve firing ends."], ["Lungs", "Severe envenoming can cause pulmonary edema. Reversing the autonomic storm helps the lungs clear."], ["Heart and vessels", "Dangerous hypertension and fast heart rate can be followed by low blood pressure in late severe cases; antivenom aims to prevent this progression."], ["Immune system", "Rabbit protein can cause allergic-type reactions, though these are reported as uncommon with this product."]],
  "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with sterile water and given by slow IV injection in hospital. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating toxin. Clinical improvement often begins within minutes to hours. Whole rabbit IgG is a large molecule, so it is not filtered by the kidneys and persists in the blood for days, being cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Pressure immobilization bandaging before arrival slows venom movement through the lymph.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from the immune system meeting rabbit protein. Early reactions such as hives or anaphylaxis can arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in people sensitized to rabbit products, but they are reported as uncommon. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, is possible up to about two weeks later with fever, rash and joint pain.",
  "fun": "Funnel-web venom is deadly to people and other primates, yet dogs, cats and rabbits are relatively resistant, which is why rabbits could be used to make the antivenom.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Funnel Web Spider Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins", "Isbister GK et al. Funnel-web spider bite: a systematic review. Med J Aust 2005"]
},
"stonefish-antivenom": {
  "origin": "Stonefish (Synanceia) live on reefs and shallow seabeds in the Indo-Pacific, including northern Australia. They look exactly like rocks, and people get stung when they step on them. Spines along the back inject venom, causing some of the worst pain known from any animal sting. Commonwealth Serum Laboratories (CSL) in Melbourne developed stonefish antivenom in the late 1950s, raised in horses against venom from the spines. It is one of very few antivenoms ever made against a fish. Hot water immersion remains the standard first aid, and the antivenom is reserved for severe envenoming. CSL Seqirus makes it today as horse F(ab')2 fragments. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1930", "CSL releases its first antivenom, against tiger snake venom."], ["1950s", "CSL develops stonefish antivenom raised in horses."]],
  "cell": "Stonefish venom contains large pore-forming proteins, stonustoxin (from Synanceia horrida) and verrucotoxin (from Synanceia verrucosa). These insert into cell membranes and form channels that let ions leak, damaging and killing cells near the sting. They strongly activate pain nerves, cause local swelling and tissue injury, and in severe cases cause low blood pressure, heart rhythm changes and breathing difficulty, partly through effects on vessel and heart cells. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins, block their pore-forming activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing free toxin relieves pain and systemic effects, but tissue already damaged must heal on its own.",
  "body": [["Pain nerves", "Venom causes intense, spreading pain within minutes. Antivenom often relieves severe pain within an hour when other measures fail."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Pore-forming toxins damage cells around the sting, causing swelling and sometimes tissue death. Antivenom limits further damage."], ["Heart and circulation", "Severe envenoming can lower blood pressure and disturb heart rhythm. Neutralizing circulating toxin helps reverse these effects."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness, especially with larger volumes."]],
  "journey": "Australian product information describes intramuscular injection as the preferred route, with IV infusion after dilution reserved for extreme cases. Given IM, the antibody fragments are absorbed into lymph and blood over hours; given IV, they enter the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Detailed pharmacokinetics in people are not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people; the IV route is described as more likely to provoke them. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain, and is more likely with larger volumes.",
  "fun": "Stonefish venom proteins break down with heat, which is one reason soaking the stung foot in hot water is the standard first aid.",
  "src": ["CSL Seqirus Stonefish Antivenom product information (Australia)", "Therapeutic Guidelines: Toxicology and Toxinology (Australia)", "Sutherland SK. Australian Animal Toxins"]
},
"saimr-polyvalent-snake-antivenom": {
  "origin": "The South African Institute for Medical Research (SAIMR) was founded in Johannesburg in 1912 and became the main maker of antivenom for southern Africa during the 20th century. Its polyvalent snake antivenom is raised in horses immunized with the venoms of ten dangerous local snakes: black, green and Jameson's mambas, Cape, forest, snouted and Mozambique spitting cobras, the rinkhals, the puff adder and the Gaboon adder. The horse antibodies are digested into F(ab')2 fragments. Production later moved to South African Vaccine Producers, a subsidiary of the National Health Laboratory Service, which keeps the SAIMR name on the product. It is supplied across southern Africa and to zoos and antivenom banks abroad. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1912", "The South African Institute for Medical Research is founded in Johannesburg."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]],
  "cell": "This product carries antibody fragments against three very different kinds of venom. Mamba venoms contain dendrotoxins, which block voltage-gated potassium channels on motor nerve terminals and boost acetylcholine release, and fasciculins, which inhibit acetylcholinesterase; together they cause twitching and then paralysis. Cobra and rinkhals venoms contain postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, plus cytotoxins that kill cells around the bite. Puff adder and Gaboon adder venoms are rich in metalloproteinases and other enzymes that destroy vessel walls and tissue, causing massive swelling, bleeding and blistering. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes removed by the reticuloendothelial system. They stop further progression but cannot rebuild tissue that has already died.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing mamba and cobra neurotoxins halts progressive paralysis and twitching, and postsynaptic weakness can improve over hours."], ["Breathing", "Stopping neurotoxins protects breathing muscles, though ventilation may still be needed in severe bites."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing cytotoxins and adder metalloproteinases halts rapidly spreading painful swelling, but tissue already dead does not recover."], ["Blood and vessels", "Adder venom damages small vessels, causing bleeding and fluid loss into the limb. Antivenom limits further damage."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein causes early reactions in a large share of patients, and serum sickness can follow."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is given intravenously, often diluted as an infusion, in a hospital; intraosseous use is described when veins cannot be reached, and intramuscular use is not effective. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating venom. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Detailed human pharmacokinetics are not well characterized.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein and are common with this product. Early reactions such as hives, vomiting, wheezing and low blood pressure arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and some South African protocols describe giving it in advance. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain. The product has no value for snakes not covered, such as the boomslang, berg adder and night adders.",
  "fun": "Most snakebites in southern Africa never need antivenom, because many bites are dry or cause only local symptoms.",
  "src": ["SAIMR Polyvalent Snake Antivenom product information (South African Vaccine Producers)", "African Snakebite Institute clinical resources", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites"]
},
"saimr-boomslang-antivenom": {
  "origin": "The boomslang (Dispholidus typus) is a slender, rear-fanged tree snake found across much of sub-Saharan Africa. Because its fangs sit at the back of the mouth and it is shy, it was long thought harmless to people. That changed in 1957, when the American herpetologist Karl Patterson Schmidt of the Field Museum in Chicago was bitten while identifying a young boomslang. He wrote careful notes about his symptoms and died the next day from internal bleeding. The South African Institute for Medical Research (SAIMR) in Johannesburg made a dedicated monovalent boomslang antivenom raised in horses, now produced by South African Vaccine Producers. It is scarce and expensive and is reserved for bites with laboratory evidence of coagulopathy. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1912", "The South African Institute for Medical Research is founded in Johannesburg."], ["1957", "Herpetologist Karl P. Schmidt dies after a boomslang bite and records his symptoms."]],
  "cell": "Boomslang venom is rich in metalloproteinases that activate prothrombin (and also factor X). Thrombin forms throughout the bloodstream and uses up fibrinogen and other clotting factors faster than the liver can make them. This venom-induced consumption coagulopathy leaves the blood unable to clot, along with low platelets, so bleeding starts from gums, wounds, the gut and the brain. Effects can be delayed for 24 to 48 hours. The horse F(ab')2 fragments are made specifically against boomslang venom; they bind the prothrombin activators, block them and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Once the venom is neutralized, the liver can rebuild clotting factors and bleeding stops. The polyvalent SAIMR product does not neutralize boomslang venom.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Stopping the prothrombin activators lets fibrinogen and other factors recover, so clotting tests normalize over hours."], ["Bleeding sites", "Gum, wound, gut and urinary bleeding stops as clotting returns, and the risk of brain bleeding falls."], ["Kidneys", "Severe envenoming can damage the kidneys, partly from bleeding and microangiopathy. Neutralizing venom helps limit this."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is given intravenously in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days because they are too large for kidney filtration, and they are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Clotting tests begin to improve over hours. Blood products alone generally fail while venom is still active, because the venom keeps consuming new factors.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, and IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain. Because supply is very limited, the antivenom is kept for confirmed boomslang bites with coagulopathy.",
  "fun": "Karl P. Schmidt's diary of his own fatal boomslang bite is one of the most famous documents in herpetology, recording each symptom until shortly before he died.",
  "src": ["SAIMR Boomslang Antivenom product information (South African Vaccine Producers)", "African Snakebite Institute clinical resources", "Warrell DA. Snake bite. Lancet 2010"]
},
"echitab-plus-icp": {
  "origin": "EchiTAb-Plus-ICP was born from a partnership to fight snakebite in West Africa, where the carpet viper (Echis ocellatus) causes huge numbers of bites in the savanna belt of Nigeria. In the early 2000s the EchiTAb Study Group brought together the Nigerian Federal Ministry of Health, the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine and the Instituto Clodomiro Picado (ICP) at the University of Costa Rica. ICP, founded in 1970 and named after the Costa Rican scientist Clodomiro Picado Twight, had long experience making antivenom for Central American snakes. It developed a horse antivenom against carpet viper, puff adder and black-necked spitting cobra venoms, purified by caprylic acid precipitation as whole IgG. A randomized trial in Nigeria published in 2010 compared it with a sheep-derived antivenom. It is licensed in Nigeria and other African countries but is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1970", "The Instituto Clodomiro Picado is founded at the University of Costa Rica."], ["2000s", "The EchiTAb Study Group develops antivenoms for Nigerian snakebite."], ["2010", "Abubakar and colleagues publish a randomized trial of EchiTAb-Plus-ICP in Nigeria."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]],
  "cell": "Carpet viper venom contains metalloproteinases that activate prothrombin, similar to the reference enzyme ecarin. They generate thrombin throughout the bloodstream, which consumes fibrinogen and leaves the blood unable to clot, and hemorrhagins damage small vessel walls so blood leaks out. Puff adder venom adds metalloproteinases that cause massive swelling and bleeding. Black-necked spitting cobra venom contains three-finger cytotoxins and phospholipase A2 that destroy cells and cause local necrosis. EchiTAb-Plus-ICP is whole horse IgG, with both antigen-binding arms and the Fc tail kept. It binds these toxins at many sites, blocks their activity and forms immune complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing the procoagulant enzymes lets the liver restore clotting factors.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing carpet viper prothrombin activators restores clotting, usually followed with the 20-minute whole blood clotting test."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking hemorrhagins stops further leaking from damaged vessels, which reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal organs."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing adder and spitting cobra toxins limits further swelling and tissue death, but dead tissue does not recover."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The antivenom is given by slow IV injection or infusion in a health facility. It binds venom in the blood at once. Whole IgG is about 150 kDa, so it is not filtered by the kidneys and has a long half-life, persisting for days and staying around longer than Fab or F(ab')2 fragments. That long presence helps cover venom still leaking from the bite site and lowers the chance of coagulopathy returning. It is cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions such as hives, vomiting, wheezing and low blood pressure arise through complement activation, which aggregates and the retained Fc portion can trigger, and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.",
  "fun": "An antivenom for West African snakes is made in Costa Rica, an example of how venom science from one continent was shared to help another.",
  "src": ["Instituto Clodomiro Picado product information", "Abubakar IS et al. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2010", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]
},
"indian-polyvalent-anti-snake-venom": {
  "origin": "India has more snakebite deaths than any other country, with tens of thousands each year according to the Million Death Study and later estimates. Most deaths come from the 'big four': the Indian cobra, the common krait, Russell's viper and the saw-scaled viper. Antivenom production in India grew out of public health institutes such as the Haffkine Institute in Mumbai, founded in 1899 by Waldemar Haffkine. Today several manufacturers, including Bharat Serums and Vaccines, VINS Bioproducts, Haffkine Bio-Pharmaceutical and Premium Serums, make a freeze-dried polyvalent antivenom raised in horses against the big four venoms. Much of the venom comes from the Irula snake catchers of Tamil Nadu, who formed a cooperative in 1978. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1899", "Waldemar Haffkine founds the laboratory that becomes the Haffkine Institute in Mumbai."], ["1978", "The Irula Snake Catchers Industrial Cooperative Society is founded in Tamil Nadu."], ["2011", "The Million Death Study estimates about 46,000 snakebite deaths in India in 2005."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]],
  "cell": "The four venoms work in different ways. Cobra venom carries postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, plus cytotoxins that cause local necrosis. Krait venom carries beta-bungarotoxin, a presynaptic phospholipase A2 that enters motor nerve terminals, destroys them and stops acetylcholine release, causing paralysis that is slow to recover. Russell's viper venom contains activators of factor X and factor V, and saw-scaled viper venom contains prothrombin activators; both drive consumption coagulopathy, and Russell's viper venom also injures the kidneys. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. They stop further damage but cannot regrow destroyed nerve terminals or replace consumed clotting factors.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing viper procoagulants stops factor consumption. Clotting often returns within hours as the liver makes new factors."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing cobra toxins can improve weakness, but krait paralysis from destroyed nerve terminals recovers slowly over days."], ["Breathing", "Neurotoxic bites can stop breathing. Antivenom halts progression, but ventilation is often still needed."], ["Kidneys", "Russell's viper bites often cause acute kidney injury. Early antivenom helps limit it."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing cobra and viper toxins limits spreading swelling and necrosis."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein frequently causes early reactions, and serum sickness can follow."]],
  "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with sterile water and given by slow IV injection or infusion; intramuscular use is not recommended because absorption is poor. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating venom. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein and are common. Early reactions such as hives, fever, rigors, vomiting and wheezing arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis; skin or eye sensitivity tests do not predict reactions and are not recommended. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later. Venom differs between regions of India, which can reduce effectiveness, and the product does not cover snakes such as hump-nosed pit vipers or king cobras.",
  "fun": "The Irula people of Tamil Nadu, traditional snake catchers, now supply much of the venom used to make India's antivenom through their own cooperative.",
  "src": ["Indian polyvalent antivenom product information (Indian manufacturers)", "Mohapatra B et al. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2011 (Million Death Study)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites"]
},
"qsmi-king-cobra-antivenin": {
  "origin": "The king cobra (Ophiophagus hannah) is the longest venomous snake in the world, and its name means snake eater because it feeds mostly on other snakes. A bite delivers a very large amount of venom, and paralysis can develop within about an hour. The Queen Saovabha Memorial Institute (QSMI) in Bangkok, run by the Thai Red Cross Society, was founded in the 1920s and became famous for its snake farm and antivenom production. It makes a freeze-dried monovalent king cobra antivenin raised in horses, digested into F(ab')2 fragments. Because king cobras are kept in zoos worldwide, it is also stocked by some international zoo and poison center antivenom banks. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first cobra antivenom."], ["1920s", "The Thai Red Cross founds the institute in Bangkok that becomes QSMI, with a snake farm for antivenom."]],
  "cell": "King cobra venom is dominated by three-finger alpha-neurotoxins, both short-chain and long-chain types. These bind the muscle-type nicotinic acetylcholine receptor on the motor end plate and block acetylcholine from opening it, so nerve signals cannot make muscles contract. The result is descending flaccid paralysis, starting with drooping eyelids and spreading to swallowing and breathing muscles. The venom also contains metalloproteinases and L-amino acid oxidase that damage tissue near the bite. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins, block their active sites and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because alpha-neurotoxin binding is postsynaptic and partly reversible, neutralizing free toxin can help receptors recover.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing alpha-neurotoxins halts descending paralysis of eyelids, face, throat and limbs."], ["Breathing", "Severe bites can cause respiratory failure within an hour. Antivenom limits progression, but airway support and ventilation are central to survival."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing tissue-damaging enzymes limits local swelling."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Because the block is postsynaptic, neostigmine with atropine can transiently improve strength alongside antivenom."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given by slow IV injection or infusion in a hospital with ventilator support. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Strength improves over hours.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.",
  "fun": "King cobras are the only snakes known to build nests, piling leaves into a mound where the female guards her eggs.",
  "src": ["QSMI King Cobra Antivenin product information (Thai Red Cross)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites in South-East Asia", "Warrell DA. Snake bite. Lancet 2010"]
},
"qsmi-neuro-polyvalent-antivenin": {
  "origin": "Thailand's most dangerous neurotoxic snakes are the monocled cobra, the king cobra and two kraits, the Malayan krait and the banded krait. In real cases the snake is often not seen, especially with kraits, which bite at night while people sleep on the floor. The Queen Saovabha Memorial Institute (QSMI) in Bangkok, run by the Thai Red Cross Society, already made monovalent antivenins for single species. To cover bites where the snake is unknown, it developed a neuro polyvalent antivenin raised in horses against all four venoms, freeze-dried as F(ab')2 fragments. It is used in Thailand and other Southeast Asian countries. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1920s", "The Thai Red Cross founds the Bangkok institute that becomes QSMI."]],
  "cell": "The four venoms cause paralysis in two different ways. Cobra and king cobra venoms carry postsynaptic three-finger alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. That block is partly reversible, so neutralizing free toxin can let receptors recover. Krait venoms carry beta-bungarotoxin, a presynaptic phospholipase A2 toxin that enters motor nerve terminals, damages them and stops acetylcholine release. Once a terminal is destroyed, antibodies cannot fix it, and strength returns only as nerve terminals regrow over days. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind all these toxins in blood and tissue fluid, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Early treatment matters most for krait bites, before toxin reaches the nerve terminals.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing neurotoxins halts descending paralysis. Cobra paralysis can improve over hours; krait paralysis recovers slowly."], ["Breathing", "Neurotoxic bites can cause respiratory failure, and airway protection and mechanical ventilation are central to survival."], ["Neuromuscular junction", "Neostigmine with atropine can transiently help cobra weakness but is less helpful for krait bites because the problem is presynaptic."], ["Skin and limbs", "Cobra cytotoxins can cause local swelling and necrosis. Krait bites are often nearly painless with little local change."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given by slow IV injection or infusion in a hospital with ventilator support. It binds venom in the blood right away. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.",
  "fun": "Beta-bungarotoxin and alpha-bungarotoxin from kraits became classic research tools; alpha-bungarotoxin was used to identify and count nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction.",
  "src": ["QSMI Neuro Polyvalent Snake Antivenin product information (Thai Red Cross)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites in South-East Asia", "Katzung Basic & Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"qsmi-hemato-polyvalent-antivenin": {
  "origin": "In Thailand the main snakes that cause bleeding and clotting failure are the Malayan pit viper, the Eastern Russell's viper and the white-lipped green pit viper. The Queen Saovabha Memorial Institute (QSMI) in Bangkok, run by the Thai Red Cross Society, made separate antivenins for these species and later combined them into a hemato polyvalent antivenin raised in horses and freeze-dried as F(ab')2 fragments. It is used when one of these vipers is suspected but cannot be identified. It is used in Thailand and other Southeast Asian countries. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1920s", "The Thai Red Cross founds the Bangkok institute that becomes QSMI."]],
  "cell": "Each viper disrupts clotting in a different way. Russell's viper venom contains enzymes that activate factor X and factor V, generating thrombin throughout the bloodstream. Malayan pit viper and green pit viper venoms contain thrombin-like serine proteases that clip fibrinogen directly into weak fibrin that is quickly broken down. Both routes use up fibrinogen and leave the blood unable to clot. Snake venom metalloproteinases damage small vessel walls, causing bleeding and swelling, and phospholipase A2 adds tissue injury. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Clotting then recovers over hours as the liver makes new fibrinogen and factors.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing procoagulant and thrombin-like enzymes stops fibrinogen consumption, and clotting usually improves over 6 to 24 hours."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking metalloproteinases reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal sites."], ["Kidneys", "Russell's viper bites often cause acute kidney injury. Neutralizing venom early helps limit it."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing local toxins limits spreading swelling and blistering."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given by slow IV injection or infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration, so they stay in the blood and tissue fluid for days and are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Coagulopathy can return after initial correction as venom keeps leaking from the bite, so clotting is rechecked.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain.",
  "fun": "A thrombin-like enzyme from Malayan pit viper venom, called ancrod, was studied for decades as a drug to thin fibrinogen in stroke.",
  "src": ["QSMI Hemato Polyvalent Snake Antivenin product information (Thai Red Cross)", "WHO Guidelines for the Management of Snakebites in South-East Asia", "Warrell DA. Snake bite. Lancet 2010"]
},
"viperatab": {
  "origin": "The European adder (Vipera berus) is the only venomous snake native to the United Kingdom and is found across much of northern Europe and Asia. Bites are rarely fatal but can cause marked swelling, vomiting, low blood pressure and angioedema. For many years UK hospitals used an older horse antivenom made in Zagreb. ViperaTAb was developed in the 1990s using the same sheep Fab approach later used in the US product CroFab: sheep are immunized with adder venom, the antibodies are purified by affinity binding to venom, and papain cuts them into small Fab fragments. It was used in Scandinavia and became the standard adder antivenom in the UK in the 2010s. MicroPharm in Wales makes it. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom."], ["1990s", "Ovine Fab adder antivenom is developed and studied in Europe."], ["2010s", "ViperaTAb becomes the standard adder antivenom in UK practice."]],
  "cell": "Adder venom is a mix of phospholipase A2, snake venom metalloproteinases, serine proteases and other proteins. These damage small vessel walls and raise their permeability, so fluid leaks into tissues, causing spreading limb swelling and, if widespread, low blood pressure. Some components trigger release of inflammatory mediators, which can cause vomiting, diarrhea and angioedema, and mild coagulopathy can occur. ViperaTAb is affinity-purified sheep Fab: single antigen-binding arms of about 50 kDa without the Fc tail. They bind venom proteins at many sites, block their activity and form small complexes. Because the product is monospecific for Vipera berus, its effect on other European vipers is less certain.",
  "body": [["Limbs and soft tissue", "Neutralizing venom limits progression of limb swelling. Because Fab clears quickly, swelling can progress again and extra treatment may be needed."], ["Heart and circulation", "Neutralizing vessel-damaging toxins reverses hypotension and shock in severe bites."], ["Gut", "Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea from venom-driven mediator release settle as venom is neutralized."], ["Airway and skin", "Angioedema of the face and lips from the venom can improve after antivenom."], ["Immune system", "Reactions to sheep Fab are reported as uncommon, but allergy to sheep protein or papain raises the risk."]],
  "journey": "ViperaTAb is diluted and given by IV infusion in hospital. It enters the blood at once and spreads widely into tissues because Fab fragments are small. At about 50 kDa they are filtered by the kidneys and cleared much faster than F(ab')2 or whole IgG. That short half-life means venom still leaking from the bite site can cause swelling to return after the antivenom has gone.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from sheep protein. Early hives or anaphylaxis can occur through mast cell release and IgE in sensitized people, but removing the Fc portion and affinity purification lower the foreign protein load, so reactions are reported as uncommon. Papain used in manufacturing may matter for people with papain allergy. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness is uncommon. Recurrence of swelling reflects fast Fab clearance, not a reaction.",
  "fun": "The European adder ranges farther north than any other snake, reaching beyond the Arctic Circle in Scandinavia.",
  "src": ["ViperaTAb product information (MicroPharm)", "UK TOXBASE guidance on adder bites", "Karlson-Stiber C et al. J Intern Med 1997"]
},
"antivipmyn": {
  "origin": "In Mexico and Central America, pit vipers such as rattlesnakes (Crotalus) and the terciopelo or fer-de-lance (Bothrops asper) cause most serious snakebites. Instituto Bioclon in Mexico City, part of the Silanes group, developed Antivipmyn, a horse antivenom made by immunizing horses with Crotalus and Bothrops venoms and digesting the antibodies with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Bioclon called this approach fabotherapy. It works with researchers at the National Autonomous University of Mexico (UNAM). Bioclon's experience led to Anavip, a related equine F(ab')2 rattlesnake antivenom that FDA approved in 2015. Antivipmyn itself is licensed in Mexico and other Latin American countries but is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom."], ["2015", "FDA approves Anavip, a related equine F(ab')2 pit viper antivenom made by Bioclon."]],
  "cell": "Pit viper venoms are rich in snake venom metalloproteinases, which break down the basement membrane of small vessels, causing bleeding, swelling and blistering. Thrombin-like serine proteases cut fibrinogen into unstable fibrin, using it up and causing clotting failure. Phospholipase A2 enzymes damage muscle and, in some Crotalus venoms such as Mojave and South American rattlesnakes, form crotoxin-like neurotoxins that block acetylcholine release. Antivipmyn's horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Neutralizing free venom stops further swelling and lets the liver restore fibrinogen, but tissue already destroyed must heal on its own.",
  "body": [["Limbs and soft tissue", "Neutralizing metalloproteinases halts progressive swelling, bruising and blistering from the bite."], ["Blood clotting", "Blocking thrombin-like enzymes stops fibrinogen consumption, and clotting improves over hours."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Neutralizing phospholipase A2 limits muscle injury and, for neurotoxic rattlesnakes, weakness."], ["Kidneys", "Less muscle breakdown and bleeding reduce the risk of kidney injury."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness 5 to 15 days later."]],
  "journey": "The lyophilized powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent, further diluted and given by IV infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood immediately. F(ab')2 fragments, about 100 kDa, are too large for kidney filtration and spread through the blood and tissue fluid more widely than whole IgG. They persist longer than Fab fragments, which helps prevent late return of coagulopathy as venom keeps seeping from the bite site. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early type I and anaphylactoid reactions, from rash to bronchospasm, arise through mast cell release, complement activation and IgE in sensitized people; pepsin digestion removes the Fc portion, which lowers this risk compared with whole IgG. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear 5 to 15 days later with hives and joint pain.",
  "fun": "Bioclon's approach to antivenom in Mexico led to two US-approved products: Anascorp for scorpion stings and Anavip for rattlesnake bites.",
  "src": ["Antivipmyn product information (Instituto Bioclon, Mexico)", "Anavip (crotalidae immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]
},
"alacramyn": {
  "origin": "Mexico has one of the highest rates of scorpion stings in the world, with hundreds of thousands of stings reported each year, mostly from bark scorpions of the genus Centruroides. Severe stings can be deadly in small children. Instituto Bioclon in Mexico City, working with scientists at the National Autonomous University of Mexico (UNAM) developed Alacramyn, a horse antivenom digested with pepsin into F(ab')2 fragments. Its wide use in Mexico was followed by a large fall in scorpion sting deaths. A closely related Bioclon product, Anascorp, was tested in Arizona and approved by FDA in 2011 for Centruroides scorpion envenomation. Alacramyn itself is licensed in Mexico, but it is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["2011", "FDA approves Anascorp, a related Bioclon scorpion antivenom, for US use."]],
  "cell": "Centruroides venom contains small peptide neurotoxins. Beta-toxins bind voltage-gated sodium channels on nerves and shift their activation to more negative voltages, so the channels open too easily and nerves fire over and over. Other toxins block potassium channels, further prolonging nerve activity. The result is overactivity of both somatic and autonomic nerves: muscle jerking, roving eye movements, drooling, sweating, fast heart rate and, in severe cases, breathing trouble. Alacramyn's horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins in blood and tissue fluid, block them from reaching channels and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Because the toxins act on channels rather than destroying nerves, symptoms can resolve within hours once neutralized.",
  "body": [["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing sodium channel toxins stops muscle jerking, agitation and uncoordinated movements."], ["Eyes", "Roving or jerky eye movements settle as nerve overactivity ends."], ["Autonomic nervous system", "Drooling, sweating, fast heart rate and high blood pressure resolve as excess transmitter release stops."], ["Breathing", "Severe stings can cause airway secretions and breathing failure, especially in small children. Antivenom reverses these effects."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and serum sickness 5 to 15 days later."]],
  "journey": "The lyophilized powder is reconstituted with the supplied diluent and given intravenously; product information describes IV as ideal and IM as less effective. It enters the blood at once and binds circulating toxin. Symptoms often improve within one to four hours. F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration and stay in the blood and tissue fluid longer than Fab fragments. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early type I and anaphylactoid reactions, from rash to bronchospasm, come from mast cell release, complement activation and IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear 5 to 15 days later with hives and joint pain. Sedatives used for agitation before antivenom can add to breathing depression.",
  "fun": "Scorpions glow blue-green under ultraviolet light because of compounds in their outer shell, which helps people spot them at night.",
  "src": ["Alacramyn product information (Instituto Bioclon, Mexico)", "Anascorp (centruroides immune F(ab')2 equine) FDA label", "Boyer LV et al. N Engl J Med 2009"]
},
"inoserp-pan-africa": {
  "origin": "Sub-Saharan Africa has long suffered from a shortage of affordable, effective antivenom, and WHO named snakebite envenoming a neglected tropical disease in 2017. Inoserp Pan-Africa was developed by Inosan Biopharma as a single product for many of Africa's most dangerous snakes. Horses are immunized with venoms of carpet vipers, the puff adder, the Gaboon viper, several cobras and spitting cobras and four mambas, and the antibodies are digested into freeze-dried F(ab')2 fragments. It was introduced in several African countries in the 2010s. Independent laboratory studies have reported variable potency against some African venoms, and WHO has stressed independent testing of African antivenoms. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom."], ["2010s", "Inoserp Pan-Africa is introduced in several African countries."], ["2017", "WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."], ["2019", "WHO launches a strategy aiming to halve snakebite deaths by 2030."]],
  "cell": "The product targets both viper and elapid venoms. Carpet viper (Echis) venoms contain prothrombin-activating metalloproteinases that consume fibrinogen and make the blood unable to clot, plus hemorrhagins that damage vessel walls. Puff adder and Gaboon viper venoms contain metalloproteinases and other enzymes that cause massive swelling and bleeding. Spitting cobra venoms carry three-finger cytotoxins that kill cells around the bite. Cobra venoms carry postsynaptic alpha-neurotoxins that block muscle nicotinic receptors, and mamba venoms carry dendrotoxins, which block potassium channels to increase acetylcholine release, and fasciculins, which inhibit acetylcholinesterase. Horse F(ab')2 fragments bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing carpet viper procoagulants lets clotting recover, typically over 6 to 24 hours."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking hemorrhagins reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal sites."], ["Skin and limbs", "Neutralizing cytotoxins and adder enzymes limits spreading swelling and tissue death, but dead tissue does not recover."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Neutralizing cobra and mamba neurotoxins halts progressive paralysis, though ventilation may still be needed."], ["Immune system", "Horse protein can cause early allergic-type reactions and later serum sickness."]],
  "journey": "The freeze-dried powder is reconstituted and given by slow IV injection or infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. The F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration and spread through the blood and tissue fluid, persisting longer than Fab fragments. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Early reactions, from hives to anaphylaxis, arise through complement activation and mast cell release, plus IgE in sensitized people. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, can appear days later with fever, rash and joint pain. Weak potency against a particular venom would mean more vials are needed or the venom is not fully neutralized.",
  "fun": "WHO estimates that snakebites kill tens of thousands of people each year worldwide, many of them farmers and children in rural Africa and Asia.",
  "src": ["Inoserp Pan-Africa product information (Inosan Biopharma)", "WHO Snakebite Envenoming: A Strategy for Prevention and Control, 2019", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]
},
"brazilian-antibothropic-serum": {
  "origin": "Brazilian antivenom began with the physician Vital Brazil, who started making serums at the Instituto Butantan in Sao Paulo, founded in 1901. Albert Calmette in France had made a cobra antivenom and hoped one serum might work for all snakes. Vital Brazil showed that antivenoms are specific: serum against lancehead (Bothrops) venom protected against lanceheads but not rattlesnakes, and the reverse. He developed separate antibothropic and anticrotalic serums, which shaped antivenom science worldwide. Lanceheads, especially the jararaca, cause most snakebites in Brazil. Today Butantan makes a pentavalent antibothropic serum raised in horses against five Bothrops venoms and processed into F(ab')2 fragments. Brazil's public health system supplies it free. It is not FDA approved and is not available in the US.",
  "timeline": [["1890s", "Albert Calmette develops the first snake antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1901", "Vital Brazil begins serum production at the Instituto Butantan in Sao Paulo."], ["1965", "Sergio Ferreira describes bradykinin-potentiating peptides in jararaca venom."], ["1981", "Captopril, designed from jararaca venom research, is approved by FDA."]],
  "cell": "Bothrops venoms combine several harmful enzyme groups. Snake venom metalloproteinases (hemorrhagins) digest the basement membrane of small vessels, causing bleeding, swelling and blistering. Thrombin-like serine proteases cut fibrinogen directly, and prothrombin and factor X activators generate thrombin in the bloodstream; both routes consume fibrinogen and leave the blood unable to clot. Phospholipase A2 myotoxins damage muscle near the bite and can lead to necrosis. The horse F(ab')2 fragments, made by pepsin digestion that removes the Fc portion, bind these toxins at many sites, block their activity and form complexes cleared by the reticuloendothelial system. Coagulation typically normalizes within about 24 hours, but local tissue damage already done is not reversed.",
  "body": [["Blood clotting", "Neutralizing procoagulant enzymes stops fibrinogen consumption, and clotting typically normalizes within about a day."], ["Blood vessels", "Blocking metalloproteinases reduces bleeding from gums, wounds and internal organs."], ["Limbs and soft tissue", "Neutralizing venom limits further swelling, blistering and muscle death, but existing necrosis does not reverse."], ["Kidneys", "Bothrops envenoming can cause acute kidney injury. Early neutralization helps limit it."], ["Immune system", "Mild early reactions such as itching and hives are very common, and serum sickness can follow 5 to 24 days later."]],
  "journey": "The liquid antivenom is diluted in saline or glucose solution and given by IV infusion in hospital. It binds venom in the blood at once. F(ab')2 fragments are too large for kidney filtration and spread through blood and tissue fluid, persisting longer than Fab fragments. They are cleared by protein breakdown in the reticuloendothelial system. Coagulation usually improves over the first day.",
  "sidefx": "Reactions come from horse protein. Mild early reactions such as itching, hives and flushing are very common, from mast cell release and complement activation; severe reactions such as airway swelling and shock are rare. Adrenaline (epinephrine) treats anaphylaxis, and beta-blockers can blunt its effect. Serum sickness, a type III immune complex reaction, is common and causes fever, rash, joint swelling and swollen glands 5 to 24 days later. Skin testing is not recommended because it does not predict reactions.",
  "fun": "Research on jararaca venom peptides that strengthen the effect of bradykinin led directly to captopril, the first ACE inhibitor.",
  "src": ["Instituto Butantan Soro Antibotropico product information", "Ferreira SH. Br J Pharmacol 1965", "WHO Guidelines for the Production, Control and Regulation of Snake Antivenom Immunoglobulins"]
}
});

;
